if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-98"]="It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it was the age of wisdom, it was the age of foolishness, it was the epoch of belief, it was the epoch of incredulity, it was the season of Light, it was the season of Darkness, it was the spring of hope, it was the winter of despair, we had everything before us, we had nothing before us, we were all going direct to Heaven, we were all going direct the other way--in short, the period was so far like the present period, that some of its noisiest authorities insisted on its being received, for good or for evil, in the superlative degree of comparison only.\n\nThere were a king with a large jaw and a queen with a plain face, on the throne of England; there were a king with a large jaw and a queen with a fair face, on the throne of France. In both countries it was clearer than crystal to the lords of the State preserves of loaves and fishes, that things in general were settled for ever.\n\nIt was the year of Our Lord one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five. Spiritual revelations were conceded to England at that favoured period, as at this. Mrs. Southcott had recently attained her five-and-twentieth blessed birthday, of whom a prophetic private in the Life Guards had heralded the sublime appearance by announcing that arrangements were made for the swallowing up of London and Westminster. Even the Cock-lane ghost had been laid only a round dozen of years, after rapping out its messages, as the spirits of this very year last past (supernaturally deficient in originality) rapped out theirs. Mere messages in the earthly order of events had lately come to the English Crown and People, from a congress of British subjects in America: which, strange to relate, have proved more important to the human race than any communications yet received through any of the chickens of the Cock-lane brood.\n\nFrance, less favoured on the whole as to matters spiritual than her sister of the shield and trident, rolled with exceeding smoothness down hill, making paper money and spending it. Under the guidance of her Christian pastors, she entertained herself, besides, with such humane achievements as sentencing a youth to have his hands cut off, his tongue torn out with pincers, and his body burned alive, because he had not kneeled down in the rain to do honour to a dirty procession of monks which passed within his view, at a distance of some fifty or sixty yards. It is likely enough that, rooted in the woods of France and Norway, there were growing trees, when that sufferer was put to death, already marked by the Woodman, Fate, to come down and be sawn into boards, to make a certain movable framework with a sack and a knife in it, terrible in history. It is likely enough that in the rough outhouses of some tillers of the heavy lands adjacent to Paris, there were sheltered from the weather that very day, rude carts, bespattered with rustic mire, snuffed about by pigs, and roosted in by poultry, which the Farmer, Death, had already set apart to be his tumbrils of the Revolution. But that Woodman and that Farmer, though they work unceasingly, work silently, and no one heard them as they went about with muffled tread: the rather, forasmuch as to entertain any suspicion that they were awake, was to be atheistical and traitorous.\n\nIn England, there was scarcely an amount of order and protection to justify much national boasting. Daring burglaries by armed men, and highway robberies, took place in the capital itself every night; families were publicly cautioned not to go out of town without removing their furniture to upholsterers’ warehouses for security; the highwayman in the dark was a City tradesman in the light, and, being recognised and challenged by his fellow-tradesman whom he stopped in his character of “the Captain,” gallantly shot him through the head and rode away; the mail was waylaid by seven robbers, and the guard shot three dead, and then got shot dead himself by the other four, “in consequence of the failure of his ammunition:” after which the mail was robbed in peace; that magnificent potentate, the Lord Mayor of London, was made to stand and deliver on Turnham Green, by one highwayman, who despoiled the illustrious creature in sight of all his retinue; prisoners in London gaols fought battles with their turnkeys, and the majesty of the law fired blunderbusses in among them, loaded with rounds of shot and ball; thieves snipped off diamond crosses from the necks of noble lords at Court drawing-rooms; musketeers went into St. Giles’s, to search for contraband goods, and the mob fired on the musketeers, and the musketeers fired on the mob, and nobody thought any of these occurrences much out of the common way. In the midst of them, the hangman, ever busy and ever worse than useless, was in constant requisition; now, stringing up long rows of miscellaneous criminals; now, hanging a housebreaker on Saturday who had been taken on Tuesday; now, burning people in the hand at Newgate by the dozen, and now burning pamphlets at the door of Westminster Hall; to-day, taking the life of an atrocious murderer, and to-morrow of a wretched pilferer who had robbed a farmer’s boy of sixpence.\n\nAll these things, and a thousand like them, came to pass in and close upon the dear old year one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five. Environed by them, while the Woodman and the Farmer worked unheeded, those two of the large jaws, and those other two of the plain and the fair faces, trod with stir enough, and carried their divine rights with a high hand. Thus did the year one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five conduct their Greatnesses, and myriads of small creatures--the creatures of this chronicle among the rest--along the roads that lay before them.\n\n# CHAPTER II. The Mail\n\nIt was the Dover road that lay, on a Friday night late in November, before the first of the persons with whom this history has business. The Dover road lay, as to him, beyond the Dover mail, as it lumbered up Shooter’s Hill. He walked up hill in the mire by the side of the mail, as the rest of the passengers did; not because they had the least relish for walking exercise, under the circumstances, but because the hill, and the harness, and the mud, and the mail, were all so heavy, that the horses had three times already come to a stop, besides once drawing the coach across the road, with the mutinous intent of taking it back to Blackheath. Reins and whip and coachman and guard, however, in combination, had read that article of war which forbade a purpose otherwise strongly in favour of the argument, that some brute animals are endued with Reason; and the team had capitulated and returned to their duty.\n\nWith drooping heads and tremulous tails, they mashed their way through the thick mud, floundering and stumbling between whiles, as if they were falling to pieces at the larger joints. As often as the driver rested them and brought them to a stand, with a wary “Wo-ho! so-ho-then!” the near leader violently shook his head and everything upon it--like an unusually emphatic horse, denying that the coach could be got up the hill. Whenever the leader made this rattle, the passenger started, as a nervous passenger might, and was disturbed in mind.\n\nThere was a steaming mist in all the hollows, and it had roamed in its forlornness up the hill, like an evil spirit, seeking rest and finding none. A clammy and intensely cold mist, it made its slow way through the air in ripples that visibly followed and overspread one another, as the waves of an unwholesome sea might do. It was dense enough to shut out everything from the light of the coach-lamps but these its own workings, and a few yards of road; and the reek of the labouring horses steamed into it, as if they had made it all.\n\nTwo other passengers, besides the one, were plodding up the hill by the side of the mail. All three were wrapped to the cheekbones and over the ears, and wore jack-boots. Not one of the three could have said, from anything he saw, what either of the other two was like; and each was hidden under almost as many wrappers from the eyes of the mind, as from the eyes of the body, of his two companions. In those days, travellers were very shy of being confidential on a short notice, for anybody on the road might be a robber or in league with robbers. As to the latter, when every posting-house and ale-house could produce somebody in “the Captain’s” pay, ranging from the landlord to the lowest stable non-descript, it was the likeliest thing upon the cards. So the guard of the Dover mail thought to himself, that Friday night in November, one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five, lumbering up Shooter’s Hill, as he stood on his own particular perch behind the mail, beating his feet, and keeping an eye and a hand on the arm-chest before him, where a loaded blunderbuss lay at the top of six or eight loaded horse-pistols, deposited on a substratum of cutlass.\n\nThe Dover mail was in its usual genial position that the guard suspected the passengers, the passengers suspected one another and the guard, they all suspected everybody else, and the coachman was sure of nothing but the horses; as to which cattle he could with a clear conscience have taken his oath on the two Testaments that they were not fit for the journey.\n\n“Wo-ho!” said the coachman. “So, then! One more pull and you’re at the top and be damned to you, for I have had trouble enough to get you to it!--Joe!”\n\n“Halloa!” the guard replied.\n\n“What o’clock do you make it, Joe?”\n\n“Ten minutes, good, past eleven.”\n\n“My blood!” ejaculated the vexed coachman, “and not atop of Shooter’s yet! Tst! Yah! Get on with you!”\n\nThe emphatic horse, cut short by the whip in a most decided negative, made a decided scramble for it, and the three other horses followed suit. Once more, the Dover mail struggled on, with the jack-boots of its passengers squashing along by its side. They had stopped when the coach stopped, and they kept close company with it. If any one of the three had had the hardihood to propose to another to walk on a little ahead into the mist and darkness, he would have put himself in a fair way of getting shot instantly as a highwayman.\n\nThe last burst carried the mail to the summit of the hill. The horses stopped to breathe again, and the guard got down to skid the wheel for the descent, and open the coach-door to let the passengers in.\n\n“Tst! Joe!” cried the coachman in a warning voice, looking down from his box.\n\n“What do you say, Tom?”\n\nThey both listened.\n\n“I say a horse at a canter coming up, Joe.”\n\n“I say a horse at a gallop, Tom,” returned the guard, leaving his hold of the door, and mounting nimbly to his place. “Gentlemen! In the king’s name, all of you!”\n\nWith this hurried adjuration, he cocked his blunderbuss, and stood on the offensive.\n\nThe passenger booked by this history, was on the coach-step, getting in; the two other passengers were close behind him, and about to follow. He remained on the step, half in the coach and half out of; they remained in the road below him. They all looked from the coachman to the guard, and from the guard to the coachman, and listened. The coachman looked back and the guard looked back, and even the emphatic leader pricked up his ears and looked back, without contradicting.\n\nThe stillness consequent on the cessation of the rumbling and labouring of the coach, added to the stillness of the night, made it very quiet indeed. The panting of the horses communicated a tremulous motion to the coach, as if it were in a state of agitation. The hearts of the passengers beat loud enough perhaps to be heard; but at any rate, the quiet pause was audibly expressive of people out of breath, and holding the breath, and having the pulses quickened by expectation.\n\nThe sound of a horse at a gallop came fast and furiously up the hill.\n\n“So-ho!” the guard sang out, as loud as he could roar. “Yo there! Stand! I shall fire!”\n\nThe pace was suddenly checked, and, with much splashing and floundering, a man’s voice called from the mist, “Is that the Dover mail?”\n\n“Never you mind what it is!” the guard retorted. “What are you?”\n\n“Is that the Dover mail?”\n\n“Why do you want to know?”\n\n“I want a passenger, if it is.”\n\n“What passenger?”\n\n“Mr. Jarvis Lorry.”\n\nOur booked passenger showed in a moment that it was his name. The guard, the coachman, and the two other passengers eyed him distrustfully.\n\n“Keep where you are,” the guard called to the voice in the mist, “because, if I should make a mistake, it could never be set right in your lifetime. Gentleman of the name of Lorry answer straight.”\n\n“What is the matter?” asked the passenger, then, with mildly quavering speech. “Who wants me? Is it Jerry?”\n\n(“I don’t like Jerry’s voice, if it is Jerry,” growled the guard to himself. “He’s hoarser than suits me, is Jerry.”)\n\n“Yes, Mr. Lorry.”\n\n“What is the matter?”\n\n“A despatch sent after you from over yonder. T. and Co.”\n\n“I know this messenger, guard,” said Mr. Lorry, getting down into the road--assisted from behind more swiftly than politely by the other two passengers, who immediately scrambled into the coach, shut the door, and pulled up the window. “He may come close; there’s nothing wrong.”\n\n“I hope there ain’t, but I can’t make so ‘Nation sure of that,” said the guard, in gruff soliloquy. “Hallo you!”\n\n“Well! And hallo you!” said Jerry, more hoarsely than before.\n\n“Come on at a footpace! d’ye mind me? And if you’ve got holsters to that saddle o’ yourn, don’t let me see your hand go nigh ’em. For I’m a devil at a quick mistake, and when I make one it takes the form of Lead. So now let’s look at you.”\n\nThe figures of a horse and rider came slowly through the eddying mist, and came to the side of the mail, where the passenger stood. The rider stooped, and, casting up his eyes at the guard, handed the passenger a small folded paper. The rider’s horse was blown, and both horse and rider were covered with mud, from the hoofs of the horse to the hat of the man.\n\n“Guard!” said the passenger, in a tone of quiet business confidence.\n\nThe watchful guard, with his right hand at the stock of his raised blunderbuss, his left at the barrel, and his eye on the horseman, answered curtly, “Sir.”\n\n“There is nothing to apprehend. I belong to Tellson’s Bank. You must know Tellson’s Bank in London. I am going to Paris on business. A crown to drink. I may read this?”\n\n“If so be as you’re quick, sir.”\n\nHe opened it in the light of the coach-lamp on that side, and read--first to himself and then aloud: “‘Wait at Dover for Mam’selle.’ It’s not long, you see, guard. Jerry, say that my answer was, RECALLED TO LIFE.”\n\nJerry started in his saddle. “That’s a Blazing strange answer, too,” said he, at his hoarsest.\n\n“Take that message back, and they will know that I received this, as well as if I wrote. Make the best of your way. Good night.”\n\nWith those words the passenger opened the coach-door and got in; not at all assisted by his fellow-passengers, who had expeditiously secreted their watches and purses in their boots, and were now making a general pretence of being asleep. With no more definite purpose than to escape the hazard of originating any other kind of action.\n\nThe coach lumbered on again, with heavier wreaths of mist closing round it as it began the descent. The guard soon replaced his blunderbuss in his arm-chest, and, having looked to the rest of its contents, and having looked to the supplementary pistols that he wore in his belt, looked to a smaller chest beneath his seat, in which there were a few smith’s tools, a couple of torches, and a tinder-box. For he was furnished with that completeness that if the coach-lamps had been blown and stormed out, which did occasionally happen, he had only to shut himself up inside, keep the flint and steel sparks well off the straw, and get a light with tolerable safety and ease (if he were lucky) in five minutes.\n\n“Tom!” softly over the coach roof.\n\n“Hallo, Joe.”\n\n“Did you hear the message?”\n\n“I did, Joe.”\n\n“What did you make of it, Tom?”\n\n“Nothing at all, Joe.”\n\n“That’s a coincidence, too,” the guard mused, “for I made the same of it myself.”\n\nJerry, left alone in the mist and darkness, dismounted meanwhile, not only to ease his spent horse, but to wipe the mud from his face, and shake the wet out of his hat-brim, which might be capable of holding about half a gallon. After standing with the bridle over his heavily-splashed arm, until the wheels of the mail were no longer within hearing and the night was quite still again, he turned to walk down the hill.\n\n“After that there gallop from Temple Bar, old lady, I won’t trust your fore-legs till I get you on the level,” said this hoarse messenger, glancing at his mare. “‘Recalled to life.’ That’s a Blazing strange message. Much of that wouldn’t do for you, Jerry! I say, Jerry! You’d be in a Blazing bad way, if recalling to life was to come into fashion, Jerry!”\n\n# CHAPTER III. The Night Shadows\n\nA wonderful fact to reflect upon, that every human creature is constituted to be that profound secret and mystery to every other. A solemn consideration, when I enter a great city by night, that every one of those darkly clustered houses encloses its own secret; that every room in every one of them encloses its own secret; that every beating heart in the hundreds of thousands of breasts there, is, in some of its imaginings, a secret to the heart nearest it! Something of the awfulness, even of Death itself, is referable to this. No more can I turn the leaves of this dear book that I loved, and vainly hope in time to read it all. No more can I look into the depths of this unfathomable water, wherein, as momentary lights glanced into it, I have had glimpses of buried treasure and other things submerged. It was appointed that the book should shut with a spring, for ever and for ever, when I had read but a page. It was appointed that the water should be locked in an eternal frost, when the light was playing on its surface, and I stood in ignorance on the shore. My friend is dead, my neighbour is dead, my love, the darling of my soul, is dead; it is the inexorable consolidation and perpetuation of the secret that was always in that individuality, and which I shall carry in mine to my life’s end. In any of the burial-places of this city through which I pass, is there a sleeper more inscrutable than its busy inhabitants are, in their innermost personality, to me, or than I am to them?\n\nAs to this, his natural and not to be alienated inheritance, the messenger on horseback had exactly the same possessions as the King, the first Minister of State, or the richest merchant in London. So with the three passengers shut up in the narrow compass of one lumbering old mail coach; they were mysteries to one another, as complete as if each had been in his own coach and six, or his own coach and sixty, with the breadth of a county between him and the next.\n\nThe messenger rode back at an easy trot, stopping pretty often at ale-houses by the way to drink, but evincing a tendency to keep his own counsel, and to keep his hat cocked over his eyes. He had eyes that assorted very well with that decoration, being of a surface black, with no depth in the colour or form, and much too near together--as if they were afraid of being found out in something, singly, if they kept too far apart. They had a sinister expression, under an old cocked-hat like a three-cornered spittoon, and over a great muffler for the chin and throat, which descended nearly to the wearer’s knees. When he stopped for drink, he moved this muffler with his left hand, only while he poured his liquor in with his right; as soon as that was done, he muffled again.\n\n“No, Jerry, no!” said the messenger, harping on one theme as he rode. “It wouldn’t do for you, Jerry. Jerry, you honest tradesman, it wouldn’t suit your line of business! Recalled--! Bust me if I don’t think he’d been a drinking!”\n\nHis message perplexed his mind to that degree that he was fain, several times, to take off his hat to scratch his head. Except on the crown, which was raggedly bald, he had stiff, black hair, standing jaggedly all over it, and growing down hill almost to his broad, blunt nose. It was so like Smith’s work, so much more like the top of a strongly spiked wall than a head of hair, that the best of players at leap-frog might have declined him, as the most dangerous man in the world to go over.\n\nWhile he trotted back with the message he was to deliver to the night watchman in his box at the door of Tellson’s Bank, by Temple Bar, who was to deliver it to greater authorities within, the shadows of the night took such shapes to him as arose out of the message, and took such shapes to the mare as arose out of her private topics of uneasiness. They seemed to be numerous, for she shied at every shadow on the road.\n\nWhat time, the mail-coach lumbered, jolted, rattled, and bumped upon its tedious way, with its three fellow-inscrutables inside. To whom, likewise, the shadows of the night revealed themselves, in the forms their dozing eyes and wandering thoughts suggested.\n\nTellson’s Bank had a run upon it in the mail. As the bank passenger--with an arm drawn through the leathern strap, which did what lay in it to keep him from pounding against the next passenger, and driving him into his corner, whenever the coach got a special jolt--nodded in his place, with half-shut eyes, the little coach-windows, and the coach-lamp dimly gleaming through them, and the bulky bundle of opposite passenger, became the bank, and did a great stroke of business. The rattle of the harness was the chink of money, and more drafts were honoured in five minutes than even Tellson’s, with all its foreign and home connection, ever paid in thrice the time. Then the strong-rooms underground, at Tellson’s, with such of their valuable stores and secrets as were known to the passenger (and it was not a little that he knew about them), opened before him, and he went in among them with the great keys and the feebly-burning candle, and found them safe, and strong, and sound, and still, just as he had last seen them.\n\nBut, though the bank was almost always with him, and though the coach (in a confused way, like the presence of pain under an opiate) was always with him, there was another current of impression that never ceased to run, all through the night. He was on his way to dig some one out of a grave.\n\nNow, which of the multitude of faces that showed themselves before him was the true face of the buried person, the shadows of the night did not indicate; but they were all the faces of a man of five-and-forty by years, and they differed principally in the passions they expressed, and in the ghastliness of their worn and wasted state. Pride, contempt, defiance, stubbornness, submission, lamentation, succeeded one another; so did varieties of sunken cheek, cadaverous colour, emaciated hands and figures. But the face was in the main one face, and every head was prematurely white. A hundred times the dozing passenger inquired of this spectre:\n\n“Buried how long?”\n\nThe answer was always the same: “Almost eighteen years.”\n\n“You had abandoned all hope of being dug out?”\n\n“Long ago.”\n\n“You know that you are recalled to life?”\n\n“They tell me so.”\n\n“I hope you care to live?”\n\n“I can’t say.”\n\n“Shall I show her to you? Will you come and see her?”\n\nThe answers to this question were various and contradictory. Sometimes the broken reply was, “Wait! It would kill me if I saw her too soon.” Sometimes, it was given in a tender rain of tears, and then it was, “Take me to her.” Sometimes it was staring and bewildered, and then it was, “I don’t know her. I don’t understand.”\n\nAfter such imaginary discourse, the passenger in his fancy would dig, and dig, dig--now with a spade, now with a great key, now with his hands--to dig this wretched creature out. Got out at last, with earth hanging about his face and hair, he would suddenly fan away to dust. The passenger would then start to himself, and lower the window, to get the reality of mist and rain on his cheek.\n\nYet even when his eyes were opened on the mist and rain, on the moving patch of light from the lamps, and the hedge at the roadside retreating by jerks, the night shadows outside the coach would fall into the train of the night shadows within. The real Banking-house by Temple Bar, the real business of the past day, the real strong rooms, the real express sent after him, and the real message returned, would all be there. Out of the midst of them, the ghostly face would rise, and he would accost it again.\n\n“Buried how long?”\n\n“Almost eighteen years.”\n\n“I hope you care to live?”\n\n“I can’t say.”\n\nDig--dig--dig--until an impatient movement from one of the two passengers would admonish him to pull up the window, draw his arm securely through the leathern strap, and speculate upon the two slumbering forms, until his mind lost its hold of them, and they again slid away into the bank and the grave.\n\n“Buried how long?”\n\n“Almost eighteen years.”\n\n“You had abandoned all hope of being dug out?”\n\n“Long ago.”\n\nThe words were still in his hearing as just spoken--distinctly in his hearing as ever spoken words had been in his life--when the weary passenger started to the consciousness of daylight, and found that the shadows of the night were gone.\n\nHe lowered the window, and looked out at the rising sun. There was a ridge of ploughed land, with a plough upon it where it had been left last night when the horses were unyoked; beyond, a quiet coppice-wood, in which many leaves of burning red and golden yellow still remained upon the trees. Though the earth was cold and wet, the sky was clear, and the sun rose bright, placid, and beautiful.\n\n“Eighteen years!” said the passenger, looking at the sun. “Gracious Creator of day! To be buried alive for eighteen years!”\n\n# CHAPTER IV. The Preparation\n\nWhen the mail got successfully to Dover, in the course of the forenoon, the head drawer at the Royal George Hotel opened the coach-door as his custom was. He did it with some flourish of ceremony, for a mail journey from London in winter was an achievement to congratulate an adventurous traveller upon.\n\nBy that time, there was only one adventurous traveller left be congratulated: for the two others had been set down at their respective roadside destinations. The mildewy inside of the coach, with its damp and dirty straw, its disagreeable smell, and its obscurity, was rather like a larger dog-kennel. Mr. Lorry, the passenger, shaking himself out of it in chains of straw, a tangle of shaggy wrapper, flapping hat, and muddy legs, was rather like a larger sort of dog.\n\n“There will be a packet to Calais, tomorrow, drawer?”\n\n“Yes, sir, if the weather holds and the wind sets tolerable fair. The tide will serve pretty nicely at about two in the afternoon, sir. Bed, sir?”\n\n“I shall not go to bed till night; but I want a bedroom, and a barber.”\n\n“And then breakfast, sir? Yes, sir. That way, sir, if you please. Show Concord! Gentleman’s valise and hot water to Concord. Pull off gentleman’s boots in Concord. (You will find a fine sea-coal fire, sir.) Fetch barber to Concord. Stir about there, now, for Concord!”\n\nThe Concord bed-chamber being always assigned to a passenger by the mail, and passengers by the mail being always heavily wrapped up from head to foot, the room had the odd interest for the establishment of the Royal George, that although but one kind of man was seen to go into it, all kinds and varieties of men came out of it. Consequently, another drawer, and two porters, and several maids and the landlady, were all loitering by accident at various points of the road between the Concord and the coffee-room, when a gentleman of sixty, formally dressed in a brown suit of clothes, pretty well worn, but very well kept, with large square cuffs and large flaps to the pockets, passed along on his way to his breakfast.\n\nThe coffee-room had no other occupant, that forenoon, than the gentleman in brown. His breakfast-table was drawn before the fire, and as he sat, with its light shining on him, waiting for the meal, he sat so still, that he might have been sitting for his portrait.\n\nVery orderly and methodical he looked, with a hand on each knee, and a loud watch ticking a sonorous sermon under his flapped waist-coat, as though it pitted its gravity and longevity against the levity and evanescence of the brisk fire. He had a good leg, and was a little vain of it, for his brown stockings fitted sleek and close, and were of a fine texture; his shoes and buckles, too, though plain, were trim. He wore an odd little sleek crisp flaxen wig, setting very close to his head: which wig, it is to be presumed, was made of hair, but which looked far more as though it were spun from filaments of silk or glass. His linen, though not of a fineness in accordance with his stockings, was as white as the tops of the waves that broke upon the neighbouring beach, or the specks of sail that glinted in the sunlight far at sea. A face habitually suppressed and quieted, was still lighted up under the quaint wig by a pair of moist bright eyes that it must have cost their owner, in years gone by, some pains to drill to the composed and reserved expression of Tellson’s Bank. He had a healthy colour in his cheeks, and his face, though lined, bore few traces of anxiety. But, perhaps the confidential bachelor clerks in Tellson’s Bank were principally occupied with the cares of other people; and perhaps second-hand cares, like second-hand clothes, come easily off and on.\n\nCompleting his resemblance to a man who was sitting for his portrait, Mr. Lorry dropped off to sleep. The arrival of his breakfast roused him, and he said to the drawer, as he moved his chair to it:\n\n“I wish accommodation prepared for a young lady who may come here at any time to-day. She may ask for Mr. Jarvis Lorry, or she may only ask for a gentleman from Tellson’s Bank. Please to let me know.”\n\n“Yes, sir. Tellson’s Bank in London, sir?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“Yes, sir. We have oftentimes the honour to entertain your gentlemen in their travelling backwards and forwards betwixt London and Paris, sir. A vast deal of travelling, sir, in Tellson and Company’s House.”\n\n“Yes. We are quite a French House, as well as an English one.”\n\n“Yes, sir. Not much in the habit of such travelling yourself, I think, sir?”\n\n“Not of late years. It is fifteen years since we--since I--came last from France.”\n\n“Indeed, sir? That was before my time here, sir. Before our people’s time here, sir. The George was in other hands at that time, sir.”\n\n“I believe so.”\n\n“But I would hold a pretty wager, sir, that a House like Tellson and Company was flourishing, a matter of fifty, not to speak of fifteen years ago?”\n\n“You might treble that, and say a hundred and fifty, yet not be far from the truth.”\n\n“Indeed, sir!”\n\nRounding his mouth and both his eyes, as he stepped backward from the table, the waiter shifted his napkin from his right arm to his left, dropped into a comfortable attitude, and stood surveying the guest while he ate and drank, as from an observatory or watchtower. According to the immemorial usage of waiters in all ages.\n\nWhen Mr. Lorry had finished his breakfast, he went out for a stroll on the beach. The little narrow, crooked town of Dover hid itself away from the beach, and ran its head into the chalk cliffs, like a marine ostrich. The beach was a desert of heaps of sea and stones tumbling wildly about, and the sea did what it liked, and what it liked was destruction. It thundered at the town, and thundered at the cliffs, and brought the coast down, madly. The air among the houses was of so strong a piscatory flavour that one might have supposed sick fish went up to be dipped in it, as sick people went down to be dipped in the sea. A little fishing was done in the port, and a quantity of strolling about by night, and looking seaward: particularly at those times when the tide made, and was near flood. Small tradesmen, who did no business whatever, sometimes unaccountably realised large fortunes, and it was remarkable that nobody in the neighbourhood could endure a lamplighter.\n\nAs the day declined into the afternoon, and the air, which had been at intervals clear enough to allow the French coast to be seen, became again charged with mist and vapour, Mr. Lorry’s thoughts seemed to cloud too. When it was dark, and he sat before the coffee-room fire, awaiting his dinner as he had awaited his breakfast, his mind was busily digging, digging, digging, in the live red coals.\n\nA bottle of good claret after dinner does a digger in the red coals no harm, otherwise than as it has a tendency to throw him out of work. Mr. Lorry had been idle a long time, and had just poured out his last glassful of wine with as complete an appearance of satisfaction as is ever to be found in an elderly gentleman of a fresh complexion who has got to the end of a bottle, when a rattling of wheels came up the narrow street, and rumbled into the inn-yard.\n\nHe set down his glass untouched. “This is Mam’selle!” said he.\n\nIn a very few minutes the waiter came in to announce that Miss Manette had arrived from London, and would be happy to see the gentleman from Tellson’s.\n\n“So soon?”\n\nMiss Manette had taken some refreshment on the road, and required none then, and was extremely anxious to see the gentleman from Tellson’s immediately, if it suited his pleasure and convenience.\n\nThe gentleman from Tellson’s had nothing left for it but to empty his glass with an air of stolid desperation, settle his odd little flaxen wig at the ears, and follow the waiter to Miss Manette’s apartment. It was a large, dark room, furnished in a funereal manner with black horsehair, and loaded with heavy dark tables. These had been oiled and oiled, until the two tall candles on the table in the middle of the room were gloomily reflected on every leaf; as if they were buried, in deep graves of black mahogany, and no light to speak of could be expected from them until they were dug out.\n\nThe obscurity was so difficult to penetrate that Mr. Lorry, picking his way over the well-worn Turkey carpet, supposed Miss Manette to be, for the moment, in some adjacent room, until, having got past the two tall candles, he saw standing to receive him by the table between them and the fire, a young lady of not more than seventeen, in a riding-cloak, and still holding her straw travelling-hat by its ribbon in her hand. As his eyes rested on a short, slight, pretty figure, a quantity of golden hair, a pair of blue eyes that met his own with an inquiring look, and a forehead with a singular capacity (remembering how young and smooth it was), of rifting and knitting itself into an expression that was not quite one of perplexity, or wonder, or alarm, or merely of a bright fixed attention, though it included all the four expressions--as his eyes rested on these things, a sudden vivid likeness passed before him, of a child whom he had held in his arms on the passage across that very Channel, one cold time, when the hail drifted heavily and the sea ran high. The likeness passed away, like a breath along the surface of the gaunt pier-glass behind her, on the frame of which, a hospital procession of negro cupids, several headless and all cripples, were offering black baskets of Dead Sea fruit to black divinities of the feminine gender--and he made his formal bow to Miss Manette.\n\n“Pray take a seat, sir.” In a very clear and pleasant young voice; a little foreign in its accent, but a very little indeed.\n\n“I kiss your hand, miss,” said Mr. Lorry, with the manners of an earlier date, as he made his formal bow again, and took his seat.\n\n“I received a letter from the Bank, sir, yesterday, informing me that some intelligence--or discovery--”\n\n“The word is not material, miss; either word will do.”\n\n“--respecting the small property of my poor father, whom I never saw--so long dead--”\n\nMr. Lorry moved in his chair, and cast a troubled look towards the hospital procession of negro cupids. As if they had any help for anybody in their absurd baskets!\n\n“--rendered it necessary that I should go to Paris, there to communicate with a gentleman of the Bank, so good as to be despatched to Paris for the purpose.”\n\n“Myself.”\n“As I was prepared to hear, sir.”\n\nShe curtseyed to him (young ladies made curtseys in those days), with a pretty desire to convey to him that she felt how much older and wiser he was than she. He made her another bow.\n\n“I replied to the Bank, sir, that as it was considered necessary, by those who know, and who are so kind as to advise me, that I should go to France, and that as I am an orphan and have no friend who could go with me, I should esteem it highly if I might be permitted to place myself, during the journey, under that worthy gentleman’s protection. The gentleman had left London, but I think a messenger was sent after him to beg the favour of his waiting for me here.”\n\n“I was happy,” said Mr. Lorry, “to be entrusted with the charge. I shall be more happy to execute it.”\n\n“Sir, I thank you indeed. I thank you very gratefully. It was told me by the Bank that the gentleman would explain to me the details of the business, and that I must prepare myself to find them of a surprising nature. I have done my best to prepare myself, and I naturally have a strong and eager interest to know what they are.”\n\n“Naturally,” said Mr. Lorry. “Yes--I--”\n\nAfter a pause, he added, again settling the crisp flaxen wig at the ears, “It is very difficult to begin.”\n\nHe did not begin, but, in his indecision, met her glance. The young forehead lifted itself into that singular expression--but it was pretty and characteristic, besides being singular--and she raised her hand, as if with an involuntary action she caught at, or stayed some passing shadow.\n\n“Are you quite a stranger to me, sir?”\n\n“Am I not?” Mr. Lorry opened his hands, and extended them outwards with an argumentative smile.\n\nBetween the eyebrows and just over the little feminine nose, the line of which was as delicate and fine as it was possible to be, the expression deepened itself as she took her seat thoughtfully in the chair by which she had hitherto remained standing. He watched her as she mused, and the moment she raised her eyes again, went on:\n\n“In your adopted country, I presume, I cannot do better than address you as a young English lady, Miss Manette?”\n\n“If you please, sir.”\n\n“Miss Manette, I am a man of business. I have a business charge to acquit myself of. In your reception of it, don’t heed me any more than if I was a speaking machine--truly, I am not much else. I will, with your leave, relate to you, miss, the story of one of our customers.”\n“Story!”\n\nHe seemed wilfully to mistake the word she had repeated, when he added, in a hurry, “Yes, customers; in the banking business we usually call our connection our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man of great acquirements--a Doctor.”\n\n“Not of Beauvais?”\n\n“Why, yes, of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was of Beauvais. Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was of repute in Paris. I had the honour of knowing him there. Our relations were business relations, but confidential. I was at that time in our French House, and had been--oh! twenty years.”\n\n“At that time--I may ask, at what time, sir?”\n\n“I speak, miss, of twenty years ago. He married--an English lady--and I was one of the trustees. His affairs, like the affairs of many other French gentlemen and French families, were entirely in Tellson’s hands. In a similar way I am, or I have been, trustee of one kind or other for scores of our customers. These are mere business relations, miss; there is no friendship in them, no particular interest, nothing like sentiment. I have passed from one to another, in the course of my business life, just as I pass from one of our customers to another in the course of my business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am a mere machine. To go on--”\n\n“But this is my father’s story, sir; and I begin to think”--the curiously roughened forehead was very intent upon him--“that when I was left an orphan through my mother’s surviving my father only two years, it was you who brought me to England. I am almost sure it was you.”\n\nMr. Lorry took the hesitating little hand that confidingly advanced to take his, and he put it with some ceremony to his lips. He then conducted the young lady straightway to her chair again, and, holding the chair-back with his left hand, and using his right by turns to rub his chin, pull his wig at the ears, or point what he said, stood looking down into her face while she sat looking up into his.\n\n“Miss Manette, it was I. And you will see how truly I spoke of myself just now, in saying I had no feelings, and that all the relations I hold with my fellow-creatures are mere business relations, when you reflect that I have never seen you since. No; you have been the ward of Tellson’s House since, and I have been busy with the other business of Tellson’s House since. Feelings! I have no time for them, no chance of them. I pass my whole life, miss, in turning an immense pecuniary Mangle.”\n\nAfter this odd description of his daily routine of employment, Mr. Lorry flattened his flaxen wig upon his head with both hands (which was most unnecessary, for nothing could be flatter than its shining surface was before), and resumed his former attitude.\n\n“So far, miss (as you have remarked), this is the story of your regretted father. Now comes the difference. If your father had not died when he did--Don’t be frightened! How you start!”\n\nShe did, indeed, start. And she caught his wrist with both her hands.\n\n“Pray,” said Mr. Lorry, in a soothing tone, bringing his left hand from the back of the chair to lay it on the supplicatory fingers that clasped him in so violent a tremble: “pray control your agitation--a matter of business. As I was saying--”\n\nHer look so discomposed him that he stopped, wandered, and began anew:\n\n“As I was saying; if Monsieur Manette had not died; if he had suddenly and silently disappeared; if he had been spirited away; if it had not been difficult to guess to what dreadful place, though no art could trace him; if he had an enemy in some compatriot who could exercise a privilege that I in my own time have known the boldest people afraid to speak of in a whisper, across the water there; for instance, the privilege of filling up blank forms for the consignment of any one to the oblivion of a prison for any length of time; if his wife had implored the king, the queen, the court, the clergy, for any tidings of him, and all quite in vain;--then the history of your father would have been the history of this unfortunate gentleman, the Doctor of Beauvais.”\n\n“I entreat you to tell me more, sir.”\n\n“I will. I am going to. You can bear it?”\n\n“I can bear anything but the uncertainty you leave me in at this moment.”\n\n“You speak collectedly, and you--are collected. That’s good!” (Though his manner was less satisfied than his words.) “A matter of business. Regard it as a matter of business--business that must be done. Now if this doctor’s wife, though a lady of great courage and spirit, had suffered so intensely from this cause before her little child was born--”\n\n“The little child was a daughter, sir.”\n\n“A daughter. A-a-matter of business--don’t be distressed. Miss, if the poor lady had suffered so intensely before her little child was born, that she came to the determination of sparing the poor child the inheritance of any part of the agony she had known the pains of, by rearing her in the belief that her father was dead--No, don’t kneel! In Heaven’s name why should you kneel to me!”\n\n“For the truth. O dear, good, compassionate sir, for the truth!”\n\n“A--a matter of business. You confuse me, and how can I transact business if I am confused? Let us be clear-headed. If you could kindly mention now, for instance, what nine times ninepence are, or how many shillings in twenty guineas, it would be so encouraging. I should be so much more at my ease about your state of mind.”\n\nWithout directly answering to this appeal, she sat so still when he had very gently raised her, and the hands that had not ceased to clasp his wrists were so much more steady than they had been, that she communicated some reassurance to Mr. Jarvis Lorry.\n\n“That’s right, that’s right. Courage! Business! You have business before you; useful business. Miss Manette, your mother took this course with you. And when she died--I believe broken-hearted--having never slackened her unavailing search for your father, she left you, at two years old, to grow to be blooming, beautiful, and happy, without the dark cloud upon you of living in uncertainty whether your father soon wore his heart out in prison, or wasted there through many lingering years.”\n\nAs he said the words he looked down, with an admiring pity, on the flowing golden hair; as if he pictured to himself that it might have been already tinged with grey.\n\n“You know that your parents had no great possession, and that what they had was secured to your mother and to you. There has been no new discovery, of money, or of any other property; but--”\n\nHe felt his wrist held closer, and he stopped. The expression in the forehead, which had so particularly attracted his notice, and which was now immovable, had deepened into one of pain and horror.\n\n“But he has been--been found. He is alive. Greatly changed, it is too probable; almost a wreck, it is possible; though we will hope the best. Still, alive. Your father has been taken to the house of an old servant in Paris, and we are going there: I, to identify him if I can: you, to restore him to life, love, duty, rest, comfort.”\n\nA shiver ran through her frame, and from it through his. She said, in a low, distinct, awe-stricken voice, as if she were saying it in a dream,\n\n“I am going to see his Ghost! It will be his Ghost--not him!”\n\nMr. Lorry quietly chafed the hands that held his arm. “There, there, there! See now, see now! The best and the worst are known to you, now. You are well on your way to the poor wronged gentleman, and, with a fair sea voyage, and a fair land journey, you will be soon at his dear side.”\n\nShe repeated in the same tone, sunk to a whisper, “I have been free, I have been happy, yet his Ghost has never haunted me!”\n\n“Only one thing more,” said Mr. Lorry, laying stress upon it as a wholesome means of enforcing her attention: “he has been found under another name; his own, long forgotten or long concealed. It would be worse than useless now to inquire which; worse than useless to seek to know whether he has been for years overlooked, or always designedly held prisoner. It would be worse than useless now to make any inquiries, because it would be dangerous. Better not to mention the subject, anywhere or in any way, and to remove him--for a while at all events--out of France. Even I, safe as an Englishman, and even Tellson’s, important as they are to French credit, avoid all naming of the matter. I carry about me, not a scrap of writing openly referring to it. This is a secret service altogether. My credentials, entries, and memoranda, are all comprehended in the one line, ‘Recalled to Life;’ which may mean anything. But what is the matter! She doesn’t notice a word! Miss Manette!”\n\nPerfectly still and silent, and not even fallen back in her chair, she sat under his hand, utterly insensible; with her eyes open and fixed upon him, and with that last expression looking as if it were carved or branded into her forehead. So close was her hold upon his arm, that he feared to detach himself lest he should hurt her; therefore he called out loudly for assistance without moving.\n\nA wild-looking woman, whom even in his agitation, Mr. Lorry observed to be all of a red colour, and to have red hair, and to be dressed in some extraordinary tight-fitting fashion, and to have on her head a most wonderful bonnet like a Grenadier wooden measure, and good measure too, or a great Stilton cheese, came running into the room in advance of the inn servants, and soon settled the question of his detachment from the poor young lady, by laying a brawny hand upon his chest, and sending him flying back against the nearest wall.\n\n(“I really think this must be a man!” was Mr. Lorry’s breathless reflection, simultaneously with his coming against the wall.)\n\n“Why, look at you all!” bawled this figure, addressing the inn servants. “Why don’t you go and fetch things, instead of standing there staring at me? I am not so much to look at, am I? Why don’t you go and fetch things? I’ll let you know, if you don’t bring smelling-salts, cold water, and vinegar, quick, I will.”\n\nThere was an immediate dispersal for these restoratives, and she softly laid the patient on a sofa, and tended her with great skill and gentleness: calling her “my precious!” and “my bird!” and spreading her golden hair aside over her shoulders with great pride and care.\n\n“And you in brown!” she said, indignantly turning to Mr. Lorry; “couldn’t you tell her what you had to tell her, without frightening her to death? Look at her, with her pretty pale face and her cold hands. Do you call that being a Banker?”\n\nMr. Lorry was so exceedingly disconcerted by a question so hard to answer, that he could only look on, at a distance, with much feebler sympathy and humility, while the strong woman, having banished the inn servants under the mysterious penalty of “letting them know” something not mentioned if they stayed there, staring, recovered her charge by a regular series of gradations, and coaxed her to lay her drooping head upon her shoulder.\n\n“I hope she will do well now,” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“No thanks to you in brown, if she does. My darling pretty!”\n\n“I hope,” said Mr. Lorry, after another pause of feeble sympathy and humility, “that you accompany Miss Manette to France?”\n\n“A likely thing, too!” replied the strong woman. “If it was ever intended that I should go across salt water, do you suppose Providence would have cast my lot in an island?”\n\nThis being another question hard to answer, Mr. Jarvis Lorry withdrew to consider it.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Wine-shop\n\nA large cask of wine had been dropped and broken, in the street. The accident had happened in getting it out of a cart; the cask had tumbled out with a run, the hoops had burst, and it lay on the stones just outside the door of the wine-shop, shattered like a walnut-shell.\n\nAll the people within reach had suspended their business, or their idleness, to run to the spot and drink the wine. The rough, irregular stones of the street, pointing every way, and designed, one might have thought, expressly to lame all living creatures that approached them, had dammed it into little pools; these were surrounded, each by its own jostling group or crowd, according to its size. Some men kneeled down, made scoops of their two hands joined, and sipped, or tried to help women, who bent over their shoulders, to sip, before the wine had all run out between their fingers. Others, men and women, dipped in the puddles with little mugs of mutilated earthenware, or even with handkerchiefs from women’s heads, which were squeezed dry into infants’ mouths; others made small mud-embankments, to stem the wine as it ran; others, directed by lookers-on up at high windows, darted here and there, to cut off little streams of wine that started away in new directions; others devoted themselves to the sodden and lee-dyed pieces of the cask, licking, and even champing the moister wine-rotted fragments with eager relish. There was no drainage to carry off the wine, and not only did it all get taken up, but so much mud got taken up along with it, that there might have been a scavenger in the street, if anybody acquainted with it could have believed in such a miraculous presence.\n\nA shrill sound of laughter and of amused voices--voices of men, women, and children--resounded in the street while this wine game lasted. There was little roughness in the sport, and much playfulness. There was a special companionship in it, an observable inclination on the part of every one to join some other one, which led, especially among the luckier or lighter-hearted, to frolicsome embraces, drinking of healths, shaking of hands, and even joining of hands and dancing, a dozen together. When the wine was gone, and the places where it had been most abundant were raked into a gridiron-pattern by fingers, these demonstrations ceased, as suddenly as they had broken out. The man who had left his saw sticking in the firewood he was cutting, set it in motion again; the women who had left on a door-step the little pot of hot ashes, at which she had been trying to soften the pain in her own starved fingers and toes, or in those of her child, returned to it; men with bare arms, matted locks, and cadaverous faces, who had emerged into the winter light from cellars, moved away, to descend again; and a gloom gathered on the scene that appeared more natural to it than sunshine.\n\nThe wine was red wine, and had stained the ground of the narrow street in the suburb of Saint Antoine, in Paris, where it was spilled. It had stained many hands, too, and many faces, and many naked feet, and many wooden shoes. The hands of the man who sawed the wood, left red marks on the billets; and the forehead of the woman who nursed her baby, was stained with the stain of the old rag she wound about her head again. Those who had been greedy with the staves of the cask, had acquired a tigerish smear about the mouth; and one tall joker so besmirched, his head more out of a long squalid bag of a nightcap than in it, scrawled upon a wall with his finger dipped in muddy wine-lees--BLOOD.\n\nThe time was to come, when that wine too would be spilled on the street-stones, and when the stain of it would be red upon many there.\n\nAnd now that the cloud settled on Saint Antoine, which a momentary gleam had driven from his sacred countenance, the darkness of it was heavy--cold, dirt, sickness, ignorance, and want, were the lords in waiting on the saintly presence--nobles of great power all of them; but, most especially the last. Samples of a people that had undergone a terrible grinding and regrinding in the mill, and certainly not in the fabulous mill which ground old people young, shivered at every corner, passed in and out at every doorway, looked from every window, fluttered in every vestige of a garment that the wind shook. The mill which had worked them down, was the mill that grinds young people old; the children had ancient faces and grave voices; and upon them, and upon the grown faces, and ploughed into every furrow of age and coming up afresh, was the sigh, Hunger. It was prevalent everywhere. Hunger was pushed out of the tall houses, in the wretched clothing that hung upon poles and lines; Hunger was patched into them with straw and rag and wood and paper; Hunger was repeated in every fragment of the small modicum of firewood that the man sawed off; Hunger stared down from the smokeless chimneys, and started up from the filthy street that had no offal, among its refuse, of anything to eat. Hunger was the inscription on the baker’s shelves, written in every small loaf of his scanty stock of bad bread; at the sausage-shop, in every dead-dog preparation that was offered for sale. Hunger rattled its dry bones among the roasting chestnuts in the turned cylinder; Hunger was shred into atomics in every farthing porringer of husky chips of potato, fried with some reluctant drops of oil.\n\nIts abiding place was in all things fitted to it. A narrow winding street, full of offence and stench, with other narrow winding streets diverging, all peopled by rags and nightcaps, and all smelling of rags and nightcaps, and all visible things with a brooding look upon them that looked ill. In the hunted air of the people there was yet some wild-beast thought of the possibility of turning at bay. Depressed and slinking though they were, eyes of fire were not wanting among them; nor compressed lips, white with what they suppressed; nor foreheads knitted into the likeness of the gallows-rope they mused about enduring, or inflicting. The trade signs (and they were almost as many as the shops) were, all, grim illustrations of Want. The butcher and the porkman painted up, only the leanest scrags of meat; the baker, the coarsest of meagre loaves. The people rudely pictured as drinking in the wine-shops, croaked over their scanty measures of thin wine and beer, and were gloweringly confidential together. Nothing was represented in a flourishing condition, save tools and weapons; but, the cutler’s knives and axes were sharp and bright, the smith’s hammers were heavy, and the gunmaker’s stock was murderous. The crippling stones of the pavement, with their many little reservoirs of mud and water, had no footways, but broke off abruptly at the doors. The kennel, to make amends, ran down the middle of the street--when it ran at all: which was only after heavy rains, and then it ran, by many eccentric fits, into the houses. Across the streets, at wide intervals, one clumsy lamp was slung by a rope and pulley; at night, when the lamplighter had let these down, and lighted, and hoisted them again, a feeble grove of dim wicks swung in a sickly manner overhead, as if they were at sea. Indeed they were at sea, and the ship and crew were in peril of tempest.\n\nFor, the time was to come, when the gaunt scarecrows of that region should have watched the lamplighter, in their idleness and hunger, so long, as to conceive the idea of improving on his method, and hauling up men by those ropes and pulleys, to flare upon the darkness of their condition. But, the time was not come yet; and every wind that blew over France shook the rags of the scarecrows in vain, for the birds, fine of song and feather, took no warning.\n\nThe wine-shop was a corner shop, better than most others in its appearance and degree, and the master of the wine-shop had stood outside it, in a yellow waistcoat and green breeches, looking on at the struggle for the lost wine. “It’s not my affair,” said he, with a final shrug of the shoulders. “The people from the market did it. Let them bring another.”\n\nThere, his eyes happening to catch the tall joker writing up his joke, he called to him across the way:\n\n“Say, then, my Gaspard, what do you do there?”\n\nThe fellow pointed to his joke with immense significance, as is often the way with his tribe. It missed its mark, and completely failed, as is often the way with his tribe too.\n\n“What now? Are you a subject for the mad hospital?” said the wine-shop keeper, crossing the road, and obliterating the jest with a handful of mud, picked up for the purpose, and smeared over it. “Why do you write in the public streets? Is there--tell me thou--is there no other place to write such words in?”\n\nIn his expostulation he dropped his cleaner hand (perhaps accidentally, perhaps not) upon the joker’s heart. The joker rapped it with his own, took a nimble spring upward, and came down in a fantastic dancing attitude, with one of his stained shoes jerked off his foot into his hand, and held out. A joker of an extremely, not to say wolfishly practical character, he looked, under those circumstances.\n\n“Put it on, put it on,” said the other. “Call wine, wine; and finish there.” With that advice, he wiped his soiled hand upon the joker’s dress, such as it was--quite deliberately, as having dirtied the hand on his account; and then recrossed the road and entered the wine-shop.\n\nThis wine-shop keeper was a bull-necked, martial-looking man of thirty, and he should have been of a hot temperament, for, although it was a bitter day, he wore no coat, but carried one slung over his shoulder. His shirt-sleeves were rolled up, too, and his brown arms were bare to the elbows. Neither did he wear anything more on his head than his own crisply-curling short dark hair. He was a dark man altogether, with good eyes and a good bold breadth between them. Good-humoured looking on the whole, but implacable-looking, too; evidently a man of a strong resolution and a set purpose; a man not desirable to be met, rushing down a narrow pass with a gulf on either side, for nothing would turn the man.\n\nMadame Defarge, his wife, sat in the shop behind the counter as he came in. Madame Defarge was a stout woman of about his own age, with a watchful eye that seldom seemed to look at anything, a large hand heavily ringed, a steady face, strong features, and great composure of manner. There was a character about Madame Defarge, from which one might have predicated that she did not often make mistakes against herself in any of the reckonings over which she presided. Madame Defarge being sensitive to cold, was wrapped in fur, and had a quantity of bright shawl twined about her head, though not to the concealment of her large earrings. Her knitting was before her, but she had laid it down to pick her teeth with a toothpick. Thus engaged, with her right elbow supported by her left hand, Madame Defarge said nothing when her lord came in, but coughed just one grain of cough. This, in combination with the lifting of her darkly defined eyebrows over her toothpick by the breadth of a line, suggested to her husband that he would do well to look round the shop among the customers, for any new customer who had dropped in while he stepped over the way.\n\nThe wine-shop keeper accordingly rolled his eyes about, until they rested upon an elderly gentleman and a young lady, who were seated in a corner. Other company were there: two playing cards, two playing dominoes, three standing by the counter lengthening out a short supply of wine. As he passed behind the counter, he took notice that the elderly gentleman said in a look to the young lady, “This is our man.”\n\n“What the devil do you do in that galley there?” said Monsieur Defarge to himself; “I don’t know you.”\n\nBut, he feigned not to notice the two strangers, and fell into discourse with the triumvirate of customers who were drinking at the counter.\n\n“How goes it, Jacques?” said one of these three to Monsieur Defarge. “Is all the spilt wine swallowed?”\n\n“Every drop, Jacques,” answered Monsieur Defarge.\n\nWhen this interchange of Christian name was effected, Madame Defarge, picking her teeth with her toothpick, coughed another grain of cough, and raised her eyebrows by the breadth of another line.\n\n“It is not often,” said the second of the three, addressing Monsieur Defarge, “that many of these miserable beasts know the taste of wine, or of anything but black bread and death. Is it not so, Jacques?”\n\n“It is so, Jacques,” Monsieur Defarge returned.\n\nAt this second interchange of the Christian name, Madame Defarge, still using her toothpick with profound composure, coughed another grain of cough, and raised her eyebrows by the breadth of another line.\n\nThe last of the three now said his say, as he put down his empty drinking vessel and smacked his lips.\n\n“Ah! So much the worse! A bitter taste it is that such poor cattle always have in their mouths, and hard lives they live, Jacques. Am I right, Jacques?”\n\n“You are right, Jacques,” was the response of Monsieur Defarge.\n\nThis third interchange of the Christian name was completed at the moment when Madame Defarge put her toothpick by, kept her eyebrows up, and slightly rustled in her seat.\n\n“Hold then! True!” muttered her husband. “Gentlemen--my wife!”\n\nThe three customers pulled off their hats to Madame Defarge, with three flourishes. She acknowledged their homage by bending her head, and giving them a quick look. Then she glanced in a casual manner round the wine-shop, took up her knitting with great apparent calmness and repose of spirit, and became absorbed in it.\n\n“Gentlemen,” said her husband, who had kept his bright eye observantly upon her, “good day. The chamber, furnished bachelor-fashion, that you wished to see, and were inquiring for when I stepped out, is on the fifth floor. The doorway of the staircase gives on the little courtyard close to the left here,” pointing with his hand, “near to the window of my establishment. But, now that I remember, one of you has already been there, and can show the way. Gentlemen, adieu!”\n\nThey paid for their wine, and left the place. The eyes of Monsieur Defarge were studying his wife at her knitting when the elderly gentleman advanced from his corner, and begged the favour of a word.\n\n“Willingly, sir,” said Monsieur Defarge, and quietly stepped with him to the door.\n\nTheir conference was very short, but very decided. Almost at the first word, Monsieur Defarge started and became deeply attentive. It had not lasted a minute, when he nodded and went out. The gentleman then beckoned to the young lady, and they, too, went out. Madame Defarge knitted with nimble fingers and steady eyebrows, and saw nothing.\n\nMr. Jarvis Lorry and Miss Manette, emerging from the wine-shop thus, joined Monsieur Defarge in the doorway to which he had directed his own company just before. It opened from a stinking little black courtyard, and was the general public entrance to a great pile of houses, inhabited by a great number of people. In the gloomy tile-paved entry to the gloomy tile-paved staircase, Monsieur Defarge bent down on one knee to the child of his old master, and put her hand to his lips. It was a gentle action, but not at all gently done; a very remarkable transformation had come over him in a few seconds. He had no good-humour in his face, nor any openness of aspect left, but had become a secret, angry, dangerous man.\n\n“It is very high; it is a little difficult. Better to begin slowly.” Thus, Monsieur Defarge, in a stern voice, to Mr. Lorry, as they began ascending the stairs.\n\n“Is he alone?” the latter whispered.\n\n“Alone! God help him, who should be with him!” said the other, in the same low voice.\n\n“Is he always alone, then?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Of his own desire?”\n\n“Of his own necessity. As he was, when I first saw him after they found me and demanded to know if I would take him, and, at my peril be discreet--as he was then, so he is now.”\n\n“He is greatly changed?”\n\n“Changed!”\n\nThe keeper of the wine-shop stopped to strike the wall with his hand, and mutter a tremendous curse. No direct answer could have been half so forcible. Mr. Lorry’s spirits grew heavier and heavier, as he and his two companions ascended higher and higher.\n\nSuch a staircase, with its accessories, in the older and more crowded parts of Paris, would be bad enough now; but, at that time, it was vile indeed to unaccustomed and unhardened senses. Every little habitation within the great foul nest of one high building--that is to say, the room or rooms within every door that opened on the general staircase--left its own heap of refuse on its own landing, besides flinging other refuse from its own windows. The uncontrollable and hopeless mass of decomposition so engendered, would have polluted the air, even if poverty and deprivation had not loaded it with their intangible impurities; the two bad sources combined made it almost insupportable. Through such an atmosphere, by a steep dark shaft of dirt and poison, the way lay. Yielding to his own disturbance of mind, and to his young companion’s agitation, which became greater every instant, Mr. Jarvis Lorry twice stopped to rest. Each of these stoppages was made at a doleful grating, by which any languishing good airs that were left uncorrupted, seemed to escape, and all spoilt and sickly vapours seemed to crawl in. Through the rusted bars, tastes, rather than glimpses, were caught of the jumbled neighbourhood; and nothing within range, nearer or lower than the summits of the two great towers of Notre-Dame, had any promise on it of healthy life or wholesome aspirations.\n\nAt last, the top of the staircase was gained, and they stopped for the third time. There was yet an upper staircase, of a steeper inclination and of contracted dimensions, to be ascended, before the garret story was reached. The keeper of the wine-shop, always going a little in advance, and always going on the side which Mr. Lorry took, as though he dreaded to be asked any question by the young lady, turned himself about here, and, carefully feeling in the pockets of the coat he carried over his shoulder, took out a key.\n\n“The door is locked then, my friend?” said Mr. Lorry, surprised.\n\n“Ay. Yes,” was the grim reply of Monsieur Defarge.\n\n“You think it necessary to keep the unfortunate gentleman so retired?”\n\n“I think it necessary to turn the key.” Monsieur Defarge whispered it closer in his ear, and frowned heavily.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Why! Because he has lived so long, locked up, that he would be frightened--rave--tear himself to pieces--die--come to I know not what harm--if his door was left open.”\n\n“Is it possible!” exclaimed Mr. Lorry.\n\n“Is it possible!” repeated Defarge, bitterly. “Yes. And a beautiful world we live in, when it is possible, and when many other such things are possible, and not only possible, but done--done, see you!--under that sky there, every day. Long live the Devil. Let us go on.”\n\nThis dialogue had been held in so very low a whisper, that not a word of it had reached the young lady’s ears. But, by this time she trembled under such strong emotion, and her face expressed such deep anxiety, and, above all, such dread and terror, that Mr. Lorry felt it incumbent on him to speak a word or two of reassurance.\n\n“Courage, dear miss! Courage! Business! The worst will be over in a moment; it is but passing the room-door, and the worst is over. Then, all the good you bring to him, all the relief, all the happiness you bring to him, begin. Let our good friend here, assist you on that side. That’s well, friend Defarge. Come, now. Business, business!”\n\nThey went up slowly and softly. The staircase was short, and they were soon at the top. There, as it had an abrupt turn in it, they came all at once in sight of three men, whose heads were bent down close together at the side of a door, and who were intently looking into the room to which the door belonged, through some chinks or holes in the wall. On hearing footsteps close at hand, these three turned, and rose, and showed themselves to be the three of one name who had been drinking in the wine-shop.\n\n“I forgot them in the surprise of your visit,” explained Monsieur Defarge. “Leave us, good boys; we have business here.”\n\nThe three glided by, and went silently down.\n\nThere appearing to be no other door on that floor, and the keeper of the wine-shop going straight to this one when they were left alone, Mr. Lorry asked him in a whisper, with a little anger:\n\n“Do you make a show of Monsieur Manette?”\n\n“I show him, in the way you have seen, to a chosen few.”\n\n“Is that well?”\n\n“I think it is well.”\n\n“Who are the few? How do you choose them?”\n\n“I choose them as real men, of my name--Jacques is my name--to whom the sight is likely to do good. Enough; you are English; that is another thing. Stay there, if you please, a little moment.”\n\nWith an admonitory gesture to keep them back, he stooped, and looked in through the crevice in the wall. Soon raising his head again, he struck twice or thrice upon the door--evidently with no other object than to make a noise there. With the same intention, he drew the key across it, three or four times, before he put it clumsily into the lock, and turned it as heavily as he could.\n\nThe door slowly opened inward under his hand, and he looked into the room and said something. A faint voice answered something. Little more than a single syllable could have been spoken on either side.\n\nHe looked back over his shoulder, and beckoned them to enter. Mr. Lorry got his arm securely round the daughter’s waist, and held her; for he felt that she was sinking.\n\n“A-a-a-business, business!” he urged, with a moisture that was not of business shining on his cheek. “Come in, come in!”\n\n“I am afraid of it,” she answered, shuddering.\n\n“Of it? What?”\n\n“I mean of him. Of my father.”\n\nRendered in a manner desperate, by her state and by the beckoning of their conductor, he drew over his neck the arm that shook upon his shoulder, lifted her a little, and hurried her into the room. He sat her down just within the door, and held her, clinging to him.\n\nDefarge drew out the key, closed the door, locked it on the inside, took out the key again, and held it in his hand. All this he did, methodically, and with as loud and harsh an accompaniment of noise as he could make. Finally, he walked across the room with a measured tread to where the window was. He stopped there, and faced round.\n\nThe garret, built to be a depository for firewood and the like, was dim and dark: for, the window of dormer shape, was in truth a door in the roof, with a little crane over it for the hoisting up of stores from the street: unglazed, and closing up the middle in two pieces, like any other door of French construction. To exclude the cold, one half of this door was fast closed, and the other was opened but a very little way. Such a scanty portion of light was admitted through these means, that it was difficult, on first coming in, to see anything; and long habit alone could have slowly formed in any one, the ability to do any work requiring nicety in such obscurity. Yet, work of that kind was being done in the garret; for, with his back towards the door, and his face towards the window where the keeper of the wine-shop stood looking at him, a white-haired man sat on a low bench, stooping forward and very busy, making shoes.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. The Shoemaker\n\n“Good day!” said Monsieur Defarge, looking down at the white head that bent low over the shoemaking.\n\nIt was raised for a moment, and a very faint voice responded to the salutation, as if it were at a distance:\n\n“Good day!”\n\n“You are still hard at work, I see?”\n\nAfter a long silence, the head was lifted for another moment, and the voice replied, “Yes--I am working.” This time, a pair of haggard eyes had looked at the questioner, before the face had dropped again.\n\nThe faintness of the voice was pitiable and dreadful. It was not the faintness of physical weakness, though confinement and hard fare no doubt had their part in it. Its deplorable peculiarity was, that it was the faintness of solitude and disuse. It was like the last feeble echo of a sound made long and long ago. So entirely had it lost the life and resonance of the human voice, that it affected the senses like a once beautiful colour faded away into a poor weak stain. So sunken and suppressed it was, that it was like a voice underground. So expressive it was, of a hopeless and lost creature, that a famished traveller, wearied out by lonely wandering in a wilderness, would have remembered home and friends in such a tone before lying down to die.\n\nSome minutes of silent work had passed: and the haggard eyes had looked up again: not with any interest or curiosity, but with a dull mechanical perception, beforehand, that the spot where the only visitor they were aware of had stood, was not yet empty.\n\n“I want,” said Defarge, who had not removed his gaze from the shoemaker, “to let in a little more light here. You can bear a little more?”\n\nThe shoemaker stopped his work; looked with a vacant air of listening, at the floor on one side of him; then similarly, at the floor on the other side of him; then, upward at the speaker.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“You can bear a little more light?”\n\n“I must bear it, if you let it in.” (Laying the palest shadow of a stress upon the second word.)\n\nThe opened half-door was opened a little further, and secured at that angle for the time. A broad ray of light fell into the garret, and showed the workman with an unfinished shoe upon his lap, pausing in his labour. His few common tools and various scraps of leather were at his feet and on his bench. He had a white beard, raggedly cut, but not very long, a hollow face, and exceedingly bright eyes. The hollowness and thinness of his face would have caused them to look large, under his yet dark eyebrows and his confused white hair, though they had been really otherwise; but, they were naturally large, and looked unnaturally so. His yellow rags of shirt lay open at the throat, and showed his body to be withered and worn. He, and his old canvas frock, and his loose stockings, and all his poor tatters of clothes, had, in a long seclusion from direct light and air, faded down to such a dull uniformity of parchment-yellow, that it would have been hard to say which was which.\n\nHe had put up a hand between his eyes and the light, and the very bones of it seemed transparent. So he sat, with a steadfastly vacant gaze, pausing in his work. He never looked at the figure before him, without first looking down on this side of himself, then on that, as if he had lost the habit of associating place with sound; he never spoke, without first wandering in this manner, and forgetting to speak.\n\n“Are you going to finish that pair of shoes to-day?” asked Defarge, motioning to Mr. Lorry to come forward.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“Do you mean to finish that pair of shoes to-day?”\n\n“I can’t say that I mean to. I suppose so. I don’t know.”\n\nBut, the question reminded him of his work, and he bent over it again.\n\nMr. Lorry came silently forward, leaving the daughter by the door. When he had stood, for a minute or two, by the side of Defarge, the shoemaker looked up. He showed no surprise at seeing another figure, but the unsteady fingers of one of his hands strayed to his lips as he looked at it (his lips and his nails were of the same pale lead-colour), and then the hand dropped to his work, and he once more bent over the shoe. The look and the action had occupied but an instant.\n\n“You have a visitor, you see,” said Monsieur Defarge.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“Here is a visitor.”\n\nThe shoemaker looked up as before, but without removing a hand from his work.\n\n“Come!” said Defarge. “Here is monsieur, who knows a well-made shoe when he sees one. Show him that shoe you are working at. Take it, monsieur.”\n\nMr. Lorry took it in his hand.\n\n“Tell monsieur what kind of shoe it is, and the maker’s name.”\n\nThere was a longer pause than usual, before the shoemaker replied:\n\n“I forget what it was you asked me. What did you say?”\n\n“I said, couldn’t you describe the kind of shoe, for monsieur’s information?”\n\n“It is a lady’s shoe. It is a young lady’s walking-shoe. It is in the present mode. I never saw the mode. I have had a pattern in my hand.” He glanced at the shoe with some little passing touch of pride.\n\n“And the maker’s name?” said Defarge.\n\nNow that he had no work to hold, he laid the knuckles of the right hand in the hollow of the left, and then the knuckles of the left hand in the hollow of the right, and then passed a hand across his bearded chin, and so on in regular changes, without a moment’s intermission. The task of recalling him from the vagrancy into which he always sank when he had spoken, was like recalling some very weak person from a swoon, or endeavouring, in the hope of some disclosure, to stay the spirit of a fast-dying man.\n\n“Did you ask me for my name?”\n\n“Assuredly I did.”\n\n“One Hundred and Five, North Tower.”\n\n“Is that all?”\n\n“One Hundred and Five, North Tower.”\n\nWith a weary sound that was not a sigh, nor a groan, he bent to work again, until the silence was again broken.\n\n“You are not a shoemaker by trade?” said Mr. Lorry, looking steadfastly at him.\n\nHis haggard eyes turned to Defarge as if he would have transferred the question to him: but as no help came from that quarter, they turned back on the questioner when they had sought the ground.\n\n“I am not a shoemaker by trade? No, I was not a shoemaker by trade. I-I learnt it here. I taught myself. I asked leave to--”\n\nHe lapsed away, even for minutes, ringing those measured changes on his hands the whole time. His eyes came slowly back, at last, to the face from which they had wandered; when they rested on it, he started, and resumed, in the manner of a sleeper that moment awake, reverting to a subject of last night.\n\n“I asked leave to teach myself, and I got it with much difficulty after a long while, and I have made shoes ever since.”\n\nAs he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still looking steadfastly in his face:\n\n“Monsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?”\n\nThe shoe dropped to the ground, and he sat looking fixedly at the questioner.\n\n“Monsieur Manette”; Mr. Lorry laid his hand upon Defarge’s arm; “do you remember nothing of this man? Look at him. Look at me. Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time, rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?”\n\nAs the captive of many years sat looking fixedly, by turns, at Mr. Lorry and at Defarge, some long obliterated marks of an actively intent intelligence in the middle of the forehead, gradually forced themselves through the black mist that had fallen on him. They were overclouded again, they were fainter, they were gone; but they had been there. And so exactly was the expression repeated on the fair young face of her who had crept along the wall to a point where she could see him, and where she now stood looking at him, with hands which at first had been only raised in frightened compassion, if not even to keep him off and shut out the sight of him, but which were now extending towards him, trembling with eagerness to lay the spectral face upon her warm young breast, and love it back to life and hope--so exactly was the expression repeated (though in stronger characters) on her fair young face, that it looked as though it had passed like a moving light, from him to her.\n\nDarkness had fallen on him in its place. He looked at the two, less and less attentively, and his eyes in gloomy abstraction sought the ground and looked about him in the old way. Finally, with a deep long sigh, he took the shoe up, and resumed his work.\n\n“Have you recognised him, monsieur?” asked Defarge in a whisper.\n\n“Yes; for a moment. At first I thought it quite hopeless, but I have unquestionably seen, for a single moment, the face that I once knew so well. Hush! Let us draw further back. Hush!”\n\nShe had moved from the wall of the garret, very near to the bench on which he sat. There was something awful in his unconsciousness of the figure that could have put out its hand and touched him as he stooped over his labour.\n\nNot a word was spoken, not a sound was made. She stood, like a spirit, beside him, and he bent over his work.\n\nIt happened, at length, that he had occasion to change the instrument in his hand, for his shoemaker’s knife. It lay on that side of him which was not the side on which she stood. He had taken it up, and was stooping to work again, when his eyes caught the skirt of her dress. He raised them, and saw her face. The two spectators started forward, but she stayed them with a motion of her hand. She had no fear of his striking at her with the knife, though they had.\n\nHe stared at her with a fearful look, and after a while his lips began to form some words, though no sound proceeded from them. By degrees, in the pauses of his quick and laboured breathing, he was heard to say:\n\n“What is this?”\n\nWith the tears streaming down her face, she put her two hands to her lips, and kissed them to him; then clasped them on her breast, as if she laid his ruined head there.\n\n“You are not the gaoler’s daughter?”\n\nShe sighed “No.”\n\n“Who are you?”\nNot yet trusting the tones of her voice, she sat down on the bench beside him. He recoiled, but she laid her hand upon his arm. A strange thrill struck him when she did so, and visibly passed over his frame; he laid the knife down softly, as he sat staring at her.\n\nHer golden hair, which she wore in long curls, had been hurriedly pushed aside, and fell down over her neck. Advancing his hand by little and little, he took it up and looked at it. In the midst of the action he went astray, and, with another deep sigh, fell to work at his shoemaking.\n\nBut not for long. Releasing his arm, she laid her hand upon his shoulder. After looking doubtfully at it, two or three times, as if to be sure that it was really there, he laid down his work, put his hand to his neck, and took off a blackened string with a scrap of folded rag attached to it. He opened this, carefully, on his knee, and it contained a very little quantity of hair: not more than one or two long golden hairs, which he had, in some old day, wound off upon his finger.\n\nHe took her hair into his hand again, and looked closely at it. “It is the same. How can it be! When was it! How was it!”\n\nAs the concentrated expression returned to his forehead, he seemed to become conscious that it was in hers too. He turned her full to the light, and looked at her.\n\n“She had laid her head upon my shoulder, that night when I was summoned out--she had a fear of my going, though I had none--and when I was brought to the North Tower they found these upon my sleeve. ‘You will leave me them? They can never help me to escape in the body, though they may in the spirit.’ Those were the words I said. I remember them very well.”\n\nHe formed this speech with his lips many times before he could utter it. But when he did find spoken words for it, they came to him coherently, though slowly.\n\n“How was this?--Was it you?”\n\nOnce more, the two spectators started, as he turned upon her with a frightful suddenness. But she sat perfectly still in his grasp, and only said, in a low voice, “I entreat you, good gentlemen, do not come near us, do not speak, do not move!”\n\n“Hark!” he exclaimed. “Whose voice was that?”\n\nHis hands released her as he uttered this cry, and went up to his white hair, which they tore in a frenzy. It died out, as everything but his shoemaking did die out of him, and he refolded his little packet and tried to secure it in his breast; but he still looked at her, and gloomily shook his head.\n\n“No, no, no; you are too young, too blooming. It can’t be. See what the prisoner is. These are not the hands she knew, this is not the face she knew, this is not a voice she ever heard. No, no. She was--and He was--before the slow years of the North Tower--ages ago. What is your name, my gentle angel?”\n\nHailing his softened tone and manner, his daughter fell upon her knees before him, with her appealing hands upon his breast.\n\n“O, sir, at another time you shall know my name, and who my mother was, and who my father, and how I never knew their hard, hard history. But I cannot tell you at this time, and I cannot tell you here. All that I may tell you, here and now, is, that I pray to you to touch me and to bless me. Kiss me, kiss me! O my dear, my dear!”\n\nHis cold white head mingled with her radiant hair, which warmed and lighted it as though it were the light of Freedom shining on him.\n\n“If you hear in my voice--I don’t know that it is so, but I hope it is--if you hear in my voice any resemblance to a voice that once was sweet music in your ears, weep for it, weep for it! If you touch, in touching my hair, anything that recalls a beloved head that lay on your breast when you were young and free, weep for it, weep for it! If, when I hint to you of a Home that is before us, where I will be true to you with all my duty and with all my faithful service, I bring back the remembrance of a Home long desolate, while your poor heart pined away, weep for it, weep for it!”\n\nShe held him closer round the neck, and rocked him on her breast like a child.\n\n“If, when I tell you, dearest dear, that your agony is over, and that I have come here to take you from it, and that we go to England to be at peace and at rest, I cause you to think of your useful life laid waste, and of our native France so wicked to you, weep for it, weep for it! And if, when I shall tell you of my name, and of my father who is living, and of my mother who is dead, you learn that I have to kneel to my honoured father, and implore his pardon for having never for his sake striven all day and lain awake and wept all night, because the love of my poor mother hid his torture from me, weep for it, weep for it! Weep for her, then, and for me! Good gentlemen, thank God! I feel his sacred tears upon my face, and his sobs strike against my heart. O, see! Thank God for us, thank God!”\n\nHe had sunk in her arms, and his face dropped on her breast: a sight so touching, yet so terrible in the tremendous wrong and suffering which had gone before it, that the two beholders covered their faces.\n\nWhen the quiet of the garret had been long undisturbed, and his heaving breast and shaken form had long yielded to the calm that must follow all storms--emblem to humanity, of the rest and silence into which the storm called Life must hush at last--they came forward to raise the father and daughter from the ground. He had gradually dropped to the floor, and lay there in a lethargy, worn out. She had nestled down with him, that his head might lie upon her arm; and her hair drooping over him curtained him from the light.\n\n“If, without disturbing him,” she said, raising her hand to Mr. Lorry as he stooped over them, after repeated blowings of his nose, “all could be arranged for our leaving Paris at once, so that, from the very door, he could be taken away--”\n\n“But, consider. Is he fit for the journey?” asked Mr. Lorry.\n\n“More fit for that, I think, than to remain in this city, so dreadful to him.”\n\n“It is true,” said Defarge, who was kneeling to look on and hear. “More than that; Monsieur Manette is, for all reasons, best out of France. Say, shall I hire a carriage and post-horses?”\n\n“That’s business,” said Mr. Lorry, resuming on the shortest notice his methodical manners; “and if business is to be done, I had better do it.”\n\n“Then be so kind,” urged Miss Manette, “as to leave us here. You see how composed he has become, and you cannot be afraid to leave him with me now. Why should you be? If you will lock the door to secure us from interruption, I do not doubt that you will find him, when you come back, as quiet as you leave him. In any case, I will take care of him until you return, and then we will remove him straight.”\n\nBoth Mr. Lorry and Defarge were rather disinclined to this course, and in favour of one of them remaining. But, as there were not only carriage and horses to be seen to, but travelling papers; and as time pressed, for the day was drawing to an end, it came at last to their hastily dividing the business that was necessary to be done, and hurrying away to do it.\n\nThen, as the darkness closed in, the daughter laid her head down on the hard ground close at the father’s side, and watched him. The darkness deepened and deepened, and they both lay quiet, until a light gleamed through the chinks in the wall.\n\nMr. Lorry and Monsieur Defarge had made all ready for the journey, and had brought with them, besides travelling cloaks and wrappers, bread and meat, wine, and hot coffee. Monsieur Defarge put this provender, and the lamp he carried, on the shoemaker’s bench (there was nothing else in the garret but a pallet bed), and he and Mr. Lorry roused the captive, and assisted him to his feet.\n\nNo human intelligence could have read the mysteries of his mind, in the scared blank wonder of his face. Whether he knew what had happened, whether he recollected what they had said to him, whether he knew that he was free, were questions which no sagacity could have solved. They tried speaking to him; but, he was so confused, and so very slow to answer, that they took fright at his bewilderment, and agreed for the time to tamper with him no more. He had a wild, lost manner of occasionally clasping his head in his hands, that had not been seen in him before; yet, he had some pleasure in the mere sound of his daughter’s voice, and invariably turned to it when she spoke.\n\nIn the submissive way of one long accustomed to obey under coercion, he ate and drank what they gave him to eat and drink, and put on the cloak and other wrappings, that they gave him to wear. He readily responded to his daughter’s drawing her arm through his, and took--and kept--her hand in both his own.\n\nThey began to descend; Monsieur Defarge going first with the lamp, Mr. Lorry closing the little procession. They had not traversed many steps of the long main staircase when he stopped, and stared at the roof and round at the walls.\n\n“You remember the place, my father? You remember coming up here?”\n\n“What did you say?”\n\nBut, before she could repeat the question, he murmured an answer as if she had repeated it.\n\n“Remember? No, I don’t remember. It was so very long ago.”\n\nThat he had no recollection whatever of his having been brought from his prison to that house, was apparent to them. They heard him mutter, “One Hundred and Five, North Tower;” and when he looked about him, it evidently was for the strong fortress-walls which had long encompassed him. On their reaching the courtyard he instinctively altered his tread, as being in expectation of a drawbridge; and when there was no drawbridge, and he saw the carriage waiting in the open street, he dropped his daughter’s hand and clasped his head again.\n\nNo crowd was about the door; no people were discernible at any of the many windows; not even a chance passerby was in the street. An unnatural silence and desertion reigned there. Only one soul was to be seen, and that was Madame Defarge--who leaned against the door-post, knitting, and saw nothing.\n\nThe prisoner had got into a coach, and his daughter had followed him, when Mr. Lorry’s feet were arrested on the step by his asking, miserably, for his shoemaking tools and the unfinished shoes. Madame Defarge immediately called to her husband that she would get them, and went, knitting, out of the lamplight, through the courtyard. She quickly brought them down and handed them in;--and immediately afterwards leaned against the door-post, knitting, and saw nothing.\n\nDefarge got upon the box, and gave the word “To the Barrier!” The postilion cracked his whip, and they clattered away under the feeble over-swinging lamps.\n\nUnder the over-swinging lamps--swinging ever brighter in the better streets, and ever dimmer in the worse--and by lighted shops, gay crowds, illuminated coffee-houses, and theatre-doors, to one of the city gates. Soldiers with lanterns, at the guard-house there. “Your papers, travellers!” “See here then, Monsieur the Officer,” said Defarge, getting down, and taking him gravely apart, “these are the papers of monsieur inside, with the white head. They were consigned to me, with him, at the--” He dropped his voice, there was a flutter among the military lanterns, and one of them being handed into the coach by an arm in uniform, the eyes connected with the arm looked, not an every day or an every night look, at monsieur with the white head. “It is well. Forward!” from the uniform. “Adieu!” from Defarge. And so, under a short grove of feebler and feebler over-swinging lamps, out under the great grove of stars.\n\nBeneath that arch of unmoved and eternal lights; some, so remote from this little earth that the learned tell us it is doubtful whether their rays have even yet discovered it, as a point in space where anything is suffered or done: the shadows of the night were broad and black. All through the cold and restless interval, until dawn, they once more whispered in the ears of Mr. Jarvis Lorry--sitting opposite the buried man who had been dug out, and wondering what subtle powers were for ever lost to him, and what were capable of restoration--the old inquiry:\n\n“I hope you care to be recalled to life?”\n\nAnd the old answer:\n\n“I can’t say.”\n\n# Book the Second--the Golden Thread CHAPTER I. Five Years Later\n\nTellson’s Bank by Temple Bar was an old-fashioned place, even in the year one thousand seven hundred and eighty. It was very small, very dark, very ugly, very incommodious. It was an old-fashioned place, moreover, in the moral attribute that the partners in the House were proud of its smallness, proud of its darkness, proud of its ugliness, proud of its incommodiousness. They were even boastful of its eminence in those particulars, and were fired by an express conviction that, if it were less objectionable, it would be less respectable. This was no passive belief, but an active weapon which they flashed at more convenient places of business. Tellson’s (they said) wanted no elbow-room, Tellson’s wanted no light, Tellson’s wanted no embellishment. Noakes and Co.’s might, or Snooks Brothers’ might; but Tellson’s, thank Heaven--!\n\nAny one of these partners would have disinherited his son on the question of rebuilding Tellson’s. In this respect the House was much on a par with the Country; which did very often disinherit its sons for suggesting improvements in laws and customs that had long been highly objectionable, but were only the more respectable.\n\nThus it had come to pass, that Tellson’s was the triumphant perfection of inconvenience. After bursting open a door of idiotic obstinacy with a weak rattle in its throat, you fell into Tellson’s down two steps, and came to your senses in a miserable little shop, with two little counters, where the oldest of men made your cheque shake as if the wind rustled it, while they examined the signature by the dingiest of windows, which were always under a shower-bath of mud from Fleet-street, and which were made the dingier by their own iron bars proper, and the heavy shadow of Temple Bar. If your business necessitated your seeing “the House,” you were put into a species of Condemned Hold at the back, where you meditated on a misspent life, until the House came with its hands in its pockets, and you could hardly blink at it in the dismal twilight. Your money came out of, or went into, wormy old wooden drawers, particles of which flew up your nose and down your throat when they were opened and shut. Your bank-notes had a musty odour, as if they were fast decomposing into rags again. Your plate was stowed away among the neighbouring cesspools, and evil communications corrupted its good polish in a day or two. Your deeds got into extemporised strong-rooms made of kitchens and sculleries, and fretted all the fat out of their parchments into the banking-house air. Your lighter boxes of family papers went up-stairs into a Barmecide room, that always had a great dining-table in it and never had a dinner, and where, even in the year one thousand seven hundred and eighty, the first letters written to you by your old love, or by your little children, were but newly released from the horror of being ogled through the windows, by the heads exposed on Temple Bar with an insensate brutality and ferocity worthy of Abyssinia or Ashantee.\n\nBut indeed, at that time, putting to death was a recipe much in vogue with all trades and professions, and not least of all with Tellson’s. Death is Nature’s remedy for all things, and why not Legislation’s? Accordingly, the forger was put to Death; the utterer of a bad note was put to Death; the unlawful opener of a letter was put to Death; the purloiner of forty shillings and sixpence was put to Death; the holder of a horse at Tellson’s door, who made off with it, was put to Death; the coiner of a bad shilling was put to Death; the sounders of three-fourths of the notes in the whole gamut of Crime, were put to Death. Not that it did the least good in the way of prevention--it might almost have been worth remarking that the fact was exactly the reverse--but, it cleared off (as to this world) the trouble of each particular case, and left nothing else connected with it to be looked after. Thus, Tellson’s, in its day, like greater places of business, its contemporaries, had taken so many lives, that, if the heads laid low before it had been ranged on Temple Bar instead of being privately disposed of, they would probably have excluded what little light the ground floor had, in a rather significant manner.\n\nCramped in all kinds of dim cupboards and hutches at Tellson’s, the oldest of men carried on the business gravely. When they took a young man into Tellson’s London house, they hid him somewhere till he was old. They kept him in a dark place, like a cheese, until he had the full Tellson flavour and blue-mould upon him. Then only was he permitted to be seen, spectacularly poring over large books, and casting his breeches and gaiters into the general weight of the establishment.\n\nOutside Tellson’s--never by any means in it, unless called in--was an odd-job-man, an occasional porter and messenger, who served as the live sign of the house. He was never absent during business hours, unless upon an errand, and then he was represented by his son: a grisly urchin of twelve, who was his express image. People understood that Tellson’s, in a stately way, tolerated the odd-job-man. The house had always tolerated some person in that capacity, and time and tide had drifted this person to the post. His surname was Cruncher, and on the youthful occasion of his renouncing by proxy the works of darkness, in the easterly parish church of Hounsditch, he had received the added appellation of Jerry.\n\nThe scene was Mr. Cruncher’s private lodging in Hanging-sword-alley, Whitefriars: the time, half-past seven of the clock on a windy March morning, Anno Domini seventeen hundred and eighty. (Mr. Cruncher himself always spoke of the year of our Lord as Anna Dominoes: apparently under the impression that the Christian era dated from the invention of a popular game, by a lady who had bestowed her name upon it.)\n\nMr. Cruncher’s apartments were not in a savoury neighbourhood, and were but two in number, even if a closet with a single pane of glass in it might be counted as one. But they were very decently kept. Early as it was, on the windy March morning, the room in which he lay abed was already scrubbed throughout; and between the cups and saucers arranged for breakfast, and the lumbering deal table, a very clean white cloth was spread.\n\nMr. Cruncher reposed under a patchwork counterpane, like a Harlequin at home. At first, he slept heavily, but, by degrees, began to roll and surge in bed, until he rose above the surface, with his spiky hair looking as if it must tear the sheets to ribbons. At which juncture, he exclaimed, in a voice of dire exasperation:\n\n“Bust me, if she ain’t at it agin!”\n\nA woman of orderly and industrious appearance rose from her knees in a corner, with sufficient haste and trepidation to show that she was the person referred to.\n\n“What!” said Mr. Cruncher, looking out of bed for a boot. “You’re at it agin, are you?”\n\nAfter hailing the morn with this second salutation, he threw a boot at the woman as a third. It was a very muddy boot, and may introduce the odd circumstance connected with Mr. Cruncher’s domestic economy, that, whereas he often came home after banking hours with clean boots, he often got up next morning to find the same boots covered with clay.\n\n“What,” said Mr. Cruncher, varying his apostrophe after missing his mark--“what are you up to, Aggerawayter?”\n\n“I was only saying my prayers.”\n\n“Saying your prayers! You’re a nice woman! What do you mean by flopping yourself down and praying agin me?”\n\n“I was not praying against you; I was praying for you.”\n\n“You weren’t. And if you were, I won’t be took the liberty with. Here! your mother’s a nice woman, young Jerry, going a praying agin your father’s prosperity. You’ve got a dutiful mother, you have, my son. You’ve got a religious mother, you have, my boy: going and flopping herself down, and praying that the bread-and-butter may be snatched out of the mouth of her only child.”\n\nMaster Cruncher (who was in his shirt) took this very ill, and, turning to his mother, strongly deprecated any praying away of his personal board.\n\n“And what do you suppose, you conceited female,” said Mr. Cruncher, with unconscious inconsistency, “that the worth of your prayers may be? Name the price that you put your prayers at!”\n\n“They only come from the heart, Jerry. They are worth no more than that.”\n\n“Worth no more than that,” repeated Mr. Cruncher. “They ain’t worth much, then. Whether or no, I won’t be prayed agin, I tell you. I can’t afford it. I’m not a going to be made unlucky by your sneaking. If you must go flopping yourself down, flop in favour of your husband and child, and not in opposition to ’em. If I had had any but a unnat’ral wife, and this poor boy had had any but a unnat’ral mother, I might have made some money last week instead of being counter-prayed and countermined and religiously circumwented into the worst of luck. B-u-u-ust me!” said Mr. Cruncher, who all this time had been putting on his clothes, “if I ain’t, what with piety and one blowed thing and another, been choused this last week into as bad luck as ever a poor devil of a honest tradesman met with! Young Jerry, dress yourself, my boy, and while I clean my boots keep a eye upon your mother now and then, and if you see any signs of more flopping, give me a call. For, I tell you,” here he addressed his wife once more, “I won’t be gone agin, in this manner. I am as rickety as a hackney-coach, I’m as sleepy as laudanum, my lines is strained to that degree that I shouldn’t know, if it wasn’t for the pain in ’em, which was me and which somebody else, yet I’m none the better for it in pocket; and it’s my suspicion that you’ve been at it from morning to night to prevent me from being the better for it in pocket, and I won’t put up with it, Aggerawayter, and what do you say now!”\n\nGrowling, in addition, such phrases as “Ah! yes! You’re religious, too. You wouldn’t put yourself in opposition to the interests of your husband and child, would you? Not you!” and throwing off other sarcastic sparks from the whirling grindstone of his indignation, Mr. Cruncher betook himself to his boot-cleaning and his general preparation for business. In the meantime, his son, whose head was garnished with tenderer spikes, and whose young eyes stood close by one another, as his father’s did, kept the required watch upon his mother. He greatly disturbed that poor woman at intervals, by darting out of his sleeping closet, where he made his toilet, with a suppressed cry of “You are going to flop, mother. --Halloa, father!” and, after raising this fictitious alarm, darting in again with an undutiful grin.\n\nMr. Cruncher’s temper was not at all improved when he came to his breakfast. He resented Mrs. Cruncher’s saying grace with particular animosity.\n\n“Now, Aggerawayter! What are you up to? At it again?”\n\nHis wife explained that she had merely “asked a blessing.”\n\n“Don’t do it!” said Mr. Crunches looking about, as if he rather expected to see the loaf disappear under the efficacy of his wife’s petitions. “I ain’t a going to be blest out of house and home. I won’t have my wittles blest off my table. Keep still!”\n\nExceedingly red-eyed and grim, as if he had been up all night at a party which had taken anything but a convivial turn, Jerry Cruncher worried his breakfast rather than ate it, growling over it like any four-footed inmate of a menagerie. Towards nine o’clock he smoothed his ruffled aspect, and, presenting as respectable and business-like an exterior as he could overlay his natural self with, issued forth to the occupation of the day.\n\nIt could scarcely be called a trade, in spite of his favourite description of himself as “a honest tradesman.” His stock consisted of a wooden stool, made out of a broken-backed chair cut down, which stool, young Jerry, walking at his father’s side, carried every morning to beneath the banking-house window that was nearest Temple Bar: where, with the addition of the first handful of straw that could be gleaned from any passing vehicle to keep the cold and wet from the odd-job-man’s feet, it formed the encampment for the day. On this post of his, Mr. Cruncher was as well known to Fleet-street and the Temple, as the Bar itself,--and was almost as in-looking.\n\nEncamped at a quarter before nine, in good time to touch his three-cornered hat to the oldest of men as they passed in to Tellson’s, Jerry took up his station on this windy March morning, with young Jerry standing by him, when not engaged in making forays through the Bar, to inflict bodily and mental injuries of an acute description on passing boys who were small enough for his amiable purpose. Father and son, extremely like each other, looking silently on at the morning traffic in Fleet-street, with their two heads as near to one another as the two eyes of each were, bore a considerable resemblance to a pair of monkeys. The resemblance was not lessened by the accidental circumstance, that the mature Jerry bit and spat out straw, while the twinkling eyes of the youthful Jerry were as restlessly watchful of him as of everything else in Fleet-street.\n\nThe head of one of the regular indoor messengers attached to Tellson’s establishment was put through the door, and the word was given:\n\n“Porter wanted!”\n\n“Hooray, father! Here’s an early job to begin with!”\n\nHaving thus given his parent God speed, young Jerry seated himself on the stool, entered on his reversionary interest in the straw his father had been chewing, and cogitated.\n\n“Al-ways rusty! His fingers is al-ways rusty!” muttered young Jerry. “Where does my father get all that iron rust from? He don’t get no iron rust here!”\n\n# CHAPTER II. A Sight\n\n“You know the Old Bailey well, no doubt?” said one of the oldest of clerks to Jerry the messenger.\n\n“Ye-es, sir,” returned Jerry, in something of a dogged manner. “I do know the Bailey.”\n\n“Just so. And you know Mr. Lorry.”\n\n“I know Mr. Lorry, sir, much better than I know the Bailey. Much better,” said Jerry, not unlike a reluctant witness at the establishment in question, “than I, as a honest tradesman, wish to know the Bailey.”\n\n“Very well. Find the door where the witnesses go in, and show the door-keeper this note for Mr. Lorry. He will then let you in.”\n\n“Into the court, sir?”\n\n“Into the court.”\n\nMr. Cruncher’s eyes seemed to get a little closer to one another, and to interchange the inquiry, “What do you think of this?”\n\n“Am I to wait in the court, sir?” he asked, as the result of that conference.\n\n“I am going to tell you. The door-keeper will pass the note to Mr. Lorry, and do you make any gesture that will attract Mr. Lorry’s attention, and show him where you stand. Then what you have to do, is, to remain there until he wants you.”\n\n“Is that all, sir?”\n\n“That’s all. He wishes to have a messenger at hand. This is to tell him you are there.”\n\nAs the ancient clerk deliberately folded and superscribed the note, Mr. Cruncher, after surveying him in silence until he came to the blotting-paper stage, remarked:\n\n“I suppose they’ll be trying Forgeries this morning?”\n\n“Treason!”\n\n“That’s quartering,” said Jerry. “Barbarous!”\n\n“It is the law,” remarked the ancient clerk, turning his surprised spectacles upon him. “It is the law.”\n\n“It’s hard in the law to spile a man, I think. It’s hard enough to kill him, but it’s wery hard to spile him, sir.”\n\n“Not at all,” retained the ancient clerk. “Speak well of the law. Take care of your chest and voice, my good friend, and leave the law to take care of itself. I give you that advice.”\n\n“It’s the damp, sir, what settles on my chest and voice,” said Jerry. “I leave you to judge what a damp way of earning a living mine is.”\n\n“Well, well,” said the old clerk; “we all have our various ways of gaining a livelihood. Some of us have damp ways, and some of us have dry ways. Here is the letter. Go along.”\n\nJerry took the letter, and, remarking to himself with less internal deference than he made an outward show of, “You are a lean old one, too,” made his bow, informed his son, in passing, of his destination, and went his way.\n\nThey hanged at Tyburn, in those days, so the street outside Newgate had not obtained one infamous notoriety that has since attached to it. But, the gaol was a vile place, in which most kinds of debauchery and villainy were practised, and where dire diseases were bred, that came into court with the prisoners, and sometimes rushed straight from the dock at my Lord Chief Justice himself, and pulled him off the bench. It had more than once happened, that the Judge in the black cap pronounced his own doom as certainly as the prisoner’s, and even died before him. For the rest, the Old Bailey was famous as a kind of deadly inn-yard, from which pale travellers set out continually, in carts and coaches, on a violent passage into the other world: traversing some two miles and a half of public street and road, and shaming few good citizens, if any. So powerful is use, and so desirable to be good use in the beginning. It was famous, too, for the pillory, a wise old institution, that inflicted a punishment of which no one could foresee the extent; also, for the whipping-post, another dear old institution, very humanising and softening to behold in action; also, for extensive transactions in blood-money, another fragment of ancestral wisdom, systematically leading to the most frightful mercenary crimes that could be committed under Heaven. Altogether, the Old Bailey, at that date, was a choice illustration of the precept, that “Whatever is is right;” an aphorism that would be as final as it is lazy, did it not include the troublesome consequence, that nothing that ever was, was wrong.\n\nMaking his way through the tainted crowd, dispersed up and down this hideous scene of action, with the skill of a man accustomed to make his way quietly, the messenger found out the door he sought, and handed in his letter through a trap in it. For, people then paid to see the play at the Old Bailey, just as they paid to see the play in Bedlam--only the former entertainment was much the dearer. Therefore, all the Old Bailey doors were well guarded--except, indeed, the social doors by which the criminals got there, and those were always left wide open.\n\nAfter some delay and demur, the door grudgingly turned on its hinges a very little way, and allowed Mr. Jerry Cruncher to squeeze himself into court.\n\n“What’s on?” he asked, in a whisper, of the man he found himself next to.\n\n“Nothing yet.”\n\n“What’s coming on?”\n\n“The Treason case.”\n\n“The quartering one, eh?”\n\n“Ah!” returned the man, with a relish; “he’ll be drawn on a hurdle to be half hanged, and then he’ll be taken down and sliced before his own face, and then his inside will be taken out and burnt while he looks on, and then his head will be chopped off, and he’ll be cut into quarters. That’s the sentence.”\n\n“If he’s found Guilty, you mean to say?” Jerry added, by way of proviso.\n\n“Oh! they’ll find him guilty,” said the other. “Don’t you be afraid of that.”\n\nMr. Cruncher’s attention was here diverted to the door-keeper, whom he saw making his way to Mr. Lorry, with the note in his hand. Mr. Lorry sat at a table, among the gentlemen in wigs: not far from a wigged gentleman, the prisoner’s counsel, who had a great bundle of papers before him: and nearly opposite another wigged gentleman with his hands in his pockets, whose whole attention, when Mr. Cruncher looked at him then or afterwards, seemed to be concentrated on the ceiling of the court. After some gruff coughing and rubbing of his chin and signing with his hand, Jerry attracted the notice of Mr. Lorry, who had stood up to look for him, and who quietly nodded and sat down again.\n\n“What’s he got to do with the case?” asked the man he had spoken with.\n\n“Blest if I know,” said Jerry.\n\n“What have you got to do with it, then, if a person may inquire?”\n\n“Blest if I know that either,” said Jerry.\n\nThe entrance of the Judge, and a consequent great stir and settling down in the court, stopped the dialogue. Presently, the dock became the central point of interest. Two gaolers, who had been standing there, went out, and the prisoner was brought in, and put to the bar.\n\nEverybody present, except the one wigged gentleman who looked at the ceiling, stared at him. All the human breath in the place, rolled at him, like a sea, or a wind, or a fire. Eager faces strained round pillars and corners, to get a sight of him; spectators in back rows stood up, not to miss a hair of him; people on the floor of the court, laid their hands on the shoulders of the people before them, to help themselves, at anybody’s cost, to a view of him--stood a-tiptoe, got upon ledges, stood upon next to nothing, to see every inch of him. Conspicuous among these latter, like an animated bit of the spiked wall of Newgate, Jerry stood: aiming at the prisoner the beery breath of a whet he had taken as he came along, and discharging it to mingle with the waves of other beer, and gin, and tea, and coffee, and what not, that flowed at him, and already broke upon the great windows behind him in an impure mist and rain.\n\nThe object of all this staring and blaring, was a young man of about five-and-twenty, well-grown and well-looking, with a sunburnt cheek and a dark eye. His condition was that of a young gentleman. He was plainly dressed in black, or very dark grey, and his hair, which was long and dark, was gathered in a ribbon at the back of his neck; more to be out of his way than for ornament. As an emotion of the mind will express itself through any covering of the body, so the paleness which his situation engendered came through the brown upon his cheek, showing the soul to be stronger than the sun. He was otherwise quite self-possessed, bowed to the Judge, and stood quiet.\n\nThe sort of interest with which this man was stared and breathed at, was not a sort that elevated humanity. Had he stood in peril of a less horrible sentence--had there been a chance of any one of its savage details being spared--by just so much would he have lost in his fascination. The form that was to be doomed to be so shamefully mangled, was the sight; the immortal creature that was to be so butchered and torn asunder, yielded the sensation. Whatever gloss the various spectators put upon the interest, according to their several arts and powers of self-deceit, the interest was, at the root of it, Ogreish.\n\nSilence in the court! Charles Darnay had yesterday pleaded Not Guilty to an indictment denouncing him (with infinite jingle and jangle) for that he was a false traitor to our serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, prince, our Lord the King, by reason of his having, on divers occasions, and by divers means and ways, assisted Lewis, the French King, in his wars against our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth; that was to say, by coming and going, between the dominions of our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, and those of the said French Lewis, and wickedly, falsely, traitorously, and otherwise evil-adverbiously, revealing to the said French Lewis what forces our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, had in preparation to send to Canada and North America. This much, Jerry, with his head becoming more and more spiky as the law terms bristled it, made out with huge satisfaction, and so arrived circuitously at the understanding that the aforesaid, and over and over again aforesaid, Charles Darnay, stood there before him upon his trial; that the jury were swearing in; and that Mr. Attorney-General was making ready to speak.\n\nThe accused, who was (and who knew he was) being mentally hanged, beheaded, and quartered, by everybody there, neither flinched from the situation, nor assumed any theatrical air in it. He was quiet and attentive; watched the opening proceedings with a grave interest; and stood with his hands resting on the slab of wood before him, so composedly, that they had not displaced a leaf of the herbs with which it was strewn. The court was all bestrewn with herbs and sprinkled with vinegar, as a precaution against gaol air and gaol fever.\n\nOver the prisoner’s head there was a mirror, to throw the light down upon him. Crowds of the wicked and the wretched had been reflected in it, and had passed from its surface and this earth’s together. Haunted in a most ghastly manner that abominable place would have been, if the glass could ever have rendered back its reflections, as the ocean is one day to give up its dead. Some passing thought of the infamy and disgrace for which it had been reserved, may have struck the prisoner’s mind. Be that as it may, a change in his position making him conscious of a bar of light across his face, he looked up; and when he saw the glass his face flushed, and his right hand pushed the herbs away.\n\nIt happened, that the action turned his face to that side of the court which was on his left. About on a level with his eyes, there sat, in that corner of the Judge’s bench, two persons upon whom his look immediately rested; so immediately, and so much to the changing of his aspect, that all the eyes that were turned upon him, turned to them.\n\nThe spectators saw in the two figures, a young lady of little more than twenty, and a gentleman who was evidently her father; a man of a very remarkable appearance in respect of the absolute whiteness of his hair, and a certain indescribable intensity of face: not of an active kind, but pondering and self-communing. When this expression was upon him, he looked as if he were old; but when it was stirred and broken up--as it was now, in a moment, on his speaking to his daughter--he became a handsome man, not past the prime of life.\n\nHis daughter had one of her hands drawn through his arm, as she sat by him, and the other pressed upon it. She had drawn close to him, in her dread of the scene, and in her pity for the prisoner. Her forehead had been strikingly expressive of an engrossing terror and compassion that saw nothing but the peril of the accused. This had been so very noticeable, so very powerfully and naturally shown, that starers who had had no pity for him were touched by her; and the whisper went about, “Who are they?”\n\nJerry, the messenger, who had made his own observations, in his own manner, and who had been sucking the rust off his fingers in his absorption, stretched his neck to hear who they were. The crowd about him had pressed and passed the inquiry on to the nearest attendant, and from him it had been more slowly pressed and passed back; at last it got to Jerry:\n\n“Witnesses.”\n\n“For which side?”\n\n“Against.”\n\n“Against what side?”\n\n“The prisoner’s.”\n\nThe Judge, whose eyes had gone in the general direction, recalled them, leaned back in his seat, and looked steadily at the man whose life was in his hand, as Mr. Attorney-General rose to spin the rope, grind the axe, and hammer the nails into the scaffold.\n\n# CHAPTER III. A Disappointment\n\nMr. Attorney-General had to inform the jury, that the prisoner before them, though young in years, was old in the treasonable practices which claimed the forfeit of his life. That this correspondence with the public enemy was not a correspondence of to-day, or of yesterday, or even of last year, or of the year before. That, it was certain the prisoner had, for longer than that, been in the habit of passing and repassing between France and England, on secret business of which he could give no honest account. That, if it were in the nature of traitorous ways to thrive (which happily it never was), the real wickedness and guilt of his business might have remained undiscovered. That Providence, however, had put it into the heart of a person who was beyond fear and beyond reproach, to ferret out the nature of the prisoner’s schemes, and, struck with horror, to disclose them to his Majesty’s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council. That, this patriot would be produced before them. That, his position and attitude were, on the whole, sublime. That, he had been the prisoner’s friend, but, at once in an auspicious and an evil hour detecting his infamy, had resolved to immolate the traitor he could no longer cherish in his bosom, on the sacred altar of his country. That, if statues were decreed in Britain, as in ancient Greece and Rome, to public benefactors, this shining citizen would assuredly have had one. That, as they were not so decreed, he probably would not have one. That, Virtue, as had been observed by the poets (in many passages which he well knew the jury would have, word for word, at the tips of their tongues; whereat the jury’s countenances displayed a guilty consciousness that they knew nothing about the passages), was in a manner contagious; more especially the bright virtue known as patriotism, or love of country. That, the lofty example of this immaculate and unimpeachable witness for the Crown, to refer to whom however unworthily was an honour, had communicated itself to the prisoner’s servant, and had engendered in him a holy determination to examine his master’s table-drawers and pockets, and secrete his papers. That, he (Mr. Attorney-General) was prepared to hear some disparagement attempted of this admirable servant; but that, in a general way, he preferred him to his (Mr. Attorney-General’s) brothers and sisters, and honoured him more than his (Mr. Attorney-General’s) father and mother. That, he called with confidence on the jury to come and do likewise. That, the evidence of these two witnesses, coupled with the documents of their discovering that would be produced, would show the prisoner to have been furnished with lists of his Majesty’s forces, and of their disposition and preparation, both by sea and land, and would leave no doubt that he had habitually conveyed such information to a hostile power. That, these lists could not be proved to be in the prisoner’s handwriting; but that it was all the same; that, indeed, it was rather the better for the prosecution, as showing the prisoner to be artful in his precautions. That, the proof would go back five years, and would show the prisoner already engaged in these pernicious missions, within a few weeks before the date of the very first action fought between the British troops and the Americans. That, for these reasons, the jury, being a loyal jury (as he knew they were), and being a responsible jury (as they knew they were), must positively find the prisoner Guilty, and make an end of him, whether they liked it or not. That, they never could lay their heads upon their pillows; that, they never could tolerate the idea of their wives laying their heads upon their pillows; that, they never could endure the notion of their children laying their heads upon their pillows; in short, that there never more could be, for them or theirs, any laying of heads upon pillows at all, unless the prisoner’s head was taken off. That head Mr. Attorney-General concluded by demanding of them, in the name of everything he could think of with a round turn in it, and on the faith of his solemn asseveration that he already considered the prisoner as good as dead and gone.\n\nWhen the Attorney-General ceased, a buzz arose in the court as if a cloud of great blue-flies were swarming about the prisoner, in anticipation of what he was soon to become. When toned down again, the unimpeachable patriot appeared in the witness-box.\n\nMr. Solicitor-General then, following his leader’s lead, examined the patriot: John Barsad, gentleman, by name. The story of his pure soul was exactly what Mr. Attorney-General had described it to be--perhaps, if it had a fault, a little too exactly. Having released his noble bosom of its burden, he would have modestly withdrawn himself, but that the wigged gentleman with the papers before him, sitting not far from Mr. Lorry, begged to ask him a few questions. The wigged gentleman sitting opposite, still looking at the ceiling of the court.\n\nHad he ever been a spy himself? No, he scorned the base insinuation. What did he live upon? His property. Where was his property? He didn’t precisely remember where it was. What was it? No business of anybody’s. Had he inherited it? Yes, he had. From whom? Distant relation. Very distant? Rather. Ever been in prison? Certainly not. Never in a debtors’ prison? Didn’t see what that had to do with it. Never in a debtors’ prison?--Come, once again. Never? Yes. How many times? Two or three times. Not five or six? Perhaps. Of what profession? Gentleman. Ever been kicked? Might have been. Frequently? No. Ever kicked downstairs? Decidedly not; once received a kick on the top of a staircase, and fell downstairs of his own accord. Kicked on that occasion for cheating at dice? Something to that effect was said by the intoxicated liar who committed the assault, but it was not true. Swear it was not true? Positively. Ever live by cheating at play? Never. Ever live by play? Not more than other gentlemen do. Ever borrow money of the prisoner? Yes. Ever pay him? No. Was not this intimacy with the prisoner, in reality a very slight one, forced upon the prisoner in coaches, inns, and packets? No. Sure he saw the prisoner with these lists? Certain. Knew no more about the lists? No. Had not procured them himself, for instance? No. Expect to get anything by this evidence? No. Not in regular government pay and employment, to lay traps? Oh dear no. Or to do anything? Oh dear no. Swear that? Over and over again. No motives but motives of sheer patriotism? None whatever.\n\nThe virtuous servant, Roger Cly, swore his way through the case at a great rate. He had taken service with the prisoner, in good faith and simplicity, four years ago. He had asked the prisoner, aboard the Calais packet, if he wanted a handy fellow, and the prisoner had engaged him. He had not asked the prisoner to take the handy fellow as an act of charity--never thought of such a thing. He began to have suspicions of the prisoner, and to keep an eye upon him, soon afterwards. In arranging his clothes, while travelling, he had seen similar lists to these in the prisoner’s pockets, over and over again. He had taken these lists from the drawer of the prisoner’s desk. He had not put them there first. He had seen the prisoner show these identical lists to French gentlemen at Calais, and similar lists to French gentlemen, both at Calais and Boulogne. He loved his country, and couldn’t bear it, and had given information. He had never been suspected of stealing a silver tea-pot; he had been maligned respecting a mustard-pot, but it turned out to be only a plated one. He had known the last witness seven or eight years; that was merely a coincidence. He didn’t call it a particularly curious coincidence; most coincidences were curious. Neither did he call it a curious coincidence that true patriotism was his only motive too. He was a true Briton, and hoped there were many like him.\n\nThe blue-flies buzzed again, and Mr. Attorney-General called Mr. Jarvis Lorry.\n\n“Mr. Jarvis Lorry, are you a clerk in Tellson’s bank?”\n\n“I am.”\n“On a certain Friday night in November one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five, did business occasion you to travel between London and Dover by the mail?”\n\n“It did.”\n\n“Were there any other passengers in the mail?”\n“Two.”\n\n“Did they alight on the road in the course of the night?”\n\n“They did.”\n\n“Mr. Lorry, look upon the prisoner. Was he one of those two passengers?”\n\n“I cannot undertake to say that he was.”\n\n“Does he resemble either of these two passengers?”\n\n“Both were so wrapped up, and the night was so dark, and we were all so reserved, that I cannot undertake to say even that.”\n\n“Mr. Lorry, look again upon the prisoner. Supposing him wrapped up as those two passengers were, is there anything in his bulk and stature to render it unlikely that he was one of them?”\n\n“You will not swear, Mr. Lorry, that he was not one of them?”\n\n“No.”\n“So at least you say he may have been one of them?”\n\n“Yes. Except that I remember them both to have been--like myself--timorous of highwaymen, and the prisoner has not a timorous air.”\n\n“Did you ever see a counterfeit of timidity, Mr. Lorry?”\n\n“I certainly have seen that.”\n\n“Mr. Lorry, look once more upon the prisoner. Have you seen him, to your certain knowledge, before?”\n\n“I have.”\n“When?”\n\n“I was returning from France a few days afterwards, and, at Calais, the prisoner came on board the packet-ship in which I returned, and made the voyage with me.”\n\n“At what hour did he come on board?”\n\n“At a little after midnight.”\n\n“In the dead of the night. Was he the only passenger who came on board at that untimely hour?”\n\n“He happened to be the only one.”\n\n“Never mind about ‘happening,’ Mr. Lorry. He was the only passenger who came on board in the dead of the night?”\n\n“He was.”\n\n“Were you travelling alone, Mr. Lorry, or with any companion?”\n\n“With two companions. A gentleman and lady. They are here.”\n\n“They are here. Had you any conversation with the prisoner?”\n\n“Hardly any. The weather was stormy, and the passage long and rough, and I lay on a sofa, almost from shore to shore.”\n\n“Miss Manette!”\n\nThe young lady, to whom all eyes had been turned before, and were now turned again, stood up where she had sat. Her father rose with her, and kept her hand drawn through his arm.\n\n“Miss Manette, look upon the prisoner.”\n\nTo be confronted with such pity, and such earnest youth and beauty, was far more trying to the accused than to be confronted with all the crowd. Standing, as it were, apart with her on the edge of his grave, not all the staring curiosity that looked on, could, for the moment, nerve him to remain quite still. His hurried right hand parcelled out the herbs before him into imaginary beds of flowers in a garden; and his efforts to control and steady his breathing shook the lips from which the colour rushed to his heart. The buzz of the great flies was loud again.\n\n“Miss Manette, have you seen the prisoner before?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n“Where?”\n\n“On board of the packet-ship just now referred to, sir, and on the same occasion.”\n\n“You are the young lady just now referred to?”\n\n“O! most unhappily, I am!”\n\nThe plaintive tone of her compassion merged into the less musical voice of the Judge, as he said something fiercely: “Answer the questions put to you, and make no remark upon them.”\n\n“Miss Manette, had you any conversation with the prisoner on that passage across the Channel?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n“Recall it.”\n\nIn the midst of a profound stillness, she faintly began: “When the gentleman came on board--”\n\n“Do you mean the prisoner?” inquired the Judge, knitting his brows.\n\n“Yes, my Lord.”\n\n“Then say the prisoner.”\n\n“When the prisoner came on board, he noticed that my father,” turning her eyes lovingly to him as he stood beside her, “was much fatigued and in a very weak state of health. My father was so reduced that I was afraid to take him out of the air, and I had made a bed for him on the deck near the cabin steps, and I sat on the deck at his side to take care of him. There were no other passengers that night, but we four. The prisoner was so good as to beg permission to advise me how I could shelter my father from the wind and weather, better than I had done. I had not known how to do it well, not understanding how the wind would set when we were out of the harbour. He did it for me. He expressed great gentleness and kindness for my father’s state, and I am sure he felt it. That was the manner of our beginning to speak together.”\n\n“Let me interrupt you for a moment. Had he come on board alone?”\n\n“No.”\n“How many were with him?”\n\n“Two French gentlemen.”\n\n“Had they conferred together?”\n\n“They had conferred together until the last moment, when it was necessary for the French gentlemen to be landed in their boat.”\n\n“Had any papers been handed about among them, similar to these lists?”\n\n“Some papers had been handed about among them, but I don’t know what papers.”\n\n“Like these in shape and size?”\n\n“Possibly, but indeed I don’t know, although they stood whispering very near to me: because they stood at the top of the cabin steps to have the light of the lamp that was hanging there; it was a dull lamp, and they spoke very low, and I did not hear what they said, and saw only that they looked at papers.”\n\n“Now, to the prisoner’s conversation, Miss Manette.”\n\n“The prisoner was as open in his confidence with me--which arose out of my helpless situation--as he was kind, and good, and useful to my father. I hope,” bursting into tears, “I may not repay him by doing him harm to-day.”\n\nBuzzing from the blue-flies.\n\n“Miss Manette, if the prisoner does not perfectly understand that you give the evidence which it is your duty to give--which you must give--and which you cannot escape from giving--with great unwillingness, he is the only person present in that condition. Please to go on.”\n\n“He told me that he was travelling on business of a delicate and difficult nature, which might get people into trouble, and that he was therefore travelling under an assumed name. He said that this business had, within a few days, taken him to France, and might, at intervals, take him backwards and forwards between France and England for a long time to come.”\n\n“Did he say anything about America, Miss Manette? Be particular.”\n\n“He tried to explain to me how that quarrel had arisen, and he said that, so far as he could judge, it was a wrong and foolish one on England’s part. He added, in a jesting way, that perhaps George Washington might gain almost as great a name in history as George the Third. But there was no harm in his way of saying this: it was said laughingly, and to beguile the time.”\n\nAny strongly marked expression of face on the part of a chief actor in a scene of great interest to whom many eyes are directed, will be unconsciously imitated by the spectators. Her forehead was painfully anxious and intent as she gave this evidence, and, in the pauses when she stopped for the Judge to write it down, watched its effect upon the counsel for and against. Among the lookers-on there was the same expression in all quarters of the court; insomuch, that a great majority of the foreheads there, might have been mirrors reflecting the witness, when the Judge looked up from his notes to glare at that tremendous heresy about George Washington.\n\nMr. Attorney-General now signified to my Lord, that he deemed it necessary, as a matter of precaution and form, to call the young lady’s father, Doctor Manette. Who was called accordingly.\n\n“Doctor Manette, look upon the prisoner. Have you ever seen him before?”\n\n“Once. When he called at my lodgings in London. Some three years, or three years and a half ago.”\n\n“Can you identify him as your fellow-passenger on board the packet, or speak to his conversation with your daughter?”\n\n“Sir, I can do neither.”\n\n“Is there any particular and special reason for your being unable to do either?”\n\nHe answered, in a low voice, “There is.”\n\n“Has it been your misfortune to undergo a long imprisonment, without trial, or even accusation, in your native country, Doctor Manette?”\n\nHe answered, in a tone that went to every heart, “A long imprisonment.”\n\n“Were you newly released on the occasion in question?”\n\n“They tell me so.”\n\n“Have you no remembrance of the occasion?”\n\n“None. My mind is a blank, from some time--I cannot even say what time--when I employed myself, in my captivity, in making shoes, to the time when I found myself living in London with my dear daughter here. She had become familiar to me, when a gracious God restored my faculties; but, I am quite unable even to say how she had become familiar. I have no remembrance of the process.”\n\nMr. Attorney-General sat down, and the father and daughter sat down together.\n\nA singular circumstance then arose in the case. The object in hand being to show that the prisoner went down, with some fellow-plotter untracked, in the Dover mail on that Friday night in November five years ago, and got out of the mail in the night, as a blind, at a place where he did not remain, but from which he travelled back some dozen miles or more, to a garrison and dockyard, and there collected information; a witness was called to identify him as having been at the precise time required, in the coffee-room of an hotel in that garrison-and-dockyard town, waiting for another person. The prisoner’s counsel was cross-examining this witness with no result, except that he had never seen the prisoner on any other occasion, when the wigged gentleman who had all this time been looking at the ceiling of the court, wrote a word or two on a little piece of paper, screwed it up, and tossed it to him. Opening this piece of paper in the next pause, the counsel looked with great attention and curiosity at the prisoner.\n\n“You say again you are quite sure that it was the prisoner?”\n\nThe witness was quite sure.\n\n“Did you ever see anybody very like the prisoner?”\n\nNot so like (the witness said) as that he could be mistaken.\n\n“Look well upon that gentleman, my learned friend there,” pointing to him who had tossed the paper over, “and then look well upon the prisoner. How say you? Are they very like each other?”\n\nAllowing for my learned friend’s appearance being careless and slovenly if not debauched, they were sufficiently like each other to surprise, not only the witness, but everybody present, when they were thus brought into comparison. My Lord being prayed to bid my learned friend lay aside his wig, and giving no very gracious consent, the likeness became much more remarkable. My Lord inquired of Mr. Stryver (the prisoner’s counsel), whether they were next to try Mr. Carton (name of my learned friend) for treason? But, Mr. Stryver replied to my Lord, no; but he would ask the witness to tell him whether what happened once, might happen twice; whether he would have been so confident if he had seen this illustration of his rashness sooner, whether he would be so confident, having seen it; and more. The upshot of which, was, to smash this witness like a crockery vessel, and shiver his part of the case to useless lumber.\n\nMr. Cruncher had by this time taken quite a lunch of rust off his fingers in his following of the evidence. He had now to attend while Mr. Stryver fitted the prisoner’s case on the jury, like a compact suit of clothes; showing them how the patriot, Barsad, was a hired spy and traitor, an unblushing trafficker in blood, and one of the greatest scoundrels upon earth since accursed Judas--which he certainly did look rather like. How the virtuous servant, Cly, was his friend and partner, and was worthy to be; how the watchful eyes of those forgers and false swearers had rested on the prisoner as a victim, because some family affairs in France, he being of French extraction, did require his making those passages across the Channel--though what those affairs were, a consideration for others who were near and dear to him, forbade him, even for his life, to disclose. How the evidence that had been warped and wrested from the young lady, whose anguish in giving it they had witnessed, came to nothing, involving the mere little innocent gallantries and politenesses likely to pass between any young gentleman and young lady so thrown together;--with the exception of that reference to George Washington, which was altogether too extravagant and impossible to be regarded in any other light than as a monstrous joke. How it would be a weakness in the government to break down in this attempt to practise for popularity on the lowest national antipathies and fears, and therefore Mr. Attorney-General had made the most of it; how, nevertheless, it rested upon nothing, save that vile and infamous character of evidence too often disfiguring such cases, and of which the State Trials of this country were full. But, there my Lord interposed (with as grave a face as if it had not been true), saying that he could not sit upon that Bench and suffer those allusions.\n\nMr. Stryver then called his few witnesses, and Mr. Cruncher had next to attend while Mr. Attorney-General turned the whole suit of clothes Mr. Stryver had fitted on the jury, inside out; showing how Barsad and Cly were even a hundred times better than he had thought them, and the prisoner a hundred times worse. Lastly, came my Lord himself, turning the suit of clothes, now inside out, now outside in, but on the whole decidedly trimming and shaping them into grave-clothes for the prisoner.\n\nAnd now, the jury turned to consider, and the great flies swarmed again.\n\nMr. Carton, who had so long sat looking at the ceiling of the court, changed neither his place nor his attitude, even in this excitement. While his learned friend, Mr. Stryver, massing his papers before him, whispered with those who sat near, and from time to time glanced anxiously at the jury; while all the spectators moved more or less, and grouped themselves anew; while even my Lord himself arose from his seat, and slowly paced up and down his platform, not unattended by a suspicion in the minds of the audience that his state was feverish; this one man sat leaning back, with his torn gown half off him, his untidy wig put on just as it had happened to light on his head after its removal, his hands in his pockets, and his eyes on the ceiling as they had been all day. Something especially reckless in his demeanour, not only gave him a disreputable look, but so diminished the strong resemblance he undoubtedly bore to the prisoner (which his momentary earnestness, when they were compared together, had strengthened), that many of the lookers-on, taking note of him now, said to one another they would hardly have thought the two were so alike. Mr. Cruncher made the observation to his next neighbour, and added, “I’d hold half a guinea that he don’t get no law-work to do. Don’t look like the sort of one to get any, do he?”\n\nYet, this Mr. Carton took in more of the details of the scene than he appeared to take in; for now, when Miss Manette’s head dropped upon her father’s breast, he was the first to see it, and to say audibly: “Officer! look to that young lady. Help the gentleman to take her out. Don’t you see she will fall!”\n\nThere was much commiseration for her as she was removed, and much sympathy with her father. It had evidently been a great distress to him, to have the days of his imprisonment recalled. He had shown strong internal agitation when he was questioned, and that pondering or brooding look which made him old, had been upon him, like a heavy cloud, ever since. As he passed out, the jury, who had turned back and paused a moment, spoke, through their foreman.\n\nThey were not agreed, and wished to retire. My Lord (perhaps with George Washington on his mind) showed some surprise that they were not agreed, but signified his pleasure that they should retire under watch and ward, and retired himself. The trial had lasted all day, and the lamps in the court were now being lighted. It began to be rumoured that the jury would be out a long while. The spectators dropped off to get refreshment, and the prisoner withdrew to the back of the dock, and sat down.\n\nMr. Lorry, who had gone out when the young lady and her father went out, now reappeared, and beckoned to Jerry: who, in the slackened interest, could easily get near him.\n\n“Jerry, if you wish to take something to eat, you can. But, keep in the way. You will be sure to hear when the jury come in. Don’t be a moment behind them, for I want you to take the verdict back to the bank. You are the quickest messenger I know, and will get to Temple Bar long before I can.”\n\nJerry had just enough forehead to knuckle, and he knuckled it in acknowledgment of this communication and a shilling. Mr. Carton came up at the moment, and touched Mr. Lorry on the arm.\n\n“How is the young lady?”\n\n“She is greatly distressed; but her father is comforting her, and she feels the better for being out of court.”\n\n“I’ll tell the prisoner so. It won’t do for a respectable bank gentleman like you, to be seen speaking to him publicly, you know.”\n\nMr. Lorry reddened as if he were conscious of having debated the point in his mind, and Mr. Carton made his way to the outside of the bar. The way out of court lay in that direction, and Jerry followed him, all eyes, ears, and spikes.\n\n“Mr. Darnay!”\n\nThe prisoner came forward directly.\n\n“You will naturally be anxious to hear of the witness, Miss Manette. She will do very well. You have seen the worst of her agitation.”\n\n“I am deeply sorry to have been the cause of it. Could you tell her so for me, with my fervent acknowledgments?”\n\n“Yes, I could. I will, if you ask it.”\n\nMr. Carton’s manner was so careless as to be almost insolent. He stood, half turned from the prisoner, lounging with his elbow against the bar.\n\n“I do ask it. Accept my cordial thanks.”\n\n“What,” said Carton, still only half turned towards him, “do you expect, Mr. Darnay?”\n\n“The worst.”\n\n“It’s the wisest thing to expect, and the likeliest. But I think their withdrawing is in your favour.”\n\nLoitering on the way out of court not being allowed, Jerry heard no more: but left them--so like each other in feature, so unlike each other in manner--standing side by side, both reflected in the glass above them.\n\nAn hour and a half limped heavily away in the thief-and-rascal crowded passages below, even though assisted off with mutton pies and ale. The hoarse messenger, uncomfortably seated on a form after taking that refection, had dropped into a doze, when a loud murmur and a rapid tide of people setting up the stairs that led to the court, carried him along with them.\n\n“Jerry! Jerry!” Mr. Lorry was already calling at the door when he got there.\n\n“Here, sir! It’s a fight to get back again. Here I am, sir!”\n\nMr. Lorry handed him a paper through the throng. “Quick! Have you got it?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nHastily written on the paper was the word “ACQUITTED.”\n\n“If you had sent the message, ‘Recalled to Life,’ again,” muttered Jerry, as he turned, “I should have known what you meant, this time.”\n\nHe had no opportunity of saying, or so much as thinking, anything else, until he was clear of the Old Bailey; for, the crowd came pouring out with a vehemence that nearly took him off his legs, and a loud buzz swept into the street as if the baffled blue-flies were dispersing in search of other carrion.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. Congratulatory\n\nFrom the dimly-lighted passages of the court, the last sediment of the human stew that had been boiling there all day, was straining off, when Doctor Manette, Lucie Manette, his daughter, Mr. Lorry, the solicitor for the defence, and its counsel, Mr. Stryver, stood gathered round Mr. Charles Darnay--just released--congratulating him on his escape from death.\n\nIt would have been difficult by a far brighter light, to recognise in Doctor Manette, intellectual of face and upright of bearing, the shoemaker of the garret in Paris. Yet, no one could have looked at him twice, without looking again: even though the opportunity of observation had not extended to the mournful cadence of his low grave voice, and to the abstraction that overclouded him fitfully, without any apparent reason. While one external cause, and that a reference to his long lingering agony, would always--as on the trial--evoke this condition from the depths of his soul, it was also in its nature to arise of itself, and to draw a gloom over him, as incomprehensible to those unacquainted with his story as if they had seen the shadow of the actual Bastille thrown upon him by a summer sun, when the substance was three hundred miles away.\n\nOnly his daughter had the power of charming this black brooding from his mind. She was the golden thread that united him to a Past beyond his misery, and to a Present beyond his misery: and the sound of her voice, the light of her face, the touch of her hand, had a strong beneficial influence with him almost always. Not absolutely always, for she could recall some occasions on which her power had failed; but they were few and slight, and she believed them over.\n\nMr. Darnay had kissed her hand fervently and gratefully, and had turned to Mr. Stryver, whom he warmly thanked. Mr. Stryver, a man of little more than thirty, but looking twenty years older than he was, stout, loud, red, bluff, and free from any drawback of delicacy, had a pushing way of shouldering himself (morally and physically) into companies and conversations, that argued well for his shouldering his way up in life.\n\nHe still had his wig and gown on, and he said, squaring himself at his late client to that degree that he squeezed the innocent Mr. Lorry clean out of the group: “I am glad to have brought you off with honour, Mr. Darnay. It was an infamous prosecution, grossly infamous; but not the less likely to succeed on that account.”\n\n“You have laid me under an obligation to you for life--in two senses,” said his late client, taking his hand.\n\n“I have done my best for you, Mr. Darnay; and my best is as good as another man’s, I believe.”\n\nIt clearly being incumbent on some one to say, “Much better,” Mr. Lorry said it; perhaps not quite disinterestedly, but with the interested object of squeezing himself back again.\n\n“You think so?” said Mr. Stryver. “Well! you have been present all day, and you ought to know. You are a man of business, too.”\n\n“And as such,” quoth Mr. Lorry, whom the counsel learned in the law had now shouldered back into the group, just as he had previously shouldered him out of it--“as such I will appeal to Doctor Manette, to break up this conference and order us all to our homes. Miss Lucie looks ill, Mr. Darnay has had a terrible day, we are worn out.”\n\n“Speak for yourself, Mr. Lorry,” said Stryver; “I have a night’s work to do yet. Speak for yourself.”\n\n“I speak for myself,” answered Mr. Lorry, “and for Mr. Darnay, and for Miss Lucie, and--Miss Lucie, do you not think I may speak for us all?” He asked her the question pointedly, and with a glance at her father.\n\nHis face had become frozen, as it were, in a very curious look at Darnay: an intent look, deepening into a frown of dislike and distrust, not even unmixed with fear. With this strange expression on him his thoughts had wandered away.\n\n“My father,” said Lucie, softly laying her hand on his.\n\nHe slowly shook the shadow off, and turned to her.\n\n“Shall we go home, my father?”\n\nWith a long breath, he answered “Yes.”\n\nThe friends of the acquitted prisoner had dispersed, under the impression--which he himself had originated--that he would not be released that night. The lights were nearly all extinguished in the passages, the iron gates were being closed with a jar and a rattle, and the dismal place was deserted until to-morrow morning’s interest of gallows, pillory, whipping-post, and branding-iron, should repeople it. Walking between her father and Mr. Darnay, Lucie Manette passed into the open air. A hackney-coach was called, and the father and daughter departed in it.\n\nMr. Stryver had left them in the passages, to shoulder his way back to the robing-room. Another person, who had not joined the group, or interchanged a word with any one of them, but who had been leaning against the wall where its shadow was darkest, had silently strolled out after the rest, and had looked on until the coach drove away. He now stepped up to where Mr. Lorry and Mr. Darnay stood upon the pavement.\n\n“So, Mr. Lorry! Men of business may speak to Mr. Darnay now?”\n\nNobody had made any acknowledgment of Mr. Carton’s part in the day’s proceedings; nobody had known of it. He was unrobed, and was none the better for it in appearance.\n\n“If you knew what a conflict goes on in the business mind, when the business mind is divided between good-natured impulse and business appearances, you would be amused, Mr. Darnay.”\n\nMr. Lorry reddened, and said, warmly, “You have mentioned that before, sir. We men of business, who serve a House, are not our own masters. We have to think of the House more than ourselves.”\n\n“I know, I know,” rejoined Mr. Carton, carelessly. “Don’t be nettled, Mr. Lorry. You are as good as another, I have no doubt: better, I dare say.”\n\n“And indeed, sir,” pursued Mr. Lorry, not minding him, “I really don’t know what you have to do with the matter. If you’ll excuse me, as very much your elder, for saying so, I really don’t know that it is your business.”\n\n“Business! Bless you, I have no business,” said Mr. Carton.\n\n“It is a pity you have not, sir.”\n\n“I think so, too.”\n\n“If you had,” pursued Mr. Lorry, “perhaps you would attend to it.”\n\n“Lord love you, no!--I shouldn’t,” said Mr. Carton.\n\n“Well, sir!” cried Mr. Lorry, thoroughly heated by his indifference, “business is a very good thing, and a very respectable thing. And, sir, if business imposes its restraints and its silences and impediments, Mr. Darnay as a young gentleman of generosity knows how to make allowance for that circumstance. Mr. Darnay, good night, God bless you, sir! I hope you have been this day preserved for a prosperous and happy life.--Chair there!”\n\nPerhaps a little angry with himself, as well as with the barrister, Mr. Lorry bustled into the chair, and was carried off to Tellson’s. Carton, who smelt of port wine, and did not appear to be quite sober, laughed then, and turned to Darnay:\n\n“This is a strange chance that throws you and me together. This must be a strange night to you, standing alone here with your counterpart on these street stones?”\n\n“I hardly seem yet,” returned Charles Darnay, “to belong to this world again.”\n\n“I don’t wonder at it; it’s not so long since you were pretty far advanced on your way to another. You speak faintly.”\n\n“I begin to think I am faint.”\n\n“Then why the devil don’t you dine? I dined, myself, while those numskulls were deliberating which world you should belong to--this, or some other. Let me show you the nearest tavern to dine well at.”\n\nDrawing his arm through his own, he took him down Ludgate-hill to Fleet-street, and so, up a covered way, into a tavern. Here, they were shown into a little room, where Charles Darnay was soon recruiting his strength with a good plain dinner and good wine: while Carton sat opposite to him at the same table, with his separate bottle of port before him, and his fully half-insolent manner upon him.\n\n“Do you feel, yet, that you belong to this terrestrial scheme again, Mr. Darnay?”\n\n“I am frightfully confused regarding time and place; but I am so far mended as to feel that.”\n\n“It must be an immense satisfaction!”\n\nHe said it bitterly, and filled up his glass again: which was a large one.\n\n“As to me, the greatest desire I have, is to forget that I belong to it. It has no good in it for me--except wine like this--nor I for it. So we are not much alike in that particular. Indeed, I begin to think we are not much alike in any particular, you and I.”\n\nConfused by the emotion of the day, and feeling his being there with this Double of coarse deportment, to be like a dream, Charles Darnay was at a loss how to answer; finally, answered not at all.\n\n“Now your dinner is done,” Carton presently said, “why don’t you call a health, Mr. Darnay; why don’t you give your toast?”\n\n“What health? What toast?”\n\n“Why, it’s on the tip of your tongue. It ought to be, it must be, I’ll swear it’s there.”\n\n“Miss Manette, then!”\n\n“Miss Manette, then!”\n\nLooking his companion full in the face while he drank the toast, Carton flung his glass over his shoulder against the wall, where it shivered to pieces; then, rang the bell, and ordered in another.\n\n“That’s a fair young lady to hand to a coach in the dark, Mr. Darnay!” he said, filling his new goblet.\n\nA slight frown and a laconic “Yes,” were the answer.\n\n“That’s a fair young lady to be pitied by and wept for by! How does it feel? Is it worth being tried for one’s life, to be the object of such sympathy and compassion, Mr. Darnay?”\n\nAgain Darnay answered not a word.\n\n“She was mightily pleased to have your message, when I gave it her. Not that she showed she was pleased, but I suppose she was.”\n\nThe allusion served as a timely reminder to Darnay that this disagreeable companion had, of his own free will, assisted him in the strait of the day. He turned the dialogue to that point, and thanked him for it.\n\n“I neither want any thanks, nor merit any,” was the careless rejoinder. “It was nothing to do, in the first place; and I don’t know why I did it, in the second. Mr. Darnay, let me ask you a question.”\n\n“Willingly, and a small return for your good offices.”\n\n“Do you think I particularly like you?”\n\n“Really, Mr. Carton,” returned the other, oddly disconcerted, “I have not asked myself the question.”\n\n“But ask yourself the question now.”\n\n“You have acted as if you do; but I don’t think you do.”\n\n“I don’t think I do,” said Carton. “I begin to have a very good opinion of your understanding.”\n\n“Nevertheless,” pursued Darnay, rising to ring the bell, “there is nothing in that, I hope, to prevent my calling the reckoning, and our parting without ill-blood on either side.”\n\nCarton rejoining, “Nothing in life!” Darnay rang. “Do you call the whole reckoning?” said Carton. On his answering in the affirmative, “Then bring me another pint of this same wine, drawer, and come and wake me at ten.”\n\nThe bill being paid, Charles Darnay rose and wished him good night. Without returning the wish, Carton rose too, with something of a threat of defiance in his manner, and said, “A last word, Mr. Darnay: you think I am drunk?”\n\n“I think you have been drinking, Mr. Carton.”\n\n“Think? You know I have been drinking.”\n\n“Since I must say so, I know it.”\n\n“Then you shall likewise know why. I am a disappointed drudge, sir. I care for no man on earth, and no man on earth cares for me.”\n\n“Much to be regretted. You might have used your talents better.”\n\n“May be so, Mr. Darnay; may be not. Don’t let your sober face elate you, however; you don’t know what it may come to. Good night!”\n\nWhen he was left alone, this strange being took up a candle, went to a glass that hung against the wall, and surveyed himself minutely in it.\n\n“Do you particularly like the man?” he muttered, at his own image; “why should you particularly like a man who resembles you? There is nothing in you to like; you know that. Ah, confound you! What a change you have made in yourself! A good reason for taking to a man, that he shows you what you have fallen away from, and what you might have been! Change places with him, and would you have been looked at by those blue eyes as he was, and commiserated by that agitated face as he was? Come on, and have it out in plain words! You hate the fellow.”\n\nHe resorted to his pint of wine for consolation, drank it all in a few minutes, and fell asleep on his arms, with his hair straggling over the table, and a long winding-sheet in the candle dripping down upon him.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Jackal\n\nThose were drinking days, and most men drank hard. So very great is the improvement Time has brought about in such habits, that a moderate statement of the quantity of wine and punch which one man would swallow in the course of a night, without any detriment to his reputation as a perfect gentleman, would seem, in these days, a ridiculous exaggeration. The learned profession of the law was certainly not behind any other learned profession in its Bacchanalian propensities; neither was Mr. Stryver, already fast shouldering his way to a large and lucrative practice, behind his compeers in this particular, any more than in the drier parts of the legal race.\n\nA favourite at the Old Bailey, and eke at the Sessions, Mr. Stryver had begun cautiously to hew away the lower staves of the ladder on which he mounted. Sessions and Old Bailey had now to summon their favourite, specially, to their longing arms; and shouldering itself towards the visage of the Lord Chief Justice in the Court of King’s Bench, the florid countenance of Mr. Stryver might be daily seen, bursting out of the bed of wigs, like a great sunflower pushing its way at the sun from among a rank garden-full of flaring companions.\n\nIt had once been noted at the Bar, that while Mr. Stryver was a glib man, and an unscrupulous, and a ready, and a bold, he had not that faculty of extracting the essence from a heap of statements, which is among the most striking and necessary of the advocate’s accomplishments. But, a remarkable improvement came upon him as to this. The more business he got, the greater his power seemed to grow of getting at its pith and marrow; and however late at night he sat carousing with Sydney Carton, he always had his points at his fingers’ ends in the morning.\n\nSydney Carton, idlest and most unpromising of men, was Stryver’s great ally. What the two drank together, between Hilary Term and Michaelmas, might have floated a king’s ship. Stryver never had a case in hand, anywhere, but Carton was there, with his hands in his pockets, staring at the ceiling of the court; they went the same Circuit, and even there they prolonged their usual orgies late into the night, and Carton was rumoured to be seen at broad day, going home stealthily and unsteadily to his lodgings, like a dissipated cat. At last, it began to get about, among such as were interested in the matter, that although Sydney Carton would never be a lion, he was an amazingly good jackal, and that he rendered suit and service to Stryver in that humble capacity.\n\n“Ten o’clock, sir,” said the man at the tavern, whom he had charged to wake him--“ten o’clock, sir.”\n\n“What’s the matter?”\n\n“Ten o’clock, sir.”\n\n“What do you mean? Ten o’clock at night?”\n\n“Yes, sir. Your honour told me to call you.”\n\n“Oh! I remember. Very well, very well.”\n\nAfter a few dull efforts to get to sleep again, which the man dexterously combated by stirring the fire continuously for five minutes, he got up, tossed his hat on, and walked out. He turned into the Temple, and, having revived himself by twice pacing the pavements of King’s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, turned into the Stryver chambers.\n\nThe Stryver clerk, who never assisted at these conferences, had gone home, and the Stryver principal opened the door. He had his slippers on, and a loose bed-gown, and his throat was bare for his greater ease. He had that rather wild, strained, seared marking about the eyes, which may be observed in all free livers of his class, from the portrait of Jeffries downward, and which can be traced, under various disguises of Art, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.\n\n“You are a little late, Memory,” said Stryver.\n\n“About the usual time; it may be a quarter of an hour later.”\n\nThey went into a dingy room lined with books and littered with papers, where there was a blazing fire. A kettle steamed upon the hob, and in the midst of the wreck of papers a table shone, with plenty of wine upon it, and brandy, and rum, and sugar, and lemons.\n\n“You have had your bottle, I perceive, Sydney.”\n\n“Two to-night, I think. I have been dining with the day’s client; or seeing him dine--it’s all one!”\n\n“That was a rare point, Sydney, that you brought to bear upon the identification. How did you come by it? When did it strike you?”\n\n“I thought he was rather a handsome fellow, and I thought I should have been much the same sort of fellow, if I had had any luck.”\n\nMr. Stryver laughed till he shook his precocious paunch.\n\n“You and your luck, Sydney! Get to work, get to work.”\n\nSullenly enough, the jackal loosened his dress, went into an adjoining room, and came back with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a towel or two. Steeping the towels in the water, and partially wringing them out, he folded them on his head in a manner hideous to behold, sat down at the table, and said, “Now I am ready!”\n\n“Not much boiling down to be done to-night, Memory,” said Mr. Stryver, gaily, as he looked among his papers.\n\n“How much?”\n\n“Only two sets of them.”\n\n“Give me the worst first.”\n\n“There they are, Sydney. Fire away!”\n\nThe lion then composed himself on his back on a sofa on one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own paper-bestrewn table proper, on the other side of it, with the bottles and glasses ready to his hand. Both resorted to the drinking-table without stint, but each in a different way; the lion for the most part reclining with his hands in his waistband, looking at the fire, or occasionally flirting with some lighter document; the jackal, with knitted brows and intent face, so deep in his task, that his eyes did not even follow the hand he stretched out for his glass--which often groped about, for a minute or more, before it found the glass for his lips. Two or three times, the matter in hand became so knotty, that the jackal found it imperative on him to get up, and steep his towels anew. From these pilgrimages to the jug and basin, he returned with such eccentricities of damp headgear as no words can describe; which were made the more ludicrous by his anxious gravity.\n\nAt length the jackal had got together a compact repast for the lion, and proceeded to offer it to him. The lion took it with care and caution, made his selections from it, and his remarks upon it, and the jackal assisted both. When the repast was fully discussed, the lion put his hands in his waistband again, and lay down to meditate. The jackal then invigorated himself with a bumper for his throttle, and a fresh application to his head, and applied himself to the collection of a second meal; this was administered to the lion in the same manner, and was not disposed of until the clocks struck three in the morning.\n\n“And now we have done, Sydney, fill a bumper of punch,” said Mr. Stryver.\n\nThe jackal removed the towels from his head, which had been steaming again, shook himself, yawned, shivered, and complied.\n\n“You were very sound, Sydney, in the matter of those crown witnesses to-day. Every question told.”\n\n“I always am sound; am I not?”\n\n“I don’t gainsay it. What has roughened your temper? Put some punch to it and smooth it again.”\n\nWith a deprecatory grunt, the jackal again complied.\n\n“The old Sydney Carton of old Shrewsbury School,” said Stryver, nodding his head over him as he reviewed him in the present and the past, “the old seesaw Sydney. Up one minute and down the next; now in spirits and now in despondency!”\n\n“Ah!” returned the other, sighing: “yes! The same Sydney, with the same luck. Even then, I did exercises for other boys, and seldom did my own.”\n\n“And why not?”\n\n“God knows. It was my way, I suppose.”\n\nHe sat, with his hands in his pockets and his legs stretched out before him, looking at the fire.\n\n“Carton,” said his friend, squaring himself at him with a bullying air, as if the fire-grate had been the furnace in which sustained endeavour was forged, and the one delicate thing to be done for the old Sydney Carton of old Shrewsbury School was to shoulder him into it, “your way is, and always was, a lame way. You summon no energy and purpose. Look at me.”\n\n“Oh, botheration!” returned Sydney, with a lighter and more good-humoured laugh, “don’t you be moral!”\n\n“How have I done what I have done?” said Stryver; “how do I do what I do?”\n\n“Partly through paying me to help you, I suppose. But it’s not worth your while to apostrophise me, or the air, about it; what you want to do, you do. You were always in the front rank, and I was always behind.”\n\n“I had to get into the front rank; I was not born there, was I?”\n\n“I was not present at the ceremony; but my opinion is you were,” said Carton. At this, he laughed again, and they both laughed.\n\n“Before Shrewsbury, and at Shrewsbury, and ever since Shrewsbury,” pursued Carton, “you have fallen into your rank, and I have fallen into mine. Even when we were fellow-students in the Student-Quarter of Paris, picking up French, and French law, and other French crumbs that we didn’t get much good of, you were always somewhere, and I was always nowhere.”\n\n“And whose fault was that?”\n\n“Upon my soul, I am not sure that it was not yours. You were always driving and riving and shouldering and passing, to that restless degree that I had no chance for my life but in rust and repose. It’s a gloomy thing, however, to talk about one’s own past, with the day breaking. Turn me in some other direction before I go.”\n\n“Well then! Pledge me to the pretty witness,” said Stryver, holding up his glass. “Are you turned in a pleasant direction?”\n\nApparently not, for he became gloomy again.\n\n“Pretty witness,” he muttered, looking down into his glass. “I have had enough of witnesses to-day and to-night; who’s your pretty witness?”\n\n“The picturesque doctor’s daughter, Miss Manette.”\n\n“She pretty?”\n\n“Is she not?”\n“No.”\n\n“Why, man alive, she was the admiration of the whole Court!”\n\n“Rot the admiration of the whole Court! Who made the Old Bailey a judge of beauty? She was a golden-haired doll!”\n\n“Do you know, Sydney,” said Mr. Stryver, looking at him with sharp eyes, and slowly drawing a hand across his florid face: “do you know, I rather thought, at the time, that you sympathised with the golden-haired doll, and were quick to see what happened to the golden-haired doll?”\n\n“Quick to see what happened! If a girl, doll or no doll, swoons within a yard or two of a man’s nose, he can see it without a perspective-glass. I pledge you, but I deny the beauty. And now I’ll have no more drink; I’ll get to bed.”\n\nWhen his host followed him out on the staircase with a candle, to light him down the stairs, the day was coldly looking in through its grimy windows. When he got out of the house, the air was cold and sad, the dull sky overcast, the river dark and dim, the whole scene like a lifeless desert. And wreaths of dust were spinning round and round before the morning blast, as if the desert-sand had risen far away, and the first spray of it in its advance had begun to overwhelm the city.\n\nWaste forces within him, and a desert all around, this man stood still on his way across a silent terrace, and saw for a moment, lying in the wilderness before him, a mirage of honourable ambition, self-denial, and perseverance. In the fair city of this vision, there were airy galleries from which the loves and graces looked upon him, gardens in which the fruits of life hung ripening, waters of Hope that sparkled in his sight. A moment, and it was gone. Climbing to a high chamber in a well of houses, he threw himself down in his clothes on a neglected bed, and its pillow was wet with wasted tears.\n\nSadly, sadly, the sun rose; it rose upon no sadder sight than the man of good abilities and good emotions, incapable of their directed exercise, incapable of his own help and his own happiness, sensible of the blight on him, and resigning himself to let it eat him away.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. Hundreds of People\n\nThe quiet lodgings of Doctor Manette were in a quiet street-corner not far from Soho-square. On the afternoon of a certain fine Sunday when the waves of four months had rolled over the trial for treason, and carried it, as to the public interest and memory, far out to sea, Mr. Jarvis Lorry walked along the sunny streets from Clerkenwell where he lived, on his way to dine with the Doctor. After several relapses into business-absorption, Mr. Lorry had become the Doctor’s friend, and the quiet street-corner was the sunny part of his life.\n\nOn this certain fine Sunday, Mr. Lorry walked towards Soho, early in the afternoon, for three reasons of habit. Firstly, because, on fine Sundays, he often walked out, before dinner, with the Doctor and Lucie; secondly, because, on unfavourable Sundays, he was accustomed to be with them as the family friend, talking, reading, looking out of window, and generally getting through the day; thirdly, because he happened to have his own little shrewd doubts to solve, and knew how the ways of the Doctor’s household pointed to that time as a likely time for solving them.\n\nA quainter corner than the corner where the Doctor lived, was not to be found in London. There was no way through it, and the front windows of the Doctor’s lodgings commanded a pleasant little vista of street that had a congenial air of retirement on it. There were few buildings then, north of the Oxford-road, and forest-trees flourished, and wild flowers grew, and the hawthorn blossomed, in the now vanished fields. As a consequence, country airs circulated in Soho with vigorous freedom, instead of languishing into the parish like stray paupers without a settlement; and there was many a good south wall, not far off, on which the peaches ripened in their season.\n\nThe summer light struck into the corner brilliantly in the earlier part of the day; but, when the streets grew hot, the corner was in shadow, though not in shadow so remote but that you could see beyond it into a glare of brightness. It was a cool spot, staid but cheerful, a wonderful place for echoes, and a very harbour from the raging streets.\n\nThere ought to have been a tranquil bark in such an anchorage, and there was. The Doctor occupied two floors of a large stiff house, where several callings purported to be pursued by day, but whereof little was audible any day, and which was shunned by all of them at night. In a building at the back, attainable by a courtyard where a plane-tree rustled its green leaves, church-organs claimed to be made, and silver to be chased, and likewise gold to be beaten by some mysterious giant who had a golden arm starting out of the wall of the front hall--as if he had beaten himself precious, and menaced a similar conversion of all visitors. Very little of these trades, or of a lonely lodger rumoured to live up-stairs, or of a dim coach-trimming maker asserted to have a counting-house below, was ever heard or seen. Occasionally, a stray workman putting his coat on, traversed the hall, or a stranger peered about there, or a distant clink was heard across the courtyard, or a thump from the golden giant. These, however, were only the exceptions required to prove the rule that the sparrows in the plane-tree behind the house, and the echoes in the corner before it, had their own way from Sunday morning unto Saturday night.\n\nDoctor Manette received such patients here as his old reputation, and its revival in the floating whispers of his story, brought him. His scientific knowledge, and his vigilance and skill in conducting ingenious experiments, brought him otherwise into moderate request, and he earned as much as he wanted.\n\nThese things were within Mr. Jarvis Lorry’s knowledge, thoughts, and notice, when he rang the door-bell of the tranquil house in the corner, on the fine Sunday afternoon.\n\n“Doctor Manette at home?”\n\nExpected home.\n\n“Miss Lucie at home?”\n\nExpected home.\n\n“Miss Pross at home?”\n\nPossibly at home, but of a certainty impossible for handmaid to anticipate intentions of Miss Pross, as to admission or denial of the fact.\n\n“As I am at home myself,” said Mr. Lorry, “I’ll go upstairs.”\n\nAlthough the Doctor’s daughter had known nothing of the country of her birth, she appeared to have innately derived from it that ability to make much of little means, which is one of its most useful and most agreeable characteristics. Simple as the furniture was, it was set off by so many little adornments, of no value but for their taste and fancy, that its effect was delightful. The disposition of everything in the rooms, from the largest object to the least; the arrangement of colours, the elegant variety and contrast obtained by thrift in trifles, by delicate hands, clear eyes, and good sense; were at once so pleasant in themselves, and so expressive of their originator, that, as Mr. Lorry stood looking about him, the very chairs and tables seemed to ask him, with something of that peculiar expression which he knew so well by this time, whether he approved?\n\nThere were three rooms on a floor, and, the doors by which they communicated being put open that the air might pass freely through them all, Mr. Lorry, smilingly observant of that fanciful resemblance which he detected all around him, walked from one to another. The first was the best room, and in it were Lucie’s birds, and flowers, and books, and desk, and work-table, and box of water-colours; the second was the Doctor’s consulting-room, used also as the dining-room; the third, changingly speckled by the rustle of the plane-tree in the yard, was the Doctor’s bedroom, and there, in a corner, stood the disused shoemaker’s bench and tray of tools, much as it had stood on the fifth floor of the dismal house by the wine-shop, in the suburb of Saint Antoine in Paris.\n\n“I wonder,” said Mr. Lorry, pausing in his looking about, “that he keeps that reminder of his sufferings about him!”\n\n“And why wonder at that?” was the abrupt inquiry that made him start.\n\nIt proceeded from Miss Pross, the wild red woman, strong of hand, whose acquaintance he had first made at the Royal George Hotel at Dover, and had since improved.\n\n“I should have thought--” Mr. Lorry began.\n\n“Pooh! You’d have thought!” said Miss Pross; and Mr. Lorry left off.\n\n“How do you do?” inquired that lady then--sharply, and yet as if to express that she bore him no malice.\n\n“I am pretty well, I thank you,” answered Mr. Lorry, with meekness; “how are you?”\n\n“Nothing to boast of,” said Miss Pross.\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“Ah! indeed!” said Miss Pross. “I am very much put out about my Ladybird.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“For gracious sake say something else besides ‘indeed,’ or you’ll fidget me to death,” said Miss Pross: whose character (dissociated from stature) was shortness.\n\n“Really, then?” said Mr. Lorry, as an amendment.\n\n“Really, is bad enough,” returned Miss Pross, “but better. Yes, I am very much put out.”\n\n“May I ask the cause?”\n\n“I don’t want dozens of people who are not at all worthy of Ladybird, to come here looking after her,” said Miss Pross.\n\n“Do dozens come for that purpose?”\n\n“Hundreds,” said Miss Pross.\n\nIt was characteristic of this lady (as of some other people before her time and since) that whenever her original proposition was questioned, she exaggerated it.\n\n“Dear me!” said Mr. Lorry, as the safest remark he could think of.\n\n“I have lived with the darling--or the darling has lived with me, and paid me for it; which she certainly should never have done, you may take your affidavit, if I could have afforded to keep either myself or her for nothing--since she was ten years old. And it’s really very hard,” said Miss Pross.\n\nNot seeing with precision what was very hard, Mr. Lorry shook his head; using that important part of himself as a sort of fairy cloak that would fit anything.\n\n“All sorts of people who are not in the least degree worthy of the pet, are always turning up,” said Miss Pross. “When you began it--”\n\n“I began it, Miss Pross?”\n\n“Didn’t you? Who brought her father to life?”\n\n“Oh! If that was beginning it--” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“It wasn’t ending it, I suppose? I say, when you began it, it was hard enough; not that I have any fault to find with Doctor Manette, except that he is not worthy of such a daughter, which is no imputation on him, for it was not to be expected that anybody should be, under any circumstances. But it really is doubly and trebly hard to have crowds and multitudes of people turning up after him (I could have forgiven him), to take Ladybird’s affections away from me.”\n\nMr. Lorry knew Miss Pross to be very jealous, but he also knew her by this time to be, beneath the service of her eccentricity, one of those unselfish creatures--found only among women--who will, for pure love and admiration, bind themselves willing slaves, to youth when they have lost it, to beauty that they never had, to accomplishments that they were never fortunate enough to gain, to bright hopes that never shone upon their own sombre lives. He knew enough of the world to know that there is nothing in it better than the faithful service of the heart; so rendered and so free from any mercenary taint, he had such an exalted respect for it, that in the retributive arrangements made by his own mind--we all make such arrangements, more or less--he stationed Miss Pross much nearer to the lower Angels than many ladies immeasurably better got up both by Nature and Art, who had balances at Tellson’s.\n\n“There never was, nor will be, but one man worthy of Ladybird,” said Miss Pross; “and that was my brother Solomon, if he hadn’t made a mistake in life.”\n\nHere again: Mr. Lorry’s inquiries into Miss Pross’s personal history had established the fact that her brother Solomon was a heartless scoundrel who had stripped her of everything she possessed, as a stake to speculate with, and had abandoned her in her poverty for evermore, with no touch of compunction. Miss Pross’s fidelity of belief in Solomon (deducting a mere trifle for this slight mistake) was quite a serious matter with Mr. Lorry, and had its weight in his good opinion of her.\n\n“As we happen to be alone for the moment, and are both people of business,” he said, when they had got back to the drawing-room and had sat down there in friendly relations, “let me ask you--does the Doctor, in talking with Lucie, never refer to the shoemaking time, yet?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“And yet keeps that bench and those tools beside him?”\n\n“Ah!” returned Miss Pross, shaking her head. “But I don’t say he don’t refer to it within himself.”\n\n“Do you believe that he thinks of it much?”\n\n“I do,” said Miss Pross.\n\n“Do you imagine--” Mr. Lorry had begun, when Miss Pross took him up short with:\n\n“Never imagine anything. Have no imagination at all.”\n\n“I stand corrected; do you suppose--you go so far as to suppose, sometimes?”\n\n“Now and then,” said Miss Pross.\n\n“Do you suppose,” Mr. Lorry went on, with a laughing twinkle in his bright eye, as it looked kindly at her, “that Doctor Manette has any theory of his own, preserved through all those years, relative to the cause of his being so oppressed; perhaps, even to the name of his oppressor?”\n\n“I don’t suppose anything about it but what Ladybird tells me.”\n\n“And that is--?”\n\n“Now don’t be angry at my asking all these questions; because I am a mere dull man of business, and you are a woman of business.”\n\n“Dull?” Miss Pross inquired, with placidity.\n\nRather wishing his modest adjective away, Mr. Lorry replied, “No, no, no. Surely not. To return to business:--Is it not remarkable that Doctor Manette, unquestionably innocent of any crime as we are all well assured he is, should never touch upon that question? I will not say with me, though he had business relations with me many years ago, and we are now intimate; I will say with the fair daughter to whom he is so devotedly attached, and who is so devotedly attached to him? Believe me, Miss Pross, I don’t approach the topic with you, out of curiosity, but out of zealous interest.”\n\n“Well! To the best of my understanding, and bad’s the best, you’ll tell me,” said Miss Pross, softened by the tone of the apology, “he is afraid of the whole subject.”\n\n“Afraid?”\n“It’s plain enough, I should think, why he may be. It’s a dreadful remembrance. Besides that, his loss of himself grew out of it. Not knowing how he lost himself, or how he recovered himself, he may never feel certain of not losing himself again. That alone wouldn’t make the subject pleasant, I should think.”\n\nIt was a profounder remark than Mr. Lorry had looked for. “True,” said he, “and fearful to reflect upon. Yet, a doubt lurks in my mind, Miss Pross, whether it is good for Doctor Manette to have that suppression always shut up within him. Indeed, it is this doubt and the uneasiness it sometimes causes me that has led me to our present confidence.”\n\n“Can’t be helped,” said Miss Pross, shaking her head. “Touch that string, and he instantly changes for the worse. Better leave it alone. In short, must leave it alone, like or no like. Sometimes, he gets up in the dead of the night, and will be heard, by us overhead there, walking up and down, walking up and down, in his room. Ladybird has learnt to know then that his mind is walking up and down, walking up and down, in his old prison. She hurries to him, and they go on together, walking up and down, walking up and down, until he is composed. But he never says a word of the true reason of his restlessness, to her, and she finds it best not to hint at it to him. In silence they go walking up and down together, walking up and down together, till her love and company have brought him to himself.”\n\nNotwithstanding Miss Pross’s denial of her own imagination, there was a perception of the pain of being monotonously haunted by one sad idea, in her repetition of the phrase, walking up and down, which testified to her possessing such a thing.\n\nThe corner has been mentioned as a wonderful corner for echoes; it had begun to echo so resoundingly to the tread of coming feet, that it seemed as though the very mention of that weary pacing to and fro had set it going.\n\n“Here they are!” said Miss Pross, rising to break up the conference; “and now we shall have hundreds of people pretty soon!”\n\nIt was such a curious corner in its acoustical properties, such a peculiar Ear of a place, that as Mr. Lorry stood at the open window, looking for the father and daughter whose steps he heard, he fancied they would never approach. Not only would the echoes die away, as though the steps had gone; but, echoes of other steps that never came would be heard in their stead, and would die away for good when they seemed close at hand. However, father and daughter did at last appear, and Miss Pross was ready at the street door to receive them.\n\nMiss Pross was a pleasant sight, albeit wild, and red, and grim, taking off her darling’s bonnet when she came up-stairs, and touching it up with the ends of her handkerchief, and blowing the dust off it, and folding her mantle ready for laying by, and smoothing her rich hair with as much pride as she could possibly have taken in her own hair if she had been the vainest and handsomest of women. Her darling was a pleasant sight too, embracing her and thanking her, and protesting against her taking so much trouble for her--which last she only dared to do playfully, or Miss Pross, sorely hurt, would have retired to her own chamber and cried. The Doctor was a pleasant sight too, looking on at them, and telling Miss Pross how she spoilt Lucie, in accents and with eyes that had as much spoiling in them as Miss Pross had, and would have had more if it were possible. Mr. Lorry was a pleasant sight too, beaming at all this in his little wig, and thanking his bachelor stars for having lighted him in his declining years to a Home. But, no Hundreds of people came to see the sights, and Mr. Lorry looked in vain for the fulfilment of Miss Pross’s prediction.\n\nDinner-time, and still no Hundreds of people. In the arrangements of the little household, Miss Pross took charge of the lower regions, and always acquitted herself marvellously. Her dinners, of a very modest quality, were so well cooked and so well served, and so neat in their contrivances, half English and half French, that nothing could be better. Miss Pross’s friendship being of the thoroughly practical kind, she had ravaged Soho and the adjacent provinces, in search of impoverished French, who, tempted by shillings and half-crowns, would impart culinary mysteries to her. From these decayed sons and daughters of Gaul, she had acquired such wonderful arts, that the woman and girl who formed the staff of domestics regarded her as quite a Sorceress, or Cinderella’s Godmother: who would send out for a fowl, a rabbit, a vegetable or two from the garden, and change them into anything she pleased.\n\nOn Sundays, Miss Pross dined at the Doctor’s table, but on other days persisted in taking her meals at unknown periods, either in the lower regions, or in her own room on the second floor--a blue chamber, to which no one but her Ladybird ever gained admittance. On this occasion, Miss Pross, responding to Ladybird’s pleasant face and pleasant efforts to please her, unbent exceedingly; so the dinner was very pleasant, too.\n\nIt was an oppressive day, and, after dinner, Lucie proposed that the wine should be carried out under the plane-tree, and they should sit there in the air. As everything turned upon her, and revolved about her, they went out under the plane-tree, and she carried the wine down for the special benefit of Mr. Lorry. She had installed herself, some time before, as Mr. Lorry’s cup-bearer; and while they sat under the plane-tree, talking, she kept his glass replenished. Mysterious backs and ends of houses peeped at them as they talked, and the plane-tree whispered to them in its own way above their heads.\n\nStill, the Hundreds of people did not present themselves. Mr. Darnay presented himself while they were sitting under the plane-tree, but he was only One.\n\nDoctor Manette received him kindly, and so did Lucie. But, Miss Pross suddenly became afflicted with a twitching in the head and body, and retired into the house. She was not unfrequently the victim of this disorder, and she called it, in familiar conversation, “a fit of the jerks.”\n\nThe Doctor was in his best condition, and looked specially young. The resemblance between him and Lucie was very strong at such times, and as they sat side by side, she leaning on his shoulder, and he resting his arm on the back of her chair, it was very agreeable to trace the likeness.\n\nHe had been talking all day, on many subjects, and with unusual vivacity. “Pray, Doctor Manette,” said Mr. Darnay, as they sat under the plane-tree--and he said it in the natural pursuit of the topic in hand, which happened to be the old buildings of London--“have you seen much of the Tower?”\n\n“Lucie and I have been there; but only casually. We have seen enough of it, to know that it teems with interest; little more.”\n\n“I have been there, as you remember,” said Darnay, with a smile, though reddening a little angrily, “in another character, and not in a character that gives facilities for seeing much of it. They told me a curious thing when I was there.”\n\n“What was that?” Lucie asked.\n\n“In making some alterations, the workmen came upon an old dungeon, which had been, for many years, built up and forgotten. Every stone of its inner wall was covered by inscriptions which had been carved by prisoners--dates, names, complaints, and prayers. Upon a corner stone in an angle of the wall, one prisoner, who seemed to have gone to execution, had cut as his last work, three letters. They were done with some very poor instrument, and hurriedly, with an unsteady hand. At first, they were read as D. I. C.; but, on being more carefully examined, the last letter was found to be G. There was no record or legend of any prisoner with those initials, and many fruitless guesses were made what the name could have been. At length, it was suggested that the letters were not initials, but the complete word, DIG. The floor was examined very carefully under the inscription, and, in the earth beneath a stone, or tile, or some fragment of paving, were found the ashes of a paper, mingled with the ashes of a small leathern case or bag. What the unknown prisoner had written will never be read, but he had written something, and hidden it away to keep it from the gaoler.”\n\n“My father,” exclaimed Lucie, “you are ill!”\n\nHe had suddenly started up, with his hand to his head. His manner and his look quite terrified them all.\n\n“No, my dear, not ill. There are large drops of rain falling, and they made me start. We had better go in.”\n\nHe recovered himself almost instantly. Rain was really falling in large drops, and he showed the back of his hand with rain-drops on it. But, he said not a single word in reference to the discovery that had been told of, and, as they went into the house, the business eye of Mr. Lorry either detected, or fancied it detected, on his face, as it turned towards Charles Darnay, the same singular look that had been upon it when it turned towards him in the passages of the Court House.\n\nHe recovered himself so quickly, however, that Mr. Lorry had doubts of his business eye. The arm of the golden giant in the hall was not more steady than he was, when he stopped under it to remark to them that he was not yet proof against slight surprises (if he ever would be), and that the rain had startled him.\n\nTea-time, and Miss Pross making tea, with another fit of the jerks upon her, and yet no Hundreds of people. Mr. Carton had lounged in, but he made only Two.\n\nThe night was so very sultry, that although they sat with doors and windows open, they were overpowered by heat. When the tea-table was done with, they all moved to one of the windows, and looked out into the heavy twilight. Lucie sat by her father; Darnay sat beside her; Carton leaned against a window. The curtains were long and white, and some of the thunder-gusts that whirled into the corner, caught them up to the ceiling, and waved them like spectral wings.\n\n“The rain-drops are still falling, large, heavy, and few,” said Doctor Manette. “It comes slowly.”\n\n“It comes surely,” said Carton.\n\nThey spoke low, as people watching and waiting mostly do; as people in a dark room, watching and waiting for Lightning, always do.\n\nThere was a great hurry in the streets of people speeding away to get shelter before the storm broke; the wonderful corner for echoes resounded with the echoes of footsteps coming and going, yet not a footstep was there.\n\n“A multitude of people, and yet a solitude!” said Darnay, when they had listened for a while.\n\n“Is it not impressive, Mr. Darnay?” asked Lucie. “Sometimes, I have sat here of an evening, until I have fancied--but even the shade of a foolish fancy makes me shudder to-night, when all is so black and solemn--”\n\n“Let us shudder too. We may know what it is.”\n\n“It will seem nothing to you. Such whims are only impressive as we originate them, I think; they are not to be communicated. I have sometimes sat alone here of an evening, listening, until I have made the echoes out to be the echoes of all the footsteps that are coming by-and-bye into our lives.”\n\n“There is a great crowd coming one day into our lives, if that be so,” Sydney Carton struck in, in his moody way.\n\nThe footsteps were incessant, and the hurry of them became more and more rapid. The corner echoed and re-echoed with the tread of feet; some, as it seemed, under the windows; some, as it seemed, in the room; some coming, some going, some breaking off, some stopping altogether; all in the distant streets, and not one within sight.\n\n“Are all these footsteps destined to come to all of us, Miss Manette, or are we to divide them among us?”\n\n“I don’t know, Mr. Darnay; I told you it was a foolish fancy, but you asked for it. When I have yielded myself to it, I have been alone, and then I have imagined them the footsteps of the people who are to come into my life, and my father’s.”\n\n“I take them into mine!” said Carton. “I ask no questions and make no stipulations. There is a great crowd bearing down upon us, Miss Manette, and I see them--by the Lightning.” He added the last words, after there had been a vivid flash which had shown him lounging in the window.\n\n“And I hear them!” he added again, after a peal of thunder. “Here they come, fast, fierce, and furious!”\n\nIt was the rush and roar of rain that he typified, and it stopped him, for no voice could be heard in it. A memorable storm of thunder and lightning broke with that sweep of water, and there was not a moment’s interval in crash, and fire, and rain, until after the moon rose at midnight.\n\nThe great bell of Saint Paul’s was striking one in the cleared air, when Mr. Lorry, escorted by Jerry, high-booted and bearing a lantern, set forth on his return-passage to Clerkenwell. There were solitary patches of road on the way between Soho and Clerkenwell, and Mr. Lorry, mindful of foot-pads, always retained Jerry for this service: though it was usually performed a good two hours earlier.\n\n“What a night it has been! Almost a night, Jerry,” said Mr. Lorry, “to bring the dead out of their graves.”\n\n“I never see the night myself, master--nor yet I don’t expect to--what would do that,” answered Jerry.\n\n“Good night, Mr. Carton,” said the man of business. “Good night, Mr. Darnay. Shall we ever see such a night again, together!”\n\nPerhaps. Perhaps, see the great crowd of people with its rush and roar, bearing down upon them, too.\n\n# CHAPTER VII. Monseigneur in Town\n\nMonseigneur, one of the great lords in power at the Court, held his fortnightly reception in his grand hotel in Paris. Monseigneur was in his inner room, his sanctuary of sanctuaries, the Holiest of Holiests to the crowd of worshippers in the suite of rooms without. Monseigneur was about to take his chocolate. Monseigneur could swallow a great many things with ease, and was by some few sullen minds supposed to be rather rapidly swallowing France; but, his morning’s chocolate could not so much as get into the throat of Monseigneur, without the aid of four strong men besides the Cook.\n\nYes. It took four men, all four ablaze with gorgeous decoration, and the Chief of them unable to exist with fewer than two gold watches in his pocket, emulative of the noble and chaste fashion set by Monseigneur, to conduct the happy chocolate to Monseigneur’s lips. One lacquey carried the chocolate-pot into the sacred presence; a second, milled and frothed the chocolate with the little instrument he bore for that function; a third, presented the favoured napkin; a fourth (he of the two gold watches), poured the chocolate out. It was impossible for Monseigneur to dispense with one of these attendants on the chocolate and hold his high place under the admiring Heavens. Deep would have been the blot upon his escutcheon if his chocolate had been ignobly waited on by only three men; he must have died of two.\n\nMonseigneur had been out at a little supper last night, where the Comedy and the Grand Opera were charmingly represented. Monseigneur was out at a little supper most nights, with fascinating company. So polite and so impressible was Monseigneur, that the Comedy and the Grand Opera had far more influence with him in the tiresome articles of state affairs and state secrets, than the needs of all France. A happy circumstance for France, as the like always is for all countries similarly favoured!--always was for England (by way of example), in the regretted days of the merry Stuart who sold it.\n\nMonseigneur had one truly noble idea of general public business, which was, to let everything go on in its own way; of particular public business, Monseigneur had the other truly noble idea that it must all go his way--tend to his own power and pocket. Of his pleasures, general and particular, Monseigneur had the other truly noble idea, that the world was made for them. The text of his order (altered from the original by only a pronoun, which is not much) ran: “The earth and the fulness thereof are mine, saith Monseigneur.”\n\nYet, Monseigneur had slowly found that vulgar embarrassments crept into his affairs, both private and public; and he had, as to both classes of affairs, allied himself perforce with a Farmer-General. As to finances public, because Monseigneur could not make anything at all of them, and must consequently let them out to somebody who could; as to finances private, because Farmer-Generals were rich, and Monseigneur, after generations of great luxury and expense, was growing poor. Hence Monseigneur had taken his sister from a convent, while there was yet time to ward off the impending veil, the cheapest garment she could wear, and had bestowed her as a prize upon a very rich Farmer-General, poor in family. Which Farmer-General, carrying an appropriate cane with a golden apple on the top of it, was now among the company in the outer rooms, much prostrated before by mankind--always excepting superior mankind of the blood of Monseigneur, who, his own wife included, looked down upon him with the loftiest contempt.\n\nA sumptuous man was the Farmer-General. Thirty horses stood in his stables, twenty-four male domestics sat in his halls, six body-women waited on his wife. As one who pretended to do nothing but plunder and forage where he could, the Farmer-General--howsoever his matrimonial relations conduced to social morality--was at least the greatest reality among the personages who attended at the hotel of Monseigneur that day.\n\nFor, the rooms, though a beautiful scene to look at, and adorned with every device of decoration that the taste and skill of the time could achieve, were, in truth, not a sound business; considered with any reference to the scarecrows in the rags and nightcaps elsewhere (and not so far off, either, but that the watching towers of Notre Dame, almost equidistant from the two extremes, could see them both), they would have been an exceedingly uncomfortable business--if that could have been anybody’s business, at the house of Monseigneur. Military officers destitute of military knowledge; naval officers with no idea of a ship; civil officers without a notion of affairs; brazen ecclesiastics, of the worst world worldly, with sensual eyes, loose tongues, and looser lives; all totally unfit for their several callings, all lying horribly in pretending to belong to them, but all nearly or remotely of the order of Monseigneur, and therefore foisted on all public employments from which anything was to be got; these were to be told off by the score and the score. People not immediately connected with Monseigneur or the State, yet equally unconnected with anything that was real, or with lives passed in travelling by any straight road to any true earthly end, were no less abundant. Doctors who made great fortunes out of dainty remedies for imaginary disorders that never existed, smiled upon their courtly patients in the ante-chambers of Monseigneur. Projectors who had discovered every kind of remedy for the little evils with which the State was touched, except the remedy of setting to work in earnest to root out a single sin, poured their distracting babble into any ears they could lay hold of, at the reception of Monseigneur. Unbelieving Philosophers who were remodelling the world with words, and making card-towers of Babel to scale the skies with, talked with Unbelieving Chemists who had an eye on the transmutation of metals, at this wonderful gathering accumulated by Monseigneur. Exquisite gentlemen of the finest breeding, which was at that remarkable time--and has been since--to be known by its fruits of indifference to every natural subject of human interest, were in the most exemplary state of exhaustion, at the hotel of Monseigneur. Such homes had these various notabilities left behind them in the fine world of Paris, that the spies among the assembled devotees of Monseigneur--forming a goodly half of the polite company--would have found it hard to discover among the angels of that sphere one solitary wife, who, in her manners and appearance, owned to being a Mother. Indeed, except for the mere act of bringing a troublesome creature into this world--which does not go far towards the realisation of the name of mother--there was no such thing known to the fashion. Peasant women kept the unfashionable babies close, and brought them up, and charming grandmammas of sixty dressed and supped as at twenty.\n\nThe leprosy of unreality disfigured every human creature in attendance upon Monseigneur. In the outermost room were half a dozen exceptional people who had had, for a few years, some vague misgiving in them that things in general were going rather wrong. As a promising way of setting them right, half of the half-dozen had become members of a fantastic sect of Convulsionists, and were even then considering within themselves whether they should foam, rage, roar, and turn cataleptic on the spot--thereby setting up a highly intelligible finger-post to the Future, for Monseigneur’s guidance. Besides these Dervishes, were other three who had rushed into another sect, which mended matters with a jargon about “the Centre of Truth:” holding that Man had got out of the Centre of Truth--which did not need much demonstration--but had not got out of the Circumference, and that he was to be kept from flying out of the Circumference, and was even to be shoved back into the Centre, by fasting and seeing of spirits. Among these, accordingly, much discoursing with spirits went on--and it did a world of good which never became manifest.\n\nBut, the comfort was, that all the company at the grand hotel of Monseigneur were perfectly dressed. If the Day of Judgment had only been ascertained to be a dress day, everybody there would have been eternally correct. Such frizzling and powdering and sticking up of hair, such delicate complexions artificially preserved and mended, such gallant swords to look at, and such delicate honour to the sense of smell, would surely keep anything going, for ever and ever. The exquisite gentlemen of the finest breeding wore little pendent trinkets that chinked as they languidly moved; these golden fetters rang like precious little bells; and what with that ringing, and with the rustle of silk and brocade and fine linen, there was a flutter in the air that fanned Saint Antoine and his devouring hunger far away.\n\nDress was the one unfailing talisman and charm used for keeping all things in their places. Everybody was dressed for a Fancy Ball that was never to leave off. From the Palace of the Tuileries, through Monseigneur and the whole Court, through the Chambers, the Tribunals of Justice, and all society (except the scarecrows), the Fancy Ball descended to the Common Executioner: who, in pursuance of the charm, was required to officiate “frizzled, powdered, in a gold-laced coat, pumps, and white silk stockings.” At the gallows and the wheel--the axe was a rarity--Monsieur Paris, as it was the episcopal mode among his brother Professors of the provinces, Monsieur Orleans, and the rest, to call him, presided in this dainty dress. And who among the company at Monseigneur’s reception in that seventeen hundred and eightieth year of our Lord, could possibly doubt, that a system rooted in a frizzled hangman, powdered, gold-laced, pumped, and white-silk stockinged, would see the very stars out!\n\nMonseigneur having eased his four men of their burdens and taken his chocolate, caused the doors of the Holiest of Holiests to be thrown open, and issued forth. Then, what submission, what cringing and fawning, what servility, what abject humiliation! As to bowing down in body and spirit, nothing in that way was left for Heaven--which may have been one among other reasons why the worshippers of Monseigneur never troubled it.\n\nBestowing a word of promise here and a smile there, a whisper on one happy slave and a wave of the hand on another, Monseigneur affably passed through his rooms to the remote region of the Circumference of Truth. There, Monseigneur turned, and came back again, and so in due course of time got himself shut up in his sanctuary by the chocolate sprites, and was seen no more.\n\nThe show being over, the flutter in the air became quite a little storm, and the precious little bells went ringing downstairs. There was soon but one person left of all the crowd, and he, with his hat under his arm and his snuff-box in his hand, slowly passed among the mirrors on his way out.\n\n“I devote you,” said this person, stopping at the last door on his way, and turning in the direction of the sanctuary, “to the Devil!”\n\nWith that, he shook the snuff from his fingers as if he had shaken the dust from his feet, and quietly walked downstairs.\n\nHe was a man of about sixty, handsomely dressed, haughty in manner, and with a face like a fine mask. A face of a transparent paleness; every feature in it clearly defined; one set expression on it. The nose, beautifully formed otherwise, was very slightly pinched at the top of each nostril. In those two compressions, or dints, the only little change that the face ever showed, resided. They persisted in changing colour sometimes, and they would be occasionally dilated and contracted by something like a faint pulsation; then, they gave a look of treachery, and cruelty, to the whole countenance. Examined with attention, its capacity of helping such a look was to be found in the line of the mouth, and the lines of the orbits of the eyes, being much too horizontal and thin; still, in the effect of the face made, it was a handsome face, and a remarkable one.\n\nIts owner went downstairs into the courtyard, got into his carriage, and drove away. Not many people had talked with him at the reception; he had stood in a little space apart, and Monseigneur might have been warmer in his manner. It appeared, under the circumstances, rather agreeable to him to see the common people dispersed before his horses, and often barely escaping from being run down. His man drove as if he were charging an enemy, and the furious recklessness of the man brought no check into the face, or to the lips, of the master. The complaint had sometimes made itself audible, even in that deaf city and dumb age, that, in the narrow streets without footways, the fierce patrician custom of hard driving endangered and maimed the mere vulgar in a barbarous manner. But, few cared enough for that to think of it a second time, and, in this matter, as in all others, the common wretches were left to get out of their difficulties as they could.\n\nWith a wild rattle and clatter, and an inhuman abandonment of consideration not easy to be understood in these days, the carriage dashed through streets and swept round corners, with women screaming before it, and men clutching each other and clutching children out of its way. At last, swooping at a street corner by a fountain, one of its wheels came to a sickening little jolt, and there was a loud cry from a number of voices, and the horses reared and plunged.\n\nBut for the latter inconvenience, the carriage probably would not have stopped; carriages were often known to drive on, and leave their wounded behind, and why not? But the frightened valet had got down in a hurry, and there were twenty hands at the horses’ bridles.\n\n“What has gone wrong?” said Monsieur, calmly looking out.\n\nA tall man in a nightcap had caught up a bundle from among the feet of the horses, and had laid it on the basement of the fountain, and was down in the mud and wet, howling over it like a wild animal.\n\n“Pardon, Monsieur the Marquis!” said a ragged and submissive man, “it is a child.”\n\n“Why does he make that abominable noise? Is it his child?”\n\n“Excuse me, Monsieur the Marquis--it is a pity--yes.”\n\nThe fountain was a little removed; for the street opened, where it was, into a space some ten or twelve yards square. As the tall man suddenly got up from the ground, and came running at the carriage, Monsieur the Marquis clapped his hand for an instant on his sword-hilt.\n\n“Killed!” shrieked the man, in wild desperation, extending both arms at their length above his head, and staring at him. “Dead!”\n\nThe people closed round, and looked at Monsieur the Marquis. There was nothing revealed by the many eyes that looked at him but watchfulness and eagerness; there was no visible menacing or anger. Neither did the people say anything; after the first cry, they had been silent, and they remained so. The voice of the submissive man who had spoken, was flat and tame in its extreme submission. Monsieur the Marquis ran his eyes over them all, as if they had been mere rats come out of their holes.\n\nHe took out his purse.\n\n“It is extraordinary to me,” said he, “that you people cannot take care of yourselves and your children. One or the other of you is for ever in the way. How do I know what injury you have done my horses. See! Give him that.”\n\nHe threw out a gold coin for the valet to pick up, and all the heads craned forward that all the eyes might look down at it as it fell. The tall man called out again with a most unearthly cry, “Dead!”\n\nHe was arrested by the quick arrival of another man, for whom the rest made way. On seeing him, the miserable creature fell upon his shoulder, sobbing and crying, and pointing to the fountain, where some women were stooping over the motionless bundle, and moving gently about it. They were as silent, however, as the men.\n\n“I know all, I know all,” said the last comer. “Be a brave man, my Gaspard! It is better for the poor little plaything to die so, than to live. It has died in a moment without pain. Could it have lived an hour as happily?”\n\n“You are a philosopher, you there,” said the Marquis, smiling. “How do they call you?”\n\n“They call me Defarge.”\n\n“Of what trade?”\n\n“Monsieur the Marquis, vendor of wine.”\n\n“Pick up that, philosopher and vendor of wine,” said the Marquis, throwing him another gold coin, “and spend it as you will. The horses there; are they right?”\n\nWithout deigning to look at the assemblage a second time, Monsieur the Marquis leaned back in his seat, and was just being driven away with the air of a gentleman who had accidentally broke some common thing, and had paid for it, and could afford to pay for it; when his ease was suddenly disturbed by a coin flying into his carriage, and ringing on its floor.\n\n“Hold!” said Monsieur the Marquis. “Hold the horses! Who threw that?”\n\nHe looked to the spot where Defarge the vendor of wine had stood, a moment before; but the wretched father was grovelling on his face on the pavement in that spot, and the figure that stood beside him was the figure of a dark stout woman, knitting.\n\n“You dogs!” said the Marquis, but smoothly, and with an unchanged front, except as to the spots on his nose: “I would ride over any of you very willingly, and exterminate you from the earth. If I knew which rascal threw at the carriage, and if that brigand were sufficiently near it, he should be crushed under the wheels.”\n\nSo cowed was their condition, and so long and hard their experience of what such a man could do to them, within the law and beyond it, that not a voice, or a hand, or even an eye was raised. Among the men, not one. But the woman who stood knitting looked up steadily, and looked the Marquis in the face. It was not for his dignity to notice it; his contemptuous eyes passed over her, and over all the other rats; and he leaned back in his seat again, and gave the word “Go on!”\n\nHe was driven on, and other carriages came whirling by in quick succession; the Minister, the State-Projector, the Farmer-General, the Doctor, the Lawyer, the Ecclesiastic, the Grand Opera, the Comedy, the whole Fancy Ball in a bright continuous flow, came whirling by. The rats had crept out of their holes to look on, and they remained looking on for hours; soldiers and police often passing between them and the spectacle, and making a barrier behind which they slunk, and through which they peeped. The father had long ago taken up his bundle and bidden himself away with it, when the women who had tended the bundle while it lay on the base of the fountain, sat there watching the running of the water and the rolling of the Fancy Ball--when the one woman who had stood conspicuous, knitting, still knitted on with the steadfastness of Fate. The water of the fountain ran, the swift river ran, the day ran into evening, so much life in the city ran into death according to rule, time and tide waited for no man, the rats were sleeping close together in their dark holes again, the Fancy Ball was lighted up at supper, all things ran their course.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. Monseigneur in the Country\n\nA beautiful landscape, with the corn bright in it, but not abundant. Patches of poor rye where corn should have been, patches of poor peas and beans, patches of most coarse vegetable substitutes for wheat. On inanimate nature, as on the men and women who cultivated it, a prevalent tendency towards an appearance of vegetating unwillingly--a dejected disposition to give up, and wither away.\n\nMonsieur the Marquis in his travelling carriage (which might have been lighter), conducted by four post-horses and two postilions, fagged up a steep hill. A blush on the countenance of Monsieur the Marquis was no impeachment of his high breeding; it was not from within; it was occasioned by an external circumstance beyond his control--the setting sun.\n\nThe sunset struck so brilliantly into the travelling carriage when it gained the hill-top, that its occupant was steeped in crimson. “It will die out,” said Monsieur the Marquis, glancing at his hands, “directly.”\n\nIn effect, the sun was so low that it dipped at the moment. When the heavy drag had been adjusted to the wheel, and the carriage slid down hill, with a cinderous smell, in a cloud of dust, the red glow departed quickly; the sun and the Marquis going down together, there was no glow left when the drag was taken off.\n\nBut, there remained a broken country, bold and open, a little village at the bottom of the hill, a broad sweep and rise beyond it, a church-tower, a windmill, a forest for the chase, and a crag with a fortress on it used as a prison. Round upon all these darkening objects as the night drew on, the Marquis looked, with the air of one who was coming near home.\n\nThe village had its one poor street, with its poor brewery, poor tannery, poor tavern, poor stable-yard for relays of post-horses, poor fountain, all usual poor appointments. It had its poor people too. All its people were poor, and many of them were sitting at their doors, shredding spare onions and the like for supper, while many were at the fountain, washing leaves, and grasses, and any such small yieldings of the earth that could be eaten. Expressive signs of what made them poor, were not wanting; the tax for the state, the tax for the church, the tax for the lord, tax local and tax general, were to be paid here and to be paid there, according to solemn inscription in the little village, until the wonder was, that there was any village left unswallowed.\n\nFew children were to be seen, and no dogs. As to the men and women, their choice on earth was stated in the prospect--Life on the lowest terms that could sustain it, down in the little village under the mill; or captivity and Death in the dominant prison on the crag.\n\nHeralded by a courier in advance, and by the cracking of his postilions’ whips, which twined snake-like about their heads in the evening air, as if he came attended by the Furies, Monsieur the Marquis drew up in his travelling carriage at the posting-house gate. It was hard by the fountain, and the peasants suspended their operations to look at him. He looked at them, and saw in them, without knowing it, the slow sure filing down of misery-worn face and figure, that was to make the meagreness of Frenchmen an English superstition which should survive the truth through the best part of a hundred years.\n\nMonsieur the Marquis cast his eyes over the submissive faces that drooped before him, as the like of himself had drooped before Monseigneur of the Court--only the difference was, that these faces drooped merely to suffer and not to propitiate--when a grizzled mender of the roads joined the group.\n\n“Bring me hither that fellow!” said the Marquis to the courier.\n\nThe fellow was brought, cap in hand, and the other fellows closed round to look and listen, in the manner of the people at the Paris fountain.\n\n“I passed you on the road?”\n\n“Monseigneur, it is true. I had the honour of being passed on the road.”\n\n“Coming up the hill, and at the top of the hill, both?”\n\n“Monseigneur, it is true.”\n\n“What did you look at, so fixedly?”\n\n“Monseigneur, I looked at the man.”\n\nHe stooped a little, and with his tattered blue cap pointed under the carriage. All his fellows stooped to look under the carriage.\n\n“What man, pig? And why look there?”\n\n“Pardon, Monseigneur; he swung by the chain of the shoe--the drag.”\n\n“Who?” demanded the traveller.\n\n“Monseigneur, the man.”\n\n“May the Devil carry away these idiots! How do you call the man? You know all the men of this part of the country. Who was he?”\n\n“Your clemency, Monseigneur! He was not of this part of the country. Of all the days of my life, I never saw him.”\n\n“Swinging by the chain? To be suffocated?”\n\n“With your gracious permission, that was the wonder of it, Monseigneur. His head hanging over--like this!”\n\nHe turned himself sideways to the carriage, and leaned back, with his face thrown up to the sky, and his head hanging down; then recovered himself, fumbled with his cap, and made a bow.\n\n“What was he like?”\n\n“Monseigneur, he was whiter than the miller. All covered with dust, white as a spectre, tall as a spectre!”\n\nThe picture produced an immense sensation in the little crowd; but all eyes, without comparing notes with other eyes, looked at Monsieur the Marquis. Perhaps, to observe whether he had any spectre on his conscience.\n\n“Truly, you did well,” said the Marquis, felicitously sensible that such vermin were not to ruffle him, “to see a thief accompanying my carriage, and not open that great mouth of yours. Bah! Put him aside, Monsieur Gabelle!”\n\nMonsieur Gabelle was the Postmaster, and some other taxing functionary united; he had come out with great obsequiousness to assist at this examination, and had held the examined by the drapery of his arm in an official manner.\n\n“Bah! Go aside!” said Monsieur Gabelle.\n\n“Lay hands on this stranger if he seeks to lodge in your village to-night, and be sure that his business is honest, Gabelle.”\n\n“Monseigneur, I am flattered to devote myself to your orders.”\n\n“Did he run away, fellow?--where is that Accursed?”\n\nThe accursed was already under the carriage with some half-dozen particular friends, pointing out the chain with his blue cap. Some half-dozen other particular friends promptly hauled him out, and presented him breathless to Monsieur the Marquis.\n\n“Did the man run away, Dolt, when we stopped for the drag?”\n\n“Monseigneur, he precipitated himself over the hill-side, head first, as a person plunges into the river.”\n\n“See to it, Gabelle. Go on!”\n\nThe half-dozen who were peering at the chain were still among the wheels, like sheep; the wheels turned so suddenly that they were lucky to save their skins and bones; they had very little else to save, or they might not have been so fortunate.\n\nThe burst with which the carriage started out of the village and up the rise beyond, was soon checked by the steepness of the hill. Gradually, it subsided to a foot pace, swinging and lumbering upward among the many sweet scents of a summer night. The postilions, with a thousand gossamer gnats circling about them in lieu of the Furies, quietly mended the points to the lashes of their whips; the valet walked by the horses; the courier was audible, trotting on ahead into the dull distance.\n\nAt the steepest point of the hill there was a little burial-ground, with a Cross and a new large figure of Our Saviour on it; it was a poor figure in wood, done by some inexperienced rustic carver, but he had studied the figure from the life--his own life, maybe--for it was dreadfully spare and thin.\n\nTo this distressful emblem of a great distress that had long been growing worse, and was not at its worst, a woman was kneeling. She turned her head as the carriage came up to her, rose quickly, and presented herself at the carriage-door.\n\n“It is you, Monseigneur! Monseigneur, a petition.”\n\nWith an exclamation of impatience, but with his unchangeable face, Monseigneur looked out.\n\n“How, then! What is it? Always petitions!”\n\n“Monseigneur. For the love of the great God! My husband, the forester.”\n\n“What of your husband, the forester? Always the same with you people. He cannot pay something?”\n\n“He has paid all, Monseigneur. He is dead.”\n\n“Well! He is quiet. Can I restore him to you?”\n\n“Alas, no, Monseigneur! But he lies yonder, under a little heap of poor grass.”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Monseigneur, there are so many little heaps of poor grass?”\n\n“Again, well?”\n\nShe looked an old woman, but was young. Her manner was one of passionate grief; by turns she clasped her veinous and knotted hands together with wild energy, and laid one of them on the carriage-door--tenderly, caressingly, as if it had been a human breast, and could be expected to feel the appealing touch.\n\n“Monseigneur, hear me! Monseigneur, hear my petition! My husband died of want; so many die of want; so many more will die of want.”\n\n“Again, well? Can I feed them?”\n\n“Monseigneur, the good God knows; but I don’t ask it. My petition is, that a morsel of stone or wood, with my husband’s name, may be placed over him to show where he lies. Otherwise, the place will be quickly forgotten, it will never be found when I am dead of the same malady, I shall be laid under some other heap of poor grass. Monseigneur, they are so many, they increase so fast, there is so much want. Monseigneur! Monseigneur!”\n\nThe valet had put her away from the door, the carriage had broken into a brisk trot, the postilions had quickened the pace, she was left far behind, and Monseigneur, again escorted by the Furies, was rapidly diminishing the league or two of distance that remained between him and his chateau.\n\nThe sweet scents of the summer night rose all around him, and rose, as the rain falls, impartially, on the dusty, ragged, and toil-worn group at the fountain not far away; to whom the mender of roads, with the aid of the blue cap without which he was nothing, still enlarged upon his man like a spectre, as long as they could bear it. By degrees, as they could bear no more, they dropped off one by one, and lights twinkled in little casements; which lights, as the casements darkened, and more stars came out, seemed to have shot up into the sky instead of having been extinguished.\n\nThe shadow of a large high-roofed house, and of many over-hanging trees, was upon Monsieur the Marquis by that time; and the shadow was exchanged for the light of a flambeau, as his carriage stopped, and the great door of his chateau was opened to him.\n\n“Monsieur Charles, whom I expect; is he arrived from England?”\n\n“Monseigneur, not yet.”\n\n# CHAPTER IX. The Gorgon’s Head\n\nIt was a heavy mass of building, that chateau of Monsieur the Marquis, with a large stone courtyard before it, and two stone sweeps of staircase meeting in a stone terrace before the principal door. A stony business altogether, with heavy stone balustrades, and stone urns, and stone flowers, and stone faces of men, and stone heads of lions, in all directions. As if the Gorgon’s head had surveyed it, when it was finished, two centuries ago.\n\nUp the broad flight of shallow steps, Monsieur the Marquis, flambeau preceded, went from his carriage, sufficiently disturbing the darkness to elicit loud remonstrance from an owl in the roof of the great pile of stable building away among the trees. All else was so quiet, that the flambeau carried up the steps, and the other flambeau held at the great door, burnt as if they were in a close room of state, instead of being in the open night-air. Other sound than the owl’s voice there was none, save the falling of a fountain into its stone basin; for, it was one of those dark nights that hold their breath by the hour together, and then heave a long low sigh, and hold their breath again.\n\nThe great door clanged behind him, and Monsieur the Marquis crossed a hall grim with certain old boar-spears, swords, and knives of the chase; grimmer with certain heavy riding-rods and riding-whips, of which many a peasant, gone to his benefactor Death, had felt the weight when his lord was angry.\n\nAvoiding the larger rooms, which were dark and made fast for the night, Monsieur the Marquis, with his flambeau-bearer going on before, went up the staircase to a door in a corridor. This thrown open, admitted him to his own private apartment of three rooms: his bed-chamber and two others. High vaulted rooms with cool uncarpeted floors, great dogs upon the hearths for the burning of wood in winter time, and all luxuries befitting the state of a marquis in a luxurious age and country. The fashion of the last Louis but one, of the line that was never to break--the fourteenth Louis--was conspicuous in their rich furniture; but, it was diversified by many objects that were illustrations of old pages in the history of France.\n\nA supper-table was laid for two, in the third of the rooms; a round room, in one of the chateau’s four extinguisher-topped towers. A small lofty room, with its window wide open, and the wooden jalousie-blinds closed, so that the dark night only showed in slight horizontal lines of black, alternating with their broad lines of stone colour.\n\n“My nephew,” said the Marquis, glancing at the supper preparation; “they said he was not arrived.”\n\nNor was he; but, he had been expected with Monseigneur.\n\n“Ah! It is not probable he will arrive to-night; nevertheless, leave the table as it is. I shall be ready in a quarter of an hour.”\n\nIn a quarter of an hour Monseigneur was ready, and sat down alone to his sumptuous and choice supper. His chair was opposite to the window, and he had taken his soup, and was raising his glass of Bordeaux to his lips, when he put it down.\n\n“What is that?” he calmly asked, looking with attention at the horizontal lines of black and stone colour.\n\n“Monseigneur? That?”\n\n“Outside the blinds. Open the blinds.”\n\nIt was done.\n\n“Well?”\n\n“Monseigneur, it is nothing. The trees and the night are all that are here.”\n\nThe servant who spoke, had thrown the blinds wide, had looked out into the vacant darkness, and stood with that blank behind him, looking round for instructions.\n\n“Good,” said the imperturbable master. “Close them again.”\n\nThat was done too, and the Marquis went on with his supper. He was half way through it, when he again stopped with his glass in his hand, hearing the sound of wheels. It came on briskly, and came up to the front of the chateau.\n\n“Ask who is arrived.”\n\nIt was the nephew of Monseigneur. He had been some few leagues behind Monseigneur, early in the afternoon. He had diminished the distance rapidly, but not so rapidly as to come up with Monseigneur on the road. He had heard of Monseigneur, at the posting-houses, as being before him.\n\nHe was to be told (said Monseigneur) that supper awaited him then and there, and that he was prayed to come to it. In a little while he came. He had been known in England as Charles Darnay.\n\nMonseigneur received him in a courtly manner, but they did not shake hands.\n\n“You left Paris yesterday, sir?” he said to Monseigneur, as he took his seat at table.\n\n“Yesterday. And you?”\n\n“I come direct.”\n\n“From London?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You have been a long time coming,” said the Marquis, with a smile.\n\n“On the contrary; I come direct.”\n\n“Pardon me! I mean, not a long time on the journey; a long time intending the journey.”\n\n“I have been detained by”--the nephew stopped a moment in his answer--“various business.”\n\n“Without doubt,” said the polished uncle.\n\nSo long as a servant was present, no other words passed between them. When coffee had been served and they were alone together, the nephew, looking at the uncle and meeting the eyes of the face that was like a fine mask, opened a conversation.\n\n“I have come back, sir, as you anticipate, pursuing the object that took me away. It carried me into great and unexpected peril; but it is a sacred object, and if it had carried me to death I hope it would have sustained me.”\n\n“Not to death,” said the uncle; “it is not necessary to say, to death.”\n\n“I doubt, sir,” returned the nephew, “whether, if it had carried me to the utmost brink of death, you would have cared to stop me there.”\n\nThe deepened marks in the nose, and the lengthening of the fine straight lines in the cruel face, looked ominous as to that; the uncle made a graceful gesture of protest, which was so clearly a slight form of good breeding that it was not reassuring.\n\n“Indeed, sir,” pursued the nephew, “for anything I know, you may have expressly worked to give a more suspicious appearance to the suspicious circumstances that surrounded me.”\n\n“No, no, no,” said the uncle, pleasantly.\n\n“But, however that may be,” resumed the nephew, glancing at him with deep distrust, “I know that your diplomacy would stop me by any means, and would know no scruple as to means.”\n\n“My friend, I told you so,” said the uncle, with a fine pulsation in the two marks. “Do me the favour to recall that I told you so, long ago.”\n\n“I recall it.”\n\n“Thank you,” said the Marquis--very sweetly indeed.\n\nHis tone lingered in the air, almost like the tone of a musical instrument.\n\n“In effect, sir,” pursued the nephew, “I believe it to be at once your bad fortune, and my good fortune, that has kept me out of a prison in France here.”\n\n“I do not quite understand,” returned the uncle, sipping his coffee. “Dare I ask you to explain?”\n\n“I believe that if you were not in disgrace with the Court, and had not been overshadowed by that cloud for years past, a letter de cachet would have sent me to some fortress indefinitely.”\n\n“It is possible,” said the uncle, with great calmness. “For the honour of the family, I could even resolve to incommode you to that extent. Pray excuse me!”\n\n“I perceive that, happily for me, the Reception of the day before yesterday was, as usual, a cold one,” observed the nephew.\n\n“I would not say happily, my friend,” returned the uncle, with refined politeness; “I would not be sure of that. A good opportunity for consideration, surrounded by the advantages of solitude, might influence your destiny to far greater advantage than you influence it for yourself. But it is useless to discuss the question. I am, as you say, at a disadvantage. These little instruments of correction, these gentle aids to the power and honour of families, these slight favours that might so incommode you, are only to be obtained now by interest and importunity. They are sought by so many, and they are granted (comparatively) to so few! It used not to be so, but France in all such things is changed for the worse. Our not remote ancestors held the right of life and death over the surrounding vulgar. From this room, many such dogs have been taken out to be hanged; in the next room (my bedroom), one fellow, to our knowledge, was poniarded on the spot for professing some insolent delicacy respecting his daughter--his daughter? We have lost many privileges; a new philosophy has become the mode; and the assertion of our station, in these days, might (I do not go so far as to say would, but might) cause us real inconvenience. All very bad, very bad!”\n\nThe Marquis took a gentle little pinch of snuff, and shook his head; as elegantly despondent as he could becomingly be of a country still containing himself, that great means of regeneration.\n\n“We have so asserted our station, both in the old time and in the modern time also,” said the nephew, gloomily, “that I believe our name to be more detested than any name in France.”\n\n“Let us hope so,” said the uncle. “Detestation of the high is the involuntary homage of the low.”\n\n“There is not,” pursued the nephew, in his former tone, “a face I can look at, in all this country round about us, which looks at me with any deference on it but the dark deference of fear and slavery.”\n\n“A compliment,” said the Marquis, “to the grandeur of the family, merited by the manner in which the family has sustained its grandeur. Hah!” And he took another gentle little pinch of snuff, and lightly crossed his legs.\n\nBut, when his nephew, leaning an elbow on the table, covered his eyes thoughtfully and dejectedly with his hand, the fine mask looked at him sideways with a stronger concentration of keenness, closeness, and dislike, than was comportable with its wearer’s assumption of indifference.\n\n“Repression is the only lasting philosophy. The dark deference of fear and slavery, my friend,” observed the Marquis, “will keep the dogs obedient to the whip, as long as this roof,” looking up to it, “shuts out the sky.”\n\nThat might not be so long as the Marquis supposed. If a picture of the chateau as it was to be a very few years hence, and of fifty like it as they too were to be a very few years hence, could have been shown to him that night, he might have been at a loss to claim his own from the ghastly, fire-charred, plunder-wrecked rains. As for the roof he vaunted, he might have found that shutting out the sky in a new way--to wit, for ever, from the eyes of the bodies into which its lead was fired, out of the barrels of a hundred thousand muskets.\n\n“Meanwhile,” said the Marquis, “I will preserve the honour and repose of the family, if you will not. But you must be fatigued. Shall we terminate our conference for the night?”\n\n“A moment more.”\n\n“An hour, if you please.”\n\n“Sir,” said the nephew, “we have done wrong, and are reaping the fruits of wrong.”\n\n“We have done wrong?” repeated the Marquis, with an inquiring smile, and delicately pointing, first to his nephew, then to himself.\n\n“Our family; our honourable family, whose honour is of so much account to both of us, in such different ways. Even in my father’s time, we did a world of wrong, injuring every human creature who came between us and our pleasure, whatever it was. Why need I speak of my father’s time, when it is equally yours? Can I separate my father’s twin-brother, joint inheritor, and next successor, from himself?”\n\n“Death has done that!” said the Marquis.\n\n“And has left me,” answered the nephew, “bound to a system that is frightful to me, responsible for it, but powerless in it; seeking to execute the last request of my dear mother’s lips, and obey the last look of my dear mother’s eyes, which implored me to have mercy and to redress; and tortured by seeking assistance and power in vain.”\n\n“Seeking them from me, my nephew,” said the Marquis, touching him on the breast with his forefinger--they were now standing by the hearth--“you will for ever seek them in vain, be assured.”\n\nEvery fine straight line in the clear whiteness of his face, was cruelly, craftily, and closely compressed, while he stood looking quietly at his nephew, with his snuff-box in his hand. Once again he touched him on the breast, as though his finger were the fine point of a small sword, with which, in delicate finesse, he ran him through the body, and said,\n\n“My friend, I will die, perpetuating the system under which I have lived.”\n\nWhen he had said it, he took a culminating pinch of snuff, and put his box in his pocket.\n\n“Better to be a rational creature,” he added then, after ringing a small bell on the table, “and accept your natural destiny. But you are lost, Monsieur Charles, I see.”\n\n“This property and France are lost to me,” said the nephew, sadly; “I renounce them.”\n\n“Are they both yours to renounce? France may be, but is the property? It is scarcely worth mentioning; but, is it yet?”\n\n“I had no intention, in the words I used, to claim it yet. If it passed to me from you, to-morrow--”\n\n“Which I have the vanity to hope is not probable.”\n\n“--or twenty years hence--”\n\n“You do me too much honour,” said the Marquis; “still, I prefer that supposition.”\n\n“--I would abandon it, and live otherwise and elsewhere. It is little to relinquish. What is it but a wilderness of misery and ruin!”\n\n“Hah!” said the Marquis, glancing round the luxurious room.\n\n“To the eye it is fair enough, here; but seen in its integrity, under the sky, and by the daylight, it is a crumbling tower of waste, mismanagement, extortion, debt, mortgage, oppression, hunger, nakedness, and suffering.”\n\n“Hah!” said the Marquis again, in a well-satisfied manner.\n\n“If it ever becomes mine, it shall be put into some hands better qualified to free it slowly (if such a thing is possible) from the weight that drags it down, so that the miserable people who cannot leave it and who have been long wrung to the last point of endurance, may, in another generation, suffer less; but it is not for me. There is a curse on it, and on all this land.”\n\n“And you?” said the uncle. “Forgive my curiosity; do you, under your new philosophy, graciously intend to live?”\n\n“I must do, to live, what others of my countrymen, even with nobility at their backs, may have to do some day--work.”\n\n“In England, for example?”\n\n“Yes. The family honour, sir, is safe from me in this country. The family name can suffer from me in no other, for I bear it in no other.”\n\nThe ringing of the bell had caused the adjoining bed-chamber to be lighted. It now shone brightly, through the door of communication. The Marquis looked that way, and listened for the retreating step of his valet.\n\n“England is very attractive to you, seeing how indifferently you have prospered there,” he observed then, turning his calm face to his nephew with a smile.\n\n“I have already said, that for my prospering there, I am sensible I may be indebted to you, sir. For the rest, it is my Refuge.”\n\n“They say, those boastful English, that it is the Refuge of many. You know a compatriot who has found a Refuge there? A Doctor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“With a daughter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Yes,” said the Marquis. “You are fatigued. Good night!”\n\nAs he bent his head in his most courtly manner, there was a secrecy in his smiling face, and he conveyed an air of mystery to those words, which struck the eyes and ears of his nephew forcibly. At the same time, the thin straight lines of the setting of the eyes, and the thin straight lips, and the markings in the nose, curved with a sarcasm that looked handsomely diabolic.\n\n“Yes,” repeated the Marquis. “A Doctor with a daughter. Yes. So commences the new philosophy! You are fatigued. Good night!”\n\nIt would have been of as much avail to interrogate any stone face outside the chateau as to interrogate that face of his. The nephew looked at him, in vain, in passing on to the door.\n\n“Good night!” said the uncle. “I look to the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning. Good repose! Light Monsieur my nephew to his chamber there!--And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you will,” he added to himself, before he rang his little bell again, and summoned his valet to his own bedroom.\n\nThe valet come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis walked to and fro in his loose chamber-robe, to prepare himself gently for sleep, that hot still night. Rustling about the room, his softly-slippered feet making no noise on the floor, he moved like a refined tiger:--looked like some enchanted marquis of the impenitently wicked sort, in story, whose periodical change into tiger form was either just going off, or just coming on.\n\nHe moved from end to end of his voluptuous bedroom, looking again at the scraps of the day’s journey that came unbidden into his mind; the slow toil up the hill at sunset, the setting sun, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the mender of roads with his blue cap pointing out the chain under the carriage. That fountain suggested the Paris fountain, the little bundle lying on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms up, crying, “Dead!”\n\n“I am cool now,” said Monsieur the Marquis, “and may go to bed.”\n\nSo, leaving only one light burning on the large hearth, he let his thin gauze curtains fall around him, and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he composed himself to sleep.\n\nThe stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly at the black night for three heavy hours; for three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled at their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a noise with very little resemblance in it to the noise conventionally assigned to the owl by men-poets. But it is the obstinate custom of such creatures hardly ever to say what is set down for them.\n\nFor three heavy hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly at the night. Dead darkness lay on all the landscape, dead darkness added its own hush to the hushing dust on all the roads. The burial-place had got to the pass that its little heaps of poor grass were undistinguishable from one another; the figure on the Cross might have come down, for anything that could be seen of it. In the village, taxers and taxed were fast asleep. Dreaming, perhaps, of banquets, as the starved usually do, and of ease and rest, as the driven slave and the yoked ox may, its lean inhabitants slept soundly, and were fed and freed.\n\nThe fountain in the village flowed unseen and unheard, and the fountain at the chateau dropped unseen and unheard--both melting away, like the minutes that were falling from the spring of Time--through three dark hours. Then, the grey water of both began to be ghostly in the light, and the eyes of the stone faces of the chateau were opened.\n\nLighter and lighter, until at last the sun touched the tops of the still trees, and poured its radiance over the hill. In the glow, the water of the chateau fountain seemed to turn to blood, and the stone faces crimsoned. The carol of the birds was loud and high, and, on the weather-beaten sill of the great window of the bed-chamber of Monsieur the Marquis, one little bird sang its sweetest song with all its might. At this, the nearest stone face seemed to stare amazed, and, with open mouth and dropped under-jaw, looked awe-stricken.\n\nNow, the sun was full up, and movement began in the village. Casement windows opened, crazy doors were unbarred, and people came forth shivering--chilled, as yet, by the new sweet air. Then began the rarely lightened toil of the day among the village population. Some, to the fountain; some, to the fields; men and women here, to dig and delve; men and women there, to see to the poor live stock, and lead the bony cows out, to such pasture as could be found by the roadside. In the church and at the Cross, a kneeling figure or two; attendant on the latter prayers, the led cow, trying for a breakfast among the weeds at its foot.\n\nThe chateau awoke later, as became its quality, but awoke gradually and surely. First, the lonely boar-spears and knives of the chase had been reddened as of old; then, had gleamed trenchant in the morning sunshine; now, doors and windows were thrown open, horses in their stables looked round over their shoulders at the light and freshness pouring in at doorways, leaves sparkled and rustled at iron-grated windows, dogs pulled hard at their chains, and reared impatient to be loosed.\n\nAll these trivial incidents belonged to the routine of life, and the return of morning. Surely, not so the ringing of the great bell of the chateau, nor the running up and down the stairs; nor the hurried figures on the terrace; nor the booting and tramping here and there and everywhere, nor the quick saddling of horses and riding away?\n\nWhat winds conveyed this hurry to the grizzled mender of roads, already at work on the hill-top beyond the village, with his day’s dinner (not much to carry) lying in a bundle that it was worth no crow’s while to peck at, on a heap of stones? Had the birds, carrying some grains of it to a distance, dropped one over him as they sow chance seeds? Whether or no, the mender of roads ran, on the sultry morning, as if for his life, down the hill, knee-high in dust, and never stopped till he got to the fountain.\n\nAll the people of the village were at the fountain, standing about in their depressed manner, and whispering low, but showing no other emotions than grim curiosity and surprise. The led cows, hastily brought in and tethered to anything that would hold them, were looking stupidly on, or lying down chewing the cud of nothing particularly repaying their trouble, which they had picked up in their interrupted saunter. Some of the people of the chateau, and some of those of the posting-house, and all the taxing authorities, were armed more or less, and were crowded on the other side of the little street in a purposeless way, that was highly fraught with nothing. Already, the mender of roads had penetrated into the midst of a group of fifty particular friends, and was smiting himself in the breast with his blue cap. What did all this portend, and what portended the swift hoisting-up of Monsieur Gabelle behind a servant on horseback, and the conveying away of the said Gabelle (double-laden though the horse was), at a gallop, like a new version of the German ballad of Leonora?\n\nIt portended that there was one stone face too many, up at the chateau.\n\nThe Gorgon had surveyed the building again in the night, and had added the one stone face wanting; the stone face for which it had waited through about two hundred years.\n\nIt lay back on the pillow of Monsieur the Marquis. It was like a fine mask, suddenly startled, made angry, and petrified. Driven home into the heart of the stone figure attached to it, was a knife. Round its hilt was a frill of paper, on which was scrawled:\n\n“Drive him fast to his tomb. This, from Jacques.”\n\n# CHAPTER X. Two Promises\n\nMore months, to the number of twelve, had come and gone, and Mr. Charles Darnay was established in England as a higher teacher of the French language who was conversant with French literature. In this age, he would have been a Professor; in that age, he was a Tutor. He read with young men who could find any leisure and interest for the study of a living tongue spoken all over the world, and he cultivated a taste for its stores of knowledge and fancy. He could write of them, besides, in sound English, and render them into sound English. Such masters were not at that time easily found; Princes that had been, and Kings that were to be, were not yet of the Teacher class, and no ruined nobility had dropped out of Tellson’s ledgers, to turn cooks and carpenters. As a tutor, whose attainments made the student’s way unusually pleasant and profitable, and as an elegant translator who brought something to his work besides mere dictionary knowledge, young Mr. Darnay soon became known and encouraged. He was well acquainted, more-over, with the circumstances of his country, and those were of ever-growing interest. So, with great perseverance and untiring industry, he prospered.\n\nIn London, he had expected neither to walk on pavements of gold, nor to lie on beds of roses; if he had had any such exalted expectation, he would not have prospered. He had expected labour, and he found it, and did it and made the best of it. In this, his prosperity consisted.\n\nA certain portion of his time was passed at Cambridge, where he read with undergraduates as a sort of tolerated smuggler who drove a contraband trade in European languages, instead of conveying Greek and Latin through the Custom-house. The rest of his time he passed in London.\n\nNow, from the days when it was always summer in Eden, to these days when it is mostly winter in fallen latitudes, the world of a man has invariably gone one way--Charles Darnay’s way--the way of the love of a woman.\n\nHe had loved Lucie Manette from the hour of his danger. He had never heard a sound so sweet and dear as the sound of her compassionate voice; he had never seen a face so tenderly beautiful, as hers when it was confronted with his own on the edge of the grave that had been dug for him. But, he had not yet spoken to her on the subject; the assassination at the deserted chateau far away beyond the heaving water and the long, long, dusty roads--the solid stone chateau which had itself become the mere mist of a dream--had been done a year, and he had never yet, by so much as a single spoken word, disclosed to her the state of his heart.\n\nThat he had his reasons for this, he knew full well. It was again a summer day when, lately arrived in London from his college occupation, he turned into the quiet corner in Soho, bent on seeking an opportunity of opening his mind to Doctor Manette. It was the close of the summer day, and he knew Lucie to be out with Miss Pross.\n\nHe found the Doctor reading in his arm-chair at a window. The energy which had at once supported him under his old sufferings and aggravated their sharpness, had been gradually restored to him. He was now a very energetic man indeed, with great firmness of purpose, strength of resolution, and vigour of action. In his recovered energy he was sometimes a little fitful and sudden, as he had at first been in the exercise of his other recovered faculties; but, this had never been frequently observable, and had grown more and more rare.\n\nHe studied much, slept little, sustained a great deal of fatigue with ease, and was equably cheerful. To him, now entered Charles Darnay, at sight of whom he laid aside his book and held out his hand.\n\n“Charles Darnay! I rejoice to see you. We have been counting on your return these three or four days past. Mr. Stryver and Sydney Carton were both here yesterday, and both made you out to be more than due.”\n\n“I am obliged to them for their interest in the matter,” he answered, a little coldly as to them, though very warmly as to the Doctor. “Miss Manette--”\n\n“Is well,” said the Doctor, as he stopped short, “and your return will delight us all. She has gone out on some household matters, but will soon be home.”\n\n“Doctor Manette, I knew she was from home. I took the opportunity of her being from home, to beg to speak to you.”\n\nThere was a blank silence.\n\n“Yes?” said the Doctor, with evident constraint. “Bring your chair here, and speak on.”\n\nHe complied as to the chair, but appeared to find the speaking on less easy.\n\n“I have had the happiness, Doctor Manette, of being so intimate here,” so he at length began, “for some year and a half, that I hope the topic on which I am about to touch may not--”\n\nHe was stayed by the Doctor’s putting out his hand to stop him. When he had kept it so a little while, he said, drawing it back:\n\n“Is Lucie the topic?”\n\n“She is.”\n\n“It is hard for me to speak of her at any time. It is very hard for me to hear her spoken of in that tone of yours, Charles Darnay.”\n\n“It is a tone of fervent admiration, true homage, and deep love, Doctor Manette!” he said deferentially.\n\nThere was another blank silence before her father rejoined:\n\n“I believe it. I do you justice; I believe it.”\n\nHis constraint was so manifest, and it was so manifest, too, that it originated in an unwillingness to approach the subject, that Charles Darnay hesitated.\n\n“Shall I go on, sir?”\n\nAnother blank.\n\n“Yes, go on.”\n\n“You anticipate what I would say, though you cannot know how earnestly I say it, how earnestly I feel it, without knowing my secret heart, and the hopes and fears and anxieties with which it has long been laden. Dear Doctor Manette, I love your daughter fondly, dearly, disinterestedly, devotedly. If ever there were love in the world, I love her. You have loved yourself; let your old love speak for me!”\n\nThe Doctor sat with his face turned away, and his eyes bent on the ground. At the last words, he stretched out his hand again, hurriedly, and cried:\n\n“Not that, sir! Let that be! I adjure you, do not recall that!”\n\nHis cry was so like a cry of actual pain, that it rang in Charles Darnay’s ears long after he had ceased. He motioned with the hand he had extended, and it seemed to be an appeal to Darnay to pause. The latter so received it, and remained silent.\n\n“I ask your pardon,” said the Doctor, in a subdued tone, after some moments. “I do not doubt your loving Lucie; you may be satisfied of it.”\n\nHe turned towards him in his chair, but did not look at him, or raise his eyes. His chin dropped upon his hand, and his white hair overshadowed his face:\n\n“Have you spoken to Lucie?”\n\n“No.”\n“Nor written?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“It would be ungenerous to affect not to know that your self-denial is to be referred to your consideration for her father. Her father thanks you.”\n\nHe offered his hand; but his eyes did not go with it.\n\n“I know,” said Darnay, respectfully, “how can I fail to know, Doctor Manette, I who have seen you together from day to day, that between you and Miss Manette there is an affection so unusual, so touching, so belonging to the circumstances in which it has been nurtured, that it can have few parallels, even in the tenderness between a father and child. I know, Doctor Manette--how can I fail to know--that, mingled with the affection and duty of a daughter who has become a woman, there is, in her heart, towards you, all the love and reliance of infancy itself. I know that, as in her childhood she had no parent, so she is now devoted to you with all the constancy and fervour of her present years and character, united to the trustfulness and attachment of the early days in which you were lost to her. I know perfectly well that if you had been restored to her from the world beyond this life, you could hardly be invested, in her sight, with a more sacred character than that in which you are always with her. I know that when she is clinging to you, the hands of baby, girl, and woman, all in one, are round your neck. I know that in loving you she sees and loves her mother at her own age, sees and loves you at my age, loves her mother broken-hearted, loves you through your dreadful trial and in your blessed restoration. I have known this, night and day, since I have known you in your home.”\n\nHer father sat silent, with his face bent down. His breathing was a little quickened; but he repressed all other signs of agitation.\n\n“Dear Doctor Manette, always knowing this, always seeing her and you with this hallowed light about you, I have forborne, and forborne, as long as it was in the nature of man to do it. I have felt, and do even now feel, that to bring my love--even mine--between you, is to touch your history with something not quite so good as itself. But I love her. Heaven is my witness that I love her!”\n\n“I believe it,” answered her father, mournfully. “I have thought so before now. I believe it.”\n\n“But, do not believe,” said Darnay, upon whose ear the mournful voice struck with a reproachful sound, “that if my fortune were so cast as that, being one day so happy as to make her my wife, I must at any time put any separation between her and you, I could or would breathe a word of what I now say. Besides that I should know it to be hopeless, I should know it to be a baseness. If I had any such possibility, even at a remote distance of years, harboured in my thoughts, and hidden in my heart--if it ever had been there--if it ever could be there--I could not now touch this honoured hand.”\n\nHe laid his own upon it as he spoke.\n\n“No, dear Doctor Manette. Like you, a voluntary exile from France; like you, driven from it by its distractions, oppressions, and miseries; like you, striving to live away from it by my own exertions, and trusting in a happier future; I look only to sharing your fortunes, sharing your life and home, and being faithful to you to the death. Not to divide with Lucie her privilege as your child, companion, and friend; but to come in aid of it, and bind her closer to you, if such a thing can be.”\n\nHis touch still lingered on her father’s hand. Answering the touch for a moment, but not coldly, her father rested his hands upon the arms of his chair, and looked up for the first time since the beginning of the conference. A struggle was evidently in his face; a struggle with that occasional look which had a tendency in it to dark doubt and dread.\n\n“You speak so feelingly and so manfully, Charles Darnay, that I thank you with all my heart, and will open all my heart--or nearly so. Have you any reason to believe that Lucie loves you?”\n\n“None. As yet, none.”\n\n“Is it the immediate object of this confidence, that you may at once ascertain that, with my knowledge?”\n\n“Not even so. I might not have the hopefulness to do it for weeks; I might (mistaken or not mistaken) have that hopefulness to-morrow.”\n\n“Do you seek any guidance from me?”\n\n“I ask none, sir. But I have thought it possible that you might have it in your power, if you should deem it right, to give me some.”\n\n“Do you seek any promise from me?”\n\n“I do seek that.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“I well understand that, without you, I could have no hope. I well understand that, even if Miss Manette held me at this moment in her innocent heart--do not think I have the presumption to assume so much--I could retain no place in it against her love for her father.”\n\n“If that be so, do you see what, on the other hand, is involved in it?”\n\n“I understand equally well, that a word from her father in any suitor’s favour, would outweigh herself and all the world. For which reason, Doctor Manette,” said Darnay, modestly but firmly, “I would not ask that word, to save my life.”\n\n“I am sure of it. Charles Darnay, mysteries arise out of close love, as well as out of wide division; in the former case, they are subtle and delicate, and difficult to penetrate. My daughter Lucie is, in this one respect, such a mystery to me; I can make no guess at the state of her heart.”\n\n“May I ask, sir, if you think she is--” As he hesitated, her father supplied the rest.\n\n“Is sought by any other suitor?”\n\n“It is what I meant to say.”\n\nHer father considered a little before he answered:\n\n“You have seen Mr. Carton here, yourself. Mr. Stryver is here too, occasionally. If it be at all, it can only be by one of these.”\n\n“Or both,” said Darnay.\n\n“I had not thought of both; I should not think either, likely. You want a promise from me. Tell me what it is.”\n\n“It is, that if Miss Manette should bring to you at any time, on her own part, such a confidence as I have ventured to lay before you, you will bear testimony to what I have said, and to your belief in it. I hope you may be able to think so well of me, as to urge no influence against me. I say nothing more of my stake in this; this is what I ask. The condition on which I ask it, and which you have an undoubted right to require, I will observe immediately.”\n\n“I give the promise,” said the Doctor, “without any condition. I believe your object to be, purely and truthfully, as you have stated it. I believe your intention is to perpetuate, and not to weaken, the ties between me and my other and far dearer self. If she should ever tell me that you are essential to her perfect happiness, I will give her to you. If there were--Charles Darnay, if there were--”\n\nThe young man had taken his hand gratefully; their hands were joined as the Doctor spoke:\n\n“--any fancies, any reasons, any apprehensions, anything whatsoever, new or old, against the man she really loved--the direct responsibility thereof not lying on his head--they should all be obliterated for her sake. She is everything to me; more to me than suffering, more to me than wrong, more to me--Well! This is idle talk.”\n\nSo strange was the way in which he faded into silence, and so strange his fixed look when he had ceased to speak, that Darnay felt his own hand turn cold in the hand that slowly released and dropped it.\n\n“You said something to me,” said Doctor Manette, breaking into a smile. “What was it you said to me?”\n\nHe was at a loss how to answer, until he remembered having spoken of a condition. Relieved as his mind reverted to that, he answered:\n\n“Your confidence in me ought to be returned with full confidence on my part. My present name, though but slightly changed from my mother’s, is not, as you will remember, my own. I wish to tell you what that is, and why I am in England.”\n\n“Stop!” said the Doctor of Beauvais.\n\n“I wish it, that I may the better deserve your confidence, and have no secret from you.”\n\n“Stop!”\n\nFor an instant, the Doctor even had his two hands at his ears; for another instant, even had his two hands laid on Darnay’s lips.\n\n“Tell me when I ask you, not now. If your suit should prosper, if Lucie should love you, you shall tell me on your marriage morning. Do you promise?”\n\n“Willingly.\n\n“Give me your hand. She will be home directly, and it is better she should not see us together to-night. Go! God bless you!”\n\nIt was dark when Charles Darnay left him, and it was an hour later and darker when Lucie came home; she hurried into the room alone--for Miss Pross had gone straight up-stairs--and was surprised to find his reading-chair empty.\n\n“My father!” she called to him. “Father dear!”\n\nNothing was said in answer, but she heard a low hammering sound in his bedroom. Passing lightly across the intermediate room, she looked in at his door and came running back frightened, crying to herself, with her blood all chilled, “What shall I do! What shall I do!”\n\nHer uncertainty lasted but a moment; she hurried back, and tapped at his door, and softly called to him. The noise ceased at the sound of her voice, and he presently came out to her, and they walked up and down together for a long time.\n\nShe came down from her bed, to look at him in his sleep that night. He slept heavily, and his tray of shoemaking tools, and his old unfinished work, were all as usual.\n\n# CHAPTER XI. A Companion Picture\n\n“Sydney,” said Mr. Stryver, on that self-same night, or morning, to his jackal; “mix another bowl of punch; I have something to say to you.”\n\nSydney had been working double tides that night, and the night before, and the night before that, and a good many nights in succession, making a grand clearance among Mr. Stryver’s papers before the setting in of the long vacation. The clearance was effected at last; the Stryver arrears were handsomely fetched up; everything was got rid of until November should come with its fogs atmospheric, and fogs legal, and bring grist to the mill again.\n\nSydney was none the livelier and none the soberer for so much application. It had taken a deal of extra wet-towelling to pull him through the night; a correspondingly extra quantity of wine had preceded the towelling; and he was in a very damaged condition, as he now pulled his turban off and threw it into the basin in which he had steeped it at intervals for the last six hours.\n\n“Are you mixing that other bowl of punch?” said Stryver the portly, with his hands in his waistband, glancing round from the sofa where he lay on his back.\n\n“I am.”\n“Now, look here! I am going to tell you something that will rather surprise you, and that perhaps will make you think me not quite as shrewd as you usually do think me. I intend to marry.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“Yes. And not for money. What do you say now?”\n\n“I don’t feel disposed to say much. Who is she?”\n\n“Guess.”\n\n“Do I know her?”\n\n“Guess.”\n\n“I am not going to guess, at five o’clock in the morning, with my brains frying and sputtering in my head. If you want me to guess, you must ask me to dinner.”\n\n“Well then, I’ll tell you,” said Stryver, coming slowly into a sitting posture. “Sydney, I rather despair of making myself intelligible to you, because you are such an insensible dog.”\n\n“And you,” returned Sydney, busy concocting the punch, “are such a sensitive and poetical spirit--”\n\n“Come!” rejoined Stryver, laughing boastfully, “though I don’t prefer any claim to being the soul of Romance (for I hope I know better), still I am a tenderer sort of fellow than you.”\n\n“You are a luckier, if you mean that.”\n\n“I don’t mean that. I mean I am a man of more--more--”\n\n“Say gallantry, while you are about it,” suggested Carton.\n\n“Well! I’ll say gallantry. My meaning is that I am a man,” said Stryver, inflating himself at his friend as he made the punch, “who cares more to be agreeable, who takes more pains to be agreeable, who knows better how to be agreeable, in a woman’s society, than you do.”\n\n“Go on,” said Sydney Carton.\n\n“No; but before I go on,” said Stryver, shaking his head in his bullying way, “I’ll have this out with you. You’ve been at Doctor Manette’s house as much as I have, or more than I have. Why, I have been ashamed of your moroseness there! Your manners have been of that silent and sullen and hangdog kind, that, upon my life and soul, I have been ashamed of you, Sydney!”\n\n“It should be very beneficial to a man in your practice at the bar, to be ashamed of anything,” returned Sydney; “you ought to be much obliged to me.”\n\n“You shall not get off in that way,” rejoined Stryver, shouldering the rejoinder at him; “no, Sydney, it’s my duty to tell you--and I tell you to your face to do you good--that you are a devilish ill-conditioned fellow in that sort of society. You are a disagreeable fellow.”\n\nSydney drank a bumper of the punch he had made, and laughed.\n\n“Look at me!” said Stryver, squaring himself; “I have less need to make myself agreeable than you have, being more independent in circumstances. Why do I do it?”\n\n“I never saw you do it yet,” muttered Carton.\n\n“I do it because it’s politic; I do it on principle. And look at me! I get on.”\n\n“You don’t get on with your account of your matrimonial intentions,” answered Carton, with a careless air; “I wish you would keep to that. As to me--will you never understand that I am incorrigible?”\n\nHe asked the question with some appearance of scorn.\n\n“You have no business to be incorrigible,” was his friend’s answer, delivered in no very soothing tone.\n\n“I have no business to be, at all, that I know of,” said Sydney Carton. “Who is the lady?”\n\n“Now, don’t let my announcement of the name make you uncomfortable, Sydney,” said Mr. Stryver, preparing him with ostentatious friendliness for the disclosure he was about to make, “because I know you don’t mean half you say; and if you meant it all, it would be of no importance. I make this little preface, because you once mentioned the young lady to me in slighting terms.”\n\n“Certainly; and in these chambers.”\n\nSydney Carton looked at his punch and looked at his complacent friend; drank his punch and looked at his complacent friend.\n\n“You made mention of the young lady as a golden-haired doll. The young lady is Miss Manette. If you had been a fellow of any sensitiveness or delicacy of feeling in that kind of way, Sydney, I might have been a little resentful of your employing such a designation; but you are not. You want that sense altogether; therefore I am no more annoyed when I think of the expression, than I should be annoyed by a man’s opinion of a picture of mine, who had no eye for pictures: or of a piece of music of mine, who had no ear for music.”\n\nSydney Carton drank the punch at a great rate; drank it by bumpers, looking at his friend.\n\n“Now you know all about it, Syd,” said Mr. Stryver. “I don’t care about fortune: she is a charming creature, and I have made up my mind to please myself: on the whole, I think I can afford to please myself. She will have in me a man already pretty well off, and a rapidly rising man, and a man of some distinction: it is a piece of good fortune for her, but she is worthy of good fortune. Are you astonished?”\n\nCarton, still drinking the punch, rejoined, “Why should I be astonished?”\n\n“You approve?”\n\nCarton, still drinking the punch, rejoined, “Why should I not approve?”\n\n“Well!” said his friend Stryver, “you take it more easily than I fancied you would, and are less mercenary on my behalf than I thought you would be; though, to be sure, you know well enough by this time that your ancient chum is a man of a pretty strong will. Yes, Sydney, I have had enough of this style of life, with no other as a change from it; I feel that it is a pleasant thing for a man to have a home when he feels inclined to go to it (when he doesn’t, he can stay away), and I feel that Miss Manette will tell well in any station, and will always do me credit. So I have made up my mind. And now, Sydney, old boy, I want to say a word to you about your prospects. You are in a bad way, you know; you really are in a bad way. You don’t know the value of money, you live hard, you’ll knock up one of these days, and be ill and poor; you really ought to think about a nurse.”\n\nThe prosperous patronage with which he said it, made him look twice as big as he was, and four times as offensive.\n\n“Now, let me recommend you,” pursued Stryver, “to look it in the face. I have looked it in the face, in my different way; look it in the face, you, in your different way. Marry. Provide somebody to take care of you. Never mind your having no enjoyment of women’s society, nor understanding of it, nor tact for it. Find out somebody. Find out some respectable woman with a little property--somebody in the landlady way, or lodging-letting way--and marry her, against a rainy day. That’s the kind of thing for you. Now think of it, Sydney.”\n\n“I’ll think of it,” said Sydney.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. The Fellow of Delicacy\n\nMr. Stryver having made up his mind to that magnanimous bestowal of good fortune on the Doctor’s daughter, resolved to make her happiness known to her before he left town for the Long Vacation. After some mental debating of the point, he came to the conclusion that it would be as well to get all the preliminaries done with, and they could then arrange at their leisure whether he should give her his hand a week or two before Michaelmas Term, or in the little Christmas vacation between it and Hilary.\n\nAs to the strength of his case, he had not a doubt about it, but clearly saw his way to the verdict. Argued with the jury on substantial worldly grounds--the only grounds ever worth taking into account--it was a plain case, and had not a weak spot in it. He called himself for the plaintiff, there was no getting over his evidence, the counsel for the defendant threw up his brief, and the jury did not even turn to consider. After trying it, Stryver, C. J., was satisfied that no plainer case could be.\n\nAccordingly, Mr. Stryver inaugurated the Long Vacation with a formal proposal to take Miss Manette to Vauxhall Gardens; that failing, to Ranelagh; that unaccountably failing too, it behoved him to present himself in Soho, and there declare his noble mind.\n\nTowards Soho, therefore, Mr. Stryver shouldered his way from the Temple, while the bloom of the Long Vacation’s infancy was still upon it. Anybody who had seen him projecting himself into Soho while he was yet on Saint Dunstan’s side of Temple Bar, bursting in his full-blown way along the pavement, to the jostlement of all weaker people, might have seen how safe and strong he was.\n\nHis way taking him past Tellson’s, and he both banking at Tellson’s and knowing Mr. Lorry as the intimate friend of the Manettes, it entered Mr. Stryver’s mind to enter the bank, and reveal to Mr. Lorry the brightness of the Soho horizon. So, he pushed open the door with the weak rattle in its throat, stumbled down the two steps, got past the two ancient cashiers, and shouldered himself into the musty back closet where Mr. Lorry sat at great books ruled for figures, with perpendicular iron bars to his window as if that were ruled for figures too, and everything under the clouds were a sum.\n\n“Halloa!” said Mr. Stryver. “How do you do? I hope you are well!”\n\nIt was Stryver’s grand peculiarity that he always seemed too big for any place, or space. He was so much too big for Tellson’s, that old clerks in distant corners looked up with looks of remonstrance, as though he squeezed them against the wall. The House itself, magnificently reading the paper quite in the far-off perspective, lowered displeased, as if the Stryver head had been butted into its responsible waistcoat.\n\nThe discreet Mr. Lorry said, in a sample tone of the voice he would recommend under the circumstances, “How do you do, Mr. Stryver? How do you do, sir?” and shook hands. There was a peculiarity in his manner of shaking hands, always to be seen in any clerk at Tellson’s who shook hands with a customer when the House pervaded the air. He shook in a self-abnegating way, as one who shook for Tellson and Co.\n\n“Can I do anything for you, Mr. Stryver?” asked Mr. Lorry, in his business character.\n\n“Why, no, thank you; this is a private visit to yourself, Mr. Lorry; I have come for a private word.”\n\n“Oh indeed!” said Mr. Lorry, bending down his ear, while his eye strayed to the House afar off.\n\n“I am going,” said Mr. Stryver, leaning his arms confidentially on the desk: whereupon, although it was a large double one, there appeared to be not half desk enough for him: “I am going to make an offer of myself in marriage to your agreeable little friend, Miss Manette, Mr. Lorry.”\n\n“Oh dear me!” cried Mr. Lorry, rubbing his chin, and looking at his visitor dubiously.\n\n“Oh dear me, sir?” repeated Stryver, drawing back. “Oh dear you, sir? What may your meaning be, Mr. Lorry?”\n\n“My meaning,” answered the man of business, “is, of course, friendly and appreciative, and that it does you the greatest credit, and--in short, my meaning is everything you could desire. But--really, you know, Mr. Stryver--” Mr. Lorry paused, and shook his head at him in the oddest manner, as if he were compelled against his will to add, internally, “you know there really is so much too much of you!”\n\n“Well!” said Stryver, slapping the desk with his contentious hand, opening his eyes wider, and taking a long breath, “if I understand you, Mr. Lorry, I’ll be hanged!”\n\nMr. Lorry adjusted his little wig at both ears as a means towards that end, and bit the feather of a pen.\n\n“D--n it all, sir!” said Stryver, staring at him, “am I not eligible?”\n\n“Oh dear yes! Yes. Oh yes, you’re eligible!” said Mr. Lorry. “If you say eligible, you are eligible.”\n\n“Am I not prosperous?” asked Stryver.\n\n“Oh! if you come to prosperous, you are prosperous,” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“And advancing?”\n\n“If you come to advancing you know,” said Mr. Lorry, delighted to be able to make another admission, “nobody can doubt that.”\n\n“Then what on earth is your meaning, Mr. Lorry?” demanded Stryver, perceptibly crestfallen.\n\n“Well! I--Were you going there now?” asked Mr. Lorry.\n\n“Straight!” said Stryver, with a plump of his fist on the desk.\n\n“Then I think I wouldn’t, if I was you.”\n\n“Why?” said Stryver. “Now, I’ll put you in a corner,” forensically shaking a forefinger at him. “You are a man of business and bound to have a reason. State your reason. Why wouldn’t you go?”\n\n“Because,” said Mr. Lorry, “I wouldn’t go on such an object without having some cause to believe that I should succeed.”\n\n“D--n me!” cried Stryver, “but this beats everything.”\n\nMr. Lorry glanced at the distant House, and glanced at the angry Stryver.\n\n“Here’s a man of business--a man of years--a man of experience--in a Bank,” said Stryver; “and having summed up three leading reasons for complete success, he says there’s no reason at all! Says it with his head on!” Mr. Stryver remarked upon the peculiarity as if it would have been infinitely less remarkable if he had said it with his head off.\n\n“When I speak of success, I speak of success with the young lady; and when I speak of causes and reasons to make success probable, I speak of causes and reasons that will tell as such with the young lady. The young lady, my good sir,” said Mr. Lorry, mildly tapping the Stryver arm, “the young lady. The young lady goes before all.”\n\n“Then you mean to tell me, Mr. Lorry,” said Stryver, squaring his elbows, “that it is your deliberate opinion that the young lady at present in question is a mincing Fool?”\n\n“Not exactly so. I mean to tell you, Mr. Stryver,” said Mr. Lorry, reddening, “that I will hear no disrespectful word of that young lady from any lips; and that if I knew any man--which I hope I do not--whose taste was so coarse, and whose temper was so overbearing, that he could not restrain himself from speaking disrespectfully of that young lady at this desk, not even Tellson’s should prevent my giving him a piece of my mind.”\n\nThe necessity of being angry in a suppressed tone had put Mr. Stryver’s blood-vessels into a dangerous state when it was his turn to be angry; Mr. Lorry’s veins, methodical as their courses could usually be, were in no better state now it was his turn.\n\n“That is what I mean to tell you, sir,” said Mr. Lorry. “Pray let there be no mistake about it.”\n\nMr. Stryver sucked the end of a ruler for a little while, and then stood hitting a tune out of his teeth with it, which probably gave him the toothache. He broke the awkward silence by saying:\n\n“This is something new to me, Mr. Lorry. You deliberately advise me not to go up to Soho and offer myself--myself, Stryver of the King’s Bench bar?”\n\n“Do you ask me for my advice, Mr. Stryver?”\n\n“Yes, I do.”\n\n“Very good. Then I give it, and you have repeated it correctly.”\n\n“And all I can say of it is,” laughed Stryver with a vexed laugh, “that this--ha, ha!--beats everything past, present, and to come.”\n\n“Now understand me,” pursued Mr. Lorry. “As a man of business, I am not justified in saying anything about this matter, for, as a man of business, I know nothing of it. But, as an old fellow, who has carried Miss Manette in his arms, who is the trusted friend of Miss Manette and of her father too, and who has a great affection for them both, I have spoken. The confidence is not of my seeking, recollect. Now, you think I may not be right?”\n\n“Not I!” said Stryver, whistling. “I can’t undertake to find third parties in common sense; I can only find it for myself. I suppose sense in certain quarters; you suppose mincing bread-and-butter nonsense. It’s new to me, but you are right, I dare say.”\n\n“What I suppose, Mr. Stryver, I claim to characterise for myself--And understand me, sir,” said Mr. Lorry, quickly flushing again, “I will not--not even at Tellson’s--have it characterised for me by any gentleman breathing.”\n\n“There! I beg your pardon!” said Stryver.\n\n“Granted. Thank you. Well, Mr. Stryver, I was about to say:--it might be painful to you to find yourself mistaken, it might be painful to Doctor Manette to have the task of being explicit with you, it might be very painful to Miss Manette to have the task of being explicit with you. You know the terms upon which I have the honour and happiness to stand with the family. If you please, committing you in no way, representing you in no way, I will undertake to correct my advice by the exercise of a little new observation and judgment expressly brought to bear upon it. If you should then be dissatisfied with it, you can but test its soundness for yourself; if, on the other hand, you should be satisfied with it, and it should be what it now is, it may spare all sides what is best spared. What do you say?”\n\n“How long would you keep me in town?”\n\n“Oh! It is only a question of a few hours. I could go to Soho in the evening, and come to your chambers afterwards.”\n\n“Then I say yes,” said Stryver: “I won’t go up there now, I am not so hot upon it as that comes to; I say yes, and I shall expect you to look in to-night. Good morning.”\n\nThen Mr. Stryver turned and burst out of the Bank, causing such a concussion of air on his passage through, that to stand up against it bowing behind the two counters, required the utmost remaining strength of the two ancient clerks. Those venerable and feeble persons were always seen by the public in the act of bowing, and were popularly believed, when they had bowed a customer out, still to keep on bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in.\n\nThe barrister was keen enough to divine that the banker would not have gone so far in his expression of opinion on any less solid ground than moral certainty. Unprepared as he was for the large pill he had to swallow, he got it down. “And now,” said Mr. Stryver, shaking his forensic forefinger at the Temple in general, when it was down, “my way out of this, is, to put you all in the wrong.”\n\nIt was a bit of the art of an Old Bailey tactician, in which he found great relief. “You shall not put me in the wrong, young lady,” said Mr. Stryver; “I’ll do that for you.”\n\nAccordingly, when Mr. Lorry called that night as late as ten o’clock, Mr. Stryver, among a quantity of books and papers littered out for the purpose, seemed to have nothing less on his mind than the subject of the morning. He even showed surprise when he saw Mr. Lorry, and was altogether in an absent and preoccupied state.\n\n“Well!” said that good-natured emissary, after a full half-hour of bootless attempts to bring him round to the question. “I have been to Soho.”\n\n“To Soho?” repeated Mr. Stryver, coldly. “Oh, to be sure! What am I thinking of!”\n\n“And I have no doubt,” said Mr. Lorry, “that I was right in the conversation we had. My opinion is confirmed, and I reiterate my advice.”\n\n“I assure you,” returned Mr. Stryver, in the friendliest way, “that I am sorry for it on your account, and sorry for it on the poor father’s account. I know this must always be a sore subject with the family; let us say no more about it.”\n\n“I don’t understand you,” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“I dare say not,” rejoined Stryver, nodding his head in a smoothing and final way; “no matter, no matter.”\n\n“But it does matter,” Mr. Lorry urged.\n\n“No it doesn’t; I assure you it doesn’t. Having supposed that there was sense where there is no sense, and a laudable ambition where there is not a laudable ambition, I am well out of my mistake, and no harm is done. Young women have committed similar follies often before, and have repented them in poverty and obscurity often before. In an unselfish aspect, I am sorry that the thing is dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view; in a selfish aspect, I am glad that the thing has dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view--it is hardly necessary to say I could have gained nothing by it. There is no harm at all done. I have not proposed to the young lady, and, between ourselves, I am by no means certain, on reflection, that I ever should have committed myself to that extent. Mr. Lorry, you cannot control the mincing vanities and giddinesses of empty-headed girls; you must not expect to do it, or you will always be disappointed. Now, pray say no more about it. I tell you, I regret it on account of others, but I am satisfied on my own account. And I am really very much obliged to you for allowing me to sound you, and for giving me your advice; you know the young lady better than I do; you were right, it never would have done.”\n\nMr. Lorry was so taken aback, that he looked quite stupidly at Mr. Stryver shouldering him towards the door, with an appearance of showering generosity, forbearance, and goodwill, on his erring head. “Make the best of it, my dear sir,” said Stryver; “say no more about it; thank you again for allowing me to sound you; good night!”\n\nMr. Lorry was out in the night, before he knew where he was. Mr. Stryver was lying back on his sofa, winking at his ceiling.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. The Fellow of No Delicacy\n\nIf Sydney Carton ever shone anywhere, he certainly never shone in the house of Doctor Manette. He had been there often, during a whole year, and had always been the same moody and morose lounger there. When he cared to talk, he talked well; but, the cloud of caring for nothing, which overshadowed him with such a fatal darkness, was very rarely pierced by the light within him.\n\nAnd yet he did care something for the streets that environed that house, and for the senseless stones that made their pavements. Many a night he vaguely and unhappily wandered there, when wine had brought no transitory gladness to him; many a dreary daybreak revealed his solitary figure lingering there, and still lingering there when the first beams of the sun brought into strong relief, removed beauties of architecture in spires of churches and lofty buildings, as perhaps the quiet time brought some sense of better things, else forgotten and unattainable, into his mind. Of late, the neglected bed in the Temple Court had known him more scantily than ever; and often when he had thrown himself upon it no longer than a few minutes, he had got up again, and haunted that neighbourhood.\n\nOn a day in August, when Mr. Stryver (after notifying to his jackal that “he had thought better of that marrying matter”) had carried his delicacy into Devonshire, and when the sight and scent of flowers in the City streets had some waifs of goodness in them for the worst, of health for the sickliest, and of youth for the oldest, Sydney’s feet still trod those stones. From being irresolute and purposeless, his feet became animated by an intention, and, in the working out of that intention, they took him to the Doctor’s door.\n\nHe was shown up-stairs, and found Lucie at her work, alone. She had never been quite at her ease with him, and received him with some little embarrassment as he seated himself near her table. But, looking up at his face in the interchange of the first few common-places, she observed a change in it.\n\n“I fear you are not well, Mr. Carton!”\n\n“No. But the life I lead, Miss Manette, is not conducive to health. What is to be expected of, or by, such profligates?”\n\n“Is it not--forgive me; I have begun the question on my lips--a pity to live no better life?”\n\n“God knows it is a shame!”\n\n“Then why not change it?”\n\nLooking gently at him again, she was surprised and saddened to see that there were tears in his eyes. There were tears in his voice too, as he answered:\n\n“It is too late for that. I shall never be better than I am. I shall sink lower, and be worse.”\n\nHe leaned an elbow on her table, and covered his eyes with his hand. The table trembled in the silence that followed.\n\nShe had never seen him softened, and was much distressed. He knew her to be so, without looking at her, and said:\n\n“Pray forgive me, Miss Manette. I break down before the knowledge of what I want to say to you. Will you hear me?”\n\n“If it will do you any good, Mr. Carton, if it would make you happier, it would make me very glad!”\n\n“God bless you for your sweet compassion!”\n\nHe unshaded his face after a little while, and spoke steadily.\n\n“Don’t be afraid to hear me. Don’t shrink from anything I say. I am like one who died young. All my life might have been.”\n\n“No, Mr. Carton. I am sure that the best part of it might still be; I am sure that you might be much, much worthier of yourself.”\n\n“Say of you, Miss Manette, and although I know better--although in the mystery of my own wretched heart I know better--I shall never forget it!”\n\nShe was pale and trembling. He came to her relief with a fixed despair of himself which made the interview unlike any other that could have been holden.\n\n“If it had been possible, Miss Manette, that you could have returned the love of the man you see before yourself--flung away, wasted, drunken, poor creature of misuse as you know him to be--he would have been conscious this day and hour, in spite of his happiness, that he would bring you to misery, bring you to sorrow and repentance, blight you, disgrace you, pull you down with him. I know very well that you can have no tenderness for me; I ask for none; I am even thankful that it cannot be.”\n\n“Without it, can I not save you, Mr. Carton? Can I not recall you--forgive me again!--to a better course? Can I in no way repay your confidence? I know this is a confidence,” she modestly said, after a little hesitation, and in earnest tears, “I know you would say this to no one else. Can I turn it to no good account for yourself, Mr. Carton?”\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n“To none. No, Miss Manette, to none. If you will hear me through a very little more, all you can ever do for me is done. I wish you to know that you have been the last dream of my soul. In my degradation I have not been so degraded but that the sight of you with your father, and of this home made such a home by you, has stirred old shadows that I thought had died out of me. Since I knew you, I have been troubled by a remorse that I thought would never reproach me again, and have heard whispers from old voices impelling me upward, that I thought were silent for ever. I have had unformed ideas of striving afresh, beginning anew, shaking off sloth and sensuality, and fighting out the abandoned fight. A dream, all a dream, that ends in nothing, and leaves the sleeper where he lay down, but I wish you to know that you inspired it.”\n\n“Will nothing of it remain? O Mr. Carton, think again! Try again!”\n\n“No, Miss Manette; all through it, I have known myself to be quite undeserving. And yet I have had the weakness, and have still the weakness, to wish you to know with what a sudden mastery you kindled me, heap of ashes that I am, into fire--a fire, however, inseparable in its nature from myself, quickening nothing, lighting nothing, doing no service, idly burning away.”\n\n“Since it is my misfortune, Mr. Carton, to have made you more unhappy than you were before you knew me--”\n\n“Don’t say that, Miss Manette, for you would have reclaimed me, if anything could. You will not be the cause of my becoming worse.”\n\n“Since the state of your mind that you describe, is, at all events, attributable to some influence of mine--this is what I mean, if I can make it plain--can I use no influence to serve you? Have I no power for good, with you, at all?”\n\n“The utmost good that I am capable of now, Miss Manette, I have come here to realise. Let me carry through the rest of my misdirected life, the remembrance that I opened my heart to you, last of all the world; and that there was something left in me at this time which you could deplore and pity.”\n\n“Which I entreated you to believe, again and again, most fervently, with all my heart, was capable of better things, Mr. Carton!”\n\n“Entreat me to believe it no more, Miss Manette. I have proved myself, and I know better. I distress you; I draw fast to an end. Will you let me believe, when I recall this day, that the last confidence of my life was reposed in your pure and innocent breast, and that it lies there alone, and will be shared by no one?”\n\n“If that will be a consolation to you, yes.”\n\n“Not even by the dearest one ever to be known to you?”\n\n“Mr. Carton,” she answered, after an agitated pause, “the secret is yours, not mine; and I promise to respect it.”\n\n“Thank you. And again, God bless you.”\n\nHe put her hand to his lips, and moved towards the door.\n\n“Be under no apprehension, Miss Manette, of my ever resuming this conversation by so much as a passing word. I will never refer to it again. If I were dead, that could not be surer than it is henceforth. In the hour of my death, I shall hold sacred the one good remembrance--and shall thank and bless you for it--that my last avowal of myself was made to you, and that my name, and faults, and miseries were gently carried in your heart. May it otherwise be light and happy!”\n\nHe was so unlike what he had ever shown himself to be, and it was so sad to think how much he had thrown away, and how much he every day kept down and perverted, that Lucie Manette wept mournfully for him as he stood looking back at her.\n\n“Be comforted!” he said, “I am not worth such feeling, Miss Manette. An hour or two hence, and the low companions and low habits that I scorn but yield to, will render me less worth such tears as those, than any wretch who creeps along the streets. Be comforted! But, within myself, I shall always be, towards you, what I am now, though outwardly I shall be what you have heretofore seen me. The last supplication but one I make to you, is, that you will believe this of me.”\n\n“I will, Mr. Carton.”\n\n“My last supplication of all, is this; and with it, I will relieve you of a visitor with whom I well know you have nothing in unison, and between whom and you there is an impassable space. It is useless to say it, I know, but it rises out of my soul. For you, and for any dear to you, I would do anything. If my career were of that better kind that there was any opportunity or capacity of sacrifice in it, I would embrace any sacrifice for you and for those dear to you. Try to hold me in your mind, at some quiet times, as ardent and sincere in this one thing. The time will come, the time will not be long in coming, when new ties will be formed about you--ties that will bind you yet more tenderly and strongly to the home you so adorn--the dearest ties that will ever grace and gladden you. O Miss Manette, when the little picture of a happy father’s face looks up in yours, when you see your own bright beauty springing up anew at your feet, think now and then that there is a man who would give his life, to keep a life you love beside you!”\n\nHe said, “Farewell!” said a last “God bless you!” and left her.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. The Honest Tradesman\n\nTo the eyes of Mr. Jeremiah Cruncher, sitting on his stool in Fleet-street with his grisly urchin beside him, a vast number and variety of objects in movement were every day presented. Who could sit upon anything in Fleet-street during the busy hours of the day, and not be dazed and deafened by two immense processions, one ever tending westward with the sun, the other ever tending eastward from the sun, both ever tending to the plains beyond the range of red and purple where the sun goes down!\n\nWith his straw in his mouth, Mr. Cruncher sat watching the two streams, like the heathen rustic who has for several centuries been on duty watching one stream--saving that Jerry had no expectation of their ever running dry. Nor would it have been an expectation of a hopeful kind, since a small part of his income was derived from the pilotage of timid women (mostly of a full habit and past the middle term of life) from Tellson’s side of the tides to the opposite shore. Brief as such companionship was in every separate instance, Mr. Cruncher never failed to become so interested in the lady as to express a strong desire to have the honour of drinking her very good health. And it was from the gifts bestowed upon him towards the execution of this benevolent purpose, that he recruited his finances, as just now observed.\n\nTime was, when a poet sat upon a stool in a public place, and mused in the sight of men. Mr. Cruncher, sitting on a stool in a public place, but not being a poet, mused as little as possible, and looked about him.\n\nIt fell out that he was thus engaged in a season when crowds were few, and belated women few, and when his affairs in general were so unprosperous as to awaken a strong suspicion in his breast that Mrs. Cruncher must have been “flopping” in some pointed manner, when an unusual concourse pouring down Fleet-street westward, attracted his attention. Looking that way, Mr. Cruncher made out that some kind of funeral was coming along, and that there was popular objection to this funeral, which engendered uproar.\n\n“Young Jerry,” said Mr. Cruncher, turning to his offspring, “it’s a buryin’.”\n\n“Hooroar, father!” cried Young Jerry.\n\nThe young gentleman uttered this exultant sound with mysterious significance. The elder gentleman took the cry so ill, that he watched his opportunity, and smote the young gentleman on the ear.\n\n“What d’ye mean? What are you hooroaring at? What do you want to conwey to your own father, you young Rip? This boy is a getting too many for me!” said Mr. Cruncher, surveying him. “Him and his hooroars! Don’t let me hear no more of you, or you shall feel some more of me. D’ye hear?”\n\n“I warn’t doing no harm,” Young Jerry protested, rubbing his cheek.\n\n“Drop it then,” said Mr. Cruncher; “I won’t have none of your no harms. Get a top of that there seat, and look at the crowd.”\n\nHis son obeyed, and the crowd approached; they were bawling and hissing round a dingy hearse and dingy mourning coach, in which mourning coach there was only one mourner, dressed in the dingy trappings that were considered essential to the dignity of the position. The position appeared by no means to please him, however, with an increasing rabble surrounding the coach, deriding him, making grimaces at him, and incessantly groaning and calling out: “Yah! Spies! Tst! Yaha! Spies!” with many compliments too numerous and forcible to repeat.\n\nFunerals had at all times a remarkable attraction for Mr. Cruncher; he always pricked up his senses, and became excited, when a funeral passed Tellson’s. Naturally, therefore, a funeral with this uncommon attendance excited him greatly, and he asked of the first man who ran against him:\n\n“What is it, brother? What’s it about?”\n\n“I don’t know,” said the man. “Spies! Yaha! Tst! Spies!”\n\nHe asked another man. “Who is it?”\n\n“I don’t know,” returned the man, clapping his hands to his mouth nevertheless, and vociferating in a surprising heat and with the greatest ardour, “Spies! Yaha! Tst, tst! Spi--ies!”\n\nAt length, a person better informed on the merits of the case, tumbled against him, and from this person he learned that the funeral was the funeral of one Roger Cly.\n\n“Was he a spy?” asked Mr. Cruncher.\n\n“Old Bailey spy,” returned his informant. “Yaha! Tst! Yah! Old Bailey Spi--i--ies!”\n\n“Why, to be sure!” exclaimed Jerry, recalling the Trial at which he had assisted. “I’ve seen him. Dead, is he?”\n\n“Dead as mutton,” returned the other, “and can’t be too dead. Have ’em out, there! Spies! Pull ’em out, there! Spies!”\n\nThe idea was so acceptable in the prevalent absence of any idea, that the crowd caught it up with eagerness, and loudly repeating the suggestion to have ’em out, and to pull ’em out, mobbed the two vehicles so closely that they came to a stop. On the crowd’s opening the coach doors, the one mourner scuffled out by himself and was in their hands for a moment; but he was so alert, and made such good use of his time, that in another moment he was scouring away up a bye-street, after shedding his cloak, hat, long hatband, white pocket-handkerchief, and other symbolical tears.\n\nThese, the people tore to pieces and scattered far and wide with great enjoyment, while the tradesmen hurriedly shut up their shops; for a crowd in those times stopped at nothing, and was a monster much dreaded. They had already got the length of opening the hearse to take the coffin out, when some brighter genius proposed instead, its being escorted to its destination amidst general rejoicing. Practical suggestions being much needed, this suggestion, too, was received with acclamation, and the coach was immediately filled with eight inside and a dozen out, while as many people got on the roof of the hearse as could by any exercise of ingenuity stick upon it. Among the first of these volunteers was Jerry Cruncher himself, who modestly concealed his spiky head from the observation of Tellson’s, in the further corner of the mourning coach.\n\nThe officiating undertakers made some protest against these changes in the ceremonies; but, the river being alarmingly near, and several voices remarking on the efficacy of cold immersion in bringing refractory members of the profession to reason, the protest was faint and brief. The remodelled procession started, with a chimney-sweep driving the hearse--advised by the regular driver, who was perched beside him, under close inspection, for the purpose--and with a pieman, also attended by his cabinet minister, driving the mourning coach. A bear-leader, a popular street character of the time, was impressed as an additional ornament, before the cavalcade had gone far down the Strand; and his bear, who was black and very mangy, gave quite an Undertaking air to that part of the procession in which he walked.\n\nThus, with beer-drinking, pipe-smoking, song-roaring, and infinite caricaturing of woe, the disorderly procession went its way, recruiting at every step, and all the shops shutting up before it. Its destination was the old church of Saint Pancras, far off in the fields. It got there in course of time; insisted on pouring into the burial-ground; finally, accomplished the interment of the deceased Roger Cly in its own way, and highly to its own satisfaction.\n\nThe dead man disposed of, and the crowd being under the necessity of providing some other entertainment for itself, another brighter genius (or perhaps the same) conceived the humour of impeaching casual passers-by, as Old Bailey spies, and wreaking vengeance on them. Chase was given to some scores of inoffensive persons who had never been near the Old Bailey in their lives, in the realisation of this fancy, and they were roughly hustled and maltreated. The transition to the sport of window-breaking, and thence to the plundering of public-houses, was easy and natural. At last, after several hours, when sundry summer-houses had been pulled down, and some area-railings had been torn up, to arm the more belligerent spirits, a rumour got about that the Guards were coming. Before this rumour, the crowd gradually melted away, and perhaps the Guards came, and perhaps they never came, and this was the usual progress of a mob.\n\nMr. Cruncher did not assist at the closing sports, but had remained behind in the churchyard, to confer and condole with the undertakers. The place had a soothing influence on him. He procured a pipe from a neighbouring public-house, and smoked it, looking in at the railings and maturely considering the spot.\n\n“Jerry,” said Mr. Cruncher, apostrophising himself in his usual way, “you see that there Cly that day, and you see with your own eyes that he was a young ’un and a straight made ’un.”\n\nHaving smoked his pipe out, and ruminated a little longer, he turned himself about, that he might appear, before the hour of closing, on his station at Tellson’s. Whether his meditations on mortality had touched his liver, or whether his general health had been previously at all amiss, or whether he desired to show a little attention to an eminent man, is not so much to the purpose, as that he made a short call upon his medical adviser--a distinguished surgeon--on his way back.\n\nYoung Jerry relieved his father with dutiful interest, and reported No job in his absence. The bank closed, the ancient clerks came out, the usual watch was set, and Mr. Cruncher and his son went home to tea.\n\n“Now, I tell you where it is!” said Mr. Cruncher to his wife, on entering. “If, as a honest tradesman, my wenturs goes wrong to-night, I shall make sure that you’ve been praying again me, and I shall work you for it just the same as if I seen you do it.”\n\nThe dejected Mrs. Cruncher shook her head.\n\n“Why, you’re at it afore my face!” said Mr. Cruncher, with signs of angry apprehension.\n\n“I am saying nothing.”\n\n“Well, then; don’t meditate nothing. You might as well flop as meditate. You may as well go again me one way as another. Drop it altogether.”\n\n“Yes, Jerry.”\n\n“Yes, Jerry,” repeated Mr. Cruncher sitting down to tea. “Ah! It is yes, Jerry. That’s about it. You may say yes, Jerry.”\n\nMr. Cruncher had no particular meaning in these sulky corroborations, but made use of them, as people not unfrequently do, to express general ironical dissatisfaction.\n\n“You and your yes, Jerry,” said Mr. Cruncher, taking a bite out of his bread-and-butter, and seeming to help it down with a large invisible oyster out of his saucer. “Ah! I think so. I believe you.”\n\n“You are going out to-night?” asked his decent wife, when he took another bite.\n\n“Yes, I am.”\n\n“May I go with you, father?” asked his son, briskly.\n\n“No, you mayn’t. I’m a going--as your mother knows--a fishing. That’s where I’m going to. Going a fishing.”\n\n“Your fishing-rod gets rayther rusty; don’t it, father?”\n\n“Never you mind.”\n\n“Shall you bring any fish home, father?”\n\n“If I don’t, you’ll have short commons, to-morrow,” returned that gentleman, shaking his head; “that’s questions enough for you; I ain’t a going out, till you’ve been long abed.”\n\nHe devoted himself during the remainder of the evening to keeping a most vigilant watch on Mrs. Cruncher, and sullenly holding her in conversation that she might be prevented from meditating any petitions to his disadvantage. With this view, he urged his son to hold her in conversation also, and led the unfortunate woman a hard life by dwelling on any causes of complaint he could bring against her, rather than he would leave her for a moment to her own reflections. The devoutest person could have rendered no greater homage to the efficacy of an honest prayer than he did in this distrust of his wife. It was as if a professed unbeliever in ghosts should be frightened by a ghost story.\n\n“And mind you!” said Mr. Cruncher. “No games to-morrow! If I, as a honest tradesman, succeed in providing a jinte of meat or two, none of your not touching of it, and sticking to bread. If I, as a honest tradesman, am able to provide a little beer, none of your declaring on water. When you go to Rome, do as Rome does. Rome will be a ugly customer to you, if you don’t. I’m your Rome, you know.”\n\nThen he began grumbling again:\n\n“With your flying into the face of your own wittles and drink! I don’t know how scarce you mayn’t make the wittles and drink here, by your flopping tricks and your unfeeling conduct. Look at your boy: he is your’n, ain’t he? He’s as thin as a lath. Do you call yourself a mother, and not know that a mother’s first duty is to blow her boy out?”\n\nThis touched Young Jerry on a tender place; who adjured his mother to perform her first duty, and, whatever else she did or neglected, above all things to lay especial stress on the discharge of that maternal function so affectingly and delicately indicated by his other parent.\n\nThus the evening wore away with the Cruncher family, until Young Jerry was ordered to bed, and his mother, laid under similar injunctions, obeyed them. Mr. Cruncher beguiled the earlier watches of the night with solitary pipes, and did not start upon his excursion until nearly one o’clock. Towards that small and ghostly hour, he rose up from his chair, took a key out of his pocket, opened a locked cupboard, and brought forth a sack, a crowbar of convenient size, a rope and chain, and other fishing tackle of that nature. Disposing these articles about him in skilful manner, he bestowed a parting defiance on Mrs. Cruncher, extinguished the light, and went out.\n\nYoung Jerry, who had only made a feint of undressing when he went to bed, was not long after his father. Under cover of the darkness he followed out of the room, followed down the stairs, followed down the court, followed out into the streets. He was in no uneasiness concerning his getting into the house again, for it was full of lodgers, and the door stood ajar all night.\n\nImpelled by a laudable ambition to study the art and mystery of his father’s honest calling, Young Jerry, keeping as close to house fronts, walls, and doorways, as his eyes were close to one another, held his honoured parent in view. The honoured parent steering Northward, had not gone far, when he was joined by another disciple of Izaak Walton, and the two trudged on together.\n\nWithin half an hour from the first starting, they were beyond the winking lamps, and the more than winking watchmen, and were out upon a lonely road. Another fisherman was picked up here--and that so silently, that if Young Jerry had been superstitious, he might have supposed the second follower of the gentle craft to have, all of a sudden, split himself into two.\n\nThe three went on, and Young Jerry went on, until the three stopped under a bank overhanging the road. Upon the top of the bank was a low brick wall, surmounted by an iron railing. In the shadow of bank and wall the three turned out of the road, and up a blind lane, of which the wall--there, risen to some eight or ten feet high--formed one side. Crouching down in a corner, peeping up the lane, the next object that Young Jerry saw, was the form of his honoured parent, pretty well defined against a watery and clouded moon, nimbly scaling an iron gate. He was soon over, and then the second fisherman got over, and then the third. They all dropped softly on the ground within the gate, and lay there a little--listening perhaps. Then, they moved away on their hands and knees.\n\nIt was now Young Jerry’s turn to approach the gate: which he did, holding his breath. Crouching down again in a corner there, and looking in, he made out the three fishermen creeping through some rank grass! and all the gravestones in the churchyard--it was a large churchyard that they were in--looking on like ghosts in white, while the church tower itself looked on like the ghost of a monstrous giant. They did not creep far, before they stopped and stood upright. And then they began to fish.\n\nThey fished with a spade, at first. Presently the honoured parent appeared to be adjusting some instrument like a great corkscrew. Whatever tools they worked with, they worked hard, until the awful striking of the church clock so terrified Young Jerry, that he made off, with his hair as stiff as his father’s.\n\nBut, his long-cherished desire to know more about these matters, not only stopped him in his running away, but lured him back again. They were still fishing perseveringly, when he peeped in at the gate for the second time; but, now they seemed to have got a bite. There was a screwing and complaining sound down below, and their bent figures were strained, as if by a weight. By slow degrees the weight broke away the earth upon it, and came to the surface. Young Jerry very well knew what it would be; but, when he saw it, and saw his honoured parent about to wrench it open, he was so frightened, being new to the sight, that he made off again, and never stopped until he had run a mile or more.\n\nHe would not have stopped then, for anything less necessary than breath, it being a spectral sort of race that he ran, and one highly desirable to get to the end of. He had a strong idea that the coffin he had seen was running after him; and, pictured as hopping on behind him, bolt upright, upon its narrow end, always on the point of overtaking him and hopping on at his side--perhaps taking his arm--it was a pursuer to shun. It was an inconsistent and ubiquitous fiend too, for, while it was making the whole night behind him dreadful, he darted out into the roadway to avoid dark alleys, fearful of its coming hopping out of them like a dropsical boy’s kite without tail and wings. It hid in doorways too, rubbing its horrible shoulders against doors, and drawing them up to its ears, as if it were laughing. It got into shadows on the road, and lay cunningly on its back to trip him up. All this time it was incessantly hopping on behind and gaining on him, so that when the boy got to his own door he had reason for being half dead. And even then it would not leave him, but followed him upstairs with a bump on every stair, scrambled into bed with him, and bumped down, dead and heavy, on his breast when he fell asleep.\n\nFrom his oppressed slumber, Young Jerry in his closet was awakened after daybreak and before sunrise, by the presence of his father in the family room. Something had gone wrong with him; at least, so Young Jerry inferred, from the circumstance of his holding Mrs. Cruncher by the ears, and knocking the back of her head against the head-board of the bed.\n\n“I told you I would,” said Mr. Cruncher, “and I did.”\n\n“Jerry, Jerry, Jerry!” his wife implored.\n\n“You oppose yourself to the profit of the business,” said Jerry, “and me and my partners suffer. You was to honour and obey; why the devil don’t you?”\n\n“I try to be a good wife, Jerry,” the poor woman protested, with tears.\n\n“Is it being a good wife to oppose your husband’s business? Is it honouring your husband to dishonour his business? Is it obeying your husband to disobey him on the wital subject of his business?”\n\n“You hadn’t taken to the dreadful business then, Jerry.”\n\n“It’s enough for you,” retorted Mr. Cruncher, “to be the wife of a honest tradesman, and not to occupy your female mind with calculations when he took to his trade or when he didn’t. A honouring and obeying wife would let his trade alone altogether. Call yourself a religious woman? If you’re a religious woman, give me a irreligious one! You have no more nat’ral sense of duty than the bed of this here Thames river has of a pile, and similarly it must be knocked into you.”\n\nThe altercation was conducted in a low tone of voice, and terminated in the honest tradesman’s kicking off his clay-soiled boots, and lying down at his length on the floor. After taking a timid peep at him lying on his back, with his rusty hands under his head for a pillow, his son lay down too, and fell asleep again.\n\nThere was no fish for breakfast, and not much of anything else. Mr. Cruncher was out of spirits, and out of temper, and kept an iron pot-lid by him as a projectile for the correction of Mrs. Cruncher, in case he should observe any symptoms of her saying Grace. He was brushed and washed at the usual hour, and set off with his son to pursue his ostensible calling.\n\nYoung Jerry, walking with the stool under his arm at his father’s side along sunny and crowded Fleet-street, was a very different Young Jerry from him of the previous night, running home through darkness and solitude from his grim pursuer. His cunning was fresh with the day, and his qualms were gone with the night--in which particulars it is not improbable that he had compeers in Fleet-street and the City of London, that fine morning.\n\n“Father,” said Young Jerry, as they walked along: taking care to keep at arm’s length and to have the stool well between them: “what’s a Resurrection-Man?”\n\nMr. Cruncher came to a stop on the pavement before he answered, “How should I know?”\n\n“I thought you knowed everything, father,” said the artless boy.\n\n“Hem! Well,” returned Mr. Cruncher, going on again, and lifting off his hat to give his spikes free play, “he’s a tradesman.”\n\n“What’s his goods, father?” asked the brisk Young Jerry.\n\n“His goods,” said Mr. Cruncher, after turning it over in his mind, “is a branch of Scientific goods.”\n\n“Persons’ bodies, ain’t it, father?” asked the lively boy.\n\n“I believe it is something of that sort,” said Mr. Cruncher.\n\n“Oh, father, I should so like to be a Resurrection-Man when I’m quite growed up!”\n\nMr. Cruncher was soothed, but shook his head in a dubious and moral way. “It depends upon how you dewelop your talents. Be careful to dewelop your talents, and never to say no more than you can help to nobody, and there’s no telling at the present time what you may not come to be fit for.” As Young Jerry, thus encouraged, went on a few yards in advance, to plant the stool in the shadow of the Bar, Mr. Cruncher added to himself: “Jerry, you honest tradesman, there’s hopes wot that boy will yet be a blessing to you, and a recompense to you for his mother!”\n\n# CHAPTER XV. Knitting\n\nThere had been earlier drinking than usual in the wine-shop of Monsieur Defarge. As early as six o’clock in the morning, sallow faces peeping through its barred windows had descried other faces within, bending over measures of wine. Monsieur Defarge sold a very thin wine at the best of times, but it would seem to have been an unusually thin wine that he sold at this time. A sour wine, moreover, or a souring, for its influence on the mood of those who drank it was to make them gloomy. No vivacious Bacchanalian flame leaped out of the pressed grape of Monsieur Defarge: but, a smouldering fire that burnt in the dark, lay hidden in the dregs of it.\n\nThis had been the third morning in succession, on which there had been early drinking at the wine-shop of Monsieur Defarge. It had begun on Monday, and here was Wednesday come. There had been more of early brooding than drinking; for, many men had listened and whispered and slunk about there from the time of the opening of the door, who could not have laid a piece of money on the counter to save their souls. These were to the full as interested in the place, however, as if they could have commanded whole barrels of wine; and they glided from seat to seat, and from corner to corner, swallowing talk in lieu of drink, with greedy looks.\n\nNotwithstanding an unusual flow of company, the master of the wine-shop was not visible. He was not missed; for, nobody who crossed the threshold looked for him, nobody asked for him, nobody wondered to see only Madame Defarge in her seat, presiding over the distribution of wine, with a bowl of battered small coins before her, as much defaced and beaten out of their original impress as the small coinage of humanity from whose ragged pockets they had come.\n\nA suspended interest and a prevalent absence of mind, were perhaps observed by the spies who looked in at the wine-shop, as they looked in at every place, high and low, from the king’s palace to the criminal’s gaol. Games at cards languished, players at dominoes musingly built towers with them, drinkers drew figures on the tables with spilt drops of wine, Madame Defarge herself picked out the pattern on her sleeve with her toothpick, and saw and heard something inaudible and invisible a long way off.\n\nThus, Saint Antoine in this vinous feature of his, until midday. It was high noontide, when two dusty men passed through his streets and under his swinging lamps: of whom, one was Monsieur Defarge: the other a mender of roads in a blue cap. All adust and athirst, the two entered the wine-shop. Their arrival had lighted a kind of fire in the breast of Saint Antoine, fast spreading as they came along, which stirred and flickered in flames of faces at most doors and windows. Yet, no one had followed them, and no man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, though the eyes of every man there were turned upon them.\n\n“Good day, gentlemen!” said Monsieur Defarge.\n\nIt may have been a signal for loosening the general tongue. It elicited an answering chorus of “Good day!”\n\n“It is bad weather, gentlemen,” said Defarge, shaking his head.\n\nUpon which, every man looked at his neighbour, and then all cast down their eyes and sat silent. Except one man, who got up and went out.\n\n“My wife,” said Defarge aloud, addressing Madame Defarge: “I have travelled certain leagues with this good mender of roads, called Jacques. I met him--by accident--a day and half’s journey out of Paris. He is a good child, this mender of roads, called Jacques. Give him to drink, my wife!”\n\nA second man got up and went out. Madame Defarge set wine before the mender of roads called Jacques, who doffed his blue cap to the company, and drank. In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse dark bread; he ate of this between whiles, and sat munching and drinking near Madame Defarge’s counter. A third man got up and went out.\n\nDefarge refreshed himself with a draught of wine--but, he took less than was given to the stranger, as being himself a man to whom it was no rarity--and stood waiting until the countryman had made his breakfast. He looked at no one present, and no one now looked at him; not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting, and was at work.\n\n“Have you finished your repast, friend?” he asked, in due season.\n\n“Yes, thank you.”\n\n“Come, then! You shall see the apartment that I told you you could occupy. It will suit you to a marvel.”\n\nOut of the wine-shop into the street, out of the street into a courtyard, out of the courtyard up a steep staircase, out of the staircase into a garret--formerly the garret where a white-haired man sat on a low bench, stooping forward and very busy, making shoes.\n\nNo white-haired man was there now; but, the three men were there who had gone out of the wine-shop singly. And between them and the white-haired man afar off, was the one small link, that they had once looked in at him through the chinks in the wall.\n\nDefarge closed the door carefully, and spoke in a subdued voice:\n\n“Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three! This is the witness encountered by appointment, by me, Jacques Four. He will tell you all. Speak, Jacques Five!”\n\nThe mender of roads, blue cap in hand, wiped his swarthy forehead with it, and said, “Where shall I commence, monsieur?”\n\n“Commence,” was Monsieur Defarge’s not unreasonable reply, “at the commencement.”\n\n“I saw him then, messieurs,” began the mender of roads, “a year ago this running summer, underneath the carriage of the Marquis, hanging by the chain. Behold the manner of it. I leaving my work on the road, the sun going to bed, the carriage of the Marquis slowly ascending the hill, he hanging by the chain--like this.”\n\nAgain the mender of roads went through the whole performance; in which he ought to have been perfect by that time, seeing that it had been the infallible resource and indispensable entertainment of his village during a whole year.\n\nJacques One struck in, and asked if he had ever seen the man before?\n\n“Never,” answered the mender of roads, recovering his perpendicular.\n\nJacques Three demanded how he afterwards recognised him then?\n\n“By his tall figure,” said the mender of roads, softly, and with his finger at his nose. “When Monsieur the Marquis demands that evening, ‘Say, what is he like?’ I make response, ‘Tall as a spectre.’”\n\n“You should have said, short as a dwarf,” returned Jacques Two.\n\n“But what did I know? The deed was not then accomplished, neither did he confide in me. Observe! Under those circumstances even, I do not offer my testimony. Monsieur the Marquis indicates me with his finger, standing near our little fountain, and says, ‘To me! Bring that rascal!’ My faith, messieurs, I offer nothing.”\n\n“He is right there, Jacques,” murmured Defarge, to him who had interrupted. “Go on!”\n\n“Good!” said the mender of roads, with an air of mystery. “The tall man is lost, and he is sought--how many months? Nine, ten, eleven?”\n\n“No matter, the number,” said Defarge. “He is well hidden, but at last he is unluckily found. Go on!”\n\n“I am again at work upon the hill-side, and the sun is again about to go to bed. I am collecting my tools to descend to my cottage down in the village below, where it is already dark, when I raise my eyes, and see coming over the hill six soldiers. In the midst of them is a tall man with his arms bound--tied to his sides--like this!”\n\nWith the aid of his indispensable cap, he represented a man with his elbows bound fast at his hips, with cords that were knotted behind him.\n\n“I stand aside, messieurs, by my heap of stones, to see the soldiers and their prisoner pass (for it is a solitary road, that, where any spectacle is well worth looking at), and at first, as they approach, I see no more than that they are six soldiers with a tall man bound, and that they are almost black to my sight--except on the side of the sun going to bed, where they have a red edge, messieurs. Also, I see that their long shadows are on the hollow ridge on the opposite side of the road, and are on the hill above it, and are like the shadows of giants. Also, I see that they are covered with dust, and that the dust moves with them as they come, tramp, tramp! But when they advance quite near to me, I recognise the tall man, and he recognises me. Ah, but he would be well content to precipitate himself over the hill-side once again, as on the evening when he and I first encountered, close to the same spot!”\n\nHe described it as if he were there, and it was evident that he saw it vividly; perhaps he had not seen much in his life.\n\n“I do not show the soldiers that I recognise the tall man; he does not show the soldiers that he recognises me; we do it, and we know it, with our eyes. ‘Come on!’ says the chief of that company, pointing to the village, ‘bring him fast to his tomb!’ and they bring him faster. I follow. His arms are swelled because of being bound so tight, his wooden shoes are large and clumsy, and he is lame. Because he is lame, and consequently slow, they drive him with their guns--like this!”\n\nHe imitated the action of a man’s being impelled forward by the butt-ends of muskets.\n\n“As they descend the hill like madmen running a race, he falls. They laugh and pick him up again. His face is bleeding and covered with dust, but he cannot touch it; thereupon they laugh again. They bring him into the village; all the village runs to look; they take him past the mill, and up to the prison; all the village sees the prison gate open in the darkness of the night, and swallow him--like this!”\n\nHe opened his mouth as wide as he could, and shut it with a sounding snap of his teeth. Observant of his unwillingness to mar the effect by opening it again, Defarge said, “Go on, Jacques.”\n\n“All the village,” pursued the mender of roads, on tiptoe and in a low voice, “withdraws; all the village whispers by the fountain; all the village sleeps; all the village dreams of that unhappy one, within the locks and bars of the prison on the crag, and never to come out of it, except to perish. In the morning, with my tools upon my shoulder, eating my morsel of black bread as I go, I make a circuit by the prison, on my way to my work. There I see him, high up, behind the bars of a lofty iron cage, bloody and dusty as last night, looking through. He has no hand free, to wave to me; I dare not call to him; he regards me like a dead man.”\n\nDefarge and the three glanced darkly at one another. The looks of all of them were dark, repressed, and revengeful, as they listened to the countryman’s story; the manner of all of them, while it was secret, was authoritative too. They had the air of a rough tribunal; Jacques One and Two sitting on the old pallet-bed, each with his chin resting on his hand, and his eyes intent on the road-mender; Jacques Three, equally intent, on one knee behind them, with his agitated hand always gliding over the network of fine nerves about his mouth and nose; Defarge standing between them and the narrator, whom he had stationed in the light of the window, by turns looking from him to them, and from them to him.\n\n“Go on, Jacques,” said Defarge.\n\n“He remains up there in his iron cage some days. The village looks at him by stealth, for it is afraid. But it always looks up, from a distance, at the prison on the crag; and in the evening, when the work of the day is achieved and it assembles to gossip at the fountain, all faces are turned towards the prison. Formerly, they were turned towards the posting-house; now, they are turned towards the prison. They whisper at the fountain, that although condemned to death he will not be executed; they say that petitions have been presented in Paris, showing that he was enraged and made mad by the death of his child; they say that a petition has been presented to the King himself. What do I know? It is possible. Perhaps yes, perhaps no.”\n\n“Listen then, Jacques,” Number One of that name sternly interposed. “Know that a petition was presented to the King and Queen. All here, yourself excepted, saw the King take it, in his carriage in the street, sitting beside the Queen. It is Defarge whom you see here, who, at the hazard of his life, darted out before the horses, with the petition in his hand.”\n\n“And once again listen, Jacques!” said the kneeling Number Three: his fingers ever wandering over and over those fine nerves, with a strikingly greedy air, as if he hungered for something--that was neither food nor drink; “the guard, horse and foot, surrounded the petitioner, and struck him blows. You hear?”\n\n“I hear, messieurs.”\n\n“Go on then,” said Defarge.\n\n“Again; on the other hand, they whisper at the fountain,” resumed the countryman, “that he is brought down into our country to be executed on the spot, and that he will very certainly be executed. They even whisper that because he has slain Monseigneur, and because Monseigneur was the father of his tenants--serfs--what you will--he will be executed as a parricide. One old man says at the fountain, that his right hand, armed with the knife, will be burnt off before his face; that, into wounds which will be made in his arms, his breast, and his legs, there will be poured boiling oil, melted lead, hot resin, wax, and sulphur; finally, that he will be torn limb from limb by four strong horses. That old man says, all this was actually done to a prisoner who made an attempt on the life of the late King, Louis Fifteen. But how do I know if he lies? I am not a scholar.”\n\n“Listen once again then, Jacques!” said the man with the restless hand and the craving air. “The name of that prisoner was Damiens, and it was all done in open day, in the open streets of this city of Paris; and nothing was more noticed in the vast concourse that saw it done, than the crowd of ladies of quality and fashion, who were full of eager attention to the last--to the last, Jacques, prolonged until nightfall, when he had lost two legs and an arm, and still breathed! And it was done--why, how old are you?”\n\n“Thirty-five,” said the mender of roads, who looked sixty.\n\n“It was done when you were more than ten years old; you might have seen it.”\n\n“Enough!” said Defarge, with grim impatience. “Long live the Devil! Go on.”\n\n“Well! Some whisper this, some whisper that; they speak of nothing else; even the fountain appears to fall to that tune. At length, on Sunday night when all the village is asleep, come soldiers, winding down from the prison, and their guns ring on the stones of the little street. Workmen dig, workmen hammer, soldiers laugh and sing; in the morning, by the fountain, there is raised a gallows forty feet high, poisoning the water.”\n\nThe mender of roads looked through rather than at the low ceiling, and pointed as if he saw the gallows somewhere in the sky.\n\n“All work is stopped, all assemble there, nobody leads the cows out, the cows are there with the rest. At midday, the roll of drums. Soldiers have marched into the prison in the night, and he is in the midst of many soldiers. He is bound as before, and in his mouth there is a gag--tied so, with a tight string, making him look almost as if he laughed.” He suggested it, by creasing his face with his two thumbs, from the corners of his mouth to his ears. “On the top of the gallows is fixed the knife, blade upwards, with its point in the air. He is hanged there forty feet high--and is left hanging, poisoning the water.”\n\nThey looked at one another, as he used his blue cap to wipe his face, on which the perspiration had started afresh while he recalled the spectacle.\n\n“It is frightful, messieurs. How can the women and the children draw water! Who can gossip of an evening, under that shadow! Under it, have I said? When I left the village, Monday evening as the sun was going to bed, and looked back from the hill, the shadow struck across the church, across the mill, across the prison--seemed to strike across the earth, messieurs, to where the sky rests upon it!”\n\nThe hungry man gnawed one of his fingers as he looked at the other three, and his finger quivered with the craving that was on him.\n\n“That’s all, messieurs. I left at sunset (as I had been warned to do), and I walked on, that night and half next day, until I met (as I was warned I should) this comrade. With him, I came on, now riding and now walking, through the rest of yesterday and through last night. And here you see me!”\n\nAfter a gloomy silence, the first Jacques said, “Good! You have acted and recounted faithfully. Will you wait for us a little, outside the door?”\n\n“Very willingly,” said the mender of roads. Whom Defarge escorted to the top of the stairs, and, leaving seated there, returned.\n\nThe three had risen, and their heads were together when he came back to the garret.\n\n“How say you, Jacques?” demanded Number One. “To be registered?”\n\n“To be registered, as doomed to destruction,” returned Defarge.\n\n“Magnificent!” croaked the man with the craving.\n\n“The chateau, and all the race?” inquired the first.\n\n“The chateau and all the race,” returned Defarge. “Extermination.”\n\nThe hungry man repeated, in a rapturous croak, “Magnificent!” and began gnawing another finger.\n\n“Are you sure,” asked Jacques Two, of Defarge, “that no embarrassment can arise from our manner of keeping the register? Without doubt it is safe, for no one beyond ourselves can decipher it; but shall we always be able to decipher it--or, I ought to say, will she?”\n\n“Jacques,” returned Defarge, drawing himself up, “if madame my wife undertook to keep the register in her memory alone, she would not lose a word of it--not a syllable of it. Knitted, in her own stitches and her own symbols, it will always be as plain to her as the sun. Confide in Madame Defarge. It would be easier for the weakest poltroon that lives, to erase himself from existence, than to erase one letter of his name or crimes from the knitted register of Madame Defarge.”\n\nThere was a murmur of confidence and approval, and then the man who hungered, asked: “Is this rustic to be sent back soon? I hope so. He is very simple; is he not a little dangerous?”\n\n“He knows nothing,” said Defarge; “at least nothing more than would easily elevate himself to a gallows of the same height. I charge myself with him; let him remain with me; I will take care of him, and set him on his road. He wishes to see the fine world--the King, the Queen, and Court; let him see them on Sunday.”\n\n“What?” exclaimed the hungry man, staring. “Is it a good sign, that he wishes to see Royalty and Nobility?”\n\n“Jacques,” said Defarge; “judiciously show a cat milk, if you wish her to thirst for it. Judiciously show a dog his natural prey, if you wish him to bring it down one day.”\n\nNothing more was said, and the mender of roads, being found already dozing on the topmost stair, was advised to lay himself down on the pallet-bed and take some rest. He needed no persuasion, and was soon asleep.\n\nWorse quarters than Defarge’s wine-shop, could easily have been found in Paris for a provincial slave of that degree. Saving for a mysterious dread of madame by which he was constantly haunted, his life was very new and agreeable. But, madame sat all day at her counter, so expressly unconscious of him, and so particularly determined not to perceive that his being there had any connection with anything below the surface, that he shook in his wooden shoes whenever his eye lighted on her. For, he contended with himself that it was impossible to foresee what that lady might pretend next; and he felt assured that if she should take it into her brightly ornamented head to pretend that she had seen him do a murder and afterwards flay the victim, she would infallibly go through with it until the play was played out.\n\nTherefore, when Sunday came, the mender of roads was not enchanted (though he said he was) to find that madame was to accompany monsieur and himself to Versailles. It was additionally disconcerting to have madame knitting all the way there, in a public conveyance; it was additionally disconcerting yet, to have madame in the crowd in the afternoon, still with her knitting in her hands as the crowd waited to see the carriage of the King and Queen.\n\n“You work hard, madame,” said a man near her.\n\n“Yes,” answered Madame Defarge; “I have a good deal to do.”\n\n“What do you make, madame?”\n\n“Many things.”\n\n“For instance--”\n\n“For instance,” returned Madame Defarge, composedly, “shrouds.”\n\nThe man moved a little further away, as soon as he could, and the mender of roads fanned himself with his blue cap: feeling it mightily close and oppressive. If he needed a King and Queen to restore him, he was fortunate in having his remedy at hand; for, soon the large-faced King and the fair-faced Queen came in their golden coach, attended by the shining Bull’s Eye of their Court, a glittering multitude of laughing ladies and fine lords; and in jewels and silks and powder and splendour and elegantly spurning figures and handsomely disdainful faces of both sexes, the mender of roads bathed himself, so much to his temporary intoxication, that he cried Long live the King, Long live the Queen, Long live everybody and everything! as if he had never heard of ubiquitous Jacques in his time. Then, there were gardens, courtyards, terraces, fountains, green banks, more King and Queen, more Bull’s Eye, more lords and ladies, more Long live they all! until he absolutely wept with sentiment. During the whole of this scene, which lasted some three hours, he had plenty of shouting and weeping and sentimental company, and throughout Defarge held him by the collar, as if to restrain him from flying at the objects of his brief devotion and tearing them to pieces.\n\n“Bravo!” said Defarge, clapping him on the back when it was over, like a patron; “you are a good boy!”\n\nThe mender of roads was now coming to himself, and was mistrustful of having made a mistake in his late demonstrations; but no.\n\n“You are the fellow we want,” said Defarge, in his ear; “you make these fools believe that it will last for ever. Then, they are the more insolent, and it is the nearer ended.”\n\n“Hey!” cried the mender of roads, reflectively; “that’s true.”\n\n“These fools know nothing. While they despise your breath, and would stop it for ever and ever, in you or in a hundred like you rather than in one of their own horses or dogs, they only know what your breath tells them. Let it deceive them, then, a little longer; it cannot deceive them too much.”\n\nMadame Defarge looked superciliously at the client, and nodded in confirmation.\n\n“As to you,” said she, “you would shout and shed tears for anything, if it made a show and a noise. Say! Would you not?”\n\n“Truly, madame, I think so. For the moment.”\n\n“If you were shown a great heap of dolls, and were set upon them to pluck them to pieces and despoil them for your own advantage, you would pick out the richest and gayest. Say! Would you not?”\n\n“Truly yes, madame.”\n\n“Yes. And if you were shown a flock of birds, unable to fly, and were set upon them to strip them of their feathers for your own advantage, you would set upon the birds of the finest feathers; would you not?”\n\n“It is true, madame.”\n\n“You have seen both dolls and birds to-day,” said Madame Defarge, with a wave of her hand towards the place where they had last been apparent; “now, go home!”\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. Still Knitting\n\nMadame Defarge and monsieur her husband returned amicably to the bosom of Saint Antoine, while a speck in a blue cap toiled through the darkness, and through the dust, and down the weary miles of avenue by the wayside, slowly tending towards that point of the compass where the chateau of Monsieur the Marquis, now in his grave, listened to the whispering trees. Such ample leisure had the stone faces, now, for listening to the trees and to the fountain, that the few village scarecrows who, in their quest for herbs to eat and fragments of dead stick to burn, strayed within sight of the great stone courtyard and terrace staircase, had it borne in upon their starved fancy that the expression of the faces was altered. A rumour just lived in the village--had a faint and bare existence there, as its people had--that when the knife struck home, the faces changed, from faces of pride to faces of anger and pain; also, that when that dangling figure was hauled up forty feet above the fountain, they changed again, and bore a cruel look of being avenged, which they would henceforth bear for ever. In the stone face over the great window of the bed-chamber where the murder was done, two fine dints were pointed out in the sculptured nose, which everybody recognised, and which nobody had seen of old; and on the scarce occasions when two or three ragged peasants emerged from the crowd to take a hurried peep at Monsieur the Marquis petrified, a skinny finger would not have pointed to it for a minute, before they all started away among the moss and leaves, like the more fortunate hares who could find a living there.\n\nChateau and hut, stone face and dangling figure, the red stain on the stone floor, and the pure water in the village well--thousands of acres of land--a whole province of France--all France itself--lay under the night sky, concentrated into a faint hair-breadth line. So does a whole world, with all its greatnesses and littlenesses, lie in a twinkling star. And as mere human knowledge can split a ray of light and analyse the manner of its composition, so, sublimer intelligences may read in the feeble shining of this earth of ours, every thought and act, every vice and virtue, of every responsible creature on it.\n\nThe Defarges, husband and wife, came lumbering under the starlight, in their public vehicle, to that gate of Paris whereunto their journey naturally tended. There was the usual stoppage at the barrier guardhouse, and the usual lanterns came glancing forth for the usual examination and inquiry. Monsieur Defarge alighted; knowing one or two of the soldiery there, and one of the police. The latter he was intimate with, and affectionately embraced.\n\nWhen Saint Antoine had again enfolded the Defarges in his dusky wings, and they, having finally alighted near the Saint’s boundaries, were picking their way on foot through the black mud and offal of his streets, Madame Defarge spoke to her husband:\n\n“Say then, my friend; what did Jacques of the police tell thee?”\n\n“Very little to-night, but all he knows. There is another spy commissioned for our quarter. There may be many more, for all that he can say, but he knows of one.”\n\n“Eh well!” said Madame Defarge, raising her eyebrows with a cool business air. “It is necessary to register him. How do they call that man?”\n\n“He is English.”\n\n“So much the better. His name?”\n\n“Barsad,” said Defarge, making it French by pronunciation. But, he had been so careful to get it accurately, that he then spelt it with perfect correctness.\n\n“Barsad,” repeated madame. “Good. Christian name?”\n\n“John.”\n\n“John Barsad,” repeated madame, after murmuring it once to herself. “Good. His appearance; is it known?”\n\n“Age, about forty years; height, about five feet nine; black hair; complexion dark; generally, rather handsome visage; eyes dark, face thin, long, and sallow; nose aquiline, but not straight, having a peculiar inclination towards the left cheek; expression, therefore, sinister.”\n\n“Eh my faith. It is a portrait!” said madame, laughing. “He shall be registered to-morrow.”\n\nThey turned into the wine-shop, which was closed (for it was midnight), and where Madame Defarge immediately took her post at her desk, counted the small moneys that had been taken during her absence, examined the stock, went through the entries in the book, made other entries of her own, checked the serving man in every possible way, and finally dismissed him to bed. Then she turned out the contents of the bowl of money for the second time, and began knotting them up in her handkerchief, in a chain of separate knots, for safe keeping through the night. All this while, Defarge, with his pipe in his mouth, walked up and down, complacently admiring, but never interfering; in which condition, indeed, as to the business and his domestic affairs, he walked up and down through life.\n\nThe night was hot, and the shop, close shut and surrounded by so foul a neighbourhood, was ill-smelling. Monsieur Defarge’s olfactory sense was by no means delicate, but the stock of wine smelt much stronger than it ever tasted, and so did the stock of rum and brandy and aniseed. He whiffed the compound of scents away, as he put down his smoked-out pipe.\n\n“You are fatigued,” said madame, raising her glance as she knotted the money. “There are only the usual odours.”\n\n“I am a little tired,” her husband acknowledged.\n\n“You are a little depressed, too,” said madame, whose quick eyes had never been so intent on the accounts, but they had had a ray or two for him. “Oh, the men, the men!”\n\n“But my dear!” began Defarge.\n\n“But my dear!” repeated madame, nodding firmly; “but my dear! You are faint of heart to-night, my dear!”\n\n“Well, then,” said Defarge, as if a thought were wrung out of his breast, “it is a long time.”\n\n“It is a long time,” repeated his wife; “and when is it not a long time? Vengeance and retribution require a long time; it is the rule.”\n\n“It does not take a long time to strike a man with Lightning,” said Defarge.\n\n“How long,” demanded madame, composedly, “does it take to make and store the lightning? Tell me.”\n\nDefarge raised his head thoughtfully, as if there were something in that too.\n\n“It does not take a long time,” said madame, “for an earthquake to swallow a town. Eh well! Tell me how long it takes to prepare the earthquake?”\n\n“A long time, I suppose,” said Defarge.\n\n“But when it is ready, it takes place, and grinds to pieces everything before it. In the meantime, it is always preparing, though it is not seen or heard. That is your consolation. Keep it.”\n\nShe tied a knot with flashing eyes, as if it throttled a foe.\n\n“I tell thee,” said madame, extending her right hand, for emphasis, “that although it is a long time on the road, it is on the road and coming. I tell thee it never retreats, and never stops. I tell thee it is always advancing. Look around and consider the lives of all the world that we know, consider the faces of all the world that we know, consider the rage and discontent to which the Jacquerie addresses itself with more and more of certainty every hour. Can such things last? Bah! I mock you.”\n\n“My brave wife,” returned Defarge, standing before her with his head a little bent, and his hands clasped at his back, like a docile and attentive pupil before his catechist, “I do not question all this. But it has lasted a long time, and it is possible--you know well, my wife, it is possible--that it may not come, during our lives.”\n\n“Eh well! How then?” demanded madame, tying another knot, as if there were another enemy strangled.\n\n“Well!” said Defarge, with a half complaining and half apologetic shrug. “We shall not see the triumph.”\n\n“We shall have helped it,” returned madame, with her extended hand in strong action. “Nothing that we do, is done in vain. I believe, with all my soul, that we shall see the triumph. But even if not, even if I knew certainly not, show me the neck of an aristocrat and tyrant, and still I would--”\n\nThen madame, with her teeth set, tied a very terrible knot indeed.\n\n“Hold!” cried Defarge, reddening a little as if he felt charged with cowardice; “I too, my dear, will stop at nothing.”\n\n“Yes! But it is your weakness that you sometimes need to see your victim and your opportunity, to sustain you. Sustain yourself without that. When the time comes, let loose a tiger and a devil; but wait for the time with the tiger and the devil chained--not shown--yet always ready.”\n\nMadame enforced the conclusion of this piece of advice by striking her little counter with her chain of money as if she knocked its brains out, and then gathering the heavy handkerchief under her arm in a serene manner, and observing that it was time to go to bed.\n\nNext noontide saw the admirable woman in her usual place in the wine-shop, knitting away assiduously. A rose lay beside her, and if she now and then glanced at the flower, it was with no infraction of her usual preoccupied air. There were a few customers, drinking or not drinking, standing or seated, sprinkled about. The day was very hot, and heaps of flies, who were extending their inquisitive and adventurous perquisitions into all the glutinous little glasses near madame, fell dead at the bottom. Their decease made no impression on the other flies out promenading, who looked at them in the coolest manner (as if they themselves were elephants, or something as far removed), until they met the same fate. Curious to consider how heedless flies are!--perhaps they thought as much at Court that sunny summer day.\n\nA figure entering at the door threw a shadow on Madame Defarge which she felt to be a new one. She laid down her knitting, and began to pin her rose in her head-dress, before she looked at the figure.\n\nIt was curious. The moment Madame Defarge took up the rose, the customers ceased talking, and began gradually to drop out of the wine-shop.\n\n“Good day, madame,” said the new-comer.\n\n“Good day, monsieur.”\n\nShe said it aloud, but added to herself, as she resumed her knitting: “Hah! Good day, age about forty, height about five feet nine, black hair, generally rather handsome visage, complexion dark, eyes dark, thin, long and sallow face, aquiline nose but not straight, having a peculiar inclination towards the left cheek which imparts a sinister expression! Good day, one and all!”\n\n“Have the goodness to give me a little glass of old cognac, and a mouthful of cool fresh water, madame.”\n\nMadame complied with a polite air.\n\n“Marvellous cognac this, madame!”\n\nIt was the first time it had ever been so complimented, and Madame Defarge knew enough of its antecedents to know better. She said, however, that the cognac was flattered, and took up her knitting. The visitor watched her fingers for a few moments, and took the opportunity of observing the place in general.\n\n“You knit with great skill, madame.”\n\n“I am accustomed to it.”\n\n“A pretty pattern too!”\n\n“You think so?” said madame, looking at him with a smile.\n\n“Decidedly. May one ask what it is for?”\n\n“Pastime,” said madame, still looking at him with a smile while her fingers moved nimbly.\n\n“Not for use?”\n\n“That depends. I may find a use for it one day. If I do--Well,” said madame, drawing a breath and nodding her head with a stern kind of coquetry, “I’ll use it!”\n\nIt was remarkable; but, the taste of Saint Antoine seemed to be decidedly opposed to a rose on the head-dress of Madame Defarge. Two men had entered separately, and had been about to order drink, when, catching sight of that novelty, they faltered, made a pretence of looking about as if for some friend who was not there, and went away. Nor, of those who had been there when this visitor entered, was there one left. They had all dropped off. The spy had kept his eyes open, but had been able to detect no sign. They had lounged away in a poverty-stricken, purposeless, accidental manner, quite natural and unimpeachable.\n\n“John,” thought madame, checking off her work as her fingers knitted, and her eyes looked at the stranger. “Stay long enough, and I shall knit ‘BARSAD’ before you go.”\n\n“You have a husband, madame?”\n\n“I have.”\n\n“Children?”\n\n“No children.”\n\n“Business seems bad?”\n\n“Business is very bad; the people are so poor.”\n\n“Ah, the unfortunate, miserable people! So oppressed, too--as you say.”\n\n“As you say,” madame retorted, correcting him, and deftly knitting an extra something into his name that boded him no good.\n\n“Pardon me; certainly it was I who said so, but you naturally think so. Of course.”\n\n“I think?” returned madame, in a high voice. “I and my husband have enough to do to keep this wine-shop open, without thinking. All we think, here, is how to live. That is the subject we think of, and it gives us, from morning to night, enough to think about, without embarrassing our heads concerning others. I think for others? No, no.”\n\nThe spy, who was there to pick up any crumbs he could find or make, did not allow his baffled state to express itself in his sinister face; but, stood with an air of gossiping gallantry, leaning his elbow on Madame Defarge’s little counter, and occasionally sipping his cognac.\n\n“A bad business this, madame, of Gaspard’s execution. Ah! the poor Gaspard!” With a sigh of great compassion.\n\n“My faith!” returned madame, coolly and lightly, “if people use knives for such purposes, they have to pay for it. He knew beforehand what the price of his luxury was; he has paid the price.”\n\n“I believe,” said the spy, dropping his soft voice to a tone that invited confidence, and expressing an injured revolutionary susceptibility in every muscle of his wicked face: “I believe there is much compassion and anger in this neighbourhood, touching the poor fellow? Between ourselves.”\n\n“Is there?” asked madame, vacantly.\n\n“Is there not?”\n\n“--Here is my husband!” said Madame Defarge.\n\nAs the keeper of the wine-shop entered at the door, the spy saluted him by touching his hat, and saying, with an engaging smile, “Good day, Jacques!” Defarge stopped short, and stared at him.\n\n“Good day, Jacques!” the spy repeated; with not quite so much confidence, or quite so easy a smile under the stare.\n\n“You deceive yourself, monsieur,” returned the keeper of the wine-shop. “You mistake me for another. That is not my name. I am Ernest Defarge.”\n\n“It is all the same,” said the spy, airily, but discomfited too: “good day!”\n\n“Good day!” answered Defarge, drily.\n\n“I was saying to madame, with whom I had the pleasure of chatting when you entered, that they tell me there is--and no wonder!--much sympathy and anger in Saint Antoine, touching the unhappy fate of poor Gaspard.”\n\n“No one has told me so,” said Defarge, shaking his head. “I know nothing of it.”\n\nHaving said it, he passed behind the little counter, and stood with his hand on the back of his wife’s chair, looking over that barrier at the person to whom they were both opposed, and whom either of them would have shot with the greatest satisfaction.\n\nThe spy, well used to his business, did not change his unconscious attitude, but drained his little glass of cognac, took a sip of fresh water, and asked for another glass of cognac. Madame Defarge poured it out for him, took to her knitting again, and hummed a little song over it.\n\n“You seem to know this quarter well; that is to say, better than I do?” observed Defarge.\n\n“Not at all, but I hope to know it better. I am so profoundly interested in its miserable inhabitants.”\n\n“Hah!” muttered Defarge.\n\n“The pleasure of conversing with you, Monsieur Defarge, recalls to me,” pursued the spy, “that I have the honour of cherishing some interesting associations with your name.”\n\n“Indeed!” said Defarge, with much indifference.\n\n“Yes, indeed. When Doctor Manette was released, you, his old domestic, had the charge of him, I know. He was delivered to you. You see I am informed of the circumstances?”\n\n“Such is the fact, certainly,” said Defarge. He had had it conveyed to him, in an accidental touch of his wife’s elbow as she knitted and warbled, that he would do best to answer, but always with brevity.\n\n“It was to you,” said the spy, “that his daughter came; and it was from your care that his daughter took him, accompanied by a neat brown monsieur; how is he called?--in a little wig--Lorry--of the bank of Tellson and Company--over to England.”\n\n“Such is the fact,” repeated Defarge.\n\n“Very interesting remembrances!” said the spy. “I have known Doctor Manette and his daughter, in England.”\n\n“Yes?” said Defarge.\n\n“You don’t hear much about them now?” said the spy.\n\n“No,” said Defarge.\n\n“In effect,” madame struck in, looking up from her work and her little song, “we never hear about them. We received the news of their safe arrival, and perhaps another letter, or perhaps two; but, since then, they have gradually taken their road in life--we, ours--and we have held no correspondence.”\n\n“Perfectly so, madame,” replied the spy. “She is going to be married.”\n\n“Going?” echoed madame. “She was pretty enough to have been married long ago. You English are cold, it seems to me.”\n\n“Oh! You know I am English.”\n\n“I perceive your tongue is,” returned madame; “and what the tongue is, I suppose the man is.”\n\nHe did not take the identification as a compliment; but he made the best of it, and turned it off with a laugh. After sipping his cognac to the end, he added:\n\n“Yes, Miss Manette is going to be married. But not to an Englishman; to one who, like herself, is French by birth. And speaking of Gaspard (ah, poor Gaspard! It was cruel, cruel!), it is a curious thing that she is going to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was exalted to that height of so many feet; in other words, the present Marquis. But he lives unknown in England, he is no Marquis there; he is Mr. Charles Darnay. D’Aulnais is the name of his mother’s family.”\n\nMadame Defarge knitted steadily, but the intelligence had a palpable effect upon her husband. Do what he would, behind the little counter, as to the striking of a light and the lighting of his pipe, he was troubled, and his hand was not trustworthy. The spy would have been no spy if he had failed to see it, or to record it in his mind.\n\nHaving made, at least, this one hit, whatever it might prove to be worth, and no customers coming in to help him to any other, Mr. Barsad paid for what he had drunk, and took his leave: taking occasion to say, in a genteel manner, before he departed, that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again. For some minutes after he had emerged into the outer presence of Saint Antoine, the husband and wife remained exactly as he had left them, lest he should come back.\n\n“Can it be true,” said Defarge, in a low voice, looking down at his wife as he stood smoking with his hand on the back of her chair: “what he has said of Ma’amselle Manette?”\n\n“As he has said it,” returned madame, lifting her eyebrows a little, “it is probably false. But it may be true.”\n\n“If it is--” Defarge began, and stopped.\n\n“If it is?” repeated his wife.\n\n“--And if it does come, while we live to see it triumph--I hope, for her sake, Destiny will keep her husband out of France.”\n\n“Her husband’s destiny,” said Madame Defarge, with her usual composure, “will take him where he is to go, and will lead him to the end that is to end him. That is all I know.”\n\n“But it is very strange--now, at least, is it not very strange”--said Defarge, rather pleading with his wife to induce her to admit it, “that, after all our sympathy for Monsieur her father, and herself, her husband’s name should be proscribed under your hand at this moment, by the side of that infernal dog’s who has just left us?”\n\n“Stranger things than that will happen when it does come,” answered madame. “I have them both here, of a certainty; and they are both here for their merits; that is enough.”\n\nShe rolled up her knitting when she had said those words, and presently took the rose out of the handkerchief that was wound about her head. Either Saint Antoine had an instinctive sense that the objectionable decoration was gone, or Saint Antoine was on the watch for its disappearance; howbeit, the Saint took courage to lounge in, very shortly afterwards, and the wine-shop recovered its habitual aspect.\n\nIn the evening, at which season of all others Saint Antoine turned himself inside out, and sat on door-steps and window-ledges, and came to the corners of vile streets and courts, for a breath of air, Madame Defarge with her work in her hand was accustomed to pass from place to place and from group to group: a Missionary--there were many like her--such as the world will do well never to breed again. All the women knitted. They knitted worthless things; but, the mechanical work was a mechanical substitute for eating and drinking; the hands moved for the jaws and the digestive apparatus: if the bony fingers had been still, the stomachs would have been more famine-pinched.\n\nBut, as the fingers went, the eyes went, and the thoughts. And as Madame Defarge moved on from group to group, all three went quicker and fiercer among every little knot of women that she had spoken with, and left behind.\n\nHer husband smoked at his door, looking after her with admiration. “A great woman,” said he, “a strong woman, a grand woman, a frightfully grand woman!”\n\nDarkness closed around, and then came the ringing of church bells and the distant beating of the military drums in the Palace Courtyard, as the women sat knitting, knitting. Darkness encompassed them. Another darkness was closing in as surely, when the church bells, then ringing pleasantly in many an airy steeple over France, should be melted into thundering cannon; when the military drums should be beating to drown a wretched voice, that night all potent as the voice of Power and Plenty, Freedom and Life. So much was closing in about the women who sat knitting, knitting, that they their very selves were closing in around a structure yet unbuilt, where they were to sit knitting, knitting, counting dropping heads.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. One Night\n\nNever did the sun go down with a brighter glory on the quiet corner in Soho, than one memorable evening when the Doctor and his daughter sat under the plane-tree together. Never did the moon rise with a milder radiance over great London, than on that night when it found them still seated under the tree, and shone upon their faces through its leaves.\n\nLucie was to be married to-morrow. She had reserved this last evening for her father, and they sat alone under the plane-tree.\n\n“You are happy, my dear father?”\n\n“Quite, my child.”\n\nThey had said little, though they had been there a long time. When it was yet light enough to work and read, she had neither engaged herself in her usual work, nor had she read to him. She had employed herself in both ways, at his side under the tree, many and many a time; but, this time was not quite like any other, and nothing could make it so.\n\n“And I am very happy to-night, dear father. I am deeply happy in the love that Heaven has so blessed--my love for Charles, and Charles’s love for me. But, if my life were not to be still consecrated to you, or if my marriage were so arranged as that it would part us, even by the length of a few of these streets, I should be more unhappy and self-reproachful now than I can tell you. Even as it is--”\n\nEven as it was, she could not command her voice.\n\nIn the sad moonlight, she clasped him by the neck, and laid her face upon his breast. In the moonlight which is always sad, as the light of the sun itself is--as the light called human life is--at its coming and its going.\n\n“Dearest dear! Can you tell me, this last time, that you feel quite, quite sure, no new affections of mine, and no new duties of mine, will ever interpose between us? I know it well, but do you know it? In your own heart, do you feel quite certain?”\n\nHer father answered, with a cheerful firmness of conviction he could scarcely have assumed, “Quite sure, my darling! More than that,” he added, as he tenderly kissed her: “my future is far brighter, Lucie, seen through your marriage, than it could have been--nay, than it ever was--without it.”\n\n“If I could hope that, my father!--”\n\n“Believe it, love! Indeed it is so. Consider how natural and how plain it is, my dear, that it should be so. You, devoted and young, cannot fully appreciate the anxiety I have felt that your life should not be wasted--”\n\nShe moved her hand towards his lips, but he took it in his, and repeated the word.\n\n“--wasted, my child--should not be wasted, struck aside from the natural order of things--for my sake. Your unselfishness cannot entirely comprehend how much my mind has gone on this; but, only ask yourself, how could my happiness be perfect, while yours was incomplete?”\n\n“If I had never seen Charles, my father, I should have been quite happy with you.”\n\nHe smiled at her unconscious admission that she would have been unhappy without Charles, having seen him; and replied:\n\n“My child, you did see him, and it is Charles. If it had not been Charles, it would have been another. Or, if it had been no other, I should have been the cause, and then the dark part of my life would have cast its shadow beyond myself, and would have fallen on you.”\n\nIt was the first time, except at the trial, of her ever hearing him refer to the period of his suffering. It gave her a strange and new sensation while his words were in her ears; and she remembered it long afterwards.\n\n“See!” said the Doctor of Beauvais, raising his hand towards the moon. “I have looked at her from my prison-window, when I could not bear her light. I have looked at her when it has been such torture to me to think of her shining upon what I had lost, that I have beaten my head against my prison-walls. I have looked at her, in a state so dull and lethargic, that I have thought of nothing but the number of horizontal lines I could draw across her at the full, and the number of perpendicular lines with which I could intersect them.” He added in his inward and pondering manner, as he looked at the moon, “It was twenty either way, I remember, and the twentieth was difficult to squeeze in.”\n\nThe strange thrill with which she heard him go back to that time, deepened as he dwelt upon it; but, there was nothing to shock her in the manner of his reference. He only seemed to contrast his present cheerfulness and felicity with the dire endurance that was over.\n\n“I have looked at her, speculating thousands of times upon the unborn child from whom I had been rent. Whether it was alive. Whether it had been born alive, or the poor mother’s shock had killed it. Whether it was a son who would some day avenge his father. (There was a time in my imprisonment, when my desire for vengeance was unbearable.) Whether it was a son who would never know his father’s story; who might even live to weigh the possibility of his father’s having disappeared of his own will and act. Whether it was a daughter who would grow to be a woman.”\n\nShe drew closer to him, and kissed his cheek and his hand.\n\n“I have pictured my daughter, to myself, as perfectly forgetful of me--rather, altogether ignorant of me, and unconscious of me. I have cast up the years of her age, year after year. I have seen her married to a man who knew nothing of my fate. I have altogether perished from the remembrance of the living, and in the next generation my place was a blank.”\n\n“My father! Even to hear that you had such thoughts of a daughter who never existed, strikes to my heart as if I had been that child.”\n\n“You, Lucie? It is out of the Consolation and restoration you have brought to me, that these remembrances arise, and pass between us and the moon on this last night.--What did I say just now?”\n\n“She knew nothing of you. She cared nothing for you.”\n\n“So! But on other moonlight nights, when the sadness and the silence have touched me in a different way--have affected me with something as like a sorrowful sense of peace, as any emotion that had pain for its foundations could--I have imagined her as coming to me in my cell, and leading me out into the freedom beyond the fortress. I have seen her image in the moonlight often, as I now see you; except that I never held her in my arms; it stood between the little grated window and the door. But, you understand that that was not the child I am speaking of?”\n\n“The figure was not; the--the--image; the fancy?”\n\n“No. That was another thing. It stood before my disturbed sense of sight, but it never moved. The phantom that my mind pursued, was another and more real child. Of her outward appearance I know no more than that she was like her mother. The other had that likeness too--as you have--but was not the same. Can you follow me, Lucie? Hardly, I think? I doubt you must have been a solitary prisoner to understand these perplexed distinctions.”\n\nHis collected and calm manner could not prevent her blood from running cold, as he thus tried to anatomise his old condition.\n\n“In that more peaceful state, I have imagined her, in the moonlight, coming to me and taking me out to show me that the home of her married life was full of her loving remembrance of her lost father. My picture was in her room, and I was in her prayers. Her life was active, cheerful, useful; but my poor history pervaded it all.”\n\n“I was that child, my father, I was not half so good, but in my love that was I.”\n\n“And she showed me her children,” said the Doctor of Beauvais, “and they had heard of me, and had been taught to pity me. When they passed a prison of the State, they kept far from its frowning walls, and looked up at its bars, and spoke in whispers. She could never deliver me; I imagined that she always brought me back after showing me such things. But then, blessed with the relief of tears, I fell upon my knees, and blessed her.”\n\n“I am that child, I hope, my father. O my dear, my dear, will you bless me as fervently to-morrow?”\n\n“Lucie, I recall these old troubles in the reason that I have to-night for loving you better than words can tell, and thanking God for my great happiness. My thoughts, when they were wildest, never rose near the happiness that I have known with you, and that we have before us.”\n\nHe embraced her, solemnly commended her to Heaven, and humbly thanked Heaven for having bestowed her on him. By-and-bye, they went into the house.\n\nThere was no one bidden to the marriage but Mr. Lorry; there was even to be no bridesmaid but the gaunt Miss Pross. The marriage was to make no change in their place of residence; they had been able to extend it, by taking to themselves the upper rooms formerly belonging to the apocryphal invisible lodger, and they desired nothing more.\n\nDoctor Manette was very cheerful at the little supper. They were only three at table, and Miss Pross made the third. He regretted that Charles was not there; was more than half disposed to object to the loving little plot that kept him away; and drank to him affectionately.\n\nSo, the time came for him to bid Lucie good night, and they separated. But, in the stillness of the third hour of the morning, Lucie came downstairs again, and stole into his room; not free from unshaped fears, beforehand.\n\nAll things, however, were in their places; all was quiet; and he lay asleep, his white hair picturesque on the untroubled pillow, and his hands lying quiet on the coverlet. She put her needless candle in the shadow at a distance, crept up to his bed, and put her lips to his; then, leaned over him, and looked at him.\n\nInto his handsome face, the bitter waters of captivity had worn; but, he covered up their tracks with a determination so strong, that he held the mastery of them even in his sleep. A more remarkable face in its quiet, resolute, and guarded struggle with an unseen assailant, was not to be beheld in all the wide dominions of sleep, that night.\n\nShe timidly laid her hand on his dear breast, and put up a prayer that she might ever be as true to him as her love aspired to be, and as his sorrows deserved. Then, she withdrew her hand, and kissed his lips once more, and went away. So, the sunrise came, and the shadows of the leaves of the plane-tree moved upon his face, as softly as her lips had moved in praying for him.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. Nine Days\n\nThe marriage-day was shining brightly, and they were ready outside the closed door of the Doctor’s room, where he was speaking with Charles Darnay. They were ready to go to church; the beautiful bride, Mr. Lorry, and Miss Pross--to whom the event, through a gradual process of reconcilement to the inevitable, would have been one of absolute bliss, but for the yet lingering consideration that her brother Solomon should have been the bridegroom.\n\n“And so,” said Mr. Lorry, who could not sufficiently admire the bride, and who had been moving round her to take in every point of her quiet, pretty dress; “and so it was for this, my sweet Lucie, that I brought you across the Channel, such a baby! Lord bless me! How little I thought what I was doing! How lightly I valued the obligation I was conferring on my friend Mr. Charles!”\n\n“You didn’t mean it,” remarked the matter-of-fact Miss Pross, “and therefore how could you know it? Nonsense!”\n\n“Really? Well; but don’t cry,” said the gentle Mr. Lorry.\n\n“I am not crying,” said Miss Pross; “you are.”\n\n“I, my Pross?” (By this time, Mr. Lorry dared to be pleasant with her, on occasion.)\n\n“You were, just now; I saw you do it, and I don’t wonder at it. Such a present of plate as you have made ’em, is enough to bring tears into anybody’s eyes. There’s not a fork or a spoon in the collection,” said Miss Pross, “that I didn’t cry over, last night after the box came, till I couldn’t see it.”\n\n“I am highly gratified,” said Mr. Lorry, “though, upon my honour, I had no intention of rendering those trifling articles of remembrance invisible to any one. Dear me! This is an occasion that makes a man speculate on all he has lost. Dear, dear, dear! To think that there might have been a Mrs. Lorry, any time these fifty years almost!”\n\n“Not at all!” From Miss Pross.\n\n“You think there never might have been a Mrs. Lorry?” asked the gentleman of that name.\n\n“Pooh!” rejoined Miss Pross; “you were a bachelor in your cradle.”\n\n“Well!” observed Mr. Lorry, beamingly adjusting his little wig, “that seems probable, too.”\n\n“And you were cut out for a bachelor,” pursued Miss Pross, “before you were put in your cradle.”\n\n“Then, I think,” said Mr. Lorry, “that I was very unhandsomely dealt with, and that I ought to have had a voice in the selection of my pattern. Enough! Now, my dear Lucie,” drawing his arm soothingly round her waist, “I hear them moving in the next room, and Miss Pross and I, as two formal folks of business, are anxious not to lose the final opportunity of saying something to you that you wish to hear. You leave your good father, my dear, in hands as earnest and as loving as your own; he shall be taken every conceivable care of; during the next fortnight, while you are in Warwickshire and thereabouts, even Tellson’s shall go to the wall (comparatively speaking) before him. And when, at the fortnight’s end, he comes to join you and your beloved husband, on your other fortnight’s trip in Wales, you shall say that we have sent him to you in the best health and in the happiest frame. Now, I hear Somebody’s step coming to the door. Let me kiss my dear girl with an old-fashioned bachelor blessing, before Somebody comes to claim his own.”\n\nFor a moment, he held the fair face from him to look at the well-remembered expression on the forehead, and then laid the bright golden hair against his little brown wig, with a genuine tenderness and delicacy which, if such things be old-fashioned, were as old as Adam.\n\nThe door of the Doctor’s room opened, and he came out with Charles Darnay. He was so deadly pale--which had not been the case when they went in together--that no vestige of colour was to be seen in his face. But, in the composure of his manner he was unaltered, except that to the shrewd glance of Mr. Lorry it disclosed some shadowy indication that the old air of avoidance and dread had lately passed over him, like a cold wind.\n\nHe gave his arm to his daughter, and took her down-stairs to the chariot which Mr. Lorry had hired in honour of the day. The rest followed in another carriage, and soon, in a neighbouring church, where no strange eyes looked on, Charles Darnay and Lucie Manette were happily married.\n\nBesides the glancing tears that shone among the smiles of the little group when it was done, some diamonds, very bright and sparkling, glanced on the bride’s hand, which were newly released from the dark obscurity of one of Mr. Lorry’s pockets. They returned home to breakfast, and all went well, and in due course the golden hair that had mingled with the poor shoemaker’s white locks in the Paris garret, were mingled with them again in the morning sunlight, on the threshold of the door at parting.\n\nIt was a hard parting, though it was not for long. But her father cheered her, and said at last, gently disengaging himself from her enfolding arms, “Take her, Charles! She is yours!”\n\nAnd her agitated hand waved to them from a chaise window, and she was gone.\n\nThe corner being out of the way of the idle and curious, and the preparations having been very simple and few, the Doctor, Mr. Lorry, and Miss Pross, were left quite alone. It was when they turned into the welcome shade of the cool old hall, that Mr. Lorry observed a great change to have come over the Doctor; as if the golden arm uplifted there, had struck him a poisoned blow.\n\nHe had naturally repressed much, and some revulsion might have been expected in him when the occasion for repression was gone. But, it was the old scared lost look that troubled Mr. Lorry; and through his absent manner of clasping his head and drearily wandering away into his own room when they got up-stairs, Mr. Lorry was reminded of Defarge the wine-shop keeper, and the starlight ride.\n\n“I think,” he whispered to Miss Pross, after anxious consideration, “I think we had best not speak to him just now, or at all disturb him. I must look in at Tellson’s; so I will go there at once and come back presently. Then, we will take him a ride into the country, and dine there, and all will be well.”\n\nIt was easier for Mr. Lorry to look in at Tellson’s, than to look out of Tellson’s. He was detained two hours. When he came back, he ascended the old staircase alone, having asked no question of the servant; going thus into the Doctor’s rooms, he was stopped by a low sound of knocking.\n\n“Good God!” he said, with a start. “What’s that?”\n\nMiss Pross, with a terrified face, was at his ear. “O me, O me! All is lost!” cried she, wringing her hands. “What is to be told to Ladybird? He doesn’t know me, and is making shoes!”\n\nMr. Lorry said what he could to calm her, and went himself into the Doctor’s room. The bench was turned towards the light, as it had been when he had seen the shoemaker at his work before, and his head was bent down, and he was very busy.\n\n“Doctor Manette. My dear friend, Doctor Manette!”\n\nThe Doctor looked at him for a moment--half inquiringly, half as if he were angry at being spoken to--and bent over his work again.\n\nHe had laid aside his coat and waistcoat; his shirt was open at the throat, as it used to be when he did that work; and even the old haggard, faded surface of face had come back to him. He worked hard--impatiently--as if in some sense of having been interrupted.\n\nMr. Lorry glanced at the work in his hand, and observed that it was a shoe of the old size and shape. He took up another that was lying by him, and asked what it was.\n\n“A young lady’s walking shoe,” he muttered, without looking up. “It ought to have been finished long ago. Let it be.”\n\n“But, Doctor Manette. Look at me!”\n\nHe obeyed, in the old mechanically submissive manner, without pausing in his work.\n\n“You know me, my dear friend? Think again. This is not your proper occupation. Think, dear friend!”\n\nNothing would induce him to speak more. He looked up, for an instant at a time, when he was requested to do so; but, no persuasion would extract a word from him. He worked, and worked, and worked, in silence, and words fell on him as they would have fallen on an echoless wall, or on the air. The only ray of hope that Mr. Lorry could discover, was, that he sometimes furtively looked up without being asked. In that, there seemed a faint expression of curiosity or perplexity--as though he were trying to reconcile some doubts in his mind.\n\nTwo things at once impressed themselves on Mr. Lorry, as important above all others; the first, that this must be kept secret from Lucie; the second, that it must be kept secret from all who knew him. In conjunction with Miss Pross, he took immediate steps towards the latter precaution, by giving out that the Doctor was not well, and required a few days of complete rest. In aid of the kind deception to be practised on his daughter, Miss Pross was to write, describing his having been called away professionally, and referring to an imaginary letter of two or three hurried lines in his own hand, represented to have been addressed to her by the same post.\n\nThese measures, advisable to be taken in any case, Mr. Lorry took in the hope of his coming to himself. If that should happen soon, he kept another course in reserve; which was, to have a certain opinion that he thought the best, on the Doctor’s case.\n\nIn the hope of his recovery, and of resort to this third course being thereby rendered practicable, Mr. Lorry resolved to watch him attentively, with as little appearance as possible of doing so. He therefore made arrangements to absent himself from Tellson’s for the first time in his life, and took his post by the window in the same room.\n\nHe was not long in discovering that it was worse than useless to speak to him, since, on being pressed, he became worried. He abandoned that attempt on the first day, and resolved merely to keep himself always before him, as a silent protest against the delusion into which he had fallen, or was falling. He remained, therefore, in his seat near the window, reading and writing, and expressing in as many pleasant and natural ways as he could think of, that it was a free place.\n\nDoctor Manette took what was given him to eat and drink, and worked on, that first day, until it was too dark to see--worked on, half an hour after Mr. Lorry could not have seen, for his life, to read or write. When he put his tools aside as useless, until morning, Mr. Lorry rose and said to him:\n\n“Will you go out?”\n\nHe looked down at the floor on either side of him in the old manner, looked up in the old manner, and repeated in the old low voice:\n\n“Out?”\n\n“Yes; for a walk with me. Why not?”\n\nHe made no effort to say why not, and said not a word more. But, Mr. Lorry thought he saw, as he leaned forward on his bench in the dusk, with his elbows on his knees and his head in his hands, that he was in some misty way asking himself, “Why not?” The sagacity of the man of business perceived an advantage here, and determined to hold it.\n\nMiss Pross and he divided the night into two watches, and observed him at intervals from the adjoining room. He paced up and down for a long time before he lay down; but, when he did finally lay himself down, he fell asleep. In the morning, he was up betimes, and went straight to his bench and to work.\n\nOn this second day, Mr. Lorry saluted him cheerfully by his name, and spoke to him on topics that had been of late familiar to them. He returned no reply, but it was evident that he heard what was said, and that he thought about it, however confusedly. This encouraged Mr. Lorry to have Miss Pross in with her work, several times during the day; at those times, they quietly spoke of Lucie, and of her father then present, precisely in the usual manner, and as if there were nothing amiss. This was done without any demonstrative accompaniment, not long enough, or often enough to harass him; and it lightened Mr. Lorry’s friendly heart to believe that he looked up oftener, and that he appeared to be stirred by some perception of inconsistencies surrounding him.\n\nWhen it fell dark again, Mr. Lorry asked him as before:\n\n“Dear Doctor, will you go out?”\n\nAs before, he repeated, “Out?”\n\n“Yes; for a walk with me. Why not?”\n\nThis time, Mr. Lorry feigned to go out when he could extract no answer from him, and, after remaining absent for an hour, returned. In the meanwhile, the Doctor had removed to the seat in the window, and had sat there looking down at the plane-tree; but, on Mr. Lorry’s return, he slipped away to his bench.\n\nThe time went very slowly on, and Mr. Lorry’s hope darkened, and his heart grew heavier again, and grew yet heavier and heavier every day. The third day came and went, the fourth, the fifth. Five days, six days, seven days, eight days, nine days.\n\nWith a hope ever darkening, and with a heart always growing heavier and heavier, Mr. Lorry passed through this anxious time. The secret was well kept, and Lucie was unconscious and happy; but he could not fail to observe that the shoemaker, whose hand had been a little out at first, was growing dreadfully skilful, and that he had never been so intent on his work, and that his hands had never been so nimble and expert, as in the dusk of the ninth evening.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. An Opinion\n\nWorn out by anxious watching, Mr. Lorry fell asleep at his post. On the tenth morning of his suspense, he was startled by the shining of the sun into the room where a heavy slumber had overtaken him when it was dark night.\n\nHe rubbed his eyes and roused himself; but he doubted, when he had done so, whether he was not still asleep. For, going to the door of the Doctor’s room and looking in, he perceived that the shoemaker’s bench and tools were put aside again, and that the Doctor himself sat reading at the window. He was in his usual morning dress, and his face (which Mr. Lorry could distinctly see), though still very pale, was calmly studious and attentive.\n\nEven when he had satisfied himself that he was awake, Mr. Lorry felt giddily uncertain for some few moments whether the late shoemaking might not be a disturbed dream of his own; for, did not his eyes show him his friend before him in his accustomed clothing and aspect, and employed as usual; and was there any sign within their range, that the change of which he had so strong an impression had actually happened?\n\nIt was but the inquiry of his first confusion and astonishment, the answer being obvious. If the impression were not produced by a real corresponding and sufficient cause, how came he, Jarvis Lorry, there? How came he to have fallen asleep, in his clothes, on the sofa in Doctor Manette’s consulting-room, and to be debating these points outside the Doctor’s bedroom door in the early morning?\n\nWithin a few minutes, Miss Pross stood whispering at his side. If he had had any particle of doubt left, her talk would of necessity have resolved it; but he was by that time clear-headed, and had none. He advised that they should let the time go by until the regular breakfast-hour, and should then meet the Doctor as if nothing unusual had occurred. If he appeared to be in his customary state of mind, Mr. Lorry would then cautiously proceed to seek direction and guidance from the opinion he had been, in his anxiety, so anxious to obtain.\n\nMiss Pross, submitting herself to his judgment, the scheme was worked out with care. Having abundance of time for his usual methodical toilette, Mr. Lorry presented himself at the breakfast-hour in his usual white linen, and with his usual neat leg. The Doctor was summoned in the usual way, and came to breakfast.\n\nSo far as it was possible to comprehend him without overstepping those delicate and gradual approaches which Mr. Lorry felt to be the only safe advance, he at first supposed that his daughter’s marriage had taken place yesterday. An incidental allusion, purposely thrown out, to the day of the week, and the day of the month, set him thinking and counting, and evidently made him uneasy. In all other respects, however, he was so composedly himself, that Mr. Lorry determined to have the aid he sought. And that aid was his own.\n\nTherefore, when the breakfast was done and cleared away, and he and the Doctor were left together, Mr. Lorry said, feelingly:\n\n“My dear Manette, I am anxious to have your opinion, in confidence, on a very curious case in which I am deeply interested; that is to say, it is very curious to me; perhaps, to your better information it may be less so.”\n\nGlancing at his hands, which were discoloured by his late work, the Doctor looked troubled, and listened attentively. He had already glanced at his hands more than once.\n\n“Doctor Manette,” said Mr. Lorry, touching him affectionately on the arm, “the case is the case of a particularly dear friend of mine. Pray give your mind to it, and advise me well for his sake--and above all, for his daughter’s--his daughter’s, my dear Manette.”\n\n“If I understand,” said the Doctor, in a subdued tone, “some mental shock--?”\n\n“Yes!”\n“Be explicit,” said the Doctor. “Spare no detail.”\n\nMr. Lorry saw that they understood one another, and proceeded.\n\n“My dear Manette, it is the case of an old and a prolonged shock, of great acuteness and severity to the affections, the feelings, the--the--as you express it--the mind. The mind. It is the case of a shock under which the sufferer was borne down, one cannot say for how long, because I believe he cannot calculate the time himself, and there are no other means of getting at it. It is the case of a shock from which the sufferer recovered, by a process that he cannot trace himself--as I once heard him publicly relate in a striking manner. It is the case of a shock from which he has recovered, so completely, as to be a highly intelligent man, capable of close application of mind, and great exertion of body, and of constantly making fresh additions to his stock of knowledge, which was already very large. But, unfortunately, there has been,” he paused and took a deep breath--“a slight relapse.”\n\nThe Doctor, in a low voice, asked, “Of how long duration?”\n\n“Nine days and nights.”\n\n“How did it show itself? I infer,” glancing at his hands again, “in the resumption of some old pursuit connected with the shock?”\n\n“That is the fact.”\n\n“Now, did you ever see him,” asked the Doctor, distinctly and collectedly, though in the same low voice, “engaged in that pursuit originally?”\n“Once.”\n\n“And when the relapse fell on him, was he in most respects--or in all respects--as he was then?”\n\n“I think in all respects.”\n\n“You spoke of his daughter. Does his daughter know of the relapse?”\n\n“No. It has been kept from her, and I hope will always be kept from her. It is known only to myself, and to one other who may be trusted.”\n\nThe Doctor grasped his hand, and murmured, “That was very kind. That was very thoughtful!” Mr. Lorry grasped his hand in return, and neither of the two spoke for a little while.\n\n“Now, my dear Manette,” said Mr. Lorry, at length, in his most considerate and most affectionate way, “I am a mere man of business, and unfit to cope with such intricate and difficult matters. I do not possess the kind of information necessary; I do not possess the kind of intelligence; I want guiding. There is no man in this world on whom I could so rely for right guidance, as on you. Tell me, how does this relapse come about? Is there danger of another? Could a repetition of it be prevented? How should a repetition of it be treated? How does it come about at all? What can I do for my friend? No man ever can have been more desirous in his heart to serve a friend, than I am to serve mine, if I knew how.\n\n“But I don’t know how to originate, in such a case. If your sagacity, knowledge, and experience, could put me on the right track, I might be able to do so much; unenlightened and undirected, I can do so little. Pray discuss it with me; pray enable me to see it a little more clearly, and teach me how to be a little more useful.”\n\nDoctor Manette sat meditating after these earnest words were spoken, and Mr. Lorry did not press him.\n\n“I think it probable,” said the Doctor, breaking silence with an effort, “that the relapse you have described, my dear friend, was not quite unforeseen by its subject.”\n\n“Was it dreaded by him?” Mr. Lorry ventured to ask.\n\n“Very much.” He said it with an involuntary shudder.\n“You have no idea how such an apprehension weighs on the sufferer’s mind, and how difficult--how almost impossible--it is, for him to force himself to utter a word upon the topic that oppresses him.”\n\n“Would he,” asked Mr. Lorry, “be sensibly relieved if he could prevail upon himself to impart that secret brooding to any one, when it is on him?”\n\n“I think so. But it is, as I have told you, next to impossible. I even believe it--in some cases--to be quite impossible.”\n\n“Now,” said Mr. Lorry, gently laying his hand on the Doctor’s arm again, after a short silence on both sides, “to what would you refer this attack?”\n\n“I believe,” returned Doctor Manette, “that there had been a strong and extraordinary revival of the train of thought and remembrance that was the first cause of the malady. Some intense associations of a most distressing nature were vividly recalled, I think. It is probable that there had long been a dread lurking in his mind, that those associations would be recalled--say, under certain circumstances--say, on a particular occasion. He tried to prepare himself in vain; perhaps the effort to prepare himself made him less able to bear it.”\n\n“Would he remember what took place in the relapse?” asked Mr. Lorry, with natural hesitation.\n\nThe Doctor looked desolately round the room, shook his head, and answered, in a low voice, “Not at all.”\n\n“Now, as to the future,” hinted Mr. Lorry.\n\n“As to the future,” said the Doctor, recovering firmness, “I should have great hope. As it pleased Heaven in its mercy to restore him so soon, I should have great hope. He, yielding under the pressure of a complicated something, long dreaded and long vaguely foreseen and contended against, and recovering after the cloud had burst and passed, I should hope that the worst was over.”\n\n“Well, well! That’s good comfort. I am thankful!” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“I am thankful!” repeated the Doctor, bending his head with reverence.\n\n“There are two other points,” said Mr. Lorry, “on which I am anxious to be instructed. I may go on?”\n\n“You cannot do your friend a better service.” The Doctor gave him his hand.\n\n“To the first, then. He is of a studious habit, and unusually energetic; he applies himself with great ardour to the acquisition of professional knowledge, to the conducting of experiments, to many things. Now, does he do too much?”\n\n“I think not. It may be the character of his mind, to be always in singular need of occupation. That may be, in part, natural to it; in part, the result of affliction. The less it was occupied with healthy things, the more it would be in danger of turning in the unhealthy direction. He may have observed himself, and made the discovery.”\n\n“You are sure that he is not under too great a strain?”\n\n“I think I am quite sure of it.”\n\n“My dear Manette, if he were overworked now--”\n\n“My dear Lorry, I doubt if that could easily be. There has been a violent stress in one direction, and it needs a counterweight.”\n\n“Excuse me, as a persistent man of business. Assuming for a moment, that he was overworked; it would show itself in some renewal of this disorder?”\n\n“I do not think so. I do not think,” said Doctor Manette with the firmness of self-conviction, “that anything but the one train of association would renew it. I think that, henceforth, nothing but some extraordinary jarring of that chord could renew it. After what has happened, and after his recovery, I find it difficult to imagine any such violent sounding of that string again. I trust, and I almost believe, that the circumstances likely to renew it are exhausted.”\n\nHe spoke with the diffidence of a man who knew how slight a thing would overset the delicate organisation of the mind, and yet with the confidence of a man who had slowly won his assurance out of personal endurance and distress. It was not for his friend to abate that confidence. He professed himself more relieved and encouraged than he really was, and approached his second and last point. He felt it to be the most difficult of all; but, remembering his old Sunday morning conversation with Miss Pross, and remembering what he had seen in the last nine days, he knew that he must face it.\n\n“The occupation resumed under the influence of this passing affliction so happily recovered from,” said Mr. Lorry, clearing his throat, “we will call--Blacksmith’s work, Blacksmith’s work. We will say, to put a case and for the sake of illustration, that he had been used, in his bad time, to work at a little forge. We will say that he was unexpectedly found at his forge again. Is it not a pity that he should keep it by him?”\n\nThe Doctor shaded his forehead with his hand, and beat his foot nervously on the ground.\n\n“He has always kept it by him,” said Mr. Lorry, with an anxious look at his friend. “Now, would it not be better that he should let it go?”\n\nStill, the Doctor, with shaded forehead, beat his foot nervously on the ground.\n\n“You do not find it easy to advise me?” said Mr. Lorry. “I quite understand it to be a nice question. And yet I think--” And there he shook his head, and stopped.\n\n“You see,” said Doctor Manette, turning to him after an uneasy pause, “it is very hard to explain, consistently, the innermost workings of this poor man’s mind. He once yearned so frightfully for that occupation, and it was so welcome when it came; no doubt it relieved his pain so much, by substituting the perplexity of the fingers for the perplexity of the brain, and by substituting, as he became more practised, the ingenuity of the hands, for the ingenuity of the mental torture; that he has never been able to bear the thought of putting it quite out of his reach. Even now, when I believe he is more hopeful of himself than he has ever been, and even speaks of himself with a kind of confidence, the idea that he might need that old employment, and not find it, gives him a sudden sense of terror, like that which one may fancy strikes to the heart of a lost child.”\n\nHe looked like his illustration, as he raised his eyes to Mr. Lorry’s face.\n\n“But may not--mind! I ask for information, as a plodding man of business who only deals with such material objects as guineas, shillings, and bank-notes--may not the retention of the thing involve the retention of the idea? If the thing were gone, my dear Manette, might not the fear go with it? In short, is it not a concession to the misgiving, to keep the forge?”\n\nThere was another silence.\n\n“You see, too,” said the Doctor, tremulously, “it is such an old companion.”\n\n“I would not keep it,” said Mr. Lorry, shaking his head; for he gained in firmness as he saw the Doctor disquieted. “I would recommend him to sacrifice it. I only want your authority. I am sure it does no good. Come! Give me your authority, like a dear good man. For his daughter’s sake, my dear Manette!”\n\nVery strange to see what a struggle there was within him!\n\n“In her name, then, let it be done; I sanction it. But, I would not take it away while he was present. Let it be removed when he is not there; let him miss his old companion after an absence.”\n\nMr. Lorry readily engaged for that, and the conference was ended. They passed the day in the country, and the Doctor was quite restored. On the three following days he remained perfectly well, and on the fourteenth day he went away to join Lucie and her husband. The precaution that had been taken to account for his silence, Mr. Lorry had previously explained to him, and he had written to Lucie in accordance with it, and she had no suspicions.\n\nOn the night of the day on which he left the house, Mr. Lorry went into his room with a chopper, saw, chisel, and hammer, attended by Miss Pross carrying a light. There, with closed doors, and in a mysterious and guilty manner, Mr. Lorry hacked the shoemaker’s bench to pieces, while Miss Pross held the candle as if she were assisting at a murder--for which, indeed, in her grimness, she was no unsuitable figure. The burning of the body (previously reduced to pieces convenient for the purpose) was commenced without delay in the kitchen fire; and the tools, shoes, and leather, were buried in the garden. So wicked do destruction and secrecy appear to honest minds, that Mr. Lorry and Miss Pross, while engaged in the commission of their deed and in the removal of its traces, almost felt, and almost looked, like accomplices in a horrible crime.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. A Plea\n\nWhen the newly-married pair came home, the first person who appeared, to offer his congratulations, was Sydney Carton. They had not been at home many hours, when he presented himself. He was not improved in habits, or in looks, or in manner; but there was a certain rugged air of fidelity about him, which was new to the observation of Charles Darnay.\n\nHe watched his opportunity of taking Darnay aside into a window, and of speaking to him when no one overheard.\n\n“Mr. Darnay,” said Carton, “I wish we might be friends.”\n\n“We are already friends, I hope.”\n\n“You are good enough to say so, as a fashion of speech; but, I don’t mean any fashion of speech. Indeed, when I say I wish we might be friends, I scarcely mean quite that, either.”\n\nCharles Darnay--as was natural--asked him, in all good-humour and good-fellowship, what he did mean?\n\n“Upon my life,” said Carton, smiling, “I find that easier to comprehend in my own mind, than to convey to yours. However, let me try. You remember a certain famous occasion when I was more drunk than--than usual?”\n\n“I remember a certain famous occasion when you forced me to confess that you had been drinking.”\n\n“I remember it too. The curse of those occasions is heavy upon me, for I always remember them. I hope it may be taken into account one day, when all days are at an end for me! Don’t be alarmed; I am not going to preach.”\n\n“I am not at all alarmed. Earnestness in you, is anything but alarming to me.”\n\n“Ah!” said Carton, with a careless wave of his hand, as if he waved that away. “On the drunken occasion in question (one of a large number, as you know), I was insufferable about liking you, and not liking you. I wish you would forget it.”\n\n“I forgot it long ago.”\n\n“Fashion of speech again! But, Mr. Darnay, oblivion is not so easy to me, as you represent it to be to you. I have by no means forgotten it, and a light answer does not help me to forget it.”\n\n“If it was a light answer,” returned Darnay, “I beg your forgiveness for it. I had no other object than to turn a slight thing, which, to my surprise, seems to trouble you too much, aside. I declare to you, on the faith of a gentleman, that I have long dismissed it from my mind. Good Heaven, what was there to dismiss! Have I had nothing more important to remember, in the great service you rendered me that day?”\n\n“As to the great service,” said Carton, “I am bound to avow to you, when you speak of it in that way, that it was mere professional claptrap, I don’t know that I cared what became of you, when I rendered it.--Mind! I say when I rendered it; I am speaking of the past.”\n\n“You make light of the obligation,” returned Darnay, “but I will not quarrel with your light answer.”\n\n“Genuine truth, Mr. Darnay, trust me! I have gone aside from my purpose; I was speaking about our being friends. Now, you know me; you know I am incapable of all the higher and better flights of men. If you doubt it, ask Stryver, and he’ll tell you so.”\n\n“I prefer to form my own opinion, without the aid of his.”\n\n“Well! At any rate you know me as a dissolute dog, who has never done any good, and never will.”\n\n“I don’t know that you ‘never will.’”\n\n“But I do, and you must take my word for it. Well! If you could endure to have such a worthless fellow, and a fellow of such indifferent reputation, coming and going at odd times, I should ask that I might be permitted to come and go as a privileged person here; that I might be regarded as an useless (and I would add, if it were not for the resemblance I detected between you and me, an unornamental) piece of furniture, tolerated for its old service, and taken no notice of. I doubt if I should abuse the permission. It is a hundred to one if I should avail myself of it four times in a year. It would satisfy me, I dare say, to know that I had it.”\n\n“Will you try?”\n\n“That is another way of saying that I am placed on the footing I have indicated. I thank you, Darnay. I may use that freedom with your name?”\n\n“I think so, Carton, by this time.”\n\nThey shook hands upon it, and Sydney turned away. Within a minute afterwards, he was, to all outward appearance, as unsubstantial as ever.\n\nWhen he was gone, and in the course of an evening passed with Miss Pross, the Doctor, and Mr. Lorry, Charles Darnay made some mention of this conversation in general terms, and spoke of Sydney Carton as a problem of carelessness and recklessness. He spoke of him, in short, not bitterly or meaning to bear hard upon him, but as anybody might who saw him as he showed himself.\n\nHe had no idea that this could dwell in the thoughts of his fair young wife; but, when he afterwards joined her in their own rooms, he found her waiting for him with the old pretty lifting of the forehead strongly marked.\n\n“We are thoughtful to-night!” said Darnay, drawing his arm about her.\n\n“Yes, dearest Charles,” with her hands on his breast, and the inquiring and attentive expression fixed upon him; “we are rather thoughtful to-night, for we have something on our mind to-night.”\n\n“What is it, my Lucie?”\n\n“Will you promise not to press one question on me, if I beg you not to ask it?”\n\n“Will I promise? What will I not promise to my Love?”\n\nWhat, indeed, with his hand putting aside the golden hair from the cheek, and his other hand against the heart that beat for him!\n\n“I think, Charles, poor Mr. Carton deserves more consideration and respect than you expressed for him to-night.”\n\n“Indeed, my own? Why so?”\n\n“That is what you are not to ask me. But I think--I know--he does.”\n\n“If you know it, it is enough. What would you have me do, my Life?”\n\n“I would ask you, dearest, to be very generous with him always, and very lenient on his faults when he is not by. I would ask you to believe that he has a heart he very, very seldom reveals, and that there are deep wounds in it. My dear, I have seen it bleeding.”\n\n“It is a painful reflection to me,” said Charles Darnay, quite astounded, “that I should have done him any wrong. I never thought this of him.”\n\n“My husband, it is so. I fear he is not to be reclaimed; there is scarcely a hope that anything in his character or fortunes is reparable now. But, I am sure that he is capable of good things, gentle things, even magnanimous things.”\n\nShe looked so beautiful in the purity of her faith in this lost man, that her husband could have looked at her as she was for hours.\n\n“And, O my dearest Love!” she urged, clinging nearer to him, laying her head upon his breast, and raising her eyes to his, “remember how strong we are in our happiness, and how weak he is in his misery!”\n\nThe supplication touched him home. “I will always remember it, dear Heart! I will remember it as long as I live.”\n\nHe bent over the golden head, and put the rosy lips to his, and folded her in his arms. If one forlorn wanderer then pacing the dark streets, could have heard her innocent disclosure, and could have seen the drops of pity kissed away by her husband from the soft blue eyes so loving of that husband, he might have cried to the night--and the words would not have parted from his lips for the first time--\n\n“God bless her for her sweet compassion!”\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. Echoing Footsteps\n\nA wonderful corner for echoes, it has been remarked, that corner where the Doctor lived. Ever busily winding the golden thread which bound her husband, and her father, and herself, and her old directress and companion, in a life of quiet bliss, Lucie sat in the still house in the tranquilly resounding corner, listening to the echoing footsteps of years.\n\nAt first, there were times, though she was a perfectly happy young wife, when her work would slowly fall from her hands, and her eyes would be dimmed. For, there was something coming in the echoes, something light, afar off, and scarcely audible yet, that stirred her heart too much. Fluttering hopes and doubts--hopes, of a love as yet unknown to her: doubts, of her remaining upon earth, to enjoy that new delight--divided her breast. Among the echoes then, there would arise the sound of footsteps at her own early grave; and thoughts of the husband who would be left so desolate, and who would mourn for her so much, swelled to her eyes, and broke like waves.\n\nThat time passed, and her little Lucie lay on her bosom. Then, among the advancing echoes, there was the tread of her tiny feet and the sound of her prattling words. Let greater echoes resound as they would, the young mother at the cradle side could always hear those coming. They came, and the shady house was sunny with a child’s laugh, and the Divine friend of children, to whom in her trouble she had confided hers, seemed to take her child in his arms, as He took the child of old, and made it a sacred joy to her.\n\nEver busily winding the golden thread that bound them all together, weaving the service of her happy influence through the tissue of all their lives, and making it predominate nowhere, Lucie heard in the echoes of years none but friendly and soothing sounds. Her husband’s step was strong and prosperous among them; her father’s firm and equal. Lo, Miss Pross, in harness of string, awakening the echoes, as an unruly charger, whip-corrected, snorting and pawing the earth under the plane-tree in the garden!\n\nEven when there were sounds of sorrow among the rest, they were not harsh nor cruel. Even when golden hair, like her own, lay in a halo on a pillow round the worn face of a little boy, and he said, with a radiant smile, “Dear papa and mamma, I am very sorry to leave you both, and to leave my pretty sister; but I am called, and I must go!” those were not tears all of agony that wetted his young mother’s cheek, as the spirit departed from her embrace that had been entrusted to it. Suffer them and forbid them not. They see my Father’s face. O Father, blessed words!\n\nThus, the rustling of an Angel’s wings got blended with the other echoes, and they were not wholly of earth, but had in them that breath of Heaven. Sighs of the winds that blew over a little garden-tomb were mingled with them also, and both were audible to Lucie, in a hushed murmur--like the breathing of a summer sea asleep upon a sandy shore--as the little Lucie, comically studious at the task of the morning, or dressing a doll at her mother’s footstool, chattered in the tongues of the Two Cities that were blended in her life.\n\nThe Echoes rarely answered to the actual tread of Sydney Carton. Some half-dozen times a year, at most, he claimed his privilege of coming in uninvited, and would sit among them through the evening, as he had once done often. He never came there heated with wine. And one other thing regarding him was whispered in the echoes, which has been whispered by all true echoes for ages and ages.\n\nNo man ever really loved a woman, lost her, and knew her with a blameless though an unchanged mind, when she was a wife and a mother, but her children had a strange sympathy with him--an instinctive delicacy of pity for him. What fine hidden sensibilities are touched in such a case, no echoes tell; but it is so, and it was so here. Carton was the first stranger to whom little Lucie held out her chubby arms, and he kept his place with her as she grew. The little boy had spoken of him, almost at the last. “Poor Carton! Kiss him for me!”\n\nMr. Stryver shouldered his way through the law, like some great engine forcing itself through turbid water, and dragged his useful friend in his wake, like a boat towed astern. As the boat so favoured is usually in a rough plight, and mostly under water, so, Sydney had a swamped life of it. But, easy and strong custom, unhappily so much easier and stronger in him than any stimulating sense of desert or disgrace, made it the life he was to lead; and he no more thought of emerging from his state of lion’s jackal, than any real jackal may be supposed to think of rising to be a lion. Stryver was rich; had married a florid widow with property and three boys, who had nothing particularly shining about them but the straight hair of their dumpling heads.\n\nThese three young gentlemen, Mr. Stryver, exuding patronage of the most offensive quality from every pore, had walked before him like three sheep to the quiet corner in Soho, and had offered as pupils to Lucie’s husband: delicately saying “Halloa! here are three lumps of bread-and-cheese towards your matrimonial picnic, Darnay!” The polite rejection of the three lumps of bread-and-cheese had quite bloated Mr. Stryver with indignation, which he afterwards turned to account in the training of the young gentlemen, by directing them to beware of the pride of Beggars, like that tutor-fellow. He was also in the habit of declaiming to Mrs. Stryver, over his full-bodied wine, on the arts Mrs. Darnay had once put in practice to “catch” him, and on the diamond-cut-diamond arts in himself, madam, which had rendered him “not to be caught.” Some of his King’s Bench familiars, who were occasionally parties to the full-bodied wine and the lie, excused him for the latter by saying that he had told it so often, that he believed it himself--which is surely such an incorrigible aggravation of an originally bad offence, as to justify any such offender’s being carried off to some suitably retired spot, and there hanged out of the way.\n\nThese were among the echoes to which Lucie, sometimes pensive, sometimes amused and laughing, listened in the echoing corner, until her little daughter was six years old. How near to her heart the echoes of her child’s tread came, and those of her own dear father’s, always active and self-possessed, and those of her dear husband’s, need not be told. Nor, how the lightest echo of their united home, directed by herself with such a wise and elegant thrift that it was more abundant than any waste, was music to her. Nor, how there were echoes all about her, sweet in her ears, of the many times her father had told her that he found her more devoted to him married (if that could be) than single, and of the many times her husband had said to her that no cares and duties seemed to divide her love for him or her help to him, and asked her “What is the magic secret, my darling, of your being everything to all of us, as if there were only one of us, yet never seeming to be hurried, or to have too much to do?”\n\nBut, there were other echoes, from a distance, that rumbled menacingly in the corner all through this space of time. And it was now, about little Lucie’s sixth birthday, that they began to have an awful sound, as of a great storm in France with a dreadful sea rising.\n\nOn a night in mid-July, one thousand seven hundred and eighty-nine, Mr. Lorry came in late, from Tellson’s, and sat himself down by Lucie and her husband in the dark window. It was a hot, wild night, and they were all three reminded of the old Sunday night when they had looked at the lightning from the same place.\n\n“I began to think,” said Mr. Lorry, pushing his brown wig back, “that I should have to pass the night at Tellson’s. We have been so full of business all day, that we have not known what to do first, or which way to turn. There is such an uneasiness in Paris, that we have actually a run of confidence upon us! Our customers over there, seem not to be able to confide their property to us fast enough. There is positively a mania among some of them for sending it to England.”\n\n“That has a bad look,” said Darnay--\n\n“A bad look, you say, my dear Darnay? Yes, but we don’t know what reason there is in it. People are so unreasonable! Some of us at Tellson’s are getting old, and we really can’t be troubled out of the ordinary course without due occasion.”\n\n“Still,” said Darnay, “you know how gloomy and threatening the sky is.”\n\n“I know that, to be sure,” assented Mr. Lorry, trying to persuade himself that his sweet temper was soured, and that he grumbled, “but I am determined to be peevish after my long day’s botheration. Where is Manette?”\n\n“Here he is,” said the Doctor, entering the dark room at the moment.\n\n“I am quite glad you are at home; for these hurries and forebodings by which I have been surrounded all day long, have made me nervous without reason. You are not going out, I hope?”\n\n“No; I am going to play backgammon with you, if you like,” said the Doctor.\n\n“I don’t think I do like, if I may speak my mind. I am not fit to be pitted against you to-night. Is the teaboard still there, Lucie? I can’t see.”\n\n“Of course, it has been kept for you.”\n\n“Thank ye, my dear. The precious child is safe in bed?”\n\n“And sleeping soundly.”\n\n“That’s right; all safe and well! I don’t know why anything should be otherwise than safe and well here, thank God; but I have been so put out all day, and I am not as young as I was! My tea, my dear! Thank ye. Now, come and take your place in the circle, and let us sit quiet, and hear the echoes about which you have your theory.”\n\n“Not a theory; it was a fancy.”\n\n“A fancy, then, my wise pet,” said Mr. Lorry, patting her hand. “They are very numerous and very loud, though, are they not? Only hear them!”\n\nHeadlong, mad, and dangerous footsteps to force their way into anybody’s life, footsteps not easily made clean again if once stained red, the footsteps raging in Saint Antoine afar off, as the little circle sat in the dark London window.\n\nSaint Antoine had been, that morning, a vast dusky mass of scarecrows heaving to and fro, with frequent gleams of light above the billowy heads, where steel blades and bayonets shone in the sun. A tremendous roar arose from the throat of Saint Antoine, and a forest of naked arms struggled in the air like shrivelled branches of trees in a winter wind: all the fingers convulsively clutching at every weapon or semblance of a weapon that was thrown up from the depths below, no matter how far off.\n\nWho gave them out, whence they last came, where they began, through what agency they crookedly quivered and jerked, scores at a time, over the heads of the crowd, like a kind of lightning, no eye in the throng could have told; but, muskets were being distributed--so were cartridges, powder, and ball, bars of iron and wood, knives, axes, pikes, every weapon that distracted ingenuity could discover or devise. People who could lay hold of nothing else, set themselves with bleeding hands to force stones and bricks out of their places in walls. Every pulse and heart in Saint Antoine was on high-fever strain and at high-fever heat. Every living creature there held life as of no account, and was demented with a passionate readiness to sacrifice it.\n\nAs a whirlpool of boiling waters has a centre point, so, all this raging circled round Defarge’s wine-shop, and every human drop in the caldron had a tendency to be sucked towards the vortex where Defarge himself, already begrimed with gunpowder and sweat, issued orders, issued arms, thrust this man back, dragged this man forward, disarmed one to arm another, laboured and strove in the thickest of the uproar.\n\n“Keep near to me, Jacques Three,” cried Defarge; “and do you, Jacques One and Two, separate and put yourselves at the head of as many of these patriots as you can. Where is my wife?”\n\n“Eh, well! Here you see me!” said madame, composed as ever, but not knitting to-day. Madame’s resolute right hand was occupied with an axe, in place of the usual softer implements, and in her girdle were a pistol and a cruel knife.\n\n“Where do you go, my wife?”\n\n“I go,” said madame, “with you at present. You shall see me at the head of women, by-and-bye.”\n\n“Come, then!” cried Defarge, in a resounding voice. “Patriots and friends, we are ready! The Bastille!”\n\nWith a roar that sounded as if all the breath in France had been shaped into the detested word, the living sea rose, wave on wave, depth on depth, and overflowed the city to that point. Alarm-bells ringing, drums beating, the sea raging and thundering on its new beach, the attack began.\n\nDeep ditches, double drawbridge, massive stone walls, eight great towers, cannon, muskets, fire and smoke. Through the fire and through the smoke--in the fire and in the smoke, for the sea cast him up against a cannon, and on the instant he became a cannonier--Defarge of the wine-shop worked like a manful soldier, Two fierce hours.\n\nDeep ditch, single drawbridge, massive stone walls, eight great towers, cannon, muskets, fire and smoke. One drawbridge down! “Work, comrades all, work! Work, Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques One Thousand, Jacques Two Thousand, Jacques Five-and-Twenty Thousand; in the name of all the Angels or the Devils--which you prefer--work!” Thus Defarge of the wine-shop, still at his gun, which had long grown hot.\n\n“To me, women!” cried madame his wife. “What! We can kill as well as the men when the place is taken!” And to her, with a shrill thirsty cry, trooping women variously armed, but all armed alike in hunger and revenge.\n\nCannon, muskets, fire and smoke; but, still the deep ditch, the single drawbridge, the massive stone walls, and the eight great towers. Slight displacements of the raging sea, made by the falling wounded. Flashing weapons, blazing torches, smoking waggonloads of wet straw, hard work at neighbouring barricades in all directions, shrieks, volleys, execrations, bravery without stint, boom smash and rattle, and the furious sounding of the living sea; but, still the deep ditch, and the single drawbridge, and the massive stone walls, and the eight great towers, and still Defarge of the wine-shop at his gun, grown doubly hot by the service of Four fierce hours.\n\nA white flag from within the fortress, and a parley--this dimly perceptible through the raging storm, nothing audible in it--suddenly the sea rose immeasurably wider and higher, and swept Defarge of the wine-shop over the lowered drawbridge, past the massive stone outer walls, in among the eight great towers surrendered!\n\nSo resistless was the force of the ocean bearing him on, that even to draw his breath or turn his head was as impracticable as if he had been struggling in the surf at the South Sea, until he was landed in the outer courtyard of the Bastille. There, against an angle of a wall, he made a struggle to look about him. Jacques Three was nearly at his side; Madame Defarge, still heading some of her women, was visible in the inner distance, and her knife was in her hand. Everywhere was tumult, exultation, deafening and maniacal bewilderment, astounding noise, yet furious dumb-show.\n\n“The Prisoners!”\n\n“The Records!”\n\n“The secret cells!”\n\n“The instruments of torture!”\n\n“The Prisoners!”\n\nOf all these cries, and ten thousand incoherences, “The Prisoners!” was the cry most taken up by the sea that rushed in, as if there were an eternity of people, as well as of time and space. When the foremost billows rolled past, bearing the prison officers with them, and threatening them all with instant death if any secret nook remained undisclosed, Defarge laid his strong hand on the breast of one of these men--a man with a grey head, who had a lighted torch in his hand--separated him from the rest, and got him between himself and the wall.\n\n“Show me the North Tower!” said Defarge. “Quick!”\n\n“I will faithfully,” replied the man, “if you will come with me. But there is no one there.”\n\n“What is the meaning of One Hundred and Five, North Tower?” asked Defarge. “Quick!”\n\n“The meaning, monsieur?”\n\n“Does it mean a captive, or a place of captivity? Or do you mean that I shall strike you dead?”\n\n“Kill him!” croaked Jacques Three, who had come close up.\n\n“Monsieur, it is a cell.”\n\n“Show it me!”\n\n“Pass this way, then.”\n\nJacques Three, with his usual craving on him, and evidently disappointed by the dialogue taking a turn that did not seem to promise bloodshed, held by Defarge’s arm as he held by the turnkey’s. Their three heads had been close together during this brief discourse, and it had been as much as they could do to hear one another, even then: so tremendous was the noise of the living ocean, in its irruption into the Fortress, and its inundation of the courts and passages and staircases. All around outside, too, it beat the walls with a deep, hoarse roar, from which, occasionally, some partial shouts of tumult broke and leaped into the air like spray.\n\nThrough gloomy vaults where the light of day had never shone, past hideous doors of dark dens and cages, down cavernous flights of steps, and again up steep rugged ascents of stone and brick, more like dry waterfalls than staircases, Defarge, the turnkey, and Jacques Three, linked hand and arm, went with all the speed they could make. Here and there, especially at first, the inundation started on them and swept by; but when they had done descending, and were winding and climbing up a tower, they were alone. Hemmed in here by the massive thickness of walls and arches, the storm within the fortress and without was only audible to them in a dull, subdued way, as if the noise out of which they had come had almost destroyed their sense of hearing.\n\nThe turnkey stopped at a low door, put a key in a clashing lock, swung the door slowly open, and said, as they all bent their heads and passed in:\n\n“One hundred and five, North Tower!”\n\nThere was a small, heavily-grated, unglazed window high in the wall, with a stone screen before it, so that the sky could be only seen by stooping low and looking up. There was a small chimney, heavily barred across, a few feet within. There was a heap of old feathery wood-ashes on the hearth. There was a stool, and table, and a straw bed. There were the four blackened walls, and a rusted iron ring in one of them.\n\n“Pass that torch slowly along these walls, that I may see them,” said Defarge to the turnkey.\n\nThe man obeyed, and Defarge followed the light closely with his eyes.\n\n“Stop!--Look here, Jacques!”\n\n“A. M.!” croaked Jacques Three, as he read greedily.\n\n“Alexandre Manette,” said Defarge in his ear, following the letters with his swart forefinger, deeply engrained with gunpowder. “And here he wrote ‘a poor physician.’ And it was he, without doubt, who scratched a calendar on this stone. What is that in your hand? A crowbar? Give it me!”\n\nHe had still the linstock of his gun in his own hand. He made a sudden exchange of the two instruments, and turning on the worm-eaten stool and table, beat them to pieces in a few blows.\n\n“Hold the light higher!” he said, wrathfully, to the turnkey. “Look among those fragments with care, Jacques. And see! Here is my knife,” throwing it to him; “rip open that bed, and search the straw. Hold the light higher, you!”\n\nWith a menacing look at the turnkey he crawled upon the hearth, and, peering up the chimney, struck and prised at its sides with the crowbar, and worked at the iron grating across it. In a few minutes, some mortar and dust came dropping down, which he averted his face to avoid; and in it, and in the old wood-ashes, and in a crevice in the chimney into which his weapon had slipped or wrought itself, he groped with a cautious touch.\n\n“Nothing in the wood, and nothing in the straw, Jacques?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Let us collect them together, in the middle of the cell. So! Light them, you!”\n\nThe turnkey fired the little pile, which blazed high and hot. Stooping again to come out at the low-arched door, they left it burning, and retraced their way to the courtyard; seeming to recover their sense of hearing as they came down, until they were in the raging flood once more.\n\nThey found it surging and tossing, in quest of Defarge himself. Saint Antoine was clamorous to have its wine-shop keeper foremost in the guard upon the governor who had defended the Bastille and shot the people. Otherwise, the governor would not be marched to the Hotel de Ville for judgment. Otherwise, the governor would escape, and the people’s blood (suddenly of some value, after many years of worthlessness) be unavenged.\n\nIn the howling universe of passion and contention that seemed to encompass this grim old officer conspicuous in his grey coat and red decoration, there was but one quite steady figure, and that was a woman’s. “See, there is my husband!” she cried, pointing him out. “See Defarge!” She stood immovable close to the grim old officer, and remained immovable close to him; remained immovable close to him through the streets, as Defarge and the rest bore him along; remained immovable close to him when he was got near his destination, and began to be struck at from behind; remained immovable close to him when the long-gathering rain of stabs and blows fell heavy; was so close to him when he dropped dead under it, that, suddenly animated, she put her foot upon his neck, and with her cruel knife--long ready--hewed off his head.\n\nThe hour was come, when Saint Antoine was to execute his horrible idea of hoisting up men for lamps to show what he could be and do. Saint Antoine’s blood was up, and the blood of tyranny and domination by the iron hand was down--down on the steps of the Hotel de Ville where the governor’s body lay--down on the sole of the shoe of Madame Defarge where she had trodden on the body to steady it for mutilation. “Lower the lamp yonder!” cried Saint Antoine, after glaring round for a new means of death; “here is one of his soldiers to be left on guard!” The swinging sentinel was posted, and the sea rushed on.\n\nThe sea of black and threatening waters, and of destructive upheaving of wave against wave, whose depths were yet unfathomed and whose forces were yet unknown. The remorseless sea of turbulently swaying shapes, voices of vengeance, and faces hardened in the furnaces of suffering until the touch of pity could make no mark on them.\n\nBut, in the ocean of faces where every fierce and furious expression was in vivid life, there were two groups of faces--each seven in number--so fixedly contrasting with the rest, that never did sea roll which bore more memorable wrecks with it. Seven faces of prisoners, suddenly released by the storm that had burst their tomb, were carried high overhead: all scared, all lost, all wondering and amazed, as if the Last Day were come, and those who rejoiced around them were lost spirits. Other seven faces there were, carried higher, seven dead faces, whose drooping eyelids and half-seen eyes awaited the Last Day. Impassive faces, yet with a suspended--not an abolished--expression on them; faces, rather, in a fearful pause, as having yet to raise the dropped lids of the eyes, and bear witness with the bloodless lips, “THOU DIDST IT!”\n\nSeven prisoners released, seven gory heads on pikes, the keys of the accursed fortress of the eight strong towers, some discovered letters and other memorials of prisoners of old time, long dead of broken hearts,--such, and such--like, the loudly echoing footsteps of Saint Antoine escort through the Paris streets in mid-July, one thousand seven hundred and eighty-nine. Now, Heaven defeat the fancy of Lucie Darnay, and keep these feet far out of her life! For, they are headlong, mad, and dangerous; and in the years so long after the breaking of the cask at Defarge’s wine-shop door, they are not easily purified when once stained red.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. The Sea Still Rises\n\nHaggard Saint Antoine had had only one exultant week, in which to soften his modicum of hard and bitter bread to such extent as he could, with the relish of fraternal embraces and congratulations, when Madame Defarge sat at her counter, as usual, presiding over the customers. Madame Defarge wore no rose in her head, for the great brotherhood of Spies had become, even in one short week, extremely chary of trusting themselves to the saint’s mercies. The lamps across his streets had a portentously elastic swing with them.\n\nMadame Defarge, with her arms folded, sat in the morning light and heat, contemplating the wine-shop and the street. In both, there were several knots of loungers, squalid and miserable, but now with a manifest sense of power enthroned on their distress. The raggedest nightcap, awry on the wretchedest head, had this crooked significance in it: “I know how hard it has grown for me, the wearer of this, to support life in myself; but do you know how easy it has grown for me, the wearer of this, to destroy life in you?” Every lean bare arm, that had been without work before, had this work always ready for it now, that it could strike. The fingers of the knitting women were vicious, with the experience that they could tear. There was a change in the appearance of Saint Antoine; the image had been hammering into this for hundreds of years, and the last finishing blows had told mightily on the expression.\n\nMadame Defarge sat observing it, with such suppressed approval as was to be desired in the leader of the Saint Antoine women. One of her sisterhood knitted beside her. The short, rather plump wife of a starved grocer, and the mother of two children withal, this lieutenant had already earned the complimentary name of The Vengeance.\n\n“Hark!” said The Vengeance. “Listen, then! Who comes?”\n\nAs if a train of powder laid from the outermost bound of Saint Antoine Quarter to the wine-shop door, had been suddenly fired, a fast-spreading murmur came rushing along.\n\n“It is Defarge,” said madame. “Silence, patriots!”\n\nDefarge came in breathless, pulled off a red cap he wore, and looked around him! “Listen, everywhere!” said madame again. “Listen to him!” Defarge stood, panting, against a background of eager eyes and open mouths, formed outside the door; all those within the wine-shop had sprung to their feet.\n\n“Say then, my husband. What is it?”\n\n“News from the other world!”\n\n“How, then?” cried madame, contemptuously. “The other world?”\n\n“Does everybody here recall old Foulon, who told the famished people that they might eat grass, and who died, and went to Hell?”\n\n“Everybody!” from all throats.\n\n“The news is of him. He is among us!”\n\n“Among us!” from the universal throat again. “And dead?”\n\n“Not dead! He feared us so much--and with reason--that he caused himself to be represented as dead, and had a grand mock-funeral. But they have found him alive, hiding in the country, and have brought him in. I have seen him but now, on his way to the Hotel de Ville, a prisoner. I have said that he had reason to fear us. Say all! Had he reason?”\n\nWretched old sinner of more than threescore years and ten, if he had never known it yet, he would have known it in his heart of hearts if he could have heard the answering cry.\n\nA moment of profound silence followed. Defarge and his wife looked steadfastly at one another. The Vengeance stooped, and the jar of a drum was heard as she moved it at her feet behind the counter.\n\n“Patriots!” said Defarge, in a determined voice, “are we ready?”\n\nInstantly Madame Defarge’s knife was in her girdle; the drum was beating in the streets, as if it and a drummer had flown together by magic; and The Vengeance, uttering terrific shrieks, and flinging her arms about her head like all the forty Furies at once, was tearing from house to house, rousing the women.\n\nThe men were terrible, in the bloody-minded anger with which they looked from windows, caught up what arms they had, and came pouring down into the streets; but, the women were a sight to chill the boldest. From such household occupations as their bare poverty yielded, from their children, from their aged and their sick crouching on the bare ground famished and naked, they ran out with streaming hair, urging one another, and themselves, to madness with the wildest cries and actions. Villain Foulon taken, my sister! Old Foulon taken, my mother! Miscreant Foulon taken, my daughter! Then, a score of others ran into the midst of these, beating their breasts, tearing their hair, and screaming, Foulon alive! Foulon who told the starving people they might eat grass! Foulon who told my old father that he might eat grass, when I had no bread to give him! Foulon who told my baby it might suck grass, when these breasts were dry with want! O mother of God, this Foulon! O Heaven our suffering! Hear me, my dead baby and my withered father: I swear on my knees, on these stones, to avenge you on Foulon! Husbands, and brothers, and young men, Give us the blood of Foulon, Give us the head of Foulon, Give us the heart of Foulon, Give us the body and soul of Foulon, Rend Foulon to pieces, and dig him into the ground, that grass may grow from him! With these cries, numbers of the women, lashed into blind frenzy, whirled about, striking and tearing at their own friends until they dropped into a passionate swoon, and were only saved by the men belonging to them from being trampled under foot.\n\nNevertheless, not a moment was lost; not a moment! This Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville, and might be loosed. Never, if Saint Antoine knew his own sufferings, insults, and wrongs! Armed men and women flocked out of the Quarter so fast, and drew even these last dregs after them with such a force of suction, that within a quarter of an hour there was not a human creature in Saint Antoine’s bosom but a few old crones and the wailing children.\n\nNo. They were all by that time choking the Hall of Examination where this old man, ugly and wicked, was, and overflowing into the adjacent open space and streets. The Defarges, husband and wife, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three, were in the first press, and at no great distance from him in the Hall.\n\n“See!” cried madame, pointing with her knife. “See the old villain bound with ropes. That was well done to tie a bunch of grass upon his back. Ha, ha! That was well done. Let him eat it now!” Madame put her knife under her arm, and clapped her hands as at a play.\n\nThe people immediately behind Madame Defarge, explaining the cause of her satisfaction to those behind them, and those again explaining to others, and those to others, the neighbouring streets resounded with the clapping of hands. Similarly, during two or three hours of drawl, and the winnowing of many bushels of words, Madame Defarge’s frequent expressions of impatience were taken up, with marvellous quickness, at a distance: the more readily, because certain men who had by some wonderful exercise of agility climbed up the external architecture to look in from the windows, knew Madame Defarge well, and acted as a telegraph between her and the crowd outside the building.\n\nAt length the sun rose so high that it struck a kindly ray as of hope or protection, directly down upon the old prisoner’s head. The favour was too much to bear; in an instant the barrier of dust and chaff that had stood surprisingly long, went to the winds, and Saint Antoine had got him!\n\nIt was known directly, to the furthest confines of the crowd. Defarge had but sprung over a railing and a table, and folded the miserable wretch in a deadly embrace--Madame Defarge had but followed and turned her hand in one of the ropes with which he was tied--The Vengeance and Jacques Three were not yet up with them, and the men at the windows had not yet swooped into the Hall, like birds of prey from their high perches--when the cry seemed to go up, all over the city, “Bring him out! Bring him to the lamp!”\n\nDown, and up, and head foremost on the steps of the building; now, on his knees; now, on his feet; now, on his back; dragged, and struck at, and stifled by the bunches of grass and straw that were thrust into his face by hundreds of hands; torn, bruised, panting, bleeding, yet always entreating and beseeching for mercy; now full of vehement agony of action, with a small clear space about him as the people drew one another back that they might see; now, a log of dead wood drawn through a forest of legs; he was hauled to the nearest street corner where one of the fatal lamps swung, and there Madame Defarge let him go--as a cat might have done to a mouse--and silently and composedly looked at him while they made ready, and while he besought her: the women passionately screeching at him all the time, and the men sternly calling out to have him killed with grass in his mouth. Once, he went aloft, and the rope broke, and they caught him shrieking; twice, he went aloft, and the rope broke, and they caught him shrieking; then, the rope was merciful, and held him, and his head was soon upon a pike, with grass enough in the mouth for all Saint Antoine to dance at the sight of.\n\nNor was this the end of the day’s bad work, for Saint Antoine so shouted and danced his angry blood up, that it boiled again, on hearing when the day closed in that the son-in-law of the despatched, another of the people’s enemies and insulters, was coming into Paris under a guard five hundred strong, in cavalry alone. Saint Antoine wrote his crimes on flaring sheets of paper, seized him--would have torn him out of the breast of an army to bear Foulon company--set his head and heart on pikes, and carried the three spoils of the day, in Wolf-procession through the streets.\n\nNot before dark night did the men and women come back to the children, wailing and breadless. Then, the miserable bakers’ shops were beset by long files of them, patiently waiting to buy bad bread; and while they waited with stomachs faint and empty, they beguiled the time by embracing one another on the triumphs of the day, and achieving them again in gossip. Gradually, these strings of ragged people shortened and frayed away; and then poor lights began to shine in high windows, and slender fires were made in the streets, at which neighbours cooked in common, afterwards supping at their doors.\n\nScanty and insufficient suppers those, and innocent of meat, as of most other sauce to wretched bread. Yet, human fellowship infused some nourishment into the flinty viands, and struck some sparks of cheerfulness out of them. Fathers and mothers who had had their full share in the worst of the day, played gently with their meagre children; and lovers, with such a world around them and before them, loved and hoped.\n\nIt was almost morning, when Defarge’s wine-shop parted with its last knot of customers, and Monsieur Defarge said to madame his wife, in husky tones, while fastening the door:\n\n“At last it is come, my dear!”\n\n“Eh well!” returned madame. “Almost.”\n\nSaint Antoine slept, the Defarges slept: even The Vengeance slept with her starved grocer, and the drum was at rest. The drum’s was the only voice in Saint Antoine that blood and hurry had not changed. The Vengeance, as custodian of the drum, could have wakened him up and had the same speech out of him as before the Bastille fell, or old Foulon was seized; not so with the hoarse tones of the men and women in Saint Antoine’s bosom.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. Fire Rises\n\nThere was a change on the village where the fountain fell, and where the mender of roads went forth daily to hammer out of the stones on the highway such morsels of bread as might serve for patches to hold his poor ignorant soul and his poor reduced body together. The prison on the crag was not so dominant as of yore; there were soldiers to guard it, but not many; there were officers to guard the soldiers, but not one of them knew what his men would do--beyond this: that it would probably not be what he was ordered.\n\nFar and wide lay a ruined country, yielding nothing but desolation. Every green leaf, every blade of grass and blade of grain, was as shrivelled and poor as the miserable people. Everything was bowed down, dejected, oppressed, and broken. Habitations, fences, domesticated animals, men, women, children, and the soil that bore them--all worn out.\n\nMonseigneur (often a most worthy individual gentleman) was a national blessing, gave a chivalrous tone to things, was a polite example of luxurious and shining life, and a great deal more to equal purpose; nevertheless, Monseigneur as a class had, somehow or other, brought things to this. Strange that Creation, designed expressly for Monseigneur, should be so soon wrung dry and squeezed out! There must be something short-sighted in the eternal arrangements, surely! Thus it was, however; and the last drop of blood having been extracted from the flints, and the last screw of the rack having been turned so often that its purchase crumbled, and it now turned and turned with nothing to bite, Monseigneur began to run away from a phenomenon so low and unaccountable.\n\nBut, this was not the change on the village, and on many a village like it. For scores of years gone by, Monseigneur had squeezed it and wrung it, and had seldom graced it with his presence except for the pleasures of the chase--now, found in hunting the people; now, found in hunting the beasts, for whose preservation Monseigneur made edifying spaces of barbarous and barren wilderness. No. The change consisted in the appearance of strange faces of low caste, rather than in the disappearance of the high caste, chiselled, and otherwise beautified and beautifying features of Monseigneur.\n\nFor, in these times, as the mender of roads worked, solitary, in the dust, not often troubling himself to reflect that dust he was and to dust he must return, being for the most part too much occupied in thinking how little he had for supper and how much more he would eat if he had it--in these times, as he raised his eyes from his lonely labour, and viewed the prospect, he would see some rough figure approaching on foot, the like of which was once a rarity in those parts, but was now a frequent presence. As it advanced, the mender of roads would discern without surprise, that it was a shaggy-haired man, of almost barbarian aspect, tall, in wooden shoes that were clumsy even to the eyes of a mender of roads, grim, rough, swart, steeped in the mud and dust of many highways, dank with the marshy moisture of many low grounds, sprinkled with the thorns and leaves and moss of many byways through woods.\n\nSuch a man came upon him, like a ghost, at noon in the July weather, as he sat on his heap of stones under a bank, taking such shelter as he could get from a shower of hail.\n\nThe man looked at him, looked at the village in the hollow, at the mill, and at the prison on the crag. When he had identified these objects in what benighted mind he had, he said, in a dialect that was just intelligible:\n\n“How goes it, Jacques?”\n\n“All well, Jacques.”\n\n“Touch then!”\n\nThey joined hands, and the man sat down on the heap of stones.\n\n“No dinner?”\n\n“Nothing but supper now,” said the mender of roads, with a hungry face.\n\n“It is the fashion,” growled the man. “I meet no dinner anywhere.”\n\nHe took out a blackened pipe, filled it, lighted it with flint and steel, pulled at it until it was in a bright glow: then, suddenly held it from him and dropped something into it from between his finger and thumb, that blazed and went out in a puff of smoke.\n\n“Touch then.” It was the turn of the mender of roads to say it this time, after observing these operations. They again joined hands.\n\n“To-night?” said the mender of roads.\n\n“To-night,” said the man, putting the pipe in his mouth.\n\n“Where?”\n\n“Here.”\n\nHe and the mender of roads sat on the heap of stones looking silently at one another, with the hail driving in between them like a pigmy charge of bayonets, until the sky began to clear over the village.\n\n“Show me!” said the traveller then, moving to the brow of the hill.\n\n“See!” returned the mender of roads, with extended finger. “You go down here, and straight through the street, and past the fountain--”\n\n“To the Devil with all that!” interrupted the other, rolling his eye over the landscape. “I go through no streets and past no fountains. Well?”\n\n“Well! About two leagues beyond the summit of that hill above the village.”\n\n“Good. When do you cease to work?”\n\n“At sunset.”\n\n“Will you wake me, before departing? I have walked two nights without resting. Let me finish my pipe, and I shall sleep like a child. Will you wake me?”\n\n“Surely.”\n\nThe wayfarer smoked his pipe out, put it in his breast, slipped off his great wooden shoes, and lay down on his back on the heap of stones. He was fast asleep directly.\n\nAs the road-mender plied his dusty labour, and the hail-clouds, rolling away, revealed bright bars and streaks of sky which were responded to by silver gleams upon the landscape, the little man (who wore a red cap now, in place of his blue one) seemed fascinated by the figure on the heap of stones. His eyes were so often turned towards it, that he used his tools mechanically, and, one would have said, to very poor account. The bronze face, the shaggy black hair and beard, the coarse woollen red cap, the rough medley dress of home-spun stuff and hairy skins of beasts, the powerful frame attenuated by spare living, and the sullen and desperate compression of the lips in sleep, inspired the mender of roads with awe. The traveller had travelled far, and his feet were footsore, and his ankles chafed and bleeding; his great shoes, stuffed with leaves and grass, had been heavy to drag over the many long leagues, and his clothes were chafed into holes, as he himself was into sores. Stooping down beside him, the road-mender tried to get a peep at secret weapons in his breast or where not; but, in vain, for he slept with his arms crossed upon him, and set as resolutely as his lips. Fortified towns with their stockades, guard-houses, gates, trenches, and drawbridges, seemed to the mender of roads, to be so much air as against this figure. And when he lifted his eyes from it to the horizon and looked around, he saw in his small fancy similar figures, stopped by no obstacle, tending to centres all over France.\n\nThe man slept on, indifferent to showers of hail and intervals of brightness, to sunshine on his face and shadow, to the paltering lumps of dull ice on his body and the diamonds into which the sun changed them, until the sun was low in the west, and the sky was glowing. Then, the mender of roads having got his tools together and all things ready to go down into the village, roused him.\n\n“Good!” said the sleeper, rising on his elbow. “Two leagues beyond the summit of the hill?”\n“About.”\n\n“About. Good!”\n\nThe mender of roads went home, with the dust going on before him according to the set of the wind, and was soon at the fountain, squeezing himself in among the lean kine brought there to drink, and appearing even to whisper to them in his whispering to all the village. When the village had taken its poor supper, it did not creep to bed, as it usually did, but came out of doors again, and remained there. A curious contagion of whispering was upon it, and also, when it gathered together at the fountain in the dark, another curious contagion of looking expectantly at the sky in one direction only. Monsieur Gabelle, chief functionary of the place, became uneasy; went out on his house-top alone, and looked in that direction too; glanced down from behind his chimneys at the darkening faces by the fountain below, and sent word to the sacristan who kept the keys of the church, that there might be need to ring the tocsin by-and-bye.\n\nThe night deepened. The trees environing the old chateau, keeping its solitary state apart, moved in a rising wind, as though they threatened the pile of building massive and dark in the gloom. Up the two terrace flights of steps the rain ran wildly, and beat at the great door, like a swift messenger rousing those within; uneasy rushes of wind went through the hall, among the old spears and knives, and passed lamenting up the stairs, and shook the curtains of the bed where the last Marquis had slept. East, West, North, and South, through the woods, four heavy-treading, unkempt figures crushed the high grass and cracked the branches, striding on cautiously to come together in the courtyard. Four lights broke out there, and moved away in different directions, and all was black again.\n\nBut, not for long. Presently, the chateau began to make itself strangely visible by some light of its own, as though it were growing luminous. Then, a flickering streak played behind the architecture of the front, picking out transparent places, and showing where balustrades, arches, and windows were. Then it soared higher, and grew broader and brighter. Soon, from a score of the great windows, flames burst forth, and the stone faces awakened, stared out of fire.\n\nA faint murmur arose about the house from the few people who were left there, and there was a saddling of a horse and riding away. There was spurring and splashing through the darkness, and bridle was drawn in the space by the village fountain, and the horse in a foam stood at Monsieur Gabelle’s door. “Help, Gabelle! Help, every one!” The tocsin rang impatiently, but other help (if that were any) there was none. The mender of roads, and two hundred and fifty particular friends, stood with folded arms at the fountain, looking at the pillar of fire in the sky. “It must be forty feet high,” said they, grimly; and never moved.\n\nThe rider from the chateau, and the horse in a foam, clattered away through the village, and galloped up the stony steep, to the prison on the crag. At the gate, a group of officers were looking at the fire; removed from them, a group of soldiers. “Help, gentlemen--officers! The chateau is on fire; valuable objects may be saved from the flames by timely aid! Help, help!” The officers looked towards the soldiers who looked at the fire; gave no orders; and answered, with shrugs and biting of lips, “It must burn.”\n\nAs the rider rattled down the hill again and through the street, the village was illuminating. The mender of roads, and the two hundred and fifty particular friends, inspired as one man and woman by the idea of lighting up, had darted into their houses, and were putting candles in every dull little pane of glass. The general scarcity of everything, occasioned candles to be borrowed in a rather peremptory manner of Monsieur Gabelle; and in a moment of reluctance and hesitation on that functionary’s part, the mender of roads, once so submissive to authority, had remarked that carriages were good to make bonfires with, and that post-horses would roast.\n\nThe chateau was left to itself to flame and burn. In the roaring and raging of the conflagration, a red-hot wind, driving straight from the infernal regions, seemed to be blowing the edifice away. With the rising and falling of the blaze, the stone faces showed as if they were in torment. When great masses of stone and timber fell, the face with the two dints in the nose became obscured: anon struggled out of the smoke again, as if it were the face of the cruel Marquis, burning at the stake and contending with the fire.\n\nThe chateau burned; the nearest trees, laid hold of by the fire, scorched and shrivelled; trees at a distance, fired by the four fierce figures, begirt the blazing edifice with a new forest of smoke. Molten lead and iron boiled in the marble basin of the fountain; the water ran dry; the extinguisher tops of the towers vanished like ice before the heat, and trickled down into four rugged wells of flame. Great rents and splits branched out in the solid walls, like crystallisation; stupefied birds wheeled about and dropped into the furnace; four fierce figures trudged away, East, West, North, and South, along the night-enshrouded roads, guided by the beacon they had lighted, towards their next destination. The illuminated village had seized hold of the tocsin, and, abolishing the lawful ringer, rang for joy.\n\nNot only that; but the village, light-headed with famine, fire, and bell-ringing, and bethinking itself that Monsieur Gabelle had to do with the collection of rent and taxes--though it was but a small instalment of taxes, and no rent at all, that Gabelle had got in those latter days--became impatient for an interview with him, and, surrounding his house, summoned him to come forth for personal conference. Whereupon, Monsieur Gabelle did heavily bar his door, and retire to hold counsel with himself. The result of that conference was, that Gabelle again withdrew himself to his housetop behind his stack of chimneys; this time resolved, if his door were broken in (he was a small Southern man of retaliative temperament), to pitch himself head foremost over the parapet, and crush a man or two below.\n\nProbably, Monsieur Gabelle passed a long night up there, with the distant chateau for fire and candle, and the beating at his door, combined with the joy-ringing, for music; not to mention his having an ill-omened lamp slung across the road before his posting-house gate, which the village showed a lively inclination to displace in his favour. A trying suspense, to be passing a whole summer night on the brink of the black ocean, ready to take that plunge into it upon which Monsieur Gabelle had resolved! But, the friendly dawn appearing at last, and the rush-candles of the village guttering out, the people happily dispersed, and Monsieur Gabelle came down bringing his life with him for that while.\n\nWithin a hundred miles, and in the light of other fires, there were other functionaries less fortunate, that night and other nights, whom the rising sun found hanging across once-peaceful streets, where they had been born and bred; also, there were other villagers and townspeople less fortunate than the mender of roads and his fellows, upon whom the functionaries and soldiery turned with success, and whom they strung up in their turn. But, the fierce figures were steadily wending East, West, North, and South, be that as it would; and whosoever hung, fire burned. The altitude of the gallows that would turn to water and quench it, no functionary, by any stretch of mathematics, was able to calculate successfully.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. Drawn to the Loadstone Rock\n\nIn such risings of fire and risings of sea--the firm earth shaken by the rushes of an angry ocean which had now no ebb, but was always on the flow, higher and higher, to the terror and wonder of the beholders on the shore--three years of tempest were consumed. Three more birthdays of little Lucie had been woven by the golden thread into the peaceful tissue of the life of her home.\n\nMany a night and many a day had its inmates listened to the echoes in the corner, with hearts that failed them when they heard the thronging feet. For, the footsteps had become to their minds as the footsteps of a people, tumultuous under a red flag and with their country declared in danger, changed into wild beasts, by terrible enchantment long persisted in.\n\nMonseigneur, as a class, had dissociated himself from the phenomenon of his not being appreciated: of his being so little wanted in France, as to incur considerable danger of receiving his dismissal from it, and this life together. Like the fabled rustic who raised the Devil with infinite pains, and was so terrified at the sight of him that he could ask the Enemy no question, but immediately fled; so, Monseigneur, after boldly reading the Lord’s Prayer backwards for a great number of years, and performing many other potent spells for compelling the Evil One, no sooner beheld him in his terrors than he took to his noble heels.\n\nThe shining Bull’s Eye of the Court was gone, or it would have been the mark for a hurricane of national bullets. It had never been a good eye to see with--had long had the mote in it of Lucifer’s pride, Sardanapalus’s luxury, and a mole’s blindness--but it had dropped out and was gone. The Court, from that exclusive inner circle to its outermost rotten ring of intrigue, corruption, and dissimulation, was all gone together. Royalty was gone; had been besieged in its Palace and “suspended,” when the last tidings came over.\n\nThe August of the year one thousand seven hundred and ninety-two was come, and Monseigneur was by this time scattered far and wide.\n\nAs was natural, the head-quarters and great gathering-place of Monseigneur, in London, was Tellson’s Bank. Spirits are supposed to haunt the places where their bodies most resorted, and Monseigneur without a guinea haunted the spot where his guineas used to be. Moreover, it was the spot to which such French intelligence as was most to be relied upon, came quickest. Again: Tellson’s was a munificent house, and extended great liberality to old customers who had fallen from their high estate. Again: those nobles who had seen the coming storm in time, and anticipating plunder or confiscation, had made provident remittances to Tellson’s, were always to be heard of there by their needy brethren. To which it must be added that every new-comer from France reported himself and his tidings at Tellson’s, almost as a matter of course. For such variety of reasons, Tellson’s was at that time, as to French intelligence, a kind of High Exchange; and this was so well known to the public, and the inquiries made there were in consequence so numerous, that Tellson’s sometimes wrote the latest news out in a line or so and posted it in the Bank windows, for all who ran through Temple Bar to read.\n\nOn a steaming, misty afternoon, Mr. Lorry sat at his desk, and Charles Darnay stood leaning on it, talking with him in a low voice. The penitential den once set apart for interviews with the House, was now the news-Exchange, and was filled to overflowing. It was within half an hour or so of the time of closing.\n\n“But, although you are the youngest man that ever lived,” said Charles Darnay, rather hesitating, “I must still suggest to you--”\n\n“I understand. That I am too old?” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“Unsettled weather, a long journey, uncertain means of travelling, a disorganised country, a city that may not be even safe for you.”\n\n“My dear Charles,” said Mr. Lorry, with cheerful confidence, “you touch some of the reasons for my going: not for my staying away. It is safe enough for me; nobody will care to interfere with an old fellow of hard upon fourscore when there are so many people there much better worth interfering with. As to its being a disorganised city, if it were not a disorganised city there would be no occasion to send somebody from our House here to our House there, who knows the city and the business, of old, and is in Tellson’s confidence. As to the uncertain travelling, the long journey, and the winter weather, if I were not prepared to submit myself to a few inconveniences for the sake of Tellson’s, after all these years, who ought to be?”\n\n“I wish I were going myself,” said Charles Darnay, somewhat restlessly, and like one thinking aloud.\n\n“Indeed! You are a pretty fellow to object and advise!” exclaimed Mr. Lorry. “You wish you were going yourself? And you a Frenchman born? You are a wise counsellor.”\n\n“My dear Mr. Lorry, it is because I am a Frenchman born, that the thought (which I did not mean to utter here, however) has passed through my mind often. One cannot help thinking, having had some sympathy for the miserable people, and having abandoned something to them,” he spoke here in his former thoughtful manner, “that one might be listened to, and might have the power to persuade to some restraint. Only last night, after you had left us, when I was talking to Lucie--”\n\n“When you were talking to Lucie,” Mr. Lorry repeated. “Yes. I wonder you are not ashamed to mention the name of Lucie! Wishing you were going to France at this time of day!”\n\n“However, I am not going,” said Charles Darnay, with a smile. “It is more to the purpose that you say you are.”\n\n“And I am, in plain reality. The truth is, my dear Charles,” Mr. Lorry glanced at the distant House, and lowered his voice, “you can have no conception of the difficulty with which our business is transacted, and of the peril in which our books and papers over yonder are involved. The Lord above knows what the compromising consequences would be to numbers of people, if some of our documents were seized or destroyed; and they might be, at any time, you know, for who can say that Paris is not set afire to-day, or sacked to-morrow! Now, a judicious selection from these with the least possible delay, and the burying of them, or otherwise getting of them out of harm’s way, is within the power (without loss of precious time) of scarcely any one but myself, if any one. And shall I hang back, when Tellson’s knows this and says this--Tellson’s, whose bread I have eaten these sixty years--because I am a little stiff about the joints? Why, I am a boy, sir, to half a dozen old codgers here!”\n\n“How I admire the gallantry of your youthful spirit, Mr. Lorry.”\n\n“Tut! Nonsense, sir!--And, my dear Charles,” said Mr. Lorry, glancing at the House again, “you are to remember, that getting things out of Paris at this present time, no matter what things, is next to an impossibility. Papers and precious matters were this very day brought to us here (I speak in strict confidence; it is not business-like to whisper it, even to you), by the strangest bearers you can imagine, every one of whom had his head hanging on by a single hair as he passed the Barriers. At another time, our parcels would come and go, as easily as in business-like Old England; but now, everything is stopped.”\n\n“And do you really go to-night?”\n\n“I really go to-night, for the case has become too pressing to admit of delay.”\n\n“And do you take no one with you?”\n\n“All sorts of people have been proposed to me, but I will have nothing to say to any of them. I intend to take Jerry. Jerry has been my bodyguard on Sunday nights for a long time past and I am used to him. Nobody will suspect Jerry of being anything but an English bull-dog, or of having any design in his head but to fly at anybody who touches his master.”\n\n“I must say again that I heartily admire your gallantry and youthfulness.”\n\n“I must say again, nonsense, nonsense! When I have executed this little commission, I shall, perhaps, accept Tellson’s proposal to retire and live at my ease. Time enough, then, to think about growing old.”\n\nThis dialogue had taken place at Mr. Lorry’s usual desk, with Monseigneur swarming within a yard or two of it, boastful of what he would do to avenge himself on the rascal-people before long. It was too much the way of Monseigneur under his reverses as a refugee, and it was much too much the way of native British orthodoxy, to talk of this terrible Revolution as if it were the only harvest ever known under the skies that had not been sown--as if nothing had ever been done, or omitted to be done, that had led to it--as if observers of the wretched millions in France, and of the misused and perverted resources that should have made them prosperous, had not seen it inevitably coming, years before, and had not in plain words recorded what they saw. Such vapouring, combined with the extravagant plots of Monseigneur for the restoration of a state of things that had utterly exhausted itself, and worn out Heaven and earth as well as itself, was hard to be endured without some remonstrance by any sane man who knew the truth. And it was such vapouring all about his ears, like a troublesome confusion of blood in his own head, added to a latent uneasiness in his mind, which had already made Charles Darnay restless, and which still kept him so.\n\nAmong the talkers, was Stryver, of the King’s Bench Bar, far on his way to state promotion, and, therefore, loud on the theme: broaching to Monseigneur, his devices for blowing the people up and exterminating them from the face of the earth, and doing without them: and for accomplishing many similar objects akin in their nature to the abolition of eagles by sprinkling salt on the tails of the race. Him, Darnay heard with a particular feeling of objection; and Darnay stood divided between going away that he might hear no more, and remaining to interpose his word, when the thing that was to be, went on to shape itself out.\n\nThe House approached Mr. Lorry, and laying a soiled and unopened letter before him, asked if he had yet discovered any traces of the person to whom it was addressed? The House laid the letter down so close to Darnay that he saw the direction--the more quickly because it was his own right name. The address, turned into English, ran:\n\n“Very pressing. To Monsieur heretofore the Marquis St. Evrémonde, of France. Confided to the cares of Messrs. Tellson and Co., Bankers, London, England.”\n\nOn the marriage morning, Doctor Manette had made it his one urgent and express request to Charles Darnay, that the secret of this name should be--unless he, the Doctor, dissolved the obligation--kept inviolate between them. Nobody else knew it to be his name; his own wife had no suspicion of the fact; Mr. Lorry could have none.\n\n“No,” said Mr. Lorry, in reply to the House; “I have referred it, I think, to everybody now here, and no one can tell me where this gentleman is to be found.”\n\nThe hands of the clock verging upon the hour of closing the Bank, there was a general set of the current of talkers past Mr. Lorry’s desk. He held the letter out inquiringly; and Monseigneur looked at it, in the person of this plotting and indignant refugee; and Monseigneur looked at it in the person of that plotting and indignant refugee; and This, That, and The Other, all had something disparaging to say, in French or in English, concerning the Marquis who was not to be found.\n\n“Nephew, I believe--but in any case degenerate successor--of the polished Marquis who was murdered,” said one. “Happy to say, I never knew him.”\n\n“A craven who abandoned his post,” said another--this Monseigneur had been got out of Paris, legs uppermost and half suffocated, in a load of hay--“some years ago.”\n\n“Infected with the new doctrines,” said a third, eyeing the direction through his glass in passing; “set himself in opposition to the last Marquis, abandoned the estates when he inherited them, and left them to the ruffian herd. They will recompense him now, I hope, as he deserves.”\n\n“Hey?” cried the blatant Stryver. “Did he though? Is that the sort of fellow? Let us look at his infamous name. D--n the fellow!”\n\nDarnay, unable to restrain himself any longer, touched Mr. Stryver on the shoulder, and said:\n\n“I know the fellow.”\n\n“Do you, by Jupiter?” said Stryver. “I am sorry for it.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Why, Mr. Darnay? D’ye hear what he did? Don’t ask, why, in these times.”\n“But I do ask why?”\n\n“Then I tell you again, Mr. Darnay, I am sorry for it. I am sorry to hear you putting any such extraordinary questions. Here is a fellow, who, infected by the most pestilent and blasphemous code of devilry that ever was known, abandoned his property to the vilest scum of the earth that ever did murder by wholesale, and you ask me why I am sorry that a man who instructs youth knows him? Well, but I’ll answer you. I am sorry because I believe there is contamination in such a scoundrel. That’s why.”\n\nMindful of the secret, Darnay with great difficulty checked himself, and said: “You may not understand the gentleman.”\n\n“I understand how to put you in a corner, Mr. Darnay,” said Bully Stryver, “and I’ll do it. If this fellow is a gentleman, I don’t understand him. You may tell him so, with my compliments. You may also tell him, from me, that after abandoning his worldly goods and position to this butcherly mob, I wonder he is not at the head of them. But, no, gentlemen,” said Stryver, looking all round, and snapping his fingers, “I know something of human nature, and I tell you that you’ll never find a fellow like this fellow, trusting himself to the mercies of such precious protégés. No, gentlemen; he’ll always show ’em a clean pair of heels very early in the scuffle, and sneak away.”\n\nWith those words, and a final snap of his fingers, Mr. Stryver shouldered himself into Fleet-street, amidst the general approbation of his hearers. Mr. Lorry and Charles Darnay were left alone at the desk, in the general departure from the Bank.\n\n“Will you take charge of the letter?” said Mr. Lorry. “You know where to deliver it?”\n\n“I do.”\n\n“Will you undertake to explain, that we suppose it to have been addressed here, on the chance of our knowing where to forward it, and that it has been here some time?”\n\n“I will do so. Do you start for Paris from here?”\n\n“From here, at eight.”\n\n“I will come back, to see you off.”\n\nVery ill at ease with himself, and with Stryver and most other men, Darnay made the best of his way into the quiet of the Temple, opened the letter, and read it. These were its contents:\n\n“Prison of the Abbaye, Paris.\n\n“After having long been in danger of my life at the hands of the village, I have been seized, with great violence and indignity, and brought a long journey on foot to Paris. On the road I have suffered a great deal. Nor is that all; my house has been destroyed--razed to the ground.\n\n“The crime for which I am imprisoned, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, and for which I shall be summoned before the tribunal, and shall lose my life (without your so generous help), is, they tell me, treason against the majesty of the people, in that I have acted against them for an emigrant. It is in vain I represent that I have acted for them, and not against, according to your commands. It is in vain I represent that, before the sequestration of emigrant property, I had remitted the imposts they had ceased to pay; that I had collected no rent; that I had had recourse to no process. The only response is, that I have acted for an emigrant, and where is that emigrant?\n\n“Ah! most gracious Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, where is that emigrant? I cry in my sleep where is he? I demand of Heaven, will he not come to deliver me? No answer. Ah Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, I send my desolate cry across the sea, hoping it may perhaps reach your ears through the great bank of Tilson known at Paris!\n\n“For the love of Heaven, of justice, of generosity, of the honour of your noble name, I supplicate you, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, to succour and release me. My fault is, that I have been true to you. Oh Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, I pray you be you true to me!\n\n“From this prison here of horror, whence I every hour tend nearer and nearer to destruction, I send you, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, the assurance of my dolorous and unhappy service.\n\n“Your afflicted,\n\n“Gabelle.”\n\nThe latent uneasiness in Darnay’s mind was roused to vigourous life by this letter. The peril of an old servant and a good one, whose only crime was fidelity to himself and his family, stared him so reproachfully in the face, that, as he walked to and fro in the Temple considering what to do, he almost hid his face from the passersby.\n\nHe knew very well, that in his horror of the deed which had culminated the bad deeds and bad reputation of the old family house, in his resentful suspicions of his uncle, and in the aversion with which his conscience regarded the crumbling fabric that he was supposed to uphold, he had acted imperfectly. He knew very well, that in his love for Lucie, his renunciation of his social place, though by no means new to his own mind, had been hurried and incomplete. He knew that he ought to have systematically worked it out and supervised it, and that he had meant to do it, and that it had never been done.\n\nThe happiness of his own chosen English home, the necessity of being always actively employed, the swift changes and troubles of the time which had followed on one another so fast, that the events of this week annihilated the immature plans of last week, and the events of the week following made all new again; he knew very well, that to the force of these circumstances he had yielded:--not without disquiet, but still without continuous and accumulating resistance. That he had watched the times for a time of action, and that they had shifted and struggled until the time had gone by, and the nobility were trooping from France by every highway and byway, and their property was in course of confiscation and destruction, and their very names were blotting out, was as well known to himself as it could be to any new authority in France that might impeach him for it.\n\nBut, he had oppressed no man, he had imprisoned no man; he was so far from having harshly exacted payment of his dues, that he had relinquished them of his own will, thrown himself on a world with no favour in it, won his own private place there, and earned his own bread. Monsieur Gabelle had held the impoverished and involved estate on written instructions, to spare the people, to give them what little there was to give--such fuel as the heavy creditors would let them have in the winter, and such produce as could be saved from the same grip in the summer--and no doubt he had put the fact in plea and proof, for his own safety, so that it could not but appear now.\n\nThis favoured the desperate resolution Charles Darnay had begun to make, that he would go to Paris.\n\nYes. Like the mariner in the old story, the winds and streams had driven him within the influence of the Loadstone Rock, and it was drawing him to itself, and he must go. Everything that arose before his mind drifted him on, faster and faster, more and more steadily, to the terrible attraction. His latent uneasiness had been, that bad aims were being worked out in his own unhappy land by bad instruments, and that he who could not fail to know that he was better than they, was not there, trying to do something to stay bloodshed, and assert the claims of mercy and humanity. With this uneasiness half stifled, and half reproaching him, he had been brought to the pointed comparison of himself with the brave old gentleman in whom duty was so strong; upon that comparison (injurious to himself) had instantly followed the sneers of Monseigneur, which had stung him bitterly, and those of Stryver, which above all were coarse and galling, for old reasons. Upon those, had followed Gabelle’s letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, in danger of death, to his justice, honour, and good name.\n\nHis resolution was made. He must go to Paris.\n\nYes. The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he must sail on, until he struck. He knew of no rock; he saw hardly any danger. The intention with which he had done what he had done, even although he had left it incomplete, presented it before him in an aspect that would be gratefully acknowledged in France on his presenting himself to assert it. Then, that glorious vision of doing good, which is so often the sanguine mirage of so many good minds, arose before him, and he even saw himself in the illusion with some influence to guide this raging Revolution that was running so fearfully wild.\n\nAs he walked to and fro with his resolution made, he considered that neither Lucie nor her father must know of it until he was gone. Lucie should be spared the pain of separation; and her father, always reluctant to turn his thoughts towards the dangerous ground of old, should come to the knowledge of the step, as a step taken, and not in the balance of suspense and doubt. How much of the incompleteness of his situation was referable to her father, through the painful anxiety to avoid reviving old associations of France in his mind, he did not discuss with himself. But, that circumstance too, had had its influence in his course.\n\nHe walked to and fro, with thoughts very busy, until it was time to return to Tellson’s and take leave of Mr. Lorry. As soon as he arrived in Paris he would present himself to this old friend, but he must say nothing of his intention now.\n\nA carriage with post-horses was ready at the Bank door, and Jerry was booted and equipped.\n\n“I have delivered that letter,” said Charles Darnay to Mr. Lorry. “I would not consent to your being charged with any written answer, but perhaps you will take a verbal one?”\n\n“That I will, and readily,” said Mr. Lorry, “if it is not dangerous.”\n\n“Not at all. Though it is to a prisoner in the Abbaye.”\n\n“What is his name?” said Mr. Lorry, with his open pocket-book in his hand.\n\n“Gabelle.”\n\n“Gabelle. And what is the message to the unfortunate Gabelle in prison?”\n\n“Simply, ‘that he has received the letter, and will come.’”\n\n“Any time mentioned?”\n\n“He will start upon his journey to-morrow night.”\n\n“Any person mentioned?”\n\nHe helped Mr. Lorry to wrap himself in a number of coats and cloaks, and went out with him from the warm atmosphere of the old Bank, into the misty air of Fleet-street. “My love to Lucie, and to little Lucie,” said Mr. Lorry at parting, “and take precious care of them till I come back.” Charles Darnay shook his head and doubtfully smiled, as the carriage rolled away.\n\nThat night--it was the fourteenth of August--he sat up late, and wrote two fervent letters; one was to Lucie, explaining the strong obligation he was under to go to Paris, and showing her, at length, the reasons that he had, for feeling confident that he could become involved in no personal danger there; the other was to the Doctor, confiding Lucie and their dear child to his care, and dwelling on the same topics with the strongest assurances. To both, he wrote that he would despatch letters in proof of his safety, immediately after his arrival.\n\nIt was a hard day, that day of being among them, with the first reservation of their joint lives on his mind. It was a hard matter to preserve the innocent deceit of which they were profoundly unsuspicious. But, an affectionate glance at his wife, so happy and busy, made him resolute not to tell her what impended (he had been half moved to do it, so strange it was to him to act in anything without her quiet aid), and the day passed quickly. Early in the evening he embraced her, and her scarcely less dear namesake, pretending that he would return by-and-bye (an imaginary engagement took him out, and he had secreted a valise of clothes ready), and so he emerged into the heavy mist of the heavy streets, with a heavier heart.\n\nThe unseen force was drawing him fast to itself, now, and all the tides and winds were setting straight and strong towards it. He left his two letters with a trusty porter, to be delivered half an hour before midnight, and no sooner; took horse for Dover; and began his journey. “For the love of Heaven, of justice, of generosity, of the honour of your noble name!” was the poor prisoner’s cry with which he strengthened his sinking heart, as he left all that was dear on earth behind him, and floated away for the Loadstone Rock.\nThe end of the second book.\n\n# Book the Third--the Track of a Storm CHAPTER I. In Secret\n\nThe traveller fared slowly on his way, who fared towards Paris from England in the autumn of the year one thousand seven hundred and ninety-two. More than enough of bad roads, bad equipages, and bad horses, he would have encountered to delay him, though the fallen and unfortunate King of France had been upon his throne in all his glory; but, the changed times were fraught with other obstacles than these. Every town-gate and village taxing-house had its band of citizen-patriots, with their national muskets in a most explosive state of readiness, who stopped all comers and goers, cross-questioned them, inspected their papers, looked for their names in lists of their own, turned them back, or sent them on, or stopped them and laid them in hold, as their capricious judgment or fancy deemed best for the dawning Republic One and Indivisible, of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death.\n\nA very few French leagues of his journey were accomplished, when Charles Darnay began to perceive that for him along these country roads there was no hope of return until he should have been declared a good citizen at Paris. Whatever might befall now, he must on to his journey’s end. Not a mean village closed upon him, not a common barrier dropped across the road behind him, but he knew it to be another iron door in the series that was barred between him and England. The universal watchfulness so encompassed him, that if he had been taken in a net, or were being forwarded to his destination in a cage, he could not have felt his freedom more completely gone.\n\nThis universal watchfulness not only stopped him on the highway twenty times in a stage, but retarded his progress twenty times in a day, by riding after him and taking him back, riding before him and stopping him by anticipation, riding with him and keeping him in charge. He had been days upon his journey in France alone, when he went to bed tired out, in a little town on the high road, still a long way from Paris.\n\nNothing but the production of the afflicted Gabelle’s letter from his prison of the Abbaye would have got him on so far. His difficulty at the guard-house in this small place had been such, that he felt his journey to have come to a crisis. And he was, therefore, as little surprised as a man could be, to find himself awakened at the small inn to which he had been remitted until morning, in the middle of the night.\n\nAwakened by a timid local functionary and three armed patriots in rough red caps and with pipes in their mouths, who sat down on the bed.\n\n“Emigrant,” said the functionary, “I am going to send you on to Paris, under an escort.”\n\n“Citizen, I desire nothing more than to get to Paris, though I could dispense with the escort.”\n\n“Silence!” growled a red-cap, striking at the coverlet with the butt-end of his musket. “Peace, aristocrat!”\n\n“It is as the good patriot says,” observed the timid functionary. “You are an aristocrat, and must have an escort--and must pay for it.”\n\n“I have no choice,” said Charles Darnay.\n\n“Choice! Listen to him!” cried the same scowling red-cap. “As if it was not a favour to be protected from the lamp-iron!”\n\n“It is always as the good patriot says,” observed the functionary. “Rise and dress yourself, emigrant.”\n\nDarnay complied, and was taken back to the guard-house, where other patriots in rough red caps were smoking, drinking, and sleeping, by a watch-fire. Here he paid a heavy price for his escort, and hence he started with it on the wet, wet roads at three o’clock in the morning.\n\nThe escort were two mounted patriots in red caps and tri-coloured cockades, armed with national muskets and sabres, who rode one on either side of him.\n\nThe escorted governed his own horse, but a loose line was attached to his bridle, the end of which one of the patriots kept girded round his wrist. In this state they set forth with the sharp rain driving in their faces: clattering at a heavy dragoon trot over the uneven town pavement, and out upon the mire-deep roads. In this state they traversed without change, except of horses and pace, all the mire-deep leagues that lay between them and the capital.\n\nThey travelled in the night, halting an hour or two after daybreak, and lying by until the twilight fell. The escort were so wretchedly clothed, that they twisted straw round their bare legs, and thatched their ragged shoulders to keep the wet off. Apart from the personal discomfort of being so attended, and apart from such considerations of present danger as arose from one of the patriots being chronically drunk, and carrying his musket very recklessly, Charles Darnay did not allow the restraint that was laid upon him to awaken any serious fears in his breast; for, he reasoned with himself that it could have no reference to the merits of an individual case that was not yet stated, and of representations, confirmable by the prisoner in the Abbaye, that were not yet made.\n\nBut when they came to the town of Beauvais--which they did at eventide, when the streets were filled with people--he could not conceal from himself that the aspect of affairs was very alarming. An ominous crowd gathered to see him dismount of the posting-yard, and many voices called out loudly, “Down with the emigrant!”\n\nHe stopped in the act of swinging himself out of his saddle, and, resuming it as his safest place, said:\n\n“Emigrant, my friends! Do you not see me here, in France, of my own will?”\n\n“You are a cursed emigrant,” cried a farrier, making at him in a furious manner through the press, hammer in hand; “and you are a cursed aristocrat!”\n\nThe postmaster interposed himself between this man and the rider’s bridle (at which he was evidently making), and soothingly said, “Let him be; let him be! He will be judged at Paris.”\n\n“Judged!” repeated the farrier, swinging his hammer. “Ay! and condemned as a traitor.” At this the crowd roared approval.\n\nChecking the postmaster, who was for turning his horse’s head to the yard (the drunken patriot sat composedly in his saddle looking on, with the line round his wrist), Darnay said, as soon as he could make his voice heard:\n\n“Friends, you deceive yourselves, or you are deceived. I am not a traitor.”\n\n“He lies!” cried the smith. “He is a traitor since the decree. His life is forfeit to the people. His cursed life is not his own!”\n\nAt the instant when Darnay saw a rush in the eyes of the crowd, which another instant would have brought upon him, the postmaster turned his horse into the yard, the escort rode in close upon his horse’s flanks, and the postmaster shut and barred the crazy double gates. The farrier struck a blow upon them with his hammer, and the crowd groaned; but, no more was done.\n\n“What is this decree that the smith spoke of?” Darnay asked the postmaster, when he had thanked him, and stood beside him in the yard.\n\n“Truly, a decree for selling the property of emigrants.”\n\n“When passed?”\n\n“On the fourteenth.”\n\n“The day I left England!”\n\n“Everybody says it is but one of several, and that there will be others--if there are not already--banishing all emigrants, and condemning all to death who return. That is what he meant when he said your life was not your own.”\n\n“But there are no such decrees yet?”\n\n“What do I know!” said the postmaster, shrugging his shoulders; “there may be, or there will be. It is all the same. What would you have?”\n\nThey rested on some straw in a loft until the middle of the night, and then rode forward again when all the town was asleep. Among the many wild changes observable on familiar things which made this wild ride unreal, not the least was the seeming rarity of sleep. After long and lonely spurring over dreary roads, they would come to a cluster of poor cottages, not steeped in darkness, but all glittering with lights, and would find the people, in a ghostly manner in the dead of the night, circling hand in hand round a shrivelled tree of Liberty, or all drawn up together singing a Liberty song. Happily, however, there was sleep in Beauvais that night to help them out of it and they passed on once more into solitude and loneliness: jingling through the untimely cold and wet, among impoverished fields that had yielded no fruits of the earth that year, diversified by the blackened remains of burnt houses, and by the sudden emergence from ambuscade, and sharp reining up across their way, of patriot patrols on the watch on all the roads.\n\nDaylight at last found them before the wall of Paris. The barrier was closed and strongly guarded when they rode up to it.\n\n“Where are the papers of this prisoner?” demanded a resolute-looking man in authority, who was summoned out by the guard.\n\nNaturally struck by the disagreeable word, Charles Darnay requested the speaker to take notice that he was a free traveller and French citizen, in charge of an escort which the disturbed state of the country had imposed upon him, and which he had paid for.\n\n“Where,” repeated the same personage, without taking any heed of him whatever, “are the papers of this prisoner?”\n\nThe drunken patriot had them in his cap, and produced them. Casting his eyes over Gabelle’s letter, the same personage in authority showed some disorder and surprise, and looked at Darnay with a close attention.\n\nHe left escort and escorted without saying a word, however, and went into the guard-room; meanwhile, they sat upon their horses outside the gate. Looking about him while in this state of suspense, Charles Darnay observed that the gate was held by a mixed guard of soldiers and patriots, the latter far outnumbering the former; and that while ingress into the city for peasants’ carts bringing in supplies, and for similar traffic and traffickers, was easy enough, egress, even for the homeliest people, was very difficult. A numerous medley of men and women, not to mention beasts and vehicles of various sorts, was waiting to issue forth; but, the previous identification was so strict, that they filtered through the barrier very slowly. Some of these people knew their turn for examination to be so far off, that they lay down on the ground to sleep or smoke, while others talked together, or loitered about. The red cap and tri-colour cockade were universal, both among men and women.\n\nWhen he had sat in his saddle some half-hour, taking note of these things, Darnay found himself confronted by the same man in authority, who directed the guard to open the barrier. Then he delivered to the escort, drunk and sober, a receipt for the escorted, and requested him to dismount. He did so, and the two patriots, leading his tired horse, turned and rode away without entering the city.\n\nHe accompanied his conductor into a guard-room, smelling of common wine and tobacco, where certain soldiers and patriots, asleep and awake, drunk and sober, and in various neutral states between sleeping and waking, drunkenness and sobriety, were standing and lying about. The light in the guard-house, half derived from the waning oil-lamps of the night, and half from the overcast day, was in a correspondingly uncertain condition. Some registers were lying open on a desk, and an officer of a coarse, dark aspect, presided over these.\n\n“Citizen Defarge,” said he to Darnay’s conductor, as he took a slip of paper to write on. “Is this the emigrant Evrémonde?”\n\n“This is the man.”\n\n“Your age, Evrémonde?”\n\n“Thirty-seven.”\n\n“Married, Evrémonde?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Where married?”\n\n“In England.”\n\n“Without doubt. Where is your wife, Evrémonde?”\n\n“In England.”\n\n“Without doubt. You are consigned, Evrémonde, to the prison of La Force.”\n\n“Just Heaven!” exclaimed Darnay. “Under what law, and for what offence?”\n\nThe officer looked up from his slip of paper for a moment.\n\n“We have new laws, Evrémonde, and new offences, since you were here.” He said it with a hard smile, and went on writing.\n\n“I entreat you to observe that I have come here voluntarily, in response to that written appeal of a fellow-countryman which lies before you. I demand no more than the opportunity to do so without delay. Is not that my right?”\n\n“Emigrants have no rights, Evrémonde,” was the stolid reply. The officer wrote until he had finished, read over to himself what he had written, sanded it, and handed it to Defarge, with the words “In secret.”\n\nDefarge motioned with the paper to the prisoner that he must accompany him. The prisoner obeyed, and a guard of two armed patriots attended them.\n\n“Is it you,” said Defarge, in a low voice, as they went down the guardhouse steps and turned into Paris, “who married the daughter of Doctor Manette, once a prisoner in the Bastille that is no more?”\n\n“Yes,” replied Darnay, looking at him with surprise.\n\n“My name is Defarge, and I keep a wine-shop in the Quarter Saint Antoine. Possibly you have heard of me.”\n\n“My wife came to your house to reclaim her father? Yes!”\n\nThe word “wife” seemed to serve as a gloomy reminder to Defarge, to say with sudden impatience, “In the name of that sharp female newly-born, and called La Guillotine, why did you come to France?”\n\n“You heard me say why, a minute ago. Do you not believe it is the truth?”\n\n“A bad truth for you,” said Defarge, speaking with knitted brows, and looking straight before him.\n\n“Indeed I am lost here. All here is so unprecedented, so changed, so sudden and unfair, that I am absolutely lost. Will you render me a little help?”\n\n“None.” Defarge spoke, always looking straight before him.\n\n“Will you answer me a single question?”\n\n“Perhaps. According to its nature. You can say what it is.”\n\n“In this prison that I am going to so unjustly, shall I have some free communication with the world outside?”\n\n“You will see.”\n\n“I am not to be buried there, prejudged, and without any means of presenting my case?”\n\n“You will see. But, what then? Other people have been similarly buried in worse prisons, before now.”\n\n“But never by me, Citizen Defarge.”\n\nDefarge glanced darkly at him for answer, and walked on in a steady and set silence. The deeper he sank into this silence, the fainter hope there was--or so Darnay thought--of his softening in any slight degree. He, therefore, made haste to say:\n\n“It is of the utmost importance to me (you know, Citizen, even better than I, of how much importance), that I should be able to communicate to Mr. Lorry of Tellson’s Bank, an English gentleman who is now in Paris, the simple fact, without comment, that I have been thrown into the prison of La Force. Will you cause that to be done for me?”\n\n“I will do,” Defarge doggedly rejoined, “nothing for you. My duty is to my country and the People. I am the sworn servant of both, against you. I will do nothing for you.”\n\nCharles Darnay felt it hopeless to entreat him further, and his pride was touched besides. As they walked on in silence, he could not but see how used the people were to the spectacle of prisoners passing along the streets. The very children scarcely noticed him. A few passers turned their heads, and a few shook their fingers at him as an aristocrat; otherwise, that a man in good clothes should be going to prison, was no more remarkable than that a labourer in working clothes should be going to work. In one narrow, dark, and dirty street through which they passed, an excited orator, mounted on a stool, was addressing an excited audience on the crimes against the people, of the king and the royal family. The few words that he caught from this man’s lips, first made it known to Charles Darnay that the king was in prison, and that the foreign ambassadors had one and all left Paris. On the road (except at Beauvais) he had heard absolutely nothing. The escort and the universal watchfulness had completely isolated him.\n\nThat he had fallen among far greater dangers than those which had developed themselves when he left England, he of course knew now. That perils had thickened about him fast, and might thicken faster and faster yet, he of course knew now. He could not but admit to himself that he might not have made this journey, if he could have foreseen the events of a few days. And yet his misgivings were not so dark as, imagined by the light of this later time, they would appear. Troubled as the future was, it was the unknown future, and in its obscurity there was ignorant hope. The horrible massacre, days and nights long, which, within a few rounds of the clock, was to set a great mark of blood upon the blessed garnering time of harvest, was as far out of his knowledge as if it had been a hundred thousand years away. The “sharp female newly-born, and called La Guillotine,” was hardly known to him, or to the generality of people, by name. The frightful deeds that were to be soon done, were probably unimagined at that time in the brains of the doers. How could they have a place in the shadowy conceptions of a gentle mind?\n\nOf unjust treatment in detention and hardship, and in cruel separation from his wife and child, he foreshadowed the likelihood, or the certainty; but, beyond this, he dreaded nothing distinctly. With this on his mind, which was enough to carry into a dreary prison courtyard, he arrived at the prison of La Force.\n\nA man with a bloated face opened the strong wicket, to whom Defarge presented “The Emigrant Evrémonde.”\n\n“What the Devil! How many more of them!” exclaimed the man with the bloated face.\n\nDefarge took his receipt without noticing the exclamation, and withdrew, with his two fellow-patriots.\n\n“What the Devil, I say again!” exclaimed the gaoler, left with his wife. “How many more!”\n\nThe gaoler’s wife, being provided with no answer to the question, merely replied, “One must have patience, my dear!” Three turnkeys who entered responsive to a bell she rang, echoed the sentiment, and one added, “For the love of Liberty;” which sounded in that place like an inappropriate conclusion.\n\nThe prison of La Force was a gloomy prison, dark and filthy, and with a horrible smell of foul sleep in it. Extraordinary how soon the noisome flavour of imprisoned sleep, becomes manifest in all such places that are ill cared for!\n\n“In secret, too,” grumbled the gaoler, looking at the written paper. “As if I was not already full to bursting!”\n\nHe stuck the paper on a file, in an ill-humour, and Charles Darnay awaited his further pleasure for half an hour: sometimes, pacing to and fro in the strong arched room: sometimes, resting on a stone seat: in either case detained to be imprinted on the memory of the chief and his subordinates.\n\n“Come!” said the chief, at length taking up his keys, “come with me, emigrant.”\n\nThrough the dismal prison twilight, his new charge accompanied him by corridor and staircase, many doors clanging and locking behind them, until they came into a large, low, vaulted chamber, crowded with prisoners of both sexes. The women were seated at a long table, reading and writing, knitting, sewing, and embroidering; the men were for the most part standing behind their chairs, or lingering up and down the room.\n\nIn the instinctive association of prisoners with shameful crime and disgrace, the new-comer recoiled from this company. But the crowning unreality of his long unreal ride, was, their all at once rising to receive him, with every refinement of manner known to the time, and with all the engaging graces and courtesies of life.\n\nSo strangely clouded were these refinements by the prison manners and gloom, so spectral did they become in the inappropriate squalor and misery through which they were seen, that Charles Darnay seemed to stand in a company of the dead. Ghosts all! The ghost of beauty, the ghost of stateliness, the ghost of elegance, the ghost of pride, the ghost of frivolity, the ghost of wit, the ghost of youth, the ghost of age, all waiting their dismissal from the desolate shore, all turning on him eyes that were changed by the death they had died in coming there.\n\nIt struck him motionless. The gaoler standing at his side, and the other gaolers moving about, who would have been well enough as to appearance in the ordinary exercise of their functions, looked so extravagantly coarse contrasted with sorrowing mothers and blooming daughters who were there--with the apparitions of the coquette, the young beauty, and the mature woman delicately bred--that the inversion of all experience and likelihood which the scene of shadows presented, was heightened to its utmost. Surely, ghosts all. Surely, the long unreal ride some progress of disease that had brought him to these gloomy shades!\n\n“In the name of the assembled companions in misfortune,” said a gentleman of courtly appearance and address, coming forward, “I have the honour of giving you welcome to La Force, and of condoling with you on the calamity that has brought you among us. May it soon terminate happily! It would be an impertinence elsewhere, but it is not so here, to ask your name and condition?”\n\nCharles Darnay roused himself, and gave the required information, in words as suitable as he could find.\n\n“But I hope,” said the gentleman, following the chief gaoler with his eyes, who moved across the room, “that you are not in secret?”\n\n“I do not understand the meaning of the term, but I have heard them say so.”\n\n“Ah, what a pity! We so much regret it! But take courage; several members of our society have been in secret, at first, and it has lasted but a short time.” Then he added, raising his voice, “I grieve to inform the society--in secret.”\n\nThere was a murmur of commiseration as Charles Darnay crossed the room to a grated door where the gaoler awaited him, and many voices--among which, the soft and compassionate voices of women were conspicuous--gave him good wishes and encouragement. He turned at the grated door, to render the thanks of his heart; it closed under the gaoler’s hand; and the apparitions vanished from his sight forever.\n\nThe wicket opened on a stone staircase, leading upward. When they had ascended forty steps (the prisoner of half an hour already counted them), the gaoler opened a low black door, and they passed into a solitary cell. It struck cold and damp, but was not dark.\n\n“Yours,” said the gaoler.\n\n“Why am I confined alone?”\n\n“How do I know!”\n\n“I can buy pen, ink, and paper?”\n\n“Such are not my orders. You will be visited, and can ask then. At present, you may buy your food, and nothing more.”\n\nThere were in the cell, a chair, a table, and a straw mattress. As the gaoler made a general inspection of these objects, and of the four walls, before going out, a wandering fancy wandered through the mind of the prisoner leaning against the wall opposite to him, that this gaoler was so unwholesomely bloated, both in face and person, as to look like a man who had been drowned and filled with water. When the gaoler was gone, he thought in the same wandering way, “Now am I left, as if I were dead.” Stopping then, to look down at the mattress, he turned from it with a sick feeling, and thought, “And here in these crawling creatures is the first condition of the body after death.”\n\n“Five paces by four and a half, five paces by four and a half, five paces by four and a half.” The prisoner walked to and fro in his cell, counting its measurement, and the roar of the city arose like muffled drums with a wild swell of voices added to them. “He made shoes, he made shoes, he made shoes.” The prisoner counted the measurement again, and paced faster, to draw his mind with him from that latter repetition. “The ghosts that vanished when the wicket closed. There was one among them, the appearance of a lady dressed in black, who was leaning in the embrasure of a window, and she had a light shining upon her golden hair, and she looked like * * * * Let us ride on again, for God’s sake, through the illuminated villages with the people all awake! * * * * He made shoes, he made shoes, he made shoes. * * * * Five paces by four and a half.” With such scraps tossing and rolling upward from the depths of his mind, the prisoner walked faster and faster, obstinately counting and counting; and the roar of the city changed to this extent--that it still rolled in like muffled drums, but with the wail of voices that he knew, in the swell that rose above them.\n\n# CHAPTER II. The Grindstone\n\nTellson’s Bank, established in the Saint Germain Quarter of Paris, was in a wing of a large house, approached by a courtyard and shut off from the street by a high wall and a strong gate. The house belonged to a great nobleman who had lived in it until he made a flight from the troubles, in his own cook’s dress, and got across the borders. A mere beast of the chase flying from hunters, he was still in his metempsychosis no other than the same Monseigneur, the preparation of whose chocolate for whose lips had once occupied three strong men besides the cook in question.\n\nMonseigneur gone, and the three strong men absolving themselves from the sin of having drawn his high wages, by being more than ready and willing to cut his throat on the altar of the dawning Republic one and indivisible of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, Monseigneur’s house had been first sequestrated, and then confiscated. For, all things moved so fast, and decree followed decree with that fierce precipitation, that now upon the third night of the autumn month of September, patriot emissaries of the law were in possession of Monseigneur’s house, and had marked it with the tri-colour, and were drinking brandy in its state apartments.\n\nA place of business in London like Tellson’s place of business in Paris, would soon have driven the House out of its mind and into the Gazette. For, what would staid British responsibility and respectability have said to orange-trees in boxes in a Bank courtyard, and even to a Cupid over the counter? Yet such things were. Tellson’s had whitewashed the Cupid, but he was still to be seen on the ceiling, in the coolest linen, aiming (as he very often does) at money from morning to night. Bankruptcy must inevitably have come of this young Pagan, in Lombard-street, London, and also of a curtained alcove in the rear of the immortal boy, and also of a looking-glass let into the wall, and also of clerks not at all old, who danced in public on the slightest provocation. Yet, a French Tellson’s could get on with these things exceedingly well, and, as long as the times held together, no man had taken fright at them, and drawn out his money.\n\nWhat money would be drawn out of Tellson’s henceforth, and what would lie there, lost and forgotten; what plate and jewels would tarnish in Tellson’s hiding-places, while the depositors rusted in prisons, and when they should have violently perished; how many accounts with Tellson’s never to be balanced in this world, must be carried over into the next; no man could have said, that night, any more than Mr. Jarvis Lorry could, though he thought heavily of these questions. He sat by a newly-lighted wood fire (the blighted and unfruitful year was prematurely cold), and on his honest and courageous face there was a deeper shade than the pendent lamp could throw, or any object in the room distortedly reflect--a shade of horror.\n\nHe occupied rooms in the Bank, in his fidelity to the House of which he had grown to be a part, like strong root-ivy. It chanced that they derived a kind of security from the patriotic occupation of the main building, but the true-hearted old gentleman never calculated about that. All such circumstances were indifferent to him, so that he did his duty. On the opposite side of the courtyard, under a colonnade, was extensive standing--for carriages--where, indeed, some carriages of Monseigneur yet stood. Against two of the pillars were fastened two great flaring flambeaux, and in the light of these, standing out in the open air, was a large grindstone: a roughly mounted thing which appeared to have hurriedly been brought there from some neighbouring smithy, or other workshop. Rising and looking out of window at these harmless objects, Mr. Lorry shivered, and retired to his seat by the fire. He had opened, not only the glass window, but the lattice blind outside it, and he had closed both again, and he shivered through his frame.\n\nFrom the streets beyond the high wall and the strong gate, there came the usual night hum of the city, with now and then an indescribable ring in it, weird and unearthly, as if some unwonted sounds of a terrible nature were going up to Heaven.\n\n“Thank God,” said Mr. Lorry, clasping his hands, “that no one near and dear to me is in this dreadful town to-night. May He have mercy on all who are in danger!”\n\nSoon afterwards, the bell at the great gate sounded, and he thought, “They have come back!” and sat listening. But, there was no loud irruption into the courtyard, as he had expected, and he heard the gate clash again, and all was quiet.\n\nThe nervousness and dread that were upon him inspired that vague uneasiness respecting the Bank, which a great change would naturally awaken, with such feelings roused. It was well guarded, and he got up to go among the trusty people who were watching it, when his door suddenly opened, and two figures rushed in, at sight of which he fell back in amazement.\n\nLucie and her father! Lucie with her arms stretched out to him, and with that old look of earnestness so concentrated and intensified, that it seemed as though it had been stamped upon her face expressly to give force and power to it in this one passage of her life.\n\n“What is this?” cried Mr. Lorry, breathless and confused. “What is the matter? Lucie! Manette! What has happened? What has brought you here? What is it?”\n\nWith the look fixed upon him, in her paleness and wildness, she panted out in his arms, imploringly, “O my dear friend! My husband!”\n\n“Your husband, Lucie?”\n\n“Charles.”\n\n“What of Charles?”\n“Here.\n\n“Here, in Paris?”\n\n“Has been here some days--three or four--I don’t know how many--I can’t collect my thoughts. An errand of generosity brought him here unknown to us; he was stopped at the barrier, and sent to prison.”\n\nThe old man uttered an irrepressible cry. Almost at the same moment, the bell of the great gate rang again, and a loud noise of feet and voices came pouring into the courtyard.\n\n“What is that noise?” said the Doctor, turning towards the window.\n\n“Don’t look!” cried Mr. Lorry. “Don’t look out! Manette, for your life, don’t touch the blind!”\n\nThe Doctor turned, with his hand upon the fastening of the window, and said, with a cool, bold smile:\n\n“My dear friend, I have a charmed life in this city. I have been a Bastille prisoner. There is no patriot in Paris--in Paris? In France--who, knowing me to have been a prisoner in the Bastille, would touch me, except to overwhelm me with embraces, or carry me in triumph. My old pain has given me a power that has brought us through the barrier, and gained us news of Charles there, and brought us here. I knew it would be so; I knew I could help Charles out of all danger; I told Lucie so.--What is that noise?” His hand was again upon the window.\n\n“Don’t look!” cried Mr. Lorry, absolutely desperate. “No, Lucie, my dear, nor you!” He got his arm round her, and held her. “Don’t be so terrified, my love. I solemnly swear to you that I know of no harm having happened to Charles; that I had no suspicion even of his being in this fatal place. What prison is he in?”\n“La Force!”\n\n“La Force! Lucie, my child, if ever you were brave and serviceable in your life--and you were always both--you will compose yourself now, to do exactly as I bid you; for more depends upon it than you can think, or I can say. There is no help for you in any action on your part to-night; you cannot possibly stir out. I say this, because what I must bid you to do for Charles’s sake, is the hardest thing to do of all. You must instantly be obedient, still, and quiet. You must let me put you in a room at the back here. You must leave your father and me alone for two minutes, and as there are Life and Death in the world you must not delay.”\n\n“I will be submissive to you. I see in your face that you know I can do nothing else than this. I know you are true.”\n\nThe old man kissed her, and hurried her into his room, and turned the key; then, came hurrying back to the Doctor, and opened the window and partly opened the blind, and put his hand upon the Doctor’s arm, and looked out with him into the courtyard.\n\nLooked out upon a throng of men and women: not enough in number, or near enough, to fill the courtyard: not more than forty or fifty in all. The people in possession of the house had let them in at the gate, and they had rushed in to work at the grindstone; it had evidently been set up there for their purpose, as in a convenient and retired spot.\n\nBut, such awful workers, and such awful work!\n\nThe grindstone had a double handle, and, turning at it madly were two men, whose faces, as their long hair flapped back when the whirlings of the grindstone brought their faces up, were more horrible and cruel than the visages of the wildest savages in their most barbarous disguise. False eyebrows and false moustaches were stuck upon them, and their hideous countenances were all bloody and sweaty, and all awry with howling, and all staring and glaring with beastly excitement and want of sleep. As these ruffians turned and turned, their matted locks now flung forward over their eyes, now flung backward over their necks, some women held wine to their mouths that they might drink; and what with dropping blood, and what with dropping wine, and what with the stream of sparks struck out of the stone, all their wicked atmosphere seemed gore and fire. The eye could not detect one creature in the group free from the smear of blood. Shouldering one another to get next at the sharpening-stone, were men stripped to the waist, with the stain all over their limbs and bodies; men in all sorts of rags, with the stain upon those rags; men devilishly set off with spoils of women’s lace and silk and ribbon, with the stain dyeing those trifles through and through. Hatchets, knives, bayonets, swords, all brought to be sharpened, were all red with it. Some of the hacked swords were tied to the wrists of those who carried them, with strips of linen and fragments of dress: ligatures various in kind, but all deep of the one colour. And as the frantic wielders of these weapons snatched them from the stream of sparks and tore away into the streets, the same red hue was red in their frenzied eyes;--eyes which any unbrutalised beholder would have given twenty years of life, to petrify with a well-directed gun.\n\nAll this was seen in a moment, as the vision of a drowning man, or of any human creature at any very great pass, could see a world if it were there. They drew back from the window, and the Doctor looked for explanation in his friend’s ashy face.\n\n“They are,” Mr. Lorry whispered the words, glancing fearfully round at the locked room, “murdering the prisoners. If you are sure of what you say; if you really have the power you think you have--as I believe you have--make yourself known to these devils, and get taken to La Force. It may be too late, I don’t know, but let it not be a minute later!”\n\nDoctor Manette pressed his hand, hastened bareheaded out of the room, and was in the courtyard when Mr. Lorry regained the blind.\n\nHis streaming white hair, his remarkable face, and the impetuous confidence of his manner, as he put the weapons aside like water, carried him in an instant to the heart of the concourse at the stone. For a few moments there was a pause, and a hurry, and a murmur, and the unintelligible sound of his voice; and then Mr. Lorry saw him, surrounded by all, and in the midst of a line of twenty men long, all linked shoulder to shoulder, and hand to shoulder, hurried out with cries of--“Live the Bastille prisoner! Help for the Bastille prisoner’s kindred in La Force! Room for the Bastille prisoner in front there! Save the prisoner Evrémonde at La Force!” and a thousand answering shouts.\n\nHe closed the lattice again with a fluttering heart, closed the window and the curtain, hastened to Lucie, and told her that her father was assisted by the people, and gone in search of her husband. He found her child and Miss Pross with her; but, it never occurred to him to be surprised by their appearance until a long time afterwards, when he sat watching them in such quiet as the night knew.\n\nLucie had, by that time, fallen into a stupor on the floor at his feet, clinging to his hand. Miss Pross had laid the child down on his own bed, and her head had gradually fallen on the pillow beside her pretty charge. O the long, long night, with the moans of the poor wife! And O the long, long night, with no return of her father and no tidings!\n\nTwice more in the darkness the bell at the great gate sounded, and the irruption was repeated, and the grindstone whirled and spluttered. “What is it?” cried Lucie, affrighted. “Hush! The soldiers’ swords are sharpened there,” said Mr. Lorry. “The place is national property now, and used as a kind of armoury, my love.”\n\nTwice more in all; but, the last spell of work was feeble and fitful. Soon afterwards the day began to dawn, and he softly detached himself from the clasping hand, and cautiously looked out again. A man, so besmeared that he might have been a sorely wounded soldier creeping back to consciousness on a field of slain, was rising from the pavement by the side of the grindstone, and looking about him with a vacant air. Shortly, this worn-out murderer descried in the imperfect light one of the carriages of Monseigneur, and, staggering to that gorgeous vehicle, climbed in at the door, and shut himself up to take his rest on its dainty cushions.\n\nThe great grindstone, Earth, had turned when Mr. Lorry looked out again, and the sun was red on the courtyard. But, the lesser grindstone stood alone there in the calm morning air, with a red upon it that the sun had never given, and would never take away.\n\n# CHAPTER III. The Shadow\n\nOne of the first considerations which arose in the business mind of Mr. Lorry when business hours came round, was this:--that he had no right to imperil Tellson’s by sheltering the wife of an emigrant prisoner under the Bank roof. His own possessions, safety, life, he would have hazarded for Lucie and her child, without a moment’s demur; but the great trust he held was not his own, and as to that business charge he was a strict man of business.\n\nAt first, his mind reverted to Defarge, and he thought of finding out the wine-shop again and taking counsel with its master in reference to the safest dwelling-place in the distracted state of the city. But, the same consideration that suggested him, repudiated him; he lived in the most violent Quarter, and doubtless was influential there, and deep in its dangerous workings.\n\nNoon coming, and the Doctor not returning, and every minute’s delay tending to compromise Tellson’s, Mr. Lorry advised with Lucie. She said that her father had spoken of hiring a lodging for a short term, in that Quarter, near the Banking-house. As there was no business objection to this, and as he foresaw that even if it were all well with Charles, and he were to be released, he could not hope to leave the city, Mr. Lorry went out in quest of such a lodging, and found a suitable one, high up in a removed by-street where the closed blinds in all the other windows of a high melancholy square of buildings marked deserted homes.\n\nTo this lodging he at once removed Lucie and her child, and Miss Pross: giving them what comfort he could, and much more than he had himself. He left Jerry with them, as a figure to fill a doorway that would bear considerable knocking on the head, and returned to his own occupations. A disturbed and doleful mind he brought to bear upon them, and slowly and heavily the day lagged on with him.\n\nIt wore itself out, and wore him out with it, until the Bank closed. He was again alone in his room of the previous night, considering what to do next, when he heard a foot upon the stair. In a few moments, a man stood in his presence, who, with a keenly observant look at him, addressed him by his name.\n\n“Your servant,” said Mr. Lorry. “Do you know me?”\n\nHe was a strongly made man with dark curling hair, from forty-five to fifty years of age. For answer he repeated, without any change of emphasis, the words:\n\n“Do you know me?”\n\n“I have seen you somewhere.”\n\n“Perhaps at my wine-shop?”\n\nMuch interested and agitated, Mr. Lorry said: “You come from Doctor Manette?”\n\n“Yes. I come from Doctor Manette.”\n\n“And what says he? What does he send me?”\n\nDefarge gave into his anxious hand, an open scrap of paper. It bore the words in the Doctor’s writing:\n\n“Charles is safe, but I cannot safely leave this place yet. I have obtained the favour that the bearer has a short note from Charles to his wife. Let the bearer see his wife.”\n\nIt was dated from La Force, within an hour.\n\n“Will you accompany me,” said Mr. Lorry, joyfully relieved after reading this note aloud, “to where his wife resides?”\n\n“Yes,” returned Defarge.\n\nScarcely noticing as yet, in what a curiously reserved and mechanical way Defarge spoke, Mr. Lorry put on his hat and they went down into the courtyard. There, they found two women; one, knitting.\n\n“Madame Defarge, surely!” said Mr. Lorry, who had left her in exactly the same attitude some seventeen years ago.\n\n“It is she,” observed her husband.\n\n“Does Madame go with us?” inquired Mr. Lorry, seeing that she moved as they moved.\n\n“Yes. That she may be able to recognise the faces and know the persons. It is for their safety.”\n\nBeginning to be struck by Defarge’s manner, Mr. Lorry looked dubiously at him, and led the way. Both the women followed; the second woman being The Vengeance.\n\nThey passed through the intervening streets as quickly as they might, ascended the staircase of the new domicile, were admitted by Jerry, and found Lucie weeping, alone. She was thrown into a transport by the tidings Mr. Lorry gave her of her husband, and clasped the hand that delivered his note--little thinking what it had been doing near him in the night, and might, but for a chance, have done to him.\n\n“DEAREST,--Take courage. I am well, and your father has influence around me. You cannot answer this. Kiss our child for me.”\n\nThat was all the writing. It was so much, however, to her who received it, that she turned from Defarge to his wife, and kissed one of the hands that knitted. It was a passionate, loving, thankful, womanly action, but the hand made no response--dropped cold and heavy, and took to its knitting again.\n\nThere was something in its touch that gave Lucie a check. She stopped in the act of putting the note in her bosom, and, with her hands yet at her neck, looked terrified at Madame Defarge. Madame Defarge met the lifted eyebrows and forehead with a cold, impassive stare.\n\n“My dear,” said Mr. Lorry, striking in to explain; “there are frequent risings in the streets; and, although it is not likely they will ever trouble you, Madame Defarge wishes to see those whom she has the power to protect at such times, to the end that she may know them--that she may identify them. I believe,” said Mr. Lorry, rather halting in his reassuring words, as the stony manner of all the three impressed itself upon him more and more, “I state the case, Citizen Defarge?”\n\nDefarge looked gloomily at his wife, and gave no other answer than a gruff sound of acquiescence.\n\n“You had better, Lucie,” said Mr. Lorry, doing all he could to propitiate, by tone and manner, “have the dear child here, and our good Pross. Our good Pross, Defarge, is an English lady, and knows no French.”\n\nThe lady in question, whose rooted conviction that she was more than a match for any foreigner, was not to be shaken by distress and, danger, appeared with folded arms, and observed in English to The Vengeance, whom her eyes first encountered, “Well, I am sure, Boldface! I hope you are pretty well!” She also bestowed a British cough on Madame Defarge; but, neither of the two took much heed of her.\n\n“Is that his child?” said Madame Defarge, stopping in her work for the first time, and pointing her knitting-needle at little Lucie as if it were the finger of Fate.\n\n“Yes, madame,” answered Mr. Lorry; “this is our poor prisoner’s darling daughter, and only child.”\n\nThe shadow attendant on Madame Defarge and her party seemed to fall so threatening and dark on the child, that her mother instinctively kneeled on the ground beside her, and held her to her breast. The shadow attendant on Madame Defarge and her party seemed then to fall, threatening and dark, on both the mother and the child.\n\n“It is enough, my husband,” said Madame Defarge. “I have seen them. We may go.”\n\nBut, the suppressed manner had enough of menace in it--not visible and presented, but indistinct and withheld--to alarm Lucie into saying, as she laid her appealing hand on Madame Defarge’s dress:\n\n“You will be good to my poor husband. You will do him no harm. You will help me to see him if you can?”\n\n“Your husband is not my business here,” returned Madame Defarge, looking down at her with perfect composure. “It is the daughter of your father who is my business here.”\n\n“For my sake, then, be merciful to my husband. For my child’s sake! She will put her hands together and pray you to be merciful. We are more afraid of you than of these others.”\n\nMadame Defarge received it as a compliment, and looked at her husband. Defarge, who had been uneasily biting his thumb-nail and looking at her, collected his face into a sterner expression.\n\n“What is it that your husband says in that little letter?” asked Madame Defarge, with a lowering smile. “Influence; he says something touching influence?”\n\n“That my father,” said Lucie, hurriedly taking the paper from her breast, but with her alarmed eyes on her questioner and not on it, “has much influence around him.”\n\n“Surely it will release him!” said Madame Defarge. “Let it do so.”\n\n“As a wife and mother,” cried Lucie, most earnestly, “I implore you to have pity on me and not to exercise any power that you possess, against my innocent husband, but to use it in his behalf. O sister-woman, think of me. As a wife and mother!”\n\nMadame Defarge looked, coldly as ever, at the suppliant, and said, turning to her friend The Vengeance:\n\n“The wives and mothers we have been used to see, since we were as little as this child, and much less, have not been greatly considered? We have known their husbands and fathers laid in prison and kept from them, often enough? All our lives, we have seen our sister-women suffer, in themselves and in their children, poverty, nakedness, hunger, thirst, sickness, misery, oppression and neglect of all kinds?”\n\n“We have seen nothing else,” returned The Vengeance.\n\n“We have borne this a long time,” said Madame Defarge, turning her eyes again upon Lucie. “Judge you! Is it likely that the trouble of one wife and mother would be much to us now?”\n\nShe resumed her knitting and went out. The Vengeance followed. Defarge went last, and closed the door.\n\n“Courage, my dear Lucie,” said Mr. Lorry, as he raised her. “Courage, courage! So far all goes well with us--much, much better than it has of late gone with many poor souls. Cheer up, and have a thankful heart.”\n\n“I am not thankless, I hope, but that dreadful woman seems to throw a shadow on me and on all my hopes.”\n\n“Tut, tut!” said Mr. Lorry; “what is this despondency in the brave little breast? A shadow indeed! No substance in it, Lucie.”\n\nBut the shadow of the manner of these Defarges was dark upon himself, for all that, and in his secret mind it troubled him greatly.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. Calm in Storm\n\nDoctor Manette did not return until the morning of the fourth day of his absence. So much of what had happened in that dreadful time as could be kept from the knowledge of Lucie was so well concealed from her, that not until long afterwards, when France and she were far apart, did she know that eleven hundred defenceless prisoners of both sexes and all ages had been killed by the populace; that four days and nights had been darkened by this deed of horror; and that the air around her had been tainted by the slain. She only knew that there had been an attack upon the prisons, that all political prisoners had been in danger, and that some had been dragged out by the crowd and murdered.\n\nTo Mr. Lorry, the Doctor communicated under an injunction of secrecy on which he had no need to dwell, that the crowd had taken him through a scene of carnage to the prison of La Force. That, in the prison he had found a self-appointed Tribunal sitting, before which the prisoners were brought singly, and by which they were rapidly ordered to be put forth to be massacred, or to be released, or (in a few cases) to be sent back to their cells. That, presented by his conductors to this Tribunal, he had announced himself by name and profession as having been for eighteen years a secret and unaccused prisoner in the Bastille; that, one of the body so sitting in judgment had risen and identified him, and that this man was Defarge.\n\nThat, hereupon he had ascertained, through the registers on the table, that his son-in-law was among the living prisoners, and had pleaded hard to the Tribunal--of whom some members were asleep and some awake, some dirty with murder and some clean, some sober and some not--for his life and liberty. That, in the first frantic greetings lavished on himself as a notable sufferer under the overthrown system, it had been accorded to him to have Charles Darnay brought before the lawless Court, and examined. That, he seemed on the point of being at once released, when the tide in his favour met with some unexplained check (not intelligible to the Doctor), which led to a few words of secret conference. That, the man sitting as President had then informed Doctor Manette that the prisoner must remain in custody, but should, for his sake, be held inviolate in safe custody. That, immediately, on a signal, the prisoner was removed to the interior of the prison again; but, that he, the Doctor, had then so strongly pleaded for permission to remain and assure himself that his son-in-law was, through no malice or mischance, delivered to the concourse whose murderous yells outside the gate had often drowned the proceedings, that he had obtained the permission, and had remained in that Hall of Blood until the danger was over.\n\nThe sights he had seen there, with brief snatches of food and sleep by intervals, shall remain untold. The mad joy over the prisoners who were saved, had astounded him scarcely less than the mad ferocity against those who were cut to pieces. One prisoner there was, he said, who had been discharged into the street free, but at whom a mistaken savage had thrust a pike as he passed out. Being besought to go to him and dress the wound, the Doctor had passed out at the same gate, and had found him in the arms of a company of Samaritans, who were seated on the bodies of their victims. With an inconsistency as monstrous as anything in this awful nightmare, they had helped the healer, and tended the wounded man with the gentlest solicitude--had made a litter for him and escorted him carefully from the spot--had then caught up their weapons and plunged anew into a butchery so dreadful, that the Doctor had covered his eyes with his hands, and swooned away in the midst of it.\n\nAs Mr. Lorry received these confidences, and as he watched the face of his friend now sixty-two years of age, a misgiving arose within him that such dread experiences would revive the old danger.\n\nBut, he had never seen his friend in his present aspect: he had never at all known him in his present character. For the first time the Doctor felt, now, that his suffering was strength and power. For the first time he felt that in that sharp fire, he had slowly forged the iron which could break the prison door of his daughter’s husband, and deliver him. “It all tended to a good end, my friend; it was not mere waste and ruin. As my beloved child was helpful in restoring me to myself, I will be helpful now in restoring the dearest part of herself to her; by the aid of Heaven I will do it!” Thus, Doctor Manette. And when Jarvis Lorry saw the kindled eyes, the resolute face, the calm strong look and bearing of the man whose life always seemed to him to have been stopped, like a clock, for so many years, and then set going again with an energy which had lain dormant during the cessation of its usefulness, he believed.\n\nGreater things than the Doctor had at that time to contend with, would have yielded before his persevering purpose. While he kept himself in his place, as a physician, whose business was with all degrees of mankind, bond and free, rich and poor, bad and good, he used his personal influence so wisely, that he was soon the inspecting physician of three prisons, and among them of La Force. He could now assure Lucie that her husband was no longer confined alone, but was mixed with the general body of prisoners; he saw her husband weekly, and brought sweet messages to her, straight from his lips; sometimes her husband himself sent a letter to her (though never by the Doctor’s hand), but she was not permitted to write to him: for, among the many wild suspicions of plots in the prisons, the wildest of all pointed at emigrants who were known to have made friends or permanent connections abroad.\n\nThis new life of the Doctor’s was an anxious life, no doubt; still, the sagacious Mr. Lorry saw that there was a new sustaining pride in it. Nothing unbecoming tinged the pride; it was a natural and worthy one; but he observed it as a curiosity. The Doctor knew, that up to that time, his imprisonment had been associated in the minds of his daughter and his friend, with his personal affliction, deprivation, and weakness. Now that this was changed, and he knew himself to be invested through that old trial with forces to which they both looked for Charles’s ultimate safety and deliverance, he became so far exalted by the change, that he took the lead and direction, and required them as the weak, to trust to him as the strong. The preceding relative positions of himself and Lucie were reversed, yet only as the liveliest gratitude and affection could reverse them, for he could have had no pride but in rendering some service to her who had rendered so much to him. “All curious to see,” thought Mr. Lorry, in his amiably shrewd way, “but all natural and right; so, take the lead, my dear friend, and keep it; it couldn’t be in better hands.”\n\nBut, though the Doctor tried hard, and never ceased trying, to get Charles Darnay set at liberty, or at least to get him brought to trial, the public current of the time set too strong and fast for him. The new era began; the king was tried, doomed, and beheaded; the Republic of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, declared for victory or death against the world in arms; the black flag waved night and day from the great towers of Notre Dame; three hundred thousand men, summoned to rise against the tyrants of the earth, rose from all the varying soils of France, as if the dragon’s teeth had been sown broadcast, and had yielded fruit equally on hill and plain, on rock, in gravel, and alluvial mud, under the bright sky of the South and under the clouds of the North, in fell and forest, in the vineyards and the olive-grounds and among the cropped grass and the stubble of the corn, along the fruitful banks of the broad rivers, and in the sand of the sea-shore. What private solicitude could rear itself against the deluge of the Year One of Liberty--the deluge rising from below, not falling from above, and with the windows of Heaven shut, not opened!\n\nThere was no pause, no pity, no peace, no interval of relenting rest, no measurement of time. Though days and nights circled as regularly as when time was young, and the evening and morning were the first day, other count of time there was none. Hold of it was lost in the raging fever of a nation, as it is in the fever of one patient. Now, breaking the unnatural silence of a whole city, the executioner showed the people the head of the king--and now, it seemed almost in the same breath, the head of his fair wife which had had eight weary months of imprisoned widowhood and misery, to turn it grey.\n\nAnd yet, observing the strange law of contradiction which obtains in all such cases, the time was long, while it flamed by so fast. A revolutionary tribunal in the capital, and forty or fifty thousand revolutionary committees all over the land; a law of the Suspected, which struck away all security for liberty or life, and delivered over any good and innocent person to any bad and guilty one; prisons gorged with people who had committed no offence, and could obtain no hearing; these things became the established order and nature of appointed things, and seemed to be ancient usage before they were many weeks old. Above all, one hideous figure grew as familiar as if it had been before the general gaze from the foundations of the world--the figure of the sharp female called La Guillotine.\n\nIt was the popular theme for jests; it was the best cure for headache, it infallibly prevented the hair from turning grey, it imparted a peculiar delicacy to the complexion, it was the National Razor which shaved close: who kissed La Guillotine, looked through the little window and sneezed into the sack. It was the sign of the regeneration of the human race. It superseded the Cross. Models of it were worn on breasts from which the Cross was discarded, and it was bowed down to and believed in where the Cross was denied.\n\nIt sheared off heads so many, that it, and the ground it most polluted, were a rotten red. It was taken to pieces, like a toy-puzzle for a young Devil, and was put together again when the occasion wanted it. It hushed the eloquent, struck down the powerful, abolished the beautiful and good. Twenty-two friends of high public mark, twenty-one living and one dead, it had lopped the heads off, in one morning, in as many minutes. The name of the strong man of Old Scripture had descended to the chief functionary who worked it; but, so armed, he was stronger than his namesake, and blinder, and tore away the gates of God’s own Temple every day.\n\nAmong these terrors, and the brood belonging to them, the Doctor walked with a steady head: confident in his power, cautiously persistent in his end, never doubting that he would save Lucie’s husband at last. Yet the current of the time swept by, so strong and deep, and carried the time away so fiercely, that Charles had lain in prison one year and three months when the Doctor was thus steady and confident. So much more wicked and distracted had the Revolution grown in that December month, that the rivers of the South were encumbered with the bodies of the violently drowned by night, and prisoners were shot in lines and squares under the southern wintry sun. Still, the Doctor walked among the terrors with a steady head. No man better known than he, in Paris at that day; no man in a stranger situation. Silent, humane, indispensable in hospital and prison, using his art equally among assassins and victims, he was a man apart. In the exercise of his skill, the appearance and the story of the Bastille Captive removed him from all other men. He was not suspected or brought in question, any more than if he had indeed been recalled to life some eighteen years before, or were a Spirit moving among mortals.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Wood-Sawyer\n\nOne year and three months. During all that time Lucie was never sure, from hour to hour, but that the Guillotine would strike off her husband’s head next day. Every day, through the stony streets, the tumbrils now jolted heavily, filled with Condemned. Lovely girls; bright women, brown-haired, black-haired, and grey; youths; stalwart men and old; gentle born and peasant born; all red wine for La Guillotine, all daily brought into light from the dark cellars of the loathsome prisons, and carried to her through the streets to slake her devouring thirst. Liberty, equality, fraternity, or death;--the last, much the easiest to bestow, O Guillotine!\n\nIf the suddenness of her calamity, and the whirling wheels of the time, had stunned the Doctor’s daughter into awaiting the result in idle despair, it would but have been with her as it was with many. But, from the hour when she had taken the white head to her fresh young bosom in the garret of Saint Antoine, she had been true to her duties. She was truest to them in the season of trial, as all the quietly loyal and good will always be.\n\nAs soon as they were established in their new residence, and her father had entered on the routine of his avocations, she arranged the little household as exactly as if her husband had been there. Everything had its appointed place and its appointed time. Little Lucie she taught, as regularly, as if they had all been united in their English home. The slight devices with which she cheated herself into the show of a belief that they would soon be reunited--the little preparations for his speedy return, the setting aside of his chair and his books--these, and the solemn prayer at night for one dear prisoner especially, among the many unhappy souls in prison and the shadow of death--were almost the only outspoken reliefs of her heavy mind.\n\nShe did not greatly alter in appearance. The plain dark dresses, akin to mourning dresses, which she and her child wore, were as neat and as well attended to as the brighter clothes of happy days. She lost her colour, and the old and intent expression was a constant, not an occasional, thing; otherwise, she remained very pretty and comely. Sometimes, at night on kissing her father, she would burst into the grief she had repressed all day, and would say that her sole reliance, under Heaven, was on him. He always resolutely answered: “Nothing can happen to him without my knowledge, and I know that I can save him, Lucie.”\n\nThey had not made the round of their changed life many weeks, when her father said to her, on coming home one evening:\n\n“My dear, there is an upper window in the prison, to which Charles can sometimes gain access at three in the afternoon. When he can get to it--which depends on many uncertainties and incidents--he might see you in the street, he thinks, if you stood in a certain place that I can show you. But you will not be able to see him, my poor child, and even if you could, it would be unsafe for you to make a sign of recognition.”\n\n“O show me the place, my father, and I will go there every day.”\n\nFrom that time, in all weathers, she waited there two hours. As the clock struck two, she was there, and at four she turned resignedly away. When it was not too wet or inclement for her child to be with her, they went together; at other times she was alone; but, she never missed a single day.\n\nIt was the dark and dirty corner of a small winding street. The hovel of a cutter of wood into lengths for burning, was the only house at that end; all else was wall. On the third day of her being there, he noticed her.\n\n“Good day, citizeness.”\n\n“Good day, citizen.”\n\nThis mode of address was now prescribed by decree. It had been established voluntarily some time ago, among the more thorough patriots; but, was now law for everybody.\n\n“Walking here again, citizeness?”\n\n“You see me, citizen!”\n\nThe wood-sawyer, who was a little man with a redundancy of gesture (he had once been a mender of roads), cast a glance at the prison, pointed at the prison, and putting his ten fingers before his face to represent bars, peeped through them jocosely.\n\n“But it’s not my business,” said he. And went on sawing his wood.\n\nNext day he was looking out for her, and accosted her the moment she appeared.\n\n“What? Walking here again, citizeness?”\n\n“Yes, citizen.”\n\n“Ah! A child too! Your mother, is it not, my little citizeness?”\n\n“Do I say yes, mamma?” whispered little Lucie, drawing close to her.\n\n“Yes, dearest.”\n\n“Yes, citizen.”\n\n“Ah! But it’s not my business. My work is my business. See my saw! I call it my Little Guillotine. La, la, la; La, la, la! And off his head comes!”\n\nThe billet fell as he spoke, and he threw it into a basket.\n\n“I call myself the Samson of the firewood guillotine. See here again! Loo, loo, loo; Loo, loo, loo! And off her head comes! Now, a child. Tickle, tickle; Pickle, pickle! And off its head comes. All the family!”\n\nLucie shuddered as he threw two more billets into his basket, but it was impossible to be there while the wood-sawyer was at work, and not be in his sight. Thenceforth, to secure his good will, she always spoke to him first, and often gave him drink-money, which he readily received.\n\nHe was an inquisitive fellow, and sometimes when she had quite forgotten him in gazing at the prison roof and grates, and in lifting her heart up to her husband, she would come to herself to find him looking at her, with his knee on his bench and his saw stopped in its work. “But it’s not my business!” he would generally say at those times, and would briskly fall to his sawing again.\n\nIn all weathers, in the snow and frost of winter, in the bitter winds of spring, in the hot sunshine of summer, in the rains of autumn, and again in the snow and frost of winter, Lucie passed two hours of every day at this place; and every day on leaving it, she kissed the prison wall. Her husband saw her (so she learned from her father) it might be once in five or six times: it might be twice or thrice running: it might be, not for a week or a fortnight together. It was enough that he could and did see her when the chances served, and on that possibility she would have waited out the day, seven days a week.\n\nThese occupations brought her round to the December month, wherein her father walked among the terrors with a steady head. On a lightly-snowing afternoon she arrived at the usual corner. It was a day of some wild rejoicing, and a festival. She had seen the houses, as she came along, decorated with little pikes, and with little red caps stuck upon them; also, with tricoloured ribbons; also, with the standard inscription (tricoloured letters were the favourite), Republic One and Indivisible. Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death!\n\nThe miserable shop of the wood-sawyer was so small, that its whole surface furnished very indifferent space for this legend. He had got somebody to scrawl it up for him, however, who had squeezed Death in with most inappropriate difficulty. On his house-top, he displayed pike and cap, as a good citizen must, and in a window he had stationed his saw inscribed as his “Little Sainte Guillotine”--for the great sharp female was by that time popularly canonised. His shop was shut and he was not there, which was a relief to Lucie, and left her quite alone.\n\nBut, he was not far off, for presently she heard a troubled movement and a shouting coming along, which filled her with fear. A moment afterwards, and a throng of people came pouring round the corner by the prison wall, in the midst of whom was the wood-sawyer hand in hand with The Vengeance. There could not be fewer than five hundred people, and they were dancing like five thousand demons. There was no other music than their own singing. They danced to the popular Revolution song, keeping a ferocious time that was like a gnashing of teeth in unison. Men and women danced together, women danced together, men danced together, as hazard had brought them together. At first, they were a mere storm of coarse red caps and coarse woollen rags; but, as they filled the place, and stopped to dance about Lucie, some ghastly apparition of a dance-figure gone raving mad arose among them. They advanced, retreated, struck at one another’s hands, clutched at one another’s heads, spun round alone, caught one another and spun round in pairs, until many of them dropped. While those were down, the rest linked hand in hand, and all spun round together: then the ring broke, and in separate rings of two and four they turned and turned until they all stopped at once, began again, struck, clutched, and tore, and then reversed the spin, and all spun round another way. Suddenly they stopped again, paused, struck out the time afresh, formed into lines the width of the public way, and, with their heads low down and their hands high up, swooped screaming off. No fight could have been half so terrible as this dance. It was so emphatically a fallen sport--a something, once innocent, delivered over to all devilry--a healthy pastime changed into a means of angering the blood, bewildering the senses, and steeling the heart. Such grace as was visible in it, made it the uglier, showing how warped and perverted all things good by nature were become. The maidenly bosom bared to this, the pretty almost-child’s head thus distracted, the delicate foot mincing in this slough of blood and dirt, were types of the disjointed time.\n\nThis was the Carmagnole. As it passed, leaving Lucie frightened and bewildered in the doorway of the wood-sawyer’s house, the feathery snow fell as quietly and lay as white and soft, as if it had never been.\n\n“O my father!” for he stood before her when she lifted up the eyes she had momentarily darkened with her hand; “such a cruel, bad sight.”\n\n“I know, my dear, I know. I have seen it many times. Don’t be frightened! Not one of them would harm you.”\n\n“I am not frightened for myself, my father. But when I think of my husband, and the mercies of these people--”\n\n“We will set him above their mercies very soon. I left him climbing to the window, and I came to tell you. There is no one here to see. You may kiss your hand towards that highest shelving roof.”\n\n“I do so, father, and I send him my Soul with it!”\n\n“You cannot see him, my poor dear?”\n\n“No, father,” said Lucie, yearning and weeping as she kissed her hand, “no.”\n\nA footstep in the snow. Madame Defarge. “I salute you, citizeness,” from the Doctor. “I salute you, citizen.” This in passing. Nothing more. Madame Defarge gone, like a shadow over the white road.\n\n“Give me your arm, my love. Pass from here with an air of cheerfulness and courage, for his sake. That was well done;” they had left the spot; “it shall not be in vain. Charles is summoned for to-morrow.”\n\n“For to-morrow!”\n\n“There is no time to lose. I am well prepared, but there are precautions to be taken, that could not be taken until he was actually summoned before the Tribunal. He has not received the notice yet, but I know that he will presently be summoned for to-morrow, and removed to the Conciergerie; I have timely information. You are not afraid?”\n\nShe could scarcely answer, “I trust in you.”\n\n“Do so, implicitly. Your suspense is nearly ended, my darling; he shall be restored to you within a few hours; I have encompassed him with every protection. I must see Lorry.”\n\nHe stopped. There was a heavy lumbering of wheels within hearing. They both knew too well what it meant. One. Two. Three. Three tumbrils faring away with their dread loads over the hushing snow.\n\n“I must see Lorry,” the Doctor repeated, turning her another way.\n\nThe staunch old gentleman was still in his trust; had never left it. He and his books were in frequent requisition as to property confiscated and made national. What he could save for the owners, he saved. No better man living to hold fast by what Tellson’s had in keeping, and to hold his peace.\n\nA murky red and yellow sky, and a rising mist from the Seine, denoted the approach of darkness. It was almost dark when they arrived at the Bank. The stately residence of Monseigneur was altogether blighted and deserted. Above a heap of dust and ashes in the court, ran the letters: National Property. Republic One and Indivisible. Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death!\n\nWho could that be with Mr. Lorry--the owner of the riding-coat upon the chair--who must not be seen? From whom newly arrived, did he come out, agitated and surprised, to take his favourite in his arms? To whom did he appear to repeat her faltering words, when, raising his voice and turning his head towards the door of the room from which he had issued, he said: “Removed to the Conciergerie, and summoned for to-morrow?”\n\n# CHAPTER VI. Triumph\n\nThe dread tribunal of five Judges, Public Prosecutor, and determined Jury, sat every day. Their lists went forth every evening, and were read out by the gaolers of the various prisons to their prisoners. The standard gaoler-joke was, “Come out and listen to the Evening Paper, you inside there!”\n\n“Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay!”\n\nSo at last began the Evening Paper at La Force.\n\nWhen a name was called, its owner stepped apart into a spot reserved for those who were announced as being thus fatally recorded. Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay, had reason to know the usage; he had seen hundreds pass away so.\n\nHis bloated gaoler, who wore spectacles to read with, glanced over them to assure himself that he had taken his place, and went through the list, making a similar short pause at each name. There were twenty-three names, but only twenty were responded to; for one of the prisoners so summoned had died in gaol and been forgotten, and two had already been guillotined and forgotten. The list was read, in the vaulted chamber where Darnay had seen the associated prisoners on the night of his arrival. Every one of those had perished in the massacre; every human creature he had since cared for and parted with, had died on the scaffold.\n\nThere were hurried words of farewell and kindness, but the parting was soon over. It was the incident of every day, and the society of La Force were engaged in the preparation of some games of forfeits and a little concert, for that evening. They crowded to the grates and shed tears there; but, twenty places in the projected entertainments had to be refilled, and the time was, at best, short to the lock-up hour, when the common rooms and corridors would be delivered over to the great dogs who kept watch there through the night. The prisoners were far from insensible or unfeeling; their ways arose out of the condition of the time. Similarly, though with a subtle difference, a species of fervour or intoxication, known, without doubt, to have led some persons to brave the guillotine unnecessarily, and to die by it, was not mere boastfulness, but a wild infection of the wildly shaken public mind. In seasons of pestilence, some of us will have a secret attraction to the disease--a terrible passing inclination to die of it. And all of us have like wonders hidden in our breasts, only needing circumstances to evoke them.\n\nThe passage to the Conciergerie was short and dark; the night in its vermin-haunted cells was long and cold. Next day, fifteen prisoners were put to the bar before Charles Darnay’s name was called. All the fifteen were condemned, and the trials of the whole occupied an hour and a half.\n\n“Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay,” was at length arraigned.\n\nHis judges sat upon the Bench in feathered hats; but the rough red cap and tricoloured cockade was the head-dress otherwise prevailing. Looking at the Jury and the turbulent audience, he might have thought that the usual order of things was reversed, and that the felons were trying the honest men. The lowest, cruelest, and worst populace of a city, never without its quantity of low, cruel, and bad, were the directing spirits of the scene: noisily commenting, applauding, disapproving, anticipating, and precipitating the result, without a check. Of the men, the greater part were armed in various ways; of the women, some wore knives, some daggers, some ate and drank as they looked on, many knitted. Among these last, was one, with a spare piece of knitting under her arm as she worked. She was in a front row, by the side of a man whom he had never seen since his arrival at the Barrier, but whom he directly remembered as Defarge. He noticed that she once or twice whispered in his ear, and that she seemed to be his wife; but, what he most noticed in the two figures was, that although they were posted as close to himself as they could be, they never looked towards him. They seemed to be waiting for something with a dogged determination, and they looked at the Jury, but at nothing else. Under the President sat Doctor Manette, in his usual quiet dress. As well as the prisoner could see, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there, unconnected with the Tribunal, who wore their usual clothes, and had not assumed the coarse garb of the Carmagnole.\n\nCharles Evrémonde, called Darnay, was accused by the public prosecutor as an emigrant, whose life was forfeit to the Republic, under the decree which banished all emigrants on pain of Death. It was nothing that the decree bore date since his return to France. There he was, and there was the decree; he had been taken in France, and his head was demanded.\n\n“Take off his head!” cried the audience. “An enemy to the Republic!”\n\nThe President rang his bell to silence those cries, and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived many years in England?\n\nUndoubtedly it was.\n\nWas he not an emigrant then? What did he call himself?\n\nNot an emigrant, he hoped, within the sense and spirit of the law.\n\nWhy not? the President desired to know.\n\nBecause he had voluntarily relinquished a title that was distasteful to him, and a station that was distasteful to him, and had left his country--he submitted before the word emigrant in the present acceptation by the Tribunal was in use--to live by his own industry in England, rather than on the industry of the overladen people of France.\n\nWhat proof had he of this?\n\nHe handed in the names of two witnesses; Theophile Gabelle, and Alexandre Manette.\n\nBut he had married in England? the President reminded him.\n\nTrue, but not an English woman.\n\nA citizeness of France?\n\nYes. By birth.\n\nHer name and family?\n\n“Lucie Manette, only daughter of Doctor Manette, the good physician who sits there.”\n\nThis answer had a happy effect upon the audience. Cries in exaltation of the well-known good physician rent the hall. So capriciously were the people moved, that tears immediately rolled down several ferocious countenances which had been glaring at the prisoner a moment before, as if with impatience to pluck him out into the streets and kill him.\n\nOn these few steps of his dangerous way, Charles Darnay had set his foot according to Doctor Manette’s reiterated instructions. The same cautious counsel directed every step that lay before him, and had prepared every inch of his road.\n\nThe President asked, why had he returned to France when he did, and not sooner?\n\nHe had not returned sooner, he replied, simply because he had no means of living in France, save those he had resigned; whereas, in England, he lived by giving instruction in the French language and literature. He had returned when he did, on the pressing and written entreaty of a French citizen, who represented that his life was endangered by his absence. He had come back, to save a citizen’s life, and to bear his testimony, at whatever personal hazard, to the truth. Was that criminal in the eyes of the Republic?\n\nThe populace cried enthusiastically, “No!” and the President rang his bell to quiet them. Which it did not, for they continued to cry “No!” until they left off, of their own will.\n\nThe President required the name of that citizen. The accused explained that the citizen was his first witness. He also referred with confidence to the citizen’s letter, which had been taken from him at the Barrier, but which he did not doubt would be found among the papers then before the President.\n\nThe Doctor had taken care that it should be there--had assured him that it would be there--and at this stage of the proceedings it was produced and read. Citizen Gabelle was called to confirm it, and did so. Citizen Gabelle hinted, with infinite delicacy and politeness, that in the pressure of business imposed on the Tribunal by the multitude of enemies of the Republic with which it had to deal, he had been slightly overlooked in his prison of the Abbaye--in fact, had rather passed out of the Tribunal’s patriotic remembrance--until three days ago; when he had been summoned before it, and had been set at liberty on the Jury’s declaring themselves satisfied that the accusation against him was answered, as to himself, by the surrender of the citizen Evrémonde, called Darnay.\n\nDoctor Manette was next questioned. His high personal popularity, and the clearness of his answers, made a great impression; but, as he proceeded, as he showed that the Accused was his first friend on his release from his long imprisonment; that, the accused had remained in England, always faithful and devoted to his daughter and himself in their exile; that, so far from being in favour with the Aristocrat government there, he had actually been tried for his life by it, as the foe of England and friend of the United States--as he brought these circumstances into view, with the greatest discretion and with the straightforward force of truth and earnestness, the Jury and the populace became one. At last, when he appealed by name to Monsieur Lorry, an English gentleman then and there present, who, like himself, had been a witness on that English trial and could corroborate his account of it, the Jury declared that they had heard enough, and that they were ready with their votes if the President were content to receive them.\n\nAt every vote (the Jurymen voted aloud and individually), the populace set up a shout of applause. All the voices were in the prisoner’s favour, and the President declared him free.\n\nThen, began one of those extraordinary scenes with which the populace sometimes gratified their fickleness, or their better impulses towards generosity and mercy, or which they regarded as some set-off against their swollen account of cruel rage. No man can decide now to which of these motives such extraordinary scenes were referable; it is probable, to a blending of all the three, with the second predominating. No sooner was the acquittal pronounced, than tears were shed as freely as blood at another time, and such fraternal embraces were bestowed upon the prisoner by as many of both sexes as could rush at him, that after his long and unwholesome confinement he was in danger of fainting from exhaustion; none the less because he knew very well, that the very same people, carried by another current, would have rushed at him with the very same intensity, to rend him to pieces and strew him over the streets.\n\nHis removal, to make way for other accused persons who were to be tried, rescued him from these caresses for the moment. Five were to be tried together, next, as enemies of the Republic, forasmuch as they had not assisted it by word or deed. So quick was the Tribunal to compensate itself and the nation for a chance lost, that these five came down to him before he left the place, condemned to die within twenty-four hours. The first of them told him so, with the customary prison sign of Death--a raised finger--and they all added in words, “Long live the Republic!”\n\nThe five had had, it is true, no audience to lengthen their proceedings, for when he and Doctor Manette emerged from the gate, there was a great crowd about it, in which there seemed to be every face he had seen in Court--except two, for which he looked in vain. On his coming out, the concourse made at him anew, weeping, embracing, and shouting, all by turns and all together, until the very tide of the river on the bank of which the mad scene was acted, seemed to run mad, like the people on the shore.\n\nThey put him into a great chair they had among them, and which they had taken either out of the Court itself, or one of its rooms or passages. Over the chair they had thrown a red flag, and to the back of it they had bound a pike with a red cap on its top. In this car of triumph, not even the Doctor’s entreaties could prevent his being carried to his home on men’s shoulders, with a confused sea of red caps heaving about him, and casting up to sight from the stormy deep such wrecks of faces, that he more than once misdoubted his mind being in confusion, and that he was in the tumbril on his way to the Guillotine.\n\nIn wild dreamlike procession, embracing whom they met and pointing him out, they carried him on. Reddening the snowy streets with the prevailing Republican colour, in winding and tramping through them, as they had reddened them below the snow with a deeper dye, they carried him thus into the courtyard of the building where he lived. Her father had gone on before, to prepare her, and when her husband stood upon his feet, she dropped insensible in his arms.\n\nAs he held her to his heart and turned her beautiful head between his face and the brawling crowd, so that his tears and her lips might come together unseen, a few of the people fell to dancing. Instantly, all the rest fell to dancing, and the courtyard overflowed with the Carmagnole. Then, they elevated into the vacant chair a young woman from the crowd to be carried as the Goddess of Liberty, and then swelling and overflowing out into the adjacent streets, and along the river’s bank, and over the bridge, the Carmagnole absorbed them every one and whirled them away.\n\nAfter grasping the Doctor’s hand, as he stood victorious and proud before him; after grasping the hand of Mr. Lorry, who came panting in breathless from his struggle against the waterspout of the Carmagnole; after kissing little Lucie, who was lifted up to clasp her arms round his neck; and after embracing the ever zealous and faithful Pross who lifted her; he took his wife in his arms, and carried her up to their rooms.\n\n“Lucie! My own! I am safe.”\n\n“O dearest Charles, let me thank God for this on my knees as I have prayed to Him.”\n\nThey all reverently bowed their heads and hearts. When she was again in his arms, he said to her:\n\n“And now speak to your father, dearest. No other man in all this France could have done what he has done for me.”\n\nShe laid her head upon her father’s breast, as she had laid his poor head on her own breast, long, long ago. He was happy in the return he had made her, he was recompensed for his suffering, he was proud of his strength. “You must not be weak, my darling,” he remonstrated; “don’t tremble so. I have saved him.”\n\n# CHAPTER VII. A Knock at the Door\n\n“I have saved him.” It was not another of the dreams in which he had often come back; he was really here. And yet his wife trembled, and a vague but heavy fear was upon her.\n\nAll the air round was so thick and dark, the people were so passionately revengeful and fitful, the innocent were so constantly put to death on vague suspicion and black malice, it was so impossible to forget that many as blameless as her husband and as dear to others as he was to her, every day shared the fate from which he had been clutched, that her heart could not be as lightened of its load as she felt it ought to be. The shadows of the wintry afternoon were beginning to fall, and even now the dreadful carts were rolling through the streets. Her mind pursued them, looking for him among the Condemned; and then she clung closer to his real presence and trembled more.\n\nHer father, cheering her, showed a compassionate superiority to this woman’s weakness, which was wonderful to see. No garret, no shoemaking, no One Hundred and Five, North Tower, now! He had accomplished the task he had set himself, his promise was redeemed, he had saved Charles. Let them all lean upon him.\n\nTheir housekeeping was of a very frugal kind: not only because that was the safest way of life, involving the least offence to the people, but because they were not rich, and Charles, throughout his imprisonment, had had to pay heavily for his bad food, and for his guard, and towards the living of the poorer prisoners. Partly on this account, and partly to avoid a domestic spy, they kept no servant; the citizen and citizeness who acted as porters at the courtyard gate, rendered them occasional service; and Jerry (almost wholly transferred to them by Mr. Lorry) had become their daily retainer, and had his bed there every night.\n\nIt was an ordinance of the Republic One and Indivisible of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, that on the door or doorpost of every house, the name of every inmate must be legibly inscribed in letters of a certain size, at a certain convenient height from the ground. Mr. Jerry Cruncher’s name, therefore, duly embellished the doorpost down below; and, as the afternoon shadows deepened, the owner of that name himself appeared, from overlooking a painter whom Doctor Manette had employed to add to the list the name of Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay.\n\nIn the universal fear and distrust that darkened the time, all the usual harmless ways of life were changed. In the Doctor’s little household, as in very many others, the articles of daily consumption that were wanted were purchased every evening, in small quantities and at various small shops. To avoid attracting notice, and to give as little occasion as possible for talk and envy, was the general desire.\n\nFor some months past, Miss Pross and Mr. Cruncher had discharged the office of purveyors; the former carrying the money; the latter, the basket. Every afternoon at about the time when the public lamps were lighted, they fared forth on this duty, and made and brought home such purchases as were needful. Although Miss Pross, through her long association with a French family, might have known as much of their language as of her own, if she had had a mind, she had no mind in that direction; consequently she knew no more of that “nonsense” (as she was pleased to call it) than Mr. Cruncher did. So her manner of marketing was to plump a noun-substantive at the head of a shopkeeper without any introduction in the nature of an article, and, if it happened not to be the name of the thing she wanted, to look round for that thing, lay hold of it, and hold on by it until the bargain was concluded. She always made a bargain for it, by holding up, as a statement of its just price, one finger less than the merchant held up, whatever his number might be.\n\n“Now, Mr. Cruncher,” said Miss Pross, whose eyes were red with felicity; “if you are ready, I am.”\n\nJerry hoarsely professed himself at Miss Pross’s service. He had worn all his rust off long ago, but nothing would file his spiky head down.\n\n“There’s all manner of things wanted,” said Miss Pross, “and we shall have a precious time of it. We want wine, among the rest. Nice toasts these Redheads will be drinking, wherever we buy it.”\n\n“It will be much the same to your knowledge, miss, I should think,” retorted Jerry, “whether they drink your health or the Old Un’s.”\n\n“Who’s he?” said Miss Pross.\n\nMr. Cruncher, with some diffidence, explained himself as meaning “Old Nick’s.”\n\n“Ha!” said Miss Pross, “it doesn’t need an interpreter to explain the meaning of these creatures. They have but one, and it’s Midnight Murder, and Mischief.”\n\n“Hush, dear! Pray, pray, be cautious!” cried Lucie.\n\n“Yes, yes, yes, I’ll be cautious,” said Miss Pross; “but I may say among ourselves, that I do hope there will be no oniony and tobaccoey smotherings in the form of embracings all round, going on in the streets. Now, Ladybird, never you stir from that fire till I come back! Take care of the dear husband you have recovered, and don’t move your pretty head from his shoulder as you have it now, till you see me again! May I ask a question, Doctor Manette, before I go?”\n\n“I think you may take that liberty,” the Doctor answered, smiling.\n\n“For gracious sake, don’t talk about Liberty; we have quite enough of that,” said Miss Pross.\n\n“Hush, dear! Again?” Lucie remonstrated.\n\n“Well, my sweet,” said Miss Pross, nodding her head emphatically, “the short and the long of it is, that I am a subject of His Most Gracious Majesty King George the Third;” Miss Pross curtseyed at the name; “and as such, my maxim is, Confound their politics, Frustrate their knavish tricks, On him our hopes we fix, God save the King!”\n\nMr. Cruncher, in an access of loyalty, growlingly repeated the words after Miss Pross, like somebody at church.\n\n“I am glad you have so much of the Englishman in you, though I wish you had never taken that cold in your voice,” said Miss Pross, approvingly. “But the question, Doctor Manette. Is there”--it was the good creature’s way to affect to make light of anything that was a great anxiety with them all, and to come at it in this chance manner--“is there any prospect yet, of our getting out of this place?”\n\n“I fear not yet. It would be dangerous for Charles yet.”\n\n“Heigh-ho-hum!” said Miss Pross, cheerfully repressing a sigh as she glanced at her darling’s golden hair in the light of the fire, “then we must have patience and wait: that’s all. We must hold up our heads and fight low, as my brother Solomon used to say. Now, Mr. Cruncher!--Don’t you move, Ladybird!”\n\nThey went out, leaving Lucie, and her husband, her father, and the child, by a bright fire. Mr. Lorry was expected back presently from the Banking House. Miss Pross had lighted the lamp, but had put it aside in a corner, that they might enjoy the fire-light undisturbed. Little Lucie sat by her grandfather with her hands clasped through his arm: and he, in a tone not rising much above a whisper, began to tell her a story of a great and powerful Fairy who had opened a prison-wall and let out a captive who had once done the Fairy a service. All was subdued and quiet, and Lucie was more at ease than she had been.\n\n“What is that?” she cried, all at once.\n\n“My dear!” said her father, stopping in his story, and laying his hand on hers, “command yourself. What a disordered state you are in! The least thing--nothing--startles you! You, your father’s daughter!”\n\n“I thought, my father,” said Lucie, excusing herself, with a pale face and in a faltering voice, “that I heard strange feet upon the stairs.”\n\n“My love, the staircase is as still as Death.”\n\nAs he said the word, a blow was struck upon the door.\n\n“Oh father, father. What can this be! Hide Charles. Save him!”\n\n“My child,” said the Doctor, rising, and laying his hand upon her shoulder, “I have saved him. What weakness is this, my dear! Let me go to the door.”\n\nHe took the lamp in his hand, crossed the two intervening outer rooms, and opened it. A rude clattering of feet over the floor, and four rough men in red caps, armed with sabres and pistols, entered the room.\n\n“The Citizen Evrémonde, called Darnay,” said the first.\n\n“Who seeks him?” answered Darnay.\n\n“I seek him. We seek him. I know you, Evrémonde; I saw you before the Tribunal to-day. You are again the prisoner of the Republic.”\n\nThe four surrounded him, where he stood with his wife and child clinging to him.\n\n“Tell me how and why am I again a prisoner?”\n\n“It is enough that you return straight to the Conciergerie, and will know to-morrow. You are summoned for to-morrow.”\n\nDoctor Manette, whom this visitation had so turned into stone, that he stood with the lamp in his hand, as if he were a statue made to hold it, moved after these words were spoken, put the lamp down, and confronting the speaker, and taking him, not ungently, by the loose front of his red woollen shirt, said:\n\n“You know him, you have said. Do you know me?”\n\n“Yes, I know you, Citizen Doctor.”\n\n“We all know you, Citizen Doctor,” said the other three.\n\nHe looked abstractedly from one to another, and said, in a lower voice, after a pause:\n\n“Will you answer his question to me then? How does this happen?”\n\n“Citizen Doctor,” said the first, reluctantly, “he has been denounced to the Section of Saint Antoine. This citizen,” pointing out the second who had entered, “is from Saint Antoine.”\n\nThe citizen here indicated nodded his head, and added:\n\n“He is accused by Saint Antoine.”\n\n“Of what?” asked the Doctor.\n\n“Citizen Doctor,” said the first, with his former reluctance, “ask no more. If the Republic demands sacrifices from you, without doubt you as a good patriot will be happy to make them. The Republic goes before all. The People is supreme. Evrémonde, we are pressed.”\n\n“One word,” the Doctor entreated. “Will you tell me who denounced him?”\n\n“It is against rule,” answered the first; “but you can ask Him of Saint Antoine here.”\n\nThe Doctor turned his eyes upon that man. Who moved uneasily on his feet, rubbed his beard a little, and at length said:\n\n“Well! Truly it is against rule. But he is denounced--and gravely--by the Citizen and Citizeness Defarge. And by one other.”\n\n“What other?”\n\n“Do you ask, Citizen Doctor?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Then,” said he of Saint Antoine, with a strange look, “you will be answered to-morrow. Now, I am dumb!”\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. A Hand at Cards\n\nHappily unconscious of the new calamity at home, Miss Pross threaded her way along the narrow streets and crossed the river by the bridge of the Pont-Neuf, reckoning in her mind the number of indispensable purchases she had to make. Mr. Cruncher, with the basket, walked at her side. They both looked to the right and to the left into most of the shops they passed, had a wary eye for all gregarious assemblages of people, and turned out of their road to avoid any very excited group of talkers. It was a raw evening, and the misty river, blurred to the eye with blazing lights and to the ear with harsh noises, showed where the barges were stationed in which the smiths worked, making guns for the Army of the Republic. Woe to the man who played tricks with that Army, or got undeserved promotion in it! Better for him that his beard had never grown, for the National Razor shaved him close.\n\nHaving purchased a few small articles of grocery, and a measure of oil for the lamp, Miss Pross bethought herself of the wine they wanted. After peeping into several wine-shops, she stopped at the sign of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, not far from the National Palace, once (and twice) the Tuileries, where the aspect of things rather took her fancy. It had a quieter look than any other place of the same description they had passed, and, though red with patriotic caps, was not so red as the rest. Sounding Mr. Cruncher, and finding him of her opinion, Miss Pross resorted to the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, attended by her cavalier.\n\nSlightly observant of the smoky lights; of the people, pipe in mouth, playing with limp cards and yellow dominoes; of the one bare-breasted, bare-armed, soot-begrimed workman reading a journal aloud, and of the others listening to him; of the weapons worn, or laid aside to be resumed; of the two or three customers fallen forward asleep, who in the popular high-shouldered shaggy black spencer looked, in that attitude, like slumbering bears or dogs; the two outlandish customers approached the counter, and showed what they wanted.\n\nAs their wine was measuring out, a man parted from another man in a corner, and rose to depart. In going, he had to face Miss Pross. No sooner did he face her, than Miss Pross uttered a scream, and clapped her hands.\n\nIn a moment, the whole company were on their feet. That somebody was assassinated by somebody vindicating a difference of opinion was the likeliest occurrence. Everybody looked to see somebody fall, but only saw a man and a woman standing staring at each other; the man with all the outward aspect of a Frenchman and a thorough Republican; the woman, evidently English.\n\nWhat was said in this disappointing anti-climax, by the disciples of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, except that it was something very voluble and loud, would have been as so much Hebrew or Chaldean to Miss Pross and her protector, though they had been all ears. But, they had no ears for anything in their surprise. For, it must be recorded, that not only was Miss Pross lost in amazement and agitation, but, Mr. Cruncher--though it seemed on his own separate and individual account--was in a state of the greatest wonder.\n\n“What is the matter?” said the man who had caused Miss Pross to scream; speaking in a vexed, abrupt voice (though in a low tone), and in English.\n\n“Oh, Solomon, dear Solomon!” cried Miss Pross, clapping her hands again. “After not setting eyes upon you or hearing of you for so long a time, do I find you here!”\n\n“Don’t call me Solomon. Do you want to be the death of me?” asked the man, in a furtive, frightened way.\n\n“Brother, brother!” cried Miss Pross, bursting into tears. “Have I ever been so hard with you that you ask me such a cruel question?”\n\n“Then hold your meddlesome tongue,” said Solomon, “and come out, if you want to speak to me. Pay for your wine, and come out. Who’s this man?”\n\nMiss Pross, shaking her loving and dejected head at her by no means affectionate brother, said through her tears, “Mr. Cruncher.”\n\n“Let him come out too,” said Solomon. “Does he think me a ghost?”\n\nApparently, Mr. Cruncher did, to judge from his looks. He said not a word, however, and Miss Pross, exploring the depths of her reticule through her tears with great difficulty paid for her wine. As she did so, Solomon turned to the followers of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, and offered a few words of explanation in the French language, which caused them all to relapse into their former places and pursuits.\n\n“Now,” said Solomon, stopping at the dark street corner, “what do you want?”\n\n“How dreadfully unkind in a brother nothing has ever turned my love away from!” cried Miss Pross, “to give me such a greeting, and show me no affection.”\n\n“There. Confound it! There,” said Solomon, making a dab at Miss Pross’s lips with his own. “Now are you content?”\n\nMiss Pross only shook her head and wept in silence.\n\n“If you expect me to be surprised,” said her brother Solomon, “I am not surprised; I knew you were here; I know of most people who are here. If you really don’t want to endanger my existence--which I half believe you do--go your ways as soon as possible, and let me go mine. I am busy. I am an official.”\n\n“My English brother Solomon,” mourned Miss Pross, casting up her tear-fraught eyes, “that had the makings in him of one of the best and greatest of men in his native country, an official among foreigners, and such foreigners! I would almost sooner have seen the dear boy lying in his--”\n\n“I said so!” cried her brother, interrupting. “I knew it. You want to be the death of me. I shall be rendered Suspected, by my own sister. Just as I am getting on!”\n\n“The gracious and merciful Heavens forbid!” cried Miss Pross. “Far rather would I never see you again, dear Solomon, though I have ever loved you truly, and ever shall. Say but one affectionate word to me, and tell me there is nothing angry or estranged between us, and I will detain you no longer.”\n\nGood Miss Pross! As if the estrangement between them had come of any culpability of hers. As if Mr. Lorry had not known it for a fact, years ago, in the quiet corner in Soho, that this precious brother had spent her money and left her!\n\nHe was saying the affectionate word, however, with a far more grudging condescension and patronage than he could have shown if their relative merits and positions had been reversed (which is invariably the case, all the world over), when Mr. Cruncher, touching him on the shoulder, hoarsely and unexpectedly interposed with the following singular question:\n\n“I say! Might I ask the favour? As to whether your name is John Solomon, or Solomon John?”\n\nThe official turned towards him with sudden distrust. He had not previously uttered a word.\n\n“Come!” said Mr. Cruncher. “Speak out, you know.” (Which, by the way, was more than he could do himself.) “John Solomon, or Solomon John? She calls you Solomon, and she must know, being your sister. And I know you’re John, you know. Which of the two goes first? And regarding that name of Pross, likewise. That warn’t your name over the water.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“Well, I don’t know all I mean, for I can’t call to mind what your name was, over the water.”\n\n“No?”\n“No. But I’ll swear it was a name of two syllables.”\n\n“Indeed?”\n\n“Yes. T’other one’s was one syllable. I know you. You was a spy--witness at the Bailey. What, in the name of the Father of Lies, own father to yourself, was you called at that time?”\n\n“Barsad,” said another voice, striking in.\n\n“That’s the name for a thousand pound!” cried Jerry.\n\nThe speaker who struck in, was Sydney Carton. He had his hands behind him under the skirts of his riding-coat, and he stood at Mr. Cruncher’s elbow as negligently as he might have stood at the Old Bailey itself.\n\n“Don’t be alarmed, my dear Miss Pross. I arrived at Mr. Lorry’s, to his surprise, yesterday evening; we agreed that I would not present myself elsewhere until all was well, or unless I could be useful; I present myself here, to beg a little talk with your brother. I wish you had a better employed brother than Mr. Barsad. I wish for your sake Mr. Barsad was not a Sheep of the Prisons.”\n\nSheep was a cant word of the time for a spy, under the gaolers. The spy, who was pale, turned paler, and asked him how he dared--\n\n“I’ll tell you,” said Sydney. “I lighted on you, Mr. Barsad, coming out of the prison of the Conciergerie while I was contemplating the walls, an hour or more ago. You have a face to be remembered, and I remember faces well. Made curious by seeing you in that connection, and having a reason, to which you are no stranger, for associating you with the misfortunes of a friend now very unfortunate, I walked in your direction. I walked into the wine-shop here, close after you, and sat near you. I had no difficulty in deducing from your unreserved conversation, and the rumour openly going about among your admirers, the nature of your calling. And gradually, what I had done at random, seemed to shape itself into a purpose, Mr. Barsad.”\n\n“What purpose?” the spy asked.\n\n“It would be troublesome, and might be dangerous, to explain in the street. Could you favour me, in confidence, with some minutes of your company--at the office of Tellson’s Bank, for instance?”\n“Under a threat?”\n\n“Oh! Did I say that?”\n\n“Then, why should I go there?”\n\n“Really, Mr. Barsad, I can’t say, if you can’t.”\n\n“Do you mean that you won’t say, sir?” the spy irresolutely asked.\n\n“You apprehend me very clearly, Mr. Barsad. I won’t.”\n\nCarton’s negligent recklessness of manner came powerfully in aid of his quickness and skill, in such a business as he had in his secret mind, and with such a man as he had to do with. His practised eye saw it, and made the most of it.\n\n“Now, I told you so,” said the spy, casting a reproachful look at his sister; “if any trouble comes of this, it’s your doing.”\n\n“Come, come, Mr. Barsad!” exclaimed Sydney. “Don’t be ungrateful. But for my great respect for your sister, I might not have led up so pleasantly to a little proposal that I wish to make for our mutual satisfaction. Do you go with me to the Bank?”\n\n“I’ll hear what you have got to say. Yes, I’ll go with you.”\n\n“I propose that we first conduct your sister safely to the corner of her own street. Let me take your arm, Miss Pross. This is not a good city, at this time, for you to be out in, unprotected; and as your escort knows Mr. Barsad, I will invite him to Mr. Lorry’s with us. Are we ready? Come then!”\n\nMiss Pross recalled soon afterwards, and to the end of her life remembered, that as she pressed her hands on Sydney’s arm and looked up in his face, imploring him to do no hurt to Solomon, there was a braced purpose in the arm and a kind of inspiration in the eyes, which not only contradicted his light manner, but changed and raised the man. She was too much occupied then with fears for the brother who so little deserved her affection, and with Sydney’s friendly reassurances, adequately to heed what she observed.\n\nThey left her at the corner of the street, and Carton led the way to Mr. Lorry’s, which was within a few minutes’ walk. John Barsad, or Solomon Pross, walked at his side.\n\nMr. Lorry had just finished his dinner, and was sitting before a cheery little log or two of fire--perhaps looking into their blaze for the picture of that younger elderly gentleman from Tellson’s, who had looked into the red coals at the Royal George at Dover, now a good many years ago. He turned his head as they entered, and showed the surprise with which he saw a stranger.\n\n“Miss Pross’s brother, sir,” said Sydney. “Mr. Barsad.”\n\n“Barsad?” repeated the old gentleman, “Barsad? I have an association with the name--and with the face.”\n\n“I told you you had a remarkable face, Mr. Barsad,” observed Carton, coolly. “Pray sit down.”\n\nAs he took a chair himself, he supplied the link that Mr. Lorry wanted, by saying to him with a frown, “Witness at that trial.” Mr. Lorry immediately remembered, and regarded his new visitor with an undisguised look of abhorrence.\n\n“Mr. Barsad has been recognised by Miss Pross as the affectionate brother you have heard of,” said Sydney, “and has acknowledged the relationship. I pass to worse news. Darnay has been arrested again.”\n\nStruck with consternation, the old gentleman exclaimed, “What do you tell me! I left him safe and free within these two hours, and am about to return to him!”\n\n“Arrested for all that. When was it done, Mr. Barsad?”\n\n“Just now, if at all.”\n\n“Mr. Barsad is the best authority possible, sir,” said Sydney, “and I have it from Mr. Barsad’s communication to a friend and brother Sheep over a bottle of wine, that the arrest has taken place. He left the messengers at the gate, and saw them admitted by the porter. There is no earthly doubt that he is retaken.”\n\nMr. Lorry’s business eye read in the speaker’s face that it was loss of time to dwell upon the point. Confused, but sensible that something might depend on his presence of mind, he commanded himself, and was silently attentive.\n\n“Now, I trust,” said Sydney to him, “that the name and influence of Doctor Manette may stand him in as good stead to-morrow--you said he would be before the Tribunal again to-morrow, Mr. Barsad?--”\n\n“Yes; I believe so.”\n\n“--In as good stead to-morrow as to-day. But it may not be so. I own to you, I am shaken, Mr. Lorry, by Doctor Manette’s not having had the power to prevent this arrest.”\n\n“He may not have known of it beforehand,” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“But that very circumstance would be alarming, when we remember how identified he is with his son-in-law.”\n\n“That’s true,” Mr. Lorry acknowledged, with his troubled hand at his chin, and his troubled eyes on Carton.\n\n“In short,” said Sydney, “this is a desperate time, when desperate games are played for desperate stakes. Let the Doctor play the winning game; I will play the losing one. No man’s life here is worth purchase. Any one carried home by the people to-day, may be condemned tomorrow. Now, the stake I have resolved to play for, in case of the worst, is a friend in the Conciergerie. And the friend I purpose to myself to win, is Mr. Barsad.”\n\n“You need have good cards, sir,” said the spy.\n\n“I’ll run them over. I’ll see what I hold,--Mr. Lorry, you know what a brute I am; I wish you’d give me a little brandy.”\n\nIt was put before him, and he drank off a glassful--drank off another glassful--pushed the bottle thoughtfully away.\n\n“Mr. Barsad,” he went on, in the tone of one who really was looking over a hand at cards: “Sheep of the prisons, emissary of Republican committees, now turnkey, now prisoner, always spy and secret informer, so much the more valuable here for being English that an Englishman is less open to suspicion of subornation in those characters than a Frenchman, represents himself to his employers under a false name. That’s a very good card. Mr. Barsad, now in the employ of the republican French government, was formerly in the employ of the aristocratic English government, the enemy of France and freedom. That’s an excellent card. Inference clear as day in this region of suspicion, that Mr. Barsad, still in the pay of the aristocratic English government, is the spy of Pitt, the treacherous foe of the Republic crouching in its bosom, the English traitor and agent of all mischief so much spoken of and so difficult to find. That’s a card not to be beaten. Have you followed my hand, Mr. Barsad?”\n\n“Not to understand your play,” returned the spy, somewhat uneasily.\n\n“I play my Ace, Denunciation of Mr. Barsad to the nearest Section Committee. Look over your hand, Mr. Barsad, and see what you have. Don’t hurry.”\n\nHe drew the bottle near, poured out another glassful of brandy, and drank it off. He saw that the spy was fearful of his drinking himself into a fit state for the immediate denunciation of him. Seeing it, he poured out and drank another glassful.\n\n“Look over your hand carefully, Mr. Barsad. Take time.”\n\nIt was a poorer hand than he suspected. Mr. Barsad saw losing cards in it that Sydney Carton knew nothing of. Thrown out of his honourable employment in England, through too much unsuccessful hard swearing there--not because he was not wanted there; our English reasons for vaunting our superiority to secrecy and spies are of very modern date--he knew that he had crossed the Channel, and accepted service in France: first, as a tempter and an eavesdropper among his own countrymen there: gradually, as a tempter and an eavesdropper among the natives. He knew that under the overthrown government he had been a spy upon Saint Antoine and Defarge’s wine-shop; had received from the watchful police such heads of information concerning Doctor Manette’s imprisonment, release, and history, as should serve him for an introduction to familiar conversation with the Defarges; and tried them on Madame Defarge, and had broken down with them signally. He always remembered with fear and trembling, that that terrible woman had knitted when he talked with her, and had looked ominously at him as her fingers moved. He had since seen her, in the Section of Saint Antoine, over and over again produce her knitted registers, and denounce people whose lives the guillotine then surely swallowed up. He knew, as every one employed as he was did, that he was never safe; that flight was impossible; that he was tied fast under the shadow of the axe; and that in spite of his utmost tergiversation and treachery in furtherance of the reigning terror, a word might bring it down upon him. Once denounced, and on such grave grounds as had just now been suggested to his mind, he foresaw that the dreadful woman of whose unrelenting character he had seen many proofs, would produce against him that fatal register, and would quash his last chance of life. Besides that all secret men are men soon terrified, here were surely cards enough of one black suit, to justify the holder in growing rather livid as he turned them over.\n\n“You scarcely seem to like your hand,” said Sydney, with the greatest composure. “Do you play?”\n\n“I think, sir,” said the spy, in the meanest manner, as he turned to Mr. Lorry, “I may appeal to a gentleman of your years and benevolence, to put it to this other gentleman, so much your junior, whether he can under any circumstances reconcile it to his station to play that Ace of which he has spoken. I admit that I am a spy, and that it is considered a discreditable station--though it must be filled by somebody; but this gentleman is no spy, and why should he so demean himself as to make himself one?”\n\n“I play my Ace, Mr. Barsad,” said Carton, taking the answer on himself, and looking at his watch, “without any scruple, in a very few minutes.”\n\n“I should have hoped, gentlemen both,” said the spy, always striving to hook Mr. Lorry into the discussion, “that your respect for my sister--”\n\n“I could not better testify my respect for your sister than by finally relieving her of her brother,” said Sydney Carton.\n\n“You think not, sir?”\n\n“I have thoroughly made up my mind about it.”\n\nThe smooth manner of the spy, curiously in dissonance with his ostentatiously rough dress, and probably with his usual demeanour, received such a check from the inscrutability of Carton,--who was a mystery to wiser and honester men than he,--that it faltered here and failed him. While he was at a loss, Carton said, resuming his former air of contemplating cards:\n\n“And indeed, now I think again, I have a strong impression that I have another good card here, not yet enumerated. That friend and fellow-Sheep, who spoke of himself as pasturing in the country prisons; who was he?”\n\n“French. You don’t know him,” said the spy, quickly.\n\n“French, eh?” repeated Carton, musing, and not appearing to notice him at all, though he echoed his word. “Well; he may be.”\n\n“Is, I assure you,” said the spy; “though it’s not important.”\n\n“Though it’s not important,” repeated Carton, in the same mechanical way--“though it’s not important--No, it’s not important. No. Yet I know the face.”\n\n“I think not. I am sure not. It can’t be,” said the spy.\n\n“It-can’t-be,” muttered Sydney Carton, retrospectively, and idling his glass (which fortunately was a small one) again. “Can’t-be. Spoke good French. Yet like a foreigner, I thought?”\n\n“Provincial,” said the spy.\n\n“No. Foreign!” cried Carton, striking his open hand on the table, as a light broke clearly on his mind. “Cly! Disguised, but the same man. We had that man before us at the Old Bailey.”\n\n“Now, there you are hasty, sir,” said Barsad, with a smile that gave his aquiline nose an extra inclination to one side; “there you really give me an advantage over you. Cly (who I will unreservedly admit, at this distance of time, was a partner of mine) has been dead several years. I attended him in his last illness. He was buried in London, at the church of Saint Pancras-in-the-Fields. His unpopularity with the blackguard multitude at the moment prevented my following his remains, but I helped to lay him in his coffin.”\n\nHere, Mr. Lorry became aware, from where he sat, of a most remarkable goblin shadow on the wall. Tracing it to its source, he discovered it to be caused by a sudden extraordinary rising and stiffening of all the risen and stiff hair on Mr. Cruncher’s head.\n\n“Let us be reasonable,” said the spy, “and let us be fair. To show you how mistaken you are, and what an unfounded assumption yours is, I will lay before you a certificate of Cly’s burial, which I happened to have carried in my pocket-book,” with a hurried hand he produced and opened it, “ever since. There it is. Oh, look at it, look at it! You may take it in your hand; it’s no forgery.”\n\nHere, Mr. Lorry perceived the reflection on the wall to elongate, and Mr. Cruncher rose and stepped forward. His hair could not have been more violently on end, if it had been that moment dressed by the Cow with the crumpled horn in the house that Jack built.\n\nUnseen by the spy, Mr. Cruncher stood at his side, and touched him on the shoulder like a ghostly bailiff.\n\n“That there Roger Cly, master,” said Mr. Cruncher, with a taciturn and iron-bound visage. “So you put him in his coffin?”\n\n“I did.”\n\n“Who took him out of it?”\n\nBarsad leaned back in his chair, and stammered, “What do you mean?”\n\n“I mean,” said Mr. Cruncher, “that he warn’t never in it. No! Not he! I’ll have my head took off, if he was ever in it.”\n\nThe spy looked round at the two gentlemen; they both looked in unspeakable astonishment at Jerry.\n\n“I tell you,” said Jerry, “that you buried paving-stones and earth in that there coffin. Don’t go and tell me that you buried Cly. It was a take in. Me and two more knows it.”\n\n“How do you know it?”\n\n“What’s that to you? Ecod!” growled Mr. Cruncher, “it’s you I have got a old grudge again, is it, with your shameful impositions upon tradesmen! I’d catch hold of your throat and choke you for half a guinea.”\n\nSydney Carton, who, with Mr. Lorry, had been lost in amazement at this turn of the business, here requested Mr. Cruncher to moderate and explain himself.\n\n“At another time, sir,” he returned, evasively, “the present time is ill-conwenient for explainin’. What I stand to, is, that he knows well wot that there Cly was never in that there coffin. Let him say he was, in so much as a word of one syllable, and I’ll either catch hold of his throat and choke him for half a guinea;” Mr. Cruncher dwelt upon this as quite a liberal offer; “or I’ll out and announce him.”\n\n“Humph! I see one thing,” said Carton. “I hold another card, Mr. Barsad. Impossible, here in raging Paris, with Suspicion filling the air, for you to outlive denunciation, when you are in communication with another aristocratic spy of the same antecedents as yourself, who, moreover, has the mystery about him of having feigned death and come to life again! A plot in the prisons, of the foreigner against the Republic. A strong card--a certain Guillotine card! Do you play?”\n\n“No!” returned the spy. “I throw up. I confess that we were so unpopular with the outrageous mob, that I only got away from England at the risk of being ducked to death, and that Cly was so ferreted up and down, that he never would have got away at all but for that sham. Though how this man knows it was a sham, is a wonder of wonders to me.”\n\n“Never you trouble your head about this man,” retorted the contentious Mr. Cruncher; “you’ll have trouble enough with giving your attention to that gentleman. And look here! Once more!”--Mr. Cruncher could not be restrained from making rather an ostentatious parade of his liberality--“I’d catch hold of your throat and choke you for half a guinea.”\n\nThe Sheep of the prisons turned from him to Sydney Carton, and said, with more decision, “It has come to a point. I go on duty soon, and can’t overstay my time. You told me you had a proposal; what is it? Now, it is of no use asking too much of me. Ask me to do anything in my office, putting my head in great extra danger, and I had better trust my life to the chances of a refusal than the chances of consent. In short, I should make that choice. You talk of desperation. We are all desperate here. Remember! I may denounce you if I think proper, and I can swear my way through stone walls, and so can others. Now, what do you want with me?”\n\n“Not very much. You are a turnkey at the Conciergerie?”\n\n“I tell you once for all, there is no such thing as an escape possible,” said the spy, firmly.\n\n“Why need you tell me what I have not asked? You are a turnkey at the Conciergerie?”\n\n“I am sometimes.”\n\n“You can be when you choose?”\n\n“I can pass in and out when I choose.”\n\nSydney Carton filled another glass with brandy, poured it slowly out upon the hearth, and watched it as it dropped. It being all spent, he said, rising:\n\n“So far, we have spoken before these two, because it was as well that the merits of the cards should not rest solely between you and me. Come into the dark room here, and let us have one final word alone.”\n\n# CHAPTER IX. The Game Made\n\nWhile Sydney Carton and the Sheep of the prisons were in the adjoining dark room, speaking so low that not a sound was heard, Mr. Lorry looked at Jerry in considerable doubt and mistrust. That honest tradesman’s manner of receiving the look, did not inspire confidence; he changed the leg on which he rested, as often as if he had fifty of those limbs, and were trying them all; he examined his finger-nails with a very questionable closeness of attention; and whenever Mr. Lorry’s eye caught his, he was taken with that peculiar kind of short cough requiring the hollow of a hand before it, which is seldom, if ever, known to be an infirmity attendant on perfect openness of character.\n\n“Jerry,” said Mr. Lorry. “Come here.”\n\nMr. Cruncher came forward sideways, with one of his shoulders in advance of him.\n\n“What have you been, besides a messenger?”\n\nAfter some cogitation, accompanied with an intent look at his patron, Mr. Cruncher conceived the luminous idea of replying, “Agicultooral character.”\n\n“My mind misgives me much,” said Mr. Lorry, angrily shaking a forefinger at him, “that you have used the respectable and great house of Tellson’s as a blind, and that you have had an unlawful occupation of an infamous description. If you have, don’t expect me to befriend you when you get back to England. If you have, don’t expect me to keep your secret. Tellson’s shall not be imposed upon.”\n\n“I hope, sir,” pleaded the abashed Mr. Cruncher, “that a gentleman like yourself wot I’ve had the honour of odd jobbing till I’m grey at it, would think twice about harming of me, even if it wos so--I don’t say it is, but even if it wos. And which it is to be took into account that if it wos, it wouldn’t, even then, be all o’ one side. There’d be two sides to it. There might be medical doctors at the present hour, a picking up their guineas where a honest tradesman don’t pick up his fardens--fardens! no, nor yet his half fardens--half fardens! no, nor yet his quarter--a banking away like smoke at Tellson’s, and a cocking their medical eyes at that tradesman on the sly, a going in and going out to their own carriages--ah! equally like smoke, if not more so. Well, that ’ud be imposing, too, on Tellson’s. For you cannot sarse the goose and not the gander. And here’s Mrs. Cruncher, or leastways wos in the Old England times, and would be to-morrow, if cause given, a floppin’ again the business to that degree as is ruinating--stark ruinating! Whereas them medical doctors’ wives don’t flop--catch ’em at it! Or, if they flop, their floppings goes in favour of more patients, and how can you rightly have one without t’other? Then, wot with undertakers, and wot with parish clerks, and wot with sextons, and wot with private watchmen (all awaricious and all in it), a man wouldn’t get much by it, even if it wos so. And wot little a man did get, would never prosper with him, Mr. Lorry. He’d never have no good of it; he’d want all along to be out of the line, if he, could see his way out, being once in--even if it wos so.”\n\n“Ugh!” cried Mr. Lorry, rather relenting, nevertheless, “I am shocked at the sight of you.”\n\n“Now, what I would humbly offer to you, sir,” pursued Mr. Cruncher, “even if it wos so, which I don’t say it is--”\n\n“Don’t prevaricate,” said Mr. Lorry.\n\n“No, I will not, sir,” returned Mr. Crunches as if nothing were further from his thoughts or practice--“which I don’t say it is--wot I would humbly offer to you, sir, would be this. Upon that there stool, at that there Bar, sets that there boy of mine, brought up and growed up to be a man, wot will errand you, message you, general-light-job you, till your heels is where your head is, if such should be your wishes. If it wos so, which I still don’t say it is (for I will not prewaricate to you, sir), let that there boy keep his father’s place, and take care of his mother; don’t blow upon that boy’s father--do not do it, sir--and let that father go into the line of the reg’lar diggin’, and make amends for what he would have undug--if it wos so--by diggin’ of ’em in with a will, and with conwictions respectin’ the futur’ keepin’ of ’em safe. That, Mr. Lorry,” said Mr. Cruncher, wiping his forehead with his arm, as an announcement that he had arrived at the peroration of his discourse, “is wot I would respectfully offer to you, sir. A man don’t see all this here a goin’ on dreadful round him, in the way of Subjects without heads, dear me, plentiful enough fur to bring the price down to porterage and hardly that, without havin’ his serious thoughts of things. And these here would be mine, if it wos so, entreatin’ of you fur to bear in mind that wot I said just now, I up and said in the good cause when I might have kep’ it back.”\n\n“That at least is true,” said Mr. Lorry. “Say no more now. It may be that I shall yet stand your friend, if you deserve it, and repent in action--not in words. I want no more words.”\n\nMr. Cruncher knuckled his forehead, as Sydney Carton and the spy returned from the dark room. “Adieu, Mr. Barsad,” said the former; “our arrangement thus made, you have nothing to fear from me.”\n\nHe sat down in a chair on the hearth, over against Mr. Lorry. When they were alone, Mr. Lorry asked him what he had done?\n\n“Not much. If it should go ill with the prisoner, I have ensured access to him, once.”\n\nMr. Lorry’s countenance fell.\n\n“It is all I could do,” said Carton. “To propose too much, would be to put this man’s head under the axe, and, as he himself said, nothing worse could happen to him if he were denounced. It was obviously the weakness of the position. There is no help for it.”\n\n“But access to him,” said Mr. Lorry, “if it should go ill before the Tribunal, will not save him.”\n\n“I never said it would.”\n\nMr. Lorry’s eyes gradually sought the fire; his sympathy with his darling, and the heavy disappointment of his second arrest, gradually weakened them; he was an old man now, overborne with anxiety of late, and his tears fell.\n\n“You are a good man and a true friend,” said Carton, in an altered voice. “Forgive me if I notice that you are affected. I could not see my father weep, and sit by, careless. And I could not respect your sorrow more, if you were my father. You are free from that misfortune, however.”\n\nThough he said the last words, with a slip into his usual manner, there was a true feeling and respect both in his tone and in his touch, that Mr. Lorry, who had never seen the better side of him, was wholly unprepared for. He gave him his hand, and Carton gently pressed it.\n\n“To return to poor Darnay,” said Carton. “Don’t tell Her of this interview, or this arrangement. It would not enable Her to go to see him. She might think it was contrived, in case of the worse, to convey to him the means of anticipating the sentence.”\n\nMr. Lorry had not thought of that, and he looked quickly at Carton to see if it were in his mind. It seemed to be; he returned the look, and evidently understood it.\n\n“She might think a thousand things,” Carton said, “and any of them would only add to her trouble. Don’t speak of me to her. As I said to you when I first came, I had better not see her. I can put my hand out, to do any little helpful work for her that my hand can find to do, without that. You are going to her, I hope? She must be very desolate to-night.”\n\n“I am going now, directly.”\n\n“I am glad of that. She has such a strong attachment to you and reliance on you. How does she look?”\n\n“Anxious and unhappy, but very beautiful.”\n\n“Ah!”\n\nIt was a long, grieving sound, like a sigh--almost like a sob. It attracted Mr. Lorry’s eyes to Carton’s face, which was turned to the fire. A light, or a shade (the old gentleman could not have said which), passed from it as swiftly as a change will sweep over a hill-side on a wild bright day, and he lifted his foot to put back one of the little flaming logs, which was tumbling forward. He wore the white riding-coat and top-boots, then in vogue, and the light of the fire touching their light surfaces made him look very pale, with his long brown hair, all untrimmed, hanging loose about him. His indifference to fire was sufficiently remarkable to elicit a word of remonstrance from Mr. Lorry; his boot was still upon the hot embers of the flaming log, when it had broken under the weight of his foot.\n\n“I forgot it,” he said.\n\nMr. Lorry’s eyes were again attracted to his face. Taking note of the wasted air which clouded the naturally handsome features, and having the expression of prisoners’ faces fresh in his mind, he was strongly reminded of that expression.\n\n“And your duties here have drawn to an end, sir?” said Carton, turning to him.\n\n“Yes. As I was telling you last night when Lucie came in so unexpectedly, I have at length done all that I can do here. I hoped to have left them in perfect safety, and then to have quitted Paris. I have my Leave to Pass. I was ready to go.”\n\nThey were both silent.\n\n“Yours is a long life to look back upon, sir?” said Carton, wistfully.\n\n“I am in my seventy-eighth year.”\n\n“You have been useful all your life; steadily and constantly occupied; trusted, respected, and looked up to?”\n\n“I have been a man of business, ever since I have been a man. Indeed, I may say that I was a man of business when a boy.”\n\n“See what a place you fill at seventy-eight. How many people will miss you when you leave it empty!”\n\n“A solitary old bachelor,” answered Mr. Lorry, shaking his head. “There is nobody to weep for me.”\n\n“How can you say that? Wouldn’t She weep for you? Wouldn’t her child?”\n\n“Yes, yes, thank God. I didn’t quite mean what I said.”\n\n“It is a thing to thank God for; is it not?”\n\n“Surely, surely.”\n\n“If you could say, with truth, to your own solitary heart, to-night, ‘I have secured to myself the love and attachment, the gratitude or respect, of no human creature; I have won myself a tender place in no regard; I have done nothing good or serviceable to be remembered by!’ your seventy-eight years would be seventy-eight heavy curses; would they not?”\n\n“You say truly, Mr. Carton; I think they would be.”\n\nSydney turned his eyes again upon the fire, and, after a silence of a few moments, said:\n\n“I should like to ask you:--Does your childhood seem far off? Do the days when you sat at your mother’s knee, seem days of very long ago?”\n\nResponding to his softened manner, Mr. Lorry answered:\n\n“Twenty years back, yes; at this time of my life, no. For, as I draw closer and closer to the end, I travel in the circle, nearer and nearer to the beginning. It seems to be one of the kind smoothings and preparings of the way. My heart is touched now, by many remembrances that had long fallen asleep, of my pretty young mother (and I so old!), and by many associations of the days when what we call the World was not so real with me, and my faults were not confirmed in me.”\n\n“I understand the feeling!” exclaimed Carton, with a bright flush. “And you are the better for it?”\n\n“I hope so.”\n\nCarton terminated the conversation here, by rising to help him on with his outer coat; “But you,” said Mr. Lorry, reverting to the theme, “you are young.”\n\n“Yes,” said Carton. “I am not old, but my young way was never the way to age. Enough of me.”\n\n“And of me, I am sure,” said Mr. Lorry. “Are you going out?”\n\n“I’ll walk with you to her gate. You know my vagabond and restless habits. If I should prowl about the streets a long time, don’t be uneasy; I shall reappear in the morning. You go to the Court to-morrow?”\n\n“Yes, unhappily.”\n\n“I shall be there, but only as one of the crowd. My Spy will find a place for me. Take my arm, sir.”\n\nMr. Lorry did so, and they went down-stairs and out in the streets. A few minutes brought them to Mr. Lorry’s destination. Carton left him there; but lingered at a little distance, and turned back to the gate again when it was shut, and touched it. He had heard of her going to the prison every day. “She came out here,” he said, looking about him, “turned this way, must have trod on these stones often. Let me follow in her steps.”\n\nIt was ten o’clock at night when he stood before the prison of La Force, where she had stood hundreds of times. A little wood-sawyer, having closed his shop, was smoking his pipe at his shop-door.\n\n“Good night, citizen,” said Sydney Carton, pausing in going by; for, the man eyed him inquisitively.\n\n“Good night, citizen.”\n\n“How goes the Republic?”\n\n“You mean the Guillotine. Not ill. Sixty-three to-day. We shall mount to a hundred soon. Samson and his men complain sometimes, of being exhausted. Ha, ha, ha! He is so droll, that Samson. Such a Barber!”\n\n“Do you often go to see him--”\n\n“Shave? Always. Every day. What a barber! You have seen him at work?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“Go and see him when he has a good batch. Figure this to yourself, citizen; he shaved the sixty-three to-day, in less than two pipes! Less than two pipes. Word of honour!”\n\nAs the grinning little man held out the pipe he was smoking, to explain how he timed the executioner, Carton was so sensible of a rising desire to strike the life out of him, that he turned away.\n\n“But you are not English,” said the wood-sawyer, “though you wear English dress?”\n\n“Yes,” said Carton, pausing again, and answering over his shoulder.\n\n“You speak like a Frenchman.”\n\n“I am an old student here.”\n\n“Aha, a perfect Frenchman! Good night, Englishman.”\n\n“Good night, citizen.”\n\n“But go and see that droll dog,” the little man persisted, calling after him. “And take a pipe with you!”\n\nSydney had not gone far out of sight, when he stopped in the middle of the street under a glimmering lamp, and wrote with his pencil on a scrap of paper. Then, traversing with the decided step of one who remembered the way well, several dark and dirty streets--much dirtier than usual, for the best public thoroughfares remained uncleansed in those times of terror--he stopped at a chemist’s shop, which the owner was closing with his own hands. A small, dim, crooked shop, kept in a tortuous, up-hill thoroughfare, by a small, dim, crooked man.\n\nGiving this citizen, too, good night, as he confronted him at his counter, he laid the scrap of paper before him. “Whew!” the chemist whistled softly, as he read it. “Hi! hi! hi!”\n\nSydney Carton took no heed, and the chemist said:\n\n“For you, citizen?”\n“For me.”\n\n“You will be careful to keep them separate, citizen? You know the consequences of mixing them?”\n\n“Perfectly.”\n\nCertain small packets were made and given to him. He put them, one by one, in the breast of his inner coat, counted out the money for them, and deliberately left the shop. “There is nothing more to do,” said he, glancing upward at the moon, “until to-morrow. I can’t sleep.”\n\nIt was not a reckless manner, the manner in which he said these words aloud under the fast-sailing clouds, nor was it more expressive of negligence than defiance. It was the settled manner of a tired man, who had wandered and struggled and got lost, but who at length struck into his road and saw its end.\n\nLong ago, when he had been famous among his earliest competitors as a youth of great promise, he had followed his father to the grave. His mother had died, years before. These solemn words, which had been read at his father’s grave, arose in his mind as he went down the dark streets, among the heavy shadows, with the moon and the clouds sailing on high above him. “I am the resurrection and the life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me, shall never die.”\n\nIn a city dominated by the axe, alone at night, with natural sorrow rising in him for the sixty-three who had been that day put to death, and for to-morrow’s victims then awaiting their doom in the prisons, and still of to-morrow’s and to-morrow’s, the chain of association that brought the words home, like a rusty old ship’s anchor from the deep, might have been easily found. He did not seek it, but repeated them and went on.\n\nWith a solemn interest in the lighted windows where the people were going to rest, forgetful through a few calm hours of the horrors surrounding them; in the towers of the churches, where no prayers were said, for the popular revulsion had even travelled that length of self-destruction from years of priestly impostors, plunderers, and profligates; in the distant burial-places, reserved, as they wrote upon the gates, for Eternal Sleep; in the abounding gaols; and in the streets along which the sixties rolled to a death which had become so common and material, that no sorrowful story of a haunting Spirit ever arose among the people out of all the working of the Guillotine; with a solemn interest in the whole life and death of the city settling down to its short nightly pause in fury; Sydney Carton crossed the Seine again for the lighter streets.\n\nFew coaches were abroad, for riders in coaches were liable to be suspected, and gentility hid its head in red nightcaps, and put on heavy shoes, and trudged. But, the theatres were all well filled, and the people poured cheerfully out as he passed, and went chatting home. At one of the theatre doors, there was a little girl with a mother, looking for a way across the street through the mud. He carried the child over, and before the timid arm was loosed from his neck asked her for a kiss.\n\n“I am the resurrection and the life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me, shall never die.”\n\nNow, that the streets were quiet, and the night wore on, the words were in the echoes of his feet, and were in the air. Perfectly calm and steady, he sometimes repeated them to himself as he walked; but, he heard them always.\n\nThe night wore out, and, as he stood upon the bridge listening to the water as it splashed the river-walls of the Island of Paris, where the picturesque confusion of houses and cathedral shone bright in the light of the moon, the day came coldly, looking like a dead face out of the sky. Then, the night, with the moon and the stars, turned pale and died, and for a little while it seemed as if Creation were delivered over to Death’s dominion.\n\nBut, the glorious sun, rising, seemed to strike those words, that burden of the night, straight and warm to his heart in its long bright rays. And looking along them, with reverently shaded eyes, a bridge of light appeared to span the air between him and the sun, while the river sparkled under it.\n\nThe strong tide, so swift, so deep, and certain, was like a congenial friend, in the morning stillness. He walked by the stream, far from the houses, and in the light and warmth of the sun fell asleep on the bank. When he awoke and was afoot again, he lingered there yet a little longer, watching an eddy that turned and turned purposeless, until the stream absorbed it, and carried it on to the sea.--“Like me.”\n\nA trading-boat, with a sail of the softened colour of a dead leaf, then glided into his view, floated by him, and died away. As its silent track in the water disappeared, the prayer that had broken up out of his heart for a merciful consideration of all his poor blindnesses and errors, ended in the words, “I am the resurrection and the life.”\n\nMr. Lorry was already out when he got back, and it was easy to surmise where the good old man was gone. Sydney Carton drank nothing but a little coffee, ate some bread, and, having washed and changed to refresh himself, went out to the place of trial.\n\nThe court was all astir and a-buzz, when the black sheep--whom many fell away from in dread--pressed him into an obscure corner among the crowd. Mr. Lorry was there, and Doctor Manette was there. She was there, sitting beside her father.\n\nWhen her husband was brought in, she turned a look upon him, so sustaining, so encouraging, so full of admiring love and pitying tenderness, yet so courageous for his sake, that it called the healthy blood into his face, brightened his glance, and animated his heart. If there had been any eyes to notice the influence of her look, on Sydney Carton, it would have been seen to be the same influence exactly.\n\nBefore that unjust Tribunal, there was little or no order of procedure, ensuring to any accused person any reasonable hearing. There could have been no such Revolution, if all laws, forms, and ceremonies, had not first been so monstrously abused, that the suicidal vengeance of the Revolution was to scatter them all to the winds.\n\nEvery eye was turned to the jury. The same determined patriots and good republicans as yesterday and the day before, and to-morrow and the day after. Eager and prominent among them, one man with a craving face, and his fingers perpetually hovering about his lips, whose appearance gave great satisfaction to the spectators. A life-thirsting, cannibal-looking, bloody-minded juryman, the Jacques Three of St. Antoine. The whole jury, as a jury of dogs empannelled to try the deer.\n\nEvery eye then turned to the five judges and the public prosecutor. No favourable leaning in that quarter to-day. A fell, uncompromising, murderous business-meaning there. Every eye then sought some other eye in the crowd, and gleamed at it approvingly; and heads nodded at one another, before bending forward with a strained attention.\n\nCharles Evrémonde, called Darnay. Released yesterday. Reaccused and retaken yesterday. Indictment delivered to him last night. Suspected and Denounced enemy of the Republic, Aristocrat, one of a family of tyrants, one of a race proscribed, for that they had used their abolished privileges to the infamous oppression of the people. Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay, in right of such proscription, absolutely Dead in Law.\n\nTo this effect, in as few or fewer words, the Public Prosecutor.\n\nThe President asked, was the Accused openly denounced or secretly?\n\n“Openly, President.”\n\n“By whom?”\n\n“Three voices. Ernest Defarge, wine-vendor of St. Antoine.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Thérèse Defarge, his wife.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“Alexandre Manette, physician.”\n\nA great uproar took place in the court, and in the midst of it, Doctor Manette was seen, pale and trembling, standing where he had been seated.\n\n“President, I indignantly protest to you that this is a forgery and a fraud. You know the accused to be the husband of my daughter. My daughter, and those dear to her, are far dearer to me than my life. Who and where is the false conspirator who says that I denounce the husband of my child!”\n\n“Citizen Manette, be tranquil. To fail in submission to the authority of the Tribunal would be to put yourself out of Law. As to what is dearer to you than life, nothing can be so dear to a good citizen as the Republic.”\n\nLoud acclamations hailed this rebuke. The President rang his bell, and with warmth resumed.\n\n“If the Republic should demand of you the sacrifice of your child herself, you would have no duty but to sacrifice her. Listen to what is to follow. In the meanwhile, be silent!”\n\nFrantic acclamations were again raised. Doctor Manette sat down, with his eyes looking around, and his lips trembling; his daughter drew closer to him. The craving man on the jury rubbed his hands together, and restored the usual hand to his mouth.\n\nDefarge was produced, when the court was quiet enough to admit of his being heard, and rapidly expounded the story of the imprisonment, and of his having been a mere boy in the Doctor’s service, and of the release, and of the state of the prisoner when released and delivered to him. This short examination followed, for the court was quick with its work.\n\n“You did good service at the taking of the Bastille, citizen?”\n\n“I believe so.”\n\nHere, an excited woman screeched from the crowd: “You were one of the best patriots there. Why not say so? You were a cannonier that day there, and you were among the first to enter the accursed fortress when it fell. Patriots, I speak the truth!”\n\nIt was The Vengeance who, amidst the warm commendations of the audience, thus assisted the proceedings. The President rang his bell; but, The Vengeance, warming with encouragement, shrieked, “I defy that bell!” wherein she was likewise much commended.\n\n“Inform the Tribunal of what you did that day within the Bastille, citizen.”\n\n“I knew,” said Defarge, looking down at his wife, who stood at the bottom of the steps on which he was raised, looking steadily up at him; “I knew that this prisoner, of whom I speak, had been confined in a cell known as One Hundred and Five, North Tower. I knew it from himself. He knew himself by no other name than One Hundred and Five, North Tower, when he made shoes under my care. As I serve my gun that day, I resolve, when the place shall fall, to examine that cell. It falls. I mount to the cell, with a fellow-citizen who is one of the Jury, directed by a gaoler. I examine it, very closely. In a hole in the chimney, where a stone has been worked out and replaced, I find a written paper. This is that written paper. I have made it my business to examine some specimens of the writing of Doctor Manette. This is the writing of Doctor Manette. I confide this paper, in the writing of Doctor Manette, to the hands of the President.”\n\n“Let it be read.”\n\nIn a dead silence and stillness--the prisoner under trial looking lovingly at his wife, his wife only looking from him to look with solicitude at her father, Doctor Manette keeping his eyes fixed on the reader, Madame Defarge never taking hers from the prisoner, Defarge never taking his from his feasting wife, and all the other eyes there intent upon the Doctor, who saw none of them--the paper was read, as follows.\n\n# CHAPTER X. The Substance of the Shadow\n\n“I, Alexandre Manette, unfortunate physician, native of Beauvais, and afterwards resident in Paris, write this melancholy paper in my doleful cell in the Bastille, during the last month of the year, 1767. I write it at stolen intervals, under every difficulty. I design to secrete it in the wall of the chimney, where I have slowly and laboriously made a place of concealment for it. Some pitying hand may find it there, when I and my sorrows are dust.\n\n“These words are formed by the rusty iron point with which I write with difficulty in scrapings of soot and charcoal from the chimney, mixed with blood, in the last month of the tenth year of my captivity. Hope has quite departed from my breast. I know from terrible warnings I have noted in myself that my reason will not long remain unimpaired, but I solemnly declare that I am at this time in the possession of my right mind--that my memory is exact and circumstantial--and that I write the truth as I shall answer for these my last recorded words, whether they be ever read by men or not, at the Eternal Judgment-seat.\n\n“One cloudy moonlight night, in the third week of December (I think the twenty-second of the month) in the year 1757, I was walking on a retired part of the quay by the Seine for the refreshment of the frosty air, at an hour’s distance from my place of residence in the Street of the School of Medicine, when a carriage came along behind me, driven very fast. As I stood aside to let that carriage pass, apprehensive that it might otherwise run me down, a head was put out at the window, and a voice called to the driver to stop.\n\n“The carriage stopped as soon as the driver could rein in his horses, and the same voice called to me by my name. I answered. The carriage was then so far in advance of me that two gentlemen had time to open the door and alight before I came up with it.\n\n“I observed that they were both wrapped in cloaks, and appeared to conceal themselves. As they stood side by side near the carriage door, I also observed that they both looked of about my own age, or rather younger, and that they were greatly alike, in stature, manner, voice, and (as far as I could see) face too.\n\n“‘You are Doctor Manette?’ said one.\n“I am.”\n\n“‘Doctor Manette, formerly of Beauvais,’ said the other; ‘the young physician, originally an expert surgeon, who within the last year or two has made a rising reputation in Paris?’\n\n“‘Gentlemen,’ I returned, ‘I am that Doctor Manette of whom you speak so graciously.’\n\n“‘We have been to your residence,’ said the first, ‘and not being so fortunate as to find you there, and being informed that you were probably walking in this direction, we followed, in the hope of overtaking you. Will you please to enter the carriage?’\n\n“The manner of both was imperious, and they both moved, as these words were spoken, so as to place me between themselves and the carriage door. They were armed. I was not.\n\n“‘Gentlemen,’ said I, ‘pardon me; but I usually inquire who does me the honour to seek my assistance, and what is the nature of the case to which I am summoned.’\n\n“The reply to this was made by him who had spoken second. ‘Doctor, your clients are people of condition. As to the nature of the case, our confidence in your skill assures us that you will ascertain it for yourself better than we can describe it. Enough. Will you please to enter the carriage?’\n\n“I could do nothing but comply, and I entered it in silence. They both entered after me--the last springing in, after putting up the steps. The carriage turned about, and drove on at its former speed.\n\n“I repeat this conversation exactly as it occurred. I have no doubt that it is, word for word, the same. I describe everything exactly as it took place, constraining my mind not to wander from the task. Where I make the broken marks that follow here, I leave off for the time, and put my paper in its hiding-place.\n\n“The carriage left the streets behind, passed the North Barrier, and emerged upon the country road. At two-thirds of a league from the Barrier--I did not estimate the distance at that time, but afterwards when I traversed it--it struck out of the main avenue, and presently stopped at a solitary house, We all three alighted, and walked, by a damp soft footpath in a garden where a neglected fountain had overflowed, to the door of the house. It was not opened immediately, in answer to the ringing of the bell, and one of my two conductors struck the man who opened it, with his heavy riding glove, across the face.\n\n“There was nothing in this action to attract my particular attention, for I had seen common people struck more commonly than dogs. But, the other of the two, being angry likewise, struck the man in like manner with his arm; the look and bearing of the brothers were then so exactly alike, that I then first perceived them to be twin brothers.\n\n“From the time of our alighting at the outer gate (which we found locked, and which one of the brothers had opened to admit us, and had relocked), I had heard cries proceeding from an upper chamber. I was conducted to this chamber straight, the cries growing louder as we ascended the stairs, and I found a patient in a high fever of the brain, lying on a bed.\n\n“The patient was a woman of great beauty, and young; assuredly not much past twenty. Her hair was torn and ragged, and her arms were bound to her sides with sashes and handkerchiefs. I noticed that these bonds were all portions of a gentleman’s dress. On one of them, which was a fringed scarf for a dress of ceremony, I saw the armorial bearings of a Noble, and the letter E.\n\n“I saw this, within the first minute of my contemplation of the patient; for, in her restless strivings she had turned over on her face on the edge of the bed, had drawn the end of the scarf into her mouth, and was in danger of suffocation. My first act was to put out my hand to relieve her breathing; and in moving the scarf aside, the embroidery in the corner caught my sight.\n\n“I turned her gently over, placed my hands upon her breast to calm her and keep her down, and looked into her face. Her eyes were dilated and wild, and she constantly uttered piercing shrieks, and repeated the words, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ and then counted up to twelve, and said, ‘Hush!’ For an instant, and no more, she would pause to listen, and then the piercing shrieks would begin again, and she would repeat the cry, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ and would count up to twelve, and say, ‘Hush!’ There was no variation in the order, or the manner. There was no cessation, but the regular moment’s pause, in the utterance of these sounds.\n\n“‘How long,’ I asked, ‘has this lasted?’\n\n“To distinguish the brothers, I will call them the elder and the younger; by the elder, I mean him who exercised the most authority. It was the elder who replied, ‘Since about this hour last night.’\n\n“‘She has a husband, a father, and a brother?’\n\n“‘A brother.’\n\n“‘I do not address her brother?’\n\n“He answered with great contempt, ‘No.’\n\n“‘She has some recent association with the number twelve?’\n\n“The younger brother impatiently rejoined, ‘With twelve o’clock?’\n\n“‘See, gentlemen,’ said I, still keeping my hands upon her breast, ‘how useless I am, as you have brought me! If I had known what I was coming to see, I could have come provided. As it is, time must be lost. There are no medicines to be obtained in this lonely place.’\n\n“The elder brother looked to the younger, who said haughtily, ‘There is a case of medicines here;’ and brought it from a closet, and put it on the table.\n\n“I opened some of the bottles, smelt them, and put the stoppers to my lips. If I had wanted to use anything save narcotic medicines that were poisons in themselves, I would not have administered any of those.\n\n“‘Do you doubt them?’ asked the younger brother.\n\n“‘You see, monsieur, I am going to use them,’ I replied, and said no more.\n\n“I made the patient swallow, with great difficulty, and after many efforts, the dose that I desired to give. As I intended to repeat it after a while, and as it was necessary to watch its influence, I then sat down by the side of the bed. There was a timid and suppressed woman in attendance (wife of the man down-stairs), who had retreated into a corner. The house was damp and decayed, indifferently furnished--evidently, recently occupied and temporarily used. Some thick old hangings had been nailed up before the windows, to deaden the sound of the shrieks. They continued to be uttered in their regular succession, with the cry, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ the counting up to twelve, and ‘Hush!’ The frenzy was so violent, that I had not unfastened the bandages restraining the arms; but, I had looked to them, to see that they were not painful. The only spark of encouragement in the case, was, that my hand upon the sufferer’s breast had this much soothing influence, that for minutes at a time it tranquillised the figure. It had no effect upon the cries; no pendulum could be more regular.\n\n“For the reason that my hand had this effect (I assume), I had sat by the side of the bed for half an hour, with the two brothers looking on, before the elder said:\n\n“‘There is another patient.’\n\n“I was startled, and asked, ‘Is it a pressing case?’\n\n“‘You had better see,’ he carelessly answered; and took up a light.\n\n“The other patient lay in a back room across a second staircase, which was a species of loft over a stable. There was a low plastered ceiling to a part of it; the rest was open, to the ridge of the tiled roof, and there were beams across. Hay and straw were stored in that portion of the place, fagots for firing, and a heap of apples in sand. I had to pass through that part, to get at the other. My memory is circumstantial and unshaken. I try it with these details, and I see them all, in this my cell in the Bastille, near the close of the tenth year of my captivity, as I saw them all that night.\n\n“On some hay on the ground, with a cushion thrown under his head, lay a handsome peasant boy--a boy of not more than seventeen at the most. He lay on his back, with his teeth set, his right hand clenched on his breast, and his glaring eyes looking straight upward. I could not see where his wound was, as I kneeled on one knee over him; but, I could see that he was dying of a wound from a sharp point.\n\n“‘I am a doctor, my poor fellow,’ said I. ‘Let me examine it.’\n\n“‘I do not want it examined,’ he answered; ‘let it be.’\n\n“It was under his hand, and I soothed him to let me move his hand away. The wound was a sword-thrust, received from twenty to twenty-four hours before, but no skill could have saved him if it had been looked to without delay. He was then dying fast. As I turned my eyes to the elder brother, I saw him looking down at this handsome boy whose life was ebbing out, as if he were a wounded bird, or hare, or rabbit; not at all as if he were a fellow-creature.\n\n“‘How has this been done, monsieur?’ said I.\n\n“‘A crazed young common dog! A serf! Forced my brother to draw upon him, and has fallen by my brother’s sword--like a gentleman.’\n\n“There was no touch of pity, sorrow, or kindred humanity, in this answer. The speaker seemed to acknowledge that it was inconvenient to have that different order of creature dying there, and that it would have been better if he had died in the usual obscure routine of his vermin kind. He was quite incapable of any compassionate feeling about the boy, or about his fate.\n\n“The boy’s eyes had slowly moved to him as he had spoken, and they now slowly moved to me.\n\n“‘Doctor, they are very proud, these Nobles; but we common dogs are proud too, sometimes. They plunder us, outrage us, beat us, kill us; but we have a little pride left, sometimes. She--have you seen her, Doctor?’\n\n“The shrieks and the cries were audible there, though subdued by the distance. He referred to them, as if she were lying in our presence.\n\n“I said, ‘I have seen her.’\n\n“‘She is my sister, Doctor. They have had their shameful rights, these Nobles, in the modesty and virtue of our sisters, many years, but we have had good girls among us. I know it, and have heard my father say so. She was a good girl. She was betrothed to a good young man, too: a tenant of his. We were all tenants of his--that man’s who stands there. The other is his brother, the worst of a bad race.’\n\n“It was with the greatest difficulty that the boy gathered bodily force to speak; but, his spirit spoke with a dreadful emphasis.\n\n“‘We were so robbed by that man who stands there, as all we common dogs are by those superior Beings--taxed by him without mercy, obliged to work for him without pay, obliged to grind our corn at his mill, obliged to feed scores of his tame birds on our wretched crops, and forbidden for our lives to keep a single tame bird of our own, pillaged and plundered to that degree that when we chanced to have a bit of meat, we ate it in fear, with the door barred and the shutters closed, that his people should not see it and take it from us--I say, we were so robbed, and hunted, and were made so poor, that our father told us it was a dreadful thing to bring a child into the world, and that what we should most pray for, was, that our women might be barren and our miserable race die out!’\n\n“I had never before seen the sense of being oppressed, bursting forth like a fire. I had supposed that it must be latent in the people somewhere; but, I had never seen it break out, until I saw it in the dying boy.\n\n“‘Nevertheless, Doctor, my sister married. He was ailing at that time, poor fellow, and she married her lover, that she might tend and comfort him in our cottage--our dog-hut, as that man would call it. She had not been married many weeks, when that man’s brother saw her and admired her, and asked that man to lend her to him--for what are husbands among us! He was willing enough, but my sister was good and virtuous, and hated his brother with a hatred as strong as mine. What did the two then, to persuade her husband to use his influence with her, to make her willing?’\n\n“The boy’s eyes, which had been fixed on mine, slowly turned to the looker-on, and I saw in the two faces that all he said was true. The two opposing kinds of pride confronting one another, I can see, even in this Bastille; the gentleman’s, all negligent indifference; the peasant’s, all trodden-down sentiment, and passionate revenge.\n\n“‘You know, Doctor, that it is among the Rights of these Nobles to harness us common dogs to carts, and drive us. They so harnessed him and drove him. You know that it is among their Rights to keep us in their grounds all night, quieting the frogs, in order that their noble sleep may not be disturbed. They kept him out in the unwholesome mists at night, and ordered him back into his harness in the day. But he was not persuaded. No! Taken out of harness one day at noon, to feed--if he could find food--he sobbed twelve times, once for every stroke of the bell, and died on her bosom.’\n\n“Nothing human could have held life in the boy but his determination to tell all his wrong. He forced back the gathering shadows of death, as he forced his clenched right hand to remain clenched, and to cover his wound.\n\n“‘Then, with that man’s permission and even with his aid, his brother took her away; in spite of what I know she must have told his brother--and what that is, will not be long unknown to you, Doctor, if it is now--his brother took her away--for his pleasure and diversion, for a little while. I saw her pass me on the road. When I took the tidings home, our father’s heart burst; he never spoke one of the words that filled it. I took my young sister (for I have another) to a place beyond the reach of this man, and where, at least, she will never be his vassal. Then, I tracked the brother here, and last night climbed in--a common dog, but sword in hand.--Where is the loft window? It was somewhere here?’\n\n“The room was darkening to his sight; the world was narrowing around him. I glanced about me, and saw that the hay and straw were trampled over the floor, as if there had been a struggle.\n\n“‘She heard me, and ran in. I told her not to come near us till he was dead. He came in and first tossed me some pieces of money; then struck at me with a whip. But I, though a common dog, so struck at him as to make him draw. Let him break into as many pieces as he will, the sword that he stained with my common blood; he drew to defend himself--thrust at me with all his skill for his life.’\n\n“My glance had fallen, but a few moments before, on the fragments of a broken sword, lying among the hay. That weapon was a gentleman’s. In another place, lay an old sword that seemed to have been a soldier’s.\n\n“‘Now, lift me up, Doctor; lift me up. Where is he?’\n\n“‘He is not here,’ I said, supporting the boy, and thinking that he referred to the brother.\n\n“‘He! Proud as these nobles are, he is afraid to see me. Where is the man who was here? Turn my face to him.’\n\n“I did so, raising the boy’s head against my knee. But, invested for the moment with extraordinary power, he raised himself completely: obliging me to rise too, or I could not have still supported him.\n\n“‘Marquis,’ said the boy, turned to him with his eyes opened wide, and his right hand raised, ‘in the days when all these things are to be answered for, I summon you and yours, to the last of your bad race, to answer for them. I mark this cross of blood upon you, as a sign that I do it. In the days when all these things are to be answered for, I summon your brother, the worst of the bad race, to answer for them separately. I mark this cross of blood upon him, as a sign that I do it.’\n\n“Twice, he put his hand to the wound in his breast, and with his forefinger drew a cross in the air. He stood for an instant with the finger yet raised, and as it dropped, he dropped with it, and I laid him down dead.\n\n“When I returned to the bedside of the young woman, I found her raving in precisely the same order of continuity. I knew that this might last for many hours, and that it would probably end in the silence of the grave.\n\n“I repeated the medicines I had given her, and I sat at the side of the bed until the night was far advanced. She never abated the piercing quality of her shrieks, never stumbled in the distinctness or the order of her words. They were always ‘My husband, my father, and my brother! One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, twelve. Hush!’\n\n“This lasted twenty-six hours from the time when I first saw her. I had come and gone twice, and was again sitting by her, when she began to falter. I did what little could be done to assist that opportunity, and by-and-bye she sank into a lethargy, and lay like the dead.\n\n“It was as if the wind and rain had lulled at last, after a long and fearful storm. I released her arms, and called the woman to assist me to compose her figure and the dress she had torn. It was then that I knew her condition to be that of one in whom the first expectations of being a mother have arisen; and it was then that I lost the little hope I had had of her.\n\n“‘Is she dead?’ asked the Marquis, whom I will still describe as the elder brother, coming booted into the room from his horse.\n\n“‘Not dead,’ said I; ‘but like to die.’\n\n“‘What strength there is in these common bodies!’ he said, looking down at her with some curiosity.\n\n“‘There is prodigious strength,’ I answered him, ‘in sorrow and despair.’\n\n“He first laughed at my words, and then frowned at them. He moved a chair with his foot near to mine, ordered the woman away, and said in a subdued voice,\n\n“‘Doctor, finding my brother in this difficulty with these hinds, I recommended that your aid should be invited. Your reputation is high, and, as a young man with your fortune to make, you are probably mindful of your interest. The things that you see here, are things to be seen, and not spoken of.’\n\n“I listened to the patient’s breathing, and avoided answering.\n\n“‘Do you honour me with your attention, Doctor?’\n\n“‘Monsieur,’ said I, ‘in my profession, the communications of patients are always received in confidence.’ I was guarded in my answer, for I was troubled in my mind with what I had heard and seen.\n\n“Her breathing was so difficult to trace, that I carefully tried the pulse and the heart. There was life, and no more. Looking round as I resumed my seat, I found both the brothers intent upon me.\n\n“I write with so much difficulty, the cold is so severe, I am so fearful of being detected and consigned to an underground cell and total darkness, that I must abridge this narrative. There is no confusion or failure in my memory; it can recall, and could detail, every word that was ever spoken between me and those brothers.\n\n“She lingered for a week. Towards the last, I could understand some few syllables that she said to me, by placing my ear close to her lips. She asked me where she was, and I told her; who I was, and I told her. It was in vain that I asked her for her family name. She faintly shook her head upon the pillow, and kept her secret, as the boy had done.\n\n“I had no opportunity of asking her any question, until I had told the brothers she was sinking fast, and could not live another day. Until then, though no one was ever presented to her consciousness save the woman and myself, one or other of them had always jealously sat behind the curtain at the head of the bed when I was there. But when it came to that, they seemed careless what communication I might hold with her; as if--the thought passed through my mind--I were dying too.\n\n“I always observed that their pride bitterly resented the younger brother’s (as I call him) having crossed swords with a peasant, and that peasant a boy. The only consideration that appeared to affect the mind of either of them was the consideration that this was highly degrading to the family, and was ridiculous. As often as I caught the younger brother’s eyes, their expression reminded me that he disliked me deeply, for knowing what I knew from the boy. He was smoother and more polite to me than the elder; but I saw this. I also saw that I was an incumbrance in the mind of the elder, too.\n\n“My patient died, two hours before midnight--at a time, by my watch, answering almost to the minute when I had first seen her. I was alone with her, when her forlorn young head drooped gently on one side, and all her earthly wrongs and sorrows ended.\n\n“The brothers were waiting in a room down-stairs, impatient to ride away. I had heard them, alone at the bedside, striking their boots with their riding-whips, and loitering up and down.\n\n“‘At last she is dead?’ said the elder, when I went in.\n\n“‘She is dead,’ said I.\n\n“‘I congratulate you, my brother,’ were his words as he turned round.\n\n“He had before offered me money, which I had postponed taking. He now gave me a rouleau of gold. I took it from his hand, but laid it on the table. I had considered the question, and had resolved to accept nothing.\n\n“‘Pray excuse me,’ said I. ‘Under the circumstances, no.’\n\n“They exchanged looks, but bent their heads to me as I bent mine to them, and we parted without another word on either side.\n\n“I am weary, weary, weary--worn down by misery. I cannot read what I have written with this gaunt hand.\n\n“Early in the morning, the rouleau of gold was left at my door in a little box, with my name on the outside. From the first, I had anxiously considered what I ought to do. I decided, that day, to write privately to the Minister, stating the nature of the two cases to which I had been summoned, and the place to which I had gone: in effect, stating all the circumstances. I knew what Court influence was, and what the immunities of the Nobles were, and I expected that the matter would never be heard of; but, I wished to relieve my own mind. I had kept the matter a profound secret, even from my wife; and this, too, I resolved to state in my letter. I had no apprehension whatever of my real danger; but I was conscious that there might be danger for others, if others were compromised by possessing the knowledge that I possessed.\n\n“I was much engaged that day, and could not complete my letter that night. I rose long before my usual time next morning to finish it. It was the last day of the year. The letter was lying before me just completed, when I was told that a lady waited, who wished to see me.\n\n“I am growing more and more unequal to the task I have set myself. It is so cold, so dark, my senses are so benumbed, and the gloom upon me is so dreadful.\n\n“The lady was young, engaging, and handsome, but not marked for long life. She was in great agitation. She presented herself to me as the wife of the Marquis St. Evrémonde. I connected the title by which the boy had addressed the elder brother, with the initial letter embroidered on the scarf, and had no difficulty in arriving at the conclusion that I had seen that nobleman very lately.\n\n“My memory is still accurate, but I cannot write the words of our conversation. I suspect that I am watched more closely than I was, and I know not at what times I may be watched. She had in part suspected, and in part discovered, the main facts of the cruel story, of her husband’s share in it, and my being resorted to. She did not know that the girl was dead. Her hope had been, she said in great distress, to show her, in secret, a woman’s sympathy. Her hope had been to avert the wrath of Heaven from a House that had long been hateful to the suffering many.\n\n“She had reasons for believing that there was a young sister living, and her greatest desire was, to help that sister. I could tell her nothing but that there was such a sister; beyond that, I knew nothing. Her inducement to come to me, relying on my confidence, had been the hope that I could tell her the name and place of abode. Whereas, to this wretched hour I am ignorant of both.\n\n“These scraps of paper fail me. One was taken from me, with a warning, yesterday. I must finish my record to-day.\n\n“She was a good, compassionate lady, and not happy in her marriage. How could she be! The brother distrusted and disliked her, and his influence was all opposed to her; she stood in dread of him, and in dread of her husband too. When I handed her down to the door, there was a child, a pretty boy from two to three years old, in her carriage.\n\n“‘For his sake, Doctor,’ she said, pointing to him in tears, ‘I would do all I can to make what poor amends I can. He will never prosper in his inheritance otherwise. I have a presentiment that if no other innocent atonement is made for this, it will one day be required of him. What I have left to call my own--it is little beyond the worth of a few jewels--I will make it the first charge of his life to bestow, with the compassion and lamenting of his dead mother, on this injured family, if the sister can be discovered.’\n\n“She kissed the boy, and said, caressing him, ‘It is for thine own dear sake. Thou wilt be faithful, little Charles?’ The child answered her bravely, ‘Yes!’ I kissed her hand, and she took him in her arms, and went away caressing him. I never saw her more.\n\n“As she had mentioned her husband’s name in the faith that I knew it, I added no mention of it to my letter. I sealed my letter, and, not trusting it out of my own hands, delivered it myself that day.\n\n“That night, the last night of the year, towards nine o’clock, a man in a black dress rang at my gate, demanded to see me, and softly followed my servant, Ernest Defarge, a youth, up-stairs. When my servant came into the room where I sat with my wife--O my wife, beloved of my heart! My fair young English wife!--we saw the man, who was supposed to be at the gate, standing silent behind him.\n\n“An urgent case in the Rue St. Honore, he said. It would not detain me, he had a coach in waiting.\n\n“It brought me here, it brought me to my grave. When I was clear of the house, a black muffler was drawn tightly over my mouth from behind, and my arms were pinioned. The two brothers crossed the road from a dark corner, and identified me with a single gesture. The Marquis took from his pocket the letter I had written, showed it me, burnt it in the light of a lantern that was held, and extinguished the ashes with his foot. Not a word was spoken. I was brought here, I was brought to my living grave.\n\n“If it had pleased God to put it in the hard heart of either of the brothers, in all these frightful years, to grant me any tidings of my dearest wife--so much as to let me know by a word whether alive or dead--I might have thought that He had not quite abandoned them. But, now I believe that the mark of the red cross is fatal to them, and that they have no part in His mercies. And them and their descendants, to the last of their race, I, Alexandre Manette, unhappy prisoner, do this last night of the year 1767, in my unbearable agony, denounce to the times when all these things shall be answered for. I denounce them to Heaven and to earth.”\n\nA terrible sound arose when the reading of this document was done. A sound of craving and eagerness that had nothing articulate in it but blood. The narrative called up the most revengeful passions of the time, and there was not a head in the nation but must have dropped before it.\n\nLittle need, in presence of that tribunal and that auditory, to show how the Defarges had not made the paper public, with the other captured Bastille memorials borne in procession, and had kept it, biding their time. Little need to show that this detested family name had long been anathematised by Saint Antoine, and was wrought into the fatal register. The man never trod ground whose virtues and services would have sustained him in that place that day, against such denunciation.\n\nAnd all the worse for the doomed man, that the denouncer was a well-known citizen, his own attached friend, the father of his wife. One of the frenzied aspirations of the populace was, for imitations of the questionable public virtues of antiquity, and for sacrifices and self-immolations on the people’s altar. Therefore when the President said (else had his own head quivered on his shoulders), that the good physician of the Republic would deserve better still of the Republic by rooting out an obnoxious family of Aristocrats, and would doubtless feel a sacred glow and joy in making his daughter a widow and her child an orphan, there was wild excitement, patriotic fervour, not a touch of human sympathy.\n\n“Much influence around him, has that Doctor?” murmured Madame Defarge, smiling to The Vengeance. “Save him now, my Doctor, save him!”\n\nAt every juryman’s vote, there was a roar. Another and another. Roar and roar.\n\nUnanimously voted. At heart and by descent an Aristocrat, an enemy of the Republic, a notorious oppressor of the People. Back to the Conciergerie, and Death within four-and-twenty hours!\n\n# CHAPTER XI. Dusk\n\nThe wretched wife of the innocent man thus doomed to die, fell under the sentence, as if she had been mortally stricken. But, she uttered no sound; and so strong was the voice within her, representing that it was she of all the world who must uphold him in his misery and not augment it, that it quickly raised her, even from that shock.\n\nThe Judges having to take part in a public demonstration out of doors, the Tribunal adjourned. The quick noise and movement of the court’s emptying itself by many passages had not ceased, when Lucie stood stretching out her arms towards her husband, with nothing in her face but love and consolation.\n\n“If I might touch him! If I might embrace him once! O, good citizens, if you would have so much compassion for us!”\n\nThere was but a gaoler left, along with two of the four men who had taken him last night, and Barsad. The people had all poured out to the show in the streets. Barsad proposed to the rest, “Let her embrace him then; it is but a moment.” It was silently acquiesced in, and they passed her over the seats in the hall to a raised place, where he, by leaning over the dock, could fold her in his arms.\n\n“Farewell, dear darling of my soul. My parting blessing on my love. We shall meet again, where the weary are at rest!”\n\nThey were her husband’s words, as he held her to his bosom.\n\n“I can bear it, dear Charles. I am supported from above: don’t suffer for me. A parting blessing for our child.”\n\n“I send it to her by you. I kiss her by you. I say farewell to her by you.”\n\n“My husband. No! A moment!” He was tearing himself apart from her. “We shall not be separated long. I feel that this will break my heart by-and-bye; but I will do my duty while I can, and when I leave her, God will raise up friends for her, as He did for me.”\n\nHer father had followed her, and would have fallen on his knees to both of them, but that Darnay put out a hand and seized him, crying:\n\n“No, no! What have you done, what have you done, that you should kneel to us! We know now, what a struggle you made of old. We know, now what you underwent when you suspected my descent, and when you knew it. We know now, the natural antipathy you strove against, and conquered, for her dear sake. We thank you with all our hearts, and all our love and duty. Heaven be with you!”\n\nHer father’s only answer was to draw his hands through his white hair, and wring them with a shriek of anguish.\n\n“It could not be otherwise,” said the prisoner. “All things have worked together as they have fallen out. It was the always-vain endeavour to discharge my poor mother’s trust that first brought my fatal presence near you. Good could never come of such evil, a happier end was not in nature to so unhappy a beginning. Be comforted, and forgive me. Heaven bless you!”\n\nAs he was drawn away, his wife released him, and stood looking after him with her hands touching one another in the attitude of prayer, and with a radiant look upon her face, in which there was even a comforting smile. As he went out at the prisoners’ door, she turned, laid her head lovingly on her father’s breast, tried to speak to him, and fell at his feet.\n\nThen, issuing from the obscure corner from which he had never moved, Sydney Carton came and took her up. Only her father and Mr. Lorry were with her. His arm trembled as it raised her, and supported her head. Yet, there was an air about him that was not all of pity--that had a flush of pride in it.\n\n“Shall I take her to a coach? I shall never feel her weight.”\n\nHe carried her lightly to the door, and laid her tenderly down in a coach. Her father and their old friend got into it, and he took his seat beside the driver.\n\nWhen they arrived at the gateway where he had paused in the dark not many hours before, to picture to himself on which of the rough stones of the street her feet had trodden, he lifted her again, and carried her up the staircase to their rooms. There, he laid her down on a couch, where her child and Miss Pross wept over her.\n\n“Don’t recall her to herself,” he said, softly, to the latter, “she is better so. Don’t revive her to consciousness, while she only faints.”\n\n“Oh, Carton, Carton, dear Carton!” cried little Lucie, springing up and throwing her arms passionately round him, in a burst of grief. “Now that you have come, I think you will do something to help mamma, something to save papa! O, look at her, dear Carton! Can you, of all the people who love her, bear to see her so?”\n\nHe bent over the child, and laid her blooming cheek against his face. He put her gently from him, and looked at her unconscious mother.\n\n“Before I go,” he said, and paused--“I may kiss her?”\n\nIt was remembered afterwards that when he bent down and touched her face with his lips, he murmured some words. The child, who was nearest to him, told them afterwards, and told her grandchildren when she was a handsome old lady, that she heard him say, “A life you love.”\n\nWhen he had gone out into the next room, he turned suddenly on Mr. Lorry and her father, who were following, and said to the latter:\n\n“You had great influence but yesterday, Doctor Manette; let it at least be tried. These judges, and all the men in power, are very friendly to you, and very recognisant of your services; are they not?”\n\n“Nothing connected with Charles was concealed from me. I had the strongest assurances that I should save him; and I did.” He returned the answer in great trouble, and very slowly.\n\n“Try them again. The hours between this and to-morrow afternoon are few and short, but try.”\n\n“I intend to try. I will not rest a moment.”\n\n“That’s well. I have known such energy as yours do great things before now--though never,” he added, with a smile and a sigh together, “such great things as this. But try! Of little worth as life is when we misuse it, it is worth that effort. It would cost nothing to lay down if it were not.”\n\n“I will go,” said Doctor Manette, “to the Prosecutor and the President straight, and I will go to others whom it is better not to name. I will write too, and--But stay! There is a Celebration in the streets, and no one will be accessible until dark.”\n\n“That’s true. Well! It is a forlorn hope at the best, and not much the forlorner for being delayed till dark. I should like to know how you speed; though, mind! I expect nothing! When are you likely to have seen these dread powers, Doctor Manette?”\n\n“Immediately after dark, I should hope. Within an hour or two from this.”\n\n“It will be dark soon after four. Let us stretch the hour or two. If I go to Mr. Lorry’s at nine, shall I hear what you have done, either from our friend or from yourself?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“May you prosper!”\n\nMr. Lorry followed Sydney to the outer door, and, touching him on the shoulder as he was going away, caused him to turn.\n\n“I have no hope,” said Mr. Lorry, in a low and sorrowful whisper.\n\n“Nor have I.”\n\n“If any one of these men, or all of these men, were disposed to spare him--which is a large supposition; for what is his life, or any man’s to them!--I doubt if they durst spare him after the demonstration in the court.”\n\n“And so do I. I heard the fall of the axe in that sound.”\n\nMr. Lorry leaned his arm upon the door-post, and bowed his face upon it.\n\n“Don’t despond,” said Carton, very gently; “don’t grieve. I encouraged Doctor Manette in this idea, because I felt that it might one day be consolatory to her. Otherwise, she might think ‘his life was wantonly thrown away or wasted,’ and that might trouble her.”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes,” returned Mr. Lorry, drying his eyes, “you are right. But he will perish; there is no real hope.”\n\n“Yes. He will perish: there is no real hope,” echoed Carton.\n\nAnd walked with a settled step, down-stairs.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. Darkness\n\nSydney Carton paused in the street, not quite decided where to go. “At Tellson’s banking-house at nine,” he said, with a musing face. “Shall I do well, in the mean time, to show myself? I think so. It is best that these people should know there is such a man as I here; it is a sound precaution, and may be a necessary preparation. But care, care, care! Let me think it out!”\n\nChecking his steps which had begun to tend towards an object, he took a turn or two in the already darkening street, and traced the thought in his mind to its possible consequences. His first impression was confirmed. “It is best,” he said, finally resolved, “that these people should know there is such a man as I here.” And he turned his face towards Saint Antoine.\n\nDefarge had described himself, that day, as the keeper of a wine-shop in the Saint Antoine suburb. It was not difficult for one who knew the city well, to find his house without asking any question. Having ascertained its situation, Carton came out of those closer streets again, and dined at a place of refreshment and fell sound asleep after dinner. For the first time in many years, he had no strong drink. Since last night he had taken nothing but a little light thin wine, and last night he had dropped the brandy slowly down on Mr. Lorry’s hearth like a man who had done with it.\n\nIt was as late as seven o’clock when he awoke refreshed, and went out into the streets again. As he passed along towards Saint Antoine, he stopped at a shop-window where there was a mirror, and slightly altered the disordered arrangement of his loose cravat, and his coat-collar, and his wild hair. This done, he went on direct to Defarge’s, and went in.\n\nThere happened to be no customer in the shop but Jacques Three, of the restless fingers and the croaking voice. This man, whom he had seen upon the Jury, stood drinking at the little counter, in conversation with the Defarges, man and wife. The Vengeance assisted in the conversation, like a regular member of the establishment.\n\nAs Carton walked in, took his seat and asked (in very indifferent French) for a small measure of wine, Madame Defarge cast a careless glance at him, and then a keener, and then a keener, and then advanced to him herself, and asked him what it was he had ordered.\n\nHe repeated what he had already said.\n\n“English?” asked Madame Defarge, inquisitively raising her dark eyebrows.\n\nAfter looking at her, as if the sound of even a single French word were slow to express itself to him, he answered, in his former strong foreign accent. “Yes, madame, yes. I am English!”\n\nMadame Defarge returned to her counter to get the wine, and, as he took up a Jacobin journal and feigned to pore over it puzzling out its meaning, he heard her say, “I swear to you, like Evrémonde!”\n\nDefarge brought him the wine, and gave him Good Evening.\n\n“Good evening.”\n\n“Oh! Good evening, citizen,” filling his glass. “Ah! and good wine. I drink to the Republic.”\n\nDefarge went back to the counter, and said, “Certainly, a little like.” Madame sternly retorted, “I tell you a good deal like.” Jacques Three pacifically remarked, “He is so much in your mind, see you, madame.” The amiable Vengeance added, with a laugh, “Yes, my faith! And you are looking forward with so much pleasure to seeing him once more to-morrow!”\n\nCarton followed the lines and words of his paper, with a slow forefinger, and with a studious and absorbed face. They were all leaning their arms on the counter close together, speaking low. After a silence of a few moments, during which they all looked towards him without disturbing his outward attention from the Jacobin editor, they resumed their conversation.\n\n“It is true what madame says,” observed Jacques Three. “Why stop? There is great force in that. Why stop?”\n\n“Well, well,” reasoned Defarge, “but one must stop somewhere. After all, the question is still where?”\n\n“At extermination,” said madame.\n\n“Magnificent!” croaked Jacques Three. The Vengeance, also, highly approved.\n\n“Extermination is good doctrine, my wife,” said Defarge, rather troubled; “in general, I say nothing against it. But this Doctor has suffered much; you have seen him to-day; you have observed his face when the paper was read.”\n\n“I have observed his face!” repeated madame, contemptuously and angrily. “Yes. I have observed his face. I have observed his face to be not the face of a true friend of the Republic. Let him take care of his face!”\n\n“And you have observed, my wife,” said Defarge, in a deprecatory manner, “the anguish of his daughter, which must be a dreadful anguish to him!”\n\n“I have observed his daughter,” repeated madame; “yes, I have observed his daughter, more times than one. I have observed her to-day, and I have observed her other days. I have observed her in the court, and I have observed her in the street by the prison. Let me but lift my finger--!” She seemed to raise it (the listener’s eyes were always on his paper), and to let it fall with a rattle on the ledge before her, as if the axe had dropped.\n\n“The citizeness is superb!” croaked the Juryman.\n\n“She is an Angel!” said The Vengeance, and embraced her.\n\n“As to thee,” pursued madame, implacably, addressing her husband, “if it depended on thee--which, happily, it does not--thou wouldst rescue this man even now.”\n\n“No!” protested Defarge. “Not if to lift this glass would do it! But I would leave the matter there. I say, stop there.”\n\n“See you then, Jacques,” said Madame Defarge, wrathfully; “and see you, too, my little Vengeance; see you both! Listen! For other crimes as tyrants and oppressors, I have this race a long time on my register, doomed to destruction and extermination. Ask my husband, is that so.”\n\n“It is so,” assented Defarge, without being asked.\n\n“In the beginning of the great days, when the Bastille falls, he finds this paper of to-day, and he brings it home, and in the middle of the night when this place is clear and shut, we read it, here on this spot, by the light of this lamp. Ask him, is that so.”\n\n“It is so,” assented Defarge.\n\n“That night, I tell him, when the paper is read through, and the lamp is burnt out, and the day is gleaming in above those shutters and between those iron bars, that I have now a secret to communicate. Ask him, is that so.”\n\n“It is so,” assented Defarge again.\n\n“I communicate to him that secret. I smite this bosom with these two hands as I smite it now, and I tell him, ‘Defarge, I was brought up among the fishermen of the sea-shore, and that peasant family so injured by the two Evrémonde brothers, as that Bastille paper describes, is my family. Defarge, that sister of the mortally wounded boy upon the ground was my sister, that husband was my sister’s husband, that unborn child was their child, that brother was my brother, that father was my father, those dead are my dead, and that summons to answer for those things descends to me!’ Ask him, is that so.”\n\n“It is so,” assented Defarge once more.\n\n“Then tell Wind and Fire where to stop,” returned madame; “but don’t tell me.”\n\nBoth her hearers derived a horrible enjoyment from the deadly nature of her wrath--the listener could feel how white she was, without seeing her--and both highly commended it. Defarge, a weak minority, interposed a few words for the memory of the compassionate wife of the Marquis; but only elicited from his own wife a repetition of her last reply. “Tell the Wind and the Fire where to stop; not me!”\n\nCustomers entered, and the group was broken up. The English customer paid for what he had had, perplexedly counted his change, and asked, as a stranger, to be directed towards the National Palace. Madame Defarge took him to the door, and put her arm on his, in pointing out the road. The English customer was not without his reflections then, that it might be a good deed to seize that arm, lift it, and strike under it sharp and deep.\n\nBut, he went his way, and was soon swallowed up in the shadow of the prison wall. At the appointed hour, he emerged from it to present himself in Mr. Lorry’s room again, where he found the old gentleman walking to and fro in restless anxiety. He said he had been with Lucie until just now, and had only left her for a few minutes, to come and keep his appointment. Her father had not been seen, since he quitted the banking-house towards four o’clock. She had some faint hopes that his mediation might save Charles, but they were very slight. He had been more than five hours gone: where could he be?\n\nMr. Lorry waited until ten; but, Doctor Manette not returning, and he being unwilling to leave Lucie any longer, it was arranged that he should go back to her, and come to the banking-house again at midnight. In the meanwhile, Carton would wait alone by the fire for the Doctor.\n\nHe waited and waited, and the clock struck twelve; but Doctor Manette did not come back. Mr. Lorry returned, and found no tidings of him, and brought none. Where could he be?\n\nThey were discussing this question, and were almost building up some weak structure of hope on his prolonged absence, when they heard him on the stairs. The instant he entered the room, it was plain that all was lost.\n\nWhether he had really been to any one, or whether he had been all that time traversing the streets, was never known. As he stood staring at them, they asked him no question, for his face told them everything.\n\n“I cannot find it,” said he, “and I must have it. Where is it?”\n\nHis head and throat were bare, and, as he spoke with a helpless look straying all around, he took his coat off, and let it drop on the floor.\n\n“Where is my bench? I have been looking everywhere for my bench, and I can’t find it. What have they done with my work? Time presses: I must finish those shoes.”\n\nThey looked at one another, and their hearts died within them.\n\n“Come, come!” said he, in a whimpering miserable way; “let me get to work. Give me my work.”\n\nReceiving no answer, he tore his hair, and beat his feet upon the ground, like a distracted child.\n\n“Don’t torture a poor forlorn wretch,” he implored them, with a dreadful cry; “but give me my work! What is to become of us, if those shoes are not done to-night?”\n\nLost, utterly lost!\n\nIt was so clearly beyond hope to reason with him, or try to restore him, that--as if by agreement--they each put a hand upon his shoulder, and soothed him to sit down before the fire, with a promise that he should have his work presently. He sank into the chair, and brooded over the embers, and shed tears. As if all that had happened since the garret time were a momentary fancy, or a dream, Mr. Lorry saw him shrink into the exact figure that Defarge had had in keeping.\n\nAffected, and impressed with terror as they both were, by this spectacle of ruin, it was not a time to yield to such emotions. His lonely daughter, bereft of her final hope and reliance, appealed to them both too strongly. Again, as if by agreement, they looked at one another with one meaning in their faces. Carton was the first to speak:\n\n“The last chance is gone: it was not much. Yes; he had better be taken to her. But, before you go, will you, for a moment, steadily attend to me? Don’t ask me why I make the stipulations I am going to make, and exact the promise I am going to exact; I have a reason--a good one.”\n\n“I do not doubt it,” answered Mr. Lorry. “Say on.”\n\nThe figure in the chair between them, was all the time monotonously rocking itself to and fro, and moaning. They spoke in such a tone as they would have used if they had been watching by a sick-bed in the night.\n\nCarton stooped to pick up the coat, which lay almost entangling his feet. As he did so, a small case in which the Doctor was accustomed to carry the lists of his day’s duties, fell lightly on the floor. Carton took it up, and there was a folded paper in it. “We should look at this!” he said. Mr. Lorry nodded his consent. He opened it, and exclaimed, “Thank God!”\n\n“What is it?” asked Mr. Lorry, eagerly.\n\n“A moment! Let me speak of it in its place. First,” he put his hand in his coat, and took another paper from it, “that is the certificate which enables me to pass out of this city. Look at it. You see--Sydney Carton, an Englishman?”\n\nMr. Lorry held it open in his hand, gazing in his earnest face.\n\n“Keep it for me until to-morrow. I shall see him to-morrow, you remember, and I had better not take it into the prison.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“I don’t know; I prefer not to do so. Now, take this paper that Doctor Manette has carried about him. It is a similar certificate, enabling him and his daughter and her child, at any time, to pass the barrier and the frontier! You see?”\n\n“Yes!”\n\n“Perhaps he obtained it as his last and utmost precaution against evil, yesterday. When is it dated? But no matter; don’t stay to look; put it up carefully with mine and your own. Now, observe! I never doubted until within this hour or two, that he had, or could have such a paper. It is good, until recalled. But it may be soon recalled, and, I have reason to think, will be.”\n\n“They are not in danger?”\n\n“They are in great danger. They are in danger of denunciation by Madame Defarge. I know it from her own lips. I have overheard words of that woman’s, to-night, which have presented their danger to me in strong colours. I have lost no time, and since then, I have seen the spy. He confirms me. He knows that a wood-sawyer, living by the prison wall, is under the control of the Defarges, and has been rehearsed by Madame Defarge as to his having seen Her”--he never mentioned Lucie’s name--“making signs and signals to prisoners. It is easy to foresee that the pretence will be the common one, a prison plot, and that it will involve her life--and perhaps her child’s--and perhaps her father’s--for both have been seen with her at that place. Don’t look so horrified. You will save them all.”\n\n“Heaven grant I may, Carton! But how?”\n\n“I am going to tell you how. It will depend on you, and it could depend on no better man. This new denunciation will certainly not take place until after to-morrow; probably not until two or three days afterwards; more probably a week afterwards. You know it is a capital crime, to mourn for, or sympathise with, a victim of the Guillotine. She and her father would unquestionably be guilty of this crime, and this woman (the inveteracy of whose pursuit cannot be described) would wait to add that strength to her case, and make herself doubly sure. You follow me?”\n\n“So attentively, and with so much confidence in what you say, that for the moment I lose sight,” touching the back of the Doctor’s chair, “even of this distress.”\n\n“You have money, and can buy the means of travelling to the seacoast as quickly as the journey can be made. Your preparations have been completed for some days, to return to England. Early to-morrow have your horses ready, so that they may be in starting trim at two o’clock in the afternoon.”\n\n“It shall be done!”\n\nHis manner was so fervent and inspiring, that Mr. Lorry caught the flame, and was as quick as youth.\n\n“You are a noble heart. Did I say we could depend upon no better man? Tell her, to-night, what you know of her danger as involving her child and her father. Dwell upon that, for she would lay her own fair head beside her husband’s cheerfully.” He faltered for an instant; then went on as before. “For the sake of her child and her father, press upon her the necessity of leaving Paris, with them and you, at that hour. Tell her that it was her husband’s last arrangement. Tell her that more depends upon it than she dare believe, or hope. You think that her father, even in this sad state, will submit himself to her; do you not?”\n\n“I am sure of it.”\n\n“I thought so. Quietly and steadily have all these arrangements made in the courtyard here, even to the taking of your own seat in the carriage. The moment I come to you, take me in, and drive away.”\n\n“I understand that I wait for you under all circumstances?”\n\n“You have my certificate in your hand with the rest, you know, and will reserve my place. Wait for nothing but to have my place occupied, and then for England!”\n\n“Why, then,” said Mr. Lorry, grasping his eager but so firm and steady hand, “it does not all depend on one old man, but I shall have a young and ardent man at my side.”\n\n“By the help of Heaven you shall! Promise me solemnly that nothing will influence you to alter the course on which we now stand pledged to one another.”\n\n“Nothing, Carton.”\n“Remember these words to-morrow: change the course, or delay in it--for any reason--and no life can possibly be saved, and many lives must inevitably be sacrificed.”\n\n“I will remember them. I hope to do my part faithfully.”\n\n“And I hope to do mine. Now, good bye!”\n\nThough he said it with a grave smile of earnestness, and though he even put the old man’s hand to his lips, he did not part from him then. He helped him so far to arouse the rocking figure before the dying embers, as to get a cloak and hat put upon it, and to tempt it forth to find where the bench and work were hidden that it still moaningly besought to have. He walked on the other side of it and protected it to the courtyard of the house where the afflicted heart--so happy in the memorable time when he had revealed his own desolate heart to it--outwatched the awful night. He entered the courtyard and remained there for a few moments alone, looking up at the light in the window of her room. Before he went away, he breathed a blessing towards it, and a Farewell.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. Fifty-two\n\nIn the black prison of the Conciergerie, the doomed of the day awaited their fate. They were in number as the weeks of the year. Fifty-two were to roll that afternoon on the life-tide of the city to the boundless everlasting sea. Before their cells were quit of them, new occupants were appointed; before their blood ran into the blood spilled yesterday, the blood that was to mingle with theirs to-morrow was already set apart.\n\nTwo score and twelve were told off. From the farmer-general of seventy, whose riches could not buy his life, to the seamstress of twenty, whose poverty and obscurity could not save her. Physical diseases, engendered in the vices and neglects of men, will seize on victims of all degrees; and the frightful moral disorder, born of unspeakable suffering, intolerable oppression, and heartless indifference, smote equally without distinction.\n\nCharles Darnay, alone in a cell, had sustained himself with no flattering delusion since he came to it from the Tribunal. In every line of the narrative he had heard, he had heard his condemnation. He had fully comprehended that no personal influence could possibly save him, that he was virtually sentenced by the millions, and that units could avail him nothing.\n\nNevertheless, it was not easy, with the face of his beloved wife fresh before him, to compose his mind to what it must bear. His hold on life was strong, and it was very, very hard, to loosen; by gradual efforts and degrees unclosed a little here, it clenched the tighter there; and when he brought his strength to bear on that hand and it yielded, this was closed again. There was a hurry, too, in all his thoughts, a turbulent and heated working of his heart, that contended against resignation. If, for a moment, he did feel resigned, then his wife and child who had to live after him, seemed to protest and to make it a selfish thing.\n\nBut, all this was at first. Before long, the consideration that there was no disgrace in the fate he must meet, and that numbers went the same road wrongfully, and trod it firmly every day, sprang up to stimulate him. Next followed the thought that much of the future peace of mind enjoyable by the dear ones, depended on his quiet fortitude. So, by degrees he calmed into the better state, when he could raise his thoughts much higher, and draw comfort down.\n\nBefore it had set in dark on the night of his condemnation, he had travelled thus far on his last way. Being allowed to purchase the means of writing, and a light, he sat down to write until such time as the prison lamps should be extinguished.\n\nHe wrote a long letter to Lucie, showing her that he had known nothing of her father’s imprisonment, until he had heard of it from herself, and that he had been as ignorant as she of his father’s and uncle’s responsibility for that misery, until the paper had been read. He had already explained to her that his concealment from herself of the name he had relinquished, was the one condition--fully intelligible now--that her father had attached to their betrothal, and was the one promise he had still exacted on the morning of their marriage. He entreated her, for her father’s sake, never to seek to know whether her father had become oblivious of the existence of the paper, or had had it recalled to him (for the moment, or for good), by the story of the Tower, on that old Sunday under the dear old plane-tree in the garden. If he had preserved any definite remembrance of it, there could be no doubt that he had supposed it destroyed with the Bastille, when he had found no mention of it among the relics of prisoners which the populace had discovered there, and which had been described to all the world. He besought her--though he added that he knew it was needless--to console her father, by impressing him through every tender means she could think of, with the truth that he had done nothing for which he could justly reproach himself, but had uniformly forgotten himself for their joint sakes. Next to her preservation of his own last grateful love and blessing, and her overcoming of her sorrow, to devote herself to their dear child, he adjured her, as they would meet in Heaven, to comfort her father.\n\nTo her father himself, he wrote in the same strain; but, he told her father that he expressly confided his wife and child to his care. And he told him this, very strongly, with the hope of rousing him from any despondency or dangerous retrospect towards which he foresaw he might be tending.\n\nTo Mr. Lorry, he commended them all, and explained his worldly affairs. That done, with many added sentences of grateful friendship and warm attachment, all was done. He never thought of Carton. His mind was so full of the others, that he never once thought of him.\n\nHe had time to finish these letters before the lights were put out. When he lay down on his straw bed, he thought he had done with this world.\n\nBut, it beckoned him back in his sleep, and showed itself in shining forms. Free and happy, back in the old house in Soho (though it had nothing in it like the real house), unaccountably released and light of heart, he was with Lucie again, and she told him it was all a dream, and he had never gone away. A pause of forgetfulness, and then he had even suffered, and had come back to her, dead and at peace, and yet there was no difference in him. Another pause of oblivion, and he awoke in the sombre morning, unconscious where he was or what had happened, until it flashed upon his mind, “this is the day of my death!”\n\nThus, had he come through the hours, to the day when the fifty-two heads were to fall. And now, while he was composed, and hoped that he could meet the end with quiet heroism, a new action began in his waking thoughts, which was very difficult to master.\n\nHe had never seen the instrument that was to terminate his life. How high it was from the ground, how many steps it had, where he would be stood, how he would be touched, whether the touching hands would be dyed red, which way his face would be turned, whether he would be the first, or might be the last: these and many similar questions, in nowise directed by his will, obtruded themselves over and over again, countless times. Neither were they connected with fear: he was conscious of no fear. Rather, they originated in a strange besetting desire to know what to do when the time came; a desire gigantically disproportionate to the few swift moments to which it referred; a wondering that was more like the wondering of some other spirit within his, than his own.\n\nThe hours went on as he walked to and fro, and the clocks struck the numbers he would never hear again. Nine gone for ever, ten gone for ever, eleven gone for ever, twelve coming on to pass away. After a hard contest with that eccentric action of thought which had last perplexed him, he had got the better of it. He walked up and down, softly repeating their names to himself. The worst of the strife was over. He could walk up and down, free from distracting fancies, praying for himself and for them.\n\nTwelve gone for ever.\n\nHe had been apprised that the final hour was Three, and he knew he would be summoned some time earlier, inasmuch as the tumbrils jolted heavily and slowly through the streets. Therefore, he resolved to keep Two before his mind, as the hour, and so to strengthen himself in the interval that he might be able, after that time, to strengthen others.\n\nWalking regularly to and fro with his arms folded on his breast, a very different man from the prisoner, who had walked to and fro at La Force, he heard One struck away from him, without surprise. The hour had measured like most other hours. Devoutly thankful to Heaven for his recovered self-possession, he thought, “There is but another now,” and turned to walk again.\n\nFootsteps in the stone passage outside the door. He stopped.\n\nThe key was put in the lock, and turned. Before the door was opened, or as it opened, a man said in a low voice, in English: “He has never seen me here; I have kept out of his way. Go you in alone; I wait near. Lose no time!”\n\nThe door was quickly opened and closed, and there stood before him face to face, quiet, intent upon him, with the light of a smile on his features, and a cautionary finger on his lip, Sydney Carton.\n\nThere was something so bright and remarkable in his look, that, for the first moment, the prisoner misdoubted him to be an apparition of his own imagining. But, he spoke, and it was his voice; he took the prisoner’s hand, and it was his real grasp.\n\n“Of all the people upon earth, you least expected to see me?” he said.\n\n“I could not believe it to be you. I can scarcely believe it now. You are not”--the apprehension came suddenly into his mind--“a prisoner?”\n\n“No. I am accidentally possessed of a power over one of the keepers here, and in virtue of it I stand before you. I come from her--your wife, dear Darnay.”\n\nThe prisoner wrung his hand.\n\n“I bring you a request from her.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“A most earnest, pressing, and emphatic entreaty, addressed to you in the most pathetic tones of the voice so dear to you, that you well remember.”\n\nThe prisoner turned his face partly aside.\n\n“You have no time to ask me why I bring it, or what it means; I have no time to tell you. You must comply with it--take off those boots you wear, and draw on these of mine.”\n\nThere was a chair against the wall of the cell, behind the prisoner. Carton, pressing forward, had already, with the speed of lightning, got him down into it, and stood over him, barefoot.\n\n“Draw on these boots of mine. Put your hands to them; put your will to them. Quick!”\n\n“Carton, there is no escaping from this place; it never can be done. You will only die with me. It is madness.”\n\n“It would be madness if I asked you to escape; but do I? When I ask you to pass out at that door, tell me it is madness and remain here. Change that cravat for this of mine, that coat for this of mine. While you do it, let me take this ribbon from your hair, and shake out your hair like this of mine!”\n\nWith wonderful quickness, and with a strength both of will and action, that appeared quite supernatural, he forced all these changes upon him. The prisoner was like a young child in his hands.\n\n“Carton! Dear Carton! It is madness. It cannot be accomplished, it never can be done, it has been attempted, and has always failed. I implore you not to add your death to the bitterness of mine.”\n\n“Do I ask you, my dear Darnay, to pass the door? When I ask that, refuse. There are pen and ink and paper on this table. Is your hand steady enough to write?”\n\n“It was when you came in.”\n\n“Steady it again, and write what I shall dictate. Quick, friend, quick!”\n\nPressing his hand to his bewildered head, Darnay sat down at the table. Carton, with his right hand in his breast, stood close beside him.\n\n“Write exactly as I speak.”\n\n“To whom do I address it?”\n\n“To no one.” Carton still had his hand in his breast.\n\n“Do I date it?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe prisoner looked up, at each question. Carton, standing over him with his hand in his breast, looked down.\n\n“‘If you remember,’” said Carton, dictating, “‘the words that passed between us, long ago, you will readily comprehend this when you see it. You do remember them, I know. It is not in your nature to forget them.’”\n\nHe was drawing his hand from his breast; the prisoner chancing to look up in his hurried wonder as he wrote, the hand stopped, closing upon something.\n\n“Have you written ‘forget them’?” Carton asked.\n\n“I have. Is that a weapon in your hand?”\n\n“No; I am not armed.”\n\n“What is it in your hand?”\n\n“You shall know directly. Write on; there are but a few words more.” He dictated again. “‘I am thankful that the time has come, when I can prove them. That I do so is no subject for regret or grief.’” As he said these words with his eyes fixed on the writer, his hand slowly and softly moved down close to the writer’s face.\n\nThe pen dropped from Darnay’s fingers on the table, and he looked about him vacantly.\n\n“What vapour is that?” he asked.\n\n“Vapour?”\n\n“Something that crossed me?”\n\n“I am conscious of nothing; there can be nothing here. Take up the pen and finish. Hurry, hurry!”\n\nAs if his memory were impaired, or his faculties disordered, the prisoner made an effort to rally his attention. As he looked at Carton with clouded eyes and with an altered manner of breathing, Carton--his hand again in his breast--looked steadily at him.\n\n“Hurry, hurry!”\n\nThe prisoner bent over the paper, once more.\n\n“‘If it had been otherwise;’” Carton’s hand was again watchfully and softly stealing down; “‘I never should have used the longer opportunity. If it had been otherwise;’” the hand was at the prisoner’s face; “‘I should but have had so much the more to answer for. If it had been otherwise--’” Carton looked at the pen and saw it was trailing off into unintelligible signs.\n\nCarton’s hand moved back to his breast no more. The prisoner sprang up with a reproachful look, but Carton’s hand was close and firm at his nostrils, and Carton’s left arm caught him round the waist. For a few seconds he faintly struggled with the man who had come to lay down his life for him; but, within a minute or so, he was stretched insensible on the ground.\n\nQuickly, but with hands as true to the purpose as his heart was, Carton dressed himself in the clothes the prisoner had laid aside, combed back his hair, and tied it with the ribbon the prisoner had worn. Then, he softly called, “Enter there! Come in!” and the Spy presented himself.\n\n“You see?” said Carton, looking up, as he kneeled on one knee beside the insensible figure, putting the paper in the breast: “is your hazard very great?”\n\n“Mr. Carton,” the Spy answered, with a timid snap of his fingers, “my hazard is not that, in the thick of business here, if you are true to the whole of your bargain.”\n\n“Don’t fear me. I will be true to the death.”\n\n“You must be, Mr. Carton, if the tale of fifty-two is to be right. Being made right by you in that dress, I shall have no fear.”\n\n“Have no fear! I shall soon be out of the way of harming you, and the rest will soon be far from here, please God! Now, get assistance and take me to the coach.”\n\n“You?” said the Spy nervously.\n\n“Him, man, with whom I have exchanged. You go out at the gate by which you brought me in?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“I was weak and faint when you brought me in, and I am fainter now you take me out. The parting interview has overpowered me. Such a thing has happened here, often, and too often. Your life is in your own hands. Quick! Call assistance!”\n\n“You swear not to betray me?” said the trembling Spy, as he paused for a last moment.\n\n“Man, man!” returned Carton, stamping his foot; “have I sworn by no solemn vow already, to go through with this, that you waste the precious moments now? Take him yourself to the courtyard you know of, place him yourself in the carriage, show him yourself to Mr. Lorry, tell him yourself to give him no restorative but air, and to remember my words of last night, and his promise of last night, and drive away!”\n\nThe Spy withdrew, and Carton seated himself at the table, resting his forehead on his hands. The Spy returned immediately, with two men.\n\n“How, then?” said one of them, contemplating the fallen figure. “So afflicted to find that his friend has drawn a prize in the lottery of Sainte Guillotine?”\n\n“A good patriot,” said the other, “could hardly have been more afflicted if the Aristocrat had drawn a blank.”\n\nThey raised the unconscious figure, placed it on a litter they had brought to the door, and bent to carry it away.\n\n“The time is short, Evrémonde,” said the Spy, in a warning voice.\n\n“I know it well,” answered Carton. “Be careful of my friend, I entreat you, and leave me.”\n\n“Come, then, my children,” said Barsad. “Lift him, and come away!”\n\nThe door closed, and Carton was left alone. Straining his powers of listening to the utmost, he listened for any sound that might denote suspicion or alarm. There was none. Keys turned, doors clashed, footsteps passed along distant passages: no cry was raised, or hurry made, that seemed unusual. Breathing more freely in a little while, he sat down at the table, and listened again until the clock struck Two.\n\nSounds that he was not afraid of, for he divined their meaning, then began to be audible. Several doors were opened in succession, and finally his own. A gaoler, with a list in his hand, looked in, merely saying, “Follow me, Evrémonde!” and he followed into a large dark room, at a distance. It was a dark winter day, and what with the shadows within, and what with the shadows without, he could but dimly discern the others who were brought there to have their arms bound. Some were standing; some seated. Some were lamenting, and in restless motion; but, these were few. The great majority were silent and still, looking fixedly at the ground.\n\nAs he stood by the wall in a dim corner, while some of the fifty-two were brought in after him, one man stopped in passing, to embrace him, as having a knowledge of him. It thrilled him with a great dread of discovery; but the man went on. A very few moments after that, a young woman, with a slight girlish form, a sweet spare face in which there was no vestige of colour, and large widely opened patient eyes, rose from the seat where he had observed her sitting, and came to speak to him.\n\n“Citizen Evrémonde,” she said, touching him with her cold hand. “I am a poor little seamstress, who was with you in La Force.”\n\nHe murmured for answer: “True. I forget what you were accused of?”\n\n“Plots. Though the just Heaven knows that I am innocent of any. Is it likely? Who would think of plotting with a poor little weak creature like me?”\n\nThe forlorn smile with which she said it, so touched him, that tears started from his eyes.\n\n“I am not afraid to die, Citizen Evrémonde, but I have done nothing. I am not unwilling to die, if the Republic which is to do so much good to us poor, will profit by my death; but I do not know how that can be, Citizen Evrémonde. Such a poor weak little creature!”\n\nAs the last thing on earth that his heart was to warm and soften to, it warmed and softened to this pitiable girl.\n\n“I heard you were released, Citizen Evrémonde. I hoped it was true?”\n\n“It was. But, I was again taken and condemned.”\n\n“If I may ride with you, Citizen Evrémonde, will you let me hold your hand? I am not afraid, but I am little and weak, and it will give me more courage.”\n\nAs the patient eyes were lifted to his face, he saw a sudden doubt in them, and then astonishment. He pressed the work-worn, hunger-worn young fingers, and touched his lips.\n\n“Are you dying for him?” she whispered.\n\n“And his wife and child. Hush! Yes.”\n\n“O you will let me hold your brave hand, stranger?”\n\n“Hush! Yes, my poor sister; to the last.”\n\nThe same shadows that are falling on the prison, are falling, in that same hour of the early afternoon, on the Barrier with the crowd about it, when a coach going out of Paris drives up to be examined.\n\n“Who goes here? Whom have we within? Papers!”\n\nThe papers are handed out, and read.\n\n“Alexandre Manette. Physician. French. Which is he?”\n\nThis is he; this helpless, inarticulately murmuring, wandering old man pointed out.\n\n“Apparently the Citizen-Doctor is not in his right mind? The Revolution-fever will have been too much for him?”\n\nGreatly too much for him.\n\n“Hah! Many suffer with it. Lucie. His daughter. French. Which is she?”\n\nThis is she.\n\n“Apparently it must be. Lucie, the wife of Evrémonde; is it not?”\n\nIt is.\n\n“Hah! Evrémonde has an assignation elsewhere. Lucie, her child. English. This is she?”\n\nShe and no other.\n\n“Kiss me, child of Evrémonde. Now, thou hast kissed a good Republican; something new in thy family; remember it! Sydney Carton. Advocate. English. Which is he?”\n\nHe lies here, in this corner of the carriage. He, too, is pointed out.\n\n“Apparently the English advocate is in a swoon?”\n\nIt is hoped he will recover in the fresher air. It is represented that he is not in strong health, and has separated sadly from a friend who is under the displeasure of the Republic.\n\n“Is that all? It is not a great deal, that! Many are under the displeasure of the Republic, and must look out at the little window. Jarvis Lorry. Banker. English. Which is he?”\n\n“I am he. Necessarily, being the last.”\n\nIt is Jarvis Lorry who has replied to all the previous questions. It is Jarvis Lorry who has alighted and stands with his hand on the coach door, replying to a group of officials. They leisurely walk round the carriage and leisurely mount the box, to look at what little luggage it carries on the roof; the country-people hanging about, press nearer to the coach doors and greedily stare in; a little child, carried by its mother, has its short arm held out for it, that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.\n\n“Behold your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.”\n\n“One can depart, citizen?”\n\n“One can depart. Forward, my postilions! A good journey!”\n\n“I salute you, citizens.--And the first danger passed!”\n\nThese are again the words of Jarvis Lorry, as he clasps his hands, and looks upward. There is terror in the carriage, there is weeping, there is the heavy breathing of the insensible traveller.\n\n“Are we not going too slowly? Can they not be induced to go faster?” asks Lucie, clinging to the old man.\n\n“It would seem like flight, my darling. I must not urge them too much; it would rouse suspicion.”\n\n“Look back, look back, and see if we are pursued!”\n\n“The road is clear, my dearest. So far, we are not pursued.”\n\nHouses in twos and threes pass by us, solitary farms, ruinous buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, open country, avenues of leafless trees. The hard uneven pavement is under us, the soft deep mud is on either side. Sometimes, we strike into the skirting mud, to avoid the stones that clatter us and shake us; sometimes, we stick in ruts and sloughs there. The agony of our impatience is then so great, that in our wild alarm and hurry we are for getting out and running--hiding--doing anything but stopping.\n\nOut of the open country, in again among ruinous buildings, solitary farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in twos and threes, avenues of leafless trees. Have these men deceived us, and taken us back by another road? Is not this the same place twice over? Thank Heaven, no. A village. Look back, look back, and see if we are pursued! Hush! the posting-house.\n\nLeisurely, our four horses are taken out; leisurely, the coach stands in the little street, bereft of horses, and with no likelihood upon it of ever moving again; leisurely, the new horses come into visible existence, one by one; leisurely, the new postilions follow, sucking and plaiting the lashes of their whips; leisurely, the old postilions count their money, make wrong additions, and arrive at dissatisfied results. All the time, our overfraught hearts are beating at a rate that would far outstrip the fastest gallop of the fastest horses ever foaled.\n\nAt length the new postilions are in their saddles, and the old are left behind. We are through the village, up the hill, and down the hill, and on the low watery grounds. Suddenly, the postilions exchange speech with animated gesticulation, and the horses are pulled up, almost on their haunches. We are pursued?\n\n“Ho! Within the carriage there. Speak then!”\n\n“What is it?” asks Mr. Lorry, looking out at window.\n\n“How many did they say?”\n\n“I do not understand you.”\n\n“--At the last post. How many to the Guillotine to-day?”\n\n“Fifty-two.”\n\n“I said so! A brave number! My fellow-citizen here would have it forty-two; ten more heads are worth having. The Guillotine goes handsomely. I love it. Hi forward. Whoop!”\n\nThe night comes on dark. He moves more; he is beginning to revive, and to speak intelligibly; he thinks they are still together; he asks him, by his name, what he has in his hand. O pity us, kind Heaven, and help us! Look out, look out, and see if we are pursued.\n\nThe wind is rushing after us, and the clouds are flying after us, and the moon is plunging after us, and the whole wild night is in pursuit of us; but, so far, we are pursued by nothing else.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. The Knitting Done\n\nIn that same juncture of time when the Fifty-Two awaited their fate Madame Defarge held darkly ominous council with The Vengeance and Jacques Three of the Revolutionary Jury. Not in the wine-shop did Madame Defarge confer with these ministers, but in the shed of the wood-sawyer, erst a mender of roads. The sawyer himself did not participate in the conference, but abided at a little distance, like an outer satellite who was not to speak until required, or to offer an opinion until invited.\n\n“But our Defarge,” said Jacques Three, “is undoubtedly a good Republican? Eh?”\n\n“There is no better,” the voluble Vengeance protested in her shrill notes, “in France.”\n\n“Peace, little Vengeance,” said Madame Defarge, laying her hand with a slight frown on her lieutenant’s lips, “hear me speak. My husband, fellow-citizen, is a good Republican and a bold man; he has deserved well of the Republic, and possesses its confidence. But my husband has his weaknesses, and he is so weak as to relent towards this Doctor.”\n\n“It is a great pity,” croaked Jacques Three, dubiously shaking his head, with his cruel fingers at his hungry mouth; “it is not quite like a good citizen; it is a thing to regret.”\n\n“See you,” said madame, “I care nothing for this Doctor, I. He may wear his head or lose it, for any interest I have in him; it is all one to me. But, the Evrémonde people are to be exterminated, and the wife and child must follow the husband and father.”\n\n“She has a fine head for it,” croaked Jacques Three. “I have seen blue eyes and golden hair there, and they looked charming when Samson held them up.” Ogre that he was, he spoke like an epicure.\n\nMadame Defarge cast down her eyes, and reflected a little.\n\n“The child also,” observed Jacques Three, with a meditative enjoyment of his words, “has golden hair and blue eyes. And we seldom have a child there. It is a pretty sight!”\n\n“In a word,” said Madame Defarge, coming out of her short abstraction, “I cannot trust my husband in this matter. Not only do I feel, since last night, that I dare not confide to him the details of my projects; but also I feel that if I delay, there is danger of his giving warning, and then they might escape.”\n\n“That must never be,” croaked Jacques Three; “no one must escape. We have not half enough as it is. We ought to have six score a day.”\n\n“In a word,” Madame Defarge went on, “my husband has not my reason for pursuing this family to annihilation, and I have not his reason for regarding this Doctor with any sensibility. I must act for myself, therefore. Come hither, little citizen.”\n\nThe wood-sawyer, who held her in the respect, and himself in the submission, of mortal fear, advanced with his hand to his red cap.\n\n“Touching those signals, little citizen,” said Madame Defarge, sternly, “that she made to the prisoners; you are ready to bear witness to them this very day?”\n\n“Ay, ay, why not!” cried the sawyer. “Every day, in all weathers, from two to four, always signalling, sometimes with the little one, sometimes without. I know what I know. I have seen with my eyes.”\n\nHe made all manner of gestures while he spoke, as if in incidental imitation of some few of the great diversity of signals that he had never seen.\n\n“Clearly plots,” said Jacques Three. “Transparently!”\n\n“There is no doubt of the Jury?” inquired Madame Defarge, letting her eyes turn to him with a gloomy smile.\n\n“Rely upon the patriotic Jury, dear citizeness. I answer for my fellow-Jurymen.”\n\n“Now, let me see,” said Madame Defarge, pondering again. “Yet once more! Can I spare this Doctor to my husband? I have no feeling either way. Can I spare him?”\n\n“He would count as one head,” observed Jacques Three, in a low voice. “We really have not heads enough; it would be a pity, I think.”\n\n“He was signalling with her when I saw her,” argued Madame Defarge; “I cannot speak of one without the other; and I must not be silent, and trust the case wholly to him, this little citizen here. For, I am not a bad witness.”\n\nThe Vengeance and Jacques Three vied with each other in their fervent protestations that she was the most admirable and marvellous of witnesses. The little citizen, not to be outdone, declared her to be a celestial witness.\n\n“He must take his chance,” said Madame Defarge. “No, I cannot spare him! You are engaged at three o’clock; you are going to see the batch of to-day executed.--You?”\n\nThe question was addressed to the wood-sawyer, who hurriedly replied in the affirmative: seizing the occasion to add that he was the most ardent of Republicans, and that he would be in effect the most desolate of Republicans, if anything prevented him from enjoying the pleasure of smoking his afternoon pipe in the contemplation of the droll national barber. He was so very demonstrative herein, that he might have been suspected (perhaps was, by the dark eyes that looked contemptuously at him out of Madame Defarge’s head) of having his small individual fears for his own personal safety, every hour in the day.\n\n“I,” said madame, “am equally engaged at the same place. After it is over--say at eight to-night--come you to me, in Saint Antoine, and we will give information against these people at my Section.”\n\nThe wood-sawyer said he would be proud and flattered to attend the citizeness. The citizeness looking at him, he became embarrassed, evaded her glance as a small dog would have done, retreated among his wood, and hid his confusion over the handle of his saw.\n\nMadame Defarge beckoned the Juryman and The Vengeance a little nearer to the door, and there expounded her further views to them thus:\n\n“She will now be at home, awaiting the moment of his death. She will be mourning and grieving. She will be in a state of mind to impeach the justice of the Republic. She will be full of sympathy with its enemies. I will go to her.”\n\n“What an admirable woman; what an adorable woman!” exclaimed Jacques Three, rapturously. “Ah, my cherished!” cried The Vengeance; and embraced her.\n\n“Take you my knitting,” said Madame Defarge, placing it in her lieutenant’s hands, “and have it ready for me in my usual seat. Keep me my usual chair. Go you there, straight, for there will probably be a greater concourse than usual, to-day.”\n\n“I willingly obey the orders of my Chief,” said The Vengeance with alacrity, and kissing her cheek. “You will not be late?”\n\n“I shall be there before the commencement.”\n\n“And before the tumbrils arrive. Be sure you are there, my soul,” said The Vengeance, calling after her, for she had already turned into the street, “before the tumbrils arrive!”\n\nMadame Defarge slightly waved her hand, to imply that she heard, and might be relied upon to arrive in good time, and so went through the mud, and round the corner of the prison wall. The Vengeance and the Juryman, looking after her as she walked away, were highly appreciative of her fine figure, and her superb moral endowments.\n\nThere were many women at that time, upon whom the time laid a dreadfully disfiguring hand; but, there was not one among them more to be dreaded than this ruthless woman, now taking her way along the streets. Of a strong and fearless character, of shrewd sense and readiness, of great determination, of that kind of beauty which not only seems to impart to its possessor firmness and animosity, but to strike into others an instinctive recognition of those qualities; the troubled time would have heaved her up, under any circumstances. But, imbued from her childhood with a brooding sense of wrong, and an inveterate hatred of a class, opportunity had developed her into a tigress. She was absolutely without pity. If she had ever had the virtue in her, it had quite gone out of her.\n\nIt was nothing to her, that an innocent man was to die for the sins of his forefathers; she saw, not him, but them. It was nothing to her, that his wife was to be made a widow and his daughter an orphan; that was insufficient punishment, because they were her natural enemies and her prey, and as such had no right to live. To appeal to her, was made hopeless by her having no sense of pity, even for herself. If she had been laid low in the streets, in any of the many encounters in which she had been engaged, she would not have pitied herself; nor, if she had been ordered to the axe to-morrow, would she have gone to it with any softer feeling than a fierce desire to change places with the man who sent her there.\n\nSuch a heart Madame Defarge carried under her rough robe. Carelessly worn, it was a becoming robe enough, in a certain weird way, and her dark hair looked rich under her coarse red cap. Lying hidden in her bosom, was a loaded pistol. Lying hidden at her waist, was a sharpened dagger. Thus accoutred, and walking with the confident tread of such a character, and with the supple freedom of a woman who had habitually walked in her girlhood, bare-foot and bare-legged, on the brown sea-sand, Madame Defarge took her way along the streets.\n\nNow, when the journey of the travelling coach, at that very moment waiting for the completion of its load, had been planned out last night, the difficulty of taking Miss Pross in it had much engaged Mr. Lorry’s attention. It was not merely desirable to avoid overloading the coach, but it was of the highest importance that the time occupied in examining it and its passengers, should be reduced to the utmost; since their escape might depend on the saving of only a few seconds here and there. Finally, he had proposed, after anxious consideration, that Miss Pross and Jerry, who were at liberty to leave the city, should leave it at three o’clock in the lightest-wheeled conveyance known to that period. Unencumbered with luggage, they would soon overtake the coach, and, passing it and preceding it on the road, would order its horses in advance, and greatly facilitate its progress during the precious hours of the night, when delay was the most to be dreaded.\n\nSeeing in this arrangement the hope of rendering real service in that pressing emergency, Miss Pross hailed it with joy. She and Jerry had beheld the coach start, had known who it was that Solomon brought, had passed some ten minutes in tortures of suspense, and were now concluding their arrangements to follow the coach, even as Madame Defarge, taking her way through the streets, now drew nearer and nearer to the else-deserted lodging in which they held their consultation.\n\n“Now what do you think, Mr. Cruncher,” said Miss Pross, whose agitation was so great that she could hardly speak, or stand, or move, or live: “what do you think of our not starting from this courtyard? Another carriage having already gone from here to-day, it might awaken suspicion.”\n\n“My opinion, miss,” returned Mr. Cruncher, “is as you’re right. Likewise wot I’ll stand by you, right or wrong.”\n\n“I am so distracted with fear and hope for our precious creatures,” said Miss Pross, wildly crying, “that I am incapable of forming any plan. Are you capable of forming any plan, my dear good Mr. Cruncher?”\n\n“Respectin’ a future spear o’ life, miss,” returned Mr. Cruncher, “I hope so. Respectin’ any present use o’ this here blessed old head o’ mine, I think not. Would you do me the favour, miss, to take notice o’ two promises and wows wot it is my wishes fur to record in this here crisis?”\n\n“Oh, for gracious sake!” cried Miss Pross, still wildly crying, “record them at once, and get them out of the way, like an excellent man.”\n\n“First,” said Mr. Cruncher, who was all in a tremble, and who spoke with an ashy and solemn visage, “them poor things well out o’ this, never no more will I do it, never no more!”\n\n“I am quite sure, Mr. Cruncher,” returned Miss Pross, “that you never will do it again, whatever it is, and I beg you not to think it necessary to mention more particularly what it is.”\n\n“No, miss,” returned Jerry, “it shall not be named to you. Second: them poor things well out o’ this, and never no more will I interfere with Mrs. Cruncher’s flopping, never no more!”\n\n“Whatever housekeeping arrangement that may be,” said Miss Pross, striving to dry her eyes and compose herself, “I have no doubt it is best that Mrs. Cruncher should have it entirely under her own superintendence.--O my poor darlings!”\n\n“I go so far as to say, miss, moreover,” proceeded Mr. Cruncher, with a most alarming tendency to hold forth as from a pulpit--“and let my words be took down and took to Mrs. Cruncher through yourself--that wot my opinions respectin’ flopping has undergone a change, and that wot I only hope with all my heart as Mrs. Cruncher may be a flopping at the present time.”\n\n“There, there, there! I hope she is, my dear man,” cried the distracted Miss Pross, “and I hope she finds it answering her expectations.”\n\n“Forbid it,” proceeded Mr. Cruncher, with additional solemnity, additional slowness, and additional tendency to hold forth and hold out, “as anything wot I have ever said or done should be wisited on my earnest wishes for them poor creeturs now! Forbid it as we shouldn’t all flop (if it was anyways conwenient) to get ’em out o’ this here dismal risk! Forbid it, miss! Wot I say, for-bid it!” This was Mr. Cruncher’s conclusion after a protracted but vain endeavour to find a better one.\n\nAnd still Madame Defarge, pursuing her way along the streets, came nearer and nearer.\n\n“If we ever get back to our native land,” said Miss Pross, “you may rely upon my telling Mrs. Cruncher as much as I may be able to remember and understand of what you have so impressively said; and at all events you may be sure that I shall bear witness to your being thoroughly in earnest at this dreadful time. Now, pray let us think! My esteemed Mr. Cruncher, let us think!”\n\nStill, Madame Defarge, pursuing her way along the streets, came nearer and nearer.\n\n“If you were to go before,” said Miss Pross, “and stop the vehicle and horses from coming here, and were to wait somewhere for me; wouldn’t that be best?”\n\nMr. Cruncher thought it might be best.\n\n“Where could you wait for me?” asked Miss Pross.\n\nMr. Cruncher was so bewildered that he could think of no locality but Temple Bar. Alas! Temple Bar was hundreds of miles away, and Madame Defarge was drawing very near indeed.\n\n“By the cathedral door,” said Miss Pross. “Would it be much out of the way, to take me in, near the great cathedral door between the two towers?”\n\n“No, miss,” answered Mr. Cruncher.\n\n“Then, like the best of men,” said Miss Pross, “go to the posting-house straight, and make that change.”\n\n“I am doubtful,” said Mr. Cruncher, hesitating and shaking his head, “about leaving of you, you see. We don’t know what may happen.”\n\n“Heaven knows we don’t,” returned Miss Pross, “but have no fear for me. Take me in at the cathedral, at Three o’Clock, or as near it as you can, and I am sure it will be better than our going from here. I feel certain of it. There! Bless you, Mr. Cruncher! Think-not of me, but of the lives that may depend on both of us!”\n\nThis exordium, and Miss Pross’s two hands in quite agonised entreaty clasping his, decided Mr. Cruncher. With an encouraging nod or two, he immediately went out to alter the arrangements, and left her by herself to follow as she had proposed.\n\nThe having originated a precaution which was already in course of execution, was a great relief to Miss Pross. The necessity of composing her appearance so that it should attract no special notice in the streets, was another relief. She looked at her watch, and it was twenty minutes past two. She had no time to lose, but must get ready at once.\n\nAfraid, in her extreme perturbation, of the loneliness of the deserted rooms, and of half-imagined faces peeping from behind every open door in them, Miss Pross got a basin of cold water and began laving her eyes, which were swollen and red. Haunted by her feverish apprehensions, she could not bear to have her sight obscured for a minute at a time by the dripping water, but constantly paused and looked round to see that there was no one watching her. In one of those pauses she recoiled and cried out, for she saw a figure standing in the room.\n\nThe basin fell to the ground broken, and the water flowed to the feet of Madame Defarge. By strange stern ways, and through much staining blood, those feet had come to meet that water.\n\nMadame Defarge looked coldly at her, and said, “The wife of Evrémonde; where is she?”\n\nIt flashed upon Miss Pross’s mind that the doors were all standing open, and would suggest the flight. Her first act was to shut them. There were four in the room, and she shut them all. She then placed herself before the door of the chamber which Lucie had occupied.\n\nMadame Defarge’s dark eyes followed her through this rapid movement, and rested on her when it was finished. Miss Pross had nothing beautiful about her; years had not tamed the wildness, or softened the grimness, of her appearance; but, she too was a determined woman in her different way, and she measured Madame Defarge with her eyes, every inch.\n\n“You might, from your appearance, be the wife of Lucifer,” said Miss Pross, in her breathing. “Nevertheless, you shall not get the better of me. I am an Englishwoman.”\n\nMadame Defarge looked at her scornfully, but still with something of Miss Pross’s own perception that they two were at bay. She saw a tight, hard, wiry woman before her, as Mr. Lorry had seen in the same figure a woman with a strong hand, in the years gone by. She knew full well that Miss Pross was the family’s devoted friend; Miss Pross knew full well that Madame Defarge was the family’s malevolent enemy.\n\n“On my way yonder,” said Madame Defarge, with a slight movement of her hand towards the fatal spot, “where they reserve my chair and my knitting for me, I am come to make my compliments to her in passing. I wish to see her.”\n\n“I know that your intentions are evil,” said Miss Pross, “and you may depend upon it, I’ll hold my own against them.”\n\nEach spoke in her own language; neither understood the other’s words; both were very watchful, and intent to deduce from look and manner, what the unintelligible words meant.\n\n“It will do her no good to keep herself concealed from me at this moment,” said Madame Defarge. “Good patriots will know what that means. Let me see her. Go tell her that I wish to see her. Do you hear?”\n\n“If those eyes of yours were bed-winches,” returned Miss Pross, “and I was an English four-poster, they shouldn’t loose a splinter of me. No, you wicked foreign woman; I am your match.”\n\nMadame Defarge was not likely to follow these idiomatic remarks in detail; but, she so far understood them as to perceive that she was set at naught.\n\n“Woman imbecile and pig-like!” said Madame Defarge, frowning. “I take no answer from you. I demand to see her. Either tell her that I demand to see her, or stand out of the way of the door and let me go to her!” This, with an angry explanatory wave of her right arm.\n\n“I little thought,” said Miss Pross, “that I should ever want to understand your nonsensical language; but I would give all I have, except the clothes I wear, to know whether you suspect the truth, or any part of it.”\n\nNeither of them for a single moment released the other’s eyes. Madame Defarge had not moved from the spot where she stood when Miss Pross first became aware of her; but, she now advanced one step.\n\n“I am a Briton,” said Miss Pross, “I am desperate. I don’t care an English Twopence for myself. I know that the longer I keep you here, the greater hope there is for my Ladybird. I’ll not leave a handful of that dark hair upon your head, if you lay a finger on me!”\n\nThus Miss Pross, with a shake of her head and a flash of her eyes between every rapid sentence, and every rapid sentence a whole breath. Thus Miss Pross, who had never struck a blow in her life.\n\nBut, her courage was of that emotional nature that it brought the irrepressible tears into her eyes. This was a courage that Madame Defarge so little comprehended as to mistake for weakness. “Ha, ha!” she laughed, “you poor wretch! What are you worth! I address myself to that Doctor.” Then she raised her voice and called out, “Citizen Doctor! Wife of Evrémonde! Child of Evrémonde! Any person but this miserable fool, answer the Citizeness Defarge!”\n\nPerhaps the following silence, perhaps some latent disclosure in the expression of Miss Pross’s face, perhaps a sudden misgiving apart from either suggestion, whispered to Madame Defarge that they were gone. Three of the doors she opened swiftly, and looked in.\n\n“Those rooms are all in disorder, there has been hurried packing, there are odds and ends upon the ground. There is no one in that room behind you! Let me look.”\n\n“Never!” said Miss Pross, who understood the request as perfectly as Madame Defarge understood the answer.\n\n“If they are not in that room, they are gone, and can be pursued and brought back,” said Madame Defarge to herself.\n\n“As long as you don’t know whether they are in that room or not, you are uncertain what to do,” said Miss Pross to herself; “and you shall not know that, if I can prevent your knowing it; and know that, or not know that, you shall not leave here while I can hold you.”\n\n“I have been in the streets from the first, nothing has stopped me, I will tear you to pieces, but I will have you from that door,” said Madame Defarge.\n\n“We are alone at the top of a high house in a solitary courtyard, we are not likely to be heard, and I pray for bodily strength to keep you here, while every minute you are here is worth a hundred thousand guineas to my darling,” said Miss Pross.\n\nMadame Defarge made at the door. Miss Pross, on the instinct of the moment, seized her round the waist in both her arms, and held her tight. It was in vain for Madame Defarge to struggle and to strike; Miss Pross, with the vigorous tenacity of love, always so much stronger than hate, clasped her tight, and even lifted her from the floor in the struggle that they had. The two hands of Madame Defarge buffeted and tore her face; but, Miss Pross, with her head down, held her round the waist, and clung to her with more than the hold of a drowning woman.\n\nSoon, Madame Defarge’s hands ceased to strike, and felt at her encircled waist. “It is under my arm,” said Miss Pross, in smothered tones, “you shall not draw it. I am stronger than you, I bless Heaven for it. I hold you till one or other of us faints or dies!”\n\nMadame Defarge’s hands were at her bosom. Miss Pross looked up, saw what it was, struck at it, struck out a flash and a crash, and stood alone--blinded with smoke.\n\nAll this was in a second. As the smoke cleared, leaving an awful stillness, it passed out on the air, like the soul of the furious woman whose body lay lifeless on the ground.\n\nIn the first fright and horror of her situation, Miss Pross passed the body as far from it as she could, and ran down the stairs to call for fruitless help. Happily, she bethought herself of the consequences of what she did, in time to check herself and go back. It was dreadful to go in at the door again; but, she did go in, and even went near it, to get the bonnet and other things that she must wear. These she put on, out on the staircase, first shutting and locking the door and taking away the key. She then sat down on the stairs a few moments to breathe and to cry, and then got up and hurried away.\n\nBy good fortune she had a veil on her bonnet, or she could hardly have gone along the streets without being stopped. By good fortune, too, she was naturally so peculiar in appearance as not to show disfigurement like any other woman. She needed both advantages, for the marks of gripping fingers were deep in her face, and her hair was torn, and her dress (hastily composed with unsteady hands) was clutched and dragged a hundred ways.\n\nIn crossing the bridge, she dropped the door key in the river. Arriving at the cathedral some few minutes before her escort, and waiting there, she thought, what if the key were already taken in a net, what if it were identified, what if the door were opened and the remains discovered, what if she were stopped at the gate, sent to prison, and charged with murder! In the midst of these fluttering thoughts, the escort appeared, took her in, and took her away.\n\n“Is there any noise in the streets?” she asked him.\n\n“The usual noises,” Mr. Cruncher replied; and looked surprised by the question and by her aspect.\n\n“I don’t hear you,” said Miss Pross. “What do you say?”\n\nIt was in vain for Mr. Cruncher to repeat what he said; Miss Pross could not hear him. “So I’ll nod my head,” thought Mr. Cruncher, amazed, “at all events she’ll see that.” And she did.\n\n“Is there any noise in the streets now?” asked Miss Pross again, presently.\n\nAgain Mr. Cruncher nodded his head.\n\n“I don’t hear it.”\n\n“Gone deaf in an hour?” said Mr. Cruncher, ruminating, with his mind much disturbed; “wot’s come to her?”\n\n“I feel,” said Miss Pross, “as if there had been a flash and a crash, and that crash was the last thing I should ever hear in this life.”\n\n“Blest if she ain’t in a queer condition!” said Mr. Cruncher, more and more disturbed. “Wot can she have been a takin’, to keep her courage up? Hark! There’s the roll of them dreadful carts! You can hear that, miss?”\n\n“I can hear,” said Miss Pross, seeing that he spoke to her, “nothing. O, my good man, there was first a great crash, and then a great stillness, and that stillness seems to be fixed and unchangeable, never to be broken any more as long as my life lasts.”\n\n“If she don’t hear the roll of those dreadful carts, now very nigh their journey’s end,” said Mr. Cruncher, glancing over his shoulder, “it’s my opinion that indeed she never will hear anything else in this world.”\n\nAnd indeed she never did.\n\n# CHAPTER XV. The Footsteps Die Out For Ever\n\nAlong the Paris streets, the death-carts rumble, hollow and harsh. Six tumbrils carry the day’s wine to La Guillotine. All the devouring and insatiate Monsters imagined since imagination could record itself, are fused in the one realisation, Guillotine. And yet there is not in France, with its rich variety of soil and climate, a blade, a leaf, a root, a sprig, a peppercorn, which will grow to maturity under conditions more certain than those that have produced this horror. Crush humanity out of shape once more, under similar hammers, and it will twist itself into the same tortured forms. Sow the same seed of rapacious license and oppression over again, and it will surely yield the same fruit according to its kind.\n\nSix tumbrils roll along the streets. Change these back again to what they were, thou powerful enchanter, Time, and they shall be seen to be the carriages of absolute monarchs, the equipages of feudal nobles, the toilettes of flaring Jezebels, the churches that are not my father’s house but dens of thieves, the huts of millions of starving peasants! No; the great magician who majestically works out the appointed order of the Creator, never reverses his transformations. “If thou be changed into this shape by the will of God,” say the seers to the enchanted, in the wise Arabian stories, “then remain so! But, if thou wear this form through mere passing conjuration, then resume thy former aspect!” Changeless and hopeless, the tumbrils roll along.\n\nAs the sombre wheels of the six carts go round, they seem to plough up a long crooked furrow among the populace in the streets. Ridges of faces are thrown to this side and to that, and the ploughs go steadily onward. So used are the regular inhabitants of the houses to the spectacle, that in many windows there are no people, and in some the occupation of the hands is not so much as suspended, while the eyes survey the faces in the tumbrils. Here and there, the inmate has visitors to see the sight; then he points his finger, with something of the complacency of a curator or authorised exponent, to this cart and to this, and seems to tell who sat here yesterday, and who there the day before.\n\nOf the riders in the tumbrils, some observe these things, and all things on their last roadside, with an impassive stare; others, with a lingering interest in the ways of life and men. Some, seated with drooping heads, are sunk in silent despair; again, there are some so heedful of their looks that they cast upon the multitude such glances as they have seen in theatres, and in pictures. Several close their eyes, and think, or try to get their straying thoughts together. Only one, and he a miserable creature, of a crazed aspect, is so shattered and made drunk by horror, that he sings, and tries to dance. Not one of the whole number appeals by look or gesture, to the pity of the people.\n\nThere is a guard of sundry horsemen riding abreast of the tumbrils, and faces are often turned up to some of them, and they are asked some question. It would seem to be always the same question, for, it is always followed by a press of people towards the third cart. The horsemen abreast of that cart, frequently point out one man in it with their swords. The leading curiosity is, to know which is he; he stands at the back of the tumbril with his head bent down, to converse with a mere girl who sits on the side of the cart, and holds his hand. He has no curiosity or care for the scene about him, and always speaks to the girl. Here and there in the long street of St. Honore, cries are raised against him. If they move him at all, it is only to a quiet smile, as he shakes his hair a little more loosely about his face. He cannot easily touch his face, his arms being bound.\n\nOn the steps of a church, awaiting the coming-up of the tumbrils, stands the Spy and prison-sheep. He looks into the first of them: not there. He looks into the second: not there. He already asks himself, “Has he sacrificed me?” when his face clears, as he looks into the third.\n\n“Which is Evrémonde?” says a man behind him.\n\n“That. At the back there.”\n\n“With his hand in the girl’s?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nThe man cries, “Down, Evrémonde! To the Guillotine all aristocrats! Down, Evrémonde!”\n\n“Hush, hush!” the Spy entreats him, timidly.\n\n“And why not, citizen?”\n\n“He is going to pay the forfeit: it will be paid in five minutes more. Let him be at peace.”\n\nBut the man continuing to exclaim, “Down, Evrémonde!” the face of Evrémonde is for a moment turned towards him. Evrémonde then sees the Spy, and looks attentively at him, and goes his way.\n\nThe clocks are on the stroke of three, and the furrow ploughed among the populace is turning round, to come on into the place of execution, and end. The ridges thrown to this side and to that, now crumble in and close behind the last plough as it passes on, for all are following to the Guillotine. In front of it, seated in chairs, as in a garden of public diversion, are a number of women, busily knitting. On one of the fore-most chairs, stands The Vengeance, looking about for her friend.\n\n“Thérèse!” she cries, in her shrill tones. “Who has seen her? Thérèse Defarge!”\n\n“She never missed before,” says a knitting-woman of the sisterhood.\n\n“No; nor will she miss now,” cries The Vengeance, petulantly. “Thérèse.”\n\n“Louder,” the woman recommends.\n\nAy! Louder, Vengeance, much louder, and still she will scarcely hear thee. Louder yet, Vengeance, with a little oath or so added, and yet it will hardly bring her. Send other women up and down to seek her, lingering somewhere; and yet, although the messengers have done dread deeds, it is questionable whether of their own wills they will go far enough to find her!\n\n“Bad Fortune!” cries The Vengeance, stamping her foot in the chair, “and here are the tumbrils! And Evrémonde will be despatched in a wink, and she not here! See her knitting in my hand, and her empty chair ready for her. I cry with vexation and disappointment!”\n\nAs The Vengeance descends from her elevation to do it, the tumbrils begin to discharge their loads. The ministers of Sainte Guillotine are robed and ready. Crash!--A head is held up, and the knitting-women who scarcely lifted their eyes to look at it a moment ago when it could think and speak, count One.\n\nThe second tumbril empties and moves on; the third comes up. Crash!--And the knitting-women, never faltering or pausing in their Work, count Two.\n\nThe supposed Evrémonde descends, and the seamstress is lifted out next after him. He has not relinquished her patient hand in getting out, but still holds it as he promised. He gently places her with her back to the crashing engine that constantly whirrs up and falls, and she looks into his face and thanks him.\n\n“But for you, dear stranger, I should not be so composed, for I am naturally a poor little thing, faint of heart; nor should I have been able to raise my thoughts to Him who was put to death, that we might have hope and comfort here to-day. I think you were sent to me by Heaven.”\n\n“Or you to me,” says Sydney Carton. “Keep your eyes upon me, dear child, and mind no other object.”\n\n“I mind nothing while I hold your hand. I shall mind nothing when I let it go, if they are rapid.”\n\n“They will be rapid. Fear not!”\n\nThe two stand in the fast-thinning throng of victims, but they speak as if they were alone. Eye to eye, voice to voice, hand to hand, heart to heart, these two children of the Universal Mother, else so wide apart and differing, have come together on the dark highway, to repair home together, and to rest in her bosom.\n\n“Brave and generous friend, will you let me ask you one last question? I am very ignorant, and it troubles me--just a little.”\n\n“Tell me what it is.”\n\n“I have a cousin, an only relative and an orphan, like myself, whom I love very dearly. She is five years younger than I, and she lives in a farmer’s house in the south country. Poverty parted us, and she knows nothing of my fate--for I cannot write--and if I could, how should I tell her! It is better as it is.”\n\n“Yes, yes: better as it is.”\n\n“What I have been thinking as we came along, and what I am still thinking now, as I look into your kind strong face which gives me so much support, is this:--If the Republic really does good to the poor, and they come to be less hungry, and in all ways to suffer less, she may live a long time: she may even live to be old.”\n\n“What then, my gentle sister?”\n\n“Do you think:” the uncomplaining eyes in which there is so much endurance, fill with tears, and the lips part a little more and tremble: “that it will seem long to me, while I wait for her in the better land where I trust both you and I will be mercifully sheltered?”\n\n“It cannot be, my child; there is no Time there, and no trouble there.”\n\n“You comfort me so much! I am so ignorant. Am I to kiss you now? Is the moment come?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nShe kisses his lips; he kisses hers; they solemnly bless each other. The spare hand does not tremble as he releases it; nothing worse than a sweet, bright constancy is in the patient face. She goes next before him--is gone; the knitting-women count Twenty-Two.\n\n“I am the Resurrection and the Life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die.”\n\nThe murmuring of many voices, the upturning of many faces, the pressing on of many footsteps in the outskirts of the crowd, so that it swells forward in a mass, like one great heave of water, all flashes away. Twenty-Three.\n\nThey said of him, about the city that night, that it was the peacefullest man’s face ever beheld there. Many added that he looked sublime and prophetic.\n\nOne of the most remarkable sufferers by the same axe--a woman--had asked at the foot of the same scaffold, not long before, to be allowed to write down the thoughts that were inspiring her. If he had given any utterance to his, and they were prophetic, they would have been these:\n\n“I see Barsad, and Cly, Defarge, The Vengeance, the Juryman, the Judge, long ranks of the new oppressors who have risen on the destruction of the old, perishing by this retributive instrument, before it shall cease out of its present use. I see a beautiful city and a brilliant people rising from this abyss, and, in their struggles to be truly free, in their triumphs and defeats, through long years to come, I see the evil of this time and of the previous time of which this is the natural birth, gradually making expiation for itself and wearing out.\n\n“I see the lives for which I lay down my life, peaceful, useful, prosperous and happy, in that England which I shall see no more. I see Her with a child upon her bosom, who bears my name. I see her father, aged and bent, but otherwise restored, and faithful to all men in his healing office, and at peace. I see the good old man, so long their friend, in ten years’ time enriching them with all he has, and passing tranquilly to his reward.\n\n“I see that I hold a sanctuary in their hearts, and in the hearts of their descendants, generations hence. I see her, an old woman, weeping for me on the anniversary of this day. I see her and her husband, their course done, lying side by side in their last earthly bed, and I know that each was not more honoured and held sacred in the other’s soul, than I was in the souls of both.\n\n“I see that child who lay upon her bosom and who bore my name, a man winning his way up in that path of life which once was mine. I see him winning it so well, that my name is made illustrious there by the light of his. I see the blots I threw upon it, faded away. I see him, fore-most of just judges and honoured men, bringing a boy of my name, with a forehead that I know and golden hair, to this place--then fair to look upon, with not a trace of this day’s disfigurement--and I hear him tell the child my story, with a tender and a faltering voice.\n\n“It is a far, far better thing that I do, than I have ever done; it is a far, far better rest that I go to than I have ever known.”";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-98"]="최고의 시절이자 최악의 시절이었고, 지혜의 시대이자 어리석음의 시대였으며, 믿음의 세기이자 불신의 세기였고, 빛의 계절이자 어둠의 계절이었으며, 희망의 봄이자 절망의 겨울이었다. 우리 앞에는 모든 것이 있었고, 우리 앞에는 아무것도 없었다. 우리는 모두 곧장 천국으로 가고 있었고, 우리는 모두 곧장 그 반대편으로 가고 있었다. 요컨대 그 시절은 지금의 시절과 너무도 닮아서, 당대의 떠들썩한 권위자들 가운데 일부는 그 시절을 좋게든 나쁘게든 오직 최상급의 비교로만 받아들여야 한다고 우겼다.\n\n영국의 왕좌에는 턱이 큰 왕과 얼굴이 평범한 왕비가 앉아 있었고, 프랑스의 왕좌에는 턱이 큰 왕과 얼굴이 고운 왕비가 앉아 있었다. 두 나라 모두에서, 빵과 물고기로 이루어진 국가의 특권을 누리는 영주들에게는 만사가 영원히 정해져 있다는 것이 수정보다도 명백했다.\n\n때는 주님의 해 1775년이었다. 영적인 계시는 지금처럼 그 은혜로운 시절에도 영국에 베풀어졌다. 사우스코트 부인은 최근 스물다섯번째 축복받은 생일을 맞이했는데, 근위대의 어느 예언자적 사병이 런던과 웨스트민스터가 삼켜질 채비가 되어 있다고 선포함으로써 그녀의 숭고한 출현을 미리 알린 바 있었다. 콕레인의 유령조차 메시지를 두드려 전한 뒤 겨우 열두 해 남짓 잠들어 있었을 뿐인데, 바로 지난해의 영들도 (초자연적으로 독창성이 결여된 채) 똑같이 자기네 메시지를 두드려 전했다. 한낱 지상의 사건들 가운데서 온 메시지들은 최근 아메리카에 모인 영국 신민들의 의회로부터 영국 왕실과 국민에게 전해졌는데, 이상하게도 그것은 콕레인 무리의 어떤 병아리들을 통해 지금껏 받은 그 어떤 통신보다도 인류에게 더 중대한 것으로 판명되었다.\n\n방패와 삼지창을 든 자매 나라보다 영적인 일에서는 대체로 덜 은혜받은 프랑스는 지폐를 찍어내고 또 써대면서 더할 나위 없이 매끄럽게 비탈을 굴러 내려갔다. 게다가 프랑스는 기독교 목자들의 인도 아래, 어느 젊은이가 오십이나 육십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 자기 눈앞을 지나가는 더러운 수도사 행렬에 경의를 표하느라 빗속에서 무릎 꿇지 않았다는 이유로, 그 두 손을 잘리고 펜치로 혀를 뽑히며 산 채로 그 몸을 태워야 한다는 선고를 내리는 따위의 인도적인 위업으로 스스로를 즐겁게 했다. 그 수난자가 처형되던 무렵, 프랑스와 노르웨이의 숲속에 뿌리내린 채 자라나던 나무들이 있었을 법한데, 그 나무들은 이미 운명이라는 나무꾼에게 표시가 되어, 베어 넘어뜨려져 널빤지로 켜지고, 자루와 칼이 달린 어떤 이동식 장치로 만들어질 운명이었으니, 역사 속에서 무시무시한 그것이었다. 빠리에 인접한 무거운 땅을 일구는 어느 농부들의 허름한 헛간에는 바로 그날에도 비바람을 피해 들여놓은 투박한 수레들이 있었을 법한데, 시골 진흙이 잔뜩 튀고 돼지들이 코를 킁킁대며 들쑤시고 닭들이 올라앉던 그 수레들은 이미 죽음이라는 농부가 혁명의 호송 수레로 쓰려고 따로 빼두었던 것이었다. 그러나 그 나무꾼과 그 농부는 쉼 없이 일하면서도 소리 없이 일했고, 발소리를 죽여 다니는 그들을 아무도 듣지 못했다. 더욱이 그들이 깨어 있으리라는 의심을 품는 것 자체가 무신론적이고 반역적인 일이었기 때문이다.\n\n영국에서는 국가적 자랑거리로 삼을 만한 질서와 보호가 거의 없었다. 무장한 자들의 대담한 가택 침입과 노상강도가 수도 한복판에서 밤마다 벌어졌다. 가구를 안전하게 보관하려고 가구상의 창고로 옮겨놓지 않은 채 시외로 나가지 말라는 경고가 가족들에게 공공연히 내려졌다. 어둠 속의 노상강도는 밝은 데서는 도시의 상인이었고, \"두목\"이라는 신분으로 길을 막아 세웠던 동료 상인에게 알아보여 도전을 받자, 늠름하게 그 머리에 총을 쏘고는 말을 몰아 달아났다. 우편마차가 강도 일곱에게 습격당해, 호송대원이 그 가운데 셋을 쏘아 죽였으나 \"탄약이 떨어진 탓에\" 나머지 넷에게 자신이 총을 맞아 죽었고, 그 뒤 우편마차는 평온하게 털렸다. 저 위풍당당한 권세가, 런던 시장은 노상강도 하나에게 턴엄 그린에서 멈춰 서서 내놓으라는 협박을 당했으니, 그 강도는 시장의 모든 수행원들이 보는 앞에서 그 저명한 인물을 약탈했다. 런던 감옥의 죄수들은 간수들과 싸움을 벌였고, 법의 위엄은 산탄과 탄환을 잔뜩 재운 나팔총을 그들 한가운데로 쏘아댔다. 도둑들은 궁정 응접실에서 귀족 영주들의 목에서 다이아몬드 십자가를 잘라냈다. 머스킷 총병들이 밀수품을 수색하러 세인트자일스에 들어갔고, 군중은 총병들에게 총을 쏘았으며, 총병들은 군중에게 총을 쏘았으나, 이런 사건들 가운데 그 어느 것도 별스러운 일이라고 여기는 사람은 없었다. 그 한복판에서 늘 분주하고 늘 무익함보다 더 못한 교수형 집행인이 끊임없이 불려 다녔다. 어느 때는 잡다한 범죄자들을 줄줄이 목매달고, 어느 때는 화요일에 잡힌 가택 침입자를 토요일에 목매달고, 어느 때는 뉴게이트에서 사람들의 손바닥을 한꺼번에 열두 명씩 지지고, 어느 때는 웨스트민스터 홀 문간에서 소책자들을 불태웠다. 오늘은 흉악한 살인범의 목숨을 거두고, 내일은 어느 농부의 아이에게서 육 펜스를 훔친 가련한 좀도둑의 목숨을 거두었다.\n\n이 모든 일들이, 그리고 그와 비슷한 수천 가지 일들이, 그리운 옛 1775년에, 그리고 그 무렵에 벌어졌다. 그것들에 둘러싸여, 나무꾼과 농부가 아무도 모르게 일하는 동안, 턱이 큰 그 두 사람과 평범한 얼굴 그리고 고운 얼굴의 그 다른 두 사람은 어지간한 소동을 일으키며 거닐었고, 자신들의 신성한 권리를 거만하게 휘둘렀다. 이렇게 1775년은 그들 고귀하신 분들과, 그밖의 무수한 미천한 존재들을 — 이 연대기 속 존재들도 그 가운데 끼어 있었다 — 그들 앞에 놓인 길을 따라 이끌어 갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER II. The Mail\n\n11월 말의 어느 금요일 밤, 이 이야기와 관련이 있는 첫번째 인물 앞에 놓인 것은 도버로 가는 길이었다. 그에게는, 도버로 가는 길이 슈터스힐을 덜컹덜컹 올라가는 도버행 우편마차 너머에 펼쳐져 있었다. 그는 다른 승객들이 그러하듯 우편마차 곁에서 진창길을 따라 언덕을 걸어 올라갔다. 그런 상황에서 걷는 일에 조금이라도 흥미가 있어서가 아니라, 언덕과 마구와 진흙과 우편마차가 모두 너무 무거워서, 말들이 벌써 세 번이나 멈춰 섰고, 게다가 한번은 마차를 길을 가로질러 끌고 가 블랙히스로 되돌아가려는 반항적인 속셈까지 드러냈기 때문이다. 그러나 고삐와 채찍과 마부와 호송대원이 합심하여, 일부 짐승에게도 이성이 깃들어 있다는 주장에 강력한 근거가 될 법한 그 의도를 금지하는 전시법 조항을 읽어주자, 마차를 끌던 말들은 항복하고 제 본분으로 돌아왔다.\n\n머리를 떨어뜨리고 꼬리를 부들부들 떨면서, 말들은 진한 진흙을 헤치며 나아갔는데, 이따금 비틀거리고 발을 헛디뎌 큼직한 관절 마디마다 산산이 부서지는 것만 같았다. 마부가 조심스럽게 \"워-어! 워-어, 자!\" 하며 말들을 쉬게 하고 멈춰 세울 때마다, 왼쪽 선두 말은 머리와 그 위에 달린 모든 것을 사납게 흔들어댔으니, 마치 마차를 언덕 위로 끌어올릴 수 없다고 우기는, 유난히 단호한 말 같았다. 선두 말이 이렇게 덜그럭거릴 때마다 그 승객은 신경이 예민한 승객이 그러할 법하게 흠칫 놀라며 마음이 어지러워졌다.\n\n움푹 팬 곳마다 김이 서리는 안개가 자욱했고, 그 안개는 쉴 곳을 찾아 헤매지만 찾지 못하는 악령처럼 쓸쓸하게 언덕 위로 떠돌았다. 끈적하고 지독하게 차가운 안개는, 불길한 바다의 물결이 그러하듯, 눈에 보이게 서로를 뒤따르고 뒤덮으며 잔물결을 이루어 공기 속을 느릿느릿 헤쳐나갔다. 그 안개는 마차 등불의 불빛에서 자기 자신의 움직임과 몇 야드 앞의 길을 빼고는 모든 것을 가려버릴 만큼 짙었다. 힘겹게 끄는 말들에게서 피어오르는 김이 그 안개 속으로 서려들었으니, 마치 그 모든 안개를 그들이 만들어내기라도 한 것 같았다.\n\n그 한 사람 말고도 다른 두 승객이 우편마차 곁에서 터덜터덜 언덕을 올라가고 있었다. 셋 모두 광대뼈까지, 그리고 귀 위까지 몸을 칭칭 감싸고, 목이 긴 장화를 신고 있었다. 셋 가운데 어느 누구도 자기가 본 것만으로는 다른 두 사람이 어떻게 생겼는지 말할 수 없었을 것이다. 각자는 두 동행의 육체의 눈에서뿐 아니라 마음의 눈에서도 거의 똑같이 두꺼운 감싸개 아래 숨어 있었다. 그 시절에는 여행자들이 갑작스레 속을 터놓기를 몹시 꺼렸으니, 길 위의 누구나 강도이거나 강도와 한패일 수 있었기 때문이다. 후자로 말하자면, 역참과 선술집마다 주인부터 마구간의 이름 모를 최하층 일꾼에 이르기까지 \"두목\"의 돈을 받는 누군가를 댈 수 있었으니, 그것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다. 그리하여 1775년 11월의 그 금요일 밤, 슈터스힐을 덜컹덜컹 올라가던 도버행 우편마차의 호송대원은, 우편마차 뒤편 자기만의 특별한 자리에 서서 발을 동동 구르며, 자기 앞에 놓인 무기 상자에서 한시도 눈과 손을 떼지 않은 채 속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 그 상자 안에는 단검을 바닥에 깔고 그 위에 장전된 기병총 여섯 자루인가 여덟 자루를 얹은 다음, 맨 위에 장전된 나팔총 한 자루가 놓여 있었다.\n\n도버행 우편마차는 평소의 화기애애한 처지에 놓여 있었으니, 호송대원은 승객들을 의심하고, 승객들은 서로를 그리고 호송대원을 의심하고, 모두가 다른 모든 이를 의심했으며, 마부는 말들 말고는 그 무엇도 확신하지 못했다. 그 말들로 말하자면, 마부는 그것들이 이번 여정에 적합하지 못하다고 양심에 거리낌 없이 신약과 구약 두 성서에 걸고 맹세할 수 있었다.\n\n\"워-어!\" 마부가 말했다. \"자, 그래! 한번만 더 끌면 꼭대기다, 빌어먹을 것들, 여기까지 오느라 내가 어지간히 애를 먹었으니! — 조!\"\n\n\"여어!\" 호송대원이 대답했다.\n\n\"지금 몇 시쯤 됐나, 조?\"\n\n\"십일 시 십 분쯤, 잘 잡아서.\"\n\n\"제기랄!\" 짜증이 난 마부가 내뱉었다. \"아직 슈터스힐 꼭대기도 못 올랐는데! 쯧! 야! 좀 가자!\"\n\n채찍이 단호하게 가로막으며 안 된다고 못 박자, 그 단호한 말은 단호하게 기어오르기 시작했고, 나머지 세 말도 뒤따랐다. 다시 한번, 도버행 우편마차는 승객들의 목 긴 장화가 그 곁에서 철벅거리며 따라오는 가운데 힘겹게 나아갔다. 그들은 마차가 멈출 때 멈췄고, 마차에 바짝 붙어 다녔다. 셋 가운데 누구라도 다른 누구에게 안개와 어둠 속으로 조금 앞서 걸어가자고 대담하게 제안했다면, 노상강도로 몰려 그 자리에서 총에 맞기 십상이었을 것이다.\n\n마지막으로 힘껏 끌어 우편마차는 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀다. 말들은 다시 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 섰고, 호송대원은 내려가 내리막에 대비해 바퀴에 제동장치를 걸고, 승객들을 태우려 마차 문을 열었다.\n\n\"쯧! 조!\" 마부가 마부석에서 내려다보며 경고하는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 톰?\"\n\n둘은 함께 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"말 한 마리가 천천히 달려 올라오는 것 같네, 조.\"\n\n\"내가 듣기엔 전속력으로 달려오는데, 톰.\" 호송대원이 문에서 손을 떼고 잽싸게 제자리로 올라가며 대꾸했다. \"여러분! 국왕의 이름으로, 다들 꼼짝 마시오!\"\n\n이렇게 다급히 명령한 뒤, 그는 나팔총의 공이치기를 당기고 공격 태세를 취했다.\n\n이 이야기에 예약된 승객은 마차 발판에 올라서서 타려던 참이었다. 다른 두 승객은 그의 바로 뒤에 붙어 뒤따라 오르려 하고 있었다. 그는 발판에 그대로 서 있었으니, 몸은 마차 안에 절반, 밖에 절반이 걸쳐 있었다. 두 사람은 그 아래 길에 그대로 서 있었다. 그들은 모두 마부에게서 호송대원에게로, 다시 호송대원에게서 마부에게로 시선을 옮기며 귀를 기울였다. 마부도 뒤를 돌아보았고 호송대원도 뒤를 돌아보았으며, 심지어 그 단호한 선두 말까지도 귀를 쫑긋 세우고는 거역하지 않고 뒤를 돌아보았다.\n\n마차의 덜컹거림과 힘겨운 안간힘이 그치면서 찾아온 고요가 밤의 고요에 더해져, 사위는 참으로 적막했다. 말들이 헐떡이는 통에 마차에 부르르 떨리는 진동이 전해져, 마치 마차가 동요하고 있는 것 같았다. 승객들의 심장은 어쩌면 들릴 만큼 크게 뛰었을 것이다. 어찌 됐든 그 고요한 정적은 숨을 헐떡이다가 숨을 죽이고 기대로 맥박이 빨라진 사람들의 모습을 또렷이 드러내고 있었다.\n\n말 한 마리가 전속력으로 달려오는 소리가 언덕 위로 빠르고 맹렬하게 다가왔다.\n\n\"워-어!\" 호송대원이 있는 힘껏 고함쳤다. \"거기 너! 서라! 쏘겠다!\"\n\n말발굽 소리가 갑자기 멎더니, 한참 철벅거리고 허우적대는 가운데 한 남자의 목소리가 안개 속에서 외쳤다. \"그게 도버행 우편마차요?\"\n\n\"그게 뭐든 네가 알 바 아니다!\" 호송대원이 받아쳤다. \"너는 누구냐?\"\n\n\"그게 도버행 우편마차냐고요?\"\n\n\"왜 알려고 하느냐?\"\n\n\"맞다면, 승객 한 사람을 찾고 있소.\"\n\n\"무슨 승객?\"\n\n\"자비스 로리 씨요.\"\n\n이 이야기에 예약된 우리의 승객이 곧바로 그것이 자기 이름임을 드러냈다. 호송대원과 마부와 다른 두 승객이 그를 미심쩍게 바라보았다.\n\n\"있는 자리에 그대로 있어라.\" 호송대원이 안개 속 목소리를 향해 외쳤다. \"내가 실수라도 하면, 네 평생에 다시는 바로잡을 수 없을 테니까. 로리라는 이름의 신사분, 곧장 대답하시오.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그러자 승객이 부드럽게 떨리는 말투로 물었다. \"누가 나를 찾는 거요? 제리인가?\"\n\n(\"저게 제리라면, 난 제리 목소리가 영 마음에 안 드는데.\" 호송대원이 속으로 투덜거렸다. \"내 취향보다 더 쉰 목소리야, 저 제리란 놈은.\")\n\n\"그렇습니다, 로리 씨.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"저 너머에서 보내온 급보입니다. T. 회사에서요.\"\n\n\"이 전령은 내가 아는 사람이오, 호송대원.\" 로리 씨가 길로 내려서며 말했다. 다른 두 승객이 뒤에서 정중하다기보다는 잽싸게 그를 거들어 내려보낸 뒤, 곧장 마차 안으로 기어 올라가 문을 닫고 창문을 끌어올렸다. \"저 사람을 가까이 오게 해도 되오. 잘못된 건 아무것도 없으니.\"\n\n\"잘못된 게 없기를 바랍니다만, 저로서야 그렇게 빌어먹을 만큼 확신할 수가 없지요.\" 호송대원이 무뚝뚝하게 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸다. \"어이, 너!\"\n\n\"여어! 그래, 너도 여어!\" 제리가 아까보다 더 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"걷는 속도로 와! 알아들었나? 그리고 그 안장에 권총집을 달고 있거든, 거기 손이 닿는 걸 내 눈에 띄게 하지 마라. 나는 잽싸게 실수하는 데는 귀신이고, 한번 실수했다 하면 그게 납덩이 꼴로 나타나니까. 자, 그럼 네 꼴을 좀 보자.\"\n\n말과 기수의 형체가 소용돌이치는 안개를 뚫고 천천히 다가와, 승객이 서 있는 우편마차 곁에 이르렀다. 기수는 몸을 숙여 호송대원을 향해 눈을 치켜뜨면서, 승객에게 작게 접은 종이 한 장을 건넸다. 기수의 말은 잔뜩 지쳐 헐떡였고, 말과 기수 둘 다 말발굽에서 사람의 모자까지 진흙을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"호송대원!\" 승객이 조용하고 사무적인 자신감이 깃든 어조로 말했다.\n\n경계를 늦추지 않던 호송대원은 오른손을 치켜든 나팔총 개머리판에, 왼손을 총신에 댄 채, 시선은 기수에게 둔 채로, 짧게 대답했다. \"예.\"\n\n\"걱정할 것은 없소. 나는 텔슨 은행 사람이오. 런던의 텔슨 은행은 알고 있을 거요. 나는 볼일이 있어 빠리로 가는 길이오. 자, 한잔 마시게 일 크라운 받으시오. 이걸 읽어도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"그러시려거든 빨리 하십쇼, 나리.\"\n\n그는 그쪽 마차 등불 빛에 종이를 펴서 읽었으니, 먼저 속으로 읽고 그다음 소리 내어 읽었다. \"'마드무아젤을 모시고 도버에서 대기하라.' 보다시피 길지 않소, 호송대원. 제리, 내 답이 '되살아나다'였다고 전하게.\"\n\n제리가 안장 위에서 흠칫했다. \"그것도 참 기막히게 이상한 답이로군요.\" 그가 더없이 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그 전갈을 가지고 돌아가게. 그러면 그쪽에서는 내가 이걸 받았다는 걸, 내가 직접 쓴 거나 다름없이 알 걸세. 되도록 서둘러 가게. 잘 가게.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 승객은 마차 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 시계와 지갑을 잽싸게 장화 속에 감춰두고는 이제 다들 잠든 척하던 동승객들은 그를 조금도 거들어주지 않았다. 다른 어떤 행동도 새로 일으키는 위험을 피하겠다는 것 말고는 별다른 뜻이 없었다.\n\n우편마차는 다시 덜컹거리며 나아갔고, 내리막을 시작하자 더 짙은 안개 자락이 그 둘레로 휘감겨들었다. 호송대원은 이내 나팔총을 무기 상자에 도로 넣고, 상자 안의 나머지 내용물을 살피고, 자기 허리띠에 찬 예비 권총을 살핀 다음, 좌석 밑의 더 작은 상자를 들여다보았는데, 그 안에는 대장간 연장 몇 가지와 횃불 두 자루, 그리고 부싯깃통이 들어 있었다. 그는 만반의 준비를 갖추고 있었으니, 이따금 실제로 일어나는 일이지만 마차 등불이 바람에 꺼져버린다 해도, 안에 들어앉아 부싯돌과 부시의 불꽃을 짚에서 잘 떼어놓기만 하면, (운이 좋다면) 오 분 만에 그런대로 안전하고 손쉽게 불을 얻을 수 있었다.\n\n\"톰!\" 마차 지붕 너머로 나직이.\n\n\"여어, 조.\"\n\n\"그 전갈 들었나?\"\n\n\"들었지, 조.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린지 알겠던가, 톰?\"\n\n\"하나도 모르겠던데, 조.\"\n\n\"그거 참 묘하게 들어맞는군.\" 호송대원이 곰곰이 생각했다. \"나도 똑같이 하나도 모르겠으니 말이야.\"\n\n한편 안개와 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨진 제리는 말에서 내렸으니, 지친 말을 쉬게 하려는 것만이 아니라 얼굴에 묻은 진흙을 닦아내고, 반 갤런쯤은 너끈히 담길 듯한 모자챙에서 빗물을 털어내려는 것이었다. 진흙이 잔뜩 튄 팔에 고삐를 걸친 채, 우편마차의 바퀴 소리가 더는 들리지 않고 밤이 다시 아주 고요해질 때까지 서 있다가, 그는 언덕을 걸어 내려가려 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"템플바에서 그렇게 내달렸으니, 이 늙은 아가씨야, 평지에 닿을 때까지는 네 앞다리를 못 믿겠다.\" 이 쉰 목소리의 전령이 자기 암말을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"'되살아나다'라. 그것 참 기막히게 이상한 전갈이야. 너한테는 그런 게 별로 안 어울리지, 제리! 이봐, 제리! 되살아나는 게 유행이라도 됐다간, 넌 아주 기막히게 곤란해질 거다, 제리!\"\n\n# CHAPTER III. The Night Shadows\n\n깊이 새겨볼 만한 놀라운 사실은, 모든 인간이 다른 모든 인간에게 그토록 깊은 비밀이자 신비가 되도록 만들어져 있다는 것이다. 밤에 거대한 도시로 들어설 때면, 어둡게 무리지어 모인 저 집들 하나하나가 저마다의 비밀을 품고 있고, 그 집들의 방 하나하나가 저마다의 비밀을 품고 있으며, 그곳 수십만 가슴 속에서 뛰는 심장 하나하나가 그 상상의 어느 한구석에서는 가장 가까운 심장에게조차 비밀이라는 것이, 엄숙한 상념으로 다가온다! 죽음 그 자체의 두려움마저도 어느 정도는 여기에 닿아 있다. 내가 사랑했던 이 소중한 책의 책장을 더는 넘길 수 없게 되고, 언젠가 그것을 다 읽으리라 헛되이 바랄 수밖에 없게 된다. 순간순간 빛이 비쳐 들 때 그 속에 묻힌 보물과 잠겨 있는 다른 것들을 언뜻언뜻 보았던 이 헤아릴 수 없는 물의 깊은 속을 더는 들여다볼 수 없게 된다. 내가 겨우 한 페이지를 읽었을 때 그 책이 용수철처럼 탁 닫혀 영원히 그리고 영원히 닫혀버리도록 정해져 있었던 것이다. 빛이 그 수면 위에서 일렁이고 내가 무지한 채 물가에 서 있을 때 그 물이 영원한 서리 속에 갇혀버리도록 정해져 있었던 것이다. 내 친구가 죽었고, 내 이웃이 죽었고, 내 사랑, 내 영혼의 연인이 죽었으니, 그것은 늘 그 개별성 안에 있었던, 그리고 내가 내 생의 끝까지 내 안에 지니고 갈 비밀의 가차없는 응고이자 영속이다. 내가 지나가는 이 도시의 묘지 어디에서든, 거기 잠든 자가 그 가장 깊은 인격에 있어 나에게, 혹은 내가 그들에게 그러하듯, 분주한 이곳 주민들보다 더 헤아리기 어려운 존재일까?\n\n이것에 관해서라면, 이 타고난, 그리고 결코 빼앗길 수 없는 유산에 관해서라면, 말을 탄 전령은 왕이나 국무대신, 혹은 런던에서 가장 부유한 상인과 정확히 똑같은 소유물을 지니고 있었다. 덜컹거리는 낡은 우편마차의 좁은 공간에 갇힌 세 승객도 마찬가지였으니, 그들은 서로에게 완벽한 신비였으며, 마치 각자 자신의 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차, 혹은 예순 마리 말이 끄는 마차에 탄 채 다음 사람과의 사이에 한 주(州)만큼의 거리를 두고 있는 듯했다.\n\n전령은 느긋한 속보로 되돌아갔는데, 길가의 술집에 꽤 자주 멈춰 서서 술을 들이켜면서도 제 속내는 혼자 간직하고 모자는 눈 위로 비스듬히 눌러쓰려는 경향을 보였다. 그의 두 눈은 그 장식과 무척 잘 어울렸으니, 빛깔도 형체도 깊이가 없는 표면적인 검은색이었고, 너무 가까이 붙어 있었다. 마치 그 둘이 너무 멀리 떨어져 있으면 따로따로 무언가를 들킬까 두려워하는 것 같았다. 세 귀퉁이가 진 타구(唾具)처럼 생긴 낡은 삼각모 아래, 그리고 착용자의 무릎까지 거의 내려오는 턱과 목의 커다란 목도리 위로, 그 눈은 음흉한 표정을 띠고 있었다. 술을 마시려고 멈출 때면 그는 왼손으로 이 목도리를 끌어내렸는데, 오직 오른손으로 술을 따라 넣는 동안만 그랬고, 다 끝내자마자 다시 목도리로 감쌌다.\n\n\"안 돼, 제리, 안 돼!\" 전령은 말을 몰며 한 가지 주제만 되뇌었다. \"그건 자네한테 안 어울려, 제리. 제리, 자네 같은 정직한 장사꾼한테는 그게 자네 장사에 맞지가 않는다고! 다시 살아난다고—! 그 양반이 술을 마신 게 아니라면 내 목을 걸겠어!\"\n\n그가 전해야 할 그 전언은 그의 마음을 어찌나 어지럽혔던지, 그는 여러 차례 모자를 벗고 머리를 긁적이지 않을 수 없었다. 너덜너덜하게 벗어진 정수리만 빼면 그의 머리에는 뻣뻣한 검은 머리칼이 사방으로 들쭉날쭉 솟아 있었고, 그것은 그의 넓적하고 뭉툭한 코까지 비탈을 따라 내려오듯 자라 있었다. 그것이 어찌나 대장장이의 솜씨 같았던지, 머리칼이라기보다는 단단히 못을 박은 담장 꼭대기에 훨씬 가까워서, 등넘기 놀이의 명수라도 그를 세상에서 가장 위험한, 결코 넘어갈 수 없는 사람으로 여겨 마다했을 법했다.\n\n그가 템플바 옆 텔슨 은행 문간의 초소에 있는 야경꾼에게 전해야 할 그 전언을 가지고 속보로 되돌아가는 동안 — 그 야경꾼은 그것을 다시 안쪽의 더 높은 권위자들에게 전할 참이었다 — 밤의 그림자들은 그에게는 그 전언에서 솟아나는 형상들로, 암말에게는 그 자신의 사사로운 불안거리에서 솟아나는 형상들로 나타났다. 그 형상들은 꽤 많았던 모양인지, 암말은 길 위의 그림자마다 깜짝깜짝 놀라 비켜섰다.\n\n그동안 우편마차는 헤아릴 수 없는 세 길동무를 안에 태운 채 그 지루한 길 위를 덜컹덜컹 흔들리고 달그락거리며 굴러갔다. 그들에게도 마찬가지로, 밤의 그림자들은 졸음에 겨운 눈과 떠도는 생각이 암시하는 형상들로 제 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n텔슨 은행은 그 우편마차 안에서 한바탕 인출 소동을 겪었다. 은행에서 온 그 승객이 — 마차가 유독 심하게 덜컹거릴 때마다 옆 승객에게 부딪혀 그를 구석으로 밀어붙이지 않도록 막아주는 가죽끈에 한쪽 팔을 꿰고서 — 반쯤 감은 눈으로 제자리에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 동안, 작은 마차 창문들과 그것을 통해 어슴푸레 새어드는 마차 등불, 그리고 맞은편 승객의 부피 큰 보따리가 모두 은행이 되어 한바탕 큰 거래를 벌였다. 마구의 달그락거리는 소리는 돈이 짤랑거리는 소리였고, 텔슨 은행이 국내외의 온갖 연줄을 다 동원해 세 배의 시간을 들여 지불하는 것보다도 더 많은 어음이 단 오 분 만에 결제되었다. 그러더니 텔슨 은행 지하의 금고실들이, 그 승객이 알고 있는 값진 보관물과 비밀들과 함께 (그가 그것들에 대해 아는 바는 적지 않았다) 그의 앞에 열렸고, 그는 커다란 열쇠 꾸러미와 희미하게 타는 촛불을 들고 그것들 사이로 들어가, 마지막으로 보았을 때와 똑같이 안전하고 단단하고 멀쩡하고 고요한 채로 있는 것을 확인했다.\n\n그러나 은행이 거의 늘 그와 함께 있었고, 또 마차가 (마취제에 취한 채 느끼는 고통의 존재처럼 흐릿하게) 늘 그와 함께 있었지만, 밤 내내 끊임없이 흐르는 또 하나의 인상의 물줄기가 있었다. 그는 누군가를 무덤에서 파내러 가는 길이었다.\n\n그런데 그의 앞에 나타나는 수많은 얼굴 가운데 어느 것이 그 묻힌 사람의 진짜 얼굴인지를 밤의 그림자들은 알려주지 않았다. 하지만 그 얼굴들은 모두 햇수로 마흔다섯 된 한 남자의 얼굴이었고, 주로 그것들이 드러내는 정념에서, 그리고 닳고 야윈 상태의 섬뜩함에서 서로 달랐다. 자긍심, 경멸, 반항, 완고함, 굴종, 비탄이 차례차례 이어졌고, 움푹 꺼진 뺨, 송장 같은 빛깔, 수척한 손과 형상도 갖가지 모습으로 이어졌다. 그러나 그 얼굴은 대체로 하나의 얼굴이었고, 머리는 하나같이 때 이르게 하얗게 세어 있었다. 졸고 있던 승객은 백 번도 넘게 이 유령에게 물었다.\n\n\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\"\n\n대답은 언제나 같았다. \"거의 십팔 년이오.\"\n\n\"파내질 거라는 희망은 다 버렸었소?\"\n\n\"오래전에.\"\n\n\"당신이 다시 살아났다는 걸 알고 있소?\"\n\n\"그렇게들 말하더군.\"\n\n\"부디 살고 싶은 마음이 있기를 바라오?\"\n\n\"모르겠소.\"\n\n\"그녀를 보여드릴까요? 와서 그녀를 보시겠소?\"\n\n이 물음에 대한 대답은 갖가지였고 서로 모순되었다. 어떤 때는 끊어지는 목소리로 \"기다려주오! 너무 일찍 그녀를 보면 나는 죽고 말 거요.\" 어떤 때는 부드러운 눈물의 비 속에서 \"그녀에게 데려다주오.\" 어떤 때는 멍하니 어리둥절한 채 \"나는 그녀를 모르오. 무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\"\n\n그런 가상의 문답이 오간 뒤, 승객은 상상 속에서 파고 또 파고, 파들어 갔다 — 어떤 때는 삽으로, 어떤 때는 커다란 열쇠로, 어떤 때는 맨손으로 — 이 가련한 존재를 파내려고. 마침내 얼굴과 머리칼에 흙이 매달린 채 끌려나오면, 그는 별안간 부채질하듯 흩어져 먼지가 되어버렸다. 그러면 승객은 깜짝 놀라 정신을 차리고는, 안개와 비의 실체를 뺨에 느끼려고 창문을 내렸다.\n\n그러나 그의 눈이 안개와 비, 등불에서 비쳐 움직이는 빛의 자락, 그리고 덜컹덜컹 뒤로 물러나는 길가의 산울타리 위로 떠지고 나서도, 마차 밖의 밤 그림자들은 마차 안의 밤 그림자들의 행렬 속으로 스며들었다. 템플바 옆의 진짜 은행, 지난 하루의 진짜 업무, 진짜 금고실, 그를 뒤쫓아 보낸 진짜 급사, 그리고 되돌려 보낸 진짜 전언, 그 모든 것이 거기 있었다. 그것들 한복판에서 그 유령 같은 얼굴이 솟아오르면, 그는 다시 그것에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\"\n\n\"거의 십팔 년이오.\"\n\n\"부디 살고 싶은 마음이 있기를 바라오?\"\n\n\"모르겠소.\"\n\n파고 — 파고 — 파들어 가다가, 두 승객 가운데 하나가 짜증스럽게 몸을 움직이면 그제야 그는 창문을 끌어올리고, 가죽끈에 팔을 단단히 꿰고서, 잠든 두 형체를 골똘히 헤아리다가, 마침내 그의 마음이 그것들을 놓아버리면 그것들은 다시 은행과 무덤 속으로 미끄러져 사라졌다.\n\n\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\"\n\n\"거의 십팔 년이오.\"\n\n\"파내질 거라는 희망은 다 버렸었소?\"\n\n\"오래전에.\"\n\n그 말들이 마치 방금 입에서 나온 듯 여전히 그의 귓가에 — 평생 들어온 그 어떤 말 못지않게 또렷하게 그의 귓가에 — 남아 있을 때, 지친 승객은 화들짝 깨어 대낮의 의식 속으로 돌아왔고, 밤의 그림자들이 사라졌음을 알게 되었다.\n\n그는 창문을 내리고 떠오르는 해를 내다보았다. 갈아엎은 밭이랑이 있었고, 그 위에는 어젯밤 말들의 멍에를 벗길 때 버려둔 쟁기 한 대가 놓여 있었다. 그 너머로는 고요한 잡목림이 있었는데, 그 나무들에는 타는 듯한 붉은빛과 황금빛 나뭇잎이 아직 많이 남아 있었다. 땅은 차갑고 축축했지만 하늘은 맑았고, 해는 환하고 평온하고 아름답게 떠올랐다.\n\n\"십팔 년이라니!\" 승객은 해를 바라보며 말했다. \"자비로우신 낮의 창조주여! 십팔 년 동안이나 산 채로 묻혀 있다니!\"\n\n# CHAPTER IV. The Preparation\n\n우편마차가 오전 나절에 무사히 도버에 다다랐을 때, 로열조지 호텔의 수석 급사가 늘 하던 대로 마차 문을 열었다. 그는 다소 격식을 차린 몸짓으로 그렇게 했으니, 겨울에 런던에서 출발하는 우편마차 여행이란 모험심 강한 여행자에게 축하를 건넬 만한 업적이었던 것이다.\n* 도버: 영국 남동부 켄트의 항구 도시. 영국해협을 건너 프랑스로 가는 관문이다.\n\n그때쯤 축하받을 모험심 강한 여행자는 단 한 사람뿐이었다. 나머지 두 사람은 각자의 길가 목적지에 내려졌기 때문이다. 곰팡내 나는 마차 안은 축축하고 더러운 짚, 불쾌한 냄새, 어둑함까지 어우러져 차라리 커다란 개집과 닮아 있었다. 거기서 짚 부스러기를 주렁주렁 매단 채 빠져나온 승객 로리 씨는 텁수룩하게 엉킨 외투 자락과 펄럭이는 모자, 진흙투성이 다리까지 더해져, 차라리 좀 큰 종류의 개를 닮아 있었다.\n\"내일 깔레로 가는 연락선이 있겠나, 급사?\"\n\n* 깔레: 프랑스 북부의 항구 도시. 도버와 영국해협을 사이에 두고 마주 본다.\n\n\"예, 손님. 날씨가 버텨주고 바람이 그런대로 순하게 분다면요. 조수는 오후 두시쯤 꽤 알맞게 들어올 겁니다, 손님. 잠자리는요, 손님?\"\n\n\"밤이 되기 전까지는 잠자리에 들지 않겠네. 하지만 침실 하나와 이발사가 필요하네.\"\n\n\"그러고 나서 아침식사도요, 손님? 예, 손님. 이쪽입니다, 손님. 콩코드 방으로 모셔라! 신사분 여행 가방과 더운물을 콩코드로. 콩코드에서 신사분 장화를 벗겨드려라. (좋은 석탄불을 보시게 될 겁니다, 손님.) 이발사를 콩코드로 불러와라. 자, 어서들 움직여라, 콩코드 방이다!\"\n\n콩코드 침실은 늘 우편마차 승객에게 배정되었고, 우편마차 승객은 늘 머리끝부터 발끝까지 두툼하게 싸매고 있었던 까닭에, 그 방은 로열조지 호텔 사람들에게 묘한 흥밋거리가 되었다. 들어가는 사람은 오직 한 종류뿐인데, 나오는 사람은 온갖 종류와 갖가지 부류였기 때문이다. 그리하여 또다른 급사 하나와 짐꾼 둘, 하녀 여럿과 안주인까지, 갈색 옷을 격식 있게 차려입은 예순 살가량의 신사가 아침식사를 하러 지나갈 때, 콩코드 방과 식당 사이 길목 곳곳에 우연인 척 어슬렁거리고 있었다. 그 옷은 꽤 닳았지만 매우 잘 손질되어 있었고, 커다란 네모난 소맷부리와 큼직한 주머니 덮개가 달려 있었다.\n\n그날 오전 식당에는 그 갈색 옷의 신사 말고는 아무도 없었다. 아침식사 식탁이 불 앞에 차려졌고, 불빛이 그의 몸을 비추는 가운데 식사를 기다리며 앉아 있는 그의 모습은 어찌나 미동도 없는지, 마치 초상화를 위해 자세를 잡고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n그는 단정하고 빈틈없어 보였다. 두 손을 각각 무릎에 얹은 채였고, 덮개 달린 조끼 아래에서는 큼직한 회중시계가 낭랑한 설교 같은 똑딱 소리를 내고 있었으니, 마치 활활 타오르는 불의 경박함과 덧없음에 맞서 자신의 무게와 장수를 겨루기라도 하는 듯했다. 그는 다리맵시가 좋았고 그 점을 조금 으스대고 있었으니, 갈색 양말은 매끈하고 꼭 맞았으며 결이 고운 천이었기 때문이다. 그의 구두와 버클도 수수하긴 했으나 맵시 있었다. 그는 머리에 바싹 붙는 묘하게 작고 매끄러운 아마빛 가발을 쓰고 있었는데, 그 가발은 짐작건대 머리카락으로 만든 것이었겠으나 차라리 비단이나 유리의 가는 실을 자아 만든 것처럼 보였다. 그의 셔츠는 양말에 걸맞은 고급은 아니었으나, 이웃한 바닷가에 부서지는 파도의 마루처럼, 또는 먼바다에서 햇빛에 반짝이는 점점이 보이는 돛처럼 새하얬다. 습관적으로 억눌리고 차분해진 얼굴은, 그 별난 가발 아래에서 한 쌍의 촉촉하고 밝은 눈에 의해 여전히 빛나고 있었다. 그 눈은 지난 세월 동안 텔슨 은행 특유의 침착하고 절제된 표정으로 길들이느라 임자가 적잖이 애를 먹었을 법한 것이었다. 그의 두 뺨에는 건강한 혈색이 돌았고, 얼굴에는 주름이 잡혀 있었으나 근심의 흔적은 거의 없었다. 하지만 어쩌면 텔슨 은행의 미혼 신임 사무원들은 주로 남의 근심을 떠안고 살았는지도 모른다. 그리고 어쩌면 헌 옷처럼 남의 근심이란 입고 벗기가 수월한 법인지도 모른다.\n\n초상화를 위해 자세를 잡은 사람과의 닮음을 완성하기라도 하듯, 로리 씨는 깜빡 잠이 들었다. 아침식사가 도착하자 그는 잠에서 깨어났고, 의자를 식탁 쪽으로 당기며 급사에게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 아무 때고 이리로 올지 모르는 젊은 숙녀분을 위해 방을 하나 마련해주게. 그분은 자비스 로리 씨를 찾을 수도 있고, 아니면 그저 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 찾을 수도 있네. 오면 내게 알려주게.\"\n\n\"예, 손님. 런던의 텔슨 은행 말씀이십니까, 손님?\"\n\n\"그렇다네.\"\n\"예, 손님. 저희는 손님네 신사분들이 런던과 빠리 사이를 오가실 때 모시는 영광을 여러번 누렸습니다, 손님. 텔슨 상회는 여행이 무척 잦으시지요, 손님.\"\n\n\"그렇지. 우리는 영국 상회이면서 또한 어엿한 프랑스 상회이기도 하니까.\"\n\n\"예, 손님. 손님께서는 그런 여행에 그리 익숙하신 편은 아니신 듯합니다만, 손님?\"\n\n\"근래에는 그렇네. 우리가, 아니 내가, 프랑스에서 마지막으로 돌아온 게 십오 년 전이니까.\"\n\n\"그러십니까, 손님? 그건 제가 여기 오기 전이로군요, 손님. 저희 사람들이 여기 오기 전입니다, 손님. 그때 조지 호텔은 다른 사람 손에 있었지요, 손님.\"\n\n\"그랬겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 꽤 큰돈을 걸겠습니다, 손님. 텔슨 상회 같은 상회라면 십오 년은커녕 오십 년 전에도 번창하고 있었으리라고요, 손님?\"\n\n\"거기에 세 곱을 해서 백오십 년이라 해도 진실에서 그리 멀지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"그러십니까, 손님!\"\n\n식탁에서 뒷걸음질 치며 입과 두 눈을 동그랗게 만 급사는, 냅킨을 오른팔에서 왼팔로 옮기고는 편안한 자세를 취하더니, 손님이 먹고 마시는 동안 마치 관측소나 망루에서 내려다보듯 서서 그를 살폈다. 예나 지금이나 변함없는 모든 시대 급사들의 태곳적 관습 그대로였다.\n\n로리 씨는 아침식사를 마치자 바닷가로 산책을 나갔다. 좁고 구불구불한 작은 도시 도버는 바닷가로부터 몸을 숨긴 채, 마치 바다 타조처럼 백악 절벽에 머리를 처박고 있었다. 바닷가는 바다와 돌무더기가 거칠게 나뒹구는 황무지였고, 바다는 제멋대로였으며, 그것이 좋아하는 일이란 파괴였다. 바다는 도시를 향해 천둥 치고 절벽을 향해 천둥 치며, 미친 듯이 해안을 무너뜨렸다. 집들 사이의 공기는 생선 냄새가 어찌나 진동했던지, 병든 사람들이 바닷물에 몸을 담그러 내려가듯 병든 물고기들이 그 공기에 몸을 담그러 올라온 것이 아닐까 싶을 정도였다. 항구에서는 고기잡이가 조금 이루어졌고, 밤에는 어슬렁거리며 바다 쪽을 바라보는 일이 잦았으니, 특히 조수가 밀려들어 만조에 가까워지는 때가 그러했다. 도무지 아무 장사도 하지 않는 영세 상인들이 이따금 영문 모를 큰 재산을 모으기도 했는데, 그 동네에서는 아무도 가로등지기를 견뎌내지 못하는 것이 눈에 띄는 점이었다.\n\n날이 오후로 기울고, 한동안 프랑스 해안이 보일 만큼 맑았던 공기가 다시 안개와 수증기로 가득 차오르자, 로리 씨의 생각도 흐려지는 듯했다. 어두워졌을 때, 그는 아침식사를 기다렸던 것처럼 저녁식사를 기다리며 식당 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었고, 그의 마음은 벌겋게 살아 있는 석탄 속을 부지런히 파고, 파고, 또 파고 있었다.\n\n저녁식사 뒤에 마시는 질 좋은 끌라레 한 병은 벌건 석탄 속을 파는 자에게 해될 것이 없으니, 다만 그를 일에서 떼어놓는 경향이 있을 뿐이다. 로리 씨는 오랫동안 빈둥거리다가, 한 병을 다 비운 혈색 좋은 노신사에게서나 찾아볼 수 있는 그토록 흡족한 표정으로 마지막 잔을 막 따른 참이었다. 그때 좁은 거리로 덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리가 올라오더니 여관 안마당으로 우르르 굴러 들어왔다.\n\n그는 입에 대지도 않은 잔을 내려놓았다. \"그분이로군!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n* 그분: 원문은 프랑스어 '마드무아젤'(아가씨)이다.\n몇분도 채 지나지 않아 급사가 들어와 매네뜨 양이 런던에서 도착하여 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 뵙고 싶어한다고 알렸다.\n\n\"이렇게 빨리?\"\n\n매네뜨 양은 오는 길에 요기를 좀 했으므로 지금은 아무것도 필요하지 않으며, 다만 신사분께서 좋으시고 편하시다면 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 즉시 뵙기를 몹시 간절히 바란다고 했다.\n\n텔슨 은행에서 온 신사로서는 무덤덤한 체념의 표정으로 잔을 비우고, 묘하게 작은 아마빛 가발을 귀에 맞추어 매만진 뒤, 매네뜨 양의 방으로 급사를 따라가는 수밖에 없었다. 그곳은 크고 어두운 방으로, 검은 말총으로 장례식처럼 음울하게 꾸며져 있었고 묵직하고 검은 탁자들이 잔뜩 놓여 있었다. 그 탁자들은 거듭거듭 기름칠이 되어, 방 한가운데 탁자에 놓인 키 큰 양초 두 자루가 탁자 면마다 음침하게 비쳐 보일 정도였다. 마치 그 양초들이 검은 마호가니의 깊은 무덤 속에 파묻혀, 거기서 파내기 전에는 빛이라 할 만한 것을 도무지 기대할 수 없는 듯했다.\n\n어둠을 뚫고 보기가 어찌나 어려웠던지, 닳고 닳은 터키산 양탄자 위를 더듬더듬 나아가던 로리 씨는 매네뜨 양이 잠시 어느 옆방에 있으려니 짐작했다. 그러나 키 큰 양초 두 자루를 지나치자, 양초와 난로 사이의 탁자 곁에서 자신을 맞이하려 서 있는 열일곱이 채 안 되어 보이는 젊은 숙녀가 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀는 승마용 외투를 걸치고 짚으로 엮은 여행용 모자를 아직 리본을 쥔 채 손에 들고 있었다. 작고 가냘프고 어여쁜 자태, 풍성한 금발, 의아한 눈빛으로 자신의 눈과 마주친 한 쌍의 푸른 눈, 그리고 (그것이 얼마나 젊고 매끄러운지를 떠올려보면) 곤혹스러움이나 놀라움, 경악, 혹은 그저 또렷하고 고정된 주의력이라고만 하기에는 — 그 네 가지 표정을 모두 담고 있으면서도 — 어느 하나로 단정 짓기 어려운 표정으로 갈라지고 일그러지는 남다른 능력을 지닌 이마, 그의 눈이 이런 것들에 머무는 순간, 우박이 거세게 흩날리고 바다가 높이 일렁이던 어느 추운 날 바로 그 해협을 건너며 자신의 품에 안았던 아이의 모습이 문득 또렷이 그의 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔다. 그 닮은 모습은, 그녀 뒤에 놓인 길쭉한 체경 표면을 스치는 한 줄기 입김처럼 사라져버렸다. 그 거울 틀에는 머리가 없는 것도 여럿이고 모두가 불구인, 흑인 큐피드들의 병원 행렬이 사해의 열매가 담긴 검은 바구니를 여성 흑인 신들에게 바치고 있었다. 그러고서 그는 매네뜨 양에게 격식 있게 인사를 올렸다.\n\n\"자리에 앉으시지요, 어르신.\" 매우 또렷하고 다정한 젊은 목소리였다. 억양이 조금 외국 티가 났으나, 정말 아주 조금이었다.\n\n\"손에 입맞춤을 드립니다, 아가씨.\" 로리 씨는 한 세대 전의 예법으로 다시 격식 있게 인사하며 그렇게 말하고는 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"어제 은행에서 편지를 한 통 받았습니다, 어르신. 어떤 소식이, 아니 발견된 것이 —\"\n\n\"단어는 중요치 않습니다, 아가씨. 어느 쪽이든 무방합니다.\"\n\n\"— 한 번도 뵌 적 없는, 그토록 오래전에 돌아가신 가엾은 제 아버지의 얼마 안 되는 재산에 관한 것이라고요 —\"\n\n로리 씨는 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며, 흑인 큐피드들의 병원 행렬 쪽으로 곤혹스러운 시선을 던졌다. 마치 그 우스꽝스러운 바구니 속에 누군가를 도울 무언가가 들어 있기라도 하다는 듯이!\n\n\"— 그래서 제가 빠리로 가서, 그 일을 위해 빠리로 파견될 만큼 친절하신 은행의 신사 한 분과 의논하는 것이 필요하게 되었다고요.\"\n\"제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"그러리라 짐작하고 있었습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 무릎 굽혀 인사했고(그 시절 젊은 숙녀들은 무릎 굽혀 인사했다), 그가 자신보다 얼마나 더 연륜 있고 현명한지 느끼고 있음을 전하고 싶어하는 어여쁜 마음이 거기 묻어났다. 그는 그녀에게 다시 한번 인사했다.\n\n\"제가 은행에 답장하기를, 어르신, 사정을 잘 알고 또 친절하게도 제게 조언해주시는 분들이 제가 프랑스로 가야 한다고 판단하셨고, 저는 고아이며 함께 가줄 벗도 없으니, 여행 동안 그 훌륭하신 신사분의 보호 아래 제 몸을 맡길 수 있다면 더없이 영광이겠다고 했습니다. 그 신사분은 런던을 떠나셨으나, 그분께 여기서 저를 기다려주십사 청하는 전갈을 뒤따라 보냈으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"기뻤습니다.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"그 임무를 맡게 되어서요. 그것을 수행하게 된다면 더 기쁠 것입니다.\"\n\n\"어르신, 정말 감사드립니다. 진심으로 감사드립니다. 은행에서 말하기를, 그 신사분께서 일의 자세한 내용을 제게 설명해주실 것이며, 저는 그것이 놀라운 성질의 것임을 각오해야 한다고 했습니다. 저는 마음의 준비를 하려 최선을 다했고, 당연히 그것이 무엇인지 알고 싶은 마음이 강하고도 간절합니다.\"\n\n\"당연하지요.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"예 — 저 —\"\n\n잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그는 다시 매끄러운 아마빛 가발을 귀에 맞추어 매만지며 덧붙였다. \"이야기를 꺼내기가 무척 어렵군요.\"\n\n그는 이야기를 꺼내지 못한 채, 우유부단함 속에서 그녀의 시선과 마주쳤다. 그 젊은 이마가 들어올려지며 그 남다른 표정을 지었다. 그러나 그것은 남다른 동시에 어여쁘고 그녀다운 표정이었다. 그녀는 마치 무심결에 지나가는 어떤 그림자를 붙잡거나 멈춰 세우려는 듯 손을 들어올렸다.\n\n\"제게는 전혀 모르는 분이신가요, 어르신?\"\n\n\"그렇지 않겠습니까?\" 로리 씨는 두 손을 펼쳐 바깥으로 내밀며 따져 묻는 듯한 미소를 지었다.\n\n눈썹 사이, 더없이 섬세하고 고운 선을 그린 그 자그마한 여성스러운 코 바로 위에서, 그 표정은 한층 깊어졌다. 그녀는 그때껏 곁에 서 있던 의자에 생각에 잠긴 채 앉았다. 그는 그녀가 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 모습을 지켜보다가, 그녀가 다시 눈을 들어올리는 순간 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아가씨께서 정착하신 나라를 생각하면, 매네뜨 양, 젊은 영국 숙녀로 부르는 것이 가장 좋겠지요?\"\n\n\"그러시지요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"매네뜨 양, 저는 사무를 보는 사람입니다. 처리해야 할 사무상의 임무가 있지요. 그것을 들으실 때, 저를 말하는 기계 이상으로 여기지 말아주십시오. 사실 저는 그것 외에 별다른 것이 아닙니다. 괜찮으시다면, 아가씨, 저희 고객 한 분의 이야기를 들려드리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이야기라고요!\"\n그는 그녀가 되뇐 그 단어를 일부러 잘못 알아들은 척하는 듯, 서둘러 덧붙였다. \"예, 고객 말입니다. 은행 일에서 우리는 보통 거래처를 고객이라 부르지요. 그분은 프랑스 신사였습니다. 학식 있는 신사였고, 큰 학문을 이룬 분이었지요. 의사였습니다.\"\n\n\"보베의 의사는 아니지요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇습니다, 보베의 의사이지요. 아가씨의 아버님이신 매네뜨 씨처럼, 그 신사도 보베 출신이었습니다. 아가씨의 아버님이신 매네뜨 씨처럼, 그 신사도 빠리에서 명망이 높았지요. 저는 거기서 그분을 알게 되는 영광을 누렸습니다. 우리 사이는 업무상의 관계였으나, 막역한 사이였습니다. 그때 저는 우리 프랑스 상회에 있었고, 그렇게 — 아! 이십 년을 있었지요.\"\n\n\"그때라면 — 여쭤봐도 될까요, 언제를 말씀하시는지요, 어르신?\"\n\n\"저는, 아가씨, 이십 년 전의 일을 말하는 것입니다. 그분은 영국 숙녀와 결혼했고, 저는 그 재산 관리인 가운데 한 사람이었습니다. 다른 많은 프랑스 신사들과 프랑스 가문들의 사정처럼, 그분의 재산도 전부 텔슨 은행의 손에 맡겨져 있었지요. 마찬가지로 저는 우리 고객 수십 명을 위해 이런저런 종류의 관리인이거나, 관리인이었습니다. 이것은 한낱 업무상의 관계입니다, 아가씨. 거기에는 우정도, 특별한 관심도, 정 같은 것도 없습니다. 저는 업무를 보는 인생을 살며 이 사람에서 저 사람으로 옮겨다녔으니, 하루의 업무 속에서 이 고객에서 저 고객으로 옮겨다니는 것과 똑같습니다. 요컨대 저는 감정이 없습니다. 저는 한낱 기계일 뿐입니다. 계속하자면 —\"\n\n\"하지만 이건 제 아버지의 이야기로군요, 어르신. 그리고 저는 생각하기 시작했습니다\" — 묘하게 거칠어진 이마가 그를 골똘히 주시하고 있었다 — \"제 어머니가 아버지보다 겨우 두 해를 더 사신 뒤에 제가 고아가 되어 영국으로 옮겨졌을 때, 저를 영국으로 데려오신 분이 바로 어르신이었다고요. 거의 틀림없이 어르신이었어요.\"\n\n로리 씨는 자신의 손을 잡으려고 미덥게 다가온 그 머뭇거리는 작은 손을 잡아, 다소 격식을 차려 입술에 가져다 댔다. 그러고는 젊은 숙녀를 곧장 다시 의자로 이끌어, 왼손으로 의자 등받이를 잡고 오른손으로는 번갈아 턱을 문지르거나 가발을 귀에서 당기거나 하는 말에 손짓을 곁들이며, 그녀가 올려다보는 동안 그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보고 서 있었다.\n\n\"매네뜨 양, 그건 바로 저였습니다. 그리고 조금 전에 제가 감정이 없으며 동료 인간들과 맺은 모든 관계가 한낱 업무상의 관계일 뿐이라고 한 말이 얼마나 진실이었는지 아시게 될 겁니다. 그 뒤로 제가 아가씨를 한 번도 뵌 적 없다는 것을 헤아려보시면 말입니다. 그렇습니다, 그 뒤로 아가씨는 텔슨 상회의 피후견인이었고, 저는 그 뒤로 텔슨 상회의 다른 업무로 분주했지요. 감정이라니요! 저는 그럴 시간도 없고, 그럴 기회도 없습니다. 아가씨, 저는 평생을 거대한 금전의 짜내는 기계를 돌리는 데 바치고 있습니다.\"\n\n자신의 나날의 업무를 이렇게 묘한 말로 설명한 뒤, 로리 씨는 두 손으로 아마빛 가발을 머리에 납작하게 눌렀다(그건 전혀 불필요한 일이었으니, 그 반들반들한 표면은 이미 그보다 더 납작할 수 없었다). 그러고는 이전의 자세로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"여기까지는, 아가씨, (말씀하신 대로) 아가씨의 그리운 아버님 이야기입니다. 이제 다른 점이 나옵니다. 만일 아가씨의 아버님이 그때 돌아가신 게 아니라면 — 놀라지 마십시오! 어찌 그리 흠칫하십니까!\"\n\n그녀는 정말로 흠칫했다. 그리고 두 손으로 그의 손목을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"부디,\" 로리 씨가 달래는 어조로, 의자 등받이에서 왼손을 떼어, 그토록 격하게 떨며 자신을 붙잡은 애원하는 손가락 위에 얹으며 말했다. \"부디 동요를 가라앉히십시오. 업무상의 일입니다. 제가 말씀드리던 대로 —\"\n\n그녀의 표정이 그를 어찌나 당황하게 했던지, 그는 말을 멈추고 갈피를 잃었다가 다시 시작했다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드리던 대로, 만일 매네뜨 씨가 돌아가신 게 아니라면, 만일 그분이 갑자기 소리 없이 자취를 감추셨다면, 만일 그분이 어디론가 끌려가셨다면, 어떤 수단으로도 그분을 추적할 수 없었으되 그 끔찍한 곳이 어디인지 짐작하기가 어렵지 않았다면, 만일 어느 동포 가운데 그분의 적이 있어, 제가 한창때에 가장 대담한 사람들조차 저 바다 건너에서는 속삭임으로도 입에 올리기를 두려워하던 어떤 특권을 행사할 수 있었다면 — 이를테면 누구든 원하는 만큼 오래 감옥의 망각 속에 처넣을 수 있도록 백지 영장을 채워 넣는 특권 말입니다 — 만일 그분의 부인이 왕과 왕비, 궁정과 성직자들에게 그분의 소식을 애원했으되 모두 헛수고였다면, 그렇다면 아가씨 아버님의 사연은 바로 이 불운한 신사, 보베의 의사의 사연이 되었을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"더 말씀해주시기를 간청합니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"그러겠습니다. 그러려는 참입니다. 견디실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"지금 이 순간 어르신께서 저를 두신 이 불확실함 말고는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"침착하게 말씀하시는군요, 그리고 침착하십니다. 좋습니다!\" (그러나 그의 태도는 말만큼 흡족하지 못했다.) \"업무상의 일입니다. 그저 업무상의 일로 여기십시오. 반드시 처리해야 할 업무로요. 자, 만일 이 의사의 부인이, 대단한 용기와 기개를 지닌 숙녀였음에도 불구하고, 그 어린 아이가 태어나기 전에 이 일로 너무도 극심하게 고통받았다면 —\"\n\n\"그 어린 아이는 딸이었지요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"딸이었지요. 업, 업무상의 일입니다. 괴로워 마십시오. 아가씨, 만일 그 가엾은 부인이 어린 아이가 태어나기 전에 너무도 극심하게 고통받은 나머지, 가엾은 그 아이에게 자신이 겪은 고뇌의 그 어떤 부분도 물려주지 않으려고, 아버지가 죽었다고 믿게 키워 그 아이를 지켜주기로 결심했다면 — 아니, 무릎 꿇지 마십시오! 맙소사, 어찌하여 제게 무릎을 꿇으십니까!\"\n\n\"진실을 위해서예요. 오, 친애하고 선하고 자비로우신 어르신, 진실을 위해서예요!\"\n\n\"업, 업무상의 일입니다. 아가씨께서 저를 혼란스럽게 하시는데, 제가 혼란스러우면 어떻게 업무를 처리하겠습니까? 우리 머리를 맑게 합시다. 이를테면 지금 친절히 말씀해주시겠습니까, 구 펜스의 아홉 곱이 얼마인지, 혹은 이십 기니가 몇 실링인지를요. 그러면 무척 힘이 날 것입니다. 아가씨의 마음 상태에 대해 제가 한결 안심할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n이 호소에 곧바로 답하지는 않았으나, 그가 아주 부드럽게 그녀를 일으켜 세웠을 때 그녀는 어찌나 잠잠히 앉아 있었던지, 그리고 그의 손목을 놓지 않던 두 손이 전보다 훨씬 안정되어 있었던지, 그녀는 자비스 로리 씨에게 얼마간 안도감을 전해주었다.\n\n\"바로 그겁니다, 바로 그래요. 용기를 내십시오! 업무입니다! 아가씨 앞에는 업무가 있습니다. 유익한 업무지요. 매네뜨 양, 아가씨의 어머님은 아가씨에게 이런 방도를 택하셨습니다. 그리고 아가씨 아버님을 찾는 헛된 수색을 한 번도 늦추지 않으신 채, 어머님이 돌아가셨을 때 — 제 생각에 가슴이 미어져 돌아가신 듯합니다 — 그분은 두 살 난 아가씨를 남기셨으니, 아버지가 감옥에서 머지않아 가슴이 닳아 없어졌는지, 아니면 거기서 여러 더딘 세월을 거치며 시들어갔는지 알 수 없는 불확실함 속에서 살아가는 어두운 먹구름을 아가씨에게 드리우지 않은 채, 꽃처럼 피어나고 아름답고 행복하게 자라도록 하신 것입니다.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 그는 흘러내리는 금발을 흠모 어린 연민의 눈으로 내려다보았다. 마치 그 머리카락이 이미 잿빛으로 물들었을 수도 있었으리라 마음속으로 그려보기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n\"아가씨의 부모님께서 큰 재산을 갖고 계시지 않았다는 것, 그리고 가지신 것은 어머님과 아가씨께 안전하게 확보되어 있었다는 것은 아실 겁니다. 돈이든 다른 어떤 재산이든 새로이 발견된 것은 없습니다. 그러나 —\"\n\n그는 손목이 한층 꽉 쥐어지는 것을 느끼고는 말을 멈췄다. 그토록 유난히 그의 주의를 끌었고 이제는 미동도 없던 그 이마의 표정이, 한층 깊어져 고통과 공포의 표정이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"그러나 그분이 — 발견되셨습니다. 그분은 살아 계십니다. 크게 변하셨을 가능성이 높습니다. 거의 폐인이 되셨을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 우리는 가장 좋은 쪽으로 바라야겠지요. 어쨌든 살아 계십니다. 아가씨의 아버님은 빠리에 있는 옛 하인의 집으로 모셔졌고, 우리는 그리로 갈 것입니다. 저는 가능하다면 그분의 신원을 확인하기 위해, 아가씨는 그분께 생명과 사랑, 도리와 안식과 위안을 되돌려드리기 위해서요.\"\n\n그녀의 온몸에 전율이 흘렀고, 그것이 그녀에게서 그에게로 옮겨갔다. 그녀는 마치 꿈속에서 말하기라도 하듯, 낮고 또렷하며 경외에 사로잡힌 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"저는 아버지의 유령을 보러 가는군요! 그분이 아니라 그분의 유령일 거예요!\"\n\n로리 씨는 자신의 팔을 붙잡은 두 손을 가만히 문질렀다. \"자, 자, 자! 보십시오, 보세요! 이제 가장 좋은 것과 가장 나쁜 것을 다 아셨습니다. 아가씨는 부당하게 고초를 겪으신 그 가엾은 신사분께로 가는 길에 잘 올라서신 겁니다. 순조로운 바닷길과 순조로운 육로 여행을 거치면, 곧 그분의 정다운 곁에 다다르실 겁니다.\"\n\n그녀는 같은 어조로, 속삭임으로 잦아들며 되뇌었다. \"저는 자유로웠고, 저는 행복했어요, 그런데도 그분의 유령은 한 번도 저를 따라다니지 않았어요!\"\n\n\"한 가지만 더 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 로리 씨는 그녀의 주의를 끌어들이는 유익한 수단으로 그 점을 강조하며 말했다. \"그분은 다른 이름으로 발견되셨습니다. 본래의 이름은 오래 잊혔거나 오래 숨겨져 있었지요. 지금 어느 쪽인지 묻는 것은 부질없는 것만도 못한 일입니다. 그분이 여러 해 동안 잊힌 채 방치되셨는지, 아니면 줄곧 의도적으로 갇혀 계셨는지 알아내려 애쓰는 것도 부질없는 것만도 못한 일입니다. 지금 어떤 탐문이든 하는 것은 부질없는 것만도 못한 일이니, 위험한 일이기 때문입니다. 어디서든 어떤 식으로든 이 문제를 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫고, 그분을 — 적어도 당분간은 — 프랑스 밖으로 모셔내는 편이 낫습니다. 영국인이라 안전한 저조차, 또 프랑스 신용에 그토록 중요한 텔슨 은행조차도 이 문제를 입에 올리는 일은 전부 피합니다. 저는 이 일을 드러내놓고 언급하는 글이라곤 한 조각도 몸에 지니고 다니지 않습니다. 이것은 전적으로 비밀 임무입니다. 제 신임장과 기록, 비망록은 전부 단 한 줄에 담겨 있으니, '되살아나다'라는 말입니다. 무엇이든 뜻할 수 있는 말이지요. 그런데 어찌 된 일입니까! 그녀는 한 마디도 듣고 있지 않습니다! 매네뜨 양!\"\n\n* 되살아나다: 원문 'Recalled to Life'. 소설 1부의 제목이기도 하다.\n완전히 미동도 없이 소리도 없이, 의자에 등을 기대지조차 않은 채, 그녀는 그의 손 아래에서 전혀 의식 없이 앉아 있었다. 두 눈을 뜬 채 그에게 고정하고, 그 마지막 표정을 마치 이마에 새겨지거나 낙인찍힌 듯 띤 채로. 그녀가 그의 팔을 어찌나 꽉 붙잡고 있었던지, 그는 그녀를 다치게 할까 두려워 몸을 떼지 못했다. 그래서 그는 꼼짝하지 않은 채 큰 소리로 도움을 청했다.\n\n거칠어 보이는 한 여자가, 로리 씨가 동요하는 중에도 온통 붉은 빛깔이며 머리가 붉고, 무언가 유난히 몸에 딱 붙는 차림새에, 머리에는 척탄병의 나무 됫박, 그것도 넉넉한 됫박이나 커다란 스틸턴 치즈 같은 더없이 놀라운 보닛을 쓰고 있음을 알아챈 그 여자가, 여관 하인들보다 앞서 방으로 달려 들어왔다. 그러고는 억센 손을 그의 가슴에 들이밀어 그를 가장 가까운 벽으로 나가떨어지게 함으로써, 가엾은 젊은 숙녀에게서 그를 떼어내는 문제를 단숨에 해결했다.\n\n(\"이건 정말이지 남자가 틀림없어!\" 라는 것이, 벽에 부딪치는 것과 동시에 떠오른 로리 씨의 숨 가쁜 생각이었다.)\n\n\"아니, 다들 뭣들 하는 거예요!\" 그 인물이 여관 하인들을 향해 고함쳤다. \"왜 가서 물건들을 가져오지 않고, 거기 서서 나를 빤히 쳐다보기만 하는 거예요? 내가 그렇게 볼만한 구경거리예요? 왜 가서 물건들을 가져오지 않는 거냐고요? 정신 차리는 약과 찬물과 식초를 얼른 가져오지 않으면, 어디 두고 봐요, 정말이지 가만 안 둘 테니!\"\n\n이 회복제들을 가지러 즉시 흩어졌고, 그녀는 환자를 소파에 부드럽게 눕히고 대단한 솜씨와 다정함으로 보살폈다. \"내 보물!\", \"내 새!\"라고 부르며, 금발을 어깨 너머로 펼쳐주는 모습에는 큰 자부심과 정성이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 거기 갈색 옷 양반!\" 그녀가 분개하여 로리 씨를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이 아가씨를 죽을 만큼 겁주지 않고는 할 말을 못 했어요? 좀 봐요, 저 어여쁘고 창백한 얼굴이며 차가운 손을. 그러고도 당신이 은행원이라고요?\"\n\n로리 씨는 답하기가 너무도 어려운 그 질문에 어찌나 당황했던지, 멀찍이 서서 훨씬 기운 빠진 동정과 겸손함으로 지켜볼 수밖에 없었다. 그동안 그 억센 여자는, 여관 하인들이 거기 서서 빤히 쳐다보고만 있으면 입에 담지 않은 무언가를 \"가만 안 두겠다\"는 알 수 없는 협박으로 쫓아낸 뒤, 차근차근 단계를 밟아 자신이 맡은 사람을 회복시켰고, 축 늘어진 그녀의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대도록 어르고 달랬다.\n\n\"이제 괜찮아지겠지요.\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮아진다 해도 거기 갈색 옷 양반 덕은 아니에요. 내 사랑스러운 아가씨!\"\n\n\"바라건대,\" 로리 씨가 다시 한 차례 기운 빠진 동정과 겸손함의 침묵 끝에 말했다. \"당신께서 매네뜨 양과 함께 프랑스로 가주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"그것참 그럴듯한 소리네요!\" 억센 여자가 대꾸했다. \"내가 짠 바닷물을 건너도록 점지되었다면, 신께서 내 운명을 섬에 던져놓으셨을 거라고 생각해요?\"\n\n이 또한 답하기 어려운 질문이었으므로, 자비스 로리 씨는 그것을 곰곰이 생각해보려 물러났다.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Wine-shop\n\n포도주가 가득 담긴 커다란 통 하나가 거리에 떨어져 깨졌다. 그 사고는 통을 수레에서 내리던 중에 일어났다. 통은 굴러떨어지면서 테가 터졌고, 술집 문 바로 앞 돌바닥에 호두 껍데기처럼 산산이 부서진 채 나뒹굴었다.\n\n가까이에 있던 사람들은 모두 하던 일이나 빈둥거림을 멈추고 그 자리로 달려와 포도주를 마셨다. 사방으로 뾰족하게 솟아, 가까이 다가오는 살아 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 절름발이로 만들려고 일부러 깔아놓은 것이 아닌가 싶은 거칠고 들쭉날쭉한 거리의 돌들이 포도주를 가두어 작은 웅덩이들을 만들었다. 그 웅덩이마다 크기에 따라 저마다 떼밀고 부대끼는 무리나 군중이 둘러쌌다. 어떤 남자들은 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 모아 바가지를 만들어 홀짝였고, 또는 자기 어깨 너머로 몸을 숙인 여자들이 포도주가 손가락 사이로 다 흘러내리기 전에 홀짝이도록 도우려 했다. 다른 사람들은, 남자든 여자든, 이가 빠진 작은 질그릇 잔으로, 또는 여자들의 머리에서 벗긴 손수건으로 웅덩이를 적셔, 그것을 짜내 갓난아기의 입에 넣어주었다. 또 어떤 사람들은 흘러내리는 포도주를 막으려고 작은 진흙 둑을 쌓았고, 또 어떤 사람들은 높은 창문에서 내려다보는 구경꾼들의 지시를 받아 새로운 방향으로 흘러가기 시작한 가느다란 포도주 줄기를 끊어내려고 이리저리 내달렸다. 또 어떤 사람들은 흠뻑 젖어 찌꺼기로 물든 통 조각에 매달려, 더 축축하게 포도주에 절은 부스러기들을 핥고 심지어 게걸스러운 입맛으로 우적우적 씹어댔다. 포도주를 흘려보낼 배수로가 없었으니, 포도주가 한 방울 남김없이 거두어졌을 뿐 아니라 진흙까지도 잔뜩 거두어져서, 거리에는 청소부가 다녀간 듯했다 — 그런 기적 같은 존재를 믿을 만한 사람이 그곳에 있었다면 말이다.\n\n이 포도주 놀음이 이어지는 동안, 날카로운 웃음소리와 흥겨워하는 목소리들 — 남자들과 여자들과 아이들의 목소리 — 이 거리에 울려 퍼졌다. 그 놀이에는 거친 구석이 별로 없었고 장난기가 가득했다. 거기에는 어떤 특별한 동료애가 있었으니, 저마다 다른 누군가와 어울리려는 눈에 띄는 성향이 있어서, 특히 운이 좋거나 마음이 가벼운 이들 사이에서는 들뜬 포옹과 건배, 악수, 심지어 손을 맞잡고 열댓 명이 한데 어울려 춤추는 데까지 이어졌다. 포도주가 다 없어지고, 그것이 가장 흥건했던 자리들이 손가락으로 긁혀 석쇠 무늬가 되자, 이 떠들썩한 광경은 시작될 때처럼 갑작스레 멎었다. 톱질하던 장작에 톱을 박아둔 채로 떠났던 남자는 다시 톱질을 시작했다. 자신의 굶주려 곱은 손가락과 발가락의, 또는 제 아이의 손발가락의 아픔을 누그러뜨리려고 뜨거운 잿가루가 담긴 작은 단지를 문간에 놓아두었던 여자는 그리로 돌아갔다. 맨팔에 엉킨 머리, 시체 같은 얼굴을 한 채 지하실에서 겨울 햇빛 속으로 나왔던 남자들은 다시 내려가려고 자리를 떴다. 그리고 햇빛보다도 그곳에 더 어울려 보이는 음울함이 그 광경 위로 내려앉았다.\n\n그 포도주는 붉은 포도주였고, 그것이 쏟아진 빠리의 쌩땅뚜안 변두리, 그 좁은 거리의 바닥을 붉게 물들였다. 그것은 또 많은 손을, 많은 얼굴을, 많은 맨발을, 많은 나막신을 물들였다. 나무를 톱질하던 남자의 손은 장작에 붉은 자국을 남겼고, 아기에게 젖을 물리던 여자의 이마는 다시 머리에 둘러맨 낡은 헝겊의 얼룩으로 물들었다. 통의 널빤지를 게걸스레 핥던 자들은 입가에 호랑이 같은 자국이 묻었고, 그렇게 얼룩진 키 큰 익살꾼 하나는, 길고 더러운 자루 같은 잠자리 모자 속보다 바깥으로 머리를 더 내민 채, 진흙 섞인 포도주 찌꺼기에 손가락을 적셔 벽에다 갈겨썼다 — 피.\n\n그 포도주마저도 거리의 돌바닥에 쏟아지고, 그 얼룩이 그곳 많은 이들 위에 붉게 물들 때가 오게 되어 있었다.\n\n그리고 이제, 한순간의 빛줄기가 쌩땅뚜안의 거룩한 얼굴에서 몰아냈던 구름이 다시 그 위에 내려앉으니, 그 어둠은 무거웠다 — 추위와 더러움과 병과 무지와 궁핍이 그 성스러운 존재를 시중드는 영주들이었다 — 모두가 막강한 권세를 가진 귀족들이었으나, 그중에서도 특히 마지막 것이 그러했다. 방앗간에서 무시무시하게 갈리고 또 갈린 — 그것도 늙은이를 젊게 갈아내는 전설 속의 방앗간에서 갈린 것이 결코 아닌 — 백성의 표본들이 길모퉁이마다 떨고 있었고, 문간마다 들고났으며, 창문마다 내다보았고, 바람에 흔들리는 누더기 옷의 자락마다 펄럭였다. 그들을 갈아내린 그 방앗간은 젊은이를 늙게 갈아내는 방앗간이었다. 아이들은 늙은 얼굴에 무거운 목소리를 지녔다. 그들 위에, 그리고 어른들의 얼굴 위에, 나이의 주름마다 깊이 패고 또 새로이 솟아나는 것은 한숨, 곧 굶주림이었다. 그것은 어디에나 만연했다. 굶주림은 높다란 집집마다 장대와 빨랫줄에 걸린 비참한 옷가지 속에서 비어져 나왔다. 굶주림은 짚과 헝겊과 나무와 종이로 그 옷에 덧대어졌다. 굶주림은 남자가 톱질해 낸 그 얼마 안 되는 장작 부스러기 한 조각 한 조각마다 되풀이되었다. 굶주림은 연기 없는 굴뚝에서 내려다보았고, 쓰레기 더미 속에 먹을 만한 찌꺼기 하나 없는 더러운 거리에서 솟구쳐 올랐다. 굶주림은 빵집 선반의 표어였으니, 변변찮은 재고로 쌓인 질 나쁜 빵의 작은 덩어리 하나하나에 쓰여 있었고, 소시지 가게에서는 죽은 개로 만든 듯한 모든 상품마다 적혀 있었다. 굶주림은 돌아가는 원통 속 군밤들 사이에서 마른 뼈를 달그락거렸다. 굶주림은 마지못해 떨어뜨린 기름 몇 방울에 튀긴 푸석한 감자 조각이 담긴 한 푼짜리 죽 그릇마다 잘게 부스러져 있었다.\n\n그것이 깃든 곳은 모두 그것에 어울리는 것들 천지였다. 좁고 구불구불한 거리 하나가 더러움과 악취로 가득했고, 거기서 또 다른 좁고 구불구불한 거리들이 갈라져 나갔으니, 온통 누더기와 잠자리 모자를 쓴 사람들로 들어찼고, 온통 누더기와 잠자리 모자 냄새가 났으며, 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 병들어 보이는 음울한 기색을 띠고 있었다. 사람들의 쫓기는 듯한 기색 속에는 그래도 막다른 곳에서 돌아서서 맞설 수도 있으리라는 어떤 야수 같은 생각이 남아 있었다. 풀이 죽고 슬금슬금 피해 다니기는 했으나, 그들 가운데 불타는 눈빛이 없지 않았고, 억누른 것 때문에 새하얗게 질려 꼭 다문 입술도 없지 않았으며, 견뎌내거나 휘두를까 곰곰이 생각하는 교수대 밧줄을 닮은 모양으로 찌푸린 이마도 없지 않았다. 가게 간판들은 (가게 수만큼이나 많았는데) 하나같이 궁핍을 음산하게 그려 보였다. 정육점과 돼지고기 가게는 가장 빈약한 고기 부스러기만을 그려놓았고, 빵집은 가장 거칠고 메마른 빵 덩어리를 그려놓았다. 술집에서 포도주를 마시는 모습으로 투박하게 그려진 사람들은 멀건 포도주와 맥주를 변변찮은 양으로 들이켜며 쉰 목소리로 투덜대고, 서로 노려보듯 은밀히 속닥거렸다. 연장과 무기 말고는 그 무엇도 번창하는 모습으로 그려진 것이 없었으나, 칼장수의 칼과 도끼는 날카롭고 번뜩였고, 대장장이의 망치는 묵직했으며, 총포상의 재고는 살벌했다. 진흙과 물이 고인 작은 웅덩이가 무수히 패인, 사람을 절름발이로 만드는 포장도로의 돌들에는 인도가 따로 없이 문간에서 갑작스레 끝나버렸다. 그 대신, 도랑이 거리 한복판으로 흘러내렸으니 — 하기야 흐를 때만 그랬다. 그것은 폭우가 쏟아진 뒤에야 그랬는데, 그럴 때면 갖가지 변덕스러운 발작을 일으키며 집 안으로 흘러들었다. 거리를 가로질러, 넓은 간격을 두고, 투박한 등 하나씩이 밧줄과 도르래에 매달려 있었다. 밤이면 점등부가 이것들을 내려 불을 켜고 다시 끌어올렸는데, 그러면 어슴푸레한 심지들이 이룬 빈약한 숲이 머리 위에서 병든 듯 흔들렸으니, 마치 바다 위에 떠 있는 것 같았다. 사실 그들은 바다 위에 있었고, 배와 선원은 폭풍의 위험에 처해 있었다.\n\n머지않아, 그 지역의 깡마른 허수아비들이 빈둥거림과 굶주림 속에서 점등부를 하도 오래 지켜본 끝에, 그의 방식을 개량해 그 밧줄과 도르래로 사람들을 끌어올려 자기네 처지의 어둠 위에 매달아 보여줄 생각을 떠올릴 때가 오게 되어 있었다. 그러나 그때는 아직 오지 않았다. 프랑스 위로 불어대는 바람은 죄다 허수아비들의 누더기를 헛되이 흔들 뿐이었으니, 깃털 곱고 노래 좋은 새들은 아무런 경고도 알아채지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n그 술집은 모퉁이 가게로, 외양이나 격에서 다른 대부분의 가게보다 나았는데, 술집 주인은 노란 조끼에 초록 반바지를 입고 가게 밖에 서서 쏟아진 포도주를 두고 벌어지는 다툼을 지켜보고 있었다. \"내 알 바 아니지.\" 그는 마지막으로 어깨를 한번 으쓱하며 말했다. \"시장 사람들이 저질러놓은 일이니까. 다른 통을 가져오라고 해.\"\n\n거기서 그의 눈이 우연히 제 익살을 갈겨쓰고 있는 키 큰 익살꾼에게 가닿자, 그는 길 건너로 그를 불렀다.\n\n\"이봐, 가스빠르, 거기서 뭐 하는 거야?\"\n\n사내는 제 무리가 흔히 그러듯, 엄청나게 의미심장한 몸짓으로 자기 익살을 가리켰다. 하지만 그것은 역시 제 무리가 흔히 그러듯, 과녁을 빗나가 완전히 실패하고 말았다.\n\n\"또 뭐야? 자네 미친 사람들 병원에나 들어갈 작정인가?\" 술집 주인은 길을 건너가, 그러려고 한 줌 집어 든 진흙을 그 익살 위에 발라 문질러 지워버리며 말했다. \"왜 공공연한 길거리에 글을 쓰는 거야? 이봐, 말해 보게 — 그런 말을 쓸 다른 데가 없단 말인가?\"\n\n그렇게 따지면서 그는 (어쩌면 우연히, 어쩌면 아니게) 더 깨끗한 손을 익살꾼의 가슴팍에 떨어뜨렸다. 익살꾼은 제 손으로 그 손을 탁 치고는 날렵하게 위로 뛰어올라, 얼룩진 신발 한 짝을 발에서 홱 벗겨 손에 쥐고 내밀며 기묘한 춤추는 자세로 내려섰다. 그런 상황에서 그는, 늑대 같다고까지는 못하더라도 지극히 실리적인 성격의 익살꾼으로 보였다.\n\n\"신어, 신으라고.\" 상대가 말했다. \"포도주는 포도주라고 부르고, 거기서 끝내게.\" 그렇게 충고하고 나서 그는 그 익살꾼의 — 그래봤자 변변찮은 — 옷에다 더러워진 손을 닦았다. 그것도 아주 일부러, 익살꾼 때문에 손을 더럽혔다는 듯이 그랬다. 그러고는 다시 길을 건너 술집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n이 술집 주인은 황소처럼 목이 굵고 군인 같은 인상의 서른 살 사내였는데, 성미가 뜨거운 사람임에 틀림없었으니, 살을 에는 추운 날인데도 외투를 입지 않고 어깨에 둘러메고만 다녔기 때문이다. 셔츠 소매도 걷어 올려서, 갈색 팔이 팔꿈치까지 드러나 있었다. 머리에도 짧고 곱슬거리는 검은 머리카락 말고는 아무것도 쓰지 않았다. 그는 전체적으로 거무스름한 사내로, 눈매가 좋았고 그 눈 사이가 시원스레 넓었다. 대체로 쾌활해 보였으나, 또한 무자비해 보이기도 했다. 분명 강인한 결단과 굳은 목적을 지닌 사내였다. 양옆이 낭떠러지인 좁은 길목을 내달릴 때 마주치고 싶지 않은 사내였으니, 그 무엇도 그를 돌려세우지 못할 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n그의 아내 마담 드파르주는 그가 들어왔을 때 가게 안 계산대 뒤에 앉아 있었다. 마담 드파르주는 남편과 비슷한 나이의 건장한 여자로, 좀처럼 무엇을 바라보는 것 같지 않은 경계하는 눈, 반지를 잔뜩 낀 커다란 손, 흔들림 없는 얼굴, 강한 이목구비, 그리고 더없이 침착한 태도를 지녔다. 마담 드파르주에게는 어떤 됨됨이가 있어서, 그것을 보면 그녀가 자신이 맡아 처리하는 어떤 셈에서든 좀처럼 제 손해 보는 실수를 저지르지 않으리라고 점칠 수 있었다. 마담 드파르주는 추위를 잘 타서 모피를 둘렀고, 머리에는 화사한 숄을 잔뜩 둘러 감았으나, 그래도 커다란 귀고리는 가려지지 않았다. 뜨개질거리가 앞에 놓여 있었지만, 그녀는 그것을 내려놓고 이쑤시개로 이를 쑤시고 있었다. 그렇게 왼손으로 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 받친 채, 마담 드파르주는 그 주인이 들어와도 아무 말 없이 그저 기침을 딱 한 알갱이 했다. 이것은, 이쑤시개 너머로 짙게 그려진 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 치켜올린 것과 더불어, 남편에게 그가 길 건너로 가 있는 동안 들른 새 손님이 있는지 가게 안의 손님들을 한번 둘러보는 게 좋겠다는 뜻을 일러주었다.\n\n이에 술집 주인은 눈동자를 굴려, 마침내 한쪽 구석에 앉아 있는 나이 지긋한 신사와 젊은 아가씨에게 시선을 멈추었다. 다른 손님들도 있었다. 둘은 카드놀이를, 둘은 도미노 놀이를 하고 있었고, 셋은 계산대에 기대서서 얼마 안 되는 포도주를 질질 끌며 마시고 있었다. 계산대 뒤를 지나가면서 그는, 그 나이 지긋한 신사가 젊은 아가씨에게 눈빛으로 \"이 사람이 우리가 찾던 자요\"라고 말하는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"대체 당신은 저기서 무슨 영문으로 그러고 있는 거지?\" 무슈 드파르주는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"당신을 모르겠는데.\"\n\n그러나 그는 두 낯선 사람을 알아채지 못한 척하며, 계산대에서 마시고 있던 세 손님의 삼두체와 이야기를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n\"어떻게 돼가나, 자끄?\" 그 셋 가운데 하나가 무슈 드파르주에게 물었다. \"쏟아진 포도주는 다 들이켜졌나?\"\n\n\"한 방울도 남김없이, 자끄.\" 무슈 드파르주가 대답했다.\n\n이렇게 세례명을 주고받고 나자, 마담 드파르주는 이쑤시개로 이를 쑤시면서 또 기침을 한 알갱이 하고, 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 더 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"이런 가련한 짐승들이 포도주 맛을, 아니 검은 빵과 죽음 말고 다른 어떤 것의 맛을 알게 되는 일은 흔치 않지.\" 셋 가운데 둘째가 무슈 드파르주에게 말했다. \"안 그런가, 자끄?\"\n\n\"그렇지, 자끄.\" 무슈 드파르주가 대꾸했다.\n\n이렇게 두 번째로 세례명을 주고받자, 마담 드파르주는 여전히 더없이 침착하게 이쑤시개를 놀리면서 또 기침을 한 알갱이 하고, 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 더 치켜올렸다.\n\n이제 셋 가운데 마지막 사람이 빈 술잔을 내려놓고 입맛을 다시며 제 할 말을 했다.\n\n\"아! 그러니 더욱 딱하지! 그런 불쌍한 가축들 입에는 늘 쓴맛이 감돌고, 사는 것도 고달프지, 자끄. 내 말이 맞지, 자끄?\"\n\n\"자네 말이 맞네, 자끄.\" 무슈 드파르주가 응답했다.\n\n이렇게 세 번째로 세례명을 주고받는 일이 끝나는 바로 그 순간, 마담 드파르주는 이쑤시개를 내려놓고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채로 자리에서 살짝 부스럭거렸다.\n\n\"가만! 옳지!\" 그녀의 남편이 중얼거렸다. \"여러분 — 내 아내요!\"\n\n세 손님은 세 번 멋을 부려 모자를 벗어 마담 드파르주에게 인사했다. 그녀는 고개를 숙이고 그들에게 재빠른 눈길을 한번 던지며 그 경의를 받아들였다. 그러고는 무심한 듯 술집 안을 한번 둘러보고, 겉보기로 더없이 차분하고 평온한 마음으로 뜨개질거리를 집어 들어 거기에 몰두했다.\n\n\"여러분,\" 그동안 빛나는 눈으로 그녀를 유심히 지켜보고 있던 남편이 말했다. \"안녕히 가시오. 여러분이 보고 싶어하던, 내가 밖에 나가 있을 때 물어보던 그 독신자용으로 꾸민 방은 오 층에 있소. 계단 입구는 여기 바로 왼쪽,\" 그는 손으로 가리켰다. \"내 가게 창문 근처의 작은 안뜰로 통하오. 한데 가만 보니, 그중 한 분은 이미 거기 가본 적이 있어 길을 안내할 수 있겠군. 여러분, 안녕히!\"\n\n그들은 포도주값을 치르고 그곳을 떠났다. 무슈 드파르주의 눈이 뜨개질하는 아내를 살피고 있을 때, 나이 지긋한 신사가 구석에서 다가와 한마디 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 청했다.\n\n\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생.\" 무슈 드파르주가 말하고는, 그와 함께 조용히 문가로 걸어갔다.\n\n그들의 의논은 아주 짧았으나 아주 단호했다. 거의 첫마디에 무슈 드파르주는 흠칫 놀라더니 깊이 귀를 기울였다. 그것은 일 분도 채 이어지지 않았는데, 그는 고개를 끄덕이고 밖으로 나갔다. 그러자 그 신사가 젊은 아가씨에게 손짓했고, 그들도 밖으로 나갔다. 마담 드파르주는 날렵한 손놀림과 흔들림 없는 눈썹으로 뜨개질을 했고, 아무것도 보지 못했다.\n\n이렇게 술집에서 나온 자비스 로리 씨와 마네뜨 양은, 무슈 드파르주가 바로 조금 전에 자기 일행을 안내했던 그 문간에서 그와 합류했다. 그 문은 악취 풍기는 작고 컴컴한 안뜰로 열려 있었고, 수많은 사람들이 사는 거대한 집 더미로 들어가는 공동 출입구였다. 음울하게 타일을 깐 입구에서 음울하게 타일을 깐 계단으로 이어지는 곳에서, 무슈 드파르주는 옛 주인의 딸 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그것은 정중한 행동이었으나, 결코 정중하게 행해지지는 않았다. 몇 초 사이에 그에게 아주 놀라운 변화가 일어났다. 그의 얼굴에는 쾌활함도, 솔직함도 더는 남아 있지 않았고, 그는 어느새 비밀스럽고 성나 있으며 위험한 사내가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"매우 높습니다. 좀 힘들지요. 천천히 시작하는 게 좋습니다.\" 계단을 오르기 시작하면서 무슈 드파르주가 로리 씨에게 엄한 목소리로 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그가 혼자 있소?\" 로리 씨가 속삭였다.\n\n\"혼자라니! 신께서 그를 도우시길, 누가 그와 함께 있을 수 있겠소!\" 상대가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 그는 늘 혼자요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"제 뜻으로?\"\n\n\"제 처지가 그래서요. 그들이 나를 찾아내, 내가 그를 맡아 위험을 무릅쓰고 입을 다물 수 있겠느냐고 물은 뒤에 내가 처음 그를 보았을 때 그랬듯이 — 그때 그랬던 그대로, 지금도 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그가 많이 변했소?\"\n\n\"변했냐고요!\"\n\n술집 주인은 걸음을 멈추고 손으로 벽을 치며 끔찍한 저주를 중얼거렸다. 어떤 직접적인 대답도 그 절반만큼도 강렬하지 못했을 것이다. 로리 씨와 두 동행이 점점 더 높이 올라갈수록, 로리 씨의 마음은 점점 더 무거워졌다.\n\n빠리의 더 오래되고 더 붐비는 구역에 있는 그런 계단은, 거기 딸린 것들까지 합쳐, 지금 봐도 충분히 고약할 것이다. 그러나 그 시절에는 익숙지 않고 단련되지 않은 감각에는 정말이지 끔찍했다. 그 높다란 한 채의 거대한 더러운 둥지 안에 있는 자그마한 거처들 — 다시 말해, 공동 계단으로 통하는 문마다 그 안에 있는 방 하나 또는 방들 — 은 저마다 제 쓰레기 더미를 제 층계참에 쌓아두었고, 그 외에도 다른 쓰레기들을 제 창문 밖으로 내던졌다. 그렇게 생겨난, 걷잡을 수 없고 어찌해볼 도리 없는 부패 덩어리는, 설령 가난과 궁핍이 거기에 그 형체 없는 더러움을 보태지 않았더라도 공기를 오염시켰을 것이다. 이 두 가지 나쁜 원천이 합쳐지니, 거의 견딜 수 없을 지경이었다. 그런 공기를 뚫고, 더러움과 독기로 이루어진 가파르고 어두운 수직 통로를 따라 길이 나 있었다. 자기 마음의 동요에, 그리고 시시각각 더 커지는 젊은 동행의 격동에 못 이겨, 자비스 로리 씨는 두 번이나 멈춰 쉬었다. 이렇게 멈춘 곳은 매번 음울한 쇠창살 앞이었으니, 거기서 아직 썩지 않고 남아 있던 그나마 좋은 공기는 죄다 빠져나가는 듯했고, 온갖 상하고 병든 독기는 죄다 기어드는 듯했다. 녹슨 창살 너머로, 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉킨 이웃 동네가 눈에 들어온다기보다 차라리 맛으로 느껴졌다. 노트르담의 두 거대한 탑 꼭대기보다 가깝거나 낮은 곳에는, 시야가 닿는 범위 안에 건강한 삶이나 건전한 소망을 약속하는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n마침내 계단 꼭대기에 다다라, 그들은 세 번째로 걸음을 멈추었다. 다락방 층에 이르기까지는 아직 더 가파르고 폭이 좁은 위층 계단을 하나 더 올라가야 했다. 늘 조금 앞서 가고, 늘 로리 씨가 선 쪽으로 가던 — 마치 젊은 아가씨에게서 무슨 질문이라도 받을까 두려워하는 듯이 — 술집 주인은, 여기서 몸을 돌려 어깨에 걸친 외투 주머니를 조심스레 더듬어 열쇠 하나를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그러면 문이 잠겨 있는 거요, 친구?\" 로리 씨가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 그렇소.\" 무슈 드파르주가 음울하게 대답했다.\n\n\"그 불행한 신사를 그토록 숨겨둘 필요가 있다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"열쇠를 돌릴 필요가 있다고 생각하오.\" 무슈 드파르주는 그의 귀에 더 바싹 대고 속삭이며 심하게 얼굴을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"왜냐고! 그가 너무 오래 갇혀 지낸 탓에, 문을 열어두면 겁에 질려 — 미쳐 날뛰고 — 제 몸을 갈가리 찢고 — 죽고 — 무슨 해를 입을지 알 수 없기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그럴 수가!\" 로리 씨가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"그럴 수가!\" 드파르주가 씁쓸하게 되뇌었다. \"그렇소. 그런 일이 정말로 가능하고, 그런 다른 일들도 숱하게 가능하며, 가능할 뿐 아니라 실제로 행해지는 — 행해지는, 알겠소! — 저 하늘 아래서 날마다 행해지는, 그런 아름다운 세상에 우리가 살고 있는 거요. 악마 만세. 그만 올라갑시다.\"\n\n이 대화는 너무도 나직한 속삭임으로 오갔기에, 그 가운데 단 한마디도 젊은 아가씨의 귀에 닿지 않았다. 그러나 이때쯤 그녀는 너무도 격한 감정에 떨고 있었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 너무도 깊은 불안을, 무엇보다 너무도 큰 두려움과 공포를 드러내고 있어서, 로리 씨는 안심시키는 말을 한두 마디 건네야겠다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"용기를 내요, 아가씨! 용기를! 일이오! 최악은 곧 지나갈 거요. 그저 저 방문을 지나기만 하면 최악은 끝나오. 그러면 당신이 그에게 가져다줄 온갖 좋은 것이, 온갖 위안이, 온갖 행복이 비로소 시작되는 거요. 여기 우리의 좋은 친구가 그쪽에서 당신을 거들게 합시다. 됐소, 드파르주. 자, 이제. 일이오, 일!\"\n\n그들은 천천히, 조용히 올라갔다. 계단은 짧았고, 그들은 곧 꼭대기에 이르렀다. 거기서 계단이 급하게 꺾여 있던 탓에, 그들은 갑자기 세 남자를 보게 되었으니, 그자들은 어느 문 옆에 머리를 바싹 맞대고 숙인 채, 벽의 어떤 틈이나 구멍을 통해 그 문이 달린 방 안을 골똘히 들여다보고 있었다. 바로 가까이서 들리는 발소리에 이 셋은 돌아서서 몸을 일으켰고, 술집에서 술을 마시던 한 이름의 세 사람임이 드러났다.\n\n\"당신들이 찾아온 게 너무 놀라워 이 사람들을 깜빡했군요.\" 무슈 드파르주가 설명했다. \"물러가게, 착한 친구들. 여기 볼일이 있으니.\"\n\n세 사람은 스쳐 지나가 소리 없이 내려갔다.\n\n그 층에는 다른 문이 없는 듯했고, 그들끼리만 남게 되자 술집 주인이 곧장 이 문으로 향하니, 로리 씨는 약간 화가 나서 그에게 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n\"무슈 마네뜨를 구경거리로 삼는 거요?\"\n\n\"방금 보신 그 방식으로, 가려 뽑은 몇 사람에게만 보여주는 거요.\"\n\n\"그게 잘하는 일이오?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔 잘하는 일이오.\"\n\n\"그 몇 사람이란 누구요? 어떻게 가려 뽑소?\"\n\n\"나와 같은 이름을 지닌 — 자끄가 내 이름이오 — 진짜 사내들로, 이 광경이 도움이 될 만한 자들을 뽑소. 됐소. 당신은 영국 사람이니, 그건 또 다른 문제요. 부디 거기서 잠깐만 계시오.\"\n\n그들을 물러서 있게 하는 주의의 몸짓을 하고서, 그는 몸을 숙여 벽 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다. 이내 다시 머리를 들고는, 문을 두세 번 두드렸다 — 분명 거기서 소리를 내려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었다. 같은 의도로, 그는 열쇠를 문에 서너 번 긁어댄 다음에야 그것을 자물쇠에 어설프게 꽂아 넣고, 있는 힘껏 묵직하게 돌렸다.\n\n문이 그의 손길에 따라 천천히 안쪽으로 열렸고, 그는 방 안을 들여다보며 뭐라고 말했다. 희미한 목소리가 뭐라고 대답했다. 양쪽 다 한 음절 남짓밖에 말하지 않았을 것이다.\n\n그는 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 그들에게 들어오라고 손짓했다. 로리 씨는 딸의 허리를 팔로 단단히 감싸 그녀를 부축했으니, 그녀가 주저앉으려 하는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n\"이-이-일이오, 일!\" 그는 일과는 무관한 물기를 뺨에 번뜩이며 재촉했다. \"들어가요, 들어가!\"\n\n\"무서워요.\" 그녀가 몸을 떨며 대답했다.\n\n\"그게? 무엇이?\"\n\n\"제 말은 그분이요. 제 아버지가요.\"\n\n그녀의 상태에, 그리고 안내자의 손짓에 어쩐지 다급해진 그는, 자기 어깨 위에서 떨리던 그녀의 팔을 자기 목에 두르고, 그녀를 살짝 들어 올려 서둘러 방 안으로 데리고 들어갔다. 그는 문 바로 안쪽에 그녀를 앉히고, 자기에게 매달리는 그녀를 붙들었다.\n\n드파르주는 열쇠를 빼내 문을 닫고, 안쪽에서 잠근 다음, 다시 열쇠를 빼서 손에 쥐었다. 이 모든 것을 그는 차근차근, 그리고 낼 수 있는 한 크고 거친 소리를 내며 해냈다. 마침내 그는 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 방을 가로질러 창문이 있는 데로 갔다. 그는 거기서 멈춰 서서 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n장작 따위를 쟁여두는 곳으로 지어진 그 다락방은 어둑하고 캄캄했다. 지붕창 모양의 그 창문은 사실 지붕에 난 문으로, 그 위에는 거리에서 물건을 끌어올리는 작은 기중기가 달려 있었고, 유리가 없이, 다른 프랑스식 문이 다 그러하듯 가운데가 두 짝으로 닫히게 되어 있었다. 추위를 막으려고 이 문의 한쪽은 단단히 닫혀 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 아주 조금만 열려 있었다. 이런 식으로 들어오는 빛이 워낙 인색해서, 처음 들어와서는 무엇 하나 보기가 어려웠고, 오랜 습관만이 누군가에게 그런 어둠 속에서 정교함을 요하는 일을 해낼 능력을 더디게나마 길러줄 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 종류의 일이 그 다락방에서 행해지고 있었다. 문을 등지고, 술집 주인이 서서 자신을 바라보고 있는 창문 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린 채, 백발의 한 남자가 낮은 의자에 앉아, 앞으로 몸을 숙이고 아주 분주하게, 신발을 만들고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. The Shoemaker\n\n“안녕하시오!” 드파르주 씨가 구두 짓는 일에 깊이 수그러진 흰 머리를 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n머리가 잠시 들렸고, 마치 멀리서 들려오는 듯 아주 희미한 목소리가 그 인사에 응답했다.\n\n“안녕하시오!”\n\n“아직도 일에 열중이시군요?”\n\n긴 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 머리가 또 한번 잠시 들렸고, 목소리가 대답했다. “그렇소ㅡ나는 일하고 있소.” 이번에는 한 쌍의 초췌한 눈이 묻는 이를 바라본 다음에야 얼굴이 다시 떨구어졌다.\n\n그 목소리의 희미함은 가련하고도 두려운 것이었다. 그것은 육체적 쇠약에서 비롯된 희미함이 아니었으나, 감금과 거친 음식이 거기에 한몫한 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그 목소리의 가련한 특징은, 그것이 고독과 오랜 침묵에서 비롯된 희미함이라는 데 있었다. 그것은 오래오래 전에 울렸던 어떤 소리의 마지막 가냘픈 메아리 같았다. 인간 목소리의 생기와 울림을 완전히 잃어버려서, 한때 아름다웠으나 이제는 보잘것없이 옅은 얼룩으로 바래버린 빛깔처럼 감각에 와닿았다. 그것은 너무도 가라앉고 억눌려 있어서 땅속에서 나오는 목소리 같았다. 그것은 희망 없이 길 잃은 어떤 존재를 너무도 잘 드러내고 있어서, 황야에서 외로이 헤매다 지쳐버린 굶주린 나그네라면 죽음을 앞두고 눕기 전에 그런 어조로 고향과 벗들을 떠올렸으리라.\n\n몇 분간 말없는 작업이 이어졌다. 그러더니 초췌한 눈이 다시 위를 향했다. 어떤 관심이나 호기심에서가 아니라, 자신이 알고 있는 유일한 방문객이 서 있던 그 자리가 아직 비어 있지 않다는 것을, 둔하고 기계적인 지각으로 미리 알아챘기 때문이었다.\n\n“여기에 빛을 좀더 들이고 싶소만.” 구두장이에게서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 드파르주가 말했다. “조금 더 견딜 수 있겠소?”\n\n구두장이가 일손을 멈추었다. 무언가에 귀 기울이는 듯한 멍한 표정으로 한쪽 바닥을 보더니, 같은 모양으로 다른 쪽 바닥을 보고, 그런 다음 위쪽의 말하는 이를 올려다보았다.\n\n“뭐라고 했소?”\n\n“빛을 좀더 견딜 수 있겠소?”\n\n“당신이 들인다면 견뎌야겠지.” (두번째 낱말에 더없이 옅은 강세의 그림자를 드리우며.)\n\n반쯤 열려 있던 문이 조금 더 열렸고, 잠시 그 각도로 고정되었다. 넓은 빛줄기가 다락방으로 떨어져, 무릎 위에 미완성의 구두를 올려놓고 일손을 멈춘 일꾼의 모습을 드러냈다. 변변찮은 연장 몇 개와 갖가지 가죽 조각이 그의 발치와 작업대 위에 놓여 있었다. 그는 거칠게 잘려 있으나 그리 길지는 않은 흰 수염을 기르고 있었고, 얼굴은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 두 눈은 유난히 빛났다. 그 얼굴의 움푹함과 여윔 때문에, 아직은 검은 눈썹과 헝클어진 흰 머리칼 아래에서 그 눈은, 실제로는 그렇지 않았더라도 크게 보였을 것이다. 그러나 그 눈은 본래 컸고, 부자연스러울 만큼 크게 보였다. 누렇게 바랜 누더기 셔츠는 목께가 벌어져, 시들고 닳은 그의 몸을 드러냈다. 그와, 그의 낡은 삼베 작업복과, 헐렁한 양말과, 가련한 누더기 옷가지들은, 직사광선과 바깥공기로부터 오래 격리된 탓에 양피지 같은 누런빛으로 한결같이 칙칙하게 바래어, 어느 것이 어느 것인지 분간하기 어려울 정도였다.\n\n그는 눈과 빛 사이에 한 손을 들어 올렸는데, 그 손의 뼈마디까지 투명해 보였다. 그렇게 그는 일손을 멈춘 채 한결같이 멍한 시선으로 앉아 있었다. 그는 자기 앞의 모습을 바라볼 때면 언제나 먼저 자기 옆 이쪽을 내려다보고, 그다음 저쪽을 내려다보았는데, 마치 소리를 장소와 연결 짓는 습관을 잃어버린 듯했다. 그는 말을 할 때면 언제나 먼저 이런 식으로 헤매다가, 말하기를 잊어버리곤 했다.\n\n“오늘 그 구두 한 켤레를 마무리할 작정이오?” 드파르주가 로리 씨에게 앞으로 나오라고 손짓하며 물었다.\n\n“뭐라고 했소?”\n\n“오늘 그 구두 한 켤레를 마무리할 작정이냐고 했소.”\n\n“작정이라고는 못하겠소. 그러려나 보오. 모르겠소.”\n\n그러나 그 물음이 그에게 자기 일을 일깨워주었고, 그는 다시 그 일 위로 수그러들었다.\n\n로리 씨가 딸을 문가에 남겨둔 채 말없이 앞으로 나왔다. 그가 일이 분쯤 드파르주 곁에 서 있자, 구두장이가 올려다보았다. 그는 또다른 모습을 보고도 놀라는 기색이 없었으나, 그것을 바라보는 동안 한 손의 떨리는 손가락들이 그의 입술께로 향했고(그의 입술과 손톱은 똑같이 창백한 납빛이었다), 그런 다음 그 손은 일감 위로 떨어졌고, 그는 또 한번 구두 위로 수그러들었다. 그 표정과 동작은 단지 한순간을 차지했을 뿐이었다.\n\n“보다시피 손님이 오셨소.” 드파르주 씨가 말했다.\n\n“뭐라고 했소?”\n\n“여기 손님이 오셨소.”\n\n구두장이는 아까처럼 올려다보았으나, 일감에서 손을 떼지는 않았다.\n\n“이보시오!” 드파르주가 말했다. “여기 이 신사분은 잘 만든 구두를 보면 알아보는 분이오. 당신이 짓고 있는 그 구두를 좀 보여드리시오. 신사분, 그것을 받으시오.”\n\n로리 씨가 그것을 손에 들었다.\n\n“이게 어떤 종류의 구두인지, 또 만든 사람의 이름을 신사분께 말씀드리시오.”\n\n구두장이가 대답하기까지 평소보다 긴 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n“당신이 내게 뭘 물었는지 잊었소. 뭐라고 했소?”\n\n“신사분께 알려드리게, 이게 어떤 종류의 구두인지 설명해줄 수 없겠느냐고 했소.”\n\n“이건 부인용 구두요. 젊은 부인용 산책화요. 요즘 유행이지. 나는 그 유행을 본 적은 없소. 본을 손에 쥐어본 적은 있소.” 그는 스쳐 지나는 작은 자랑스러움이 깃든 눈길로 그 구두를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n“그럼 만든 사람의 이름은?” 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n이제 쥐고 있을 일감이 없어지자, 그는 오른손 마디를 왼손 오목한 곳에 놓았다가, 다시 왼손 마디를 오른손 오목한 곳에 놓았다가, 그런 다음 수염 난 턱을 한 손으로 쓸었고, 그렇게 한순간의 쉼도 없이 규칙적으로 동작을 바꾸었다. 말을 마치고 나면 늘 빠져들곤 하는 그 정신의 방황으로부터 그를 불러내는 일은, 몹시 쇠약한 사람을 혼절 상태에서 깨우거나, 무언가를 털어놓게 하려는 바람에서 빠르게 죽어가는 사람의 영혼을 붙잡아두려 애쓰는 것과 같았다.\n\n“내 이름을 물었소?”\n\n“그렇소, 분명히.”\n\n“북쪽 탑, 백오번.”\n\n“그게 다요?”\n\n“북쪽 탑, 백오번.”\n\n한숨도 아니고 신음도 아닌 지친 소리를 내며 그는 다시 일감 위로 수그러들었고, 마침내 다시 침묵이 깨졌다.\n\n“직업이 구두장이는 아니지요?” 로리 씨가 그를 한결같이 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그의 초췌한 눈은 그 물음을 떠넘기려는 듯 드파르주 쪽으로 돌아갔다. 그러나 그쪽에서 아무 도움도 오지 않자, 그 눈은 바닥을 더듬은 뒤 다시 묻는 이에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n“내 직업이 구두장이가 아니라고? 그렇소, 나는 직업이 구두장이가 아니었소. 나는ㅡ나는 여기서 그것을 배웠소. 혼자 익혔지. 나는 허락을 청해서ㅡ”\n\n그는, 그동안 내내 두 손으로 그 절도 있는 동작을 되풀이하면서, 몇 분 동안이나 다시 정신을 놓아버렸다. 마침내 그의 눈이 천천히, 그것이 떠나갔던 얼굴로 되돌아왔다. 그 얼굴에 눈길이 머물자, 그는 흠칫하더니, 방금 깨어난 잠든 이가 지난밤의 이야기로 되돌아가듯 말을 이었다.\n\n“혼자 익히도록 허락해달라고 청했고, 오랜 시간이 지난 뒤 무척 어렵게 그 허락을 얻어, 그 뒤로 줄곧 구두를 지어왔소.”\n\n빼앗겼던 구두를 받으려고 손을 내밀 때, 로리 씨가 여전히 그의 얼굴을 한결같이 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“마네뜨 씨, 나에 대해 아무것도 기억나는 게 없소?”\n\n구두가 바닥으로 떨어졌고, 그는 묻는 이를 뚫어지게 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n“마네뜨 씨.” 로리 씨가 드파르주의 팔에 손을 얹었다. “이 사람에 대해서는 아무것도 기억나는 게 없소? 이 사람을 보시오. 나를 보시오. 마네뜨 씨, 당신 마음속에 떠오르는 옛 은행가도, 옛 사업도, 옛 하인도, 옛 시절도 없단 말이오?”\n\n여러 해를 갇혀 지낸 그가 로리 씨와 드파르주를 번갈아 뚫어지게 바라보며 앉아 있는 동안, 이마 한가운데에 오래전 지워졌던, 활발하고 골똘했던 지성의 어떤 흔적들이, 그에게 드리워졌던 검은 안개를 뚫고 차츰 솟아올랐다. 그것들은 다시 구름에 가려졌고, 더 희미해졌으며, 사라졌다. 그러나 그것들은 거기 있었던 것이다. 그리고 그 표정은, 벽을 따라 그를 볼 수 있는 지점까지 살금살금 다가가, 이제 거기 서서 그를 바라보고 있는 젊은 여인의 고운 얼굴 위에 똑같이 되풀이되었다. 그녀의 두 손은 처음에는, 그를 막고 그 모습을 가려 보이지 않게 하려는 것까지는 아니더라도, 겁에 질린 연민에서 그저 들어 올려졌을 뿐이었으나, 이제는 그 유령 같은 얼굴을 자신의 따뜻하고 젊은 가슴에 끌어안아 다시 생명과 희망으로 사랑해 되살리고 싶은 간절함에 떨면서 그를 향해 뻗어 있었다. 그 표정이 그녀의 고운 젊은 얼굴 위에 (더 강렬한 선으로이긴 했으나) 너무도 똑같이 되풀이되어서, 마치 그것이 움직이는 빛처럼 그에게서 그녀에게로 옮겨간 듯이 보였다.\n\n그 대신 어둠이 그에게 내려앉았다. 그는 점점 덜 주의 깊게 두 사람을 바라보았고, 그의 눈은 침울한 멍함 속에서 바닥을 더듬다가 옛 방식대로 주위를 둘러보았다. 마침내 깊고 긴 한숨과 함께 그는 구두를 집어 들고 다시 일을 시작했다.\n\n“그를 알아보셨소, 신사분?” 드파르주가 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n“그렇소, 한순간이지만. 처음엔 가망이 전혀 없다고 여겼는데, 한순간이나마 한때 그토록 잘 알았던 얼굴을 의심할 여지 없이 보았소. 쉿! 좀더 뒤로 물러납시다. 쉿!”\n\n그녀는 다락방의 벽에서 떨어져, 그가 앉아 있는 작업대 아주 가까이까지 다가가 있었다. 일감 위로 수그린 그를, 손을 뻗으면 닿을 만한 곳까지 다가온 그 모습을 그가 전혀 알아채지 못한다는 데에는 무언가 두려운 것이 있었다.\n\n한마디 말도 오가지 않았고, 어떤 소리도 나지 않았다. 그녀는 망령처럼 그의 곁에 서 있었고, 그는 일감 위로 수그러들었다.\n\n마침내, 그가 손에 든 도구를 구두장이 칼로 바꾸어야 할 일이 생겼다. 칼은 그녀가 서 있는 쪽이 아닌 반대편에 놓여 있었다. 그가 그것을 집어 들고 다시 일하려고 수그릴 때, 그의 눈에 그녀의 치맛자락이 들어왔다. 그가 눈을 들어 그녀의 얼굴을 보았다. 두 구경꾼은 앞으로 달려 나갔으나, 그녀가 손짓 하나로 그들을 멈춰 세웠다. 그녀는 그가 칼로 자신을 내리칠까봐 두려워하지 않았다. 그들은 두려워했지만 말이다.\n\n그는 두려운 눈빛으로 그녀를 응시했고, 잠시 후 그의 입술이 무슨 말을 지어내기 시작했으나, 거기서는 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않았다. 가쁘고 힘겨운 숨결 사이의 멈춤 동안, 차츰 그가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다.\n\n“이게 어찌 된 일인가?”\n\n눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 가운데, 그녀는 두 손을 입술에 대고 그를 향해 입맞춤을 보냈다. 그런 다음 마치 그 부서진 머리를 거기에 누이는 듯 그 두 손을 자기 가슴에 모았다.\n“당신은 간수의 딸이 아니지?”\n\n그녀는 “아니에요” 하고 한숨지었다.\n\n“당신은 누구지?”\n\n자기 목소리의 어조를 아직 믿지 못한 채, 그녀는 그의 곁 작업대에 앉았다. 그가 움찔 물러섰으나, 그녀가 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다. 그녀가 그렇게 하자 기이한 전율이 그를 덮쳐 그의 몸을 눈에 띄게 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 그녀를 응시한 채 앉아서 칼을 가만히 내려놓았다.\n\n긴 곱슬머리로 늘어뜨린 그녀의 금빛 머리칼이 황급히 옆으로 밀려 목 위로 흘러내려 있었다. 그가 조금씩 조금씩 손을 내밀어 그것을 집어 들고 바라보았다. 그러던 도중에 그는 정신을 놓았고, 또 한번 깊은 한숨과 함께 구두 짓는 일에 빠져들었다.\n\n그러나 오래가지는 않았다. 그녀가 그의 팔을 놓아주고 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다. 정말 거기 있는 것이 맞는지 확인하려는 듯 두세 번 미심쩍게 그것을 바라본 뒤, 그는 일감을 내려놓고 손을 목으로 가져가, 접힌 헝겊 조각이 달린 거뭇한 끈 하나를 풀어냈다. 그는 그것을 무릎 위에서 조심스레 펼쳤고, 그 안에는 아주 적은 양의 머리카락이 들어 있었다. 길고 금빛인 한두 올의 머리카락에 지나지 않았는데, 그가 언젠가 옛날에 손가락에 감아 두었던 것이었다.\n\n그는 다시 그녀의 머리카락을 손에 쥐고 가까이서 들여다보았다. “똑같아. 어떻게 이럴 수가! 그게 언제였더라! 어떻게 된 거였더라!”\n\n골똘한 표정이 그의 이마로 되돌아오면서, 그는 그것이 그녀의 이마에도 있다는 것을 의식하는 듯했다. 그는 그녀를 빛 쪽으로 온전히 돌려세우고 바라보았다.\n\n“그애는 내가 불려 나가던 그날 밤 내 어깨에 머리를 기댔지ㅡ나는 두렵지 않았는데 그애는 내가 가는 것을 두려워했어ㅡ그리고 내가 북쪽 탑으로 끌려갔을 때, 사람들이 내 소매에서 이것을 찾아냈지. ‘이것을 내게 남겨주시겠소? 이것이 내 몸이 달아나는 데는 결코 도움이 못 되겠지만, 영혼이 달아나는 데는 도움이 될지 모르오.’ 그게 내가 한 말이었어. 나는 그 말을 아주 잘 기억하고 있어.”\n\n그는 그 말을 입 밖에 내기 전에 입술로 여러 번 그것을 빚어냈다. 그러나 마침내 그것에 들어맞는 말을 찾아냈을 때, 그 말은 비록 느리게나마 조리 있게 그에게서 흘러나왔다.\n\n“이게 어찌 된 일인가?ㅡ당신이었나?”\n\n그가 무시무시할 만큼 갑작스레 그녀를 향해 돌아서자, 또 한번 두 구경꾼이 흠칫했다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 손아귀 안에서 미동도 없이 앉은 채, 낮은 목소리로 이렇게만 말했다. “선량하신 분들, 간청합니다, 우리에게 가까이 오지 마세요, 말씀하지 마세요, 움직이지 마세요!”\n\n“가만!” 그가 외쳤다. “저 목소리는 누구의 것이었지?”\n\n이 외침을 내뱉으면서 그의 손이 그녀를 놓고 그의 흰 머리칼로 올라가, 광란하듯 그것을 쥐어뜯었다. 그것은, 구두 짓는 일 말고는 그에게서 모든 것이 사라지듯, 사그라들었고, 그는 그 작은 꾸러미를 다시 접어 가슴속에 간직하려 했다. 그러나 그는 여전히 그녀를 바라보며 침울하게 고개를 저었다.\n\n“아니, 아니, 아니야. 당신은 너무 젊고, 너무 한창때야. 그럴 리가 없어. 이 죄수가 어떤 꼴인지 보라고. 이건 그애가 알던 손이 아니고, 이건 그애가 알던 얼굴이 아니고, 이건 그애가 한 번도 들어본 적 없는 목소리야. 아니, 아니야. 그애는ㅡ그리고 그는ㅡ북쪽 탑의 더딘 세월 이전이지ㅡ아득한 옛날이야. 당신 이름이 뭐요, 나의 다정한 천사여?”\n\n부드러워진 그의 어조와 태도를 반기며, 그의 딸이 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 호소하는 두 손을 그의 가슴에 얹었다.\n\n“오, 아버지, 다른 때에 제 이름과, 제 어머니가 누구였는지, 제 아버지가 누구인지, 그리고 제가 그분들의 고되고도 고된 사연을 어떻게 한 번도 몰랐는지 아시게 될 거예요. 하지만 지금은 말씀드릴 수 없고, 여기서는 말씀드릴 수 없어요. 여기서 지금 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 부디 저를 어루만지고 축복해주십사 비는 것뿐이에요. 입맞춰주세요, 입맞춰주세요! 오, 사랑하는 분, 사랑하는 분!”\n\n그의 차갑고 흰 머리가 그녀의 빛나는 머리칼과 뒤섞였고, 그 머리칼은 마치 그에게 비추는 자유의 빛이기라도 한 듯 그것을 따뜻하게 데우고 환히 밝혔다.\n\n“제 목소리에서ㅡ정말 그런지는 모르겠지만, 그러기를 바라요ㅡ제 목소리에서, 한때 당신의 귓가에 감미로운 음악이었던 어떤 목소리와 닮은 데가 들리거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요! 제 머리칼을 어루만지다가, 당신이 젊고 자유롭던 시절에 당신의 가슴에 기대었던 사랑스러운 머리를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 만져지거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요! 제가 우리 앞에 놓인 보금자리에 대해, 제가 온 의무와 온 충실한 보살핌으로 당신께 진실할 그 보금자리에 대해 넌지시 말씀드릴 때, 당신의 가련한 마음이 시들어가는 동안 오래도록 황폐했던 보금자리의 기억을 제가 되살리거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!”\n\n그녀가 그의 목을 더 가까이 끌어안고, 어린아이처럼 그를 자기 가슴에 안고 어르었다.\n\n“가장 사랑하는 분, 당신의 고통은 끝났고, 제가 당신을 그 고통에서 데려가려고 여기 왔으며, 우리가 평화와 안식을 누리러 영국으로 간다고 제가 말씀드릴 때, 만일 제가 당신께 헛되이 버려진 당신의 쓸모 있던 삶을, 그리고 당신께 그토록 모질었던 우리 조국 프랑스를 떠올리게 하거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요! 그리고 제가 제 이름과, 살아 계신 제 아버지와, 돌아가신 제 어머니에 대해 말씀드릴 때, 제가 존경하는 아버지께 무릎 꿇고, 그분을 위해 온종일 애쓰지도 못하고 밤새 깨어 울지도 못했던 일에 대해 용서를 빌어야 한다는 것을 당신이 아시게 되거든ㅡ제 가련한 어머니의 사랑이 그분의 고통을 제게서 숨겼던 탓이었지만요ㅡ그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요! 그렇거든 그분을 위해, 그리고 저를 위해 우세요! 선량하신 분들, 하느님께 감사드려요! 그분의 거룩한 눈물이 제 얼굴 위에 느껴지고, 그분의 흐느낌이 제 가슴을 두드려요. 오, 보세요! 우리를 위해 하느님께 감사드려요, 하느님께 감사드려요!”\n\n그가 그녀의 품에 무너져 내렸고, 그의 얼굴이 그녀의 가슴 위로 떨구어졌다. 그것은 너무도 가슴 저미는 광경이었으나, 그에 앞서 있었던 엄청난 부당함과 고통 때문에 또한 너무도 끔찍한 광경이어서, 두 구경꾼은 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n다락방의 고요가 오래도록 흐트러지지 않고, 그의 들먹이던 가슴과 떨리던 몸이 오래도록 가라앉아ㅡ삶이라는 폭풍이 마침내 잠잠해질 그 안식과 침묵을 인류에게 보여주는 표상인, 모든 폭풍 뒤에 반드시 찾아오는 그 고요에 자리를 내준 뒤에ㅡ그들이 앞으로 나와 아버지와 딸을 바닥에서 일으켜 세우려 했다. 그는 차츰 바닥으로 미끄러져 내려, 기진맥진한 채 무기력하게 누워 있었다. 그녀는 그의 머리가 자기 팔 위에 누이도록 그와 함께 웅크렸고, 그를 덮어 드리운 그녀의 머리칼이 그를 빛으로부터 가려주고 있었다.\n\n“만일 그분을 깨우지 않고서,” 코를 거듭 푼 뒤 그들 위로 수그린 로리 씨를 향해 손을 들어 올리며 그녀가 말했다. “당장 빠리를 떠날 수 있도록 모든 채비를 갖춰서, 바로 이 문에서부터 그분을 모셔 갈 수 있다면ㅡ”\n\n“하지만, 생각해보시오. 그분이 여행을 견딜 만하겠소?” 로리 씨가 물었다.\n\n“그분에게 그토록 끔찍한 이 도시에 머무는 것보다는, 그편이 더 견딜 만하리라 생각해요.”\n\n“맞는 말씀이오.” 무릎을 꿇고 지켜보며 듣고 있던 드파르주가 말했다. “그뿐만이 아니오. 마네뜨 씨는 모든 면에서 프랑스를 떠나는 게 가장 좋소. 자, 내가 마차와 역마를 빌려 올까요?”\n\n“그건 사무적인 일이오.” 로리 씨가 지체 없이 자신의 빈틈없는 태도를 되찾으며 말했다. “사무를 처리해야 한다면, 내가 처리하는 편이 낫겠소.”\n\n“그럼 부디,” 마네뜨 양이 청했다. “우리를 여기 남겨두고 가주세요. 그분이 얼마나 차분해지셨는지 보세요. 이제 저와 함께 그분을 두고 가셔도 두려워하실 일은 없어요. 어찌 두려워하시겠어요? 방해받지 않도록 문을 잠가주시면, 돌아오셨을 때 떠날 때와 다름없이 평온한 그분을 보시게 되리라는 걸 의심하지 않아요. 어떻든 두 분이 돌아오실 때까지 제가 그분을 보살피겠고, 그런 다음 곧장 그분을 모셔 가겠어요.”\n\n로리 씨도 드파르주도 이런 방식이 다소 내키지 않았고, 둘 중 하나는 남는 편을 바랐다. 그러나 마차와 말뿐 아니라 여행 증명서도 챙겨야 했고, 하루가 저물어가서 시간이 촉박했으므로, 결국 그들은 처리해야 할 일을 황급히 나누어 맡고, 그것을 처리하러 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n이윽고 어둠이 닫혀 들자, 딸은 아버지 곁 딱딱한 바닥에 머리를 누이고 그를 지켜보았다. 어둠은 점점 더 짙어졌고, 벽 틈새로 불빛이 어른거릴 때까지 두 사람은 가만히 누워 있었다.\n\n로리 씨와 드파르주 씨는 여행 채비를 모두 갖추고서, 여행용 외투와 덮개 외에도 빵과 고기, 포도주, 그리고 뜨거운 커피를 가져왔다. 드파르주 씨는 이 먹을거리와 자신이 들고 온 등불을 구두장이의 작업대 위에 올려놓았고(다락방에는 짚자리 침상 말고는 아무것도 없었다), 그와 로리 씨가 갇혀 지냈던 이를 깨워 일으켜 세웠다.\n\n겁에 질린 멍한 경이로 가득한 그의 얼굴에서, 어떤 인간의 지력으로도 그의 마음속 신비를 읽어낼 수는 없었을 것이다. 그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 아는지, 그들이 자기에게 한 말을 기억하는지, 자신이 자유로워졌다는 것을 아는지, 그 어떤 명민함으로도 풀 수 없는 물음이었다. 그들이 그에게 말을 걸어보았다. 그러나 그가 너무도 혼란스러워하고 대답이 너무도 더디어서, 그들은 그의 어리둥절함에 겁이 나, 당분간 그를 더 건드리지 않기로 뜻을 모았다. 그에게는 이따금 두 손으로 자기 머리를 부여잡는, 이전에는 보이지 않던 거칠고 넋 나간 태도가 있었다. 그러면서도 그는 딸의 목소리가 들려오는 그 소리 자체에서 얼마간 기쁨을 느꼈고, 그녀가 말할 때면 어김없이 그쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n강압 아래 복종하는 데 오래 길든 이의 순순한 태도로, 그는 그들이 먹고 마시라고 준 것을 먹고 마셨으며, 그들이 입으라고 준 외투와 그밖의 덮개를 걸쳤다. 그는 딸이 자기 팔에 팔짱을 끼는 데 선뜻 응했고, 그녀의 손을 자신의 두 손에 쥐고ㅡ놓지 않았다.\n\n그들은 내려가기 시작했다. 드파르주 씨가 등불을 들고 앞장섰고, 로리 씨가 그 작은 행렬의 맨 뒤를 맡았다. 길고 큰 계단을 몇 걸음 채 내려가지 않아 그가 멈춰 서서, 천장을 응시하고 벽을 둘러보았다.\n\n“이곳을 기억하세요, 아버지? 여기 올라오던 것을 기억하세요?”\n\n“뭐라고 했지?”\n\n그러나 그녀가 그 물음을 되풀이하기도 전에, 그는 마치 그녀가 되풀이하기라도 한 듯 중얼거리며 대답했다.\n\n“기억이라고? 아니, 기억나지 않아. 너무도 오래전 일이야.”\n\n그가 감옥에서 그 집으로 끌려왔던 일을 전혀 기억하지 못한다는 것이 그들에게 분명해졌다. 그들은 그가 “북쪽 탑, 백오번” 하고 중얼거리는 것을 들었다. 그리고 그가 주위를 둘러볼 때, 그것은 분명 오래도록 그를 에워쌌던 견고한 요새의 벽을 찾는 것이었다. 안마당에 이르자 그는 도개교를 예상하는 듯 본능적으로 걸음걸이를 바꾸었다. 그리고 도개교가 없고, 트인 거리에 마차가 기다리고 있는 것을 보자, 그는 딸의 손을 놓고 다시 자기 머리를 부여잡았다.\n\n문 둘레에 사람은 모여 있지 않았다. 그 숱한 창문 어디에서도 사람을 알아볼 수 없었고, 거리에는 우연히 지나가는 행인조차 없었다. 그곳에는 부자연스러운 침묵과 적막이 감돌았다. 단 하나의 인영만 보였는데, 그것은 드파르주 부인이었다. 그녀는 문기둥에 기대어 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 아무것도 보지 않았다.\n\n죄수가 마차에 올랐고 딸이 그를 따라 올랐을 때, 발판에 올라서던 로리 씨의 발이 그 자리에 붙들렸으니, 그가 비참하게 자신의 구두 짓는 연장과 미완성의 구두를 찾았기 때문이었다. 드파르주 부인이 즉시 남편에게 자기가 가져오겠노라 외치고는, 뜨개질을 하며 등불 빛을 벗어나 안마당을 가로질러 갔다. 그녀는 그것들을 재빨리 들고 내려와 안으로 건네주었다.ㅡ그러고는 곧바로 문기둥에 기대어 뜨개질을 하며, 아무것도 보지 않았다.\n\n드파르주가 마부석에 올라 “관문으로!”라고 일렀다. 마부가 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 그들은 머리 위에서 어슴푸레 흔들리는 등불들 아래로 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다.\n\n머리 위에서 흔들리는 등불들 아래로ㅡ나은 거리에서는 점점 밝게 흔들리고, 못한 거리에서는 점점 어둑하게 흔들리는 그 등불들 아래로ㅡ불 밝힌 상점들과 흥겨운 군중과 환히 빛나는 커피점과 극장 문들을 지나, 도시의 어느 성문 가운데 한 곳에 이르렀다. 그곳 위병소에는 등롱을 든 병사들이 있었다. “증명서요, 여행자들!” “자, 이걸 보시오, 장교 나리.” 드파르주가 마차에서 내려, 그를 점잖게 한쪽으로 데리고 가며 말했다. “이건 안에 계신, 흰 머리의 신사분의 증명서요. 이분과 함께 그분이ㅡ에서 내게 맡겨진 것이오.” 그가 목소리를 낮추자, 군대의 등롱들 사이에 동요가 일었고, 그중 하나가 제복 입은 팔에 들려 마차 안으로 들어갔으며, 그 팔에 딸린 두 눈이 흰 머리의 신사분을 바라보았으니, 그것은 여느 낮이나 여느 밤의 눈길이 아니었다. “좋소. 출발하시오!” 제복 쪽에서 말했다. “안녕히!” 드파르주 쪽에서 말했다. 그리하여, 점점 더 어슴푸레하게 머리 위에서 흔들리는 등불들의 짤막한 숲을 지나, 별들의 거대한 숲 아래로 나아갔다.\n\n미동도 없는 영원한 빛들의 그 아치 아래에서ㅡ그중 어떤 것들은 이 작은 지구로부터 너무도 멀어서, 학자들은 그 빛이 무언가가 고통받거나 행해지는 공간의 한 점으로서 이 지구를 아직 발견하기나 했는지조차 의심스럽다고 우리에게 말한다ㅡ밤의 그림자들은 넓고 검었다. 동이 트기까지 춥고 불안한 그 시간 내내, 그것들은 또 한번 자비스 로리 씨의 귓가에 속삭였다. 파묻혀 있다가 파헤쳐진 그 사람을 맞은편에 두고 앉아, 그에게서 어떤 미묘한 능력들이 영영 사라져버렸으며 또 어떤 능력들이 회복될 수 있을지 헤아리는 그에게, 그 오래된 물음을.\n\n“되살아나기를 바라시오?”\n\n그리고 그 오래된 대답을.\n\n“모르겠소.”\n\n# Book the Second--the Golden Thread CHAPTER I. Five Years Later\n\n템플바 근처의 텔슨 은행은 1780년에도 이미 구식 건물이었다. 무척 비좁고, 무척 어둡고, 무척 흉하고, 무척 불편했다. 게다가 도덕적인 면에서도 구식이었으니, 그 은행의 동업자들은 그 비좁음을 자랑스러워했고, 그 어두움을 자랑스러워했고, 그 흉함을 자랑스러워했고, 그 불편함을 자랑스러워했다. 그들은 그런 점에서 자기네가 두드러진다는 것을 자랑하기까지 했으며, 만약 그것이 덜 거슬렸다면 덜 존경받았으리라는 명백한 확신에 불타올랐다. 이것은 수동적인 신념이 아니라 그들이 더 편리한 영업장들을 향해 번뜩이는 능동적인 무기였다. 텔슨은 (그들 말로는) 팔꿈치를 펼 공간을 바라지 않았고, 텔슨은 빛을 바라지 않았고, 텔슨은 장식을 바라지 않았다. 노우크스 상회는 그럴지 몰라도, 스누크스 형제 상회는 그럴지 몰라도, 텔슨은, 천만다행으로 — !\n\n이 동업자들 가운데 누구라도 텔슨을 다시 짓는 문제를 두고서라면 제 아들을 상속에서 폐했을 것이다. 이 점에서 그 은행은 이 나라와 다를 바가 없었으니, 이 나라 역시 오래도록 몹시 거슬렸으나 그저 그래서 더욱 존경받기만 하던 법과 관습을 개선하자고 제안하는 아들들을 매우 자주 상속에서 폐하곤 했던 것이다.\n\n이렇게 해서 텔슨은 불편함의 의기양양한 완성품이 되어 있었다. 목구멍에서 가냘픈 덜컹 소리를 내는, 멍청하리만치 완강한 문을 박차고 들어가면, 두 계단 아래로 텔슨 안에 떨어졌고, 작은 계산대가 두 개 놓인 초라한 작은 가게 안에서 정신을 차리게 되었는데, 그곳에서는 가장 늙은 노인들이 가장 침침한 창문 곁에서 당신의 수표 서명을 검사했고, 그들이 들고 있는 수표는 마치 바람이 스치는 양 흔들렸으니, 그 창문들은 늘 플리트가에서 날아드는 진흙의 소나기를 뒤집어쓰고 있었고, 거기 박힌 철창과 템플바의 무거운 그림자 탓에 한층 더 침침했다. 만약 볼일 때문에 \"은행 측\"을 만나야 한다면, 당신은 뒤편의 일종의 사형수 감방 같은 곳으로 끌려가, 은행 측이 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 나타날 때까지 헛되이 보낸 인생을 곱씹게 되는데, 그 음울한 어스름 속에서 당신은 좀처럼 눈도 깜박이기 어려웠다. 당신의 돈은 벌레 먹은 낡은 나무 서랍에서 나오거나 그 안으로 들어갔는데, 그것들을 여닫을 때면 부스러기가 당신의 코로 날아들고 목구멍으로 넘어갔다. 당신의 지폐에서는 곰팡내가 났으니, 마치 다시 누더기로 빠르게 썩어가는 듯했다. 당신의 은그릇은 이웃한 오물 구덩이들 틈에 처박혀, 나쁜 교제가 그 고운 광택을 하루이틀 새 망쳐놓았다. 당신의 증서들은 부엌과 설거지방을 임시변통으로 만든 금고실로 들어가, 그 양피지의 기름기를 죄다 은행 안 공기 속으로 졸여 보냈다. 가족 서류가 든 좀 더 가벼운 상자들은 위층의 바르메시드 방으로 올라갔는데, 거기엔 늘 커다란 식탁이 놓여 있었으나 한 번도 식사가 차려진 적은 없었고, 1780년에 이르러서도, 옛 연인이나 어린 자식들이 당신에게 처음 써 보낸 편지들이 창밖으로 노려보이던 공포에서 갓 풀려난 참이었으니, 그 창밖에서는 템플바에 효수된 머리들이, 아비시니아나 아샨티에나 어울릴 법한 무감각한 잔혹함과 흉포함으로 그것들을 곁눈질하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n* 바르메시드 방: 『아라비안나이트』에서 바르메시드 가문의 한 사람이 빈 접시로 거지에게 가짜 잔치를 베푼 이야기에서 유래한 표현으로, 실속 없이 형식만 갖춘 것을 뜻한다.\n그러나 실로 그 시절에는, 죽음에 처하는 것이 온갖 직종과 직업에서 크게 유행하던 처방이었고, 텔슨도 결코 예외가 아니었다. 죽음은 만물에 대한 자연의 치유책이거늘, 어찌 입법의 치유책이 되지 못하겠는가? 그리하여 위조범은 죽음에 처해졌고, 부정 어음을 유통한 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 불법으로 편지를 뜯은 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 사십 실링 육 펜스를 훔친 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 텔슨 문 앞에서 말을 맡아두었다가 그것을 끌고 달아난 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 가짜 실링을 만든 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 범죄의 온 음계 가운데 사분의 삼에 해당하는 음을 울린 자들은 죽음에 처해졌다. 그렇다고 그것이 예방의 면에서 조금이라도 효험이 있었던 것은 아니다 — 오히려 사실은 정반대였다고 짚어둘 만하기까지 했다 — 다만, 그것은 (이 세상의 일로 치자면) 각 사건의 골칫거리를 말끔히 치웠고, 그와 관련해 더 살펴야 할 다른 일은 아무것도 남기지 않았다. 이렇게 해서 텔슨은 그 시절에, 같은 시대의 더 큰 영업장들과 마찬가지로, 그토록 많은 목숨을 거두었으니, 만약 그 앞에서 베어진 머리들이 은밀히 처리되는 대신 템플바에 줄지어 걸렸더라면, 아마도 일층에 들던 그 얼마 안 되는 빛마저 꽤나 의미심장한 방식으로 가로막았을 것이다.\n\n텔슨 안의 온갖 침침한 벽장과 칸막이 우리 속에 갇힌 채, 가장 늙은 노인들이 엄숙하게 영업을 이어갔다. 텔슨의 런던 본점에 젊은이를 받아들일 때면, 그가 늙을 때까지 어딘가에 숨겨두었다. 그가 텔슨다운 풍미를 흠뻑 갖추고 그 위에 푸른곰팡이가 피어날 때까지, 마치 치즈처럼 어두운 곳에 보관해두었다. 그제야 비로소 그는 사람들 눈에 띄도록 허락받아, 커다란 장부를 들여다보며 거창하게 일하고, 자신의 반바지와 각반을 그 은행의 전체 무게에 보태었다.\n\n텔슨 밖에는 — 불려 들어가지 않는 한 결코 안으로는 들어가지 못했다 — 잡일꾼 하나가 있었으니, 이따금 짐꾼이며 심부름꾼 노릇을 하던 그는 그 은행의 살아 있는 간판 구실을 했다. 그는 심부름을 가지 않는 한 영업시간 동안 한 번도 자리를 비우지 않았고, 심부름을 갈 때면 제 아들이 그를 대신했으니, 그 아들은 열두 살 난 음산한 개구쟁이로 아비를 그대로 빼다 박은 모습이었다. 사람들은 텔슨이 위엄 있는 태도로 그 잡일꾼을 너그러이 봐준다고 이해했다. 그 은행은 늘 누군가를 그런 자격으로 너그러이 봐주어 왔고, 세월의 흐름이 이 사람을 그 자리로 떠밀어 온 것이었다. 그의 성은 크런처였고, 어린 시절 하운즈디치의 동쪽 교구 교회에서 대리인을 통해 어둠의 일들을 버리겠노라 서약하던 때에, 제리라는 이름을 더 얻었다.\n\n* 어둠의 일들을 버리겠노라 서약: 영국 국교회 세례식에서 대부·대모가 유아를 대신해 악마와 그 소행을 끊겠다고 맹세하는 절차를 가리킨다.\n무대는 화이트프라이어스의 행잉스워드 골목에 있는 크런처 씨의 사사로운 거처였고, 때는 1780년 어느 바람 부는 삼월 아침 일곱시 반이었다. (크런처 씨 본인은 주님의 해라는 말을 늘 \"주님의 도미노\"라고 했는데, 보아하니 그리스도교 기원이 어떤 부인이 자기 이름을 붙여 만든 인기 놀이의 발명에서 비롯되었다고 여기는 듯했다.)\n\n* 주님의 해: 라틴어 Anno Domini를 가리킨다. 크런처는 이를 발음이 비슷한 Anna Dominoes(애나 도미노)로 잘못 알아들어 도미노 놀이와 연관 짓고 있다.\n크런처 씨의 거처는 향기로운 동네에 있지 않았고, 유리 한 장 끼운 벽장을 하나로 친다 해도 방이 둘뿐이었다. 그러나 무척 단정하게 관리되고 있었다. 바람 부는 삼월 아침, 이른 시각인데도 그가 누워 있는 방은 이미 구석구석 닦여 있었고, 아침 식사를 위해 차려놓은 잔과 받침 사이에는, 그리고 투박한 전나무 식탁 위에는 무척 깨끗한 흰 식탁보가 펼쳐져 있었다.\n\n크런처 씨는 마치 집에 있는 어릿광대처럼 조각보 이불 아래에 누워 쉬고 있었다. 처음에는 곤히 잠들었으나, 차츰 침대에서 몸을 굴리고 뒤척이기 시작하더니, 마침내 수면 위로 솟아올랐는데, 그 뻣뻣하게 곤두선 머리칼은 흡사 침대보를 갈가리 찢어놓을 듯했다. 바로 그 순간 그는 끔찍하게 격분한 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 저 여편네가 또 저 짓을 하고 있잖아!\"\n\n정돈되고 부지런해 보이는 한 여인이 구석에서 무릎을 꿇고 있다가 일어섰는데, 그 황급하고 떨리는 기색만으로도 자신이 바로 그 지목된 사람임이 충분히 드러났다.\n\n\"뭐!\" 크런처 씨가 부츠를 찾으려고 침대 밖을 내다보며 말했다. \"또 그 짓이지, 그렇지?\"\n\n이 두번째 인사로 아침을 맞이한 뒤, 그는 세번째 인사 삼아 여인에게 부츠 한 짝을 집어던졌다. 그것은 진흙투성이 부츠였는데, 여기서 크런처 씨의 집안 살림과 얽힌 묘한 사정 하나를 소개할 만하다. 곧, 그는 은행 영업이 끝난 뒤 깨끗한 부츠를 신고 귀가하는 일이 흔했는데, 이튿날 아침에 일어나 보면 바로 그 부츠가 진흙범벅이 되어 있는 일이 흔했다는 것이다.\n\n\"뭐,\" 표적을 빗맞힌 뒤 호칭을 바꾸어 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"뭘 하는 거야, 이 악담쟁이야?\"\n\n\"그저 기도를 드리고 있었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"기도를 드린다고! 잘난 여편네일세! 무슨 셈으로 털썩 엎드려서 나한테 맞서 기도를 한단 말이야?\"\n\n\"당신한테 맞서 기도한 게 아니에요. 당신을 위해 기도했어요.\"\n\n\"아니지. 설령 그랬다 해도, 나는 그런 참견은 사양하겠어. 야! 네 어미가 잘난 여편네다, 어린 제리야, 글쎄 네 아비의 번영에 맞서 기도를 하고 있구나. 너한테는 효심 깊은 어미가 있구나, 아들아. 너한테는 신앙심 깊은 어미가 있구나, 얘야. 글쎄 털썩 엎드려서, 하나뿐인 제 자식의 입에서 버터 바른 빵을 낚아채 가게 해달라고 기도를 하고 있다니.\"\n\n(셔츠 바람이던) 크런처 도련님은 이를 무척 언짢게 받아들이고는, 어머니 쪽으로 돌아서서 제 끼니가 기도로 날아가는 일에 강력히 반대했다.\n\n\"그래 도대체 네 생각엔, 이 우쭐대는 여편네야,\" 크런처 씨가 무심결에 앞뒤 안 맞는 소리로 말했다. \"네 기도의 값어치가 얼마나 될 것 같으냐? 네 기도에 매기는 값을 한번 대 봐!\"\n\n\"제 기도는 그저 마음에서 우러나는 것일 뿐이에요, 제리. 그 이상의 값어치는 없어요.\"\n\n\"그 이상의 값어치는 없다고,\" 크런처 씨가 따라 말했다. \"그렇다면 별 값어치도 없겠군. 어찌 됐든, 내게 맞서는 기도는 받지 않겠어, 똑똑히 일러두는데. 나는 그럴 형편이 못 돼. 네 슬그머니 하는 짓 탓에 운수 사나워질 생각은 없단 말이야. 굳이 털썩 엎드려야겠다면, 네 남편과 자식을 위해 엎드려라, 그들에게 맞서지 말고. 만약 내게 거꾸로 된 마누라만 아니었고, 이 가엾은 녀석한테 거꾸로 된 어미만 아니었더라면, 나는 지난주에 돈 좀 만질 수도 있었을 텐데, 맞기도질에 시달리고, 음모에 휘말리고, 신앙심에 에둘러져서 최악의 운수로 끌려가고 말았지. 빌-어-먹-을!\" 이러는 내내 옷을 입고 있던 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"신앙이니 뭐니 별의별 빌어먹을 일들 탓에, 지난주에 정직한 장사꾼 가엾은 놈팡이가 일찍이 당해 본 적 없을 만큼 지독한 불운으로 내가 속아 넘어간 게 아니라면 말이지! 어린 제리야, 옷 입어라, 얘야, 그리고 내가 부츠를 닦는 동안 이따금 네 어미를 지켜보다가, 또 엎드릴 기미가 보이거든 나를 불러라. 왜냐하면, 똑똑히 일러두는데,\" 여기서 그는 다시 한번 아내에게 말을 건넸다. \"이런 식으로 또 당하지는 않겠어. 나는 삯마차처럼 삐걱거리고, 아편팅크처럼 졸리고, 살갗은 어찌나 당기는지, 그 아픔이 아니었더라면 어디까지가 나고 어디부터가 딴 사람인지도 모를 지경인데, 그래 봤자 주머니 사정은 조금도 나아진 게 없으니, 내가 의심하기로는 네가 아침부터 밤까지 그 짓을 해대며 내 주머니 사정이 나아지는 걸 막아 온 거야, 그러니 나는 그냥 참고 있지 않겠다, 이 악담쟁이야, 그래 이제 뭐라 할 테냐!\"\n\n여기에 더해 \"아! 그래! 너도 신앙심이 깊으시지. 설마 네 남편과 자식의 이익에 맞서기야 하겠어, 안 그래? 천만에!\" 따위의 말을 으르렁대고, 빙빙 도는 제 분노의 숫돌에서 다른 비아냥의 불꽃들도 튀겨내면서, 크런처 씨는 부츠 닦기에, 그리고 그날의 영업을 위한 전반적인 채비에 들어갔다. 그동안, 좀 더 보드라운 가시 같은 머리칼로 머리가 장식되고, 아비처럼 두 어린 눈이 서로 바싹 붙어 있는 그 아들은, 어머니를 향해 시키는 대로 망을 보았다. 그는 몸단장을 하던 잠자는 벽장에서 \"엎드리려 하시네, 어머니. — 이봐요, 아버지!\" 하고 억눌린 외침과 함께 불쑥 뛰쳐나와, 이 거짓 경보를 울린 뒤 불효막심한 히죽거림을 지으며 다시 뛰어들어가, 이따금 그 가엾은 여인을 크게 어지럽혔다.\n\n크런처 씨의 기분은 아침 식사 자리에 이르러서도 조금도 나아지지 않았다. 그는 크런처 부인이 식전 기도를 올리는 것에 각별한 적의를 품고 분개했다.\n\n\"이봐, 악담쟁이야! 뭘 하는 거야? 또 그 짓이야?\"\n\n아내는 그저 \"축복을 빌었을\" 뿐이라고 설명했다.\n\n\"하지 마!\" 크런처 씨가 두리번거리며 말했는데, 마치 아내의 간청이 효험을 발휘해 빵 덩어리가 사라지기라도 할까 봐 살피는 듯했다. \"나는 기도질에 집도 살림도 다 날릴 생각이 없어. 내 음식이 식탁에서 축복으로 날아가게 두지 않겠어. 가만히 있어!\"\n\n밤새도록 흥겨움과는 거리가 먼 방향으로 흘러간 어떤 잔치에 끼어 있기라도 했던 양 두 눈이 몹시 충혈되고 험상궂은 얼굴로, 제리 크런처는 아침을 먹는다기보다 들볶았으니, 동물원의 네발짐승처럼 그 위에서 으르렁댔다. 아홉시가 가까워지자 그는 헝클어진 인상을 가다듬고, 타고난 제 모습 위에 덧입힐 수 있는 한 가장 존경스럽고 사무적인 외양을 차린 채, 그날의 일거리를 향해 나섰다.\n\n스스로를 즐겨 \"정직한 장사꾼\"이라 일컫긴 했으나, 그것은 도무지 장사라 부르기 어려웠다. 그의 밑천이라곤 등받이가 부러진 의자를 잘라 만든 나무 걸상 하나가 전부였는데, 그 걸상을 어린 제리가 아비 곁에서 걸으며 매일 아침 템플바에서 가장 가까운 은행 창문 아래로 날라다 놓았다. 거기에, 지나가는 아무 마차에서나 주워 모을 수 있는 첫 한 줌의 짚을 더해 잡일꾼의 발을 추위와 습기로부터 막으면, 그것이 그날의 진지가 되었다. 이 자기 자리에서 크런처 씨는 플리트가와 템플 일대에 템플바 그 자체만큼이나 잘 알려져 있었고 — 그만큼이나 험상궂어 보였다.\n\n아홉시 십오분 전에 진지를 차려, 노인들이 텔슨으로 들어갈 때 그 삼각모에 손을 갖다 대 인사할 만큼 넉넉한 시간에, 제리는 바람 부는 이 삼월 아침의 자기 위치에 자리를 잡았고, 어린 제리도 그 곁에 서 있었으니, 다만 어린 제리는 템플바 안팎으로 출격해, 제 흐뭇한 목적에 알맞을 만큼 자그마한 지나가는 사내아이들에게 심신에 격렬한 상처를 입히지 않을 때만 그러했다. 서로 몹시 닮은 부자가, 저마다 두 눈이 서로 가까이 붙어 있듯 두 머리를 서로 바싹 맞대고서, 플리트가의 아침 통행을 말없이 바라보는 모습은 원숭이 한 쌍과 상당히 닮아 보였다. 어른 제리가 짚을 씹다가 뱉어내고, 어린 제리의 반짝이는 두 눈이 플리트가의 온갖 것을 살피듯 그를 쉴 새 없이 살피던 우연한 사정도 그 닮음을 덜어주지 못했다.\n\n텔슨 본점에 딸린 정규 내부 심부름꾼 하나가 문틈으로 고개를 내밀고 일렀다.\n\n\"짐꾼 부른다!\"\n\n\"만세, 아버지! 시작부터 이른 일거리예요!\"\n\n이렇게 아비에게 잘 다녀오라 빈 뒤, 어린 제리는 걸상에 걸터앉아, 아비가 씹던 짚에 대한 상속권을 행사하며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"늘-언제나 녹투성이야! 아버지 손가락은 늘-언제나 녹투성이라니까!\" 어린 제리가 중얼거렸다. \"우리 아버지는 대체 저 쇠 녹을 다 어디서 묻혀 오는 거지? 여기서는 쇠 녹이 묻을 데가 없는데!\"\n\n# CHAPTER II. A Sight\n\n\"올드 베일리는 잘 알고 있겠지, 분명히?\" 가장 나이 든 사무원 가운데 하나가 심부름꾼 제리에게 말했다.\n\n* 올드 베일리(Old Bailey): 런던의 중앙형사재판소.\n\"네에, 그렇습니다요.\" 제리가 다소 무뚝뚝한 투로 대꾸했다. \"베일리야 잘 알지요.\"\n\n\"그래. 그리고 로리 씨도 알지.\"\n\n\"로리 씨는 베일리보다 훨씬 잘 압니다요, 나리. 정직한 장사꾼으로서 베일리를 알고 싶은 정도보다 훨씬 더 잘 알지요.\" 제리가 마치 그 재판소에 끌려 나온 마지못한 증인이라도 되는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"좋아. 증인들이 들어가는 문을 찾아서, 문지기에게 로리 씨에게 보내는 이 쪽지를 보여주게. 그럼 안으로 들여보내 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"법정 안으로 말입니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"법정 안으로.\"\n\n크런처 씨의 두 눈은 서로 조금 가까워지면서, \"이걸 어떻게 생각해?\"라는 질문을 주고받는 듯했다.\n\n\"법정 안에서 기다려야 합니까요, 나리?\" 그 협의의 결과로 그가 물었다.\n\n\"이제 말해 주겠네. 문지기가 쪽지를 로리 씨에게 전할 테니, 자네는 로리 씨의 주의를 끌 만한 몸짓을 해서 자네가 서 있는 곳을 알려주게. 그런 다음에 자네가 할 일은, 그분이 자네를 찾을 때까지 거기 그대로 있는 것일세.\"\n\n\"그게 전부입니까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그게 전부야. 그분은 가까이에 심부름꾼을 두고 싶어 하셔. 이건 자네가 거기 있다는 걸 알리려는 거야.\"\n\n나이 든 사무원이 천천히 쪽지를 접어 겉봉에 글씨를 쓰는 동안, 크런처 씨는 그가 압지를 대는 단계에 이를 때까지 잠자코 그를 살펴보다가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"오늘 아침엔 위조죄 재판을 하겠지요?\"\n\n\"반역죄야!\"\n\n\"그건 사지를 찢는 거잖습니까.\" 제리가 말했다. \"야만적이네요!\"\n\n\"그게 법이야.\" 나이 든 사무원이 놀란 안경알을 그에게 돌리며 말했다. \"그게 법이라고.\"\n\n\"사람을 그렇게 망쳐놓는 건 법으로도 너무하다 싶은데요. 죽이는 것만 해도 충분히 가혹한데, 그렇게 망쳐놓는 건 정말 너무합니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않네.\" 나이 든 사무원이 받아쳤다. \"법을 좋게 말하게. 여보게, 자네 가슴과 목소리나 잘 챙기고, 법은 알아서 제 앞가림을 하도록 내버려 두게. 내가 그렇게 충고하겠네.\"\n\n\"제 가슴과 목소리에 내려앉는 건 습기랍니다요, 나리.\" 제리가 말했다. \"제가 얼마나 습한 방식으로 벌어먹고 사는지는 나리께서 판단하시지요.\"\n\n\"자, 자.\" 나이 든 사무원이 말했다. \"우리 모두 저마다 살아가는 방식이 다른 법이지. 누구는 습한 방식이고, 누구는 마른 방식이고. 여기 편지 있네. 어서 가게.\"\n\n제리는 편지를 받아 들고는, 겉으로 드러낸 것보다 속으로는 덜 공손하게 \"당신도 비쩍 마른 늙은이로군\" 하고 혼잣말을 하면서 인사를 하고, 지나가는 길에 아들에게 자기가 갈 곳을 알려준 뒤 제 길을 갔다.\n\n그 시절에는 타이번에서 교수형을 집행했으므로, 뉴게이트 바깥의 거리는 훗날 그곳에 따라붙게 된 악명을 아직 얻지 않았다. 그러나 그 감옥은 온갖 방탕과 악행이 자행되는 더러운 곳이었고, 그곳에서 무서운 질병들이 자라나 죄수들과 함께 법정으로 들어왔으며, 때로는 피고석에서 곧장 대법관 각하에게 달려들어 그를 자리에서 끌어내리기도 했다. 검은 모자를 쓴 판사가 죄수의 운명을 선고하듯이 자신의 운명까지 확실하게 선고하고, 심지어 죄수보다 먼저 죽는 일이 한두 번 일어난 것이 아니었다. 그밖에도 올드 베일리는 일종의 치명적인 여관 마당으로 유명했으니, 그곳에서 창백한 나그네들이 끊임없이 수레와 마차에 실려 저세상으로 가는 험한 여정을 떠났고, 그 길은 한 이 마일 반쯤 되는 공공 거리와 도로를 가로질렀으되, 설령 그 광경에 부끄러움을 느끼는 선량한 시민이 있다 한들 그 수는 거의 없었다. 그만큼 관습이란 강력한 것이며, 그러니 처음부터 좋은 관습이 되는 것이 그토록 바람직한 것이다. 그곳은 또한 칼*로도 유명했으니, 그 누구도 그 형벌이 어느 정도에 이를지 예측할 수 없는 형벌을 가하는 지혜로운 옛 제도였고, 또한 태형 기둥으로도 유명했으니, 그것이 실제로 작동하는 모습을 보고 있노라면 사람을 무척이나 인간답고 부드럽게 만들어주는 또 하나의 정겨운 옛 제도였으며, 또한 피값을 거래하는 광범위한 흥정으로도 유명했으니, 그것은 하늘 아래에서 저질러질 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 돈벌이 범죄로 조직적으로 이어지는 또 한 조각의 조상 전래의 지혜였다. 통틀어 말하자면, 그 시절의 올드 베일리는 \"있는 것은 무엇이든 옳다\"는 격언의 더없이 좋은 본보기였으니, 이는 게으른 만큼이나 결정적인 경구였으되, 다만 일찍이 있었던 것은 무엇이든 그른 것이 아니었다는 골치 아픈 결론까지 포함하지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다.\n* 칼(pillory): 죄인의 목과 두 손을 끼워 묶어 두던 형틀.\n\n조용히 길을 헤쳐 나가는 데 익숙한 사람의 솜씨로, 이 끔찍한 활동 무대 위아래로 흩어져 있는 오염된 군중 사이를 뚫고 나아간 끝에, 심부름꾼은 찾던 문을 찾아내어 거기 달린 쪽문으로 편지를 들이밀었다. 그 시절 사람들은 베들럼*에서 구경거리를 보려고 돈을 냈듯이, 올드 베일리에서 구경거리를 보려고 돈을 냈던 것이다. 다만 전자의 오락거리가 훨씬 비쌌다. 그래서 올드 베일리의 모든 문은 철저히 지켜졌다. 물론 범죄자들이 그곳에 들어오게 되는 그 정겨운 문만은 예외였으니, 그 문들은 언제나 활짝 열려 있었다.\n* 베들럼(Bedlam): 런던의 정신병원. 당시 사람들이 돈을 내고 환자들을 구경하기도 했다.\n\n얼마간 지체와 실랑이 끝에, 문은 못마땅하다는 듯 경첩 위에서 아주 조금 돌아가며 제리 크런처 씨가 몸을 비집고 법정 안으로 들어가도록 허락했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있소?\" 그는 어쩌다 곁에 서게 된 사람에게 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n\"아직 아무것도.\"\n\n\"무슨 사건이 다뤄지오?\"\n\n\"반역죄 사건.\"\n\n\"사지를 찢는 그거 말이오, 응?\"\n\n\"아!\" 그 사람이 입맛을 다시며 대꾸했다. \"썰매에 묶여 끌려 나와 반쯤 목이 매달렸다가, 끌어내려져 제 눈앞에서 살이 베이고, 그다음엔 제가 보는 가운데 내장이 끄집어내져 불태워지고, 그다음엔 목이 잘리고, 그다음엔 네 토막으로 잘릴 거요. 그게 판결이오.\"\n\n\"유죄로 밝혀지면 말이지요?\" 제리가 단서를 붙이듯 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오! 유죄로 밝혀질 거요.\" 상대가 말했다. \"그건 걱정 마쇼.\"\n\n여기서 크런처 씨의 주의는 문지기에게로 쏠렸는데, 그가 쪽지를 손에 들고 로리 씨에게 다가가는 모습이 보였던 것이다. 로리 씨는 가발을 쓴 신사들 사이의 탁자에 앉아 있었다. 자기 앞에 큰 서류 뭉치를 쌓아둔, 피고 측 변호인인 가발 쓴 신사로부터 멀지 않은 곳이었고, 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 또 다른 가발 쓴 신사와는 거의 마주 보는 자리였는데, 크런처 씨가 그때나 그 뒤에나 그를 볼 때면 그의 온 주의력은 법정 천장에 쏠려 있는 듯했다. 몇 차례 거칠게 기침을 하고 턱을 문지르고 손으로 신호를 보낸 끝에, 제리는 그를 찾으려고 일어서 있던 로리 씨의 주의를 끌었고, 로리 씨는 조용히 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 다시 앉았다.\n\n\"저 사람은 이 사건이랑 무슨 상관이오?\" 그가 말을 나누었던 사람이 물었다.\n\n\"낸들 알 게 뭐요.\" 제리가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 댁은 이 사건이랑 무슨 상관이오, 물어봐도 된다면?\"\n\n\"그것도 낸들 알 게 뭐요.\" 제리가 말했다.\n\n판사가 입정하고, 그에 따라 법정 안이 한바탕 술렁이다가 가라앉는 바람에 대화가 멈추었다. 이내 피고석이 관심의 중심이 되었다. 거기 서 있던 두 명의 간수가 나가고, 죄수가 끌려 들어와 피고석에 세워졌다.\n\n천장을 바라보던 그 가발 쓴 신사 한 사람만 빼고, 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람이 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그곳에 있는 모든 인간의 숨결이 바다처럼, 바람처럼, 불길처럼 그에게로 밀려들었다. 열성적인 얼굴들이 그를 한번 보려고 기둥과 모퉁이 너머로 목을 빼고 안간힘을 썼다. 뒷줄의 구경꾼들은 그의 머리카락 한 올도 놓치지 않으려고 일어섰다. 법정 바닥에 있는 사람들은 누구의 희생을 무릅쓰고서라도 그를 보려고 앞사람의 어깨에 손을 얹고 몸을 일으켰고, 발끝으로 서고, 턱진 곳에 올라서고, 거의 아무것도 아닌 데에 발을 디디고서 그의 한 치 한 치를 다 보려 했다. 이 뒤쪽 무리 가운데 단연 눈에 띄게, 마치 뉴게이트의 가시 박힌 담장이 살아 움직이는 한 조각이라도 되는 듯이, 제리가 서 있었다. 그는 오는 길에 한잔 걸친 맥주 냄새 나는 숨을 죄수에게로 내뿜었고, 그것은 그에게로 흘러드는 다른 맥주와 진과 차와 커피와 그밖에 온갖 것들의 물결과 뒤섞였으니, 그 물결은 이미 그의 등 뒤 커다란 창문들에 부딪쳐 탁한 안개와 비가 되어 흩어지고 있었다.\n\n이 모든 응시와 떠들썩함의 대상은 스물다섯쯤 된 청년으로, 체격이 좋고 풍채가 훤했으며, 볕에 그을린 뺨과 검은 눈을 지니고 있었다. 그의 처지는 젊은 신사의 처지였다. 그는 수수하게 검은색, 혹은 아주 짙은 회색 옷을 입었고, 길고 검은 머리카락은 장식을 위해서라기보다 거치적거리지 않게 하려고 목덜미에서 리본으로 묶여 있었다. 마음의 동요가 몸을 가린 어떤 것을 통해서든 드러나기 마련이듯이, 그의 처지가 빚어낸 창백함이 뺨의 갈색을 뚫고 배어 나와, 영혼이 태양보다 강하다는 것을 보여주었다. 그밖에는 그는 꽤나 침착했고, 판사에게 인사를 하고는 조용히 서 있었다.\n\n이 사람이 응시와 숨결의 대상이 된 그 관심의 종류는 인간성을 고양하는 종류의 것이 아니었다. 그가 덜 끔찍한 판결의 위험에 처해 있었더라면 — 그 야만적인 세부 사항 가운데 어느 하나라도 면제받을 가능성이 있었더라면 — 그만큼 그의 매력은 줄어들었을 것이다. 그토록 수치스럽게 갈가리 찢기도록 운명 지어진 그 육체가 바로 구경거리였고, 그토록 도살되고 사지가 찢길 그 불멸의 피조물이 그 짜릿함을 자아냈다. 여러 구경꾼들이 저마다의 기교와 자기기만의 능력에 따라 그 관심에 어떤 윤을 입히든, 그 관심은 뿌리부터 식인귀 같은 것이었다.\n\n법정에 정숙을! 샤를 다네는 어제, (한없는 미사여구를 늘어놓으며) 그가 우리의 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 군주, 우리의 주군이신 국왕 폐하에 대한 거짓된 반역자라고 규탄하는 기소장에 대해 무죄를 주장한 바 있었다. 그 이유인즉, 그가 여러 차례에 걸쳐 여러 수단과 방법으로 프랑스 국왕 루이가 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분에 맞서 벌이는 전쟁을 도왔다는 것이었다. 다시 말해, 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분의 영토와 위에서 말한 프랑스인 루이의 영토 사이를 오가면서, 사악하게, 거짓되게, 반역적으로, 그밖에 갖가지 사악한 부사적 방식으로, 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분이 캐나다와 북아메리카로 보내려고 준비해 둔 병력이 어떤 것인지를 위에서 말한 프랑스인 루이에게 누설했다는 것이었다. 법률 용어들이 그를 곤두서게 하는 통에 머리가 점점 더 뾰족해지는 가운데, 제리는 이만큼을 대단히 만족스럽게 알아들었고, 그리하여 빙 돌아서, 앞서 말했고 거듭거듭 앞서 말한 그 샤를 다네가 지금 자기 앞에 서서 재판을 받고 있다는 것, 배심원들이 선서를 하고 있다는 것, 그리고 검찰총장이 발언할 채비를 하고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n거기 있는 모든 사람들에게 마음속으로 목매달리고, 목이 잘리고, 사지가 찢기고 있는 피고는(그리고 자기가 그렇다는 것을 알고 있었던 피고는) 그 처지 앞에서 움츠러들지도, 거기에 어떤 연극적인 태도를 꾸미지도 않았다. 그는 조용하고 주의 깊었으며, 진지한 관심을 가지고 개정 절차를 지켜보았다. 그리고 두 손을 앞에 놓인 나무 판자 위에 올려놓고 어찌나 차분하게 서 있었던지, 그 판자 위에 흩뿌려진 약초의 잎사귀 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않았다. 법정에는 감옥의 공기와 감옥 열병을 막는 예방책으로 온통 약초가 흩뿌려지고 식초가 뿌려져 있었다.\n\n죄수의 머리 위에는 빛을 그에게 내리비추기 위한 거울이 하나 있었다. 사악하고 비참한 자들의 무리가 거기에 비쳤다가, 그 표면과 이 세상에서 함께 사라져 갔다. 만약 그 유리가 언젠가 바다가 죽은 자들을 내어놓듯 자기가 비추었던 영상들을 되돌려 줄 수 있었더라면, 그 가증스러운 곳은 더없이 소름 끼치는 방식으로 유령에 시달렸을 것이다. 그 거울이 어떤 오욕과 치욕을 위해 마련된 것인가 하는 생각이 문득 죄수의 마음을 스쳐 갔을지도 모른다. 어쨌든, 자세를 바꾸다가 빛줄기 하나가 자기 얼굴을 가로지르는 것을 의식하게 된 그는 위를 올려다보았고, 그 거울을 보자 얼굴이 붉어지면서 오른손으로 약초를 밀쳐냈다.\n\n그 동작 때문에 그의 얼굴이 법정의 왼쪽에 있는 쪽으로 돌려지게 되었다. 그의 눈높이와 거의 같은 자리에, 판사석의 그 모퉁이에, 두 사람이 앉아 있었는데, 그의 시선이 곧바로 그들에게 머물렀다. 어찌나 곧바로, 또 어찌나 그의 표정을 바꾸어 놓았던지, 그에게 쏠려 있던 모든 눈길이 그들에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n구경꾼들은 그 두 사람에게서, 갓 스무 살 남짓한 젊은 숙녀와, 분명 그녀의 아버지로 보이는 신사를 보았다. 머리카락이 완전히 새하얗다는 점과, 뭐라 형언할 수 없는 얼굴의 어떤 강렬함이라는 점에서 무척 눈에 띄는 외모의 남자였는데, 그 강렬함은 활동적인 종류가 아니라 깊이 생각에 잠기고 스스로와 대화하는 종류의 것이었다. 이 표정이 그에게 떠올라 있을 때면 그는 늙어 보였다. 그러나 그것이 흔들려 흩어질 때면 — 지금 그가 딸에게 말을 거는 순간 그러했듯이 — 그는 인생의 한창때를 아직 넘기지 않은 잘생긴 남자가 되었다.\n\n그의 딸은 그의 곁에 앉아 한 손을 그의 팔에 끼우고, 다른 손으로 그 팔을 누르고 있었다. 그녀는 그 광경에 대한 두려움 때문에, 또 죄수에 대한 연민 때문에 그에게 바싹 다가앉아 있었다. 그녀의 이마는 피고의 위험밖에는 아무것도 보지 못하는, 온통 마음을 사로잡는 공포와 동정을 놀랍도록 또렷하게 드러내고 있었다. 이것이 어찌나 두드러지게, 어찌나 강렬하고 자연스럽게 드러났던지, 그를 가엾게 여기지 않던 구경꾼들마저 그녀에게 마음이 움직였다. 그리고 \"저들은 누구지?\" 하는 속삭임이 떠돌았다.\n\n심부름꾼 제리는, 제 나름의 방식으로 제 나름의 관찰을 해왔고, 골똘히 빠진 채 손가락의 녹을 빨아대고 있었는데, 그들이 누구인지 들으려고 목을 빼었다. 그를 둘러싼 군중은 그 질문을 가장 가까운 정리에게 밀어 넘겼고, 그에게서 그 질문은 더 천천히 밀려서 되돌아왔다. 마침내 그것이 제리에게 이르렀다.\n\n\"증인들이래.\"\n\n\"어느 편의?\"\n\n\"반대편.\"\n\n\"무엇에 반대하는 편 말이오?\"\n\n\"죄수에게.\"\n\n판사는, 그쪽 방향으로 향했던 눈길을 거두어들이고는, 자리에 등을 기대고서, 검찰총장이 일어나 밧줄을 꼬고, 도끼를 갈고, 처형대에 못을 박기 시작하는 동안, 그 목숨이 자기 손에 달린 그 사람을 물끄러미 바라보았다.\n\n# CHAPTER III. A Disappointment\n\n검찰총장은 배심원단에게 다음과 같이 알려야 했다. 그들 앞에 선 피고인은 나이는 젊으나 그 목숨을 몰수당해 마땅한 반역 행위에 있어서는 노련한 자라고. 적국과 주고받은 이 내통은 오늘이나 어제, 심지어 작년이나 재작년의 일도 아니라고. 피고인이 그보다도 훨씬 오래전부터, 정직하게 해명할 수 없는 비밀 임무를 띠고 프랑스와 영국을 오가는 일을 습관으로 삼아왔음이 확실하다고. 만일 반역의 길이 번창하는 것이 그 본성이었다면(다행히 결코 그렇지 않았으나) 그가 하는 일의 진짜 사악함과 죄과는 발각되지 않은 채 남았을지도 모른다고. 그러나 섭리는 두려움도 비난도 초월한 어느 인물의 마음속에, 피고인이 꾸민 음모의 실체를 캐내고, 공포에 사로잡힌 채 그것을 폐하의 국무대신과 더없이 존귀한 추밀원에 폭로하도록 하는 뜻을 심어주었다고. 이 애국자가 그들 앞에 출두할 것이라고. 그의 입장과 태도는 대체로 숭고하다고. 그는 피고인의 친구였으나, 길하면서도 흉한 어느 한순간에 그의 파렴치함을 간파하고는, 더이상 가슴에 품을 수 없게 된 그 반역자를 조국의 신성한 제단에 제물로 바치기로 결심했다고. 만일 영국에서도 고대 그리스와 로마에서처럼 공공의 은인들에게 동상을 세워주기로 정해져 있다면, 이 빛나는 시민에게도 틀림없이 하나가 세워졌으리라고. 그러나 그렇게 정해져 있지 않으니, 그는 아마 동상을 얻지 못할 것이라고. 미덕이란, 시인들이 (그가 알기로 배심원단이 글자 하나 틀리지 않고 혀끝에 줄줄 욀 만한 수많은 구절 속에서 — 여기서 배심원단의 표정에는 그 구절들에 관해 아무것도 모른다는 죄스러운 자각이 드러났다) 노래한 바와 같이, 어떤 의미에서 전염성을 띤다고. 특히 애국심, 곧 조국애로 알려진 저 빛나는 미덕이 더욱 그러하다고. 변변치 못하게나마 언급하는 것만으로도 영광인, 이 흠 하나 없고 나무랄 데 없는 왕실 측 증인의 드높은 본보기가 피고인의 하인에게도 전해져, 그 하인의 마음속에 주인의 책상 서랍과 호주머니를 뒤지고 그 서류를 몰래 빼돌리겠다는 거룩한 결심을 낳았다고. 그(검찰총장)는 이 감탄스러운 하인을 깎아내리려는 시도가 다소 있으리라 각오하고 있으나, 대체로 그는 그 하인을 자신(검찰총장)의 형제자매보다 더 좋아하며, 자신(검찰총장)의 아버지와 어머니보다 더 존경한다고. 그는 배심원단도 나와서 똑같이 해주기를 자신 있게 촉구한다고. 이 두 증인의 증언은, 그들이 발견했으며 곧 제출될 문서들과 더불어, 피고인이 바다와 육지 양쪽에서의 폐하의 병력과 그 배치 및 준비 상황의 명단을 입수하고 있었음을 드러낼 것이고, 그가 그런 정보를 적대 세력에 상습적으로 넘겨왔음에 의심의 여지를 남기지 않을 것이라고. 이 명단들이 피고인의 필적이라고 입증할 수는 없으나, 그것은 다 마찬가지라고. 실로 그것은 피고인이 경계에 교활했음을 보여주므로 오히려 검찰 측에 더 유리하다고. 입증은 오년 전으로 거슬러 올라가, 영국군과 미국인 사이에 벌어진 바로 그 최초의 교전 날짜로부터 불과 몇 주 전에 이미 피고인이 이 해로운 임무에 종사하고 있었음을 보여줄 것이라고. 이런 이유들로 인해, 충직한 배심원단인(그가 알기로 그들은 충직했다) 동시에 책임 있는 배심원단인(그들 자신이 알기로 그들은 책임이 있었다) 그들은, 좋든 싫든 반드시 피고인에게 유죄를 선고하여 그를 끝장내야 한다고. 그들은 결코 자기네 베개에 머리를 누일 수 없으리라고. 그들은 결코 자기 아내가 베개에 머리를 누인다는 생각을 견딜 수 없으리라고. 그들은 결코 자기 자식들이 베개에 머리를 누인다는 관념을 참을 수 없으리라고. 요컨대 피고인의 머리를 베어내지 않는 한, 그들에게도 그들의 가족에게도 결코 다시는 베개에 머리를 누이는 일 따위는 있을 수 없으리라고. 검찰총장은 자신이 생각해낼 수 있는, 단호한 매듭이 들어간 온갖 것의 이름을 걸고, 또 자신이 이미 피고인을 죽은 것이나 다름없는 자로 여긴다는 엄숙한 단언을 믿고서, 그들에게 바로 그 머리를 요구하며 말을 맺었다.\n\n검찰총장이 말을 그치자, 마치 한 무리의 커다란 쉬파리떼가 곧 피고인이 될 그 무엇을 미리 내다보며 그의 둘레로 떼지어 몰려드는 듯이, 법정에 웅성거림이 일었다. 그것이 다시 가라앉자, 그 나무랄 데 없는 애국자가 증인석에 나타났다.\n\n이어서 차장검사가 상관의 이끎을 좇아 그 애국자를 신문했으니, 이름은 존 바사드, 신사였다. 그 순결한 영혼의 이야기는 검찰총장이 묘사한 그대로였다 — 흠이라면 어쩌면 너무도 그대로라는 점이었다. 자신의 고결한 가슴에서 짐을 덜어낸 그는 겸손하게 물러나려 했으나, 로리 씨에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 앞에 서류를 둔 가발 쓴 신사가 몇 가지 묻기를 청했다. 맞은편에 앉은 가발 쓴 신사는 여전히 법정 천장을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n본인은 스파이였던 적이 있는가? 아니다, 그는 그런 비열한 암시를 경멸한다. 무엇으로 생계를 꾸리는가? 자기 재산으로. 그 재산은 어디에 있는가? 어디에 있는지 정확히는 기억나지 않는다. 어떤 재산인가? 누구의 상관할 바도 아니다. 물려받은 것인가? 그렇다, 물려받았다. 누구에게서? 먼 친척에게서. 아주 먼? 꽤. 감옥에 있은 적이 있는가? 물론 없다. 채무자 감옥에 있은 적은 없는가? 그게 이 사건과 무슨 상관인지 모르겠다. 채무자 감옥에 있은 적이 없는가? — 자, 한번 더. 결코 없는가? 있다. 몇 번인가? 두세 번. 대여섯 번은 아닌가? 어쩌면. 직업은 무엇인가? 신사다. 발길질을 당한 적이 있는가? 그랬을지도 모른다. 자주? 아니다. 계단 아래로 걷어차인 적이 있는가? 단연코 없다. 한번은 계단 꼭대기에서 발길질을 받고 제 발로 계단을 굴러 내려간 적이 있다. 그때 주사위 노름에서 속임수를 썼다고 걷어차인 것인가? 폭행을 저지른 그 술 취한 거짓말쟁이가 그 비슷한 말을 했으나, 사실이 아니다. 사실이 아니라고 맹세하는가? 단언컨대. 노름에서 속여 생계를 꾸린 적이 있는가? 결코 없다. 노름으로 생계를 꾸린 적은? 다른 신사들보다 더하지는 않다. 피고인에게서 돈을 빌린 적이 있는가? 있다. 갚은 적은? 없다. 피고인과의 이 친분이란 실은 아주 보잘것없는 것으로, 마차와 여관과 연락선 안에서 피고인에게 억지로 떠맡겨진 것이 아닌가? 아니다. 피고인이 이 명단들을 가지고 있는 것을 분명히 보았는가? 확실하다. 명단에 관해 더 아는 것은 없는가? 없다. 이를테면 그것들을 본인이 직접 구한 것은 아닌가? 아니다. 이 증언으로 무언가 얻기를 기대하는가? 아니다. 함정을 놓으라고 정부에 정식으로 고용되어 급료를 받는 것은 아닌가? 천만에, 아니다. 혹은 무슨 일이라도 하라고? 천만에, 아니다. 그것을 맹세하는가? 거듭거듭. 순전한 애국심의 동기 말고 다른 동기는 없는가? 전혀 없다.\n\n그 덕망 높은 하인, 로저 클라이는 엄청난 속도로 사건을 헤치며 맹세를 늘어놓았다. 그는 사년 전에 신의와 순박함으로 피고인의 고용살이에 들어갔다. 깔레행 연락선에서 그는 피고인에게 부릴 만한 사람이 필요한지 물었고, 피고인이 그를 고용했다. 그는 그 부릴 만한 사람을 자선 삼아 거두어달라고 청하지 않았다 — 그런 것은 생각해본 적도 없다. 그는 머지않아 피고인을 의심하기 시작했고 그를 주시하게 되었다. 여행 중에 옷을 정리하다가, 그는 피고인의 호주머니에서 이것들과 비슷한 명단을 거듭거듭 보았다. 그는 이 명단들을 피고인의 책상 서랍에서 꺼냈다. 처음에 그것들을 그곳에 넣어둔 것은 그가 아니다. 그는 피고인이 깔레에서 프랑스 신사들에게 바로 이 명단들을 보여주는 것을, 또 깔레와 불로뉴 양쪽에서 프랑스 신사들에게 비슷한 명단들을 보여주는 것을 보았다. 그는 조국을 사랑했고, 차마 견딜 수 없어 정보를 제공했다. 그는 은제 찻주전자를 훔쳤다는 혐의를 받은 적이 결코 없다. 겨자단지 일로 비방을 받은 적은 있으나, 그것은 도금한 것에 지나지 않은 것으로 밝혀졌다. 그는 앞서의 증인을 칠팔년 알고 지냈는데, 그것은 그저 우연의 일치였다. 그는 그것을 딱히 기이한 우연의 일치라고 부르지는 않았다. 대부분의 우연의 일치란 기이한 법이다. 또한 그는 진정한 애국심이 그에게도 유일한 동기라는 것을 기이한 우연의 일치라 부르지도 않았다. 그는 진정한 영국인이며, 자기 같은 사람이 많기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n쉬파리떼가 다시 윙윙거렸고, 검찰총장이 자비스 로리 씨를 불렀다.\n\n\"자비스 로리 씨, 당신은 텔슨 은행의 직원입니까?\"\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"천칠백칠십오년 십일월 어느 금요일 밤에, 볼일이 있어 우편마차로 런던과 도버 사이를 오갈 일이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"있었습니다.\"\n\n\"우편마차에 다른 승객이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"둘 있었습니다.\"\n\"그들은 밤중에 도중에 내렸습니까?\"\n\n\"내렸습니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 피고인을 보십시오. 그가 그 두 승객 가운데 하나였습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 단언할 수는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 그 두 승객 가운데 누군가와 닮았습니까?\"\n\n\"둘 다 너무 꽁꽁 싸매고 있었고, 밤은 너무 어두웠으며, 우리 모두 너무 말이 없었던 터라, 그것조차 단언할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 피고인을 다시 보십시오. 그가 그 두 승객처럼 몸을 싸매고 있었다고 가정한다면, 그의 덩치와 키에 그가 그들 가운데 하나였을 법하지 않다고 할 만한 점이 있습니까?\"\n\"없습니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 그가 그들 가운데 하나가 아니었다고 맹세하지는 않으시겠지요?\"\n\"하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 적어도 그가 그들 가운데 하나였을 수도 있다고는 말씀하시는 거군요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 다만 그 두 사람은 — 저와 마찬가지로 — 노상강도를 무서워했던 것으로 기억하는데, 피고인에게는 무서워하는 기색이 없다는 점만 빼면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 두려움을 가장하는 것을 본 적이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"분명 본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 피고인을 한번 더 보십시오. 당신이 확실히 아는 한, 전에 그를 본 적이 있습니까?\"\n\"있습니다.\"\n\n\"언제입니까?\"\n\n\"며칠 뒤에 제가 프랑스에서 돌아오고 있었는데, 깔레에서 피고인이 제가 타고 돌아오던 연락선에 올라, 저와 함께 항해를 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 몇 시에 배에 올랐습니까?\"\n\n\"자정이 조금 지나서였습니다.\"\n\n\"한밤중에 말이군요. 그 같은 늦은 시각에 배에 오른 승객은 그 사람뿐이었습니까?\"\n\n\"공교롭게도 그가 유일한 승객이었습니다.\"\n\n\"'공교롭게도'는 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 로리 씨. 한밤중에 배에 오른 승객은 그 사람뿐이었습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"로리 씨, 당신은 혼자 여행하고 있었습니까, 아니면 일행이 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"일행이 둘 있었습니다. 신사 한 분과 숙녀 한 분이었지요. 그분들은 여기 와 있습니다.\"\n\n\"여기 와 있군요. 당신은 피고인과 무슨 대화를 나누었습니까?\"\n\n\"거의 없었습니다. 날씨는 험악했고, 항해는 길고 거칠었으며, 저는 이쪽 기슭에서 저쪽 기슭에 이르도록 거의 내내 소파에 누워 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"마네뜨 양!\"\n\n앞서 모든 눈길이 쏠렸다가 이제 다시 쏠리는 그 젊은 숙녀가 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어섰다. 그녀의 아버지도 그녀와 함께 일어서서, 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼운 채 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"마네뜨 양, 피고인을 보십시오.\"\n\n이런 연민, 이런 진실한 젊음과 아름다움과 마주하는 것은 피고인에게 군중 전체와 마주하는 것보다 훨씬 더 견디기 힘든 일이었다. 말하자면 자기 무덤가에서 그녀와 따로 떨어져 서 있는 셈이었으니, 지켜보는 온갖 호기심 어린 시선을 다 합쳐도 그 순간 그가 꼼짝 않고 서 있도록 다잡아줄 수는 없었다. 그의 다급한 오른손은 앞에 놓인 약초들을 정원의 가상의 화단으로 나누어 심었고, 호흡을 억누르고 가다듬으려는 그의 안간힘에 입술이 떨렸으며 그 입술에서 핏기가 가슴으로 몰려갔다. 커다란 쉬파리들의 윙윙거림이 다시 요란해졌다.\n\n\"마네뜨 양, 전에 피고인을 본 적이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"어디서였습니까?\"\n\"방금 언급된 그 연락선 위에서, 같은 그 일이 있을 때였습니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 방금 언급된 그 젊은 숙녀입니까?\"\n\n\"오! 더없이 불행하게도, 그렇습니다!\"\n\n그녀의 가엾어하는 애처로운 목소리는 판사의 덜 듣기 좋은 목소리에 묻혀버렸으니, 그가 사납게 무어라 말했던 것이다. \"당신에게 던져진 질문에 답하시오. 그에 관해 토를 달지 마시오.\"\n\n\"마네뜨 양, 그 해협을 건너는 항해에서 피고인과 무슨 대화를 나누었습니까?\"\n\"네, 나누었습니다.\"\n\n\"그것을 되살려보시오.\"\n\n깊은 고요 속에서, 그녀는 가냘프게 입을 떼었다. \"그 신사분이 배에 올랐을 때―\"\n\n\"피고인을 말하는 거요?\" 판사가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 판사님.\"\n\n\"그러면 피고인이라 하시오.\"\n\n\"피고인이 배에 올랐을 때, 그는 제 아버지가\" 하고 그녀가 곁에 선 아버지에게 사랑스러운 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"몹시 지쳐 있고 건강이 매우 쇠약한 상태임을 알아차렸습니다. 아버지는 너무도 쇠약해서, 저는 아버지를 바깥바람에서 안으로 모실 엄두가 나지 않아, 선실 계단 가까운 갑판에 잠자리를 마련해드리고, 아버지를 돌보려고 그 곁 갑판에 앉아 있었습니다. 그날 밤 다른 승객은 없었고, 우리 넷뿐이었습니다. 피고인은 친절하게도, 제가 했던 것보다 더 잘 아버지를 바람과 날씨로부터 막아드릴 방법을 일러주겠다고 청했습니다. 저는 그 일을 잘하는 법을 몰랐는데, 항구를 벗어나면 바람이 어느 쪽으로 불지 알지 못했기 때문입니다. 그가 저를 위해 그 일을 해주었습니다. 그는 제 아버지의 처지에 대해 깊은 다정함과 친절함을 보였고, 저는 그가 진심으로 그렇게 느꼈다고 확신합니다. 그렇게 해서 우리는 서로 이야기를 나누기 시작했습니다.\"\n\n\"잠깐 끼어들겠소. 그는 혼자 배에 올랐소?\"\n\"아닙니다.\"\n\n\"몇 사람이 그와 함께 있었소?\"\n\n\"프랑스 신사 두 분이었습니다.\"\n\n\"그들은 서로 의논을 했소?\"\n\n\"그들은 마지막 순간까지 서로 의논을 했고, 그때 프랑스 신사분들은 자기네 보트로 내려 상륙해야 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그들 사이에 이 명단들과 비슷한 서류가 오가지 않았소?\"\n\n\"그들 사이에 어떤 서류가 오가긴 했으나, 무슨 서류인지는 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"이것들과 모양과 크기가 비슷한 것이었소?\"\n\n\"아마 그랬을 테지만, 정말이지 모르겠습니다. 그들이 제 아주 가까이에 서서 소곤거리기는 했어도, 그들은 거기 걸린 등불의 빛을 받으려고 선실 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었고, 그 등불은 침침했으며, 그들은 아주 낮게 말했고, 저는 그들이 하는 말을 듣지 못했으며, 그저 그들이 서류를 들여다보고 있었다는 것만 보았으니까요.\"\n\n\"이제, 피고인과의 대화로 넘어갑시다, 마네뜨 양.\"\n\n\"피고인은 제 아버지에게 친절하고 자상하며 도움이 되었던 것과 마찬가지로, 저에게도 — 저의 무력한 처지에서 비롯된 것이지만 — 거리낌 없이 속내를 털어놓았습니다. 저는 바라건대,\" 하고 그녀가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"오늘 그에게 해를 끼쳐 그 은혜를 갚는 일이 없기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n쉬파리떼의 윙윙거림.\n\n\"마네뜨 양, 만일 당신이 마지못해 — 당신이 마땅히 해야 하고, 반드시 해야 하며, 결코 피할 수 없는 — 그 증언을 매우 내키지 않은 마음으로 하고 있다는 것을 피고인이 온전히 이해하지 못한다면, 이 자리에서 그것을 이해하지 못하는 사람은 그 한 사람뿐일 것입니다. 계속하십시오.\"\n\n\"그는 제게, 자신이 미묘하고 까다로운 성격의 일로 여행 중인데, 그 일이 사람들을 곤경에 빠뜨릴 수도 있어 그래서 가명으로 여행하고 있다고 말했습니다. 그는 이 일 때문에 며칠 사이에 프랑스에 다녀왔으며, 앞으로도 오랫동안 이따금씩 프랑스와 영국 사이를 오가게 될지도 모른다고 말했습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 미국에 관해 무슨 말을 했습니까, 마네뜨 양? 자세히 말하시오.\"\n\n\"그는 그 분쟁이 어떻게 일어났는지 제게 설명하려 했고, 자기가 판단하기로는 그것이 영국 측의 그릇되고 어리석은 처사라고 말했습니다. 그는 농담조로, 어쩌면 조지 워싱턴이 조지 삼세에 거의 맞먹는 큰 이름을 역사에 남길지도 모른다고 덧붙였습니다. 하지만 그가 그렇게 말한 데에는 아무런 악의도 없었습니다. 그것은 웃으며 한 말이었고, 무료한 시간을 달래려고 한 것이었습니다.\"\n\n큰 관심을 끄는 장면에서 여러 눈길이 쏠리는 주역 인물이 짓는 어떤 뚜렷한 표정은, 구경꾼들에 의해 무의식중에 흉내내어지게 마련이다. 그녀가 이 증언을 하는 동안 그녀의 이마는 고통스러우리만치 근심에 차고 골똘했으며, 판사가 그것을 받아 적느라 그녀가 말을 멈춘 짬마다, 그녀는 검찰 측과 변호 측 양쪽 변호인에게 그것이 미치는 영향을 살폈다. 구경꾼들 사이에서도 법정 사방에 똑같은 표정이 떠올라 있었으니, 판사가 조지 워싱턴에 관한 저 엄청난 이단을 노려보려고 필기에서 눈을 들었을 때, 그곳에 있던 이마들의 절대다수는 그 증인을 비추는 거울이라 해도 좋을 정도였다.\n\n이제 검찰총장은 판사에게, 신중을 기하고 형식을 갖추는 차원에서 그 젊은 숙녀의 아버지인 마네뜨 박사를 부르는 것이 필요하다고 여겨진다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 그리하여 그가 호명되었다.\n\n\"마네뜨 박사, 피고인을 보십시오. 전에 그를 본 적이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"한번 있습니다. 그가 런던의 제 거처로 찾아왔을 때입니다. 한 삼년, 아니면 삼년 반쯤 전입니다.\"\n\n\"피고인이 연락선에서 당신과 동승한 승객임을 알아볼 수 있습니까, 혹은 그가 당신의 따님과 나눈 대화에 대해 진술할 수 있습니까?\"\n\n\"판사님, 저는 그 어느 것도 할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그 어느 것도 하지 못하는 특별하고 각별한 이유가 있습니까?\"\n\n그는 낮은 목소리로 답했다. \"있습니다.\"\n\n\"마네뜨 박사, 당신은 당신의 조국에서 재판도, 심지어 기소조차 없이 오랜 투옥을 겪는 불행을 당했습니까?\"\n\n그는 모두의 가슴을 파고드는 어조로 답했다. \"오랜 투옥이었습니다.\"\n\n\"문제의 그때 당신은 갓 풀려난 참이었습니까?\"\n\n\"사람들이 그렇다고 합니다.\"\n\n\"그때 일을 전혀 기억하지 못합니까?\"\n\n\"전혀요. 제 마음은, 어느 때부터 — 언제인지조차 말할 수 없는 어느 때부터 — 백지입니다. 갇혀 있는 동안 신발 만드는 일에 저 자신을 붙들어두던 그때부터, 여기 이 사랑하는 딸과 런던에서 살고 있는 저 자신을 발견한 그때까지가요. 자비로운 하느님께서 제 정신을 되돌려주셨을 때 딸은 제게 낯익은 사람이 되어 있었습니다. 하지만 어떻게 그애가 낯익은 사람이 되었는지조차 도무지 말할 수가 없습니다. 그 과정에 대한 기억이 제겐 없습니다.\"\n\n검찰총장이 자리에 앉았고, 아버지와 딸도 함께 자리에 앉았다.\n\n그때 사건에 기묘한 정황이 떠올랐다. 당면한 과제는, 오년 전 십일월의 그 금요일 밤에 피고인이 행적이 추적되지 않은 어느 공모자와 함께 도버행 우편마차로 내려가, 눈속임으로 밤중에 어느 곳에서 우편마차를 내렸으되 그곳에 머물지 않고 거기서 다시 열두 마일 남짓을 되짚어 수비대와 조선소가 있는 곳으로 가서 거기서 정보를 수집했음을 보여주는 것이었다. 그래서 피고인이 바로 그 요구된 시각에 그 수비대 겸 조선소가 있는 도시의 한 호텔 다실에서 다른 사람을 기다리고 있었음을 확인해줄 증인이 호명되었다. 피고인 측 변호인은 이 증인을 반대신문했으나, 다른 어느 경우에도 피고인을 본 적이 없다는 것 외에는 아무런 소득이 없었는데, 그때 줄곧 법정 천장을 바라보고 있던 그 가발 쓴 신사가 작은 종잇조각에 한두 마디를 적어 그것을 구겨 그에게 던졌다. 다음번 짬에 이 종잇조각을 펼쳐본 변호인은 대단한 주의와 호기심을 띠고 피고인을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그것이 피고인이었음을 다시금 아주 확신한다고 말하는 거요?\"\n\n증인은 아주 확신했다.\n\n\"피고인과 아주 닮은 사람을 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n(증인이 말하기를) 잘못 볼 만큼 그렇게 닮은 사람은 없었다.\n\n\"저기 계신 저 신사, 저의 박식한 동료를 잘 보시오.\" 하고 종잇조각을 던진 사람을 가리키며 말했다. \"그런 다음 피고인을 잘 보시오. 어떻소? 두 사람이 서로 아주 닮지 않았소?\"\n\n박식한 동료의 행색이 방탕하다고까지는 못해도 단정치 못하고 허름하다는 점을 감안하더라도, 둘은 이렇게 비교해놓고 보니 증인뿐 아니라 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람을 놀라게 할 만큼 서로 닮아 있었다. 판사에게 박식한 동료더러 가발을 벗으라 명해달라는 청이 올라갔고, 판사가 그리 흔쾌치 않게 동의하자, 그 닮은 정도는 훨씬 더 두드러졌다. 판사가 스트라이버 씨(피고인 측 변호인)에게, 그렇다면 다음으로 카튼 씨(그 박식한 동료의 이름)를 반역죄로 재판하려는 것이냐고 물었다. 그러나 스트라이버 씨는 판사에게 아니라고 답했다. 다만 그는 증인에게, 한번 일어난 일이 두번도 일어날 수 있는지, 만일 자신의 경솔함을 보여주는 이 실례를 좀더 일찍 보았더라면 그토록 확신했을 것인지, 그것을 본 지금도 그토록 확신하는지, 그리고 그밖의 것들을 묻겠다고 했다. 그 결과는, 이 증인을 질그릇 단지처럼 박살내어, 그가 맡은 사건의 몫을 쓸모없는 잡동사니로 산산이 부숴버린 것이었다.\n\n이때쯤 크런처 씨는 증언을 좇아 따라가느라 손가락에서 녹을 한 점심거리만큼이나 뜯어내고 있었다. 이제 그는 스트라이버 씨가 마치 몸에 꼭 맞는 한 벌 옷을 입히듯이 피고인의 사건을 배심원단에게 끼워 맞추는 동안 지켜보아야 했다. 그 애국자 바사드가 어떻게 고용된 스파이이자 반역자이며, 낯빛 하나 붉히지 않고 피를 거래하는 자이고, 저주받은 유다 이후 지상에서 가장 큰 악당 가운데 하나인지를 — 사실 그는 다소 그렇게 보이기도 했다 — 보여주었다. 그 덕망 높은 하인 클라이가 어떻게 그의 친구이자 동업자였으며, 또 그럴 만한 자였는지. 어떻게 그 위조꾼이자 위증꾼들의 빈틈없는 눈이 피고인을 제물로 점찍었는지를 — 프랑스 혈통인 피고인에게 프랑스에서의 어떤 집안일 때문에 그 해협을 건너는 일이 필요했던 까닭에 — 보여주었다. 다만 그 집안일이 무엇이었는지는, 그에게 가깝고 소중한 다른 이들에 대한 배려 때문에, 제 목숨이 걸렸더라도 밝힐 수 없었다. 어떻게 그 젊은 숙녀에게서 — 그녀가 그것을 진술하며 겪은 고뇌를 그들이 지켜본 바로 그 숙녀에게서 — 비틀고 쥐어짜낸 증언이 결국 아무것도 아닌 것으로 드러나, 그렇게 함께 어울리게 된 어느 젊은 신사와 젊은 숙녀 사이에 으레 오갈 법한 그저 사소하고 순진한 정중함과 예의에 지나지 않았는지를 보여주었다. 다만 저 조지 워싱턴에 관한 언급만은 예외였으니, 그것은 너무도 터무니없고 있을 법하지 않아 무시무시한 농담 외의 다른 어떤 것으로도 여길 수 없었다. 정부가 가장 저급한 국민적 반감과 두려움에 기대어 인기를 얻으려는 이 시도에서 무너지는 것은 정부의 약점을 드러내는 일이 될 것이므로, 검찰총장이 그것을 최대한 이용한 것이라고. 그럼에도 그것은, 이런 사건들을 너무도 흔히 흉하게 만드는, 그리고 이 나라의 국사범 재판이 가득 차 있는 저 비열하고 악명 높은 종류의 증언 외에는 아무것에도 근거하고 있지 않다고. 그러나 거기서 판사가 (그것이 사실이 아니기라도 한 양 자못 근엄한 얼굴로) 끼어들어, 자신은 저 법대에 앉아 그런 암시를 용납할 수 없노라고 말했다.\n\n이어서 스트라이버 씨가 자기 측 몇 안 되는 증인들을 불렀고, 다음으로 크런처 씨는 검찰총장이 스트라이버 씨가 배심원단에게 입혀놓은 옷 한 벌을 통째로 안팎을 뒤집는 동안 지켜보아야 했다. 검찰총장은 바사드와 클라이가 자기가 생각했던 것보다 백 배는 더 훌륭하고, 피고인은 백 배는 더 나쁜 자임을 보여주었다. 마지막으로 판사 자신이 나서서, 그 옷 한 벌을 어느 때는 안팎을, 어느 때는 겉뒤를 뒤집었으나, 대체로 단연 피고인에게 입힐 수의로 손질하고 매만지는 쪽이었다.\n\n이제 배심원단이 평의에 들어갔고, 커다란 쉬파리떼가 다시 떼지어 몰려들었다.\n\n그토록 오래 법정 천장만 바라보고 앉아 있던 카튼 씨는, 이 소동 속에서도 자리도 자세도 바꾸지 않았다. 그의 박식한 동료 스트라이버 씨가 앞에 서류를 모아 쌓으며 가까이 앉은 이들과 소곤거리고 때때로 근심스레 배심원단을 흘끔거리는 동안, 모든 구경꾼이 어느 정도씩 자리를 옮기며 새로이 무리를 이루는 동안, 심지어 판사 자신마저 자리에서 일어나 자기 단상을 천천히 오락가락하며 — 그의 상태가 흥분해 있는 것이 아닌가 하는 의심이 청중의 마음에 어리는 가운데 — 거니는 동안, 이 한 사람만은 등을 기댄 채 앉아, 찢어진 법복은 반쯤 흘러내려 있고, 흐트러진 가발은 벗어두었다가 머리에 떨어진 그대로 아무렇게나 얹혀 있으며, 두 손은 호주머니에 찌르고, 두 눈은 온종일 그래왔듯 천장에 둔 채였다. 그의 태도에 깃든 무언가 유달리 무모한 데가 그에게 평판 사나운 인상을 줄 뿐 아니라, 그가 피고인에게 틀림없이 지닌 그 짙은 닮은꼴을 (둘을 함께 비교했을 때 그 순간의 진지함이 도드라지게 했던 그 닮은꼴을) 어찌나 흐려놓았던지, 이제 그를 눈여겨본 구경꾼들 가운데 많은 이들이 서로에게, 두 사람이 그렇게나 닮았다고는 도무지 생각하지 못했겠다고 말했다. 크런처 씨도 옆 사람에게 그렇게 말하고는 덧붙였다. \"내 반 기니를 걸겠수, 저 양반은 법률 일거리 하나 못 얻을 거요. 그런 거 얻을 부류로는 안 보이잖수, 그렇잖수?\"\n\n그래도 이 카튼 씨는 겉으로 보이는 것보다 그 장면의 세세한 부분을 더 많이 받아들이고 있었다. 이제 마네뜨 양의 고개가 아버지의 가슴으로 떨구어지는 순간, 그것을 맨 먼저 알아채고 또렷이 소리내어 말한 것이 바로 그였으니 말이다. \"정리! 저 젊은 숙녀를 살피시오. 저 신사분이 그녀를 데리고 나가는 것을 도와드리시오. 그녀가 쓰러지려는 게 안 보이오!\"\n\n그녀가 부축되어 나갈 때 많은 이들이 그녀를 가엾이 여겼고, 그녀의 아버지에게도 많은 동정을 보냈다. 투옥의 나날들을 떠올리는 것이 그에게는 분명 크나큰 괴로움이었다. 신문을 받을 때 그는 격렬한 내면의 동요를 드러냈고, 그를 늙어 보이게 하던 그 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴, 혹은 골똘히 시름하는 표정이 그때 이후 무거운 구름처럼 그에게 드리워 있었다. 그가 밖으로 나갈 때, 돌아서서 잠시 멈춰 섰던 배심원단이 그 대표를 통해 입을 열었다.\n\n그들은 의견이 일치하지 않아 퇴정하기를 바랐다. 판사는 (어쩌면 조지 워싱턴이 머릿속에 있었던 듯) 그들의 의견이 일치하지 않는 것에 다소 놀라움을 드러냈으나, 감시와 보호하에 그들이 퇴정해도 좋다는 뜻을 밝히고 자신도 퇴정했다. 재판은 온종일 이어졌고, 이제 법정의 등불에 불이 켜지고 있었다. 배심원단이 오래도록 나와 있지 않으리라는 소문이 돌기 시작했다. 구경꾼들은 요기를 하러 하나둘 빠져나갔고, 피고인은 피고석 뒤쪽으로 물러나 앉았다.\n\n젊은 숙녀와 그녀의 아버지가 나갈 때 함께 나갔던 로리 씨가 이제 다시 나타나, 제리에게 손짓했다. 제리는 관심이 느슨해진 틈에 어렵잖게 그에게 다가갈 수 있었다.\n\n\"제리, 뭐 좀 먹고 싶거든 그렇게 하게. 다만 가까운 데서 대기하고 있게나. 배심원단이 들어오면 자네는 틀림없이 알게 될 걸세. 그들보다 한순간도 뒤처지지 말게. 평결을 은행에 가지고 돌아가는 일을 자네에게 맡기고 싶으니 말일세. 자네는 내가 아는 가장 빠른 심부름꾼이고, 나보다 한참 앞서 템플 바에 닿을 테니까.\"\n\n제리는 손마디로 두드릴 만한 이마가 겨우 있을 뿐이었는데, 그 이마를 손마디로 두드려 이 전갈과 일 실링에 대한 답례로 삼았다. 바로 그때 카튼 씨가 다가와 로리 씨의 팔을 건드렸다.\n\n\"그 젊은 숙녀는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"몹시 괴로워하고 있습니다. 하지만 아버지가 그녀를 달래주고 있고, 법정에서 나온 덕분에 한결 나아진 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 피고인에게 그렇게 전해주지요. 당신처럼 점잖은 은행의 신사가 그에게 공공연히 말을 거는 모습을 보여서야 안 될 일이니까요, 아시겠지만.\"\n\n로리 씨는 마음속으로 그 문제를 따져보고 있었음을 의식하기라도 한 듯 얼굴을 붉혔고, 카튼 씨는 난간 바깥쪽으로 나아갔다. 법정 밖으로 나가는 길이 그쪽으로 나 있었던 터라, 제리도 온 신경을 곤두세운 채 그를 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"다네이 씨!\"\n\n피고인이 곧장 앞으로 나왔다.\n\n\"증인 마네뜨 양에 대해 듣고 싶어 안달이 나 있겠지요. 그녀는 곧 괜찮아질 겁니다. 당신은 그녀가 가장 심하게 동요했을 때를 본 거니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 동요의 원인이 된 것이 깊이 송구합니다. 저의 간곡한 사례와 함께, 그녀에게 그렇게 전해주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"네, 그럴 수 있습니다. 당신이 청한다면, 그렇게 하지요.\"\n\n카튼 씨의 태도는 어찌나 무심한지 거의 무례할 지경이었다. 그는 피고인에게서 몸을 반쯤 돌린 채, 팔꿈치를 난간에 괴고 비스듬히 기대 서 있었다.\n\n\"청하는 바입니다. 진심 어린 감사를 받아주십시오.\"\n\n\"무엇을,\" 하고 카튼이 여전히 그를 향해 반쯤만 돌아선 채 말했다. \"기대하고 있습니까, 다네이 씨?\"\n\n\"최악을요.\"\n\n\"그게 기대하기에 가장 현명한 일이고, 또 가장 그럴 법한 일이지요. 하지만 그들이 퇴정한 것은 당신에게 유리하다고 봅니다.\"\n\n법정에서 나가는 길에 어정거리는 것은 허락되지 않았으므로, 제리는 더는 듣지 못했다. 다만 그들을 — 이목구비는 그토록 서로 닮았으되 태도는 그토록 서로 다른 두 사람을 — 나란히 서서 둘 다 머리 위의 유리에 비친 모습으로 남겨둔 채 떠났다.\n\n도둑과 무뢰한이 들끓는 아래층 통로에서, 양고기 파이와 에일로 거든다 해도, 한 시간 반이 무겁게 절뚝거리며 흘러갔다. 그 쉰 목소리의 심부름꾼은 그 요기를 마친 뒤 긴 의자에 불편하게 걸터앉아 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 법정으로 이어지는 계단을 우르르 올라오는 요란한 웅성거림과 빠른 인파의 물결에 휩쓸려 함께 떠밀려 갔다.\n\n\"제리! 제리!\" 그가 문에 이르렀을 때 로리 씨가 벌써 문간에서 부르고 있었다.\n\n\"여기 있습니다, 나리! 다시 비집고 들어오기가 한바탕 싸움입니다요. 저 여기 있습니다, 나리!\"\n\n로리 씨가 인파를 헤치고 그에게 종이 한 장을 건넸다. \"어서! 받았나?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n종이에는 \"무죄 방면\"이라는 말이 급히 휘갈겨져 있었다.\n\n\"또 '소생'이라는 전갈을 보내셨더라면,\" 하고 제리가 돌아서며 중얼거렸다. \"이번엔 무슨 뜻인지 알아들었을 텐데 말이지.\"\n\n그는 올드베일리를 벗어날 때까지 다른 무엇을 말할, 아니 생각할 겨를조차 없었다. 군중이 어찌나 거센 기세로 쏟아져 나오는지 하마터면 그를 넘어뜨릴 뻔했고, 좌절한 쉬파리떼가 다른 썩은 고기를 찾아 흩어지기라도 하듯 요란한 윙윙거림이 거리로 휩쓸려 나갔기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. Congratulatory\n\n법정의 어슴푸레한 통로에서, 온종일 그곳에서 끓어오르던 인간 잡탕의 마지막 찌끼가 빠져나가고 있을 무렵, 마네뜨 박사와 그의 딸 루시 마네뜨, 로리 씨, 변호인 측 사무 변호사, 그리고 그 변호사 스트라이버 씨가, 방금 풀려난 찰스 다네이 씨 주위에 모여 서서, 죽음을 면한 그를 축하하고 있었다.\n\n훨씬 밝은 빛 아래에서라도, 얼굴에 지성이 어리고 자세가 꼿꼿한 마네뜨 박사에게서 빠리의 다락방 구두장이를 알아보기란 어려웠을 것이다. 그러나 누구든 그를 한번 보면 다시 보지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 관찰의 기회가 그의 낮고 무거운 목소리의 구슬픈 억양에까지, 그리고 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 이따금 그를 뒤덮는 그 멍한 상태에까지 미치지 못했다 하더라도 말이다. 한가지 외부적인 원인, 곧 그의 오래 끌어온 고통에 대한 언급은, 재판에서 그러했듯이 언제나 그의 영혼 깊은 곳에서 이 상태를 불러일으키곤 했지만, 그것은 또한 본디 제 스스로 떠올라 그에게 음울함을 드리우기도 했으니, 그의 사연을 모르는 이들에게는 그것이 마치 실제 바스띠유의 그림자가 — 그 실체는 삼백 마일 떨어져 있는데도 — 여름 햇살에 그의 위로 드리워진 것을 본 것만큼이나 이해할 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n오직 그의 딸만이 이 검은 우수를 그의 마음에서 걷어낼 힘을 지녔다. 그녀는 그를 그의 불행 너머의 과거와, 그의 불행 너머의 현재로 이어주는 황금실이었다. 그녀의 목소리, 그녀의 얼굴빛, 그녀의 손길은 거의 언제나 그에게 강력하고도 이로운 영향을 미쳤다. 절대적으로 언제나 그런 것은 아니었으니, 그녀는 자신의 힘이 듣지 않았던 몇몇 경우를 떠올릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그런 경우는 드물고 사소했으며, 그녀는 그것이 다 지나갔다고 믿었다.\n\n다네이 씨는 그녀의 손에 열렬하고 고마운 마음으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 스트라이버 씨에게 돌아서서 따뜻하게 감사를 표했다. 스트라이버 씨는 서른을 갓 넘긴 사내였으나 제 나이보다 스무 살은 더 들어 보였고, 몸집이 우람하고 목소리가 크며 얼굴이 붉고 무뚝뚝했으며 섬세함이라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 약점이 있었는데, (도덕적으로든 신체적으로든) 무리와 대화 속으로 어깨를 들이밀며 밀고 들어가는 저돌적인 방식을 지녔으니, 이는 그가 인생에서도 어깨로 밀치며 출세해 나가리라는 것을 능히 짐작케 했다.\n\n그는 아직도 가발과 법복을 걸친 채, 자신의 방금 전 의뢰인을 향해 어찌나 떡 버티고 섰던지 죄 없는 로리 씨를 무리에서 말끔히 밀어내 버리며 이렇게 말했다. \"명예롭게 당신을 빼내드려 기쁩니다, 다네이 씨. 그것은 파렴치한 기소였습니다, 지독히도 파렴치한 기소였지요. 그렇다고 해서 성공할 가능성이 덜한 것도 아니었지만 말입니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 두가지 의미에서 평생 갚을 신세를 제게 지우셨습니다.\" 방금 전 그의 의뢰인이 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"당신을 위해 최선을 다했습니다, 다네이 씨. 그리고 제 최선은 다른 누구의 최선 못지않다고 믿습니다.\"\n\n여기서 누군가가 \"훨씬 더 낫지요\"라고 말해야 마땅했으므로, 로리 씨가 그 말을 했다. 어쩌면 전혀 사심이 없었던 것은 아니고, 도로 자기를 비집고 들어가려는 사심 어린 목적에서였을 테지만 말이다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하시오?\" 스트라이버 씨가 말했다. \"음! 당신은 온종일 자리에 계셨으니 잘 아시겠지요. 당신 또한 사업가이시니까.\"\n\n\"그리고 사업가로서,\" 법에 정통한 그 변호사가 앞서 무리에서 그를 밀어냈던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 이제 다시 무리 안으로 밀어 넣어준 로리 씨가 입을 열었다. \"사업가로서 저는 마네뜨 박사께 호소하여 이 모임을 끝내고 우리 모두를 각자의 집으로 보내자고 명해 주시길 청하겠습니다. 루시 양은 안색이 좋지 않고, 다네이 씨는 끔찍한 하루를 보냈으며, 우리는 지칠 대로 지쳤으니까요.\"\n\n\"당신 사정이나 말씀하시오, 로리 씨.\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"나는 아직 밤새 해야 할 일이 있소. 당신 사정이나 말씀하시오.\"\n\n\"제 사정을 말하는 겁니다.\" 로리 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 다네이 씨와 루시 양을 위해서도 말입니다. 그리고 — 루시 양, 제가 우리 모두를 대신해 말해도 되지 않겠습니까?\" 그는 그녀에게 또렷이 그 질문을 던지며, 그녀의 아버지를 흘끗 바라보았다.\n\n그의 얼굴은 다네이를 향한 자못 기이한 표정으로, 말하자면 얼어붙어 있었다. 골똘한 그 눈길은 혐오와 불신의 찌푸림으로 깊어졌고, 거기엔 두려움마저 섞여 있었다. 그 야릇한 표정을 띤 채 그의 생각은 어딘가로 떠돌아가 있었다.\n\n\"아버지.\" 루시가 그의 손 위에 가만히 자신의 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n그는 천천히 그 그림자를 떨쳐내고 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"집으로 갈까요, 아버지?\"\n\n긴 숨을 내쉬며 그는 \"그래\"라고 대답했다.\n\n무죄 방면된 죄수의 친구들은, 그가 그날 밤에는 풀려나지 못하리라는 인상을 받고 — 그 인상은 다네이 자신이 퍼뜨린 것이었다 — 이미 흩어진 뒤였다. 통로의 불은 거의 다 꺼졌고, 철문은 삐걱대고 덜컹대는 소리와 함께 닫히고 있었으며, 그 음산한 곳은 이튿날 아침 교수대와 칼, 채찍 기둥과 낙인 인두에 쏠릴 관심이 다시 사람들을 불러 모을 때까지 텅 비어 있을 터였다. 아버지와 다네이 씨 사이를 걸어, 루시 마네뜨는 바깥 공기 속으로 나섰다. 삯마차가 불려 왔고, 아버지와 딸은 그것을 타고 떠났다.\n\n스트라이버 씨는 통로에서 그들과 헤어져, 어깨로 밀치며 법복실로 되돌아갔다. 그 무리에 끼지도, 그들 가운데 누구와도 말 한마디 나누지도 않은 채, 그림자가 가장 짙게 드리운 벽에 기대어 서 있던 또 한 사람이, 나머지 사람들의 뒤를 따라 소리 없이 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나와, 마차가 떠나갈 때까지 지켜보았다. 그는 이제 로리 씨와 다네이 씨가 보도 위에 서 있는 곳으로 다가섰다.\n\n\"자, 로리 씨! 이제 사업가들이 다네이 씨께 말을 걸어도 되겠지요?\"\n\n그날의 재판 과정에서 카턴 씨가 맡은 몫을 인정해 준 사람은 아무도 없었다. 아무도 그것을 알지 못했다. 그는 법복을 벗고 있었는데, 그렇다고 겉모습이 더 나아 보이지도 않았다.\n\n\"마음씨 좋은 충동과 사업가의 체면 사이에서 사업가의 마음이 갈라질 때, 그 안에서 어떤 갈등이 벌어지는지 아신다면, 다네이 씨, 재미있어하실 겁니다.\"\n\n로리 씨는 얼굴을 붉히며 발끈해서 말했다. \"그 말씀은 전에도 하셨소이다, 선생. 우리 사업가들은, 어느 상회를 섬기는 몸이라 제 마음대로 할 수 없는 처지요. 우리는 우리 자신보다 상회를 더 생각해야 하는 사람들이오.\"\n\n\"나도 알아요, 나도 압니다.\" 카턴 씨가 무심하게 대꾸했다. \"발끈하지 마시오, 로리 씨. 당신은 다른 누구 못지않게 훌륭한 사람이오, 의심할 바 없이. 감히 말하건대, 더 낫지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 정말이지, 선생,\" 로리 씨가 그에게 개의치 않고 말을 이었다. \"이 일과 당신이 무슨 상관인지 도무지 모르겠소. 당신보다 한참 연장자로서 이런 말을 하는 것을 양해해 주신다면, 정말이지 이게 당신이 관여할 일인지 모르겠단 말이오.\"\n\n\"관여할 일이라! 원 별말씀을, 나야 관여할 일이라곤 아무것도 없소이다.\" 카턴 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그것 참 안된 일이군요, 선생.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각하오.\"\n\n\"관여할 일이 있었더라면,\" 로리 씨가 말을 이었다. \"아마 그 일에 신경을 쓰셨겠지요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 천만에요! 그러지 않았을 겁니다.\" 카턴 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이거 참, 선생!\" 그의 무관심에 완전히 달아오른 로리 씨가 소리쳤다. \"일이란 아주 좋은 것이고, 아주 존경할 만한 것이오. 그리고 선생, 만일 일이 그 나름의 제약과 침묵과 방해를 강요한다 해도, 다네이 씨는 너그러운 젊은 신사로서 그런 사정을 어떻게 헤아려야 하는지 아실 거요. 다네이 씨, 안녕히 가십시오, 신의 가호가 함께하기를! 오늘 당신이 번창하고 행복한 삶을 위해 살아남으셨기를 바랍니다. — 가마 여기로!\"\n\n어쩌면 그 변호사뿐 아니라 자기 자신에게도 조금 화가 났던지, 로리 씨는 허둥지둥 가마에 올라 텔슨 상회로 실려 갔다. 포도주 냄새를 풍기며 도무지 멀쩡히 깨어 있는 것 같지 않은 카턴은, 그제야 웃음을 터뜨리며 다네이를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"당신과 나를 한자리에 던져놓다니, 이것 참 묘한 우연이군요. 이 거리의 돌바닥 위에 당신의 분신과 단둘이 서 있다니, 당신에게는 참 묘한 밤이겠습니다?\"\n\n\"아직은 도무지,\" 찰스 다네이가 대답했다. \"이 세상에 다시 속한 것 같지가 않습니다.\"\n\n\"놀랄 것도 없지요. 당신이 또 다른 세상으로 가는 길에 꽤 멀리까지 나아갔던 게 그리 오래된 일도 아니니까요. 목소리에 기운이 없군요.\"\n\n\"제가 정말 기운이 없는 모양이라는 생각이 들기 시작하는군요.\"\n\n\"그럼 도대체 왜 식사를 안 하시오? 나는, 저 멍청이들이 당신이 이 세상에 속할지 다른 세상에 속할지를 따지고 있는 동안에 혼자 식사를 했소. 잘 먹을 수 있는 가장 가까운 선술집을 알려드리리다.\"\n\n그는 다네이의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고는, 그를 데리고 러드게이트 언덕을 내려가 플리트 거리로, 그리고 지붕 덮인 통로를 따라 올라가 어느 선술집으로 들어갔다. 거기서 그들은 작은 방으로 안내되었고, 찰스 다네이는 곧 변변한 정식과 좋은 포도주로 기력을 되찾고 있었다. 그동안 카턴은 같은 식탁의 맞은편에 앉아, 제 몫의 포트와인 병을 앞에 두고, 절반쯤은 거만한 태도를 잔뜩 부린 채였다.\n\n\"이제 다시 이 지상의 질서에 속해 있다는 게 실감이 나시오, 다네이 씨?\"\n\n\"시간과 장소에 관해서는 끔찍하게 혼란스럽습니다. 그래도 그 정도는 느낄 만큼은 회복되었습니다.\"\n\n\"그거야말로 엄청난 만족이겠소이다!\"\n\n그는 그 말을 씁쓸하게 내뱉고는 다시 잔을 채웠다. 큼직한 잔이었다.\n\n\"나로 말하면, 내가 가진 가장 큰 소망은 내가 이 세상에 속해 있다는 사실을 잊는 것이오. 이 세상에는 나를 위한 좋은 것이라곤 없소 — 이런 포도주를 빼면 말이오 — 나 또한 이 세상을 위한 좋은 것이 못 되고. 그러니 그 점에서 우리는 별로 닮지 않았소. 사실, 당신과 나는 그 어떤 점에서도 별로 닮지 않았다는 생각이 들기 시작하는구려.\"\n\n그날의 격정에 혼란스러운데다, 거동이 거친 이 분신과 이 자리에 함께 있는 것이 마치 꿈만 같아서, 찰스 다네이는 무어라 대답해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했고, 끝내 아무 대답도 하지 못했다.\n\n\"이제 식사를 마치셨으니,\" 카턴이 이윽고 말했다. \"건배 한번 청하시는 게 어떻소, 다네이 씨. 축배를 드는 게 어떻소?\"\n\n\"무엇을 위한 건배요? 무슨 축배 말이오?\"\n\n\"이런, 그건 당신 혀끝에 맴돌고 있잖소. 그래야 마땅하고, 분명히 그럴 테고, 맹세코 거기 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 마네뜨 양을 위하여!\"\n\n\"그럼, 마네뜨 양을 위하여!\"\n\n축배를 들이켜는 동안 일행의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보던 카턴은, 잔을 어깨 너머로 벽에다 내던졌고, 잔은 산산조각이 났다. 그러고는 종을 울려 또 한 잔을 가져오라 일렀다.\n\n\"어둠 속에서 마차에 부축해 태워줄 만한 어여쁜 아가씨더군요, 다네이 씨!\" 그는 새 술잔을 채우며 말했다.\n\n가벼운 찌푸림과 짤막한 \"그렇소\"가 그 대답이었다.\n\n\"동정받고 눈물 흘려질 만한 어여쁜 아가씨라니! 기분이 어떻소? 그런 동정과 연민의 대상이 되는 것이, 목숨을 걸고 재판을 받을 만한 가치가 있는 일이오, 다네이 씨?\"\n\n다네이는 또다시 한마디도 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 당신 전갈을 전해주었을 때, 그녀는 그걸 받고 무척이나 기뻐하더군요. 기뻐하는 기색을 내보인 건 아니지만, 기뻐했으리라 짐작하오.\"\n\n그 언급은 이 불쾌한 일행이 그날의 위기에서 제 발로 자청해 자신을 도와주었다는 사실을, 다네이에게 때맞춰 일깨워 주었다. 그는 대화를 그 점으로 돌려, 그에게 그 일에 대해 감사를 표했다.\n\n\"나는 감사 따위 바라지도 않고, 받을 자격도 없소.\" 무심한 대꾸였다. \"우선 그건 아무것도 아닌 일이었고, 둘째로 내가 왜 그랬는지도 모르겠소. 다네이 씨, 한가지 물어봅시다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이요. 당신의 호의에 대한 작은 보답이 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"내가 당신을 특별히 좋아한다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"솔직히, 카턴 씨,\" 묘하게 당혹스러워하며 상대가 대답했다. \"그런 질문을 제 자신에게 던져본 적이 없소이다.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금 자신에게 그 질문을 던져보시오.\"\n\n\"좋아하는 것처럼 행동하셨소. 하지만 좋아하시는 것 같지는 않습니다.\"\n\n\"나도 좋아하는 것 같지 않소.\" 카턴이 말했다. \"당신의 이해력에 대해 아주 좋게 보기 시작하는구려.\"\n\n\"그렇더라도,\" 다네이가 종을 울리려고 일어서며 말을 이었다. \"그 때문에 제가 셈을 치르고, 양쪽 다 악감정 없이 헤어지지 못할 까닭은 없겠지요.\"\n\n카턴이 \"결단코 없소이다!\"라고 받자 다네이가 종을 울렸다. \"계산을 통째로 치르시려오?\" 카턴이 말했다. 그가 그렇다고 대답하자, \"그럼 같은 포도주로 한 파인트 더 가져오게, 종업원, 그리고 열시에 와서 나를 깨워주게.\"\n\n계산을 마치고, 찰스 다네이는 일어서서 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다. 그 인사에 답하지도 않은 채, 카턴 역시 어딘가 반항적인 위협의 기색을 풍기며 일어서서 말했다. \"마지막으로 한마디 하리다, 다네이 씨. 내가 취했다고 생각하시오?\"\n\n\"술을 드신 것 같습니다, 카턴 씨.\"\n\n\"생각이라? 내가 술을 마셨다는 걸 당신은 알고 있잖소.\"\n\n\"꼭 말해야 한다면, 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 까닭도 알아두시오. 나는 실의에 빠진 막일꾼이오, 선생. 나는 이 세상 어느 누구도 마음에 두지 않고, 이 세상 어느 누구도 나를 마음에 두지 않소.\"\n\n\"몹시 안타까운 일이군요. 당신의 재능을 더 잘 쓰실 수도 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 다네이 씨. 아닐지도 모르고. 하지만 당신의 그 멀쩡한 얼굴로 우쭐대지는 마시오. 그게 어떻게 될지 당신은 모르니까. 잘 가시오!\"\n\n홀로 남게 되자, 이 기이한 사내는 촛불을 집어 들고 벽에 걸린 거울로 다가가, 그 안의 제 모습을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다.\n\n\"너는 저 사내를 특별히 좋아하느냐?\" 그는 거울에 비친 제 모습을 향해 중얼거렸다. \"어째서 너를 닮은 사내를 특별히 좋아해야 한단 말이냐? 네 안에는 좋아할 만한 구석이라곤 없잖느냐. 너도 그건 알 테지. 아, 빌어먹을 놈! 너 스스로를 얼마나 망쳐놓았느냐! 어떤 사내에게 마음이 끌릴 만한 그럴듯한 이유로구나, 그가 네가 어디서부터 굴러떨어졌는지, 네가 어떤 사람이 될 수도 있었는지를 보여준다는 것이! 저자와 처지를 바꿔보아라, 그러면 너도 그가 그러했듯 저 푸른 눈동자의 바라봄을 받고, 저 떨리는 얼굴의 연민을 받았겠느냐? 자, 솔직한 말로 결판을 내보아라! 너는 저 녀석이 미운 게다.\"\n\n그는 위안을 얻으려 한 파인트의 포도주에 의지해, 몇 분 만에 그것을 죄다 들이켜고는, 두 팔에 엎드려 잠들어 버렸다. 머리카락이 식탁 위로 흐트러져 있었고, 촛농이 길게 늘어진 수의처럼 그의 위로 뚝뚝 떨어져 내렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Jackal\n\n술을 마셔대는 시절이었고, 대부분의 사람들이 독하게 마셨다. 그런 습관에서 세월이 가져다준 개선이 어찌나 큰지, 완벽한 신사로서의 명성에 아무런 손상도 입지 않은 채 한 사람이 하룻밤 사이에 들이켤 수 있던 포도주와 펀치의 양을 적당히 진술하기만 해도, 요즘 사람들에게는 터무니없는 과장으로 보일 것이다. 법이라는 학식 있는 직업도 바커스적 기질에서는 그 어떤 학식 있는 직업에도 결코 뒤지지 않았다. 또한 이미 크고 수지맞는 변론 업무를 향해 빠르게 어깨로 밀치며 나아가고 있던 스트라이버 씨도, 법조계 경주의 더 메마른 부분에서와 마찬가지로 이 점에서도 자기 동료들에게 뒤지지 않았다.\n\n* 바커스적: 술과 향락의 신 바커스(디오니소스)에서 유래한 표현으로, 흥청대며 술을 마시는 성향을 가리킨다.\n올드 베일리에서 인기를 끌었고 순회재판에서도 마찬가지였던 스트라이버 씨는, 자신이 올라선 사다리의 아래쪽 가로대를 조심스레 잘라내기 시작했다. 이제 순회재판과 올드 베일리는 그들의 총아를 특별히, 갈망하는 두 팔로 불러들여야 했으며, 왕좌재판소에서 대법관의 얼굴을 향해 어깨로 밀치고 나아가는 스트라이버 씨의 불그레한 얼굴은, 무성한 정원 가득 화려하게 피어난 동료 꽃들 사이에서 해를 향해 솟아오르는 커다란 해바라기처럼, 가발의 화단을 뚫고 솟구치는 모습을 날마다 볼 수 있었다.\n\n* 올드 베일리: 런던의 중앙형사재판소.\n법조계에서 한때 주목된 바, 스트라이버 씨는 말솜씨 좋고, 거리낌 없으며, 재빠르고, 대담한 사람이었으나, 변호사의 가장 두드러지고 가장 필요한 자질에 속하는, 진술 더미에서 핵심을 뽑아내는 능력만은 갖추지 못했다. 그러나 이 점에서 그에게 놀라운 진전이 찾아왔다. 일을 더 많이 맡을수록 그 골수와 정수를 파고드는 그의 능력은 점점 더 커지는 듯했으며, 밤늦도록 시드니 카턴과 흥청거리며 앉아 있었어도 아침이면 늘 요점을 손끝에 쥐고 있었다.\n\n사람들 가운데 가장 게으르고 가장 가망 없는 자인 시드니 카턴은 스트라이버의 든든한 동맹이었다. 두 사람이 힐러리 개정기와 미카엘 축일 사이에 함께 마셔댄 술이라면 왕의 군함이라도 띄울 수 있었을 것이다. 스트라이버가 어디서든 사건을 맡을 때면 카턴이 늘 거기 있었으니, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 법정 천장을 응시하고 있었다. 둘은 같은 순회재판을 돌았고, 거기서도 늘 하던 흥청망청을 밤늦도록 이어갔으며, 카턴은 환한 대낮에 타락한 고양이처럼 휘청거리며 슬그머니 하숙집으로 돌아가는 모습이 목격된다는 소문이 돌았다. 마침내 이 일에 관심 있는 자들 사이에서는, 시드니 카턴이 결코 사자는 되지 못하겠지만 놀랍도록 훌륭한 자칼이며, 그 미천한 자격으로 스트라이버에게 봉사와 시중을 든다는 말이 퍼지기 시작했다.\n* 힐러리 개정기와 미카엘 축일: 영국 법정의 개정기를 나타내는 구분. 힐러리 개정기는 겨울철, 미카엘 축일은 9월 29일로 가을철 개정기를 가리킨다.\n\n\"열시입니다, 손님.\" 술집에서 그가 깨워달라고 일러둔 남자가 말했다. \"열시입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"열시입니다, 손님.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 밤 열시라고?\"\n\n\"네, 손님. 깨워달라고 하셨잖습니까.\"\n\n\"아! 생각났어. 됐어, 됐어.\"\n\n다시 잠들어 보려는 멍한 시도를 몇번 했으나, 남자가 오 분 동안 끊임없이 불을 들쑤시며 능숙하게 막아냈고, 그는 일어나 모자를 휙 눌러쓰고 밖으로 나갔다. 그는 템플로 접어들어, 왕좌재판소 보도와 페이퍼 빌딩의 포도(鋪道)를 두 차례 거닐며 정신을 추스른 뒤, 스트라이버의 사무실로 들어섰다.\n\n이런 회합에는 한번도 거든 적 없는 스트라이버의 서기는 집으로 돌아갔고, 주인장 스트라이버가 문을 열었다. 그는 슬리퍼를 신고 헐렁한 실내복을 걸쳤으며, 한결 편안하라고 목을 풀어헤치고 있었다. 그의 눈가에는 다소 사납고, 긴장되고, 그을린 듯한 자국이 있었는데, 이는 제프리스의 초상화 이래로 그와 같은 부류의 모든 방탕한 자들에게서 볼 수 있는 것이며, 온갖 예술적 분장 아래서도 모든 음주 시대의 초상화마다 더듬어 찾아낼 수 있는 것이었다.\n* 제프리스: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 판사 조지 제프리스를 가리킨다.\n\n\"좀 늦었군, 기억력 양반.\" 스트라이버가 말했다.\n\n\"늘 오던 시간쯤이지. 십오 분쯤 늦었을지도.\"\n\n그들은 책으로 둘러싸이고 서류가 어질러진 침침한 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 활활 타오르는 불이 있었다. 화로 선반 위에서 주전자가 김을 뿜었고, 서류 잔해 한가운데 탁자가 번들거렸으니, 그 위에는 포도주와 브랜디와 럼과 설탕과 레몬이 그득했다.\n\n\"보아하니 자네 술을 한병 비웠군, 시드니.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 두 병인 것 같은데. 오늘 의뢰인과 저녁을 먹었거든. 아니, 그가 먹는 걸 지켜봤다고나 할까. 그게 그거지!\"\n\n\"오늘 신원 확인에 자네가 끌어댄 그 논점은 보기 드문 것이었어, 시드니. 그걸 어떻게 떠올렸나? 언제 생각이 났어?\"\n\n\"그자가 꽤 잘생긴 친구라고 생각했고, 나도 운이 좀 따랐다면 그와 별반 다르지 않은 부류가 되었으리라고 생각했지.\"\n\n스트라이버 씨는 일찌감치 나온 똥배가 출렁이도록 웃어댔다.\n\n\"자네하고 자네 운이라니, 시드니! 일이나 하게, 일이나 해.\"\n\n자칼은 시무룩하게 옷매무새를 풀고, 옆방으로 가서 찬물이 가득 든 커다란 항아리와 대야와 수건 한두 장을 들고 돌아왔다. 수건을 물에 적셔 대충 짜낸 뒤, 보기에 끔찍한 모양새로 머리에 둘둘 감고는, 탁자에 앉아 말했다. \"자, 준비됐어!\"\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 졸여낼 게 그리 많지 않아, 기억력 양반.\" 스트라이버 씨가 서류를 뒤적이며 유쾌하게 말했다.\n\n\"얼마나 되는데?\"\n\n\"두 묶음뿐이야.\"\n\n\"제일 고약한 것부터 줘.\"\n\n\"여기 있네, 시드니. 자, 시작!\"\n\n그러고 나서 사자는 술 탁자 한쪽에 놓인 소파에 등을 대고 자리를 잡았고, 자칼은 그 맞은편에 놓인 자기 서류투성이 탁자에 앉았는데, 술병과 잔들을 손 닿는 곳에 갖춰두고 있었다. 둘 다 거리낌 없이 술 탁자에 의지했으나, 저마다 방식이 달랐다. 사자는 대개 두 손을 허리띠에 찔러넣은 채 비스듬히 기대어 불을 바라보거나, 이따금 가벼운 문서 따위를 건성으로 들춰보았다. 자칼은 미간을 찌푸린 채 골똘한 얼굴로 일에 얼마나 깊이 빠져 있던지, 잔을 향해 뻗은 손조차 눈으로 좇지 않았으니, 그 손은 종종 입에 댈 잔을 찾기까지 일 분 남짓 더듬거리곤 했다. 두세 번쯤 다루던 문제가 어찌나 까다로워지던지, 자칼은 일어나 수건을 새로 적셔야만 한다고 느꼈다. 항아리와 대야로의 이 순례에서 그는 말로는 도저히 형용할 수 없을 만큼 기괴한 모양으로 축축한 머리쓰개를 두르고 돌아왔는데, 그 진지하고 안달하는 표정 때문에 한층 더 우스꽝스러웠다.\n\n마침내 자칼은 사자를 위한 알찬 한 끼 식사를 차려내어 그에게 내놓았다. 사자는 그것을 조심스럽고 신중하게 받아들어, 그 가운데서 골라내고 그것에 대해 논평했으며, 자칼은 그 두 가지를 다 거들었다. 그 식사가 충분히 검토되자, 사자는 다시 두 손을 허리띠에 찔러넣고 누워 깊이 생각에 잠겼다. 그러자 자칼은 목구멍을 위해 한 잔 가득 들이켜고 머리에 다시 한번 물을 적셔 기운을 차린 뒤, 두번째 식사를 차리는 일에 매달렸다. 이것 역시 같은 방식으로 사자에게 제공되었고, 새벽 세시를 알리는 종이 울릴 때까지 처리되지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 이제 끝났으니, 시드니, 펀치를 한 잔 가득 채우게.\" 스트라이버 씨가 말했다.\n\n자칼은 다시 김을 뿜고 있던 수건을 머리에서 벗겨내고, 몸을 한번 떨고, 하품을 하고, 부르르 진저리를 치고는, 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n\"오늘 그 검찰측 증인들 건에서 자네는 아주 빈틈없었어, 시드니. 질문마다 먹혀들었지.\"\n\n\"난 늘 빈틈없잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"부정하진 않겠네. 무엇이 자네 성미를 거칠게 했나? 펀치를 좀 부어 다시 매끄럽게 가라앉히게.\"\n\n자칼은 못마땅한 듯 투덜대며 또다시 시키는 대로 했다.\n\n\"옛 슈루즈베리 학교 시절의 옛 시드니 카턴이로군.\" 스트라이버는 그를 현재와 과거 속에서 찬찬히 살피며 머리 위로 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그 옛 시소 같은 시드니. 금세 올라갔다가 다음 순간엔 내려가고, 한때는 기운이 넘쳤다가 또 한때는 풀이 죽고!\"\n\n\"아!\" 상대가 한숨을 쉬며 대꾸했다. \"그래! 똑같은 시드니에, 똑같은 운이지. 그때도 난 다른 애들 숙제를 대신 해주면서 정작 내 것은 좀처럼 하지 않았어.\"\n\n\"그건 또 왜?\"\n\n\"누가 알겠나. 그게 내 방식이었던 모양이지.\"\n\n그는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣고 두 다리를 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 채 앉아, 불을 바라보았다.\n\n\"카턴.\" 그의 친구가 윽박지르는 태도로 그를 향해 자세를 곧추세우며 말했는데, 마치 그 난로가 꾸준한 노력을 벼려내는 용광로이고, 옛 슈루즈베리 학교 시절의 옛 시드니 카턴을 위해 해줄 단 하나의 섬세한 일이란 그를 그 안으로 떠밀어 넣는 것이기라도 한 듯했다. \"자네 방식은 절름발이 같은 방식이고, 늘 그래왔어. 자넨 도무지 기력도 목적도 끌어모으질 못해. 나를 보게.\"\n\n\"아, 성가셔 죽겠군!\" 시드니는 한결 가볍고 더 너그러운 웃음으로 대꾸했다. \"제발 자네가 도덕군자 노릇 좀 하지 마!\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 지금의 이걸 이뤘겠나?\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 지금의 이 일을 해내겠어?\"\n\n\"내게 돈을 주어 자네를 돕게 한 덕도 얼마간 있겠지. 하지만 그 일로 나를, 아니면 허공을 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 가치는 없어. 자넨 하고 싶은 일은 하고 마는 사람이야. 자넨 늘 맨 앞줄에 있었고, 난 늘 뒤에 있었지.\"\n\n\"난 맨 앞줄로 비집고 들어가야만 했네. 거기서 태어난 게 아니잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그 의식에 내가 참석한 건 아니지만, 내 생각엔 자넨 거기서 태어났어.\" 카턴이 말했다. 이 말에 그는 다시 웃었고, 둘 다 웃었다.\n\n\"슈루즈베리 이전에도, 슈루즈베리에서도, 슈루즈베리 이후로도 줄곧.\" 카턴이 말을 이었다. \"자넨 자네 자리로 떨어졌고, 난 내 자리로 떨어졌어. 우리가 빠리의 학생 구역에서 동급생으로 지내며 프랑스어와 프랑스 법, 그밖에 별 쓸모도 없던 프랑스의 부스러기들을 주워 모으던 시절에조차, 자넨 늘 어딘가에 있었고, 난 늘 아무 데도 없었지.\"\n\n\"그게 누구 탓이었나?\"\n\n\"맹세코, 그게 자네 탓이 아니라고는 장담 못하겠네. 자넨 늘 밀어붙이고 헤집고 어깨로 밀치고 앞질러 나갔는데, 그 쉴 줄 모르는 정도가 어찌나 심하던지, 나로선 녹슬어 가라앉아 쉬는 것 말고는 평생 기회가 없었어. 하지만 날이 밝아오는데 자기 과거를 이야기하는 건 음울한 일이야. 가기 전에 나를 다른 쪽으로 돌려놓게.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼! 그 어여쁜 증인을 위해 나와 함께 잔을 들지.\" 스트라이버가 잔을 치켜들며 말했다. \"이제 좀 유쾌한 쪽으로 돌아섰나?\"\n\n아무래도 그렇지 못한 듯, 그는 다시 음울해졌다.\n\n\"어여쁜 증인이라.\" 그가 잔 속을 들여다보며 중얼거렸다. \"오늘 낮과 밤에 증인이라면 신물이 나도록 봤어. 자네의 어여쁜 증인이 누구야?\"\n\n\"그 그림 같은 의사의 딸, 마네뜨 양 말일세.\"\n\n\"그 여자가 어여쁘다고?\"\n\n\"어여쁘지 않은가?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\"이런, 이 사람아, 그 여자는 법정 전체의 찬탄을 받았다고!\"\n\n\"법정 전체의 찬탄이라니 썩어빠질! 누가 올드 베일리를 미모의 심판관으로 세웠나? 그 여잔 금발 머리 인형이었어!\"\n\n\"이보게, 시드니.\" 스트라이버 씨가 날카로운 눈으로 그를 바라보며, 불그레한 얼굴 위로 한 손을 천천히 쓸어내리며 말했다. \"이보게, 난 그때 어쩐지 자네가 그 금발 머리 인형에게 동정심을 품었고, 그 금발 머리 인형에게 무슨 일이 일어나는지 재빨리 알아챘다고 생각했네만?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 일어나는지 재빨리 알아챘다고! 한 처녀가, 인형이건 아니건, 사내 코앞 한두 야드 안에서 기절하면, 그야 망원경 없이도 보이는 법이지. 자네와 잔은 들겠지만, 그 미모만은 인정 못해. 그리고 이제 술은 그만 마시겠어. 잠자리에 들어야겠네.\"\n\n주인장이 그를 계단 아래로 비춰주려고 촛불을 들고 층계참까지 뒤따라 나왔을 때, 날은 때 낀 창문을 통해 싸늘하게 들여다보고 있었다. 그가 집에서 나오자 공기는 차갑고 서글펐으며, 흐릿한 하늘은 잔뜩 흐렸고, 강물은 어둡고 침침했으며, 온 풍경이 생기 없는 사막 같았다. 그리고 아침 돌풍 앞에서 먼지 소용돌이가 빙글빙글 맴돌았으니, 마치 저 멀리서 사막의 모래가 일어나 그 진군의 첫 물보라가 도시를 뒤덮기 시작하기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n안에는 허비된 힘을, 사방에는 사막을 둔 채, 이 사내는 적막한 광장을 가로질러 가던 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 한순간 자기 앞의 황야에 누운, 명예로운 야망과 극기와 인내의 신기루를 보았다. 이 환상 속의 아름다운 도시에는 사랑과 우아함이 그를 내려다보는 허공의 회랑들이 있었고, 삶의 열매가 무르익어 매달린 정원들이 있었으며, 그의 눈앞에서 반짝이는 희망의 물결이 있었다. 한순간, 그것은 사라졌다. 빼곡한 집들로 이루어진 우물 같은 곳의 높다란 방으로 올라가, 그는 옷을 입은 채로 방치된 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 그 베개는 헛되이 흘린 눈물로 젖었다.\n\n서글프게, 서글프게 해가 떠올랐다. 그 해가 비추는 그 어떤 광경도, 훌륭한 재능과 훌륭한 감정을 지녔으나 그것을 제대로 발휘할 줄 모르고, 스스로를 돕지도 스스로 행복해지지도 못하며, 제 몸을 좀먹는 황폐를 느끼면서도 그것이 자신을 갉아먹도록 체념해 내맡긴 이 사내보다 더 서글프지는 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI. Hundreds of People\n\n마네뜨 박사의 조용한 거처는 소호 광장에서 멀지 않은 어느 조용한 길모퉁이에 있었다. 반역죄 재판이 있은 지 넉 달이라는 파도가 흘러가, 대중의 관심과 기억 속에서 그 사건을 저 먼 바다로 실어내 보낸 어느 화창한 일요일 오후, 자비스 로리 씨는 자신이 사는 클러큰웰에서 햇살 가득한 거리를 따라 박사와 만찬을 함께하러 가는 길을 걷고 있었다. 몇 차례 다시 업무에 파묻혔다 빠져나온 끝에, 로리 씨는 박사의 친구가 되어 있었고, 그 조용한 길모퉁이는 그의 삶에서 햇살이 비치는 부분이었다.\n\n이 화창한 일요일에, 로리 씨는 이른 오후부터 습관에서 비롯된 세 가지 이유로 소호를 향해 걸었다. 첫째, 화창한 일요일이면 그는 만찬에 앞서 박사 및 루씨와 자주 산책을 나갔기 때문이고, 둘째, 날씨가 궂은 일요일에는 집안의 친구로서 그들과 함께 머물며 이야기하고, 책을 읽고, 창밖을 내다보면서 대체로 하루를 보내곤 했기 때문이며, 셋째, 마침 자신만의 자그마한 예리한 의문을 풀어야 했고, 박사 집안의 일상이 그 시간이야말로 그것을 풀기에 적당한 때임을 어떻게 가리키는지 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n박사가 사는 모퉁이보다 더 정취 있는 모퉁이는 런던에서 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그곳을 가로지르는 길은 없었고, 박사 거처의 정면 창문에서는 한적한 분위기가 어우러진, 정겹고 자그마한 거리 풍경이 한눈에 들어왔다. 그 무렵 옥스퍼드 거리 북쪽에는 건물이 거의 없었고, 지금은 사라진 들판에서는 숲의 나무들이 무성했고, 들꽃이 자랐으며, 산사나무가 꽃을 피웠다. 그 결과, 시골 공기가 거처할 곳 없는 떠돌이 빈민처럼 교구 안으로 흘러들어 시들어버리는 대신, 소호에는 활기차고 거침없이 감돌았다. 게다가 그리 멀지 않은 곳에는 제철이면 복숭아가 익어가는 좋은 남향 담장이 여럿 있었다.\n\n여름 햇살은 하루의 이른 시간에는 그 모퉁이를 눈부시게 비추었으나, 거리가 달아오를 무렵이면 모퉁이는 그늘에 잠겼다. 그렇다고 그 그늘 너머로 환한 빛의 광채가 보이지 않을 만큼 외진 그늘은 아니었다. 그곳은 서늘한 곳이었고, 차분하면서도 명랑했으며, 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 곳이었고, 사납게 들끓는 거리로부터 피할 수 있는 바로 그런 항구였다.\n\n그런 정박지에는 마땅히 평온한 거룻배 한 척이 있어야 했고, 실제로 있었다. 박사는 크고 딱딱한 건물의 두 층을 차지하고 있었는데, 그 건물에서는 낮 동안 여러 생업이 이루어진다고들 했으나 어느 날이고 들리는 소리는 거의 없었고, 밤이면 그들 모두가 그곳을 멀리했다. 뒤편 건물 하나는 플라타너스 한 그루가 푸른 잎을 바스락거리는 안뜰을 지나야 닿을 수 있었는데, 그곳에서는 교회 오르간을 만든다고 했고, 은을 새긴다고 했으며, 마찬가지로 금을 두드린다고 했다. 그 일을 하는 이는 정면 현관의 벽에서 금빛 팔 하나가 튀어나와 있는 어느 신비로운 거인이었으니, 마치 자기 자신을 두드려 귀하게 만들어놓고는 찾아오는 모든 이들에게도 그와 비슷한 변모를 가하겠다고 위협하는 듯했다. 이런 생업들이나, 위층에 산다고 소문난 외로운 하숙인이나, 아래층에 사무실을 두고 있다고들 하는 어렴풋한 마차 장식 제조업자에 관해서 들리거나 보이는 것은 거의 없었다. 이따금씩 길 잃은 일꾼 하나가 외투를 걸치며 현관을 가로지르거나, 낯선 사람 하나가 그곳을 기웃거리거나, 안뜰 너머로 멀리서 짤그랑거리는 소리가 들리거나, 금빛 거인이 쾅 두드리는 소리가 들리곤 했다. 그러나 이런 것들은, 집 뒤편 플라타너스의 참새들과 집 앞 모퉁이의 메아리들이 일요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지 제멋대로 지낸다는 규칙을 입증하기 위해 필요한 예외들에 지나지 않았다.\n\n마네뜨 박사는 이곳에서, 그의 오랜 명성과 그의 이야기를 떠도는 속삭임 속에서 그 명성이 되살아남으로써 찾아오는 환자들을 받았다. 그의 과학적 지식, 그리고 기발한 실험을 수행하는 그의 빈틈없음과 솜씨는 또 다른 방면에서 그에게 적당한 청을 불러들였고, 그는 자신이 바라는 만큼은 벌었다.\n\n이 화창한 일요일 오후, 모퉁이의 그 평온한 집의 초인종을 눌렀을 때, 이런 것들이 자비스 로리 씨가 알고, 생각하고, 마음에 두고 있는 바였다.\n\n\"마네뜨 박사 댁에 계신가?\"\n\n곧 돌아오실 예정입니다.\n\n\"루씨 아가씨 댁에 계신가?\"\n\n곧 돌아오실 예정입니다.\n\n\"프로스 양 댁에 계신가?\"\n\n아마 계실 테지만, 그분의 출입을 허락하실지 거절하실지 그 의중을 하녀로서 미리 짐작하기란 도무지 불가능했다.\n\n\"나야 제집처럼 드나드는 사람이니,\" 로리 씨가 말했다, \"위층으로 올라가겠네.\"\n\n박사의 딸은 자신이 태어난 나라에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했지만, 그 나라에서 가장 유용하고 가장 흐뭇한 특성 가운데 하나인, 적은 것으로 많은 것을 이루어내는 그 능력만큼은 천부적으로 물려받은 듯했다. 가구는 소박했으나, 그 자체로는 별 가치가 없고 다만 취향과 운치만으로 빛나는 자그마한 장식들이 그토록 많이 곁들여져 있어, 그 효과가 흐뭇했다. 가장 큰 것에서 가장 작은 것에 이르기까지 방안 모든 물건의 배치, 색의 조화, 자질구레한 데서 아끼는 알뜰함과 섬세한 손과 맑은 눈과 분별로 얻어낸 우아한 변화와 대조, 그 모든 것이 그 자체로 너무도 흐뭇하고 또 그것을 빚어낸 이를 너무도 잘 드러내고 있어서, 로리 씨가 서서 주위를 둘러보고 있노라니, 의자며 탁자며 하는 것들마저 이제는 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그 독특한 표정 같은 것을 띠고서, 마음에 드시느냐고 그에게 묻는 듯했다.\n\n한 층에는 방이 셋 있었고, 공기가 그 모든 방을 자유로이 통하도록 그것들을 잇는 문이 죄다 열려 있어서, 로리 씨는 사방에서 자신이 알아챈 그 기발한 닮음을 빙긋이 눈여겨보며 이 방에서 저 방으로 걸어 다녔다. 첫번째 방은 가장 좋은 방이었는데, 그곳에는 루씨의 새들과 꽃들과 책들과 책상과 작업대와 수채물감 상자가 있었다. 두번째 방은 박사의 진료실로, 식당으로도 쓰였다. 세번째 방은 안뜰의 플라타너스가 바스락거리며 시시각각 얼룩무늬를 드리우는 박사의 침실이었는데, 그곳 한구석에는 이제는 쓰지 않는 구두장이의 작업대와 연장 쟁반이, 빠리의 쌩땅뚜안 변두리에 있던 그 음울한 집, 술집 곁 그 집 오층에 놓여 있던 그대로 거의 변함없이 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"이상하군,\" 로리 씨가 둘러보던 것을 멈추고 말했다, \"그가 자기 고난의 저런 기억거리를 곁에 두고 있다니!\"\n\n\"그게 뭐가 이상한가요?\" 그를 흠칫 놀라게 한 불쑥 던진 질문이었다.\n\n그것은 프로스 양에게서 나온 것이었으니, 손아귀 힘이 센 그 거칠고 불그레한 여인은 로리 씨가 도버의 로열 조지 호텔에서 처음 알게 되어 그 뒤로 친분을 쌓아온 사이였다.\n\n\"나는 생각하기를—\" 로리 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"흥! 생각하셨겠지요!\" 프로스 양이 말하자, 로리 씨는 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"그래, 안녕하셨어요?\" 그러더니 그 부인이 물었다—퉁명스럽게, 그러나 그에게 악의는 없다는 것을 나타내려는 듯이.\n\n\"덕분에 잘 지냅니다,\" 로리 씨가 온순하게 대답했다. \"당신은 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"내세울 거 하나 없어요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"그런가요?\"\n\n\"아! 그렇다니까요!\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"우리 무당벌레 때문에 속이 영 상해서요.\"\n\n\"그런가요?\"\n\n\"제발 '그런가요' 말고 다른 말 좀 해보세요, 안 그러면 답답해 죽겠어요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. 그녀의 성격은 (키와는 따로 떼어놓고 보자면) 무뚝뚝함이었다.\n\n\"그래, 정말이요?\" 로리 씨가 고쳐 말했다.\n\n\"'정말'도 충분히 못마땅하지만,\" 프로스 양이 받았다, \"그래도 낫네요. 그래요, 속이 영 상했어요.\"\n\n\"그 까닭을 물어봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"무당벌레한테 도무지 어울리지도 않는 사람들이 수십 명씩 여기로 그애를 보러 찾아오는 게 싫어요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"정말로 수십 명이 그러려고 온다는 말입니까?\"\n\n\"수백 명이요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n이 부인의 특징은 (그녀 이전과 이후의 어떤 사람들도 그렇듯이) 자신의 처음 주장이 의심받을 때마다 그것을 과장한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이런, 저런!\" 로리 씨가, 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 무난한 말로 응수했다.\n\n\"나는 그 귀한 애와 함께 살아왔어요—아니, 그애가 나와 함께 살면서 그 값을 치러주었지요. 내가 나든 그애든 거저 먹여 살릴 형편만 되었다면 그애는 절대로 그래선 안 됐던 거예요, 맹세코 그래요—그애가 열 살 때부터 말이에요. 그런데 이건 정말 너무 힘든 일이라고요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n무엇이 그토록 힘든 일인지 정확히는 알 수 없었으므로, 로리 씨는 고개를 가로저었다. 그는 자신의 그 중요한 부위를, 무엇에든 어울리는 요술 망토처럼 써먹었다.\n\n\"그 귀염둥이한테는 조금도 어울리지 않는 온갖 부류의 사람들이 늘 나타나요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"당신이 시작한 일이니까—\"\n\n\"내가요, 프로스 양?\"\n\n\"아니었나요? 그애 아버지를 되살려놓은 게 누구죠?\"\n\n\"오! 그게 시작한 일이라면—\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 끝낸 일은 아니었을 테죠? 내 말은, 당신이 시작했을 때, 그것만으로도 충분히 힘들었다는 거예요. 마네뜨 박사한테 나무랄 데가 있다는 게 아니에요, 그런 딸한테 어울리지 않는다는 것만 빼면요. 하지만 그건 그분 탓이 아니지요, 어떤 처지에서든 누구도 그애한테 어울릴 거라고는 기대할 수 없으니까요. 그렇지만 그분을 뒤따라 사람들이 떼로 무리로 나타나서 (그분이야 용서할 수 있었지요) 무당벌레의 정을 내게서 앗아가려 드는 건 정말이지 두 배 세 배로 힘든 일이라고요.\"\n\n로리 씨는 프로스 양이 몹시 질투심이 강하다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 이제는 그녀가 그 별난 행동의 이면에 자리한 사심 없는 존재들 가운데 하나—오직 여인들에게서만 볼 수 있는 부류—라는 것 또한 알고 있었다. 그런 이들은 순수한 사랑과 흠모에서, 이미 잃어버린 젊음에, 한 번도 가져보지 못한 아름다움에, 다행히 얻어본 적 없는 재주에, 자신들의 어두운 삶에는 한 번도 비춘 적 없는 밝은 희망에, 기꺼이 자신을 노예로 옭아매는 법이다. 그는 세상물정을 충분히 알았기에, 세상에 마음에서 우러나는 충실한 헌신보다 더 나은 것은 없음을 알고 있었다. 그처럼 바쳐지고 그처럼 어떤 타산의 때도 묻지 않은 그 헌신에 그가 얼마나 드높은 경의를 품었던지, 자기 마음속에서 행한 응보의 배치에서—우리는 누구나 많든 적든 그런 배치를 한다—그는 프로스 양을, 자연과 기교 양쪽으로 비할 데 없이 잘 차려입었고 텔슨 은행에 잔고를 둔 수많은 귀부인들보다도 한층 더 낮은 천사들 가까이에 자리매김해 두었다.\n\n\"무당벌레한테 어울리는 남자는 단 한 사람뿐이었고 앞으로도 그럴 거예요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"그건 우리 오빠 솔로몬이었어요, 인생에서 실수만 저지르지 않았더라면 말이지요.\"\n\n여기서 또: 프로스 양의 개인사에 관한 로리 씨의 탐문은, 그녀의 오빠 솔로몬이라는 자가 투기 밑천으로 삼겠다며 그녀가 가진 모든 것을 빼앗아가고는, 양심의 가책이라곤 한 점 없이 가난에 빠진 그녀를 영영 내버린 무정한 악당이라는 사실을 밝혀낸 바 있었다. 솔로몬에 대한 프로스 양의 변함없는 믿음은 (이 사소한 실수에 대해 자그마한 것만 깎아낸다면) 로리 씨에게는 자못 진지한 문제였고, 그녀에 대한 그의 호의 어린 평가에서도 적잖은 무게를 지녔다.\n\n\"마침 우리 둘만 잠시 있게 됐고, 또 우리 둘 다 사무적인 사람들이니,\" 그들이 응접실로 돌아와 다정한 사이로 그곳에 앉았을 때 그가 말했다, \"한 가지 물읍시다—박사는 루씨와 이야기할 때, 아직도 구두 짓던 시절을 한 번도 언급하지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"한 번도요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 저 작업대와 저 연장들은 곁에 두고요?\"\n\n\"아!\" 프로스 양이 고개를 가로저으며 받았다. \"하지만 그분이 마음속으로도 그 생각을 안 한다고는 말 안 해요.\"\n\n\"그가 그 생각을 많이 한다고 보십니까?\"\n\n\"그래요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"혹시 상상하시기로는—\" 로리 씨가 입을 떼자, 프로스 양이 그의 말을 짧게 끊었다.\n\n\"아무것도 상상하지 마세요. 상상력 따위는 아예 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"제 말이 잘못됐군요. 그럼 짐작하시기로는—이따금 짐작쯤은 하시지요?\"\n\n\"가끔은요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n\"짐작하시기로는,\" 로리 씨가, 그녀를 다정하게 바라보는 그 빛나는 눈에 웃음기를 반짝이며 말을 이었다, \"마네뜨 박사가, 자신이 그토록 억눌리게 된 까닭에 관해서—어쩌면 자신을 억누른 자의 이름에 관해서까지도—그 모든 세월 내내 간직해온 자기 나름의 어떤 견해를 갖고 있다고 보십니까?\"\n\n\"무당벌레가 내게 말해주는 것 말고는 그 일에 관해 아무것도 짐작 안 해요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"그애는 그분이 그런 견해를 갖고 있다고 생각한다는 거예요.\"\n\"이런 질문들을 다 해댄다고 화내지는 마세요. 나야 그저 둔한 사무가일 뿐이고, 당신은 사무에 밝은 여인이니까요.\"\n\n\"둔하다고요?\" 프로스 양이 차분하게 물었다.\n\n자신의 그 겸손한 형용사를 거두어들이고 싶어진 로리 씨가 대답했다. \"아니, 아니, 아닙니다. 결코 그렇지 않지요. 다시 본론으로 돌아가서—우리 모두가 그렇게 굳게 믿고 있다시피 마네뜨 박사는 어떤 죄도 짓지 않은 게 분명한데, 그가 그 문제를 결코 입에 올리지 않는다는 게 주목할 만하지 않습니까? 나한테 그렇다는 게 아닙니다, 비록 그가 여러 해 전에 나와 사무적인 관계를 맺었고 지금은 우리가 가까운 사이이긴 하지만요. 그가 그토록 헌신적으로 아끼고, 또 그를 그토록 헌신적으로 아끼는 그 어여쁜 딸에게도 그렇다는 말입니다. 믿어주세요, 프로스 양, 나는 호기심에서 당신에게 이 화제를 꺼내는 게 아니라, 간절한 관심에서 그러는 거예요.\"\n\n\"글쎄요! 내가 이해하는 한에서, 그게 시원찮은 이해이긴 하지만, 들어보세요,\" 사과의 어조에 누그러진 프로스 양이 말했다, \"그분은 그 문제 전체를 두려워하는 거예요.\"\n\"두려워한다고요?\"\n\n\"왜 그럴 만한지는 충분히 뻔하다고 생각해요. 끔찍한 기억이니까요. 그뿐 아니라, 그분이 자기 자신을 잃어버린 것도 거기서 비롯됐지요. 어떻게 자기 자신을 잃었는지, 또 어떻게 되찾았는지 모르니, 다시 자기 자신을 잃지 않으리라고 결코 확신하지 못할 거예요. 그것 하나만으로도 그 문제가 유쾌할 리 없다고 생각해요.\"\n\n그것은 로리 씨가 기대했던 것보다 한층 깊이 있는 말이었다. \"맞는 말입니다,\" 그가 말했다, \"생각만 해도 두려운 일이지요. 하지만 프로스 양, 내 마음속에는 한 가지 의문이 도사리고 있어요, 마네뜨 박사가 그 억눌린 것을 늘 속에 가둬두는 것이 과연 그에게 좋은 일일까 하는 거지요. 사실, 바로 이 의문과, 그것이 이따금 내게 불러일으키는 불안 때문에 우리가 지금 이렇게 속내를 털어놓게 된 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어쩔 수 없어요,\" 프로스 양이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그 줄을 건드리면, 그분은 당장 더 나쁘게 변해요. 그냥 내버려두는 게 나아요. 한마디로, 좋든 싫든 내버려둘 수밖에 없어요. 가끔, 그분은 한밤중에 일어나서는, 위층에 있는 우리한테, 방안을 이리저리, 이리저리 거니는 소리가 들려요. 무당벌레는 그럴 때면 그분의 마음이 옛 감옥 안을 이리저리, 이리저리 거닐고 있다는 걸 알아차리게 됐지요. 그애는 서둘러 그분한테 가고, 둘이 함께 이리저리, 이리저리, 그분이 진정될 때까지 거닐어요. 하지만 그분은 자신이 안절부절못하는 진짜 까닭은 그애한테 한마디도 하지 않고, 그애도 그것을 그분한테 넌지시 비추지 않는 게 가장 좋다는 걸 알지요. 말없이 둘은 함께 이리저리, 이리저리 거닐어요, 함께 이리저리, 이리저리, 그애의 사랑과 함께함이 그분을 제정신으로 돌려놓을 때까지요.\"\n\n자신에게는 상상력이 없다는 프로스 양의 부인에도 불구하고, 이리저리 거닌다는 그 구절을 거듭 되풀이하는 데에는 한 가지 슬픈 생각에 단조롭게 시달리는 그 고통에 대한 감지가 깃들어 있었으니, 그것이 그녀가 그런 것을 지니고 있음을 입증했다.\n\n그 모퉁이는 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 모퉁이라고 앞서 말한 바 있다. 다가오는 발소리에 그곳이 어찌나 쩌렁쩌렁 울리기 시작했던지, 마치 이리저리 오가는 그 고단한 거닒을 입에 올린 것만으로 그 메아리가 일어나기 시작한 듯했다.\n\n\"오셨네요!\" 프로스 양이 대화를 끝맺으려 일어서며 말했다. \"이제 곧 수백 명의 사람들이 들이닥치겠군요!\"\n\n그곳은 그 음향적 특성에 있어 어찌나 묘한 모퉁이였던지, 어찌나 독특한 귀를 가진 장소였던지, 로리 씨가 열린 창가에 서서 자신이 그 발소리를 듣고 있는 아버지와 딸을 찾고 있노라니, 그들이 결코 다가오지 않으리라는 생각이 들었다. 마치 발소리가 멀어져버린 듯 메아리가 잦아들 뿐만 아니라, 그 대신 결코 오지 않는 다른 발소리의 메아리가 들려오다가, 그것이 바로 가까이 다가온 듯싶을 때 영영 잦아들곤 했다. 그러나 아버지와 딸은 마침내 모습을 드러냈고, 프로스 양은 그들을 맞이하러 길로 난 문 앞에 채비를 하고 서 있었다.\n\n프로스 양은, 비록 거칠고 불그레하고 무뚝뚝하긴 해도, 보기 좋은 광경이었다. 그녀의 귀한 애가 위층으로 올라오자 그애의 보닛을 벗겨주고, 손수건 끄트머리로 그것을 매만지고, 먼지를 후후 불어내고, 치워둘 채비로 외투를 개고, 그애의 풍성한 머리칼을 쓸어 넘기는 그 모습은, 그녀가 만일 세상에서 가장 허영심 많고 가장 아름다운 여인이었더라도 자기 머리칼에 그러했을 만큼이나 큰 자부심을 담고 있었다. 그녀의 귀한 애 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 그녀를 끌어안고 고마워하며, 자기 때문에 그토록 수고를 들이는 것에 항의했는데—이 마지막 것은 짐짓 장난스럽게밖에는 감히 하지 못했으니, 안 그러면 프로스 양이 몹시 마음 상해 자기 방으로 물러가 울어버렸을 터였다. 박사 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 그 둘을 바라보면서, 루씨를 어찌나 오냐오냐하느냐고 프로스 양에게 일렀는데, 그 어조와 눈빛에는 프로스 양 못지않은 응석받이의 정이, 가능하기만 했다면 그보다 더한 정이 담겨 있었다. 로리 씨 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 자그마한 가발을 쓴 채 이 모든 것에 환히 미소 지으며, 노년에 이르러 한 가정으로 자신을 인도해준 독신자의 별들에게 감사했다. 그러나 그 광경을 보러 수백 명의 사람들이 오는 일은 없었고, 로리 씨는 프로스 양의 예언이 이루어지기를 기다렸으나 헛일이었다.\n\n만찬 시간이 되었으나 여전히 수백 명의 사람들은 없었다. 이 자그마한 집안의 운영에서, 프로스 양은 아래층 영역을 맡았는데, 언제나 놀랍도록 제 몫을 해냈다. 매우 소박한 그녀의 만찬은 어찌나 잘 요리되고 어찌나 잘 차려졌는지, 또 반은 영국식이고 반은 프랑스식인 그 솜씨가 어찌나 깔끔했는지, 그보다 더 나을 수는 없었다. 프로스 양의 우정은 철저히 실용적인 종류여서, 그녀는 가난해진 프랑스 사람들을 찾아 소호와 그 인근 지방을 샅샅이 휘젓고 다녔는데, 그들은 몇 실링이나 반 크라운에 혹해서 그녀에게 요리의 비법을 전수해주곤 했다. 이 몰락한 골족의 아들딸들로부터 그녀는 어찌나 놀라운 솜씨를 익혔던지, 하인의 진용을 이루는 여인과 처녀는 그녀를 영락없는 마녀, 혹은 신데렐라의 대모쯤으로 여겼으니, 닭 한 마리, 토끼 한 마리, 정원에서 난 채소 한두 가지를 시켜다가, 그것들을 제 마음에 드는 무엇으로든 바꿔놓는 사람이었던 것이다.\n\n일요일이면 프로스 양은 박사의 식탁에서 만찬을 들었으나, 다른 날에는 알 수 없는 시각에, 아래층 영역에서든 이층 자기 방에서든 식사하기를 고집했다—그 방은 푸른 방으로, 그녀의 무당벌레 말고는 누구도 들어가본 적 없는 곳이었다. 이번에는, 자신을 기쁘게 하려는 무당벌레의 정겨운 얼굴과 정겨운 노력에 화답하여, 프로스 양이 더없이 누그러졌으니, 만찬 또한 매우 즐거웠다.\n\n찌는 듯한 날이었던지라, 만찬 뒤에 루씨는 포도주를 플라타너스 아래로 가지고 나가, 바깥바람을 쐬며 거기 앉자고 제안했다. 모든 것이 그녀를 중심으로 돌아가고 그녀를 축으로 움직였으므로, 그들은 플라타너스 아래로 나갔고, 그녀는 특별히 로리 씨를 위해 포도주를 내려 가져갔다. 얼마 전부터 그녀는 스스로 로리 씨의 술 따르는 시종 노릇을 자처해온 터였다. 그래서 그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그녀는 그의 잔을 계속 채워주었다. 그들이 이야기하는 동안 신비로운 집들의 뒷면과 끄트머리가 그들을 엿보았고, 플라타너스는 그들 머리 위에서 제 나름의 방식으로 그들에게 속삭였다.\n\n여전히, 수백 명의 사람들은 나타나지 않았다. 그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있는 동안 다네이 씨가 나타나기는 했으나, 그는 단 한 사람일 뿐이었다.\n\n마네뜨 박사는 그를 다정하게 맞이했고, 루씨 또한 그러했다. 그러나 프로스 양은 별안간 머리와 몸을 씰룩거리는 발작에 시달려 집안으로 물러갔다. 그녀는 드물지 않게 이 병의 희생자가 되곤 했는데, 허물없는 대화에서 그것을 \"씰룩증 발작\"이라고 불렀다.\n\n박사는 더없이 좋은 상태였고, 유난히 젊어 보였다. 그와 루씨가 닮은 점은 그런 때면 매우 두드러졌고, 그들이 나란히 앉아, 그녀가 그의 어깨에 기대고 그가 그녀의 의자 등받이에 팔을 얹고 있노라니, 그 닮음을 짚어보는 것이 무척 흐뭇했다.\n\n그는 온종일, 여러 주제에 관해, 유난한 활기를 띠고 이야기해온 터였다. \"마네뜨 박사님,\" 그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있을 때 다네이 씨가 말했다—그가 그 말을 한 것은, 마침 화제에 오른 런던의 오래된 건물들에 관해 자연스럽게 이야기를 이어가던 차였다—\"탑은 많이 둘러보셨습니까?\"\n* 탑: 런던 탑(Tower of London). 감옥으로도 쓰였다.\n\n\"루씨와 나도 가본 적은 있지만, 그저 지나는 길에 들른 정도였소. 흥미로 가득한 곳이라는 걸 알 만큼은 보았지요. 그 이상은 거의 못 보았소.\"\n\n\"저는, 기억하시겠지만, 거기 가본 적이 있지요,\" 다네이가, 살짝 성난 듯 얼굴을 붉히면서도 미소 지으며 말했다, \"다른 처지로 말입니다, 그것도 그곳을 많이 둘러볼 만한 편의를 주는 처지는 아니었지요. 거기 있을 때 묘한 이야기를 하나 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐였나요?\" 루씨가 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 개축 공사를 하다가, 인부들이 오래된 지하 감방 하나를 발견했답니다. 여러 해 동안 벽으로 막혀 잊혀 있던 곳이었지요. 그 안쪽 벽은 돌마다 죄수들이 새겨놓은 글귀들로 뒤덮여 있었답니다—날짜며, 이름이며, 하소연이며, 기도며 하는 것들이었지요. 벽 모서리의 어느 귓돌에는, 처형장으로 끌려간 듯한 죄수 하나가 마지막 작업으로 글자 셋을 새겨놓았더랍니다. 매우 변변찮은 도구로, 떨리는 손으로, 황급히 새긴 것이었지요. 처음에는 그것을 D. I. C.로 읽었답니다. 그러나 더 꼼꼼히 살펴보니, 마지막 글자가 G임이 드러났지요. 그런 머리글자를 가진 죄수에 관한 기록이나 전설은 없었고, 그 이름이 무엇이었을지 부질없는 추측이 숱하게 오갔답니다. 마침내, 그 글자들은 머리글자가 아니라 완전한 한 단어, 즉 DIG(파라)였으리라는 의견이 나왔지요. 그 글귀 아래 바닥을 매우 꼼꼼히 살펴보니, 어떤 돌이나 타일이나 포석 조각 밑의 흙 속에서, 종이 한 장의 재가, 자그마한 가죽 상자인지 주머니인지의 재와 뒤섞인 채 발견되었답니다. 그 무명의 죄수가 무엇을 썼는지는 영영 읽을 수 없겠지만, 그는 무언가를 썼고, 그것을 간수에게서 지키려고 숨겨두었던 거지요.\"\n\n\"아버지,\" 루씨가 외쳤다, \"편찮으세요!\"\n\n그가 별안간 손을 머리에 짚으며 벌떡 일어섰던 것이다. 그의 거동과 표정에 그들 모두가 적잖이 놀랐다.\n\n\"아니다, 얘야, 편찮은 게 아니야. 굵은 빗방울이 떨어지는구나, 그게 나를 흠칫하게 했어. 들어가는 게 좋겠다.\"\n\n그는 거의 곧바로 평정을 되찾았다. 정말로 굵은 빗방울이 떨어지고 있었고, 그는 빗방울이 맺힌 손등을 내보였다. 그러나 앞서 들려준 그 발견에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그들이 집안으로 들어갈 때, 로리 씨의 사무가다운 눈은, 그의 얼굴이 찰스 다네이 쪽으로 돌려질 때 그 위에서, 법원 복도에서 그것이 자신 쪽으로 돌려졌을 때 어려 있던 바로 그 기이한 표정을 알아챘거나, 알아챘다고 여겼다.\n\n그러나 그가 어찌나 빨리 평정을 되찾았던지, 로리 씨는 자신의 사무가다운 눈을 의심하게 되었다. 그가 현관의 금빛 거인 아래 멈춰 서서, 자신은 아직 사소한 놀람에도 끄떡없을 만큼은 못 되며 (앞으로 그렇게 될지는 몰라도) 비가 자신을 흠칫하게 했다고 그들에게 말했을 때, 그는 그 금빛 거인의 팔 못지않게 의연했다.\n\n차 마시는 시간이 되어, 프로스 양은 또 한 차례 씰룩증 발작에 시달리며 차를 끓였고, 그래도 여전히 수백 명의 사람들은 없었다. 카튼 씨가 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오기는 했으나, 그로써 겨우 두 사람일 뿐이었다.\n\n밤이 어찌나 후텁지근했던지, 그들이 문과 창을 열어둔 채 앉아 있는데도 더위에 압도되었다. 찻상을 물리고 나서, 그들은 모두 창가 하나로 옮겨가, 무겁게 내려앉은 황혼 속을 내다보았다. 루씨는 아버지 곁에 앉았고, 다네이는 그녀 옆에 앉았으며, 카튼은 창에 기대섰다. 커튼은 길고 희었는데, 모퉁이로 휘몰아쳐 들어온 천둥 바람의 돌풍 몇 가닥이 그것을 천장까지 끌어올려, 유령 같은 날개처럼 펄럭이게 했다.\n\n\"빗방울이 아직도 떨어지는군요, 굵고, 무겁고, 드문드문.\" 마네뜨 박사가 말했다. \"천천히 오는군요.\"\n\n\"하지만 틀림없이 옵니다,\" 카튼이 말했다.\n\n그들은 나직이 이야기했으니, 지켜보며 기다리는 사람들이 대개 그러하듯이, 어두운 방에서 번개를 지켜보며 기다리는 사람들이 언제나 그러하듯이.\n\n거리에는 폭풍우가 몰아치기 전에 비를 피할 곳을 찾아 서둘러 달아나는 사람들로 큰 소란이 일었다. 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 그 모퉁이는 오가는 발소리의 메아리로 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸으나, 정작 거기에 발소리 하나 없었다.\n\n\"사람의 무리, 그런데도 고독이라니!\" 한동안 귀 기울이던 끝에 다네이가 말했다.\n\n\"인상적이지 않나요, 다네이 씨?\" 루씨가 물었다. \"이따금, 저는 저녁이면 여기 앉아 있다가, 그만 상상하게 되곤 했어요—하지만 이렇게 온통 캄캄하고 엄숙한 오늘 밤엔, 어리석은 상상의 그림자만으로도 몸서리가 쳐지네요—\"\n\n\"우리도 함께 몸서리치게 해주십시오. 그게 무엇인지 알 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"당신께는 아무것도 아닌 것처럼 여겨질 거예요. 그런 변덕스러운 생각이란 우리가 스스로 그것을 빚어낼 때에만 인상적인 것 같아요. 남에게 전해질 수 있는 게 아니지요. 저는 가끔 저녁이면 여기 홀로 앉아 귀 기울이다가, 그 메아리가 머지않아 우리 삶 속으로 들어오게 될 모든 발소리의 메아리라고 여기게 되곤 했어요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇다면, 언젠가 큰 무리가 우리 삶 속으로 들어오게 되겠군요.\" 시드니 카튼이 그 침울한 투로 끼어들었다.\n\n발소리는 끊임이 없었고, 그 다급함은 점점 더 빨라졌다. 모퉁이는 발소리에 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다. 어떤 것은 창문 아래에서인 듯, 어떤 것은 방안에서인 듯, 어떤 것은 다가오고, 어떤 것은 멀어지고, 어떤 것은 뚝 끊기고, 어떤 것은 아주 멎어버렸다. 모두가 먼 거리에서 나는 것이었고, 시야에 든 것은 하나도 없었다.\n\n\"이 모든 발소리가 우리 모두에게 오기로 정해진 걸까요, 마네뜨 양, 아니면 우리가 그것들을 우리끼리 나눠 가지게 될까요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 다네이 씨. 어리석은 상상이라고 말씀드렸잖아요, 그래도 청하시기에. 제가 거기에 몸을 내맡기곤 했을 때는, 저 혼자였고, 그럴 때면 그것들을 제 삶과 제 아버지의 삶 속으로 들어올 사람들의 발소리라고 상상하곤 했어요.\"\n\n\"저는 그것들을 제 삶 속으로 받아들이겠습니다!\" 카튼이 말했다. \"저야 아무것도 묻지 않고 아무 조건도 달지 않지요. 큰 무리가 우리를 향해 밀려오고 있어요, 마네뜨 양, 그리고 저는 그들이 보입니다—번갯불로요.\" 그는 그를 창가에 어슬렁어슬렁 기댄 모습으로 비춘 한 줄기 선명한 섬광이 지나간 뒤에 이 마지막 말을 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그리고 그들의 소리가 들립니다!\" 그가 천둥소리가 한바탕 지나간 뒤 다시 덧붙였다. \"여기 그들이 옵니다, 빠르고, 사납고, 맹렬하게!\"\n\n그것은 그가 빗대어 표현한 비의 돌진과 포효였으니, 그것이 그의 말을 멈추게 했다. 그 속에서는 어떤 목소리도 들릴 수 없었기 때문이다. 그 한바탕 물줄기와 더불어 천둥과 번개의 잊지 못할 폭풍우가 몰아쳤고, 자정에 달이 떠오른 뒤까지, 천둥 치고, 불 번득이고, 비 쏟아지는 데 한순간의 틈도 없었다.\n\n대기가 맑게 갠 가운데 쎄인트폴 대성당의 큰 종이 한 시를 칠 무렵, 로리 씨는 긴 장화를 신고 등불을 든 제리의 호위를 받으며, 클러큰웰로 돌아가는 길에 나섰다. 소호와 클러큰웰 사이의 길에는 외진 구간이 군데군데 있었으므로, 로리 씨는 노상강도를 염두에 두고 늘 이 일에 제리를 거느렸다. 다만 평소에는 이보다 족히 두 시간은 일찍 그 일이 치러졌다.\n\n\"참 대단한 밤이었네! 거의, 제리,\" 로리 씨가 말했다, \"죽은 자들을 무덤에서 불러낼 만한 밤이었어.\"\n\n\"저야 그런 밤을 본 적이 없습죠, 나리—그럴 일도 없을 것 같고요—그런 일을 하실 밤 말입죠,\" 제리가 대답했다.\n\n\"잘 가게, 카튼 씨,\" 사무가가 말했다. \"잘 가시오, 다네이 씨. 우리가 함께 이런 밤을 또 보게 될까요!\"\n\n어쩌면. 어쩌면, 그 돌진과 포효와 더불어 그들을 향해 밀려오는 사람들의 큰 무리 또한 보게 될지도.\n\n# CHAPTER VII. Monseigneur in Town\n\n궁정에서 권세를 누리는 대영주들 가운데 하나인 영주는 빠리에 있는 자신의 화려한 저택에서 격주로 접견을 베풀었다. 영주는 안쪽 방, 그의 성소 중의 성소, 바깥 여러 방에 모인 숭배자 무리에게는 지성소와도 같은 곳에 들어가 있었다. 영주는 막 초콜릿을 들려는 참이었다. 영주는 수많은 것들을 손쉽게 삼킬 수 있었고, 몇몇 시무룩한 사람들은 그가 프랑스마저 꽤 빠른 속도로 삼키고 있다고 여겼다. 그러나 영주의 아침 초콜릿은 요리사 외에 건장한 사내 넷의 도움이 없이는 영주의 목구멍으로 넘어갈 수조차 없었다.\n\n그렇다. 그 행복한 초콜릿을 영주의 입술까지 모셔가는 데에는 네 사람이 필요했으니, 넷 모두 호화로운 장식으로 번쩍였고, 그들의 우두머리는 영주가 정해놓은 고귀하고 정숙한 유행을 본받아 주머니에 금시계 두 개 미만으로는 도무지 존재할 수 없는 자였다. 한 하인은 초콜릿 주전자를 신성한 어전으로 받들어 들고 갔고, 두번째 하인은 그 일에 쓰려고 지니고 다니는 작은 도구로 초콜릿을 휘저어 거품을 냈으며, 세번째 하인은 영광스러운 냅킨을 바쳤고, 네번째 하인(금시계 두 개를 지닌 그자)은 초콜릿을 따랐다. 영주가 초콜릿 시중을 드는 이 시종들 가운데 하나라도 없이 지내면서, 흠모하는 하늘 아래 그 드높은 자리를 지킨다는 것은 불가능한 일이었다. 만약 그의 초콜릿이 단지 세 사람의 비천한 시중밖에 받지 못했다면 그의 가문 문장에는 깊은 오점이 남았으리라. 둘뿐이었다면 그는 죽어버렸을 것이 분명했다.\n\n영주는 어젯밤 어느 소박한 만찬에 다녀왔는데, 그곳에서는 희극과 그랜드 오페라가 매혹적으로 펼쳐졌다. 영주는 거의 매일 밤 매혹적인 친구들과 더불어 소박한 만찬에 나갔다. 영주는 어찌나 점잖고 또 감수성이 예민한지, 따분한 국사며 국가 기밀의 문제에 있어서는 희극과 그랜드 오페라가 프랑스 전체의 필요보다 그에게 훨씬 더 큰 영향을 미쳤다. 프랑스로서는 행복한 일이었으니, 비슷한 은총을 입은 모든 나라가 늘 그러하듯이! 일례로, 나라를 팔아넘긴 저 유쾌한 스튜어트 왕가의 안타까운 시절 영국이 늘 그러했듯이.\n\n영주는 일반적인 공무에 관해 참으로 고귀한 생각 하나를 품고 있었으니, 모든 것이 제멋대로 굴러가게 내버려두자는 것이었다. 특정한 공무에 관해서는 모든 일이 자신의 뜻대로 풀려야 한다는, 즉 자신의 권력과 주머니에 보탬이 되어야 한다는 또 하나의 참으로 고귀한 생각을 품고 있었다. 일반적이든 특정한 것이든 자신의 쾌락에 관해서는, 세상이 그 쾌락을 위해 만들어졌다는 또 하나의 참으로 고귀한 생각을 품고 있었다. 그가 내린 명령의 본문은 (원문에서 대명사 하나만 바꾼 것이니, 대단한 일은 아니다) 이러했다. \"땅과 거기 가득 찬 것이 다 내 것이로다, 영주께서 이르시니라.\"\n\n그러나 영주는 사적으로나 공적으로나 자신의 일에 천박한 곤경이 스며들고 있음을 차츰 깨닫게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 두 부류의 일에 관해 부득이 어느 징세청부업자와 손을 잡았다. 공적인 재정에 관해서는, 영주가 그것을 도무지 어찌해볼 수 없었던 까닭에 그럴 수 있는 누군가에게 도급으로 넘겨야 했기 때문이고, 사적인 재정에 관해서는, 징세청부업자들이 부유한 반면, 영주는 여러 세대에 걸친 대단한 사치와 낭비 끝에 점점 가난해지고 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 영주는 자신의 누이를, 닥쳐오는 베일 — 그녀가 걸칠 수 있는 가장 값싼 옷이었다 — 을 막아낼 시간이 아직 남아 있을 때 수녀원에서 데려와, 가문은 보잘것없으나 매우 부유한 어느 징세청부업자에게 상으로 하사했다. 그 징세청부업자는 꼭대기에 황금 사과가 달린 어울리는 지팡이를 짚고서, 지금 바깥 방에 모인 손님들 가운데 끼어 있었으며, 사람들 앞에서 한껏 굽실거림을 받고 있었다. 다만 영주의 핏줄을 이은 우월한 사람들만은 예외였으니, 그자의 아내까지 포함해서, 그들은 더없이 도도한 경멸로 그를 내려다보았다.\n\n징세청부업자는 호사스러운 인물이었다. 그의 마구간에는 말 서른 마리가 있었고, 그의 홀에는 스물네 명의 남자 하인이 앉아 있었으며, 여섯 명의 시녀가 그의 아내의 시중을 들었다. 닥치는 대로 약탈하고 노략질하는 것 말고는 아무 일도 하는 체하지 않는 자로서, 징세청부업자는 — 그의 혼인 관계가 사회의 도덕에 어떤 식으로 이바지했든 간에 — 적어도 그날 영주의 저택에 모인 인물들 가운데 가장 큰 실체였다.\n\n방들은 보기에 아름다운 풍경이었고, 당대의 취향과 기교가 이룰 수 있는 온갖 장식의 묘를 다해 꾸며져 있었으나, 사실 그것은 건전한 거래가 못 되었다. 다른 곳에서 누더기와 잠자리 모자를 걸치고 있는 허수아비들에 견주어 본다면 (게다가 그리 멀지도 않은 곳이어서, 그 양극단으로부터 거의 같은 거리에 있는 노트르담의 망루들은 양쪽을 다 내려다볼 수 있었다) 그것은 지극히 불편한 거래였으리라 — 영주의 저택에서 그것이 누군가의 관심사가 될 수 있었다면 말이다. 군사 지식이라곤 없는 군 장교들, 배에 대한 개념이 없는 해군 장교들, 사무라곤 모르는 행정 관리들, 세속 중의 세속에 물든 뻔뻔한 성직자들, 음탕한 눈빛과 헤픈 혀와 더 헤픈 삶을 지닌 자들, 모두 저마다의 소임에 전혀 어울리지 않으면서도 거기에 속한 척 끔찍하게 거짓말을 늘어놓는 자들, 그러나 모두가 가깝게든 멀게든 영주의 부류에 속하여, 그리하여 무엇이든 얻어낼 것이 있는 온갖 공직마다 슬그머니 끼어든 자들, 이런 자들이 수십 명씩 또 수십 명씩 헤아려졌다. 영주나 국가와 직접적인 관련이 없으면서도, 실재하는 그 무엇과도, 또는 어떤 참된 지상의 목적을 향해 곧은길로 나아가며 보낸 삶과도 똑같이 무관한 사람들 또한 그에 못지않게 넘쳐났다. 결코 존재한 적도 없는 가상의 질병에 대한 고상한 치료법으로 큰 재산을 모은 의사들이 영주의 대기실에서 자기네 궁정풍 환자들에게 미소를 지었다. 단 하나의 악이라도 진심으로 뿌리 뽑으려 일에 나서는 치료법만 빼고, 국가가 시달리는 자잘한 폐단 하나하나마다 온갖 치료법을 발견한 기획가들이, 영주의 접견에서 붙잡을 수 있는 모든 귀에 대고 정신 사나운 헛소리를 쏟아부었다. 말로써 세상을 다시 빚어내고, 하늘에 닿으려고 바벨의 카드탑을 쌓는 불신의 철학자들이, 금속의 변성에 눈독을 들이는 불신의 화학자들과 더불어, 영주가 그러모은 이 놀라운 모임에서 이야기를 나누었다. 가장 빼어난 가문 출신의 세련된 신사들 — 그 시절, 그 주목할 만한 때에, 그리고 그 이후로도, 인간의 관심을 끄는 모든 자연스러운 주제에 대한 무관심이라는 열매로 그 정체를 알아볼 수 있는 자들 — 이 영주의 저택에서 더없이 모범적인 권태의 상태에 빠져 있었다. 이 다양한 명사들이 빠리의 화려한 사교계에 남겨두고 온 가정이라는 것이 어떠했는가 하면, 영주를 떠받드는 신도 무리에 섞인 첩자들이 — 그 점잖은 손님들의 족히 절반은 차지했다 — 그 영역의 천사들 가운데서 그 몸가짐과 차림새로 보아 어머니임을 자처하는 단 한 명의 아내라도 찾아내기가 어려울 정도였다. 사실, 성가신 생명 하나를 이 세상에 데려오는 단순한 행위를 빼면 — 이것만으로는 어머니라는 이름을 실현하는 데 그다지 보탬이 되지 못한다 — 그런 것은 유행에 알려진 바가 없었다. 농촌 아낙들이 유행에 뒤떨어진 갓난아기들을 곁에 두고 길렀고, 매력적인 예순 살 할머니들은 스무 살처럼 차려입고 만찬을 들었다.\n\n비현실이라는 나병이 영주의 시중을 드는 모든 인간을 흉하게 일그러뜨렸다. 가장 바깥쪽 방에는 몇 해 전부터, 세상만사가 어쩐지 잘못되어 가고 있다는 막연한 불안을 품어온 예외적인 인물 여섯이 있었다. 그것을 바로잡을 유망한 방도라며, 그 여섯 중 절반은 어느 기괴한 경련파의 일원이 되어, 바로 그 자리에서 거품을 물고 미쳐 날뛰고 울부짖으며 강경증에 빠져들지 말지를, 그리하여 영주의 길잡이가 될 미래로 향하는 더없이 알기 쉬운 이정표를 세울지 말지를 그때까지도 마음속으로 궁리하고 있었다. 이 데르비시들 외에 다른 세 사람은 또 다른 종파로 뛰어들었는데, 그들은 \"진리의 중심\"에 관한 횡설수설로 사태를 바로잡으려 했다. 그들이 주장하기를, 인간은 진리의 중심에서 벗어났으나 — 이건 굳이 증명할 것도 없었다 — 그 원주 밖으로까지 나가지는 않았으며, 단식과 영혼을 보는 것으로써 인간이 원주 밖으로 날아가지 못하게 막고, 나아가 중심으로 도로 밀어 넣어야 한다는 것이었다. 그리하여 이들 사이에서는 영혼들과 나누는 많은 대화가 오갔으나 — 그것은 결코 드러난 적 없는 무량한 선을 베풀었다.\n\n그러나 위안이 되는 것은, 영주의 화려한 저택에 모인 손님들이 모두 완벽하게 차려입었다는 사실이었다. 최후의 심판일이 정장을 갖춰야 하는 날로 확정되기만 했다면, 그곳의 모든 이가 영원히 흠잡을 데 없었으리라. 그토록 곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고 위로 빗어 올린 머리, 그토록 정교하게 인위적으로 보존하고 손질한 섬세한 안색, 그토록 보기에 늠름한 검들, 그토록 후각에 섬세한 경의를 표하는 향내라면, 분명 무엇이든 영원토록 굴러가게 할 수 있으리라. 가장 빼어난 가문 출신의 세련된 신사들은 나른하게 움직일 때마다 짤랑이는 작은 장신구들을 매달고 다녔으니, 이 황금 족쇄는 값진 작은 종처럼 울렸다. 그렇게 울리는 소리와 더불어 비단과 금란과 고운 아마포가 사각거리는 소리에, 공기 중에 일렁임이 일어나 생땅뚜안과 그곳을 집어삼키는 굶주림을 저 멀리로 부채질해 보냈다.\n\n복식이야말로 모든 것을 제자리에 붙들어두는 데 쓰이는 단 하나의 어김없는 부적이자 마력이었다. 누구나 결코 끝나지 않을 가장무도회를 위해 차려입고 있었다. 뛸르리 궁전으로부터, 영주와 궁정 전체를 거쳐, 의회와 사법재판소를 거쳐, 그리고 (허수아비들을 뺀) 사회 전체를 거쳐, 이 가장무도회는 사형 집행인에게까지 내려갔으니, 그 또한 마력에 따라 \"곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고, 금실로 수놓은 외투에 단화와 흰 비단 양말 차림으로\" 직무를 수행하도록 요구받았다. 교수대와 형차에서 — 도끼는 드물었다 — 무슈 빠리는, 지방의 동료 집행인들인 무슈 오를레앙 등과 마찬가지로 그를 그렇게 주교식으로 부르는 것이 관례였는데, 이 우아한 차림으로 의식을 주재했다. 주님의 해 1780년에 영주의 접견에 모인 손님들 가운데, 곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고 금실 수를 놓고 단화에 흰 비단 양말을 신은 사형 집행인에 뿌리내린 체제가 별들이 다 사그라드는 날까지 살아남으리라는 것을 의심할 자가 어디 있었으랴!\n\n영주가 네 사내의 짐을 덜어주고 초콜릿을 든 뒤, 지성소의 문을 활짝 열게 하고서 밖으로 나섰다. 그러자 그 얼마나 굽실거림이며, 그 얼마나 비굴한 알랑거림이며, 그 얼마나 비루한 비굴함이며, 그 얼마나 천한 굴종이었던가! 몸과 마음을 다해 머리를 조아리는 것으로 말하자면, 하늘에 바칠 몫이라곤 그런 식으로는 하나도 남아 있지 않았다 — 이것이야말로 영주의 숭배자들이 결코 하늘을 번거롭게 하지 않은 여러 이유들 가운데 하나였을 것이다.\n\n여기서는 약속의 말 한마디, 저기서는 미소 한번, 어느 행복한 노예에게는 속삭임 한마디, 또 다른 노예에게는 손짓 한번을 베풀면서, 영주는 상냥하게 여러 방을 지나 진리의 원주 끄트머리 영역까지 나아갔다. 그곳에서 영주는 돌아서서 다시 되돌아왔고, 그리하여 때가 되자 초콜릿 요정들의 손에 이끌려 자신의 성소에 갇혀버리니, 다시는 모습을 보이지 않았다.\n\n구경거리가 끝나자 공기 중의 일렁임은 자못 작은 폭풍이 되었고, 값진 작은 종들은 짤랑거리며 아래층으로 내려갔다. 이윽고 그 많은 무리 가운데 단 한 사람만이 남았는데, 그는 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 코담뱃갑을 손에 든 채, 거울들 사이를 천천히 지나 밖으로 향했다.\n\n\"네놈을 바치노라.\" 그 사람이 나가는 길의 마지막 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 성소 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"악마에게!\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 그는 마치 발에서 먼지를 털어내듯 손가락에 묻은 코담배를 털고는 조용히 아래층으로 걸어 내려갔다.\n\n그는 예순 살가량의 남자로, 잘 차려입었고, 거동이 거만했으며, 정교한 가면 같은 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 투명하리만치 창백한 얼굴, 이목구비 하나하나가 또렷이 새겨진 얼굴, 그 위에 굳어 있는 단 하나의 표정. 그밖에는 아름답게 빚어진 코는 양쪽 콧구멍 위가 아주 살짝 오므라져 있었다. 그 두 군데 죈 자국, 곧 옴폭한 곳에 그 얼굴이 보이는 유일하게 자그마한 변화가 깃들어 있었다. 그것들은 이따금 끈질기게 빛깔을 바꾸었고, 때때로 희미한 맥박 같은 무언가에 따라 벌어졌다 오므라들었다. 그러면 얼굴 전체에 음흉함과 잔인함의 기색이 어렸다. 주의 깊게 살펴보면, 그런 표정을 거드는 그 능력은 입매와 두 눈구멍의 윤곽이 지나치게 수평적이고 가느다란 데서 비롯됨을 알 수 있었다. 그래도 얼굴이 자아내는 인상으로 보자면 그것은 잘생긴 얼굴이었고, 또한 비범한 얼굴이었다.\n\n그 임자는 아래층 안뜰로 내려가 마차에 올라 떠나갔다. 접견에서 그와 이야기를 나눈 사람은 많지 않았으니, 그는 조금 떨어진 좁은 자리에 서 있었고, 영주도 그를 좀 더 따뜻하게 대할 수 있었으련만 그러지 않았다. 이런 상황에서, 자신의 말들 앞으로 평민들이 흩어지고 종종 가까스로 깔려 죽기를 면하는 광경을 보는 것이 그에게는 자못 흐뭇한 일인 듯했다. 그의 마부는 적진으로 돌격하듯 마차를 몰았고, 마부의 그 사납고 무모한 짓에도 주인의 얼굴이나 입가에는 아무런 제지의 기색이 떠오르지 않았다. 이따금, 그 귀먹은 도시와 벙어리 같은 시대에서조차, 인도조차 없는 좁은 거리에서 험하게 마차를 모는 귀족들의 사나운 관습이 한낱 천민들을 야만스럽게 위태롭게 하고 불구로 만든다는 불평이 들려오곤 했다. 그러나 그 일을 두 번 생각할 만큼 마음 쓰는 이는 거의 없었고, 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그 가련한 비참한 이들은 알아서 곤경을 헤쳐 나가도록 내버려졌다.\n\n요즈음에는 좀처럼 이해하기 어려운, 거칠게 덜컹대고 쩔걱대는 소리와 함께, 그리고 비인간적으로 배려심이라곤 내팽개친 채, 마차는 거리를 내달리며 모퉁이를 휙휙 돌았고, 그 앞에서 여자들은 비명을 질렀으며 남자들은 서로를 부둥켜안고 아이들을 그 길에서 낚아챘다. 마침내 어느 샘가 길모퉁이를 덮치듯 돌아드는데, 바퀴 하나가 속이 메스꺼울 만큼 작게 덜컹였고, 여러 목소리에서 큰 외침이 터져 나왔으며, 말들이 앞발을 쳐들고 날뛰었다.\n\n이 마지막 사달이 아니었더라면 마차는 아마 멈추지 않았으리라. 마차들이 그대로 달려가 부상자를 뒤에 남겨두고 가는 일은 흔히 알려진 바였으니, 안 될 것이 무엇이랴? 그러나 겁에 질린 시종이 황급히 내려왔고, 말들의 고삐에는 스무 개의 손이 매달려 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 잘못됐느냐?\" 무슈가 차분히 내다보며 말했다.\n\n잠자리 모자를 쓴 키 큰 남자가 말들의 발치에서 보따리 하나를 집어 올려 샘의 받침대 위에 뉘어놓고는, 진흙과 물구덩이에 엎드린 채 짐승처럼 그 위에서 울부짖고 있었다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 후작 나리!\" 누더기를 걸친 비굴한 남자가 말했다. \"아이입니다.\"\n\n\"왜 저놈은 저 끔찍한 소리를 내느냐? 저자의 아이냐?\"\n\n\"송구합니다, 후작 나리 — 가엾게도 — 그렇습니다.\"\n\n샘은 조금 떨어져 있었으니, 그곳에서는 거리가 트여 사방 열에서 열두 야드쯤 되는 공터로 이어졌다. 키 큰 남자가 갑자기 땅바닥에서 일어나 마차를 향해 달려오자, 후작은 한순간 검 자루에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"죽었어!\" 남자가 사납게 절망하여 두 팔을 머리 위로 한껏 뻗고 그를 노려보며 비명을 질렀다. \"죽었다고!\"\n\n사람들이 둘러서서 후작을 바라보았다. 그를 바라보는 그 숱한 눈에서 드러나는 것은 오로지 경계심과 간절함뿐, 눈에 보이는 위협이나 분노는 없었다. 사람들은 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 처음의 그 외침 이후로 그들은 입을 다물었고, 그대로 침묵을 지켰다. 아까 말을 한 비굴한 남자의 목소리는 그 극진한 굴종 속에서 밋밋하고 맥이 없었다. 후작은 그들 모두를, 마치 제 굴에서 기어 나온 한낱 쥐들인 양 눈으로 훑었다.\n\n그는 지갑을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"나로서는 참 별일이다.\" 그가 말했다. \"너희 같은 것들이 제 한 몸과 제 자식 하나 건사하지 못한다는 것이. 너희 중 누구 하나는 늘 길을 막고 있단 말이다. 너희가 내 말들에 무슨 상해를 입혔는지 내 어찌 알겠느냐. 옜다! 저자에게 이걸 주어라.\"\n\n그는 시종이 줍도록 금화 한 닢을 던졌고, 그것이 떨어질 때 모든 눈이 그것을 내려다보려고 모든 머리가 앞으로 쭉 빼졌다. 키 큰 남자는 다시 더없이 섬뜩한 비명으로 외쳤다. \"죽었어!\"\n\n또 다른 남자가 잽싸게 다가오는 바람에 그는 외침을 멈추었고, 나머지 사람들은 그에게 길을 비켜주었다. 그를 보자 그 가엾은 사내는 그의 어깨에 쓰러져 흐느끼며 울었고, 샘 쪽을 가리켰는데, 그곳에서는 몇몇 여자들이 미동도 없는 보따리 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 그것을 조심스럽게 매만지고 있었다. 그러나 그 여자들 또한 남자들처럼 말이 없었다.\n\n\"다 알아, 다 안다네.\" 마지막에 온 남자가 말했다. \"용감한 사람이 되게, 내 친구 가스빠르! 저 가엾은 어린것에게는 저렇게 사느니 죽는 편이 낫다네. 고통 없이 한순간에 죽었어. 한 시간인들 저렇게 행복하게 살 수 있었겠나?\"\n\n\"이봐, 거기 자네, 자네는 철학자로군.\" 후작이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이름이 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"드파르주라고 합니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 일을 하는가?\"\n\n\"후작 나리, 포도주 장수입니다.\"\n\n\"그것을 집어라, 철학자이자 포도주 장수여.\" 후작이 그에게 또 다른 금화 한 닢을 던지며 말했다. \"그러고는 좋을 대로 써버려라. 거기 말들은, 괜찮으냐?\"\n\n후작은 다시 한번 그 무리를 거들떠보지도 않은 채 좌석에 등을 기댔고, 어쩌다 흔해빠진 물건 하나를 깨뜨리고 그 값을 치렀으며 또 능히 치를 수 있는 신사의 태도로 막 마차를 몰아 떠나려는 참이었다. 그때 동전 한 닢이 그의 마차 안으로 날아들어 바닥에 쨍그랑 울리는 바람에 그의 평온이 별안간 깨졌다.\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 후작이 말했다. \"말들을 멈춰라! 누가 저것을 던졌느냐?\"\n\n그는 조금 전까지 포도주 장수 드파르주가 서 있던 자리를 바라보았다. 그러나 그 비참한 아버지는 바로 그 자리 포석 위에 얼굴을 박고 엎드려 있었고, 그 곁에 서 있는 모습은 뜨개질을 하는, 가무잡잡하고 다부진 여자의 모습이었다.\n\n\"이 개 같은 것들!\" 후작이 말했으나, 코 위의 그 자국만은 예외로, 매끄럽게, 흐트러짐 없는 표정으로 말했다. \"너희 중 누구라도 기꺼이 깔아뭉개서 이 땅에서 쓸어버리고 싶구나. 어느 놈이 마차에 던졌는지 안다면, 그리고 그 도적놈이 충분히 가까이 있다면, 바퀴 아래 짓이겨놓을 텐데.\"\n\n그들의 처지는 그토록 주눅 들어 있었고, 그런 사내가 법의 테두리 안에서든 밖에서든 그들에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있는지 오래도록 모질게 겪어왔던 터라, 목소리 하나, 손 하나, 심지어 눈길 하나조차 들지 않았다. 남자들 가운데는 단 하나도 없었다. 그러나 서서 뜨개질을 하던 여자만은 흔들림 없이 고개를 들어 후작의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그것을 알은체하는 것은 그의 체면에 어울리지 않는 일이었다. 그의 경멸에 찬 눈길은 그녀를, 그리고 다른 모든 쥐들을 스쳐 지나갔다. 그러고는 다시 좌석에 등을 기대고 \"가자!\" 하고 명령을 내렸다.\n\n마차는 다시 내달렸고, 다른 마차들이 잇따라 빠르게 휙휙 지나갔으니, 장관, 국가 기획가, 징세청부업자, 의사, 변호사, 성직자, 그랜드 오페라, 희극, 그 가장무도회 전체가 환하게 끊임없이 흐르며 휙휙 지나갔다. 쥐들은 제 굴에서 기어 나와 구경을 했고, 몇 시간이고 구경을 계속했다. 군인들과 경찰이 자주 그들과 그 구경거리 사이를 오가며 장벽을 만들었고, 그들은 그 뒤로 슬그머니 숨어 그 사이로 엿보았다. 아버지는 이미 오래전에 제 보따리를 거두어 그것을 안고 어딘가로 숨어버렸고, 보따리가 샘의 받침대 위에 놓여 있는 동안 그것을 돌보던 여자들은 그곳에 앉아 흐르는 물과 굴러가는 가장무도회를 지켜보았다 — 눈에 띄게 서서 뜨개질을 하던 그 한 여자는 여전히 운명의 한결같음으로 뜨개질을 계속했다. 샘물이 흘렀고, 빠른 강물이 흘렀고, 낮은 흘러 저녁이 되었고, 도시의 그 많은 생명이 정해진 이치대로 죽음으로 흘러갔으며, 세월과 물때는 그 누구도 기다려주지 않았고, 쥐들은 다시 어두운 굴속에 옹기종기 모여 잠들었으며, 가장무도회는 만찬에 환히 불을 밝혔고, 만물이 제 갈 길을 갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. Monseigneur in the Country\n\n옥수수가 환히 빛나지만 그리 풍성하지는 않은 아름다운 풍경. 옥수수가 자라야 할 곳에는 메마른 호밀이 듬성듬성, 메마른 완두와 콩이 듬성듬성, 밀을 대신할 가장 거친 채소들이 듬성듬성 들어차 있었다. 생명 없는 자연 위에도, 그것을 일구는 남자와 여자들 위에서와 마찬가지로, 마지못해 겨우 자라나는 듯한 기색이 만연해 있었다 — 포기하고 시들어버리고 싶다는 풀죽은 성정이.\n\n후작 나리는 여행용 마차에 올라 — 좀더 가벼웠어도 좋았을 마차였다 — 네 마리 역마와 두 명의 마부에게 이끌려 가파른 언덕을 힘겹게 올라갔다. 후작 나리의 얼굴에 떠오른 홍조는 그의 고귀한 혈통을 흠잡을 만한 것이 못 되었으니, 그것은 안에서 비롯된 것이 아니라 그가 어찌할 수 없는 외부의 사정 — 지는 해 — 에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n마차가 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀을 때 노을이 어찌나 찬란하게 여행용 마차 안으로 비쳐들었던지, 그 안에 탄 사람이 진홍빛에 흠뻑 잠겼다. \"곧 사그라지겠지.\" 후작 나리가 자기 두 손을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"당장에.\"\n\n실제로 해가 어찌나 낮게 걸려 있었던지 바로 그 순간 가라앉아버렸다. 묵직한 제동장치가 바퀴에 맞춰지고 마차가 잿내를 풍기며 흙먼지 구름 속에서 언덕을 미끄러져 내려갈 무렵, 붉은 광채는 빠르게 사라졌다. 해와 후작이 함께 저물어, 제동장치가 풀렸을 때는 아무런 광채도 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n그러나 험준한 땅이, 가파르고 탁 트인 채로 남아 있었고, 언덕 아래에는 작은 마을이 있었으며, 그 너머로는 넓게 펼쳐져 솟아오른 비탈과 교회의 탑, 풍차, 사냥터로 쓰이는 숲, 그리고 감옥으로 쓰이는 요새가 얹힌 바위 절벽이 있었다. 밤이 다가오면서 점점 어두워지는 이 모든 것들을, 후작은 집에 가까워지는 사람의 기색으로 둘러보았다.\n\n마을에는 초라한 거리가 하나 있었고, 거기에는 초라한 양조장, 초라한 무두질 작업장, 초라한 선술집, 역마를 갈아매는 초라한 마구간 마당, 초라한 샘이 있었으니, 모두가 으레 있을 법한 초라한 시설이었다. 마을에는 초라한 사람들도 있었다. 마을 사람들은 모두 가난했고, 그들 가운데 많은 이들이 저녁거리로 쓸 자투리 양파 따위를 다듬으며 문간에 앉아 있었고, 또 많은 이들은 샘가에서 먹을 수 있는 잎사귀며 풀이며 땅에서 거둔 자잘한 소출 따위를 씻고 있었다. 무엇이 그들을 가난하게 만들었는지를 또렷이 드러내는 표지가 모자라지 않았으니, 국가에 바치는 세금, 교회에 바치는 세금, 영주에게 바치는 세금, 지방세와 일반세를 이곳에서도 저곳에서도 내야 한다는 것이 그 작은 마을에 엄숙히 새겨져 있었고, 마침내 통째로 삼켜지지 않고 남아난 마을이 있다는 것이 오히려 놀라울 지경이었다.\n\n아이들은 거의 보이지 않았고, 개는 한 마리도 없었다. 남자와 여자들로 말하자면, 이 세상에서 그들이 택할 수 있는 길은 눈앞의 전망 속에 다 드러나 있었으니 — 방앗간 아래 작은 마을에서 목숨을 부지할 수 있는 최저한의 조건으로 사는 삶, 아니면 바위 절벽 위에 군림하는 감옥에서의 감금과 죽음이었다.\n\n앞서 달려가는 전령과, 마치 복수의 여신들을 거느리고 오기라도 하는 듯 저녁 공기 속에서 뱀처럼 머리 위로 휘감기며 울리는 마부들의 채찍 소리에 알려져, 후작 나리는 여행용 마차를 역참 문 앞에 세웠다. 그곳은 샘 바로 곁이었고, 농부들은 하던 일을 멈추고 그를 바라보았다. 그도 그들을 바라보았으나, 자신은 알지 못한 채, 그들에게서 비참함에 닳고 닳은 얼굴과 몸이 더디지만 틀림없이 깎여나가는 것을 보았으니, 그것은 프랑스인의 깡마름을 영국인의 미신으로 만들어, 백 년의 대부분 동안 그 진실을 넘어 살아남게 할 바로 그것이었다.\n\n후작 나리는 자기 앞에 고개 숙인 순종적인 얼굴들 위로 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 자신과 같은 부류가 궁정의 영주 앞에 고개 숙였던 것과 같은 모습이었다 — 다만 다른 점이라면, 이 얼굴들은 그저 견디려고 고개를 숙였을 뿐, 비위를 맞추려는 것이 아니었다 — 그때 머리가 희끗희끗한 도로 보수공 하나가 그 무리에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"저자를 이리로 데려오너라!\" 후작이 전령에게 말했다.\n\n그 사내가 모자를 손에 든 채 끌려왔고, 다른 사내들은 빠리의 샘가에서 사람들이 그러했던 것과 같은 모양새로 보고 들으려고 빙 둘러섰다.\n\n\"내가 길에서 너를 지나쳤지?\"\n\n\"나리, 그렇습니다. 길에서 지나쳐주시는 영광을 입었습니다.\"\n\n\"언덕을 올라올 때, 그리고 언덕 꼭대기에서, 둘 다였더냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇을 그토록 뚫어지게 보고 있었느냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 저는 그 사내를 보고 있었습니다.\"\n\n그는 몸을 약간 숙이고는 너덜너덜한 파란 모자로 마차 밑을 가리켰다. 그의 동료들도 모두 몸을 숙여 마차 밑을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"무슨 사내냐, 이 돼지 같은 놈아? 그리고 왜 거기를 보느냐?\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 나리. 그자가 신발의 사슬에 — 그 제동장치에 매달려 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"누가?\" 여행객이 다그쳤다.\n\n\"나리, 그 사내가요.\"\n\n\"이 멍청이들을 악마가 데려가버렸으면! 그 사내를 뭐라고 부르느냐? 너는 이 고장 사람들을 다 알 텐데. 그자가 누구였느냐?\"\n\n\"자비를 베푸소서, 나리! 그자는 이 고장 사람이 아니었습니다. 제 평생 단 한 번도 본 적 없는 자입니다.\"\n\n\"사슬에 매달려서? 질식이라도 하려고?\"\n\n\"황송하오나, 바로 그게 이상한 점이었습니다, 나리. 그자가 머리를 이렇게 늘어뜨린 채로 — 이렇게요!\"\n\n그는 몸을 옆으로 돌려 마차 쪽을 향하더니, 얼굴을 하늘로 쳐든 채 머리를 아래로 늘어뜨리고 뒤로 젖혔다. 그러고는 다시 몸을 바로 세우고 모자를 만지작거리며 절을 했다.\n\n\"그자가 어떻게 생겼더냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 그자는 방앗간 주인보다도 더 하얬습니다. 온통 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고, 유령처럼 하얗고, 유령처럼 키가 컸습니다!\"\n\n이 묘사는 작은 무리 가운데 엄청난 반향을 일으켰다. 그러나 모든 눈길이, 서로 의견을 견주어보지도 않고, 후작 나리를 바라보았다. 아마도 그의 양심에 어떤 유령이라도 걸려 있는지 살펴보려는 것이었으리라.\n\n\"참으로 잘했구나.\" 후작이, 그런 벌레 같은 것들 따위로는 자신이 동요하지 않으리라는 것을 흡족하게 깨달으며 말했다. \"도둑놈 하나가 내 마차에 따라붙는 것을 보고도 그 큰 입을 열지 않다니. 흥! 저자를 치워버려라, 가벨 씨!\"\n\n가벨 씨는 역참 관리인이자 다른 징세 관리직을 겸한 자였다. 그는 이 심문을 거들려고 잔뜩 굽실거리며 나와서, 심문받는 자의 옷소매를 관에서 하듯 붙잡고 있었다.\n\n\"흥! 비켜라!\" 가벨 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"이 낯선 자가 오늘 밤 네 마을에 묵으려 하거든 붙잡아 두고, 그자의 용무가 정당한 것인지 반드시 확인하라, 가벨.\"\n\n\"나리, 분부에 제 몸을 바칠 수 있다니 영광입니다.\"\n\n\"그자가 달아났느냐, 이놈아? — 그 빌어먹을 놈은 어디 있느냐?\"\n\n그 빌어먹을 놈은 이미 여섯 명쯤 되는 막역한 친구들과 함께 마차 밑에 들어가, 파란 모자로 사슬을 가리키고 있었다. 또다른 여섯 명쯤 되는 막역한 친구들이 잽싸게 그를 끌어내, 숨을 헐떡이는 그를 후작 나리 앞에 대령했다.\n\n\"우리가 제동장치 때문에 멈춰 섰을 때 그 사내가 달아났느냐, 이 둔한 놈아?\"\n\n\"나리, 그자는 사람이 강물에 뛰어들 듯이 머리부터 언덕 비탈 너머로 곤두박질쳤습니다.\"\n\n\"잘 처리하라, 가벨. 출발!\"\n\n사슬을 들여다보던 그 여섯 명은 양 떼처럼 여전히 바퀴들 사이에 있었다. 바퀴가 어찌나 갑작스레 돌아갔던지, 그들이 살가죽과 뼈를 건사한 것만도 다행이었다. 그밖에는 건사할 것이 거의 없었으니, 아니었다면 그토록 운이 좋지는 못했을지도 모른다.\n\n마차가 마을을 빠져나와 그 너머의 비탈을 오르며 내달린 기세는, 언덕의 가파름 탓에 곧 꺾이고 말았다. 마차는 점차 걸음걸이 속도로 잦아들어, 여름밤의 갖가지 달콤한 향기 사이를 흔들거리며 덜컹덜컹 올라갔다. 마부들은 복수의 여신들 대신 천 마리의 아른거리는 각다귀들에게 둘러싸인 채, 채찍 끝의 매듭을 조용히 손보았다. 시종은 말들 곁에서 걸었고, 전령이 흐릿한 먼 곳으로 종종걸음 쳐 나아가는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n언덕에서 가장 가파른 지점에 작은 묘지가 있었는데, 거기에는 십자가와 새로 만든 커다란 구세주 상이 세워져 있었다. 그것은 경험이 부족한 어느 시골 조각가가 만든 초라한 나무 상이었으나, 그는 실물을 — 어쩌면 자기 자신의 삶을 — 본떠 그 상을 빚은 듯했으니, 그것이 무시무시하게 깡마르고 여위어 있었기 때문이다.\n\n오래도록 점점 더 나빠져왔으나 아직 최악에는 이르지 않은 큰 고난을 나타내는 이 비통한 표상 앞에, 한 여인이 무릎 꿇고 있었다. 마차가 다가오자 그녀는 고개를 돌려 재빨리 일어서더니 마차 문 앞으로 다가섰다.\n\n\"나리시군요! 나리, 청이 하나 있습니다.\"\n\n참다못한 탄성을 내뱉으면서도 변함없는 얼굴로, 영주는 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n\"또 뭐냐! 무슨 일이냐? 늘 청원이로구나!\"\n\n\"나리. 위대하신 하느님의 사랑을 걸고! 제 남편, 그 산지기 말입니다.\"\n\n\"네 남편, 그 산지기가 어쨌다는 것이냐? 너희들은 늘 똑같구나. 무언가를 못 내겠다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"그이는 다 냈습니다, 나리. 죽었으니까요.\"\n\n\"음! 그러면 편히 쉬고 있겠구나. 내가 그를 네게 되돌려줄 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아아, 그건 안 되지요, 나리! 하지만 그이는 저기, 보잘것없는 풀이 작게 쌓인 무더기 밑에 누워 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"나리, 보잘것없는 풀이 작게 쌓인 무더기가 너무도 많습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서, 또?\"\n\n그녀는 노파처럼 보였으나 젊었다. 그녀의 태도에는 격정적인 슬픔이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 핏줄이 불거지고 마디진 두 손을 사납게 마주 그러쥐었다가, 그중 한 손을 마차 문에 얹기를 거듭했으니 — 마치 그것이 사람의 가슴이기라도 한 듯, 그래서 그 호소하는 손길을 느낄 수라도 있는 듯, 부드럽고 어루만지듯이.\n\n\"나리, 제 말을 들어주십시오! 나리, 제 청을 들어주십시오! 제 남편은 굶주려 죽었습니다. 그토록 많은 이들이 굶주려 죽고, 앞으로 더 많은 이들이 굶주려 죽을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서, 또? 내가 그들을 먹여 살리기라도 하란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 선하신 하느님께서는 아십니다. 하지만 저는 그것을 청하는 게 아닙니다. 제 청은, 제 남편의 이름을 새긴 돌이나 나무 조각을 그이 위에 세워 그가 어디 누워 있는지 표시해 달라는 것입니다. 그렇지 않으면 그 자리는 금세 잊힐 것이고, 제가 같은 병으로 죽고 나면 결코 찾을 수 없게 될 것이며, 저 또한 보잘것없는 풀이 쌓인 또다른 무더기 밑에 묻히게 될 것입니다. 나리, 그런 무더기가 너무 많고, 너무 빨리 늘어나고, 너무도 큰 결핍이 있습니다. 나리! 나리!\"\n\n시종이 그녀를 문에서 떼어놓았고, 마차는 활기찬 속보로 내달리기 시작했으며, 마부들이 속도를 높이자 그녀는 멀찍이 뒤에 남겨졌다. 다시 복수의 여신들을 거느린 영주는, 그와 그의 성 사이에 남은 일이 리외쯤의 거리를 빠르게 좁혀가고 있었다.\n\n여름밤의 달콤한 향기가 그를 에워싸고 피어올랐고, 그것은 비가 내리듯 공평하게, 멀지 않은 샘가에서 먼지투성이가 되어 누더기를 걸친 채 노동에 닳아빠진 무리 위에도 피어올랐다. 그들 곁에서 도로 보수공은, 그것이 없으면 아무것도 아닌 자신의 파란 모자의 도움을 받아, 그들이 견딜 수 있는 한 줄곧 유령 같은 그 사내에 대해 늘어놓았다. 더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 그들은 차츰 하나둘 떨어져 나갔고, 작은 여닫이창들에 불빛이 깜박였다. 그 불빛들은, 여닫이창이 어두워지고 더 많은 별이 돋아남에 따라, 꺼졌다기보다는 하늘로 쏘아 올려진 듯이 보였다.\n\n그 무렵 후작 나리 위로는 지붕이 높고 커다란 집의 그림자와 휘늘어진 수많은 나무들의 그림자가 드리워 있었다. 그리고 그 그림자는 그의 마차가 멈추고 성의 큰 문이 그를 맞아 열리면서 횃불의 빛으로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"내가 기다리는 샤를 씨, 영국에서 도착했느냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 아직입니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER IX. The Gorgon’s Head\n\n후작 나리의 그 성관은 묵직한 건물 덩어리였다. 앞에는 너른 돌마당이 있었고, 두 줄기 돌계단이 반원을 그리며 올라가 정문 앞 돌 테라스에서 만났다. 어디를 보나 온통 돌투성이여서, 묵직한 돌난간이며 돌항아리며 돌꽃이며 사람의 돌얼굴이며 사자의 돌머리가 사방에 널려 있었다. 마치 이백 년 전 그것이 완성되었을 때 고르곤의 머리가 그것을 둘러본 것만 같았다.\n\n후작 나리는 횃불을 앞세우고, 폭이 넓고 낮은 계단을 밟고 올라가 마차에서 내렸다. 그 소란은 어둠을 어지간히 흔들어, 나무들 사이 멀찍이 자리한 거대한 마구간 건물 지붕에서 부엉이 한 마리가 요란하게 항의하도록 만들기에 충분했다. 다른 모든 것은 어찌나 고요한지, 계단 위로 들고 올라온 횃불도 정문 앞에 들고 선 또 다른 횃불도, 탁 트인 밤공기 속이 아니라 닫힌 어전(御殿)의 방 안에 있는 것처럼 타올랐다. 부엉이의 울음소리 말고는, 분수가 돌 수반으로 떨어지는 소리 외에 아무 소리도 없었다. 그날 밤은 한 시간씩 통째로 숨을 죽이고 있다가 낮고 긴 한숨을 토하고는 다시 숨을 죽이는, 그런 캄캄한 밤들 가운데 하나였기 때문이다.\n\n육중한 문이 그의 등 뒤에서 쩌렁 닫혔고, 후작 나리는 묵은 멧돼지 창과 칼과 사냥용 단검 따위로 음산한 홀을 가로질렀다. 그보다 더 음산한 것은 묵직한 승마용 채찍과 가죽 채찍들이었으니, 그의 영주가 노여워할 때 그것들의 무게를 직접 느껴본 농부가, 자신의 은인인 죽음에게로 떠나간 농부가 한둘이 아니었다.\n\n밤을 맞아 어둡고 단단히 잠긴 더 큰 방들을 피해, 후작 나리는 횃불잡이를 앞세우고 계단을 올라 어느 복도의 문 앞에 이르렀다. 그 문이 활짝 열리자 그는 세 칸으로 된 자신만의 사적인 거처로 들어섰다. 침실과 다른 두 방이었다. 높은 둥근 천장에 양탄자 없는 서늘한 바닥, 겨울철 장작을 태우기 위한 큼직한 장작받침이 난로마다 놓여 있었고, 사치스러운 시대와 나라에서 후작의 신분에 걸맞은 온갖 호사가 갖추어져 있었다. 결코 끊어지지 않을 그 혈통의, 마지막 루이에서 하나 앞선 루이 — 즉 루이 14세 — 의 양식이 호화로운 가구마다 두드러졌다. 그러나 거기에는 프랑스 역사의 옛 장면들을 보여주는 숱한 물건들이 한데 섞여 변화를 주고 있었다.\n\n세 방 가운데 셋째 방에 두 사람을 위한 저녁 식탁이 차려져 있었다. 성관의 네 모퉁이에 솟은, 촛불 끄개를 얹은 듯한 탑들 가운데 하나에 자리한 둥근 방이었다. 작고 천장이 높은 방으로, 창문은 활짝 열려 있었으나 나무로 된 발 모양 덧창이 닫혀 있어서, 캄캄한 밤은 그저 검은빛의 가느다란 가로줄로만, 넓은 돌빛 가로줄과 번갈아 가며 어렴풋이 비칠 뿐이었다.\n\n\"내 조카 말이다.\" 저녁 차림을 흘긋 보며 후작이 말했다. \"그 애가 아직 도착하지 않았다고들 하던데.\"\n\n과연 그는 도착하지 않았다. 그러나 나리와 함께 오리라 예상되고 있었다.\n\n\"아! 오늘 밤 안에 그 애가 도착할 것 같지는 않구나. 그래도 식탁은 그대로 두어라. 나는 십오 분 뒤에 준비가 될 것이다.\"\n\n십오 분 뒤 나리는 준비가 되어, 호화롭고 정갈한 저녁상 앞에 홀로 앉았다. 그의 의자는 창문을 마주 보고 있었는데, 수프를 비우고 보르도 포도주잔을 입술로 가져가다가 그것을 내려놓았다.\n\n\"저게 무엇이냐?\" 그는 검은빛과 돌빛의 가로줄들을 유심히 바라보며 차분하게 물었다.\n\n\"나리? 무엇 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"덧창 바깥 말이다. 덧창을 열어라.\"\n\n분부대로 되었다.\n\n\"어떠냐?\"\n\n\"나리, 아무것도 없습니다. 여기에는 나무와 밤뿐입니다.\"\n\n말을 한 하인은 덧창을 활짝 젖히고 텅 빈 어둠 속을 내다보았으며, 그 공허를 등진 채 서서 지시를 기다리느라 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"좋다.\" 동요 없는 주인이 말했다. \"다시 닫아라.\"\n\n그 또한 분부대로 되었고, 후작은 저녁 식사를 계속했다. 절반쯤 먹었을 때, 그는 다시 손에 잔을 든 채 멈췄으니, 바퀴 소리가 들렸기 때문이다. 그 소리는 경쾌하게 다가와 성관 정면에 이르렀다.\n\n\"누가 도착했는지 물어보아라.\"\n\n나리의 조카였다. 그날 이른 오후에 그는 나리보다 몇 리그 뒤처져 있었다. 그는 그 거리를 빠르게 좁혔으나, 도중에 나리를 따라잡을 만큼 빠르지는 못했다. 그는 역참에서 나리가 앞서 가고 있다는 소식을 들었던 것이다.\n\n(나리가 분부하기를) 그에게 전하라, 저녁상이 지금 여기서 그를 기다리고 있으니 와서 들기를 청한다고. 잠시 뒤 그가 들어왔다. 그는 영국에서 찰스 다네이라는 이름으로 알려져 있었다.\n\n나리는 정중하게 그를 맞이했으나, 두 사람은 악수하지 않았다.\n\n\"어제 빠리를 떠나셨습니까, 나리?\" 식탁에 자리를 잡으며 그가 나리에게 말했다.\n\n\"어제. 너는?\"\n\n\"저는 곧장 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"런던에서?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"오는 데 오래 걸렸구나.\" 후작이 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"그 반대입니다. 저는 곧장 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"미안하구나! 내 말은, 여정이 오래 걸렸다는 게 아니라, 여정에 나서기까지 오래 걸렸다는 뜻이다.\"\n\n\"여러 가지……\" 조카는 대답하다가 잠시 멈췄다. \"여러 가지 일에 발이 묶여 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그야 의심할 바 없겠지.\" 세련된 숙부가 말했다.\n\n하인이 곁에 있는 동안에는 두 사람 사이에 다른 말이 오가지 않았다. 커피가 나오고 단둘이 남게 되자, 조카는 숙부를 바라보며, 잘 빚은 가면 같은 그 얼굴의 눈과 마주치고는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"짐작하시는 대로, 저는 저를 떠나게 했던 그 목적을 좇아 돌아왔습니다. 그것은 저를 크고 뜻밖의 위험으로 몰아넣었습니다. 그러나 그것은 신성한 목적이며, 설령 그것이 저를 죽음으로 이끌었다 해도 그것이 저를 지탱해주리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"죽음이라니.\" 숙부가 말했다. \"죽음 운운할 것까지야 없지.\"\n\n\"의심스럽습니다, 나리.\" 조카가 응수했다. \"설령 그것이 저를 죽음의 벼랑 끝까지 몰고 갔다 한들, 나리께서 거기서 저를 막아주려 하셨을지 말입니다.\"\n\n콧등에 깊이 패인 자국과, 그 잔혹한 얼굴에 곧고 가느다란 선들이 길어지는 모양새가, 그 점에 관해서는 불길해 보였다. 숙부는 우아하게 항의하는 몸짓을 했는데, 그것이 너무도 분명하게 가벼운 교양의 형식에 지나지 않아서 도무지 안심이 되지 않았다.\n\n\"실은, 나리.\" 조카가 말을 이었다. \"제가 알기로는, 저를 둘러싼 의심스러운 정황에 한층 더 의심스러운 빛을 더하려고 나리께서 일부러 손을 쓰셨을 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아니다, 아니야, 아니지.\" 숙부가 유쾌하게 말했다.\n\n\"하나, 그것이 어찌 되었든 간에,\" 깊은 불신의 눈으로 그를 흘긋 보며 조카가 다시 말했다. \"나리의 수완이라면 어떤 수단을 써서라도 저를 막으려 들 것이며, 수단에 관해서는 일말의 거리낌도 없으리라는 것을 저는 압니다.\"\n\n\"이 사람아, 내가 그렇게 말하지 않았더냐.\" 두 줄기 자국을 곱게 떨며 숙부가 말했다. \"내가 오래전에 그렇게 말했다는 것을 기억해주는 호의를 베풀어다오.\"\n\n\"기억하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙구나.\" 후작이 말했다 — 참으로 더없이 다정하게.\n\n그의 어조는 거의 악기의 음색처럼 공중에 여운을 남겼다.\n\n\"사실은, 나리.\" 조카가 말을 이었다. \"저를 이곳 프랑스의 감옥 밖에 머물러 있게 한 것은, 나리의 불운인 동시에 저의 행운이라고 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"잘 이해가 가지 않는구나.\" 숙부가 커피를 홀짝이며 응수했다. \"설명을 부탁드려도 되겠느냐?\"\n\n\"제 믿음으로는, 나리께서 궁정의 미움을 사지 않으셨고, 지난 여러 해 동안 그 먹구름에 가려지지 않으셨더라면, 봉인장 한 장이면 저를 어느 요새엔가 기약 없이 처넣었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"가능한 일이지.\" 숙부가 더없이 침착하게 말했다. \"가문의 명예를 위해서라면, 나는 그 정도까지 너를 불편하게 할 결심도 할 수 있을 게다. 부디 너그러이 보아다오!\"\n\n* 봉인장(lettre de cachet): 프랑스 국왕의 명의로 발부되어, 재판 없이 특정인을 무기한 투옥하거나 추방할 수 있게 한 봉인된 영장.\n\n\"저로서는 다행스럽게도, 그저께의 그 접견이 늘 그렇듯 냉랭했음을 알겠습니다.\" 조카가 한마디 했다.\n\"다행이라고는 하지 않으련다, 이 사람아.\" 숙부가 세련된 정중함으로 응수했다. \"나라면 그것을 장담하지 않겠다. 고독이라는 이점에 둘러싸여 곰곰이 생각해볼 좋은 기회가, 네 스스로 네 운명을 좌우하는 것보다 훨씬 더 이롭게 네 운명을 좌우할 수도 있으니 말이다. 하나 이 문제를 논해봐야 부질없지. 네 말마따나 나는 불리한 처지에 있구나. 이 자그마한 교정 도구들, 가문의 권세와 명예를 돕는 이 점잖은 수단들, 너를 그토록 불편하게 할 수 있는 이 사소한 은전들은, 이제 연줄과 끈질긴 청원 없이는 얻을 수가 없게 되었다. 워낙 많은 이들이 그것을 구하고, (상대적으로) 워낙 소수에게만 그것이 주어지니! 예전에는 그렇지 않았다만, 프랑스는 이런 일이라면 죄다 더 나쁜 쪽으로 변하고 말았다. 그리 멀지 않은 우리 조상들은 주위의 천한 것들에 대해 생사여탈의 권리를 쥐고 있었다. 이 방에서, 그런 개 같은 자들이 숱하게 끌려 나가 교수형을 당했지. 옆방 — 내 침실 — 에서는, 우리가 아는 바로는, 한 녀석이 제 딸에 관해 무엄하게도 어쩌고 하는 까탈을 부렸다는 이유로 그 자리에서 칼에 찔려 죽었다. 제 딸이라니? 우리는 숱한 특권을 잃었다. 새로운 철학이 유행이 되었으니, 요즈음 우리의 신분을 내세우는 것은 (정말로 그러리라고까지는 말하지 않겠다만) 우리에게 실질적인 불편을 끼칠 수도 있다. 다 아주 고약한 일이야, 아주 고약해!\"\n\n후작은 코담배를 점잖게 한 자밤 집어넣고는 고개를 저었다. 자기 자신이라는, 그 위대한 재생의 수단을 아직 품고 있는 나라를 두고 보여줄 수 있는 한 가장 우아하게 낙담한 모습으로.\n\n\"우리는 옛 시절에도 또 요즈음에도 우리의 신분을 어찌나 내세웠던지,\" 조카가 침울하게 말했다. \"저는 우리 이름이 프랑스의 그 어떤 이름보다도 더 혐오받고 있으리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇기를 바라자.\" 숙부가 말했다. \"높은 자에 대한 혐오는 낮은 자의 마지못한 경의이니라.\"\n\n\"이 일대의 온 나라를 둘러보아도,\" 조카가 앞서의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"제가 마주 볼 수 있는 얼굴 가운데, 두려움과 노예근성에서 비롯된 어두운 굴종 말고 다른 경의를 담아 저를 바라보는 얼굴은 하나도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그것은,\" 후작이 말했다. \"가문의 위세에 대한 찬사이며, 그 위세를 떠받쳐온 가문의 방식에 마땅히 따르는 것이지. 하!\" 그러고는 다시 코담배를 점잖게 한 자밤 집어넣고는 가볍게 다리를 꼬았다.\n\n그러나 그의 조카가 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 눈을 가린 채 깊은 생각에 잠겨 의기소침해 있을 때, 그 잘 빚은 가면은 그것을 쓴 자가 짐짓 꾸민 무관심과는 어울리지 않을 만큼 더욱 강렬하게 응축된 날카로움과 음험함과 혐오를 담아 곁눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"억압이야말로 유일하게 오래가는 철학이다. 두려움과 노예근성에서 비롯된 어두운 굴종은, 이 사람아,\" 후작이 한마디 했다. \"이 지붕이,\" 그것을 올려다보며, \"하늘을 가려주는 한, 개들을 채찍에 순종하게 만들어줄 것이다.\"\n\n그것은 후작이 생각하는 만큼 오래가지 않을 수도 있었다. 불과 몇 해 뒤의 이 성관의 모습이, 그리고 그것과 마찬가지로 불과 몇 해 뒤가 될 비슷한 성관 쉰 채의 모습이 그날 밤 그에게 보여졌더라면, 그는 그 끔찍하게 불에 그을리고 약탈로 폐허가 된 잔해들 가운데서 자기 것을 가려내느라 난감해했을지도 모른다. 그가 으스대던 지붕으로 말하자면, 그는 그것이 새로운 방식으로 하늘을 가려주는 것을 보았을지도 모른다 — 다시 말해, 십만 자루의 머스킷 총신에서 발사된 그 지붕의 납덩이가 박혀버린 시신들의 눈에서, 영원히 하늘을 가려주는 것을 말이다.\n\n\"그동안,\" 후작이 말했다. \"네가 하지 않겠다면 나는 가문의 명예와 안녕을 지키겠다. 그러나 너는 피곤하겠구나. 오늘 밤 우리의 담화는 이쯤에서 끝낼까?\"\n\n\"잠시만 더.\"\n\n\"한 시간이라도 좋다.\"\n\n\"나리,\" 조카가 말했다. \"우리는 잘못을 저질렀고, 그 잘못의 열매를 거두고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 잘못을 저질렀다고?\" 후작이 묻는 듯한 미소를 띠고, 먼저 조카를, 다음에 자기 자신을 섬세하게 가리키며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"우리 가문, 우리의 명예로운 가문 말입니다. 그 명예가 우리 둘 모두에게 — 비록 서로 너무나 다른 방식으로이지만 — 그토록 중요한 그 가문 말입니다. 제 아버지의 시절에도 우리는 한없이 많은 잘못을 저질렀으니, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 우리와 우리의 쾌락 사이에 끼어든 모든 인간을 해쳤습니다. 그것이 똑같이 나리의 시절이기도 한데, 제 아버지의 시절을 굳이 말할 필요가 있겠습니까? 제 아버지의 쌍둥이 형제이자, 공동 상속자이며, 바로 다음 계승자인 나리를 제가 아버지와 따로 떼어 생각할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"죽음이 그렇게 만들어주었지!\" 후작이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 죽음은 저를,\" 조카가 대답했다. \"저로서는 끔찍하기 짝이 없는 어떤 체제에 옭아맨 채로, 그것에 대한 책임은 지우되 그 안에서는 무력하기만 한 채로 남겨놓았습니다. 자비를 베풀고 바로잡으라고 간청하셨던, 제 사랑하는 어머니의 입술이 남긴 마지막 부탁을 이행하려, 또 제 사랑하는 어머니의 눈이 남긴 마지막 눈길에 따르려 애쓰면서, 그러나 도움과 힘을 헛되이 구하느라 고통받으면서 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그것을 나에게서 구하다니, 내 조카야,\" 후작이 집게손가락으로 그의 가슴을 건드리며 말했다 — 두 사람은 이제 난로 옆에 서 있었다 — \"두고 보아라, 너는 그것을 영원히 헛되이 구하게 될 것이다.\"\n\n그가 손에 코담뱃갑을 든 채 조용히 조카를 바라보며 서 있는 동안, 그 맑고 새하얀 얼굴의 곧고 가느다란 선들은 하나하나 잔혹하게, 음흉하게, 빈틈없이 오므라들었다. 그는 다시 한번 그의 가슴을 건드렸으니, 마치 그 손가락이 작은 칼의 가느다란 칼끝이어서, 섬세한 술책으로 그 칼을 조카의 몸에 꿰뚫어 박기라도 하는 것 같았다. 그러고는 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람아, 나는 내가 살아온 그 체제를 길이 이어가며 죽을 것이다.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 코담배를 한 자밤 마지막으로 집어넣고는 갑을 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n\"이성적인 인간이 되는 편이 낫지.\" 그는 식탁 위의 작은 종을 울리고 나서 덧붙였다. \"그리고 네 타고난 운명을 받아들이는 편이. 그러나 너는 가망이 없는 게로구나, 찰스, 보아하니.\"\n\n\"이 재산도 프랑스도 제게는 잃어버린 것입니다.\" 조카가 서글프게 말했다. \"저는 그것들을 버립니다.\"\n\n\"그 둘 다 네가 버릴 만큼 네 것이더냐? 프랑스는 그럴지 모르나, 재산이 그러하더냐? 입에 올릴 가치도 거의 없다만, 아직 네 것이더냐?\"\n\n\"제가 한 말로 그것을 지금 제 것이라 주장하려던 의도는 없었습니다. 만약 그것이 나리에게서 제게 넘어온다면, 내일이라도—\"\n\n\"내가 허영으로나마 바라건대, 그럴 것 같지는 않다만.\"\n\n\"—혹은 이십 년 뒤에라도—\"\n\n\"내게 과분한 영예로구나.\" 후작이 말했다. \"그래도 나는 그쪽 가정이 더 마음에 드는구나.\"\n\n\"—저는 그것을 내버리고, 다른 곳에서 다르게 살겠습니다. 단념하기에 그것은 하찮은 것입니다. 그것이 비참과 폐허의 황무지 말고 무엇이겠습니까!\"\n\n\"하!\" 후작이 호화로운 방을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"여기서는, 눈으로 보기에 그럴듯해 보입니다. 그러나 하늘 아래 대낮의 빛으로 그 전모를 보면, 그것은 낭비와 실정과 강탈과 부채와 저당과 압제와 굶주림과 헐벗음과 고통으로 이루어진, 허물어져가는 탑입니다.\"\n\n\"하!\" 후작이 흡족한 투로 다시 말했다.\n\n\"만약 그것이 언젠가 제 것이 된다면, 그것을 짓누르는 그 무게로부터 (그럴 수만 있다면) 그것을 서서히 풀어줄 더 나은 자격을 갖춘 손에 맡길 것입니다. 그리하여 그곳을 떠날 수 없고 오래도록 견딤의 마지막 한계까지 쥐어짜인 그 가련한 사람들이, 다음 세대에는 덜 고통받게 말입니다. 그러나 그것은 제가 할 일이 아닙니다. 그것에는, 그리고 이 온 땅에는 저주가 내려 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"그러면 너는?\" 숙부가 말했다. \"내 호기심을 용서해다오. 너는 그 새로운 철학에 따라, 황송하게도 살아갈 작정이냐?\"\n\n\"살아가려면, 저는 다른 동포들이, 비록 등 뒤에 귀족 신분을 두고 있는 자들조차, 언젠가는 하게 될지도 모를 일을 해야 합니다 — 일 말입니다.\"\n\n\"가령, 영국에서?\"\n\n\"예. 가문의 명예는, 나리, 이 나라에서는 저로 인해 안전합니다. 가문의 이름은 다른 어떤 나라에서도 저로 인해 손상될 일이 없으니, 다른 어떤 나라에서도 제가 그 이름을 쓰지 않기 때문입니다.\"\n\n종을 울린 탓에 옆방 침실에 불이 밝혀졌다. 이제 그 불빛이 사이를 잇는 문을 통해 환하게 비쳐 들었다. 후작은 그쪽을 바라보며, 시종이 물러가는 발소리에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"네가 영국에서 그토록 시원찮게 형편을 폈던 것을 보면, 영국이 너에게는 무척 매력적인 모양이로구나.\" 그러고서 그는 차분한 얼굴을 미소와 함께 조카에게로 돌리며 한마디 했다.\n\n\"이미 말씀드렸듯이, 제가 그곳에서 형편을 편 것에 대해서는 나리께 신세를 졌을지도 모른다는 것을 잘 알고 있습니다. 그 나머지로 말하자면, 그곳은 저의 피난처입니다.\"\n\n\"잘난 체하는 그 영국인들이 말하기를, 그곳은 숱한 이들의 피난처라더구나. 그곳에서 피난처를 찾은 동포 한 사람을 알고 있느냐? 어떤 의사 말이다.\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"딸을 둔?\"\n\n\"예.\"\n\n\"그래.\" 후작이 말했다. \"너는 피곤하겠구나. 잘 자거라!\"\n\n그가 더없이 정중한 태도로 고개를 숙일 때, 그 미소 띤 얼굴에는 무언가 은밀함이 어려 있었고, 그는 그 말에 알 수 없는 분위기를 실어 보냈으니, 그것이 조카의 눈과 귀를 강하게 때렸다. 동시에, 눈가의 가느다랗고 곧은 선들과, 가느다랗고 곧은 입술과, 콧등의 자국들이, 곱상하게 악마적으로 보이는 빈정거림으로 휘어졌다.\n\n\"그래.\" 후작이 되뇌었다. \"딸을 둔 의사라. 그래. 새로운 철학은 이렇게 시작되는 게로구나! 너는 피곤하겠다. 잘 자거라!\"\n\n성관 바깥의 그 어떤 돌얼굴을 캐묻는 것이나, 그의 그 얼굴을 캐묻는 것이나 매한가지로 부질없을 터였다. 조카는 문 쪽으로 걸어가면서 그를 바라보았으나, 헛일이었다.\n\n\"잘 자거라!\" 숙부가 말했다. \"아침에 너를 다시 보는 즐거움을 고대하마. 편히 쉬어라! 거기 내 조카 나리를 침소로 안내해 불을 밝혀드려라! — 그리고 좋다면, 내 조카 나리를 침대째 태워버려라.\" 그는 다시 작은 종을 울려 시종을 자기 침실로 부르기에 앞서, 혼잣말로 덧붙였다.\n\n시종이 왔다 가고 나서, 후작 나리는 그 무덥고 고요한 밤에 헐렁한 실내복 차림으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며, 잠을 청하려 천천히 채비를 했다. 부드러운 슬리퍼를 신은 그의 발이 바닥에 아무 소리도 내지 않은 채, 그는 방 안을 사부작거리며, 마치 세련된 호랑이처럼 움직였다. 이야기 속에 나오는, 회개를 모르는 사악한 부류의 어느 마법에 걸린 후작 같았으니, 주기적으로 호랑이 형상으로 바뀌는 변신이 막 끝나가는 참이거나 막 시작되려는 참인 그런 후작 말이다.\n\n그는 자신의 관능적인 침실을 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 거닐며, 청하지도 않았는데 마음속에 떠오르는 그날 여정의 단편들을 다시 떠올렸다. 해 질 녘 언덕을 더디게 오르던 일, 지던 해, 내리막길, 물방앗간, 바위 위의 감옥, 우묵한 곳의 작은 마을, 분수가의 농부들, 그리고 푸른 모자를 쓴 채 마차 밑의 쇠사슬을 가리키던 도로 보수공. 그 분수는 빠리의 분수를, 계단에 놓여 있던 작은 보따리를, 그 위로 몸을 굽히던 여인들을, 그리고 두 팔을 치켜든 채 \"죽었어!\" 하고 외치던 키 큰 사내를 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"이제 마음이 가라앉았으니,\" 후작 나리가 말했다. \"잠자리에 들어도 되겠구나.\"\n\n그리하여 큰 난로에 등불 하나만을 켜둔 채, 그는 얇은 망사 휘장을 둘레로 드리우고, 잠을 청하려 몸을 누일 때 밤이 긴 한숨으로 그 정적을 깨는 소리를 들었다.\n\n바깥벽의 돌얼굴들은 무거운 세 시간 동안 캄캄한 밤을 맹목적으로 응시했다. 무거운 세 시간 동안, 마구간의 말들은 여물통에 대고 덜그럭거렸고, 개들은 짖었으며, 부엉이는 사람 시인들이 관습적으로 부엉이에게 부여한 소리와는 거의 닮지 않은 소리를 냈다. 그러나 자기들에게 정해진 대로 좀처럼 말하는 법이 없는 것이, 그런 짐승들의 고집스러운 습성이다.\n\n무거운 세 시간 동안, 성관의 돌얼굴들은, 사자며 사람이며, 밤을 맹목적으로 응시했다. 죽은 듯한 어둠이 온 풍경 위에 깔렸고, 죽은 듯한 어둠은 온 길 위에 내려앉아 잠재우는 먼지에 제 나름의 고요를 더했다. 묘지는 빈약한 풀더미들을 서로 분간할 수 없는 지경에 이르렀고, 십자가 위의 형상은, 눈에 보이는 한에서는, 내려와버렸을 법도 했다. 마을에서는 세금을 매기는 자와 세금을 무는 자가 깊이 잠들어 있었다. 굶주린 자들이 흔히 그러하듯 잔칫상을 꿈꾸고, 내몰린 노예와 멍에 멘 황소가 그러할 법하듯 안락과 휴식을 꿈꾸며, 그 비쩍 마른 주민들은 곤히 잠들어, 배불리 먹고 자유를 누렸다.\n\n마을의 분수는 보이지도 들리지도 않게 흘렀고, 성관의 분수는 보이지도 들리지도 않게 떨어졌다 — 둘 다, 세월의 샘에서 떨어지는 시간들처럼 녹아 사라지며 — 캄캄한 세 시간을 통과해서. 이윽고 양쪽의 잿빛 물이 빛 속에서 유령처럼 어렴풋해지기 시작했고, 성관의 돌얼굴들의 눈이 떠졌다.\n\n점점 더 밝아져, 마침내 해가 고요한 나무들의 우듬지에 닿아 그 광휘를 언덕 위로 쏟아부었다. 그 광채 속에서, 성관 분수의 물은 핏빛으로 변하는 듯했고, 돌얼굴들은 진홍빛으로 물들었다. 새들의 지저귐이 드높이 울려 퍼졌고, 후작 나리의 침실 큰 창문의 비바람에 닳은 창턱에서, 작은 새 한 마리가 온 힘을 다해 더없이 고운 노래를 불렀다. 이에 가장 가까운 돌얼굴은 놀라 멍하니 바라보는 듯했고, 입을 벌리고 아래턱을 늘어뜨린 채 경외에 사로잡힌 모습이었다.\n\n이제 해가 완전히 떠올랐고, 마을에 움직임이 일기 시작했다. 여닫이창들이 열리고, 부서질 듯한 문들의 빗장이 풀렸으며, 사람들이 — 새로 든 달콤한 공기에 아직 으슬으슬 떨면서 — 밖으로 나왔다. 그러고는 마을 사람들 사이에 좀처럼 누그러질 줄 모르는 그날의 노역이 시작되었다. 더러는 분수가로, 더러는 들판으로. 여기서는 남자와 여자가 땅을 파고 일구러, 저기서는 남자와 여자가 가련한 가축을 보살피고 뼈만 앙상한 소들을 끌어내 길가에서 찾아낼 수 있는 만큼의 풀밭으로 데려가러. 교회에서, 또 십자가 앞에서, 무릎 꿇은 형상이 한둘. 후자의 기도에 시중드는 것은, 끌려온 그 소였으니, 발치의 잡초들 사이에서 아침거리를 찾고 있었다.\n\n성관은 그 신분에 걸맞게 더 늦게 깨어났으나, 차츰차츰 그러나 어김없이 깨어났다. 먼저, 외따로 걸린 멧돼지 창과 사냥용 단검들이 예전처럼 붉게 물들었다. 그러고는 아침 햇살 속에 날카롭게 번득였다. 이제, 문과 창문이 활짝 열렸고, 마구간의 말들은 어깨너머로 문간으로 쏟아져 드는 빛과 신선함을 돌아보았으며, 쇠창살 창에서는 나뭇잎들이 반짝이며 바스락거렸고, 개들은 쇠사슬을 힘껏 잡아당기며 풀려나고 싶어 안달하며 곧추섰다.\n\n이 모든 사소한 일들은 삶의 일상에, 그리고 아침의 돌아옴에 속하는 것이었다. 그러나 성관의 큰 종이 울리는 것이며, 계단을 오르내리며 뛰어다니는 것이며, 테라스 위를 서두르는 형상들이며, 여기저기 사방에서 구둣발을 구르며 쿵쾅거리는 것이며, 황급히 말에 안장을 얹고 달려나가는 것이, 설마 그러할 리는 없지 않은가?\n\n어떤 바람이 이 소란을, 마을 너머 언덕 꼭대기에서 이미 일하고 있던 그 희끗희끗한 도로 보수공에게 실어 날랐을까? 그날의 점심밥은 (가져갈 것도 별로 없어) 돌무더기 위에 보따리째 놓여 있어, 까마귀가 쪼아볼 만한 값어치도 없었다. 새들이 그 낟알 몇 알을 멀리 나르다가, 우연한 씨앗을 뿌리듯 한 알을 그의 머리 위로 떨어뜨리기라도 했을까? 그랬든 안 그랬든, 도로 보수공은 그 무더운 아침에 마치 목숨이라도 걸린 듯, 무릎까지 빠지는 먼지를 헤치며 언덕을 달려 내려가, 분수가에 이르기까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다.\n\n마을 사람들이 죄다 분수가에 나와, 그 풀 죽은 모습으로 서성이며 낮게 소곤거렸으나, 음울한 호기심과 놀라움 말고 다른 감정은 드러내지 않았다. 황급히 끌려와 붙들어 맬 수 있는 아무것에나 매어둔 소들은, 멍하니 바라보거나, 끊긴 어슬렁거림 중에 주워 먹은, 수고에 별로 보답이 되지도 않을 그 무엇도 아닌 것을 되새김질하며 누워 있었다. 성관 사람들 몇과 역참 사람들 몇, 그리고 세금을 매기는 관리들 모두가 어지간히 무장한 채, 작은 길 건너편에 아무 목적도 없는 모양새로 몰려 있었는데, 그 모양새는 더없이 아무 의미도 없는 것으로 가득 차 있었다. 도로 보수공은 어느새 쉰 명쯤 되는 절친한 벗들 무리 한복판에 파고들어, 푸른 모자로 제 가슴을 치고 있었다. 이 모든 것은 무엇을 예고하는 것이며, 가벨 씨가 말에 탄 어느 하인 뒤로 잽싸게 끌어올려지는 것은, 또 (말이 두 사람을 짊어졌는데도) 그 가벨 씨가 마치 「레오노라」라는 독일 발라드의 새로운 판본처럼 질주하여 실려 가는 것은 무엇을 예고하는 것이었던가?\n\n그것은 저 위 성관에 돌얼굴이 하나 더 많아졌음을 예고하는 것이었다.\n\n고르곤이 밤사이 그 건물을 다시 한번 둘러보고는, 모자라던 그 돌얼굴 하나를 보태놓았던 것이다. 그것이 이백 년 가까이 기다려온 그 돌얼굴을.\n\n그것은 후작 나리의 베개를 베고 누워 있었다. 그것은 잘 빚은 가면 같았으니, 별안간 흠칫 놀라, 노여워하다가, 그대로 굳어버린 가면이었다. 거기에 딸린 돌 형상의 심장 깊숙이 박혀 있는 것은, 칼이었다. 그 칼자루 둘레에는 종잇조각이 주름져 감겨 있었고, 거기에는 이렇게 휘갈겨 쓰여 있었다.\n\n\"이자를 어서 무덤으로 몰아가라. 자끄가.\"\n\n# CHAPTER X. Two Promises\n\n더 많은 달들이, 그 수가 열두 달에 이르도록 오고 갔으며, 찰스 다네이 씨는 프랑스 문학에 정통한 프랑스어 고급 교사로서 영국에 자리를 잡았다. 이 시대였다면 그는 교수가 되었을 테고, 그 시대에 그는 가정교사였다. 그는 전세계에서 두루 쓰이는 살아 있는 언어를 공부할 만한 여가와 흥미를 지닌 젊은이들과 함께 책을 읽었고, 그 언어가 담은 지식과 상상의 보고를 음미하는 취향을 길렀다. 게다가 그는 그것들에 관해 훌륭한 영어로 글을 쓸 수 있었고, 그것들을 훌륭한 영어로 옮겨낼 수 있었다. 그런 스승은 그 시절 쉬이 찾을 수 있는 존재가 아니었다. 한때 왕자였던 자들도, 장차 왕이 될 자들도 아직 교사 계층은 아니었고, 텔슨 은행의 장부에서 떨어져나와 요리사나 목수로 전락한 몰락 귀족도 아직은 없었다. 그 학식이 학생의 길을 유달리 즐겁고 유익하게 만들어주는 가정교사로서, 또 단순한 사전적 지식을 넘어서는 무언가를 자기 일에 불어넣는 우아한 번역가로서, 젊은 다네이 씨는 곧 이름이 알려지고 격려를 받았다. 더욱이 그는 제 조국의 사정에 밝았는데, 그 사정은 갈수록 더 큰 관심을 끄는 것이었다. 그리하여 그는 대단한 끈기와 지칠 줄 모르는 근면으로 번창했다.\n\n런던에서 그는 황금의 포도 위를 걷거나 장미꽃 침대에 눕기를 기대하지 않았다. 만일 그가 그토록 드높은 기대를 품었더라면, 그는 번창하지 못했을 것이다. 그는 노고를 기대했고, 그것을 찾아냈으며, 그것을 해냈고, 그것을 최대한 활용했다. 바로 그 점에 그의 번영이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n그의 시간 가운데 일정 부분은 케임브리지에서 보내졌는데, 그곳에서 그는 그리스어와 라틴어를 세관으로 운반하는 대신 유럽 언어로 밀무역을 벌이는, 일종의 묵인받는 밀수꾼처럼 학부생들과 함께 책을 읽었다. 나머지 시간은 런던에서 보냈다.\n\n자, 에덴이 늘 여름이던 시절부터, 타락한 위도에서 대개 겨울인 이 시절에 이르기까지, 한 남자의 세계는 변함없이 한 방향으로 — 찰스 다네이의 방향으로, 한 여자의 사랑이라는 방향으로 — 흘러왔다.\n\n그는 위기에 처했던 순간부터 루시 마네뜨를 사랑했다. 그는 그녀의 연민에 찬 목소리만큼 달콤하고 정겨운 소리를 들어본 적이 없었다. 그는 그를 위해 파놓은 무덤의 가장자리에서 그의 얼굴과 마주했을 때의 그녀 얼굴만큼 부드럽게 아름다운 얼굴을 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 그는 아직 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 말하지 않았다. 일렁이는 바다와 길고도 긴 먼지투성이 길을 한참 건너간 저 멀리 버려진 성에서 벌어진 그 암살 사건은 — 그 자체가 한낱 꿈의 안개가 되어버린 그 견고한 석조 성에서 벌어진 일은 — 일 년 전 일이었건만, 그는 아직 단 한 마디 말로도 그녀에게 제 마음 상태를 털어놓은 적이 없었다.\n\n이렇게 하는 데에 자기 나름의 이유가 있음을 그는 잘 알았다. 다시 여름날이었는데, 대학 일을 마치고 막 런던에 돌아온 그가 마네뜨 박사에게 마음을 열 기회를 찾으려는 마음으로 소호의 고요한 모퉁이로 접어들었다. 여름날이 저물 무렵이었고, 그는 루시가 프로스 양과 함께 외출했음을 알고 있었다.\n\n그는 박사가 창가의 안락의자에 앉아 책을 읽고 있는 것을 보았다. 한때 그가 옛 고난을 견디게 해주는 동시에 그 고통의 날카로움을 더하기도 했던 그 활력이 차츰차츰 그에게 되돌아와 있었다. 이제 그는 참으로 정력적인 사람이었고, 목적의 굳건함과 결의의 힘과 행동의 기운이 넘쳤다. 되찾은 활력 속에서 그는 이따금 다소 변덕스럽고 갑작스러웠는데, 다른 능력들을 되찾아 발휘하기 시작했던 처음에 그랬던 것과 마찬가지였다. 그러나 이런 일은 결코 자주 눈에 띈 적이 없었고, 점점 더 드물어졌다.\n\n그는 많이 공부했고, 잠을 적게 잤으며, 상당한 피로를 거뜬히 견뎌냈고, 한결같이 명랑했다. 그런 그에게 이제 찰스 다네이가 들어섰고, 그를 보자 그는 책을 옆으로 치우고 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"찰스 다네이! 자네를 보니 기쁘구먼. 지난 사나흘 동안 우리는 자네가 돌아오기만 기다리고 있었다네. 스트라이버 씨와 시드니 카턴이 어제 둘 다 여기 왔었는데, 둘 다 자네가 진작 돌아왔어야 했다더군.\"\n\n\"그 일에 관심을 가져주셔서 그분들께 감사할 따름입니다.\" 그는 그들에 대해서는 다소 냉랭하게, 그러나 박사에 대해서는 매우 따뜻하게 대답했다. \"마네뜨 양은—\"\n\n\"잘 있다네.\" 그가 말을 끊자 박사가 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 돌아왔으니 우리 모두 기뻐할 걸세. 그애는 집안일 때문에 나갔지만, 곧 돌아올 거야.\"\n\n\"마네뜨 박사님, 저는 그녀가 집에 없는 걸 알고 있었습니다. 그녀가 집에 없는 틈을 타서, 박사님께 드릴 말씀이 있어 청을 드리려고 합니다.\"\n\n텅 빈 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"그래?\" 박사가 뚜렷한 거북함을 띠고 말했다. \"의자를 이리 가져와서 말해보게.\"\n\n그는 의자에 관해서는 순순히 따랐으나, 말을 이어나가는 일은 그만큼 쉽지 않은 듯했다.\n\n\"마네뜨 박사님, 저는 이곳에서 이토록 친밀하게 지내는 행복을 누려왔는데,\" 마침내 그가 입을 열었다. \"어느덧 일 년 반쯤 되었으니, 제가 이제 꺼내려는 화제가 부디—\"\n\n박사가 손을 내밀어 그를 멈추게 하는 바람에 그는 말을 멈추었다. 잠시 그렇게 손을 든 채로 있다가, 박사는 그 손을 거두며 말했다.\n\n\"루시가 그 화제인가?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"나로서는 언제든 그애에 관해 말하기가 힘들다네. 그애가 자네의 그런 어조로 거론되는 걸 듣는 건 더더욱 힘들고, 찰스 다네이.\"\n\n\"이건 열렬한 흠모와 진정한 경의, 그리고 깊은 사랑의 어조입니다, 마네뜨 박사님!\" 그가 공손하게 말했다.\n\n그녀의 아버지가 다시 입을 열기까지 또 한 번 텅 빈 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"믿네. 자네를 공정하게 보고 있어. 믿는다네.\"\n\n그의 거북함은 너무도 분명했고, 그것이 그 화제에 다가서기를 꺼리는 데서 비롯되었다는 것 또한 너무도 분명했기에, 찰스 다네이는 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"계속할까요, 선생님?\"\n\n또 한 번 침묵.\n\n\"그래, 계속하게.\"\n\n\"박사님께서는 제가 하려는 말을 미리 짐작하고 계십니다. 다만 제 은밀한 마음과, 그 마음에 오래도록 실려 있던 희망과 두려움과 불안을 모르신 채로는, 제가 얼마나 간절히 이 말을 하는지, 얼마나 간절히 이것을 느끼는지는 아실 수 없겠지요. 사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님, 저는 박사님의 따님을 다정하게, 애틋하게, 사심 없이, 헌신적으로 사랑합니다. 이 세상에 사랑이라는 것이 있었던 적이 있다면, 저는 그녀를 사랑합니다. 박사님께서도 사랑을 해보셨으니, 부디 박사님의 옛 사랑이 저를 대신해 말하게 해주십시오!\"\n\n박사는 얼굴을 돌리고 눈을 바닥에 떨군 채 앉아 있었다. 그 마지막 말에 그는 다시 황급히 손을 뻗으며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그것만은 안 되네, 여보게! 그건 내버려두게! 부탁하건대, 그것만은 떠올리게 하지 말아주게!\"\n\n그 외침은 실제 고통의 외침과 너무도 흡사해서, 그가 외침을 멈춘 뒤로도 오래도록 찰스 다네이의 귓전에 울렸다. 그는 뻗었던 손으로 무언가 몸짓을 했는데, 다네이에게 잠시 멈춰달라는 호소처럼 보였다. 다네이는 그렇게 받아들이고 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"용서하게.\" 잠시 후 박사가 가라앉은 어조로 말했다. \"자네가 루시를 사랑한다는 걸 의심하는 건 아니야. 그 점은 안심해도 좋네.\"\n\n그는 의자에 앉은 채 그를 향해 몸을 돌렸으나, 그를 쳐다보지도 눈을 들지도 않았다. 턱이 손 위로 떨어지고, 백발이 그의 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n\"루시에게 말한 적이 있나?\"\n\"아닙니다.\"\n\n\"편지를 쓴 적도?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"자네의 자제가 그애 아버지를 배려한 데서 비롯된 것임을 모르는 척하는 건 너그럽지 못한 일이겠지. 그애 아버지가 자네에게 고맙다네.\"\n\n그는 손을 내밀었으나, 그 눈길은 손과 함께 가지 않았다.\n\n\"저는 압니다.\" 다네이가 정중하게 말했다. \"어찌 모를 수 있겠습니까, 마네뜨 박사님. 날마다 두 분이 함께 계신 모습을 보아온 제가, 박사님과 마네뜨 양 사이에 너무도 보기 드물고, 너무도 가슴 뭉클하며, 그 애정이 자라난 환경과 너무도 깊이 맞닿아 있어서, 부녀간의 다정함 가운데서조차 견줄 만한 예가 거의 없는 그런 애정이 있다는 것을 말입니다. 저는 압니다, 마네뜨 박사님 — 어찌 모를 수 있겠습니까 — 여인이 된 딸의 애정과 의무에 더하여, 그녀의 마음속에는 박사님을 향한, 유아기 그 자체의 온갖 사랑과 의지가 깃들어 있음을 말입니다. 어린 시절 그녀에게 부모가 없었던 것처럼, 그녀가 이제 현재의 나이와 성품이 지닌 온갖 한결같음과 열정으로, 그리고 박사님을 잃었던 어린 날들의 신뢰와 애착이 어우러진 채 박사님께 헌신하고 있음을 저는 압니다. 만일 박사님이 이승 너머의 세계에서 그녀에게 돌아오셨다 한들, 그녀의 눈에 박사님이 지금 늘 그녀 곁에 계신 모습 이상으로 신성한 성격을 지닌 존재로 여겨질 수는 없으리라는 것을 저는 완벽하게 압니다. 그녀가 박사님께 매달릴 때면, 갓난아기와 소녀와 여인의 손이 하나가 되어 박사님의 목을 감싸고 있음을 저는 압니다. 박사님을 사랑하면서 그녀가 제 어머니를 자기 또래의 나이로 보고 사랑하며, 박사님을 제 나이의 모습으로 보고 사랑하고, 가슴이 찢어진 어머니를 사랑하며, 그 끔찍한 시련을 겪는 박사님과 축복받은 회복 속의 박사님을 사랑함을 저는 압니다. 박사님 댁에서 박사님을 알게 된 이래로, 저는 밤낮으로 이것을 알아왔습니다.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지는 얼굴을 떨군 채 잠자코 앉아 있었다. 그의 숨결이 조금 빨라졌으나, 그는 동요의 다른 모든 기색을 억눌렀다.\n\n\"사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님, 늘 이것을 알았기에, 늘 박사님과 그녀를 그 신성한 빛에 둘러싸인 모습으로 보아왔기에, 저는 사람의 본성으로 견딜 수 있는 한 참고 또 참았습니다. 저는 제 사랑을 — 그것이 제 사랑일지라도 — 두 분 사이에 들이미는 것은 박사님의 역사를 그 자체만큼 좋지는 못한 무언가로 건드리는 일이라고 느껴왔고, 지금도 그리 느낍니다. 그러나 저는 그녀를 사랑합니다. 제가 그녀를 사랑함을 하늘이 증언해줍니다!\"\n\n\"믿네.\" 그녀의 아버지가 구슬프게 대답했다. \"전부터 그리 생각해왔어. 믿는다네.\"\n\n\"그러나 믿지는 말아주십시오,\" 그 구슬픈 목소리가 책망하는 소리처럼 그의 귀를 친 다네이가 말했다. \"만일 제 운명이 그녀를 아내로 맞는 행복을 어느 날 누리게 되면서도 언제든 그녀와 박사님 사이에 어떤 이별을 들이밀어야 하는 식으로 정해진 것이라면, 제가 지금 드리는 이 말을 한 마디라도 입에 담을 수 있거나 입에 담으리라고는 믿지 말아주십시오. 그것이 가망 없는 일임을 알기에 앞서, 그것이 비열한 짓임을 저는 알 것입니다. 만일 제가 그런 가능성을, 설령 아주 먼 훗날의 일로라도, 제 생각 속에 품고 제 마음속에 숨겨두었더라면 — 그것이 한 번이라도 그 안에 있었더라면 — 그것이 한 번이라도 그 안에 있을 수 있었더라면 — 저는 지금 이 존경하는 손을 잡을 수 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n그는 말하면서 자기 손을 그 위에 얹었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님. 박사님처럼 프랑스에서 스스로 망명해온 자로서, 박사님처럼 그 나라의 혼란과 억압과 비참에 떠밀려 그곳을 떠나온 자로서, 박사님처럼 제 힘으로 그곳을 벗어나 살아가려 애쓰며 더 행복한 미래를 믿는 자로서, 저는 오직 박사님의 운명을 함께 나누고, 박사님의 삶과 가정을 함께 나누며, 죽을 때까지 박사님께 충실하기만을 바랍니다. 박사님의 자식이자 동반자이자 벗으로서 루시가 누리는 특권을 그녀와 나누어 갖자는 것이 아니라, 그 특권을 돕고, 그럴 수만 있다면 그녀를 박사님께 더욱 가까이 묶어두자는 것입니다.\"\n\n그의 손길은 여전히 그녀 아버지의 손 위에 머물러 있었다. 잠시 그 손길에 응답하면서도 냉랭하지는 않게, 그녀의 아버지는 두 손을 의자의 팔걸이에 얹고는, 이 대화가 시작된 이래 처음으로 고개를 들었다. 그의 얼굴에는 분명 어떤 싸움이 어려 있었다. 어두운 의심과 두려움으로 기우는 경향을 지닌 그 이따금의 표정과의 싸움이었다.\n\n\"자네는 너무도 진심 어리게, 또 너무도 사내답게 말하는군, 찰스 다네이. 그래서 진심으로 자네에게 고맙고, 내 마음을 죄다 — 아니, 거의 죄다 — 열어 보이겠네. 루시가 자네를 사랑한다고 믿을 만한 어떤 근거라도 있나?\"\n\n\"없습니다. 아직은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 알고 있다는 것을 등에 업고 당장 그것을 확인하려는 게 이 고백의 당면한 목적인가?\"\n\n\"그렇지도 않습니다. 저는 몇 주가 지나도 그럴 만한 희망을 못 가질 수도 있고, (잘못 생각한 것이든 아니든) 내일 당장 그런 희망을 가질 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"내게서 무슨 인도라도 구하는 건가?\"\n\n\"구하지 않습니다, 선생님. 다만 박사님께서 옳다고 여기신다면, 제게 어떤 인도를 베푸실 힘이 박사님께 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 해보았습니다.\"\n\n\"내게서 무슨 약속이라도 구하는 건가?\"\n\n\"그건 구합니다.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"박사님이 안 계시면 제게 아무런 희망도 없으리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다. 설령 이 순간 마네뜨 양이 자신의 순결한 마음속에 저를 품고 있다 한들 — 제가 감히 그만큼을 넘겨짚을 만큼 주제넘다고는 여기지 말아주십시오 — 그녀가 아버지를 향해 품은 사랑에 맞서서는 제가 그 마음속에 자리를 지킬 수 없으리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"그게 그렇다면, 한편으로 거기에 무엇이 얽혀 있는지 알겠나?\"\n\n\"마찬가지로 잘 압니다. 어떤 구혼자든 그를 두둔하는 아버지의 한 마디 말이면, 그녀 자신과 온 세상보다도 더 무게가 나가리라는 것을 말입니다. 그렇기 때문에, 마네뜨 박사님,\" 다네이가 겸손하지만 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 제 목숨을 구하기 위해서라도 그 한 마디 말을 청하지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 확신하네. 찰스 다네이, 비밀이란 넓은 단절에서뿐 아니라 가까운 사랑에서도 생겨나는 법이야. 후자의 경우, 그 비밀은 미묘하고 섬세하여 꿰뚫어보기가 어렵지. 내 딸 루시는 바로 이 한 가지 점에서 내게 그런 비밀이라네. 나는 그애 마음의 상태를 도무지 짐작할 수가 없어.\"\n\n\"여쭤도 될까요, 선생님, 그녀가 혹시—\" 그가 머뭇거리자, 그녀의 아버지가 나머지를 채워주었다.\n\n\"다른 구혼자가 구애하고 있느냐고?\"\n\n\"제가 하려던 말이 그것입니다.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지는 대답하기 전에 잠시 생각했다.\n\n\"자네도 카턴 씨가 여기 오는 걸 직접 보았지. 스트라이버 씨도 가끔 여기 온다네. 만일 그런 일이 있다면, 이 둘 중 하나일 수밖에 없겠지.\"\n\n\"아니면 둘 다이거나요.\" 다네이가 말했다.\n\n\"둘 다라고는 생각지 못했네. 어느 쪽도 그럴 법하다고는 여기지 않아. 자네는 내게서 약속을 원하는군. 그게 무엇인지 말해보게.\"\n\n\"이런 것입니다. 만일 마네뜨 양이 언제든 그녀 자신의 입으로, 제가 감히 박사님께 털어놓은 것과 같은 고백을 박사님께 가져온다면, 제가 한 말과, 그 말에 대한 박사님의 믿음을 증언해주십사 하는 것입니다. 부디 박사님께서 저를 좋게 보아주셔서, 제게 불리한 어떤 영향력도 행사하지 않으시기를 바랍니다. 이 일에 걸린 제 몫에 관해서는 더 말하지 않겠습니다. 이것이 제가 청하는 바입니다. 제가 그것을 청하는 조건, 그리고 박사님께서 요구하실 의심할 바 없는 권리를 지니신 그 조건은, 제가 곧바로 지키겠습니다.\"\n\n\"약속하겠네.\" 박사가 말했다. \"아무런 조건 없이 말일세. 자네의 목적이 자네가 말한 그대로, 순수하고 진실하다고 믿네. 자네의 의도가 나와, 또다른 나, 나보다 훨씬 더 소중한 나 사이의 끈을 약하게 하는 것이 아니라 영원토록 이어가려는 것이라 믿어. 만일 그애가 언제고 자네가 자기의 온전한 행복에 없어서는 안 될 사람이라고 내게 말한다면, 나는 그애를 자네에게 주겠네. 만일 — 찰스 다네이, 만일 —\"\n\n젊은이는 고맙게 그의 손을 잡았고, 박사가 말하는 동안 두 사람의 손은 맞잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"— 그애가 진정으로 사랑하는 사람에 맞서는 어떤 환상이든, 어떤 이유든, 어떤 불안이든, 새롭든 오래되었든 그 무엇이든 — 그 직접적인 책임이 그 사람의 머리 위에 있지 않은 한 — 그것은 모두 그애를 위해 지워져야 하네. 그애는 내게 전부일세. 내게는 고통보다도 더하고, 부당함보다도 더하며, 더 — 자, 됐네! 이건 부질없는 얘기야.\"\n\n그가 침묵 속으로 잦아드는 모양새가 어찌나 기이했던지, 또 말을 멈추었을 때 그 고정된 눈빛이 어찌나 기이했던지, 다네이는 천천히 자기 손을 놓아 떨어뜨리는 그 손 안에서 제 손이 차갑게 식는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"자네가 내게 무슨 말을 했지.\" 마네뜨 박사가 빙긋 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"내게 한 말이 무엇이었나?\"\n\n그는 어떻게 대답해야 할지 막막했으나, 어떤 조건에 관해 말했던 것을 떠올렸다. 그쪽으로 생각이 돌아가며 마음이 놓인 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"저에 대한 박사님의 신뢰에는 제 쪽에서도 온전한 신뢰로 보답해야 마땅합니다. 지금의 제 이름은, 비록 어머니의 이름에서 조금 바뀐 것에 지나지 않지만, 기억하시다시피 제 본래 이름이 아닙니다. 그것이 무엇인지, 그리고 제가 왜 영국에 와 있는지를 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"멈추게!\" 보베의 의사가 말했다. * 보베: 마네뜨 박사가 한때 의사로 일하던 프랑스 북부의 도시.\n\n\"그래야 박사님의 신뢰를 더 잘 받을 자격을 갖추고, 박사님께 아무런 비밀도 없게 될 것 같아 바라는 일입니다.\"\n\n\"멈추게!\"\n\n한순간, 박사는 두 손을 제 귀에 갖다 대기까지 했다. 또 한순간에는 두 손을 다네이의 입술에 얹기까지 했다.\n\n\"내가 묻거든 그때 말해주게, 지금이 아니라. 만일 자네의 구애가 잘 풀려서, 루시가 자네를 사랑하게 된다면, 자네 혼인날 아침에 내게 말해주게. 약속하겠나?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다.\"\n\n\"손을 주게. 그애가 곧 돌아올 텐데, 오늘밤은 우리 둘이 함께 있는 걸 보지 않는 편이 낫겠어. 가게! 신의 가호가 함께하길!\"\n\n찰스 다네이가 그를 떠날 때는 날이 어두웠고, 루시가 집에 돌아왔을 때는 그보다 한 시간 뒤라 더 어두웠다. 그녀는 혼자 방으로 급히 들어왔는데 — 프로스 양은 곧장 위층으로 올라갔기 때문이다 — 그의 독서용 의자가 비어 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다.\n\n\"아버지!\" 그녀가 그를 불렀다. \"아버지!\"\n\n대답으로 아무 말도 들리지 않았으나, 그녀는 그의 침실에서 낮게 망치 두드리는 소리를 들었다. 그 사이의 방을 가볍게 가로질러 그의 방문 안을 들여다본 그녀는 겁에 질려 달려 돌아왔고, 온몸의 피가 싸늘하게 식은 채 혼잣말로 외쳤다. \"어떡하지! 어떡하지!\"\n\n그녀의 망설임은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 서둘러 돌아가 그의 방문을 두드리고 부드럽게 그를 불렀다. 그녀의 목소리가 들리자 그 소리는 멎었고, 그가 이내 그녀에게로 나왔으며, 두 사람은 오래도록 함께 거닐었다.\n\n그날 밤 그녀는 잠든 그를 보려고 침대에서 내려왔다. 그는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 구두 만드는 연장들이 담긴 쟁반과, 미처 끝내지 못한 그의 옛 작업물은 모두 평소 그대로였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XI. A Companion Picture\n\n“시드니,” 바로 그날 밤, 아니 새벽에, 스트라이버 씨가 자기 하수인에게 말했다. “펀치를 한 사발 더 타게. 자네한테 할 말이 있어.”\n\n시드니는 그날 밤도, 그 전날 밤도, 또 그 전날 밤도, 그리고 연이은 여러 밤을 곱절로 일했으니, 긴 휴정기가 시작되기 전에 스트라이버 씨의 서류들을 대대적으로 정리해두기 위함이었다. 그 정리는 마침내 끝났다. 스트라이버의 밀린 일들은 말끔하게 처리되었고, 십일월이 대기의 안개와 법정의 안개를 몰고 와 방앗간에 다시 빻을 곡식을 가져올 때까지 모든 것이 처분되었다.\n\n시드니는 그토록 매달려 일하고도 더 활기차지도, 더 말짱하지도 않았다. 그 밤을 버텨내느라 적신 수건을 어지간히 많이 동원해야 했고, 그 수건질에 앞서 그에 걸맞게 술도 많이 들이켰던 터라, 그는 무척 망가진 상태였다. 그는 이제 머리에 두르고 있던 터번을 벗어, 지난 여섯 시간 동안 틈틈이 적셔두었던 대야 속에 던져 넣었다.\n\n“그 펀치 한 사발 더 타고 있는 건가?” 등을 대고 누워 있던 소파에서 고개를 돌리며, 허리춤에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 풍채 좋은 스트라이버가 말했다.\n“타고 있네.”\n\n“자, 이걸 보게! 자네를 좀 놀라게 할, 그리고 어쩌면 자네가 늘 여기던 것만큼 내가 빈틈없는 사람은 아니라고 생각하게 만들지도 모를 이야기를 하나 하려네. 나 결혼할 작정이야.”\n\n“정말인가?”\n\n“그래. 돈 때문도 아니지. 이제 뭐라고 하겠나?”\n\n“딱히 할 말이 떠오르지 않는군. 그 여자가 누군가?”\n\n“맞혀보게.”\n\n“내가 아는 사람인가?”\n\n“맞혀보게.”\n\n“새벽 다섯시에, 머릿속이 지글지글 끓고 튀고 있는데 나더러 맞히라고. 정 맞히기를 바란다면 저녁 식사에라도 초대해야지.”\n\n“그럼 좋아, 내가 말해주지.” 스트라이버가 천천히 일어나 앉으며 말했다. “시드니, 자네한테 내 뜻을 알아듣게 하기란 어지간히 가망 없는 일이야, 자네가 그렇게나 무딘 인간이니 말이지.”\n\n“그리고 자네는,” 펀치를 만드느라 분주한 시드니가 받아넘겼다, “그토록 섬세하고 시적인 영혼이고—”\n\n“이보게!” 스트라이버가 으스대며 웃으며 응수했다. “내가 낭만의 화신이라고 주장하는 건 아니지만(난 그 정도는 분별할 줄 안다고 믿으니까), 그래도 나는 자네보다는 다정한 부류의 사내거든.”\n\n“그게 운이 더 좋다는 뜻이라면 그렇겠지.”\n\n“그런 뜻이 아니야. 내가 더—더—그런 사내라는 거지.”\n\n“이왕 말이 나온 김에 풍류라고 해두게.” 카턴이 거들었다.\n\n“좋아! 풍류라고 하지. 내 말은,” 펀치를 만드는 친구를 향해 스스로를 부풀리며 스트라이버가 말했다, “나는 여자들과 어울릴 때 호감을 사는 데 더 마음을 쓰고, 호감을 사려고 더 공을 들이고, 호감을 살 줄 더 잘 아는 사내라는 거지, 자네보다 말이야.”\n\n“계속하게.” 시드니 카턴이 말했다.\n\n“아니지, 계속하기 전에,” 스트라이버가 윽박지르듯 고개를 가로저으며 말했다, “이건 자네하고 분명히 짚고 넘어가야겠어. 자네도 나만큼이나, 아니 나보다 더 자주 마네뜨 박사 댁에 드나들었지. 아니, 그 댁에서 보인 자네의 그 시무룩한 꼴이 부끄러울 지경이었어! 자네의 그 태도란 게 어찌나 말없고 뚱하고 풀죽은 꼴이던지, 정말이지 내 목숨과 영혼을 걸고 말하건대, 시드니, 난 자네가 부끄러웠다네!”\n\n“법정에서 일하는 사람이 무언가를 부끄러워한다는 건 자네에게 무척 이로운 일일 텐데.” 시드니가 받아넘겼다. “자네는 내게 크게 고마워해야 해.”\n\n“그런 식으로 빠져나가게 둘 순 없지.” 스트라이버가 어깨로 떠밀듯 응수했다. “안 돼, 시드니, 자네한테 이 말을 해주는 게 내 의무야—그리고 자네 잘되라고 면전에 대고 말하는 거야—자네는 그런 자리에서는 지독히도 심사 사나운 작자라는 걸세. 자네는 불쾌한 작자라고.”\n\n시드니는 자기가 만든 펀치를 한 잔 가득 들이켜고는 웃었다.\n\n“나를 보게!” 스트라이버가 어깨를 펴며 말했다. “나는 자네보다 호감을 살 필요가 덜한 사람이야, 형편이 더 넉넉하니 말이지. 그런데도 내가 왜 그러겠나?”\n\n“아직 한 번도 자네가 그러는 걸 본 적이 없네만.” 카턴이 중얼거렸다.\n\n“그게 처세에 이로우니까 하는 거지. 신조에 따라 하는 거야. 그리고 나를 보게! 나는 잘나가고 있잖아.”\n\n“자네 결혼 계획에 대한 이야기는 잘 나아가지 못하고 있네만.” 카턴이 무심한 태도로 대꾸했다. “그 얘기나 계속하면 좋겠군. 나로 말할 것 같으면—내가 구제 불능이라는 걸 자네는 영영 모르겠나?”\n\n그는 어딘지 경멸하는 기색으로 그 물음을 던졌다.\n\n“자네가 구제 불능일 까닭이 없지.” 친구의 대답은 그다지 다독이는 어조가 아니었다.\n\n“내가 알기로는 무엇이 될 까닭조차 통 없다네.” 시드니 카턴이 말했다. “그 숙녀가 누군가?”\n\n“자, 내가 그 이름을 밝힌다고 해서 거북해하지는 말게, 시드니.” 스트라이버 씨가 곧 털어놓을 사실을 앞두고 요란스레 다정한 태도로 그를 어르며 말했다. “자네가 하는 말의 절반도 진심이 아니란 걸 내가 아니까. 설령 전부 진심이라 해도 대수롭지 않을 테고. 이렇게 짤막한 서두를 다는 건, 언젠가 자네가 그 젊은 숙녀를 두고 깔보는 투로 말한 적이 있어서야.”\n\n“분명히 그랬지, 바로 이 방에서.”\n\n시드니 카턴은 자기 펀치를 바라보다가, 흡족해하는 친구를 바라보았다. 펀치를 들이켜고는 다시 흡족해하는 친구를 바라보았다.\n\n“자네는 그 젊은 숙녀를 금발 인형이라고 했지. 그 젊은 숙녀가 바로 마네뜨 양이라네. 만일 자네가 그런 방면으로 조금이라도 예민하고 섬세한 감정을 지닌 사내였다면, 시드니, 자네가 그런 호칭을 쓴 것에 내가 약간은 분개했을지도 모르지. 하지만 자네는 그렇지 않으니까. 자네는 그런 감각이 통째로 없으니까. 그러니 그 표현을 떠올린다 해도 나는 조금도 거슬리지 않아, 그림을 볼 줄 모르는 자가 내 그림에 대해 늘어놓는 평이나, 음악을 들을 귀가 없는 자가 내 음악에 대해 늘어놓는 평에 내가 거슬려할 까닭이 없는 것처럼 말이지.”\n\n시드니 카턴은 엄청난 속도로 펀치를 들이켰다. 친구를 바라보며 잔째로 들이켰다.\n\n“이제 자네도 전부 알았네, 시드.” 스트라이버 씨가 말했다. “나는 재산 따위는 개의치 않아. 그 여자는 매력적인 사람이고, 나는 내 마음에 흡족할 대로 하기로 작정했어. 대체로 보아 내 마음에 흡족하게 굴 만한 여유는 있다고 보네. 그 여자는 나라는, 이미 제법 형편이 좋고, 빠르게 출세하는 중이며, 어느 정도 명망도 있는 사내를 얻게 되는 거지. 그 여자에게는 더없는 행운이지만, 그 여자도 행운을 누릴 만한 사람이지. 놀랐나?”\n\n여전히 펀치를 들이켜며 카턴이 받았다. “내가 왜 놀라야 하나?”\n\n“찬성하나?”\n\n여전히 펀치를 들이켜며 카턴이 받았다. “내가 왜 찬성하지 않겠나?”\n\n“음!” 친구 스트라이버가 말했다. “자네는 내가 생각했던 것보다 한결 담담하게 받아들이고, 내 입장에서 보면 내가 생각했던 것보다 한결 욕심 없이 구는군. 하기야 이쯤 되면 자네도 자네의 그 오랜 벗이 제법 의지가 굳센 사내라는 걸 잘 알겠지. 그래, 시드니, 이런 식의 생활은 이제 지긋지긋해, 달리 기분 전환거리도 없이 말이야. 사내라면 마음 내킬 때 돌아갈 가정이 있다는 게 흐뭇한 일이라고 느끼게 됐어(내키지 않으면 안 가도 그만이지). 그리고 마네뜨 양은 어떤 처지에서든 잘 어울릴 것이며, 늘 내 체면을 세워주리라고 느끼네. 그래서 마음을 굳혔지. 자, 그리고 시드니, 이 친구야, 이제 자네의 앞날에 대해 한마디 하고 싶네. 자네는 지금 처지가 안 좋아, 알겠나. 정말이지 처지가 안 좋다고. 자네는 돈의 가치를 모르고, 험하게 살고, 머잖아 어느 날 쓰러져서 병들고 가난해질 걸세. 자네야말로 자네를 돌봐줄 사람을 생각해봐야 해.”\n\n번창하는 자의 생색을 내며 그렇게 말하니, 그는 실제보다 두 배는 커 보였고, 네 배는 거슬려 보였다.\n\n“자, 내가 자네한테 권하건대,” 스트라이버가 말을 이었다. “현실을 똑바로 마주하게. 나는 내 나름의 방식으로 현실을 똑바로 마주했어. 자네도 자네 나름의 방식으로 현실을 똑바로 마주하라고. 결혼하게. 자네를 보살펴줄 누군가를 마련하라고. 자네가 여자들과 어울리는 걸 즐기지도 못하고, 이해하지도 못하고, 그럴 요령도 없다는 건 괘념치 말게. 누군가를 찾아내. 약간의 재산이 있는, 점잖은 여자를 찾아내라고—집주인이라든가, 하숙을 치는 부류의 여자 말이지—그리고 그 여자랑 결혼해, 비 오는 날에 대비해서 말이야. 그게 자네에게 어울리는 거야. 자, 그걸 생각해보게, 시드니.”\n\n“생각해보지.” 시드니가 말했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. The Fellow of Delicacy\n\n스트라이버 씨는 박사의 딸에게 행운을 베푸는 그 관대한 처분을 내리기로 마음을 굳혔으니, 장기 휴정에 들어가 도시를 떠나기 전에 그녀에게 행복을 알려주리라 결심했다. 그 점을 두고 머릿속으로 얼마간 논의한 끝에, 그는 예비 절차를 모조리 끝내두는 편이 좋겠다는 결론에 이르렀고, 그러고 나면 미가엘 축일 학기 시작 한두 주 전에 그녀에게 손을 내밀 것인지, 아니면 그 학기와 힐러리 학기 사이의 짧은 성탄 휴가 동안 그리할 것인지를 두 사람이 한가로이 정할 수 있으리라 보았다.\n* 미가엘 축일 학기·힐러리 학기: 영국 법조계와 대학의 학기 구분으로, 미가엘 축일 학기는 가을, 힐러리 학기는 겨울에 시작된다.\n\n자기 쪽 논거의 견실함에 대해서는 그는 한 점 의심도 없었고, 평결로 가는 길이 또렷이 보였다. 오직 고려할 가치가 있는 유일한 근거인 실질적이고 세속적인 근거를 들어 배심원단을 상대로 변론한다면, 이는 명백한 사건이었고 약점이라곤 하나도 없었다. 그는 원고로서 자신을 불러 세웠고, 그 증언은 도무지 반박할 길이 없었으며, 피고측 변호인은 사건 적요서를 내던졌고, 배심원단은 심의하려고 돌아앉을 필요조차 없었다. 한번 따져본 끝에, 수석 재판관 스트라이버는 이보다 더 명백한 사건은 있을 수 없다고 흡족해했다.\n\n그리하여 스트라이버 씨는 마네뜨 양을 복스홀 가든으로 데려가겠노라는 격식 차린 제안으로 장기 휴정을 열기로 했다. 그것이 실패하면 래닐러로, 그것마저 영문 모르게 실패하면, 그가 직접 소호에 모습을 드러내어 거기서 자신의 고결한 뜻을 선언해야 마땅했다.\n\n그리하여 스트라이버 씨는 장기 휴정의 첫머리에 어린 꽃이 아직 피어 있을 무렵, 사람들을 헤치며 템플에서 소호 쪽으로 나아갔다. 그가 아직 템플 바의 세인트 던스턴 쪽에 있으면서 소호를 향해 자신을 내던지듯, 한껏 부푼 기세로 보도를 따라 돌진하며 더 약한 사람들을 모조리 떠밀어대는 모습을 본 사람이라면, 그가 얼마나 안전하고 강건한지 알아보았을 것이다.\n\n가는 길이 텔슨 은행을 지나게 되어 있었고, 그 자신이 텔슨 은행과 거래하는 데다 로리 씨를 마네뜨 가의 막역한 친구로 알고 있었으므로, 스트라이버 씨는 은행에 들러 로리 씨에게 소호 지평선의 환한 빛을 알려주어야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그래서 그는 목구멍에서 힘없이 덜그럭거리는 소리가 나는 문을 밀어젖히고, 두 계단을 비틀거리며 내려가, 늙은 출납계원 둘을 지나쳐, 곰팡내 나는 안쪽 골방으로 어깨를 들이밀고 들어갔다. 거기서는 로리 씨가 숫자를 기입하도록 줄이 그어진 커다란 장부들을 앞에 놓고 앉아 있었는데, 창문에는 마치 그것도 숫자를 기입하도록 줄이 그어진 양, 그리고 하늘 아래 모든 것이 하나의 계산인 양, 수직의 쇠창살이 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"여어!\" 스트라이버 씨가 말했다. \"안녕하시오? 무탈하시길 바라오!\"\n\n언제나 어떤 장소나 공간에도 너무 크게 보인다는 것이 스트라이버의 대단한 특징이었다. 그는 텔슨 은행에는 너무도 컸으므로, 먼 구석의 늙은 직원들이 마치 그가 자기들을 벽에 짓눌러대기라도 하는 듯 항의하는 눈빛으로 고개를 들었다. 멀찍한 원경에서 위풍당당하게 신문을 읽고 있던 은행 본체조차, 마치 스트라이버의 머리가 그 책임 막중한 조끼를 들이받기라도 한 듯 못마땅하게 낯을 찌푸렸다.\n\n신중한 로리 씨는 그런 상황에서라면 권하고 싶은 목소리의 본보기 같은 어조로, \"안녕하시오, 스트라이버 씨? 안녕하시오, 선생?\" 하고 말하며 악수를 했다. 그의 악수하는 방식에는 한 가지 특징이 있었으니, 은행 본체의 기운이 공기 중에 가득할 때 고객과 악수하는 텔슨 은행의 직원이라면 누구에게서나 늘 볼 수 있는 것이었다. 그는 자신을 지워버리는 듯한 태도로, 마치 텔슨 상회를 대신해 악수하는 사람처럼 손을 흔들었다.\n\n\"제가 무얼 도와드릴까요, 스트라이버 씨?\" 로리 씨가 업무용 인격으로 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 됐소, 고맙소. 이건 당신을 찾아온 사사로운 방문이오, 로리 씨. 사적인 이야기를 좀 하러 왔소.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군요!\" 로리 씨는 한쪽 귀를 기울이면서도 눈길은 저 멀리 은행 본체 쪽으로 흘려보내며 말했다.\n\n\"실은 말이오,\" 스트라이버 씨가 은밀하게 책상에 두 팔을 괴며 말했는데, 그러자 그 책상은 큼직한 이인용이었는데도 그에게는 반쪽도 모자라 보였다. \"당신의 그 상냥한 어린 친구 마네뜨 양에게, 나 자신을 결혼 상대로 청해볼 작정이오, 로리 씨.\"\n\n\"오, 저런!\" 로리 씨가 턱을 문지르며, 미심쩍은 눈으로 손님을 바라보며 외쳤다.\n\n\"오, 저런이라니, 선생?\" 스트라이버가 몸을 뒤로 빼며 되받았다. \"오, 당신께 저런이라니, 선생? 그게 무슨 뜻이오, 로리 씨?\"\n\n\"제 뜻은,\" 그 업무의 사나이가 대답했다, \"물론 우호적이고 높이 평가하는 것이며, 당신께 더없는 영예가 된다는 것이고, 그러니까—한마디로, 제 뜻은 당신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것입니다. 그렇지만—정말이지, 그게 말이오, 스트라이버 씨—\" 로리 씨는 말을 멈추고는 더없이 기묘한 태도로 그를 향해 고개를 저었으니, 마치 자기 의지와는 무관하게, 속으로 \"당신은 정말이지 너무 과하다는 걸 아셔야 한다오!\" 하고 덧붙이도록 떠밀리기라도 한 것 같았다.\n\n\"이거 참!\" 스트라이버가 시비조의 손으로 책상을 철썩 치고, 눈을 더 크게 뜨고, 긴 숨을 들이쉬며 말했다. \"내가 당신 말을 제대로 알아들었다면, 내 목을 매달아도 좋소!\"\n\n로리 씨는 그 목적을 위한 방편이라는 듯 작은 가발을 양쪽 귀에서 매만지고는, 펜의 깃을 깨물었다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을, 선생!\" 스트라이버가 그를 빤히 노려보며 말했다. \"내가 자격이 없단 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 천만에! 있고말고요. 오, 그럼요, 자격이 있으시지요!\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"자격이 있다고 하시면, 자격이 있는 거지요.\"\n\n\"내가 번창하지 않았소?\" 스트라이버가 물었다.\n\n\"오! 번창을 따지신다면, 번창하셨지요.\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 출세하고 있지 않소?\"\n\n\"출세를 따지신다면 말이지요,\" 로리 씨가 또 한 가지 인정할 거리가 생겨 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그건 아무도 의심할 수 없지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 도대체 무슨 뜻이오, 로리 씨?\" 스트라이버가 눈에 띄게 풀이 죽어 따져 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요! 저는—지금 그리로 가시려던 참이었습니까?\" 로리 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"곧장!\" 스트라이버가 주먹을 책상에 쿵 내리치며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 제가 당신이라면 안 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"자, 내가 당신을 궁지로 몰아넣겠소,\" 하고는 변론하듯 그를 향해 집게손가락을 흔들었다. \"당신은 업무의 사나이이니, 마땅히 이유가 있을 거요. 그 이유를 대시오. 왜 안 가겠다는 거요?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 로리 씨가 말했다, \"저라면 성공하리라고 믿을 만한 어떤 근거도 없이 그런 일을 하러 가지는 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 스트라이버가 외쳤다. \"이건 정말 모든 걸 능가하는군.\"\n\n로리 씨는 멀리 있는 은행 본체를 흘끗 보고, 화가 난 스트라이버를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"여기 한 업무의 사나이가 있소—연륜이 있는 사람이—경험이 있는 사람이—은행 안에 있단 말이오,\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"그런데 완전한 성공의 주된 이유를 셋이나 합산해놓고도, 이유가 도무지 없다고 하다니! 그것도 머리를 멀쩡히 달고 그런 말을 하다니!\" 스트라이버 씨는 그 기이함을 마치, 머리를 떼어놓고 그런 말을 했다면 한없이 덜 기이했으리라는 듯이 지적했다.\n\n\"제가 성공을 말할 때는, 그 아가씨와의 성공을 말하는 겁니다. 그리고 제가 성공을 가능케 할 원인과 이유를 말할 때는, 그 아가씨에게 그렇게 작용할 원인과 이유를 말하는 겁니다. 그 아가씨 말입니다, 선생,\" 로리 씨가 스트라이버의 팔을 부드럽게 두드리며 말했다. \"그 아가씨 말이오. 그 아가씨가 무엇보다 우선입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 내게 이렇게 말하려는 거요, 로리 씨,\" 스트라이버가 양 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리며 말했다. \"지금 문제가 되고 있는 그 아가씨가 새침을 떠는 바보라는 게 당신의 신중한 견해라고 말이오?\"\n\n\"꼭 그런 건 아닙니다. 제가 당신께 말하려는 것은, 스트라이버 씨,\" 로리 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그 어떤 입에서 나온 것이든 그 아가씨에 대한 불경한 말은 한마디도 듣지 않겠다는 것입니다. 그리고 만일 제가 어떤 사람을—그런 자를 안다고는 생각하고 싶지 않습니다만—취향이 그토록 천하고 성정이 그토록 오만하여, 이 책상 앞에서 그 아가씨를 두고 불경하게 말하는 것을 스스로 자제하지 못하는 자를 안다면, 텔슨 은행이라 해도 제가 그자에게 한 소리 해주는 것을 막지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n화를 내야 할 차례가 되었을 때 억눌린 어조로 화를 내야 한다는 필요는 스트라이버 씨의 혈관을 위태로운 지경에 몰아넣었었는데, 평소에는 더없이 규칙적으로 흐를 수 있는 로리 씨의 혈관도 이제 그의 차례가 되자 그보다 나을 게 없는 상태가 되었다.\n\n\"그것이 제가 당신께 말하려던 바입니다, 선생,\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"부디 이 점에 오해가 없기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n스트라이버 씨는 잠시 자 끝을 빨다가, 이번에는 그걸로 이를 두드려 곡조를 뽑아내며 서 있었는데, 아마 그 바람에 이가 시큰거렸을 것이다. 그가 어색한 침묵을 깨고 말했다.\n\n\"이건 내게 처음 듣는 소리요, 로리 씨. 당신은 신중히 생각한 끝에, 내게 소호로 올라가 나 자신을—이 나, 왕좌부 법정의 스트라이버를—내놓지 말라고 충고하는 거요?\"\n\n\"제 충고를 구하시는 겁니까, 스트라이버 씨?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 구하는 거요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다. 그러면 충고를 드리는 거고, 당신은 그걸 정확히 되풀이하신 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤,\" 스트라이버가 분한 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"이것이—하, 하!—과거와 현재와 미래의 모든 걸 능가한다는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"자, 제 말을 이해해주십시오,\" 로리 씨가 말을 이었다. \"업무의 사나이로서 저는 이 문제에 대해 무어라 말할 자격이 없습니다. 업무의 사나이로서 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르니까요. 그렇지만 마네뜨 양을 두 팔에 안아 키웠고, 마네뜨 양과 그 부친 양쪽 모두의 신임받는 친구이며, 두 사람을 깊이 아끼는 늙은이로서, 저는 이렇게 말한 겁니다. 이 비밀스러운 이야기는 제가 청한 것이 아님을 기억해주십시오. 자, 당신은 제 말이 옳지 않을지도 모른다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"천만에요!\" 스트라이버가 휘파람을 불며 말했다. \"나로서는 다른 사람들에게서 상식을 찾아낼 재간이 없소. 나 자신에게서나 찾을 수 있을 뿐이지. 나는 어떤 부류에게 분별이 있으리라 짐작하는데, 당신은 새침떠는 빵과 버터 같은 헛소리를 짐작하는군. 내게는 처음 듣는 얘기지만, 당신 말이 옳을 거요, 아마도.\"\n\n\"제가 무얼 짐작하는지는, 스트라이버 씨, 저 스스로 규정하겠습니다—그리고 제 말을 이해해주십시오, 선생,\" 로리 씨가 다시 얼른 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"저는 그것을—텔슨 은행에서라 해도—숨 쉬는 그 어떤 신사가 저 대신 규정하도록 두지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이런! 미안하오!\" 스트라이버가 말했다.\n\n\"받아들이겠습니다. 고맙습니다. 자, 스트라이버 씨, 제가 말하려던 것은 이겁니다—당신이 잘못 짚었음을 알게 되는 것은 당신께 고통스러울 수 있고, 마네뜨 박사가 당신께 분명히 밝혀야 하는 임무를 떠맡는 것은 박사께 고통스러울 수 있으며, 마네뜨 양이 당신께 분명히 밝혀야 하는 임무를 떠맡는 것은 마네뜨 양에게 매우 고통스러울 수 있습니다. 제가 그 가족과 어떤 사이로 영광스럽고 행복하게 지내는지 당신도 아실 겁니다. 괜찮으시다면, 당신을 어떤 식으로도 연루시키지 않고, 어떤 식으로도 대변하지 않으면서, 제가 이 문제에 일부러 적용한 약간의 새로운 관찰과 판단을 통해 제 충고를 바로잡는 일을 떠맡겠습니다. 만일 그러고도 당신이 그 충고에 불만이라면, 당신 스스로 그 타당성을 시험해보실 수밖에 없겠지요. 반대로 당신이 그 충고에 만족한다면, 그리고 그것이 지금과 같은 내용이라면, 모두에게 가장 아끼는 게 좋은 것을 아낄 수 있을 겁니다. 어떠십니까?\"\n\n\"나를 얼마나 오래 도시에 붙들어두려는 거요?\"\n\n\"오! 그저 몇 시간의 문제일 뿐입니다. 제가 저녁에 소호로 가서, 그 뒤에 당신 사무실로 들르면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋다고 하겠소,\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"지금 거기 올라가지 않겠소. 그렇게 안달이 난 것도 아니니까. 좋다고 하겠고, 당신이 오늘밤 들러주기를 기대하겠소. 안녕히 가시오.\"\n\n그러고 나서 스트라이버 씨는 돌아서서 은행을 박차고 나갔는데, 그가 지나가며 일으킨 공기의 충격이 어찌나 컸던지, 두 카운터 뒤에서 허리를 굽힌 채 그 바람에 맞서 버티려면 두 늙은 직원에게 남아 있는 온 힘이 다 들 정도였다. 그 점잖고 노쇠한 사람들은 손님들 눈에 늘 허리를 굽히는 모습으로만 보였고, 한 손님을 굽실거리며 배웅하고 나면 또 다른 손님을 굽실거리며 맞아들일 때까지 텅 빈 사무실에서도 줄곧 허리를 굽히고 있으리라고 사람들은 으레 믿었다.\n\n그 법정 변호사는, 은행가가 도덕적 확신만큼 견실한 근거가 아니고서야 그토록 멀리까지 자기 의견을 표명하지는 않았으리라는 것을 꿰뚫어볼 만큼 예리했다. 삼켜야 할 그 큰 알약에 미처 대비하지 못했으면서도, 그는 그것을 넘겨버렸다. \"자, 이제,\" 그것을 넘기고 나자 스트라이버 씨가 법정에서처럼 집게손가락을 템플 전체를 향해 흔들며 말했다. \"여기서 빠져나갈 내 방도는, 너희 모두를 잘못한 쪽으로 만드는 것이다.\"\n\n그것은 올드 베일리 전술가의 기술 한 토막이었으니, 그는 거기서 큰 위안을 얻었다. \"당신이 나를 잘못한 쪽으로 만들지는 못할 거요, 아가씨,\" 스트라이버 씨가 말했다. \"그건 당신을 위해 내가 해드리지.\"\n\n그리하여 그날 밤 로리 씨가 열 시나 되어 늦게 찾아왔을 때, 스트라이버 씨는 일부러 늘어놓은 잔뜩 쌓인 책과 서류에 둘러싸인 채, 그 아침의 화제 따위는 도무지 안중에도 없는 것처럼 보였다. 그는 로리 씨를 보고는 놀라는 기색마저 보였고, 전체적으로 멍하니 딴 데 정신이 팔린 상태였다.\n\n\"자!\" 그 마음씨 좋은 사절이, 그를 그 문제로 다시 끌어들이려는 부질없는 시도로 꼬박 반시간을 보낸 뒤에 말했다. \"제가 소호에 다녀왔습니다.\"\n\n\"소호에요?\" 스트라이버 씨가 차갑게 되뇌었다. \"오, 그렇지! 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거람!\"\n\n\"그리고 저는 추호도 의심하지 않습니다,\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"우리가 나눈 대화에서 제가 옳았다는 것을요. 제 의견은 확인되었고, 저는 제 충고를 거듭 말씀드립니다.\"\n\n\"분명히 말하건대,\" 스트라이버 씨가 더없이 다정한 투로 대꾸했다. \"당신을 봐서도 그 일이 안됐고, 그 가엾은 부친을 봐서도 안됐소. 나도 이게 그 가족에게는 늘 아픈 화제일 수밖에 없다는 걸 아오. 더는 그 얘기를 하지 맙시다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"모르실 테지요,\" 스트라이버가 무마하듯, 결론을 짓듯 고개를 끄덕이며 받았다. \"괜찮소, 괜찮아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 괜찮지 않습니다.\" 로리 씨가 다그쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 괜찮소. 분명히 말하건대 괜찮소. 분별이 없는 데 분별이 있으리라, 칭찬할 만한 야심이 없는 데 칭찬할 만한 야심이 있으리라 짐작했던 것인데, 이제 그 착각에서 깨끗이 벗어났으니, 아무 해도 없는 셈이오. 젊은 여자들은 전에도 흔히 그와 비슷한 어리석은 짓을 저질렀고, 전에도 흔히 가난과 무명 속에서 그것을 뉘우쳤소. 사심 없는 관점에서 보자면, 이 일이 그만둬진 것이 안타깝소. 세속적인 관점에서 내게 나쁜 일이었을 테니까. 사심 있는 관점에서 보자면, 이 일이 그만둬진 것이 기쁘오. 세속적인 관점에서 내게 나쁜 일이었을 테니까—내가 그걸로 아무것도 얻을 게 없었으리라는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지. 아무런 해도 없소. 나는 그 아가씨에게 청혼하지 않았고, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 곰곰이 따져보면, 내가 과연 그 지경까지 나 자신을 옭아맸을지 결코 확신할 수 없소. 로리 씨, 머리가 텅 빈 처녀들의 새침떠는 허영과 들뜸을 당신은 어쩌지 못하오. 그걸 어쩌리라 기대해서도 안 되오. 그러면 늘 실망만 할 테니까. 자, 부디 더는 그 얘기를 하지 맙시다. 다시 말하지만, 나는 남들을 봐서는 그게 안타깝지만, 나 자신을 봐서는 만족스럽소. 그리고 내가 당신을 떠볼 수 있게 해주고, 충고를 해준 것에 대해 정말 대단히 고맙게 생각하오. 당신은 나보다 그 아가씨를 더 잘 아니, 당신 말이 옳았소. 절대 될 일이 아니었던 거요.\"\n\n로리 씨는 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 스트라이버가 자신의 그릇된 머리 위로 관대함과 아량과 호의를 마구 뿌려대는 시늉을 하며 그를 문 쪽으로 떠미는 것을 그저 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었다. \"마음 편히 받아들이시오, 친애하는 선생,\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"더는 그 얘기를 마시오. 내가 당신을 떠볼 수 있게 해준 것에 대해 다시 한번 고맙소. 안녕히 가시오!\"\n\n로리 씨는 자기가 어디 있는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 밤거리로 나와 있었다. 스트라이버 씨는 소파에 등을 기댄 채, 천장을 향해 눈을 끔벅이고 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. The Fellow of No Delicacy\n\n시드니 카턴이 어디선가 빛난 적이 있었다 해도, 마네뜨 박사의 집에서만큼은 결코 빛난 적이 없었다. 그는 꼬박 한 해 동안 그곳을 자주 드나들었지만, 언제나 변함없이 침울하고 무뚝뚝한 빈둥거리는 자였다. 마음 내켜 말할 때면 말솜씨가 좋았으나, 무엇에도 마음 쓰지 않는 그 구름은 치명적인 어둠으로 그를 뒤덮어, 그의 내면의 빛이 그것을 뚫고 나오는 일은 좀처럼 없었다.\n\n그래도 그는 그 집을 둘러싼 거리들에, 그리고 그 포장도로를 이룬 무심한 돌들에 얼마간 마음을 두었다. 술이 그에게 덧없는 즐거움조차 가져다주지 못한 숱한 밤, 그는 막연하고 불행한 심정으로 그곳을 떠돌았다. 음울한 여명이 그 홀로 선 모습을 그곳에서 서성이며 드러낸 날도 여러 날이었으니, 첫 햇살이 교회 첨탑과 우뚝 솟은 건물들의 사라졌던 건축미를 또렷이 돋보이게 할 때에도 그는 여전히 그곳에 머물러 있었고, 어쩌면 그 고요한 시각이 다른 때라면 잊히고 도달할 수 없는 더 나은 것들에 대한 어떤 감각을 그의 마음속으로 불러들였는지도 모른다. 근래 들어 템플 코트의 방치된 침대는 그 어느 때보다 그를 드물게 맞았으니, 그가 거기에 몸을 던지고 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 다시 일어나 그 동네를 배회하는 일이 잦았다.\n\n8월의 어느 날, 스트라이버 씨가 (자기 자칼에게 \"그 결혼 문제는 다시 생각해보니 그만두기로 했네\"라고 통보한 뒤) 자신의 섬세함을 데번셔로 실어 가버렸을 때, 그리고 도시의 거리에 핀 꽃들의 모습과 향기가 가장 못된 자에게는 한 가닥 선함을, 가장 병약한 자에게는 한 가닥 건강함을, 가장 늙은 자에게는 한 가닥 젊음을 떠도는 부스러기로나마 품고 있을 무렵, 시드니의 발은 여전히 그 돌들을 밟고 있었다. 우유부단하고 목적 없던 그의 발은 어떤 의도로 활기를 띠게 되었고, 그 의도를 이행하는 과정에서 그를 박사의 문 앞으로 데려갔다.\n\n그는 위층으로 안내되었고, 루시가 혼자 일감을 붙들고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 그와 함께 있을 때 한 번도 완전히 편안한 적이 없었기에, 그가 그녀의 탁자 가까이에 자리를 잡고 앉자 약간의 어색함을 띠며 그를 맞이했다. 그러나 처음 몇 마디 의례적인 인사를 주고받으며 그의 얼굴을 올려다본 그녀는 거기에 변화가 일어난 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"몸이 편찮으신 게 아닌가 걱정스럽네요, 카턴 씨!\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 하지만 제가 사는 삶이란 게, 마네뜨 양, 건강에 이로울 게 없지요. 저 같은 방탕한 자에게서, 혹은 그런 자에게 무엇을 기대할 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"더 나은 삶을 살지 못하는 것이―용서하세요, 입에 담은 김에 끝까지 묻겠어요―안타까운 일이 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"하느님은 그것이 부끄러운 일임을 아십니다!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 어째서 바꾸지 않으세요?\"\n\n다시 그를 부드럽게 바라보던 그녀는 그의 눈에 눈물이 고여 있는 것을 보고 놀라며 마음 아파했다. 그가 대답할 때 그의 목소리에도 눈물이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"그러기엔 너무 늦었습니다. 저는 지금의 저보다 결코 나아지지 못할 겁니다. 저는 더 낮은 데로 가라앉고, 더 나빠질 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 탁자에 한쪽 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다. 뒤이은 침묵 속에서 탁자가 떨렸다.\n\n그녀는 그가 누그러진 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없었기에 몹시 괴로웠다. 그는 그녀를 바라보지 않고도 그녀가 그러함을 알고서 말했다.\n\n\"부디 용서해주십시오, 마네뜨 양. 당신에게 하고 싶은 말을 떠올리니 무너지고 맙니다. 제 말을 들어주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그것이 당신에게 조금이라도 도움이 된다면, 카턴 씨, 그것이 당신을 더 행복하게 한다면, 저는 무척 기쁠 거예요!\"\n\n\"당신의 그 다정한 연민에 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n그는 잠시 후 얼굴을 가린 손을 거두고 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"제 말을 듣기를 두려워하지 마십시오. 제가 하는 어떤 말에도 움츠러들지 마십시오. 저는 젊어서 죽은 사람과 같습니다. 제 삶 전부가 그러했을 수도 있었지요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 카턴 씨. 저는 그중 가장 좋은 부분이 아직 가능하다고 확신해요. 당신이 스스로에게 훨씬, 훨씬 더 떳떳한 사람이 될 수 있다고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"당신에게 떳떳한 사람이라고 말씀하시지요, 마네뜨 양, 그리고 저는 그렇지 않음을 더 잘 알지만―제 비참한 가슴속 깊은 곳에서 더 잘 알지만―저는 결코 그 말을 잊지 못할 겁니다!\"\n\n그녀는 창백해진 채 떨고 있었다. 그는 스스로에 대한 굳어진 절망을 품고 그녀를 구하러 나섰으니, 그것이 이 만남을 다른 어떤 만남과도 다른 것으로 만들었다.\n\n\"만약, 마네뜨 양, 당신이 지금 당신 앞에 있는 이 사내의 사랑에―당신이 알다시피 내던져지고, 허비되고, 술에 절고, 잘못 쓰인 가련한 존재인 이 사내의 사랑에―보답할 수 있는 일이 가능했다 하더라도, 그는 자신의 행복에도 불구하고 바로 오늘 이 순간에 알고 있었을 겁니다. 자신이 당신을 불행으로, 슬픔과 후회로 이끌고, 당신을 시들게 하고, 당신을 욕되게 하고, 당신을 자신과 함께 끌어내리리라는 것을 말입니다. 당신이 제게 어떤 애틋함도 품을 수 없음을 저는 아주 잘 압니다. 저는 그것을 청하지도 않습니다. 그것이 가능하지 않다는 사실에 저는 오히려 감사할 따름입니다.\"\n\n\"그것 없이는, 제가 당신을 구할 수 없을까요, 카턴 씨? 제가 당신을―다시 한번 용서하세요!―더 나은 길로 불러낼 수 없을까요? 제가 당신의 신뢰에 어떻게도 보답할 수 없을까요? 저는 이것이 신뢰임을 알아요.\" 그녀는 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 진심에서 우러난 눈물을 흘리며 겸손하게 말했다. \"당신이 이런 말을 다른 누구에게도 하지 않으리라는 것을 알아요. 제가 이것을 당신을 위한 어떤 좋은 결실로 바꿀 수 없을까요, 카턴 씨?\"\n\n그는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"아무것으로도 바꿀 수 없습니다. 아니요, 마네뜨 양, 아무것으로도. 조금만 더 제 말을 끝까지 들어주신다면, 당신이 저를 위해 해줄 수 있는 모든 일은 이미 다 한 셈이 됩니다. 저는 당신이 제 영혼의 마지막 꿈이었음을 알아주시기를 바랍니다. 제 타락 속에서도 저는, 당신의 아버지와 함께 있는 당신의 모습과 당신이 그토록 가정답게 만든 이 가정을 보고서, 제게서 죽어 사라졌다고 여겼던 옛 그림자들이 일렁였을 만큼은 완전히 타락하지는 않았습니다. 당신을 안 뒤로 저는, 다시는 저를 책망하지 않으리라 여겼던 회한에 시달렸고, 영원히 잠잠해졌다고 여겼던 옛 목소리들이 저를 위로 떠밀며 속삭이는 것을 들었습니다. 저는 새로이 분발하고, 다시 시작하고, 나태와 향락을 떨쳐버리고, 내던졌던 싸움을 끝까지 싸워보겠다는 어렴풋한 생각들을 품었습니다. 한낱 꿈, 모든 것이 꿈이었으니, 아무것에도 이르지 못한 채 끝나고 잠든 자를 누웠던 그 자리에 그대로 남겨둘 따름이지만, 저는 당신이 그 꿈을 불러일으켰음을 알아주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그중 아무것도 남지 않을까요? 오, 카턴 씨, 다시 생각해보세요! 다시 한번 해보세요!\"\n\n\"아니요, 마네뜨 양. 그 모든 과정에서 저는 제 자신이 전혀 자격이 없음을 알았습니다. 그런데도 저는, 잿더미에 불과한 제가 당신으로 인해 얼마나 갑작스러운 위세로 불붙었는지를 당신에게 알리고 싶은 약함을 품었고, 지금도 그 약함을 품고 있습니다. 비록 그 불은 그 본성상 저 자신과 떼어놓을 수 없어, 아무것도 살리지 못하고, 아무것도 밝히지 못하며, 아무 쓸모도 없이 헛되이 타들어갈 뿐이지만 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 알기 전보다 당신을 더 불행하게 만든 것이 제 불운이라면, 카턴 씨―\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 마네뜨 양. 무언가가 저를 되살릴 수 있었다면 그것은 바로 당신이었을 테니까요. 당신은 제가 더 나빠지는 원인이 되지 않을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 말씀하시는 그 마음의 상태가, 어쨌든, 저의 어떤 영향에서 비롯된 것이라면―제 말을 분명히 할 수 있다면, 제 뜻은 이런 거예요―제가 당신을 위해 그 영향을 쓸 수는 없을까요? 제게는 당신에게 좋은 일을 할 힘이 조금도 없는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"제가 지금 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일을, 마네뜨 양, 저는 이루기 위해 이곳에 왔습니다. 잘못 이끌어온 제 남은 삶 동안, 제가 온 세상에서 가장 마지막으로 당신에게 마음을 열었다는 것, 그리고 이때의 제 안에 당신이 애석해하고 가엾이 여길 만한 무언가가 남아 있었다는 기억을 간직하게 해주십시오.\"\n\n\"제가 거듭거듭, 더없이 간절하게, 온 마음을 다해 더 나은 것을 이룰 수 있다고 믿어달라 애원했던 그 무언가 말이에요, 카턴 씨!\"\n\n\"더는 그것을 믿어달라 애원하지 마십시오, 마네뜨 양. 저는 제 자신을 시험해보았고, 더 잘 압니다. 제가 당신을 괴롭히고 있군요. 저는 빠르게 끝을 향해 가고 있습니다. 제가 이날을 떠올릴 때, 제 삶의 마지막 신뢰가 당신의 순수하고 결백한 가슴에 맡겨졌다는 것, 그리고 그것이 오직 그곳에만 깃들어 누구와도 나뉘지 않으리라는 것을 믿게 해주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그것이 당신에게 위안이 된다면, 그러겠어요.\"\n\n\"앞으로 당신에게 가장 소중해질 사람과도 나누지 않으시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"카턴 씨.\" 그녀는 동요하며 잠시 멈춘 뒤 대답했다. \"그 비밀은 당신의 것이지 저의 것이 아니에요. 저는 그것을 지키겠다고 약속하겠어요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 그리고 다시 한번, 하느님의 축복이 당신과 함께하기를.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 손을 자기 입술에 가져다 댄 뒤 문 쪽으로 발을 옮겼다.\n\n\"걱정하지 마십시오, 마네뜨 양. 제가 이 대화를 지나가는 말 한마디로라도 다시 꺼내는 일은 결코 없을 겁니다. 저는 두 번 다시 그것을 입에 담지 않겠습니다. 제가 죽는다 해도 이보다 더 확실할 수는 없을 만큼, 이제부터 그렇습니다. 제가 죽는 순간에, 저는 단 하나의 좋은 기억을 신성하게 간직할 것이며―그것으로 당신에게 감사하고 당신을 축복할 것입니다―제 자신에 대한 마지막 고백이 당신에게 바쳐졌다는 것, 그리고 제 이름과 허물과 비참함이 당신의 가슴속에 다정히 품어졌다는 그 기억을 말입니다. 그 밖의 모든 것은 가볍고 행복하기를!\"\n\n그는 여태껏 자신이 보여온 모습과 너무도 달랐고, 그가 얼마나 많은 것을 내던져버렸는지, 그리고 날마다 얼마나 많은 것을 짓누르고 비뚤어지게 만드는지를 생각하니 너무도 서글퍼, 그가 멈춰 서서 그녀를 돌아보는 동안 루시 마네뜨는 그를 위해 애처롭게 울었다.\n\n\"위로받으십시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그런 마음을 받을 가치가 없는 자입니다, 마네뜨 양. 한두 시간 뒤면, 제가 경멸하면서도 굴복하고 마는 저급한 동료들과 저급한 습관들이 저를, 거리를 기어다니는 그 어떤 가련한 자보다도 그런 눈물을 받을 자격이 없는 자로 만들어버릴 겁니다. 위로받으십시오! 하지만 제 안에서는, 당신을 향한 저는, 겉으로 당신이 지금껏 보아온 모습 그대로일지언정, 언제나 지금의 저로 남아 있을 겁니다. 제가 당신에게 청하는 마지막에서 두 번째 간청은, 이것을 저에 대한 진실로 믿어달라는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그러겠어요, 카턴 씨.\"\n\n\"제 마지막 간청은 이것입니다. 그리고 이것으로, 당신과 조금도 통하는 바가 없고 당신과의 사이에 건널 수 없는 거리가 놓인 한 방문객을 당신에게서 물러나게 하겠습니다. 부질없는 말인 줄 알지만, 이것은 제 영혼에서 우러난 것입니다. 당신을 위해서라면, 그리고 당신에게 소중한 누구를 위해서라면, 저는 무엇이든 하겠습니다. 제 삶이, 희생할 기회나 역량이 조금이라도 있는 그런 더 나은 부류였다면, 저는 당신과 당신에게 소중한 이들을 위해 어떤 희생이든 기꺼이 받아들였을 겁니다. 어느 고요한 때에, 이 한 가지 일에서만큼은 제가 뜨겁고 진실했다고 마음에 새겨주려 애써주십시오. 때가 올 겁니다. 그 때가 오는 데 오래 걸리지 않을 겁니다. 당신 곁에 새로운 인연들이 맺어질 때가―당신이 그토록 빛내는 그 가정에 당신을 한층 더 다정하고 굳게 묶어줄 인연들이, 당신을 영원히 빛내고 기쁘게 할 가장 소중한 인연들이 맺어질 때가 말입니다. 오, 마네뜨 양, 행복한 아버지의 얼굴을 한 작은 그림 같은 것이 당신을 올려다볼 때, 당신 자신의 환한 아름다움이 발치에서 새로이 피어나는 것을 볼 때, 때때로 떠올려주십시오. 당신이 사랑하는 한 생명을 당신 곁에 지키기 위해서라면 제 목숨을 바칠 사내가 있었다는 것을!\"\n\n그는 \"안녕히 계십시오!\"라고 말하고, 마지막으로 \"하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\"이라고 말한 뒤, 그녀를 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. The Honest Tradesman\n\n제리마이어 크런처 씨가 플리트가에서 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 곁에 그 험상궂은 사내아이를 데리고 있는 그의 눈앞에는, 움직이는 온갖 사물들이 날마다 헤아릴 수 없이 많고도 다양하게 펼쳐졌다. 한낮의 분주한 시간에 플리트가에서 무엇인가 위에 앉아 있으면서, 두 개의 거대한 행렬에 어찔하고 귀먹먹해지지 않을 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠는가. 하나는 해를 따라 끊임없이 서쪽으로 향하고, 다른 하나는 해로부터 끊임없이 동쪽으로 향하니, 둘 다 해가 지는 붉고 자줏빛인 저 산마루 너머의 평원을 향해 끝없이 흘러가는 것이었다!\n\n크런처 씨는 입에 지푸라기를 문 채, 마치 여러 세기 동안 하나의 물줄기를 지켜보는 임무를 맡아온 이교도 시골뜨기처럼 두 흐름을 바라보며 앉아 있었다 — 다만 제리는 그 흐름이 언젠가 말라붙으리라고는 결코 기대하지 않았다는 점이 달랐다. 그것은 희망적인 종류의 기대도 못 되었을 것이니, 그의 수입 가운데 작은 몫이 겁 많은 여인들(대개 살집이 풍만하고 중년을 넘긴 이들)을 텔슨 은행 쪽 강기슭에서 맞은편 기슭으로 안내해주는 데서 나왔기 때문이다. 그런 동행은 매번 짧기 그지없었으나, 크런처 씨는 한 번도 빠짐없이 그 부인에게 깊은 관심을 보이며 그녀의 건강을 위해 한잔할 영광을 누리고 싶다는 강한 소망을 표명하곤 했다. 그리고 바로 그 자비로운 목적을 이루기 위해 그에게 내려진 선물들로부터, 방금 말한 대로 그는 재정을 보충했던 것이다.\n\n한때는 어느 시인이 공공장소의 의자에 앉아 사람들의 눈앞에서 사색에 잠기던 시절도 있었다. 크런처 씨는 공공장소의 의자에 앉아 있었으나 시인이 아니었기에 되도록 사색을 적게 하고 주위를 두리번거렸다.\n\n그가 이렇게 시간을 보내던 무렵은 마침 군중도 드물고 때늦은 여인들도 드물었으며, 그의 사업 형편이 전반적으로 너무도 불황이라 그의 가슴속에 크런처 부인이 또 어떤 노골적인 방식으로 \"엎드려 빌고\" 있었음이 틀림없다는 강한 의심을 불러일으킬 정도였는데, 바로 그때 플리트가를 따라 서쪽으로 쏟아져 내려오는 예사롭지 않은 무리가 그의 주의를 끌었다. 그쪽을 바라보던 크런처 씨는 어떤 종류의 장례 행렬이 다가오고 있으며, 그 장례에 대한 민중의 반감이 소동을 일으키고 있음을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"어린 제리야,\" 크런처 씨가 자기 자식을 돌아보며 말했다, \"저건 장사 행렬이다.\"\n\n\"만세, 아버지!\" 어린 제리가 외쳤다.\n\n이 어린 신사는 알 수 없는 의미를 담아 환호성을 질렀다. 나이 든 신사는 그 외침을 몹시 못마땅하게 여긴 나머지, 기회를 엿보다가 그 어린 신사의 귀싸대기를 후려쳤다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이냐? 뭣 땜에 만세를 부르는 거야? 친아비한테 대체 뭘 전하려는 게냐, 이 망나니 녀석아? 이놈이 나한테 너무 까불기 시작하는구나!\" 크런처 씨가 아이를 노려보며 말했다. \"이놈하고 이놈의 만세 타령이라니! 다시는 그런 소리 듣게 하지 마라, 안 그러면 내 맛을 더 보게 될 테니. 알아들었어?\"\n\n\"난 아무 해코지도 안 했단 말예요,\" 어린 제리가 뺨을 문지르며 항변했다.\n\n\"그럼 집어치워,\" 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"난 네 해코지인지 아닌지는 알 바 아니다. 저기 저 자리 위에 올라가서 군중이나 봐라.\"\n\n그의 아들은 시키는 대로 했고, 군중이 다가왔다. 그들은 칙칙한 영구차와 칙칙한 상주 마차를 둘러싸고 야유하고 쉿쉿거리고 있었는데, 그 상주 마차 안에는 상주가 단 한 사람뿐이었고, 그는 그 자리의 품위에 필수적이라 여겨지는 칙칙한 상복 차림이었다. 그러나 그 자리가 그에게는 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 듯했으니, 점점 불어나는 천한 무리가 마차를 둘러싼 채 그를 조롱하고 얼굴을 찡그려 보이며 끊임없이 신음하듯 소리쳐댔기 때문이다. \"야! 첩자! 쳇! 야하! 첩자!\" 게다가 다시 옮기기엔 너무 많고도 격한 온갖 찬사까지 곁들였다.\n\n장례 행렬은 언제나 크런처 씨에게 각별한 매력을 지녔다. 텔슨 은행 앞으로 장례가 지나갈 때면 그는 늘 감각을 곤두세우고 흥분하곤 했다. 그러니 당연하게도, 이처럼 보기 드문 수행을 거느린 장례는 그를 크게 흥분시켰고, 그는 자기와 부딪힌 첫번째 사내에게 물었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오, 형씨? 뭐 때문에 이러는 거요?\"\n\n\"나야 모르지,\" 그 사내가 말했다. \"첩자! 야하! 쳇! 첩자!\"\n\n그는 또 다른 사내에게 물었다. \"누구 장례요?\"\n\n\"나야 모르지,\" 그 사내가 대답하면서도 두 손을 입에 모으고 놀라운 열기와 더할 나위 없는 열성으로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"첩자! 야하! 쳇, 쳇! 처어업자!\"\n\n마침내 이 사건의 내막을 좀 더 잘 아는 사람이 그에게 부딪쳐왔고, 이 사람으로부터 그는 그 장례가 로저 클라이라는 자의 장례임을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"그자가 첩자였소?\" 크런처 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"올드 베일리 첩자였지,\" 그 정보 제공자가 대답했다. \"야하! 쳇! 야! 올드 베일리 처업자!\"\n\n\"아니, 그러고 보니!\" 제리는 자기가 입회했던 그 재판을 떠올리며 소리쳤다. \"그자를 본 적이 있지. 죽었단 말이오?\"\n\n\"양고기처럼 죽었지,\" 상대가 대답했다. \"아무리 죽어도 모자랄 놈이야. 끌어내라, 거기! 첩자다! 끌어내라, 거기! 첩자야!\"\n\n이렇다 할 어떤 생각도 통 없던 판국이라 그 발상은 어찌나 그럴듯했던지, 군중은 그것을 열렬히 받아들여 끌어내라, 끄집어내라는 제안을 큰 소리로 되풀이하면서 두 대의 마차를 어찌나 바짝 에워쌌던지 마차가 멈춰서고 말았다. 군중이 마차 문을 열어젖히자 단 한 사람인 상주가 혼자서 허둥지둥 기어 나왔고 잠시 그들 손아귀에 잡혔다. 하지만 그는 어찌나 날쌔고 시간을 잘 활용했던지, 다음 순간 망토와 모자와 긴 모자 띠와 흰 손수건과 그 밖의 상징적인 눈물들을 벗어 던지고 어느 옆골목으로 쏜살같이 내빼고 있었다.\n\n이것들을 사람들은 갈가리 찢어 신나게 사방으로 흩뿌렸고, 그 사이 상인들은 부랴부랴 가게 문을 닫았다. 그 시절의 군중은 못 할 짓이 없었고, 몹시 두려운 괴물이었기 때문이다. 그들은 이미 관을 꺼내려고 영구차를 열어젖히던 참이었는데, 그때 좀 더 명민한 천재 하나가 그 대신 관을 목적지까지 모두의 환호 속에 호위해 가자고 제안했다. 실용적인 제안이 몹시 아쉬운 터라 이 제안 역시 갈채를 받았고, 마차에는 즉시 안에 여덟 명, 밖에 열두 명이 가득 들어찼으며, 영구차 지붕 위에도 갖은 재간을 부려 매달릴 수 있는 만큼 많은 사람이 올라탔다. 이 지원자들 가운데 가장 먼저 나선 이가 바로 제리 크런처 본인이었으니, 그는 상주 마차의 가장 안쪽 구석에 자기 가시 같은 머리를 텔슨 은행의 눈에 띄지 않도록 얌전히 숨겼다.\n\n장례를 집전하던 장의사들이 이렇게 의식이 바뀌는 것에 다소 항의했으나, 강이 위태로울 만큼 가까이 있었고, 또 여럿이 그 업종에서 말 안 듣는 자들을 정신 차리게 하는 데는 찬물에 담가버리는 것이 효험 있다고 떠들어대자, 그 항의는 미약하고 짧았다. 개편된 행렬이 출발했으니, 영구차는 굴뚝 청소부가 몰았는데 — 바로 그 목적으로 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 감시하는 본래 마부의 조언을 받으며 몰았다 — 상주 마차는 파이 장수가, 역시 자기 측근 대신을 거느리고 몰았다. 그 시절 인기 있던 길거리 인물인 곰 부리는 사내도 추가 장식으로 징발되었으니, 행렬이 스트랜드가를 멀리 내려가기도 전의 일이었다. 그리고 그가 끌던 곰은 검고 옴이 잔뜩 올라 있었는데, 그가 걷는 행렬의 그 부분에 자못 장의사다운 분위기를 더해주었다.\n\n이렇게, 맥주를 마시고 담배를 피우고 노래를 고래고래 부르며 슬픔을 한없이 희화화하면서, 그 무질서한 행렬은 발걸음마다 사람을 불려가며 나아갔고, 그 앞에서는 모든 가게가 문을 닫았다. 행렬의 목적지는 들판 멀리 떨어진 세인트 팽크라스의 옛 교회였다. 행렬은 때맞춰 거기 다다랐고, 묘지 안으로 몰려들어가겠다고 우겨댔으며, 마침내 고인 로저 클라이의 매장을 제 나름의 방식으로, 더없이 흡족하게 치러냈다.\n\n죽은 자를 처리하고 나자 군중은 스스로 또 다른 놀이를 마련해야 할 처지가 되었고, 또 다른 명민한 천재(어쩌면 같은 자였을지도)가 지나가는 행인들을 올드 베일리 첩자로 몰아세워 그들에게 복수하는 익살을 떠올렸다. 이 기발한 생각을 실현하느라 평생 올드 베일리 근처에도 가본 적 없는 무고한 사람 수십 명이 쫓기게 되었고, 거칠게 떠밀리고 학대당했다. 거기서 유리창 깨기 놀이로, 다시 술집 약탈로 넘어가는 것은 쉽고도 자연스러웠다. 마침내 몇 시간이 지나, 여기저기 정자가 헐리고 좀 더 호전적인 무리를 무장시키려고 몇몇 지하실 난간이 뜯겨나간 뒤에, 근위대가 오고 있다는 소문이 퍼졌다. 이 소문 앞에서 군중은 차츰 흩어졌고, 어쩌면 근위대가 왔을 수도 있고 어쩌면 끝내 오지 않았을 수도 있으니, 이것이 폭도가 으레 밟는 수순이었다.\n\n크런처 씨는 그 마무리 놀이에는 가담하지 않고 교회 묘지에 남아 장의사들과 이야기를 나누며 위로를 건넸다. 그곳은 그에게 마음을 가라앉히는 영향을 미쳤다. 그는 이웃 술집에서 파이프를 하나 얻어 와 피우며, 난간 안을 들여다보고 그 자리를 곰곰이 살펴보았다.\n\n\"제리,\" 크런처 씨가 늘 하던 식으로 자기 자신에게 말을 걸었다. \"그날 저 클라이를 봤지, 그리고 네 두 눈으로 그자가 젊은 놈이고 몸이 곧게 빠진 놈이라는 걸 봤지.\"\n\n파이프를 다 피우고 좀 더 곱씹은 뒤, 그는 폐점 시간 전에 텔슨 은행의 제자리에 모습을 보이려고 발길을 돌렸다. 죽음에 대한 그의 명상이 간을 건드렸는지, 아니면 평소에 건강이 어딘가 좋지 않았던 것인지, 아니면 어느 저명한 인물에게 약간의 경의를 표하고 싶었던 것인지는, 그가 돌아가는 길에 자기 주치의 — 어느 저명한 외과의사 — 를 잠시 들렀다는 사실만큼 중요한 것은 아니다.\n\n어린 제리는 의무에 충실한 관심으로 아버지와 교대했고, 부재중에 일거리가 없었다고 보고했다. 은행이 문을 닫고, 늙은 직원들이 나오고, 늘 하던 대로 경비가 세워졌으며, 크런처 씨와 그의 아들은 차를 마시러 집으로 갔다.\n\n\"자, 내가 일러두겠는데!\" 크런처 씨가 집에 들어서며 아내에게 말했다. \"정직한 장사꾼인 내가 오늘 밤 일을 그르치게 되면, 난 당신이 또 나한테 맞서서 빌었다고 확신할 거고, 마치 당신이 그러는 걸 내 눈으로 본 것처럼 똑같이 당신을 손봐줄 거요.\"\n\n풀이 죽은 크런처 부인은 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"아니, 내 면전에서 그 짓을 하고 있군!\" 크런처 씨가 성난 의심의 기색을 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"난 아무 말도 안 하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"흥, 그럼 아무 명상도 하지 마. 명상하느니 차라리 엎드려 비는 게 낫겠어. 이러나저러나 나한테 맞서기는 마찬가지지. 아예 다 집어치워.\"\n\n\"네, 제리.\"\n\n\"네, 제리,\" 크런처 씨가 차를 마시려 앉으며 되뇌었다. \"허! 그래, 네, 제리렷다. 바로 그거지. 네, 제리라고 말할 만하지.\"\n\n크런처 씨는 이 뚱한 맞장구에 무슨 특별한 의미를 둔 것이 아니라, 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 그저 막연한 반어적 불만을 토로하려고 그것을 써먹은 것이었다.\n\n\"당신하고 당신의 네, 제리라니,\" 크런처 씨가 버터 바른 빵을 한입 베어 물고는, 받침접시에서 커다란 보이지 않는 굴이라도 꺼내 그것을 넘기는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"허! 그래, 그럴 테지. 내 당신을 믿어주지.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤에 나가시려고요?\" 그가 또 한입 베어 물자 그의 점잖은 아내가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 나갈 거요.\"\n\n\"저도 같이 가도 돼요, 아버지?\" 그의 아들이 활기차게 물었다.\n\n\"안 된다. 난 가는 거다 — 네 어미도 알다시피 — 낚시하러. 거기로 가는 거다. 낚시하러 가는 거야.\"\n\n\"아버지 낚싯대는 좀 녹이 슬었던데요, 안 그래요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"네가 상관할 바 아니다.\"\n\n\"물고기 좀 집에 가져오실 거예요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"못 가져오면 너희는 내일 끼니가 부실할 게다,\" 그 신사가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"질문은 그쯤이면 충분하다. 난 네가 잠자리에 든 지 한참 지나기 전엔 안 나갈 거야.\"\n\n그는 그날 저녁 남은 시간 내내 크런처 부인을 더없이 빈틈없이 감시하고, 그녀가 자기에게 불리한 어떤 청원도 명상하지 못하도록 뚱한 얼굴로 줄곧 대화에 붙들어두는 데 전념했다. 이런 속셈으로 그는 아들에게도 어미를 대화에 붙들어두라고 다그쳤고, 그 불운한 여인이 단 한순간이라도 혼자만의 생각에 빠지게 두느니, 그녀에게 들이댈 수 있는 온갖 불평거리를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 고달프게 만들었다. 아무리 독실한 사람이라도 정직한 기도의 효험에, 이 사내가 자기 아내를 이렇게 불신하며 바친 것보다 더 큰 경의를 바칠 수는 없었을 것이다. 그것은 마치 유령을 믿지 않는다고 공언하던 자가 유령 이야기에 겁먹는 격이었다.\n\n\"그리고 명심해!\" 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"내일은 무슨 수작도 부리지 마! 정직한 장사꾼인 내가 고기 한두 토막을 마련하는 데 성공하면, 손도 안 대고 빵만 고집하는 짓일랑 하지 마. 정직한 장사꾼인 내가 맥주를 좀 마련할 수 있게 되면, 물만 마시겠다고 선언하는 짓일랑 하지 마. 로마에 가면 로마법을 따르랬어. 안 그러면 로마가 당신한테 고약한 상대가 될 거야. 내가 당신의 로마라고, 알겠어.\"\n\n그러더니 그는 다시 투덜대기 시작했다.\n\n\"제 먹을 것과 마실 것한테 대드는 꼴이라니! 당신이 엎드려 비는 수작과 인정머리 없는 행실로, 여기 먹을 것과 마실 것을 얼마나 귀하게 만들어놓을지 난 모르겠소. 당신 애를 봐. 저게 당신 애 맞지, 안 그래? 막대기처럼 비쩍 말랐잖아. 당신이 어미라면서, 어미의 첫째 의무가 제 자식을 토실토실하게 살찌우는 거란 걸 모른단 말이야?\"\n\n이 말은 어린 제리의 아픈 데를 건드렸다. 그는 어머니에게 첫째 의무를 다하라고 간청하면서, 다른 무엇을 하든 안 하든, 무엇보다도 다른 한쪽 어버이가 그토록 가슴 뭉클하고 섬세하게 일러준 그 어미로서의 직분을 다하는 데 특별히 힘써달라고 호소했다.\n\n이렇게 크런처 가족에게 저녁이 흘러갔고, 마침내 어린 제리는 잠자리에 들라는 명을 받았으며, 그의 어머니도 비슷한 지시에 따랐다. 크런처 씨는 밤의 이른 시각을 홀로 피우는 파이프로 달래며 보냈고, 거의 한 시가 다 되어서야 외출에 나섰다. 그 작고 으스스한 시각이 다가오자, 그는 의자에서 일어나 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼내 잠긴 찬장을 열고는, 자루 하나와 적당한 크기의 쇠지렛대 하나, 밧줄과 사슬, 그리고 그런 따위의 다른 낚시 도구들을 꺼냈다. 이 물건들을 능숙한 솜씨로 몸에 갖춰 넣은 뒤, 그는 크런처 부인에게 작별의 으름장을 한 번 던지고는 불을 끄고 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n잠자리에 들 때 옷을 벗는 시늉만 했던 어린 제리는 아버지 뒤를 곧바로 따라나섰다. 어둠을 틈타 그는 방을 나와 따라가고, 계단을 내려가 따라가고, 안마당을 지나 따라가고, 거리로 나와 따라갔다. 집에 다시 들어가는 일은 걱정할 것이 없었으니, 집에 하숙인이 가득해 문이 밤새 빠끔히 열려 있었기 때문이다.\n\n아버지의 정직한 생업의 기술과 비밀을 배우고자 하는 갸륵한 야심에 이끌려, 어린 제리는 두 눈이 서로 바짝 붙어 있는 만큼이나 집 정면과 담벼락과 문간에 바짝 붙어, 자기의 존경하는 어버이를 시야에서 놓치지 않았다. 북쪽으로 방향을 잡은 그 존경하는 어버이가 얼마 가지 않았을 때, 또 다른 아이작 월턴의 제자가 그에게 합류했고, 둘은 함께 터벅터벅 걸어갔다.\n\n* 아이작 월턴: 17세기 영국의 작가로, 낚시에 관한 고전 「조어대전(釣魚大全)」의 저자. 여기서 '아이작 월턴의 제자'란 낚시꾼을 뜻하는 익살스러운 표현이다.\n처음 출발한 지 반 시간도 안 되어, 그들은 깜박이는 가로등과 깜박임이나 다름없는 야경꾼들을 지나, 인적 없는 길로 나섰다. 여기서 또 다른 낚시꾼이 합류했는데 — 그것이 어찌나 소리 없이 이루어졌던지, 어린 제리가 미신을 믿는 아이였다면 이 점잖은 업종의 두 번째 추종자가 갑자기 둘로 쪼개졌다고 여겼을 법했다.\n\n셋은 계속 나아갔고, 어린 제리도 계속 나아갔으니, 마침내 셋이 길 위로 비스듬히 솟은 둑 아래에서 멈출 때까지 그랬다. 둑 꼭대기에는 낮은 벽돌담이 있었고 그 위에 쇠 난간이 둘려 있었다. 둑과 담의 그늘 속에서 셋은 길에서 벗어나, 그 담이 — 거기서는 여덟 자에서 열 자쯤 높이로 솟아 있었는데 — 한쪽 면을 이루는 막다른 골목으로 올라갔다. 한 모퉁이에 웅크리고 앉아 골목 위쪽을 훔쳐보던 어린 제리가 그다음으로 본 것은, 물기 어린 흐린 달을 배경으로 제법 또렷하게 윤곽이 드러난 채 쇠문을 날렵하게 기어오르는 그의 존경하는 어버이의 모습이었다. 그는 곧 넘어갔고, 이어 두 번째 낚시꾼이 넘어갔으며, 그다음 세 번째도 넘어갔다. 그들은 모두 문 안쪽 땅 위로 사뿐히 내려앉아 잠시 그곳에 엎드려 있었다 — 아마도 귀를 기울이는 듯했다. 그러더니 그들은 두 손과 두 무릎으로 기어 멀어져 갔다.\n\n이제 어린 제리가 문에 다가갈 차례였고, 그는 숨을 죽인 채 그렇게 했다. 거기 다시 한 모퉁이에 웅크리고 앉아 안을 들여다보던 그는, 세 낚시꾼이 우거진 풀숲을 헤치며 기어가는 모습을 알아보았다! 그리고 묘지의 모든 묘비들이 — 그들이 들어가 있는 곳은 커다란 묘지였다 — 흰옷 입은 유령들처럼 지켜보았고, 교회의 탑 그 자체는 거대한 거인의 유령처럼 내려다보았다. 그들은 멀리 기어가지 않아 멈춰서서 똑바로 일어섰다. 그러더니 낚시를 시작했다.\n\n그들은 처음에 삽으로 낚시를 했다. 이윽고 그 존경하는 어버이가 커다란 코르크 마개뽑이 같은 어떤 도구를 맞추는 듯했다. 그들이 무슨 연장으로 일하든, 그들은 열심히 일했으니, 마침내 교회 시계가 무시무시하게 울리는 바람에 어린 제리는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 머리카락을 아버지 것만큼이나 빳빳하게 곤두세운 채 줄행랑을 쳤다.\n\n그러나 이런 일들에 대해 더 알고 싶다는 그의 오래 간직해온 소망은, 그를 달아나다 멈춰 세웠을 뿐 아니라 다시 그곳으로 꾀어 돌아오게 했다. 그가 두 번째로 문 안을 들여다보았을 때 그들은 여전히 끈질기게 낚시를 하고 있었다. 하지만 이제는 입질이 온 듯했다. 저 아래쪽에서 무언가 비틀어 돌리는 소리와 삐걱대는 소리가 났고, 그들의 구부린 몸은 마치 무게에 짓눌린 듯 잔뜩 긴장해 있었다. 그 무게가 차츰차츰 그 위의 흙을 밀어내고 지면으로 떠올랐다. 어린 제리는 그것이 무엇일지 잘 알고 있었으나, 막상 그것을 보고, 자기의 존경하는 어버이가 그것을 비틀어 열려는 것을 보자, 그런 광경이 처음인지라 어찌나 겁이 났던지 다시 줄행랑을 쳐서, 일 마일 남짓 달리고 나서야 비로소 멈춰 섰다.\n\n숨을 쉬는 것보다 덜 절박한 일이었다면 그는 그때도 멈추지 않았을 것이니, 그가 달린 것은 유령에게 쫓기는 듯한 종류의 질주였고, 어떻게든 그 끝에 다다르고 싶은 질주였기 때문이다. 그는 자기가 본 그 관이 자기 뒤를 쫓아오고 있다는 강한 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 그 관이 좁은 한쪽 끝으로 꼿꼿이 선 채 그의 뒤에서 깡충깡충 따라오며, 언제라도 그를 따라잡아 그의 옆구리에 붙어 깡충거릴 — 어쩌면 그의 팔짱을 낄 — 태세인 모습이 그려지니, 그것은 피해야 할 추격자였다. 게다가 그것은 종잡을 수 없고 어디에나 있는 악귀였으니, 그것이 그의 등 뒤로 온 밤을 무시무시하게 만드는 동안, 그는 어두운 골목을 피하려고 큰길로 튀어나갔으니, 그것이 꼬리도 날개도 없는 부어오른 사내아이의 연처럼 골목에서 깡충거리며 튀어나올까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그것은 문간에도 숨어 있어, 그 끔찍한 어깨를 문에 비벼대며 마치 웃기라도 하듯 문을 제 귀까지 끌어올렸다. 그것은 길 위의 그늘 속으로 들어가, 그를 걸어 넘어뜨리려고 교활하게 등을 대고 누워 있었다. 그러는 내내 그것은 끊임없이 등 뒤에서 깡충거리며 그를 따라잡고 있었으니, 그 소년이 자기 집 문에 다다랐을 때 반쯤 죽을 만한 까닭이 있었던 것이다. 그러고 나서도 그것은 그를 놓아주지 않고, 계단마다 쿵쿵 부딪히며 그를 따라 위층까지 올라와, 그와 함께 침대로 기어들었고, 그가 잠들자 죽은 듯 무겁게 그의 가슴 위로 쿵 내려앉았다.\n\n벽장 속의 어린 제리는 새벽이 지나고 해가 뜨기 전, 짓눌린 잠에서 가족 방에 들어온 아버지의 기척에 깨어났다. 아버지에게 무언가 잘못된 일이 있었다. 적어도 어린 제리는 아버지가 크런처 부인의 귀를 붙들고 그녀의 뒤통수를 침대 머리판에 찧어대고 있는 정황으로 미루어 그렇게 짐작했다.\n\n\"내가 그러겠다고 했지,\" 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"그래서 했고.\"\n\n\"제리, 제리, 제리!\" 그의 아내가 애원했다.\n\n\"당신은 사업의 이익에 맞서고 있어,\" 제리가 말했다. \"그래서 나와 내 동업자들이 손해를 보잖아. 당신은 남편을 공경하고 따르기로 했어. 그런데 빌어먹을, 왜 안 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"난 좋은 아내가 되려고 애쓰고 있어요, 제리,\" 그 가엾은 여인이 눈물을 흘리며 항변했다.\n\n\"남편의 사업에 맞서는 게 좋은 아내야? 남편의 사업에 먹칠을 하는 게 남편을 공경하는 거야? 남편 사업이라는 중대한 문제에서 남편을 거역하는 게 남편을 따르는 거냔 말이야?\"\n\n\"그땐 당신이 그 끔찍한 사업을 하지 않았잖아요, 제리.\"\n\n\"당신은,\" 크런처 씨가 쏘아붙였다, \"정직한 장사꾼의 아내라는 걸로 충분하지, 그자가 언제 그 생업을 시작했는지 안 했는지 따위 계산으로 그 여자 머리를 채울 게 아니야. 남편을 공경하고 따르는 아내라면 그자의 생업 따위 아예 건드리지 않을 거라고. 자기가 독실한 여자라고? 당신이 독실한 여자라면, 차라리 불경한 여자를 내게 다오! 당신은 여기 이 템스강 바닥이 말뚝에 대해 타고난 의무감을 갖고 있는 정도밖에 의무감이 없어, 그래서 마찬가지로 그걸 두들겨 박아 넣어야 하는 거지.\"\n\n이 언쟁은 낮은 목소리로 진행되었고, 결국 그 정직한 장사꾼이 진흙투성이 장화를 걷어차 벗고 바닥에 큰 대자로 드러눕는 것으로 끝났다. 녹슨 두 손을 베개 삼아 머리 밑에 괴고 등을 대고 누운 아버지를 겁먹은 듯 슬쩍 엿본 뒤, 그의 아들도 자리에 누워 다시 잠이 들었다.\n\n아침 식사에는 물고기도 없었고 다른 것도 별로 없었다. 크런처 씨는 기운도 없고 심기도 사나웠으며, 크런처 부인이 식전 기도를 올릴 기미라도 보이면 그것을 바로잡으려고 던질 무기로 쇠 냄비 뚜껑을 곁에 두었다. 그는 여느 때 시각에 솔질을 하고 세수를 했으며, 표면상의 생업에 나서려고 아들과 함께 길을 떠났다.\n\n햇볕이 내리쬐고 붐비는 플리트가를 따라 의자를 옆구리에 끼고 아버지 곁에서 걷는 어린 제리는, 어둠과 적막 속을 그 무시무시한 추격자에게 쫓겨 집으로 달려가던 전날 밤의 어린 제리와는 사뭇 다른 어린 제리였다. 그의 약삭빠름은 낮과 함께 새로워졌고, 그의 거리낌은 밤과 함께 사라졌으니 — 이런 점에서라면 그 화창한 아침에 플리트가와 런던 시내에 그와 같은 동류들이 있었다 해도 이상할 것은 없었다.\n\n\"아버지,\" 어린 제리가 길을 걸으며 말했다. 팔 하나 길이만큼 거리를 두고 둘 사이에 의자를 잘 끼워 넣도록 조심하면서 말했다. \"부활업자가 뭐예요?\"\n\n크런처 씨는 대답하기 전에 보도 위에 멈춰 섰다. \"내가 그걸 어떻게 아냐?\"\n\n\"아버지는 뭐든 다 아시는 줄 알았는데요,\" 그 천진한 소년이 말했다.\n\n\"흠! 그래,\" 크런처 씨가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 가시 같은 머리털이 마음껏 놀도록 모자를 들어 올리면서 대답했다. \"그건 장사꾼이다.\"\n\n\"그 사람 물건은 뭐예요, 아버지?\" 활기찬 어린 제리가 물었다.\n\n\"그자 물건은,\" 크런처 씨가 머릿속에서 곰곰이 굴려본 뒤에 말했다, \"과학용 물건의 한 갈래지.\"\n\n\"사람 몸뚱이죠, 그렇죠, 아버지?\" 그 발랄한 소년이 물었다.\n\n\"그런 비슷한 거지,\" 크런처 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아버지, 저는 다 자라면 꼭 부활업자가 되고 싶어요!\"\n\n크런처 씨는 흐뭇해했으나, 미심쩍고 도덕적인 투로 고개를 저었다. \"그건 네가 네 재능을 어떻게 갈고닦느냐에 달렸지. 네 재능을 갈고닦도록 조심하고, 어느 누구한테도 안 해도 될 말은 한마디도 하지 마라, 그러면 네가 지금으로선 장차 무엇이 될 자격을 갖추게 될지 알 수 없는 거야.\" 이렇게 격려를 받은 어린 제리가 의자를 바 의 그늘에 세워두려고 몇 야드 앞서 나아가자, 크런처 씨는 혼잣말로 덧붙였다. \"제리야, 이 정직한 장사꾼아, 저 아이가 언젠가 너에게 축복이 되고, 제 어미 몫까지 너에게 보답이 될 거라는 희망이 있구나!\"\n* 바(Bar): 런던 플리트가와 스트랜드가 사이에 있던 옛 시문(市門) '템플 바'를 가리킨다.\n\n# CHAPTER XV. Knitting\n\n드파르주 씨의 술집에서는 평소보다 이른 시간부터 술이 오갔다. 아침 여섯시밖에 안 된 시각인데도, 창살 친 창문 너머로 들여다보던 누런 얼굴들은 안에서 술잔에 머리를 숙이고 있는 다른 얼굴들을 알아보았다. 드파르주 씨는 형편이 가장 좋을 때에도 몹시 묽은 술을 팔았지만, 이맘때 그가 팔던 술은 유난히 더 묽은 듯했다. 게다가 시큼한 술, 혹은 시어가는 술이었으니, 그것을 마시는 자들의 기분에 미치는 영향은 그들을 음울하게 만드는 것이었기 때문이다. 드파르주 씨가 짜낸 포도에서 흥겨운 바쿠스의 불길은 튀어 오르지 않았다. 다만 어둠 속에서 타들어 가는 음울한 불씨가 그 찌꺼기 속에 숨어 있었다.\n\n드파르주 씨의 술집에서 이른 아침부터 술이 오간 것은 이날로 사흘째 연속이었다. 그것은 월요일에 시작되었고, 이제 수요일이 되었다. 술보다는 이른 시간부터 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는 일이 더 많았다. 문이 열리는 시각부터 그곳에서 귀를 기울이고 소곤거리며 몰래 드나드는 자들이 많았지만, 그들은 제 영혼을 구하기 위해서라도 계산대에 동전 한 닢 올려놓을 수 없는 처지였기 때문이다. 그렇지만 그들은 술통을 통째로 부릴 수 있기라도 한 듯이 그곳에 한껏 관심을 쏟았다. 그들은 자리에서 자리로, 구석에서 구석으로 미끄러지듯 옮겨 다니며, 술 대신 이야기를 게걸스러운 눈빛으로 집어삼켰다.\n\n손님들이 유난히 들끓는데도, 술집 주인의 모습은 보이지 않았다. 그가 없다고 아쉬워하는 사람도 없었다. 문지방을 넘어 들어온 누구도 그를 찾지 않았고, 누구도 그를 찾아 묻지 않았으며, 드파르주 부인이 홀로 자리를 지키며 술 분배를 주관하는 것을 보고도 이상하게 여기는 이가 없었다. 그녀 앞에는 닳고 두들겨 맞아 본래의 새김이 다 지워진 잔돈 동전들이 사발에 담겨 있었는데, 그것은 그 돈이 흘러나온 누더기 주머니 임자들, 곧 인류라는 잔돈만큼이나 형체를 잃은 것이었다.\n\n술집을 들여다보는 첩자들의 눈에는 — 그들은 왕의 궁전에서 죄수의 감옥에 이르기까지 높은 곳이든 낮은 곳이든 어디나 들여다보았으니 — 멈춰버린 관심과 만연한 멍한 기색이 어쩌면 포착되었을지도 모른다. 카드놀이는 시들했고, 도미노를 하던 자들은 골똘히 그것으로 탑을 쌓았으며, 술꾼들은 흘린 술방울로 탁자 위에 그림을 그렸고, 드파르주 부인 자신은 이쑤시개로 소맷자락의 무늬를 콕콕 짚으면서, 멀리 떨어진 곳의 들리지도 보이지도 않는 무언가를 보고 들었다.\n\n이렇듯 쌩땅뚜안은 술집의 이런 면모를 띤 채 한낮까지 이어졌다. 먼지투성이 사내 둘이 그곳 거리들을 지나, 흔들거리는 등불 아래를 통과한 것은 한낮이 절정에 이른 때였다. 그중 하나는 드파르주 씨였고, 다른 하나는 푸른 모자를 쓴 도로 보수공이었다. 온통 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 목이 탄 두 사람은 술집으로 들어섰다. 그들의 도착은 쌩땅뚜안의 가슴속에 일종의 불을 지폈으니, 그것은 그들이 다가옴에 따라 빠르게 번지며, 거의 모든 문과 창에서 얼굴이라는 불꽃으로 일렁이고 너울거렸다. 그러나 아무도 그들을 뒤따르지 않았고, 그들이 술집에 들어섰을 때 입을 여는 사람은 없었으니, 그곳의 모든 사내들의 눈이 그들에게 쏠려 있었음에도 그러했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 여러분!\" 드파르주 씨가 말했다.\n\n그것은 다들 입을 풀라는 신호였는지도 모른다. \"안녕하시오!\" 하는 화답의 합창이 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"날이 궂군요, 여러분.\" 드파르주가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n그러자 사내들은 저마다 옆 사람을 바라보았고, 이윽고 모두 눈을 내리깔고 잠자코 앉아 있었다. 다만 한 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"여보,\" 드파르주가 큰 소리로 드파르주 부인을 향해 말했다. \"내가 자끄라고 불리는 이 착한 도로 보수공과 함께 몇 리외를 걸어왔소. 그를 만난 건 — 우연히 — 빠리에서 하루 반 거리 떨어진 곳이었지. 자끄라고 불리는 이 도로 보수공은 착한 친구요. 마실 것을 좀 주구려, 여보!\"\n\n두번째 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다. 드파르주 부인은 자끄라고 불리는 도로 보수공 앞에 술을 놓았고, 그는 일동에게 푸른 모자를 벗어 인사하고 마셨다. 그의 작업복 가슴께에는 거칠고 검은 빵이 들어 있었는데, 그는 틈틈이 그것을 먹으며 드파르주 부인의 계산대 가까이에 앉아 우물우물 씹으며 마셨다. 세번째 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n드파르주는 술 한 모금으로 기운을 차렸으나 — 손님에게 따라준 것보다는 적게 마셨으니, 그 자신에게는 술이 드문 것이 아니었기 때문이다 — 시골 사람이 아침 식사를 마칠 때까지 서서 기다렸다. 그는 그 자리의 누구도 바라보지 않았고, 이제 누구도 그를 바라보지 않았다. 뜨개질감을 집어 들고 일에 몰두하고 있던 드파르주 부인조차도 그러했다.\n\n\"식사를 마쳤소, 친구?\" 그가 때맞춰 물었다.\n\n\"네, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이리 오시오! 당신이 묵을 수 있다고 했던 그 방을 보여주겠소. 당신에게 더없이 잘 맞을 거요.\"\n\n술집에서 거리로, 거리에서 안마당으로, 안마당에서 가파른 계단을 올라, 계단에서 다락방으로 — 예전에 백발의 남자가 낮은 걸상에 앉아 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 매우 바쁘게 구두를 만들던 바로 그 다락방으로.\n\n이제 그곳에 백발의 남자는 없었다. 다만 술집에서 하나씩 따로 나간 세 사내가 거기 있었다. 그리고 그들과 멀리 떨어진 백발의 남자 사이에는, 그들이 한때 벽 틈으로 그를 들여다본 적이 있다는 작은 연결고리 하나가 있었다.\n\n드파르주는 조심스럽게 문을 닫고, 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"자끄 하나, 자끄 둘, 자끄 셋! 이 사람이 나, 자끄 넷이 약속을 잡아 만난 증인이오. 그가 여러분에게 모든 걸 말해줄 거요. 말하시오, 자끄 다섯!\"\n\n도로 보수공은 손에 든 푸른 모자로 거무스름한 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"어디서부터 시작할까요, 나리?\"\n\n\"시작하시오,\" 드파르주 씨가 더없이 합당하게 대답했다. \"처음부터.\"\n\n\"그러니까 제가 그를 본 건, 여러분,\" 도로 보수공이 입을 열었다. \"이번 여름이면 꼭 한해 전이었는데, 후작님의 마차 밑에서 쇠사슬에 매달려 있었습죠. 그 모양이 어땠는지 보십시오. 저는 도로에서 일을 마치는 참이었고, 해는 잠자리에 들고 있었고, 후작님의 마차는 천천히 언덕을 오르고 있었고, 그자는 쇠사슬에 매달려 있었는데 — 이렇게요.\"\n\n도로 보수공은 다시 한번 그 모든 동작을 처음부터 끝까지 해 보였다. 그때쯤이면 그 동작에 완벽해졌을 법도 했으니, 그것이 한해 내내 그의 마을에서 결코 빗나가지 않는 화제거리이자 없어서는 안 될 여흥이었기 때문이다.\n\n자끄 하나가 끼어들어, 전에 그 사내를 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"한번도 없습죠.\" 도로 보수공이 다시 똑바로 서며 대답했다.\n\n자끄 셋이 그렇다면 나중에 어떻게 그를 알아보았느냐고 따져 물었다.\n\n\"키가 크다는 걸로요.\" 도로 보수공이 손가락을 코에 댄 채 나직이 말했다. \"후작님이 그날 저녁에 '어디, 그자가 어떻게 생겼더냐?' 하고 물으셨을 때, 저는 '유령처럼 키가 컸습니다'라고 대답했습죠.\"\n\n\"난쟁이처럼 작다고 말했어야지.\" 자끄 둘이 받아쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 뭘 알았겠습니까? 그땐 아직 그 일이 저질러지지도 않았고, 그자가 제게 속내를 털어놓지도 않았는걸요. 보십시오! 그런 상황에서조차 저는 제 증언을 내놓지 않았습죠. 후작님이 우리 마을 작은 샘 가까이 서서 손가락으로 저를 가리키며 '내게로! 저 놈을 끌고 오너라!' 하셨을 때도, 정말이지 여러분, 저는 아무것도 내놓지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"저 사람 말이 옳소, 자끄.\" 드파르주가 끼어들었던 자에게 나직이 말했다. \"계속하시오!\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 도로 보수공이 신비스러운 태도로 말했다. \"키 큰 사내는 종적을 감추었고, 사람들이 그를 찾았습죠 — 몇 달 동안이었을까요? 아홉 달, 열 달, 열한 달?\"\n\n\"몇 달인지는 중요치 않소.\" 드파르주가 말했다. \"그는 잘 숨어 있었지만, 결국 운 나쁘게 발견되었지. 계속하시오!\"\n\n\"저는 또다시 언덕배기에서 일하고 있었고, 해는 또다시 잠자리에 들려는 참이었습죠. 저 아래 마을에 있는 제 오두막으로 내려가려고 — 거긴 이미 어둑했지요 — 연장을 챙기던 참에 눈을 들어 보니, 군인 여섯이 언덕을 넘어오는 게 보였습니다. 그 한가운데에 두 팔이 묶인 키 큰 사내가 있었는데 — 양옆구리에 붙들어 매여 있었습죠 — 이렇게요!\"\n\n그는 없어서는 안 될 모자의 도움을 빌려, 등 뒤에서 매듭지은 끈으로 두 팔꿈치를 허리께에 단단히 묶인 사내를 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"저는 한쪽으로 물러서서, 여러분, 제 돌무더기 옆에 서서, 군인들과 그 죄수가 지나가는 걸 보았습죠 (그곳은 외딴 길이라, 무슨 구경거리든 볼만한 가치가 있거든요). 처음에 그들이 다가올 때는, 군인 여섯에 묶인 키 큰 사내 하나라는 것, 그리고 그들이 제 눈에는 거의 새카맣게 보인다는 것 — 다만 해가 지는 쪽으로는 가장자리가 붉었습죠, 여러분 — 그것 말고는 더 보이지 않았습니다. 또 보니, 그들의 긴 그림자가 길 건너편의 움푹 꺼진 등성이에 드리워지고, 그 위 언덕에도 드리워져, 마치 거인들의 그림자 같았습니다. 또 보니, 그들은 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 있었고, 그들이 다가옴에 따라 그 먼지도 함께 움직여, 저벅, 저벅! 하지만 그들이 제게 아주 가까이 다가왔을 때, 저는 그 키 큰 사내를 알아보았고, 그자도 저를 알아보았습죠. 아아, 그러나 그자는 저와 처음 마주쳤던 그 저녁처럼, 바로 그 자리 가까이에서 다시 한번 언덕배기로 몸을 던지고 싶었을 겁니다!\"\n\n그는 마치 자기가 그 자리에 있는 듯이 그것을 묘사했고, 그것을 생생하게 떠올리고 있음이 분명했다. 어쩌면 그는 살면서 본 것이 별로 없었는지도 모른다.\n\n\"저는 군인들에게 그 키 큰 사내를 알아본 티를 내지 않았고, 그자도 군인들에게 저를 알아본 티를 내지 않았습죠. 우리는 눈으로 그 짓을 했고, 눈으로 그걸 알았습니다. '가자!' 그 무리의 대장이 마을을 가리키며 말했습죠. '저놈을 어서 무덤으로 데려가라!' 그러고는 그를 더 빨리 끌고 갔습니다. 저는 뒤따랐죠. 그의 두 팔은 어찌나 꽉 묶였던지 퉁퉁 부어 있었고, 그의 나막신은 크고 투박했고, 그는 절름발이였습니다. 절름발이라 느릴 수밖에 없으니, 그들은 총으로 그를 몰아댔습죠 — 이렇게요!\"\n\n그는 머스킷 총 개머리판으로 사람을 앞으로 떠미는 동작을 흉내 냈다.\n\n\"미친 사람들이 경주라도 하듯 언덕을 내달리며 내려가니, 그가 쓰러집니다. 그들은 웃으며 그를 다시 일으켜 세웁니다. 그의 얼굴은 피투성이에 먼지투성이지만, 그는 손을 댈 수가 없으니, 그러자 그들은 또 웃습니다. 그들은 그를 마을로 끌고 들어옵니다. 온 마을이 보려고 달려나오고요. 그들은 그를 방앗간을 지나 감옥까지 데려갑니다. 온 마을은 그 밤의 어둠 속에서 감옥 문이 열려 그를 삼키는 걸 봅니다 — 이렇게요!\"\n\n그는 입을 한껏 크게 벌렸다가 이를 딱 소리 나게 부딪치며 다물었다. 다시 입을 벌려 그 효과를 망치고 싶어하지 않는 것을 알아채고, 드파르주가 말했다. \"계속하시오, 자끄.\"\n\n\"온 마을이,\" 도로 보수공이 발끝으로 서서 낮은 목소리로 이어갔다. \"물러갑니다. 온 마을이 샘가에서 소곤거리고요. 온 마을이 잠듭니다. 온 마을이 그 불행한 자의 꿈을 꿉니다, 바위산 위 감옥의 자물쇠와 빗장 안에 갇혀, 죽지 않고서는 결코 그곳에서 나오지 못할 그자의 꿈을요. 아침이 되어, 어깨에 연장을 메고 가는 길에 검은 빵 부스러기를 뜯어 먹으면서, 저는 일터로 가는 길에 감옥을 빙 둘러 갑니다. 거기서 저는 그를 봅니다, 높은 곳, 높다란 철창 우리의 창살 뒤에서, 간밤처럼 피투성이에 먼지투성이로, 밖을 내다보고 있는 그를요. 그에겐 제게 흔들어 보일 자유로운 손 하나 없습니다. 저는 감히 그를 부르지 못합니다. 그는 죽은 사람처럼 저를 바라봅니다.\"\n\n드파르주와 세 사내는 어두운 눈빛으로 서로를 흘긋 보았다. 시골 사람의 이야기에 귀 기울이는 그들 모두의 표정은 어둡고, 억눌려 있고, 복수심에 차 있었다. 그들 모두의 태도는 은밀하면서도 위엄이 있었다. 그들에게는 거친 법정 같은 분위기가 감돌았다. 자끄 하나와 둘은 낡은 간이침대에 앉아, 저마다 손으로 턱을 괸 채 도로 보수공에게 눈길을 고정하고 있었다. 자끄 셋은 그에 못지않게 골똘히, 그들 뒤에서 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 안절부절못하는 손을 입과 코 언저리의 가느다란 신경 다발 위로 끊임없이 미끄러뜨리고 있었다. 드파르주는 그들과 이야기꾼 사이에 서서, 창문의 빛 속에 세워둔 그를 번갈아 보았으니, 그에게서 그들에게로, 그들에게서 그에게로 시선을 옮겼다.\n\n\"계속하시오, 자끄.\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"그는 며칠 동안 그 위 철창 우리에 갇혀 있습니다. 마을은 두려운 나머지 몰래 그를 바라봅니다. 하지만 마을은 늘 멀찍이서 바위산 위의 감옥을 올려다봅죠. 그리고 저녁이 되어, 하루의 일을 마치고 샘가에 모여 이야기를 나눌 때면, 모든 얼굴이 감옥 쪽을 향합니다. 예전에는 역참 쪽을 향했지만, 이제는 감옥 쪽을 향하지요. 사람들은 샘가에서 소곤거립니다, 그자가 사형 선고를 받긴 했어도 처형되지는 않을 거라고요. 빠리에 탄원서가 제출되었는데, 그자가 제 자식의 죽음 때문에 격분해서 미쳐버렸다는 내용이라고들 합니다. 국왕 폐하께 직접 탄원서가 올라갔다고도 하고요. 제가 뭘 알겠습니까? 그럴 수도 있겠죠. 그럴지도, 아닐지도.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 들어보시오, 자끄.\" 그 이름을 가진 첫번째 사내가 엄하게 끼어들었다. \"국왕과 왕비에게 탄원서가 제출되었다는 걸 알아두시오. 당신만 빼고 여기 있는 모두가, 국왕이 거리에서 마차 안에 왕비 곁에 앉아 그것을 받는 걸 보았소. 목숨을 걸고 말들 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그 탄원서를 손에 든 사람이, 바로 여기 보고 있는 이 드파르주요.\"\n\n\"그리고 다시 한번 들어보시오, 자끄!\" 무릎 꿇은 세번째 사내가 말했다. 그의 손가락들은 마치 무언가를 — 먹을 것도 마실 것도 아닌 무언가를 — 갈망하기라도 하듯, 놀랍도록 게걸스러운 기색으로 그 가느다란 신경 위를 끊임없이 더듬고 또 더듬었다. \"기마병과 보병으로 이루어진 호위대가 탄원자를 둘러싸고 두들겨 팼소. 듣고 있소?\"\n\n\"듣고 있습니다, 여러분.\"\n\n\"그럼 계속하시오.\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"또요, 다른 한편으로는, 사람들이 샘가에서 소곤거립니다.\" 시골 사람이 다시 이어갔다. \"그자가 우리 고장으로 끌려 내려온 건 그 자리에서 처형하기 위해서이고, 그자는 틀림없이 처형될 거라고요. 그자가 영주님을 죽였고, 영주님은 그자의 소작인들 — 농노들 — 뭐라 부르든 간에 — 그들의 아버지였으니, 그자는 친부 살해범으로 처형될 거라고까지 소곤거립니다. 어떤 노인이 샘가에서 그러는데, 그자의 오른손, 곧 칼을 쥐었던 손이 그자의 얼굴 앞에서 불에 태워질 거라고요. 그자의 두 팔과 가슴과 다리에 상처를 내고, 거기에 끓는 기름과 녹인 납과 뜨거운 송진과 밀랍과 유황을 부을 거라고요. 마지막에는 힘센 말 네 마리가 그자의 사지를 갈가리 찢어놓을 거라고 합니다. 그 노인 말이, 이 모든 게 선왕이신 루이 십오세의 목숨을 노렸던 어느 죄수에게 실제로 행해졌다는군요. 하지만 그 노인이 거짓말하는 건지 제가 어찌 알겠습니까? 저는 학자가 아니걸요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 다시 한번 들어보시오, 자끄!\" 쉴 새 없는 손과 갈망하는 기색의 사내가 말했다. \"그 죄수의 이름은 다미앵이었고, 그 모든 게 백주 대낮에, 이 빠리 시의 탁 트인 거리에서 행해졌소. 그리고 그것을 지켜본 어마어마한 군중 가운데 가장 눈길을 끈 것은, 끝까지 — 끝까지요, 자끄, 그자가 두 다리와 한 팔을 잃고도 여전히 숨이 붙어 있던 밤까지 늘어진 그 끝까지 — 열렬한 관심으로 가득 차 있던 지체 높고 멋들어진 귀부인들의 무리였소! 그리고 그 일이 행해진 게 — 그래, 당신은 나이가 어떻게 되오?\"\n\n\"서른다섯입니다.\" 예순은 되어 보이는 도로 보수공이 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 열 살이 넘었을 때 행해진 일이오. 당신도 그걸 봤을지 모르지.\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐소!\" 드파르주가 험상궂게 안달하며 말했다. \"악마 만세! 계속하시오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다! 누구는 이걸 소곤거리고, 누구는 저걸 소곤거립니다. 사람들은 온통 그 얘기뿐이지요. 샘물까지도 그 가락에 맞춰 흐르는 것 같습니다. 마침내 일요일 밤, 온 마을이 잠든 사이에 군인들이 와서, 감옥에서부터 구불구불 내려오는데, 그들의 총이 좁은 거리의 돌바닥에 부딪쳐 쟁그랑 소리를 냅니다. 일꾼들이 땅을 파고, 일꾼들이 망치질을 하고, 군인들이 웃고 노래합니다. 아침에 샘가에는 마흔 자 높이의 교수대가 솟아올라, 물을 더럽힙니다.\"\n\n도로 보수공은 낮은 천장을 바라보았다기보다 그것을 꿰뚫어 보았고, 마치 하늘 어딘가에 교수대가 보이기라도 하는 듯 손가락질을 했다.\n\n\"모든 일이 멈추고, 모두가 그곳에 모입니다. 아무도 소를 끌고 나가지 않으니, 소들도 다른 사람들과 함께 거기 있습니다. 한낮이 되자, 북소리가 울립니다. 군인들이 밤사이 감옥으로 행군해 들어가 있었고, 그자는 수많은 군인들 한가운데에 있습니다. 그는 전처럼 묶여 있고, 그의 입에는 재갈이 물려 있는데 — 팽팽한 끈으로 이렇게 묶어서, 거의 웃고 있는 것처럼 보이게 했습니다.\" 그는 두 엄지를 입꼬리에서 귀까지 가져가 얼굴을 일그러뜨리며 그 모습을 흉내 냈다. \"교수대 꼭대기에는 칼이 고정되어 있는데, 날이 위를 향하고 칼끝이 허공을 찌르고 있습니다. 그는 거기 마흔 자 높이에 매달립니다 — 그러고는 매달린 채로 버려져, 물을 더럽힙니다.\"\n\n그가 푸른 모자로 얼굴을 닦는 동안 — 그 구경거리를 떠올리느라 다시 땀이 솟아난 얼굴이었다 — 그들은 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"끔찍합니다, 여러분. 여자들과 아이들이 어떻게 물을 길을 수 있겠습니까! 누가 저녁에 그 그림자 아래서 한담을 나눌 수 있겠습니까! 그 아래서라고 했던가요? 제가 월요일 저녁, 해가 잠자리에 들 무렵 마을을 떠나면서 언덕에서 돌아보니, 그 그림자가 교회를 가로지르고, 방앗간을 가로지르고, 감옥을 가로질러 — 마치 땅을 가로질러, 여러분, 하늘이 땅에 닿아 있는 데까지 뻗치는 것 같았습니다!\"\n\n그 굶주린 사내는 다른 세 사람을 바라보며 손가락 하나를 깨물었고, 그의 손가락은 그를 사로잡은 갈망으로 떨렸다.\n\n\"그게 다입니다, 여러분. 저는 (그러라는 일러둠을 받은 대로) 해 질 녘에 떠났고, 그날 밤과 이튿날 한낮까지 걸어, (만나게 될 거라는 일러둠을 받은 대로) 이 동지를 만났습니다. 그와 함께, 때로는 타고 때로는 걸어서, 어제 남은 시간과 간밤 내내 줄곧 왔습니다. 그래서 여기 이렇게 와 있는 거지요!\"\n\n음울한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 첫번째 자끄가 말했다. \"좋소! 당신은 충실하게 행동했고 충실하게 이야기했소. 문밖에서 잠시 우리를 기다려주겠소?\"\n\n\"기꺼이요.\" 도로 보수공이 말했다. 드파르주는 그를 계단 꼭대기까지 바래다주고, 거기 앉혀둔 채 돌아왔다.\n\n그가 다락방으로 돌아왔을 때, 세 사람은 일어서서 머리를 맞대고 있었다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각하시오, 자끄?\" 첫번째가 물었다. \"등록할까요?\"\n\n\"멸망이 정해진 자로 등록합시다.\" 드파르주가 대답했다.\n\n\"훌륭하군!\" 갈망하는 기색의 사내가 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"성도, 그 일족 전부도?\" 첫번째가 물었다.\n\n\"성도, 그 일족 전부도.\" 드파르주가 대답했다. \"몰살이오.\"\n\n그 굶주린 사내는 황홀한 쉰 목소리로 \"훌륭하군!\" 하고 되뇌고는, 또 다른 손가락을 깨물기 시작했다.\n\n\"분명하오?\" 자끄 둘이 드파르주에게 물었다. \"우리가 명부를 보관하는 방식 때문에 곤란한 일이 생기지 않으리라는 게 확실하오? 의심할 여지 없이 안전하지요, 우리 말고는 누구도 그걸 해독할 수 없으니까요. 하지만 우리가 언제까지나 그걸 해독할 수 있겠소 — 아니, 이렇게 말해야겠군, 부인은 그럴 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"자끄,\" 드파르주가 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다. \"내 아내가 그 명부를 오로지 기억만으로 간직하겠다고 나선다 해도, 그녀는 한마디도 — 한 음절도 — 놓치지 않을 거요. 그녀 자신의 코와 그녀 자신의 기호로 떠 넣은 것이니, 그녀에게는 언제까지나 해처럼 환할 거요. 드파르주 부인을 믿으시오. 세상에 둘도 없는 비겁한 겁쟁이라도 제 존재 자체를 지워 없애는 편이, 제 이름이나 죄목의 글자 하나를 드파르주 부인의 뜨개질 명부에서 지워내는 것보다 더 쉬울 거요.\"\n\n신뢰와 동의의 웅성거림이 일었고, 이윽고 그 굶주린 사내가 물었다. \"이 촌사람은 곧 돌려보낼 거요? 그러길 바라오. 저자는 아주 단순해. 좀 위험하지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"저자는 아무것도 모르오.\" 드파르주가 말했다. \"적어도 저 자신을 같은 높이의 교수대로 손쉽게 올려보낼 만한 것 이상은 아무것도. 저자는 내가 맡겠소. 나와 함께 있게 하시오. 내가 그를 돌보아, 길을 떠나게 하겠소. 저자는 화려한 세상 — 국왕과 왕비와 궁정 — 을 보고 싶어하니, 일요일에 그것들을 보게 합시다.\"\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 굶주린 사내가 빤히 쳐다보며 외쳤다. \"왕족과 귀족을 보고 싶어한다는 게 좋은 징조란 말이오?\"\n\n\"자끄,\" 드파르주가 말했다. \"고양이가 우유를 탐내게 하고 싶거든, 우유를 솜씨 좋게 보여주시오. 개가 언젠가 제 먹잇감을 쓰러뜨리게 하고 싶거든, 타고난 먹잇감을 솜씨 좋게 보여주시오.\"\n\n더 이상 말은 없었고, 도로 보수공은 어느새 맨 위 계단에서 졸고 있는 것이 발견되어, 간이침대에 누워 좀 쉬라는 권유를 받았다. 그는 설득할 필요도 없이 곧 잠들었다.\n\n그 정도 신분의 시골 노예에게는, 빠리에서 드파르주의 술집보다 더 못한 거처도 얼마든지 찾을 수 있었을 것이다. 끊임없이 그를 따라다니는, 부인에 대한 알 수 없는 두려움만 빼면, 그의 생활은 무척 새롭고 유쾌했다. 그러나 부인은 온종일 계산대에 앉아, 어찌나 노골적으로 그를 의식하지 않는 척하는지, 또 그가 그곳에 있는 것이 겉으로 드러나지 않는 그 무엇과도 관련이 있다는 것을 어찌나 한사코 알아채지 않으려 하는지, 그는 그녀에게 눈길이 닿을 때마다 나막신 속에서 부들부들 떨었다. 저 부인이 다음번엔 또 무슨 시늉을 할지 도무지 예측할 수 없다고 그는 속으로 되뇌었기 때문이다. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그 화려하게 꾸민 머릿속에서, 자기가 그를 보았다는 — 그가 살인을 저지르고 그 후에 희생자의 가죽을 벗기는 것을 보았다는 — 시늉을 하기로 마음먹는다면, 그녀는 그 연극이 다 끝날 때까지 틀림없이 끝까지 밀고 나갈 거라고 그는 확신했다.\n\n그리하여 일요일이 되자, 도로 보수공은 부인이 주인 양반과 자기와 함께 베르사유에 동행하리라는 것을 알고 (좋다고 말은 했지만) 마냥 신이 나지는 않았다. 게다가 부인이 공공 마차 안에서 가는 내내 뜨개질을 하고 있는 것이 한층 더 마음을 어수선하게 했다. 한층 더 마음을 어수선하게 한 것은, 오후에 군중이 국왕과 왕비의 마차를 보려고 기다리는 동안, 부인이 여전히 손에 뜨개질감을 든 채 군중 속에 끼어 있다는 사실이었다.\n\n\"열심히 일하시는군요, 부인.\" 그녀 곁의 한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 드파르주 부인이 대답했다. \"할 일이 많아서요.\"\n\n\"무얼 만드십니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"여러가지요.\"\n\n\"이를테면 —\"\n\n\"이를테면,\" 드파르주 부인이 침착하게 대답했다. \"수의(壽衣) 같은 거요.\"\n\n사내는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 조금 더 멀찍이 물러났고, 도로 보수공은 푸른 모자로 부채질을 했다. 몹시 후텁지근하고 숨이 막히는 것 같았기 때문이다. 그가 기운을 되찾으려면 국왕과 왕비가 필요했다면, 그는 그 처방을 바로 곁에 두고 있는 셈이니 운이 좋았다. 이내 얼굴이 큰 국왕과 얼굴이 고운 왕비가 황금 마차를 타고 나타났는데, 빛나는 궁정의 측근들이 — 웃어대는 귀부인들과 멋진 영주들의 반짝이는 무리가 — 뒤따랐다. 보석과 비단과 분과 화려함, 그리고 우아하게 발길질하는 자태와 점잖게 거들먹거리는 양성의 얼굴들 속에서, 도로 보수공은 한껏 멱을 감았다. 그것이 어찌나 그를 일시적으로 도취시켰던지, 그는 국왕 만세, 왕비 만세, 만인 만세, 만물 만세! 하고 외쳤으니, 마치 그가 살면서 어디에나 있는 자끄에 대해 들어본 적도 없는 듯했다. 이어서 정원과 안마당과 테라스와 분수와 푸른 둔덕이 있었고, 또다른 국왕과 왕비가, 또다른 측근들이, 또다른 영주와 귀부인들이, 또다시 그들 모두 만세! 하는 외침이 이어졌으니, 마침내 그는 감격에 겨워 그야말로 눈물을 흘렸다. 세 시간쯤 이어진 이 광경 내내, 그에게는 함께 소리치고 울고 감격하는 동무들이 넘쳐났고, 그동안 드파르주는 마치 그가 잠시 동안의 숭배 대상에게 달려들어 갈가리 찢어놓지 못하게 막기라도 하듯 그의 멱살을 붙잡고 있었다.\n\n\"잘했네!\" 일이 끝나자 드파르주가 후원자처럼 그의 등을 두드리며 말했다. \"자넨 착한 친구야!\"\n\n도로 보수공은 이제 정신이 들었고, 방금 자기가 한 짓에 무슨 잘못이라도 저지른 게 아닌가 미심쩍어했다. 그러나 그렇지 않았다.\n\n\"자넨 우리가 원하는 바로 그런 사람일세.\" 드파르주가 그의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"자넨 저 어리석은 것들에게 이게 영원히 이어질 거라고 믿게 만들지. 그러면 저들은 더욱 거만해지고, 그럴수록 끝이 더 가까워지는 거야.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 도로 보수공이 곰곰이 생각하며 외쳤다. \"그게 참말이군요.\"\n\n\"저 어리석은 것들은 아무것도 몰라. 저들은 자네 숨결을 경멸하면서, 제 말이나 개 한 마리보다도 자네나 자네 같은 백 명의 숨을 영영 끊어놓으려 하지만, 저들이 아는 건 오직 자네 숨결이 일러주는 것뿐이지. 그러니 그 숨결로 저들을 조금 더 속이게나. 아무리 속여도 지나치지 않을 테니.\"\n\n드파르주 부인이 거만한 눈초리로 그 손님을 바라보며 동의한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"당신으로 말하자면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"볼거리가 있고 떠들썩하기만 하면, 당신은 무엇에든 소리치고 눈물을 흘릴 사람이야. 말해봐! 안 그래?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인, 제 생각에도 그렇습니다. 그 순간만큼은요.\"\n\n\"인형이 한 무더기 쌓여 있는 걸 당신에게 보여주고, 당신더러 그것들을 갈가리 뜯어내 당신 잇속을 차리라고 풀어놓는다면, 당신은 가장 값지고 가장 화려한 걸 골라내겠지. 말해봐! 안 그래?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇습니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"그래. 그리고 날지 못하는 새 떼를 당신에게 보여주고, 당신더러 당신 잇속을 차리려고 그 깃털을 뽑으라고 풀어놓는다면, 당신은 가장 고운 깃털을 가진 새들에게 달려들겠지. 안 그래?\"\n\n\"맞습니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"당신은 오늘 인형도 새도 다 보았어.\" 드파르주 부인이 그것들이 마지막으로 보였던 곳을 향해 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"자, 이제 집으로 돌아가!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XVI. Still Knitting\n\n드파르주 부인과 그녀의 남편 되는 무슈는 사이좋게 쌩땅뚜안의 품으로 돌아갔고, 그러는 동안 파란 모자를 쓴 점 하나가 어둠을 뚫고, 먼지를 뚫고, 길가의 가로수 길을 따라 지친 발걸음으로 이정표를 헤아리며, 이제 무덤에 누운 후작 무슈의 성관이 속삭이는 나무들의 소리에 귀 기울이고 있는 그 방위를 향해 천천히 나아갔다. 이제 그 돌 얼굴들에게는 나무와 분수의 소리에 귀 기울일 시간이 넉넉히 주어졌으니, 먹을 풀과 태울 마른 나뭇가지 조각을 찾아 헤매던 몇 안 되는 마을의 허수아비 같은 사람들이 그 거대한 돌 안뜰과 테라스 계단이 보이는 곳까지 흘러들어 왔다가, 그 굶주린 상상 속에서 얼굴들의 표정이 달라졌다는 느낌을 받았다. 마을 안에는 하나의 소문이 가까스로 살아 있었으니 — 그곳 사람들이 그러하듯 희미하고 헐벗은 존재로 — 칼이 그 깊숙이 박혔을 때 얼굴들이 변하여, 오만한 얼굴에서 분노와 고통의 얼굴로 바뀌었다는 것이었고, 또한 그 매달린 형상이 분수 위 사십 피트 높이로 끌어 올려졌을 때 다시 한번 변하여, 복수를 이루었다는 잔혹한 표정을 띠었으며, 이후로 그 표정을 영원히 지니게 되었다는 것이었다. 살인이 저질러진 침실의 큰 창문 위 돌 얼굴에는, 조각된 코에 두 줄기 미세한 자국이 나 있었으니, 누구나 그것을 알아보았으나 예전에는 아무도 본 적이 없는 자국이었다. 그리고 누더기를 걸친 농부 두셋이 군중 속에서 빠져나와 돌처럼 굳어버린 후작 무슈를 황급히 훔쳐보던 드문 경우에, 앙상한 손가락이 그것을 가리킨 지 채 일 분도 지나지 않아, 그들은 모두 이끼와 나뭇잎 사이로 달아나버렸으니, 그곳에서 먹고살 길을 찾을 수 있던 좀더 운 좋은 산토끼들과 다를 바 없었다.\n\n성관과 오두막, 돌 얼굴과 매달린 형상, 돌바닥의 붉은 얼룩, 그리고 마을 우물의 맑은 물 — 수천 에이커의 땅 — 프랑스의 한 지방 전체 — 프랑스 그 자체가 모두 — 밤하늘 아래 놓여, 머리카락 한 올 너비의 희미한 선 안으로 응축되었다. 마찬가지로 온 세계가 그 모든 위대함과 하찮음을 안고 반짝이는 별 하나 속에 놓여 있다. 그리고 한낱 인간의 지식이 빛줄기 하나를 쪼개어 그것이 어떻게 이루어졌는지를 분석할 수 있듯이, 더 숭고한 지성이라면 우리네 이 지상의 미약한 빛 속에서, 그 위에 있는 책임 있는 모든 존재의 온갖 생각과 행위, 온갖 악덕과 미덕을 읽어낼 수 있으리라.\n\n드파르주 부부는 별빛 아래 그들의 합승마차를 타고 덜컹거리며, 그들의 여정이 자연히 향하게 마련인 빠리의 그 성문에 다다랐다. 거기에는 늘 그렇듯 검문소에서의 정차가 있었고, 늘 그렇듯 등불들이 늘 그렇듯 검문과 심문을 위해 흔들리며 다가왔다. 드파르주 무슈가 마차에서 내렸으니, 거기 군인 한둘과 경찰 한 명을 알고 있었던 것이다. 후자와는 친한 사이여서, 다정하게 끌어안았다.\n\n쌩땅뚜안이 다시 한번 그 거뭇한 날개로 드파르주 부부를 감싸 안았을 때, 그리고 그들이 마침내 그 성인의 경계 부근에서 내려, 그 거리의 시커먼 진창과 오물을 헤치며 걸어서 길을 찾아갈 때, 드파르주 부인이 남편에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"그래서, 여보, 경찰의 자끄가 당신한테 뭐라고 했어요?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤엔 별로 없지만, 그가 아는 건 다 말해줬소. 우리 구역에 또다른 끄나풀이 임명됐다더군. 그가 말할 수 있는 한에서는 더 많을지도 모르지만, 그가 아는 건 한 명이오.\"\n\n\"좋아요!\" 드파르주 부인이 차분하고 사무적인 태도로 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. \"그자를 등록해두는 게 필요하겠군요. 그 사내를 뭐라고 부른답니까?\"\n\n\"영국인이오.\"\n\n\"그럴수록 더 좋지요. 이름은?\"\n\n\"바사드요.\" 드파르주가 발음하여 프랑스식으로 만들며 말했다. 그러나 그는 그것을 정확히 알아두려고 무척 신경을 쓴 터라, 이어 한 치의 오차도 없이 철자를 댔다.\n\n\"바사드라.\" 부인이 되뇌었다. \"좋아요. 세례명은?\"\n\n\"존이오.\"\n\n\"존 바사드.\" 부인이 혼잣말로 한 번 중얼거린 뒤 되뇌었다. \"좋아요. 그자의 생김새는, 알려져 있나요?\"\n\n\"나이는 마흔 안팎, 키는 오 피트 구 인치쯤, 검은 머리에 가무잡잡한 피부, 대체로 제법 잘생긴 얼굴, 짙은 눈, 가늘고 길며 누르스름한 얼굴, 매부리코지만 곧지 않고 왼쪽 뺨 쪽으로 묘하게 기울어져 있어, 그 때문에 표정이 음흉하오.\"\n\n\"이런, 맙소사. 이건 초상화로군요!\" 부인이 웃으며 말했다. \"내일 그자를 등록해두겠어요.\"\n\n그들은 술집으로 들어섰는데, (자정이었으므로) 문이 닫혀 있었다. 드파르주 부인은 곧장 계산대의 자기 자리에 앉아, 자리를 비운 사이 들어온 잔돈을 세고, 재고를 살피고, 장부의 기재 사항을 훑어보고, 자기 나름의 기재 사항을 적어 넣고, 가능한 모든 방식으로 점원을 점검한 뒤, 마침내 그를 잠자리로 보냈다. 그러고는 두 번째로 돈 그릇의 내용물을 쏟아, 밤새 안전하게 간수하려고 손수건에다 따로따로 매듭을 지어 사슬처럼 묶기 시작했다. 그러는 내내 드파르주는 파이프를 입에 문 채 위아래로 거닐며, 흐뭇하게 감탄하면서도 결코 참견하지 않았으니, 실로 장사와 집안일에 관한 한, 그는 평생을 그러한 상태로 거닐며 살아왔던 것이다.\n\n밤은 무더웠고, 꽉 닫힌 채 그토록 더러운 동네에 둘러싸인 술집에서는 고약한 냄새가 났다. 드파르주 무슈의 후각은 결코 예민하지 않았으나, 재어둔 포도주는 그 맛보다 훨씬 강한 냄새를 풍겼고, 럼주와 브랜디와 아니스 술도 마찬가지였다. 그는 다 피운 파이프를 내려놓으며, 그 뒤섞인 냄새들을 후 불어 날려버렸다.\n\n\"피곤하신가봐요.\" 부인이 돈을 묶다가 시선을 들며 말했다. \"여느 때 나는 냄새일 뿐인데요.\"\n\n\"좀 피곤하군.\" 남편이 인정했다.\n\n\"좀 우울하시기도 하고요.\" 부인이 말했는데, 그 날카로운 눈은 장부에 그토록 골똘히 박혀 있던 적이 없었으나, 그래도 남편에게 한두 줄기 시선을 던지고 있었다. \"아이고, 남자들이란, 남자들이란!\"\n\n\"하지만 여보!\" 드파르주가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"하지만 여보!\" 부인이 단호하게 고개를 끄덕이며 되뇌었다. \"하지만 여보라니! 당신 오늘 밤 마음이 약해졌어요, 여보!\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다면,\" 드파르주가 마치 가슴속에서 어떤 생각이 짜내어지듯 말했다. \"이건 오랜 시간이 걸리는 일이야.\"\n\n\"오랜 시간이 걸리는 일이지요.\" 그의 아내가 되뇌었다. \"그런데 언제는 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않았나요? 복수와 응징에는 오랜 시간이 걸려요. 그게 법칙이에요.\"\n\n\"벼락으로 사람을 내려치는 데에는 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않잖소.\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"그 벼락을 만들어 쌓아두는 데에는,\" 부인이 침착하게 물었다. \"얼마나 오래 걸리지요? 말해보세요.\"\n\n드파르주는 거기에도 무언가 있다는 듯, 생각에 잠겨 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"지진이 도시 하나를 삼키는 데에는,\" 부인이 말했다. \"오랜 시간이 걸리지 않아요. 그렇지요! 그럼 그 지진을 준비하는 데에는 얼마나 오래 걸리는지 말해보세요.\"\n\n\"오래 걸리겠지, 아마.\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 일단 준비가 되면, 그것은 일어나서, 앞에 놓인 모든 것을 산산이 부숴버려요. 그러는 동안에도 그것은 늘 준비되고 있지요, 비록 보이지도 들리지도 않지만. 그게 당신에게 위안이에요. 그 위안을 간직해두세요.\"\n\n그녀는 마치 적의 목을 조르듯, 번득이는 눈으로 매듭 하나를 묶었다.\n\n\"내 당신에게 말하지만,\" 부인이 강조하느라 오른손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"비록 그것이 오는 길이 멀다 해도, 그것은 길 위에 있고 오고 있어요. 내 당신에게 말하지만, 그것은 결코 물러서지 않고, 결코 멈추지 않아요. 내 당신에게 말하지만, 그것은 늘 전진하고 있어요. 주위를 둘러보고 우리가 아는 온 세상 사람들의 삶을 생각해보세요, 우리가 아는 온 세상 사람들의 얼굴을 생각해보세요, 자끄리가 시시각각 점점 더 확실하게 호소하고 있는 그 분노와 불만을 생각해보세요. 이런 일들이 오래갈 수 있을까요? 흥! 당신을 비웃어주겠어요.\"\n\n\"내 용감한 아내여,\" 드파르주가 고개를 약간 숙이고 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡은 채, 마치 교리문답 선생 앞에 선 온순하고 주의 깊은 학생처럼 그녀 앞에 서서 대답했다. \"나는 이 모든 걸 의심하지 않소. 하지만 이건 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 또 가능한 일이오 — 당신도 잘 알다시피, 여보, 가능한 일이오 — 그것이 우리 생애 동안에는 오지 않을 수도 있다는 게.\"\n\n\"그래요! 그러면 어떻단 말이에요?\" 부인이 마치 또다른 적의 목을 조르듯 또 하나의 매듭을 묶으며 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄!\" 드파르주가 반쯤은 불평하고 반쯤은 변명하는 투로 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"우리는 그 승리를 보지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"우리는 거기에 보탬이 되었을 거예요.\" 부인이 뻗은 손을 힘차게 놀리며 대답했다. \"우리가 하는 일은 그 어느 것도 헛되지 않아요. 나는 온 영혼을 다해, 우리가 그 승리를 보리라고 믿어요. 하지만 설령 그렇지 못한다 해도, 설령 내가 분명히 못 보리라는 걸 안다 해도, 귀족과 폭군의 목덜미를 내게 보여주기만 하면, 나는 그래도 —\"\n\n그러고 나서 부인은 이를 악물고, 실로 무시무시한 매듭 하나를 묶었다.\n\n\"그만!\" 드파르주가 마치 비겁하다는 비난을 받은 듯 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"나도, 여보, 어떤 것에도 멈추지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"그래요! 하지만 가끔 자신을 지탱하기 위해 자기 희생자와 자기 기회를 봐야만 한다는 게 당신의 약점이에요. 그런 것 없이도 자신을 지탱하세요. 때가 오면, 호랑이와 악마를 풀어놓으세요. 하지만 그때가 오기를 기다리는 동안에는 호랑이와 악마를 사슬에 묶어두세요 — 드러내지 말되 — 그러나 늘 준비된 채로.\"\n\n부인은 마치 그것의 골을 박살내듯 자신의 작은 계산대를 돈 사슬로 내리치며 이 충고의 결론을 강조한 뒤, 차분한 태도로 그 묵직한 손수건을 팔 밑에 챙겨 들고는, 이제 잘 시간이라고 말했다.\n\n이튿날 정오, 그 훌륭한 여인은 술집의 늘 그렇듯 제자리에서 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있었다. 그녀 곁에는 장미 한 송이가 놓여 있었는데, 이따금 그 꽃을 흘끗 보더라도, 그것은 평소의 골똘한 태도를 조금도 흩뜨리지 않았다. 손님 몇이 술을 마시거나 마시지 않거나, 서 있거나 앉아 있거나 하며 여기저기 흩어져 있었다. 그날은 몹시 무더웠고, 떼 지은 파리들이 부인 가까이에 놓인 끈적한 작은 잔마다 호기심 어린 모험심으로 탐사를 넓혀 가다가, 그 바닥에서 죽어 나자빠졌다. 그것들의 죽음은 산책하러 나온 다른 파리들에게는 아무런 감흥도 주지 못했으니, 그것들은 마치 자기네가 코끼리거나 그만큼이나 동떨어진 무엇이라도 되는 양 더없이 태연하게 죽은 것들을 바라보다가, 결국 자기들도 같은 운명을 맞았다. 파리들이 얼마나 무심한지를 생각하면 묘하다! — 아마 그 햇살 좋은 여름날 궁정에서도 다들 그만큼이나 생각했을 터이다.\n\n문으로 들어선 어떤 형상이 드파르주 부인에게 그림자를 드리웠는데, 그녀는 그것이 낯선 그림자임을 느꼈다. 그녀는 뜨개질감을 내려놓고, 그 형상을 보기에 앞서, 머리쓰개에 장미를 꽂기 시작했다.\n\n기이한 일이었다. 드파르주 부인이 장미를 집어 든 바로 그 순간, 손님들은 이야기를 멈추고, 차츰 술집에서 빠져나가기 시작했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 부인.\" 새로 들어온 자가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 무슈.\"\n\n그녀는 소리 내어 그렇게 말했으나, 뜨개질을 다시 시작하면서 속으로 덧붙였다. \"하! 안녕하시오, 나이는 마흔 안팎, 키는 오 피트 구 인치쯤, 검은 머리, 대체로 제법 잘생긴 얼굴, 가무잡잡한 피부, 짙은 눈, 가늘고 길며 누르스름한 얼굴, 매부리코지만 곧지 않고 왼쪽 뺨 쪽으로 묘하게 기울어져 음흉한 표정을 자아내는 자! 안녕하시오, 하나도 빠짐없이!\"\n\n\"오래된 꼬냑 한 잔하고, 시원하고 신선한 물 한 모금 베풀어주시면 고맙겠소, 부인.\"\n\n부인이 정중한 태도로 응했다.\n\n\"이 꼬냑, 기막히군요, 부인!\"\n\n그것이 그토록 칭찬받은 것은 처음 있는 일이었고, 드파르주 부인은 그 내력을 잘 알고 있던 터라 그렇지 않다는 걸 알고 있었다. 그래도 그녀는 꼬냑이 과찬을 받았다고 말한 뒤, 뜨개질감을 집어 들었다. 손님은 잠시 그녀의 손가락을 지켜보다가, 그 틈을 타 가게 안 전체를 살폈다.\n\n\"솜씨가 대단하시군요, 부인.\"\n\n\"익숙해서요.\"\n\n\"무늬도 예쁘고요!\"\n\n\"그렇게 보이세요?\" 부인이 미소 지으며 그를 바라보고 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없이. 무엇에 쓰는 건지 여쭤도 될까요?\"\n\n\"심심풀이지요.\" 부인이 손가락을 날렵하게 놀리면서도 여전히 미소 지으며 그를 바라보고 말했다.\n\n\"쓸 데가 있는 건 아니고요?\"\n\n\"그건 모를 일이지요. 언젠가 쓸 데를 찾을지도요. 그렇게 되면 — 글쎄,\" 부인이 숨을 들이쉬고 준엄한 교태 같은 것으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그땐 쓸 거예요!\"\n\n기이한 일이었으나, 쌩땅뚜안의 취향은 드파르주 부인의 머리쓰개에 꽂힌 장미에 확연히 반대하는 듯했다. 두 사내가 따로따로 들어와 술을 주문하려다가, 그 새로운 물건을 발견하고는 머뭇거리며, 마치 거기 있지도 않은 친구를 찾는 척하며 두리번거리다가, 가버렸다. 또한 이 손님이 들어왔을 때 거기 있던 자들 가운데, 남아 있는 자는 하나도 없었다. 그들은 모두 빠져나갔다. 끄나풀은 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었으나, 아무런 낌새도 알아채지 못했다. 그들은 궁색하고, 목적 없고, 우연한 태도로, 지극히 자연스럽고 흠잡을 데 없이 어슬렁어슬렁 빠져나갔던 것이다.\n\n'존이라.' 부인은 손가락으로 뜨개질을 하면서 자기 일감을 점검하고, 눈으로는 그 낯선 자를 바라보며 생각했다. '오래 머물러봐, 그럼 네가 가기 전에 내가 「바사드」를 떠 넣어줄 테니.'\n\n\"부군이 있으시오, 부인?\"\n\n\"있어요.\"\n\n\"자녀는?\"\n\n\"자녀는 없어요.\"\n\n\"장사가 신통치 않은가보군요?\"\n\n\"장사는 아주 신통치 않아요. 사람들이 워낙 가난해서요.\"\n\n\"아, 불운하고, 비참한 사람들이지요! 게다가 그토록 억압받고 — 당신 말마따나.\"\n\n\"당신 말마따나지요.\" 부인이 그를 바로잡으며 받아쳤고, 그의 이름에다 그에게 이로울 게 없는 무언가를 한 코 더 능숙하게 떠 넣었다.\n\n\"미안하오. 분명히 그렇게 말한 건 나였지만, 당신도 당연히 그리 생각하겠지요. 물론이오.\"\n\n\"내가 생각한다고요?\" 부인이 높은 목소리로 받았다. \"나하고 내 남편은 생각할 겨를도 없이 이 술집을 열어두는 것만으로도 벅차요. 여기서 우리가 생각하는 거라곤 어떻게 먹고사느냐 하는 것뿐이에요. 그게 우리가 생각하는 주제이고, 그것만으로도 아침부터 밤까지 머릿속을 남들 일로 번거롭게 할 것도 없이 생각할 거리가 차고 넘쳐요. 내가 남들 생각을 한다고요? 아니, 아니에요.\"\n\n끄나풀은 찾아내거나 만들어낼 수 있는 부스러기라면 무엇이든 주워 모으려고 거기 와 있던 터라, 자신의 낭패한 상태를 그 음흉한 얼굴에 드러내지 않았다. 다만, 드파르주 부인의 작은 계산대에 팔꿈치를 괴고서, 한담을 나누는 듯한 정중한 태도로 서서, 이따금 꼬냑을 홀짝였다.\n\n\"가스빠르의 처형 말인데, 그건 안된 일이었소, 부인. 아! 가엾은 가스빠르!\" 깊은 연민의 한숨과 함께였다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 부인이 차갑고 가볍게 받았다. \"사람들이 그런 목적으로 칼을 쓴다면, 그 대가를 치러야지요. 그자는 자기 사치의 값이 무엇인지 미리 알고 있었어요. 그 값을 치른 거고요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 끄나풀이 부드러운 목소리를 더욱 낮춰 신뢰를 자아내는 어조로, 그 사악한 얼굴 근육 하나하나에 상처 입은 혁명가의 감수성을 드러내며 말했다. \"내 생각엔 이 동네에 그 가엾은 친구를 두고 연민과 분노가 많은 것 같소만? 우리끼리 얘기지만.\"\n\n\"그런가요?\" 부인이 멍하니 물었다.\n\n\"그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"— 마침 내 남편이 오는군요!\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다.\n\n술집 주인이 문으로 들어서자, 끄나풀이 모자에 손을 대며 인사하고는, 호감을 사려는 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 자끄!\" 드파르주는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그를 응시했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 자끄!\" 끄나풀이 되뇌었으나, 그 응시 아래서 그리 자신만만하지도, 그리 편안한 미소도 짓지 못했다.\n\n\"착각하셨소, 무슈.\" 술집 주인이 받았다. \"나를 다른 사람으로 잘못 보셨군요. 그건 내 이름이 아니오. 나는 에르네스뜨 드파르주요.\"\n\n\"그게 그거지요.\" 끄나풀이 가볍게 말했으나 당황한 기색도 있었다. \"안녕하시오!\"\n\n\"안녕하시오!\" 드파르주가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n\"당신이 들어왔을 때 부인과 즐겁게 얘기를 나누던 참인데, 부인께 말하던 게 있소. 사람들 말로는 — 당연한 일이지만! — 쌩땅뚜안에 가엾은 가스빠르의 불행한 운명을 두고 동정과 분노가 많다더군요.\"\n\n\"나한테는 아무도 그런 말을 하지 않았소.\" 드파르주가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"나는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르오.\"\n\n이렇게 말한 뒤, 그는 작은 계산대 뒤로 돌아가, 아내의 의자 등받이에 손을 얹고 서서, 그 칸막이 너머로 그들 두 사람이 다 같이 적대하는 자를, 그리고 둘 중 누구라도 더없이 흡족하게 쏘아 죽였을 법한 자를 바라보았다.\n\n자기 일에 잘 단련된 끄나풀은 그 무심한 태도를 바꾸지 않고, 작은 꼬냑 잔을 비우고, 신선한 물을 한 모금 마신 뒤, 꼬냑을 한 잔 더 청했다. 드파르주 부인은 그것을 따라주고, 다시 뜨개질감을 집어 들고는, 그 위에 대고 짧은 노래를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"이 구역을 잘 아시는 것 같군요. 다시 말해, 나보다 더 잘?\" 드파르주가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"천만에요, 하지만 더 잘 알고 싶소. 나는 이곳의 비참한 주민들에게 깊은 관심이 있으니까요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 드파르주가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"당신과 이야기를 나누는 즐거움을 누리니 떠오르는 게 있소, 드파르주 무슈.\" 끄나풀이 말을 이었다. \"당신 이름과 더불어 흥미로운 인연을 소중히 간직하는 영광을 누리고 있다는 거 말이오.\"\n\n\"그래요!\" 드파르주가 자못 무심하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 정말로. 마네뜨 박사가 풀려났을 때, 그의 옛 하인이던 당신이 그를 맡았다고 내가 알고 있소. 그가 당신에게 인도되었지요. 보다시피 나는 그 정황을 알고 있다오?\"\n\n\"분명히 그건 사실이오.\" 드파르주가 말했다. 그는 아내가 뜨개질을 하며 노래를 흥얼거리다가 팔꿈치로 우연인 듯 슬쩍 건드린 것을 통해, 대답하되 늘 짤막하게 하는 것이 상책이라는 뜻을 전달받았다.\n\n\"그의 딸이 찾아온 것도 당신에게였고,\" 끄나풀이 말했다. \"그의 딸이 그를 데려간 것도 당신의 보살핌으로부터였소. 깔끔한 갈색 옷차림의 무슈가 동행했는데, 그를 뭐라고 부르더라? — 작은 가발을 쓴 — 로리 — 뗄슨 상회의 — 영국으로 데려갔지요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이오.\" 드파르주가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"무척 흥미로운 추억이지요!\" 끄나풀이 말했다. \"나는 영국에서 마네뜨 박사와 그 딸을 알게 됐소.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"요즘은 그들 소식을 별로 못 들으시오?\" 끄나풀이 말했다.\n\n\"못 듣소.\" 드파르주가 말했다.\n\n\"사실인즉,\" 부인이 일감과 짧은 노래에서 눈을 들며 끼어들었다. \"우리는 그들 소식을 전혀 못 들어요. 그들이 무사히 도착했다는 소식을 받았고, 아마 편지를 한 통, 어쩌면 두 통쯤 더 받았지요. 하지만 그 뒤로는 그들이 차츰 자기네 삶의 길을 갔고 — 우리는 우리의 길을 갔고 — 서로 아무 연락도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇겠지요, 부인.\" 끄나풀이 대답했다. \"그 딸이 결혼한다더군요.\"\n\n\"한다고요?\" 부인이 되받았다. \"예쁘기로는 진작에 결혼했을 만한데요. 영국 사람들은 냉랭한가봐요, 내 보기엔.\"\n\n\"오! 내가 영국인인 걸 아시는군요.\"\n\n\"당신 혀가 영국 혀라는 건 알겠어요.\" 부인이 받았다. \"그리고 혀가 그렇다면, 사람도 그러하겠지요.\"\n\n그는 그 단정을 칭찬으로 받아들이지는 않았으나, 되도록 좋게 넘기려고 웃음으로 흘려버렸다. 꼬냑을 끝까지 홀짝인 뒤, 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그렇소, 마네뜨 양이 결혼한다오. 하지만 영국인하고는 아니고, 자기처럼 태생이 프랑스인인 자하고요. 그리고 가스빠르 얘기가 나왔으니 말이지만 (아, 가엾은 가스빠르! 그건 잔혹한, 잔혹한 일이었소!), 묘한 일은, 그녀가 후작 무슈의 조카하고 결혼한다는 거요. 가스빠르가 그토록 여러 피트 높이로 매달려 떠받들어졌던 바로 그 후작 말이오. 다시 말해, 지금의 후작이지요. 하지만 그자는 영국에서 신분을 감춘 채 살고 있어, 거기서는 후작이 아니라오. 그자는 샤를 다네 씨요. 도네는 그자 어머니 집안의 성이오.\"\n\n드파르주 부인은 꾸준히 뜨개질을 했으나, 그 정보는 그녀의 남편에게는 뚜렷한 효과를 미쳤다. 작은 계산대 뒤에서 불을 켜고 파이프에 불을 붙이는 일을 하면서도, 그는 아무리 애를 써도 마음이 흔들렸고, 손은 미덥지 못했다. 끄나풀이 그것을 못 보았거나, 마음속에 새겨두지 못했다면 끄나풀이 아니었을 것이다.\n\n적어도 이 한 방은 — 그것이 무슨 값어치가 있다고 판명되든 간에 — 먹였고, 다른 어떤 한 방을 거들어줄 손님도 들어오지 않았으므로, 바사드 씨는 마신 값을 치르고 자리를 떴다. 떠나기에 앞서, 점잖은 태도로, 드파르주 무슈와 부인을 다시 만나는 즐거움을 고대한다고 말할 기회를 잡았다. 그가 쌩땅뚜안의 바깥 세계로 나선 뒤로 몇 분 동안, 부부는 그가 되돌아올까봐 그가 떠날 때 그대로의 모습으로 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"사실일 수 있을까,\" 드파르주가 아내의 의자 등받이에 손을 얹고 담배를 피우며 서서 그녀를 내려다보고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그자가 마네뜨 양에 대해 한 말 말이오?\"\n\n\"그자가 한 말이고 보면,\" 부인이 눈썹을 약간 치켜올리며 받았다. \"아마 거짓일 거예요. 하지만 사실일 수도 있지요.\"\n\n\"만약 사실이라면 —\" 드파르주가 입을 열다가 멈췄다.\n\n\"사실이라면?\" 그의 아내가 되뇌었다.\n\n\"— 그리고 만약 그것이 오게 된다면, 우리가 그 승리를 보도록 살아 있는 동안에 — 나는, 그녀를 위해서라도, 운명이 그녀 남편을 프랑스 밖에 두기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"그녀 남편의 운명은,\" 드파르주 부인이 여느 때의 침착함으로 말했다. \"그가 가야 할 곳으로 그를 데려갈 것이고, 그를 끝장낼 종말로 그를 이끌어 갈 거예요. 내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 참 이상하잖소 — 적어도 지금으로선, 참 이상하지 않소\" — 드파르주가 아내에게 인정하도록 거의 사정하다시피 말했다. \"그녀 아버지 무슈와 그녀 자신을 향한 우리의 그 모든 동정에도 불구하고, 그녀 남편의 이름이 바로 이 순간 당신 손으로, 우리를 막 떠난 그 지긋지긋한 개 같은 자의 이름 곁에 등록되어야 한다는 게 말이오.\"\n\n\"그것이 오게 되면 그보다 더 이상한 일들도 일어날 거예요.\" 부인이 대답했다. \"나는 그 둘을 다 여기 분명히 가지고 있어요. 그리고 둘 다 제 죗값으로 여기 있는 거고요. 그거면 충분해요.\"\n\n그 말을 마친 뒤 그녀는 뜨개질감을 둘둘 말았고, 이윽고 머리에 두른 손수건에서 장미를 빼냈다. 쌩땅뚜안이 그 거슬리는 장식이 사라졌다는 것을 본능적으로 알아챘든, 아니면 쌩땅뚜안이 그것이 사라지기를 망보고 있었든, 어쨌든 그 성인은 곧 용기를 내어 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔고, 술집은 평소의 모습을 되찾았다.\n\n저녁이면, 다른 어느 때보다도 바로 그 시간에 쌩땅뚜안은 속을 죄다 뒤집어내듯 하여, 사람들이 바람 한 줄기 쐬려고 문간 계단과 창턱에 걸터앉고 더러운 거리와 안뜰 모퉁이로 나왔는데, 드파르주 부인은 일감을 손에 들고 이곳에서 저곳으로, 이 무리에서 저 무리로 옮겨 다니곤 했다. 한 사람의 전도사였으니 — 그녀 같은 이는 많았다 — 세상으로서는 다시는 길러내지 않는 편이 좋을 그런 부류였다. 여인들은 모두 뜨개질을 했다. 그들은 쓸모없는 것들을 떴으나, 그 기계적인 노동은 먹고 마시는 일의 기계적인 대용물이었다. 손은 턱과 소화기관을 위해 움직였으니, 만일 그 앙상한 손가락이 멈춰 있었다면, 위장은 기아로 더욱 옥죄였을 것이다.\n\n그러나 손가락이 움직이는 대로, 눈도 움직였고, 생각도 움직였다. 그리고 드파르주 부인이 이 무리에서 저 무리로 옮겨 가는 동안, 그녀가 말을 건넨 뒤 뒤에 남겨두고 떠난 여인들의 작은 매듭마다, 그 셋은 모두 더 빠르고 더 사납게 움직였다.\n\n그녀의 남편은 자기 집 문간에서 담배를 피우며, 감탄의 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. \"대단한 여자야.\" 그가 말했다. \"강한 여자, 위대한 여자, 무섭도록 위대한 여자라고!\"\n\n어둠이 사방으로 내려앉았고, 이윽고 교회 종소리와 궁정 안뜰에서 울리는 군악대 북소리가 멀리서 들려왔다. 여인들은 앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 했다. 어둠이 그들을 에워쌌다. 또 하나의 어둠이 그만큼이나 어김없이 다가오고 있었으니, 그때가 되면 프랑스 곳곳의 수많은 가뿐한 첨탑에서 지금은 흥겹게 울리는 그 교회 종들이 녹아내려 천둥 치는 대포가 될 것이며, 그 군악대 북은 그 밤에는 권세와 풍요, 자유와 생명의 목소리처럼 더없이 강력한 어느 비참한 목소리를 묻어버리려고 두드려댈 것이었다. 앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 하던 그 여인들 주위로 그토록 많은 것이 다가들고 있었으니, 그들 자신이야말로 아직 세워지지도 않은 어떤 구조물을 에워싸고 다가들고 있었던 것이다. 그곳에서 그들은 앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 하며, 떨어지는 머리를 헤아리게 될 터였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII. One Night\n\n의사와 그의 딸이 함께 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있던 어느 잊지 못할 저녁만큼, 소호의 그 조용한 모퉁이 위로 태양이 그토록 찬란한 영광을 지니고 저문 적은 없었다. 그날 밤, 그들이 여전히 나무 아래 앉아 있는 것을 발견하고 잎사귀 사이로 그들의 얼굴을 비추던 그때만큼, 거대한 런던 위로 달이 그토록 부드러운 광채를 띠고 떠오른 적도 없었다.\n\n루시는 내일 결혼할 예정이었다. 그녀는 이 마지막 저녁을 아버지를 위해 남겨두었고, 그래서 두 사람은 플라타너스 아래 단둘이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"행복하세요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그럼, 아주 행복하단다.\"\n\n오랜 시간 그곳에 있었지만 그들은 말이 거의 없었다. 아직 일하거나 책을 읽을 만큼 빛이 남아 있었을 때에도, 그녀는 평소처럼 일감을 손에 쥐지도, 아버지에게 책을 읽어드리지도 않았다. 그녀는 나무 아래 그의 곁에서 그 두 가지로 시간을 보낸 적이 여러 번, 아주 여러 번 있었지만, 이번은 그 어느 때와도 사뭇 달랐고, 무엇으로도 그렇게 만들 수는 없었다.\n\n\"그리고 저는 오늘 밤 무척 행복해요, 아버지. 하늘이 이토록 축복해주신 사랑 안에서 저는 깊이 행복합니다 — 샤를을 향한 제 사랑과, 저를 향한 샤를의 사랑이요. 하지만 만약 제 인생이 더 이상 아버지께 바쳐지지 않는 것이라면, 또는 제 결혼이 우리를 이 거리 몇 개의 길이만큼이라도 갈라놓도록 정해진 것이라면, 저는 지금 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 더 불행하고 자책에 시달릴 거예요. 지금 이대로도—\"\n\n지금 이대로도, 그녀는 자신의 목소리를 다스릴 수 없었다.\n\n슬픈 달빛 속에서 그녀는 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 그의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻었다. 태양 그 자체의 빛이 그러하듯이 — 인간의 삶이라 불리는 빛이 그러하듯이 — 그 오고 감에 있어 언제나 슬픈 그 달빛 속에서.\n\n\"사랑하는 아버지! 이 마지막으로 제게 말씀해주실 수 있나요, 제 어떤 새로운 애정도, 제 어떤 새로운 의무도 결코 우리 사이를 가로막지 않으리라고 정말로, 정말로 확신하신다고요? 저는 그것을 잘 알아요, 하지만 아버지께서도 아시나요? 아버지 마음속으로, 정말 확실하다고 느끼세요?\"\n\n아버지는 그가 좀처럼 꾸며낼 수 없을 법한 명랑하고 굳건한 확신을 담아 대답했다. \"정말 확실하단다, 내 사랑! 그뿐만이 아니야,\" 그가 그녀에게 다정하게 입맞추며 덧붙였다. \"네 결혼을 통해 바라보는 내 미래는, 루시야, 그것이 없었을 때보다 — 아니, 지금껏 한 번도 그랬던 적이 없을 만큼 — 훨씬 더 밝단다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 바랄 수만 있다면요, 아버지!—\"\n\n\"믿으렴, 얘야! 정말로 그러하니까. 그것이 마땅히 그래야 한다는 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 얼마나 명백한지 생각해보렴. 헌신적이고 젊은 너는 네 인생이 헛되이 버려지지 않기를 바라며 내가 느껴온 그 근심을 온전히 헤아릴 수 없을 거다—\"\n\n그녀가 손을 그의 입술 쪽으로 가져갔지만, 그는 그 손을 자신의 손으로 잡고는 그 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"—헛되이, 내 아이야 — 헛되이 버려지지 않기를, 사물의 자연스러운 질서에서 — 나 때문에 — 비껴나지 않기를 말이다. 네 이타심으로는 내 마음이 이 문제에 얼마나 매달려왔는지 온전히 이해할 수 없겠지. 하지만 스스로 한번 물어보렴, 네 행복이 불완전한데 어떻게 내 행복이 완전할 수 있겠느냐?\"\n\n\"제가 만약 샤를을 한 번도 보지 못했더라면요, 아버지, 저는 아버지와 함께 아주 행복했을 거예요.\"\n\n샤를을 본 이상 그가 없었다면 자신이 불행했으리라는, 그녀의 무심결의 고백에 그는 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"내 아이야, 너는 그를 보았고, 그가 바로 샤를이지. 만약 그가 샤를이 아니었다면, 다른 사람이었을 게다. 또는, 다른 누구도 아니었다면, 그건 내가 원인이었을 테고, 그러면 내 인생의 어두운 부분이 그 그림자를 나 자신 너머로 드리워 네게 떨어졌겠지.\"\n\n재판 때를 제외하고, 그녀가 그가 고통받던 시절을 언급하는 것을 들은 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 그의 말이 귓가에 머무는 동안 그것은 그녀에게 기이하고 새로운 감각을 주었다. 그리고 그녀는 그 일을 오래도록 기억했다.\n\n\"저것 보렴!\" 보베의 의사가 손을 달을 향해 들어 올리며 말했다. \"나는 감옥 창문으로 저 달을 바라보았지, 그 빛을 견딜 수 없을 때에도. 내가 잃어버린 것 위로 달이 비치고 있다는 생각이 어찌나 고통스러웠던지, 감옥 벽에 머리를 찧어가며 달을 바라본 적도 있었다. 너무도 멍하고 무기력한 상태로 달을 바라보면서, 보름달일 때 달을 가로지르며 그을 수 있는 수평선의 수와, 그것들과 교차시켜 그을 수 있는 수직선의 수 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 못한 적도 있었지.\" 그는 달을 바라보며 속으로 곱씹는 듯한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"어느 쪽이든 스무 개였던 게 기억나는구나, 그리고 스무번째 선을 비집어 넣기가 어려웠지.\"\n\n그가 그 시절로 돌아가는 것을 들으며 느낀 기이한 전율은 그가 그 일을 곱씹을수록 더욱 깊어졌다. 하지만 그가 그것을 언급하는 태도에는 그녀를 놀라게 할 만한 것이 전혀 없었다. 그는 다만 지금의 명랑함과 행복을, 이제는 지나간 그 끔찍한 인내와 견주어보는 듯할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"나는 달을 바라보며, 나에게서 떨어져 나간, 아직 태어나지 않은 아이에 대해 수천 번을 헤아려보았다. 그 아이가 살아 있을까. 살아서 태어났을까, 아니면 가엾은 어미의 충격이 그 아이를 죽였을까. 언젠가 제 아비의 원수를 갚아줄 아들일까. (감옥에 갇혀 있는 동안 복수에 대한 내 갈망이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 컸던 때가 있었지.) 제 아비의 이야기를 결코 알지 못할 아들일까. 제 아비가 제 스스로의 뜻과 행동으로 사라졌을 가능성을 저울질하며 살아갈 수도 있는 아들일까. 자라서 여인이 될 딸일까.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 다가가 그의 뺨과 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"나는 내 딸이 나를 완전히 잊은 모습을 — 아니, 나를 전혀 모르고, 나의 존재조차 의식하지 못하는 모습을 그려보았다. 나는 그 아이의 나이를 한 해 한 해 헤아려보았지. 내 운명에 대해 아무것도 모르는 남자와 결혼한 그 아이를 보았다. 나는 산 자들의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌고, 다음 세대에서 내 자리는 텅 빈 공백이었지.\"\n\n\"아버지! 한 번도 존재한 적 없는 딸에 대해 그런 생각을 하셨다는 말을 듣는 것만으로도, 마치 제가 그 아이였던 것처럼 제 가슴이 미어집니다.\"\n\n\"네가 말이냐, 루시? 이런 기억들이 떠오르는 것은, 그리고 이 마지막 밤에 우리와 달 사이를 지나가는 것은, 바로 네가 내게 가져다준 위안과 회복에서 비롯된 것이란다.—내가 방금 무어라 말했더라?\"\n\n\"그 아이가 아버지에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고요. 아버지를 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다고요.\"\n\n\"그래! 하지만 다른 달밤이면, 슬픔과 고요가 사뭇 다른 방식으로 나를 어루만질 때면 — 고통을 그 바탕에 둔 어떤 감정이 그럴 수 있는 한도 내에서, 슬픔 어린 평화의 느낌 같은 것으로 나를 적실 때면 — 나는 그 아이가 내 감방으로 찾아와 나를 그 요새 너머의 자유로 이끌어 내는 모습을 상상하곤 했단다. 지금 내가 너를 보듯이, 나는 달빛 속에서 그 아이의 모습을 자주 보았지. 다만 한 번도 그 아이를 내 품에 안아본 적은 없었다. 그것은 작은 쇠창살 창과 문 사이에 서 있었지. 하지만 그것이 내가 지금 말하는 그 아이가 아니라는 것을, 너는 이해하겠지?\"\n\n\"그 형상이 아니라요. 그—그—영상, 그 환상이요?\"\n\n\"그래. 그것은 다른 것이었다. 그것은 어지러워진 내 시각 앞에 서 있었지만, 결코 움직이지 않았어. 내 마음이 좇던 그 환영은 또 다른, 더 실재하는 아이였단다. 그 아이의 겉모습에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 제 어미를 닮았다는 것뿐이지. 다른 그것도 그 닮음을 지니고 있었어 — 네가 그러하듯이 — 하지만 같은 것은 아니었다. 내 말을 따라올 수 있겠느냐, 루시? 어렵겠지, 그렇지? 이 뒤얽힌 구분을 이해하려면 너도 외로운 수인이 되어보았어야 할 것 같구나.\"\n\n침착하고 차분한 그의 태도도, 그가 이렇게 자신의 옛 처지를 해부하려 들 때 그녀의 피가 차갑게 식는 것을 막지는 못했다.\n\n\"그 더 평온한 상태에서, 나는 그 아이가 달빛 속에서 내게로 와, 나를 데리고 나가, 그 아이가 결혼해 꾸린 가정이 잃어버린 아비에 대한 사랑스러운 기억으로 가득하다는 것을 보여주는 모습을 상상했다. 내 초상화가 그 아이의 방에 걸려 있었고, 나는 그 아이의 기도 속에 있었지. 그 아이의 삶은 활기차고, 명랑하고, 쓸모 있었다. 하지만 나의 가엾은 사연이 그 모든 것에 스며들어 있었어.\"\n\n\"제가 그 아이였어요, 아버지. 저는 그 절반만큼도 착하지 못했지만, 제 사랑에 있어서는 제가 바로 그 아이였어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그 아이는 내게 자기 아이들을 보여주었지,\" 보베의 의사가 말했다. \"그 아이들은 내 이야기를 들어 알고 있었고, 나를 가엾이 여기도록 가르침받았지. 그들은 국가의 감옥을 지날 때면 그 험상궂은 벽에서 멀찍이 떨어져, 창살을 올려다보며 속삭이듯 이야기했단다. 그 아이는 결코 나를 구해낼 수 없었어. 나는 그 아이가 그런 것들을 보여준 뒤에 늘 나를 다시 데려다놓는다고 상상했지. 하지만 그러고 나면, 눈물이라는 위안의 축복을 받아, 나는 무릎을 꿇고 그 아이를 축복했단다.\"\n\n\"제가 그 아이이기를 바라요, 아버지. 오 사랑하는 아버지, 사랑하는 아버지, 내일도 그렇게 간절하게 저를 축복해주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"루시야, 내가 이 오래된 괴로움들을 떠올리는 것은, 오늘 밤 내가 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 너를 더욱 사랑하고, 내 크나큰 행복에 대해 하느님께 감사드릴 까닭이 있기 때문이란다. 내 생각이 가장 거칠었던 때에도, 그것은 내가 너와 함께 알게 된 행복, 그리고 우리 앞에 놓인 그 행복 근처에는 결코 다다르지 못했지.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 끌어안고, 엄숙하게 그녀를 하늘에 의탁하며, 그녀를 자신에게 내려주신 하늘에 겸허히 감사드렸다. 이윽고 두 사람은 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n결혼식에 청해진 사람은 로리 씨뿐이었다. 신부 들러리조차 수척한 프로스 양 외에는 없을 터였다. 결혼으로 그들의 거처가 바뀌지는 않을 것이었다. 그들은 예전에 그 정체 모를 보이지 않는 세입자의 것이었던 위층 방들을 차지함으로써 거처를 넓힐 수 있었고, 그 이상은 아무것도 바라지 않았다.\n\n마네뜨 의사는 그 조촐한 저녁식사 자리에서 무척 명랑했다. 식탁에는 단 세 사람뿐이었고, 프로스 양이 그 셋 중 하나였다. 그는 샤를이 그 자리에 없는 것을 아쉬워했고, 그를 떼어놓은 그 사랑스러운 작은 모의에 반쯤 넘게 항의할 마음이 들었으며, 그를 위해 다정하게 잔을 들었다.\n\n그렇게, 그가 루시에게 잘 자라고 인사할 시간이 되어, 두 사람은 헤어졌다. 그러나 새벽 세 시의 고요 속에서, 루시는 다시 아래층으로 내려와 그의 방으로 몰래 들어갔다. 미리부터 형체 없는 두려움에서 자유롭지 못한 채였다.\n\n그러나 모든 것이 제자리에 있었다. 모든 것이 고요했다. 그리고 그는 잠들어 누워 있었으니, 그의 백발이 평온한 베개 위에 그림처럼 놓여 있었고, 두 손은 이불 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었다. 그녀는 필요 없게 된 촛불을 멀찍이 그늘 속에 내려놓고, 그의 침대로 살금살금 다가가, 그의 입술에 자신의 입술을 갖다 댔다. 그러고는 그에게 몸을 숙여 그를 바라보았다.\n\n그의 잘생긴 얼굴에는 갇혀 지낸 세월의 쓰디쓴 물결이 닳아 새겨놓은 흔적이 있었다. 하지만 그는 그 자취를 어찌나 강한 결의로 덮어버렸던지, 잠들어 있는 와중에도 그것을 다스리고 있었다. 그날 밤, 보이지 않는 공격자와 벌이는 조용하고 단호하며 경계심 어린 그 싸움 속에서, 그보다 더 주목할 만한 얼굴은 잠이라는 광대한 영토 어디에서도 볼 수 없었다.\n\n그녀는 조심스레 그의 사랑하는 가슴에 손을 얹고, 자신의 사랑이 바라는 만큼, 그리고 그의 슬픔이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼, 그에게 영원히 진실하기를 기도했다. 그러고는 손을 거두고, 그의 입술에 다시 한 번 입맞춘 뒤, 떠나갔다. 그렇게 해돋이가 찾아왔고, 플라타너스 잎사귀의 그림자가 그의 얼굴 위에서, 그를 위해 기도하던 그녀의 입술이 움직이던 것만큼이나 부드럽게 일렁였다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII. Nine Days\n\n결혼식 날은 환하게 빛나고 있었고, 그들은 의사 선생의 방 닫힌 문 밖에서 준비를 마치고 있었다. 그 방에서 의사 선생은 찰스 다네이와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그들은 교회로 갈 채비가 되어 있었으니, 아름다운 신부와 로리 씨, 그리고 프로스 양이었다. 프로스 양으로 말하자면, 피할 수 없는 일을 점차 받아들이는 과정을 거치며 이 사건은 그녀에게 더할 나위 없는 기쁨이 되었을 터이나, 신랑이 마땅히 자기 오라비 솔로몬이었어야 한다는 미련이 아직도 가시지 않아 그렇지는 못했다.\n\n\"그러니까,\" 신부를 아무리 보아도 감탄이 가시지 않았던 로리 씨가 신부의 단정하고 어여쁜 옷차림을 구석구석 눈에 담으려 그 주위를 빙빙 돌면서 말했다. \"그러니까 이렇게 되려고, 나의 사랑스러운 루시, 내가 너를 그토록 어린 아기였을 적에 해협 건너로 데려왔던 게로구나! 맙소사! 내가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 어찌 그리 몰랐을까! 내 친구 찰스 군에게 베푸는 그 은혜를 어쩌면 그리 가볍게 여겼을까!\"\n\n\"일부러 그러신 게 아니잖아요,\" 현실적인 프로스 양이 한마디 했다. \"그러니 어떻게 아실 수 있었겠어요? 말도 안 되는 소리!\"\n\n\"그런가요? 그래도, 울지는 말아요,\" 점잖은 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"전 울지 않아요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"우는 건 어르신이죠.\"\n\n\"내가, 나의 프로스 양?\" (이 무렵 로리 씨는 때때로 그녀에게 농을 건넬 만큼 대담해져 있었다.)\n\n\"방금 우셨어요. 제 눈으로 봤는걸요. 그도 그럴 만하지요. 어르신이 저들에게 선물한 그 은식기 한 벌만 봐도 누구든 눈물이 날 만하다고요. 그 한 벌 가운데 포크 하나, 숟가락 하나 빠짐없이,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"어젯밤 상자가 도착한 뒤로 더는 안 보일 때까지 제가 울지 않은 게 없는걸요.\"\n\n\"참으로 흐뭇하구려,\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"허나 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 그 보잘것없는 기념품들을 누구의 눈에도 안 보이게 할 의도는 전혀 없었소. 이런! 이런 날이면 사내란 자신이 잃어버린 모든 것을 떠올리게 되는 법이지. 저런, 저런, 저런! 지난 오십년 가까운 세월 가운데 어느 때쯤이라도 로리 부인이라는 사람이 있었을지도 모른다고 생각하면 말이오!\"\n\n\"천만의 말씀!\" 프로스 양이 받았다.\n\n\"로리 부인이라는 사람이 도무지 있을 수 없었으리라 생각하시오?\" 그 이름의 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"흥!\" 프로스 양이 응수했다. \"어르신은 요람 속에서부터 독신자였는걸요.\"\n\n\"그렇구려!\" 로리 씨가 작은 가발을 흐뭇하게 매만지며 말했다. \"그것도 그럴듯하군.\"\n\n\"게다가 요람에 눕혀지기도 전에,\" 프로스 양이 계속했다. \"독신자로 점지되셨고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 내 생각엔,\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"나는 참으로 박하게 대접받은 셈이고, 내 본을 고르는 데에는 내 발언권도 있었어야 마땅하오. 그만하면 됐소! 자, 나의 사랑하는 루시,\" 그가 달래듯 한 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두르며 말했다. \"옆방에서 그들이 움직이는 소리가 들리는구나. 프로스 양과 나는 격식을 차리는 두 사무가로서, 네가 듣고 싶어 할 말을 전할 마지막 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않단다. 얘야, 너는 너의 훌륭한 아버님을 너 자신만큼이나 진심 어리고 다정한 손에 맡기고 떠나는 것이란다. 아버님은 생각할 수 있는 온갖 보살핌을 받으실 것이야. 네가 워릭셔며 그 인근에 머무는 앞으로 두 주 동안, 텔슨 은행마저도 (견주어 말하자면) 아버님 다음으로 밀려나게 될 게다. 그리고 두 주가 끝날 무렵 아버님이 너와 네 사랑하는 남편을 만나러 와서 웨일스로 떠나는 또 다른 두 주의 여행에 합류하실 때면, 우리가 더없이 좋은 건강과 더없이 행복한 마음으로 아버님을 네게 보내드렸다고 너는 말하게 될 게다. 자, 누군가 문으로 걸어오는 발소리가 들리는구나. 누군가 자기 사람을 데리러 오기 전에, 이 늙은 독신자가 옛 방식으로 축복하며 나의 사랑하는 아가씨에게 입 맞추게 해다오.\"\n\n그는 잠시 그 고운 얼굴을 자기 앞에 떼어 두고 익히 기억하는 이마의 표정을 들여다보았고, 이윽고 그 빛나는 금빛 머리칼을 자신의 작은 갈색 가발에 가져다 대었으니, 그 진실한 다정함과 섬세함은, 만일 그런 것들이 구식이라 한다면, 아담만큼이나 오래된 것이었다.\n\n의사 선생의 방문이 열리고, 그가 찰스 다네이와 함께 나왔다. 그는 너무도 새파랗게 질려 있어서 — 함께 들어갔을 때만 해도 그렇지 않았건만 — 그 얼굴에서는 핏기라곤 한 점도 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그러나 거동의 침착함만은 변함이 없었으니, 다만 로리 씨의 날카로운 눈에는 회피와 두려움이라는 옛 기색이 차가운 바람처럼 최근 그를 스치고 지나갔다는 어렴풋한 그림자가 어렴풋이 비쳤을 뿐이었다.\n\n그는 딸에게 팔을 내주고, 로리 씨가 이날을 기려 빌린 마차로 그녀를 데리고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 나머지 사람들은 다른 마차로 뒤따랐고, 머지않아 낯선 눈길이 지켜보는 이 없는 이웃 교회에서, 찰스 다네이와 루시 마네뜨는 행복하게 부부의 연을 맺었다.\n\n식이 끝났을 때 그 작은 무리의 미소 사이로 반짝인 눈물 말고도, 매우 밝고 반짝이는 다이아몬드 몇 알이 신부의 손에서 반짝였으니, 그것은 로리 씨의 호주머니 가운데 하나의 어두운 구석에서 막 풀려난 것이었다. 그들은 아침 식사를 하러 집으로 돌아왔고, 모든 일이 잘 풀렸으며, 이윽고 빠리의 다락방에서 가련한 구두장이의 흰 머리칼과 뒤섞였던 그 금빛 머리칼이, 헤어지는 문턱 위에서 아침 햇살을 받으며 다시 그 흰 머리칼과 뒤섞였다.\n\n비록 오래는 아니었으나 힘겨운 작별이었다. 그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 다독였고, 마침내 자신을 끌어안은 그녀의 팔에서 부드럽게 몸을 빼며 말했다. \"이 아이를 데려가게, 찰스! 이 아이는 자네 사람이야!\"\n\n그리고 떨리는 그녀의 손이 사륜마차의 창에서 그들을 향해 흔들렸고, 그녀는 떠나갔다.\n\n길모퉁이는 한가한 자들과 호기심 많은 자들의 발길에서 비켜나 있었고, 준비도 매우 단출하고 적었던 터라, 의사 선생과 로리 씨, 그리고 프로스 양만이 덩그러니 남았다. 그들이 서늘하고 오래된 현관의 반가운 그늘로 접어들었을 때, 로리 씨는 의사 선생에게 커다란 변화가 닥쳤음을 알아차렸다. 마치 그곳에 치켜올려진 금빛 팔이 그에게 독을 묻힌 일격을 가하기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n그는 본디 많은 것을 억눌러 왔던 터라, 억누를 일이 사라지고 나면 어떤 반동이 그에게 일어나리라 짐작할 만도 했다. 그러나 로리 씨를 불안케 한 것은 옛날의 그 겁먹고 길 잃은 듯한 표정이었다. 그리고 그들이 위층으로 올라갔을 때 멍하니 머리를 감싸 쥐고 음울하게 자기 방으로 걸어 들어가는 그 모습에서, 로리 씨는 포도주 가게 주인 드파르주와 별빛 아래의 마차길을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 그가 근심스레 곰곰이 생각한 끝에 프로스 양에게 속삭였다. \"내 생각엔 지금은 그에게 말을 걸지도, 그를 조금이라도 방해하지도 않는 게 상책일 듯하오. 나는 텔슨 은행에 잠깐 들러야 하니, 곧장 거기 다녀와서 이내 돌아오겠소. 그런 다음 우리가 그를 시골로 데리고 가 마차를 태우고 거기서 식사를 하면, 만사가 잘될 거요.\"\n\n로리 씨에게는 텔슨 은행에 잠깐 들르는 것보다 텔슨 은행에서 빠져나오는 것이 더 어려웠다. 그는 두 시간이나 발이 묶였다. 돌아왔을 때 그는 하인에게 아무것도 묻지 않은 채 홀로 오래된 계단을 올랐다. 그렇게 의사 선생의 방으로 들어가던 그는 나직이 두드리는 소리에 발걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 그가 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"저게 무슨 소리지?\"\n\n프로스 양이 겁에 질린 얼굴로 그의 귓전에 다가와 있었다. \"아이고, 어쩌나, 어쩌나! 다 틀렸어요!\" 그녀가 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다. \"이 일을 어떻게 우리 귀여운 아기씨에게 알리지요? 저를 알아보지도 못하고, 구두를 만들고 계세요!\"\n\n로리 씨는 그녀를 진정시키려 할 수 있는 말을 한 뒤, 몸소 의사 선생의 방으로 들어갔다. 작업대는 빛이 드는 쪽으로 돌려져 있었으니, 전에 그 구두장이가 일하는 모습을 보았을 때와 똑같았고, 그는 고개를 숙인 채 몹시 분주했다.\n\n\"마네뜨 선생. 나의 친애하는 벗, 마네뜨 선생!\"\n\n의사 선생은 잠시 그를 바라보았다 — 반은 캐묻는 듯, 반은 말을 걸어온 것에 노한 듯 — 그러고는 다시 일거리 위로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n그는 외투와 조끼를 벗어 두고 있었다. 셔츠는 그 일을 하던 시절 늘 그랬듯 목께가 풀려 있었고, 심지어 핼쑥하고 빛바랜 옛 얼굴빛마저 그에게 되돌아와 있었다. 그는 열심히 — 조바심을 내며 — 마치 어떤 의미로든 방해받았다는 듯이 일했다.\n\n로리 씨는 그의 손에 들린 일거리를 흘긋 보고는, 그것이 옛 치수와 모양의 구두임을 알아차렸다. 그는 그의 곁에 놓인 또 다른 구두를 집어 들고는 무엇이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"젊은 아가씨가 신을 산책용 구두지요.\" 그가 고개도 들지 않은 채 중얼거렸다. \"벌써 한참 전에 끝냈어야 하는 것을. 그냥 두시오.\"\n\n\"하지만, 마네뜨 선생. 날 좀 보시오!\"\n\n그는 일손을 멈추지 않은 채, 옛날의 그 기계적으로 순종하는 태도로 그 말에 따랐다.\n\n\"나를 알아보겠소, 나의 친애하는 벗? 다시 생각해보시오. 이건 당신에게 맞는 일이 아니오. 생각해보시오, 친애하는 벗!\"\n\n무엇으로도 그가 더는 말을 하게 할 수 없었다. 그는 그렇게 해달라는 청을 받으면 한 번에 한순간씩 고개를 들었으나, 어떤 설득으로도 그에게서 한마디조차 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그는 일하고, 또 일하고, 또 일했으니, 묵묵히 그러했고, 말들은 메아리 없는 벽에 떨어지듯, 혹은 허공에 떨어지듯 그에게 떨어졌다. 로리 씨가 찾아낼 수 있었던 단 하나의 희망의 빛은, 그가 이따금 청하지도 않았는데 슬그머니 고개를 든다는 것이었다. 그 안에는 호기심이나 당혹스러움의 희미한 기색이 어려 있는 듯했다 — 마치 마음속 어떤 의혹을 풀어보려 애쓰기라도 하는 듯이.\n\n두 가지가 한꺼번에 무엇보다도 중요한 일로 로리 씨의 머릿속에 새겨졌다. 첫째는 이 일을 루시에게 비밀로 해야 한다는 것이었고, 둘째는 그를 아는 모든 이에게 비밀로 해야 한다는 것이었다. 그는 프로스 양과 손을 맞잡고 후자의 방비를 위해 즉시 조치를 취하였으니, 의사 선생이 몸이 좋지 않아 며칠간 완전한 휴식이 필요하다고 둘러댔다. 그의 딸에게 행할 그 다정한 속임수를 거들기 위해, 프로스 양이 편지를 써서 그가 직업상 불려 갔다고 적고, 그가 직접 쓴 급히 갈겨쓴 두세 줄짜리 가상의 편지를 언급하기로 했으니, 그 편지는 같은 우편으로 그녀에게 부쳐진 것처럼 꾸며질 터였다.\n\n어떤 경우에든 취해 두는 편이 나을 이러한 조치들을, 로리 씨는 그가 제정신으로 돌아오기를 바라는 마음에서 취했다. 만일 그 일이 곧 일어난다면, 그는 따로 마련해 둔 또 다른 방책이 있었으니, 그것은 의사 선생의 증상에 관해 자신이 최선이라 여기는 어떤 의견을 구하는 것이었다.\n\n그가 회복되어 이 세 번째 방책에 의지하는 것이 가능해지기를 바라는 마음에서, 로리 씨는 가능한 한 그러는 티를 내지 않으면서 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보기로 결심했다. 그리하여 그는 평생 처음으로 텔슨 은행을 비우도록 일을 꾸며 두고, 같은 방의 창가에 자기 자리를 잡았다.\n\n그는 오래지 않아 그에게 말을 거는 것이 쓸데없을 뿐 아니라 오히려 해롭다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 다그치면 그가 안절부절못했기 때문이다. 그는 첫날에 그 시도를 단념하고, 그저 늘 그의 앞에 자신을 두기로만 마음먹었으니, 그가 빠져들었거나 빠져들고 있는 그 망상에 대한 말 없는 항의로서였다. 그리하여 그는 창가의 자기 자리에 머물며 책을 읽고 글을 썼고, 자신이 떠올릴 수 있는 한 갖가지 유쾌하고 자연스러운 방식으로, 이곳이 자유로운 자리임을 표현했다.\n\n마네뜨 선생은 차려준 먹을 것과 마실 것을 받아들고는, 첫날 내내 너무 어두워져 보이지 않을 때까지 계속 일했다 — 로리 씨가 무슨 수를 써도 읽거나 쓸 수 없게 된 뒤로도 반 시간을 더 일했다. 그가 연장들을 아침까지 쓸모없다며 옆으로 치워 놓자, 로리 씨가 일어나 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"밖에 나가시겠소?\"\n\n그는 옛날 방식 그대로 자기 양옆의 바닥을 내려다보고, 옛날 방식 그대로 고개를 들고는, 옛날의 그 나직한 목소리로 되뇌었다.\n\n\"밖에?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 나와 함께 산책하러요. 어떻소?\"\n\n그는 어떻소라고 하는지 말하려는 노력조차 하지 않았고, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 로리 씨는 어스름 속에서 그가 작업대 위로 몸을 기울여 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 얹고 두 손으로 머리를 감싼 채, 어떤 어렴풋한 방식으로 \"어떻소?\" 하고 스스로에게 묻고 있다고 본 듯했다. 사무가의 명민함이 여기서 한 가닥 가망을 알아챘고, 그것을 붙잡아 두기로 마음먹었다.\n\n프로스 양과 그는 밤을 두 차례의 당번으로 나누어, 옆방에서 틈틈이 그를 지켜보았다. 그는 자리에 눕기 전 오랫동안 이리저리 거닐었으나, 마침내 자리에 눕고 나서는 잠이 들었다. 아침이면 그는 일찍 일어나 곧장 작업대로, 일거리로 갔다.\n\n이 둘째 날에 로리 씨는 그의 이름을 부르며 밝게 인사했고, 근래 그들에게 익숙해진 화제들로 그에게 말을 걸었다. 그는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 하는 말을 듣고 있고, 비록 혼란스럽게나마 그것을 곱씹고 있음은 분명했다. 이에 힘을 얻은 로리 씨는 그날 낮 동안 여러 차례 프로스 양을 일감과 함께 안으로 불러들였다. 그럴 때면 그들은 루시에 관해, 그리고 그 자리에 있는 그녀의 아버지에 관해, 꼭 평소와 다름없는 방식으로, 마치 아무 탈도 없다는 듯이 조용히 이야기를 나누었다. 이는 어떤 요란한 곁들임도 없이 행해졌고, 그를 괴롭힐 만큼 길지도 잦지도 않았다. 그가 더 자주 고개를 드는 듯하고, 자신을 둘러싼 어떤 어긋남을 알아챈 기색에 흔들리는 듯해 보인다고 믿게 되니, 로리 씨의 다정한 마음이 한결 가벼워졌다.\n\n다시 어둠이 내리자, 로리 씨는 전처럼 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"친애하는 선생, 밖에 나가시겠소?\"\n\n전처럼 그가 되뇌었다. \"밖에?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 나와 함께 산책하러요. 어떻소?\"\n\n이번에 로리 씨는 그에게서 아무 대답도 끌어낼 수 없자 밖에 나가는 척하고는, 한 시간 동안 자리를 비웠다가 돌아왔다. 그사이 의사 선생은 창가 자리로 옮겨 앉아 플라타너스 나무를 내려다보고 있었으나, 로리 씨가 돌아오자 슬그머니 자기 작업대로 자리를 옮겼다.\n\n시간은 더디게 흘러갔고, 로리 씨의 희망은 어두워졌으며, 그의 마음은 다시 무거워졌고, 날마다 더욱더 무거워졌다. 셋째 날이 오고 갔으며, 넷째 날, 다섯째 날도 그러했다. 닷새, 엿새, 이레, 여드레, 아흐레.\n\n갈수록 어두워지는 희망과 늘 더욱더 무거워지는 마음을 안고, 로리 씨는 이 근심스러운 시간을 견뎌냈다. 비밀은 잘 지켜졌고, 루시는 아무것도 모른 채 행복했다. 그러나 그는, 처음에는 손이 다소 서툴렀던 그 구두장이가 무섭도록 능숙해져 가고 있음을, 그리고 아흐렛날 저녁의 어스름 속에서만큼 그가 일에 골몰한 적도, 그 손이 그토록 날래고 능란했던 적도 없었음을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX. An Opinion\n\n불안한 간호로 지친 나머지, 로리 씨는 자기 자리에서 잠이 들고 말았다. 마음 졸이며 보낸 열흘째 되는 아침, 그는 깊은 밤 짙은 잠에 빠졌던 그 방으로 햇살이 비쳐드는 바람에 깜짝 놀라 깨어났다.\n\n그는 눈을 비비고 정신을 차렸으나, 그러고 나서도 자신이 여전히 잠들어 있는 게 아닌지 의심스러웠다. 박사의 방문으로 가서 안을 들여다보니, 제화공의 작업대와 연장이 다시 한쪽으로 치워져 있고, 박사 본인은 창가에 앉아 책을 읽고 있었던 것이다. 그는 평소의 아침 차림이었고, 그 얼굴은(로리 씨가 또렷이 볼 수 있었다) 여전히 몹시 창백하긴 했으나 차분하게 무언가에 몰두한 채 정신을 집중하고 있었다.\n\n자신이 깨어 있음을 확인하고 나서도, 로리 씨는 몇 순간 동안 지난번의 제화일이 그저 자신의 어지러운 꿈은 아니었나 하는 아찔한 불확실함에 휩싸였다. 눈앞에 보이는 그의 친구가 익숙한 옷차림과 모습으로, 여느 때처럼 일에 몰두해 있지 않은가. 그토록 강렬한 인상으로 남은 그 변화가 실제로 일어났다는 어떤 흔적이 시야 안에 있단 말인가.\n\n그러나 그것은 처음의 혼란과 경악에서 나온 물음일 뿐, 답은 명백했다. 만약 그 인상이 실재하는, 그에 상응하고 충분한 원인에서 나온 것이 아니라면, 어떻게 자비스 로리 자신이 그곳에 있단 말인가. 어떻게 옷을 입은 채로 머네뜨 박사의 진찰실 소파에서 잠이 들었으며, 이른 아침에 박사의 침실 문 밖에서 이런 점들을 따지고 있단 말인가.\n\n몇 분도 지나지 않아, 프로스 양이 그의 곁에 서서 속삭이고 있었다. 만약 그에게 한 점의 의심이라도 남아 있었다면 그녀의 말이 어김없이 그것을 풀어주었겠지만, 그때쯤 그는 머리가 맑았고 의심이라곤 없었다. 그는 평소의 아침식사 시간이 될 때까지 그냥 시간을 흘려보낸 뒤, 아무런 별일도 없었다는 듯이 박사를 대하자고 제안했다. 만약 그가 평소의 정신 상태로 보인다면, 로리 씨는 그제야 조심스럽게, 그가 그토록 애타게 얻고 싶어했던 의견으로부터 방향과 인도를 구해보려는 것이었다.\n\n프로스 양이 그의 판단에 따르기로 하면서, 그 계획은 신중하게 실행되었다. 평소의 꼼꼼한 몸단장을 할 시간이 넉넉했으므로, 로리 씨는 아침식사 시간에 여느 때처럼 흰 리넨 차림에 여느 때처럼 단정한 종아리를 하고서 나타났다. 박사는 평소처럼 불려나와 아침식사 자리로 왔다.\n\n로리 씨가 유일하게 안전한 접근이라고 여긴 그 미묘하고 점진적인 접근의 선을 넘지 않으면서 그를 헤아릴 수 있는 한에서 보자면, 박사는 처음에 자기 딸의 결혼이 어제 있었던 것으로 알고 있는 듯했다. 일부러 슬쩍 던진, 무슨 요일이고 며칠인가에 대한 우연한 언급에 그는 생각하고 헤아리기 시작했고, 분명히 불안해했다. 그러나 그 밖의 모든 점에서는 그토록 차분하게 제정신이었으므로, 로리 씨는 그가 구하던 도움을 받기로 결심했다. 그리고 그 도움이란 곧 박사 자신이었다.\n\n그리하여 아침식사가 끝나 식탁이 치워지고 그와 박사 둘만 남게 되자, 로리 씨는 진심을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"머네뜨 박사, 내가 깊이 마음 쓰고 있는 아주 묘한 사례에 대해 자네의 의견을 은밀히 듣고 싶네. 그러니까, 나에게는 아주 묘한 사례인데, 자네의 더 나은 식견으로 보면 덜 그럴지도 모르지.\"\n\n지난번 일로 변색된 자기 두 손을 흘끗 보면서, 박사는 괴로운 표정을 짓더니 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. 그는 이미 한 번 넘게 자기 손을 흘끗거린 터였다.\n\n\"머네뜨 박사.\" 로리 씨가 다정하게 그의 팔을 어루만지며 말했다. \"이 사례는 특별히 소중한 내 친구의 사례라네. 부디 마음을 기울여, 그를 위해—그리고 무엇보다도 그의 딸을 위해—그의 딸을 위해서 좋은 조언을 해주게, 친애하는 머네뜨.\"\n\n\"내가 제대로 이해했다면,\" 박사가 낮게 가라앉은 어조로 말했다. \"어떤 정신적 충격을—?\"\n\"그렇다네!\"\n\n\"분명하게 말해주게.\" 박사가 말했다. \"어떤 세부도 빼놓지 말고.\"\n\n로리 씨는 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음을 알아차리고 말을 이어갔다.\n\n\"친애하는 머네뜨, 이것은 오래되고 오래 지속된 충격의 사례라네. 애정과 감정, 그리고—그—자네 표현대로—그 정신에 대단히 날카롭고 혹독했던 충격이지. 그 정신 말일세. 그 충격에 짓눌려 시달린 사례인데, 얼마나 오래였는지는 말할 수 없네. 그 본인도 그 시간을 헤아릴 수 없으리라고 보며, 달리 알아낼 방도도 없기 때문이지. 그것은 그 사람 자신도 그 과정을 더듬어볼 수 없는 식으로 그 충격에서 회복한 사례라네—언젠가 그가 사람들 앞에서 인상적으로 그 이야기를 하는 것을 내가 들은 적이 있지. 그것은 그가 너무도 완전히 회복하여, 정신을 면밀히 집중하고 몸을 크게 쓸 줄 알며, 이미 매우 방대했던 자기 지식의 곳간에 끊임없이 새로운 것을 보태나가는, 대단히 명민한 사람이 된 그런 사례라네. 그런데, 불행히도,\" 그는 말을 멈추고 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다. \"가벼운 재발이 있었다네.\"\n\n박사가 낮은 목소리로 물었다. \"얼마나 오래 지속되었나?\"\n\n\"아흐레 밤낮일세.\"\n\n\"어떤 식으로 나타났나? 짐작건대,\" 다시 자기 손을 흘끗 보며, \"그 충격과 관련된 어떤 옛 일거리를 다시 시작한 것이겠지?\"\n\n\"바로 그렇다네.\"\n\n\"그런데, 자네는 그가,\" 박사가 또렷하고 침착하게, 그러나 여전히 낮은 목소리로 물었다. \"처음에 그 일에 매달려 있는 것을 본 적이 있나?\"\n\n\"한 번 있네.\"\n\"그리고 재발이 그를 덮쳤을 때, 그는 대부분의 점에서—아니, 모든 점에서—그때와 같았나?\"\n\n\"모든 점에서 그랬다고 보네.\"\n\n\"자네가 그의 딸 이야기를 했지. 그 딸은 재발에 대해 아나?\"\n\n\"아닐세. 그녀에게는 숨겨왔고, 앞으로도 늘 숨길 수 있기를 바라네. 오직 나 자신과, 믿을 만한 또 한 사람만이 알고 있지.\"\n\n박사는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고 중얼거렸다. \"참으로 친절한 일이군. 참으로 사려 깊은 일이야!\" 로리 씨도 그 손을 마주 쥐었고, 두 사람은 잠시 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"그런데, 친애하는 머네뜨,\" 마침내 로리 씨가 더없이 신중하고 더없이 다정한 태도로 말했다. \"나는 그저 사무를 보는 사람일 뿐이라, 이렇게 복잡하고 어려운 일에 맞설 만한 위인이 못 되네. 나는 그런 데 필요한 종류의 지식도 없고, 그런 종류의 분별력도 없어서, 인도가 필요하다네. 이 세상에 올바른 인도를 받기 위해 자네만큼 의지할 수 있는 사람은 없네. 말해주게, 이 재발은 어떻게 일어나는 것인가? 또다시 일어날 위험이 있는가? 재발을 막을 수 있는가? 재발은 어떻게 다루어야 하는가? 도대체 어떻게 일어나는 것인가? 내 친구를 위해 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠나? 친구를 돕고자 하는 마음으로 따지자면, 만약 그 방법을 알기만 한다면 나만큼 진심으로 내 친구를 돕고 싶어한 사람은 일찍이 없었을 걸세.\n\n\"하지만 이런 사례에서 어디서부터 시작해야 할지를 모르겠네. 자네의 명민함과 지식과 경험이 나를 올바른 길로 들여놓아준다면, 나는 많은 것을 할 수 있을지도 모르네. 깨우침도 인도도 없으니 내가 할 수 있는 것이 너무도 적다네. 부디 나와 함께 이 일을 의논해주게. 부디 내가 이것을 조금 더 분명히 볼 수 있게 해주고, 내가 조금이라도 더 쓸모 있게 되는 법을 가르쳐주게.\"\n\n이 간곡한 말이 끝나고 머네뜨 박사는 앉은 채로 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼고, 로리 씨는 그를 재촉하지 않았다.\n\n\"내 생각에는 아마,\" 박사가 애써 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"친애하는 친구여, 자네가 묘사한 그 재발이 그 당사자에게 전혀 예견되지 않았던 것은 아닌 듯하네.\"\n\n\"그가 그것을 두려워했단 말인가?\" 로리 씨가 조심스레 물었다.\n\n\"무척이나.\" 그는 자기도 모르게 몸서리치며 그렇게 말했다. \"그런 두려움이 시달리는 사람의 마음을 얼마나 짓누르는지, 그리고 자기를 짓누르는 그 화제에 대해 한마디라도 억지로 입 밖에 내는 것이 그에게 얼마나 어려운—얼마나 거의 불가능한—일인지 자네는 짐작도 못 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그가,\" 로리 씨가 물었다. \"그 은밀한 번민이 자신을 사로잡고 있을 때, 누군가에게 그것을 털어놓도록 스스로를 다잡을 수만 있다면 눈에 띄게 가벼워질 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"그럴 걸세. 하지만 그것은, 내가 말했듯이, 거의 불가능한 일이라네. 나는 심지어 그것이—어떤 경우에는—전혀 불가능하다고 믿네.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 양쪽이 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 로리 씨가 다시 부드럽게 박사의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"이번 발작의 원인을 자네는 무엇으로 보겠나?\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 머네뜨 박사가 대답했다. \"그 병의 첫번째 원인이었던 일련의 생각과 기억이 강렬하고도 예사롭지 않게 되살아난 것이라네. 더없이 괴로운 종류의 어떤 강렬한 연상들이 생생하게 되살아난 것이라고 보네. 아마 그의 마음속에는 그런 연상들이 되살아나리라는 두려움이 오랫동안—이를테면 어떤 상황에서, 이를테면 어떤 특정한 순간에—도사리고 있었을 걸세. 그는 헛되이 마음의 준비를 하려 애썼고, 어쩌면 그렇게 대비하려는 노력 자체가 그것을 견뎌낼 힘을 더 약하게 만들었는지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그가 재발 중에 일어난 일을 기억할까?\" 로리 씨가 자연스레 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n박사는 황량한 눈빛으로 방 안을 둘러보고, 고개를 저으며 낮은 목소리로 답했다. \"전혀 못 하네.\"\n\n\"그럼, 앞날에 대해서는,\" 로리 씨가 넌지시 운을 뗐다.\n\n\"앞날에 대해서라면,\" 박사가 단호함을 되찾으며 말했다. \"나는 크게 희망을 품겠네. 하늘이 자비를 베푸시어 그를 이토록 빨리 회복시켜주셨으니, 나는 크게 희망을 품겠네. 오랫동안 두려워하고 오랫동안 어렴풋이 예견하며 맞서 싸워온 어떤 복잡한 것의 압박에 굴복했다가, 그 먹구름이 터지고 지나간 뒤 회복한 것이니, 나는 최악의 고비는 지났기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"그렇군, 그래! 그것 참 다행한 위안일세. 고맙네!\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"감사한 일이지!\" 박사가 경건하게 고개를 숙이며 따라 말했다.\n\n\"두 가지 더 짚고 싶은 점이 있네.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"그 점에 대해 자네의 가르침을 꼭 받고 싶은데. 계속해도 되겠나?\"\n\n\"자네 친구에게 그보다 더 좋은 도움을 줄 수는 없을 걸세.\" 박사가 그에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"그럼 첫번째 점일세. 그는 공부하는 습성이 있고, 유달리 정력적이라네. 그는 대단한 열정으로 직업적 지식을 쌓고, 실험을 수행하고, 여러 일에 매달리지. 그런데, 그가 너무 무리하는 것은 아닐까?\"\n\n\"그렇지는 않다고 보네. 늘 유난히 일거리를 필요로 하는 것이 그 사람 정신의 본성일지도 모르지. 그것은 일부는 타고난 것이고, 일부는 시련의 결과일 걸세. 건전한 일에 덜 매달릴수록, 불건전한 방향으로 돌아설 위험이 더 커질 테지. 어쩌면 그 자신이 스스로를 관찰해서 그 사실을 알아냈는지도 모르네.\"\n\n\"그가 너무 큰 부담을 받고 있는 것이 아니라고 확신하나?\"\n\n\"꽤 확신한다고 보네.\"\n\n\"친애하는 머네뜨, 만약 그가 지금 과로하고 있다면—\"\n\n\"친애하는 로리, 그런 일은 쉽게 일어나기 어려우리라고 보네. 한 방향으로 격렬한 압박이 있었으니, 거기에는 평형추가 필요하다네.\"\n\n\"끈질긴 사무가의 입장에서 미안하네만. 잠시 그가 정말로 과로하고 있다고 가정해보세. 그러면 이 장애가 어떤 식으로든 다시 나타나는 형태로 드러날까?\"\n\n\"그렇지는 않다고 보네. 나는,\" 머네뜨 박사가 스스로 확신하는 단호함으로 말했다. \"오직 그 하나의 연상의 흐름 말고는 어떤 것도 그것을 다시 일으키지 못하리라고 보네. 이제부터는 그 현(絃)을 예사롭지 않게 뒤흔드는 무언가가 아니고서는 그것을 되살리지 못하리라고 보네. 일어났던 일과 그의 회복 이후로는, 그 줄이 다시 그토록 격렬하게 울리는 일은 상상하기 어렵다네. 나는 그것을 되살릴 만한 상황들이 다 소진되었다고 믿으며, 거의 그렇게 확신한다네.\"\n\n그는 정신이라는 섬세한 조직을 무너뜨리는 데 얼마나 하찮은 것으로도 충분한지를 아는 사람의 조심스러움으로, 그러면서도 몸소 겪은 인내와 고통 속에서 천천히 확신을 얻어낸 사람의 자신감으로 말했다. 그 친구로서는 그 자신감을 꺾을 일이 아니었다. 로리 씨는 실제보다 더 안심하고 더 고무된 척하면서, 두번째이자 마지막 점으로 다가갔다. 그는 그것이 무엇보다 가장 어려운 문제임을 느꼈으나, 프로스 양과 나누었던 지난 일요일 아침의 대화를 떠올리고, 지난 아흐레 동안 자신이 본 것을 떠올리며, 그것을 정면으로 마주해야 한다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"이번에 그토록 다행히 회복된 일시적 시련의 영향으로 다시 시작된 그 일을,\" 로리 씨가 목을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"우리 대장간 일이라고 부르세, 대장간 일이라고. 비유 삼아 한 가지 경우를 들자면, 그가 좋지 않던 시절에 자그마한 화덕에서 일하는 데 익숙했다고 해보세. 그가 뜻밖에도 그 화덕 앞에 다시 있는 것이 발견되었다고 해보지. 그가 그것을 곁에 두는 것은 안타까운 일이 아닐까?\"\n\n박사는 손으로 이마를 가리고, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 두드렸다.\n\n\"그는 늘 그것을 곁에 두어왔네.\" 로리 씨가 친구를 불안하게 바라보며 말했다. \"그런데, 그것을 치워버리는 편이 더 낫지 않겠나?\"\n\n여전히 박사는 이마를 가린 채로, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 두드렸다.\n\n\"내게 조언하는 것이 쉽지 않은 모양이군?\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이것이 까다로운 문제임을 나도 충분히 알겠네. 그래도 내 생각에는—\" 그러더니 그는 고개를 젓고 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"보게나,\" 머네뜨 박사가 거북한 침묵 끝에 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"이 가엾은 사람의 마음 가장 깊은 곳의 움직임을 앞뒤가 맞게 설명하기란 매우 어렵다네. 그는 한때 그 일거리를 그토록 무섭게 갈망했고, 그것이 찾아왔을 때 그토록 반가워했지. 의심할 바 없이 그것은 머릿속의 혼란을 손가락의 혼란으로 대신하게 하고, 또 그가 점점 익숙해짐에 따라 정신적 고문의 교묘함을 손의 교묘함으로 대신하게 함으로써, 그의 고통을 그토록 덜어주었던 것이지. 그래서 그는 그것을 자기 손 닿지 않는 곳에 아주 치워버린다는 생각을 결코 견뎌내지 못했네. 그가 그 어느 때보다 자신에 대해 더 희망을 품고 있고, 심지어 일종의 자신감을 갖고 스스로를 이야기하기까지 한다고 내가 믿는 지금에도, 그 옛 일거리가 필요해질지 모르는데 그것을 찾지 못할지도 모른다는 생각은 그에게 별안간 공포감을 안겨준다네. 길 잃은 아이의 가슴에 사무친다고 상상해봄 직한 그런 공포 말일세.\"\n\n그가 로리 씨의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그 모습은 자신이 든 그 비유 그대로였다.\n\n\"하지만—잘 듣게! 나는 기니화와 실링화와 지폐 같은 그런 물질적인 것만 다루는, 한 우직한 사무가로서 알고 싶어 묻는 것이네만—그 물건을 간직하는 것이 곧 그 생각을 간직하는 것을 수반하지는 않겠나? 그 물건이 사라진다면, 친애하는 머네뜨, 그 두려움도 함께 사라지지 않겠나? 요컨대, 그 화덕을 간직한다는 것은 그 불안에 굴복하는 것이 아니겠나?\"\n\n또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"게다가 자네도 알다시피,\" 박사가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그것은 그토록 오래된 동무라네.\"\n\n\"나라면 그것을 간직하지 않겠네.\" 로리 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. 박사가 불안해하는 것을 보면서 그는 오히려 단호해졌던 것이다. \"나라면 그것을 희생시키라고 권하겠네. 나는 다만 자네의 승인을 바랄 뿐이야. 그것이 아무 도움도 안 된다고 나는 확신하네. 자, 어서! 마음씨 고운 사람답게 내게 승인을 주게. 그의 딸을 위해서 말일세, 친애하는 머네뜨!\"\n\n그의 내면에 얼마나 큰 갈등이 일고 있었는지를 보는 것은 참으로 묘한 일이었다!\n\n\"그렇다면 그녀의 이름으로 그렇게 하게나. 내가 그것을 승인하네. 다만, 그가 있을 때 그것을 가져가지는 않겠네. 그가 없을 때 치워주게. 한동안 자리를 비웠다 돌아와서야 그 옛 동무가 없어진 것을 그가 알아채도록 하게.\"\n\n로리 씨는 기꺼이 그렇게 하기로 약속했고, 그것으로 의논은 끝났다. 그들은 그날 하루를 시골에서 보냈고, 박사는 완전히 회복되었다. 이어진 사흘 동안 그는 더없이 건강했고, 열나흘째 되는 날 루씨와 그녀의 남편에게 가기 위해 길을 떠났다. 그의 침묵을 둘러대기 위해 취해둔 조치에 대해서는 로리 씨가 미리 그에게 설명해두었고, 그는 그에 맞추어 루씨에게 편지를 써두었으므로, 그녀는 아무런 의심도 하지 않았다.\n\n그가 집을 떠난 날 밤, 로리 씨는 도끼와 톱과 끌과 망치를 들고 그의 방으로 들어갔고, 프로스 양이 등불을 들고 함께 했다. 거기서 문을 닫아건 채로, 은밀하고 죄지은 듯한 태도로, 로리 씨는 제화공의 작업대를 산산조각으로 쪼개버렸고, 그동안 프로스 양은 마치 살인을 거드는 사람처럼 촛불을 들고 있었다—실로 그 험상궂은 모습으로 보아 그녀는 그 일에 어울리지 않는 인물도 아니었다. (그 목적에 알맞게 미리 토막 낸) 그 몸뚱이를 태우는 일은 지체 없이 부엌 아궁이에서 시작되었고, 연장과 신발과 가죽은 정원에 묻었다. 정직한 마음에는 파괴와 은폐가 어찌나 사악해 보이는지, 로리 씨와 프로스 양은 그 일을 저지르고 그 흔적을 없애는 동안 거의, 어떤 끔찍한 범죄의 공범처럼 느꼈고 또 그렇게 보이기까지 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XX. A Plea\n\n신혼부부가 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 축하 인사를 건네려고 가장 먼저 나타난 사람은 시드니 카턴이었다. 부부가 집에 돌아온 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 그가 모습을 드러냈다. 그는 습관에서도, 외모에서도, 태도에서도 나아진 데가 없었다. 그러나 그에게는 어딘가 거칠지만 진실한 분위기가 감돌았는데, 그것은 찰스 다네이의 관찰에는 새로운 것이었다.\n\n그는 다네이를 창가로 따로 데려가, 아무도 엿듣지 않을 때 그에게 말할 기회를 엿보았다.\n\n\"다네이 씨,\" 카턴이 말했다. \"우리가 친구가 될 수 있으면 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"우리는 이미 친구가 아닙니까, 그러길 바랍니다만.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해주시니 고맙습니다, 인사치레로 하는 말이라도. 하지만 저는 인사치레로 하는 말이 아닙니다. 사실, 우리가 친구가 될 수 있으면 좋겠다고 말할 때도, 저는 그저 그것만을 뜻하는 것도 아닙니다.\"\n\n찰스 다네이는 — 당연한 일이지만 — 더없이 명랑하고 우애로운 태도로, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"맹세코,\" 카턴이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그게 제 마음속에서 이해하기는 쉬워도 당신께 전하기는 더 어렵군요. 그래도, 한번 해보지요. 제가 — 평소보다도 — 더 취해 있던 어떤 유명한 일이 있었던 걸 기억하십니까?\"\n\n\"당신이 술을 마셨다는 걸 제게 인정하라고 강요했던 어떤 유명한 일이 있었던 건 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그걸 기억합니다. 그런 일들의 저주가 제게 무겁게 얹혀 있지요, 저는 늘 그것들을 기억하니까요. 언젠가, 제게 모든 날이 끝나는 날, 그것이 참작되기를 바랍니다! 놀라지 마세요, 설교를 늘어놓으려는 건 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"전혀 놀라지 않습니다. 당신의 진지함이라면, 제게는 결코 놀랄 일이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 카턴은 그것을 떨쳐버리려는 듯 손을 무심하게 내저으며 말했다. \"문제의 그 취했던 일에서 (아시다시피 수많은 일들 가운데 하나지요), 저는 당신을 좋아한다느니 좋아하지 않는다느니 하면서 참을 수 없을 정도로 굴었습니다. 그걸 잊어주셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"저는 오래전에 잊었습니다.\"\n\n\"또 인사치레로군요! 하지만 다네이 씨, 망각이란 당신이 말씀하시는 만큼 제게는 쉽지가 않습니다. 저는 결코 그걸 잊지 못했고, 가벼운 대답은 제가 그걸 잊는 데 도움이 되지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 가벼운 대답이었다면,\" 다네이가 대답했다. \"그 점 용서를 빕니다. 저는 그저, 놀랍게도 당신을 너무 괴롭히는 듯한 사소한 일을 한쪽으로 치워버리려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었습니다. 신사의 신의를 걸고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저는 오래전에 그걸 마음에서 떨쳐버렸습니다. 맙소사, 떨쳐버릴 게 뭐가 있었겠습니까! 그날 당신이 제게 베풀어준 그 큰 도움에서, 제가 기억해야 할 더 중요한 것이 없었겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그 큰 도움이라는 것에 관해서라면,\" 카턴이 말했다. \"당신이 그런 식으로 말씀하시니, 솔직히 털어놓아야겠는데, 그건 한낱 직업적인 허풍이었을 뿐입니다. 그걸 베풀었을 때, 제가 당신이 어떻게 되든 신경을 썼는지조차 모르겠습니다. — 잘 들으세요! 베풀었을 때라고 말씀드린 겁니다, 저는 과거를 이야기하고 있는 거예요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그 은혜를 가볍게 여기시는군요,\" 다네이가 대답했다. \"하지만 당신의 가벼운 대답을 두고 다투지는 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"진실한 진심입니다, 다네이 씨, 저를 믿어주세요! 제가 본론에서 벗어났군요. 저는 우리가 친구가 되는 것에 대해 말하고 있었습니다. 자, 당신은 저를 아십니다. 제가 사람들이 도달하는 더 높고 더 나은 경지에는 도무지 이를 수 없는 자라는 걸 아시지요. 의심스러우시면 스트라이버에게 물어보세요, 그가 그렇다고 말해줄 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저는 그의 도움 없이 제 나름의 견해를 세우는 편이 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"좋습니다! 어쨌든 당신은 저를 방탕한 개 같은 놈으로, 좋은 일이라고는 한 적도 없고 앞으로도 결코 하지 않을 자로 알고 계시지요.\"\n\n\"당신이 '앞으로도 결코 하지 않을' 거라는 건 모르겠는데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 압니다, 제 말을 믿으셔야 합니다. 좋습니다! 만약 이렇게 쓸모없는 자, 이렇게 변변찮은 평판을 지닌 자가 이따금 드나드는 걸 견딜 수 있으시다면, 저는 이곳에 특권을 가진 사람으로 드나드는 것을 허락받기를 청하고 싶습니다. 옛 봉사를 봐서 너그러이 봐주는, 누구도 눈여겨보지 않는, 쓸모없는 (그리고 당신과 저 사이에서 제가 발견한 닮은 점이 아니었다면, 볼품없는, 이라고 덧붙이고 싶군요) 가구 한 점쯤으로 여겨지기를 청합니다. 제가 그 허락을 남용할 것 같지는 않습니다. 일년에 네 번이라도 그걸 이용한다면 그건 백에 하나 있을까 말까 한 일이지요. 단언컨대, 그저 그런 허락을 받았다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 저는 만족할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"한번 해보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 제가 말씀드린 그런 입장에 놓였다는 걸 달리 표현하는 말씀이군요. 고맙습니다, 다네이. 당신의 이름을 그렇게 격의 없이 불러도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이제는 그래도 될 것 같습니다, 카턴.\"\n\n그들은 그 일을 두고 악수를 나누었고, 시드니는 돌아섰다. 그러고는 일분도 채 지나지 않아, 겉으로 보기에 그는 여느 때처럼 실속 없는 사람으로 되돌아가 있었다.\n\n그가 떠난 뒤, 프로스 양과 박사와 로리 씨와 함께 보낸 저녁 동안, 찰스 다네이는 이 대화를 대략적으로 언급하면서, 시드니 카턴을 무신경과 무모함의 문제로 이야기했다. 요컨대 그는 카턴을 두고 신랄하게 말하거나 그를 모질게 깎아내리려는 것이 아니라, 그가 스스로 드러내 보이는 모습 그대로 그를 본 사람이라면 누구라도 했을 법한 식으로 말했다.\n\n그는 이것이 어여쁘고 젊은 아내의 생각에 머물러 있으리라고는 미처 생각지 못했다. 그러나 나중에 그가 자기네 방에서 아내와 합류했을 때, 그는 예의 그 어여쁘게 이마를 치켜올리는 표정을 뚜렷이 지은 채 자신을 기다리는 그녀를 보았다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 우리는 생각이 많은가 보군!\" 다네이가 그녀에게 팔을 두르며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 사랑하는 찰스,\" 그의 가슴에 두 손을 얹고 묻는 듯 골똘한 표정을 그에게 고정한 채 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 우리는 좀 생각이 많아요, 오늘 밤 우리 마음에 무언가 걸리는 게 있거든요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요, 나의 루시?\"\n\n\"제가 묻지 말아달라고 청하면, 그 한 가지 질문을 제게 강요하지 않겠다고 약속해주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"약속하겠냐고? 내 사랑에게 무엇인들 약속하지 못하겠소?\"\n\n정말이지, 한 손으로는 그녀의 뺨에서 금빛 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 다른 한 손은 자신을 위해 뛰는 그 가슴에 댄 채로 무엇인들!\n\n\"제 생각엔 말이에요, 찰스, 가엾은 카턴 씨는 당신이 오늘 밤 그에 대해 표현한 것보다 더 많은 배려와 존중을 받을 만한 분이에요.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇소, 내 사랑? 어째서 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 제게 물으시면 안 되는 거예요. 하지만 저는 생각해요 — 알아요 — 그분이 그렇다는 걸.\"\n\n\"당신이 안다면, 그걸로 충분하오. 내가 어떻게 하길 바라오, 내 생명?\"\n\n\"청하고 싶어요, 여보, 늘 그분에게 아주 너그럽게 대하시고, 그분이 곁에 없을 때 그분의 잘못을 아주 관대하게 봐주시기를요. 그분에게는 아주, 아주 드물게만 드러내는 마음이 있고, 그 마음에 깊은 상처가 있다는 걸 믿어주시기를 청하고 싶어요. 여보, 저는 그것이 피 흘리는 것을 보았어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그에게 무슨 잘못을 했을지도 모른다는 건,\" 찰스 다네이가 몹시 놀라며 말했다. \"내게는 고통스러운 생각이오. 나는 그에 대해 그런 생각은 한 번도 해본 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"여보, 정말 그래요. 저는 그분이 구제될 수 없을까 봐 두려워요. 이제 그분의 성품이나 처지에서 무언가 바로잡힐 수 있으리라는 희망은 거의 없어요. 하지만 저는 그분이 선한 일, 다정한 일, 심지어 고결한 일까지도 해낼 수 있는 분이라고 확신해요.\"\n\n이 길 잃은 남자에 대한 자신의 믿음이 지닌 순결함 속에서 그녀가 어찌나 아름다워 보였던지, 그녀의 남편은 그 모습 그대로의 그녀를 몇 시간이라도 바라볼 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n\"그리고, 오 사랑하는 나의 임이여!\" 그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 매달리며, 그의 가슴에 머리를 얹고, 두 눈을 들어 그를 올려다보며 간청했다. \"우리가 우리의 행복 속에서 얼마나 강한지, 그리고 그분이 그분의 불행 속에서 얼마나 약한지를 기억해주세요!\"\n\n그 간청이 그의 마음 깊이 와닿았다. \"언제나 기억하리다, 사랑하는 그대여! 살아 있는 동안 내내 기억하리다.\"\n\n그는 금빛 머리 위로 몸을 굽혀, 장밋빛 입술에 자신의 입술을 대고, 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸안았다. 만약 그때 어두운 거리를 거닐던 어느 쓸쓸한 방랑자가 그녀의 순결한 고백을 들을 수 있었더라면, 그리고 그 남편을 그토록 사랑하는 부드러운 푸른 눈에서 남편이 입맞춤으로 닦아내는 연민의 눈물방울을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 밤을 향해 외쳤을지도 모른다 — 그리고 그 말이 그의 입술에서 떠나는 것은 처음이 아니었으리라 —\n\n\"그녀의 그 다정한 연민에 신의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n# CHAPTER XXI. Echoing Footsteps\n\n의사가 살았던 그 모퉁이는 메아리가 울리기에 더없이 멋진 곳이라고들 했다. 남편과 아버지, 자기 자신, 그리고 자신의 옛 보호자이자 동무를 고요한 행복의 삶 속으로 묶어주는 황금실을 늘 부지런히 감으며, 루시는 잔잔히 메아리치는 그 모퉁이의 고요한 집에 앉아, 세월의 메아리치는 발소리에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n처음에는, 그녀가 더할 나위 없이 행복한 젊은 아내였음에도, 손에서 일감이 천천히 떨어지고 두 눈이 흐려지는 때가 있었다. 메아리 속에서 무언가가 다가오고 있었기 때문이다. 가볍고, 아득히 멀고, 아직 거의 들리지 않는 무언가가 그녀의 마음을 너무도 뒤흔들었다. 나풀거리는 희망과 의혹이 — 아직 그녀가 알지 못하는 사랑에 대한 희망, 그리고 그 새로운 기쁨을 누리며 지상에 남아 있을 수 있을지에 대한 의혹이 — 그녀의 가슴을 갈라놓았다. 그러면 그 메아리 속에서 그녀 자신의 이른 무덤가에 들려오는 발소리가 일어났고, 그토록 쓸쓸히 홀로 남아 그녀를 그토록 깊이 애도할 남편을 떠올리는 생각이 두 눈에 차올라 파도처럼 부서졌다.\n\n그 시절은 지나갔고, 어린 루시가 그녀의 품에 안겼다. 그러자 다가오는 메아리 속에는 그 작은 발의 디딤 소리와 옹알거리는 말소리가 섞여 있었다. 더 큰 메아리들이 제멋대로 울려대도, 요람 곁의 젊은 어머니는 언제나 그 다가오는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그 소리가 다가오면, 그늘진 집은 아이의 웃음으로 환해졌고, 그녀가 어려움 속에서 자기 아이를 맡겼던 어린아이들의 거룩한 친구가 옛날에 어린아이를 안았듯이 그녀의 아이를 품에 안아, 그것을 그녀에게 성스러운 기쁨으로 만들어주는 듯했다.\n\n그들 모두를 함께 묶어주는 황금실을 늘 부지런히 감으며, 자신의 행복한 영향력의 봉사를 그들 삶의 직물 전체에 짜 넣으면서도 그것이 어느 곳에서도 두드러지지 않게 하면서, 루시는 세월의 메아리 속에서 다정하고 위안을 주는 소리밖에 듣지 못했다. 남편의 발걸음은 그 속에서 굳세고 번창했으며, 아버지의 발걸음은 단단하고 한결같았다. 보라, 프로스 양은 끈으로 된 마구를 차고서, 채찍질에 길든 거친 군마처럼 콧김을 내뿜고 정원의 플라타너스 아래 땅을 앞발로 긁으며 메아리를 깨우고 있었다!\n\n다른 소리들 가운데 슬픔의 소리가 섞여 있을 때조차, 그것들은 거칠지도 잔혹하지도 않았다. 그녀 자신의 것과 같은 황금빛 머리카락이 어느 어린 사내아이의 야윈 얼굴을 후광처럼 두르며 베개 위에 펼쳐져 있고, 그 아이가 환한 미소를 지으며 \"사랑하는 아빠 엄마, 두 분을 떠나고 예쁜 누이를 떠나게 되어 정말 슬퍼요. 하지만 저는 부름을 받았으니, 가야만 해요!\"라고 말했을 때조차도, 그 영혼이 그것을 맡았던 그녀의 품에서 떠나가는 동안 젊은 어머니의 뺨을 적신 그 눈물이 온통 고통의 눈물만은 아니었다. 어린아이들이 내게 오는 것을 용납하고 막지 말라. 그들은 내 아버지의 얼굴을 보느니라. 오, 아버지, 복된 말씀이여!\n\n이렇게 천사의 날개가 스치는 소리가 다른 메아리들과 어우러졌고, 그것들은 온전히 지상의 것이 아니라 그 속에 천국의 숨결을 품고 있었다. 어느 작은 정원 무덤 위로 부는 바람의 한숨도 그것들과 뒤섞였으니, 그 둘 모두가 루시에게는 — 모래 해변에서 잠든 여름 바다의 숨결처럼 — 나직한 속삭임으로 들려왔고, 그러는 동안 어린 루시는 우스꽝스러우리만치 진지하게 아침 공부에 몰두하거나, 어머니의 발치 의자에서 인형에게 옷을 입히면서, 자기 삶에 한데 섞여 있는 두 도시의 언어로 재잘거렸다.\n\n그 메아리들은 시드니 카턴의 실제 발소리에 좀처럼 응답하지 않았다. 그는 기껏해야 일 년에 대여섯 번쯤 초대받지 않고 찾아드는 자신의 특권을 내세웠고, 한때 자주 그랬듯이 저녁 내내 그들 가운데 앉아 있곤 했다. 그는 술에 달아오른 채로 그곳에 온 적이 없었다. 그리고 그에 관한 또 한 가지가 메아리 속에서 속삭여졌는데, 그것은 진실한 모든 메아리가 오랜 세월 동안 속삭여온 것이었다.\n\n진정으로 한 여자를 사랑했고, 그녀를 잃었으며, 그녀가 아내가 되고 어머니가 된 뒤에도 변함없으면서도 흠 없는 마음으로 그녀를 알고 지낸 남자치고, 그 아이들이 그에게 묘한 공감을 — 그를 향한 본능적이고도 섬세한 연민을 — 품지 않은 경우는 없었다. 그런 경우에 어떤 고운 숨은 감수성이 건드려지는지는 어떤 메아리도 말해주지 않지만, 어쨌든 그러하며, 이곳에서도 그러했다. 카턴은 어린 루시가 통통한 팔을 내민 첫 낯선 사람이었고, 아이가 자라는 동안에도 그녀 곁에서 자기 자리를 지켰다. 그 어린 사내아이도 거의 마지막 순간에 그에 관해 말했다. \"불쌍한 카턴 아저씨! 저 대신 입맞춰주세요!\"\n\n스트라이버 씨는 마치 탁한 물살을 헤치며 나아가는 어떤 거대한 기관처럼 어깨로 밀어붙여 법조계를 헤쳐나갔고, 자신의 쓸모 있는 친구를 뒤에 매단 거룻배처럼 끌고 다녔다. 그렇게 끌려가는 배가 대개는 험한 처지에 놓여 대부분 물속에 잠겨 있듯이, 시드니의 삶도 물에 잠긴 삶이었다. 그러나 안일하고 끈질긴 습관이 — 불행하게도 그에게는 공적이나 치욕에 대한 그 어떤 자극적 의식보다 훨씬 더 안일하고 끈질긴 그 습관이 — 그것을 그가 살아갈 삶으로 만들어버렸으니, 그는 진짜 자칼이 사자로 올라설 생각을 하지 않듯이, 자신의 사자의 자칼이라는 처지에서 벗어날 생각을 더는 하지 않았다. 스트라이버는 부유했다. 재산과 세 아들을 가진 혈색 좋은 과부와 결혼했는데, 그 아들들이라야 만두처럼 둥근 머리에 곧게 뻗은 머리카락 말고는 특별히 빛나는 구석이 없었다.\n\n스트라이버 씨는 모공마다 더없이 불쾌한 종류의 생색을 풍기며, 이 세 젊은 신사를 마치 양 세 마리처럼 앞세워 소호의 그 조용한 모퉁이로 데려가서는, 루시의 남편에게 제자로 받아달라고 내밀었다. \"이보게! 자네 결혼 소풍에 보태라고 빵과 치즈 덩어리 셋을 가져왔네, 다네이!\"라고 점잖게 말하면서 말이다. 그 빵과 치즈 덩어리 셋이 정중히 거절당하자 스트라이버 씨는 분노로 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐는데, 나중에 그는 그것을 젊은 신사들의 교육에 써먹어, 그 가정교사 같은 작자처럼 거지의 자존심을 조심하라고 그들에게 일렀다. 또한 그는 묵직한 포도주를 들이켜며 스트라이버 부인에게, 다네이 부인이 한때 자기를 \"낚으려고\" 부려먹은 수작에 관하여, 그리고 자신을 \"낚이지 않게\" 만들어준 자기 안의, 부인, 강철로 강철을 깎는 듯한 수완에 관하여 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 버릇이 있었다. 이따금 그 묵직한 포도주와 그 거짓말에 동석하곤 했던 그의 왕좌재판소 친구들 가운데 몇몇은, 그가 하도 자주 그 말을 하다 보니 스스로도 그것을 믿게 된 거라고 말하며 그 후자를 변명해주었는데 — 이는 본디도 못된 죄를 도무지 어찌해볼 수 없을 만큼 악화시키는 짓이어서, 그런 죄인이라면 어딘가 알맞게 외진 곳으로 끌려가 거치적거리지 않게 목매달려 마땅하다 할 만했다.\n\n이런 것들이, 어린 딸이 여섯 살이 될 때까지 루시가 그 메아리치는 모퉁이에서 — 때로는 생각에 잠겨, 때로는 즐거워하며 웃으면서 — 귀 기울인 메아리들 가운데 끼어 있었다. 아이의 발소리 메아리가, 그리고 늘 활기차고 침착한 자기 아버지의 발소리 메아리가, 또 사랑하는 남편의 발소리 메아리가 그녀의 마음에 얼마나 가까이 다가왔는지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없다. 어떤 낭비보다도 더 풍요로울 만큼 현명하고 우아한 절약으로 그녀 자신이 꾸려가는 그 화목한 가정의 가장 가벼운 메아리가 그녀에게 얼마나 음악 같았는지도 굳이 말할 필요가 없다. 또한 그녀의 귀에 감미롭게 들려오는 메아리들이 그녀 둘레에 가득했으니, 아버지가 그녀에게 미혼일 때보다 결혼한 뒤에 (그럴 수만 있다면) 자신에게 더 헌신적인 것 같다고 여러 번 말했던 일, 남편이 그녀에게 그 어떤 근심과 의무도 자신을 향한 그녀의 사랑이나 자신에 대한 그녀의 도움을 갈라놓는 것 같지 않다고 여러 번 말하며 \"여보, 우리가 단 한 사람뿐인 것처럼 당신이 우리 모두에게 그 모든 것이 되어주면서도, 결코 서두르거나 할 일이 너무 많은 것처럼 보이지 않는, 그 마법 같은 비결이 대체 뭐요?\"라고 물었던 일에 관한 메아리들이 그러했다는 것도, 굳이 말할 필요가 없다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 세월 동안, 멀리서부터 그 모퉁이에 위협적으로 우르릉거리는 다른 메아리들이 있었다. 그리고 어린 루시의 여섯번째 생일 무렵인 바로 지금, 그것들이 무시무시한 소리를 — 무서운 바다가 솟구치는 프랑스의 거대한 폭풍 같은 소리를 — 내기 시작했다.\n\n천칠백팔십구년 칠월 중순의 어느 밤, 로리 씨가 텔슨 은행에서 늦게 돌아와, 어두운 창가에 앉은 루시와 그녀의 남편 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 무덥고 거친 밤이었고, 세 사람 모두 예전에 같은 자리에서 번개를 바라보았던 그 옛 일요일 밤을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 텔슨에서 지새워야 하나 싶은 생각이 들기 시작했답니다.\" 로리 씨가 갈색 가발을 뒤로 밀어 올리며 말했다. \"하루 종일 일이 어찌나 밀려들었는지, 뭘 먼저 해야 할지, 어느 쪽으로 손을 대야 할지 알 수가 없었어요. 빠리가 어찌나 불안한지, 우리한테 실제로 예금이 밀려들고 있다니까요! 저쪽 우리 고객들이 자기 재산을 우리한테 맡기지 못해 안달인 것 같아요. 그걸 영국으로 보내려는 광적인 열기가 그들 가운데 일부에게는 분명히 일고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그거 안 좋은 조짐이로군요.\" 다네이가 말했다.\n\n\"안 좋은 조짐이라고요, 친애하는 다네이? 그렇소, 하지만 그 안에 무슨 까닭이 있는지는 우리도 모르오. 사람들이란 어찌나 비합리적인지! 우리 텔슨 사람들 가운데 몇몇은 늙어가고 있어서, 정당한 이유 없이는 통상적인 일과를 벗어나는 수고를 정말 견디기 어렵다오.\"\n\n\"그래도,\" 다네이가 말했다. \"하늘이 얼마나 음울하고 위협적인지는 아시잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그야 물론 알지요.\" 로리 씨가 동의하면서, 자신의 온화한 성미가 틀어졌다는 둥, 자신이 투덜대고 있다는 둥 스스로를 설득하려 애쓰며 말했다. \"하지만 나는 하루 종일 시달린 끝이니 좀 까다롭게 굴기로 작정했소이다. 마네뜨는 어디 있소?\"\n\n\"여기 있습니다.\" 바로 그 순간 어두운 방으로 들어서며 의사가 말했다.\n\n\"댁에 계시다니 참 다행이오. 하루 종일 나를 둘러싼 이 부산함과 불길한 예감 탓에, 까닭 없이 신경이 곤두서 있었거든요. 외출하실 건 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 괜찮으시다면 함께 주사위놀이나 할까 합니다.\" 의사가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말해도 된다면, 별로 내키지 않는구려. 오늘 밤은 당신과 겨룰 만한 상태가 못 되오. 차 쟁반이 아직 거기 있나, 루시? 잘 안 보이는구나.\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 그대로 두었답니다.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 얘야. 그 귀한 아이는 잠자리에 잘 들었느냐?\"\n\n\"새근새근 잘 자고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 됐다. 다들 무사하고 평안하니! 하느님 덕분에, 어째서 여기 무엇 하나라도 무사하고 평안하지 않을 까닭이 있겠느냐만, 종일 어찌나 심란했는지, 게다가 나도 예전만큼 젊지가 않으니! 내 차 좀 주렴, 얘야! 고맙구나. 자, 와서 다 같이 둘러앉으세나, 가만히 앉아서, 자네가 그 이론을 펼친다는 메아리나 들어봅시다.\"\n\n\"이론이 아니에요. 공상이었지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 공상이라 합시다, 내 슬기로운 아가씨.\" 로리 씨가 그녀의 손을 토닥이며 말했다. \"그래도 그 메아리들이 무척 많고 무척 시끄럽지 않소? 저것 좀 들어봐요!\"\n\n누군가의 삶 속으로 억지로 밀고 들어오려는, 무모하고 광기 어리고 위험한 발소리들, 일단 붉게 물들면 다시 깨끗하게 되기 어려운 발소리들, 그 작은 무리가 어두운 런던의 창가에 앉아 있는 동안, 저 멀리 쌩땅뚜안에서 광란하는 발소리들.\n\n쌩땅뚜안은 그날 아침, 이리저리 출렁이는 허수아비 떼의 거대하고 어슴푸레한 덩어리였으니, 물결치는 머리들 위로 강철 칼날과 총검이 햇빛에 번득이며 자주 빛을 뿜었다. 쌩땅뚜안의 목구멍에서 어마어마한 포효가 솟아올랐고, 헐벗은 팔들의 숲이 겨울바람 속에 쪼그라든 나뭇가지처럼 허공에서 몸부림쳤으니, 모든 손가락이 저 아래 깊은 곳에서 던져 올려지는 온갖 무기나 무기 비슷한 것이라면 아무리 멀리 있어도 가리지 않고 발작하듯 움켜쥐었다.\n\n누가 그것들을 나누어 주었는지, 그것들이 마지막으로 어디서 왔는지, 어디서 시작되었는지, 무슨 작용으로 그것들이 일종의 번개처럼 한 번에 수십 개씩 군중의 머리 위로 비뚜로 떨리며 홱홱 움직였는지, 그 무리 가운데 어느 누구의 눈도 말해줄 수 없었으리라. 그러나 머스킷 총이 나누어지고 있었고, 탄약통과 화약과 탄환, 쇠막대와 나무막대, 칼과 도끼와 창, 광란하는 독창성이 찾아내거나 고안해낼 수 있는 온갖 무기가 그렇게 나누어지고 있었다. 다른 아무것도 손에 쥘 수 없던 사람들은 피 흘리는 손으로 벽에서 돌과 벽돌을 억지로 뽑아내는 일에 매달렸다. 쌩땅뚜안의 모든 맥박과 심장이 고열의 긴장 속에, 고열의 열기 속에 있었다. 그곳의 살아 있는 모든 존재가 목숨을 하찮게 여겼고, 그것을 바치려는 격정적인 각오로 미쳐 있었다.\n\n펄펄 끓는 물의 소용돌이에 중심점이 있듯이, 이 모든 광란은 드파르주의 술집을 중심으로 소용돌이쳤고, 그 가마솥 속 인간의 물방울 하나하나가 그 소용돌이의 중심으로 빨려드는 경향이 있었으니, 거기서 드파르주 자신은 이미 화약과 땀으로 시커멓게 된 채, 명령을 내리고 무기를 나누어 주고, 이 사람을 뒤로 밀치고 저 사람을 앞으로 끌어당기고, 한 사람을 무장 해제시켜 다른 사람을 무장시키며, 그 아우성의 한복판에서 애쓰고 분투했다.\n\n\"내 곁에 붙어 있게, 자끄 셋.\" 드파르주가 외쳤다. \"그리고 자끄 하나와 둘, 자네들은 갈라져서 이 애국자들을 되도록 많이 거느리고 선두에 서게. 내 아내는 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"아니, 뭐예요! 여기 내가 있잖아요!\" 마담이 여느 때처럼 침착하게 말했으나, 오늘은 뜨개질을 하고 있지 않았다. 마담의 단호한 오른손에는 평소의 부드러운 도구 대신 도끼가 들려 있었고, 허리띠에는 권총과 잔혹한 칼이 꽂혀 있었다.\n\n\"어디로 갈 거요, 여보?\"\n\n\"나는,\" 마담이 말했다. \"지금은 당신과 함께 가요. 이따가 여자들의 선두에 선 나를 보게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 갑시다!\" 드파르주가 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 외쳤다. \"애국자들이여, 동지들이여, 우리는 준비됐다! 바스띠유로!\"\n\n마치 프랑스의 온 숨결이 그 가증스러운 한마디로 빚어진 듯한 포효와 함께, 살아 있는 바다가 물결 위에 물결로, 깊이 위에 깊이로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 지점을 향해 도시를 넘쳐흘렀다. 경종이 울리고, 북이 울리고, 바다가 새로운 해변에서 광란하며 천둥처럼 부딪히는 가운데, 공격이 시작되었다.\n\n깊은 해자, 이중의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기. 그 불을 뚫고 그 연기를 뚫고 — 그 불 속에서 그 연기 속에서, 바다가 그를 어느 대포 앞으로 떠밀어 던졌고 그 순간 그는 포수가 되었으므로 — 술집의 드파르주는 사나운 두 시간 동안 용감한 병사처럼 일했다.\n\n깊은 해자, 단 하나의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기. 도개교 하나가 내려졌다! \"일하라, 동지들이여 모두, 일하라! 일하라, 자끄 하나, 자끄 둘, 자끄 천, 자끄 이천, 자끄 이만 오천이여. 모든 천사들의 이름으로든 악마들의 이름으로든 — 어느 쪽이든 좋을 대로 — 일하라!\" 술집의 드파르주는 진작부터 뜨겁게 달아오른 자기 대포 곁에서 그렇게 외쳤다.\n\n\"내게로, 여인들이여!\" 그의 아내인 마담이 외쳤다. \"뭐예요! 일단 함락되면 우리도 남자들 못지않게 죽일 수 있어요!\" 그러자 그녀에게로, 날카롭고 굶주린 외침과 함께, 가지각색으로 무장한, 그러나 모두가 한결같이 굶주림과 복수로 무장한 여인들이 떼 지어 몰려들었다.\n\n대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기. 그러나 여전히 깊은 해자, 단 하나의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 그리고 여덟 개의 거대한 탑. 쓰러지는 부상자들로 인해 광란하는 바다가 조금씩 자리를 옮겼다. 번득이는 무기들, 활활 타오르는 횃불들, 젖은 짚을 가득 실어 연기를 뿜는 수레들, 사방의 이웃 바리케이드에서 벌어지는 고된 작업, 비명, 일제 사격, 저주, 끝없는 용맹, 쾅 하고 부서지고 덜그럭거리는 소리, 그리고 살아 있는 바다의 격렬한 울림. 그러나 여전히 깊은 해자, 그리고 단 하나의 도개교, 그리고 육중한 돌벽, 그리고 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 그리고 여전히 술집의 드파르주는, 사나운 네 시간의 노역으로 갑절이나 뜨거워진 자기 대포 곁에 있었다.\n\n요새 안에서 백기가 오르고, 협상이 벌어졌으나 — 광란하는 폭풍 사이로 이것이 어렴풋이 보였을 뿐, 그 안에서 들리는 것은 아무것도 없었다 — 갑자기 바다가 헤아릴 수 없이 더 넓고 더 높이 솟구쳐, 술집의 드파르주를 내려진 도개교 너머로, 육중한 돌 외벽 너머로, 항복한 여덟 개의 거대한 탑 한가운데로 쓸어 넣었다!\n\n그를 떠밀고 가는 그 대양의 힘이 어찌나 거역할 수 없었던지, 숨을 쉬거나 고개를 돌리는 것조차, 마치 남쪽 바다의 파도 속에서 발버둥치기라도 하는 것처럼 불가능했으니, 그는 마침내 바스띠유의 바깥뜰에 내려놓였다. 거기서 그는 어느 벽 모퉁이에 기대어 둘레를 둘러보려고 안간힘을 썼다. 자끄 셋이 거의 그의 곁에 있었다. 여전히 자기 여인들 가운데 일부를 이끄는 마담 드파르주가 안쪽 멀찍이 보였고, 그녀의 손에는 칼이 들려 있었다. 사방이 소동과 환희, 귀가 먹먹하고 광기 어린 혼란, 어마어마한 소음, 그러면서도 격렬한 무언극이었다.\n\n\"죄수들을!\"\n\n\"기록들을!\"\n\n\"비밀 독방들을!\"\n\n\"고문 도구들을!\"\n\n\"죄수들을!\"\n\n이 모든 외침과 만 가지 두서없는 소리 가운데, \"죄수들을!\"이 밀려드는 바다가 가장 많이 받아 외친 외침이었으니, 마치 시간과 공간뿐 아니라 사람도 영원무궁한 듯했다. 맨 앞의 물결이 교도소 관리들을 떠밀고서 굴러 지나가며, 만약 드러나지 않은 비밀 구석이라도 남아 있으면 모조리 즉시 죽이겠다고 그들 모두를 위협할 때, 드파르주는 그 사람들 가운데 하나의 — 백발에, 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 든 사람의 — 가슴에 억센 손을 얹어, 나머지에게서 떼어내고 자기와 벽 사이에 끼워 넣었다.\n\n\"북쪽 탑을 보여다오!\" 드파르주가 말했다. \"빨리!\"\n\n\"성심껏 그러지요.\" 그 사람이 대답했다. \"저와 함께 가신다면요. 하지만 거기엔 아무도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"북쪽 탑 백오 호가 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 드파르주가 물었다. \"빨리!\"\n\n\"뜻이라니요, 무슈?\"\n\n\"그게 죄수를 뜻하느냐, 아니면 감금하는 장소를 뜻하느냐? 아니면 내가 너를 쳐 죽이기라도 바라는 거냐?\"\n\n\"죽여버려요!\" 가까이 다가온 자끄 셋이 쉰 목소리로 내뱉었다.\n\n\"무슈, 그것은 독방입니다.\"\n\n\"내게 보여다오!\"\n\n\"그럼 이쪽으로 오시지요.\"\n\n자끄 셋은 여느 때의 갈망에 사로잡혀, 유혈을 약속할 것 같지 않은 쪽으로 대화가 흘러가는 데 명백히 실망한 채, 드파르주가 간수의 팔을 붙들듯이 드파르주의 팔을 붙들었다. 이 짧은 대화 동안 세 사람의 머리는 바짝 맞붙어 있었으나, 그렇게 했는데도 서로의 말을 알아듣기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었다. 요새 안으로 난입하여 안뜰과 통로와 계단을 범람하는 살아 있는 대양의 소음이 그토록 어마어마했기 때문이다. 바깥에서도 사방으로, 바다는 깊고 쉰 포효로 벽을 두드렸으니, 이따금 그 속에서 부분적인 소동의 함성이 터져 나와 물보라처럼 허공으로 솟구쳤다.\n\n햇빛이 한 번도 비친 적 없는 음침한 지하실들을 지나고, 어두운 굴과 우리의 흉측한 문들을 지나, 동굴 같은 계단을 한참 내려갔다가, 다시 계단이라기보다는 메마른 폭포에 가까운 돌과 벽돌의 가파르고 험한 오르막을 올라, 드파르주와 간수와 자끄 셋은 서로 손과 팔을 맞잡고 낼 수 있는 한 빠른 속도로 나아갔다. 여기저기서, 특히 처음에는, 범람한 물결이 그들에게 덮쳐와 지나갔다. 그러나 다 내려가서 어느 탑을 휘돌아 오를 때쯤에는 그들뿐이었다. 여기서는 육중한 두께의 벽과 아치에 둘러싸여, 요새 안팎의 폭풍이 그들에게는 둔하고 잦아든 소리로만 들렸으니, 마치 그들이 빠져나온 그 소음이 그들의 청각을 거의 망가뜨려놓기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n간수가 어느 낮은 문 앞에서 멈춰, 철커덕거리는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 꽂아 문을 천천히 밀어 열고는, 그들 모두가 머리를 숙이고 들어설 때 말했다.\n\n\"북쪽 탑, 백오 호!\"\n\n벽 높은 곳에 작고, 쇠창살이 촘촘하고, 유리도 끼우지 않은 창이 하나 있었는데, 그 앞에 돌 가림막이 있어서 하늘은 몸을 잔뜩 굽혀 올려다보아야만 보였다. 안쪽으로 몇 자 들어간 곳에 작은 굴뚝이 하나 있었는데, 가로로 쇠창살이 촘촘히 박혀 있었다. 화덕에는 깃털처럼 묵은 나무재가 한 무더기 쌓여 있었다. 걸상 하나와 탁자 하나, 짚으로 된 침상 하나가 있었다. 시커멓게 그을린 네 벽이 있었고, 그중 한 벽에는 녹슨 쇠고리 하나가 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"저 횃불을 이 벽들을 따라 천천히 비춰다오, 내가 볼 수 있게.\" 드파르주가 간수에게 말했다.\n\n그 사람이 시키는 대로 했고, 드파르주는 그 불빛을 두 눈으로 바짝 좇았다.\n\n\"멈춰라! 여기 보게, 자끄!\"\n\n\"아, 엠!\" 자끄 셋이 게걸스럽게 읽으며 쉰 목소리로 내뱉었다.\n\n\"알렉상드르 마네뜨.\" 화약이 깊이 배어든 거뭇한 집게손가락으로 글자를 따라가며 드파르주가 그의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"그리고 여기 그는 '가련한 의사'라고 썼군. 그리고 이 돌에 달력을 새겨놓은 것도 틀림없이 그 사람일 거야. 자네 손에 든 게 뭔가? 쇠지레? 그걸 이리 주게!\"\n\n그는 아직 자기 대포의 점화봉을 손에 들고 있었다. 그는 그 두 도구를 갑자기 맞바꾸더니, 벌레 먹은 걸상과 탁자로 돌아서서 몇 번 내리쳐 산산조각 냈다.\n\n\"횃불을 더 높이 들어!\" 그가 간수에게 노기 띤 목소리로 말했다. \"저 조각들 사이를 잘 살펴보게, 자끄. 그리고 봐! 여기 내 칼이 있네.\" 그것을 그에게 던지며 말했다. \"저 침상을 째서 짚을 뒤져보게. 횃불을 더 높이 들라니까, 자네!\"\n\n간수를 위협적으로 한 번 쏘아본 뒤 그는 화덕 위로 기어 올라가, 굴뚝 안을 들여다보며 쇠지레로 그 옆벽을 치고 비집었으며, 굴뚝을 가로지른 쇠창살에 매달려 일했다. 몇 분 만에 회반죽과 먼지가 조금 떨어져 내렸고, 그는 그것을 피하려고 얼굴을 돌렸다. 그러고는 그 속에서, 그리고 묵은 나무재 속에서, 또 그의 연장이 미끄러져 들어갔거나 비집어 들어간 굴뚝의 틈새 속에서, 조심스러운 손길로 더듬었다.\n\n\"나무에도 아무것도 없고, 짚에도 아무것도 없나, 자끄?\"\n\n\"아무것도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그것들을 한데 모으세, 독방 한가운데에. 그렇지! 거기에 불을 붙이게, 자네!\"\n\n간수가 그 작은 무더기에 불을 댕기자, 높고 뜨겁게 타올랐다. 다시 몸을 숙여 그 낮은 아치문으로 나서며, 그들은 그것을 타게 내버려둔 채 안뜰로 가는 길을 되짚어갔다. 내려가는 동안 청각이 되살아나는 듯하더니, 마침내 다시 한번 광란하는 홍수 속에 들어섰다.\n\n그들은 그것이 드파르주 자신을 찾아 출렁이며 들끓고 있는 것을 발견했다. 쌩땅뚜안은 바스띠유를 지키며 민중에게 총을 쏘았던 그 사령관을 호위하는 일에 자기네 술집 주인이 선두에 서야 한다고 시끄럽게 외쳐댔다. 그러지 않으면 그 사령관은 재판을 받으러 시청으로 끌려가지 않을 것이었다. 그러지 않으면 그 사령관은 달아날 것이고, 민중의 피는 (여러 해 동안 무가치하다가 갑자기 얼마간의 가치를 띠게 된 그 피는) 복수받지 못할 것이었다.\n\n* 시청(Hotel de Ville): 빠리 시청사. 혁명기에 봉기의 거점이자 즉결 재판이 이루어지던 곳.\n회색 외투와 붉은 훈장으로 눈에 띄는 이 험상궂은 늙은 장교를 에워싼 듯한, 격정과 다툼이 울부짖는 그 세계 속에서, 단 하나 아주 침착한 형체가 있었으니, 그것은 한 여자의 모습이었다. \"보라, 저기 내 남편이 있다!\" 그녀가 그를 가리키며 외쳤다. \"드파르주를 보라!\" 그녀는 그 험상궂은 늙은 장교 바로 곁에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었고, 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었다. 드파르주와 나머지 사람들이 그를 끌고 거리를 지나가는 동안 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었고, 그가 목적지 가까이 끌려가 뒤에서 얻어맞기 시작했을 때에도 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었으며, 오래 쌓여온 칼질과 매질의 비가 무겁게 쏟아져 내릴 때에도 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었다. 그가 그 아래 죽어 쓰러졌을 때에도 그의 곁에 어찌나 바짝 붙어 있었던지, 그녀는 갑자기 활기를 띠며 그의 목에 발을 올려놓고, 진작부터 준비해둔 그 잔혹한 칼로 그의 머리를 베어냈다.\n\n쌩땅뚜안이 인간을 등불처럼 매달아 올려, 자기가 무엇이 될 수 있고 무엇을 할 수 있는지를 보여주겠다는 그 끔찍한 발상을 실행할 때가 온 것이다. 쌩땅뚜안의 피가 끓어올랐고, 쇠 같은 손으로 휘두르던 폭정과 지배의 피는 떨어졌으니 — 사령관의 시신이 누운 시청 계단 위로 떨어졌고, 절단을 위해 시신을 고정하려고 그것을 밟았던 마담 드파르주의 신발 밑창 위로 떨어졌다. \"저기 등불을 내려라!\" 새로운 죽음의 수단을 찾아 사방을 노려보던 쌩땅뚜안이 외쳤다. \"여기 보초로 세워둘 그의 병사가 하나 있다!\" 그 흔들리는 보초가 세워졌고, 바다는 계속 밀려들었다.\n\n검고 위협적인 물의 바다, 물결이 물결을 향해 파괴적으로 솟구치는 그 바다, 그 깊이는 아직 헤아려지지 않았고 그 힘은 아직 알려지지 않은 그 바다. 격렬하게 출렁이는 형체들, 복수의 목소리들, 그리고 고통의 용광로 속에서 단단해져 연민의 손길로도 흔적 하나 남길 수 없게 된 얼굴들의, 무자비한 바다.\n\n그러나 사납고 격렬한 표정이 하나하나 생생히 살아 있는 그 얼굴의 대양 속에, 두 무리의 얼굴이 — 각각 일곱씩인 — 나머지와 너무도 확연히 대조를 이루며 있었으니, 그보다 더 기억할 만한 난파선을 싣고 굽이친 바다는 일찍이 없었다. 그들의 무덤을 터뜨려 갑자기 풀려난 일곱 죄수의 얼굴이 머리 위 높이 떠받들려 갔으니, 모두가 겁에 질리고, 모두가 넋이 나가고, 모두가 의아해하며 놀라워했으니, 마치 최후의 날이 왔고 자기들 둘레에서 환호하는 자들이 지옥에 떨어진 영혼들이기라도 한 듯했다. 또 다른 일곱 얼굴이 더 높이 떠받들려 갔으니, 일곱 개의 죽은 얼굴이었으며, 그 처진 눈꺼풀과 반쯤 드러난 눈은 최후의 날을 기다리고 있었다. 무표정한 얼굴들, 그러나 그 위에는 — 사라진 것이 아니라 — 유예된 표정이 어려 있었다. 차라리, 두려운 멈춤 속에 있는 얼굴들이었으니, 아직 처진 눈꺼풀을 들어 올려, 핏기 없는 입술로 \"네가 그 짓을 했노라!\"라고 증언해야 할 것만 같았다.\n\n풀려난 일곱 죄수, 창끝에 꽂힌 일곱 개의 피투성이 머리, 여덟 개의 굳센 탑을 가진 그 저주받은 요새의 열쇠들, 오래전에 상심하여 죽은 옛 시절 죄수들의 발견된 편지 몇 통과 다른 유품들 — 이런 것들이, 또 이런 것들이, 천칠백팔십구년 칠월 중순 빠리의 거리를 지나며 쌩땅뚜안이 요란하게 메아리치는 발소리로 호위해 가는 것들이었다. 자, 하늘이여, 루시 다네이의 공상을 무찔러, 이 발들이 그녀의 삶에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 하소서! 그것들은 무모하고, 광기 어리고, 위험하니, 드파르주의 술집 문간에서 술통이 깨진 그토록 오랜 세월 뒤에도, 일단 붉게 물들면 좀처럼 깨끗해지지 않기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII. The Sea Still Rises\n\n초췌한 쌩땅뜨완은 형제애의 포옹과 축하라는 양념으로 자기 몫의 딱딱하고 쓴 빵을 할 수 있는 만큼 부드럽게 누릴 수 있었던 단 한 주의 환희에 찬 시간을 보냈을 뿐이었는데, 그동안 마담 드파르주는 여느 때처럼 계산대에 앉아 손님들을 주재하고 있었다. 마담 드파르주는 머리에 장미를 꽂고 있지 않았으니, 첩자들의 거대한 형제단이 단 한 주 만에도 이 성자의 자비에 자기 몸을 맡기기를 극도로 꺼리게 되었기 때문이다. 그의 거리 곳곳에 걸린 등잔들은 불길하리만큼 탄력 있게 흔들거렸다.\n\n마담 드파르주는 팔짱을 낀 채 아침의 빛과 열기 속에 앉아, 술집과 거리를 바라보았다. 양쪽 모두에 빈둥거리는 자들이 몇 무리씩 있었는데, 누추하고 비참했지만 이제는 자신들의 고난 위에 권력이 자리 잡았다는 명백한 감각을 지니고 있었다. 가장 가련한 머리에 비뚜름하게 얹힌 가장 너덜너덜한 잠모자에는 이런 비뚤어진 의미가 담겨 있었다. \"이것을 쓴 내가 내 안의 목숨을 부지하기가 얼마나 힘들어졌는지 나는 안다. 그러나 이것을 쓴 내가 네 안의 목숨을 끊어버리기가 얼마나 쉬워졌는지 너는 아느냐?\" 전에는 일거리 없이 놀던 앙상하고 헐벗은 팔마다 이제는 늘 준비된 일거리가 있었으니, 곧 후려칠 수 있다는 것이었다. 뜨개질하는 여자들의 손가락은, 찢어발길 수 있다는 경험으로 인해 사악했다. 쌩땅뜨완의 모습에는 변화가 있었다. 그 형상은 수백 년에 걸쳐 망치로 두들겨져 빚어졌는데, 마지막 마무리 일격들이 그 표정에 강력하게 새겨진 것이다.\n\n마담 드파르주는 그것을 지켜보며 앉아 있었으니, 쌩땅뜨완 여인들의 지도자에게서 바랄 법한 그런 억눌린 인정이 그 안에 담겨 있었다. 그 자매단 중 하나가 그녀 곁에서 뜨개질을 했다. 굶주린 식료품상의 키 작고 다소 통통한 아내이자 두 아이의 어머니이기도 한 이 부관은 이미 '복수'라는 칭송의 이름을 얻은 터였다.\n\n\"들어봐!\" 하고 복수가 말했다. \"그러니까 들어봐! 누가 오는 거지?\"\n\n쌩땅뜨완 구역의 가장 바깥 경계에서 술집 문까지 깔아놓은 화약 도화선에 갑자기 불이 붙기라도 한 듯, 빠르게 번지는 웅성거림이 밀려왔다.\n\n\"드파르주예요.\" 하고 마담이 말했다. \"조용히, 동지들!\"\n\n드파르주가 숨을 헐떡이며 들어와, 쓰고 있던 붉은 모자를 벗고 주위를 둘러보았다! \"어디서나, 들어요!\" 하고 마담이 다시 말했다. \"이 사람 말을 들어요!\" 드파르주는 헐떡이며 서 있었고, 그 배경으로 문밖에는 간절한 눈빛과 벌어진 입들이 모여들어 있었다. 술집 안에 있던 자들은 모두 벌떡 일어나 있었다.\n\n\"그러니까 말해봐요, 여보. 무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"저세상에서 온 소식이오!\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이에요?\" 하고 마담이 경멸하듯 외쳤다. \"저세상이라니?\"\n\n\"여기 다들 늙은 풀롱을 기억하지? 굶주린 사람들에게 풀이나 뜯어 먹으라고 한 그자, 죽어서 지옥에 갔다던 그자 말이오.\"\n\n\"다들 기억하지!\" 모든 목구멍에서 터져 나왔다.\n\n\"바로 그자에 관한 소식이오. 그가 우리 가운데 있소!\"\n\n\"우리 가운데!\" 다시 한목소리로 터져 나왔다. \"그리고 죽었다며?\"\n\n\"죽지 않았소! 그는 우리를 너무도 두려워한 나머지—그럴 만한 이유가 있었지—자신을 죽은 것으로 꾸미고 성대한 가짜 장례까지 치렀던 거요. 그런데 시골에 숨어 살아 있는 그자를 찾아내 끌고 왔소. 방금 그자가 죄수의 몸으로 시청으로 끌려가는 걸 내 눈으로 보았소. 그가 우리를 두려워할 이유가 있었다고 내가 말했지. 다들 말해보시오! 그자에게 정말로 그럴 이유가 있었소?\"\n\n칠십을 넘긴 가련한 늙은 죄인은, 설사 지금껏 한 번도 그것을 알지 못했다 해도, 그 응답하는 함성을 들을 수 있었다면 마음 깊은 곳에서 절로 깨달았을 것이다.\n\n깊은 침묵의 순간이 뒤따랐다. 드파르주와 그의 아내는 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 복수는 몸을 굽혔고, 그녀가 계산대 뒤 발치에서 그것을 옮길 때 북이 덜그럭거리는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"동지들!\" 하고 드파르주가 단호한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리는 준비가 됐소?\"\n\n순식간에 마담 드파르주의 칼은 그녀의 허리띠에 꽂혔다. 마치 북과 북 치는 자가 마법으로 함께 날아오기라도 한 듯 거리에서는 북소리가 울리고 있었다. 그리고 복수는 무시무시한 비명을 지르며 마치 사십 명의 복수의 여신을 한꺼번에 합쳐놓은 것처럼 머리 위로 두 팔을 휘둘러대면서 이 집 저 집을 휘젓고 다니며 여자들을 깨우고 있었다.\n\n남자들은 창밖을 내다보고 손에 잡히는 무기를 집어 들고 거리로 쏟아져 나오는 그 피에 굶주린 분노로 끔찍했다. 그러나 여자들은 아무리 대담한 자라도 오싹하게 할 광경이었다. 적나라한 가난이 허락한 정도의 가사 노동에서, 자기 아이들에게서, 헐벗고 굶주린 채 맨땅에 웅크린 노인과 병자에게서 벗어나, 그들은 머리카락을 흩날리며 뛰쳐나와 서로를, 그리고 자신들을 가장 거친 비명과 행동으로 광기로 몰아갔다. 악당 풀롱이 잡혔어, 언니! 늙은 풀롱이 잡혔어, 엄마! 비열한 풀롱이 잡혔어, 얘야! 그러자 또 다른 스무 명쯤이 이들 한가운데로 달려들며 가슴을 치고 머리를 쥐어뜯고 비명을 질렀다, 풀롱이 살아 있다! 굶주린 사람들에게 풀이나 뜯어 먹으라고 한 풀롱이! 내가 드릴 빵이 없을 때 내 늙은 아버지에게 풀이나 드시라고 한 풀롱이! 이 젖가슴이 굶주려 말라붙었을 때 내 아기에게 풀이나 빨라고 한 풀롱이! 오 하느님의 어머니시여, 이 풀롱 놈을! 오 하늘이시여, 우리의 고통을! 들어다오, 죽은 내 아기야, 시들어버린 내 아버지야. 나는 무릎을 꿇고, 이 돌바닥 위에서 맹세한다, 너희를 위해 풀롱에게 복수하겠다고! 남편들이여, 형제들이여, 젊은이들이여, 우리에게 풀롱의 피를 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 머리를 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 심장을 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 몸과 영혼을 다오, 풀롱을 갈가리 찢어 땅에 파묻어라, 그놈에게서 풀이 자라나도록! 이런 외침과 함께, 수많은 여자들이 눈먼 광란에 휘몰려 빙빙 돌며 제 친구들을 후려치고 쥐어뜯다가 격렬한 실신 상태로 쓰러졌고, 자신들에게 딸린 남자들이 아니었더라면 발에 짓밟히지 않을 도리가 없었다.\n\n그럼에도 단 한순간도 허비되지 않았다. 단 한순간도! 이 풀롱은 시청에 있었고, 풀려날지도 몰랐다. 쌩땅뜨완이 자신의 고통과 모욕과 부당함을 안다면, 결코 그럴 수 없었다! 무장한 남자들과 여자들이 어찌나 빠르게 구역에서 쏟아져 나왔는지, 그리고 그 마지막 찌꺼기들까지 어찌나 강한 흡인력으로 끌어당겼는지, 십오 분도 채 못 되어 쌩땅뜨완의 품 안에는 몇몇 늙은 노파들과 울부짖는 아이들 말고는 사람이라곤 하나도 남지 않았다.\n\n그렇다. 그들은 그 무렵 추하고 사악한 이 늙은이가 있던 심문장을 가득 메워 숨 막히게 하고, 인접한 빈터와 거리로 넘쳐흐르고 있었다. 부부인 드파르주 내외와 복수와 자끄 쓰리는 가장 앞쪽 무리에 끼어 있었고, 심문장 안에서 그자와 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있었다.\n\n\"보라고!\" 하고 마담이 칼로 가리키며 외쳤다. \"저 늙은 악당이 밧줄로 묶여 있는 걸 봐. 그자 등에 풀 한 다발을 묶어놓은 건 정말 잘한 일이야. 하, 하! 정말 잘했어. 이제 그놈더러 먹으라고 해!\" 마담은 칼을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 마치 연극을 보듯 두 손뼉을 쳤다.\n\n마담 드파르주 바로 뒤에 있던 사람들이 그녀가 흡족해하는 까닭을 그 뒤의 사람들에게 설명하고, 그들이 다시 다른 이들에게, 그들이 또 다른 이들에게 설명하니, 인근 거리들은 손뼉 소리로 울려 퍼졌다. 마찬가지로, 두세 시간에 걸쳐 늘어지는 동안, 그리고 여러 부셸이나 되는 말을 까부르는 동안, 마담 드파르주가 자주 내비치던 초조함의 표시들이 놀라운 속도로 멀리까지 받아들여졌다. 더욱 손쉽게 그리되었으니, 어떤 놀라운 민첩성으로 건물 외부 구조물을 타고 올라가 창문으로 들여다보던 몇몇 남자들이 마담 드파르주를 잘 알고 있어서, 그녀와 건물 바깥 군중 사이의 전신기 노릇을 했기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 해가 어찌나 높이 솟았던지, 희망이나 보호처럼 자애로운 한 줄기 빛이 늙은 죄수의 머리 바로 위로 곧장 내리쬐었다. 그 은총은 견디기에 너무도 과했다. 한순간에, 놀랍도록 오래 버티고 있던 먼지와 검불의 장벽이 바람결에 흩어졌고, 쌩땅뜨완은 그를 손에 넣었다!\n\n그 사실은 곧장 군중의 가장 먼 끝까지 알려졌다. 드파르주가 난간 하나와 탁자 하나를 뛰어넘어 그 가련한 자를 죽음의 포옹으로 감싸 안았을 뿐이고—마담 드파르주가 그저 뒤따라가 그자를 묶고 있던 밧줄 중 하나에 손을 비틀어 넣었을 뿐이고—복수와 자끄 쓰리는 아직 그들에게 미치지 못했고, 창가의 남자들도 아직 높은 횃대에서 내리덮치는 맹금처럼 심문장 안으로 덮쳐들지 않았을 때—온 도시에 함성이 솟아오르는 듯했다, \"그놈을 끌어내라! 그놈을 등잔대로 끌고 가라!\"\n\n내려갔다가, 올라왔다가, 건물 계단에서 머리부터 곤두박질치고, 어느 때는 무릎을 꿇고, 어느 때는 두 발로 서고, 어느 때는 등을 대고 자빠진 채, 끌려가고, 얻어맞고, 수백 개의 손이 얼굴에 처박는 풀과 짚 다발에 숨이 막히고, 찢기고, 멍들고, 헐떡이고, 피 흘리면서도 줄곧 자비를 애원하고 빌었다. 어느 때는 격렬한 몸부림의 고통에 가득 차서, 사람들이 보려고 서로를 뒤로 끌어당기는 통에 그의 주위로 작은 빈 공간이 생겼고, 어느 때는 다리들의 숲 사이로 끌려가는 죽은 통나무 토막이었다. 그는 치명적인 등잔대 하나가 흔들거리는 가장 가까운 길모퉁이로 끌려갔고, 거기서 마담 드파르주는 그를 놓아주었다—고양이가 쥐를 가지고 놀듯—그러고는 그들이 준비하는 동안, 그리고 그가 그녀에게 애원하는 동안 말없이 침착하게 그를 바라보았다. 그동안 여자들은 줄곧 그에게 격렬하게 악을 써댔고, 남자들은 그놈의 입에 풀을 물린 채 죽이라고 매섭게 소리쳤다. 한번은 그가 위로 올라갔으나 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 그들은 비명을 지르는 그를 받아냈다. 두번째도 그가 위로 올라갔으나 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 그들은 비명을 지르는 그를 받아냈다. 그러더니 밧줄이 자비를 베풀어 그를 붙들었고, 그의 머리는 곧 창끝에 꽂혔으며, 입에는 쌩땅뜨완 전체가 그 광경을 보고 춤을 출 만큼 충분한 풀이 물려 있었다.\n\n이것이 그날의 끔찍한 소행의 끝은 아니었으니, 쌩땅뜨완이 어찌나 소리치고 춤추며 분노의 피를 끓어오르게 했던지, 날이 저물 무렵 처형된 자의 사위가, 또 한 명의 백성의 적이자 능멸자가, 기병만 오백 명에 이르는 호위대에 둘러싸여 빠리로 들어오고 있다는 소식을 듣자 그 피가 다시 끓어올랐다. 쌩땅뜨완은 그의 죄목을 활활 타오르는 종이에 적어 그를 붙잡았고—풀롱과 짝을 지어주려고 군대의 품 안에서라도 그를 잡아 뜯어냈을 것이며—그의 머리와 심장을 창끝에 꽂아, 그날의 세 가지 전리품을 늑대의 행렬로 거리거리 들고 다녔다.\n\n칠흑 같은 밤이 되어서야 남자들과 여자들은 울부짖으며 빵 한 조각 없는 아이들에게 돌아왔다. 그러고 나서, 그 비참한 빵집들 앞에는 형편없는 빵을 사려고 참을성 있게 기다리는 긴 줄이 늘어섰다. 그들은 어지럽고 텅 빈 위장으로 기다리는 동안, 그날의 승리에 대해 서로를 끌어안고, 그것들을 잡담으로 다시 이루어내면서 시간을 달랬다. 차츰 이 누더기 차림 사람들의 줄은 짧아지고 풀어져 흩어졌다. 그러고 나서 높은 창문들에 초라한 불빛이 비치기 시작했고, 거리에는 가냘픈 불이 피워졌으며, 그 곁에서 이웃들이 함께 음식을 지어 나중에 제 집 문간에서 저녁을 먹었다.\n\n빈약하고 모자란 저녁 식사였으니, 비참한 빵에 곁들일 다른 대다수 양념은 물론이요 고기라곤 한 점도 없었다. 그래도 인간의 동료애가 그 돌처럼 딱딱한 음식에 약간의 자양분을 불어넣었고, 거기서 약간의 명랑한 불꽃을 튀겨냈다. 그날의 가장 험한 일에 제 몫을 톡톡히 치렀던 아버지와 어머니들은 야윈 아이들과 다정하게 놀아주었고, 연인들은 그런 세상을 주위에, 그리고 앞에 두고도 사랑하고 희망을 품었다.\n\n거의 아침이 다 되어서야 드파르주의 술집은 마지막 손님 무리를 떠나보냈고, 무슈 드파르주는 문을 잠그면서 아내인 마담에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"마침내 그날이 왔구려, 여보!\"\n\n\"글쎄요!\" 하고 마담이 대답했다. \"거의 다 왔지요.\"\n\n쌩땅뜨완은 잠들었고, 드파르주 내외도 잠들었다. 복수마저 굶주린 식료품상 남편과 함께 잠들었고, 북도 쉬고 있었다. 피와 소동이 바꿔놓지 못한 쌩땅뜨완의 유일한 목소리는 북소리였다. 북의 관리자인 복수는 그것을 깨워 바스띠유가 함락되기 전이나 늙은 풀롱이 잡히기 전과 똑같은 말을 시킬 수 있었으리라. 그러나 쌩땅뜨완의 품 안에 있는 남자들과 여자들의 쉰 목소리는 그렇지 못했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII. Fire Rises\n\n분수가 떨어지던 그 마을, 그리고 도로 보수공이 날마다 나가 큰길의 돌에서 자신의 가련하고 무지한 영혼과 가련하게 쪼그라든 몸뚱이를 한데 붙들어 맬 만한 빵 조각이나마 망치로 두드려 내던 그 마을에, 어떤 변화가 일어났다. 바위 위의 감옥은 예전만큼 위압적이지 않았다. 그것을 지키는 병사들이 있었으나 많지 않았고, 그 병사들을 지키는 장교들이 있었으나, 그 가운데 누구도 자기 부하들이 무슨 짓을 할지 알지 못했다. 다만 그것이 자신이 명령한 것과는 아마도 다르리라는 것만은 알았다.\n\n멀리까지 폐허가 된 땅이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 땅은 황폐함 말고는 아무것도 내지 못했다. 푸른 잎새 하나하나, 풀잎과 곡식 줄기 하나하나가 저 비참한 사람들만큼이나 시들고 빈약했다. 모든 것이 고개를 숙이고, 의기소침하고, 짓눌리고, 부서져 있었다. 거처도, 울타리도, 가축들도, 남자들과 여자들과 아이들도, 그리고 그들을 길러낸 흙마저도 — 모두가 닳아 빠져 있었다.\n\n영주님은(흔히 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 개별 신사이기도 했다) 국가적 축복이었고, 만사에 기사도적 기품을 부여했으며, 사치스럽고 찬란한 삶의 정중한 본보기였고, 그밖에도 같은 취지의 많은 것을 의미했다. 그럼에도 하나의 계급으로서 영주님은 어찌어찌하여 사태를 이 지경으로 몰고 왔다. 오로지 영주님을 위해 마련된 천지창조가 이토록 빨리 쥐어짜이고 말라붙어 버리다니 기이한 일이었다! 영원한 섭리 안에 분명 무언가 근시안적인 데가 있었던 것이리라! 그러나 사실이 그러했다. 그리하여 부싯돌에서 마지막 핏방울까지 짜내고, 고문대의 나사가 하도 여러 번 돌려진 나머지 물려 있던 자리가 으스러져 이제는 아무것도 물지 못한 채 헛돌고 또 헛돌게 되자, 영주님은 그토록 비천하고 설명할 수 없는 현상으로부터 달아나기 시작했다.\n\n그러나 이것이 그 마을과, 그와 비슷한 수많은 마을에 일어난 변화는 아니었다. 지난 수십 년 동안 영주님은 마을을 쥐어짜고 비틀었으되, 사냥의 즐거움을 위해서가 아니고서는 좀처럼 그곳을 자신의 행차로 빛내는 일이 없었다. 어떤 때는 사람들을 사냥하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았고, 어떤 때는 짐승들을 사냥하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았으니, 영주님은 그 짐승들의 보존을 위해 야만적이고 메마른 황무지를 교화적인 공간으로 마련해 두었다. 아니다. 그 변화란, 깎아 다듬어진 데다 여러모로 아름답게 꾸며지고 또 주위를 아름답게 하던 영주님의 높은 신분의 이목구비가 사라진 데 있다기보다는, 낮은 신분의 낯선 얼굴들이 나타난 데 있었다.\n\n이 시절에는, 도로 보수공이 먼지 속에서 홀로 일하면서, 자신이 흙에서 났으니 흙으로 돌아가야 한다는 사실을 곰곰이 되새기느라 마음 쓰는 일은 드물었다. 대개는 저녁거리가 얼마나 보잘것없는지, 그것이 있다면 얼마나 더 먹어 치울 수 있을지를 궁리하느라 너무 바빴기 때문이다. 그런 시절에, 그가 외로운 노동에서 눈을 들어 앞을 바라보면, 어떤 거친 형체가 걸어서 다가오는 것을 보곤 했다. 한때 그 일대에서는 좀처럼 보기 드물던 그런 모습이 이제는 흔히 나타나게 된 것이다. 그것이 가까이 다가오면, 도로 보수공은 놀라는 기색도 없이 알아보았다. 그것은 봉두난발에 거의 야만인 같은 풍모를 한 키 큰 사내로, 도로 보수공의 눈에도 투박해 보이는 나막신을 신고 있었으며, 험상궂고 거칠며 가무잡잡하고, 숱한 큰길의 진흙과 먼지에 절어 있었고, 숱한 저지대의 습기로 축축했으며, 숲을 지나는 숱한 샛길의 가시와 잎과 이끼를 뒤집어쓰고 있었다.\n\n그런 사내 하나가, 7월 어느 한낮에 유령처럼 그에게 나타났다. 그가 우박을 피해 둑 아래 돌무더기 위에 앉아 그나마 얻을 수 있는 만큼의 비바람막이를 삼고 있던 때였다.\n\n사내는 그를 바라보았고, 골짜기의 마을을, 물방앗간을, 그리고 바위 위의 감옥을 바라보았다. 그가 어둑한 머릿속으로 이 대상들을 가려낸 뒤, 겨우 알아들을 만한 사투리로 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 지내나, 자끄?\"\n\n\"다 잘 지내네, 자끄.\"\n\n\"그럼, 손!\"\n\n그들은 손을 맞잡았고, 사내는 돌무더기 위에 앉았다.\n\n\"점심은 없나?\"\n\n\"이젠 저녁밖에 없다네.\" 도로 보수공이 굶주린 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"그게 요즘 유행이지.\" 사내가 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"어디를 가도 점심이라곤 만날 수가 없으니.\"\n\n그는 거뭇해진 파이프를 꺼내 담배를 채우고, 부싯돌과 쇳조각으로 불을 붙여, 환히 타오를 때까지 빨아들였다. 그러더니 별안간 파이프를 입에서 떼어, 엄지와 검지 사이에 쥐고 있던 무언가를 그 안에 떨어뜨렸는데, 그것은 확 타오르더니 한 줄기 연기 속에 꺼져 버렸다.\n\n\"그럼, 손.\" 이번에는 이 동작들을 지켜본 도로 보수공이 그렇게 말할 차례였다. 그들은 다시 손을 맞잡았다.\n\n\"오늘 밤인가?\" 도로 보수공이 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤이지.\" 사내가 파이프를 입에 물며 말했다.\n\n\"어디서?\"\n\n\"여기서.\"\n\n그와 도로 보수공은 돌무더기 위에 앉아 말없이 서로를 바라보았고, 그동안 우박이 마치 난쟁이 총검대의 돌격처럼 둘 사이로 들이쳤다. 그러다 마을 위로 하늘이 개기 시작했다.\n\n\"보여주게!\" 그러자 나그네가 언덕 마루로 옮겨가며 말했다.\n\n\"보게나!\" 도로 보수공이 손가락을 펴 가리키며 대답했다. \"여기로 내려가서, 거리를 곧장 가로질러, 분수를 지나면—\"\n\n\"그딴 건 다 악마한테나 가버리라지!\" 상대가 풍경 위로 눈길을 굴리며 말을 끊었다. \"나는 거리도 가로지르지 않고 분수도 지나지 않아. 그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서! 마을 위 저 언덕 꼭대기에서 두 리외쯤 더 간 곳일세.\"\n\n\"좋아. 자네는 언제 일을 마치나?\"\n\n\"해질녘에.\"\n\n\"떠나기 전에 나를 깨워 주겠나? 이틀 밤을 쉬지 않고 걸었네. 파이프를 다 피우게 해주면, 어린애처럼 잘 거야. 깨워 주겠나?\"\n\n\"물론이지.\"\n\n나그네는 파이프를 다 피우고 나서 그것을 품에 넣고, 커다란 나막신을 벗어 던지고는, 돌무더기 위에 벌렁 드러누웠다. 그는 곧장 깊이 잠들었다.\n\n도로 보수공이 먼지투성이 일을 계속하는 동안, 우박을 머금은 구름이 굴러가며 밝은 띠와 줄무늬 같은 하늘을 드러냈고, 그에 화답하듯 풍경 위로 은빛 광채가 어렸다. 그 작은 사내는(이제는 파란 모자 대신 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었다) 돌무더기 위의 형체에 홀린 듯했다. 그의 눈은 하도 자주 그쪽으로 향한 나머지, 그는 연장을 기계적으로 놀렸으니, 누가 보든 일은 형편없이 진행되는 셈이었다. 청동빛 얼굴, 봉두난발의 검은 머리칼과 수염, 거친 양모로 짠 빨간 모자, 손으로 짠 천과 짐승의 털가죽이 뒤섞인 투박한 옷차림, 변변찮은 끼니로 야위어 버린 강건한 골격, 그리고 잠결에도 부루퉁하고 결연하게 다물린 입술은 도로 보수공에게 외경심을 불러일으켰다. 나그네는 멀리서부터 걸어온 터라 발이 부르트고, 발목은 쓸려서 피가 났다. 잎과 풀로 채운 그의 커다란 신발은 그 숱한 긴 리외를 끌고 오기에 무거웠고, 그의 옷은 쓸려서 구멍이 났으니, 그 자신이 헐어 상처가 난 것과 마찬가지였다. 도로 보수공은 그의 곁에 몸을 굽히고, 그의 품속이나 어딘가에 감춰진 무기를 슬쩍 엿보려 했으나 헛수고였으니, 사내는 두 팔을 가슴 위에 엇갈려 얹은 채, 그 입술만큼이나 단호하게 잠들어 있었다. 도로 보수공이 보기에, 방책과 위병소와 성문과 참호와 도개교를 갖춘 요새 도시도 이 형체에 견주면 한낱 허공에 지나지 않을 듯했다. 그가 그 형체에서 지평선으로 눈을 들어 사방을 둘러보자, 그의 작은 상상 속에서, 어떤 장애물에도 멈추지 않고 프랑스 곳곳의 중심지들을 향해 나아가는 비슷한 형체들이 보였다.\n\n사내는 계속 잠들어 있었으니, 우박이 쏟아지든 잠깐씩 날이 개든, 얼굴에 햇살이 내리든 그늘이 지든, 둔탁한 얼음덩이가 몸 위로 후두둑 떨어지든 햇빛이 그것을 다이아몬드로 바꾸어 놓든, 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그러다 해가 서편으로 낮게 기울고 하늘이 붉게 타오를 무렵, 도로 보수공은 연장을 챙기고 마을로 내려갈 채비를 다 마친 뒤 그를 깨웠다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 잠자던 사내가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"언덕 꼭대기에서 두 리외 더 간 곳이라고?\"\n\n\"대략 그쯤이지.\"\n\n\"대략 그쯤. 좋아!\"\n도로 보수공은 바람결 따라 앞서 날리는 먼지를 거느리고 집으로 돌아가, 이내 분수에 이르렀고, 물을 마시러 그곳에 끌려온 비쩍 마른 소떼 틈에 끼어들었으며, 마을 전체에 속삭이듯 그 소들에게도 속삭이는 듯 보였다. 마을이 그 보잘것없는 저녁을 먹고 난 뒤, 여느 때처럼 슬며시 잠자리로 기어들지 않고, 다시 문밖으로 나와 거기 머물러 있었다. 기묘한 속삭임의 전염이 마을에 번졌고, 또한 어둠 속 분수가에 모여들었을 때는, 오직 한 방향으로만 기대에 차서 하늘을 바라보는 또 하나의 기묘한 전염이 번졌다. 그곳의 우두머리 관리인 가벨 씨는 불안해졌다. 홀로 자기 집 지붕에 올라가 그 역시 그쪽을 바라보았고, 굴뚝 뒤에서 아래쪽 분수가의 어두워져 가는 얼굴들을 내려다보았으며, 교회 열쇠를 지닌 성당지기에게 머지않아 경종을 울려야 할지도 모른다고 전갈을 보냈다.\n\n밤이 깊어 갔다. 외따로 떨어진 그 적막한 상태를 지키던 옛 성을 둘러싼 나무들이, 점점 거세지는 바람결에 흔들리며, 마치 어둠 속에 육중하고 검게 솟은 그 건물 더미를 위협하는 듯했다. 두 단의 테라스 계단을 따라 빗물이 사납게 흘러내려 큰 문을 두드렸으니, 마치 안에 있는 자들을 깨우려는 잰 사자(使者) 같았다. 불안한 바람이 휙휙 홀을 가로질러 낡은 창과 칼들 사이를 지나, 탄식하듯 계단을 타고 올라가, 마지막 후작이 잠들었던 침대의 휘장을 흔들었다. 동, 서, 남, 북으로, 숲을 지나, 무겁게 발을 디디는 봉두난발의 형체 넷이 키 큰 풀을 짓밟고 나뭇가지를 부러뜨리며, 안마당에서 한데 모이려고 조심스레 성큼성큼 나아갔다. 거기서 불빛 넷이 일더니, 제각기 다른 방향으로 옮겨갔고, 다시 온통 캄캄해졌다.\n\n그러나 오래가지는 않았다. 이윽고 성은 무언가 제 안의 빛으로, 마치 스스로 환히 빛을 내듯 기이하게 모습을 드러내기 시작했다. 그러더니 깜박이는 한 줄기 빛이 정면 건축물 뒤에서 어른거리며, 빛이 투과되는 곳들을 짚어내어, 난간과 아치와 창문이 어디 있는지를 드러냈다. 그러더니 그 빛은 더 높이 솟아오르며, 더 넓고 더 밝아졌다. 이내 스무남은 개의 큰 창문에서 불길이 터져 나왔고, 돌로 된 얼굴들이 깨어나, 불 속에서 노려보았다.\n\n거기 남아 있던 몇 안 되는 사람들로부터 집 주위로 희미한 웅성거림이 일었고, 말 한 마리에 안장을 얹어 타고 달려 나가는 일이 벌어졌다. 어둠을 뚫고 박차를 가하며 첨벙거리는 소리가 났고, 마을 분수가의 공터에서 고삐가 당겨졌으며, 거품을 문 말이 가벨 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다. \"도와주시오, 가벨! 모두들 도와주시오!\" 경종이 다급하게 울렸으나, 다른 도움은(그것이 도움이라 할 수 있다면) 전혀 없었다. 도로 보수공과 이백쉰 명의 각별한 벗들은 팔짱을 낀 채 분수가에 서서, 하늘로 치솟는 불기둥을 바라보았다. \"마흔 자는 되겠군.\" 그들은 험상궂게 말했고, 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다.\n\n성에서 온 기수와 거품을 문 말은 마을을 지나 요란하게 달려, 돌투성이 비탈을 질주해 올라가, 바위 위의 감옥에 이르렀다. 성문에서는 한 무리의 장교들이 불을 바라보고 있었고, 그들로부터 떨어진 곳에 한 무리의 병사들이 있었다. \"도와주십시오, 장교 나리들! 성에 불이 났습니다. 제때에 손을 쓰면 값진 물건들을 불길에서 건질 수 있습니다! 도와주십시오, 도와주십시오!\" 장교들은 불을 바라보는 병사들 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸고, 아무런 명령도 내리지 않았으며, 어깨를 으쓱하고 입술을 깨물며 대답했다. \"타게 내버려 두는 수밖에.\"\n\n기수가 다시 언덕을 덜그럭거리며 내려와 거리를 지날 무렵, 마을은 환하게 밝혀지고 있었다. 도로 보수공과 그 이백쉰 명의 각별한 벗들은, 불을 밝히자는 생각에 마치 한 사람의 남자와 한 사람의 여자처럼 의기투합하여, 저마다 집으로 달려 들어가, 흐릿한 유리창마다 촛불을 꽂고 있었다. 만물이 두루 모자라던 터라, 촛불은 가벨 씨에게서 다소 강압적인 방식으로 빌려 왔다. 그 관리가 마지못해 머뭇거리던 순간, 한때 그토록 권위에 고분고분하던 도로 보수공이, 마차란 모닥불 피우기에 좋고, 역마(驛馬)란 구워 먹기에 좋겠다고 한마디 던졌다.\n\n성은 제멋대로 타오르고 불사르도록 내버려졌다. 활활 타오르며 사납게 미쳐 날뛰는 불길 속에서, 지옥에서 곧장 불어오는 듯한 시뻘겋게 달아오른 바람이 그 건물을 통째로 날려 보내는 듯했다. 화염이 솟구쳤다 가라앉음에 따라, 돌로 된 얼굴들은 마치 고통에 시달리는 것처럼 보였다. 커다란 돌덩이와 목재가 우르르 무너져 내릴 때면, 코에 두 군데 움푹 팬 자국이 있는 그 얼굴이 가려졌다가, 이윽고 다시 연기 속에서 헤쳐 나왔으니, 마치 화형대에서 불타며 불길과 다투는 잔인한 후작의 얼굴 같았다.\n\n성이 불탔다. 가장 가까운 나무들은 불길에 사로잡혀 그슬리고 오그라들었고, 멀리 있는 나무들은 사나운 형체 넷에 의해 불붙어, 타오르는 건물을 새로운 연기의 숲으로 에워쌌다. 녹은 납과 쇠가 분수의 대리석 수반 안에서 끓어올랐고, 물은 말라 버렸으며, 탑 꼭대기의 소화구(消火具) 모양 첨탑들은 열기 앞에서 얼음처럼 사라져, 우락부락한 화염의 우물 넷으로 녹아 흘러내렸다. 단단한 벽에는 마치 결정(結晶)이 자라나듯 커다란 균열과 틈새가 갈라져 뻗어 나갔고, 얼이 빠진 새들은 빙빙 맴돌다 화덕 속으로 떨어졌다. 사나운 형체 넷은 동, 서, 남, 북으로, 밤에 휩싸인 길을 따라 터벅터벅 떠나갔으니, 자기네가 밝혀 놓은 봉화의 인도를 받아 다음 목적지를 향했다. 환하게 밝혀진 마을은 경종을 거머쥐었고, 합법적인 종지기를 내쫓고서, 기쁨에 겨워 종을 울렸다.\n\n그뿐이 아니었다. 굶주림과 불길과 종소리에 들떠 어질어질해진 마을은, 가벨 씨가 지대와 세금 징수에 관여한다는 데 생각이 미치자—그가 근래에 거둬들인 것이라곤 세금의 일부에 지나지 않았고, 지대는 전혀 없었지만—그와의 면담을 안달하며 갈망하게 되었고, 그의 집을 에워싸고는 몸소 나와 대면하라고 그를 불러댔다. 그러자 가벨 씨는 자기 집 문을 단단히 빗장 지르고 물러나, 혼자서 궁리를 거듭했다. 그 숙고의 결과, 가벨은 다시 굴뚝 무더기 뒤편 지붕으로 물러났는데, 이번에는 만일 문이 부서져 들어오면(그는 보복심이 강한 기질의 자그마한 남부 사람이었다), 난간 너머로 머리부터 곤두박질쳐서 아래에 있는 한두 사람을 깔아뭉개기로 작심한 터였다.\n\n아마 가벨 씨는 그 위에서 긴 밤을 지새웠을 것이다. 멀리 보이는 성을 불이자 촛불로 삼고, 문을 두드려대는 소리에 환희의 종소리가 어우러진 것을 음악으로 삼은 채로 말이다. 게다가 그의 역참 문 앞 길 위에는 불길한 등이 가로질러 매달려 있었으니, 마을은 그를 위해 친히 그것을 옮겨 주려는 발랄한 의향을 드러내 보였다. 검은 바다의 벼랑 끝에서 여름밤 내내, 가벨 씨가 결심한 그 몸 던지기를 언제라도 감행할 채비를 한 채 버틴다는 것은, 참으로 견디기 힘든 마음 졸임이었으리라! 그러나 마침내 정다운 새벽이 밝아 오고, 마을의 골풀 촛불들이 깜박거리며 꺼져가자, 사람들은 다행히도 흩어졌고, 가벨 씨는 그 한동안 제 목숨을 부지한 채 지붕에서 내려왔다.\n\n백 마일 안쪽에서, 다른 불빛 아래에서는, 그날 밤과 다른 밤들에 그만큼 운이 좋지 못한 다른 관리들이 있었으니, 떠오르는 해는 자기들이 나고 자란 한때 평화롭던 거리에 매달려 있는 그들을 발견했다. 또한 도로 보수공과 그 동료들만큼 운이 좋지 못한 다른 마을 사람들과 읍내 사람들도 있었으니, 관리들과 군대가 그들에게 성공적으로 맞서, 이번에는 그들을 줄줄이 매달았다. 그러나 사나운 형체들은 어찌 되었든 한결같이 동, 서, 남, 북으로 나아가고 있었다. 그리고 누가 매달리든 간에, 불은 타올랐다. 어떤 관리도, 수학을 아무리 끌어다 써본들, 그 불을 물로 바꾸어 꺼버릴 만한 교수대의 높이를 끝내 계산해 낼 수 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXIV. Drawn to the Loadstone Rock\n\n이렇게 불길이 치솟고 바다가 솟구치는 가운데 — 굳건한 대지가, 이제는 썰물이라곤 없이 늘 밀물로만, 점점 더 높이 차오르며 해변에서 지켜보는 이들에게 공포와 경이를 안기던 성난 대양의 거센 돌격에 흔들리는 가운데 — 세 해의 폭풍이 다 타들어 갔다. 어린 루씨의 생일 세 번이 또 황금실에 엮여 그 집안의 평화로운 삶의 직물 속으로 짜여 들어갔다.\n\n수많은 밤과 수많은 낮 동안 그 집의 식구들은 모퉁이의 메아리에 귀를 기울였고, 떼 지어 몰려드는 발소리를 들을 때면 가슴이 내려앉았다. 그 발소리가 이제는 그들의 마음속에서, 붉은 깃발 아래 소란을 떨며 조국이 위기에 처했다고 선포된 가운데, 오래도록 끈질기게 이어진 끔찍한 마법에 걸려 야수로 변해버린 한 민족의 발소리처럼 여겨졌기 때문이다.\n\n므슈* 영주들은 하나의 계급으로서, 자신들이 평가받지 못하는 현상으로부터 발을 떼어버렸다. 프랑스에서 자신들이 너무도 쓸모없어진 나머지, 그곳으로부터, 그리고 이 삶 자체로부터 해고당할 상당한 위험에 처해 있다는 현상으로부터 말이다. 무한한 노고를 들여 악마를 불러냈으나 정작 그 모습을 보자 너무도 겁에 질려 적에게 아무런 질문도 하지 못한 채 곧장 도망쳐버렸다는 우화 속의 그 시골뜨기처럼, 므슈 영주들도 여러 해 동안 대담하게 주기도문을 거꾸로 외고 사악한 자를 불러내려 다른 온갖 강력한 주문을 행하다가, 막상 그 무시무시한 모습을 보자마자 고귀한 두 발로 줄행랑을 쳤다.\n\n궁정의 빛나는 황소 눈*은 사라졌으니, 그렇지 않았다면 국민이 쏘아대는 총탄의 폭풍 속 표적이 되었으리라. 그것은 결코 잘 보는 눈이었던 적이 없었으니 — 오래도록 루씨퍼의 오만과 사르다나팔루스의 사치와 두더지의 맹목이라는 티가 박혀 있었다 — 그러나 그것은 빠져나가 사라져버렸다. 궁정은, 그 폐쇄적인 내부 핵심으로부터 음모와 부패와 위선으로 이루어진 가장 바깥의 썩어빠진 고리에 이르기까지, 모조리 함께 사라져버렸다. 왕권도 사라졌으니, 마지막 소식이 전해졌을 때는 궁전에서 포위된 채 \"정지\"되어 있었다.\n\n* 황소 눈: 천장의 둥근 채광창을 가리키는 동시에 궁정의 권력자들을 비유한 표현.\n1792년의 8월이 왔고, 므슈 영주들은 이 무렵 멀리멀리 흩어져 있었다.\n\n당연한 일이지만, 런던에서 므슈 영주들의 본부이자 큰 집결지는 뗄슨 은행이었다. 영혼은 그 육신이 가장 자주 드나들던 장소를 떠돈다고들 하는데, 한 닢의 기니도 없는 므슈 영주들은 자신들의 기니가 놓여 있던 그 자리를 떠돌았다. 게다가 그곳은 가장 믿을 만한 프랑스 소식이 가장 빨리 당도하는 자리이기도 했다. 또한 뗄슨 은행은 후한 곳이어서, 높은 지위에서 추락한 옛 고객들에게 넉넉한 아량을 베풀었다. 또한 다가오는 폭풍을 제때 알아채고 약탈이나 몰수를 예견하여 미리 뗄슨에 송금해둔 귀족들의 소식은, 궁핍해진 동료들이 언제든 그곳에서 들을 수 있었다. 거기에 덧붙여, 프랑스에서 오는 모든 새 인물은 거의 당연한 일처럼 뗄슨에서 자신의 신원과 소식을 알렸다. 이렇듯 다양한 이유로, 뗄슨은 그 무렵 프랑스 소식에 관한 한 일종의 거대한 거래소였다. 그리고 이것은 대중에게 너무도 잘 알려져 있어서 그곳에 들어오는 문의가 그만큼 빈번했던 까닭에, 뗄슨은 이따금 최신 소식을 한두 줄로 적어 은행 창문에 게시하여, 템플 바를 지나는 모든 이가 읽을 수 있게 했다.\n\n후텁지근하고 안개 낀 어느 오후, 로리 씨는 책상 앞에 앉아 있었고 찰스 다네이는 거기에 기대선 채 낮은 목소리로 그와 이야기를 나누었다. 한때 은행과의 면담을 위해 따로 마련되었던 그 참회의 골방은 이제 소식의 거래소가 되어 넘칠 듯이 가득 차 있었다. 마감 시각까지는 반 시간 남짓 남아 있었다.\n\n\"하지만, 비록 세상에 없을 만큼 젊으신 분이라 해도,\" 찰스 다네이가 다소 망설이며 말했다, \"저는 그래도 말씀드려야겠습니다 —\"\n\n\"알겠네. 내가 너무 늙었다는 거지?\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"날씨도 변덕스럽고, 여정은 멀고, 이동 수단은 불확실하고, 나라는 혼란에 빠졌고, 도시는 어르신께 안전하지조차 않을지 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"이보게 찰스,\" 로리 씨가 쾌활한 확신을 담아 말했다, \"자네가 짚은 건 내가 가야 하는 이유들이지, 멀리 떨어져 있어야 하는 이유가 아닐세. 나한테는 충분히 안전해. 거기에 훨씬 더 간섭할 만한 가치가 있는 사람이 그렇게 많은데, 누가 여든을 코앞에 둔 늙은이를 굳이 건드리려 하겠나. 혼란에 빠진 도시라는 점에 대해서라면, 혼란에 빠진 도시가 아니라면 그 도시와 사정을 예전부터 잘 알고 뗄슨의 신임을 받는 누군가를 여기 우리 은행에서 그곳 우리 은행으로 보낼 필요도 없겠지. 불확실한 이동이며 먼 여정이며 겨울 날씨에 대해서라면, 이 오랜 세월이 흐른 마당에 뗄슨을 위해 약간의 불편을 감수할 각오가 안 된다면, 대체 누가 그래야 한단 말인가?\"\n\n\"제가 직접 갈 수 있다면 좋으련만,\" 찰스 다네이가 다소 안절부절못하며, 마치 혼잣말을 입 밖에 내는 사람처럼 말했다.\n\n\"이런! 반대하고 충고하기 좋아하는 양반일세!\" 로리 씨가 외쳤다. \"자네가 직접 가고 싶다고? 프랑스 태생인 자네가? 자네야말로 현명한 조언자로구먼.\"\n\n\"이보세요 로리 씨, 바로 제가 프랑스 태생이기 때문에 그 생각이(물론 여기서 입 밖에 낼 생각은 없었습니다만) 종종 머릿속을 스쳐갔던 겁니다. 저 비참한 사람들에게 얼마간 연민을 느꼈고, 그들에게 무언가를 내려놓은 사람으로서,\" 그는 여기서 예전의 사색적인 태도로 돌아가 말했다, \"내가 가면 사람들이 귀를 기울여줄지도 모르고, 어느 정도 자제하도록 설득할 힘이 내게 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 하지 않을 수 없지요. 바로 어젯밤에도, 어르신께서 우리 곁을 떠나신 뒤에 제가 루씨와 이야기하다가 —\"\n\n\"자네가 루씨와 이야기하다가,\" 로리 씨가 그 말을 되받았다. \"그렇지. 자네가 루씨라는 이름을 입에 올리면서 부끄럽지 않다니 놀랍군! 이 시국에 프랑스로 가고 싶다고 하다니!\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 가지 않습니다,\" 찰스 다네이가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"어르신께서 가신다고 하시는 게 더 중요하지요.\"\n\n\"가고말고, 솔직히 말해 정말로 가네. 사실은 말이지, 이보게 찰스,\" 로리 씨는 멀리 있는 은행 쪽을 흘끗 보고는 목소리를 낮추었다, \"우리 업무가 얼마나 어렵게 처리되고 있는지, 또 저쪽에 있는 우리 장부와 서류가 얼마나 위태로운 처지에 놓여 있는지, 자네는 짐작도 못 할 걸세. 우리 문서 가운데 일부라도 압수되거나 파기된다면 수많은 사람들에게 얼마나 위험한 결과가 닥칠지는 하느님만이 아실 일이야. 게다가 그런 일은 언제든 일어날 수 있어, 알다시피, 빠리가 오늘 불바다가 되지 않으리라고, 내일 약탈당하지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠나! 이제 그것들 가운데서 가능한 한 지체 없이 신중하게 골라내어 묻어버리든가, 아니면 다른 방법으로 화를 면하게 하는 일은 (귀중한 시간을 허비하지 않고서) 나 말고는 거의 아무도, 있다 한들 거의 아무도 해낼 수 없는 일이야. 뗄슨이 이 사실을 알고 이렇게 말하는데 — 내가 육십 년 동안 빵을 얻어먹은 뗄슨이 말일세 — 관절이 좀 뻣뻣하다고 해서 내가 뒷걸음질 쳐야겠나? 원, 여기 늙다리 영감 대여섯에 비하면 나는 애송이일세!\"\n\n\"그 젊디젊은 기개가 어찌나 존경스러운지요, 로리 씨.\"\n\n\"쯧! 허튼소리 말게!— 그리고 이보게 찰스,\" 로리 씨가 다시 은행 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다, \"기억해두게나, 지금 이 시국에 빠리에서 무언가를 빼내 오는 일은, 그게 무엇이든 거의 불가능에 가깝다는 걸 말일세. 바로 오늘도 서류며 귀중품이 여기 우리에게 도착했는데(엄히 비밀로 하는 얘길세, 자네한테조차 귓속말로 흘리는 건 사업가답지 못한 일이지), 그걸 가져온 자들은 자네가 상상도 못 할 만큼 기이한 사람들이었고, 하나같이 관문을 지날 때 목이 머리카락 한 올에 달랑 매달린 처지였다네. 다른 때 같으면 우리 소포가 사업가다운 옛 잉글랜드에서처럼 손쉽게 오갔겠지만, 지금은 모든 게 막혀버렸어.\"\n\n\"그래서 정말로 오늘 밤에 떠나십니까?\"\n\n\"정말로 오늘 밤에 떠나네, 사정이 너무 다급해져서 지체할 수가 없게 되었으니.\"\n\n\"그래서 아무도 데려가지 않으십니까?\"\n\n\"온갖 사람을 추천받았지만, 그중 누구와도 상관하고 싶지 않네. 제리를 데려갈 작정이야. 제리는 오랫동안 일요일 밤마다 내 경호원 노릇을 해왔으니 나도 그에게 익숙하지. 제리를 두고 영국 불도그가 아닌 다른 무엇이라고, 또는 제 주인을 건드리는 자에게 달려드는 것 말고 다른 속셈을 품고 있다고 의심할 사람은 아무도 없을 걸세.\"\n\n\"다시 말씀드리지만, 어르신의 그 기개와 젊음에 진심으로 감탄합니다.\"\n\n\"다시 말하지만, 허튼소리, 허튼소리야! 이 자그마한 임무를 끝내고 나면, 어쩌면 은퇴해서 편히 살라는 뗄슨의 제안을 받아들일지도 모르지. 그때 가서 늙어가는 일을 생각해도 늦지 않아.\"\n\n이 대화는 로리 씨의 늘 쓰는 책상에서 오갔는데, 한두 야드 안쪽으로는 므슈 영주들이 떼 지어 들끓으며 머지않아 그 악당 같은 민중에게 어떻게 복수할 것인지를 큰소리로 떠벌리고 있었다. 망명자로서 역경에 처한 므슈 영주들의 방식이란 너무도 한결같았고, 잉글랜드 토박이의 정통 보수주의의 방식이란 한층 더 한결같았으니, 이 끔찍한 혁명을 마치 하늘 아래 씨 뿌리지 않고도 거둔 유일한 수확이라도 되는 양 — 마치 그것을 초래한 어떤 일도 행해진 적 없고 빠뜨린 적도 없다는 양 — 마치 프랑스의 그 비참한 수백만 군중과, 그들을 번영케 했어야 마땅했으나 오용되고 망가진 자원을 지켜본 이들이 몇 해 전부터 그 일이 불가피하게 다가옴을 보지 못했고, 자신들이 본 바를 명백한 말로 기록해두지 않았다는 양 떠들어댔다. 그런 허풍은, 이미 스스로 완전히 소진되어 하늘과 땅까지 자신과 함께 닳아버린 상태를 복원하겠다는 므슈 영주들의 터무니없는 음모와 뒤섞여, 진실을 아는 제정신의 사람이라면 누구든 얼마간 항변하지 않고서는 견디기 힘든 것이었다. 그리고 사방에서 귓전을 울리는 그런 허풍이, 마치 제 머릿속에서 피가 거슬러 솟구치는 듯한 성가신 혼란처럼, 마음속에 잠복해 있던 불안에 더해져, 찰스 다네이를 이미 안절부절못하게 만들었고 여전히 그를 그렇게 붙들어두고 있었다.\n\n떠드는 무리 가운데에는 왕좌 법정 변호사인 스트라이버가 있었는데, 그는 국가의 요직으로 한참 출세 가도를 달리고 있던 터라 이 주제에 대해 목청을 높였다. 그는 므슈 영주들에게 민중을 폭파시켜 지상에서 절멸시키고 그들 없이 해나갈 자신의 방안을, 그리고 독수리 떼의 꼬리에 소금을 뿌려 그 종족을 없애겠다는 발상과 본질적으로 비슷한 여러 목표를 이룰 방안을 늘어놓았다. 다네이는 특별한 거부감을 느끼며 그의 말을 들었다. 그러고는 더 듣지 않으려 자리를 떠야 할지, 아니면 남아서 한마디 끼어들어야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있는데, 마침 일어나기로 되어 있던 그 일이 점차 모습을 갖추어 나갔다.\n\n은행 측이 로리 씨에게 다가와 더럽혀진 채 개봉되지 않은 편지 한 통을 그 앞에 내려놓으며, 수신인의 행방에 대해 아직 어떤 단서도 찾지 못했는지 물었다. 은행 측이 편지를 다네이 바로 가까이에 내려놓았기에 그는 수신처를 보았는데 — 그것이 바로 자신의 본명이었던 까닭에 더욱 재빨리 알아보았다. 그 주소를 영어로 옮기면 이러했다.\n\n\"매우 긴급함. 프랑스의 전(前) 후작 므슈 쌩떼브레몽드 귀하. 잉글랜드 런던 소재 뗄슨 상회 은행가 제씨에게 위탁함.\"\n\n결혼식 날 아침, 마네뜨 의사는 찰스 다네이에게, 자신 즉 의사가 그 의무를 풀어주지 않는 한, 이 이름의 비밀을 두 사람 사이에서 절대로 지켜달라는 것을 단 하나의 절박하고 명백한 요청으로 삼았다. 그것이 그의 이름임을 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그의 아내조차 그 사실을 의심하지 못했고, 로리 씨로서도 알 도리가 없었다.\n\n\"못 찾았네,\" 로리 씨가 은행 측에 답하여 말했다. \"지금 여기 있는 모든 사람에게 물어본 것 같은데, 이 신사를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 아무도 말해주지 못하는군.\"\n\n은행 마감 시각이 다가오면서 시곗바늘이 그 시각에 가까워지자, 떠들던 무리의 흐름이 대체로 로리 씨의 책상 곁을 지나갔다. 그가 묻듯이 편지를 들어 보이자, 음모를 꾸미며 분개하는 이 망명자의 모습으로 므슈 영주가 그것을 들여다보았고, 음모를 꾸미며 분개하는 저 망명자의 모습으로 또 므슈 영주가 그것을 들여다보았으며, 이 사람 저 사람 또 다른 사람 할 것 없이 모두가 행방을 알 수 없는 그 후작에 관하여 프랑스어로든 영어로든 무언가 헐뜯을 말을 했다.\n\n\"내 짐작에 그 조카지 — 어쨌든 타락한 후계자야 — 살해당한 그 세련된 후작의 말일세,\" 한 사람이 말했다. \"다행스럽게도 나는 그를 알지 못했다네.\"\n\n\"제 자리를 내팽개친 겁쟁이지,\" 또 한 사람이 말했다 — 이 므슈 영주는 다리를 위로 향한 채 반쯤 숨이 막혀가며 건초 더미에 실려 몇 해 전 빠리를 빠져나온 자였다 — \"몇 해 전 일이지.\"\n\n\"새로운 사상에 물든 자야,\" 지나가며 외알 안경 너머로 그 수신처를 훑어본 세 번째 사람이 말했다. \"선대 후작에게 맞서 들고일어났고, 영지를 물려받자 그것을 내팽개쳐 그 불한당 무리에게 넘겨버렸지. 이제 그들이 그가 받아 마땅한 대로 보답해주기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"엥?\" 떠벌이 스트라이버가 외쳤다. \"정말 그랬다고? 그런 부류의 작자란 말이지? 그 더러운 이름이나 한번 봅시다. 빌어먹을 작자 같으니!\"\n\n더는 자제할 수 없었던 다네이가 스트라이버 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그 사람을 압니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 자네가? 정말로?\" 스트라이버가 말했다. \"안됐구먼.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"어째서라니요, 다네이 씨? 그가 무슨 짓을 했는지 못 들으셨소? 이런 시국에 어째서냐고 묻지 마시오.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 어째서냐고 묻습니다.\"\n\"그렇다면 다시 말하지요, 다네이 씨, 안됐습니다. 그런 별난 질문을 던지는 걸 들으니 안타깝군요. 여기 한 작자가 있는데, 일찍이 알려진 가장 흉악하고 신성모독적인 악마의 율법에 물들어, 일찍이 닥치는 대로 살인을 저지른 지상에서 가장 비열한 쓰레기들에게 제 재산을 내던져버렸소. 그런데 젊은이들을 가르치는 사람이 그런 작자를 안다는데 내가 어찌 안타까워하느냐고 묻는 거요? 좋소, 답해드리지요. 그런 악당에게는 오염이 깃들어 있다고 믿기에 안타까운 거요. 그래서요.\"\n\n비밀을 의식한 다네이는 무진 애를 써서 자제하며 말했다. \"그 신사를 잘못 이해하셨을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"내가 잘 아는 건 당신을 궁지로 몰아넣는 방법이오, 다네이 씨,\" 으름장꾼 스트라이버가 말했다, \"그리고 그렇게 할 작정이오. 그 작자가 신사라면 나는 그를 이해하지 못하는 거요. 내 인사와 함께 그에게 그리 전하시구려. 또 내가 하는 말이라고 전하시오, 그 백정 같은 폭도들에게 제 재산과 지위를 내던져놓고서도 정작 그들의 우두머리 자리에 앉아 있지 않다니 의아하다고 말이오. 하지만 천만에요, 여러분,\" 스트라이버가 사방을 둘러보며 손가락을 탁 튀기고는 말했다, \"나는 인간 본성을 좀 아는데, 단언컨대 여러분은 그런 귀하신 피보호자들의 자비에 자기 몸을 내맡기는 그런 작자는 결코 보지 못할 거요. 천만에요, 여러분. 그런 작자는 싸움이 벌어지면 늘 아주 일찌감치 줄행랑을 쳐서 슬그머니 빠져나갈 겁니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 마지막으로 손가락을 탁 튀기고는, 스트라이버 씨는 청중의 전반적인 갈채를 받으며 어깨로 사람들을 헤치고 플리트 스트리트로 나섰다. 사람들이 모두 은행을 빠져나가는 가운데 로리 씨와 찰스 다네이만이 책상에 남았다.\n\n\"그 편지를 맡아주겠나?\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"어디로 전달할지 알지?\"\n\n\"압니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 전달할 곳을 알지도 모른다는 짐작에 이리로 부쳐온 것이라 여긴다는 점과, 그것이 여기 온 지 얼마쯤 되었다는 점을 설명해주겠나?\"\n\n\"그렇게 하지요. 빠리로는 여기서 출발하십니까?\"\n\n\"여기서, 여덟 시에.\"\n\n\"제가 다시 와서 배웅해 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n자신에 대해서도, 또 스트라이버며 다른 대부분의 사람들에 대해서도 몹시 언짢아진 다네이는 되도록 빨리 템플의 고요 속으로 들어가 편지를 뜯어 읽었다. 그 내용은 이러했다.\n\n\"빠리 아베이 감옥에서.\n\n\"오랫동안 마을 사람들의 손에 목숨을 위협받아오다가, 저는 극심한 폭력과 모욕을 당하며 붙잡혀, 빠리까지 먼 길을 걸어서 끌려왔습니다. 도중에 저는 무척이나 고초를 겪었습니다. 그뿐이 아닙니다. 제 집은 파괴되었으니 — 땅바닥까지 헐려버렸습니다.\n\n\"제가 갇힌 죄목, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 그리고 그 죄목으로 제가 법정에 소환되어 (당신의 그토록 너그러운 도움 없이는) 목숨을 잃게 될 그 죄목은, 그들의 말로는, 민중의 존엄에 대한 반역이라고 합니다. 제가 한 망명자를 위하여 민중에게 맞서 행동했다는 것이지요. 제가 당신의 명령에 따라 민중에게 맞선 것이 아니라 그들을 위하여 행동했다고 항변해도 소용없습니다. 망명자 재산이 몰수되기 전에, 제가 그들이 더는 내지 못하게 된 부과금을 면제해주었고, 소작료를 한 푼도 거두지 않았으며, 어떤 법적 절차에도 호소하지 않았다고 항변해도 소용없습니다. 돌아오는 대답은 오직, 내가 한 망명자를 위해 행동했다는 것, 그리고 그 망명자는 어디 있느냐는 것뿐입니다.\n\n\"아! 너무도 자비로우신 전(前) 후작 므슈, 그 망명자는 어디 있습니까? 저는 잠결에도 그가 어디 있느냐고 부르짖습니다. 하늘에 따져 묻습니다, 그가 와서 나를 구해주지 않으려는가? 아무 대답이 없습니다. 아 전(前) 후작 므슈, 저는 이 황량한 부르짖음을 바다 건너로 보내며, 빠리에 알려진 저 거대한 틸슨 은행을 통해 어쩌면 그것이 당신의 귀에 가닿기를 바랍니다!\n\n\"하늘에 대한 사랑으로, 정의에 대한 사랑으로, 너그러움에 대한 사랑으로, 당신의 고귀한 이름의 명예에 대한 사랑으로, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 간청합니다, 저를 구원하여 풀어주십시오. 제 잘못은 당신께 충실했다는 것입니다. 오 전(前) 후작 므슈, 부디 당신도 제게 충실해 주시기를 빕니다!\n\n\"매시간 점점 더 파멸에 가까워지는 이 공포의 감옥에서, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 저는 당신께 비통하고 불행한 저의 충정을 다짐드립니다.\n\n\"고통받는 당신의 사람,\n\n\"가벨.\"\n\n다네이의 마음속에 잠복해 있던 불안이 이 편지로 말미암아 격렬하게 되살아났다. 그를 향한, 그리고 그 가문을 향한 충성 말고는 아무 죄도 없는 한 늙은 하인, 그것도 충직한 하인이 처한 위험이 너무도 책망하듯 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 노려보았기에, 그는 어찌해야 할지 궁리하며 템플 안을 이리저리 거니는 동안 지나가는 사람들에게서 거의 얼굴을 가리다시피 했다.\n\n그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그 오래된 가문 저택의 악행과 악명을 정점으로 끌어올린 그 행위에 대한 공포 속에서, 또 숙부에 대한 원망 섞인 의혹 속에서, 또 자신이 지탱하기로 되어 있던 그 무너져가는 구조물을 양심이 혐오스럽게 바라보던 가운데, 자신이 불완전하게 처신했음을 말이다. 그는 잘 알고 있었다. 루씨에 대한 사랑 속에서, 자신의 사회적 지위를 포기한 일이, 비록 그것이 자기 마음속에서 결코 새로운 것은 아니었다 해도, 서두른 채 미완으로 남았음을 말이다. 그는 알고 있었다, 그것을 체계적으로 마무리하고 관리했어야 했고, 또 그러려고 마음먹었으면서도 끝내 그렇게 하지 못했음을 말이다.\n\n스스로 택한 잉글랜드 가정의 행복, 늘 활발하게 일에 매여 있어야 할 필요, 그리고 너무도 빠르게 잇따라 닥쳐서 이번 주의 사건이 지난주의 설익은 계획을 무너뜨리고 그다음 주의 사건이 모든 것을 다시 새롭게 만들어버리던 시대의 급격한 변화와 혼란 — 그는 잘 알고 있었다, 자신이 이러한 정황의 힘에 굴복했음을, 불안이 없지는 않았으되 그래도 끈질기게 쌓여가는 저항 없이 그리했음을 말이다. 그가 행동에 나설 때를 기다리며 시국을 지켜보는 동안, 시국은 자꾸만 변하고 요동치다가 마침내 때가 지나가 버렸고, 귀족들은 큰길로 샛길로 떼 지어 프랑스를 떠나고 있었으며, 그들의 재산은 몰수와 파괴의 과정에 있었고, 그들의 이름조차 지워지고 있었으니 — 이 사실은, 그 일로 자신을 탄핵할지 모를 프랑스의 어떤 새로운 권력만큼이나 그 자신도 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그는 누구도 억압한 적 없었고, 누구도 가둔 적 없었다. 자기 몫의 대가를 가혹하게 거두기는커녕, 그것을 제 의지로 내려놓고서, 호의라곤 없는 세상에 자신을 내던져 그곳에서 자기만의 자리를 일구고 제 손으로 빵을 벌어왔다. 가벨 므슈는 궁핍하고 얽혀버린 영지를, 민중을 아끼고 줄 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 것이나마 — 겨울에 빚 독촉이 심한 채권자들이 그들에게 남겨줄 만한 땔감과, 여름에 그 같은 손아귀에서 건져낼 수 있는 약간의 소출을 — 그들에게 내어주라는 서면 지시에 따라 관리해왔으니, 의심할 바 없이 그는 제 안전을 위해 그 사실을 항변과 증거로 제시해두었을 터, 그러니 지금 그것이 드러나지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이러한 사정이, 찰스 다네이가 굳히기 시작한 필사적인 결심, 곧 빠리로 가겠다는 결심을 부추겼다.\n\n그렇다. 옛이야기 속의 그 뱃사람처럼, 바람과 물살이 그를 자석 바위*의 영향권 안으로 몰아넣었고, 그것은 그를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었으니, 그는 가야만 했다. 그의 마음속에 떠오르는 모든 것이 그를, 점점 더 빠르게, 점점 더 한결같이, 그 무시무시한 인력 쪽으로 떠밀었다. 그의 잠복한 불안은, 사악한 도구들에 의해 사악한 목적이 그의 불행한 조국에서 이루어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 자신이 그들보다 낫다는 것을 모를 리 없는 그가 정작 그곳에 가지 않고 있다는 것, 유혈을 막고 자비와 인류애의 주장을 내세우려 무언가 애써보지 않고 있다는 것이었다. 반쯤 억눌리고 반쯤 그를 책망하는 이 불안과 더불어, 그는 의무감이 그토록 강했던 저 용감한 노신사와 자신을 날카롭게 견주는 지경에 이르렀다. 그리고 (자신에게 불리한) 그 비교에 곧바로 므슈 영주들의 비웃음이 뒤따라 그를 쓰라리게 찔렀고, 무엇보다도 거칠고 속을 긁는, 오래된 이유에서 비롯된 스트라이버의 비웃음이 뒤따랐다. 그러한 것들에 가벨의 편지가 뒤따랐으니 — 죽음의 위험에 처한 무고한 죄수가 그의 정의와 명예와 명성에 호소하는 외침이었다.\n* 자석 바위: 옛 항해 전설에서 지나가는 배의 쇠붙이를 끌어당겨 난파시킨다고 전해지는 바위.\n\n결심은 섰다. 그는 빠리로 가야 했다.\n\n그렇다. 자석 바위가 그를 끌어당기고 있었으니, 그는 부딪칠 때까지 항해해 나아가야 했다. 그는 어떤 바위도 알지 못했고, 어떤 위험도 거의 보지 못했다. 비록 미완으로 남겼다 해도, 그가 행한 일에 담겼던 그 의도는, 자신이 직접 나서서 그것을 내세운다면 프랑스에서 감사히 인정받으리라는 모습으로 그의 앞에 떠올랐다. 그러자, 선한 일을 한다는 그 영광스러운 환영 — 그토록 많은 선한 마음들에게 그토록 자주 찾아드는 낙관의 신기루 — 이 그의 앞에 떠올랐고, 그는 그 환상 속에서, 그토록 무섭게 미쳐 날뛰는 이 광란의 혁명을 이끌어갈 얼마간의 영향력을 지닌 자기 자신의 모습마저 보았다.\n\n결심을 굳힌 채 이리저리 거니는 동안, 그는 자신이 떠나기 전까지는 루씨도 그녀의 아버지도 이 일을 알아서는 안 된다고 생각했다. 루씨에게는 이별의 고통을 면하게 해주어야 했고, 늘 위태로운 옛 기억의 영역으로 생각을 돌리기를 꺼리던 그녀의 아버지에게는, 이 결행이 망설임과 의심 속에 저울질되는 것이 아니라 이미 내디뎌진 한 걸음으로서 알려져야 했다. 자신이 처한 상황의 미완성이 얼마나 그녀의 아버지에게서, 곧 프랑스의 옛 기억을 그의 마음속에 되살리지 않으려는 고통스러운 염려에서 비롯되었는지를 그는 스스로 따져 묻지 않았다. 그러나 그 정황 역시 그의 행로에 나름의 영향을 미쳤다.\n\n그는 갖가지 생각으로 머릿속이 분주한 채, 뗄슨으로 돌아가 로리 씨에게 작별을 고할 시간이 될 때까지 이리저리 거닐었다. 빠리에 도착하는 대로 이 옛 친구를 찾아갈 작정이었으나, 지금은 자신의 의도에 대해 아무 말도 해서는 안 되었다.\n\n역마를 맨 마차 한 대가 은행 문 앞에 대기하고 있었고, 제리는 장화를 신고 채비를 갖추고 있었다.\n\n\"그 편지를 전달했습니다,\" 찰스 다네이가 로리 씨에게 말했다. \"어르신께서 서면 답장을 떠맡으시는 데는 동의할 수 없습니다만, 어쩌면 구두로 한마디 전해주실 수는 있겠지요?\"\n\n\"그러고말고, 기꺼이,\" 로리 씨가 말했다, \"위험한 일만 아니라면 말일세.\"\n\n\"전혀 위험하지 않습니다. 비록 아베이 감옥에 갇힌 죄수에게 보내는 것이긴 합니다만.\"\n\n\"이름이 뭔가?\" 수첩을 펼쳐 든 채 로리 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"가벨입니다.\"\n\n\"가벨이라. 그래, 감옥에 갇힌 그 가엾은 가벨에게 전할 말이 무엇인가?\"\n\n\"그저, '편지를 받았으며, 가겠다'고만요.\"\n\n\"시일은 언급할까?\"\n\n\"내일 밤에 여정에 오를 겁니다.\"\n\n\"누구 이름은 언급할까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n그는 로리 씨가 외투와 망토 여러 벌로 몸을 감싸는 것을 거들고는, 오래된 은행의 따뜻한 공기 속에서 그와 함께 나와 안개 낀 플리트 스트리트로 들어섰다. \"루씨와 어린 루씨에게 내 사랑을 전해주게,\" 헤어지며 로리 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 내가 돌아올 때까지 그들을 소중히 지켜주게나.\" 마차가 굴러 멀어져가는 가운데, 찰스 다네이는 고개를 가로저으며 미심쩍게 미소 지었다.\n\n그날 밤 — 8월 14일이었다 — 그는 늦도록 깨어 앉아 간곡한 편지 두 통을 썼다. 하나는 루씨에게 쓴 것으로, 자신이 빠리로 가야 하는 강한 의무를 설명하고, 그곳에서 자신이 어떤 개인적 위험에도 휘말리지 않으리라 확신하는 이유를 그녀에게 상세히 밝혔다. 다른 하나는 의사에게 쓴 것으로, 루씨와 그들의 사랑하는 아이를 그의 보살핌에 맡기며, 같은 주제들을 더없이 강한 다짐과 함께 거듭 짚었다. 두 사람 모두에게, 그는 도착하는 즉시 자신의 안전을 입증할 편지를 부치겠노라고 적었다.\n\n그날, 그들 공동의 삶에서 처음으로 숨기는 것을 마음에 품은 채 그들 곁에 있던 그날은 힘겨운 하루였다. 그들이 도무지 의심하지 못하는 그 순결한 기만을 유지하기란 힘든 일이었다. 그러나 그토록 행복하고 분주한 아내를 다정하게 한 번 바라보자, 그는 닥쳐오는 일을 그녀에게 말하지 않으리라 굳게 마음먹었고(차분한 그녀의 도움 없이 무엇이든 행한다는 것이 그에게는 너무도 낯선 일이라, 반쯤은 털어놓을 마음이 동했던 터였다), 그리하여 하루는 빠르게 지나갔다. 초저녁에 그는 아내를, 그리고 못지않게 사랑스러운 그 이름을 물려받은 아이를 끌어안으며, 곧 돌아오겠노라고 둘러댔고(가공의 약속을 핑계로 밖으로 나섰으며, 옷가지를 챙긴 여행 가방을 미리 숨겨두었다), 그리하여 한층 무거운 마음으로 무겁게 가라앉은 거리의 짙은 안개 속으로 나아갔다.\n\n보이지 않는 힘이 이제 그를 빠르게 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었고, 모든 조류와 바람이 곧장 거세게 그쪽으로 향하고 있었다. 그는 두 통의 편지를, 자정 삼십 분 전에, 그보다 일찍은 말고 전달하라며 믿을 만한 짐꾼에게 맡겼다. 그러고는 도버행 말에 올라 여정을 시작했다. \"하늘에 대한 사랑으로, 정의에 대한 사랑으로, 너그러움에 대한 사랑으로, 당신의 고귀한 이름의 명예에 대한 사랑으로!\" — 이것은 그 가엾은 죄수의 부르짖음이었으니, 지상에서 소중한 모든 것을 등 뒤에 남겨두고 자석 바위를 향해 떠밀려 가는 동안, 그는 그 외침으로 가라앉는 마음을 다잡았다.\n\n# Book the Third--the Track of a Storm CHAPTER I. In Secret\n\n천칠백구십이년 가을에 영국에서 빠리로 향하던 그 나그네는 더디게 길을 나아갔다. 몰락하여 불운에 처한 프랑스 왕이 온갖 영화를 누리며 왕좌에 앉아 있었다 해도, 그는 가는 길을 더디게 만드는 험한 길과 형편없는 마차와 형편없는 말을 충분히, 아니 넘치도록 만났을 것이다. 그러나 변해버린 시절은 그런 것들 말고도 다른 장애물들로 가득 차 있었다. 도시의 성문마다, 마을의 세관마다 시민 애국자 무리가 진을 치고 있었는데, 그들은 당장이라도 터질 듯한 준비태세를 갖춘 국민군 머스킷 총을 들고 오가는 모든 이를 멈춰 세우고, 캐묻고, 서류를 검사하고, 그들 나름의 명단에서 이름을 찾아보고는, 되돌려보내거나, 통과시키거나, 붙들어 가두었으니, 자유와 평등과 박애, 그렇지 않으면 죽음이라는, 막 동터오는 단일하고도 불가분한 공화국을 위해 그들의 변덕스러운 판단이나 기분에 가장 좋다고 여겨지는 대로 처분했다.\n\n여정 가운데 몇 프랑스 리외도 채 가기 전에, 샤를 다네는 이 시골길을 따라가는 자신에게는 빠리에서 선량한 시민으로 선언되기 전까지 돌아올 가망이 없다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다. 이제 무슨 일이 닥치든 그는 여정의 끝까지 나아가야 했다. 그의 등 뒤로 닫히는 하찮은 마을 하나하나가, 길 위로 내려지는 평범한 차단봉 하나하나가, 자신과 영국 사이에 빗장으로 잠긴 일련의 또다른 쇠문임을 그는 알았다. 어디서나 감시가 그를 에워싸고 있어, 그물에 사로잡혔거나 우리에 갇혀 목적지로 호송되는 신세라 해도 자신의 자유가 그토록 완전히 사라졌다고 느끼지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n* 리외: 옛 프랑스의 거리 단위로 약 4킬로미터에 해당한다.\n이 어디서나 펼쳐지는 감시는 한 구간에서 그를 스무 번씩 큰길 위에서 멈춰 세웠을 뿐 아니라, 하루에 스무 번씩 그의 행로를 더디게 했으니, 그의 뒤를 쫓아 와서 되돌려보내고, 앞질러 달려가 미리 막아서고, 함께 말을 달리며 그를 감시했다. 그는 프랑스에서만도 여러 날을 길에서 보냈고, 빠리까지는 아직 한참 먼 큰길가의 작은 마을에서 녹초가 된 채 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n비탄에 잠긴 가벨이 아바이 감옥에서 보낸 편지를 내보이지 않았다면 그는 여기까지 결코 오지 못했을 것이다. 이 작은 마을의 위병소에서 겪은 어려움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 자신의 여정이 고비에 다다랐다고 느꼈다. 그러므로 아침까지 머물도록 보내진 그 작은 여인숙에서 한밤중에 잠을 깨우는 것을 발견했을 때도, 그는 사람으로서 더없이 놀라지 않는 만큼만 놀랐다.\n\n소심한 그 고장 관리 하나와, 거친 붉은 모자를 쓰고 입에 파이프를 문 무장한 애국자 셋이 그를 깨웠는데, 그들은 침대 위에 걸터앉았다.\n\n\"이민자여,\" 관리가 말했다. \"당신을 호송대를 붙여 빠리로 보내려 하오.\"\n\n\"시민이여, 나는 빠리에 가는 것보다 더 바라는 것이 없소. 호송대는 없어도 되겠지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"닥쳐!\" 붉은 모자 하나가 머스킷 총 개머리판으로 이불을 내리치며 으르렁거렸다. \"조용히 해, 귀족 놈아!\"\n\n\"선량한 애국자가 하는 말 그대로요,\" 소심한 관리가 말했다. \"당신은 귀족이니 호송대를 붙여야 하고, 그 값을 치러야 하오.\"\n\n\"내겐 선택의 여지가 없군요,\" 샤를 다네가 말했다.\n\n\"선택이라니! 저 말 좀 들어보게!\" 그 인상 쓰던 붉은 모자가 외쳤다. \"마치 가로등 쇠막대에서 보호받는 것이 은혜가 아니라는 듯이!\"\n\n\"늘 선량한 애국자가 하는 말 그대로요,\" 관리가 말했다. \"일어나서 옷을 입으시오, 이민자여.\"\n\n다네는 시키는 대로 했고, 위병소로 도로 끌려갔는데, 그곳에서는 거친 붉은 모자를 쓴 또다른 애국자들이 화톳불가에서 담배를 피우고, 술을 마시고, 잠을 자고 있었다. 여기서 그는 호송대에 대한 값을 비싸게 치렀고, 새벽 세시에 그 호송대와 함께 흠뻑 젖고 또 젖은 길 위로 출발했다.\n\n호송대는 붉은 모자에 삼색 모표를 단 기마 애국자 둘로, 국민군 머스킷 총과 군도로 무장한 채 그의 양옆에 하나씩 붙어 말을 달렸다.\n\n호송되는 자는 자기 말을 직접 몰았으나, 그의 고삐에는 느슨한 줄이 매여 있었고, 그 줄 끝을 애국자 가운데 하나가 손목에 감고 있었다. 이런 상태로 그들은 얼굴에 매섭게 들이치는 빗줄기를 맞으며 출발했으니, 울퉁불퉁한 마을 포석 위를 묵직한 기병대식 속보로 덜그럭거리며 달려 진창처럼 깊은 길로 빠져나갔다. 이런 상태로, 말과 속도가 바뀐 것 말고는 아무 변화 없이, 그들과 수도 사이에 놓인 진창처럼 깊은 길의 모든 리외를 가로질러 갔다.\n\n그들은 밤에 이동했고, 동이 트고 한두 시간 뒤에 멈춰 어스름이 내릴 때까지 누워 있었다. 호송대는 어찌나 비참하게 옷을 걸쳤던지, 맨다리에 짚을 둘둘 감고 비를 막으려고 누더기 어깨에 짚을 덮었다. 그토록 호위받는 데서 오는 개인적인 불편함은 차치하고, 애국자 가운데 하나가 만성적으로 취해 머스킷 총을 매우 무모하게 다루는 데서 비롯되는 당장의 위험에 대한 우려도 차치하고서, 샤를 다네는 자신에게 가해진 구속이 가슴속에 어떤 심각한 두려움도 불러일으키도록 내버려두지 않았다. 그는 그것이 아직 진술되지도 않은 한 개인 사건의 정당성과는, 그리고 아바이 감옥의 죄수가 확인해줄 수 있으나 아직 제기되지도 않은 진술들과는 아무 관련도 없을 거라고 스스로 따져보았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그들이 보베라는 마을에 다다랐을 때 — 거리가 사람들로 가득 찬 저녁 무렵에 그곳에 들어섰는데 — 그는 사태의 양상이 매우 불온하다는 것을 스스로에게 숨길 수 없었다. 그가 역참 마당에서 말에서 내리는 것을 보려고 불길한 군중이 모여들었고, 많은 목소리가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이민자를 타도하라!\"\n\n그는 안장에서 몸을 내리려다 멈추고는, 그것이 가장 안전한 자리라 여겨 다시 올라앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"이민자라니, 친구들이여! 내가 여기, 프랑스에, 제 발로 와 있는 게 보이지 않소?\"\n\n\"넌 빌어먹을 이민자야,\" 어느 편자공이 망치를 손에 들고 인파를 헤치며 사납게 그에게 달려들면서 외쳤다. \"그리고 넌 빌어먹을 귀족 놈이야!\"\n\n역참지기가 이 사내와 말 탄 자의 고삐(그가 분명 노리고 있던) 사이로 끼어들며 달래듯 말했다. \"내버려둬, 내버려둬! 이자는 빠리에서 재판받을 거야.\"\n\n\"재판이라고!\" 편자공이 망치를 휘두르며 되뇌었다. \"그래! 반역자로 단죄될 테지.\" 이 말에 군중이 와르르 환호했다.\n\n말 머리를 마당으로 돌리려던 역참지기를 만류하며(취한 애국자는 손목에 줄을 감은 채 안장에 태연히 앉아 구경하고 있었다), 다네는 자기 목소리가 들릴 만해지자 곧바로 말했다.\n\n\"친구들이여, 그대들은 스스로를 속이고 있거나, 아니면 누군가에게 속고 있소. 나는 반역자가 아니오.\"\n\n\"저놈이 거짓말을 하네!\" 대장장이가 외쳤다. \"법령이 내린 뒤로 저놈은 반역자야. 저놈 목숨은 인민에게 몰수당했어. 저 빌어먹을 목숨은 제 것이 아니라고!\"\n\n다네가 군중의 눈에서 한순간만 더 있으면 자신에게 덮쳐올 돌진의 기미를 본 바로 그 찰나, 역참지기가 말을 마당으로 돌려세웠고, 호송대가 그의 말 옆구리에 바짝 붙어 따라 들어왔으며, 역참지기는 삐걱대는 두짝문을 닫고 빗장을 질렀다. 편자공이 망치로 그 문을 한 번 내리쳤고 군중은 신음을 토했으나, 그 이상은 아무 일도 없었다.\n\n\"그 대장장이가 말한 법령이라는 게 뭐요?\" 다네는 역참지기에게 고마움을 표하고 마당에서 그의 곁에 선 뒤에 물었다.\n\n\"실은 이민자의 재산을 매각하라는 법령이오.\"\n\n\"언제 통과됐소?\"\n\n\"십사일에요.\"\n\n\"내가 영국을 떠난 날이군!\"\n\n\"다들 그게 여럿 가운데 하나일 뿐이고, 다른 것들도 나올 거라고 — 이미 나오지 않았다면 — 합디다. 모든 이민자를 추방하고, 돌아오는 자는 모두 사형에 처하는 법령 말이오. 저자가 당신 목숨이 당신 것이 아니라고 한 게 바로 그 뜻이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 아직 그런 법령들은 없는 거잖소?\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 알겠소!\" 역참지기가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"있을 수도 있고, 곧 생길 수도 있지요. 다 마찬가지요. 어쩌겠소?\"\n\n그들은 한밤중까지 다락의 짚더미 위에서 쉬었다가, 온 마을이 잠들었을 때 다시 앞으로 말을 달렸다. 이 거친 질주를 비현실적으로 만든, 익숙한 것들에서 관찰되는 숱한 거친 변화들 가운데서도 결코 사소하지 않은 것이 잠이 드물어 보이는 점이었다. 황량한 길 위를 길고 외롭게 박차를 가하며 달리다 보면, 그들은 어둠에 잠겨 있기는커녕 온통 불빛으로 반짝이는 초라한 오두막 무리에 이르곤 했고, 죽은 듯한 한밤중에 사람들이 유령처럼 손에 손을 잡고 시들어버린 자유의 나무 둘레를 돌거나, 다 함께 늘어서서 자유의 노래를 부르고 있는 것을 보곤 했다. 그러나 다행히도 그날 밤 보베에는 잠이 있어 그들이 거기서 빠져나오도록 도와주었고, 그들은 다시 한번 적막과 고독 속으로 나아갔다. 철 이른 추위와 빗속을 짤랑거리며 지나갔으니, 그해 땅의 열매를 하나도 내지 못한 헐벗은 들판 사이로, 불탄 집들의 거뭇해진 잔해와, 매복에서 불쑥 튀어나와 그들의 앞길을 가로막고 매섭게 고삐를 당기는, 온갖 길목을 지키는 애국자 순찰대들로 변화를 더하며 지나갔다.\n\n마침내 동이 트자 그들은 빠리의 성벽 앞에 이르렀다. 그들이 말을 몰아 다가갔을 때 성문은 닫혀 있었고 삼엄하게 지켜지고 있었다.\n\n\"이 죄수의 서류는 어디 있나?\" 위병에게 불려 나온, 단호해 보이는 권위 있는 사내가 물었다.\n\n그 불쾌한 단어에 자연히 충격을 받은 샤를 다네는, 말한 자에게 자신은 자유로운 나그네이자 프랑스 시민이며, 어수선한 나라 사정 때문에 떠안게 되어 그 값까지 치른 호송대를 거느리고 있을 뿐임을 유의하라고 청했다.\n\n\"이 죄수의 서류는,\" 같은 인물이 그에게는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 되풀이했다. \"어디 있느냔 말이다?\"\n\n취한 애국자가 모자 속에 서류를 넣어두었다가 꺼냈다. 가벨의 편지를 훑어보던 그 권위 있는 인물은 약간 동요하며 놀란 기색을 보였고, 다네를 유심히 살펴보았다.\n\n그러나 그는 한마디도 없이 호송대와 호송되는 자를 남겨두고 위병소로 들어갔다. 그동안 그들은 성문 밖에서 말 위에 앉아 있었다. 이렇게 마음 졸이는 상태에서 주위를 둘러보던 샤를 다네는, 성문이 군인과 애국자가 뒤섞인 위병들에게 지켜지고 있으며 후자가 전자보다 훨씬 많다는 것을, 그리고 보급품을 실어 오는 농민의 짐수레나 그와 비슷한 통행과 통행인의 도성 진입은 충분히 쉬운 반면, 가장 보잘것없는 사람의 진출조차 매우 어렵다는 것을 알아차렸다. 짐승과 갖가지 탈것은 말할 것도 없고, 무수히 뒤섞인 남녀가 빠져나가려고 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 사전 신원 확인이 어찌나 엄격한지, 그들은 매우 느릿느릿 차단문을 통해 걸러져 나갔다. 이 사람들 가운데 일부는 자기 차례가 검사받기까지 한참 멀었음을 알고 땅바닥에 드러누워 잠을 자거나 담배를 피웠고, 다른 이들은 서로 이야기를 나누거나 어슬렁거렸다. 붉은 모자와 삼색 모표는 남자에게나 여자에게나 어디서나 볼 수 있었다.\n\n다네가 안장에 앉아 이런 것들을 눈여겨보며 반시간쯤 보냈을 때, 그는 또다시 그 권위 있는 사내와 마주하게 되었는데, 사내는 위병에게 차단문을 열라고 지시했다. 그러고는 취한 자와 멀쩡한 자, 그 호송대에게 호송된 자에 대한 영수증을 건네고는 다네에게 말에서 내리라고 청했다. 그는 그렇게 했고, 두 애국자는 지친 그의 말을 끌고 도성에는 들어가지 않은 채 몸을 돌려 말을 타고 떠나갔다.\n\n그는 자신을 안내하는 자를 따라 위병소로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서는 흔한 포도주와 담배 냄새가 났으며, 자는 자와 깨어 있는 자, 취한 자와 멀쩡한 자, 그리고 잠과 깸, 취함과 맑은 정신 사이의 갖가지 중간 상태에 있는 군인들과 애국자들이 서 있거나 여기저기 누워 있었다. 위병소의 빛은, 절반은 밤새 사위어가는 기름등잔에서, 절반은 잔뜩 흐린 한낮에서 나온 것이어서, 그에 걸맞게 어정쩡한 상태였다. 책상 위에는 명부 몇 권이 펼쳐져 있었고, 거칠고 거무튀튀한 인상의 한 장교가 그것들을 관장하고 있었다.\n\n\"드파르주 시민,\" 그가 적을 종잇조각 하나를 집어 들며 다네를 안내한 자에게 말했다. \"이자가 그 이민자 에브레몽드인가?\"\n\n\"이 사람이오.\"\n\n\"나이가 어떻게 되나, 에브레몽드?\"\n\n\"서른일곱이오.\"\n\n\"결혼은 했나, 에브레몽드?\"\n\n\"했소.\"\n\n\"어디서 했지?\"\n\n\"영국에서요.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지. 아내는 어디 있나, 에브레몽드?\"\n\n\"영국에 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지. 에브레몽드, 당신은 라포르스 감옥으로 보내진다.\"\n\n\"하느님 맙소사!\" 다네가 외쳤다. \"무슨 법으로, 무슨 죄목으로 말이오?\"\n\n장교가 잠깐 종잇조각에서 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"당신이 여기 있던 이후로, 에브레몽드, 우리에겐 새 법이 생겼고 새 죄목이 생겼지.\" 그는 모진 미소를 지으며 그렇게 말하고는 계속 써 내려갔다.\n\n\"내가 여기 자진해서 왔다는 것을 유의해주기 바라오. 당신 앞에 놓인, 동포가 써 보낸 그 호소문에 응해서 말이오. 나는 지체 없이 그렇게 할 기회 외에는 아무것도 요구하지 않소. 그것이 내 권리가 아니오?\"\n\n\"이민자에게는 권리가 없다, 에브레몽드.\" 무뚝뚝한 대답이 돌아왔다. 장교는 다 쓸 때까지 써 내려가더니, 자신이 쓴 것을 스스로 읽어보고, 모래를 뿌려 잉크를 말린 뒤, \"비밀 감금\"이라는 말과 함께 그것을 드파르주에게 건넸다.\n\n드파르주는 그 종이로 죄수에게 자신을 따라와야 한다는 몸짓을 했다. 죄수는 따랐고, 무장한 애국자 둘로 이루어진 위병이 그들을 수행했다.\n\n\"당신이,\" 위병소 계단을 내려가 빠리 시내로 접어들면서 드파르주가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"지금은 사라지고 없는 바스띠유의 죄수였던 마네뜨 박사의 딸과 결혼한 사람이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 다네가 놀라며 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 이름은 드파르주요, 쌩땅뚜안 지구에서 포도주 가게를 하고 있소. 아마 내 이야기를 들어봤을 거요.\"\n\n\"내 아내가 아버지를 도로 모셔 가려고 당신 집에 갔었지요? 맞소!\"\n\n\"아내\"라는 말이 드파르주에게 음울한 기억을 떠올리게 한 듯, 그는 별안간 짜증을 내며 말했다. \"새로 태어나 라기요띤이라 불리는 그 매서운 여자의 이름으로 묻겠소, 당신은 어째서 프랑스에 왔소?\"\n\n\"방금 그 까닭을 말하는 것을 당신도 들었잖소. 그게 진실이라는 걸 믿지 않는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신에게는 나쁜 진실이지,\" 드파르주가 미간을 찌푸린 채 똑바로 앞을 보며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 나는 여기서 길을 잃었소. 여기 모든 것이 너무도 전례 없고, 너무도 변했고, 너무도 갑작스럽고 부당해서, 나는 완전히 길을 잃었소. 내게 약간의 도움을 베풀어주겠소?\"\n\n\"안 되오.\" 드파르주는 줄곧 똑바로 앞을 보며 말했다.\n\n\"단 하나의 질문에라도 답해주겠소?\"\n\n\"아마도. 그 성격에 따라서 말이오. 무엇인지 말해보시오.\"\n\n\"내가 이토록 부당하게 끌려가는 이 감옥에서, 바깥세상과 자유로이 연락할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"두고 보면 알 거요.\"\n\n\"나는 미리 단죄당한 채, 내 사정을 호소할 아무런 수단도 없이 거기 파묻혀버리는 거요?\"\n\n\"두고 보면 알 거요. 하지만 그렇다 한들 어떻소? 다른 사람들도 지금껏 더 나쁜 감옥에 그렇게 파묻혀왔으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 손으로 그런 적은 결코 없소, 드파르주 시민.\"\n\n드파르주는 대답 대신 어둡게 그를 흘끗 보고는, 한결같이 굳은 침묵 속에서 걸어 나아갔다. 이 침묵에 깊이 가라앉을수록, 그가 조금이라도 누그러질 가망은 더욱 희미해졌다 — 적어도 다네는 그렇게 생각했다. 그래서 그는 서둘러 말했다.\n\n\"내게는 더없이 중요한 일이오(시민이여, 당신은 나보다도 그게 얼마나 중요한지 더 잘 알 거요). 지금 빠리에 와 있는 영국 신사이자 뗄슨 은행의 로리 씨에게, 내가 라포르스 감옥에 갇혔다는 단순한 사실만이라도, 아무 부연 없이 전할 수 있다면 말이오. 나를 위해 그 일을 해주겠소?\"\n\n\"나는,\" 드파르주가 완강하게 응수했다. \"당신을 위해서는 아무것도 하지 않겠소. 내 의무는 내 조국과 인민에 대한 것이오. 나는 그 둘 모두에게 맹세한 종이며, 당신에게는 맞서는 사람이오. 나는 당신을 위해 아무것도 하지 않겠소.\"\n\n샤를 다네는 그에게 더 간청해봐야 소용없다고 느꼈고, 게다가 자존심도 상했다. 그들이 말없이 걸어가는 동안, 그는 사람들이 죄수가 거리를 끌려가는 광경에 얼마나 익숙해져 있는지 보지 않을 수 없었다. 아이들조차 그에게 거의 눈길을 주지 않았다. 행인 몇이 고개를 돌렸고, 몇은 귀족이라며 그를 향해 손가락질을 했다. 그밖에는, 좋은 옷을 입은 사람이 감옥에 끌려가는 것은 작업복을 입은 노동자가 일하러 가는 것이나 다를 바 없이 별스러운 일이 아니었다. 그들이 지나가는 어느 좁고 어둡고 더러운 거리에서는, 흥분한 연설가 하나가 걸상에 올라서서 흥분한 청중을 향해 왕과 왕족이 인민에게 저지른 죄에 대해 일장연설을 하고 있었다. 이 사내의 입에서 그가 주워들은 몇 마디 말로, 샤를 다네는 비로소 왕이 감옥에 갇혔다는 것과, 외국 대사들이 하나같이 빠리를 떠났다는 것을 알게 되었다. 길 위에서(보베에서를 빼고는) 그는 정말이지 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 호송대와 어디서나 펼쳐지는 감시가 그를 완전히 고립시켰던 것이다.\n\n자신이 영국을 떠날 때 모습을 드러냈던 위험들보다 훨씬 더 큰 위험 속에 빠졌다는 것을, 그는 이제 물론 알았다. 위험이 자기 주위로 빠르게 짙어졌고, 앞으로 더욱더 빠르게 짙어질지 모른다는 것을, 그는 이제 물론 알았다. 며칠 뒤의 사건들을 미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면 이 여정을 떠나지 않았을지도 모른다는 것을, 그는 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다. 그렇지만 그의 불안은, 훗날의 빛에 비추어 상상되는 것만큼 그리 어둡지는 않았다. 미래가 아무리 어수선하다 해도 그것은 알 수 없는 미래였고, 그 어둠 속에는 무지한 희망이 깃들어 있었다. 며칠 밤낮을 이어진 그 끔찍한 학살, 시곗바늘이 몇 바퀴 돌기도 전에 축복받은 수확의 거둠철 위에 거대한 핏자국을 남기게 될 그 학살은, 십만 년쯤 떨어진 일이기라도 한 듯 그의 앎에서 까마득히 멀었다. \"새로 태어나 라기요띤이라 불리는 그 매서운 여자\"는 그에게도, 또 대다수 사람들에게도 그 이름조차 거의 알려져 있지 않았다. 머지않아 저질러질 그 무시무시한 짓들은, 아마 그 무렵 그것을 저지를 자들의 머릿속에서조차 떠올려지지 않았을 것이다. 그러니 그것들이 어찌 온화한 마음의 어렴풋한 상념 속에 자리할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n구금과 고초 속에서의 부당한 처우, 그리고 아내와 아이와의 모진 이별에 대해서는, 그는 그럴 법함을, 아니 그 확실함을 어렴풋이 내다보았다. 그러나 그 너머로는 무엇 하나 또렷이 두려워하지 않았다. 음울한 감옥 마당으로 들고 들어가기에 충분한 이런 생각을 품은 채, 그는 라포르스 감옥에 다다랐다.\n\n부풀어오른 얼굴을 한 사내가 튼튼한 쪽문을 열었고, 드파르주는 그에게 \"이민자 에브레몽드\"를 인계했다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을! 이런 게 대체 얼마나 더 오는 거야!\" 부풀어오른 얼굴의 사내가 외쳤다.\n\n드파르주는 그 외침에 아랑곳하지 않고 영수증을 받아 들고는, 두 동료 애국자와 함께 물러갔다.\n\n\"빌어먹을, 다시 말하지만!\" 아내와 함께 남겨진 간수가 외쳤다. \"얼마나 더 온단 말이야!\"\n\n간수의 아내는 그 물음에 내놓을 답이 없어, 그저 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"참아야지요, 여보!\" 그녀가 울린 종소리에 응해 들어온 옥지기 셋이 그 말에 맞장구쳤고, 하나는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"자유를 위해서 말이오.\" 그것은 그곳에서 어울리지 않는 마무리처럼 들렸다.\n\n라포르스 감옥은 음울한 감옥이었으니, 어둡고 더러웠으며, 더러운 잠에서 풍기는 끔찍한 냄새가 배어 있었다. 제대로 돌보지 않는 그런 곳마다 갇힌 잠의 역겨운 냄새가 얼마나 빨리 또렷이 드러나는지, 참으로 놀라운 일이다!\n\n\"게다가 비밀 감금이라니,\" 간수가 그 쓰인 종이를 보며 투덜거렸다. \"이미 터져나갈 만큼 꽉 찼는데 말이야!\"\n\n그는 언짢은 기분으로 종이를 서류꽂이에 꽂았고, 샤를 다네는 반시간 동안 그가 마음 내키기를 기다렸다. 때로는 튼튼한 아치형 방 안을 이리저리 거닐면서, 때로는 돌의자에 앉아 쉬면서. 어느 쪽이든 그는 우두머리와 그 부하들의 기억에 새겨지도록 붙들려 있었다.\n\n\"가자!\" 마침내 우두머리가 열쇠를 집어 들며 말했다. \"나를 따라와, 이민자여.\"\n\n음울한 감옥의 어스름을 지나, 그가 새로 떠맡은 자는 복도와 계단을 따라 그를 뒤따랐고, 그들 뒤로 숱한 문들이 쾅 닫히며 잠겼다. 마침내 그들은 넓고 천장이 낮은 둥근 천장의 방에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 남녀 죄수들로 붐볐다. 여자들은 긴 탁자에 앉아 책을 읽거나 글을 쓰고, 뜨개질을 하고, 바느질을 하고, 수를 놓고 있었다. 남자들은 대부분 의자 뒤에 서 있거나, 방 안을 오락가락 서성이고 있었다.\n\n죄수를 부끄러운 범죄나 치욕과 본능적으로 결부시키는 탓에, 새로 온 자는 이 무리에게서 움찔 물러섰다. 그러나 그토록 길고 비현실적이었던 그 질주의 절정을 이루는 비현실은, 그들이 일제히 일어서서 당대에 알려진 온갖 세련된 예법과, 삶의 온갖 매력적인 우아함과 정중함으로 그를 맞이한 것이었다.\n\n이 세련됨이 감옥의 행색과 음울함으로 어찌나 기이하게 흐려졌던지, 그것들이 보이는 어울리지 않는 누추함과 비참함 속에서 어찌나 유령 같아졌던지, 샤를 다네는 마치 죽은 자들의 무리 가운데 서 있는 듯했다. 모두가 유령이었다! 아름다움의 유령, 위엄의 유령, 우아함의 유령, 자긍심의 유령, 경박함의 유령, 재치의 유령, 젊음의 유령, 노년의 유령, 모두가 그 황량한 기슭에서 풀려나기를 기다리며, 이곳에 오는 길에 그들이 겪은 죽음으로 달라진 눈을 그에게로 돌리고 있었다.\n\n그것에 그는 옴짝달싹 못한 채 굳어버렸다. 그의 곁에 선 간수와 주위를 오가는 다른 간수들은, 평소의 직무를 수행하는 모습이라면 외양이 어지간했을 테지만, 그곳에 있는 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니들과 활짝 피어난 딸들과 견주어보면 — 아리따운 여인과 젊은 미녀와 곱게 자란 원숙한 여인의 환영들과 견주어보면 — 어찌나 터무니없이 상스러워 보였던지, 이 그림자들의 광경이 펼쳐 보이는 온갖 경험과 개연성의 역전이 더할 나위 없이 고조되었다. 분명, 모두가 유령이었다. 분명, 그 길고 비현실적이었던 질주는 그를 이 음울한 망령들에게로 데려온 어떤 병의 진행이었던 것이다!\n\n\"불운 속에 모인 동료들의 이름으로,\" 궁정풍의 외모와 말씨를 지닌 한 신사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"라포르스에 오신 당신을 환영하고, 당신을 우리 가운데로 데려온 그 재난에 대해 위로의 말을 전하는 영광을 누리겠습니다. 부디 그것이 머지않아 행복하게 끝나기를! 다른 곳에서라면 무례한 일이겠으나, 여기서는 그렇지 않으니, 당신의 이름과 처지를 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n샤를 다네는 정신을 가다듬고, 찾을 수 있는 한 가장 알맞은 말로 요청받은 정보를 알려주었다.\n\n\"하지만 바라건대,\" 방을 가로질러 가는 우두머리 간수를 눈으로 좇으며 신사가 말했다. \"당신은 비밀 감금은 아니겠지요?\"\n\n\"그 말이 무슨 뜻인지는 모르겠습니다만, 그들이 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 참으로 안된 일이군요! 우리도 무척 유감입니다! 하지만 용기를 내세요. 우리 무리 가운데 여럿이 처음엔 비밀 감금이었으나, 그것은 잠깐밖에 가지 않았습니다.\" 그러고는 목소리를 높여 덧붙였다. \"여러분께 알리게 되어 가슴 아프오만 — 비밀 감금이랍니다.\"\n\n샤를 다네가 간수가 기다리는 격자문으로 방을 가로질러 갈 때 동정의 웅성거림이 일었고, 많은 목소리가 — 그 가운데서도 여인들의 부드럽고 자비로운 목소리가 두드러졌다 — 그에게 행운과 격려를 빌어주었다. 그는 격자문 앞에서 몸을 돌려 진심에서 우러난 감사를 전하려 했다. 문은 간수의 손 아래 닫혔고, 그 환영들은 그의 시야에서 영영 사라져버렸다.\n\n쪽문은 위로 이어지는 돌계단으로 열렸다. 마흔 계단을 올라갔을 때(이미 반시간째 죄수인 그는 그것을 세고 있었다), 간수가 낮고 검은 문을 열었고, 그들은 외딴 독방으로 들어섰다. 그곳은 춥고 축축한 기운이 끼쳐왔으나, 어둡지는 않았다.\n\n\"당신 거요,\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 나를 혼자 가두는 거요?\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠소!\"\n\n\"펜과 잉크와 종이를 살 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"그건 내 지시에 없는 일이오. 면회를 받게 될 테니 그때 물어보시오. 지금으로선 음식을 살 수 있고, 그 이상은 안 되오.\"\n\n독방에는 의자 하나와 탁자 하나, 그리고 짚 매트리스가 있었다. 간수가 나가기 전에 이 물건들과 네 벽을 두루 살펴보는 동안, 그의 맞은편 벽에 기대선 죄수의 마음속으로 떠도는 상념 하나가 떠돌았으니, 이 간수가 얼굴이며 몸이며 어찌나 병적으로 부풀어 있는지 마치 물에 빠져 죽어 몸속이 물로 가득 찬 사람처럼 보인다는 것이었다. 간수가 떠나자, 그는 그 똑같이 떠도는 식으로 생각했다. \"이제 나는 마치 죽은 것처럼 남겨졌구나.\" 그러고는 멈춰 서서 매트리스를 내려다보다가, 메스꺼운 느낌에 그것에서 몸을 돌리며 생각했다. \"그리고 여기 이 기어 다니는 것들 속에, 죽은 뒤 몸이 맞는 첫 상태가 있구나.\"\n\n\"다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반, 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반, 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반.\" 죄수는 독방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 그 치수를 세었고, 도시의 굉음이 거기에 거친 함성의 너울을 보태며 둔탁한 북소리처럼 일었다. \"그는 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지.\" 죄수는 다시 치수를 세었고, 그 뒤의 되뇜에서 자기 마음을 끌어내려고 더 빠르게 걸었다. \"쪽문이 닫혔을 때 사라진 그 유령들. 그 가운데 검은 옷을 입은 한 여인의 모습이 있었지, 창문의 후미진 곳에 기대선 채, 황금빛 머리카락 위로 빛이 내리비치는 그 여인은 마치 ×××× 같았어. 제발, 우리 다시 말을 달리자, 사람들이 모두 깨어 있는 불 밝힌 마을들을 가로질러! ×××× 그는 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지. ×××× 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반.\" 마음 깊은 데서 이런 토막들이 떠올라 뒹굴고 휘도는 가운데, 죄수는 점점 더 빠르게 걸으며 고집스레 세고 또 세었다. 그리고 도시의 굉음은 이만큼 변했으니 — 여전히 둔탁한 북소리처럼 밀려들었으나, 그 위로 솟구치는 너울 속에는 그가 아는 목소리들의 울부짖음이 섞여 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER II. The Grindstone\n\n빠리의 쌩제르맹 구역에 자리 잡은 텔슨 은행은 어느 큰 저택의 한쪽 별채에 들어 있었는데, 안마당을 거쳐 드나들도록 되어 있었고 높은 담과 튼튼한 대문으로 길과 차단되어 있었다. 그 저택은 어느 대귀족의 소유였으니, 그는 이 집에서 살다가 자기 집 요리사의 옷을 입고 그 소동을 피해 달아나 국경을 넘어버린 사람이었다. 사냥꾼에게 쫓겨 달아나는 한낱 사냥감 짐승에 지나지 않았지만, 그는 윤회를 겪고서도 여전히 다름 아닌 바로 그 영주님이었으니, 한때 그의 입에 들일 초콜릿을 준비하는 데 문제의 그 요리사 말고도 건장한 사내 셋이 매달렸던 그 영주님이었다.\n\n영주님은 떠나버렸고, 그의 높은 봉급을 받아왔다는 죄를 씻으려고 그 건장한 사내 셋은 자유·평등·우애, 그도 아니면 죽음의 하나이자 나뉠 수 없는 동트는 공화국의 제단 위에서 그 영주님의 목을 베어버릴 만반의 채비가 되어 있고도 남았으며, 영주님의 저택은 처음에는 압류되었다가 이내 몰수되었다. 모든 일이 어찌나 빠르게 움직이고 포고령이 또 포고령을 따라 그토록 사납게 줄을 이었던지, 이제 가을철 구월의 사흘째 밤에 이르러 법의 애국적 사절들이 영주님의 저택을 차지하고는 삼색기로 표시를 해두었으며, 그 호화로운 거실에서 브랜디를 마시고 있었다.\n\n런던에 있는 영업소가 빠리의 텔슨 영업소 같았더라면, 그 은행은 이내 미쳐버려 파산 공고에 이름을 올리고 말았을 것이다. 점잖은 영국식 책임감과 품위가, 은행 안마당의 상자에 심긴 오렌지나무를, 게다가 계산대 위의 큐피드를 보았더라면 무어라고 했겠는가? 그러나 그런 것들이 버젓이 있었다. 텔슨 은행은 그 큐피드를 회칠로 덮어버렸으나, 큐피드는 여전히 천장에서, 더없이 시원한 아마포 차림으로 (늘 그러하듯이) 아침부터 밤까지 돈을 겨누고 있는 모습으로 보였다. 런던 롬바드 거리에서라면 이 어린 이교도 신상 탓에, 또 그 불멸의 소년 뒤편에 휘장을 친 벽감 탓에, 또 벽에 박아 넣은 거울 탓에, 또 아주 사소한 자극에도 사람들 앞에서 춤을 추는, 결코 늙지도 않은 사무원들 탓에 파산을 면치 못했을 것이다. 그러나 프랑스의 텔슨 은행은 이런 것들과 더없이 잘 지낼 수 있었으니, 시국이 그럭저럭 버텨주는 한, 그것들을 보고 겁을 집어먹어 돈을 빼간 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n앞으로 텔슨 은행에서 얼마나 많은 돈이 빠져나갈 것이며, 얼마나 많은 돈이 잃어버린 채 잊힌 채로 거기 잠겨 있을 것인지. 예금주들이 감옥에서 녹슬어가는 동안, 그리고 그들이 무참히 죽임을 당하고 난 뒤에, 얼마나 많은 식기와 보석이 텔슨 은행의 은닉처에서 빛을 잃어갈 것인지. 텔슨 은행과의 거래 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이 이승에서는 결산되지 못한 채 저승으로 넘어가야 할 것인지. 그것은, 자비스 로리 씨가 그러했듯, 그날 밤 누구도 말할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그가 이런 물음들을 무겁게 생각하긴 했어도 그러했다. 그는 갓 지핀 장작불 곁에 앉아 있었는데(시들고 결실 없는 그해는 일찌감치 추웠다), 그의 정직하고 용감한 얼굴에는 늘어뜨린 등불이 드리울 수 있는 것보다도, 방 안의 그 어떤 물건이 일그러뜨려 비출 수 있는 것보다도 더 짙은 그늘이 어려 있었다 — 공포의 그늘이었다.\n\n그는 은행 안에 방을 두고 지냈으니, 굵은 뿌리내린 담쟁이덩굴처럼 한 부분이 되어버린 그 은행에 대한 충심에서였다. 마침 본관을 애국자들이 차지하고 있던 덕분에 일종의 안전이 보장되긴 했으나, 진실한 마음을 지닌 그 노신사는 그런 것을 결코 셈하지 않았다. 그가 자기 임무를 다할 수만 있다면, 그러한 사정은 모두 그에게 아무래도 상관없는 일이었다. 안마당 맞은편, 열주랑 아래에는 마차를 세워두는 넓은 자리가 있었는데, 거기에는 정말로 영주님의 마차 몇 대가 아직 서 있었다. 두 기둥에는 활활 타오르는 커다란 횃불 두 개가 매여 있었고, 그 불빛 속에, 노천에 뚜렷이 드러나 있는 것은 커다란 숫돌이었다 — 인근의 어느 대장간이나 다른 작업장에서 황급히 가져온 듯 거칠게 받침대에 올려놓은 물건이었다. 로리 씨는 일어나 창밖으로 이 무해한 물건들을 내다보다가 몸을 떨었고, 불가의 자기 자리로 물러났다. 그는 유리창뿐 아니라 그 바깥의 격자 덧문까지 열어두었었는데, 두 가지를 다시 닫고 나서 온몸을 부르르 떨었다.\n\n높은 담과 튼튼한 대문 너머의 거리에서, 도시의 여느 밤 같은 웅성거림이 들려왔는데, 이따금 그 속에는 형언할 수 없는 울림이 섞여 있었으니, 기괴하고 이 세상 것 같지 않아서, 마치 끔찍한 본성을 지닌 어떤 심상찮은 소리들이 하늘로 솟구쳐 올라가는 것만 같았다.\n\n\"하느님, 감사합니다.\" 로리 씨가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"오늘 밤 이 무서운 도시에 제게 가깝고 소중한 사람이 아무도 없으니. 위험에 처한 모든 이들에게 자비를 베푸소서!\"\n\n곧이어 대문의 종이 울렸고, 그는 \"그들이 돌아왔구나!\" 하고 생각하며 앉아서 귀를 기울였다. 그러나 그가 예상했던 것처럼 안마당으로 요란하게 밀려드는 소리는 없었고, 그는 대문이 다시 철커덩 닫히는 소리를 들었으며, 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다.\n\n그를 사로잡은 초조함과 두려움은 은행에 대한 막연한 불안을 불러일으켰으니, 그런 감정이 일깨워진 마당에 큰 변화가 자연스레 자아낼 법한 불안이었다. 은행은 잘 지켜지고 있었고, 그는 그곳을 지키는 믿음직한 사람들 사이로 가보려고 일어섰는데, 바로 그때 그의 방문이 갑자기 열리며 두 사람이 뛰어들어왔고, 그 모습에 그는 놀라 뒷걸음질 쳤다.\n\n루시와 그녀의 아버지였다! 루시는 그를 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있었고, 그 한결같은 간절한 표정은 어찌나 응축되고 격해졌던지, 마치 그녀 생애의 이 한 대목에서 그 표정에 힘과 위력을 실어주려고 일부러 그녀의 얼굴에 찍어놓은 것만 같았다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 일이오?\" 로리 씨는 숨도 못 쉬고 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다. \"무슨 일이오? 루시! 머네뜨 박사님! 무슨 일이 일어난 거요? 무엇 때문에 여기 온 거요? 도대체 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n그를 응시한 채, 창백하고 격앙된 모습으로, 그녀는 그의 품에 안겨 애원하듯 헐떡이며 말했다. \"오, 친애하는 친구분! 제 남편이!\"\n\n\"당신 남편이라니, 루시?\"\n\n\"샤를이.\"\n\n\"샤를이 어떻다는 거요?\"\n\n\"여기 있어요.\"\n\n\"여기, 빠리에?\"\n\"며칠째 여기 있어요 — 사나흘 — 며칠인지 모르겠어요 — 정신을 차릴 수가 없어요. 너그러운 일을 하려고 우리도 모르게 여기 온 거예요. 그이는 관문에서 붙들려 감옥으로 보내졌어요.\"\n\n노인은 억누를 수 없는 비명을 내질렀다. 거의 동시에 대문의 종이 다시 울렸고, 발소리와 목소리가 요란하게 안마당으로 쏟아져 들어왔다.\n\n\"저 소리는 무엇이오?\" 박사가 창 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"보지 마십시오!\" 로리 씨가 외쳤다. \"내다보지 마십시오! 머네뜨 박사님, 목숨이 걸렸으니, 덧문에 손대지 마십시오!\"\n\n박사는 창의 걸쇠에 손을 얹은 채 돌아서서, 침착하고 대담한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 친구여, 나는 이 도시에서 마법에 걸린 듯 보호받는 목숨이라오. 나는 바스띠유의 죄수였소. 빠리에서 — 빠리뿐이겠소? 프랑스 전역에서 — 내가 바스띠유의 죄수였음을 아는 애국자라면, 나를 끌어안아 압도하거나 의기양양하게 떠메고 다니려는 것이 아니고서야 나를 건드릴 자는 없소. 내 오랜 고통이 내게 힘을 주어, 그 힘이 우리를 관문으로 통과시켰고, 거기서 샤를의 소식을 알아내게 해주었으며, 우리를 여기까지 데려왔소. 나는 그러리란 걸 알고 있었소. 샤를을 어떤 위험에서든 구해낼 수 있음을 알고 있었소. 루시에게도 그렇게 말했고 — 저 소리는 무엇이오?\" 그의 손은 다시 창에 가 있었다.\n\n\"보지 마십시오!\" 로리 씨가 그야말로 절박하게 외쳤다. \"안 됩니다, 루시, 아가야, 너도 마찬가지다!\" 그는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 붙들었다. \"그렇게 무서워하지 마라, 얘야. 내가 엄숙히 맹세하건대, 샤를에게 무슨 해가 닥쳤다는 이야기는 나는 전혀 아는 바 없으며, 그가 이 끔찍한 곳에 와 있으리라곤 의심조차 해본 적이 없단다. 그가 어느 감옥에 있느냐?\"\n\n\"라포르스 감옥이에요!\"\n\"라포르스라니! 루시, 얘야, 네가 평생 단 한번이라도 용감하고 쓸모 있게 처신한 적이 있다면 — 너는 늘 그 두 가지 다 그러했지만 — 이제 네 마음을 가다듬어, 내가 시키는 대로 똑같이 하거라. 네가 생각하는 것보다, 또 내가 말할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것이 거기에 달려 있으니까. 오늘 밤 네가 무슨 행동을 한들 도움이 될 것은 전혀 없다. 너는 결코 밖으로 나갈 수 없어. 이렇게 말하는 것은, 샤를을 위해 내가 너에게 시켜야 하는 일이 무엇보다도 가장 하기 어려운 일이기 때문이다. 너는 당장 순순히 따라야 하고, 가만히, 조용히 있어야 한다. 내가 너를 이 뒤쪽 방에 들여보내도록 해다오. 너는 두 사람만 — 네 아버지와 나만 — 이 분 동안 남겨두어야 한다. 세상에 삶과 죽음이 있는 만큼, 너는 지체해서는 안 된다.\"\n\n\"시키는 대로 따를게요. 제가 이것 말고는 달리 아무것도 할 수 없다는 걸 아저씨가 아신다는 게 얼굴에 보여요. 아저씨가 진실하다는 걸 알아요.\"\n\n노인은 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 서둘러 그녀를 자기 방으로 들여보낸 뒤 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔다. 그러고는 급히 박사에게 돌아와 창을 열고 덧문을 반쯤 연 다음, 박사의 팔에 손을 얹고 그와 함께 안마당을 내다보았다.\n\n남자들과 여자들의 무리를 내다보았다 — 안마당을 가득 메울 만큼 수가 많지도, 그만큼 가까이 있지도 않았으니, 다 합쳐 마흔이나 쉰을 넘지 않았다. 저택을 차지한 사람들이 대문으로 그들을 들여보냈고, 그들은 숫돌에서 일하려고 몰려든 것이었다. 숫돌은 분명 그들의 용도에 맞게, 편리하고 외진 자리인 양 거기 설치되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 그 얼마나 끔찍한 일꾼들이며, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 일이었던가!\n\n숫돌에는 손잡이가 둘 달려 있었고, 그것을 미친 듯이 돌리고 있는 것은 두 사내였는데, 숫돌이 빙빙 돌면서 그들의 얼굴을 위로 들어 올릴 때 긴 머리카락이 뒤로 휘날리는 그 얼굴은, 가장 야만적으로 분장한 가장 사나운 미개인의 상판보다도 더 무시무시하고 잔인했다. 그들에게는 가짜 눈썹과 가짜 콧수염이 붙어 있었고, 그 흉측한 낯짝은 온통 피와 땀투성이였으며, 고함을 질러대느라 온통 일그러져 있었고, 짐승 같은 흥분과 수면 부족으로 온통 눈을 부릅뜨고 번뜩이고 있었다. 이 무뢰한들이 돌리고 또 돌리는 동안, 엉겨 붙은 머리채가 어떤 때는 앞으로 쏟아져 눈을 덮고 어떤 때는 뒤로 넘어가 목을 덮었는데, 몇몇 여자들이 그들의 입에 포도주를 대주어 마시게 했다. 떨어지는 피와, 떨어지는 포도주와, 돌에서 튀어나오는 불꽃의 줄기로 말미암아, 그들을 둘러싼 사악한 기운은 온통 핏빛과 불빛으로 보였다. 그 무리에서 핏자국 하나 묻지 않은 자는 눈을 씻고 보아도 찾을 수 없었다. 서로 어깨를 밀치며 갈이돌에 먼저 다가가려는 것은, 허리까지 벌거벗은 채 팔다리와 몸뚱이에 온통 핏자국이 묻은 사내들이었고, 온갖 누더기를 걸친 채 그 누더기에 핏자국이 묻은 사내들이었으며, 여자들에게서 약탈한 레이스와 비단과 리본으로 악마처럼 차려입은 채 그 자질구레한 장식들에 핏자국이 속속들이 물들어버린 사내들이었다. 갈려고 가져온 손도끼, 칼, 총검, 검은 죄다 피로 시뻘겼다. 날이 이 빠진 검들 가운데 더러는 아마포 조각과 옷가지 자투리로 그것을 든 자들의 손목에 동여매여 있었으니 — 종류는 가지각색이었으나 한결같이 한 가지 색으로 짙게 물든 끈들이었다. 그리고 이 무기를 휘두르는 광란의 무리가 불꽃의 줄기에서 무기를 낚아채 거리로 내달릴 때, 똑같은 핏빛이 그들의 광기 어린 눈 속에서도 시뻘겋게 타올랐다 — 짐승으로 전락하지 않은 사람이라면 누구든 제대로 겨눈 총 한 방으로 그 눈을 굳혀버릴 수만 있다면 제 목숨에서 이십 년이라도 내놓았을 그런 눈이었다.\n\n이 모든 것은, 물에 빠진 사람의 환영처럼, 혹은 절체절명의 고비에 놓인 어떤 인간이든 만약 거기 한 세상이 있다면 그것을 볼 수 있듯이, 한순간에 눈에 들어왔다. 두 사람은 창에서 물러섰고, 박사는 친구의 잿빛이 된 얼굴에서 설명을 구했다.\n\n\"저들은,\" 로리 씨가 잠긴 방 쪽을 두려운 눈으로 흘끔거리며 그 말을 속삭였다, \"죄수들을 학살하고 있는 겁니다. 당신이 한 말에 확신이 있다면, 당신이 지녔다고 생각하는 그 힘을 정말로 지녔다면 — 나는 당신이 그것을 지녔다고 믿습니다만 — 저 악마들에게 당신이 누구인지 알리고 라포르스로 데려가달라고 하십시오. 너무 늦었을지도 모릅니다, 나도 모르지만, 단 일 분이라도 더 늦지 않게 하십시오!\"\n\n머네뜨 박사는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 서둘러 방을 나섰고, 로리 씨가 덧문가로 돌아왔을 때는 이미 안마당에 나가 있었다.\n\n흩날리는 백발과, 범상치 않은 얼굴과, 무기를 물처럼 헤치며 나아가는 그 거침없이 당당한 태도가, 그를 순식간에 숫돌 둘레로 모여든 군중의 한복판으로 데려갔다. 잠시 멈춤이 있었고, 술렁임이 있었고, 웅성거림이 있었고, 알아들을 수 없는 그의 목소리가 들렸다. 그러고는 로리 씨는, 그가 모두에게 에워싸인 채, 어깨와 어깨를, 손과 어깨를 맞물려 잇댄 스무 명 남짓한 사내들의 행렬 한가운데에서, 다음과 같은 외침과 함께 황급히 끌려 나가는 것을 보았다 — \"바스띠유의 죄수 만세! 라포르스에 있는 바스띠유 죄수의 가족을 도와라! 저 앞쪽에 바스띠유의 죄수가 지날 길을 비켜라! 라포르스의 죄수 에브레몽드를 구하라!\" 그리고 이에 화답하는 천 갈래의 함성이 일었다.\n\n그는 두근거리는 가슴으로 격자 덧문을 다시 닫고, 창과 커튼을 닫은 뒤, 서둘러 루시에게 가서, 그녀의 아버지가 사람들의 도움을 받아 남편을 찾으러 갔다고 일러주었다. 그는 그녀 곁에 그녀의 아이와 프로스 양이 함께 있는 것을 보았으나, 그들이 거기 와 있는 것에 놀라야겠다는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았으니, 한참 뒤에 밤이 허락하는 그 정도의 고요 속에서 그들을 지켜보며 앉아 있을 때에야 비로소 그러했다.\n\n그 무렵 루시는 바닥에 쓰러져, 그의 발치에서 그의 손에 매달린 채 혼절해 있었다. 프로스 양은 아이를 그의 침대에 눕혀놓았는데, 그녀의 머리는 어느새 그 사랑스러운 아이 곁의 베개 위로 떨궈져 있었다. 오, 가련한 아내의 신음 소리와 함께한 그 길고 긴 밤이여! 오, 그녀의 아버지도 돌아오지 않고 아무 소식도 없던 그 길고 긴 밤이여!\n\n어둠 속에서 대문의 종이 두 번 더 울렸고, 무리가 밀려드는 일이 되풀이되었으며, 숫돌이 빙빙 돌며 불꽃을 튀겼다. \"저게 뭐예요?\" 루시가 겁에 질려 외쳤다. \"쉿! 군인들의 검을 저기서 가는 거란다.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이제 저곳은 국가 재산이 되어서, 일종의 무기고처럼 쓰이고 있단다, 얘야.\"\n\n다 합쳐 두 번 더였다. 그러나 마지막으로 일하던 동안은 그 기세가 미약하고 띄엄띄엄했다. 곧이어 동이 트기 시작했고, 그는 매달린 손에서 살그머니 몸을 떼어, 조심스레 다시 밖을 내다보았다. 어떤 사내 하나가, 어찌나 피범벅이 되었던지 시체가 널린 들판에서 의식을 되찾으려 기어 나오는 중상 입은 병사라 해도 믿을 만한 자가, 숫돌 옆 포석에서 몸을 일으켜 멍한 표정으로 주위를 둘러보고 있었다. 이내 이 기진맥진한 살인자는 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 영주님의 마차 한 대를 발견하고는, 그 호화로운 탈것으로 비틀비틀 다가가 문으로 기어 올라가, 그 고운 방석 위에서 쉬려고 안에 틀어박혔다.\n\n로리 씨가 다시 밖을 내다보았을 때, 그 거대한 숫돌인 지구가 돌아가 있었고, 안마당에는 붉은 해가 비치고 있었다. 그러나 그보다 작은 숫돌은 잔잔한 아침 공기 속에 홀로 서 있었으니, 그 위에는 해가 결코 입혀준 적도 없고 결코 거두어가지도 못할 붉은빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER III. The Shadow\n\n업무 시간이 다시 돌아오자 로리 씨의 사무적인 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각 가운데 하나는 이것이었다. 망명자 죄수의 아내를 은행 지붕 아래 숨겨 둠으로써 텔슨을 위태롭게 할 권리가 자신에게는 없다는 것이었다. 자기 자신의 재산이며 안전이며 목숨이라면 한순간의 망설임도 없이 루시와 그 아이를 위해 내걸었을 테지만, 그가 맡고 있는 그 막중한 신탁은 그의 것이 아니었으며, 그 업무상의 책임에 관한 한 그는 엄정한 사무가였다.\n\n처음에 그의 마음은 드파르주에게로 돌아갔고, 다시 그 포도주 가게를 찾아내어 어지러운 도시 형편 속에서 가장 안전한 거처에 관해 그 주인과 상의해 볼까 생각했다. 그러나 그를 떠올리게 했던 바로 그 고려가 또한 그를 물리쳤다. 그는 가장 험악한 구역에 살고 있었고, 그곳에서 영향력이 큰 인물임에 틀림없었으며, 그 위험한 움직임에 깊이 발을 담그고 있었다.\n\n정오가 되었는데도 의사는 돌아오지 않았고, 일분 일분 지체될 때마다 텔슨이 위태로워질 형편이라, 로리 씨는 루시와 의논했다. 그녀는 아버지가 그 구역의 은행 근처에 잠시 머무를 셋방을 얻겠다는 말을 했다고 일러 주었다. 이에 대해서는 업무상 거리낄 것이 없었고, 또 설령 샤를에게 모든 일이 잘 풀려 그가 풀려난다 하더라도 도시를 떠나기는 바랄 수 없으리라는 것을 그가 내다보았으므로, 로리 씨는 그런 셋방을 찾아 나섰고, 외진 골목 높직한 곳에서 적당한 곳을 하나 찾아냈는데, 그 음울한 사각형 건물군의 다른 모든 창문에 닫힌 덧문들이 버려진 집들을 표시하고 있었다.\n\n그는 곧장 이 셋방으로 루시와 그 아이, 그리고 프로스 양을 옮겨 주었다. 자신이 줄 수 있는 만큼, 자기 자신이 누리는 것보다 훨씬 많은 위안을 그들에게 베풀었다. 그는 제리를 그들 곁에 남겨 두었는데, 어지간히 머리를 두들겨 맞아도 견딜 만한, 출입구를 채우기에 알맞은 인물이었기 때문이다. 그러고는 자신의 일로 돌아왔다. 어지럽고 침울한 마음을 안고 그 일에 매달렸으며, 그날 하루는 더디고 무겁게 그와 함께 흘러갔다.\n\n하루는 스스로 닳아 가고, 그와 더불어 그를 닳게 하다가, 마침내 은행이 문을 닫았다. 그는 다시금 전날 밤의 그 방에 홀로 있으면서 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 궁리하던 차에, 계단을 오르는 발소리를 들었다. 잠시 뒤, 한 남자가 그의 앞에 서서 날카롭게 살피는 눈길로 그를 보더니 그의 이름을 불렀다.\n\n\"무엇을 도와드릴까요.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"저를 아십니까?\"\n\n그는 검은 곱슬머리에 다부지게 생긴, 마흔다섯에서 쉰 살가량의 남자였다. 대답 대신 그는 어조에 조금도 변화를 주지 않고 그 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"저를 아십니까?\"\n\n\"어디선가 뵌 적이 있군요.\"\n\n\"혹시 제 포도주 가게에서?\"\n\n크게 흥미를 느끼며 동요한 로리 씨가 말했다. \"마네뜨 의사 선생께서 보내서 오셨습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 마네뜨 의사 선생께서 보내서 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"그분이 뭐라 하십니까? 저에게 무엇을 보내셨습니까?\"\n\n드파르주는 애가 타서 내미는 그의 손에 펼쳐진 종잇조각 하나를 건넸다. 거기에는 의사의 필체로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"샤를은 무사하나, 나는 아직 이곳을 안전하게 떠날 수가 없소. 이 사람이 샤를에게서 그 아내에게 보내는 짧은 글을 지니고 가도록 허락을 얻었소. 이 사람이 그 아내를 만나게 해 주시오.\"\n\n라포르스에서 한 시간 안에 쓴 날짜가 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"이분 아내가 사는 곳까지,\" 이 쪽지를 소리 내어 읽고 나서 기쁘게 안도한 로리 씨가 말했다. \"저와 함께 가시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러지요.\" 드파르주가 대답했다.\n\n드파르주가 얼마나 묘하게 절제된, 기계적인 태도로 말하는지 아직 거의 눈치채지 못한 채, 로리 씨는 모자를 쓰고 그들은 안마당으로 내려갔다. 거기서 그들은 두 여자를 만났는데, 그중 하나는 뜨개질을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"드파르주 부인이로군요, 틀림없이!\" 로리 씨가 말했다. 그는 십칠 년쯤 전에 바로 똑같은 자세로 있던 그녀를 두고 떠났던 것이다.\n\n\"그녀가 맞소.\" 그녀의 남편이 말했다.\n\n\"부인께서도 우리와 함께 가십니까?\" 그녀가 자기들 움직임을 따라 움직이는 것을 보고 로리 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소. 얼굴들을 알아보고 사람들을 익혀 두기 위해서요. 그들의 안전을 위한 것이오.\"\n\n드파르주의 태도에 점차 마음이 쓰이기 시작한 로리 씨는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 보고는 앞장섰다. 두 여자가 모두 뒤를 따랐는데, 두번째 여자는 복수의 여신이었다.\n\n그들은 그 사이의 거리들을 되도록 빨리 지나, 새 거처의 계단을 올라갔고, 제리의 안내를 받아 들어가, 홀로 울고 있는 루시를 발견했다. 그녀는 로리 씨가 전해 준 남편의 소식에 환희에 휩싸였고, 그 쪽지를 건네준 손을 부여잡았다. 그 손이 그날 밤 그의 곁에서 무슨 일을 했었는지, 그리고 어쩌다 한순간의 우연이 아니었던들 그에게 무슨 일을 했을지는 까맣게 모른 채.\n\n\"사랑하는 그대여, 용기를 내시오. 나는 잘 있고, 그대의 아버지가 내 주위에 영향력을 가지고 있소. 이것에 답장할 수는 없소. 나 대신 우리 아이에게 입맞춰 주시오.\"\n\n적혀 있는 것은 그것이 전부였다. 그러나 그것을 받아 든 그녀에게는 너무도 큰 것이어서, 그녀는 드파르주에게서 그 아내에게로 몸을 돌려, 뜨개질하는 그 손 가운데 하나에 입을 맞추었다. 그것은 격정적이고 사랑스럽고 감사에 찬, 여성스러운 행동이었으나, 그 손은 아무런 응답이 없었다. 차갑고 묵직하게 떨어지더니, 다시 뜨개질을 시작했다.\n\n그 손이 닿는 감촉에는 루시를 멈칫하게 하는 무언가가 있었다. 그녀는 쪽지를 품에 넣으려다가 동작을 멈추었고, 손을 아직 목께에 댄 채로, 겁에 질린 얼굴로 드파르주 부인을 바라보았다. 드파르주 부인은 치켜올라간 눈썹과 이마를 차갑고 무표정한 응시로 맞받았다.\n\n\"부인,\" 로리 씨가 끼어들어 설명했다. \"거리에서는 폭동이 자주 일어납니다. 그리고 그런 일들이 부인을 괴롭힐 것 같지는 않지만, 드파르주 부인께서는 그러한 때에 자신이 보호할 권한이 있는 이들을 보아 두기를 바라십니다. 그들을 알아 두기 위해서요, 그들을 식별할 수 있도록 말입니다. 제 생각에는,\" 세 사람 모두의 돌덩이 같은 태도가 점점 더 강하게 그를 짓누르는 통에, 그는 안심시키려는 말을 다소 더듬거리며 말했다. \"제가 사정을 제대로 말씀드린 것 같은데요, 드파르주 시민?\"\n\n드파르주는 음울한 눈으로 자기 아내를 보았고, 무뚝뚝하게 동의하는 소리 외에 다른 대답은 하지 않았다.\n\n\"루시, 사랑하는 그 아이를,\" 어조와 태도로 할 수 있는 한 그들의 비위를 맞추려 애쓰며 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이리로 데려오는 게 좋겠소. 그리고 우리의 착한 프로스도. 우리의 착한 프로스는, 드파르주, 영국 부인이고 프랑스어를 전혀 모릅니다.\"\n\n문제의 그 부인은, 어떤 외국인이라도 자기가 능히 당해 낼 수 있다는 뿌리 깊은 확신을 품고 있었고, 고통과 위험에도 흔들리지 않은 채, 팔짱을 끼고 나타나서는, 눈이 처음으로 마주친 복수의 여신을 향해 영어로 한마디 했다. \"흥, 정말이지, 뻔뻔한 양반아! 너야 아주 잘 지내시겠지!\" 그녀는 또 드파르주 부인에게는 영국식 기침을 한 번 선사했다. 그러나 두 여자 가운데 누구도 그녀에게 별 신경을 쓰지 않았다.\n\n\"저 아이가 그의 자식인가?\" 드파르주 부인이 처음으로 손을 멈추고, 마치 운명의 손가락이기라도 한 듯 뜨개바늘을 어린 루시에게 겨누며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 부인.\" 로리 씨가 대답했다. \"이 아이가 우리 가엾은 죄수의 사랑하는 딸이자 외동입니다.\"\n\n드파르주 부인과 그 일행에게 따라붙은 그림자가 그 아이 위로 어찌나 위협적이고 어둡게 드리우는 듯했던지, 아이의 어머니는 본능적으로 그 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 아이를 가슴에 끌어안았다. 그러자 드파르주 부인과 그 일행에게 따라붙은 그림자는 위협적이고 어둡게, 어머니와 아이 양쪽 위로 드리우는 듯했다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐소, 여보.\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다. \"그들을 봤소. 우리는 가도 되오.\"\n\n그러나 그 억눌린 태도에는 — 눈에 보이게 내세운 것이 아니라 어렴풋이 감추어진 것이었으나 — 충분한 위협이 깃들어 있어서, 루시는 불안에 떨며 드파르주 부인의 옷에 호소하듯 손을 얹고 말했다.\n\n\"제 가엾은 남편에게 잘해 주시겠지요. 그이를 해치지 않으시겠지요. 할 수 있으시다면 제가 그이를 만나도록 도와주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"당신 남편은 여기서 내가 상관할 바가 아니오.\" 드파르주 부인이 더없이 차분하게 그녀를 내려다보며 대답했다. \"내가 여기서 상관할 바는 당신 아버지의 딸이오.\"\n\n\"그러면 저를 보아서라도, 제 남편에게 자비를 베풀어 주세요. 제 아이를 보아서라도요! 이 아이가 두 손을 모아 자비를 베풀어 달라고 빌 거예요. 우리는 다른 누구보다도 당신이 두렵습니다.\"\n\n드파르주 부인은 그것을 칭찬으로 받아들이고는 자기 남편을 바라보았다. 불안하게 엄지손톱을 깨물며 그녀를 보고 있던 드파르주는, 자기 얼굴을 한층 더 엄한 표정으로 다잡았다.\n\n\"당신 남편이 그 짤막한 편지에서 뭐라고 한 거요?\" 드파르주 부인이 음울하게 미소 지으며 물었다. \"영향력 — 영향력에 관해 무어라고 쓴 모양인데?\"\n\n\"제 아버지가,\" 루시가 황급히 품에서 그 종이를 꺼냈지만, 그것이 아니라 묻는 이에게 겁에 질린 눈길을 둔 채로 말했다. \"그이 주위에서 큰 영향력을 갖고 있다고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 분명 그를 풀어 주겠군!\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다. \"그렇게 되도록 내버려 두지.\"\n\n\"아내이자 어머니로서,\" 루시가 더없이 간절하게 외쳤다. \"저를 가엾이 여기시어, 당신이 가진 어떤 힘도 제 죄 없는 남편에게 거슬러 쓰지 마시고, 그이를 위해 써 주시기를 애원합니다. 오, 같은 여인이여, 저를 생각해 주세요. 아내이자 어머니로서요!\"\n\n드파르주 부인은 여전히 차가운 눈으로 그 애원하는 이를 바라보더니, 자기 친구인 복수의 여신을 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 이 아이만 했을 때부터, 아니 훨씬 더 어렸을 때부터 보아 온 그 아내들과 어머니들이 별로 배려받기나 했던가? 우리는 그네들의 남편과 아버지가 감옥에 갇혀 그들에게서 떼어 놓이는 꼴을 숱하게 보지 않았던가? 평생토록 우리는, 같은 여인들이 제 몸으로, 또 제 자식들로, 가난과 헐벗음과 굶주림과 목마름과 병과 비참과 억압과 온갖 방치를 겪는 것을 보아 오지 않았던가?\"\n\n\"우리는 그것 말고 다른 것은 본 적이 없지.\" 복수의 여신이 대답했다.\n\n\"우리는 이것을 오랫동안 견뎌 왔어.\" 드파르주 부인이 다시 루시에게로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"당신이 판단해 보시오! 아내이자 어머니 하나의 괴로움이 이제 와서 우리에게 무슨 대수겠소?\"\n\n그녀는 다시 뜨개질을 시작하며 밖으로 나갔다. 복수의 여신이 뒤를 따랐다. 드파르주는 맨 마지막에 나가서 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"용기를 내요, 사랑하는 루시.\" 로리 씨가 그녀를 일으키며 말했다. \"용기를, 용기를! 지금까지는 우리에게 모든 일이 잘되어 가고 있소. 근래에 수많은 가엾은 사람들에게 닥친 것보다 훨씬, 훨씬 나은 형편이오. 기운을 내고, 감사하는 마음을 가져요.\"\n\n\"저도 감사할 줄 모르는 사람은 아닐 거예요. 하지만 저 무서운 여자가 저에게, 그리고 제 모든 희망 위에 그림자를 드리우는 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"쯧, 쯧!\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"용감한 작은 가슴속에 이 무슨 낙담이오? 그림자라니! 거기에는 실체가 없소, 루시.\"\n\n그러나 그 드파르주 부부의 태도가 드리운 그림자는, 그럼에도 불구하고 그 자신에게도 어둡게 드리워 있었고, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그를 크게 괴롭혔다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV. Calm in Storm\n\n「마네뜨」 박사는 집을 떠난 지 나흘째 되는 날 아침에야 돌아왔다. 그 끔찍한 시간 동안 일어난 일들 가운데 「루시」에게 알리지 않을 수 있는 것은 그녀에게 어찌나 철저히 감춰졌던지, 한참 뒤에 「프랑스」와 그녀가 멀리 떨어지고 나서야 그녀는 비로소 알게 되었다. 남녀노소를 가리지 않은, 무방비 상태의 죄수 천백 명이 군중에게 살해당했다는 것을, 그 공포의 만행으로 나흘 밤낮이 어둠에 잠겼다는 것을, 그리고 그녀를 둘러싼 공기가 살해된 자들로 인해 오염되었다는 것을. 그녀가 아는 것이라고는 감옥을 향한 습격이 있었다는 것, 모든 정치범이 위험에 처해 있었다는 것, 그리고 일부는 군중에게 끌려 나가 살해되었다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n「로리」 씨에게는, 박사가 굳이 강조할 필요도 없는 비밀 엄수의 다짐 아래 이렇게 털어놓았다. 군중이 그를 학살의 현장을 지나 「라포르스」 감옥으로 데려갔다는 것을. 그 감옥에서 그는 스스로 임명한 재판소가 열려 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그 앞으로 죄수들이 한 사람씩 끌려 나오면, 그 재판소는 신속하게 그들을 끌어내 학살하라거나, 석방하라거나, (몇몇 경우에는) 다시 감방으로 돌려보내라고 명령했다는 것을. 그를 데려온 자들에 의해 이 재판소 앞에 서게 되자, 그는 자신이 「바스띠유」에서 십팔 년간 기소도 없이 비밀리에 갇혀 있던 죄수였다며 이름과 신분을 밝혔다는 것을. 그렇게 자리에 앉아 심판을 내리던 무리 가운데 하나가 일어나 그를 알아보았는데, 그 사람이 바로 「드파르주」였다는 것을.\n\n이 일로 그는 탁자 위의 명부를 통해 자신의 사위가 살아 있는 죄수들 가운데 있음을 확인하고는, 재판소를 향해 그의 목숨과 자유를 위해 간절히 호소했다는 것을 — 그 재판소의 구성원들 가운데 일부는 졸고 있고 일부는 깨어 있었으며, 일부는 살인으로 더럽혀져 있고 일부는 깨끗했으며, 일부는 술에 취해 있지 않고 일부는 취해 있었다. 무너진 체제 아래서 고초를 겪은 저명한 수난자로서 그에게 쏟아진 처음의 광적인 환대 속에서, 「샤를 다네」를 그 무법의 법정 앞으로 끌어내 심문하도록 허락받았다는 것을. 그가 곧바로 석방될 듯한 찰나에, 그에게 유리하던 흐름이 (박사로서는 이해할 수 없는) 어떤 설명 없는 제동에 부딪혔고, 그것이 몇 마디 은밀한 협의로 이어졌다는 것을. 그러자 의장으로 앉아 있던 사람이 「마네뜨」 박사에게, 죄수는 계속 구금되어야 하지만, 박사를 봐서 안전한 구금 상태에서 침해받지 않도록 보호하겠다고 알렸다는 것을. 곧바로 신호가 떨어지자 죄수는 다시 감옥 안쪽으로 끌려갔으나, 박사 자신은 그곳에 남아 자기 사위가 어떤 악의나 불운으로 인해, 문밖에서 살기 어린 고함으로 종종 재판 진행 소리마저 덮어버리던 그 무리에게 넘겨지지 않았음을 직접 확인하게 해달라고 어찌나 강하게 호소했던지, 마침내 허락을 얻어 위험이 지나갈 때까지 그 피의 홀에 남아 있었다는 것을.\n\n그가 거기서 간간이 짧게 음식을 먹고 잠을 자며 목격한 광경들은 입에 담지 않은 채 두기로 하자. 목숨을 건진 죄수들을 향한 광적인 환희는, 갈가리 찢긴 자들을 향한 광적인 잔인함 못지않게 그를 경악케 했다. 그가 말하기를, 한 죄수가 자유의 몸이 되어 거리로 풀려났는데, 그가 빠져나가는 순간 어떤 착각한 야만인이 그에게 창을 찔렀다고 했다. 그에게 가서 상처를 치료해달라는 간청을 받고 박사는 같은 문으로 빠져나갔는데, 그가 자기네 희생자들의 시체 위에 걸터앉은 한 무리의 사마리아인들 품에 안겨 있는 것을 발견했다. 이 끔찍한 악몽 속의 그 무엇 못지않게 기괴한 모순으로, 그들은 그 치료자를 돕고 더할 나위 없이 다정한 보살핌으로 부상자를 돌보았으며 — 그를 위해 들것을 만들고 그 자리에서 조심스레 호위해 데려가더니 — 그러고는 다시 무기를 집어 들고, 박사가 두 손으로 눈을 가린 채 그 한복판에서 정신을 잃을 만큼 끔찍한 도살에 새로이 뛰어들었다.\n\n「로리」 씨는 이러한 비밀스러운 이야기를 전해 들으며, 이제 예순두 살이 된 친구의 얼굴을 지켜보는 동안, 그런 무서운 경험들이 옛 위험을 되살리지나 않을까 하는 불안이 마음속에 일었다.\n\n그러나 그는 지금 같은 모습의 친구를 한 번도 본 적이 없었으니, 지금 같은 성품의 그를 도무지 알지 못했던 것이다. 박사는 이제 처음으로, 자신의 고난이 곧 힘이요 권능임을 느꼈다. 그는 처음으로, 그 매서운 불길 속에서 자기 딸의 남편을 가둔 감옥의 문을 부수고 그를 구해낼 수 있는 무쇠를 천천히 벼려왔음을 느꼈다. \"이 모든 것이 좋은 결말로 이어졌다네, 친구. 그것은 한낱 낭비와 파멸이 아니었어. 내 사랑하는 아이가 나를 나 자신에게 되돌려놓는 데 도움을 주었듯이, 이제 나는 그 아이에게서 가장 소중한 부분을 그 아이에게 되돌려놓는 데 도움이 될 걸세. 하늘의 도우심으로 내 그것을 해내고 말겠네!\" 이렇게 「마네뜨」 박사는 말했다. 그리고 「자비스 로리」가, 그토록 오랜 세월 시계처럼 멈춰 있다가 그 쓸모가 멎어 있는 동안 잠들어 있던 기운으로 다시 돌아가기 시작한 것만 같던 그 사람의, 불타오르는 눈과 결연한 얼굴, 침착하고 강인한 표정과 태도를 보았을 때, 그는 믿었다.\n\n그 무렵 박사가 맞서야 했던 것보다 더 거대한 일들이라도 그의 끈질긴 결의 앞에서는 굴복했을 것이다. 그는 신분이 매인 자든 자유로운 자든, 부유한 자든 가난한 자든, 악한 자든 선한 자든 온갖 부류의 사람들을 상대하는 의사로서 자기 자리를 지키면서, 그 개인적 영향력을 어찌나 현명하게 활용했던지, 머지않아 그는 세 곳의 감옥을 감독하는 의사가 되었고, 그 가운데 「라포르스」도 있었다. 그는 이제 「루시」에게 그녀의 남편이 더는 혼자 갇혀 있지 않고 일반 죄수들 무리에 섞여 있다고 안심시킬 수 있었다. 그는 매주 그녀의 남편을 만났고, 그의 입에서 곧장 나온 다정한 전갈을 그녀에게 전해주었다. 때로는 그녀의 남편이 직접 그녀에게 편지를 보내기도 했지만 (결코 박사의 손을 거치지는 않았다), 그녀는 그에게 편지를 쓰는 것이 허락되지 않았다. 감옥 안의 음모에 대한 숱한 거친 의혹들 가운데서도 가장 거친 것은 해외에서 친구를 사귀거나 영속적인 연줄을 맺은 것으로 알려진 망명자들을 겨냥하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n박사의 이 새로운 삶은 의심할 바 없이 불안한 삶이었다. 그래도 사려 깊은 「로리」 씨는 그 안에 그를 지탱해주는 새로운 자부심이 있음을 보았다. 그 자부심에는 어울리지 않는 빛깔이라곤 조금도 섞이지 않았으니, 그것은 자연스럽고 떳떳한 것이었다. 다만 그는 그것을 진기한 것으로 눈여겨보았다. 박사는 알고 있었다. 그때까지 자신의 투옥이 딸과 친구의 마음속에서 자신의 개인적 고통과 박탈과 나약함과 결부되어 있었음을. 이제 그것이 바뀌어, 그 옛 시련을 통해 자신이 어떤 힘을 부여받았으며, 두 사람 모두 「샤를」의 궁극적 안전과 구출을 위해 바로 그 힘에 기대고 있음을 알게 되자, 그는 그 변화로 어찌나 고양되었던지 앞장서서 이끌고 지시하기에 이르렀고, 약한 자인 두 사람에게 강한 자인 자신을 믿으라고 요구했다. 자신과 「루시」의 이전 위치는 뒤바뀌었으나, 그것은 오직 더없이 생생한 감사와 애정만이 뒤바꿀 수 있는 방식의 뒤바뀜이었으니, 자신에게 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어준 그녀에게 어떤 봉사라도 하는 것 외에 그가 가질 수 있는 자부심이란 없었기 때문이다. \"다 진기하게 볼 만한 일이지.\" 「로리」 씨는 그 다정하고도 빈틈없는 방식으로 생각했다. \"하지만 다 자연스럽고 옳은 일이야. 그러니 앞장서게, 친애하는 친구여, 그리고 그 자리를 지키게. 이보다 더 좋은 손에 맡겨질 수는 없을 테니.\"\n\n그러나 박사가 「샤를 다네」를 풀어주거나, 적어도 재판에 회부되도록 하려고 무던히 애쓰며 결코 그 노력을 멈추지 않았음에도, 그 시대의 공적 흐름은 그에게 맞서기에는 너무도 강하고 빨랐다. 새로운 시대가 시작되었다. 왕은 재판에 부쳐졌고, 사형을 선고받았으며, 참수되었다. 자유, 평등, 형제애, 그렇지 않으면 죽음을 내건 공화국이 무장한 세계를 상대로 승리 아니면 죽음을 선포했다. 검은 깃발이 노트르담의 거대한 탑들에서 밤낮없이 나부꼈다. 지상의 폭군들에 맞서 일어나라는 소집을 받은 삼십만 명이, 마치 용의 이빨이 사방에 뿌려져 언덕에서나 평원에서나, 바위에서나 자갈밭에서나 충적토에서나, 남쪽의 밝은 하늘 아래서나 북쪽의 구름 아래서나, 황야와 숲에서나, 포도밭과 올리브밭에서나, 베어낸 풀밭과 곡식 그루터기 사이에서나, 너른 강의 기름진 둑을 따라서나, 바닷가 모래밭에서나 똑같이 열매를 맺기라도 한 듯이, 「프랑스」의 온갖 다채로운 토양에서 떨쳐 일어났다. 어떤 개인적 염려가 자유의 원년이라는 대홍수에 맞서 스스로를 일으켜 세울 수 있었으랴 — 위에서 쏟아져 내리는 것이 아니라 아래에서 차올라오는, 하늘의 창문이 열린 것이 아니라 닫힌 그 대홍수에!\n\n멈춤도, 연민도, 평화도, 누그러지는 안식의 틈도, 시간을 가늠할 길도 없었다. 시간이 젊었던 시절처럼 밤과 낮이 규칙적으로 돌고, 저녁과 아침이 첫째 날을 이루었음에도, 그 밖의 다른 시간의 셈이란 없었다. 한 환자의 열병 속에서 그러하듯, 한 나라의 미쳐 날뛰는 열병 속에서 시간에 대한 감각은 사라져버렸다. 이제 온 도시의 부자연스러운 침묵을 깨뜨리며 처형인이 사람들에게 왕의 머리를 보여주었고 — 그러더니 거의 같은 숨결에, 갇힌 채 과부로 지낸 여덟 달의 고단함과 비참함이 머리칼을 잿빛으로 물들여놓은 그 고운 아내의 머리를 보여주는 듯했다.\n\n그런데도, 그런 모든 경우에 적용되는 모순의 기이한 법칙을 따라, 시간은 그토록 빠르게 불꽃처럼 스쳐 가면서도 더디기만 했다. 수도에는 혁명재판소가 하나, 그리고 온 나라에는 사오만 개에 이르는 혁명위원회가 있었다. 자유나 생명에 대한 모든 안전을 앗아가고, 선량하고 무고한 사람을 누구든 악하고 죄 있는 자에게 넘겨버리는 용의자법이 있었다. 아무 죄도 짓지 않았으나 심리조차 받지 못하는 사람들로 감옥은 가득 찼다. 이런 것들이 정해진 질서이자 마땅한 사물의 본성이 되어, 생겨난 지 몇 주도 채 되지 않았는데도 마치 오래된 관습인 양 여겨졌다. 무엇보다도, 마치 세상의 기초가 놓이던 때부터 만인의 시선 앞에 있어 온 것처럼 친숙해진 하나의 흉측한 형상이 자라났으니 — 「라기요띤」이라 불리는 그 날카로운 여인의 형상이었다.\n\n그것은 농담의 단골 소재였다. 그것은 두통에 가장 잘 듣는 치료제였고, 머리칼이 잿빛으로 세는 것을 어김없이 막아주었으며, 안색에 독특한 섬세함을 더해주었고, 바짝 깎아주는 국민의 면도칼이었다. 「라기요띤」에게 입을 맞추는 자는 그 작은 창문으로 내다보다가 자루 속으로 재채기를 했다. 그것은 인류가 새로 태어났다는 표지였다. 그것은 십자가를 대신했다. 십자가를 내던진 가슴마다 그것의 모형을 달았고, 십자가가 부정된 곳에서 그것을 향해 머리를 조아리며 그것을 믿었다.\n\n그것은 어찌나 많은 머리를 베어냈던지, 그것과 그것이 가장 더럽힌 땅이 썩은 붉은빛으로 물들었다. 그것은 어린 악마를 위한 장난감 퍼즐처럼 분해되었다가, 필요한 때가 오면 다시 조립되었다. 그것은 웅변가의 입을 다물게 했고, 권세 있는 자를 거꾸러뜨렸으며, 아름답고 선한 것을 없애버렸다. 어느 날 아침 단 몇 분 만에, 그것은 세간에 이름 높은 친구 스물두 명의 — 산 자가 스물하나요 죽은 자가 하나인 — 머리를 베어냈다. 그것을 다루는 우두머리 일꾼에게는 옛 성서에 나오는 장사의 이름이 붙여졌다. 그러나 그렇게 무장한 그는 자신과 이름이 같은 그 장사보다 더 강했고, 더 눈멀었으며, 날마다 하느님의 성전 문짝을 뜯어냈다.\n\n이 공포들과, 그것들에 딸린 무리들 한가운데서 박사는 흔들림 없는 머리로 걸어다녔다. 자신의 힘을 확신하고, 자신의 목적을 향해 신중하게 끈질겼으며, 끝내 「루시」의 남편을 구해내리라는 것을 결코 의심하지 않았다. 그러나 그 시대의 흐름은 어찌나 강하고 깊게 휩쓸어 가며 어찌나 사납게 시간을 앗아갔던지, 박사가 이토록 흔들림 없이 확신에 차 있는 동안 「샤를」은 일 년 삼 개월째 감옥에 갇혀 있었다. 그 십이월 한 달 동안 혁명은 그만큼 더 사악하고 광란에 빠져, 남쪽의 강들은 밤사이 폭력적으로 익사당한 자들의 시체로 막혔고, 죄수들은 남쪽의 겨울 햇살 아래 줄줄이, 무리지어 총살당했다. 그래도 박사는 그 공포들 한가운데를 흔들림 없는 머리로 걸어다녔다. 그 시절 「파리」에서 그보다 더 잘 알려진 사람은 없었고, 그보다 더 기이한 처지에 놓인 사람도 없었다. 과묵하고, 인정 많고, 병원과 감옥에 없어서는 안 될 존재였으며, 암살자들 사이에서나 희생자들 사이에서나 똑같이 자신의 의술을 베푸는 그는, 따로 떨어진 사람이었다. 그 의술을 펼치는 데서, 「바스띠유」 수인의 풍모와 사연은 그를 다른 모든 사람들로부터 구별지었다. 그는 마치 십팔 년쯤 전에 정말로 되살아난 사람이거나 인간들 사이를 거니는 영혼이기라도 한 듯, 의심받거나 의문에 부쳐지는 일이 없었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V. The Wood-Sawyer\n\n일년 삼개월. 그 기간 내내 루시는 단 한순간도, 다음날이면 단두대가 남편의 목을 베어버리지나 않을까 하는 불안에서 벗어나지 못했다. 날마다, 돌투성이 거리를 가로질러, 이제는 사형수들을 가득 실은 호송 수레가 묵직하게 덜컹거리며 지나갔다. 사랑스러운 처녀들, 활기찬 여인들, 갈색 머리도 검은 머리도 흰 머리도, 젊은이들, 건장한 사내들과 노인들, 귀하게 태어난 자들과 천하게 태어난 자들 — 모두가 라기요면을 위한 붉은 포도주였고, 모두가 날마다 그 혐오스러운 감옥의 어두운 지하실에서 밝은 데로 끌려 나와, 거리를 지나 그녀에게로 옮겨져, 게걸스럽게 삼키는 그녀의 갈증을 적셔주었다. 자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음 — 그 마지막 것이야말로 베풀기에 가장 쉬운 것이었으니, 오 단두대여!\n\n만일 갑작스럽게 닥친 재난과 빙글빙글 돌아가는 시대의 수레바퀴가 박사의 딸을 멍하게 만들어 부질없는 절망 속에 결과만을 기다리게 했다면, 그녀의 처지도 그저 많은 이들과 다르지 않았으리라. 그러나 쌩땅뚜안의 다락방에서 그 백발의 머리를 자신의 싱싱하고 젊은 가슴에 끌어안았던 그 순간부터, 그녀는 자신의 의무에 충실했다. 조용히 충직하고 선량한 모든 이들이 늘 그러하듯, 그녀 역시 시련의 시기에 가장 충실했다.\n\n새 거처에 자리를 잡고 아버지가 일상의 직무에 들어서자마자, 그녀는 마치 남편이 거기 있기라도 한 듯 그 작은 살림을 정확하게 꾸려나갔다. 모든 것에는 정해진 자리와 정해진 시간이 있었다. 어린 루시는, 마치 그들 모두가 영국의 보금자리에 함께 있기라도 한 듯, 규칙적으로 가르쳤다. 그들이 곧 다시 만날 것이라는 믿음을 꾸며내어 스스로를 달래던 사소한 방편들 — 그의 빠른 귀환을 위한 자그마한 준비, 그의 의자와 책을 따로 챙겨두는 일 — 그리고 옥에 갇히고 죽음의 그림자에 덮인 수많은 불행한 영혼들 가운데서도 특별히 한 사람의 사랑하는 죄수를 위해 밤마다 올리는 엄숙한 기도, 이런 것들이 무거운 그녀의 마음을 풀어주는 거의 유일한 출구였다.\n\n겉모습은 크게 변하지 않았다. 그녀와 아이가 입은, 상복에 가까운 수수하고 어두운 옷들은, 행복하던 시절의 밝은 옷들 못지않게 단정하고 정갈하게 손질되어 있었다. 혈색은 잃었고, 예전의 골똘한 표정이 이제는 이따금이 아니라 늘 자리잡고 있었지만, 그밖에는 여전히 무척 곱고 어여뻤다. 이따금 밤에 아버지에게 입을 맞추다가, 하루 종일 억눌렀던 슬픔이 북받쳐 올라, 하늘 아래 자신이 의지할 분은 오직 아버지뿐이라고 말하곤 했다. 그러면 그는 언제나 단호하게 대답했다. \"내가 모르는 사이에 그애에게 무슨 일이 일어날 리 없고, 나는 그애를 구할 수 있다는 걸 알고 있단다, 루시야.\"\n\n달라진 생활을 시작한 지 몇 주 지나지 않은 어느 저녁, 집으로 돌아온 아버지가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"얘야, 감옥에 위층 창문이 하나 있는데, 샤를이 이따금 오후 세시쯤 거기로 갈 수가 있단다. 그애가 거기 닿을 수 있을 때면 — 여러가지 불확실한 사정과 우연에 달려 있긴 하다만 — 그애 생각에, 네가 내가 일러주는 어느 자리에 서 있으면 거리에 있는 너를 볼 수 있을 거라는구나. 하지만 너는 그애를 볼 수 없을 게다, 가엾은 것아. 설령 볼 수 있다 해도 알아보는 기색을 내는 건 네게 위험한 일이란다.\"\n\n\"오, 그 자리를 알려주세요, 아버지. 매일 거기 가겠어요.\"\n\n그때부터, 날씨가 어떻든, 그녀는 거기서 두 시간씩 기다렸다. 시계가 두시를 치면 그녀는 거기 있었고, 네시가 되면 체념한 듯 발길을 돌렸다. 아이를 데리고 다닐 수 없을 만큼 비가 많이 오거나 날씨가 사납지 않을 때면 둘이 함께 갔고, 그렇지 않을 때면 혼자였지만, 하루도 거르는 법이 없었다.\n\n그곳은 작고 구불구불한 거리의 어둡고 지저분한 모퉁이였다. 그쪽 끝에 있는 집이라고는 땔감 길이로 나무를 자르는 톱장이의 오두막 한 채뿐이었고, 나머지는 온통 담벼락이었다. 그녀가 거기 다닌 지 사흘째 되는 날, 그가 그녀를 알아보았다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 여성 시민.\"\n\n\"안녕하세요, 시민.\"\n\n이런 호칭 방식은 이제 법령으로 규정되어 있었다. 얼마 전 더 철저한 애국자들 사이에서 자발적으로 자리잡은 것이었지만, 이제는 모두에게 법이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"또 여기 거니시오, 여성 시민?\"\n\n\"보시는 대로예요, 시민!\"\n\n몸짓이 유난히 많은 자그마한 사내였던 톱장이는(한때 도로 보수공이었다) 감옥 쪽을 힐끗 보더니 감옥을 손가락질하고는, 열 손가락을 얼굴 앞에 갖다 대 창살 모양을 만들어 그 사이로 익살스럽게 들여다보았다.\n\n\"하긴 내 알 바 아니지.\" 그가 말했다. 그러고는 다시 나무를 켜기 시작했다.\n\n이튿날 그는 그녀를 살피고 있다가, 그녀가 나타나자마자 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"아니? 또 여기 거니시오, 여성 시민?\"\n\n\"네, 시민.\"\n\n\"아! 아이까지! 댁 어머니지, 그렇지, 우리 꼬마 여성 시민?\"\n\n\"네, 그렇다고 말할까요, 엄마?\" 어린 루시가 그녀에게 바싹 붙으며 속삭였다.\n\n\"그래, 우리 아가.\"\n\n\"네, 시민.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 내 알 바 아니지. 내 일은 내 일이니까. 내 톱 좀 보시오! 나는 이걸 내 꼬마 단두대라고 부른다오. 라, 라, 라, 라, 라, 라! 그러면 목이 댕강!\"\n\n그가 말하는 사이에 통나무 토막이 떨어졌고, 그는 그것을 바구니에 던져 넣었다.\n\n\"나는 나 자신을 땔감 단두대의 쌍송이라고 부르지. 자, 여기 또 보시오! 루, 루, 루, 루, 루, 루! 그러면 그년 목이 댕강! 자, 이번엔 아이. 간질간질, 콕콕! 그러면 그놈 목이 댕강. 온 가족이 다!\"\n\n그가 통나무 토막 두 개를 더 바구니에 던져 넣자 루시는 몸서리쳤지만, 톱장이가 일하는 동안 거기 있으면서 그의 눈에 띄지 않기란 불가능했다. 그때부터 그의 호의를 사두려고, 그녀는 늘 먼저 그에게 말을 걸었고, 이따금 술값을 쥐여주었는데, 그는 그것을 선뜻 받았다.\n\n그는 호기심 많은 사내여서, 그녀가 감옥의 지붕과 쇠창살을 올려다보며 남편에게로 마음을 끌어올리느라 그를 까맣게 잊고 있다가 정신을 차려보면, 그가 작업대에 한쪽 무릎을 올린 채 톱질을 멈추고 자기를 바라보고 있는 일이 더러 있었다. \"하긴 내 알 바 아니지!\" 그럴 때면 그는 으레 이렇게 말하고는 다시 재빨리 톱질에 매달리곤 했다.\n\n날씨가 어떻든, 겨울의 눈과 서리 속에서도, 봄의 매서운 바람 속에서도, 여름의 뜨거운 햇볕 속에서도, 가을의 빗속에서도, 그리고 다시 겨울의 눈과 서리 속에서도, 루시는 날마다 그곳에서 두 시간을 보냈다. 그리고 그곳을 떠날 때면 날마다 감옥의 담벼락에 입을 맞추었다. 남편이 그녀를 보는 것은(아버지에게 들은 바로는) 다섯번이나 여섯번에 한번쯤일 수도 있었고, 연거푸 두세번일 수도 있었으며, 한 주나 두 주 동안 한번도 없을 수도 있었다. 기회가 닿을 때 그가 그녀를 볼 수 있고 또 보았다는 것만으로도 충분했으니, 그 가능성 하나에 그녀는 한 주 내내, 하루 종일이라도 기다렸을 것이다.\n\n이런 나날을 보내는 사이에 그녀는 십이월에 이르렀고, 그 무렵 아버지는 공포의 한가운데를 침착한 머리로 누비고 다녔다. 눈발이 가볍게 날리는 어느 오후, 그녀는 늘 가던 모퉁이에 다다랐다. 그날은 한바탕 미친 듯한 환희가 벌어지는 축제일이었다. 그녀는 오는 길에, 작은 창들과 그 위에 꽂힌 작은 붉은 모자로 장식된 집들을 보았다. 또 삼색 리본으로, 또 정해진 문구(삼색 글자가 가장 인기였다)인 '하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국. 자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음!'으로 꾸며진 집들도 보았다.\n\n톱장이의 초라한 가게는 너무 작아서, 그 표면 전체로도 이 문구를 적을 자리가 변변치 않았다. 그래도 그는 누군가에게 부탁해 그것을 갈겨 써두었는데, 그 사람은 '죽음'이라는 단어를 더없이 어울리지 않게 끼워 넣느라 애를 먹은 모양이었다. 그는 선량한 시민이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 지붕 위에 창과 모자를 내걸었고, 창문에는 '꼬마 성녀 단두대'라고 적은 자기 톱을 세워두었으니 — 그 무렵에는 그 거대하고 날카로운 여인이 이미 세간에서 성녀로 추앙받고 있었기 때문이다. 가게는 닫혀 있고 그도 거기 없었는데, 이는 루시에게 다행한 일이어서 그녀를 온전히 혼자 있게 해주었다.\n\n그러나 그는 멀리 있지 않았으니, 이내 어수선한 움직임과 고함 소리가 다가오는 것이 들려와 그녀를 두려움에 휩싸이게 했다. 잠시 후, 감옥 담벼락 옆 모퉁이를 돌아 한 무리의 사람들이 쏟아져 나왔고, 그 한가운데에는 톱장이가 '복수의 여신'과 손을 맞잡고 있었다. 오백 명은 족히 되어 보였고, 그들은 오천 마리 악령처럼 춤을 추고 있었다. 그들 자신의 노랫소리 말고는 다른 음악이 없었다. 그들은 그 인기 있는 혁명가에 맞춰, 마치 일제히 이를 가는 듯한 사나운 박자를 지키며 춤을 추었다. 남자와 여자가 함께 춤추고, 여자들끼리 춤추고, 남자들끼리 춤추니, 우연히 한데 모이는 대로였다. 처음에는 그저 거친 붉은 모자들과 거친 모직 누더기들의 폭풍에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 그들이 그곳을 가득 메우고 멈춰 서서 루시를 둘러싸고 춤추자, 그들 사이에서 미쳐 날뛰는 무도의 형상이라 할 어떤 섬뜩한 환영이 솟아올랐다. 그들은 앞으로 나아가고 뒤로 물러서며, 서로의 손을 치고, 서로의 머리를 움켜쥐고, 혼자서 빙글빙글 돌다가, 서로를 붙잡고 둘씩 짝지어 빙글빙글 돌더니, 마침내 여럿이 쓰러졌다. 그자들이 쓰러져 있는 동안, 나머지는 손에 손을 맞잡고 다 함께 빙글빙글 돌았다. 그러다 고리가 풀어지면, 둘씩 넷씩 따로 고리를 이루어 돌고 또 돌다가, 일제히 한꺼번에 멈추고는 다시 시작하여, 치고, 움켜쥐고, 잡아당기고, 그러다 도는 방향을 뒤집어 모두가 반대편으로 빙글빙글 돌았다. 갑자기 그들은 다시 멈추어 잠시 쉬더니, 새로 박자를 두드리고는, 한길 너비만큼 줄을 지어, 머리는 낮게 숙이고 손은 높이 치켜든 채, 비명을 지르며 휙 몰려 사라졌다. 그 어떤 싸움도 이 춤의 반만큼도 끔찍할 수 없었으리라. 그것은 그야말로 타락한 놀이였으니 — 한때는 순결했던 것이 온갖 악마성에 내맡겨진 것이요 — 건강한 오락이 피를 끓게 하고 정신을 어지럽히며 마음을 무디게 하는 수단으로 변해버린 것이었다. 거기서 엿보이는 우아함이 도리어 그것을 더 추악하게 만들어, 본래 선한 모든 것이 얼마나 뒤틀리고 왜곡되었는지를 드러내 보였다. 처녀의 가슴이 이런 짓에 드러나고, 거의 어린아이라 할 어여쁜 머리가 이렇게 미쳐 날뛰며, 가녀린 발이 이 피와 진흙의 수렁 속에서 종종거리는 모습은, 산산이 어그러진 시대의 표상이었다.\n\n이것이 까르마뇰이었다. 그것이 지나가고, 루시를 톱장이 집 문간에 겁에 질려 어찌할 바를 모른 채 남겨둔 뒤, 깃털 같은 눈은 마치 처음부터 그런 일이 없었다는 듯 고요히 내려와 희고 부드럽게 쌓였다.\n\n\"오, 아버지!\" 그녀가 잠시 손으로 가렸던 눈을 들어보니 아버지가 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. \"참으로 잔인하고 끔찍한 광경이었어요.\"\n\n\"안다, 얘야, 알아. 나도 여러 번 보았단다. 무서워 마라! 저들 중 누구도 너를 해치지 않을 게다.\"\n\n\"제 자신이 무서운 게 아니에요, 아버지. 하지만 제 남편을, 그리고 저 사람들의 자비를 생각하면—\"\n\n\"우리는 곧 그애를 저들의 자비 위에 올려놓을 게다. 그애가 창문으로 기어오르는 걸 두고, 너한테 알려주려고 왔단다. 여기는 보는 사람이 아무도 없으니, 저 가장 높은 비탈진 지붕을 향해 손에 입을 맞추어 보내려무나.\"\n\n\"그렇게 할게요, 아버지. 거기에 제 영혼을 실어 보낼게요!\"\n\n\"그애가 보이지 않느냐, 가엾은 것아?\"\n\n\"네, 아버지.\" 루시가 손에 입을 맞추며 그리움에 젖어 흐느꼈다. \"보이지 않아요.\"\n\n눈 위에 발소리. 마담 드파르주였다. \"안녕하시오, 여성 시민.\" 박사가 인사했다. \"안녕하세요, 시민.\" 지나치는 길에 건넨 인사였다. 그뿐이었다. 마담 드파르주는 흰 길 위로 드리운 그림자처럼 사라졌다.\n\n\"내 팔을 잡으렴, 얘야. 그애를 위해서, 명랑하고 용기 있는 기색으로 여기를 떠나자. 잘했다.\" 그들은 그 자리를 떠났다. \"헛되지 않을 게다. 샤를이 내일 소환된단다.\"\n\n\"내일이라고요!\"\n\n\"지체할 시간이 없다. 나는 만반의 준비가 되어 있다만, 그애가 실제로 재판소에 소환되기 전에는 취할 수 없는 조치들이 남아 있단다. 그애는 아직 통지를 받지 못했지만, 곧 내일자로 소환되어 콩시에르주리로 옮겨지리라는 걸 나는 안다. 때맞춰 소식을 들었으니까. 무섭지 않으냐?\"\n\n그녀는 가까스로 대답했다. \"아버지를 믿어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 절대적으로 믿으렴. 네 마음 졸이는 시간도 거의 끝나간다, 아가야. 그애는 몇 시간 안에 네게 돌아올 게다. 나는 온갖 방비로 그애를 둘러쌌단다. 로리 씨를 만나봐야겠구나.\"\n\n그가 걸음을 멈추었다. 들리는 거리 안에서 수레바퀴의 묵직한 덜컹거림이 있었다. 두 사람 모두 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 너무도 잘 알았다. 하나. 둘. 셋. 호송 수레 세 대가 그 무시무시한 짐을 싣고 소리를 죽이는 눈 위를 지나 멀어져 갔다.\n\n\"로리 씨를 만나봐야겠다.\" 박사가 그녀를 다른 길로 돌려세우며 거듭 말했다.\n\n이 충직한 노신사는 여전히 자신의 직무를 맡고 있었으니, 단 한 번도 그것을 떠난 적이 없었다. 그와 그의 장부는 몰수되어 국유화된 재산을 두고 빈번히 호출되었다. 그는 원래 주인들을 위해 지킬 수 있는 것이라면 지켜냈다. 텔슨 은행이 맡아둔 것을 굳건히 지키고 또 입을 다무는 데에 그보다 나은 사람은 세상에 없었다.\n\n거무튀튀한 붉고 누런 하늘과 쎈 강에서 피어오르는 안개가 어둠이 다가옴을 알렸다. 은행에 다다랐을 때는 거의 어두워져 있었다. 무슈 영주의 위풍당당하던 저택은 온통 황폐해진 채 버려져 있었다. 안마당의 흙먼지와 잿더미 위로는 이런 글자들이 적혀 있었다. '국유 재산. 하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국. 자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음!'\n\n로리 씨와 함께 있던 그 사람 — 의자 위에 걸쳐진 승마용 외투의 주인 — 남의 눈에 띄어서는 안 될 그 사람은 누구였을까? 새로 도착한 누구에게서, 그는 그토록 동요하고 놀란 채 나와 자신이 아끼는 사람을 품에 끌어안았던 것일까? 누구를 향해, 그는 그녀가 더듬더듬 했던 말을 되풀이하는 듯 목소리를 높이고는, 자신이 막 나온 방의 문 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 이렇게 말했던 것일까. \"콩시에르주리로 옮겨져, 내일 소환된답니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER VI. Triumph\n\n다섯명의 재판관과 검사, 그리고 단호한 배심원으로 이루어진 무시무시한 법정은 날마다 열렸다. 그들의 명단은 저녁마다 나돌았고, 여러 감옥의 간수들이 죄수들에게 그것을 읽어주었다. 간수들이 으레 던지는 농담은 이러했다. \"이리 나와서 석간을 들어보시지, 거기 안에 있는 양반!\"\n\n\"샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이!\"\n\n라포르스에서 마침내 석간이 그렇게 시작되었다.\n\n이름이 불리면, 그 주인은 따로 떨어져 나가 이렇게 치명적으로 기록된 자들을 위해 마련된 자리로 들어섰다. 샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이는 그 관례를 알 만한 이유가 있었으니, 수백명이 그렇게 사라져가는 것을 보아왔던 것이다.\n\n읽을 때 안경을 쓰는 그 비대한 간수는, 그가 자리를 잡았는지 확인하려고 안경 너머로 흘긋 보고는, 이름마다 비슷하게 잠깐씩 멈추어가며 명단을 훑어 내려갔다. 이름은 스물세개였지만, 대답한 사람은 스무명뿐이었다. 그렇게 호명된 죄수 가운데 하나는 감옥에서 죽어 잊혔고, 둘은 이미 단두대에서 처형되어 잊혔기 때문이다. 명단은 다네이가 도착하던 밤에 함께 갇힌 죄수들을 보았던 그 둥근 천장의 방에서 낭독되었다. 그들은 하나같이 학살로 죽어 사라졌고, 그가 그 뒤로 마음을 쓰고 헤어졌던 모든 인간 존재들은 단두대 위에서 죽었다.\n\n서둘러 작별과 친절의 말이 오갔으나, 이별은 곧 끝났다. 그것은 날마다 있는 일이었고, 라포르스의 사람들은 그날 저녁을 위해 벌금놀이 몇가지와 작은 음악회 준비에 매달려 있었다. 그들은 쇠창살로 몰려들어 거기서 눈물을 흘렸으나, 마련된 여흥에서 스무개의 빈자리를 다시 채워야 했고, 시간은 기껏해야 폐쇄 시각까지 짧을 뿐이었으니, 그때가 되면 공동실과 복도는 밤새 그곳을 지키는 커다란 개들에게 넘겨질 터였다. 죄수들이 결코 무감각하거나 무정했던 것은 아니었다. 그들의 방식은 시대의 상황에서 비롯된 것이었다. 마찬가지로, 미묘한 차이는 있으나, 일종의 열정 혹은 도취가 있었으니, 그것이 어떤 이들로 하여금 불필요하게 단두대에 맞서게 하고 그 칼날에 죽게 했음은 의심할 여지가 없는데, 그것은 단순한 허세가 아니라, 사납게 뒤흔들린 민심의 거친 전염이었다. 역병의 시절에는 우리 가운데 누군가는 그 병에 은밀히 끌리게 마련이니, 그것에 걸려 죽고 싶다는 끔찍하고 일시적인 충동에 사로잡힌다. 그리고 우리 모두에게는 그와 같은 기이한 것들이 가슴속에 숨어 있어, 그저 그것들을 불러낼 상황만을 필요로 할 뿐이다.\n\n콩시에르주리로 가는 통로는 짧고 어두웠고, 해충이 들끓는 그곳 감방의 밤은 길고 추웠다. 이튿날, 샤를 다네이의 이름이 불리기 전에 죄수 열다섯명이 피고석에 세워졌다. 열다섯명 모두 유죄 판결을 받았고, 전체 재판에 한시간 반이 걸렸다.\n\n\"샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이\"가 마침내 기소되었다.\n\n그의 재판관들은 깃털 달린 모자를 쓰고 재판석에 앉아 있었으나, 그 밖에는 거친 붉은 모자와 삼색 표장이 두루 쓰인 머리 장식이었다. 배심원들과 소란스러운 방청객들을 바라보노라면, 그는 평소의 질서가 뒤바뀌어 중죄인들이 정직한 사람들을 재판하고 있다고 여겼을 법도 했다. 한 도시에서 가장 천하고, 가장 잔인하고, 가장 못된 백성들이 — 천하고 잔인하고 나쁜 자들이 늘 일정량은 있게 마련인 그들이 — 이 장면을 이끄는 정신이었다. 그들은 시끄럽게 논평하고, 갈채를 보내고, 못마땅해하고, 결과를 미리 점치고, 아무런 제지 없이 그 결과를 재촉했다. 남자들은 대부분 갖가지 방식으로 무장하고 있었고, 여자들 가운데 더러는 칼을, 더러는 단검을 지녔으며, 더러는 구경하면서 먹고 마셨고, 많은 이들이 뜨개질을 했다. 이 마지막 부류 가운데, 일하는 동안 팔 밑에 여분의 뜨개질감을 끼고 있는 여자가 하나 있었다. 그녀는 앞줄에 앉아 있었는데, 그 옆에는 그가 관문에 도착한 이래 한번도 보지 못한 한 남자가 있었으나, 그는 그를 드파르주라고 곧장 기억해냈다. 그는 그녀가 한두번 그의 귀에 속삭이는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 그의 아내인 듯한 것을 알아차렸다. 그러나 그가 그 두 사람에게서 가장 눈여겨본 것은, 그들이 그에게 닿을 수 있는 한 가까이 자리 잡고 있으면서도 결코 그를 향해 눈길을 주지 않는다는 점이었다. 그들은 끈질긴 결의를 품고 무언가를 기다리는 듯했고, 배심원들을 바라볼 뿐 그밖에는 아무것도 보지 않았다. 재판장 아래에는 마네뜨 박사가 평소의 차분한 옷차림으로 앉아 있었다. 피고가 보기에, 그와 로리 씨가 그곳에서 법정과 무관하게 평소의 옷을 입고 있는, 까르마뇰의 거친 복장을 걸치지 않은 유일한 사람들이었다.\n\n샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이는 망명자라는 죄목으로 검사에게 고발되었는데, 모든 망명자를 사형으로써 추방한다는 포고령에 따라 그의 목숨은 공화국에 몰수된 것이었다. 그 포고령이 그가 프랑스로 돌아온 뒤의 날짜로 되어 있다는 것은 아무 상관이 없었다. 그가 거기 있었고, 포고령이 거기 있었다. 그는 프랑스에서 체포되었고, 그의 목이 요구되었다.\n\n\"저자의 목을 쳐라!\" 방청객들이 외쳤다. \"공화국의 적이다!\"\n\n재판장은 그 외침들을 잠재우려고 종을 울리고는, 피고에게 영국에서 여러 해 살았던 것이 사실이 아니냐고 물었다.\n\n틀림없는 사실이었다.\n\n그렇다면 그는 망명자가 아닌가? 그는 자신을 무엇이라 일컫는가?\n\n법의 취지와 정신 안에서라면, 망명자가 아니기를 그는 바랐다.\n\n어째서 아닌가? 재판장은 알고 싶어 했다.\n\n그는 자신에게 거슬리는 작위와, 자신에게 거슬리는 지위를 자발적으로 내려놓고, 자기 나라를 떠나 — 그는 법정에서 현재 통용되는 의미의 망명자라는 말이 쓰이기 전에 떠났다고 진술했다 — 짓눌린 프랑스 국민의 노동에 기대기보다는 영국에서 자신의 노동으로 살아가고자 했기 때문이었다.\n\n이에 대해 그에게 무슨 증거가 있는가?\n\n그는 두 증인의 이름을 제출했다. 떼오필 가벨과 알렉상드르 마네뜨였다.\n\n그러나 그는 영국에서 결혼하지 않았는가? 재판장이 그에게 일깨웠다.\n\n사실이었으나, 영국 여자는 아니었다.\n\n프랑스 여성 시민인가?\n\n그렇다. 태생부터.\n\n그녀의 이름과 가문은?\n\n\"뤼시 마네뜨, 저기 앉아 계신 훌륭한 의사이신 마네뜨 박사의 외동딸입니다.\"\n\n이 대답은 방청객들에게 행복한 효과를 일으켰다. 잘 알려진 그 훌륭한 의사를 찬양하는 외침이 법정을 갈라놓았다. 사람들이 어찌나 변덕스럽게 움직였던지, 조금 전까지 피고를 길거리로 끌어내어 죽이지 못해 안달하듯 그를 노려보던 몇몇 사나운 얼굴에서 곧바로 눈물이 굴러내렸다.\n\n자신의 위험한 길의 이 몇 걸음에서, 샤를 다네이는 마네뜨 박사가 거듭 일러준 지시에 따라 발을 디뎌놓았다. 똑같이 신중한 그 조언이 그의 앞에 놓인 모든 발걸음을 인도했고, 그의 길을 한치 한치 마련해두었던 것이다.\n\n재판장은 물었다. 어째서 그는 더 일찍 돌아오지 않고 그제야 프랑스로 돌아왔는가?\n\n더 일찍 돌아오지 않은 것은, 그가 포기한 것 말고는 프랑스에서 살아갈 수단이 전혀 없었기 때문이며, 반면에 영국에서는 프랑스어와 프랑스 문학을 가르치며 살았기 때문이라고 그는 대답했다. 그가 그제야 돌아온 것은, 자신의 부재로 인해 목숨이 위태로워졌다고 호소하는 한 프랑스 시민의 절박하고 서면으로 된 간청 때문이었다. 그는 한 시민의 목숨을 구하고, 어떤 개인적 위험을 무릅쓰더라도 진실을 증언하기 위해 돌아온 것이었다. 그것이 공화국의 눈에 죄가 되는가?\n\n백성들이 열광하여 \"아니다!\"라고 외쳤고, 재판장은 그들을 진정시키려고 종을 울렸다. 그러나 종은 그러지 못했으니, 그들은 제 뜻으로 그칠 때까지 \"아니다!\"라고 계속 외쳤기 때문이다.\n\n재판장은 그 시민의 이름을 요구했다. 피고는 그 시민이 자신의 첫번째 증인이라고 설명했다. 그는 또한 관문에서 자신에게서 압수된 그 시민의 편지를 자신있게 언급하며, 그것이 지금 재판장 앞에 놓인 서류들 가운데 있으리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다.\n\n박사는 그것이 거기 있도록 조처해두었고 — 그것이 거기 있으리라고 그에게 확언해두었으며 — 절차의 이 단계에서 그것이 제출되어 낭독되었다. 시민 가벨이 그것을 확인하라는 부름을 받았고, 그렇게 했다. 시민 가벨은 한없는 섬세함과 정중함으로 넌지시 말하기를, 법정이 상대해야 할 공화국의 적들이 워낙 많아 그 업무의 압박 속에서 자신이 아베이 감옥에서 약간 간과되었다고 — 실은 법정의 애국적 기억에서 좀 빠져 있었다고 — 사흘 전까지는 그러했는데, 그때 그가 법정 앞에 불려나갔고, 배심원들이 그에 대한 고발은 시민 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이의 자수로써 그 자신에 관한 한 답이 되었다고 만족을 선언함에 따라 풀려났다고 했다.\n\n다음으로 마네뜨 박사가 신문을 받았다. 그의 높은 개인적 인기와 답변의 명료함은 큰 인상을 남겼다. 그러나 그가 말을 이어가면서, 피고가 자신이 오랜 투옥에서 풀려났을 때 처음으로 사귄 친구였다는 것, 피고가 영국에 머물며 망명 중인 자신의 딸과 자신에게 늘 충실하고 헌신적이었다는 것, 그곳의 귀족 정부에게 총애를 받기는커녕 실은 영국의 적이자 합중국의 벗이라 하여 그 정부에 의해 목숨을 걸고 재판받았다는 것을 밝히자 — 그가 더없는 신중함과 진실하고 진지한 진솔한 힘으로 이런 정황들을 드러내자, 배심원들과 백성들은 하나가 되었다. 마침내 그가 그 자리에 함께 있던 영국 신사 로리 씨를 이름으로 호명하며, 그 역시 자신처럼 그 영국 재판에서 증인이었고 자신의 진술을 입증할 수 있다고 호소하자, 배심원들은 충분히 들었으며 재판장이 받아들이기를 원한다면 표결할 준비가 되었다고 선언했다.\n\n표가 던져질 때마다 (배심원들은 각자 소리내어 표결했다) 백성들은 갈채의 함성을 질렀다. 모든 목소리가 피고에게 유리했고, 재판장은 그를 자유의 몸이라고 선언했다.\n\n그러자 백성들이 때로 자신들의 변덕을, 또는 관대함과 자비를 향한 더 나은 충동을 만족시키거나, 혹은 부풀어오른 잔혹한 분노의 계산서에 대한 일종의 상쇄로 여기던 그 비범한 장면 가운데 하나가 시작되었다. 그 비범한 장면들이 이 동기들 가운데 어느 것에 속하는지는 이제 아무도 단정할 수 없으니, 아마도 세가지 모두가 뒤섞인 것이되 두번째가 우세했으리라. 무죄가 선고되기가 무섭게, 다른 때에 피가 흐르듯 자유로이 눈물이 흘렀고, 그에게 달려들 수 있는 만큼의 남녀가 피고에게 그토록 형제애 넘치는 포옹을 퍼부었던 까닭에, 그는 길고 해로운 감금을 겪은 뒤라 탈진하여 기절할 위험에 처했다. 그가 너무도 잘 알고 있는 사실, 곧 바로 그 똑같은 사람들이 다른 흐름에 실리면 똑같은 격렬함으로 그에게 달려들어 그를 갈가리 찢어 거리에 뿌렸으리라는 것을 알았기에 더더욱 그러했다.\n\n재판받을 다른 피고인들에게 자리를 내주기 위해 그가 옮겨지면서, 그는 잠시 이러한 애무에서 벗어났다. 다음으로 다섯 사람이 공화국의 적으로서 함께 재판받을 참이었으니, 말로든 행동으로든 공화국을 돕지 않았다는 이유에서였다. 법정은 놓친 기회를 자신과 국가에 보상하는 데 어찌나 빨랐던지, 그가 그곳을 떠나기도 전에 그 다섯 사람이 스물네시간 안에 죽으라는 선고를 받고 그에게로 내려왔다. 그들 가운데 첫번째 사람이 죽음을 뜻하는 감옥의 관습적인 신호로 — 손가락 하나를 치켜올리며 — 그에게 그 사실을 알렸고, 그들 모두가 말로 덧붙였다. \"공화국 만세!\"\n\n사실 그 다섯 사람에게는 절차를 길게 끌어줄 방청객이 없었으니, 그와 마네뜨 박사가 문을 나섰을 때 그 둘레에 커다란 군중이 있었기 때문이다. 그 안에는 법정에서 보았던 모든 얼굴이 다 있는 듯했다 — 두 사람만 빼고, 그는 그 둘을 찾았으나 헛수고였다. 그가 나오자 군중이 새로이 그에게 몰려들어, 울고, 끌어안고, 외쳤으니, 모두 번갈아가며 그리고 모두 한꺼번에 그러했고, 마침내 이 미친 장면이 펼쳐지던 강기슭의 그 강물조차 강가의 사람들처럼 미쳐 날뛰는 것만 같았다.\n\n그들은 그들 가운데 가지고 있던 커다란 의자에 그를 앉혔는데, 그것은 법정 자체에서, 아니면 그 방이나 복도 가운데 하나에서 꺼내온 것이었다. 그들은 의자 위에 붉은 깃발을 던져 덮었고, 그 등받이에는 꼭대기에 붉은 모자를 씌운 창을 묶어두었다. 이 개선의 수레에서, 박사의 간청조차도 그가 사람들의 어깨에 실려 자기 집으로 옮겨지는 것을 막을 수 없었으니, 어지러운 붉은 모자의 바다가 그를 둘러싸고 출렁이며, 그 폭풍 같은 심연에서 사람 얼굴의 그런 잔해들을 눈앞에 떠올려놓았던 까닭에, 그는 한두번이 아니라 자기 정신이 혼란에 빠진 것이며 자신이 단두대로 끌려가는 호송 수레에 타고 있다고 의심했다.\n\n미친 듯 꿈결 같은 행렬을 이루어, 마주치는 이를 끌어안고 그를 가리키며, 그들은 그를 실어 갔다. 구불구불 돌고 발을 구르며 지나가는 눈 덮인 거리를 두루 퍼진 공화국의 색으로 붉게 물들이며 — 그들이 일찍이 눈 밑을 더 짙은 물감으로 붉게 물들였던 것처럼 — 그들은 그를 그렇게 그가 살던 건물의 안뜰로 실어 갔다. 그녀의 아버지가 그녀에게 알리려고 앞서 가 있었고, 그녀의 남편이 두 발로 서자 그녀는 정신을 잃고 그의 품에 쓰러졌다.\n\n그가 그녀를 가슴에 안고, 자신의 눈물과 그녀의 입술이 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 만날 수 있도록 그녀의 아름다운 머리를 자기 얼굴과 떠들썩한 군중 사이로 돌려놓는데, 몇몇 사람들이 춤을 추기 시작했다. 곧바로 나머지 사람들도 모두 춤을 추기 시작했고, 안뜰은 까르마뇰로 넘쳐흘렀다. 그러더니 그들은 빈 의자 위에 군중 가운데서 젊은 여자 하나를 올려 자유의 여신으로 떠받쳐 실었고, 인근 거리로, 강기슭을 따라, 다리 너머로 부풀어 넘쳐흐르며, 까르마뇰은 그들 모두를 빨아들여 휩쓸어 갔다.\n\n그는 자기 앞에 승리에 차서 자랑스럽게 서 있는 박사의 손을 움켜쥐고 나서, 까르마뇰의 물기둥과 맞서 싸우느라 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 들어온 로리 씨의 손을 움켜쥐고 나서, 들어올려져 그의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안은 어린 뤼시에게 입을 맞추고 나서, 그 아이를 들어올린 늘 열성적이고 충실한 프로스를 끌어안고 나서, 아내를 두 팔로 안아 그들의 방으로 데리고 올라갔다.\n\n\"뤼시! 내 사랑! 나는 무사하오.\"\n\n\"오, 가장 사랑하는 샤를, 제가 그분께 기도드렸던 것처럼 무릎 꿇고 이 일을 하느님께 감사드리게 해주세요.\"\n\n그들은 모두 경건하게 머리와 마음을 숙였다. 그녀가 다시 그의 품에 안기자, 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 당신 아버님께 말씀드리시오, 여보. 이 온 프랑스의 그 누구도 그분이 나를 위해 해주신 일을 해내지 못했을 거요.\"\n\n그녀는 오래오래 전에 그의 가엾은 머리를 자기 가슴에 누였던 것처럼, 자기 머리를 아버지의 가슴에 기댔다. 그는 그녀에게 베푼 보답으로 행복했고, 자신의 고통을 보상받았으며, 자신의 강인함을 자랑스러워했다. \"약해지면 안 된다, 내 사랑아.\" 그가 타일렀다. \"그렇게 떨지 마라. 내가 그를 구했단다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER VII. A Knock at the Door\n\n“내가 그를 구했다.” 이것은 그가 자주 돌아오곤 하던 그 꿈들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 그는 정말로 여기 있었다. 그런데도 그의 아내는 떨었고, 막연하지만 무거운 두려움이 그녀를 짓눌렀다.\n\n주위의 공기는 온통 너무도 답답하고 어두웠으며, 사람들은 너무도 격정적으로 복수심에 차 변덕스러웠고, 무고한 이들이 막연한 의심과 시커먼 악의 때문에 너무도 끊임없이 처형당했으며, 자기 남편만큼이나 죄 없고 자기에게 그가 소중한 만큼 다른 이들에게 소중한 많은 사람들이 그가 가까스로 벗어난 운명을 날마다 함께 겪고 있다는 사실을 잊기란 너무도 불가능해서, 그녀의 마음은 마땅히 가벼워져야 한다고 느끼는 만큼 그 짐을 덜어내지 못했다. 겨울 오후의 그림자가 내려앉기 시작했고, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 그 무시무시한 수레들이 거리를 굴러가고 있었다. 그녀의 마음은 그 수레들을 뒤쫓으며 사형수들 가운데서 그를 찾았다. 그러고는 실재하는 그의 존재에 더욱 매달리며 더욱 떨었다.\n\n그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 다독이며, 이 여자다운 연약함을 향해 가엾이 여기는 우월감을 드러냈는데, 그 모습은 보기에 놀라웠다. 이제는 다락방도 없고, 구두 짓기도 없고, 북쪽 탑 백오호도 없다! 그는 스스로에게 부과한 과업을 이루어냈고, 그의 약속은 지켜졌으며, 그는 샤를을 구했다. 모두 그에게 기대게 하라.\n\n그들의 살림은 매우 검소했다. 그것이 사람들의 반감을 가장 적게 사며 가장 안전하게 사는 길이었기 때문만은 아니라, 그들이 부유하지 않았기 때문이기도 했다. 게다가 샤를은 갇혀 있는 동안 내내 형편없는 음식값과 간수값, 그리고 더 가난한 죄수들의 생활비까지 비싸게 치러야 했던 것이다. 한편으로는 이런 이유로, 또 한편으로는 집 안의 첩자를 피하기 위해 그들은 하인을 두지 않았다. 안마당 문지기 노릇을 하는 시민 남자와 시민 여자가 이따금 잡일을 해주었고, (로리 씨가 거의 통째로 그들에게 넘겨준) 제리는 그들의 날품 시중꾼이 되어 밤마다 그곳에서 잠자리를 얻었다.\n\n자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음의 하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국의 법령에 따르면, 모든 집의 문이나 문기둥에는 거주자 전원의 이름을 일정한 크기의 글자로, 땅에서 일정하게 적당한 높이에, 또렷이 새겨 넣어야 했다. 그리하여 제리 크런처 씨의 이름이 아래쪽 문기둥을 정식으로 장식하고 있었다. 그리고 오후의 그림자가 짙어질 무렵, 그 이름의 임자가 몸소 나타났는데, 그는 마네뜨 박사가 고용해 그 명단에 다네라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드의 이름을 덧붙이게 한 칠장이를 지켜보고 오는 길이었다.\n\n그 시절을 어둡게 물들인 보편적인 두려움과 불신 속에서, 일상의 무해한 생활 방식이 죄다 바뀌었다. 박사의 작은 살림집에서도 다른 수많은 집들과 마찬가지로, 필요한 일용품들을 매일 저녁 적은 양으로 여러 작은 가게에서 사들였다. 남의 눈에 띄지 않고, 입길에 오르내리거나 시샘을 살 빌미를 되도록 적게 주는 것이 모두의 바람이었다.\n\n지난 몇 달 동안 프로스 양과 크런처 씨가 물품 조달 임무를 맡아왔다. 프로스 양은 돈을, 크런처 씨는 바구니를 들었다. 오후마다 거리의 가로등에 불이 켜질 무렵이면 그들은 이 일을 하러 길을 나서, 필요한 물건들을 사서 집으로 가져왔다. 프로스 양은 프랑스인 가족과 오래 어울려 지냈으니 마음만 먹었다면 그들의 말을 제 모국어만큼이나 알 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀에게는 그쪽으로 마음이 없었다. 따라서 그녀는 그 “허튼소리”(그녀는 즐겨 그렇게 불렀다)에 대해 크런처 씨보다 더 아는 게 없었다. 그래서 그녀가 장을 보는 방식은, 관사 따위로 운을 떼는 일도 없이 가게 주인의 머리에다 대고 명사 하나를 툭 던지는 것이었고, 그것이 마침 자기가 원하는 물건의 이름이 아닐 경우에는 그 물건을 둘러보며 찾아내 움켜쥐고는 흥정이 끝날 때까지 붙잡고 놓지 않는 것이었다. 그녀는 언제나 흥정을 했는데, 상인이 손가락 몇개를 펴 보이든 그보다 하나 적은 손가락을 정당한 값이라는 표시로 펴 보이는 식이었다.\n\n“자, 크런처 씨,” 프로스 양이 말했다. 그녀의 두 눈은 행복으로 붉어져 있었다. “당신이 준비됐으면, 나도 됐어요.”\n\n제리는 쉰 목소리로 프로스 양에게 봉사할 채비가 되어 있다고 밝혔다. 그는 오래전에 녹을 죄다 벗겨냈지만, 그 어떤 것도 그의 뾰족한 머리만은 다듬어 내릴 수 없었다.\n\n“온갖 것들이 다 필요해요,” 프로스 양이 말했다. “우리는 한바탕 진땀을 빼게 될 거예요. 그중에서도 포도주가 필요하지요. 우리가 어디서 사든, 그 빨강머리들이 멋들어진 건배를 하겠군요.”\n\n“제 생각으론, 아가씨, 그자들이 아가씨의 건강을 위해 마시든 영감님을 위해 마시든 아가씨가 알기엔 별 차이 없을 것 같습니다만,” 제리가 받아쳤다.\n\n“그 영감님이 누군데요?” 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n크런처 씨는 다소 머뭇거리며, 자기가 말한 것은 “늙은 닉”을 뜻한다고 설명했다.\n\n* 늙은 닉(Old Nick): 악마를 가리키는 영어 속어.\n“하!” 프로스 양이 말했다. “저 족속들의 속내를 풀이하는 데는 통역이 필요 없어요. 저들에겐 단 하나뿐이고, 그건 한밤중의 살인과 행패니까요.”\n\n“쉿, 그러지 마세요! 제발, 제발 조심하세요!” 루씨가 외쳤다.\n\n“그래요, 그래, 그래, 조심할게요,” 프로스 양이 말했다. “하지만 우리끼리니까 말인데, 거리에서 서로 끌어안는답시고 양파 냄새, 담배 냄새 풍기며 숨 막히게 하는 짓거리가 없었으면 정말 좋겠어요. 자, 무당벌레, 내가 돌아올 때까지 그 난롯가에서 한 발짝도 움직이지 마요! 되찾은 소중한 남편을 잘 돌보고, 나를 다시 볼 때까지 지금처럼 그 예쁜 머리를 남편 어깨에서 떼지 말아요! 박사님, 가기 전에 한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요?”\n\n“그런 자유쯤은 누리셔도 될 것 같습니다,” 박사가 미소 지으며 대답했다.\n\n“제발 자유 얘기는 마세요. 그건 지긋지긋하게 많으니까요,” 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n“쉿, 그러지 마세요! 또요?” 루씨가 타일렀다.\n\n“그래, 얘야,” 프로스 양이 힘주어 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. “요컨대 짧게 말하자면, 나는 자비로우신 국왕 조지 삼세 폐하의 신민이라는 거야.” 프로스 양은 그 이름에 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했다. “그러니 내 좌우명은 이거지. 저들의 음모를 혼란케 하시고, 저들의 간악한 술수를 좌절케 하시며, 그분께 우리의 희망을 거나니, 하느님이여 국왕을 지키소서!”\n\n크런처 씨는 충성심이 북받쳐, 교회에 온 사람처럼 프로스 양을 따라 그 말을 우물우물 되뇌었다.\n\n“당신 안에 그렇게 영국인다운 데가 많다니 기쁘군요. 다만 그 목소리에 그런 감기가 든 적이 없었다면 좋았을 텐데요,” 프로스 양이 흐뭇해하며 말했다. “그건 그렇고 박사님, 그 질문 말인데요. 혹시”—그들 모두에게 큰 근심거리인 무언가를 짐짓 대수롭지 않은 척하며 이렇듯 지나가는 투로 꺼내는 것이 이 선량한 사람의 방식이었다—“혹시 우리가 이곳을 벗어날 가망이 아직은 좀 보이나요?”\n\n“아직은 아닐 듯합니다. 아직 샤를에게는 위험한 일일 테니까요.”\n\n“에휴, 저런!” 프로스 양은 난롯불 빛 속에 빛나는 사랑하는 아씨의 금빛 머리칼을 흘끗 보며 한숨을 명랑하게 억누른 채 말했다. “그렇다면 우린 참고 기다려야겠군요. 별수 없죠. 우리 오라비 솔로몬이 늘 말하던 대로, 고개를 높이 들고 낮게 싸워야 해요. 자, 크런처 씨!—무당벌레, 움직이지 마요!”\n\n그들은 루씨와 그녀의 남편, 그녀의 아버지, 그리고 아이를 환한 난롯가에 남겨둔 채 밖으로 나갔다. 로리 씨는 곧 은행에서 돌아오기로 되어 있었다. 프로스 양은 등불을 켜두었지만, 그들이 방해받지 않고 난롯불 빛을 즐길 수 있도록 한쪽 구석에 치워두었다. 어린 루씨는 할아버지 곁에 앉아 두 손을 그의 팔에 끼워 맞잡고 있었다. 그리고 그는 속삭임보다 그리 높지 않은 어조로, 감옥 벽을 열어 한때 자신에게 은혜를 베푼 포로를 풀어준 위대하고 강력한 요정의 이야기를 그 아이에게 들려주기 시작했다. 모든 것이 가라앉고 고요했으며, 루씨는 전보다 한결 마음이 놓였다.\n\n“저게 뭐죠?” 그녀가 별안간 외쳤다.\n\n“얘야!” 그녀의 아버지가 이야기를 멈추고 그녀의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. “마음을 다잡으렴. 어쩌면 이리도 어지러운 상태에 있느냐! 아주 사소한 것에—아무것도 아닌 것에—깜짝깜짝 놀라다니! 다른 사람도 아니고 네가, 네 아버지의 딸이!”\n\n“아버지, 저는,” 루씨가 창백한 얼굴로 더듬더듬 변명하며 말했다. “계단에서 낯선 발소리가 들린 것 같았어요.”\n\n“얘야, 계단은 죽음처럼 고요하기만 하구나.”\n\n그가 그 말을 하는 순간, 문에 일격이 가해졌다.\n\n“오 아버지, 아버지. 이게 무슨 일일까요! 샤를을 숨겨요. 그이를 구해줘요!”\n\n“얘야,” 박사가 일어서며 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다. “나는 이미 그를 구했단다. 이 무슨 연약함이냐, 얘야! 내가 문으로 나가보마.”\n\n그는 손에 등불을 들고 사이에 놓인 바깥쪽 두 방을 가로질러 문을 열었다. 바닥을 거칠게 울리는 발소리가 들리더니, 붉은 모자를 쓰고 군도와 권총으로 무장한 사나운 사내 넷이 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n“다네라 불리는 시민 에브레몽드,” 첫번째 사내가 말했다.\n\n“누가 그를 찾소?” 다네가 대답했다.\n\n“내가 그를 찾는다. 우리가 그를 찾는다. 나는 당신을 안다, 에브레몽드. 오늘 재판정에서 당신을 봤다. 당신은 다시 공화국의 죄수다.”\n\n넷은 아내와 아이가 매달려 있는 그를 둘러쌌다.\n\n“내가 어떻게 해서, 왜 다시 죄수가 됐는지 말해주시오.”\n\n“당신이 콩시에르주리로 곧장 돌아가면 그것으로 충분하고, 내일이면 알게 될 것이다. 당신은 내일 소환됐다.”\n\n이 방문에 그토록 돌처럼 굳어버려, 마치 등불을 들도록 만들어진 조각상이라도 된 듯 손에 등불을 든 채 서 있던 마네뜨 박사는, 이 말이 다 떨어지자 움직여 등불을 내려놓고는, 말한 자와 마주 서서 그의 붉은 모직 셔츠 앞섶을 거칠지 않게 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n“당신은 그를 안다고 했소. 나도 아시오?”\n\n“그렇소, 당신을 압니다, 시민 박사.”\n\n“우리는 다들 당신을 알지요, 시민 박사,” 나머지 셋이 말했다.\n\n그는 멍하니 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보다가, 잠시 사이를 두고 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“그렇다면 그가 내게 한 질문에 답해주겠소? 어떻게 이런 일이 벌어진 거요?”\n\n“시민 박사,” 첫번째 사내가 마지못해 말했다. “그는 쌩땅뚜안 구역에 고발당했소. 이 시민이,” 그러면서 두번째로 들어온 자를 가리켰다. “쌩땅뚜안 사람이오.”\n\n여기서 지목된 시민이 고개를 끄덕이며 덧붙였다.\n\n“그는 쌩땅뚜안의 고발을 받았소.”\n\n“무슨 죄로?” 박사가 물었다.\n\n“시민 박사,” 첫번째 사내가 아까처럼 마지못해하며 말했다. “더는 묻지 마시오. 공화국이 당신에게 희생을 요구한다면, 의심할 바 없이 당신은 훌륭한 애국자로서 기꺼이 그것을 바칠 것이오. 공화국이 모든 것에 앞서니까. 인민이 지고하니까. 에브레몽드, 우린 시간이 촉박하오.”\n\n“한마디만,” 박사가 간청했다. “누가 그를 고발했는지 말해주겠소?”\n\n“그건 규칙에 어긋나오,” 첫번째 사내가 대답했다. “하지만 여기 쌩땅뚜안 사람에게 물어볼 수는 있겠지요.”\n\n박사는 그 사내에게 눈길을 돌렸다. 그자는 불안하게 발을 옮기며 수염을 잠시 문지르다가, 마침내 입을 열었다.\n\n“글쎄! 정말이지 이건 규칙에 어긋나오. 하지만 그는 고발당했소—그것도 무겁게—시민 드파르주와 시민 드파르주 부인에게서 말이오. 그리고 또 한 사람에게서.”\n\n“또 한 사람이라니?”\n\n“시민 박사, 당신이 그걸 묻는 거요?”\n\n“그렇소.”\n\n“그렇다면,” 쌩땅뚜안의 사내가 묘한 표정으로 말했다. “내일이면 답을 듣게 될 거요. 지금 나는 입을 다물겠소!”\n\n# CHAPTER VIII. A Hand at Cards\n\n집의 새로운 재앙을 다행히 알지 못한 채, 프로스 양은 좁은 길을 헤치며 나아가 뽕네프 다리를 건너 강을 가로질렀고, 마음속으로는 꼭 사야 할 물건들의 수를 헤아리고 있었다. 크런처 씨는 바구니를 들고 그녀 곁에서 걸었다. 두 사람은 지나치는 가게 대부분을 좌우로 들여다보았고, 무리지어 모인 사람들 전부를 경계의 눈초리로 살폈으며, 몹시 흥분해 떠드는 무리가 있으면 길을 비켜 피해 갔다. 으슬으슬한 저녁이었고, 안개 낀 강은 타오르는 불빛으로 눈앞이 흐릿하고 거친 소음으로 귀가 어지러웠는데, 그 너머에 대장장이들이 일하며 공화국 군대를 위한 총을 만드는 거룻배들이 정박해 있었다. 그 군대를 상대로 농간을 부리거나, 그 안에서 분에 넘치는 진급을 얻은 자에게는 화가 있으리라! 그런 자에게는 차라리 수염이 자라지 않았던 편이 나았으니, 국민 면도칼이 그를 바싹 깎아버렸기 때문이다.\n\n* 국민 면도칼: 단두대를 가리키는 당시의 별칭.\n식료품 몇 가지와 등잔에 쓸 기름 한 됫박을 산 뒤, 프로스 양은 자신들이 필요로 하는 포도주를 떠올렸다. 여러 술집을 기웃거린 끝에, 그녀는 한때(또 두번째로) 뛸르리 궁이었던 국민 궁에서 멀지 않은 곳에서, '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'라는 간판 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그곳의 분위기가 자못 그녀의 마음에 들었던 것이다. 그곳은 지나온 같은 부류의 어느 곳보다도 조용해 보였고, 애국적인 모자들로 붉기는 했으나 다른 곳들만큼 붉지는 않았다. 크런처 씨의 의향을 떠보고 그도 자기와 같은 생각임을 알아낸 프로스 양은, 호위 기사를 거느린 채 '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'로 향했다.\n\n연기 자욱한 불빛을, 입에 파이프를 문 채 너덜너덜한 카드와 누런 도미노를 가지고 노는 사람들을, 가슴과 팔을 드러내고 검댕을 뒤집어쓴 채 신문을 소리 내어 읽는 노동자 한 명과 그에게 귀 기울이는 다른 이들을, 몸에 차고 있거나 다시 집어들려고 한쪽에 놓아둔 무기들을, 앞으로 고꾸라져 잠든 두세 명의 손님들 — 그 자세로 보면 유행하는 어깨가 높고 텁수룩한 검은 스펜서 차림이어서 졸고 있는 곰이나 개처럼 보였다 — 을 가볍게 둘러보며, 두 이방인 손님은 계산대로 다가가 원하는 것을 보여주었다.\n\n그들의 포도주가 계량되고 있을 때, 한 사내가 구석에서 다른 사내와 헤어져 떠나려고 일어섰다. 가던 길에 그는 프로스 양과 마주 보게 되었다. 그가 그녀와 마주하기가 무섭게, 프로스 양은 비명을 지르며 두 손을 마주쳤다.\n\n순식간에 가게 안의 모든 사람들이 벌떡 일어섰다. 누군가가 의견 차이를 따지려는 누군가에게 살해당한 것이 가장 있음직한 일이었다. 다들 누군가가 쓰러지는 모습을 보려고 했으나, 한 남자와 한 여자가 서로를 노려보며 서 있는 것만 보였다. 남자는 겉모습이 영락없이 프랑스인이자 철저한 공화주의자였고, 여자는 분명히 영국인이었다.\n\n이 실망스러운 반전 속에서, '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'의 추종자들이 무어라 말했든 — 매우 거침없고 큰 소리였다는 것 말고는 — 프로스 양과 그녀의 보호자에게는, 설령 온통 귀를 곤두세웠다 한들 히브리어나 칼데아어나 마찬가지였을 것이다. 그러나 두 사람은 놀란 나머지 무엇에도 귀 기울일 여유가 없었다. 기록해두어야 할 것은, 프로스 양만 경악과 동요에 빠진 것이 아니라, 크런처 씨 또한 — 그것은 그 자신의 별개의 개인적인 이유 때문인 듯했지만 — 더할 나위 없는 경악 상태에 빠져 있었다는 점이다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 프로스 양으로 하여금 비명을 지르게 만든 사내가, 짜증스럽고 퉁명스러운 목소리로(다만 낮은 어조로), 그것도 영어로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 솔로몬, 사랑하는 솔로몬!\" 프로스 양이 다시 두 손을 마주치며 외쳤다. \"이토록 오랜 세월 너를 한 번도 보지도 못하고 소식조차 듣지도 못하다가, 여기서 너를 만나다니!\"\n\n\"날 솔로몬이라고 부르지 마. 날 죽이려는 거요?\" 사내가 은밀하고 겁먹은 투로 물었다.\n\n\"동생아, 동생아!\" 프로스 양이 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"내가 너한테 그렇게 모질게 군 적이 있어서 그런 잔인한 질문을 하는 거니?\"\n\n\"그럼 그 참견 잘하는 혀를 좀 가만두고,\" 솔로몬이 말했다, \"나한테 할 말이 있거든 밖으로 나와. 포도주값 치르고 나오라고. 이 사람은 누구요?\"\n\n프로스 양은 결코 다정하지 않은 동생을 향해 사랑스럽고 풀 죽은 고개를 흔들며, 눈물 사이로 말했다. \"크런처 씨야.\"\n\n\"이 사람도 같이 나오라고 해,\" 솔로몬이 말했다. \"나를 유령으로 아나?\"\n\n겉보기로 판단하건대, 크런처 씨는 분명 그렇게 여기는 듯했다. 그러나 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 프로스 양은 눈물 사이로 가까스로 손가방 속을 뒤져 포도주값을 치렀다. 그녀가 그러는 동안, 솔로몬은 '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'의 추종자들을 향해 프랑스어로 몇 마디 해명을 했고, 그 덕에 그들 모두 본래의 자리와 하던 일로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"자,\" 어두운 길모퉁이에서 멈춰 서며 솔로몬이 말했다, \"원하는 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"무엇으로도 내 사랑을 한 번도 돌려놓지 못한 동생이 어쩌면 이리도 끔찍하게 무정할 수가!\" 프로스 양이 외쳤다. \"나한테 이런 인사를 하고, 애정이라곤 보여주지 않다니.\"\n\n\"자. 빌어먹을! 자,\" 솔로몬이 자기 입술을 프로스 양의 입술에 가볍게 갖다대며 말했다. \"이제 됐소?\"\n\n프로스 양은 그저 고개를 흔들며 말없이 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"내가 놀라기를 바란다면,\" 동생 솔로몬이 말했다, \"난 놀라지 않았어. 누나가 여기 있는 걸 알고 있었으니까. 여기 있는 사람들 대부분을 난 알고 있다고. 정말로 내 목숨을 위태롭게 하고 싶지 않다면 — 절반쯤은 그러고 싶어하는 것 같지만 — 되도록 빨리 누나 갈 길을 가고, 나는 내 길을 가게 해줘. 난 바쁜 몸이야. 관리란 말이야.\"\n\n\"내 영국인 동생 솔로몬,\" 프로스 양이 눈물 가득한 눈을 치켜뜨며 탄식했다, \"고국에서라면 가장 훌륭하고 위대한 사람 가운데 하나가 될 자질을 지녔던 애가, 외국인들 틈에서, 그것도 그런 외국인들 틈에서 관리 노릇을 하다니! 차라리 이 사랑하는 아이가 제……에 누워 있는 꼴을 보는 게 거의 더 나을 뻔했어……\"\n\n\"내 그럴 줄 알았다니까!\" 동생이 말을 가로채며 외쳤다. \"그럴 줄 알았어. 누나는 날 죽이려는 거야. 난 친누나 때문에 '용의자'가 되겠어. 이제 막 자리를 잡아가는 마당에!\"\n\n\"자비롭고 인자하신 하늘이 그것만은 막아주시기를!\" 프로스 양이 외쳤다. \"차라리 너를 다시는 보지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫겠다, 사랑하는 솔로몬, 비록 내가 늘 진심으로 너를 사랑해왔고 앞으로도 늘 그러겠지만 말이야. 나한테 다정한 말 한마디만 해다오, 우리 사이에 노여움이나 멀어진 마음 같은 건 없다고 말해다오, 그러면 더는 너를 붙잡지 않으마.\"\n\n선량한 프로스 양이여! 마치 두 사람 사이의 소원함이 그녀의 어떤 잘못에서 비롯되기라도 한 것처럼. 마치 로리 씨가 여러 해 전 쏘호의 그 조용한 모퉁이에서, 이 소중한 동생이 누나의 돈을 다 써버리고 그녀를 버렸다는 사실을 알지 못하기라도 했던 것처럼!\n\n그러나 그는 그 다정한 말을 하고 있었는데, 만약 두 사람의 상대적 자질과 처지가 뒤바뀌었다면(이런 일은 온 세상 어디서나 한결같이 일어나는 법이다) 보였을 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 마지못한 생색과 거드름을 부리며 그렇게 하고 있었다. 그때 크런처 씨가 그의 어깨를 건드리며, 쉰 목소리로 느닷없이 다음과 같은 기묘한 질문을 끼워넣었다.\n\n\"이보쇼! 한 가지 부탁 좀 해도 되겠소? 당신 이름이 존 솔로몬이오, 아니면 솔로몬 존이오?\"\n\n그 관리는 갑작스러운 불신을 품고 그를 향해 돌아섰다. 그는 그전까지 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다.\n\n\"자!\" 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"분명히 말해보쇼, 알겠소.\" (덧붙이자면, 정작 자신은 분명히 말하지 못하는 처지였다.) \"존 솔로몬이오, 솔로몬 존이오? 누이가 당신을 솔로몬이라 부르니, 누이라면 알 게 아니오. 그리고 나는 당신이 존이라는 걸 알지, 알겠소. 둘 중 어느 게 앞에 오는 거요? 그리고 프로스라는 그 성씨에 대해서도 마찬가지요. 그건 바다 건너에서 쓰던 당신 이름이 아니었소.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 무슨 뜻인지 다 알지는 못하오, 바다 건너에서 당신 이름이 뭐였는지 도통 떠오르질 않으니 말이오.\"\n\"안 떠오른다?\"\n\n\"안 떠오르오. 하지만 그게 두 음절짜리 이름이었다는 건 맹세할 수 있소.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 다른 자의 이름은 한 음절이었지. 난 당신을 알아. 당신은 첩자였소 — 베일리 법정의 증인이었지. 거짓의 아비요 당신 자신의 친아비 이름을 걸고 묻는데, 그때 당신은 뭐라고 불렸소?\"\n\n\"바사드,\" 또다른 목소리가 끼어들며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 천 파운드짜리 이름이군!\" 제리가 외쳤다.\n\n끼어든 화자는 시드니 카튼이었다. 그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌려 승마용 외투 자락 아래에 넣은 채, 마치 올드 베일리 법정에 서 있기라도 한 양 무심한 태도로 크런처 씨의 팔꿈치 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오, 친애하는 프로스 양. 저는 어제 저녁에 로리 씨 댁에 도착해 그를 놀라게 했지요. 우리는 모든 일이 잘 풀리거나, 아니면 제가 쓸모가 있을 경우가 아니면 다른 곳에 모습을 드러내지 않기로 합의했습니다. 저는 여기 당신 동생분과 잠시 이야기를 나누고자 모습을 드러낸 겁니다. 당신께 바사드 씨보다 더 나은 일에 종사하는 동생이 있었으면 좋았을 텐데요. 당신을 위해서라도 바사드 씨가 감옥의 '양'이 아니었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\"\n* 양: 당시 간수 밑에서 일하는 첩자를 가리키던 은어.\n\n'양'은 간수들 밑에서 일하는 첩자를 가리키는 당시의 은어였다. 본래 창백했던 그 첩자는 더욱 창백해지며, 무슨 근거로 감히 그런 말을 하느냐고 물었다 —\n\n\"말해드리지요,\" 시드니가 말했다. \"바사드 씨, 한 시간쯤 전에 제가 성벽을 바라보고 있는데, 당신이 콩시에르주리 감옥에서 나오는 걸 우연히 보게 되었습니다. 당신은 기억에 남을 만한 얼굴을 가졌고, 저는 얼굴을 잘 기억하지요. 그런 상황에서 당신을 본 데다, 당신도 모르지 않을 어떤 이유로 — 지금 몹시 불행한 처지에 놓인 한 친구의 불운과 당신을 연결지을 만한 이유로 — 호기심이 일어, 저는 당신 쪽을 향해 걸어갔습니다. 당신 바로 뒤를 따라 이곳 술집으로 들어와, 당신 가까이에 앉았지요. 당신의 거리낌 없는 대화와, 당신을 흠모하는 자들 사이에 공공연히 떠도는 소문으로부터, 저는 당신의 직업이 어떤 것인지 어렵잖게 추론해냈습니다. 그리고 차츰, 제가 닥치는 대로 했던 일이 하나의 목적으로 형태를 갖추어가는 것 같았습니다, 바사드 씨.\"\n\n\"무슨 목적 말이오?\" 첩자가 물었다.\n\n\"길거리에서 설명하기엔 번거롭고, 어쩌면 위험할 수도 있겠지요. 비밀스럽게, 잠시 동행해주시는 호의를 베풀어주시겠습니까 — 이를테면 텔슨 은행 사무소에서 말입니다?\"\n\n\"협박을 받으면서 말이오?\"\n\n\"이런! 제가 그런 말을 했던가요?\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 내가 왜 거기 가야 하오?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 바사드 씨, 당신이 모르겠다면 저로서도 알 수 없는 노릇이지요.\"\n\n\"말하지 않겠다는 뜻이오, 선생?\" 첩자가 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"제 말을 아주 분명히 알아들으셨군요, 바사드 씨. 저는 말하지 않겠습니다.\"\n카튼의 무심하고 거리낌 없는 태도는, 그가 마음속에 은밀히 품은 그런 종류의 일에서, 그리고 그가 상대해야 하는 그런 종류의 인간을 다룸에 있어, 그의 민첩함과 솜씨를 강력하게 뒷받침해주었다. 그의 노련한 눈이 이를 알아차리고 최대한 활용했다.\n\n\"거봐, 내가 뭐라 그랬어,\" 첩자가 누나에게 나무라는 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"이 일로 무슨 곤란한 일이 생기면, 그건 누나 탓이야.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 바사드 씨!\" 시드니가 소리쳤다. \"은혜를 모르시는군요. 제가 당신 누님을 크게 존경하지 않았더라면, 우리 서로의 만족을 위해 제가 제안하고 싶은 작은 제안으로 이토록 기분 좋게 이끌어가지 않았을 텐데요. 저와 함께 은행으로 가시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"당신이 할 말이 뭔지 들어보지. 그래, 같이 가겠소.\"\n\n\"먼저 당신 누님을 그분 댁이 있는 거리 모퉁이까지 안전하게 모셔다드릴 것을 제안합니다. 제 팔을 잡으시지요, 프로스 양. 지금 이 시기에 이 도시는 당신이 보호도 없이 밖에 나다닐 만한 좋은 곳이 못 됩니다. 그리고 당신의 호위인이 바사드 씨를 아는 사이이니, 그도 우리와 함께 로리 씨 댁으로 청하겠습니다. 다들 준비됐습니까? 그럼 갑시다!\"\n\n프로스 양은 얼마 지나지 않아 떠올렸고, 또 평생토록 기억했다. 그녀가 두 손을 시드니의 팔에 얹은 채 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 솔로몬을 해치지 말아달라고 애원했을 때, 그 팔에는 단단히 다잡은 결의가, 그 두 눈에는 일종의 영감 같은 것이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 그의 가벼운 태도와 모순될 뿐 아니라 그 사람을 변모시키고 고양시켰다는 것을. 그러나 그때 그녀는 자신의 애정을 그토록 누릴 자격이 없는 동생을 향한 두려움과, 시드니의 다정한 위로에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 자신이 본 것을 제대로 헤아릴 겨를이 없었다.\n\n그들은 거리 모퉁이에서 그녀를 내려놓았고, 카튼은 몇 분 걸으면 닿는 로리 씨 댁으로 앞장서 갔다. 존 바사드, 다시 말해 솔로몬 프로스가 그의 곁에서 걸었다.\n\n로리 씨는 막 저녁식사를 끝내고, 기분 좋게 타오르는 자그마한 장작 한두 개 앞에 앉아 있었는데 — 어쩌면 그 불꽃 속을 들여다보며, 여러 해 전 도버의 로열 조지 여관에서 빨갛게 단 석탄을 들여다보던, 텔슨 은행의 그 더 젊었던 노신사의 모습을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다. 그는 그들이 들어오자 고개를 돌렸고, 낯선 사람을 보고 놀라는 기색을 드러냈다.\n\n\"프로스 양의 동생입니다, 어르신,\" 시드니가 말했다. \"바사드 씨이지요.\"\n\n\"바사드?\" 노신사가 되뇌었다, \"바사드? 그 이름에 — 그리고 그 얼굴에 — 떠오르는 게 있는데.\"\n\n\"제가 당신이 눈에 띄는 얼굴을 가졌다고 했잖습니까, 바사드 씨,\" 카튼이 냉정하게 말했다. \"앉으시지요.\"\n\n그는 자신도 의자에 앉으면서, 로리 씨가 필요로 하던 연결고리를 찌푸린 얼굴로 일러주었다. \"그 재판의 증인 말입니다.\" 로리 씨는 즉시 기억해냈고, 새로 온 손님을 가리는 기색 없는 혐오의 눈길로 바라보았다.\n\n\"바사드 씨는 프로스 양에게 어르신께서 익히 들으셨던 그 다정한 동생으로 알아보였고,\" 시드니가 말했다, \"그 관계를 인정했습니다. 이제 더 나쁜 소식으로 넘어가지요. 다네이가 다시 체포되었습니다.\"\n\n경악에 사로잡힌 노신사가 외쳤다. \"무슨 소릴 하는 건가! 두 시간도 안 돼 그를 무사히, 자유로운 몸으로 두고 왔고, 막 그에게 돌아가려던 참인데!\"\n\n\"그런데도 체포되었습니다. 언제 그 일이 벌어졌습니까, 바사드 씨?\"\n\n\"방금 전이오, 일이 벌어졌다면 말이지만.\"\n\n\"바사드 씨는 누구보다 확실한 정보통입니다, 어르신,\" 시드니가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 바사드 씨가 포도주 한 병을 놓고 동료 '양'인 친구에게 전한 이야기로부터, 체포가 이루어졌다는 사실을 들었습니다. 그는 전령들을 문간에 남겨두고, 문지기가 그들을 들여보내는 것을 보았다더군요. 그가 다시 잡혀갔다는 데는 추호의 의심도 없습니다.\"\n\n로리 씨의 사무적인 눈이 화자의 얼굴에서, 이 점을 두고 더 따져보아야 시간 낭비라는 것을 읽어냈다. 혼란스러웠으나, 무언가가 자신의 침착함에 달려 있을지도 모른다는 것을 알아차린 그는, 마음을 다잡고 말없이 주의를 기울였다.\n\n\"자, 저는 믿습니다,\" 시드니가 그에게 말했다, \"마네뜨 박사의 이름과 영향력이 내일도 그에게 오늘만큼이나 큰 도움이 되어주리라고 — 바사드 씨, 당신은 그가 내일 다시 재판소에 서게 될 거라고 했지요? —\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇게 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"— 내일도 오늘만큼이나 큰 도움이 되어주기를요. 하지만 그렇지 않을 수도 있습니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면, 저는 마네뜨 박사가 이 체포를 막을 힘이 없었다는 데 흔들리고 있습니다, 로리 씨.\"\n\n\"미리 알지 못했을 수도 있지,\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그가 사위와 얼마나 한 몸처럼 여겨지는지를 생각하면, 바로 그 사정이 우려스러운 것이지요.\"\n\n\"그건 맞는 말일세,\" 로리 씨가 근심 어린 손을 턱에 댄 채, 근심 어린 눈을 카튼에게 둔 채로 수긍했다.\n\n\"요컨대,\" 시드니가 말했다, \"지금은 절박한 판돈을 걸고 절박한 노름이 벌어지는 절박한 시국입니다. 박사께서는 이기는 노름을 하시게 두지요. 지는 노름은 제가 하겠습니다. 여기서는 그 누구의 목숨도 사들일 값어치가 없습니다. 오늘 사람들에게 떠받들려 집에 실려온 자라도, 내일이면 단죄될 수 있지요. 그래서, 최악의 경우에 대비해 제가 걸기로 결심한 판돈은, 콩시에르주리 안에 있는 친구 하나입니다. 그리고 제가 따내려고 마음먹은 그 친구는, 바로 바사드 씨이지요.\"\n\n\"좋은 패를 들고 계셔야 할 텐데요, 선생,\" 첩자가 말했다.\n\n\"한번 훑어보지요. 내가 무슨 패를 쥐고 있는지 살펴보겠습니다 — 로리 씨, 제가 얼마나 거친 인간인지 아시지요. 브랜디를 좀 주셨으면 합니다만.\"\n\n브랜디가 그의 앞에 놓였고, 그는 한 잔을 들이켰다 — 또 한 잔을 들이켰다 — 그러고는 생각에 잠긴 채 술병을 밀쳐놓았다.\n\n\"바사드 씨,\" 그가 정말로 손에 쥔 카드 패를 훑어보는 사람의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"감옥의 양, 공화파 위원회의 밀정, 어떤 때는 간수, 어떤 때는 죄수, 언제나 첩자이자 비밀 밀고자 — 영국인이기에 이곳에서 그만큼 더 값어치가 있으니, 그런 역할을 맡았을 때 영국인은 프랑스인보다 매수의 의심을 덜 받기 때문이지요 — 그가 고용주들에게 자신을 가짜 이름으로 소개합니다. 그건 아주 좋은 패입니다. 지금은 공화파 프랑스 정부에 고용된 바사드 씨가, 전에는 프랑스와 자유의 적인 귀족적 영국 정부에 고용되어 있었지요. 그건 빼어난 패입니다. 이런 의심의 영역에서는 추론이 대낮처럼 명백하니, 곧 바사드 씨가 여전히 귀족적 영국 정부의 봉급을 받으면서, 공화국의 품속에 웅크린 배신자 적, 피트의 첩자, 그토록 많이 입에 오르내리지만 찾아내기 어려운 영국인 반역자이자 온갖 해악의 첩자라는 것이지요. 그건 누구도 이길 수 없는 패입니다. 제 패를 따라오셨습니까, 바사드 씨?\"\n* 피트: 당시 영국 총리 윌리엄 피트(소피트)를 가리킨다.\n\n\"당신 노름이 뭔지는 모르겠소만,\" 첩자가 다소 불안해하며 받았다.\n\n\"저는 제 으뜸패를 내겠습니다 — 가장 가까운 구역 위원회에 바사드 씨를 고발하는 것이지요. 당신 패를 훑어보십시오, 바사드 씨, 무얼 들고 있는지 보세요. 서두르지 마시고요.\"\n\n그는 술병을 가까이 끌어와, 브랜디를 또 한 잔 따라서 들이켰다. 그는 첩자가, 자신이 술에 취해 당장이라도 자기를 고발할 상태가 될까 봐 두려워하는 것을 알아챘다. 그것을 알아채고는, 그는 또 한 잔을 따라 들이켰다.\n\n\"당신 패를 잘 살펴보세요, 바사드 씨. 천천히 하시고요.\"\n\n그것은 그가 짐작한 것보다 더 보잘것없는 패였다. 바사드 씨는 그 패 속에서, 시드니 카튼이 전혀 알지 못하는 지는 패들을 보았다. 영국에서 너무 많은 위증을 했으나 끝내 성공하지 못한 탓에 명예로운 직업에서 쫓겨났는데 — 그곳에서 그가 필요 없어서가 아니라, 비밀 첩자에 대한 우리의 우월성을 자랑하는 영국식 명분이라는 게 지극히 근래에 생긴 것이기 때문이다 — 그는 자신이 해협을 건너 프랑스에서 일자리를 받아들였음을 알고 있었다. 처음에는 그곳의 자기 동포들 사이에서 유혹자이자 엿듣는 자로, 차츰 그 나라 사람들 사이에서 유혹자이자 엿듣는 자로 말이다. 그는 전복된 정부 아래서, 자신이 쌩 땅뚜안과 드파르주의 술집을 감시하는 첩자였음을, 마네뜨 박사의 투옥과 석방, 그 내력에 관한 정보의 요점들을 빈틈없는 경찰로부터 받아내어 드파르주 부부와 허물없는 대화로 들어가는 실마리로 삼으려 했음을, 그래서 그것들을 드파르주 부인에게 시험해보았다가 보기 좋게 실패했음을 알고 있었다. 그는 그 무시무시한 여인이 자신이 그녀와 이야기하는 동안 뜨개질을 하면서, 손가락을 움직이며 불길하게 자신을 바라보던 것을 늘 두려움에 떨며 기억했다. 그는 그 이후로 쌩 땅뚜안 구역에서, 그녀가 거듭거듭 자신의 뜨개질로 짠 명부를 꺼내, 그 목숨이 그러고 나면 단두대에 어김없이 삼켜지는 사람들을 고발하는 것을 보았다. 그는, 그처럼 일하는 사람이라면 누구나 그렇듯, 자신이 결코 안전하지 못하다는 것을, 도망은 불가능하다는 것을, 자신이 도끼의 그림자 아래 단단히 묶여 있다는 것을, 그리고 횡행하는 공포정치에 부역하느라 온갖 변절과 배신을 다 부렸음에도, 말 한마디면 그것이 자기 머리 위로 떨어질 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 일단 고발당하기만 하면, 그것도 방금 그의 머릿속에 제시된 그런 중대한 근거로 고발당하기만 하면, 그 매정한 성품을 여러 증거로 익히 보아온 그 끔찍한 여인이 자신을 상대로 그 치명적인 명부를 꺼내, 자신의 마지막 목숨의 기회마저 짓이겨버리리라는 것을 그는 내다보았다. 게다가 비밀스러운 일을 하는 자들은 누구나 쉬이 겁먹는 법인데, 여기 같은 검은 패가 충분히 쌓여 있으니, 그 패를 든 자가 그것을 한 장 한 장 넘기며 점점 새파랗게 질려가는 것도 무리가 아니었다.\n\n\"당신 패가 영 마음에 들지 않으시는 모양이군요,\" 시드니가 더할 나위 없는 침착함으로 말했다. \"노름하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔, 선생,\" 첩자가 로리 씨를 향해 돌아서며 더없이 비굴한 태도로 말했다, \"선생 같으신 연륜과 자비를 갖추신 분께 호소해서, 선생보다 한참 젊으신 이 다른 분께 한번 여쭤봐주십사 하고 싶군요. 과연 어떤 상황에서든 저 양반이 방금 말씀하신 그 으뜸패를 내는 일을 자신의 신분과 양립시킬 수 있겠는지를요. 저야 첩자라는 걸 인정합니다, 그리고 그게 떳떳지 못한 처지로 여겨진다는 것도요 — 누군가는 맡아야 하는 일이긴 하지만 말이지요. 하지만 이 양반은 첩자가 아닌데, 어째서 스스로 첩자 노릇을 하는 데까지 자신을 떨어뜨리려 하는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"제 으뜸패를 내겠습니다, 바사드 씨,\" 카튼이 그 답을 자기가 떠맡고 회중시계를 보며 말했다, \"아무런 거리낌 없이, 아주 잠시 후에 말이지요.\"\n\n\"두 분 다 부디,\" 첩자가 줄곧 로리 씨를 논의로 끌어들이려 애쓰며 말했다, \"제 누이에 대한 존중을 생각해서라도 —\"\n\n\"제가 당신 누님에 대한 존중을 보여드릴 더 나은 방법은, 결국 그분에게서 동생을 떼어내드리는 것밖에 없겠지요,\" 시드니 카튼이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 생각하지 않으시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"저는 그 점에 대해 마음을 단단히 굳혔습니다.\"\n\n첩자의 매끄러운 태도는, 일부러 거칠게 차려입은 그의 옷차림과, 또 필시 그의 평소 행동거지와 묘하게 어긋나는 것이었는데, 카튼의 헤아릴 수 없음 — 그는 자기보다 더 현명하고 정직한 사람들에게도 수수께끼였다 — 앞에서 그토록 제동이 걸린 나머지, 여기서 흔들리며 그를 저버렸다. 그가 어찌할 바를 모르는 사이에, 카튼은 카드 패를 들여다보던 아까의 태도로 돌아가 말했다.\n\n\"그러고 보니, 이제 다시 생각해보니, 아직 헤아리지 않은 또 하나의 좋은 패를 여기 쥐고 있다는 강한 인상이 드는군요. 시골 감옥에서 풀을 뜯고 있다고 자기 입으로 말하던 그 친구이자 동료 양 말입니다. 그자는 누구지요?\"\n\n\"프랑스인이오. 당신은 모르는 자요,\" 첩자가 재빨리 말했다.\n\n\"프랑스인이라, 응?\" 카튼이 그의 말을 따라 되뇌면서도 그를 전혀 의식하지 않는 듯 골똘히 생각하며 되뇌었다. \"글쎄요, 그럴 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"틀림없소, 장담하리다,\" 첩자가 말했다, \"중요한 일은 아니지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"중요한 일은 아니다,\" 카튼이 똑같이 기계적인 투로 되뇌었다 — \"중요한 일은 아니다 — 그래요, 중요한 일은 아니지요. 그래요. 그런데 그 얼굴을 내가 알아요.\"\n\n\"아닐 거요. 틀림없이 아닐 거요. 그럴 리 없소,\" 첩자가 말했다.\n\n\"그-럴-리-없다,\" 시드니 카튼이 옛 기억을 더듬으며 중얼거리고는, (다행히 작은 잔이었던) 자기 잔을 다시 만지작거렸다. \"그럴-리-없다. 프랑스어를 잘했지. 그런데도 외국인 같았어, 안 그랬나?\"\n\n\"지방 출신이오,\" 첩자가 말했다.\n\n\"아니야. 외국인이었어!\" 카튼이 펼친 손바닥으로 탁자를 내리치며 외쳤으니, 한 줄기 빛이 그의 머릿속에 또렷이 비쳐든 것이다. \"클라이! 변장은 했지만, 같은 자야. 우리는 올드 베일리에서 그자를 앞에 두고 있었어.\"\n\n\"이런, 선생, 거기선 너무 성급하시오,\" 바사드가 매부리코를 한쪽으로 한층 더 기울게 만드는 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"거기선 정말이지 당신이 나한테 유리한 패를 쥐여주는 셈이오. 클라이는 (이만큼 세월이 흘렀으니 그가 내 동업자였다는 건 거리낌 없이 인정하겠소) 죽은 지 여러 해 됐소. 내가 그의 임종을 지켰지. 그는 런던의 쌩 팽크라스인더필즈 교회에 묻혔소. 당시 그 불한당 같은 군중들 사이에서 인심을 잃은 탓에 내가 운구를 따라가지는 못했지만, 그를 관에 눕히는 건 거들었소.\"\n\n이때 로리 씨는 자기가 앉은 자리에서, 벽에 비친 더없이 기이한 도깨비 그림자를 알아차렸다. 그 근원을 더듬어 올라가던 그는, 그것이 크런처 씨 머리에 곤두서 뻣뻣하던 머리카락이 죄다 갑자기 비상하게 솟구쳐 뻣뻣해진 탓임을 알아냈다.\n\n\"우리 이성적으로 굽시다,\" 첩자가 말했다, \"그리고 공정하게 합시다. 당신이 얼마나 잘못 짚었는지, 당신 추측이 얼마나 근거 없는 것인지 보여드리기 위해, 클라이의 매장 증명서를 당신 앞에 내놓으리다. 마침 내 수첩에 넣고 다녔지,\" 그는 황급한 손놀림으로 그것을 꺼내 펼쳤다, \"그 이후로 줄곧 말이오. 자, 여기 있소. 오, 봐요, 보라고요! 손에 들고 보셔도 좋소. 위조한 게 아니니까.\"\n\n이때 로리 씨는 벽에 비친 그림자가 길게 늘어나는 것을 알아차렸고, 크런처 씨가 일어나 앞으로 나섰다. '잭이 지은 집' 속 뿔이 구부러진 암소가 바로 그 순간 그의 머리를 매만져주었다 한들, 그의 머리카락이 더 사납게 곤두서지는 못했을 것이다.\n* 잭이 지은 집: 영국의 누적식 동요 〈This is the house that Jack built〉에 나오는 구절을 가리킨다.\n\n첩자가 알아채지 못하는 사이에, 크런처 씨는 그의 곁에 서서, 마치 유령 같은 집행관처럼 그의 어깨를 건드렸다.\n\n\"그 로저 클라이 말이오, 나리,\" 크런처 씨가 말없이 굳어 무쇠 같은 얼굴로 말했다. \"그러니까 당신이 그를 관에 눕혔다고?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"누가 그를 거기서 꺼냈소?\"\n\n바사드는 의자에 등을 기대며 더듬거렸다. \"무슨 소리요?\"\n\n\"내 말은,\" 크런처 씨가 말했다, \"그가 거기 들어 있던 적이 도통 없었다는 거요. 천만에! 그자는 아니었어! 그가 거기 들어 있었다면, 내 목을 떼어가도 좋소.\"\n\n첩자는 두 신사를 둘러보았고, 두 사람 다 이루 말할 수 없는 경악에 차서 제리를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내 말하지,\" 제리가 말했다, \"당신은 그 관에 포석 돌멩이하고 흙을 묻은 거야. 클라이를 묻었다고 나한테 와서 말하지 마쇼. 그건 속임수였어. 나하고 다른 두 사람이 그걸 안다고.\"\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 아시오?\"\n\n\"그게 당신하고 무슨 상관이오? 빌어먹을!\" 크런처 씨가 으르렁거렸다, \"내가 묵은 원한을 진 건 바로 당신이지, 안 그렇소, 그 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 장사꾼들한테 사기 친 짓거리 말이오! 반 기니만 주면 당신 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버릴 수도 있소.\"\n\n이 일의 전환에 로리 씨와 더불어 경악에 빠져 있던 시드니 카튼이, 이때 크런처 씨에게 진정하고 스스로 설명해보라고 청했다.\n\n\"다음에 합시다, 선생,\" 그가 얼버무리며 받았다, \"지금은 설명하기에 영 거시기한 때라서요. 내가 분명히 말하는 건, 저자가 그 클라이가 그 관 속에 도통 들어 있지 않았다는 걸 잘 안다는 거요. 저자가 그자가 들어 있었다고 한 음절짜리 말이라도 하면, 난 반 기니에 저자 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버리든가,\" 크런처 씨는 이것을 자못 후한 제안인 양 강조했다, \"아니면 나가서 저자를 까발리든가 하겠소.\"\n\n\"흠! 한 가지는 알겠군요,\" 카튼이 말했다. \"저는 또다른 패를 쥐고 있습니다, 바사드 씨. 의심이 공기를 가득 채운 이 광란의 빠리에서, 당신이 당신과 똑같은 전력을 지닌, 게다가 죽은 척했다가 되살아났다는 수수께끼까지 두른 또다른 귀족적 첩자와 내통하는 마당에, 당신이 고발을 면하고 살아남기란 불가능하지요! 감옥에서 외국인이 공화국에 맞서 꾸민 음모. 강력한 패입니다 — 단두대행이 확실한 패지요! 노름하시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"안 하오!\" 첩자가 받았다. \"패를 던지겠소. 솔직히 우리는 그 난폭한 폭도들 사이에서 어찌나 인심을 잃었던지, 나는 물에 처박혀 죽을 위험을 무릅쓰고서야 겨우 영국을 빠져나왔고, 클라이는 어찌나 사방으로 쫓겨다녔던지 그 위장극이 아니었더라면 도무지 빠져나오지 못했을 거요. 다만 이 사람이 그게 위장극이었다는 걸 어떻게 아는지는, 나한텐 그야말로 불가사의 중의 불가사의지만 말이오.\"\n\n\"이 사람 일로는 머리 쓸 거 없소,\" 시비조의 크런처 씨가 받아쳤다, \"저 양반한테 신경 쓰는 것만으로도 충분히 골치 아플 테니까. 그리고 이거 보쇼! 한 번 더 말하지!\" — 크런처 씨는 자신의 후한 인심을 자못 거창하게 늘어놓고픈 충동을 억누르지 못했다 — \"반 기니만 주면 당신 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버리겠소.\"\n\n감옥의 양은 그에게서 시드니 카튼 쪽으로 돌아서며, 한층 단호하게 말했다. \"이제 결판을 낼 때가 됐군. 나는 곧 근무에 들어가야 해서, 시간을 넘길 수 없소. 당신은 제안이 있다고 했는데, 그게 뭐요? 자, 나한테 너무 많은 걸 요구해봐야 소용없소. 내 직무에서 무슨 일을 하라고, 내 목을 큰 추가 위험에 빠뜨리는 일을 하라고 요구한다면, 차라리 거절의 위험에 내 목숨을 거는 편이 승낙의 위험에 거는 편보다 낫소. 요컨대 난 그 쪽을 택하겠다는 거요. 절박함을 말하는데, 여기선 우리 모두가 절박하오. 명심하쇼! 내가 적당하다고 여기면 당신을 고발할 수도 있고, 난 돌벽도 뚫고 나갈 만큼 위증할 수 있으며, 다른 자들도 그럴 수 있소. 자, 나한테 원하는 게 뭐요?\"\n\n\"그리 대단한 건 아닙니다. 당신은 콩시에르주리의 간수이지요?\"\n\n\"단번에 못박아 두지만, 탈옥 같은 건 불가능한 일이오,\" 첩자가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 묻지도 않은 걸 무엇하러 말씀하십니까? 당신은 콩시에르주리의 간수이지요?\"\n\n\"가끔은 그렇소.\"\n\n\"마음만 먹으면 될 수 있고요?\"\n\n\"마음만 먹으면 드나들 수 있소.\"\n\n시드니 카튼은 또다른 잔에 브랜디를 채워, 벽난로 위에 천천히 부으며, 그것이 떨어지는 것을 지켜보았다. 그것이 다 쏟아지자, 그는 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"여기까지는, 이 두 분 앞에서 이야기했습니다. 패의 우열이 오로지 당신과 저 사이에만 머물지 않는 편이 좋았기 때문이지요. 이쪽 어두운 방으로 들어갑시다, 그리고 단둘이서 마지막 한마디를 나누지요.\"\n\n# CHAPTER IX. The Game Made\n\n시드니 카턴과 감옥의 양이 옆방의 어두운 곳에서, 아무 소리도 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누는 동안, 로리 씨는 적잖은 의심과 불신을 품은 채 제리를 바라보았다. 그 정직한 장사꾼이 그 시선을 받아들이는 태도는 신뢰를 불러일으키지 못했다. 그는 마치 그런 다리가 쉰 개쯤 되어 그것들을 모조리 시험해보려는 듯이 몸을 기댄 다리를 자꾸 바꾸었고, 매우 미심쩍을 만큼 골똘하게 자기 손톱을 들여다보았으며, 로리 씨의 눈길이 제 눈과 마주칠 때마다 손바닥을 입 앞에 갖다 대야 하는 그 독특한 종류의 짧은 기침에 사로잡혔는데, 그것은 완전히 솔직한 성품에 따르는 병이라고는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 알려진 바 없는 것이었다.\n\n\"제리.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이리 오게.\"\n\n크런처 씨는 한쪽 어깨를 앞세운 채 옆으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"자네, 심부름꾼 말고 무슨 일을 했나?\"\n\n후원자를 골똘히 바라보며 한참 궁리한 끝에, 크런처 씨는 \"농사 일을 하는 사람\"이라고 대답한다는 기발한 착상을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"내 마음에 몹시 걸리는 것이 있어.\" 로리 씨가 그를 향해 집게손가락을 성난 듯이 흔들며 말했다. \"자네가 점잖고 큰 텔슨 상회를 눈가림으로 이용했고, 입에 담기도 부끄러운 종류의 불법적인 일을 해왔다는 의심 말일세. 만일 그랬다면, 자네가 영국으로 돌아갔을 때 내가 자네 편이 되어주리라고는 기대하지 말게. 만일 그랬다면, 내가 자네 비밀을 지켜주리라고도 기대하지 말게. 텔슨 상회는 결코 기만당하지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n\"바라건대 나리,\" 무안해진 크런처 씨가 사정하듯 말했다. \"제가 머리가 셀 때까지 영광스럽게도 자질구레한 일을 해드린 나리 같은 신사분이라면, 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 — 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만, 그렇다손 치더라도 — 저를 해치는 일을 두번은 생각해주실 거라고 봅니다요. 그리고 또 한 가지 따져볼 게 있는데요, 설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 그조차 죄다 한쪽만의 일은 아닐 겝니다. 거기엔 양쪽이 다 있는 거지요. 지금 이 시각에도 정직한 장사꾼이 동전 한닢 — 동전 한닢이라굽쇼! 아니죠, 반푼도, 반푼이라굽쇼! 아니죠, 반의반도 못 줍는 곳에서 기니 금화를 주워 담는 의사 선생들이 있을 겁니다요. 텔슨 상회에 연기처럼 돈을 쌓아두고서, 그 장사꾼한테 슬그머니 의사 눈을 부라리며, 제 마차에 타고 내리고 — 아! 그 또한 연기처럼, 아니 그보다 더하면 더했지요. 자, 그것도 텔슨 상회를 기만하는 일이올시다. 거위한테 양념을 치면 수거위한테도 쳐야 하는 법 아닙니까요. 그리고 여기 크런처 부인은, 적어도 옛 영국 시절에는 그랬고, 빌미만 주어지면 내일이라도 그럴 텐데요, 장사를 망칠 지경으로 — 아주 거덜 낼 지경으로 — 엎드려 기도질을 해댑니다요! 그런데 그 의사 선생들 마누라는 엎드려 기도질을 안 해요 — 그러는 꼴을 한번 봤어야지요! 아니, 한다손 치더라도, 그 기도질은 환자가 더 늘어나는 쪽으로 가는 거지요. 그러니 한쪽 없이 다른 쪽을 어찌 제대로 가질 수 있겠습니까요? 게다가 장의사다, 교구 서기다, 묘지기다, 사설 야경꾼이다 — 죄다 욕심꾸러기에 죄다 한통속인데 — 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 사람이 그걸로 별로 벌이도 못 해요. 그리고 사람이 벌어들인 쥐꼬리만 한 것도 결코 잘 풀리지가 않습니다, 로리 나리. 그걸로 좋은 일이라곤 절대 없을 겁니다요. 그 일에 한번 들어선 다음에야 빠져나갈 길만 보이면 줄곧 그 짓에서 손을 떼고 싶어 할 겝니다 — 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 말이지요.\"\n\n\"으흠!\" 로리 씨가 외쳤지만, 그래도 다소 누그러진 채였다. \"자네 꼴을 보니 충격일세.\"\n\n\"이제, 나리, 제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀은요,\" 크런처 씨가 말을 이었다. \"설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만—\"\n\n\"얼버무리지 말게.\" 로리 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아니죠, 절대 안 그럽니다요, 나리.\" 크런처 씨는 그런 생각이나 행실과는 거리가 멀다는 듯이 대꾸했다. \"— 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만 — 제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀은 이겝니다요. 저기 저 의자, 저기 저 칸막이 자리에 제 자식놈이 앉아 있는데, 사내로 잘 키워 다 컸으니, 나리께서 바라시기만 하면 발꿈치가 머리 있는 데로 갈 때까지 심부름이고 전갈이고 온갖 잔심부름이고 다 해드릴 겁니다요. 만일 그렇다손 치더라도, 그렇다는 건 여전히 아닙니다만 (나리께 절대 얼버무리지 않을 테니까요), 저 자식놈이 제 아비 자리를 물려받아 제 어미를 돌보게 해주십쇼. 그 애 아비를 고발하지 말아주십쇼 — 그러지 말아주십쇼, 나리 — 그리고 그 아비가 떳떳하게 땅 파는 일에 들어서서, 자기가 파내지 않았어야 할 것들을 — 설령 그랬다손 치더라도 — 열심히 도로 파묻어주고, 앞으로 그것들을 잘 지키겠다는 신념으로 보상하게 해주십쇼. 이것이, 로리 나리,\" 크런처 씨는 팔로 이마를 닦으며, 자기 연설의 마무리에 이르렀음을 알리듯 말했다. \"제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀입니다요. 사람이 제 둘레에서 이런 끔찍한 일들이 벌어지는 걸, 그러니까 머리 없는 시체들이 — 저런, 값이 짐꾼 삯, 아니 그조차도 안 될 만큼 흔해빠지게 — 굴러다니는 걸 보고서, 그 일들에 대해 진지하게 생각하지 않을 수가 없는 거지요. 그리고 설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 이게 제 생각일 겁니다요. 부디 명심해주십쇼, 제가 방금 한 말은, 도로 숨길 수도 있었는데 좋은 뜻에서 떨치고 나서서 한 말이라는 걸 말입니다요.\"\n\n\"적어도 그건 사실이군.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이제 그만하게. 자네가 그럴 자격이 있고, 말이 아니라 행동으로 뉘우친다면, 내가 아직 자네 편이 되어줄지도 모르지. 더는 말이 필요 없네.\"\n\n시드니 카턴과 첩자가 어두운 방에서 돌아오자, 크런처 씨는 주먹 마디로 이마를 문질렀다. \"안녕히 가시오, 바사드 씨.\" 앞쪽 사람이 말했다. \"이렇게 합의가 된 이상, 당신은 나한테서 두려워할 게 아무것도 없소.\"\n\n그는 로리 씨와 마주 보이는, 난롯가 의자에 앉았다. 둘만 남게 되자 로리 씨는 그에게 무슨 일을 했느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"별것 아닙니다. 죄수에게 일이 나쁘게 돌아갈 경우, 한번은 그에게 접근할 수 있도록 확보해두었습니다.\"\n\n로리 씨의 안색이 어두워졌다.\n\n\"그게 제가 할 수 있는 전부였습니다.\" 카턴이 말했다. \"너무 많은 걸 요구하는 건 이자의 목을 도끼 아래 들이미는 꼴이 됐을 겁니다. 게다가 그자 말마따나, 고발당한다 해도 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 일어날 수는 없을 테고요. 분명 그게 우리 처지의 약점이었죠. 어쩔 도리가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그에게 접근하는 것으로는,\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"재판소에서 일이 나쁘게 돌아간다 해도 그를 구할 수 없잖나.\"\n\n\"제가 구할 수 있다고는 말한 적 없습니다.\"\n\n로리 씨의 눈길이 차츰 불을 찾아갔다. 사랑하는 이에 대한 연민과, 그가 다시 체포된 데서 온 무거운 실망감이 차츰 그 눈을 약하게 만들었다. 그는 이제 늙은이였고, 근래의 불안에 짓눌려 있었으며, 그의 눈물이 떨어졌다.\n\n\"당신은 선한 분이고 진실한 벗입니다.\" 카턴이 달라진 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 마음 아파하시는 걸 제가 알아챘다 해도 용서하십시오. 저는 제 아버지가 우시는 걸 보면서 무심히 곁에 앉아 있을 수 없었을 겁니다. 그리고 당신이 제 아버지였다 해도 당신의 슬픔을 지금보다 더 존중할 수는 없었겠지요. 하지만 당신은 그런 불행에서 벗어나 있으시군요.\"\n\n마지막 말은 평소의 태도로 슬쩍 돌아가 하긴 했지만, 그의 어조에도 손길에도 진정한 감정과 존경이 담겨 있어서, 그의 더 나은 면모를 한번도 본 적 없던 로리 씨는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그가 손을 내밀자, 카턴은 그 손을 부드럽게 쥐었다.\n\n\"가엾은 다네이 이야기로 돌아가자면,\" 카턴이 말했다. \"이번 면담이나 이번 합의에 대해 그녀에게 말하지 마십시오. 그렇다고 그녀가 그를 면회하러 갈 수 있게 되는 것도 아니니까요. 그녀는 이게 최악의 경우에 대비해, 그에게 형 집행을 앞당길 방법을 전하려고 꾸민 일이라고 여길지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n로리 씨는 그런 생각을 미처 하지 못했던 터라, 그것이 그의 마음속에 있는지 보려고 재빨리 카턴을 바라보았다. 그런 듯했다. 카턴이 그 시선을 되받았고, 분명히 그 뜻을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"그녀는 천 가지 생각을 할지도 모릅니다.\" 카턴이 말했다. \"그리고 그 어느 것이든 그녀의 괴로움만 더할 뿐이겠지요. 그녀에게 제 이야기는 하지 마십시오. 처음 왔을 때 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 그녀를 만나지 않는 편이 낫습니다. 그러지 않고도 제 손이 찾아낼 수 있는 자질구레한 도움이라면 무엇이든 그녀를 위해 손을 내밀 수 있으니까요. 이제 그녀에게 가시겠지요? 오늘 밤 그녀는 무척 외로울 겁니다.\"\n\n\"지금 곧장 갈 참일세.\"\n\n\"그것 참 다행입니다. 그녀는 당신에게 그토록 깊이 의지하고 기댑니다. 그녀는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"불안하고 비통해하지만, 무척 아름답네.\"\n\n\"아!\"\n\n그것은 한숨 같은 — 거의 흐느낌 같은 — 길고 비탄에 찬 소리였다. 그 소리에 로리 씨의 눈길이 불 쪽으로 돌려진 카턴의 얼굴로 끌렸다. 빛인지 그늘인지 (그 노신사는 어느 쪽이라고 말할 수 없었으리라) 그의 얼굴에서, 거칠고 밝은 날 산비탈을 휩쓸고 지나가는 변화처럼 재빨리 스쳐 지나갔다. 그는 발을 들어, 앞으로 굴러떨어지려는 작은 장작 하나를 도로 밀어 넣었다. 그는 당시 유행하던 흰 승마복에 목 긴 장화를 신고 있었고, 그 밝은 표면에 닿는 불빛 때문에 그는 무척 창백해 보였으며, 다듬지 않은 긴 갈색 머리카락이 헝클어진 채 그의 둘레로 늘어져 있었다. 불에 대한 그의 무관심은 로리 씨가 한마디 나무랄 만큼 두드러진 것이었다. 발의 무게에 눌려 불붙은 장작이 부러졌는데도 그의 장화는 여전히 그 뜨거운 잿불 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"깜빡했군요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n로리 씨의 눈길이 다시 그의 얼굴로 끌렸다. 본래 잘생긴 이목구비를 흐리고 있는 초췌한 기색에 주목하고, 죄수들의 얼굴 표정을 마음속에 생생히 떠올리고 있던 그는, 바로 그 표정을 강렬하게 떠올렸다.\n\n\"이곳에서 맡으신 일은 다 끝나셨습니까, 나리?\" 카턴이 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다네. 어젯밤 루시가 그토록 뜻밖에 들어왔을 때 자네에게 말했듯이, 마침내 여기서 내가 할 수 있는 일은 다 해냈네. 그들을 완전히 안전한 데 두고서 빠리를 떠날 수 있기를 바랐지. 통행 허가증도 가지고 있고. 떠날 채비가 되어 있었네.\"\n\n둘 다 말이 없었다.\n\n\"나리의 삶은 돌아보기에 긴 세월이겠지요?\" 카턴이 아쉬운 듯 말했다.\n\n\"일흔여덟번째 해를 맞고 있다네.\"\n\n\"평생 쓸모 있게 사셨고, 한결같이 꾸준히 일에 매달리셨으며, 신뢰받고 존경받고 우러름을 받으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"나는 어른이 된 이후로 줄곧 사업가였네. 사실, 어릴 적에도 사업가였다고 할 수 있지.\"\n\n\"보십시오, 일흔여덟에 얼마나 큰 자리를 차지하고 계신지. 나리께서 그 자리를 비우고 떠나면 얼마나 많은 사람이 나리를 그리워하겠습니까!\"\n\n\"외로운 늙은 독신자일 뿐일세.\" 로리 씨가 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"나를 위해 울어줄 사람은 아무도 없네.\"\n\n\"어찌 그런 말씀을 하십니까? 그녀가 나리를 위해 울지 않겠습니까? 그녀의 아이가 울지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 하느님께 감사하지. 내 말이 꼭 그런 뜻은 아니었네.\"\n\n\"그건 정말이지 하느님께 감사할 일이지요. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\"\n\n\"만일 나리께서 오늘 밤 나리의 외로운 가슴에 대고 진심으로 이렇게 말할 수 있다면 — '나는 그 어떤 인간의 사랑도 애정도, 감사도 존경도 내 것으로 확보하지 못했다. 나는 그 누구의 마음속에도 다정한 자리 하나 얻지 못했다. 나는 기억될 만한 선한 일도 쓸모 있는 일도 하나 하지 못했다!' — 나리의 일흔여덟 해는 일흔여덟 가지 무거운 저주가 되겠지요. 그렇지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"옳은 말일세, 카턴 씨. 정말 그러리라 생각하네.\"\n\n시드니는 다시 불 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 몇 순간의 침묵 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"여쭙고 싶은 게 있습니다. 나리의 어린 시절이 멀게 느껴지십니까? 어머니 무릎에 앉았던 날들이 아주 오래전 일처럼 느껴지십니까?\"\n\n그의 부드러워진 태도에 응하여, 로리 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"이십 년 전이라면 그랬을 테지. 하지만 내 삶의 이 시점에서는 아니라네. 끝에 점점 더 가까이 다가갈수록, 나는 그 둘레를 돌아 시작점에 점점 더 가까이 가게 되거든. 그건 길을 부드럽게 다듬고 채비해주는 어떤 자비로운 손길 가운데 하나인 듯싶네. 지금 내 마음은, 오래 잠들어 있던 수많은 기억들에 닿아 흔들린다네. 내 예쁘고 젊은 어머니에 대한 (이렇게나 늙은 나에게!), 그리고 우리가 세상이라 부르는 것이 나에게 그토록 실감 나지 않았고 내 결점들이 내 안에 굳어지지 않았던 날들에 얽힌 수많은 추억들에 말일세.\"\n\n\"그 심정 이해합니다!\" 카턴이 환하게 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"그리고 그 덕에 더 나아지셨군요?\"\n\n\"그러기를 바라네.\"\n\n카턴은 자리에서 일어나 그가 겉옷 입는 것을 도와주며 여기서 대화를 끝맺었다. \"하지만 자네는,\" 로리 씨가 그 화제로 돌아가며 말했다. \"자네는 젊지 않은가.\"\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 카턴이 말했다. \"저는 늙지 않았습니다만, 제 젊은 시절은 결코 늙어가는 길이 아니었지요. 제 이야기는 그만하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"내 이야기도 그렇네, 분명히.\" 로리 씨가 말했다. \"자네 나가려는가?\"\n\n\"그녀의 집 문까지 함께 걷지요. 제 떠돌고 안절부절못하는 버릇은 아시지 않습니까. 제가 오래도록 거리를 어슬렁거리더라도 걱정하지 마십시오. 아침이면 다시 나타날 테니까요. 내일 법정에 가십니까?\"\n\n\"그렇다네, 불행히도.\"\n\n\"저도 거기 있을 겁니다. 하지만 군중 가운데 한 사람으로서일 뿐이지요. 제 첩자가 자리를 마련해줄 겁니다. 제 팔을 잡으시지요, 나리.\"\n\n로리 씨가 그렇게 했고, 그들은 계단을 내려가 거리로 나섰다. 몇 분 만에 로리 씨의 목적지에 다다랐다. 카턴은 거기서 그와 헤어졌으나, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 머뭇거리다가, 문이 닫힌 뒤 다시 그 문으로 돌아가 그것을 어루만졌다. 그는 그녀가 날마다 감옥에 간다는 이야기를 들은 터였다. \"그녀는 여기로 나왔겠지.\" 그는 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"이쪽으로 돌아서, 이 돌들을 자주 밟았을 거야. 그녀의 발자취를 따라가 보자.\"\n\n그녀가 수백 번 서 있었던 라포르스 감옥 앞에 그가 섰을 때는 밤 열 시였다. 자그마한 톱장이 하나가 가게 문을 닫고서 가게 문간에서 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 시민.\" 시드니 카턴은 지나가다 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. 그 사람이 캐묻듯이 그를 쳐다보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 시민.\"\n\n\"공화국은 어떻게 돌아가고 있소?\"\n\n\"단두대 말이겠지. 나쁘지 않소. 오늘 예순셋이었거든. 머잖아 백까지 올라갈 거요. 쌍송이랑 그 부하들은 가끔 기진맥진하다고 투덜대지. 하하하! 어찌나 우스운지, 그 쌍송이란 작자가. 대단한 이발사라니까!\"\n\n\"그자를 자주 보러 가시오—\"\n\n\"면도하는 거? 늘 가지. 매일같이. 대단한 이발사라니까! 일하는 걸 본 적 있소?\"\n\n\"없소.\"\n\n\"한 무더기 잘 모였을 때 가서 보시구려. 자, 한번 그려보슈, 시민. 그자가 오늘 예순셋을 파이프 두 대도 안 피우는 사이에 면도해버렸다니까! 두 대도 안 되게. 정말이라니까!\"\n\n씩 웃는 그 자그마한 사내가 자기가 사형 집행인의 솜씨를 어떻게 쟀는지 설명하려고 피우던 파이프를 내밀었을 때, 카턴은 그자의 목숨을 쳐서 끊어버리고 싶은 충동이 솟구치는 것을 너무도 또렷이 느낀 나머지, 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"한데 당신은 영국인이 아니지요?\" 톱장이가 말했다. \"영국 옷을 입고 있긴 하지만.\"\n\n\"맞소.\" 카턴은 다시 걸음을 멈추고 어깨 너머로 대답했다.\n\n\"프랑스인처럼 말하는데.\"\n\n\"여기서 오래 공부한 사람이오.\"\n\n\"아하, 완벽한 프랑스인이로군! 잘 가시오, 영국인.\"\n\n\"잘 있으시오, 시민.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 우스운 작자를 꼭 보러 가구려.\" 자그마한 사내가 끈질기게 그의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤다. \"파이프도 챙겨 가시고!\"\n\n시드니는 시야에서 멀리 벗어나기도 전에, 거리 한복판 가물거리는 등불 아래 멈춰 서서 종잇조각에 연필로 무언가를 적었다. 그러고는 길을 잘 기억하는 사람의 단호한 걸음걸이로, 여러 어둡고 더러운 거리를 — 공포의 시절이라 가장 번화한 큰길조차 청소가 안 되어 평소보다 훨씬 더 더러운 거리를 — 가로질러, 주인이 손수 문을 닫고 있는 어느 약방 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 자그맣고 침침하고 구부정한 사내가 운영하는, 구불구불 비탈진 거리에 자리한 자그맣고 침침하고 비뚤어진 가게였다.\n\n계산대에서 마주한 이 시민에게도 잘 있으라고 인사한 뒤, 그는 종잇조각을 그 앞에 내려놓았다. \"휘유!\" 약제사는 그것을 읽으며 나직이 휘파람을 불었다. \"이런! 이런! 이런!\"\n\n시드니 카턴은 아랑곳하지 않았고, 약제사가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 쓸 거요, 시민?\" \"내가 쓸 거요.\"\n\n\"이것들을 따로따로 보관하도록 조심할 거지요, 시민? 섞었을 때 어떻게 되는지 아시오?\"\n\n\"잘 아오.\"\n\n작은 꾸러미 몇 개가 만들어져 그에게 건네졌다. 그는 그것들을 하나씩 안쪽 외투의 가슴께에 넣고, 값을 헤아려 치른 뒤, 천천히 가게를 나섰다. \"더 할 일은 아무것도 없군.\" 그는 달을 올려다보며 말했다. \"내일까지는. 잠을 이룰 수가 없어.\"\n\n빠르게 흘러가는 구름 아래에서 그가 이 말을 소리 내어 한 태도에는 무모함이라곤 없었고, 무관심이라기보다는 차라리 결연함을 드러내고 있었다. 그것은 헤매고 발버둥치다 길을 잃었으나, 마침내 제 길로 들어서서 그 끝을 본 지친 사람의 차분한 태도였다.\n\n오래전, 그가 가장 이른 시절의 경쟁자들 사이에서 장래가 촉망되는 청년으로 이름났을 때, 그는 아버지를 무덤까지 따라간 적이 있었다. 어머니는 그보다 여러 해 앞서 세상을 떠났다. 아버지의 무덤가에서 낭독되었던 그 엄숙한 말씀이, 그가 머리 위로 달과 구름이 흘러가는 가운데 짙은 그림자들 사이로 어두운 거리를 지나가는 동안 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다. \"주께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\"\n\n도끼가 지배하는 도시에서, 밤에 홀로, 그날 처형당한 예순셋과, 그때 감옥에서 운명을 기다리던 내일의 희생자들과, 그리고 또 내일의, 또 내일의 희생자들에 대한 자연스러운 슬픔이 그의 안에서 솟구치는 가운데, 깊은 바다에서 끌어올린 녹슨 낡은 닻처럼 그 말씀을 마음에 와닿게 한 연상의 사슬은 쉽게 찾아볼 수 있었으리라. 그는 그것을 찾지 않았으나, 그 말씀을 되뇌며 걸어갔다.\n\n자기들을 에워싼 공포를 몇 시간의 평온 속에 잊은 채 잠자리에 들려는 사람들의 불 켜진 창문들 — 기도 소리 하나 들리지 않는 교회의 탑들, 사제 노릇을 한 사기꾼과 약탈자와 난봉꾼들에 대한 수년간의 반감이 그 자멸의 지경에까지 이른 탓이었다 — 정문에 그들이 적어놓았듯 영원한 잠을 위해 마련된 멀리 떨어진 매장지들 — 넘쳐나는 감옥들 — 그리고 너무도 흔하고 무미건조해져서, 단두대의 그 온갖 작업을 두고 사람들 사이에 떠도는 유령 이야기 하나 나오지 않을 만큼 예순 명씩 죽음을 향해 굴러가던 거리들 — 분노에 찬 그 짧은 밤의 멈춤 속에 가라앉아가는 이 도시의 삶과 죽음 전체에 대해 엄숙한 관심을 품은 채, 시드니 카턴은 더 밝은 거리들을 향해 다시 쎈 강을 건넜다.\n\n거리에 나다니는 마차는 거의 없었다. 마차를 탄 자들은 의심받기 십상이었기에, 양반네들은 붉은 잠모자 아래 고개를 숨기고 투박한 신을 신은 채 터덜터덜 걸었다. 하지만 극장들은 모두 만원이었고, 그가 지나갈 때 사람들이 즐겁게 쏟아져 나와 떠들썩하게 이야기하며 집으로 갔다. 어느 극장 문 앞에, 어머니와 함께 있던 어린 소녀 하나가 진흙탕 너머로 길을 건널 방법을 찾고 있었다. 그는 아이를 안아 건네주었고, 머뭇거리던 그 팔이 목에서 풀리기 전에 입맞춤을 청했다.\n\n\"주께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\"\n\n이제 거리가 고요해지고 밤이 깊어가자, 그 말씀이 그의 발걸음 메아리 속에 있었고, 또 공기 속에 있었다. 더없이 차분하고 흔들림 없는 채로, 그는 걸으면서 이따금 그 말씀을 혼잣말로 되뇌었으나, 늘 그 소리를 들었다.\n\n밤이 다 지나갔고, 그가 다리 위에 서서, 그림처럼 어수선한 집들과 대성당이 달빛을 받아 환히 빛나는 빠리 섬의 강변 벽에 물이 철썩이는 소리를 듣고 있을 때, 하늘에서 죽은 얼굴처럼 보이는 날이 차갑게 밝아왔다. 그러자 달과 별을 거느린 밤이 창백해지더니 죽어갔고, 잠시 동안은 마치 천지창조가 죽음의 권세에 넘겨진 듯이 보였다.\n\n그러나 영광스러운 해가 떠오르면서, 밤의 그 짐이었던 그 말씀을, 그 길고 밝은 빛줄기로 곧장 따스하게 그의 가슴에 와닿게 하는 듯했다. 그리고 경건하게 손으로 눈을 가린 채 그 빛줄기를 따라 바라보니, 빛의 다리가 그와 해 사이의 허공에 걸린 듯했고, 그 아래로 강물이 반짝였다.\n\n그토록 빠르고 깊고 확실한 거센 물살은, 아침의 고요 속에서 마음 맞는 벗과도 같았다. 그는 집들에서 멀리 떨어져 강줄기를 따라 걸었고, 햇빛과 온기 속에서 강둑에 잠들었다. 잠에서 깨어 다시 일어났을 때, 그는 거기서 잠시 더 머물며, 목적 없이 돌고 또 도는 소용돌이가 마침내 강물에 휩쓸려 바다로 실려 가는 것을 지켜보았다. \"나처럼.\"\n\n그때 죽은 잎사귀의 바랜 빛깔 같은 돛을 단 장삿배 한 척이 그의 시야에 미끄러져 들어와, 그의 곁을 떠가다가 사라져갔다. 물 위에 남은 그 고요한 자취가 사라질 무렵, 자신의 그 모든 가련한 눈멀음과 과오를 자비롭게 헤아려달라고 그의 가슴에서 솟구쳐 나온 그 기도는, \"나는 부활이요 생명이니\"라는 말씀으로 끝맺었다.\n\n그가 돌아왔을 때 로리 씨는 이미 나가고 없었고, 그 선한 노인이 어디로 갔는지는 짐작하기 어렵지 않았다. 시드니 카턴은 커피 한 잔만 마시고 빵을 좀 먹은 뒤, 몸을 씻고 옷을 갈아입어 기운을 차리고는, 재판이 열리는 곳으로 나갔다.\n\n법정은 온통 술렁이며 웅성거리고 있었고, 그 검은 양은 — 두려워서 많은 이들이 그에게서 멀어졌다 — 그를 군중 속 어느 외진 구석으로 밀어 넣었다. 로리 씨가 거기 있었고, 마네뜨 박사도 거기 있었다. 그녀도 거기, 아버지 곁에 앉아 있었다.\n\n그녀의 남편이 끌려 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그에게 시선을 보냈는데, 어찌나 떠받쳐주고, 어찌나 북돋아주고, 어찌나 흠모하는 사랑과 가엾어하는 다정함으로 가득하면서도, 그를 위해 어찌나 용감한 시선이었던지, 그것은 그의 얼굴에 건강한 핏기를 불러일으키고, 그의 눈빛을 밝히며, 그의 마음에 생기를 불어넣었다. 만일 그녀의 시선이 시드니 카턴에게 미친 영향을 알아챌 눈이 있었다면, 그것이 정확히 똑같은 영향임을 알아볼 수 있었으리라.\n\n그 부당한 재판소 앞에는, 그 어떤 피고에게도 합당한 심리를 보장하는 절차의 질서라곤 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다. 만일 모든 법과 형식과 의례가 먼저 그토록 끔찍하게 남용되지 않았더라면, 그래서 혁명의 자멸적인 복수가 그것들을 모조리 바람에 흩날려 버리려 들지 않았더라면, 그런 혁명은 결코 일어날 수 없었으리라.\n\n모든 눈이 배심원단을 향했다. 어제도 그제도, 그리고 내일도 모레도 마찬가지일 그 단호한 애국자들이자 선량한 공화주의자들이었다. 그들 가운데 열성적이고 두드러진 사람으로, 갈망하는 얼굴에 손가락을 끊임없이 입가에 가져다 대는 한 사내가 있었는데, 그의 모습은 구경꾼들에게 큰 만족을 주었다. 생명에 굶주린, 식인귀 같은 인상에, 피에 굶주린 그 배심원은 쌩땅뚜안의 자끄 쓰리였다. 배심원단 전체가, 사슴을 재판하라고 소집된 개들의 배심원단 같았다.\n\n이어 모든 눈이 다섯 명의 판사와 검사에게로 향했다. 오늘 그쪽에는 호의적인 기색이라곤 없었다. 거기엔 무자비하고 타협 없는, 살의를 띤 단호한 의도가 서려 있었다. 이어 모든 눈이 군중 속에서 다른 누군가의 눈을 찾아, 그것을 향해 동의하듯 번득였다. 그리고 긴장한 듯 골똘히 앞으로 몸을 숙이기 전에 서로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n다네이라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드. 어제 석방됨. 어제 다시 고발되어 다시 체포됨. 어젯밤 기소장이 전달됨. 공화국의 적으로 의심받고 고발된 자, 귀족, 폭군 일가의 일원, 폐지된 특권을 백성에 대한 파렴치한 압제에 이용했다는 이유로 추방당한 가문의 일원. 다네이라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드는 그러한 추방의 이유로, 법적으로 완전히 사망한 자였다.\n\n이러한 취지로, 그만큼의, 아니 그보다 더 적은 말로 검사가 진술했다.\n\n재판장이 물었다. 피고는 공공연히 고발되었는가, 아니면 은밀히 고발되었는가?\n\n\"공공연히 고발되었습니다, 재판장님.\"\n\n\"누구에 의해?\"\n\n\"세 사람의 목소리에 의해서입니다. 쌩땅뚜안의 포도주 상인 에르네스뜨 드파르주.\"\n\n\"좋소.\"\n\n\"그의 아내, 떼레즈 드파르주.\"\n\n\"좋소.\"\n\n\"의사, 알렉상드르 마네뜨.\"\n\n법정에 큰 소동이 일었고, 그 한가운데서, 창백하게 떨며 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어선 마네뜨 박사가 보였다.\n\n\"재판장, 나는 이것이 위조요 사기임을 분개하여 항의하는 바이오. 당신도 피고가 내 딸의 남편임을 알지 않소. 내 딸과 그녀가 사랑하는 이들은 내 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중하오. 내가 내 자식의 남편을 고발했다고 말하는 그 거짓 음모자는 대체 누구이며 어디 있단 말이오!\"\n\n\"마네뜨 시민, 진정하시오. 재판소의 권위에 복종하지 않는 것은 스스로를 법 밖에 두는 일이오. 당신에게 목숨보다 소중한 것이 무엇이냐 하면, 선량한 시민에게 공화국보다 더 소중한 것은 있을 수 없소.\"\n\n이 질책에 우렁찬 갈채가 쏟아졌다. 재판장은 종을 울리고는 열을 올리며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"만일 공화국이 당신에게 당신의 자식을 희생하라고 요구한다면, 당신에게는 그녀를 희생하는 것 말고 다른 의무가 없을 것이오. 이어지는 말을 들으시오. 그동안은 잠자코 계시오!\"\n\n또다시 광적인 갈채가 터져 나왔다. 마네뜨 박사는 두리번거리는 눈에 입술을 떨며 자리에 앉았다. 그의 딸이 그에게 더 바짝 다가갔다. 배심원단의 그 갈망하는 사내는 두 손을 비비더니, 늘 하던 손을 다시 입가로 가져갔다.\n\n법정이 그의 말을 들을 수 있을 만큼 조용해지자 드파르주가 불려 나왔고, 그는 그 투옥에 관한 이야기와, 자신이 한낱 소년으로 박사의 집에서 일했던 일, 그리고 석방에 관한 이야기, 또 석방되어 자신에게 넘겨졌을 때의 죄수 상태를 빠르게 설명했다. 이어 짧은 심문이 뒤따랐으니, 법정은 일을 신속하게 처리했기 때문이다.\n\n\"바스띠유 함락 때 큰 공을 세웠소, 시민?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 생각합니다.\"\n\n이때, 흥분한 여자 하나가 군중 속에서 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"당신은 거기서 가장 훌륭한 애국자 중 하나였잖소. 왜 그렇게 말하지 않는 거요? 그날 거기서 당신은 포수였고, 그 저주받은 요새가 함락됐을 때 가장 먼저 들어간 사람들 중 하나였소. 애국자들이여, 내 말이 진실이오!\"\n\n청중의 따뜻한 칭찬 속에서 이렇게 절차를 거든 것은 라벵죙스였다. 재판장이 종을 울렸으나, 라벵죙스는 격려에 한껏 신이 나서 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"그 종 따위 두렵지 않다!\" 이에 그녀는 또한 큰 칭찬을 받았다.\n\n\"그날 바스띠유 안에서 당신이 한 일을 재판소에 알리시오, 시민.\"\n\n\"나는 알았소.\" 드파르주는 자기가 올라선 계단 아래에 서서 그를 똑바로 올려다보고 있는 아내를 내려다보며 말했다. \"내가 말하는 이 죄수가 북탑 백오호로 알려진 감방에 갇혀 있었다는 것을 나는 알았소. 그자에게서 직접 들어 알았소. 그자는 내 보살핌 아래 구두를 만들 때, 자신을 북탑 백오호 말고는 다른 어떤 이름으로도 몰랐소. 그날 대포를 다루면서, 나는 이곳이 함락되면 그 감방을 살펴보리라 결심했소. 함락되었소. 나는 배심원단의 일원인 동료 시민 하나와 함께, 간수의 안내를 받아 그 감방으로 올라갔소. 나는 그곳을 아주 면밀히 살폈소. 굴뚝의 한 구멍, 돌 하나가 빼냈다가 도로 끼워진 곳에서, 나는 글이 적힌 종이 한 장을 발견했소. 이것이 그 글이 적힌 종이요. 나는 마네뜨 박사의 필적 몇 가지를 살펴보는 것을 내 일로 삼아왔소. 이것은 마네뜨 박사의 필적이오. 나는 마네뜨 박사의 필적으로 된 이 종이를 재판장의 손에 맡기는 바이오.\"\n\n\"낭독하도록 하시오.\"\n\n죽은 듯한 정적과 고요 속에서 — 재판받는 죄수는 아내를 사랑스럽게 바라보고, 그의 아내는 오직 그에게서 눈을 떼어 근심스레 제 아버지를 바라볼 뿐이며, 마네뜨 박사는 낭독자에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 드파르주 부인은 결코 죄수에게서 눈을 떼지 않으며, 드파르주는 결코 흡족해하는 제 아내에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그곳의 다른 모든 눈은 정작 그들 가운데 누구도 보지 못하는 박사에게 골똘히 쏠려 있는 가운데 — 그 종이가 다음과 같이 낭독되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER X. The Substance of the Shadow\n\n「나, 알렉상드르 마네뜨, 보베 태생의 불행한 의사이며 후일 빠리에 거주한 자는, 1767년 마지막 달에 바스띠유의 음울한 감방에서 이 비통한 글을 쓴다. 나는 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓰고 틈틈이 몰래 이 글을 쓴다. 굴뚝의 벽 안에 숨겨둘 작정인데, 그곳에 나는 더디고 고된 노력으로 이것을 감출 자리를 마련해두었다. 나와 나의 슬픔이 한 줌 흙으로 돌아갔을 때, 어느 가엾이 여기는 손길이 그것을 거기서 발견할지도 모른다.\n\n이 글자들은, 내가 갇혀 지낸 지 열 해째 되는 해의 마지막 달에, 굴뚝에서 긁어낸 검댕과 숯가루를 피와 섞어 어렵사리 써내려가는, 녹슨 쇠끝으로 새긴 것이다. 희망은 내 가슴에서 완전히 떠나버렸다. 내가 스스로에게서 알아챈 무서운 징후들로 미루어, 내 이성이 오래 온전하게 남아 있지는 못하리라는 것을 안다. 그러나 나는 엄숙히 선언하노니, 나는 지금 이 순간 제정신을 온전히 지니고 있으며 — 내 기억은 정확하고 상세하며 — 사람들이 이 글을 언젠가 읽든 읽지 않든, 나는 영원한 심판의 자리에서 마지막으로 기록하는 이 말들에 대해 책임지듯이 진실을 쓴다.\n\n1757년 12월 셋째 주의 어느 흐린 달밤(아마 그달 22일이었던 듯하다), 나는 의과대학 거리에 있던 내 거처에서 한 시간쯤 떨어진, 쎈 강가의 인적 드문 부두를 차가운 공기로 머리를 식히려고 걷고 있었다. 그때 마차 한 대가 매우 빠르게 내 뒤를 따라왔다. 자칫하면 치일까 염려되어 그 마차를 지나가게 하려고 비켜섰을 때, 창밖으로 머리 하나가 나오더니 어떤 목소리가 마부에게 멈추라고 외쳤다.\n\n마부가 말고삐를 당길 수 있게 되자마자 마차는 멈췄고, 같은 목소리가 내 이름을 불렀다. 나는 대답했다. 그때 마차가 나보다 한참 앞서 있었던 터라, 내가 다가가기도 전에 두 신사가 문을 열고 내릴 시간이 있었다.\n\n나는 그들이 둘 다 망토를 두르고 있으며 신분을 감추려는 듯한 것을 알아챘다. 그들이 마차 문 근처에 나란히 섰을 때, 나는 또한 그들이 둘 다 나와 비슷한 나이로, 아니 오히려 더 젊어 보였으며, 체격과 거동, 목소리, 그리고 (내가 볼 수 있는 한) 얼굴까지 무척 닮았다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n'당신이 마네뜨 의사요?' 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n'그렇습니다.'\n'전에 보베에 살던 마네뜨 의사 말이오.' 다른 사람이 말했다. '본래 뛰어난 외과의였다가 근래 한두 해 사이에 빠리에서 명성이 높아진 젊은 의사 말이오?'\n\n'두 분.' 나는 대답했다. '제가 바로 두 분께서 그토록 정중하게 말씀하시는 그 마네뜨 의사입니다.'\n\n'우리는 당신 거처에 갔었소.' 첫번째 사람이 말했다. '그러나 운이 좋지 못해 거기서 당신을 만나지 못했고, 당신이 아마 이쪽으로 산책을 갔을 거라는 말을 듣고서, 당신을 따라잡을까 하여 뒤쫓아왔소. 마차에 좀 오르시겠소?'\n\n두 사람의 태도는 거만했고, 이 말이 오가는 동안 둘 다 나를 자신들과 마차 문 사이에 두도록 움직였다. 그들은 무장하고 있었다. 나는 그렇지 못했다.\n\n'두 분.' 내가 말했다. '실례합니다만, 저는 으레 누가 영광스럽게도 제 도움을 청하는지, 그리고 제가 불려가는 환자의 상태가 어떤 것인지 여쭙곤 합니다.'\n\n이에 대한 대답은 두번째로 말했던 사람이 했다. '의사 양반, 당신의 의뢰인은 지체 높은 사람들이오. 환자의 상태에 관해서는, 당신의 솜씨를 믿는 우리로서는 우리가 설명하는 것보다 당신 스스로 더 잘 알아낼 거라고 확신하오. 그것으로 충분하오. 마차에 좀 오르시겠소?'\n\n나는 따르는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었고, 잠자코 마차에 올랐다. 두 사람도 내 뒤를 따라 올라탔는데 — 마지막 사람은 발판을 거둬들인 뒤 뛰어들었다. 마차는 방향을 돌려 아까의 속도로 달려갔다.\n\n나는 이 대화를 일어난 그대로 정확히 옮겨 적는다. 한마디 한마디가 그대로라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다. 나는 모든 일을 일어난 그대로 정확히 묘사하며, 내 정신이 이 일에서 벗어나지 않도록 다잡는다. 여기 이어지는 끊어진 표시를 해둘 때, 나는 한동안 글쓰기를 멈추고 종이를 숨긴 곳에 넣어둔다.\n\n마차는 거리를 뒤로하고, 북쪽 관문을 지나, 시골길로 나섰다. 관문에서 삼분의 이 리외쯤 떨어진 곳에서 — 당시에는 거리를 가늠하지 못했으나 후일 그 길을 지날 때 알았다 — 마차는 큰길에서 벗어나, 이윽고 외딴집 앞에 멈췄다. 우리 셋은 모두 내려, 버려진 분수가 넘쳐흐른 정원의 축축하고 무른 오솔길을 따라 집 문 앞까지 걸어갔다. 종을 울렸으나 곧바로 문이 열리지 않았고, 나를 데려온 두 사람 중 하나가 문을 연 남자의 얼굴을 두꺼운 승마용 장갑으로 후려쳤다.\n\n이 행동에는 내 특별한 주의를 끌 만한 것이 없었으니, 나는 평민들이 개보다 더 예사로 얻어맞는 것을 보아왔기 때문이다. 그러나 다른 한 사람도 마찬가지로 화가 났는지 같은 식으로 팔을 휘둘러 그 남자를 쳤다. 그때 두 형제의 표정과 거동이 어찌나 똑같던지, 나는 비로소 그들이 쌍둥이 형제임을 알아챘다.\n\n바깥문(잠겨 있던 것을 형제 중 하나가 우리를 들이려고 열었다가 다시 잠갔다)에서 내린 때부터, 나는 위층 방에서 새어 나오는 비명을 듣고 있었다. 나는 곧장 그 방으로 안내되었고, 계단을 올라갈수록 비명이 더 커졌으며, 나는 침대에 누운 채 뇌에 고열을 앓는 환자를 발견했다.\n\n환자는 무척 아름답고 젊은 여인으로, 분명 스무 살을 크게 넘기지 않았다. 머리카락은 쥐어뜯겨 헝클어져 있었고, 두 팔은 띠와 손수건으로 양옆에 묶여 있었다. 나는 이 묶은 것들이 모두 어느 신사의 의복 일부임을 알아챘다. 그중 하나는 예식용 의상에 두르는 술 달린 스카프였는데, 거기서 나는 어느 귀족의 문장(紋章)과 E라는 글자를 보았다.\n\n나는 환자를 살피기 시작한 지 한 일 분도 안 되어 이것을 보았다. 그녀가 불안하게 몸부림치다가 침대 가장자리에서 엎드린 자세로 돌아누우면서 스카프 끝을 입 안으로 끌어들여 질식할 위험에 처해 있었기 때문이다. 내가 맨 처음 한 일은 그녀의 숨을 틔워주려고 손을 뻗은 것이었고, 스카프를 치우다가 그 한쪽 귀퉁이의 자수가 내 눈에 들어왔다.\n\n나는 그녀를 가만히 돌려 눕히고, 진정시켜 누워 있게 하려고 그녀의 가슴에 두 손을 얹고서 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그녀의 눈은 크게 벌어진 채 사납게 번득였고, 그녀는 쉴 새 없이 날카로운 비명을 지르며 '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 말을 되풀이하더니, 열둘까지 세고는 '쉿!' 하고 말했다. 그녀는 한순간, 그 이상은 아니게, 귀를 기울이려고 멈췄다가 다시 날카로운 비명을 지르기 시작했고, '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 외침을 되풀이하더니, 열둘까지 세고는 '쉿!' 하고 말했다. 그 순서나 방식에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 규칙적인 한순간의 멈춤 말고는, 이 소리들의 발설에 끊김이 없었다.\n\n'이게 얼마나 오래되었습니까?' 나는 물었다.\n\n두 형제를 구별하기 위해, 나는 그들을 형과 동생이라 부르겠다. 형이라 함은 가장 권위를 휘두른 자를 뜻한다. 대답한 것은 형이었다. '어젯밤 이맘때쯤부터요.'\n\n'이 여인에게 남편과 아버지와 오라비가 있습니까?'\n\n'오라비요.'\n\n'제가 그 오라비분께 말씀드리는 게 아니지요?'\n\n그는 몹시 경멸하는 투로 대답했다. '아니오.'\n\n'이 여인이 최근에 열둘이라는 숫자와 관련된 무슨 일을 겪었습니까?'\n\n동생이 짜증스럽게 받아쳤다. '열두시 말이오?'\n\n'보십시오, 두 분.' 여전히 그녀의 가슴에 손을 얹은 채 내가 말했다. '두 분께서 저를 이렇게 데려오시니 제가 얼마나 쓸모없겠습니까! 제가 무엇을 보러 오는지 알았더라면 채비를 갖추고 왔을 겁니다. 이래서는 시간만 허비하게 됩니다. 이 외딴곳에서는 약을 구할 수가 없습니다.'\n\n형이 동생을 바라보자, 동생이 거만하게 말했다. '여기 약상자가 하나 있소.' 그러고는 벽장에서 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n나는 병 몇 개를 열어 냄새를 맡고, 마개를 입술에 대보았다. 그 자체로 독이나 다름없는 마취제 말고 다른 무엇을 쓰려 했다면, 나는 그것들 중 어느 것도 쓰지 않았을 것이다.\n\n'그것들을 의심하시오?' 동생이 물었다.\n\n'보시다시피, 무슈, 저는 이것들을 쓰려는 참입니다.' 나는 대답하고, 더는 말하지 않았다.\n\n나는 무척 애를 쓰며 여러 차례 시도한 끝에, 내가 주고자 한 약을 환자에게 삼키게 했다. 얼마 후 다시 먹일 작정이었고, 그 약효를 지켜볼 필요가 있었으므로, 나는 그러고 나서 침대 곁에 앉았다. 시중드는 여자(아래층 남자의 아내)가 하나 있었는데, 소심하고 주눅 든 그녀는 한쪽 구석으로 물러나 있었다. 집은 축축하고 낡았으며, 가구도 변변치 않게 갖춰져 있어 — 누가 봐도 최근에 사람이 들어와 임시로 쓰는 곳이었다. 비명 소리를 죽이려고 두꺼운 낡은 휘장 몇 개를 창문 앞에 못으로 박아두었다. 비명은 여전히 규칙적으로 이어졌으니, '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 외침과, 열둘까지 세는 것과, '쉿!'이 되풀이되었다. 광란이 어찌나 격렬한지 나는 팔을 묶은 붕대를 풀지 못했다. 다만 그것이 아프지 않도록 살펴는 두었다. 이 환자에게서 유일하게 희망의 불씨가 된 것은, 고통받는 그녀의 가슴에 얹은 내 손이 한 번에 몇 분씩 그 몸을 진정시키는, 그만한 달래는 효력이 있다는 점이었다. 그것은 비명에는 아무런 효과가 없었으니, 어떤 추도 그보다 규칙적일 수는 없었다.\n\n내 손이 이러한 효력이 있는 까닭에(나는 그리 짐작한다), 나는 두 형제가 지켜보는 가운데 반시간 동안 침대 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그제야 형이 입을 열었다.\n\n'환자가 또 하나 있소.'\n\n나는 흠칫 놀라 물었다. '급한 경우입니까?'\n\n'직접 보는 게 낫겠소.' 그는 아무렇지 않게 대답하고는, 등불을 집어 들었다.\n\n또 하나의 환자는 두번째 계단 건너편의 뒷방에 누워 있었는데, 그곳은 마구간 위의 다락 같은 곳이었다. 그 일부에는 회칠한 낮은 천장이 있었고, 나머지는 기와지붕의 용마루까지 트여 있었으며, 가로질러 들보가 놓여 있었다. 그곳 한쪽에는 건초와 짚이 쌓여 있었고, 땔감용 장작단과, 모래 속에 묻어둔 사과 더미가 있었다. 다른 환자에게 가려면 나는 그 부분을 가로질러야 했다. 내 기억은 상세하며 흔들림이 없다. 나는 이 세부들로 그것을 시험해보거니와, 내가 갇혀 지낸 지 열 해째가 끝나갈 무렵 이 바스띠유의 감방에서, 그날 밤 보았던 것들을 모두 다시 본다.\n\n바닥에 깔린 건초 위에, 머리 밑에 쿠션을 받친 채, 잘생긴 농민 소년 하나가 누워 있었으니 — 많아야 열일곱을 넘지 않은 소년이었다. 그는 등을 대고 누워, 이를 악물고, 오른손을 가슴에 움켜쥔 채, 부릅뜬 눈으로 곧장 위쪽을 올려다보고 있었다. 나는 그 위에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 앉아서도 상처가 어디인지 알 수 없었으나, 그가 날카로운 것에 찔린 상처로 죽어가고 있다는 것은 알 수 있었다.\n\n'나는 의사란다, 가엾은 녀석.' 내가 말했다. '내가 상처를 좀 보자꾸나.'\n\n'보이고 싶지 않아요.' 그가 대답했다. '그냥 두세요.'\n\n상처는 그의 손 밑에 있었고, 나는 그를 달래어 그 손을 치우게 했다. 상처는 스무 시간에서 스물네 시간 전쯤에 입은 칼에 찔린 자국이었는데, 지체 없이 손을 썼더라도 어떤 솜씨로도 그를 구하지는 못했을 것이다. 그때 그는 빠르게 죽어가고 있었다. 내가 형에게로 눈길을 돌렸을 때, 나는 그가 생명이 꺼져가는 이 잘생긴 소년을, 마치 상처 입은 새나 산토끼나 집토끼라도 되는 듯이 — 결코 같은 인간이라고는 여기지 않는 듯이 내려다보고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n'무슈, 어쩌다 이렇게 되었습니까?' 내가 말했다.\n\n'미친 천한 강아지 새끼! 농노 놈이! 내 동생이 칼을 뽑게 만들더니, 신사답게 — 내 동생의 칼에 쓰러진 거요.'\n\n이 대답에는 연민이나 슬픔, 혹은 같은 인간으로서의 인정이라곤 한 점도 없었다. 말하는 자는 저 다른 부류의 생물이 거기서 죽어가는 것이 성가신 일임을 시인하는 듯했고, 그 해충 같은 족속이 늘 그러듯 이름 없이 죽어갔더라면 더 나았으리라는 투였다. 그는 그 소년이나 그 운명에 대해 어떤 동정심도 느낄 능력이 전혀 없었다.\n\n그가 말하는 동안 소년의 눈은 천천히 그에게로 옮겨 갔고, 이제 천천히 나에게로 옮겨 왔다.\n\n'의사 선생님, 이 귀족들은 무척 거만해요. 하지만 우리 천한 강아지들도 때로는 자존심이 있어요. 저들은 우리를 약탈하고, 능욕하고, 때리고, 죽이지만, 우리에게도 때로는 작은 자존심이 남아 있어요. 누이를 — 선생님, 누이를 보셨어요?'\n\n거리 탓에 잦아들기는 했어도 비명과 외침은 거기서도 들렸다. 그는 마치 그녀가 우리 앞에 누워 있기라도 한 듯 그 소리들을 가리켰다.\n\n나는 말했다. '보았단다.'\n\n'제 누이예요, 선생님. 이 귀족들은 여러 해 동안 우리 누이들의 정숙함과 정절을 두고 그 수치스러운 권리를 누려왔지만, 우리 중에도 착한 처녀들이 있었어요. 저는 알아요, 아버지가 그렇게 말씀하시는 것도 들었고요. 누이는 착한 처녀였어요. 좋은 청년과 약혼도 했고요. 저 사람의 소작인이었지요. 우리는 모두 저 사람의 — 저기 서 있는 저 사람의 소작인이었어요. 다른 한 사람은 그 동생인데, 나쁜 족속 중에서도 가장 나쁜 자예요.'\n\n소년이 말할 기력을 몸에서 그러모으는 것은 더없이 힘겨운 일이었으나, 그의 정신은 무서운 힘을 실어 말했다.\n\n'우리는 저기 서 있는 저 사람한테 어찌나 수탈당했는지, 우리 천한 강아지들이 다 그 잘난 존재들한테 당하듯이 말이에요 — 저 사람한테 사정없이 세금을 뜯기고, 삯도 없이 그를 위해 일해야 했고, 우리 곡식을 그의 방앗간에서 빻아야 했고, 우리의 보잘것없는 수확으로 그가 키우는 길든 새 수십 마리를 먹여야 했고, 우리 자신은 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 길든 새 한 마리조차 기르는 게 금지되었어요. 어쩌다 고깃점이라도 생기면 그의 사람들이 보고 빼앗아 갈까봐 문을 걸어 잠그고 덧창을 닫은 채 두려움에 떨며 먹어야 할 만큼 약탈당하고 노략질당했어요 — 말하자면, 우리는 어찌나 수탈당하고 쫓기고 가난해졌는지, 아버지는 아이를 세상에 낳는 것이 끔찍한 일이라고, 우리가 무엇보다 빌어야 할 것은 우리 여자들이 아이를 못 배고 우리 비참한 족속이 절멸하는 것이라고 말씀하셨어요!'\n\n나는 억압받는다는 느낌이 불길처럼 터져 나오는 것을 그전에는 본 적이 없었다. 그것이 사람들 어딘가에 잠재해 있으리라고는 짐작했으나, 그것이 터져 나오는 것은, 죽어가는 그 소년에게서 보기 전까지는 본 적이 없었다.\n\n'그래도 선생님, 제 누이는 결혼했어요. 그 가엾은 사람은 그때 병을 앓고 있었는데, 누이는 자기 연인과 결혼해서 우리 오두막에서 — 저 사람 같으면 개집이라 부를 그곳에서 — 그를 돌보고 위로해주려 했어요. 결혼한 지 몇 주 되지도 않았는데, 저 사람의 동생이 누이를 보고 탐을 내더니, 저 사람한테 누이를 자기에게 빌려달라고 했어요 — 우리들 사이에서 남편이 무슨 대수겠어요! 저 사람은 기꺼이 응했지만, 제 누이는 착하고 정숙했으며, 저 사람의 동생을 저만큼이나 강하게 미워했어요. 그러자 그 두 사람은 누이의 남편을 부추겨 누이에게 영향력을 행사하게 해서, 누이가 순순히 따르도록 만들려고 무슨 짓을 했을까요?'\n\n내 눈에 박혀 있던 소년의 눈이 천천히 그 구경꾼에게로 돌아갔고, 나는 그 두 얼굴에서 그가 한 말이 모두 진실임을 보았다. 서로 맞선 두 종류의 자존심이 마주하고 있는 것을, 나는 이 바스띠유에서조차 볼 수 있다. 신사의 자존심은 온통 무심한 무관심이었고, 농민의 자존심은 온통 짓밟힌 감정과 격렬한 복수심이었다.\n\n'선생님도 아시다시피, 이 귀족들의 권리 중에는 우리 천한 강아지들을 수레에 매어 몰고 다니는 것도 있어요. 저들은 그렇게 그를 수레에 매어 몰았어요. 또 저들의 고귀한 잠이 방해받지 않도록, 밤새 우리를 자기네 영지에 붙들어두고 개구리를 조용히 시키게 하는 것도 그 권리 중 하나라는 걸 아시잖아요. 저들은 밤이면 그를 해로운 안개 속에 붙들어두고, 낮이면 다시 멍에를 메라고 명령했어요. 하지만 그는 설복당하지 않았어요. 아니에요! 어느 날 정오에 끼니를 — 먹을 것을 구할 수 있다면 — 먹으라고 멍에에서 풀려났을 때, 그는 종이 울릴 때마다 한 번씩, 열두 번을 흐느끼고는 누이의 품에서 죽었어요.'\n\n자신이 당한 부당함을 모두 털어놓겠다는 결심 말고는 어떤 인간적인 것도 그 소년에게 생명을 붙들어둘 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 움켜쥔 오른손을 그대로 움켜쥔 채 상처를 덮고 있게 하듯이, 몰려드는 죽음의 그림자를 밀어냈다.\n\n'그러고 나서, 저 사람의 허락하에, 아니 거들기까지 하는 가운데, 저 사람의 동생이 누이를 데려갔어요 — 누이가 그 동생에게 틀림없이 말했을 것임을 제가 아는데도요 — 그게 무엇인지는, 선생님이 지금 모르신다 해도 머지않아 아시게 될 거예요 — 저 사람의 동생은 누이를 데려갔어요 — 잠시 동안, 자기 쾌락과 소일거리로요. 저는 길에서 누이가 제 곁을 지나가는 것을 보았어요. 제가 그 소식을 집에 전했을 때, 아버지의 심장은 터져버렸어요. 그 심장에 가득 찬 말을 아버지는 한마디도 하지 못하셨어요. 저는 어린 누이를(또 하나가 있거든요) 이 사람의 손이 닿지 않는, 그리고 적어도 결코 그자의 종이 되지는 않을 곳으로 데려갔어요. 그러고 나서, 저는 그 동생을 여기까지 뒤쫓아, 어젯밤에 기어 올라왔어요 — 천한 강아지지만, 칼을 손에 들고요. 다락 창이 어디예요? 여기 어디쯤이었는데?'\n\n방이 그의 시야에서 어두워지고 있었고, 세계가 그의 주위로 좁아들고 있었다. 나는 둘레를 둘러보았고, 건초와 짚이 바닥 여기저기 밟혀 흩어져 있는 것이, 마치 몸싸움이라도 있었던 듯한 것을 보았다.\n\n'누이가 제 소리를 듣고 달려 들어왔어요. 저는 그가 죽기 전에는 우리 가까이 오지 말라고 일렀어요. 그가 들어와서는 먼저 제게 동전 몇 닢을 던지더니, 채찍으로 저를 후려쳤어요. 하지만 저는, 천한 강아지일망정, 그가 칼을 뽑게 만들 만큼 그를 후려쳤어요. 제 천한 피로 더럽힌 그 칼이, 산산조각 나든 말든 마음대로 하라죠. 그는 제 몸을 지키려고 칼을 뽑았어요 — 제 목숨을 노리고 온 솜씨를 다해 저를 찔렀어요.'\n\n내 눈길은 불과 조금 전에, 건초 사이에 놓인 부러진 칼의 조각들에 가닿은 적이 있었다. 그 무기는 신사의 것이었다. 다른 자리에는, 군인의 것이었던 듯한 낡은 칼 한 자루가 놓여 있었다.\n\n'이제 저를 일으켜주세요, 선생님. 저를 일으켜주세요. 그자는 어디 있어요?'\n\n'그는 여기 없단다.' 나는 그 소년을 부축하며, 그가 동생을 두고 말하는 줄로 여기며 말했다.\n\n'그자요! 이 귀족들이 아무리 거만해도, 그자는 저를 보기가 두려운 거예요. 여기 있던 사람은 어디 있어요? 제 얼굴을 그쪽으로 돌려주세요.'\n\n나는 그리해주었고, 소년의 머리를 내 무릎에 기대게 했다. 그러나 한순간 비상한 힘을 얻은 그는 완전히 몸을 일으켰다. 나도 따라 일어서지 않을 수 없게 만들었으니, 그러지 않았다면 나는 그를 계속 부축할 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n'후작.' 소년은 눈을 크게 뜨고 오른손을 치켜든 채 그를 향해 말했다. '이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날이 오면, 나는 당신과 당신의 일족을, 당신의 나쁜 족속이 마지막 하나까지, 그 책임을 물으려 불러내겠소. 내가 그리한다는 표시로, 나는 당신에게 이 피의 십자가를 새기오. 이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날이 오면, 나는 당신의 동생을, 그 나쁜 족속 중에서도 가장 나쁜 자를, 따로 그 책임을 물으려 불러내겠소. 내가 그리한다는 표시로, 나는 그에게 이 피의 십자가를 새기오.'\n\n두 번, 그는 가슴의 상처에 손을 댔다가, 집게손가락으로 허공에 십자가를 그었다. 그는 손가락을 아직 치켜든 채 한순간 서 있었고, 그것이 떨어지자 그도 함께 쓰러졌으며, 나는 죽은 그를 뉘어주었다.\n\n내가 그 젊은 여인의 침대 곁으로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그녀가 정확히 똑같은 순서로 이어가며 헛소리하고 있는 것을 보았다. 나는 이것이 여러 시간 이어질 수 있으며, 아마 무덤의 침묵으로 끝나리라는 것을 알았다.\n\n나는 그녀에게 주었던 약을 다시 먹였고, 밤이 깊도록 침대 곁에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 비명의 날카로움을 결코 누그러뜨리지 않았고, 그 말들의 또렷함이나 순서에서 결코 더듬거리지 않았다. 그것은 언제나 '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비! 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯, 일곱, 여덟, 아홉, 열, 열하나, 열둘. 쉿!'이었다.\n\n이것은 내가 그녀를 처음 본 때로부터 스물여섯 시간 동안 이어졌다. 나는 두 번 오갔으며, 다시 그녀 곁에 앉아 있을 때, 그녀가 차츰 약해지기 시작했다. 나는 그 고비를 거들기 위해 할 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 일을 했고, 이윽고 그녀는 혼수상태에 빠져 죽은 사람처럼 누워 있었다.\n\n마치 길고 무서운 폭풍이 지난 끝에 바람과 비가 마침내 잦아든 것 같았다. 나는 그녀의 팔을 풀어주고, 여자를 불러 그녀의 몸가짐과 그녀가 찢어놓은 옷을 추스르는 것을 돕게 했다. 바로 그때 나는 그녀의 상태가, 어머니가 되리라는 첫 기대가 막 생겨난 여인의 것임을 알았다. 그리고 바로 그때 나는 그녀에게 품었던 얼마 안 되는 희망마저 잃었다.\n\n'죽었소?' 후작이 — 나는 그를 여전히 형이라 일컫겠다 — 말을 타고 와서 부츠를 신은 채 방으로 들어서며 물었다.\n\n'죽지는 않았습니다.' 내가 말했다. '하지만 죽을 것 같습니다.'\n\n'이 천한 몸뚱이에 무슨 그런 기력이 있담!' 그는 얼마간 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 내려다보며 말했다.\n\n'슬픔과 절망에는.' 나는 그에게 대답했다. '놀라운 기력이 있지요.'\n\n그는 처음에는 내 말에 웃더니, 이내 그 말에 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 그는 발로 의자를 내 곁으로 끌어당기고, 여자에게 물러가라 명한 뒤, 낮춘 목소리로 말했다.\n\n'의사 양반, 내 동생이 이 촌놈들 때문에 이런 곤란을 겪는 것을 알고서, 나는 당신의 도움을 청하라고 권했소. 당신의 명성은 높고, 또 앞날을 일궈가야 할 젊은이로서 당신은 아마 자기 이익에 마음을 쓰겠지. 여기서 당신이 본 것들은, 보기만 하고 입에 담아서는 안 될 것들이오.'\n\n나는 환자의 숨소리에 귀를 기울이며, 대답을 피했다.\n\n'내 말에 주의를 기울여주시겠소, 의사 양반?'\n\n'무슈.' 내가 말했다. '제 직업에서, 환자의 이야기는 언제나 비밀로 받아들여집니다.' 나는 내가 듣고 본 것으로 마음이 어지러웠으므로, 대답에 신중을 기했다.\n\n그녀의 숨은 너무도 미약하여 좇기 어려웠으므로, 나는 조심스럽게 맥박과 심장을 짚어보았다. 생명이 있기는 했으나, 그 이상은 아니었다. 자리에 다시 앉으며 둘레를 둘러보았을 때, 나는 두 형제가 나를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n나는 너무도 힘겹게 이 글을 쓰고 있으며, 추위는 너무도 혹독하고, 발각되어 지하 감방의 완전한 어둠 속에 처박힐까봐 너무도 두려운 나머지, 이 이야기를 줄여야겠다. 내 기억에는 아무런 혼란도 결함도 없으니, 나와 그 형제들 사이에 오간 한마디 한마디를 떠올릴 수 있고, 또 낱낱이 적어낼 수도 있다.\n\n그녀는 일주일을 더 버텼다. 마지막에 이르러, 나는 그녀의 입술에 귀를 바싹 대고서 그녀가 내게 하는 몇 음절을 알아들을 수 있었다. 그녀는 내게 자기가 어디에 있는지 물었고, 나는 일러주었다. 내가 누구인지 물었고, 나는 일러주었다. 그녀에게 가문의 이름을 물어본 것은 헛일이었다. 그녀는 베개에 머리를 댄 채 희미하게 가로저으며, 소년이 그러했듯이 자기 비밀을 지켰다.\n\n내가 형제들에게 그녀가 빠르게 가라앉고 있으며 하루도 더 살지 못할 것이라고 말하기 전까지는, 그녀에게 어떤 질문도 할 기회가 없었다. 그때까지는, 그녀의 의식에 떠오른 사람이라곤 그 여자와 나뿐이었음에도, 내가 거기 있을 때면 그 둘 중 하나가 늘 시샘하듯 침대 머리맡의 휘장 뒤에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 그 지경에 이르자, 그들은 내가 그녀와 무슨 이야기를 나누든 개의치 않는 듯했으니, 마치 — 이런 생각이 내 머리를 스쳤다 — 나 또한 죽어가고 있다는 듯이.\n\n나는 그들의 자존심이, 동생(내가 그렇게 부르는 자)이 농민과, 그것도 소년인 농민과 칼을 맞댔다는 사실을 쓰라리게 분개한다는 것을 늘 느꼈다. 그 둘 중 어느 쪽의 마음에든 영향을 미치는 것으로 보인 유일한 고려는, 이것이 가문에 몹시 수치스러운 일이며 가소로운 일이라는 고려뿐이었다. 동생의 눈과 마주칠 때마다, 그 표정은 내가 소년에게서 알게 된 것을 알고 있다는 이유로 그가 나를 깊이 싫어한다는 것을 일깨워주었다. 그는 형보다 내게 더 부드럽고 더 공손했으나, 나는 이것을 보았다. 나는 또한 형의 마음에도 내가 거추장스러운 존재임을 보았다.\n\n내 환자는 자정 두 시간 전에 죽었다 — 내 시계로는, 내가 그녀를 처음 본 순간과 거의 분 단위까지 일치하는 때였다. 그녀의 처량한 젊은 머리가 한쪽으로 가만히 떨구어지며 그녀의 이승의 모든 부당함과 슬픔이 끝났을 때, 나는 그녀와 단둘이 있었다.\n\n형제들은 아래층 방에서 어서 말을 타고 떠나고 싶어 안달하며 기다리고 있었다. 나는 침대 곁에 홀로 있으면서, 그들이 승마용 채찍으로 부츠를 치며 이리저리 서성이는 소리를 들었다.\n\n'마침내 죽었소?' 내가 들어가자 형이 말했다.\n\n'죽었습니다.' 내가 말했다.\n\n'축하하네, 아우.' 그가 돌아서며 한 말이었다.\n\n그는 앞서 내게 돈을 내밀었으나, 나는 받기를 미뤘었다. 그가 이제 금화 한 두루마리를 주었다. 나는 그의 손에서 그것을 받았으나, 탁자 위에 내려놓았다. 나는 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해보았고, 아무것도 받지 않기로 마음먹었던 것이다.\n\n'부디 양해해주십시오.' 내가 말했다. '이런 사정에서는, 받지 않겠습니다.'\n\n그들은 서로 눈길을 주고받았으나, 내가 그들에게 고개를 숙이자 그들도 내게 고개를 숙였고, 우리는 어느 쪽도 더는 한마디 없이 헤어졌다.\n\n나는 지쳤다, 지쳤다, 지쳤다 — 비참함에 짓눌려 기진했다. 이 수척한 손으로 쓴 것을 나는 읽어낼 수가 없다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침, 금화 두루마리가 겉에 내 이름이 적힌 작은 상자에 담겨 내 집 문 앞에 놓여 있었다. 나는 처음부터 내가 어찌해야 할지 노심초사 궁리했다. 나는 그날, 내가 불려갔던 두 환자의 사정과 내가 갔던 장소를 — 요컨대 모든 정황을 적어 장관에게 사적으로 편지를 쓰기로 결심했다. 나는 궁정의 영향력이 어떤 것인지, 귀족들의 면책특권이 어떤 것인지 알았으므로, 그 일이 결코 거론되지 않으리라 예상했다. 그러나 나는 내 마음의 짐을 덜고 싶었다. 나는 그 일을 아내에게조차 철저히 비밀로 해두었는데, 이것 또한 내 편지에 적기로 마음먹었다. 나 자신의 진짜 위험에 대해서는 아무런 염려도 없었다. 그러나 내가 지닌 지식을 다른 이들이 알게 되어 연루된다면, 그들에게 위험이 닥칠 수 있음은 의식하고 있었다.\n\n그날 나는 일이 많아, 그날 밤 편지를 끝내지 못했다. 이튿날 아침 나는 그것을 마무리하려고 평소보다 한참 일찍 일어났다. 그날은 그해의 마지막 날이었다. 막 다 쓴 편지가 내 앞에 놓여 있을 때, 어떤 부인이 나를 보고자 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받았다.\n\n나는 내가 스스로에게 부과한 일을 점점 더 감당하지 못하게 되어간다. 너무도 춥고, 너무도 어두우며, 내 감각은 너무도 무뎌졌고, 나를 짓누르는 우울은 너무도 끔찍하다.\n\n그 부인은 젊고, 매력적이며, 아름다웠으나, 오래 살 사람으로는 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 몹시 동요하고 있었다. 그녀는 자신을 쌩떼브레몽드 후작 부인이라고 소개했다. 나는 소년이 형을 부를 때 쓴 그 칭호를 스카프에 수놓인 머리글자와 연결지었고, 어렵지 않게 내가 바로 얼마 전에 그 귀족을 보았다는 결론에 이르렀다.\n\n내 기억은 여전히 정확하나, 우리가 나눈 대화의 말들을 나는 적을 수가 없다. 내가 전보다 더 면밀히 감시당하고 있는 듯하고, 언제 감시당할지 알 수 없기 때문이다. 그녀는 그 잔혹한 이야기의 주요 사실들을, 그리고 거기에 자기 남편이 가담한 것과 내가 불려간 것을, 일부는 짐작했고 일부는 알아냈다. 그녀는 그 처녀가 죽은 것은 몰랐다. 그녀의 바람은, 몹시 괴로워하며 말하기를, 은밀히 그 처녀에게 한 여인의 동정을 보이려는 것이었다고 했다. 그녀의 바람은, 고통받는 많은 이들에게 오랫동안 미움받아온 한 가문에서 하늘의 진노를 돌려놓으려는 것이었다.\n\n그녀에게는 살아 있는 어린 누이가 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었고, 그녀의 가장 큰 소망은 그 누이를 돕는 것이었다. 나는 그런 누이가 있다는 것 말고는 그녀에게 아무것도 일러줄 수 없었다. 그 이상은 나도 아는 것이 없었다. 내 신의를 믿고 나를 찾아온 그녀의 동기는, 내가 그 이름과 거처를 일러줄 수 있으리라는 희망이었다. 그러나 이 비참한 순간까지도 나는 그 둘 다를 알지 못한다.\n\n이 종이 쪽들이 내게서 동난다. 어제는 경고와 함께 한 장을 빼앗겼다. 나는 오늘 내 기록을 끝내야 한다.\n\n그녀는 착하고, 자비로운 부인이었으며, 결혼생활은 행복하지 못했다. 어찌 행복할 수 있었겠는가! 그 동생은 그녀를 못 미더워하고 싫어했으며, 그의 영향력은 온통 그녀에게 맞섰다. 그녀는 그를 두려워했고, 자기 남편 또한 두려워했다. 내가 그녀를 부축해 문까지 내려가니, 그녀의 마차 안에 아이가, 두세 살쯤 된 예쁜 사내아이가 있었다.\n\n'이 아이를 위해서, 선생님.' 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 아이를 가리키며 말했다. '저는 제가 할 수 있는 보잘것없는 속죄를 하기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하겠어요. 그러지 않으면 이 아이는 결코 그 상속의 복을 누리지 못할 거예요. 이 일에 대해 다른 어떤 죄 없는 속죄도 이루어지지 않는다면, 언젠가 이 아이에게 그 대가가 요구되리라는 예감이 들어요. 제 것이라 부를 수 있는 것이라곤 — 보석 몇 점의 값어치를 조금 넘을 뿐이지만 — 만약 그 누이를 찾을 수 있다면, 저는 이 아이가 죽은 어미의 연민과 비탄을 담아 그 상처 입은 가문에 그것을 베푸는 일을, 이 아이 삶의 첫번째 의무로 삼게 하겠어요.'\n\n그녀는 아이에게 입을 맞추고, 그를 어루만지며 말했다. '이건 다 너 자신을 위한 거란다. 너는 신의를 지키겠지, 어린 샤를?' 아이는 의젓하게 대답했다. '네!' 나는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그녀는 아이를 품에 안고서 그를 어루만지며 떠나갔다. 나는 그녀를 다시는 보지 못했다.\n\n그녀가 내가 자기 남편의 이름을 안다고 믿고 그 이름을 입에 올렸으므로, 나는 그 이름에 대한 언급을 내 편지에 덧붙이지 않았다. 나는 편지를 봉했고, 그것을 내 손에서 내놓기가 미덥지 않아, 그날 직접 전했다.\n\n그날 밤, 그해의 마지막 밤, 아홉시쯤에, 검은 옷을 입은 남자가 내 집 대문에서 종을 울려 나를 만나기를 청하고는, 젊은이인 내 하인 에르네스뜨 드파르주를 살그머니 따라 위층으로 올라왔다. 내 하인이 내가 아내와 함께 앉아 있던 방으로 들어왔을 때 — 오, 내 마음의 사랑하는 아내여! 나의 곱고 젊은 영국인 아내여! — 우리는 대문에 있는 줄 알았던 그 남자가 하인 뒤에 말없이 서 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n쌩또노레 거리에 급한 환자가 있다고 그는 말했다. 오래 붙들지 않을 것이며, 마차를 대기시켜두었다고 했다.\n\n그것이 나를 이곳으로 데려왔고, 나를 내 무덤으로 데려왔다. 내가 집을 벗어나자, 뒤에서 검은 천이 내 입을 단단히 틀어막았고, 두 팔이 묶였다. 두 형제가 어두운 모퉁이에서 길을 건너와, 단 한 번의 몸짓으로 나를 지목했다. 후작은 주머니에서 내가 쓴 편지를 꺼내 내게 보이고는, 누군가 들고 있던 등불의 불빛에 그것을 태운 뒤, 발로 그 재를 비벼 껐다. 한마디도 오가지 않았다. 나는 이곳으로 끌려왔고, 나는 산 채로 묻힌 내 무덤으로 끌려왔다.\n\n이 모든 끔찍한 세월 동안, 만약 하느님께서 그 형제 중 어느 쪽의 모진 마음에라도 나의 가장 사랑하는 아내의 소식을 — 살았는지 죽었는지 한마디로나마 알게 해줄 만큼이라도 — 내게 전하고자 하는 뜻을 심어주셨더라면, 나는 그분께서 그들을 아주 저버리지는 않으셨다고 여겼을지도 모른다. 그러나 이제 나는 그 붉은 십자가의 표지가 그들에게 치명적이며, 그들에게는 그분의 자비에 들 몫이 없다고 믿는다. 그리고 그들과 그들의 자손을, 그 족속의 마지막 하나에 이르기까지, 나, 알렉상드르 마네뜨, 불행한 죄수는, 1767년의 이 마지막 밤에, 견딜 수 없는 고통 속에서, 이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날을 향해 고발한다. 나는 그들을 하늘과 땅에 고발한다.」\n\n이 문서의 낭독이 끝나자 무시무시한 소리가 일었다. 갈망하고 열망하는, 피 말고는 또렷한 것이라곤 하나도 담기지 않은 소리였다. 이 이야기는 그 시대의 가장 복수심에 찬 격정을 불러일으켰으니, 온 나라에서 그 앞에 떨궈지지 않을 머리는 하나도 없었을 것이다.\n\n그 법정과 그 방청객 앞에서, 드파르주 부부가 노획한 다른 바스띠유 기록물들과 함께 행렬에 실려 나온 이 문서를 공개하지 않고, 때를 기다리며 간직해두었던 까닭을 굳이 밝힐 필요는 거의 없었다. 이 가증스러운 가문의 이름이 오랫동안 쌩땅뚜안의 저주를 받아왔으며, 운명의 명부에 새겨져 있었다는 것을 굳이 밝힐 필요도 거의 없었다. 그러한 고발에 맞서 그날 그 자리에서 그를 지탱해줄 만한 미덕과 공덕을 갖춘 자란 이 세상에 없었다.\n\n게다가 그 운명 지어진 사내에게 더욱 고약했던 것은, 고발한 자가 잘 알려진 시민이자 그 자신의 헌신적인 벗이며 그의 아내의 아버지라는 점이었다. 민중의 광적인 열망 가운데 하나는, 미심쩍은 옛 시대의 공적 미덕을 본뜨는 것이었고, 민중의 제단에 바치는 희생과 자기 봉헌이었다. 그리하여 재판장이 (그러지 않았다면 그 자신의 머리가 어깨 위에서 떨렸을 것이다) 공화국의 훌륭한 의사는 가증스러운 한 귀족 가문을 뿌리 뽑음으로써 공화국에 더욱더 공헌하게 될 것이며, 자기 딸을 과부로, 그 손주를 고아로 만드는 데서 분명 신성한 기쁨과 환희를 느끼리라고 말하자, 격렬한 흥분과 애국적 열기가 일었으니, 인간적 동정이라곤 한 점도 없었다.\n\n\"그 의사, 자기 주위에 영향력이 대단하지?\" 드파르주 부인이 복수의 여신을 향해 미소 지으며 속삭였다. \"이제 그를 구해보시지, 우리 의사 선생, 그를 구해봐!\"\n\n배심원이 한 표 한 표 던질 때마다, 함성이 일었다. 또 한 표, 또 한 표. 함성에 함성.\n\n만장일치로 가결되었다. 마음으로나 혈통으로나 귀족이며, 공화국의 적이고, 악명 높은 민중의 압제자라는 것이었다. 다시 콩시에르주리로, 그리고 스물네 시간 안에 사형!\n\n# CHAPTER XI. Dusk\n\n죽음을 선고받은 죄 없는 사내의 가련한 아내는, 그 선고가 마치 치명상이라도 입힌 듯 무너져 내렸다. 그러나 그녀는 아무 소리도 내지 않았다. 세상 모든 사람들 가운데 오직 자신만이 고통 속의 남편을 떠받쳐야 하며 그 고통을 더해서는 안 된다고 일러주는 내면의 목소리가 어찌나 강했던지, 그 충격 속에서도 그녀를 곧 일으켜 세웠다.\n\n판사들이 옥외의 공개 시위에 참가해야 했으므로, 재판정은 휴정에 들어갔다. 여러 통로를 통해 법정이 비워지는 빠르고 부산한 소음과 움직임이 채 그치기도 전에, 루시는 얼굴에 사랑과 위로밖에는 어린 것이 없는 채로 남편을 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 일어섰다.\n\n\"그이를 만질 수 있다면! 한번만 안아볼 수 있다면! 오, 선량한 시민 여러분, 우리에게 그만한 자비를 베풀어주신다면!\"\n\n남아 있는 사람이라고는 간수 하나와, 어젯밤 그를 잡아간 네 사람 가운데 둘, 그리고 바사드뿐이었다. 사람들은 모두 거리의 구경거리를 향해 쏟아져 나간 터였다. 바사드가 나머지에게 제안했다. \"그러면 안아보게 합시다. 잠깐이면 되니까.\" 사람들은 말없이 동의했고, 그들은 그녀를 홀의 좌석들 너머로 옮겨 한 단 높은 자리로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 피고석 너머로 몸을 숙여 그녀를 두 팔로 감쌀 수 있었다.\n\n\"잘 가오, 내 영혼의 사랑하는 사람. 내 사랑에게 작별의 축복을 보내오. 우리는 다시 만날 거요, 지친 자들이 안식하는 그곳에서!\"\n\n그녀를 가슴에 끌어안으며 그녀의 남편이 한 말이었다.\n\n\"견딜 수 있어요, 사랑하는 찰스. 저는 위로부터 떠받쳐지고 있어요. 저 때문에 괴로워하지 마세요. 우리 아이에게도 작별의 축복을.\"\n\n\"당신을 통해 그애에게 그것을 보내오. 당신을 통해 그애에게 입맞추오. 당신을 통해 그애에게 작별을 고하오.\"\n\n\"여보. 안돼요! 잠깐만요!\" 그가 그녀에게서 몸을 떼어내고 있었다. \"우리가 오래 떨어져 있지는 않을 거예요. 머지않아 이것이 제 가슴을 찢어놓으리라는 걸 느껴요. 하지만 할 수 있는 동안은 제 도리를 다하겠어요. 그리고 제가 그애를 떠나면, 하느님이 그애를 위해 벗들을 일으켜주실 거예요, 제게 그러셨던 것처럼.\"\n\n그녀의 아버지가 그녀를 따라왔고, 두 사람 앞에 무릎이라도 꿇으려 했으나, 다네이가 손을 내밀어 그를 붙들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"안됩니다, 안돼요! 무슨 일을 하셨기에, 무슨 일을 하셨기에 저희에게 무릎을 꿇으려 하십니까! 이제 저희는 압니다, 당신이 지난날 어떤 싸움을 치르셨는지를. 이제 저희는 압니다, 제 혈통을 의심하셨을 때, 그리고 그것을 아셨을 때 당신이 무엇을 견뎌내셨는지를. 이제 저희는 압니다, 당신이 그애를 위해 맞서 싸워 이겨내신 그 본능적인 반감을. 진심을 다해, 온 사랑과 도리를 다해 당신께 감사드립니다. 하늘이 당신과 함께하시기를!\"\n\n그녀의 아버지의 유일한 대답은 하얀 머리칼 사이로 두 손을 쓸어 넣고는, 고뇌의 비명과 함께 그 손을 비트는 것이었다.\n\n\"달리 될 수는 없었습니다.\" 죄수가 말했다. \"모든 일이 닥쳐온 그대로 맞물려 돌아갔지요. 가엾은 어머니의 부탁을 이행하려던, 늘 헛되기만 했던 그 노력이 처음으로 제 운명적인 존재를 당신 곁에 데려다놓았습니다. 그런 악에서 선이 나올 수는 없었지요, 그토록 불행한 시작에 더 행복한 끝이란 자연의 이치상 있을 수 없었습니다. 마음을 다잡으시고, 저를 용서해주십시오. 하늘이 당신을 축복하시기를!\"\n\n그가 끌려나가자, 그의 아내는 그를 놓아주고, 두 손을 맞댄 기도하는 자세로 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 그 얼굴에는 환한 빛이 어렸고, 거기에는 위로하는 듯한 미소마저 깃들어 있었다. 그가 죄수용 문으로 나가자, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 아버지의 가슴에 머리를 사랑스럽게 기대고는, 그에게 무어라 말하려다가, 그의 발치에 쓰러졌다.\n\n그러자, 한 번도 움직이지 않았던 그 어두운 구석에서 나온 시드니 카턴이 다가와 그녀를 안아 일으켰다. 그녀 곁에는 아버지와 로리 씨뿐이었다. 그녀를 들어올려 머리를 받칠 때 그의 팔은 떨렸다. 그러나 그에게는 온전히 연민만은 아닌 분위기가 — 자긍심의 홍조가 어린 그런 분위기가 감돌았다.\n\n\"그녀를 마차로 옮길까요? 저는 그녀의 무게를 조금도 느끼지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 가볍게 안아 문까지 데려가, 마차 안에 부드럽게 뉘었다. 그녀의 아버지와 그들의 오랜 벗이 마차에 올랐고, 그는 마부 옆자리에 앉았다.\n\n몇 시간 전 어둠 속에서 그가 멈춰 서서, 거리의 거친 돌들 가운데 어느 것을 그녀의 발이 밟았을까 그려보던 그 문간에 다다랐을 때, 그는 다시 그녀를 들어올려 계단을 지나 그들의 방으로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 그녀를 긴 의자에 뉘었고, 그녀의 아이와 프로스 양이 그녀를 굽어보며 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"그녀를 깨우지 마세요.\" 그가 후자에게 부드럽게 말했다. \"이대로가 나아요. 잠시 기절해 있을 뿐이니, 의식을 되돌리지 마세요.\"\n\n\"오, 카턴, 카턴, 사랑하는 카턴!\" 어린 루시가 벌떡 일어나 슬픔이 북받쳐 그에게 두 팔을 열정적으로 두르며 외쳤다. \"이제 아저씨가 오셨으니, 엄마를 도와줄 무언가를, 아빠를 구해줄 무언가를 해주실 거죠! 오, 엄마를 보세요, 사랑하는 카턴 아저씨! 엄마를 사랑하는 그 모든 사람들 가운데 아저씨가, 엄마가 이런 모습인 걸 차마 보고 있을 수 있나요?\"\n\n그는 아이에게 몸을 숙여, 그 발그레한 뺨을 자기 얼굴에 가져다 댔다. 그는 아이를 가만히 떼어놓고, 의식을 잃은 아이의 어머니를 바라보았다.\n\n\"가기 전에,\" 그가 말하다 멈추었다. \"그녀에게 입맞춰도 될까요?\"\n\n훗날 사람들이 기억하기로는, 그가 몸을 숙여 입술로 그녀의 얼굴을 스칠 때 무언가 말을 중얼거렸다고 한다. 그에게 가장 가까이 있던 아이가 나중에 그것을 전했고, 또 자신이 아름다운 노부인이 되었을 때 손주들에게 전하기를, 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다고 했다. \"네가 사랑하는 한 생명을.\"\n\n옆방으로 나온 뒤, 그는 뒤따라오던 로리 씨와 그녀의 아버지를 향해 갑자기 돌아서서, 후자에게 말했다.\n\n\"바로 어제만 해도 당신은 큰 영향력이 있었지요, 마네뜨 박사님. 적어도 그것을 한번 시도해봅시다. 그 판사들과, 권력을 쥔 모든 사람들이 당신께 매우 호의적이고, 당신의 공로를 무척 인정하고 있지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"찰스와 관련된 일은 무엇 하나 내게 숨겨지지 않았소. 나는 그를 구하리라는 더없이 강한 확신을 받았고, 실제로 구해냈소.\" 그는 몹시 괴로워하며, 아주 천천히 그 대답을 돌려주었다.\n\n\"다시 한번 시도해보세요. 지금부터 내일 오후까지의 시간은 적고 짧지만, 그래도 해보세요.\"\n\n\"해볼 작정이오. 한순간도 쉬지 않겠소.\"\n\n\"잘됐습니다. 저는 당신 같은 정력이 전에도 큰일을 해내는 것을 보아왔습니다 — 비록,\" 그는 미소와 한숨을 함께 띠며 덧붙였다, \"이만한 큰일을 해낸 적은 없었지만요. 하지만 해보세요! 우리가 잘못 쓸 때 생명이란 보잘것없는 것이지만, 그래도 그만한 노력의 값어치는 있지요. 그렇지 않다면 목숨을 내려놓는 데 아무 대가도 들지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"내가 가겠소.\" 마네뜨 박사가 말했다. \"검사와 재판장에게 곧장 가고, 이름을 대지 않는 편이 나은 다른 이들에게도 가겠소. 글도 쓰겠소, 그리고 — 아니, 잠깐! 거리에서 축제가 벌어지고 있으니, 어두워지기 전에는 아무도 만날 수가 없겠군.\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요. 하긴! 잘해야 가망 없는 희망이고, 어두워질 때까지 미뤄진다고 해서 딱히 더 가망 없어질 것도 없으니까요. 일이 어떻게 되어가는지 알고 싶습니다. 하지만, 명심하세요! 저는 아무것도 기대하지 않습니다! 그 두려운 권력자들을 언제쯤 만나보실 수 있을 것 같습니까, 마네뜨 박사님?\"\n\n\"어두워진 직후이기를 바라야겠지. 지금부터 한두 시간 안에.\"\n\n\"네시가 조금 지나면 어두워질 겁니다. 그 한두 시간을 늘려 잡읍시다. 제가 아홉시에 로리 씨 댁으로 가면, 당신이 무엇을 하셨는지 우리 벗에게서든 당신에게서든 들을 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"잘되시기를!\"\n\n로리 씨는 시드니를 따라 바깥문까지 갔고, 그가 떠나려 할 때 어깨를 짚어 그를 돌아서게 했다.\n\n\"나는 아무 희망도 없네.\" 로리 씨가 낮고 슬픈 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"저도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"이 사람들 중 누구 하나라도, 아니 이 사람들 전부가 그를 살려줄 마음이 있다 한들 — 이건 너무 큰 가정이지, 저들에게 그의 목숨이, 아니 그 누구의 목숨인들 무슨 대수겠나! — 법정에서의 그 시위가 있은 뒤에 과연 감히 그를 살려줄 수 있을지 의심스럽네.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다. 저는 그 소리 속에서 도끼가 떨어지는 소리를 들었지요.\"\n\n로리 씨는 팔을 문설주에 기대고, 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"낙담하지 마세요.\" 카턴이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. \"슬퍼하지 마세요. 제가 마네뜨 박사님께 이 생각을 부추긴 것은, 언젠가 그것이 그녀에게 위안이 될지도 모른다고 느꼈기 때문입니다. 그렇지 않으면 그녀는 '그의 목숨이 부질없이 내던져졌거나 허비되었다'고 생각할지도 모르고, 그것이 그녀를 괴롭힐 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그렇지.\" 로리 씨가 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다. \"자네 말이 옳네. 하지만 그는 죽고 말 거야. 진정한 희망이란 없네.\"\n\n\"네. 그는 죽고 말겠지요. 진정한 희망이란 없습니다.\" 카턴이 메아리처럼 되받았다.\n\n그러고는 흔들림 없는 발걸음으로 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII. Darkness\n\n시드니 카턴은 어디로 갈지 정하지 못한 채 거리에서 걸음을 멈추었다. \"아홉시에 텔슨 은행에서.\" 그는 생각에 잠긴 얼굴로 말했다. \"그동안에 내 모습을 드러내두는 게 좋을까? 그래야겠지. 이 사람들이 나 같은 인간이 여기 있다는 걸 알아두는 게 가장 좋아. 빈틈없는 예방책이고, 어쩌면 꼭 필요한 준비일지도 모르지. 하지만 조심, 조심, 조심해야 해! 차근차근 생각해보자!\"\n\n어떤 목적지를 향해 이끌리기 시작한 발걸음을 멈추고, 그는 이미 어두워져가는 거리를 한두 바퀴 돌면서 그 생각이 빚어낼 수 있는 결과들을 마음속으로 더듬어보았다. 그의 첫 인상은 굳어졌다. \"가장 좋아.\" 마침내 결심을 굳히고 그는 말했다. \"이 사람들이 나 같은 인간이 여기 있다는 걸 알아두는 게.\" 그러고는 쌩땅뚜안 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸다.\n\n드파르주는 그날, 자신을 쌩땅뚜안 변두리에서 술집을 하는 사람이라고 소개한 바 있었다. 이 도시를 잘 아는 사람이라면 아무에게도 묻지 않고 그의 집을 찾기란 어렵지 않았다. 그 위치를 확인한 뒤, 카턴은 다시 그 비좁은 거리들에서 나와 어느 음식점에서 식사를 하고 식후에 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다. 여러 해 만에 처음으로, 그는 독한 술을 입에 대지 않았다. 어젯밤 이후로 그는 가볍고 묽은 포도주만 조금 마셨을 뿐이고, 어젯밤에는 브랜디를 다 끝낸 사람처럼 로리 씨의 벽난로에 천천히 떨어뜨려 버렸다.\n\n개운하게 깨어나 다시 거리로 나섰을 때는 벌써 일곱시나 되어 있었다. 쌩땅뚜안 쪽으로 걸어가다가, 그는 거울이 걸려 있는 어느 가게의 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서서, 헐겁게 맨 넥타이며 외투 깃이며 헝클어진 머리칼의 어수선한 매무새를 살짝 매만졌다. 그러고 나서 곧장 드파르주의 술집으로 가서 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n마침 가게 안에는 안절부절못하는 손가락에 쉰 목소리를 가진 자끄 쓰리 말고는 손님이 없었다. 배심원석에서 보았던 이 사내는 작은 계산대에서 술을 마시며 드파르주 부부와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 라 방죙스도 마치 이 가게의 정식 일원이라도 되는 양 그 대화에 끼어 있었다.\n\n카턴이 걸어 들어와 자리를 잡고 (아주 서툰 프랑스어로) 포도주 한 잔을 청하자, 마담 드파르주는 무심한 눈길을 한번 던지더니, 이어 더 날카롭게, 또 한층 더 날카롭게 그를 바라보다가, 마침내 직접 그에게 다가와 무엇을 시켰느냐고 물었다.\n\n그는 이미 했던 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"영국 사람이오?\" 마담 드파르주가 검은 눈썹을 의심스럽게 치켜올리며 물었다.\n\n프랑스어 단 한마디조차 입에서 더디게 나오기라도 하는 듯 그녀를 바라본 뒤, 그는 아까처럼 진한 외국인 억양으로 대답했다. \"그렇소, 부인, 그렇소. 나는 영국 사람이오!\"\n\n마담 드파르주는 포도주를 가지러 계산대로 돌아갔고, 그가 자코뱅파 신문을 집어 들고 그 뜻을 알아내려 애쓰며 골똘히 들여다보는 시늉을 하고 있을 때, 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"내 맹세코, 에브레몽드를 꼭 닮았다니까!\"\n\n드파르주가 포도주를 가져다주며 안녕하시냐고 인사했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오.\"\n\n\"오! 안녕하시오, 시민.\" 그는 잔을 채우며 말했다. \"아! 좋은 술이군. 공화국을 위하여 마시겠소.\"\n\n드파르주는 계산대로 돌아가서 말했다. \"확실히 조금 닮긴 했어.\" 마담이 매섭게 받아쳤다. \"꽤 많이 닮았다니까.\" 자끄 쓰리가 누그러뜨리려는 듯 한마디 했다. \"마담, 당신 마음속에 그자가 그토록 깊이 박혀 있는 거요.\" 상냥한 라 방죙스가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 정말이지! 게다가 내일 그자를 한번 더 보게 될 걸 그토록 즐겁게 기다리고 있잖아요!\"\n\n카턴은 느릿한 집게손가락으로 신문의 글줄과 단어들을 짚어가며, 열중해서 골몰하는 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 그들은 모두 계산대에 바짝 붙어 팔을 괴고 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있었다. 잠시 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 그들은 모두 그쪽을 바라보았으나 자코뱅파 논설에 쏠린 그의 겉으로 드러난 주의를 흩뜨리지는 않았고, 이윽고 다시 대화를 이어갔다.\n\n\"마담 말이 옳아.\" 자끄 쓰리가 말했다. \"왜 멈춘단 말이오? 그 말에는 큰 힘이 있소. 왜 멈춘단 말이오?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 글쎄.\" 드파르주가 따져보듯 말했다. \"그래도 어딘가에서는 멈춰야 하는 법이지. 결국 문제는 어디서 멈추느냐 하는 거 아니겠소?\"\n\n\"몰살에서지.\" 마담이 말했다.\n\n\"훌륭하오!\" 자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다. 라 방죙스 역시 크게 찬동했다.\n\n\"몰살이야 좋은 신조지, 여보.\" 드파르주가 다소 곤혹스러운 듯 말했다. \"대체로 나는 거기에 반대할 게 없소. 하지만 이 의사는 고초를 많이 겪은 사람이오. 당신도 오늘 그를 봤잖소. 그 서류가 낭독될 때 그의 얼굴을 똑똑히 봤을 거요.\"\n\n\"내가 그자의 얼굴을 봤지!\" 마담이 경멸스럽고 노여운 어조로 되받았다. \"그래. 그자의 얼굴을 봤어. 그자의 얼굴이 공화국의 진정한 벗의 얼굴이 아니라는 걸 봤다고. 제 얼굴이나 잘 간수하라지!\"\n\n\"그리고 당신도 봤잖소, 여보.\" 드파르주가 만류하는 투로 말했다. \"그 딸의 비통함을. 그것은 그에게 끔찍한 고통일 게 분명하오!\"\n\n\"내가 그자의 딸을 봤지.\" 마담이 되풀이했다. \"그래, 그자의 딸을 봤어, 한두 번이 아니라. 오늘도 봤고, 다른 날에도 봤어. 법정에서도 봤고, 감옥 옆 거리에서도 봤다고. 내 손가락 하나만 들어 올리면 ——!\" 그녀는 손가락을 들어 올리는 듯하더니 (귀 기울이고 있는 사내의 눈은 줄곧 신문에 가 있었다), 마치 도끼날이 떨어지기라도 하듯 앞에 놓인 계산대 선반 위로 그것을 탁 내리쳤다.\n\n\"여시민은 굉장하시오!\" 배심원이 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"천사예요!\" 라 방죙스가 말하며 그녀를 껴안았다.\n\n\"당신으로 말할 것 같으면,\" 마담이 단호하게 남편을 향해 계속 말했다. \"당신한테 달린 일이라면 —— 다행히도 그렇지 않지만 —— 당신은 지금이라도 이자를 구해주려 들 테지.\"\n\n\"아니!\" 드파르주가 항변했다. \"이 잔을 들어 올리는 것만으로 구할 수 있다 해도 그러지 않을 거요! 하지만 나는 이 일을 거기서 그만두겠소. 거기서 멈추자는 말이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 자끄.\" 마담 드파르주가 노여워하며 말했다. \"그리고 당신도 잘 들어, 나의 작은 라 방죙스도. 둘 다 잘 들어! 들으라고! 폭군이자 압제자로서 저지른 다른 죄들 때문에, 나는 그 일족을 오래전부터 내 명부에 올려두었어, 파멸과 몰살의 운명으로 정해서. 내 남편한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\"\n\n\"사실이오.\" 드파르주가 묻기도 전에 동의했다.\n\n\"위대한 날들이 시작되던 무렵, 바스띠유가 함락될 때, 그이가 오늘 나온 이 서류를 찾아내서 집으로 가져왔지. 그리고 한밤중에 이곳이 텅 비고 문이 닫혔을 때, 우리는 그것을 읽었어, 바로 이 자리에서, 이 등불 빛으로. 그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\"\n\n\"사실이오.\" 드파르주가 동의했다.\n\n\"그날 밤, 서류를 끝까지 다 읽고 등불이 다 타버리고 동이 터서 저 덧문 위로, 저 쇠창살 사이로 햇빛이 비쳐들 무렵, 나는 그이한테 이제 전해줄 비밀이 하나 있다고 했어. 그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\"\n\n\"사실이오.\" 드파르주가 다시 동의했다.\n\n\"나는 그이한테 그 비밀을 전했어. 지금 이렇게 치듯이 이 두 손으로 이 가슴을 치면서, 그이한테 말했지. '드파르주, 나는 바닷가 어부들 틈에서 자랐고, 그 바스띠유 서류에 적힌 대로 에브레몽드 형제에게 그토록 해를 입은 그 농민 가족이 바로 내 가족이오. 드파르주, 땅바닥에 쓰러져 치명상을 입은 그 소년의 누이가 내 언니였고, 그 남편이 내 형부였고, 태어나지 못한 그 아이가 그들의 아이였고, 그 오라비가 내 오빠였고, 그 아버지가 내 아버지였고, 그 죽은 이들이 내 죽은 이들이며, 그 일들의 책임을 묻는 소환장이 이제 나에게로 내려온 거요!' 그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\"\n\n\"사실이오.\" 드파르주가 한번 더 동의했다.\n\n\"그러니 바람과 불더러 어디서 멈추라고 일러.\" 마담이 받아쳤다. \"하지만 나한테는 말하지 마.\"\n\n그녀의 노여움이 품은 그 죽음 같은 본성에서, 두 청자는 끔찍한 즐거움을 끌어냈고 —— 신문에 귀 기울이던 사내는 그녀를 보지 않고도 그녀가 얼마나 새파랗게 질려 있는지 느낄 수 있었다 —— 둘 다 그것을 크게 칭송했다. 미약한 소수파인 드파르주가 후작의 그 자비로운 부인을 추모하며 몇 마디 끼워 넣었으나, 자기 아내에게서 방금 했던 그 대답만 되풀이하게 했을 뿐이었다. \"바람과 불더러 어디서 멈추라고 일러. 나한테는 말고!\"\n\n손님들이 들어오자 무리는 흩어졌다. 그 영국 손님은 자기가 마신 값을 치르고, 어리둥절한 표정으로 거스름돈을 세더니, 이방인답게 국민궁으로 가는 길을 일러달라고 청했다. 마담 드파르주는 그를 문간으로 데려가, 길을 가리키며 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 얹었다. 그 영국 손님은 그때 그 팔을 붙잡아 들어 올린 다음 그 아래를 날카롭고 깊숙이 찔러버리는 것이 어쩌면 좋은 일일지도 모른다는 생각이 들지 않은 것은 아니었다.\n\n그러나 그는 제 갈 길을 갔고, 곧 감옥 담장의 그늘 속으로 삼켜져버렸다. 약속한 시각에 그는 그곳에서 나와 다시 로리 씨의 방에 모습을 드러냈는데, 그곳에서 노신사가 불안에 안절부절못하며 방안을 서성이고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 방금 전까지 루시와 함께 있다가 약속을 지키러 오느라 잠깐 자리를 떴을 뿐이라고 말했다. 그녀의 아버지는 네시 무렵 은행을 나선 뒤로 모습을 보이지 않았다. 그녀는 아버지의 중재로 찰스를 구할 수 있을지도 모른다는 희미한 희망을 품고 있었으나, 그것은 아주 미약한 것이었다. 그가 나간 지 다섯 시간이 넘었다. 대체 어디 있는 걸까?\n\n로리 씨는 열시까지 기다렸다. 그러나 마네뜨 의사가 돌아오지 않고, 또 그로서는 더 이상 루시를 혼자 두고 싶지 않았으므로, 그가 그녀에게 돌아갔다가 자정에 다시 은행으로 오기로 정해졌다. 그동안 카턴은 의사를 기다리며 난롯가에 혼자 있기로 했다.\n\n그는 기다리고 또 기다렸고, 시계가 열두시를 쳤다. 그러나 마네뜨 의사는 돌아오지 않았다. 로리 씨가 돌아왔으나 그에 관한 아무 소식도 듣지 못했고, 아무 소식도 가져오지 못했다. 대체 어디 있는 걸까?\n\n두 사람이 이 문제를 의논하면서 그의 길어진 부재를 두고 미약한 희망의 구조물을 거의 쌓아 올릴 즈음, 그들은 계단을 오르는 그의 발소리를 들었다. 그가 방에 들어선 순간, 모든 것이 끝장났음이 분명해졌다.\n\n그가 정말로 누군가를 찾아갔던 것인지, 아니면 그 시간 내내 거리를 헤매고 다녔던 것인지는 끝내 알 수 없었다. 그가 멍하니 그들을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 그들은 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 그의 얼굴이 모든 것을 말해주었기 때문이다.\n\n\"찾을 수가 없어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 있어야 하는데. 어디 있지?\"\n\n머리와 목이 드러나 있었고, 그가 어찌할 바 모르는 눈길을 사방으로 돌리며 말하는 사이, 그는 외투를 벗어 바닥에 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n\"내 작업대는 어디 있지? 작업대를 사방으로 찾았는데, 못 찾겠어. 내 작업물을 어떻게 해버린 거야? 시간이 촉박한데. 그 구두를 마저 만들어야 해.\"\n\n두 사람은 서로를 바라보았고, 가슴속이 무너져 내렸다.\n\n\"자, 어서!\" 그가 흐느끼는 듯 비참한 투로 말했다. \"일을 하게 해줘. 내 작업물을 줘.\"\n\n아무 대답도 못 듣자, 그는 제정신이 아닌 아이처럼 머리칼을 쥐어뜯고 바닥에 발을 굴렀다.\n\n\"가엾고 버림받은 인간을 괴롭히지 말아줘.\" 그가 끔찍한 비명을 지르며 그들에게 애원했다. \"그저 내 작업물을 달라고! 그 구두를 오늘 밤 안에 마치지 못하면 우리는 어떻게 되는 거야?\"\n\n망가졌다, 완전히 망가져버렸다!\n\n그를 타이르거나 제정신으로 돌려놓으려는 것은 너무도 명백히 가망 없는 일이었으므로 —— 마치 약속이라도 한 듯 —— 두 사람은 각자 그의 어깨에 한 손씩 얹고, 곧 작업물을 가져다주겠노라 약속하며 그를 달래어 난롯가에 앉혔다. 그는 의자에 푹 주저앉아 잉걸불을 바라보며 골똘히 잠겼고, 눈물을 흘렸다. 다락방 시절 이후로 일어난 모든 일이 한순간의 환상이거나 꿈이기라도 한 듯, 로리 씨는 그가 드파르주가 데리고 있던 바로 그 모습 그대로 움츠러드는 것을 보았다.\n\n이 파멸의 광경에 두 사람 모두 마음이 흔들리고 공포에 사로잡혔으나, 지금은 그런 감정에 굴복할 때가 아니었다. 마지막 희망이자 의지처를 잃은 그의 외로운 딸이 두 사람 모두에게 너무도 강렬하게 호소해왔다. 다시금, 마치 약속이라도 한 듯, 그들은 같은 뜻을 얼굴에 담고 서로를 바라보았다. 카턴이 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n\"마지막 기회가 사라졌소. 큰 것은 아니었지만. 그래요. 저분은 따님에게 데려다드리는 게 좋겠소. 하지만 가기 전에, 잠깐 동안만, 정신을 차리고 내 말을 들어주시겠소? 내가 하려는 약조를 왜 하는지, 또 내가 받아내려는 약속을 왜 받아내려는지 묻지 마시오. 내게는 이유가 —— 충분한 이유가 있소.\"\n\n\"의심치 않소.\" 로리 씨가 대답했다. \"말씀해보시오.\"\n\n두 사람 사이 의자에 앉은 그 형상은 줄곧 단조롭게 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들며 신음하고 있었다. 그들은 마치 밤에 병상을 지키고 있기라도 한 듯한 어조로 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n카턴은 거의 발에 걸릴 듯 놓여 있는 외투를 집어 들려고 몸을 굽혔다. 그렇게 하는 사이, 의사가 그날 해야 할 일들의 목록을 넣어 다니던 작은 갑이 바닥에 가볍게 떨어졌다. 카턴이 그것을 집어 드니, 안에 접힌 종이가 한 장 들어 있었다. \"이걸 봐두는 게 좋겠소!\" 그가 말했다. 로리 씨가 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다. 그가 그것을 펼치더니 외쳤다. \"하느님, 감사합니다!\"\n\n\"그게 뭐요?\" 로리 씨가 다급하게 물었다.\n\n\"잠깐만! 차례가 되면 말하겠소. 우선,\" 그는 외투 안에 손을 넣어 또 다른 종이를 꺼냈다. \"이것은 내가 이 도시를 빠져나갈 수 있게 해주는 통행증이오. 보시오. 알겠소? —— 시드니 카턴, 영국인이라고?\"\n\n로리 씨는 그것을 손에 펼쳐 든 채, 그의 진지한 얼굴을 응시했다.\n\n\"내일까지 이걸 맡아주시오. 기억하시겠지만 나는 내일 그를 만날 텐데, 이걸 감옥에 가지고 들어가지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇소?\"\n\n\"모르겠소. 그저 그러고 싶지 않을 뿐이오. 자, 이제 마네뜨 의사가 몸에 지니고 다니던 이 서류를 받으시오. 이것도 비슷한 통행증인데, 이분과 따님, 그리고 그 아이가 언제라도 관문과 국경을 통과할 수 있게 해주는 거요! 알겠소?\"\n\n\"그렇소!\"\n\n\"아마 어제 화를 막으려는 마지막의 최후 예방책으로 이걸 얻었던 모양이오. 날짜가 언제로 되어 있소? 하지만 상관없소. 들여다보느라 지체하지 마시오. 내 것과 당신 것과 함께 조심스럽게 챙겨두시오. 자, 잘 들으시오! 한두 시간 전까지만 해도 나는 그가 그런 서류를 가지고 있거나 가질 수 있으리라곤 조금도 의심치 않았소. 그것은 취소되기 전까지는 유효하오. 하지만 곧 취소될지도 모르고, 내가 보기엔 그렇게 될 거요.\"\n\n\"그분들이 위험에 처한 거요?\"\n\n\"큰 위험에 처해 있소. 마담 드파르주에게 고발당할 위험에 말이오. 나는 그것을 그 여자 입에서 직접 들었소. 오늘 밤 그 여자의 말을 엿들었는데, 그 말이 그분들의 위험을 내게 짙은 빛깔로 보여주었소. 나는 한시도 지체하지 않았고, 그 뒤로 그 첩자를 만났소. 그자가 내 짐작을 확인해주었소. 그자가 알기로는 감옥 담장 옆에 사는 어느 톱장이가 드파르주 부부의 손아귀에 있는데, 마담 드파르주가 그자에게 그녀가 —— 그는 루시의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다 —— 죄수들에게 신호와 암호를 보내는 것을 봤다고 진술하도록 미리 연습시켜두었다는 거요. 그 구실이 흔해빠진 것, 곧 감옥 음모로 둔갑하리라는 것, 그리고 그것이 그녀의 목숨을 —— 어쩌면 그 아이의 목숨도 —— 어쩌면 그 아버지의 목숨까지도 —— 옭아매리라는 것은 쉽게 내다볼 수 있소. 두 사람 다 그곳에서 그녀와 함께 있는 것이 목격되었으니까. 그렇게 질겁한 얼굴은 하지 마시오. 당신이 그들 모두를 구할 테니.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 그럴 수 있게 해주시기를, 카턴! 하지만 어떻게 말이오?\"\n\n\"어떻게 해야 하는지 내가 말해주겠소. 그것은 당신에게 달려 있고, 당신보다 더 나은 사람에게 맡길 수는 없소. 이 새로운 고발은 분명 내일 이후에야 이루어질 거요. 아마도 이삼일은 지나서, 어쩌면 일주일은 지나서일 가능성이 더 크오. 단두대의 희생자를 애도하거나 동정하는 것이 사형에 처할 죄라는 건 당신도 알 거요. 그녀와 그 아버지는 틀림없이 이 죄를 짓게 될 것이고, 그 여자는 (그 추적의 집요함은 이루 말로 다 할 수 없소) 자기 사건에 그 무게를 더해 두 배로 확실하게 하려고 기다릴 거요. 내 말을 따라오고 있소?\"\n\n\"어찌나 주의 깊게, 또 당신 말에 어찌나 깊은 신뢰를 가지고 듣는지, 순간만큼은,\" 그가 의사의 의자 등받이를 만지며 말했다. \"이 비통함조차 잊을 정도요.\"\n\n\"당신에게는 돈이 있고, 길을 갈 수 있는 한 빨리 해안까지 갈 수단을 마련할 수 있소. 영국으로 돌아갈 준비는 며칠 전에 이미 끝나 있소. 내일 아침 일찍 말들을 준비시켜서 오후 두시에 출발할 채비가 갖추어지도록 하시오.\"\n\n\"그렇게 하겠소!\"\n\n그의 태도가 어찌나 열정적이고 사람을 고무하는지, 로리 씨도 그 불길에 옮아붙어 젊은이처럼 민첩해졌다.\n\n\"당신은 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사람이오. 당신보다 더 나은 사람에게 맡길 수 없다고 내가 말하지 않았소? 오늘 밤 그녀에게, 그 위험이 아이와 아버지까지 옭아맨다는 걸, 당신이 아는 대로 말해주시오. 그 점을 거듭 강조하시오. 그렇지 않으면 그녀는 자기 고운 머리를 남편 곁에 기꺼이 내려놓으려 할 테니.\" 그는 한순간 말을 멈칫하더니, 이내 다시 이어갔다. \"그녀의 아이와 아버지를 위해서라도, 그 시각에 그들과 당신과 함께 빠리를 떠나야 한다는 걸 그녀에게 강력히 권하시오. 그것이 남편의 마지막 당부였다고 말하시오. 그것에 그녀가 감히 믿거나 바라는 것보다 더 많은 것이 걸려 있다고 말하시오. 그녀의 아버지가, 이 슬픈 상태에서도, 그녀에게 순순히 따르리라고 생각하시오? 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"확신하오.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각했소. 여기 안마당에서 이 모든 준비를 조용히 그리고 차분하게 갖추시오, 당신이 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉는 것까지 말이오. 내가 당신에게 가는 순간, 나를 태우고 곧장 달리시오.\"\n\n\"어떤 일이 있어도 당신을 기다려야 한다는 거지요?\"\n\n\"아시다시피 내 통행증이 나머지 것들과 함께 당신 손에 있으니, 내 자리를 비워두시오. 내 자리가 채워지는 것 말고는 아무것도 기다리지 말고, 그다음엔 영국으로!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 말이오,\" 로리 씨가 열정적이면서도 그토록 굳건하고 흔들림 없는 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"이 모든 게 한 늙은이에게만 달린 게 아니라, 내 곁에 젊고 열정적인 사람이 함께 있게 되는구려.\"\n\n\"하느님의 도우심으로 그렇게 될 거요! 우리가 지금 서로에게 맹세한 이 길을 어떤 일이 있어도 바꾸지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 약속해주시오.\"\n\"무슨 일이 있어도 그러지 않겠소, 카턴.\"\n\n\"내일 이 말을 기억하시오. 길을 바꾸거나, 그 길에서 지체하면 —— 어떤 이유에서든 —— 단 한 사람의 목숨도 구할 수 없을 것이고, 많은 목숨이 어쩔 수 없이 희생되고 말 거요.\"\n\n\"기억하겠소. 내 몫을 충실히 해내기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"나도 내 몫을 해내기를 바라오. 자, 그럼, 안녕히!\"\n\n그는 진심 어린 엄숙한 미소를 띠고 그 말을 했고, 심지어 노인의 손을 자기 입술에 가져다 대기까지 했으나, 그때 그와 헤어지지는 않았다. 그는 꺼져가는 잉걸불 앞에서 몸을 흔들고 있는 그 형상을 일으켜 세우도록, 거기에 외투와 모자를 입히고, 그가 아직도 신음하며 달라고 애원하는 작업대와 작업물이 어디 숨겨져 있는지 찾으러 나가도록 꾀어내는 일을 거들었다. 그는 그 형상의 다른 한쪽에서 나란히 걸으며 그를 보호하여, 저 비통한 마음이 —— 그가 자신의 황량한 마음을 그 마음에 털어놓았던 그 잊지 못할 시절에는 그토록 행복했던 마음이 —— 그 무서운 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새우고 있는 집의 안마당까지 데려갔다. 그는 안마당으로 들어가 잠시 홀로 그곳에 남아, 그녀 방의 창문에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 올려다보았다. 떠나기 전에, 그는 그 불빛을 향해 축복을, 그리고 작별 인사를 나직이 띄워 보냈다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIII. Fifty-two\n\n검은 콩씨에르주리 감옥에서, 그날의 사형수들은 자신들의 운명을 기다리고 있었다. 그 수는 한 해의 주(週)의 수와 같았다. 쉰두 명이 그날 오후, 도시의 생명의 물결을 타고 끝없이 영원한 바다로 흘러갈 것이었다. 그들이 감방을 비우기도 전에 새로운 입주자가 정해졌고, 그들의 피가 어제 흘린 피 속으로 흘러들기도 전에 내일 그들의 피와 뒤섞일 피가 이미 따로 마련되어 있었다.\n\n쉰두 명이 호명되었다. 그 많은 재산으로도 목숨을 살 수 없었던 일흔살의 징세 청부인부터, 가난과 미천함으로도 자신을 구할 수 없었던 스무살의 침모에 이르기까지. 인간의 악덕과 태만에서 비롯된 육체의 질병이 온갖 계층의 희생자를 덮치듯, 말할 수 없는 고통과 견딜 수 없는 압제와 무정한 무관심에서 태어난 그 무시무시한 도덕적 혼란도 차별 없이 똑같이 내리쳤다.\n\n찰스 다네이는 감방에 홀로 있으면서, 재판소에서 그곳으로 돌아온 이래 어떤 달콤한 망상으로도 스스로를 지탱하지 않았다. 그가 들은 진술의 한줄 한줄에서, 그는 자신의 유죄 선고를 들었다. 그는 어떤 개인적인 영향력으로도 자신을 구할 수 없으며, 사실상 수백만의 사람들에게 선고받은 셈이고, 한두 사람의 힘으로는 아무것도 할 수 없다는 것을 충분히 깨닫고 있었다.\n\n그렇지만 사랑하는 아내의 얼굴이 눈앞에 생생한 채로, 견뎌야 할 일을 향해 마음을 가라앉히기란 쉽지 않았다. 삶을 붙드는 그의 손아귀는 강했고, 그것을 풀어놓기란 무척이나, 무척이나 힘겨웠다. 점차적인 노력과 단계를 거쳐 여기를 조금 펴놓으면, 저기가 더 단단히 움켜쥐었고, 그 손에 힘을 모아 그것이 누그러지면, 이번에는 이쪽이 다시 닫혔다. 게다가 그의 모든 생각 속에는 조급함이, 체념에 맞서 싸우는 격렬하고 뜨거운 심장의 요동이 있었다. 한순간 정말로 체념한 듯하다가도, 그가 죽은 뒤에도 살아가야 할 아내와 아이가 항의하며 그것을 이기적인 일로 만드는 것만 같았다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것은 처음의 일이었다. 머잖아, 자신이 맞이해야 할 운명에 아무런 치욕도 없으며, 수많은 사람들이 부당하게 똑같은 길을 갔고 날마다 그 길을 의연히 밟아간다는 생각이 솟아올라 그를 북돋웠다. 다음으로는 사랑하는 이들이 앞으로 누릴 수 있는 마음의 평화가 상당 부분 자신의 조용한 의연함에 달려 있다는 생각이 뒤따랐다. 그리하여 그는 차츰 더 나은 상태로 가라앉았고, 그제야 생각을 훨씬 더 높은 곳으로 끌어올려 위안을 끌어내릴 수 있었다.\n\n유죄 선고를 받은 그날 밤 어둠이 내리기 전에, 그는 마지막 길을 이만큼 걸어와 있었다. 글 쓸 도구와 등불을 살 수 있게 허락받자, 그는 감옥의 등불이 꺼질 때까지 글을 쓰려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n그는 루시에게 긴 편지를 써서, 그녀에게서 직접 듣기 전에는 그녀의 아버지가 투옥되었던 일을 전혀 몰랐으며, 그 문서가 낭독되기 전까지는 그 비참한 일에 자기 아버지와 숙부가 책임이 있었다는 사실을 그녀만큼이나 알지 못했음을 밝혔다. 그가 버린 이름을 그녀에게 숨겼던 것은 — 이제는 충분히 이해할 만한 — 그녀의 아버지가 그들의 약혼에 붙인 단 하나의 조건이었으며, 결혼식날 아침에도 여전히 요구했던 단 하나의 약속이었음을 그는 이미 그녀에게 설명한 바 있었다. 그는 그녀에게, 그녀의 아버지를 위해서라도, 그 옛날 일요일 정원의 그리운 플라타너스나무 아래에서 들은 탑 이야기로 인해 그녀의 아버지가 그 문서의 존재를 잊어버렸는지, 아니면 (일시적으로든 영원히든) 그것을 다시 떠올리게 되었는지를 결코 알려 하지 말라고 간청했다. 만일 그가 그것에 대한 어떤 분명한 기억을 간직하고 있었다 해도, 민중이 바스띠유에서 찾아내어 온 세상에 알려진 죄수들의 유물 가운데 그것에 대한 언급이 전혀 없었으니, 그것이 바스띠유와 함께 파괴되었다고 짐작했으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그는 그녀에게 — 비록 그럴 필요가 없음을 안다고 덧붙이면서도 — 그녀가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 방법으로 아버지에게, 자기는 정당하게 스스로를 책망할 만한 일을 한 적이 없으며 오로지 두 사람을 위해 한결같이 자신을 잊었을 뿐이라는 진실을 새겨주어 그를 위로해달라고 빌었다. 그녀가 그 자신의 마지막 감사의 사랑과 축복을 간직하고, 슬픔을 이겨내어 사랑하는 아이에게 헌신하는 것 다음으로, 그는 천국에서 다시 만날 것을 두고, 그녀에게 아버지를 위로해달라고 당부했다.\n\n그녀의 아버지 자신에게도 그는 같은 어조로 썼다. 다만 그는 장인에게, 자기 아내와 아이를 그의 보살핌에 분명히 맡긴다고 말했다. 그리고 장인이 빠져들지도 모른다고 예견되는 어떤 낙담이나 위험한 회상에서 그를 일깨우려는 바람에서, 이를 무척이나 힘주어 말했다.\n\n로리 씨에게는 그들 모두를 부탁하며, 자신의 세속적인 일들을 설명했다. 그 일을 마치고, 감사 어린 우정과 따뜻한 애정의 문장을 여러 줄 덧붙이니, 모든 것이 끝났다. 그는 카턴을 떠올리지 못했다. 그의 마음이 다른 이들로 가득 차 있어, 단 한번도 그를 생각하지 못했다.\n\n그는 불이 꺼지기 전에 이 편지들을 마칠 시간이 있었다. 짚으로 된 침상에 몸을 누이며, 그는 이 세상과의 인연을 다했다고 생각했다.\n\n그러나 세상은 그가 잠든 사이 그를 다시 불러들였고, 빛나는 모습으로 나타났다. 자유롭고 행복하게, 쏘호의 옛집으로 돌아와 (비록 그 안에는 진짜 집과 닮은 것이 하나도 없었지만), 영문 모르게 풀려나 마음 가벼이, 그는 루시와 다시 함께 있었고, 그녀는 그에게 그 모든 것이 꿈이었으며 그는 결코 떠난 적이 없다고 말했다. 망각의 한순간이 지나자, 그는 심지어 고통을 겪기도 했고, 죽어 평온해진 채 그녀에게 돌아왔으되, 그에게는 아무런 달라짐이 없었다. 또 한번의 망각의 순간이 지나자, 그는 어두운 아침에 깨어나, 자신이 어디 있는지 무슨 일이 있었는지도 의식하지 못하다가, 문득 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다. \"오늘이 내가 죽는 날이로구나!\"\n\n이렇게 그는 시간을 헤치고, 쉰두 개의 머리가 떨어질 그날에 이르렀다. 이제, 그가 마음을 가라앉히고 조용한 영웅적 자세로 마지막을 맞이할 수 있기를 바라고 있을 때, 깨어 있는 그의 생각 속에 새로운 작용이 시작되었으니, 다스리기가 무척 어려운 것이었다.\n\n그는 자신의 목숨을 끝낼 그 기구를 한번도 본 적이 없었다. 그것이 땅에서 얼마나 높은지, 계단이 몇 개인지, 자신이 어디에 세워질지, 어떻게 손이 닿을지, 그 닿는 손이 붉게 물들어 있을지, 자신의 얼굴이 어느 쪽으로 향할지, 자신이 첫번째일지 아니면 마지막이 될지. 이런 질문들과 그 비슷한 많은 질문들이, 그의 의지와는 전혀 무관하게, 거듭하여, 헤아릴 수 없이 여러번 끼어들었다. 그것들이 두려움과 연결된 것도 아니었으니, 그는 아무런 두려움도 의식하지 못했다. 오히려 그것들은 때가 오면 무엇을 해야 할지 알고 싶은 기이하게 끈질긴 욕구에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그것이 가리키는 짧고 빠른 몇 순간에 비하면 엄청나게 어울리지 않게 거대한 욕구였고, 그 자신의 의아함이라기보다는 그의 안에 있는 어떤 다른 영혼의 의아함에 더 가까운 것이었다.\n\n그가 이리저리 거니는 동안 시간은 흘러갔고, 시계는 그가 다시는 듣지 못할 숫자들을 쳤다. 아홉시가 영영 가버렸고, 열시가 영영 가버렸고, 열한시가 영영 가버렸고, 열두시가 다가와 지나가려 했다. 그를 마지막으로 어지럽혔던 그 기이한 생각의 작용과 힘겨운 싸움을 벌인 끝에, 그는 그것을 이겨냈다. 그는 이리저리 거닐며, 그들의 이름을 나직이 되뇌었다. 싸움의 가장 험한 고비는 지나갔다. 그는 마음을 흩뜨리는 공상에서 벗어나, 자신을 위해 그리고 그들을 위해 기도하며 이리저리 거닐 수 있었다.\n\n열두시가 영영 가버렸다.\n\n그는 마지막 시각이 세시라는 것을 들어 알고 있었고, 호송 수레가 거리를 무겁고 느리게 덜컹거리며 지나가는 만큼 그보다 얼마쯤 일찍 호출되리라는 것을 알았다. 그래서 그는 두시를 그 시각으로 마음속에 새겨두기로 했고, 그 사이에 스스로를 단단히 다져, 그 시각이 지난 뒤에는 다른 이들을 다잡을 수 있게 되기로 결심했다.\n\n가슴에 팔짱을 끼고 규칙적으로 이리저리 거닐며, 라포르스에서 이리저리 거닐던 그 죄수와는 사뭇 다른 사람이 되어, 그는 한시를 알리는 소리가 멀리서 울리는 것을 놀라움 없이 들었다. 그 한시간은 다른 대부분의 시간들과 다름없이 흘러갔던 것이다. 되찾은 침착함에 대해 하늘에 경건히 감사하며, 그는 \"이제 한시간이 남았을 뿐이다\"라고 생각하고는 다시 거닐려고 돌아섰다.\n\n문밖 돌 복도에 발소리가 났다. 그는 멈춰 섰다.\n\n열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂혀 돌아갔다. 문이 열리기 전에, 혹은 열리면서, 한 남자가 영어로 나직이 말했다. \"그는 여기서 한번도 날 본 적이 없소. 내가 그의 눈에 띄지 않게 피해 있었으니. 당신 혼자 들어가시오. 나는 근처에서 기다리겠소. 한순간도 허비하지 마시오!\"\n\n문이 재빨리 열렸다 닫혔고, 그의 앞에 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있는 이가 있었으니, 조용하게, 그에게 골똘히 시선을 모은 채, 얼굴에는 미소의 빛을 띠고, 입술에는 조심하라는 손가락을 댄, 시드니 카턴이었다.\n\n그의 표정에는 무언가 너무나 환하고 예사롭지 않은 것이 있어서, 첫 순간 죄수는 그가 자신의 상상이 빚어낸 환영이 아닐까 의심했다. 그러나 그가 말을 했고, 그것은 그의 목소리였다. 그가 죄수의 손을 잡았고, 그것은 그의 실제 손아귀였다.\n\n\"이 세상 모든 사람 가운데, 자네가 만나리라고 가장 예상하지 못한 사람이 나겠지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"자네라는 것을 믿을 수가 없었네. 지금도 좀처럼 믿기지가 않아. 자네는 설마—\" 갑자기 어떤 불안이 그의 마음에 떠올랐다. \"죄수는 아니지?\"\n\n\"아닐세. 나는 우연히 이곳 간수 하나에게 영향력을 갖게 되었고, 그 덕에 자네 앞에 서 있는 거야. 나는 그녀에게서 — 자네 아내에게서 왔네, 다네이.\"\n\n죄수는 그의 손을 꽉 잡았다.\n\n\"그녀에게서 부탁을 하나 가지고 왔네.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"자네가 잘 기억하는, 그토록 소중한 그 목소리의 더없이 애절한 어조로 자네에게 건네는, 더없이 간절하고 절박하고 단호한 청일세.\"\n\n죄수는 얼굴을 반쯤 옆으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"내가 왜 그것을 가져왔는지, 그게 무슨 뜻인지 물을 시간이 자네에겐 없네. 나도 말해줄 시간이 없고. 자네는 그것에 따라야 하네 — 자네가 신은 그 장화를 벗고, 이 내 장화를 신게.\"\n\n죄수 뒤로, 감방 벽에 의자가 하나 놓여 있었다. 카턴은 앞으로 밀고 들어가, 번개 같은 속도로 이미 그를 그 의자에 앉혀놓고는, 맨발로 그를 굽어보고 섰다.\n\n\"이 내 장화를 신게. 손을 갖다 대게. 의지를 갖다 대. 어서!\"\n\n\"카턴, 이곳에서 빠져나갈 길은 없네. 결코 해낼 수 없는 일이야. 자네만 나와 함께 죽게 될 뿐일세. 이건 미친 짓이야.\"\n\n\"내가 자네에게 도망치라고 청한다면 미친 짓이겠지. 하지만 내가 그러던가? 내가 자네더러 저 문으로 나가라고 청하거든, 그때 미친 짓이라고 말하고 여기 남게. 그 넥타이를 이 내 것으로 바꾸고, 그 외투를 이 내 것으로 바꾸게. 자네가 그러는 동안, 자네 머리에서 이 리본을 풀고, 자네 머리를 이 내 머리처럼 흐트러뜨리게 해주게!\"\n\n놀라운 민첩함으로, 그리고 자못 초자연적으로 보이는 의지와 행동의 힘으로, 그는 이 모든 변화를 그에게 강제했다. 죄수는 그의 손안에서 어린아이 같았다.\n\n\"카턴! 카턴! 이건 미친 짓일세. 이뤄질 수 없는 일이야. 결코 해낼 수 없네. 시도된 적이 있었지만 늘 실패했어. 내 죽음의 쓰라림에 자네의 죽음까지 더하지 말아주게. 제발 비네.\"\n\n\"내가 자네에게, 다네이, 저 문으로 나가라고 청하던가? 내가 그것을 청하거든 거절하게. 이 탁자 위에 펜과 잉크와 종이가 있네. 자네 손이 글을 쓸 만큼 흔들리지 않나?\"\n\n\"자네가 들어왔을 때는 그랬네.\"\n\n\"다시 다잡고, 내가 부르는 대로 쓰게. 어서, 이 사람아, 어서!\"\n\n어리둥절한 머리에 손을 짚으며, 다네이는 탁자에 앉았다. 카턴은 오른손을 가슴에 넣은 채, 그의 바로 곁에 섰다.\n\n\"내가 말하는 그대로 쓰게.\"\n\n\"누구 앞으로 쓰는 건가?\"\n\n\"아무에게도 아닐세.\" 카턴은 여전히 손을 가슴에 넣고 있었다.\n\n\"날짜를 적나?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n질문할 때마다 죄수는 고개를 들었다. 손을 가슴에 넣은 채 그를 굽어보고 선 카턴은 아래를 내려다보았다.\n\n\"'당신이 만일,'\" 카턴이 받아쓰게 했다. \"'오래전에 우리 사이에 오간 말을 기억한다면, 이것을 볼 때 쉽게 이해할 것이오. 당신이 그 말을 기억한다는 것을 나는 아오. 그것을 잊는 것은 당신의 천성에 없는 일이니.'\"\n\n그는 가슴에서 손을 빼내고 있었다. 죄수가 글을 쓰다가 다급한 의아함에 우연히 고개를 들자, 그 손이 무언가를 움켜쥔 채 멈췄다.\n\n\"'그것을 잊는'이라고 썼나?\" 카턴이 물었다.\n\n\"썼네. 자네 손에 든 게 무기인가?\"\n\n\"아닐세. 나는 무장하지 않았어.\"\n\n\"자네 손에 든 게 뭔가?\"\n\n\"곧 알게 될 걸세. 계속 쓰게. 이제 몇 마디 남지 않았어.\" 그는 다시 받아쓰게 했다. \"'나는 그것을 증명할 수 있는 때가 왔음에 감사하오. 내가 그리하는 것은 후회나 슬픔의 대상이 아니오.'\" 이 말을 하며 쓰는 이에게 눈길을 고정한 채, 그의 손이 천천히 그리고 부드럽게 쓰는 이의 얼굴 가까이로 내려갔다.\n\n펜이 다네이의 손가락에서 탁자 위로 떨어졌고, 그는 멍하니 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 김인가?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"김?\"\n\n\"무언가 내 곁을 스쳤는데?\"\n\n\"나는 아무것도 느끼지 못하네. 여기 아무것도 있을 리 없어. 펜을 들고 마저 쓰게. 어서, 어서!\"\n\n마치 기억이 손상되었거나 정신이 흐트러진 듯, 죄수는 주의를 가다듬으려 애썼다. 흐릿한 눈으로, 달라진 숨결로 카턴을 바라보았을 때, 카턴은 — 다시 손을 가슴에 넣고 — 그를 물끄러미 바라보았다.\n\n\"어서, 어서!\"\n\n죄수는 다시 한번 종이 위로 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"'만일 사정이 달랐더라면,'\" 카턴의 손이 다시 조심스럽게 그리고 부드럽게 슬며시 내려가고 있었다. \"'나는 결코 그 더 긴 기회를 쓰지 않았을 것이오. 만일 사정이 달랐더라면,'\" 손이 죄수의 얼굴에 이르렀다. \"'나는 그저 그만큼 더 많은 책임을 져야 했을 것이오. 만일 사정이 달랐더라면—'\" 카턴은 펜을 바라보았고, 그것이 알아볼 수 없는 표시로 흐려져 가는 것을 보았다.\n\n카턴의 손은 더이상 가슴으로 돌아가지 않았다. 죄수가 책망하는 눈빛으로 벌떡 일어섰으나, 카턴의 손은 그의 콧구멍에 바싹 단단히 갖다 댄 채였고, 카턴의 왼팔이 그의 허리를 휘감았다. 잠시 그는 자신을 위해 목숨을 내려놓으러 온 그 사람과 힘없이 몸싸움을 벌였으나, 일분쯤 지나자 의식을 잃고 바닥에 쭉 뻗어버렸다.\n\n재빠르게, 그러나 그 마음만큼이나 그 목적에 충실한 손으로, 카턴은 죄수가 벗어둔 옷으로 갈아입고, 머리를 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 뒤 죄수가 매고 있던 리본으로 묶었다. 그러고는 나직이 불렀다. \"거기 들어오시오! 들어와요!\" 그러자 그 첩자가 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"보이시오?\" 카턴이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 의식 잃은 사람 곁에서 그 가슴에 종이를 넣으며 고개를 들고 말했다. \"당신의 위험이 그리 큰가요?\"\n\n\"카턴 씨,\" 첩자가 겁먹은 듯 손가락을 딱 튕기며 대답했다. \"여기 일이 한창인 마당에, 당신이 거래 전부에 충실하기만 하다면, 내 위험은 그 정도는 아니오.\"\n\n\"나를 두려워 마시오. 나는 죽을 때까지 충실할 테니.\"\n\n\"그래야 하오, 카턴 씨. 쉰두 명이라는 셈이 맞으려면 말이오. 당신이 그 옷차림으로 셈을 맞춰주니, 나는 아무 두려움이 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"두려워 마시오! 나는 곧 당신을 해칠 처지에서 벗어날 테고, 나머지 사람들도 곧 이곳에서 멀리 떠나리다, 하느님 뜻이라면! 자, 사람을 불러 나를 마차로 데려가시오.\"\n\n\"당신을?\" 첩자가 초조하게 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람 말이오, 내가 신분을 바꾼 이 사람. 당신은 나를 데리고 들어온 그 문으로 나가지요?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 데리고 들어올 때 나는 약하고 어지러웠고, 당신이 나를 데리고 나가는 지금은 더 어지럽소. 작별의 면회가 나를 압도해버렸으니. 이런 일은 여기서 흔히, 너무도 흔히 있어왔소. 당신 목숨은 당신 손에 달려 있소. 어서! 사람을 부르시오!\"\n\n\"나를 배신하지 않겠다고 맹세하시오?\" 떨리는 첩자가 마지막 한순간 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람아, 이 사람아!\" 카턴이 발을 구르며 응수했다. \"내가 이 일을 끝까지 해내겠다고 이미 엄숙한 맹세를 하지 않았소, 한데 당신은 지금 이 귀중한 순간들을 허비하는 거요? 당신이 직접 그를 당신이 아는 그 안마당으로 데려가, 당신이 직접 그를 마차에 태우고, 당신이 직접 그를 로리 씨에게 보여주고, 당신이 직접 그에게 바깥 공기 말고는 어떤 회복제도 쓰지 말라고, 어젯밤 내 말과 어젯밤 그의 약속을 기억하라고 이르고, 마차를 몰아 떠나시오!\"\n\n첩자가 물러갔고, 카턴은 탁자에 앉아 두 손에 이마를 괴었다. 첩자는 곧 두 남자와 함께 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그래, 어찌 된 일이오?\" 그 가운데 하나가 쓰러진 사람을 바라보며 말했다. \"제 친구가 거룩한 단두대의 추첨에서 당첨됐다는 걸 알고 이리도 비통해한단 말이오?\"\n\n\"훌륭한 애국자라도,\" 다른 하나가 말했다. \"저 귀족이 꽝을 뽑았다 한들 이보다 더 비통해하지는 못했을 거요.\"\n\n그들은 의식 없는 사람을 일으켜, 문가에 가져다 놓은 들것에 누이고는, 몸을 숙여 그를 메고 나가려 했다.\n\n\"시간이 얼마 없소, 에브레몽드.\" 첩자가 경고하는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"잘 알고 있소.\" 카턴이 대답했다. \"부탁이니 내 친구를 조심해서 다루고, 나를 혼자 있게 해주시오.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼, 친구들,\" 바사드가 말했다. \"그를 들고, 갑시다!\"\n\n문이 닫혔고, 카턴은 홀로 남았다. 그는 귀 기울이는 힘을 한껏 곤두세워, 의심이나 경보를 알릴 만한 어떤 소리라도 들리는지 귀를 기울였다. 아무 소리도 없었다. 열쇠가 돌아가고, 문이 쾅 닫히고, 발소리가 먼 복도를 따라 지나갔다. 예사롭지 않게 여겨질 만한 외침도, 부산함도 일지 않았다. 잠시 뒤 좀더 자유로이 숨을 쉬며, 그는 탁자에 앉아, 시계가 두시를 칠 때까지 다시 귀를 기울였다.\n\n그가 두려워하지 않는 소리들이 — 그 뜻을 짐작했기에 — 그제야 들려오기 시작했다. 문들이 잇따라 열렸고, 마침내 그 자신의 문이 열렸다. 손에 명단을 든 간수가 안을 들여다보며, 그저 \"따라오시오, 에브레몽드!\"라고 말했고, 그는 멀찍이 떨어진 크고 어두운 방으로 따라 들어갔다. 어두운 겨울날이었고, 안의 그늘과 밖의 그늘이 어우러져, 그는 거기 끌려와 팔을 묶이게 된 다른 이들을 어렴풋이 분간할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 어떤 이들은 서 있었고, 어떤 이들은 앉아 있었다. 어떤 이들은 한탄하며 가만있지 못하고 움직였으나, 그런 이들은 소수였다. 대다수는 말없이 조용히, 바닥을 물끄러미 바라보고 있었다.\n\n그가 어둑한 구석 벽 곁에 서 있는 동안, 쉰두 명 가운데 몇몇이 그의 뒤를 이어 끌려 들어왔는데, 한 남자가 지나가다 멈춰 서서, 그를 아는 듯 그를 끌어안았다. 발각될지 모른다는 큰 두려움에 그는 오싹했으나, 그 남자는 그냥 지나갔다. 그러고 나서 아주 잠시 뒤, 가냘픈 소녀 같은 몸매에, 핏기라곤 한 점도 없는 곱고 야윈 얼굴에, 크고 활짝 열린 인내심 어린 눈을 한 젊은 여인이, 그가 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 자리에서 일어나, 그에게 말을 건네러 다가왔다.\n\n\"에브레몽드 시민,\" 그녀가 차가운 손으로 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"저는 라포르스에서 당신과 함께 있던 가난한 어린 침모예요.\"\n\n그가 대답으로 중얼거렸다. \"그렇군요. 당신이 무슨 죄목으로 고발당했는지 잊었는데요?\"\n\n\"음모요. 정의로운 하늘은 제가 어떤 음모와도 무관하다는 걸 알지만요. 그럴 법이나 한가요? 저처럼 가난하고 어리고 연약한 것과 누가 음모를 꾸미려 들겠어요?\"\n\n그녀가 그 말을 하며 짓는 쓸쓸한 미소가 그의 마음을 너무도 울려서, 그의 눈에서 눈물이 솟았다.\n\n\"저는 죽음이 두렵지 않아요, 에브레몽드 시민. 하지만 저는 아무 짓도 하지 않았어요. 우리 같은 가난한 사람들에게 그토록 많은 좋은 일을 해줄 공화국이 제 죽음으로 덕을 본다면, 저는 죽기를 마다하지 않아요. 하지만 그게 어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠어요, 에브레몽드 시민. 저처럼 가난하고 연약하고 어린 것이 말이에요!\"\n\n그의 마음이 따뜻해지고 부드러워질 이 세상에서의 마지막 대상으로서, 그 마음은 이 가엾은 소녀에게 따뜻해지고 부드러워졌다.\n\n\"당신이 풀려났다고 들었어요, 에브레몽드 시민. 그게 사실이기를 바랐는데요?\"\n\n\"사실이었지요. 하지만 다시 붙잡혀 선고받았소.\"\n\n\"제가 당신과 함께 타고 갈 수 있다면, 에브레몽드 시민, 당신 손을 잡게 해주시겠어요? 저는 두렵지 않지만, 저는 작고 연약하니, 그러면 좀더 용기가 날 거예요.\"\n\n그 인내심 어린 눈이 그의 얼굴로 들려졌을 때, 그는 그 눈에 문득 의혹이, 이어 놀라움이 떠오르는 것을 보았다. 그는 일에 닳고 굶주림에 닳은 그 젊은 손가락들을 꼭 쥐고, 입술을 그곳에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"당신은 그를 위해 죽는 거군요?\" 그녀가 속삭였다.\n\n\"그리고 그의 아내와 아이를 위해서요. 쉿! 그렇소.\"\n\n\"오 제가 당신의 용감한 손을 잡게 해주실 거지요, 낯선 분이여?\"\n\n\"쉿! 그렇소, 가엾은 누이여. 마지막 순간까지.\"\n\n같은 그늘이, 이른 오후의 바로 그 시각에, 감옥 위로 내리는 그 그늘이, 군중이 에워싼 관문 위로도 내리고 있었으니, 빠리를 빠져나가는 마차 한 대가 검문을 받으러 다가서고 있었다.\n\n\"누가 지나가는가? 안에 누구를 태웠나? 증명서!\"\n\n증명서가 건네지고, 읽힌다.\n\n\"알렉상드르 마네뜨. 의사. 프랑스인. 어느 쪽이 그자인가?\"\n\n이 사람이다. 이 무력하게, 알아들을 수 없이 중얼거리며, 정신을 놓은 노인이 지목된다.\n\n\"보아하니 시민-의사는 제정신이 아닌 모양이군? 혁명열이 그에게 너무 과했나 보지?\"\n\n너무도 과했다.\n\n\"하! 많은 이들이 그것으로 고생하지. 루시. 그의 딸. 프랑스인. 어느 쪽이 그 여자인가?\"\n\n이 사람이다.\n\n\"보아하니 그런 모양이군. 루시, 에브레몽드의 아내. 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n그렇다.\n\n\"하! 에브레몽드는 다른 데 만날 약속이 있지. 루시, 그녀의 아이. 영국인. 이 아이인가?\"\n\n다름 아닌 그 아이다.\n\n\"내게 입을 맞추렴, 에브레몽드의 아이야. 자, 너는 훌륭한 공화주의자에게 입을 맞췄다. 네 집안에 새로운 일이지. 잊지 마라! 시드니 카턴. 변호사. 영국인. 어느 쪽이 그자인가?\"\n\n그는 여기, 마차의 이 구석에 누워 있다. 그도 지목된다.\n\n\"보아하니 영국인 변호사는 기절했군?\"\n\n좀더 신선한 공기를 쐬면 회복하기를 바란다고 한다. 그가 건강이 좋지 못하며, 공화국의 노여움을 산 친구와 슬프게 헤어진 참이라고 설명된다.\n\n\"그게 다인가? 그쯤이야 대단한 일도 아니지! 많은 이들이 공화국의 노여움을 사고, 그 작은 창으로 내다봐야 하니까. 자비스 로리. 은행가. 영국인. 어느 쪽이 그자인가?\"\n\n\"내가 그 사람이오. 마지막이니, 당연히 나일 수밖에.\"\n\n앞선 모든 질문에 대답한 것은 자비스 로리다. 마차에서 내려, 한 손을 마차 문에 얹은 채, 한 무리의 관리들에게 대답하는 것도 자비스 로리다. 그들은 느긋이 마차 둘레를 돌고, 느긋이 마부석에 올라, 지붕에 실린 얼마 안 되는 짐을 살핀다. 주위를 어슬렁거리던 시골 사람들이 마차 문 가까이 몰려들어 탐욕스레 안을 들여다본다. 어미 품에 안긴 어린아이가, 단두대로 간 귀족의 아내를 만져볼 수 있도록 짧은 팔을 내밀어진다.\n\n\"여기 증명서요, 자비스 로리. 부서까지 마쳤소.\"\n\n\"이제 떠나도 되겠소, 시민?\"\n\n\"떠나도 좋소. 앞으로, 마부들! 부디 좋은 여행 되시길!\"\n\n\"여러분께 경의를 표하오. — 첫번째 위기는 넘겼구나!\"\n\n이것도 자비스 로리의 말이니, 두 손을 맞잡고 위를 올려다보며 하는 말이다. 마차 안에는 공포가 있고, 흐느낌이 있고, 의식 없는 길손의 거친 숨소리가 있다.\n\n\"우리가 너무 느리게 가고 있는 것 아닌가요? 좀더 빨리 가도록 할 수 없을까요?\" 루시가 노인에게 매달리며 묻는다.\n\n\"그건 도망치는 것처럼 보일 거다, 얘야. 저들을 너무 다그쳐서는 안 돼. 의심을 살 테니.\"\n\n\"뒤를 봐요, 뒤를 봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요!\"\n\n\"길이 훤하구나, 얘야. 아직까지는 쫓기지 않아.\"\n\n집들이 두세 채씩 우리 곁을 지나가고, 외딴 농가들, 허물어진 건물들, 염색 공장들, 무두질 공장들 따위가, 트인 들판이, 잎 떨어진 나무들의 가로수길이 지나간다. 단단하고 고르지 못한 포석이 우리 아래에 있고, 부드럽고 깊은 진흙이 양옆에 있다. 때로 우리는 우리를 덜컹거리고 뒤흔드는 돌멩이들을 피하려고 가장자리 진흙 속으로 파고들고, 때로는 그곳 바퀴 자국과 수렁에 박힌다. 그럴 때면 조급함의 고통이 너무도 커서, 우리는 미친 듯한 경악과 다급함에 마차에서 내려 달리고 싶어진다 — 숨고 싶어진다 — 멈추는 것 말고는 무슨 짓이든 하고 싶어진다.\n\n트인 들판을 벗어나, 다시금 허물어진 건물들, 외딴 농가들, 염색 공장들, 무두질 공장들 따위 사이로, 두세 채씩의 오두막들, 잎 떨어진 나무들의 가로수길 사이로 든다. 이자들이 우리를 속여, 다른 길로 우리를 도로 데려간 것 아닌가? 이곳이 같은 자리를 두번 지나는 것 아닌가? 다행히도, 아니다. 마을이다. 뒤를 봐요, 뒤를 봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요! 쉿! 역참이다.\n\n느긋이, 우리 네 마리 말이 풀려난다. 느긋이, 마차는 작은 거리에 말을 빼앗긴 채, 다시는 움직일 가망이라곤 보이지 않게 서 있다. 느긋이, 새 말들이 한 마리씩 눈앞에 나타난다. 느긋이, 새 마부들이 채찍 끈을 빨고 땋으며 뒤따른다. 느긋이, 옛 마부들이 받은 돈을 세고, 셈을 틀리게 하고, 불만스러운 결과에 다다른다. 그러는 내내, 짐을 너무 실은 우리 가슴은, 일찍이 태어난 가장 빠른 말의 가장 빠른 질주보다 훨씬 더 앞지를 박자로 두근거린다.\n\n마침내 새 마부들이 안장에 올라타고, 옛 마부들은 뒤에 남는다. 우리는 마을을 지나, 언덕을 오르고, 언덕을 내려, 낮고 물기 어린 땅 위에 다다른다. 갑자기 마부들이 활기찬 손짓과 함께 말을 주고받고, 말들이 거의 엉덩방아를 찧을 듯 멈춰 선다. 우리가 쫓기는 것인가?\n\n\"여보! 마차 안의 양반들. 어디 말 좀 해보쇼!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 로리 씨가 창밖을 내다보며 묻는다.\n\n\"몇 명이라고들 했소?\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"— 지난 역참에서 말이오. 오늘 단두대로 몇 명이 가오?\"\n\n\"쉰두 명.\"\n\n\"내 그랬지! 멋들어진 숫자야! 여기 내 동료 시민은 마흔두 명이라고 우겼다오. 열 개의 머리는 더 가질 만하지. 단두대가 아주 잘 돌아가는군. 나는 그게 좋아. 이랴, 앞으로. 워이!\"\n\n밤이 어둡게 다가온다. 그가 더 움직인다. 정신을 차리기 시작하여, 알아들을 수 있게 말한다. 그는 그들이 아직 함께 있는 줄 안다. 그의 이름을 부르며, 그의 손에 무엇을 들고 있느냐고 묻는다. 오 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서, 자비로운 하늘이여, 우리를 도우소서! 내다봐요, 내다봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요.\n\n바람이 우리를 뒤쫓아 몰아치고, 구름이 우리를 뒤쫓아 날아가고, 달이 우리를 뒤쫓아 내달리고, 거친 밤이 온통 우리를 뒤쫓는다. 그러나 아직까지는, 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 우리는 쫓기지 않는다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIV. The Knitting Done\n\n쉰두 명이 자기들의 운명을 기다리고 있던 바로 그 시각에, 드파르주 부인은 복수의 여신, 그리고 혁명 배심원의 자끄 쓰리와 더불어 음험하고 불길한 회의를 열고 있었다. 드파르주 부인이 이 측근들과 의논을 나눈 곳은 포도주 가게가 아니라, 한때 도로 보수공이었던 톱장이의 헛간이었다. 톱장이 자신은 그 회의에 끼지 않고 조금 떨어져서 머물러 있었는데, 부르기 전에는 입을 열어서도 안 되고 청하기 전에는 의견을 내서도 안 되는 바깥쪽 위성과도 같았다.\n\n\"하지만 우리 드파르주는,\" 자끄 쓰리가 말했다, \"틀림없이 훌륭한 공화주의자겠지? 응?\"\n\n\"프랑스에,\" 수다스러운 복수의 여신이 새된 목소리로 항변했다, \"그보다 나은 사람은 없어요.\"\n\n\"진정해라, 작은 복수의 여신아.\" 드파르주 부인이 약간 눈살을 찌푸리며 부관의 입술에 손을 갖다 대고 말했다. \"내 말을 들어. 내 남편은, 동지여, 훌륭한 공화주의자이고 담대한 사람이야. 공화국을 위해 큰 공을 세웠고, 공화국의 신임도 받고 있어. 하지만 내 남편에게는 약점이 있는데, 그는 너무 나약해서 이 의사에게 마음이 약해진단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그것 참 안된 일이로군.\" 자끄 쓰리가 의심쩍게 고개를 저으며, 잔인한 손가락을 굶주린 입가에 댄 채 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"훌륭한 시민답지가 않아. 유감스러운 일이지.\"\n\n\"보세요.\" 부인이 말했다. \"나는 이 의사 따위에는 아무 관심도 없어요. 내가 그자에게 무슨 관심이 있다고, 머리가 붙어 있든 떨어져 나가든 알 바 아니에요. 다 마찬가지죠. 하지만 에브레몽드 일족은 멸절되어야 하고, 아내와 아이는 남편이자 아비의 뒤를 따라야 해요.\"\n\n\"그 여자라면 거기에 어울리는 고운 머리를 가졌지.\" 자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"거기서 파란 눈과 금발 머리를 본 적이 있는데, 쌍송이 그걸 들어올렸을 때 아주 매력적으로 보이더군.\" 식인귀 같은 주제에, 그는 미식가처럼 말했다.\n\n드파르주 부인은 눈을 내리깔고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그 아이도,\" 자끄 쓰리가 자기 말을 음미하듯 즐기며 한마디 보탰다, \"금발 머리에 파란 눈을 가졌지. 게다가 우리는 거기서 아이를 보는 일이 드물잖아. 참 보기 좋은 광경일 거야!\"\n\n\"한마디로,\" 짧은 상념에서 깨어나며 드파르주 부인이 말했다, \"이 일에서는 내 남편을 믿을 수가 없어요. 어젯밤 이후로 내 계획의 세부 사항을 그에게 털어놓을 엄두가 나지 않을 뿐만 아니라, 내가 지체하다가는 그가 경고를 흘릴 위험이 있고, 그러면 그들이 달아날 수도 있다는 느낌이 들어요.\"\n\n\"그건 절대 안 돼.\" 자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"아무도 달아나서는 안 돼. 지금도 절반밖에 못 채우고 있는걸. 하루에 백이십 명은 처리해야 하는데.\"\n\n\"한마디로,\" 드파르주 부인이 말을 이었다, \"내 남편에게는 이 가족을 끝까지 추적해 절멸시킬 나만한 이유가 없고, 나에게는 이 의사를 어떤 정으로 대할 그이만한 이유가 없어요. 그러니 나는 나 자신을 위해 움직여야 해요. 이리 와, 작은 시민.\"\n\n죽을 듯한 공포심에서 그녀를 떠받들고 스스로 복종하던 톱장이가, 빨간 모자에 손을 갖다 댄 채 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"그 신호들 말인데, 작은 시민.\" 드파르주 부인이 엄하게 말했다. \"그 여자가 죄수들에게 보낸 그 신호들 말이야, 자네 바로 오늘 그것에 대해 증언할 준비가 되어 있겠지?\"\n\n\"예, 예, 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까!\" 톱장이가 외쳤다. \"날마다, 날씨가 어떻든, 두시부터 네시까지, 늘 신호를 보냈죠, 어떤 때는 어린것이랑, 어떤 때는 혼자서요. 저는 제가 아는 걸 압니다. 제 눈으로 똑똑히 봤다고요.\"\n\n그는 말하는 내내 온갖 몸짓을 해 보였는데, 마치 한 번도 본 적 없는 그 갖가지 신호들 가운데 몇 가지를 즉흥적으로 흉내 내기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"명백한 음모로군.\" 자끄 쓰리가 말했다. \"환히 들여다보여!\"\n\n\"배심원단은 의심할 여지가 없겠지요?\" 드파르주 부인이 음울한 미소를 띠며 그에게로 눈을 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"애국적인 배심원단을 믿으시오, 친애하는 여성 시민. 동료 배심원들에 대해서는 내가 책임지겠소.\"\n\n\"자, 어디 보자.\" 드파르주 부인이 다시 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"한 번만 더! 이 의사는 남편을 위해 살려둘 수 있을까? 나는 어느 쪽으로도 별 감정이 없어. 그자를 살려둘 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"그자도 머리 하나로 셈해질 텐데.\" 자끄 쓰리가 나직한 목소리로 한마디 했다. \"우린 정말 머리가 모자라거든. 살려두면 아까운 노릇이지, 내 생각엔.\"\n\n\"내가 그 여자를 봤을 때 그자도 그 여자와 함께 신호를 보내고 있었어요.\" 드파르주 부인이 따졌다. \"한쪽을 빼놓고 다른 쪽만 말할 수는 없어요. 게다가 나는 입을 다물고 이 일을 전부 이 사람에게, 여기 이 작은 시민에게 맡겨둘 수가 없어요. 나도 나름 괜찮은 증인이니까요.\"\n\n복수의 여신과 자끄 쓰리는 그녀야말로 가장 감탄스럽고 놀라운 증인이라고 앞다투어 열렬히 단언했다. 작은 시민도 질세라, 그녀를 천상의 증인이라고 선언했다.\n\n\"그자도 운에 맡겨야지.\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다. \"아냐, 살려둘 수 없어! 자네는 세시에 일이 있지. 자네는 오늘 처형되는 무리를 보러 갈 거야.—그렇지?\"\n\n그 질문은 톱장이를 향한 것이었고, 그는 황급히 그렇다고 대답하면서, 이 기회를 놓치지 않고, 자기야말로 가장 열렬한 공화주의자이며, 만일 무슨 일이라도 생겨서 그 익살맞은 국가의 이발사를 바라보며 오후의 담배 한 대 피우는 즐거움을 누리지 못한다면 자기야말로 가장 비참한 공화주의자가 될 거라고 덧붙였다. 그가 이 대목에서 어찌나 요란하게 떠들어대는지, 하루 종일 매시간 제 한 몸의 안위가 걱정스러워 사소하나마 개인적인 두려움을 품고 있는 게 아닌가 의심받을 만했다(아마도 드파르주 부인의 머릿속에서 경멸스럽게 그를 내려다보던 그 검은 눈에는 정말 그렇게 보였으리라).\n\n\"나도,\" 부인이 말했다, \"같은 장소에 일이 있어. 그게 끝난 뒤에—오늘 밤 여덟시쯤이라고 하지—생땅뚜안으로 나를 찾아와. 그러면 우리 구역에 이자들을 고발하는 거야.\"\n\n톱장이는 여성 시민을 모시게 되어 영광이고 황송하다고 말했다. 여성 시민이 그를 바라보자, 그는 당황해서 작은 개가 그러듯 그녀의 시선을 피해, 자기 장작더미 사이로 물러나서는, 톱 손잡이에 매달린 채 어쩔 줄 모르는 기색을 감추었다.\n\n드파르주 부인은 배심원과 복수의 여신에게 문가로 조금 더 가까이 오라고 손짓하고는, 거기서 자신의 다음 계획을 그들에게 이렇게 설명했다.\n\n\"그 여자는 지금쯤 집에서 그자가 죽을 순간을 기다리고 있을 거야. 슬퍼하고 비탄에 잠겨 있겠지. 공화국의 정의를 비난할 만한 심리 상태에 빠져 있을 테고. 공화국의 적들에게 한껏 동정심을 품고 있을 거야. 내가 그 여자한테 가야지.\"\n\n\"이 얼마나 감탄스러운 여인인가, 이 얼마나 사랑스러운 여인인가!\" 자끄 쓰리가 황홀해하며 외쳤다. \"아, 내 사랑하는 동지!\" 복수의 여신이 외치며, 그녀를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"내 뜨개질감을 가져가.\" 드파르주 부인이 자기 뜨개질감을 부관의 손에 쥐여주며 말했다. \"그리고 내 늘 앉던 자리에 챙겨놔. 내 늘 앉던 의자를 맡아둬. 곧장 거기로 가, 오늘은 아마 평소보다 사람이 더 많이 몰릴 테니까.\"\n\n\"제 대장의 명령에 기꺼이 따르지요.\" 복수의 여신이 잽싸게 대답하고는, 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다. \"늦지 않을 거죠?\"\n\n\"시작 전에 가 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"호송 수레들이 도착하기 전에도요. 꼭 거기 있어야 해요, 내 영혼이여.\" 복수의 여신이 그녀의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다, 그녀가 이미 거리로 접어들었기 때문이다. \"호송 수레들이 도착하기 전에요!\"\n\n드파르주 부인은 손을 살짝 흔들어, 들었으며 제때에 도착할 테니 믿어도 좋다는 뜻을 내비치고는, 그렇게 진흙탕을 헤치며 감옥 담장 모퉁이를 돌아갔다. 복수의 여신과 배심원은 그녀가 멀어져 가는 모습을 지켜보며, 그녀의 멋진 풍모와 빼어난 도덕적 자질을 한껏 칭송했다.\n\n그 시절 시대가 무섭도록 흉하게 그 손길을 들이댄 여인들이 많았지만, 그들 가운데 지금 거리를 따라 걷는 이 무자비한 여인보다 더 두려워할 만한 사람은 없었다. 강인하고 두려움을 모르는 성격에, 예리한 분별력과 민첩함, 굳센 결단력, 그리고 가진 자에게 굳건함과 적의를 부여하는 듯할 뿐 아니라 남들에게도 본능적으로 그러한 자질을 알아채게 만드는 부류의 아름다움까지 갖춘 그녀를, 그 어지러운 시대는 어떤 상황에서든 떠받들어 올렸을 것이다. 그러나 어린 시절부터 짓밟혔다는 음울한 의식과 한 계급에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오가 배어 있던 그녀를, 기회가 암호랑이로 키워냈다. 그녀에게는 연민이라고는 전혀 없었다. 만일 한때 그런 미덕을 지녔다 한들, 이제는 그녀에게서 완전히 빠져나가 버렸다.\n\n선조들의 죄 때문에 한 무고한 사내가 죽어야 한다는 사실 따위는 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 아니라 그들을 보고 있었다. 그의 아내가 과부가 되고 딸이 고아가 된다는 사실 또한 그녀에게는 아무것도 아니었다. 그것으로는 처벌이 모자랐는데, 그들은 그녀의 타고난 적이요 먹잇감이라, 그런 자들로서는 살아갈 권리가 없었기 때문이다. 그녀에게 호소해본들 부질없는 일이었으니, 그녀는 자기 자신에 대해서마저 연민이라는 것을 느끼지 못했기 때문이다. 그녀가 가담했던 그 숱한 충돌 가운데 어느 하나에서 거리에 쓰러져 죽었다 한들, 그녀는 자신을 가엾게 여기지 않았으리라. 또한 내일 도끼 앞으로 끌려가라는 명령을 받았다 한들, 자기를 그리로 보낸 자와 처지를 맞바꾸고 싶다는 격렬한 욕망 말고는 그 어떤 부드러운 감정도 없이 그리로 갔으리라.\n\n드파르주 부인은 그 거친 겉옷 아래 그런 심장을 품고 다녔다. 아무렇게나 걸쳤어도, 어딘지 기묘하게 잘 어울리는 겉옷이었고, 그녀의 검은 머리는 거친 빨간 모자 아래에서 풍성해 보였다. 그녀의 가슴팍에는 장전된 권총이 숨겨져 있었다. 그녀의 허리춤에는 날 선 단검이 숨겨져 있었다. 그렇게 무장한 채, 그런 성격에 어울리는 자신만만한 걸음걸이로, 소녀 시절에 갈색 바닷모래밭을 맨발에 맨다리로 늘 거닐던 여자다운 나긋한 자유로움을 지니고, 드파르주 부인은 거리를 따라 걸어갔다.\n\n그런데 바로 그 순간 짐이 다 실리기를 기다리고 있던 그 여행용 마차의 여정을 어젯밤 짜놓을 때, 프로스 양을 거기에 태우는 일의 어려움이 로리 씨의 마음을 꽤나 사로잡았었다. 단순히 마차에 짐을 너무 많이 싣는 일을 피하는 게 바람직하다는 차원이 아니라, 마차와 그 승객들을 검문하는 데 걸리는 시간을 최대한으로 줄이는 일이 무엇보다 중요했다. 그들의 탈출이 여기저기서 단 몇 초를 아끼는 데 달려 있을지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 마침내 그는 노심초사한 끝에, 도시를 떠나는 게 자유로운 프로스 양과 제리가 그 시대에 알려진 가장 가벼운 바퀴 달린 탈것으로 세시에 도시를 떠나자고 제안했다. 짐에 매이지 않은 그들은 곧 마차를 따라잡을 테고, 그것을 앞질러 길에서 먼저 가면서, 미리 말을 주문해두어, 지체가 가장 두려운 그 귀중한 밤 시간 동안 마차의 진행을 크게 수월하게 해줄 것이었다.\n\n이 계획에서 그 급박한 위기에 진정한 도움이 될 희망을 발견한 프로스 양은 그것을 기쁘게 반겼다. 그녀와 제리는 마차가 출발하는 것을 지켜보았고, 솔로몬이 누구를 데려왔는지도 알았으며, 한 십 분쯤 마음 졸이는 고통 속에서 보낸 뒤, 이제 막 마차를 뒤따를 준비를 마무리하고 있었다. 바로 그때 드파르주 부인은 거리를 헤치고 나아가, 그들이 의논을 나누고 있는, 그밖에는 텅 비어 있는 그 거처로 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 어떻게 생각하세요, 크런처 씨.\" 너무 흥분한 나머지 말하는 것도, 서 있는 것도, 움직이는 것도, 살아 있는 것조차 힘들 지경인 프로스 양이 말했다. \"이 안마당에서 출발하지 않는 게 어떨까 하는데요? 오늘 이미 다른 마차가 여기서 떠났으니, 의심을 살지도 모르잖아요.\"\n\n\"제 의견은요, 아가씨.\" 크런처 씨가 대답했다. \"아가씨 말씀이 옳다는 겁니다. 그리고 옳든 그르든 저는 아가씨 편에 서겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"우리의 그 소중한 분들 때문에 두려움과 희망에 어찌나 정신이 산란한지,\" 프로스 양이 마구 울먹이며 말했다. \"도무지 무슨 계획도 세울 수가 없네요. 당신은 무슨 계획이라도 세울 수 있겠어요, 친절하고 착한 크런처 씨?\"\n\n\"앞으로의 영생에 관한 거라면요, 아가씨.\" 크런처 씨가 대답했다. \"그러길 바랍니다. 한데 여기 이 축복받은 늙은 대가리가 지금 당장 무슨 쓸모가 있느냐 하면요, 그건 아닌 것 같습니다요. 아가씨, 제가 이 위기에 꼭 남겨두고 싶은 두 가지 약속과 맹세에 좀 주목해주시는 호의를 베풀어주시겠습니까요?\"\n\n\"아이고, 제발!\" 프로스 양이 여전히 마구 울먹이며 외쳤다. \"당장 남겨두고 처치해버려요, 훌륭한 양반답게요.\"\n\n\"첫째,\" 온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 잿빛으로 질린 엄숙한 얼굴로 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"저 가엾은 분들이 여기서 무사히 빠져나가면요, 다시는, 다시는 그 짓을 안 하겠습니다요!\"\n\n\"틀림없이요, 크런처 씨.\" 프로스 양이 대답했다. \"그게 뭐든 간에 당신은 다시는 그 짓을 안 할 거예요. 그러니 그게 뭔지 더 자세히 말씀하실 필요는 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"예, 아가씨.\" 제리가 대답했다. \"아가씨께 그게 뭔지는 입에 올리지 않겠습니다요. 둘째: 저 가엾은 분들이 여기서 무사히 빠져나가면요, 다시는, 다시는 크런처 부인이 엎드려 기도하는 데 참견하지 않겠습니다요!\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 살림살이 방식이든 간에요,\" 프로스 양이 눈물을 닦고 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰며 말했다. \"크런처 부인이 그걸 온전히 자기 뜻대로 알아서 하시는 게 가장 좋으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없네요.—오, 내 가엾은 분들!\"\n\n\"게다가 한 가지 더 말씀드리자면요, 아가씨.\" 크런처 씨가 마치 설교단에서 일장 연설이라도 늘어놓을 듯한 심히 불안한 기색으로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 제 말을 받아 적으셨다가 아가씨를 통해 크런처 부인한테 전해주십시오. 엎드려 기도하는 데 관한 제 의견이 바뀌었으니, 부디 진심으로 바라건대 크런처 부인이 바로 지금 이 순간에 엎드려 기도하고 있기를 바란다고요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 그래요! 나도 그분이 그러고 계시기를 바라요, 착한 양반.\" 정신이 산란한 프로스 양이 외쳤다. \"그리고 그분이 그 기도로 바라던 응답을 얻으시기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"부디 그런 일은 없기를요.\" 한층 더 엄숙하게, 한층 더 느릿느릿하게, 그리고 한층 더 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 손을 뻗칠 듯한 기색으로 크런처 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 일찍이 했던 말이나 행동이 지금 저 가엾은 분들을 위한 제 간절한 소망에 화로 돌아오는 일은 없기를요! 우리 모두가 (어떻게든 형편이 닿는다면) 엎드려 빌어서라도 저분들을 이 음산한 위험에서 구해내지 못하는 일은 없기를요! 부디, 아가씨! 제 말은, 부-디 그런 일은 없기를요!\" 이것이, 더 나은 결론을 찾으려 한참을 헛되이 애쓴 끝에 크런처 씨가 내린 결론이었다.\n\n그리고 드파르주 부인은 여전히 거리를 따라 나아가며, 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"우리가 고국 땅으로 다시 돌아가게 되면요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"당신이 그토록 감명 깊게 하신 말씀을 제가 기억하고 이해할 수 있는 만큼은 크런처 부인께 전해드릴 테니 믿으셔도 좋아요. 그리고 어쨌든 이 끔찍한 때에 당신이 정말 진심이었다는 걸 제가 증언해드릴 테니 안심하세요. 자, 이제 좀 생각해봅시다! 존경하는 크런처 씨, 우리 생각해봐요!\"\n\n여전히 드파르주 부인은 거리를 따라 나아가며, 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 먼저 가서요,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"탈것과 말이 이리로 오지 못하게 막고, 어디선가 저를 기다린다면요, 그게 제일 낫지 않을까요?\"\n\n크런처 씨는 그게 제일 나을 것 같다고 생각했다.\n\n\"어디서 저를 기다리시겠어요?\" 프로스 양이 물었다.\n\n크런처 씨는 어찌나 어리둥절했는지 템플 바 말고는 떠오르는 장소가 없었다. 아아! 템플 바는 수백 마일이나 떨어진 곳이었고, 드파르주 부인은 정말이지 아주 가까이 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"대성당 문 앞에서요.\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"두 탑 사이에 있는 그 큰 대성당 문 가까이에서 저를 태운다면, 길에서 그리 많이 벗어나진 않겠지요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 아가씨.\" 크런처 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼, 훌륭한 양반답게,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"곧장 역마차 정거장으로 가서 그 교환을 해주세요.\"\n\n\"미덥지가 않아서요,\" 크런처 씨가 머뭇거리며 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아가씨를 두고 가는 게 말입니다요. 무슨 일이 생길지 모르잖습니까.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이 생길지 모르는 건 하느님은 아시지요.\" 프로스 양이 대답했다. \"하지만 제 걱정은 마세요. 세시에, 아니면 거기에 최대한 가까운 시각에 대성당에서 저를 태워주세요. 그게 우리가 여기서 떠나는 것보다 분명 나을 거예요. 확신해요. 자! 당신께 축복이 있기를, 크런처 씨! 저는 생각 말고, 우리 두 사람에게 달려 있을지도 모를 그 목숨들을 생각하세요!\"\n\n이 서두와, 프로스 양의 두 손이 사뭇 애절하게 그의 손을 움켜쥔 것이 크런처 씨의 마음을 정하게 했다. 그는 격려하듯 한두 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 곧장 채비를 바꾸러 나가면서, 그녀가 제안한 대로 뒤따라오도록 그녀를 홀로 남겨두었다.\n\n이미 실행에 옮겨지고 있는 어떤 대비책을 자기가 발의했다는 사실은 프로스 양에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 거리에서 특별히 눈에 띄지 않도록 자기 차림새를 단정하게 해야 할 필요가 있다는 것도 또 다른 위안이었다. 시계를 보니 두시 이십분이었다. 지체할 시간이 없었고, 당장 채비를 갖춰야 했다.\n\n극도로 마음이 동요한 나머지, 텅 빈 방들의 적막함과, 그 안의 활짝 열린 문마다 그 뒤에서 반쯤 상상으로 빚어낸 얼굴들이 엿보는 것 같은 느낌에 겁이 난 프로스 양은, 찬물을 한 대야 떠다가 퉁퉁 붓고 빨개진 눈을 적시기 시작했다. 열에 들뜬 듯한 불안에 시달린 그녀는, 단 한순간이라도 떨어지는 물에 시야가 가려지는 것을 견딜 수 없어, 끊임없이 멈추고 둘러보며 자기를 지켜보는 이가 없는지 확인했다. 그렇게 멈추었던 어느 순간, 그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러서며 비명을 질렀으니, 방 안에 한 사람이 서 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n대야가 바닥에 떨어져 깨지고, 물이 드파르주 부인의 발치로 흘러갔다. 기이하고 가혹한 길을 거쳐, 숱한 피로 얼룩진 길을 지나, 그 두 발은 그 물과 마주하기에 이른 것이었다.\n\n드파르주 부인이 그녀를 차갑게 바라보며 말했다. \"에브레몽드의 아내, 그 여자는 어디 있지?\"\n\n프로스 양의 머릿속에 문이 죄다 열려 있어서 그들이 달아났음을 짐작하게 하리라는 생각이 번뜩 스쳤다. 그녀가 가장 먼저 한 일은 문을 닫는 것이었다. 방에는 문이 넷 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 모두 닫았다. 그런 다음 루시가 쓰던 방의 문 앞에 자기 몸을 가져다 세웠다.\n\n드파르주 부인의 검은 눈은 이 재빠른 동작을 내내 좇았고, 그것이 끝나자 그녀에게 머물렀다. 프로스 양에게는 아름다운 구석이라고는 하나도 없었다. 세월도 그 사나운 인상을 길들이지도, 그 험상궂은 모습을 누그러뜨리지도 못했다. 그러나 그녀 역시 나름의 방식으로 단호한 여자였고, 그녀도 한 치 한 치 눈으로 드파르주 부인을 가늠했다.\n\n\"당신은 그 꼴을 보아하니, 루시퍼의 아내쯤 되겠구려.\" 프로스 양이 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"그래도, 당신이 날 이기지는 못해. 나는 영국 여자야.\"\n\n드파르주 부인은 그녀를 경멸스럽게 바라보면서도, 두 사람이 막다른 처지에 몰렸다는 것을 프로스 양과 마찬가지로 어느 정도는 알아챘다. 그녀는 자기 앞에 옹골차고 단단하며 깡마른 여자가 있는 것을 보았으니, 지난날 로리 씨가 바로 그 모습에서 억센 손을 가진 여자를 보았던 것과 같았다. 그녀는 프로스 양이 그 가족의 헌신적인 친구임을 잘 알고 있었고, 프로스 양은 드파르주 부인이 그 가족의 악의에 찬 적임을 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"저기로 가는 길에 말이야.\" 드파르주 부인이 손을 그 운명의 장소 쪽으로 살짝 움직이며 말했다. \"거기 사람들이 내 의자와 내 뜨개질감을 챙겨두는 그곳으로 말이지, 지나는 길에 그 여자한테 인사나 하려고 왔어. 그 여자를 좀 봐야겠어.\"\n\n\"당신 속셈이 사악하다는 걸 알아.\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"그러니 똑똑히 알아둬, 나는 그 속셈에 맞서 끝까지 버틸 거야.\"\n\n각자 자기 언어로 말했으니, 어느 쪽도 상대의 말을 알아듣지 못했다. 둘 다 몹시 경계하며, 그 알아들을 수 없는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 표정과 태도에서 헤아려내려고 골몰했다.\n\n\"이 마당에 나한테서 몸을 숨겨봐야 그 여자한테 이로울 게 없을 텐데.\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다. \"선량한 애국자라면 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알 거야. 그 여자를 보게 해. 가서 내가 그 여자를 보고 싶어한다고 전해. 알아듣겠어?\"\n\n\"당신의 그 두 눈이 침대 죔쇠라 해도,\" 프로스 양이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 내가 영국제 사주식 침대라 해도, 내 몸에서 나무 부스러기 하나 풀어내지 못할 거야. 안 돼, 이 사악한 외국 여편네야. 나는 네 적수야.\"\n\n드파르주 부인이 이런 관용적인 말들을 일일이 알아들었을 리는 없었다. 그러나, 자기가 깡그리 무시당하고 있다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 알아들었다.\n\n\"멍청하고 돼지 같은 여편네!\" 드파르주 부인이 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"너한테서는 아무 대답도 받지 않겠다. 나는 그 여자를 보겠다고 요구하는 거야. 내가 그 여자를 보겠다고 요구한다고 그 여자한테 전하든지, 아니면 그 문 앞에서 비켜서서 내가 그 여자한테 가게 하든지 해!\" 이렇게 말하며, 그녀는 성난 듯 오른팔을 휘둘러 설명하려 했다.\n\n\"나는 꿈에도 생각 못했어.\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"내가 언젠가 네 그 터무니없는 말을 알아듣고 싶어할 줄은. 하지만 입은 옷만 빼고 내가 가진 전부를 다 줘서라도, 네가 진실을, 아니 그 일부라도 눈치챘는지 알고 싶구나.\"\n\n두 사람 모두 단 한순간도 서로의 눈에서 시선을 떼지 않았다. 드파르주 부인은 프로스 양이 처음 그녀를 알아챘을 때 서 있던 자리에서 꼼짝도 하지 않았었다. 그러나, 이제 그녀는 한 걸음 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n\"나는 영국 사람이야.\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"나는 죽기를 각오했어. 내 한 몸 따위는 영국 동전 2펜스만큼도 아깝지 않아. 내가 너를 여기 오래 붙들어둘수록, 우리 아가씨한테는 그만큼 희망이 커진다는 걸 알아. 네가 내게 손가락 하나라도 댄다면, 네 머리에 그 검은 머리카락 한 줌도 남겨두지 않을 테다!\"\n\n이렇게 프로스 양은, 빠르게 한 문장 한 문장 내뱉을 때마다 고개를 한 번씩 내젓고 두 눈을 번뜩이며, 그 빠른 문장 하나하나에 숨을 통째로 실어 말했다. 이렇게 프로스 양은, 평생 단 한 번도 누구를 때려본 적 없는 그 프로스 양은 말했다.\n\n그러나, 그녀의 용기는 그런 감정적인 부류여서, 걷잡을 수 없이 눈물이 두 눈에 차오르게 만들었다. 이것은 드파르주 부인이 너무도 이해하지 못한 나머지 나약함으로 오해할 만한 용기였다. \"하, 하!\" 그녀가 웃었다. \"이 가엾은 것아! 네까짓 게 무슨 가치가 있다고! 나는 그 의사한테 말을 거는 거야.\" 그런 다음 그녀는 목소리를 높여 외쳤다. \"의사 시민! 에브레몽드의 아내! 에브레몽드의 자식! 이 가련한 바보 말고 누구든, 시민 드파르주에게 대답하라!\"\n\n뒤이은 침묵 때문이었는지, 프로스 양의 표정에 어린 무언가 숨은 단서 때문이었는지, 아니면 그 어느 쪽 암시와도 무관한 어떤 불현듯한 의구심 때문이었는지, 무언가가 드파르주 부인에게 그들이 가버렸다고 속삭였다. 그녀는 문 셋을 잽싸게 열어젖히고 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"방들이 죄다 어질러졌고, 황급히 짐을 꾸린 흔적이 있고, 바닥에 자질구레한 것들이 널려 있군. 네 뒤에 있는 저 방에는 아무도 없을 텐데! 어디 좀 보자.\"\n\n\"절대 안 돼!\" 프로스 양이 말했으니, 그녀는 그 요구를, 드파르주 부인이 그 대답을 알아들은 것만큼이나 정확히 알아들었다.\n\n\"저 방에 그들이 없다면, 가버린 거고, 그렇다면 뒤쫓아 다시 잡아올 수 있어.\" 드파르주 부인이 속으로 말했다.\n\n\"네가 그들이 저 방에 있는지 없는지 모르는 한, 너는 어떻게 해야 할지 확신을 못할 테지.\" 프로스 양이 속으로 말했다. \"그리고 내가 네가 알아내는 걸 막을 수 있는 한, 너는 그걸 알지 못할 거야. 그걸 알든 모르든, 내가 너를 붙들 수 있는 한 너는 여기서 나가지 못해.\"\n\n\"나는 처음부터 거리를 누볐고, 아무것도 나를 막지 못했어. 너를 갈가리 찢어서라도, 저 문에서 너를 떼어내고야 말겠다.\" 드파르주 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 외딴 안마당에 선 어느 높다란 집 꼭대기에 단둘이 있고, 우리 소리는 누구한테도 들릴 리 없으니, 내가 너를 여기 붙들어둘 몸의 힘을 달라고 기도하마. 네가 여기 있는 매 순간이 우리 아가씨한테는 십만 기니의 값어치가 있으니까.\" 프로스 양이 말했다.\n\n드파르주 부인이 문으로 달려들었다. 프로스 양은 그 순간의 본능으로, 두 팔로 그녀의 허리를 끌어안고 꽉 붙들었다. 드파르주 부인이 몸부림치고 후려쳐봐도 소용없었다. 프로스 양은, 증오보다 늘 훨씬 강한 사랑의 그 억센 끈기로, 그녀를 단단히 끌어안고, 둘이 벌인 몸싸움에서 심지어 그녀를 바닥에서 들어올리기까지 했다. 드파르주 부인의 두 손이 그녀의 얼굴을 후려치고 할퀴었다. 그러나, 프로스 양은 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀의 허리를 끌어안고, 물에 빠진 여자가 매달리는 것보다 더 악착같이 그녀에게 매달렸다.\n\n이윽고, 드파르주 부인의 두 손이 후려치기를 멈추고, 그녀에게 감겨 있는 자기 허리께를 더듬었다. \"그건 내 팔 밑에 있어.\" 프로스 양이 숨죽인 목소리로 말했다. \"너는 그걸 뽑지 못해. 내가 너보다 더 세거든, 하느님께 그 점을 감사드린다. 우리 둘 중 하나가 기절하거나 죽을 때까지 너를 붙들고 있을 거야!\"\n\n드파르주 부인의 두 손이 그녀의 가슴팍에 가 닿았다. 프로스 양은 고개를 들어, 그것이 무엇인지 보고는, 그것을 후려쳤고, 한 줄기 섬광과 한 차례 굉음을 후려쳐 일으키고는, 홀로 서 있었다—연기에 눈이 먼 채로.\n\n이 모든 것이 순식간에 일어났다. 연기가 걷히며 무시무시한 정적을 남기자, 그 연기는, 바닥에 시신이 되어 누워 있는 그 격노한 여인의 영혼처럼, 허공으로 흩어져 나갔다.\n\n자기 처지에 대한 처음의 공포와 경악에 휩싸인 프로스 양은, 그 시신에서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져 그 곁을 지나, 계단을 뛰어 내려가 헛되이 도움을 청하려 했다. 다행히, 그녀는 자기가 하려는 짓의 결과를 제때 떠올리고는, 스스로를 멈춰 세우고 되돌아갔다. 그 문으로 다시 들어가는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다. 그러나, 그녀는 안으로 들어갔고, 심지어 그 곁까지 다가가, 꼭 써야 할 보닛과 그밖의 것들을 챙겼다. 그녀는 먼저 문을 닫아 잠그고 열쇠를 빼낸 다음, 그것들을 계단참에서 몸에 걸쳤다. 그런 다음 잠시 계단에 앉아 숨을 고르고 울고는, 일어나서 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n다행히도 그녀의 보닛에는 베일이 달려 있었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 거리를 따라가다가 제지당하지 않고는 도저히 갈 수 없었으리라. 또한 다행히도, 그녀는 본디 생김새가 하도 유별나서 다른 여느 여자처럼 흉한 자국을 드러내지 않았다. 그녀에게는 그 두 가지 이점이 다 필요했으니, 얼굴에는 움켜쥐었던 손가락 자국이 깊이 패었고, 머리는 헝클어졌으며, (떨리는 손으로 황급히 추슬렀던) 옷은 사방으로 움켜쥐어지고 잡아당겨져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n다리를 건너면서, 그녀는 그 문 열쇠를 강물에 떨어뜨렸다. 호위병보다 몇 분 먼저 대성당에 다다라 거기서 기다리던 중에, 그녀는 이런 생각을 했다. 그 열쇠가 벌써 그물에 걸려 올라왔으면 어쩌나, 그게 누구 것인지 밝혀졌으면 어쩌나, 그 문이 열려 시신이 발견되었으면 어쩌나, 내가 성문에서 붙들려 감옥에 끌려가 살인죄로 기소되면 어쩌나! 이렇게 마음이 뒤숭숭한 생각들에 잠겨 있는데, 호위병이 나타나, 그녀를 태우고는, 데려갔다.\n\n\"거리가 소란스럽나요?\" 그녀가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"여느 때 소란이지요.\" 크런처 씨가 대답하고는, 그 질문과 그녀의 행색에 놀란 듯한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"안 들려요.\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"뭐라고 하셨어요?\"\n\n크런처 씨가 자기가 한 말을 되풀이해봐야 소용없었으니, 프로스 양은 그의 말을 들을 수 없었던 것이다. \"그럼 고개를 끄덕여야지.\" 깜짝 놀란 크런처 씨가 생각했다. \"어쨌든 그건 보겠지.\" 과연 그녀는 보았다.\n\n\"지금 거리가 소란스러운가요?\" 프로스 양이 잠시 후 다시 물었다.\n\n크런처 씨가 또다시 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"안 들려요.\"\n\n\"한 시간 만에 귀가 먹었나?\" 마음이 몹시 어지러워진 크런처 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"이 양반한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거지?\"\n\n\"느낌이,\" 프로스 양이 말했다. \"한 줄기 섬광과 한 차례 굉음이 있었던 것 같고, 그 굉음이 제가 이 생에서 듣게 될 마지막 소리인 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"이거야 원, 이 양반 상태가 이상하잖아!\" 점점 더 마음이 어지러워진 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"용기를 북돋우려고 뭘 마셨길래 이런담? 들어봐요! 저 끔찍한 수레들이 굴러가는 소리가 들리잖아요! 그건 들리지요, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"제게는 들리지 않아요.\" 그가 자기한테 말하는 것을 알아챈 프로스 양이 말했다. \"아무것도요. 오, 착한 양반, 처음에 큰 굉음이 있었고, 그다음에 큰 정적이 왔는데, 그 정적이 못박힌 듯 변함없이 자리 잡아서, 제 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 다시는 깨지지 않을 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"이제 그 여정이 거의 끝나가는 저 끔찍한 수레들이 굴러가는 소리도 이 양반이 못 듣는다면,\" 크런처 씨가 어깨 너머로 흘끗 돌아보며 말했다. \"내 생각엔 정말이지 이 양반은 이 세상에서 다시는 그 무엇도 듣지 못할 거야.\"\n\n그리고 과연 그녀는 다시는 듣지 못했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XV. The Footsteps Die Out For Ever\n\n빠리의 거리를 따라 죽음의 수레들이 공허하고 거칠게 덜컹거리며 굴러간다. 여섯 대의 호송 수레가 그날의 포도주를 라 기요띤으로 실어 나른다. 상상이 스스로를 기록할 수 있게 된 이래로 상상해온 모든 게걸스럽고 만족을 모르는 괴물들이 단 하나의 실체, 곧 기요띤으로 녹아들었다. 그러나 토양과 기후가 풍요롭게 다양한 프랑스에도, 이 공포를 만들어낸 조건들보다 더 확실한 조건 아래서 무르익도록 자라날 풀잎 하나, 나뭇잎 하나, 뿌리 하나, 가지 하나, 후추 한 알도 없다. 비슷한 망치들 아래 인간성을 다시 한번 형체를 잃도록 으깨어보라, 그러면 그것은 똑같이 고통받는 모습으로 비틀려갈 것이다. 탐욕스러운 방종과 압제의 똑같은 씨앗을 다시 뿌려보라, 그러면 그것은 그 종류대로 반드시 똑같은 열매를 맺을 것이다.\n\n여섯 대의 호송 수레가 거리를 따라 굴러간다. 힘센 마법사인 시간이여, 이것들을 본래의 모습으로 되돌려놓아보라, 그러면 그것들이 절대군주의 마차였고, 봉건 귀족의 마차였고, 화려한 이세벨들의 화장대였고, 내 아버지의 집이 아니라 도둑들의 소굴이었던 교회였고, 굶주린 수백만 농민의 오두막이었음이 보이리라! 아니다. 창조주가 정하신 질서를 장엄하게 이루어가는 저 위대한 마법사는 결코 자신의 변형을 되돌리지 않는다. 지혜로운 아라비아 이야기 속에서, 예언자들은 마법에 걸린 자에게 이렇게 말한다. \"그대가 신의 뜻으로 이 모습으로 변했다면, 그대로 머물러 있으라! 그러나 그대가 한낱 스쳐 지나가는 주문으로 이 형상을 걸쳤을 뿐이라면, 그대의 본래 모습으로 돌아가라!\" 변함도 없고 희망도 없이, 호송 수레들은 굴러간다.\n\n여섯 대의 수레의 음울한 바퀴가 돌아가면서, 거리에 모인 군중 사이로 길고 구불구불한 고랑을 가는 것처럼 보인다. 얼굴들의 이랑이 이쪽저쪽으로 던져지고, 쟁기는 꾸준히 앞으로 나아간다. 그 집들의 붙박이 주민들은 이 광경에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 많은 창문에는 사람이 없고, 어떤 창문에서는 눈으로 호송 수레 속의 얼굴들을 훑어보면서도 손놀림조차 멈추지 않는다. 여기저기에서 그 광경을 구경하러 온 손님을 맞은 주민이 있다. 그러면 그는 박물관 관리인이나 공인된 해설가 같은 흡족함을 띠고 이 수레와 저 수레를 손가락으로 가리키며, 어제는 누가 여기 앉아 있었고 그제는 누가 저기 앉아 있었는지 일러주는 것 같다.\n\n호송 수레에 탄 자들 가운데 어떤 이들은 이런 것들을, 그리고 자신들의 마지막 길가에 있는 모든 것을 무표정한 눈길로 바라본다. 또 어떤 이들은 삶과 사람들의 방식에 미련을 둔 채 바라본다. 어떤 이들은 고개를 떨군 채 앉아 말없는 절망에 잠겨 있다. 또다른 이들은 자기 모습에 너무 신경을 쓴 나머지, 극장이나 그림에서 본 듯한 눈길을 군중에게 던진다. 몇몇은 눈을 감고 생각에 잠기거나, 흩어진 생각을 한데 모으려 애쓴다. 오직 한 사람, 정신이 나간 듯한 가련한 존재만이 공포로 산산이 부서지고 취해버려, 노래를 부르고 춤을 추려 한다. 그 전부 가운데 어느 누구도 눈길이나 몸짓으로 사람들의 동정에 호소하지 않는다.\n\n호송 수레들과 나란히 말을 탄 갖가지 기병들로 이루어진 호위대가 따라가고, 얼굴들이 종종 그들 가운데 몇몇을 향해 쳐들어지며, 그들에게 무언가를 묻는다. 늘 똑같은 질문인 듯한데, 그 질문 뒤에는 언제나 사람들이 세번째 수레 쪽으로 밀려들기 때문이다. 그 수레와 나란히 가는 기병들은 자주 칼로 그 안의 한 남자를 가리킨다. 가장 큰 호기심은 누가 그 사람인지 아는 것이다. 그는 호송 수레 뒤쪽에 서서 고개를 숙인 채, 수레 옆에 앉아 자기 손을 잡고 있는 한 어린 처녀와 이야기를 나눈다. 그는 주위의 광경에 아무런 호기심도 관심도 없이, 줄곧 그 처녀에게만 말한다. 쌩또노레의 긴 거리 여기저기에서 그를 향해 고함이 터져나온다. 그것이 그를 조금이라도 움직인다면, 그는 그저 조용한 미소를 지으며 얼굴 위로 머리카락을 조금 더 느슨하게 흔들 뿐이다. 두 팔이 묶여 있어 얼굴을 쉬이 만질 수 없는 것이다.\n\n호송 수레가 다가오기를 기다리며, 어느 교회의 계단 위에 그 첩자, 곧 감옥의 양이 서 있다. 그는 첫번째 수레를 들여다본다. 거기에 없다. 두번째 수레를 들여다본다. 거기에 없다. 그는 벌써 \"저자가 나를 희생시킨 것일까?\" 하고 자문하다가, 세번째 수레를 들여다보고는 얼굴이 환해진다.\n\n\"어느 자가 에브레몽드요?\" 그의 뒤에 있던 남자가 말한다.\n\n\"저 사람이오. 저기 뒤쪽에.\"\n\n\"처녀의 손을 잡고 있는?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n남자가 외친다. \"처형하라, 에브레몽드! 모든 귀족을 기요띤으로! 처형하라, 에브레몽드!\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿!\" 첩자가 겁먹은 듯 그에게 간청한다.\n\n\"왜 안되오, 시민?\"\n\n\"저자는 죗값을 치르러 가는 길이오. 앞으로 오 분이면 다 치를 거요. 그냥 평온하게 내버려두시오.\"\n\n그러나 남자가 계속해서 \"처형하라, 에브레몽드!\" 하고 외치자, 에브레몽드의 얼굴이 잠시 그를 향해 돌려진다. 그러자 에브레몽드는 그 첩자를 보고, 그를 유심히 바라보더니, 다시 제 길을 간다.\n\n시계는 세시를 칠 참이고, 군중 사이로 갈아놓은 고랑은 방향을 틀어, 처형장 안으로 들어와 끝을 맺으려 한다. 이쪽저쪽으로 던져졌던 이랑들이, 이제 지나가는 마지막 쟁기 뒤로 허물어져 모여드니, 모두가 기요띤을 향해 따라가기 때문이다. 그 앞에는, 마치 공공 유원지의 정원에서처럼, 의자에 앉은 여인들이 여럿 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있다. 맨 앞줄 의자 가운데 하나에는 복수의 여신이 서서 자기 친구를 두리번두리번 찾고 있다.\n\n\"떼레즈!\" 그녀가 날카로운 목소리로 외친다. \"그 사람 본 사람 있어? 떼레즈 드파르주!\"\n\n\"저 사람은 전에 한번도 빠진 적이 없는데.\" 같은 자매단의 한 뜨개질하는 여인이 말한다.\n\n\"맞아. 이번에도 빠질 리 없지.\" 복수의 여신이 짜증스럽게 외친다. \"떼레즈.\"\n\n\"더 크게 불러봐.\" 여인이 권한다.\n\n아아! 더 크게, 복수의 여신이여, 훨씬 더 크게 불러도, 그래도 그녀는 그대의 소리를 거의 듣지 못하리라. 더욱더 크게, 복수의 여신이여, 욕설이라도 한두 마디 보태어 불러도, 그래도 그녀를 데려오기는 어려우리라. 다른 여인들을 이리저리 보내어, 어딘가에서 꾸물거리고 있을 그녀를 찾게 해보라. 그러나 그 심부름꾼들이 끔찍한 짓들을 저질러온 자들이라 한들, 제 발로 그녀를 찾을 만큼 멀리까지 갈지는 의문이로다!\n\n\"운도 없지!\" 복수의 여신이 의자 위에서 발을 구르며 외친다. \"벌써 호송 수레가 왔잖아! 에브레몽드는 눈 깜짝할 새에 처치될 텐데, 그 사람은 여기 없다니! 봐, 내 손에 저 사람 뜨개질감이 있고, 빈 의자도 저 사람 자리로 준비돼 있는데. 분하고 실망스러워서 소리라도 지르고 싶어!\"\n\n복수의 여신이 그렇게 하려고 높은 자리에서 내려오는 사이, 호송 수레들이 그 짐을 부려놓기 시작한다. 성녀 기요띤의 사제들은 예복을 갖추고 채비를 마쳤다. 쾅! 머리 하나가 들어올려지고, 조금 전 그것이 생각하고 말할 수 있었을 때는 거의 눈길조차 들어 쳐다보지 않던 뜨개질하는 여인들이 하나, 하고 센다.\n\n두번째 호송 수레가 비워지고 앞으로 나아간다. 세번째 수레가 다가온다. 쾅! 그러자 뜨개질하는 여인들은 일손을 한번도 더듬거나 멈추는 일 없이, 둘, 하고 센다.\n\n에브레몽드로 여겨지는 자가 내려오고, 그다음으로 그 재봉사 처녀가 들려 내려진다. 그는 내리면서도 그녀의 참을성 있는 손을 놓지 않은 채, 약속한 대로 여전히 그 손을 잡고 있다. 그는 끊임없이 윙윙거리며 올라갔다 떨어지는 그 우당탕거리는 기계를 등지도록 그녀를 가만히 세워주고, 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그에게 고마워한다.\n\n\"낯선 분이지만 당신이 아니었다면, 저는 이렇게 침착하지 못했을 거예요. 저는 본디 가엾고 보잘것없는 데다 마음이 약한 것이라서요. 또 우리가 오늘 여기서 희망과 위안을 얻을 수 있도록 죽임을 당하신 그분께로 제 생각을 들어올리지도 못했을 거예요. 당신은 하늘이 제게 보내주신 분 같아요.\"\n\n\"아니면 당신이 내게 보내진 것이거나.\" 시드니 카턴이 말한다. \"내게서 눈을 떼지 마시오, 사랑스러운 아이여, 다른 어떤 것에도 마음 쓰지 말고.\"\n\n\"당신 손을 잡고 있는 한 저는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않아요. 이 손을 놓더라도, 그들이 빠르게만 해준다면, 저는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"빠를 거요. 두려워 마시오!\"\n\n두 사람은 빠르게 줄어드는 희생자들의 무리 가운데 서 있지만, 마치 단둘만 있는 듯 이야기를 나눈다. 눈과 눈을 맞추고, 목소리와 목소리를 맞추고, 손과 손을 맞잡고, 가슴과 가슴을 맞댄 채, 그러지 않았다면 그토록 멀리 떨어진 채 서로 달랐을 만물의 어머니의 이 두 자식이, 함께 집으로 돌아가 그 품에서 쉬려고, 어두운 한길 위에서 함께 만난 것이다.\n\n\"용감하고 너그러운 친구여, 마지막으로 한 가지만 여쭤봐도 될까요? 저는 아주 무지한 데다, 그게 저를 괴롭혀요. 그저 조금요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇인지 말해보시오.\"\n\n\"제겐 사촌이 하나 있어요. 저처럼 단 하나뿐인 혈육이고 고아인데, 제가 무척 사랑하는 아이예요. 저보다 다섯 살 어리고, 남쪽 지방의 어느 농가에서 살고 있어요. 가난이 우리를 갈라놓았고, 그 애는 제 운명에 대해 아무것도 몰라요. 저는 글을 쓸 줄 모르니까요. 또 쓸 줄 안다 한들, 어떻게 그 애에게 알리겠어요! 이대로가 더 나아요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그래요. 이대로가 더 낫소.\"\n\n\"우리가 여기로 오는 동안 제가 줄곧 생각하던 것, 그리고 제게 이토록 큰 힘을 주는 당신의 다정하고 굳센 얼굴을 올려다보는 지금도 여전히 생각하고 있는 것은 이거예요. 만약 공화국이 정말로 가난한 사람들에게 좋은 일을 해주어서, 그 애가 덜 굶주리게 되고 모든 면에서 덜 고통받게 된다면, 그 애는 오래 살지도 몰라요. 어쩌면 늙도록 살 수도 있겠지요.\"\n\n\"그러면 어떻다는 거요, 상냥한 누이여?\"\n\n\"그러면 이런 생각이 들어요.\" 그토록 많은 인내가 담긴 불평 없는 눈에 눈물이 차오르고, 입술이 조금 더 벌어지며 떨린다. \"당신과 제가 둘 다 자비롭게 보살핌받으리라 믿는 그 더 나은 땅에서, 제가 그 애를 기다리는 동안, 그 시간이 제겐 길게 느껴질까요?\"\n\n\"그럴 리 없소, 나의 아이여. 그곳에는 시간이라는 것도 없고, 괴로움도 없으니.\"\n\n\"당신은 제게 정말 큰 위안을 주시는군요! 저는 너무도 무지해요. 이제 당신께 입맞춰도 될까요? 그 순간이 온 건가요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n그녀가 그의 입술에 입맞추고, 그가 그녀의 입술에 입맞추니, 두 사람은 엄숙하게 서로를 축복한다. 그가 그녀의 가냘픈 손을 놓을 때, 그 손은 떨리지 않는다. 그 참을성 있는 얼굴에는 달콤하고 환한 한결같음 말고는 아무것도 깃들어 있지 않다. 그녀가 그보다 먼저 앞으로 나아가고, 사라진다. 뜨개질하는 여인들이 스물둘, 하고 센다.\n\n\"나는 부활이요 생명이니, 주께서 이르시되, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 무릇 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\"\n\n많은 목소리들의 웅성거림, 많은 얼굴들이 쳐들리는 모습, 많은 발소리들이 군중의 가장자리에서 밀려들어, 마치 한차례 거대한 물결처럼 무리가 앞으로 부풀어오르는 것, 이 모든 것이 한순간에 번쩍 사라진다. 스물셋.\n\n그날 밤 그 도시에서 사람들은 그에 대해, 그곳에서 일찍이 본 가장 평화로운 사람의 얼굴이었다고 말했다. 많은 이들은 그가 숭고하고 예언자적으로 보였다고 덧붙였다.\n\n같은 도끼에 스러진 가장 주목할 만한 수난자 가운데 한 사람, 곧 어느 여인은 얼마 전 바로 그 단두대 발치에서, 자신을 사로잡고 있던 생각들을 적어내려갈 수 있게 해달라고 청한 바 있었다. 만약 그가 자신의 생각들을 어떤 말로든 토로했고, 또 그 말들이 예언적인 것이었다면, 그것들은 이러했으리라.\n\n\"나는 본다. 바사드와 클라이, 드파르주, 복수의 여신, 그 배심원, 그 판사, 옛 압제자들의 파멸 위에 새로이 일어선 새 압제자들의 긴 행렬이, 이 응징의 도구가 지금의 쓰임을 다하기 전에 바로 그 도구에 의해 스러져가는 것을. 나는 본다. 이 심연에서 아름다운 도시와 빛나는 사람들이 솟아오르고, 진정 자유로워지려는 그들의 투쟁 속에서, 그들의 승리와 패배 속에서, 앞으로 다가올 긴 세월에 걸쳐, 이 시대의 악과 그 자연스러운 소산인 이전 시대의 악이 차츰 스스로 속죄하며 닳아 없어지는 것을.\n\n\"나는 본다. 내가 목숨을 바치는 그 생명들이, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 저 영국에서 평화롭고 쓸모 있고 번영하며 행복하게 사는 것을. 나는 그녀가 내 이름을 지닌 아이를 품에 안고 있는 것을 본다. 나는 그녀의 아버지가, 나이 들고 허리가 굽었으되 그밖에는 회복되어, 그 치유의 직분으로 만인에게 충실하며 평온하게 지내는 것을 본다. 나는 그토록 오래 그들의 벗이었던 그 선량한 노인이, 십 년 뒤 자신이 가진 모든 것으로 그들을 부유하게 해주고, 평온하게 그의 보상을 받으러 떠나는 것을 본다.\n\n\"나는 본다. 내가 그들의 가슴속에, 그리고 그들의 후손들 가슴속에, 여러 세대가 지나도록 성소를 차지하고 있는 것을. 나는 그녀가 늙은 여인이 되어, 이날의 기일마다 나를 위해 우는 것을 본다. 나는 그녀와 그녀의 남편이, 그들의 여정을 마치고, 이 세상에서의 마지막 잠자리에 나란히 누워 있는 것을 보며, 그 둘 가운데 어느 누구도 서로의 영혼 속에서 받은 만큼의 존경과 신성함보다, 내가 그 두 사람의 영혼 속에서 받은 존경과 신성함이 덜하지 않았음을 안다.\n\n\"나는 본다. 그녀의 품에 안겨 내 이름을 지녔던 그 아이가, 한때 나의 것이었던 그 삶의 길에서 자기 길을 헤쳐 올라가는 어른이 된 것을. 나는 그가 그 길을 너무도 훌륭히 헤쳐나가, 그의 빛으로 인해 그곳에서 내 이름이 빛나게 되는 것을 본다. 나는 내가 그 이름 위에 던졌던 오점들이 바래어 사라지는 것을 본다. 나는 본다. 그가, 의로운 판관들과 존경받는 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 사람이 되어, 내가 아는 이마와 금빛 머리카락을 지닌, 내 이름을 가진 사내아이를 이곳으로—오늘의 흉한 자취라곤 하나도 없이 바라보기에 아름다운 이곳으로—데려오는 것을. 그리고 나는 그가 다정하고 떨리는 목소리로 그 아이에게 내 이야기를 들려주는 것을 듣는다.\n\n\"내가 지금 하는 일은, 내가 일찍이 한 그 어떤 일보다도 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 일이다. 내가 지금 가서 누릴 안식은, 내가 일찍이 알던 그 어떤 안식보다도 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 안식이다.\"";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-98"]=[[["It was the best of times, it was the worst of times, it was the age of wisdom, it was the age of foolishness, it was the epoch of belief, it was the epoch of incredulity, it was the season of Light, it was the season of Darkness, it was the spring of hope, it was the winter of despair, we had everything before us, we had nothing before us, we were all going direct to Heaven, we were all going direct the other way--in short, the period was so far like the present period, that some of its noisiest authorities insisted on its being received, for good or for evil, in the superlative degree of comparison only.", "최고의 시절이자 최악의 시절이었고, 지혜의 시대이자 어리석음의 시대였으며, 믿음의 세기이자 불신의 세기였고, 빛의 계절이자 어둠의 계절이었으며, 희망의 봄이자 절망의 겨울이었다. 우리 앞에는 모든 것이 있었고, 우리 앞에는 아무것도 없었다. 우리는 모두 곧장 천국으로 가고 있었고, 우리는 모두 곧장 그 반대편으로 가고 있었다. 요컨대 그 시절은 지금의 시절과 너무도 닮아서, 당대의 떠들썩한 권위자들 가운데 일부는 그 시절을 좋게든 나쁘게든 오직 최상급의 비교로만 받아들여야 한다고 우겼다."]], [["There were a king with a large jaw and a queen with a plain face, on the throne of England; there were a king with a large jaw and a queen with a fair face, on the throne of France.", "영국의 왕좌에는 턱이 큰 왕과 얼굴이 평범한 왕비가 앉아 있었고, 프랑스의 왕좌에는 턱이 큰 왕과 얼굴이 고운 왕비가 앉아 있었다."], ["In both countries it was clearer than crystal to the lords of the State preserves of loaves and fishes, that things in general were settled for ever.", "두 나라 모두에서, 빵과 물고기로 이루어진 국가의 특권을 누리는 영주들에게는 만사가 영원히 정해져 있다는 것이 수정보다도 명백했다."]], [["It was the year of Our Lord one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five.", "때는 주님의 해 1775년이었다."], ["Spiritual revelations were conceded to England at that favoured period, as at this.", "영적인 계시는 지금처럼 그 은혜로운 시절에도 영국에 베풀어졌다."], ["Mrs. Southcott had recently attained her five-and-twentieth blessed birthday, of whom a prophetic private in the Life Guards had heralded the sublime appearance by announcing that arrangements were made for the swallowing up of London and Westminster.", "사우스코트 부인은 최근 스물다섯번째 축복받은 생일을 맞이했는데, 근위대의 어느 예언자적 사병이 런던과 웨스트민스터가 삼켜질 채비가 되어 있다고 선포함으로써 그녀의 숭고한 출현을 미리 알린 바 있었다."], ["Even the Cock-lane ghost had been laid only a round dozen of years, after rapping out its messages, as the spirits of this very year last past (supernaturally deficient in originality) rapped out theirs.", "콕레인의 유령조차 메시지를 두드려 전한 뒤 겨우 열두 해 남짓 잠들어 있었을 뿐인데, 바로 지난해의 영들도 (초자연적으로 독창성이 결여된 채) 똑같이 자기네 메시지를 두드려 전했다."], ["Mere messages in the earthly order of events had lately come to the English Crown and People, from a congress of British subjects in America: which, strange to relate, have proved more important to the human race than any communications yet received through any of the chickens of the Cock-lane brood.", "한낱 지상의 사건들 가운데서 온 메시지들은 최근 아메리카에 모인 영국 신민들의 의회로부터 영국 왕실과 국민에게 전해졌는데, 이상하게도 그것은 콕레인 무리의 어떤 병아리들을 통해 지금껏 받은 그 어떤 통신보다도 인류에게 더 중대한 것으로 판명되었다."]], [["France, less favoured on the whole as to matters spiritual than her sister of the shield and trident, rolled with exceeding smoothness down hill, making paper money and spending it.", "방패와 삼지창을 든 자매 나라보다 영적인 일에서는 대체로 덜 은혜받은 프랑스는 지폐를 찍어내고 또 써대면서 더할 나위 없이 매끄럽게 비탈을 굴러 내려갔다."], ["Under the guidance of her Christian pastors, she entertained herself, besides, with such humane achievements as sentencing a youth to have his hands cut off, his tongue torn out with pincers, and his body burned alive, because he had not kneeled down in the rain to do honour to a dirty procession of monks which passed within his view, at a distance of some fifty or sixty yards.", "게다가 프랑스는 기독교 목자들의 인도 아래, 어느 젊은이가 오십이나 육십 야드쯤 떨어진 곳에서 자기 눈앞을 지나가는 더러운 수도사 행렬에 경의를 표하느라 빗속에서 무릎 꿇지 않았다는 이유로, 그 두 손을 잘리고 펜치로 혀를 뽑히며 산 채로 그 몸을 태워야 한다는 선고를 내리는 따위의 인도적인 위업으로 스스로를 즐겁게 했다."], ["It is likely enough that, rooted in the woods of France and Norway, there were growing trees, when that sufferer was put to death, already marked by the Woodman, Fate, to come down and be sawn into boards, to make a certain movable framework with a sack and a knife in it, terrible in history.", "그 수난자가 처형되던 무렵, 프랑스와 노르웨이의 숲속에 뿌리내린 채 자라나던 나무들이 있었을 법한데, 그 나무들은 이미 운명이라는 나무꾼에게 표시가 되어, 베어 넘어뜨려져 널빤지로 켜지고, 자루와 칼이 달린 어떤 이동식 장치로 만들어질 운명이었으니, 역사 속에서 무시무시한 그것이었다."], ["It is likely enough that in the rough outhouses of some tillers of the heavy lands adjacent to Paris, there were sheltered from the weather that very day, rude carts, bespattered with rustic mire, snuffed about by pigs, and roosted in by poultry, which the Farmer, Death, had already set apart to be his tumbrils of the Revolution.", "빠리에 인접한 무거운 땅을 일구는 어느 농부들의 허름한 헛간에는 바로 그날에도 비바람을 피해 들여놓은 투박한 수레들이 있었을 법한데, 시골 진흙이 잔뜩 튀고 돼지들이 코를 킁킁대며 들쑤시고 닭들이 올라앉던 그 수레들은 이미 죽음이라는 농부가 혁명의 호송 수레로 쓰려고 따로 빼두었던 것이었다."], ["But that Woodman and that Farmer, though they work unceasingly, work silently, and no one heard them as they went about with muffled tread: the rather, forasmuch as to entertain any suspicion that they were awake, was to be atheistical and traitorous.", "그러나 그 나무꾼과 그 농부는 쉼 없이 일하면서도 소리 없이 일했고, 발소리를 죽여 다니는 그들을 아무도 듣지 못했다. 더욱이 그들이 깨어 있으리라는 의심을 품는 것 자체가 무신론적이고 반역적인 일이었기 때문이다."]], [["In England, there was scarcely an amount of order and protection to justify much national boasting. Daring burglaries by armed men, and highway robberies, took place in the capital itself every night; families were publicly cautioned not to go out of town without removing their furniture to upholsterers’ warehouses for security; the highwayman in the dark was a City tradesman in the light, and, being recognised and challenged by his fellow-tradesman whom he stopped in his character of “the Captain,” gallantly shot him through the head and rode away; the mail was waylaid by seven robbers, and the guard shot three dead, and then got shot dead himself by the other four, “in consequence of the failure of his ammunition:” after which the mail was robbed in peace; that magnificent potentate, the Lord Mayor of London, was made to stand and deliver on Turnham Green, by one highwayman, who despoiled the illustrious creature in sight of all his retinue; prisoners in London gaols fought battles with their turnkeys, and the majesty of the law fired blunderbusses in among them, loaded with rounds of shot and ball; thieves snipped off diamond crosses from the necks of noble lords at Court drawing-rooms; musketeers went into St. Giles’s, to search for contraband goods, and the mob fired on the musketeers, and the musketeers fired on the mob, and nobody thought any of these occurrences much out of the common way. In the midst of them, the hangman, ever busy and ever worse than useless, was in constant requisition; now, stringing up long rows of miscellaneous criminals; now, hanging a housebreaker on Saturday who had been taken on Tuesday; now, burning people in the hand at Newgate by the dozen, and now burning pamphlets at the door of Westminster Hall; to-day, taking the life of an atrocious murderer, and to-morrow of a wretched pilferer who had robbed a farmer’s boy of sixpence.", "영국에서는 국가적 자랑거리로 삼을 만한 질서와 보호가 거의 없었다. 무장한 자들의 대담한 가택 침입과 노상강도가 수도 한복판에서 밤마다 벌어졌다. 가구를 안전하게 보관하려고 가구상의 창고로 옮겨놓지 않은 채 시외로 나가지 말라는 경고가 가족들에게 공공연히 내려졌다. 어둠 속의 노상강도는 밝은 데서는 도시의 상인이었고, \"두목\"이라는 신분으로 길을 막아 세웠던 동료 상인에게 알아보여 도전을 받자, 늠름하게 그 머리에 총을 쏘고는 말을 몰아 달아났다. 우편마차가 강도 일곱에게 습격당해, 호송대원이 그 가운데 셋을 쏘아 죽였으나 \"탄약이 떨어진 탓에\" 나머지 넷에게 자신이 총을 맞아 죽었고, 그 뒤 우편마차는 평온하게 털렸다. 저 위풍당당한 권세가, 런던 시장은 노상강도 하나에게 턴엄 그린에서 멈춰 서서 내놓으라는 협박을 당했으니, 그 강도는 시장의 모든 수행원들이 보는 앞에서 그 저명한 인물을 약탈했다. 런던 감옥의 죄수들은 간수들과 싸움을 벌였고, 법의 위엄은 산탄과 탄환을 잔뜩 재운 나팔총을 그들 한가운데로 쏘아댔다. 도둑들은 궁정 응접실에서 귀족 영주들의 목에서 다이아몬드 십자가를 잘라냈다. 머스킷 총병들이 밀수품을 수색하러 세인트자일스에 들어갔고, 군중은 총병들에게 총을 쏘았으며, 총병들은 군중에게 총을 쏘았으나, 이런 사건들 가운데 그 어느 것도 별스러운 일이라고 여기는 사람은 없었다. 그 한복판에서 늘 분주하고 늘 무익함보다 더 못한 교수형 집행인이 끊임없이 불려 다녔다. 어느 때는 잡다한 범죄자들을 줄줄이 목매달고, 어느 때는 화요일에 잡힌 가택 침입자를 토요일에 목매달고, 어느 때는 뉴게이트에서 사람들의 손바닥을 한꺼번에 열두 명씩 지지고, 어느 때는 웨스트민스터 홀 문간에서 소책자들을 불태웠다. 오늘은 흉악한 살인범의 목숨을 거두고, 내일은 어느 농부의 아이에게서 육 펜스를 훔친 가련한 좀도둑의 목숨을 거두었다."]], [["All these things, and a thousand like them, came to pass in and close upon the dear old year one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five.", "이 모든 일들이, 그리고 그와 비슷한 수천 가지 일들이, 그리운 옛 1775년에, 그리고 그 무렵에 벌어졌다."], ["Environed by them, while the Woodman and the Farmer worked unheeded, those two of the large jaws, and those other two of the plain and the fair faces, trod with stir enough, and carried their divine rights with a high hand.", "그것들에 둘러싸여, 나무꾼과 농부가 아무도 모르게 일하는 동안, 턱이 큰 그 두 사람과 평범한 얼굴 그리고 고운 얼굴의 그 다른 두 사람은 어지간한 소동을 일으키며 거닐었고, 자신들의 신성한 권리를 거만하게 휘둘렀다."], ["Thus did the year one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five conduct their Greatnesses, and myriads of small creatures--the creatures of this chronicle among the rest--along the roads that lay before them.", "이렇게 1775년은 그들 고귀하신 분들과, 그밖의 무수한 미천한 존재들을 — 이 연대기 속 존재들도 그 가운데 끼어 있었다 — 그들 앞에 놓인 길을 따라 이끌어 갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER II. The Mail", "# CHAPTER II. The Mail"]], [["It was the Dover road that lay, on a Friday night late in November, before the first of the persons with whom this history has business.", "11월 말의 어느 금요일 밤, 이 이야기와 관련이 있는 첫번째 인물 앞에 놓인 것은 도버로 가는 길이었다."], ["The Dover road lay, as to him, beyond the Dover mail, as it lumbered up Shooter’s Hill.", "그에게는, 도버로 가는 길이 슈터스힐을 덜컹덜컹 올라가는 도버행 우편마차 너머에 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["He walked up hill in the mire by the side of the mail, as the rest of the passengers did; not because they had the least relish for walking exercise, under the circumstances, but because the hill, and the harness, and the mud, and the mail, were all so heavy, that the horses had three times already come to a stop, besides once drawing the coach across the road, with the mutinous intent of taking it back to Blackheath.", "그는 다른 승객들이 그러하듯 우편마차 곁에서 진창길을 따라 언덕을 걸어 올라갔다. 그런 상황에서 걷는 일에 조금이라도 흥미가 있어서가 아니라, 언덕과 마구와 진흙과 우편마차가 모두 너무 무거워서, 말들이 벌써 세 번이나 멈춰 섰고, 게다가 한번은 마차를 길을 가로질러 끌고 가 블랙히스로 되돌아가려는 반항적인 속셈까지 드러냈기 때문이다."], ["Reins and whip and coachman and guard, however, in combination, had read that article of war which forbade a purpose otherwise strongly in favour of the argument, that some brute animals are endued with Reason; and the team had capitulated and returned to their duty.", "그러나 고삐와 채찍과 마부와 호송대원이 합심하여, 일부 짐승에게도 이성이 깃들어 있다는 주장에 강력한 근거가 될 법한 그 의도를 금지하는 전시법 조항을 읽어주자, 마차를 끌던 말들은 항복하고 제 본분으로 돌아왔다."]], [["With drooping heads and tremulous tails, they mashed their way through the thick mud, floundering and stumbling between whiles, as if they were falling to pieces at the larger joints.", "머리를 떨어뜨리고 꼬리를 부들부들 떨면서, 말들은 진한 진흙을 헤치며 나아갔는데, 이따금 비틀거리고 발을 헛디뎌 큼직한 관절 마디마다 산산이 부서지는 것만 같았다."], ["As often as the driver rested them and brought them to a stand, with a wary “Wo-ho!", "마부가 조심스럽게 \"워-어!"], ["so-ho-then!”", "워-어, 자!\""], ["the near leader violently shook his head and everything upon it--like an unusually emphatic horse, denying that the coach could be got up the hill.", "하며 말들을 쉬게 하고 멈춰 세울 때마다, 왼쪽 선두 말은 머리와 그 위에 달린 모든 것을 사납게 흔들어댔으니, 마치 마차를 언덕 위로 끌어올릴 수 없다고 우기는, 유난히 단호한 말 같았다."], ["Whenever the leader made this rattle, the passenger started, as a nervous passenger might, and was disturbed in mind.", "선두 말이 이렇게 덜그럭거릴 때마다 그 승객은 신경이 예민한 승객이 그러할 법하게 흠칫 놀라며 마음이 어지러워졌다."]], [["There was a steaming mist in all the hollows, and it had roamed in its forlornness up the hill, like an evil spirit, seeking rest and finding none.", "움푹 팬 곳마다 김이 서리는 안개가 자욱했고, 그 안개는 쉴 곳을 찾아 헤매지만 찾지 못하는 악령처럼 쓸쓸하게 언덕 위로 떠돌았다."], ["A clammy and intensely cold mist, it made its slow way through the air in ripples that visibly followed and overspread one another, as the waves of an unwholesome sea might do.", "끈적하고 지독하게 차가운 안개는, 불길한 바다의 물결이 그러하듯, 눈에 보이게 서로를 뒤따르고 뒤덮으며 잔물결을 이루어 공기 속을 느릿느릿 헤쳐나갔다."], ["It was dense enough to shut out everything from the light of the coach-lamps but these its own workings, and a few yards of road; and the reek of the labouring horses steamed into it, as if they had made it all.", "그 안개는 마차 등불의 불빛에서 자기 자신의 움직임과 몇 야드 앞의 길을 빼고는 모든 것을 가려버릴 만큼 짙었다. 힘겹게 끄는 말들에게서 피어오르는 김이 그 안개 속으로 서려들었으니, 마치 그 모든 안개를 그들이 만들어내기라도 한 것 같았다."]], [["Two other passengers, besides the one, were plodding up the hill by the side of the mail.", "그 한 사람 말고도 다른 두 승객이 우편마차 곁에서 터덜터덜 언덕을 올라가고 있었다."], ["All three were wrapped to the cheekbones and over the ears, and wore jack-boots.", "셋 모두 광대뼈까지, 그리고 귀 위까지 몸을 칭칭 감싸고, 목이 긴 장화를 신고 있었다."], ["Not one of the three could have said, from anything he saw, what either of the other two was like; and each was hidden under almost as many wrappers from the eyes of the mind, as from the eyes of the body, of his two companions.", "셋 가운데 어느 누구도 자기가 본 것만으로는 다른 두 사람이 어떻게 생겼는지 말할 수 없었을 것이다. 각자는 두 동행의 육체의 눈에서뿐 아니라 마음의 눈에서도 거의 똑같이 두꺼운 감싸개 아래 숨어 있었다."], ["In those days, travellers were very shy of being confidential on a short notice, for anybody on the road might be a robber or in league with robbers.", "그 시절에는 여행자들이 갑작스레 속을 터놓기를 몹시 꺼렸으니, 길 위의 누구나 강도이거나 강도와 한패일 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["As to the latter, when every posting-house and ale-house could produce somebody in “the Captain’s” pay, ranging from the landlord to the lowest stable non-descript, it was the likeliest thing upon the cards.", "후자로 말하자면, 역참과 선술집마다 주인부터 마구간의 이름 모를 최하층 일꾼에 이르기까지 \"두목\"의 돈을 받는 누군가를 댈 수 있었으니, 그것은 충분히 있을 법한 일이었다."], ["So the guard of the Dover mail thought to himself, that Friday night in November, one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five, lumbering up Shooter’s Hill, as he stood on his own particular perch behind the mail, beating his feet, and keeping an eye and a hand on the arm-chest before him, where a loaded blunderbuss lay at the top of six or eight loaded horse-pistols, deposited on a substratum of cutlass.", "그리하여 1775년 11월의 그 금요일 밤, 슈터스힐을 덜컹덜컹 올라가던 도버행 우편마차의 호송대원은, 우편마차 뒤편 자기만의 특별한 자리에 서서 발을 동동 구르며, 자기 앞에 놓인 무기 상자에서 한시도 눈과 손을 떼지 않은 채 속으로 그렇게 생각했다. 그 상자 안에는 단검을 바닥에 깔고 그 위에 장전된 기병총 여섯 자루인가 여덟 자루를 얹은 다음, 맨 위에 장전된 나팔총 한 자루가 놓여 있었다."]], [["The Dover mail was in its usual genial position that the guard suspected the passengers, the passengers suspected one another and the guard, they all suspected everybody else, and the coachman was sure of nothing but the horses; as to which cattle he could with a clear conscience have taken his oath on the two Testaments that they were not fit for the journey.", "도버행 우편마차는 평소의 화기애애한 처지에 놓여 있었으니, 호송대원은 승객들을 의심하고, 승객들은 서로를 그리고 호송대원을 의심하고, 모두가 다른 모든 이를 의심했으며, 마부는 말들 말고는 그 무엇도 확신하지 못했다. 그 말들로 말하자면, 마부는 그것들이 이번 여정에 적합하지 못하다고 양심에 거리낌 없이 신약과 구약 두 성서에 걸고 맹세할 수 있었다."]], [["“Wo-ho!”", "\"워-어!\""], ["said the coachman.", "마부가 말했다."], ["“So, then!", "\"자, 그래!"], ["One more pull and you’re at the top and be damned to you, for I have had trouble enough to get you to it!--Joe!”", "한번만 더 끌면 꼭대기다, 빌어먹을 것들, 여기까지 오느라 내가 어지간히 애를 먹었으니! — 조!\""]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"여어!\""], ["the guard replied.", "호송대원이 대답했다."]], [["“What o’clock do you make it, Joe?”", "\"지금 몇 시쯤 됐나, 조?\""]], [["“Ten minutes, good, past eleven.”", "\"십일 시 십 분쯤, 잘 잡아서.\""]], [["“My blood!”", "\"제기랄!\""], ["ejaculated the vexed coachman, “and not atop of Shooter’s yet!", "짜증이 난 마부가 내뱉었다. \"아직 슈터스힐 꼭대기도 못 올랐는데!"], ["Tst!", "쯧!"], ["Yah!", "야!"], ["Get on with you!”", "좀 가자!\""]], [["The emphatic horse, cut short by the whip in a most decided negative, made a decided scramble for it, and the three other horses followed suit.", "채찍이 단호하게 가로막으며 안 된다고 못 박자, 그 단호한 말은 단호하게 기어오르기 시작했고, 나머지 세 말도 뒤따랐다."], ["Once more, the Dover mail struggled on, with the jack-boots of its passengers squashing along by its side.", "다시 한번, 도버행 우편마차는 승객들의 목 긴 장화가 그 곁에서 철벅거리며 따라오는 가운데 힘겹게 나아갔다."], ["They had stopped when the coach stopped, and they kept close company with it.", "그들은 마차가 멈출 때 멈췄고, 마차에 바짝 붙어 다녔다."], ["If any one of the three had had the hardihood to propose to another to walk on a little ahead into the mist and darkness, he would have put himself in a fair way of getting shot instantly as a highwayman.", "셋 가운데 누구라도 다른 누구에게 안개와 어둠 속으로 조금 앞서 걸어가자고 대담하게 제안했다면, 노상강도로 몰려 그 자리에서 총에 맞기 십상이었을 것이다."]], [["The last burst carried the mail to the summit of the hill.", "마지막으로 힘껏 끌어 우편마차는 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀다."], ["The horses stopped to breathe again, and the guard got down to skid the wheel for the descent, and open the coach-door to let the passengers in.", "말들은 다시 숨을 돌리려 멈춰 섰고, 호송대원은 내려가 내리막에 대비해 바퀴에 제동장치를 걸고, 승객들을 태우려 마차 문을 열었다."]], [["“Tst!", "\"쯧!"], ["Joe!”", "조!\""], ["cried the coachman in a warning voice, looking down from his box.", "마부가 마부석에서 내려다보며 경고하는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“What do you say, Tom?”", "\"무슨 일인가, 톰?\""]], [["They both listened.", "둘은 함께 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“I say a horse at a canter coming up, Joe.”", "\"말 한 마리가 천천히 달려 올라오는 것 같네, 조.\""]], [["“I say a horse at a gallop, Tom,”", "\"내가 듣기엔 전속력으로 달려오는데, 톰.\""], ["returned the guard, leaving his hold of the door, and mounting nimbly to his place.", "호송대원이 문에서 손을 떼고 잽싸게 제자리로 올라가며 대꾸했다."], ["“Gentlemen!", "\"여러분!"], ["In the king’s name, all of you!”", "국왕의 이름으로, 다들 꼼짝 마시오!\""]], [["With this hurried adjuration, he cocked his blunderbuss, and stood on the offensive.", "이렇게 다급히 명령한 뒤, 그는 나팔총의 공이치기를 당기고 공격 태세를 취했다."]], [["The passenger booked by this history, was on the coach-step, getting in; the two other passengers were close behind him, and about to follow.", "이 이야기에 예약된 승객은 마차 발판에 올라서서 타려던 참이었다. 다른 두 승객은 그의 바로 뒤에 붙어 뒤따라 오르려 하고 있었다."], ["He remained on the step, half in the coach and half out of; they remained in the road below him.", "그는 발판에 그대로 서 있었으니, 몸은 마차 안에 절반, 밖에 절반이 걸쳐 있었다. 두 사람은 그 아래 길에 그대로 서 있었다."], ["They all looked from the coachman to the guard, and from the guard to the coachman, and listened.", "그들은 모두 마부에게서 호송대원에게로, 다시 호송대원에게서 마부에게로 시선을 옮기며 귀를 기울였다."], ["The coachman looked back and the guard looked back, and even the emphatic leader pricked up his ears and looked back, without contradicting.", "마부도 뒤를 돌아보았고 호송대원도 뒤를 돌아보았으며, 심지어 그 단호한 선두 말까지도 귀를 쫑긋 세우고는 거역하지 않고 뒤를 돌아보았다."]], [["The stillness consequent on the cessation of the rumbling and labouring of the coach, added to the stillness of the night, made it very quiet indeed.", "마차의 덜컹거림과 힘겨운 안간힘이 그치면서 찾아온 고요가 밤의 고요에 더해져, 사위는 참으로 적막했다."], ["The panting of the horses communicated a tremulous motion to the coach, as if it were in a state of agitation.", "말들이 헐떡이는 통에 마차에 부르르 떨리는 진동이 전해져, 마치 마차가 동요하고 있는 것 같았다."], ["The hearts of the passengers beat loud enough perhaps to be heard; but at any rate, the quiet pause was audibly expressive of people out of breath, and holding the breath, and having the pulses quickened by expectation.", "승객들의 심장은 어쩌면 들릴 만큼 크게 뛰었을 것이다. 어찌 됐든 그 고요한 정적은 숨을 헐떡이다가 숨을 죽이고 기대로 맥박이 빨라진 사람들의 모습을 또렷이 드러내고 있었다."]], [["The sound of a horse at a gallop came fast and furiously up the hill.", "말 한 마리가 전속력으로 달려오는 소리가 언덕 위로 빠르고 맹렬하게 다가왔다."]], [["“So-ho!”", "\"워-어!\""], ["the guard sang out, as loud as he could roar.", "호송대원이 있는 힘껏 고함쳤다."], ["“Yo there!", "\"거기 너!"], ["Stand!", "서라!"], ["I shall fire!”", "쏘겠다!\""]], [["The pace was suddenly checked, and, with much splashing and floundering, a man’s voice called from the mist, “Is that the Dover mail?”", "말발굽 소리가 갑자기 멎더니, 한참 철벅거리고 허우적대는 가운데 한 남자의 목소리가 안개 속에서 외쳤다. \"그게 도버행 우편마차요?\""]], [["“Never you mind what it is!”", "\"그게 뭐든 네가 알 바 아니다!\""], ["the guard retorted.", "호송대원이 받아쳤다."], ["“What are you?”", "\"너는 누구냐?\""]], [["“Is that the Dover mail?”", "\"그게 도버행 우편마차냐고요?\""]], [["“Why do you want to know?”", "\"왜 알려고 하느냐?\""]], [["“I want a passenger, if it is.”", "\"맞다면, 승객 한 사람을 찾고 있소.\""]], [["“What passenger?”", "\"무슨 승객?\""]], [["“Mr. Jarvis Lorry.”", "\"자비스 로리 씨요.\""]], [["Our booked passenger showed in a moment that it was his name.", "이 이야기에 예약된 우리의 승객이 곧바로 그것이 자기 이름임을 드러냈다."], ["The guard, the coachman, and the two other passengers eyed him distrustfully.", "호송대원과 마부와 다른 두 승객이 그를 미심쩍게 바라보았다."]], [["“Keep where you are,”", "\"있는 자리에 그대로 있어라.\""], ["the guard called to the voice in the mist, “because, if I should make a mistake, it could never be set right in your lifetime.", "호송대원이 안개 속 목소리를 향해 외쳤다. \"내가 실수라도 하면, 네 평생에 다시는 바로잡을 수 없을 테니까."], ["Gentleman of the name of Lorry answer straight.”", "로리라는 이름의 신사분, 곧장 대답하시오.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asked the passenger, then, with mildly quavering speech.", "그러자 승객이 부드럽게 떨리는 말투로 물었다."], ["“Who wants me?", "\"누가 나를 찾는 거요?"], ["Is it Jerry?”", "제리인가?\""]], [["(“I don’t like Jerry’s voice, if it is Jerry,”", "(\"저게 제리라면, 난 제리 목소리가 영 마음에 안 드는데.\""], ["growled the guard to himself.", "호송대원이 속으로 투덜거렸다."], ["“He’s hoarser than suits me, is Jerry.”)", "\"내 취향보다 더 쉰 목소리야, 저 제리란 놈은.\")"]], [["“Yes, Mr. Lorry.”", "\"그렇습니다, 로리 씨.\""]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“A despatch sent after you from over yonder.", "\"저 너머에서 보내온 급보입니다. T."], ["T. and Co.”", "회사에서요.\""]], [["“I know this messenger, guard,” said Mr.", "\"이 전령은 내가 아는 사람이오, 호송대원.\""], ["Lorry, getting down into the road--assisted from behind more swiftly than politely by the other two passengers, who immediately scrambled into the coach, shut the door, and pulled up the window.", "로리 씨가 길로 내려서며 말했다. 다른 두 승객이 뒤에서 정중하다기보다는 잽싸게 그를 거들어 내려보낸 뒤, 곧장 마차 안으로 기어 올라가 문을 닫고 창문을 끌어올렸다."], ["“He may come close; there’s nothing wrong.”", "\"저 사람을 가까이 오게 해도 되오. 잘못된 건 아무것도 없으니.\""]], [["“I hope there ain’t, but I can’t make so ‘Nation sure of that,”", "\"잘못된 게 없기를 바랍니다만, 저로서야 그렇게 빌어먹을 만큼 확신할 수가 없지요.\""], ["said the guard, in gruff soliloquy.", "호송대원이 무뚝뚝하게 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸다."], ["“Hallo you!”", "\"어이, 너!\""]], [["“Well!", "\"여어!"], ["And hallo you!”", "그래, 너도 여어!\""], ["said Jerry, more hoarsely than before.", "제리가 아까보다 더 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Come on at a footpace!", "\"걷는 속도로 와!"], ["d’ye mind me?", "알아들었나?"], ["And if you’ve got holsters to that saddle o’ yourn, don’t let me see your hand go nigh ’em.", "그리고 그 안장에 권총집을 달고 있거든, 거기 손이 닿는 걸 내 눈에 띄게 하지 마라."], ["For I’m a devil at a quick mistake, and when I make one it takes the form of Lead.", "나는 잽싸게 실수하는 데는 귀신이고, 한번 실수했다 하면 그게 납덩이 꼴로 나타나니까."], ["So now let’s look at you.”", "자, 그럼 네 꼴을 좀 보자.\""]], [["The figures of a horse and rider came slowly through the eddying mist, and came to the side of the mail, where the passenger stood.", "말과 기수의 형체가 소용돌이치는 안개를 뚫고 천천히 다가와, 승객이 서 있는 우편마차 곁에 이르렀다."], ["The rider stooped, and, casting up his eyes at the guard, handed the passenger a small folded paper.", "기수는 몸을 숙여 호송대원을 향해 눈을 치켜뜨면서, 승객에게 작게 접은 종이 한 장을 건넸다."], ["The rider’s horse was blown, and both horse and rider were covered with mud, from the hoofs of the horse to the hat of the man.", "기수의 말은 잔뜩 지쳐 헐떡였고, 말과 기수 둘 다 말발굽에서 사람의 모자까지 진흙을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다."]], [["“Guard!”", "\"호송대원!\""], ["said the passenger, in a tone of quiet business confidence.", "승객이 조용하고 사무적인 자신감이 깃든 어조로 말했다."]], [["The watchful guard, with his right hand at the stock of his raised blunderbuss, his left at the barrel, and his eye on the horseman, answered curtly, “Sir.”", "경계를 늦추지 않던 호송대원은 오른손을 치켜든 나팔총 개머리판에, 왼손을 총신에 댄 채, 시선은 기수에게 둔 채로, 짧게 대답했다. \"예.\""]], [["“There is nothing to apprehend.", "\"걱정할 것은 없소. 나는 텔슨 은행 사람이오."], ["I belong to Tellson’s Bank.", "런던의 텔슨 은행은 알고 있을 거요."], ["You must know Tellson’s Bank in London.", "나는 볼일이 있어 빠리로 가는 길이오."], ["I am going to Paris on business. A crown to drink.", "자, 한잔 마시게 일 크라운 받으시오."], ["I may read this?”", "이걸 읽어도 되겠소?\""]], [["“If so be as you’re quick, sir.”", "\"그러시려거든 빨리 하십쇼, 나리.\""]], [["He opened it in the light of the coach-lamp on that side, and read--first to himself and then aloud: “‘Wait at Dover for Mam’selle.’ It’s not long, you see, guard.", "그는 그쪽 마차 등불 빛에 종이를 펴서 읽었으니, 먼저 속으로 읽고 그다음 소리 내어 읽었다. \"'마드무아젤을 모시고 도버에서 대기하라.'"], ["Jerry, say that my answer was, RECALLED TO LIFE.”", "보다시피 길지 않소, 호송대원. 제리, 내 답이 '되살아나다'였다고 전하게.\""]], [["Jerry started in his saddle.", "제리가 안장 위에서 흠칫했다."], ["“That’s a Blazing strange answer, too,”", "\"그것도 참 기막히게 이상한 답이로군요.\""], ["said he, at his hoarsest.", "그가 더없이 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Take that message back, and they will know that I received this, as well as if I wrote.", "\"그 전갈을 가지고 돌아가게. 그러면 그쪽에서는 내가 이걸 받았다는 걸, 내가 직접 쓴 거나 다름없이 알 걸세."], ["Make the best of your way.", "되도록 서둘러 가게."], ["Good night.”", "잘 가게.\""]], [["With those words the passenger opened the coach-door and got in; not at all assisted by his fellow-passengers, who had expeditiously secreted their watches and purses in their boots, and were now making a general pretence of being asleep.", "이 말과 함께 승객은 마차 문을 열고 안으로 들어갔다. 시계와 지갑을 잽싸게 장화 속에 감춰두고는 이제 다들 잠든 척하던 동승객들은 그를 조금도 거들어주지 않았다."], ["With no more definite purpose than to escape the hazard of originating any other kind of action.", "다른 어떤 행동도 새로 일으키는 위험을 피하겠다는 것 말고는 별다른 뜻이 없었다."]], [["The coach lumbered on again, with heavier wreaths of mist closing round it as it began the descent.", "우편마차는 다시 덜컹거리며 나아갔고, 내리막을 시작하자 더 짙은 안개 자락이 그 둘레로 휘감겨들었다."], ["The guard soon replaced his blunderbuss in his arm-chest, and, having looked to the rest of its contents, and having looked to the supplementary pistols that he wore in his belt, looked to a smaller chest beneath his seat, in which there were a few smith’s tools, a couple of torches, and a tinder-box.", "호송대원은 이내 나팔총을 무기 상자에 도로 넣고, 상자 안의 나머지 내용물을 살피고, 자기 허리띠에 찬 예비 권총을 살핀 다음, 좌석 밑의 더 작은 상자를 들여다보았는데, 그 안에는 대장간 연장 몇 가지와 횃불 두 자루, 그리고 부싯깃통이 들어 있었다."], ["For he was furnished with that completeness that if the coach-lamps had been blown and stormed out, which did occasionally happen, he had only to shut himself up inside, keep the flint and steel sparks well off the straw, and get a light with tolerable safety and ease (if he were lucky) in five minutes.", "그는 만반의 준비를 갖추고 있었으니, 이따금 실제로 일어나는 일이지만 마차 등불이 바람에 꺼져버린다 해도, 안에 들어앉아 부싯돌과 부시의 불꽃을 짚에서 잘 떼어놓기만 하면, (운이 좋다면) 오 분 만에 그런대로 안전하고 손쉽게 불을 얻을 수 있었다."]], [["“Tom!”", "\"톰!\""], ["softly over the coach roof.", "마차 지붕 너머로 나직이."]], [["“Hallo, Joe.”", "\"여어, 조.\""]], [["“Did you hear the message?”", "\"그 전갈 들었나?\""]], [["“I did, Joe.”", "\"들었지, 조.\""]], [["“What did you make of it, Tom?”", "\"그게 무슨 소린지 알겠던가, 톰?\""]], [["“Nothing at all, Joe.”", "\"하나도 모르겠던데, 조.\""]], [["“That’s a coincidence, too,”", "\"그거 참 묘하게 들어맞는군.\""], ["the guard mused, “for I made the same of it myself.”", "호송대원이 곰곰이 생각했다. \"나도 똑같이 하나도 모르겠으니 말이야.\""]], [["Jerry, left alone in the mist and darkness, dismounted meanwhile, not only to ease his spent horse, but to wipe the mud from his face, and shake the wet out of his hat-brim, which might be capable of holding about half a gallon.", "한편 안개와 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨진 제리는 말에서 내렸으니, 지친 말을 쉬게 하려는 것만이 아니라 얼굴에 묻은 진흙을 닦아내고, 반 갤런쯤은 너끈히 담길 듯한 모자챙에서 빗물을 털어내려는 것이었다."], ["After standing with the bridle over his heavily-splashed arm, until the wheels of the mail were no longer within hearing and the night was quite still again, he turned to walk down the hill.", "진흙이 잔뜩 튄 팔에 고삐를 걸친 채, 우편마차의 바퀴 소리가 더는 들리지 않고 밤이 다시 아주 고요해질 때까지 서 있다가, 그는 언덕을 걸어 내려가려 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“After that there gallop from Temple Bar, old lady, I won’t trust your fore-legs till I get you on the level,”", "\"템플바에서 그렇게 내달렸으니, 이 늙은 아가씨야, 평지에 닿을 때까지는 네 앞다리를 못 믿겠다.\""], ["said this hoarse messenger, glancing at his mare.", "이 쉰 목소리의 전령이 자기 암말을 흘긋 보며 말했다."], ["“‘Recalled to life.’ That’s a Blazing strange message.", "\"'되살아나다'라. 그것 참 기막히게 이상한 전갈이야."], ["Much of that wouldn’t do for you, Jerry!", "너한테는 그런 게 별로 안 어울리지, 제리!"], ["I say, Jerry!", "이봐, 제리!"], ["You’d be in a Blazing bad way, if recalling to life was to come into fashion, Jerry!”", "되살아나는 게 유행이라도 됐다간, 넌 아주 기막히게 곤란해질 거다, 제리!\""]], [["# CHAPTER III. The Night Shadows", "# CHAPTER III. The Night Shadows"]], [["A wonderful fact to reflect upon, that every human creature is constituted to be that profound secret and mystery to every other.", "깊이 새겨볼 만한 놀라운 사실은, 모든 인간이 다른 모든 인간에게 그토록 깊은 비밀이자 신비가 되도록 만들어져 있다는 것이다."], ["A solemn consideration, when I enter a great city by night, that every one of those darkly clustered houses encloses its own secret; that every room in every one of them encloses its own secret; that every beating heart in the hundreds of thousands of breasts there, is, in some of its imaginings, a secret to the heart nearest it!", "밤에 거대한 도시로 들어설 때면, 어둡게 무리지어 모인 저 집들 하나하나가 저마다의 비밀을 품고 있고, 그 집들의 방 하나하나가 저마다의 비밀을 품고 있으며, 그곳 수십만 가슴 속에서 뛰는 심장 하나하나가 그 상상의 어느 한구석에서는 가장 가까운 심장에게조차 비밀이라는 것이, 엄숙한 상념으로 다가온다!"], ["Something of the awfulness, even of Death itself, is referable to this.", "죽음 그 자체의 두려움마저도 어느 정도는 여기에 닿아 있다."], ["No more can I turn the leaves of this dear book that I loved, and vainly hope in time to read it all.", "내가 사랑했던 이 소중한 책의 책장을 더는 넘길 수 없게 되고, 언젠가 그것을 다 읽으리라 헛되이 바랄 수밖에 없게 된다."], ["No more can I look into the depths of this unfathomable water, wherein, as momentary lights glanced into it, I have had glimpses of buried treasure and other things submerged.", "순간순간 빛이 비쳐 들 때 그 속에 묻힌 보물과 잠겨 있는 다른 것들을 언뜻언뜻 보았던 이 헤아릴 수 없는 물의 깊은 속을 더는 들여다볼 수 없게 된다."], ["It was appointed that the book should shut with a spring, for ever and for ever, when I had read but a page.", "내가 겨우 한 페이지를 읽었을 때 그 책이 용수철처럼 탁 닫혀 영원히 그리고 영원히 닫혀버리도록 정해져 있었던 것이다."], ["It was appointed that the water should be locked in an eternal frost, when the light was playing on its surface, and I stood in ignorance on the shore.", "빛이 그 수면 위에서 일렁이고 내가 무지한 채 물가에 서 있을 때 그 물이 영원한 서리 속에 갇혀버리도록 정해져 있었던 것이다."], ["My friend is dead, my neighbour is dead, my love, the darling of my soul, is dead; it is the inexorable consolidation and perpetuation of the secret that was always in that individuality, and which I shall carry in mine to my life’s end.", "내 친구가 죽었고, 내 이웃이 죽었고, 내 사랑, 내 영혼의 연인이 죽었으니, 그것은 늘 그 개별성 안에 있었던, 그리고 내가 내 생의 끝까지 내 안에 지니고 갈 비밀의 가차없는 응고이자 영속이다."], ["In any of the burial-places of this city through which I pass, is there a sleeper more inscrutable than its busy inhabitants are, in their innermost personality, to me, or than I am to them?", "내가 지나가는 이 도시의 묘지 어디에서든, 거기 잠든 자가 그 가장 깊은 인격에 있어 나에게, 혹은 내가 그들에게 그러하듯, 분주한 이곳 주민들보다 더 헤아리기 어려운 존재일까?"]], [["As to this, his natural and not to be alienated inheritance, the messenger on horseback had exactly the same possessions as the King, the first Minister of State, or the richest merchant in London.", "이것에 관해서라면, 이 타고난, 그리고 결코 빼앗길 수 없는 유산에 관해서라면, 말을 탄 전령은 왕이나 국무대신, 혹은 런던에서 가장 부유한 상인과 정확히 똑같은 소유물을 지니고 있었다."], ["So with the three passengers shut up in the narrow compass of one lumbering old mail coach; they were mysteries to one another, as complete as if each had been in his own coach and six, or his own coach and sixty, with the breadth of a county between him and the next.", "덜컹거리는 낡은 우편마차의 좁은 공간에 갇힌 세 승객도 마찬가지였으니, 그들은 서로에게 완벽한 신비였으며, 마치 각자 자신의 여섯 마리 말이 끄는 마차, 혹은 예순 마리 말이 끄는 마차에 탄 채 다음 사람과의 사이에 한 주(州)만큼의 거리를 두고 있는 듯했다."]], [["The messenger rode back at an easy trot, stopping pretty often at ale-houses by the way to drink, but evincing a tendency to keep his own counsel, and to keep his hat cocked over his eyes.", "전령은 느긋한 속보로 되돌아갔는데, 길가의 술집에 꽤 자주 멈춰 서서 술을 들이켜면서도 제 속내는 혼자 간직하고 모자는 눈 위로 비스듬히 눌러쓰려는 경향을 보였다."], ["He had eyes that assorted very well with that decoration, being of a surface black, with no depth in the colour or form, and much too near together--as if they were afraid of being found out in something, singly, if they kept too far apart.", "그의 두 눈은 그 장식과 무척 잘 어울렸으니, 빛깔도 형체도 깊이가 없는 표면적인 검은색이었고, 너무 가까이 붙어 있었다. 마치 그 둘이 너무 멀리 떨어져 있으면 따로따로 무언가를 들킬까 두려워하는 것 같았다."], ["They had a sinister expression, under an old cocked-hat like a three-cornered spittoon, and over a great muffler for the chin and throat, which descended nearly to the wearer’s knees.", "세 귀퉁이가 진 타구(唾具)처럼 생긴 낡은 삼각모 아래, 그리고 착용자의 무릎까지 거의 내려오는 턱과 목의 커다란 목도리 위로, 그 눈은 음흉한 표정을 띠고 있었다."], ["When he stopped for drink, he moved this muffler with his left hand, only while he poured his liquor in with his right; as soon as that was done, he muffled again.", "술을 마시려고 멈출 때면 그는 왼손으로 이 목도리를 끌어내렸는데, 오직 오른손으로 술을 따라 넣는 동안만 그랬고, 다 끝내자마자 다시 목도리로 감쌌다."]], [["“No, Jerry, no!”", "\"안 돼, 제리, 안 돼!\""], ["said the messenger, harping on one theme as he rode.", "전령은 말을 몰며 한 가지 주제만 되뇌었다."], ["“It wouldn’t do for you, Jerry.", "\"그건 자네한테 안 어울려, 제리."], ["Jerry, you honest tradesman, it wouldn’t suit your line of business!", "제리, 자네 같은 정직한 장사꾼한테는 그게 자네 장사에 맞지가 않는다고!"], ["Recalled--!", "다시 살아난다고—!"], ["Bust me if I don’t think he’d been a drinking!”", "그 양반이 술을 마신 게 아니라면 내 목을 걸겠어!\""]], [["His message perplexed his mind to that degree that he was fain, several times, to take off his hat to scratch his head.", "그가 전해야 할 그 전언은 그의 마음을 어찌나 어지럽혔던지, 그는 여러 차례 모자를 벗고 머리를 긁적이지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Except on the crown, which was raggedly bald, he had stiff, black hair, standing jaggedly all over it, and growing down hill almost to his broad, blunt nose.", "너덜너덜하게 벗어진 정수리만 빼면 그의 머리에는 뻣뻣한 검은 머리칼이 사방으로 들쭉날쭉 솟아 있었고, 그것은 그의 넓적하고 뭉툭한 코까지 비탈을 따라 내려오듯 자라 있었다."], ["It was so like Smith’s work, so much more like the top of a strongly spiked wall than a head of hair, that the best of players at leap-frog might have declined him, as the most dangerous man in the world to go over.", "그것이 어찌나 대장장이의 솜씨 같았던지, 머리칼이라기보다는 단단히 못을 박은 담장 꼭대기에 훨씬 가까워서, 등넘기 놀이의 명수라도 그를 세상에서 가장 위험한, 결코 넘어갈 수 없는 사람으로 여겨 마다했을 법했다."]], [["While he trotted back with the message he was to deliver to the night watchman in his box at the door of Tellson’s Bank, by Temple Bar, who was to deliver it to greater authorities within, the shadows of the night took such shapes to him as arose out of the message, and took such shapes to the mare as arose out of her private topics of uneasiness.", "그가 템플바 옆 텔슨 은행 문간의 초소에 있는 야경꾼에게 전해야 할 그 전언을 가지고 속보로 되돌아가는 동안 — 그 야경꾼은 그것을 다시 안쪽의 더 높은 권위자들에게 전할 참이었다 — 밤의 그림자들은 그에게는 그 전언에서 솟아나는 형상들로, 암말에게는 그 자신의 사사로운 불안거리에서 솟아나는 형상들로 나타났다."], ["They seemed to be numerous, for she shied at every shadow on the road.", "그 형상들은 꽤 많았던 모양인지, 암말은 길 위의 그림자마다 깜짝깜짝 놀라 비켜섰다."]], [["What time, the mail-coach lumbered, jolted, rattled, and bumped upon its tedious way, with its three fellow-inscrutables inside.", "그동안 우편마차는 헤아릴 수 없는 세 길동무를 안에 태운 채 그 지루한 길 위를 덜컹덜컹 흔들리고 달그락거리며 굴러갔다."], ["To whom, likewise, the shadows of the night revealed themselves, in the forms their dozing eyes and wandering thoughts suggested.", "그들에게도 마찬가지로, 밤의 그림자들은 졸음에 겨운 눈과 떠도는 생각이 암시하는 형상들로 제 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["Tellson’s Bank had a run upon it in the mail.", "텔슨 은행은 그 우편마차 안에서 한바탕 인출 소동을 겪었다."], ["As the bank passenger--with an arm drawn through the leathern strap, which did what lay in it to keep him from pounding against the next passenger, and driving him into his corner, whenever the coach got a special jolt--nodded in his place, with half-shut eyes, the little coach-windows, and the coach-lamp dimly gleaming through them, and the bulky bundle of opposite passenger, became the bank, and did a great stroke of business.", "은행에서 온 그 승객이 — 마차가 유독 심하게 덜컹거릴 때마다 옆 승객에게 부딪혀 그를 구석으로 밀어붙이지 않도록 막아주는 가죽끈에 한쪽 팔을 꿰고서 — 반쯤 감은 눈으로 제자리에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 동안, 작은 마차 창문들과 그것을 통해 어슴푸레 새어드는 마차 등불, 그리고 맞은편 승객의 부피 큰 보따리가 모두 은행이 되어 한바탕 큰 거래를 벌였다."], ["The rattle of the harness was the chink of money, and more drafts were honoured in five minutes than even Tellson’s, with all its foreign and home connection, ever paid in thrice the time.", "마구의 달그락거리는 소리는 돈이 짤랑거리는 소리였고, 텔슨 은행이 국내외의 온갖 연줄을 다 동원해 세 배의 시간을 들여 지불하는 것보다도 더 많은 어음이 단 오 분 만에 결제되었다."], ["Then the strong-rooms underground, at Tellson’s, with such of their valuable stores and secrets as were known to the passenger (and it was not a little that he knew about them), opened before him, and he went in among them with the great keys and the feebly-burning candle, and found them safe, and strong, and sound, and still, just as he had last seen them.", "그러더니 텔슨 은행 지하의 금고실들이, 그 승객이 알고 있는 값진 보관물과 비밀들과 함께 (그가 그것들에 대해 아는 바는 적지 않았다) 그의 앞에 열렸고, 그는 커다란 열쇠 꾸러미와 희미하게 타는 촛불을 들고 그것들 사이로 들어가, 마지막으로 보았을 때와 똑같이 안전하고 단단하고 멀쩡하고 고요한 채로 있는 것을 확인했다."]], [["But, though the bank was almost always with him, and though the coach (in a confused way, like the presence of pain under an opiate) was always with him, there was another current of impression that never ceased to run, all through the night.", "그러나 은행이 거의 늘 그와 함께 있었고, 또 마차가 (마취제에 취한 채 느끼는 고통의 존재처럼 흐릿하게) 늘 그와 함께 있었지만, 밤 내내 끊임없이 흐르는 또 하나의 인상의 물줄기가 있었다."], ["He was on his way to dig some one out of a grave.", "그는 누군가를 무덤에서 파내러 가는 길이었다."]], [["Now, which of the multitude of faces that showed themselves before him was the true face of the buried person, the shadows of the night did not indicate; but they were all the faces of a man of five-and-forty by years, and they differed principally in the passions they expressed, and in the ghastliness of their worn and wasted state.", "그런데 그의 앞에 나타나는 수많은 얼굴 가운데 어느 것이 그 묻힌 사람의 진짜 얼굴인지를 밤의 그림자들은 알려주지 않았다. 하지만 그 얼굴들은 모두 햇수로 마흔다섯 된 한 남자의 얼굴이었고, 주로 그것들이 드러내는 정념에서, 그리고 닳고 야윈 상태의 섬뜩함에서 서로 달랐다."], ["Pride, contempt, defiance, stubbornness, submission, lamentation, succeeded one another; so did varieties of sunken cheek, cadaverous colour, emaciated hands and figures.", "자긍심, 경멸, 반항, 완고함, 굴종, 비탄이 차례차례 이어졌고, 움푹 꺼진 뺨, 송장 같은 빛깔, 수척한 손과 형상도 갖가지 모습으로 이어졌다."], ["But the face was in the main one face, and every head was prematurely white.", "그러나 그 얼굴은 대체로 하나의 얼굴이었고, 머리는 하나같이 때 이르게 하얗게 세어 있었다."], ["A hundred times the dozing passenger inquired of this spectre:", "졸고 있던 승객은 백 번도 넘게 이 유령에게 물었다."]], [["“Buried how long?”", "\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\""]], [["The answer was always the same: “Almost eighteen years.”", "대답은 언제나 같았다. \"거의 십팔 년이오.\""]], [["“You had abandoned all hope of being dug out?”", "\"파내질 거라는 희망은 다 버렸었소?\""]], [["“Long ago.”", "\"오래전에.\""]], [["“You know that you are recalled to life?”", "\"당신이 다시 살아났다는 걸 알고 있소?\""]], [["“They tell me so.”", "\"그렇게들 말하더군.\""]], [["“I hope you care to live?”", "\"부디 살고 싶은 마음이 있기를 바라오?\""]], [["“I can’t say.”", "\"모르겠소.\""]], [["“Shall I show her to you?", "\"그녀를 보여드릴까요?"], ["Will you come and see her?”", "와서 그녀를 보시겠소?\""]], [["The answers to this question were various and contradictory.", "이 물음에 대한 대답은 갖가지였고 서로 모순되었다."], ["Sometimes the broken reply was, “Wait!", "어떤 때는 끊어지는 목소리로 \"기다려주오!"], ["It would kill me if I saw her too soon.”", "너무 일찍 그녀를 보면 나는 죽고 말 거요.\""], ["Sometimes, it was given in a tender rain of tears, and then it was, “Take me to her.”", "어떤 때는 부드러운 눈물의 비 속에서 \"그녀에게 데려다주오.\""], ["Sometimes it was staring and bewildered, and then it was, “I don’t know her.", "어떤 때는 멍하니 어리둥절한 채 \"나는 그녀를 모르오."], ["I don’t understand.”", "무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""]], [["After such imaginary discourse, the passenger in his fancy would dig, and dig, dig--now with a spade, now with a great key, now with his hands--to dig this wretched creature out.", "그런 가상의 문답이 오간 뒤, 승객은 상상 속에서 파고 또 파고, 파들어 갔다 — 어떤 때는 삽으로, 어떤 때는 커다란 열쇠로, 어떤 때는 맨손으로 — 이 가련한 존재를 파내려고."], ["Got out at last, with earth hanging about his face and hair, he would suddenly fan away to dust.", "마침내 얼굴과 머리칼에 흙이 매달린 채 끌려나오면, 그는 별안간 부채질하듯 흩어져 먼지가 되어버렸다."], ["The passenger would then start to himself, and lower the window, to get the reality of mist and rain on his cheek.", "그러면 승객은 깜짝 놀라 정신을 차리고는, 안개와 비의 실체를 뺨에 느끼려고 창문을 내렸다."]], [["Yet even when his eyes were opened on the mist and rain, on the moving patch of light from the lamps, and the hedge at the roadside retreating by jerks, the night shadows outside the coach would fall into the train of the night shadows within.", "그러나 그의 눈이 안개와 비, 등불에서 비쳐 움직이는 빛의 자락, 그리고 덜컹덜컹 뒤로 물러나는 길가의 산울타리 위로 떠지고 나서도, 마차 밖의 밤 그림자들은 마차 안의 밤 그림자들의 행렬 속으로 스며들었다."], ["The real Banking-house by Temple Bar, the real business of the past day, the real strong rooms, the real express sent after him, and the real message returned, would all be there.", "템플바 옆의 진짜 은행, 지난 하루의 진짜 업무, 진짜 금고실, 그를 뒤쫓아 보낸 진짜 급사, 그리고 되돌려 보낸 진짜 전언, 그 모든 것이 거기 있었다."], ["Out of the midst of them, the ghostly face would rise, and he would accost it again.", "그것들 한복판에서 그 유령 같은 얼굴이 솟아오르면, 그는 다시 그것에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Buried how long?”", "\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\""]], [["“Almost eighteen years.”", "\"거의 십팔 년이오.\""]], [["“I hope you care to live?”", "\"부디 살고 싶은 마음이 있기를 바라오?\""]], [["“I can’t say.”", "\"모르겠소.\""]], [["Dig--dig--dig--until an impatient movement from one of the two passengers would admonish him to pull up the window, draw his arm securely through the leathern strap, and speculate upon the two slumbering forms, until his mind lost its hold of them, and they again slid away into the bank and the grave.", "파고 — 파고 — 파들어 가다가, 두 승객 가운데 하나가 짜증스럽게 몸을 움직이면 그제야 그는 창문을 끌어올리고, 가죽끈에 팔을 단단히 꿰고서, 잠든 두 형체를 골똘히 헤아리다가, 마침내 그의 마음이 그것들을 놓아버리면 그것들은 다시 은행과 무덤 속으로 미끄러져 사라졌다."]], [["“Buried how long?”", "\"얼마나 오래 묻혀 있었소?\""]], [["“Almost eighteen years.”", "\"거의 십팔 년이오.\""]], [["“You had abandoned all hope of being dug out?”", "\"파내질 거라는 희망은 다 버렸었소?\""]], [["“Long ago.”", "\"오래전에.\""]], [["The words were still in his hearing as just spoken--distinctly in his hearing as ever spoken words had been in his life--when the weary passenger started to the consciousness of daylight, and found that the shadows of the night were gone.", "그 말들이 마치 방금 입에서 나온 듯 여전히 그의 귓가에 — 평생 들어온 그 어떤 말 못지않게 또렷하게 그의 귓가에 — 남아 있을 때, 지친 승객은 화들짝 깨어 대낮의 의식 속으로 돌아왔고, 밤의 그림자들이 사라졌음을 알게 되었다."]], [["He lowered the window, and looked out at the rising sun.", "그는 창문을 내리고 떠오르는 해를 내다보았다."], ["There was a ridge of ploughed land, with a plough upon it where it had been left last night when the horses were unyoked; beyond, a quiet coppice-wood, in which many leaves of burning red and golden yellow still remained upon the trees.", "갈아엎은 밭이랑이 있었고, 그 위에는 어젯밤 말들의 멍에를 벗길 때 버려둔 쟁기 한 대가 놓여 있었다. 그 너머로는 고요한 잡목림이 있었는데, 그 나무들에는 타는 듯한 붉은빛과 황금빛 나뭇잎이 아직 많이 남아 있었다."], ["Though the earth was cold and wet, the sky was clear, and the sun rose bright, placid, and beautiful.", "땅은 차갑고 축축했지만 하늘은 맑았고, 해는 환하고 평온하고 아름답게 떠올랐다."]], [["“Eighteen years!”", "\"십팔 년이라니!\""], ["said the passenger, looking at the sun.", "승객은 해를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Gracious Creator of day!", "\"자비로우신 낮의 창조주여!"], ["To be buried alive for eighteen years!”", "십팔 년 동안이나 산 채로 묻혀 있다니!\""]], [["# CHAPTER IV. The Preparation", "# CHAPTER IV. The Preparation"]], [["When the mail got successfully to Dover, in the course of the forenoon, the head drawer at the Royal George Hotel opened the coach-door as his custom was.", "우편마차가 오전 나절에 무사히 도버에 다다랐을 때, 로열조지 호텔의 수석 급사가 늘 하던 대로 마차 문을 열었다. 그는 다소 격식을 차린 몸짓으로 그렇게 했으니, 겨울에 런던에서 출발하는 우편마차 여행이란 모험심 강한 여행자에게 축하를 건넬 만한 업적이었던 것이다."], ["He did it with some flourish of ceremony, for a mail journey from London in winter was an achievement to congratulate an adventurous traveller upon.", "* 도버: 영국 남동부 켄트의 항구 도시. 영국해협을 건너 프랑스로 가는 관문이다."]], [["By that time, there was only one adventurous traveller left be congratulated: for the two others had been set down at their respective roadside destinations.", "그때쯤 축하받을 모험심 강한 여행자는 단 한 사람뿐이었다. 나머지 두 사람은 각자의 길가 목적지에 내려졌기 때문이다."], ["The mildewy inside of the coach, with its damp and dirty straw, its disagreeable smell, and its obscurity, was rather like a larger dog-kennel. Mr.", "곰팡내 나는 마차 안은 축축하고 더러운 짚, 불쾌한 냄새, 어둑함까지 어우러져 차라리 커다란 개집과 닮아 있었다."], ["Lorry, the passenger, shaking himself out of it in chains of straw, a tangle of shaggy wrapper, flapping hat, and muddy legs, was rather like a larger sort of dog.", "거기서 짚 부스러기를 주렁주렁 매단 채 빠져나온 승객 로리 씨는 텁수룩하게 엉킨 외투 자락과 펄럭이는 모자, 진흙투성이 다리까지 더해져, 차라리 좀 큰 종류의 개를 닮아 있었다. \"내일 깔레로 가는 연락선이 있겠나, 급사?\""]], [["“There will be a packet to Calais, tomorrow, drawer?”", "* 깔레: 프랑스 북부의 항구 도시. 도버와 영국해협을 사이에 두고 마주 본다."]], [["“Yes, sir, if the weather holds and the wind sets tolerable fair.", "\"예, 손님. 날씨가 버텨주고 바람이 그런대로 순하게 분다면요."], ["The tide will serve pretty nicely at about two in the afternoon, sir.", "조수는 오후 두시쯤 꽤 알맞게 들어올 겁니다, 손님."], ["Bed, sir?”", "잠자리는요, 손님?\""]], [["“I shall not go to bed till night; but I want a bedroom, and a barber.”", "\"밤이 되기 전까지는 잠자리에 들지 않겠네. 하지만 침실 하나와 이발사가 필요하네.\""]], [["“And then breakfast, sir? Yes, sir.", "\"그러고 나서 아침식사도요, 손님?"], ["That way, sir, if you please.", "예, 손님. 이쪽입니다, 손님."], ["Show Concord!", "콩코드 방으로 모셔라!"], ["Gentleman’s valise and hot water to Concord.", "신사분 여행 가방과 더운물을 콩코드로."], ["Pull off gentleman’s boots in Concord.", "콩코드에서 신사분 장화를 벗겨드려라."], ["(You will find a fine sea-coal fire, sir.) Fetch barber to Concord.", "(좋은 석탄불을 보시게 될 겁니다, 손님.) 이발사를 콩코드로 불러와라."], ["Stir about there, now, for Concord!”", "자, 어서들 움직여라, 콩코드 방이다!\""]], [["The Concord bed-chamber being always assigned to a passenger by the mail, and passengers by the mail being always heavily wrapped up from head to foot, the room had the odd interest for the establishment of the Royal George, that although but one kind of man was seen to go into it, all kinds and varieties of men came out of it.", "콩코드 침실은 늘 우편마차 승객에게 배정되었고, 우편마차 승객은 늘 머리끝부터 발끝까지 두툼하게 싸매고 있었던 까닭에, 그 방은 로열조지 호텔 사람들에게 묘한 흥밋거리가 되었다. 들어가는 사람은 오직 한 종류뿐인데, 나오는 사람은 온갖 종류와 갖가지 부류였기 때문이다."], ["Consequently, another drawer, and two porters, and several maids and the landlady, were all loitering by accident at various points of the road between the Concord and the coffee-room, when a gentleman of sixty, formally dressed in a brown suit of clothes, pretty well worn, but very well kept, with large square cuffs and large flaps to the pockets, passed along on his way to his breakfast.", "그리하여 또다른 급사 하나와 짐꾼 둘, 하녀 여럿과 안주인까지, 갈색 옷을 격식 있게 차려입은 예순 살가량의 신사가 아침식사를 하러 지나갈 때, 콩코드 방과 식당 사이 길목 곳곳에 우연인 척 어슬렁거리고 있었다. 그 옷은 꽤 닳았지만 매우 잘 손질되어 있었고, 커다란 네모난 소맷부리와 큼직한 주머니 덮개가 달려 있었다."]], [["The coffee-room had no other occupant, that forenoon, than the gentleman in brown.", "그날 오전 식당에는 그 갈색 옷의 신사 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["His breakfast-table was drawn before the fire, and as he sat, with its light shining on him, waiting for the meal, he sat so still, that he might have been sitting for his portrait.", "아침식사 식탁이 불 앞에 차려졌고, 불빛이 그의 몸을 비추는 가운데 식사를 기다리며 앉아 있는 그의 모습은 어찌나 미동도 없는지, 마치 초상화를 위해 자세를 잡고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["Very orderly and methodical he looked, with a hand on each knee, and a loud watch ticking a sonorous sermon under his flapped waist-coat, as though it pitted its gravity and longevity against the levity and evanescence of the brisk fire.", "그는 단정하고 빈틈없어 보였다. 두 손을 각각 무릎에 얹은 채였고, 덮개 달린 조끼 아래에서는 큼직한 회중시계가 낭랑한 설교 같은 똑딱 소리를 내고 있었으니, 마치 활활 타오르는 불의 경박함과 덧없음에 맞서 자신의 무게와 장수를 겨루기라도 하는 듯했다."], ["He had a good leg, and was a little vain of it, for his brown stockings fitted sleek and close, and were of a fine texture; his shoes and buckles, too, though plain, were trim.", "그는 다리맵시가 좋았고 그 점을 조금 으스대고 있었으니, 갈색 양말은 매끈하고 꼭 맞았으며 결이 고운 천이었기 때문이다. 그의 구두와 버클도 수수하긴 했으나 맵시 있었다."], ["He wore an odd little sleek crisp flaxen wig, setting very close to his head: which wig, it is to be presumed, was made of hair, but which looked far more as though it were spun from filaments of silk or glass.", "그는 머리에 바싹 붙는 묘하게 작고 매끄러운 아마빛 가발을 쓰고 있었는데, 그 가발은 짐작건대 머리카락으로 만든 것이었겠으나 차라리 비단이나 유리의 가는 실을 자아 만든 것처럼 보였다."], ["His linen, though not of a fineness in accordance with his stockings, was as white as the tops of the waves that broke upon the neighbouring beach, or the specks of sail that glinted in the sunlight far at sea.", "그의 셔츠는 양말에 걸맞은 고급은 아니었으나, 이웃한 바닷가에 부서지는 파도의 마루처럼, 또는 먼바다에서 햇빛에 반짝이는 점점이 보이는 돛처럼 새하얬다."], ["A face habitually suppressed and quieted, was still lighted up under the quaint wig by a pair of moist bright eyes that it must have cost their owner, in years gone by, some pains to drill to the composed and reserved expression of Tellson’s Bank.", "습관적으로 억눌리고 차분해진 얼굴은, 그 별난 가발 아래에서 한 쌍의 촉촉하고 밝은 눈에 의해 여전히 빛나고 있었다. 그 눈은 지난 세월 동안 텔슨 은행 특유의 침착하고 절제된 표정으로 길들이느라 임자가 적잖이 애를 먹었을 법한 것이었다."], ["He had a healthy colour in his cheeks, and his face, though lined, bore few traces of anxiety.", "그의 두 뺨에는 건강한 혈색이 돌았고, 얼굴에는 주름이 잡혀 있었으나 근심의 흔적은 거의 없었다."], ["But, perhaps the confidential bachelor clerks in Tellson’s Bank were principally occupied with the cares of other people; and perhaps second-hand cares, like second-hand clothes, come easily off and on.", "하지만 어쩌면 텔슨 은행의 미혼 신임 사무원들은 주로 남의 근심을 떠안고 살았는지도 모른다. 그리고 어쩌면 헌 옷처럼 남의 근심이란 입고 벗기가 수월한 법인지도 모른다."]], [["Completing his resemblance to a man who was sitting for his portrait, Mr. Lorry dropped off to sleep.", "초상화를 위해 자세를 잡은 사람과의 닮음을 완성하기라도 하듯, 로리 씨는 깜빡 잠이 들었다."], ["The arrival of his breakfast roused him, and he said to the drawer, as he moved his chair to it:", "아침식사가 도착하자 그는 잠에서 깨어났고, 의자를 식탁 쪽으로 당기며 급사에게 말했다."]], [["“I wish accommodation prepared for a young lady who may come here at any time to-day.", "\"오늘 아무 때고 이리로 올지 모르는 젊은 숙녀분을 위해 방을 하나 마련해주게."], ["She may ask for Mr. Jarvis Lorry, or she may only ask for a gentleman from Tellson’s Bank.", "그분은 자비스 로리 씨를 찾을 수도 있고, 아니면 그저 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 찾을 수도 있네."], ["Please to let me know.”", "오면 내게 알려주게.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"예, 손님."], ["Tellson’s Bank in London, sir?” “Yes.”", "런던의 텔슨 은행 말씀이십니까, 손님?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"그렇다네.\""], ["We have oftentimes the honour to entertain your gentlemen in their travelling backwards and forwards betwixt London and Paris, sir.", "\"예, 손님. 저희는 손님네 신사분들이 런던과 빠리 사이를 오가실 때 모시는 영광을 여러번 누렸습니다, 손님."], ["A vast deal of travelling, sir, in Tellson and Company’s House.”", "텔슨 상회는 여행이 무척 잦으시지요, 손님.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇지."], ["We are quite a French House, as well as an English one.”", "우리는 영국 상회이면서 또한 어엿한 프랑스 상회이기도 하니까.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"예, 손님."], ["Not much in the habit of such travelling yourself, I think, sir?”", "손님께서는 그런 여행에 그리 익숙하신 편은 아니신 듯합니다만, 손님?\""]], [["“Not of late years.", "\"근래에는 그렇네."], ["It is fifteen years since we--since I--came last from France.”", "우리가, 아니 내가, 프랑스에서 마지막으로 돌아온 게 십오 년 전이니까.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir?", "\"그러십니까, 손님?"], ["That was before my time here, sir.", "그건 제가 여기 오기 전이로군요, 손님."], ["Before our people’s time here, sir.", "저희 사람들이 여기 오기 전입니다, 손님."], ["The George was in other hands at that time, sir.”", "그때 조지 호텔은 다른 사람 손에 있었지요, 손님.\""]], [["“I believe so.”", "\"그랬겠지.\""]], [["“But I would hold a pretty wager, sir, that a House like Tellson and Company was flourishing, a matter of fifty, not to speak of fifteen years ago?”", "\"하지만 저는 꽤 큰돈을 걸겠습니다, 손님. 텔슨 상회 같은 상회라면 십오 년은커녕 오십 년 전에도 번창하고 있었으리라고요, 손님?\""]], [["“You might treble that, and say a hundred and fifty, yet not be far from the truth.”", "\"거기에 세 곱을 해서 백오십 년이라 해도 진실에서 그리 멀지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“Indeed, sir!”", "\"그러십니까, 손님!\""]], [["Rounding his mouth and both his eyes, as he stepped backward from the table, the waiter shifted his napkin from his right arm to his left, dropped into a comfortable attitude, and stood surveying the guest while he ate and drank, as from an observatory or watchtower.", "식탁에서 뒷걸음질 치며 입과 두 눈을 동그랗게 만 급사는, 냅킨을 오른팔에서 왼팔로 옮기고는 편안한 자세를 취하더니, 손님이 먹고 마시는 동안 마치 관측소나 망루에서 내려다보듯 서서 그를 살폈다."], ["According to the immemorial usage of waiters in all ages.", "예나 지금이나 변함없는 모든 시대 급사들의 태곳적 관습 그대로였다."]], [["When Mr. Lorry had finished his breakfast, he went out for a stroll on the beach.", "로리 씨는 아침식사를 마치자 바닷가로 산책을 나갔다."], ["The little narrow, crooked town of Dover hid itself away from the beach, and ran its head into the chalk cliffs, like a marine ostrich.", "좁고 구불구불한 작은 도시 도버는 바닷가로부터 몸을 숨긴 채, 마치 바다 타조처럼 백악 절벽에 머리를 처박고 있었다."], ["The beach was a desert of heaps of sea and stones tumbling wildly about, and the sea did what it liked, and what it liked was destruction.", "바닷가는 바다와 돌무더기가 거칠게 나뒹구는 황무지였고, 바다는 제멋대로였으며, 그것이 좋아하는 일이란 파괴였다."], ["It thundered at the town, and thundered at the cliffs, and brought the coast down, madly.", "바다는 도시를 향해 천둥 치고 절벽을 향해 천둥 치며, 미친 듯이 해안을 무너뜨렸다."], ["The air among the houses was of so strong a piscatory flavour that one might have supposed sick fish went up to be dipped in it, as sick people went down to be dipped in the sea.", "집들 사이의 공기는 생선 냄새가 어찌나 진동했던지, 병든 사람들이 바닷물에 몸을 담그러 내려가듯 병든 물고기들이 그 공기에 몸을 담그러 올라온 것이 아닐까 싶을 정도였다."], ["A little fishing was done in the port, and a quantity of strolling about by night, and looking seaward: particularly at those times when the tide made, and was near flood.", "항구에서는 고기잡이가 조금 이루어졌고, 밤에는 어슬렁거리며 바다 쪽을 바라보는 일이 잦았으니, 특히 조수가 밀려들어 만조에 가까워지는 때가 그러했다."], ["Small tradesmen, who did no business whatever, sometimes unaccountably realised large fortunes, and it was remarkable that nobody in the neighbourhood could endure a lamplighter.", "도무지 아무 장사도 하지 않는 영세 상인들이 이따금 영문 모를 큰 재산을 모으기도 했는데, 그 동네에서는 아무도 가로등지기를 견뎌내지 못하는 것이 눈에 띄는 점이었다."]], [["As the day declined into the afternoon, and the air, which had been at intervals clear enough to allow the French coast to be seen, became again charged with mist and vapour, Mr.", "날이 오후로 기울고, 한동안 프랑스 해안이 보일 만큼 맑았던 공기가 다시 안개와 수증기로 가득 차오르자, 로리 씨의 생각도 흐려지는 듯했다."], ["Lorry’s thoughts seemed to cloud too. When it was dark, and he sat before the coffee-room fire, awaiting his dinner as he had awaited his breakfast, his mind was busily digging, digging, digging, in the live red coals.", "어두워졌을 때, 그는 아침식사를 기다렸던 것처럼 저녁식사를 기다리며 식당 난롯불 앞에 앉아 있었고, 그의 마음은 벌겋게 살아 있는 석탄 속을 부지런히 파고, 파고, 또 파고 있었다."]], [["A bottle of good claret after dinner does a digger in the red coals no harm, otherwise than as it has a tendency to throw him out of work. Mr.", "저녁식사 뒤에 마시는 질 좋은 끌라레 한 병은 벌건 석탄 속을 파는 자에게 해될 것이 없으니, 다만 그를 일에서 떼어놓는 경향이 있을 뿐이다."], ["Lorry had been idle a long time, and had just poured out his last glassful of wine with as complete an appearance of satisfaction as is ever to be found in an elderly gentleman of a fresh complexion who has got to the end of a bottle, when a rattling of wheels came up the narrow street, and rumbled into the inn-yard.", "로리 씨는 오랫동안 빈둥거리다가, 한 병을 다 비운 혈색 좋은 노신사에게서나 찾아볼 수 있는 그토록 흡족한 표정으로 마지막 잔을 막 따른 참이었다. 그때 좁은 거리로 덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리가 올라오더니 여관 안마당으로 우르르 굴러 들어왔다."]], [["He set down his glass untouched.", "그는 입에 대지도 않은 잔을 내려놓았다."], ["“This is Mam’selle!”", "\"그분이로군!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["In a very few minutes the waiter came in to announce that Miss Manette had arrived from London, and would be happy to see the gentleman from Tellson’s.", "* 그분: 원문은 프랑스어 '마드무아젤'(아가씨)이다. 몇분도 채 지나지 않아 급사가 들어와 매네뜨 양이 런던에서 도착하여 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 뵙고 싶어한다고 알렸다."]], [["“So soon?”", "\"이렇게 빨리?\""]], [["Miss Manette had taken some refreshment on the road, and required none then, and was extremely anxious to see the gentleman from Tellson’s immediately, if it suited his pleasure and convenience.", "매네뜨 양은 오는 길에 요기를 좀 했으므로 지금은 아무것도 필요하지 않으며, 다만 신사분께서 좋으시고 편하시다면 텔슨 은행에서 온 신사를 즉시 뵙기를 몹시 간절히 바란다고 했다."]], [["The gentleman from Tellson’s had nothing left for it but to empty his glass with an air of stolid desperation, settle his odd little flaxen wig at the ears, and follow the waiter to Miss Manette’s apartment.", "텔슨 은행에서 온 신사로서는 무덤덤한 체념의 표정으로 잔을 비우고, 묘하게 작은 아마빛 가발을 귀에 맞추어 매만진 뒤, 매네뜨 양의 방으로 급사를 따라가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["It was a large, dark room, furnished in a funereal manner with black horsehair, and loaded with heavy dark tables.", "그곳은 크고 어두운 방으로, 검은 말총으로 장례식처럼 음울하게 꾸며져 있었고 묵직하고 검은 탁자들이 잔뜩 놓여 있었다."], ["These had been oiled and oiled, until the two tall candles on the table in the middle of the room were gloomily reflected on every leaf; as if they were buried, in deep graves of black mahogany, and no light to speak of could be expected from them until they were dug out.", "그 탁자들은 거듭거듭 기름칠이 되어, 방 한가운데 탁자에 놓인 키 큰 양초 두 자루가 탁자 면마다 음침하게 비쳐 보일 정도였다. 마치 그 양초들이 검은 마호가니의 깊은 무덤 속에 파묻혀, 거기서 파내기 전에는 빛이라 할 만한 것을 도무지 기대할 수 없는 듯했다."]], [["The obscurity was so difficult to penetrate that Mr.", "어둠을 뚫고 보기가 어찌나 어려웠던지, 닳고 닳은 터키산 양탄자 위를 더듬더듬 나아가던 로리 씨는 매네뜨 양이 잠시 어느 옆방에 있으려니 짐작했다."], ["Lorry, picking his way over the well-worn Turkey carpet, supposed Miss Manette to be, for the moment, in some adjacent room, until, having got past the two tall candles, he saw standing to receive him by the table between them and the fire, a young lady of not more than seventeen, in a riding-cloak, and still holding her straw travelling-hat by its ribbon in her hand.", "그러나 키 큰 양초 두 자루를 지나치자, 양초와 난로 사이의 탁자 곁에서 자신을 맞이하려 서 있는 열일곱이 채 안 되어 보이는 젊은 숙녀가 눈에 들어왔다. 그녀는 승마용 외투를 걸치고 짚으로 엮은 여행용 모자를 아직 리본을 쥔 채 손에 들고 있었다."], ["As his eyes rested on a short, slight, pretty figure, a quantity of golden hair, a pair of blue eyes that met his own with an inquiring look, and a forehead with a singular capacity (remembering how young and smooth it was), of rifting and knitting itself into an expression that was not quite one of perplexity, or wonder, or alarm, or merely of a bright fixed attention, though it included all the four expressions--as his eyes rested on these things, a sudden vivid likeness passed before him, of a child whom he had held in his arms on the passage across that very Channel, one cold time, when the hail drifted heavily and the sea ran high.", "작고 가냘프고 어여쁜 자태, 풍성한 금발, 의아한 눈빛으로 자신의 눈과 마주친 한 쌍의 푸른 눈, 그리고 (그것이 얼마나 젊고 매끄러운지를 떠올려보면) 곤혹스러움이나 놀라움, 경악, 혹은 그저 또렷하고 고정된 주의력이라고만 하기에는 — 그 네 가지 표정을 모두 담고 있으면서도 — 어느 하나로 단정 짓기 어려운 표정으로 갈라지고 일그러지는 남다른 능력을 지닌 이마, 그의 눈이 이런 것들에 머무는 순간, 우박이 거세게 흩날리고 바다가 높이 일렁이던 어느 추운 날 바로 그 해협을 건너며 자신의 품에 안았던 아이의 모습이 문득 또렷이 그의 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔다. 그 닮은 모습은, 그녀 뒤에 놓인 길쭉한 체경 표면을 스치는 한 줄기 입김처럼 사라져버렸다."], ["The likeness passed away, like a breath along the surface of the gaunt pier-glass behind her, on the frame of which, a hospital procession of negro cupids, several headless and all cripples, were offering black baskets of Dead Sea fruit to black divinities of the feminine gender--and he made his formal bow to Miss Manette.", "그 거울 틀에는 머리가 없는 것도 여럿이고 모두가 불구인, 흑인 큐피드들의 병원 행렬이 사해의 열매가 담긴 검은 바구니를 여성 흑인 신들에게 바치고 있었다. 그러고서 그는 매네뜨 양에게 격식 있게 인사를 올렸다."]], [["“Pray take a seat, sir.”", "\"자리에 앉으시지요, 어르신.\""], ["In a very clear and pleasant young voice; a little foreign in its accent, but a very little indeed.", "매우 또렷하고 다정한 젊은 목소리였다. 억양이 조금 외국 티가 났으나, 정말 아주 조금이었다."]], [["“I kiss your hand, miss,” said Mr.", "\"손에 입맞춤을 드립니다, 아가씨.\""], ["Lorry, with the manners of an earlier date, as he made his formal bow again, and took his seat.", "로리 씨는 한 세대 전의 예법으로 다시 격식 있게 인사하며 그렇게 말하고는 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“I received a letter from the Bank, sir, yesterday, informing me that some intelligence--or discovery--”", "\"어제 은행에서 편지를 한 통 받았습니다, 어르신. 어떤 소식이, 아니 발견된 것이 —\""]], [["“The word is not material, miss; either word will do.”", "\"단어는 중요치 않습니다, 아가씨. 어느 쪽이든 무방합니다.\""]], [["“--respecting the small property of my poor father, whom I never saw--so long dead--”", "\"— 한 번도 뵌 적 없는, 그토록 오래전에 돌아가신 가엾은 제 아버지의 얼마 안 되는 재산에 관한 것이라고요 —\""]], [["Mr. Lorry moved in his chair, and cast a troubled look towards the hospital procession of negro cupids.", "로리 씨는 의자에서 몸을 들썩이며, 흑인 큐피드들의 병원 행렬 쪽으로 곤혹스러운 시선을 던졌다."], ["As if they had any help for anybody in their absurd baskets!", "마치 그 우스꽝스러운 바구니 속에 누군가를 도울 무언가가 들어 있기라도 하다는 듯이!"]], [["“--rendered it necessary that I should go to Paris, there to communicate with a gentleman of the Bank, so good as to be despatched to Paris for the purpose.”", "\"— 그래서 제가 빠리로 가서, 그 일을 위해 빠리로 파견될 만큼 친절하신 은행의 신사 한 분과 의논하는 것이 필요하게 되었다고요.\" \"제가 바로 그 사람입니다.\""]], [["“Myself.” “As I was prepared to hear, sir.”", "\"그러리라 짐작하고 있었습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["She curtseyed to him (young ladies made curtseys in those days), with a pretty desire to convey to him that she felt how much older and wiser he was than she.", "그녀는 그에게 무릎 굽혀 인사했고(그 시절 젊은 숙녀들은 무릎 굽혀 인사했다), 그가 자신보다 얼마나 더 연륜 있고 현명한지 느끼고 있음을 전하고 싶어하는 어여쁜 마음이 거기 묻어났다."], ["He made her another bow.", "그는 그녀에게 다시 한번 인사했다."]], [["“I replied to the Bank, sir, that as it was considered necessary, by those who know, and who are so kind as to advise me, that I should go to France, and that as I am an orphan and have no friend who could go with me, I should esteem it highly if I might be permitted to place myself, during the journey, under that worthy gentleman’s protection.", "\"제가 은행에 답장하기를, 어르신, 사정을 잘 알고 또 친절하게도 제게 조언해주시는 분들이 제가 프랑스로 가야 한다고 판단하셨고, 저는 고아이며 함께 가줄 벗도 없으니, 여행 동안 그 훌륭하신 신사분의 보호 아래 제 몸을 맡길 수 있다면 더없이 영광이겠다고 했습니다."], ["The gentleman had left London, but I think a messenger was sent after him to beg the favour of his waiting for me here.”", "그 신사분은 런던을 떠나셨으나, 그분께 여기서 저를 기다려주십사 청하는 전갈을 뒤따라 보냈으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["“I was happy,” said Mr.", "\"기뻤습니다.\""], ["Lorry, “to be entrusted with the charge.", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"그 임무를 맡게 되어서요."], ["I shall be more happy to execute it.”", "그것을 수행하게 된다면 더 기쁠 것입니다.\""]], [["“Sir, I thank you indeed.", "\"어르신, 정말 감사드립니다."], ["I thank you very gratefully.", "진심으로 감사드립니다."], ["It was told me by the Bank that the gentleman would explain to me the details of the business, and that I must prepare myself to find them of a surprising nature.", "은행에서 말하기를, 그 신사분께서 일의 자세한 내용을 제게 설명해주실 것이며, 저는 그것이 놀라운 성질의 것임을 각오해야 한다고 했습니다."], ["I have done my best to prepare myself, and I naturally have a strong and eager interest to know what they are.”", "저는 마음의 준비를 하려 최선을 다했고, 당연히 그것이 무엇인지 알고 싶은 마음이 강하고도 간절합니다.\""]], [["“Naturally,”", "\"당연하지요.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Yes--I--”", "\"예 — 저 —\""]], [["After a pause, he added, again settling the crisp flaxen wig at the ears, “It is very difficult to begin.”", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤, 그는 다시 매끄러운 아마빛 가발을 귀에 맞추어 매만지며 덧붙였다. \"이야기를 꺼내기가 무척 어렵군요.\""]], [["He did not begin, but, in his indecision, met her glance.", "그는 이야기를 꺼내지 못한 채, 우유부단함 속에서 그녀의 시선과 마주쳤다. 그 젊은 이마가 들어올려지며 그 남다른 표정을 지었다."], ["The young forehead lifted itself into that singular expression--but it was pretty and characteristic, besides being singular--and she raised her hand, as if with an involuntary action she caught at, or stayed some passing shadow.", "그러나 그것은 남다른 동시에 어여쁘고 그녀다운 표정이었다. 그녀는 마치 무심결에 지나가는 어떤 그림자를 붙잡거나 멈춰 세우려는 듯 손을 들어올렸다."]], [["“Are you quite a stranger to me, sir?”", "\"제게는 전혀 모르는 분이신가요, 어르신?\""]], [["“Am I not?” Mr.", "\"그렇지 않겠습니까?\""], ["Lorry opened his hands, and extended them outwards with an argumentative smile.", "로리 씨는 두 손을 펼쳐 바깥으로 내밀며 따져 묻는 듯한 미소를 지었다."]], [["Between the eyebrows and just over the little feminine nose, the line of which was as delicate and fine as it was possible to be, the expression deepened itself as she took her seat thoughtfully in the chair by which she had hitherto remained standing.", "눈썹 사이, 더없이 섬세하고 고운 선을 그린 그 자그마한 여성스러운 코 바로 위에서, 그 표정은 한층 깊어졌다. 그녀는 그때껏 곁에 서 있던 의자에 생각에 잠긴 채 앉았다."], ["He watched her as she mused, and the moment she raised her eyes again, went on:", "그는 그녀가 골똘히 생각에 잠긴 모습을 지켜보다가, 그녀가 다시 눈을 들어올리는 순간 말을 이었다."]], [["“In your adopted country, I presume, I cannot do better than address you as a young English lady, Miss Manette?”", "\"아가씨께서 정착하신 나라를 생각하면, 매네뜨 양, 젊은 영국 숙녀로 부르는 것이 가장 좋겠지요?\""]], [["“If you please, sir.”", "\"그러시지요, 어르신.\""]], [["“Miss Manette, I am a man of business.", "\"매네뜨 양, 저는 사무를 보는 사람입니다."], ["I have a business charge to acquit myself of.", "처리해야 할 사무상의 임무가 있지요."], ["In your reception of it, don’t heed me any more than if I was a speaking machine--truly, I am not much else.", "그것을 들으실 때, 저를 말하는 기계 이상으로 여기지 말아주십시오. 사실 저는 그것 외에 별다른 것이 아닙니다."], ["I will, with your leave, relate to you, miss, the story of one of our customers.” “Story!”", "괜찮으시다면, 아가씨, 저희 고객 한 분의 이야기를 들려드리겠습니다.\""]], [["He seemed wilfully to mistake the word she had repeated, when he added, in a hurry, “Yes, customers; in the banking business we usually call our connection our customers. He was a French gentleman; a scientific gentleman; a man of great acquirements--a Doctor.”", "\"이야기라고요!\" 그는 그녀가 되뇐 그 단어를 일부러 잘못 알아들은 척하는 듯, 서둘러 덧붙였다. \"예, 고객 말입니다. 은행 일에서 우리는 보통 거래처를 고객이라 부르지요. 그분은 프랑스 신사였습니다. 학식 있는 신사였고, 큰 학문을 이룬 분이었지요. 의사였습니다.\""]], [["“Not of Beauvais?”", "\"보베의 의사는 아니지요?\""]], [["“Why, yes, of Beauvais.", "\"아니, 그렇습니다, 보베의 의사이지요."], ["Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was of Beauvais.", "아가씨의 아버님이신 매네뜨 씨처럼, 그 신사도 보베 출신이었습니다."], ["Like Monsieur Manette, your father, the gentleman was of repute in Paris.", "아가씨의 아버님이신 매네뜨 씨처럼, 그 신사도 빠리에서 명망이 높았지요."], ["I had the honour of knowing him there.", "저는 거기서 그분을 알게 되는 영광을 누렸습니다."], ["Our relations were business relations, but confidential.", "우리 사이는 업무상의 관계였으나, 막역한 사이였습니다."], ["I was at that time in our French House, and had been--oh!", "그때 저는 우리 프랑스 상회에 있었고, 그렇게 — 아!"], ["twenty years.”", "이십 년을 있었지요.\""]], [["“At that time--I may ask, at what time, sir?”", "\"그때라면 — 여쭤봐도 될까요, 언제를 말씀하시는지요, 어르신?\""]], [["“I speak, miss, of twenty years ago.", "\"저는, 아가씨, 이십 년 전의 일을 말하는 것입니다."], ["He married--an English lady--and I was one of the trustees.", "그분은 영국 숙녀와 결혼했고, 저는 그 재산 관리인 가운데 한 사람이었습니다."], ["His affairs, like the affairs of many other French gentlemen and French families, were entirely in Tellson’s hands.", "다른 많은 프랑스 신사들과 프랑스 가문들의 사정처럼, 그분의 재산도 전부 텔슨 은행의 손에 맡겨져 있었지요. 마찬가지로 저는 우리 고객 수십 명을 위해 이런저런 종류의 관리인이거나, 관리인이었습니다."], ["In a similar way I am, or I have been, trustee of one kind or other for scores of our customers.", "이것은 한낱 업무상의 관계입니다, 아가씨. 거기에는 우정도, 특별한 관심도, 정 같은 것도 없습니다."], ["These are mere business relations, miss; there is no friendship in them, no particular interest, nothing like sentiment.", "저는 업무를 보는 인생을 살며 이 사람에서 저 사람으로 옮겨다녔으니, 하루의 업무 속에서 이 고객에서 저 고객으로 옮겨다니는 것과 똑같습니다."], ["I have passed from one to another, in the course of my business life, just as I pass from one of our customers to another in the course of my business day; in short, I have no feelings; I am a mere machine.", "요컨대 저는 감정이 없습니다. 저는 한낱 기계일 뿐입니다."], ["To go on--”", "계속하자면 —\""]], [["“But this is my father’s story, sir; and I begin to think”--the curiously roughened forehead was very intent upon him--“that when I was left an orphan through my mother’s surviving my father only two years, it was you who brought me to England.", "\"하지만 이건 제 아버지의 이야기로군요, 어르신. 그리고 저는 생각하기 시작했습니다\""], ["I am almost sure it was you.”", "— 묘하게 거칠어진 이마가 그를 골똘히 주시하고 있었다 — \"제 어머니가 아버지보다 겨우 두 해를 더 사신 뒤에 제가 고아가 되어 영국으로 옮겨졌을 때, 저를 영국으로 데려오신 분이 바로 어르신이었다고요. 거의 틀림없이 어르신이었어요.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry took the hesitating little hand that confidingly advanced to take his, and he put it with some ceremony to his lips.", "로리 씨는 자신의 손을 잡으려고 미덥게 다가온 그 머뭇거리는 작은 손을 잡아, 다소 격식을 차려 입술에 가져다 댔다."], ["He then conducted the young lady straightway to her chair again, and, holding the chair-back with his left hand, and using his right by turns to rub his chin, pull his wig at the ears, or point what he said, stood looking down into her face while she sat looking up into his.", "그러고는 젊은 숙녀를 곧장 다시 의자로 이끌어, 왼손으로 의자 등받이를 잡고 오른손으로는 번갈아 턱을 문지르거나 가발을 귀에서 당기거나 하는 말에 손짓을 곁들이며, 그녀가 올려다보는 동안 그녀의 얼굴을 내려다보고 서 있었다."]], [["“Miss Manette, it was I.", "\"매네뜨 양, 그건 바로 저였습니다."], ["And you will see how truly I spoke of myself just now, in saying I had no feelings, and that all the relations I hold with my fellow-creatures are mere business relations, when you reflect that I have never seen you since.", "그리고 조금 전에 제가 감정이 없으며 동료 인간들과 맺은 모든 관계가 한낱 업무상의 관계일 뿐이라고 한 말이 얼마나 진실이었는지 아시게 될 겁니다. 그 뒤로 제가 아가씨를 한 번도 뵌 적 없다는 것을 헤아려보시면 말입니다."], ["No; you have been the ward of Tellson’s House since, and I have been busy with the other business of Tellson’s House since.", "그렇습니다, 그 뒤로 아가씨는 텔슨 상회의 피후견인이었고, 저는 그 뒤로 텔슨 상회의 다른 업무로 분주했지요."], ["Feelings!", "감정이라니요!"], ["I have no time for them, no chance of them.", "저는 그럴 시간도 없고, 그럴 기회도 없습니다."], ["I pass my whole life, miss, in turning an immense pecuniary Mangle.”", "아가씨, 저는 평생을 거대한 금전의 짜내는 기계를 돌리는 데 바치고 있습니다.\""]], [["After this odd description of his daily routine of employment, Mr.", "자신의 나날의 업무를 이렇게 묘한 말로 설명한 뒤, 로리 씨는 두 손으로 아마빛 가발을 머리에 납작하게 눌렀다(그건 전혀 불필요한 일이었으니, 그 반들반들한 표면은 이미 그보다 더 납작할 수 없었다)."], ["Lorry flattened his flaxen wig upon his head with both hands (which was most unnecessary, for nothing could be flatter than its shining surface was before), and resumed his former attitude.", "그러고는 이전의 자세로 돌아갔다."]], [["“So far, miss (as you have remarked), this is the story of your regretted father.", "\"여기까지는, 아가씨, (말씀하신 대로) 아가씨의 그리운 아버님 이야기입니다."], ["Now comes the difference.", "이제 다른 점이 나옵니다."], ["If your father had not died when he did--Don’t be frightened!", "만일 아가씨의 아버님이 그때 돌아가신 게 아니라면 — 놀라지 마십시오!"], ["How you start!”", "어찌 그리 흠칫하십니까!\""]], [["She did, indeed, start.", "그녀는 정말로 흠칫했다."], ["And she caught his wrist with both her hands.", "그리고 두 손으로 그의 손목을 붙잡았다."]], [["“Pray,” said Mr.", "\"부디,\""], ["Lorry, in a soothing tone, bringing his left hand from the back of the chair to lay it on the supplicatory fingers that clasped him in so violent a tremble: “pray control your agitation--a matter of business.", "로리 씨가 달래는 어조로, 의자 등받이에서 왼손을 떼어, 그토록 격하게 떨며 자신을 붙잡은 애원하는 손가락 위에 얹으며 말했다. \"부디 동요를 가라앉히십시오."], ["As I was saying--”", "업무상의 일입니다. 제가 말씀드리던 대로 —\""]], [["Her look so discomposed him that he stopped, wandered, and began anew:", "그녀의 표정이 그를 어찌나 당황하게 했던지, 그는 말을 멈추고 갈피를 잃었다가 다시 시작했다."]], [["“As I was saying; if Monsieur Manette had not died; if he had suddenly and silently disappeared; if he had been spirited away; if it had not been difficult to guess to what dreadful place, though no art could trace him; if he had an enemy in some compatriot who could exercise a privilege that I in my own time have known the boldest people afraid to speak of in a whisper, across the water there; for instance, the privilege of filling up blank forms for the consignment of any one to the oblivion of a prison for any length of time; if his wife had implored the king, the queen, the court, the clergy, for any tidings of him, and all quite in vain;--then the history of your father would have been the history of this unfortunate gentleman, the Doctor of Beauvais.”", "\"제가 말씀드리던 대로, 만일 매네뜨 씨가 돌아가신 게 아니라면, 만일 그분이 갑자기 소리 없이 자취를 감추셨다면, 만일 그분이 어디론가 끌려가셨다면, 어떤 수단으로도 그분을 추적할 수 없었으되 그 끔찍한 곳이 어디인지 짐작하기가 어렵지 않았다면, 만일 어느 동포 가운데 그분의 적이 있어, 제가 한창때에 가장 대담한 사람들조차 저 바다 건너에서는 속삭임으로도 입에 올리기를 두려워하던 어떤 특권을 행사할 수 있었다면 — 이를테면 누구든 원하는 만큼 오래 감옥의 망각 속에 처넣을 수 있도록 백지 영장을 채워 넣는 특권 말입니다 — 만일 그분의 부인이 왕과 왕비, 궁정과 성직자들에게 그분의 소식을 애원했으되 모두 헛수고였다면, 그렇다면 아가씨 아버님의 사연은 바로 이 불운한 신사, 보베의 의사의 사연이 되었을 것입니다.\""]], [["“I entreat you to tell me more, sir.”", "\"더 말씀해주시기를 간청합니다, 어르신.\""]], [["“I will.", "\"그러겠습니다."], ["I am going to.", "그러려는 참입니다."], ["You can bear it?”", "견디실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“I can bear anything but the uncertainty you leave me in at this moment.”", "\"지금 이 순간 어르신께서 저를 두신 이 불확실함 말고는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“You speak collectedly, and you--are collected.", "\"침착하게 말씀하시는군요, 그리고 침착하십니다."], ["That’s good!”", "좋습니다!\""], ["(Though his manner was less satisfied than his words.) “A matter of business.", "(그러나 그의 태도는 말만큼 흡족하지 못했다.) \"업무상의 일입니다."], ["Regard it as a matter of business--business that must be done.", "그저 업무상의 일로 여기십시오. 반드시 처리해야 할 업무로요."], ["Now if this doctor’s wife, though a lady of great courage and spirit, had suffered so intensely from this cause before her little child was born--”", "자, 만일 이 의사의 부인이, 대단한 용기와 기개를 지닌 숙녀였음에도 불구하고, 그 어린 아이가 태어나기 전에 이 일로 너무도 극심하게 고통받았다면 —\""]], [["“The little child was a daughter, sir.”", "\"그 어린 아이는 딸이었지요, 어르신.\""]], [["“A daughter.", "\"딸이었지요."], ["A-a-matter of business--don’t be distressed.", "업, 업무상의 일입니다. 괴로워 마십시오."], ["Miss, if the poor lady had suffered so intensely before her little child was born, that she came to the determination of sparing the poor child the inheritance of any part of the agony she had known the pains of, by rearing her in the belief that her father was dead--No, don’t kneel!", "아가씨, 만일 그 가엾은 부인이 어린 아이가 태어나기 전에 너무도 극심하게 고통받은 나머지, 가엾은 그 아이에게 자신이 겪은 고뇌의 그 어떤 부분도 물려주지 않으려고, 아버지가 죽었다고 믿게 키워 그 아이를 지켜주기로 결심했다면 — 아니, 무릎 꿇지 마십시오!"], ["In Heaven’s name why should you kneel to me!”", "맙소사, 어찌하여 제게 무릎을 꿇으십니까!\""]], [["“For the truth.", "\"진실을 위해서예요."], ["O dear, good, compassionate sir, for the truth!”", "오, 친애하고 선하고 자비로우신 어르신, 진실을 위해서예요!\""]], [["“A--a matter of business.", "\"업, 업무상의 일입니다."], ["You confuse me, and how can I transact business if I am confused?", "아가씨께서 저를 혼란스럽게 하시는데, 제가 혼란스러우면 어떻게 업무를 처리하겠습니까?"], ["Let us be clear-headed.", "우리 머리를 맑게 합시다."], ["If you could kindly mention now, for instance, what nine times ninepence are, or how many shillings in twenty guineas, it would be so encouraging.", "이를테면 지금 친절히 말씀해주시겠습니까, 구 펜스의 아홉 곱이 얼마인지, 혹은 이십 기니가 몇 실링인지를요. 그러면 무척 힘이 날 것입니다."], ["I should be so much more at my ease about your state of mind.”", "아가씨의 마음 상태에 대해 제가 한결 안심할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["Without directly answering to this appeal, she sat so still when he had very gently raised her, and the hands that had not ceased to clasp his wrists were so much more steady than they had been, that she communicated some reassurance to Mr. Jarvis Lorry.", "이 호소에 곧바로 답하지는 않았으나, 그가 아주 부드럽게 그녀를 일으켜 세웠을 때 그녀는 어찌나 잠잠히 앉아 있었던지, 그리고 그의 손목을 놓지 않던 두 손이 전보다 훨씬 안정되어 있었던지, 그녀는 자비스 로리 씨에게 얼마간 안도감을 전해주었다."]], [["“That’s right, that’s right.", "\"바로 그겁니다, 바로 그래요."], ["Courage!", "용기를 내십시오!"], ["Business!", "업무입니다!"], ["You have business before you; useful business.", "아가씨 앞에는 업무가 있습니다. 유익한 업무지요."], ["Miss Manette, your mother took this course with you.", "매네뜨 양, 아가씨의 어머님은 아가씨에게 이런 방도를 택하셨습니다."], ["And when she died--I believe broken-hearted--having never slackened her unavailing search for your father, she left you, at two years old, to grow to be blooming, beautiful, and happy, without the dark cloud upon you of living in uncertainty whether your father soon wore his heart out in prison, or wasted there through many lingering years.”", "그리고 아가씨 아버님을 찾는 헛된 수색을 한 번도 늦추지 않으신 채, 어머님이 돌아가셨을 때 — 제 생각에 가슴이 미어져 돌아가신 듯합니다 — 그분은 두 살 난 아가씨를 남기셨으니, 아버지가 감옥에서 머지않아 가슴이 닳아 없어졌는지, 아니면 거기서 여러 더딘 세월을 거치며 시들어갔는지 알 수 없는 불확실함 속에서 살아가는 어두운 먹구름을 아가씨에게 드리우지 않은 채, 꽃처럼 피어나고 아름답고 행복하게 자라도록 하신 것입니다.\""]], [["As he said the words he looked down, with an admiring pity, on the flowing golden hair; as if he pictured to himself that it might have been already tinged with grey.", "이 말을 하면서 그는 흘러내리는 금발을 흠모 어린 연민의 눈으로 내려다보았다. 마치 그 머리카락이 이미 잿빛으로 물들었을 수도 있었으리라 마음속으로 그려보기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["“You know that your parents had no great possession, and that what they had was secured to your mother and to you.", "\"아가씨의 부모님께서 큰 재산을 갖고 계시지 않았다는 것, 그리고 가지신 것은 어머님과 아가씨께 안전하게 확보되어 있었다는 것은 아실 겁니다."], ["There has been no new discovery, of money, or of any other property; but--”", "돈이든 다른 어떤 재산이든 새로이 발견된 것은 없습니다. 그러나 —\""]], [["He felt his wrist held closer, and he stopped.", "그는 손목이 한층 꽉 쥐어지는 것을 느끼고는 말을 멈췄다."], ["The expression in the forehead, which had so particularly attracted his notice, and which was now immovable, had deepened into one of pain and horror.", "그토록 유난히 그의 주의를 끌었고 이제는 미동도 없던 그 이마의 표정이, 한층 깊어져 고통과 공포의 표정이 되어 있었다."]], [["“But he has been--been found.", "\"그러나 그분이 — 발견되셨습니다. 그분은 살아 계십니다."], ["He is alive.", "크게 변하셨을 가능성이 높습니다."], ["Greatly changed, it is too probable; almost a wreck, it is possible; though we will hope the best.", "거의 폐인이 되셨을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 우리는 가장 좋은 쪽으로 바라야겠지요."], ["Still, alive.", "어쨌든 살아 계십니다."], ["Your father has been taken to the house of an old servant in Paris, and we are going there: I, to identify him if I can: you, to restore him to life, love, duty, rest, comfort.”", "아가씨의 아버님은 빠리에 있는 옛 하인의 집으로 모셔졌고, 우리는 그리로 갈 것입니다. 저는 가능하다면 그분의 신원을 확인하기 위해, 아가씨는 그분께 생명과 사랑, 도리와 안식과 위안을 되돌려드리기 위해서요.\""]], [["A shiver ran through her frame, and from it through his.", "그녀의 온몸에 전율이 흘렀고, 그것이 그녀에게서 그에게로 옮겨갔다."], ["She said, in a low, distinct, awe-stricken voice, as if she were saying it in a dream,", "그녀는 마치 꿈속에서 말하기라도 하듯, 낮고 또렷하며 경외에 사로잡힌 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I am going to see his Ghost!", "\"저는 아버지의 유령을 보러 가는군요!"], ["It will be his Ghost--not him!”", "그분이 아니라 그분의 유령일 거예요!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry quietly chafed the hands that held his arm.", "로리 씨는 자신의 팔을 붙잡은 두 손을 가만히 문질렀다."], ["“There, there, there!", "\"자, 자, 자!"], ["See now, see now!", "보십시오, 보세요!"], ["The best and the worst are known to you, now.", "이제 가장 좋은 것과 가장 나쁜 것을 다 아셨습니다."], ["You are well on your way to the poor wronged gentleman, and, with a fair sea voyage, and a fair land journey, you will be soon at his dear side.”", "아가씨는 부당하게 고초를 겪으신 그 가엾은 신사분께로 가는 길에 잘 올라서신 겁니다. 순조로운 바닷길과 순조로운 육로 여행을 거치면, 곧 그분의 정다운 곁에 다다르실 겁니다.\""]], [["She repeated in the same tone, sunk to a whisper, “I have been free, I have been happy, yet his Ghost has never haunted me!”", "그녀는 같은 어조로, 속삭임으로 잦아들며 되뇌었다. \"저는 자유로웠고, 저는 행복했어요, 그런데도 그분의 유령은 한 번도 저를 따라다니지 않았어요!\""]], [["“Only one thing more,” said Mr.", "\"한 가지만 더 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["Lorry, laying stress upon it as a wholesome means of enforcing her attention: “he has been found under another name; his own, long forgotten or long concealed.", "로리 씨는 그녀의 주의를 끌어들이는 유익한 수단으로 그 점을 강조하며 말했다. \"그분은 다른 이름으로 발견되셨습니다."], ["It would be worse than useless now to inquire which; worse than useless to seek to know whether he has been for years overlooked, or always designedly held prisoner.", "본래의 이름은 오래 잊혔거나 오래 숨겨져 있었지요. 지금 어느 쪽인지 묻는 것은 부질없는 것만도 못한 일입니다."], ["It would be worse than useless now to make any inquiries, because it would be dangerous.", "그분이 여러 해 동안 잊힌 채 방치되셨는지, 아니면 줄곧 의도적으로 갇혀 계셨는지 알아내려 애쓰는 것도 부질없는 것만도 못한 일입니다."], ["Better not to mention the subject, anywhere or in any way, and to remove him--for a while at all events--out of France.", "지금 어떤 탐문이든 하는 것은 부질없는 것만도 못한 일이니, 위험한 일이기 때문입니다. 어디서든 어떤 식으로든 이 문제를 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫고, 그분을 — 적어도 당분간은 — 프랑스 밖으로 모셔내는 편이 낫습니다."], ["Even I, safe as an Englishman, and even Tellson’s, important as they are to French credit, avoid all naming of the matter.", "영국인이라 안전한 저조차, 또 프랑스 신용에 그토록 중요한 텔슨 은행조차도 이 문제를 입에 올리는 일은 전부 피합니다."], ["I carry about me, not a scrap of writing openly referring to it.", "저는 이 일을 드러내놓고 언급하는 글이라곤 한 조각도 몸에 지니고 다니지 않습니다."], ["This is a secret service altogether.", "이것은 전적으로 비밀 임무입니다."], ["My credentials, entries, and memoranda, are all comprehended in the one line, ‘Recalled to Life;’ which may mean anything.", "제 신임장과 기록, 비망록은 전부 단 한 줄에 담겨 있으니, '되살아나다'라는 말입니다. 무엇이든 뜻할 수 있는 말이지요."], ["But what is the matter!", "그런데 어찌 된 일입니까!"], ["She doesn’t notice a word!", "그녀는 한 마디도 듣고 있지 않습니다!"], ["Miss Manette!”", "매네뜨 양!\""]], [["Perfectly still and silent, and not even fallen back in her chair, she sat under his hand, utterly insensible; with her eyes open and fixed upon him, and with that last expression looking as if it were carved or branded into her forehead. So close was her hold upon his arm, that he feared to detach himself lest he should hurt her; therefore he called out loudly for assistance without moving.", "* 되살아나다: 원문 'Recalled to Life'. 소설 1부의 제목이기도 하다. 완전히 미동도 없이 소리도 없이, 의자에 등을 기대지조차 않은 채, 그녀는 그의 손 아래에서 전혀 의식 없이 앉아 있었다. 두 눈을 뜬 채 그에게 고정하고, 그 마지막 표정을 마치 이마에 새겨지거나 낙인찍힌 듯 띤 채로. 그녀가 그의 팔을 어찌나 꽉 붙잡고 있었던지, 그는 그녀를 다치게 할까 두려워 몸을 떼지 못했다. 그래서 그는 꼼짝하지 않은 채 큰 소리로 도움을 청했다."]], [["A wild-looking woman, whom even in his agitation, Mr.", "거칠어 보이는 한 여자가, 로리 씨가 동요하는 중에도 온통 붉은 빛깔이며 머리가 붉고, 무언가 유난히 몸에 딱 붙는 차림새에, 머리에는 척탄병의 나무 됫박, 그것도 넉넉한 됫박이나 커다란 스틸턴 치즈 같은 더없이 놀라운 보닛을 쓰고 있음을 알아챈 그 여자가, 여관 하인들보다 앞서 방으로 달려 들어왔다."], ["Lorry observed to be all of a red colour, and to have red hair, and to be dressed in some extraordinary tight-fitting fashion, and to have on her head a most wonderful bonnet like a Grenadier wooden measure, and good measure too, or a great Stilton cheese, came running into the room in advance of the inn servants, and soon settled the question of his detachment from the poor young lady, by laying a brawny hand upon his chest, and sending him flying back against the nearest wall.", "그러고는 억센 손을 그의 가슴에 들이밀어 그를 가장 가까운 벽으로 나가떨어지게 함으로써, 가엾은 젊은 숙녀에게서 그를 떼어내는 문제를 단숨에 해결했다."]], [["(“I really think this must be a man!”", "(\"이건 정말이지 남자가 틀림없어!\""], ["was Mr. Lorry’s breathless reflection, simultaneously with his coming against the wall.)", "라는 것이, 벽에 부딪치는 것과 동시에 떠오른 로리 씨의 숨 가쁜 생각이었다.)"]], [["“Why, look at you all!”", "\"아니, 다들 뭣들 하는 거예요!\""], ["bawled this figure, addressing the inn servants.", "그 인물이 여관 하인들을 향해 고함쳤다."], ["“Why don’t you go and fetch things, instead of standing there staring at me?", "\"왜 가서 물건들을 가져오지 않고, 거기 서서 나를 빤히 쳐다보기만 하는 거예요?"], ["I am not so much to look at, am I?", "내가 그렇게 볼만한 구경거리예요?"], ["Why don’t you go and fetch things?", "왜 가서 물건들을 가져오지 않는 거냐고요?"], ["I’ll let you know, if you don’t bring smelling-salts, cold water, and vinegar, quick, I will.”", "정신 차리는 약과 찬물과 식초를 얼른 가져오지 않으면, 어디 두고 봐요, 정말이지 가만 안 둘 테니!\""]], [["There was an immediate dispersal for these restoratives, and she softly laid the patient on a sofa, and tended her with great skill and gentleness: calling her “my precious!”", "이 회복제들을 가지러 즉시 흩어졌고, 그녀는 환자를 소파에 부드럽게 눕히고 대단한 솜씨와 다정함으로 보살폈다."], ["and “my bird!” and spreading her golden hair aside over her shoulders with great pride and care.", "\"내 보물!\", \"내 새!\"라고 부르며, 금발을 어깨 너머로 펼쳐주는 모습에는 큰 자부심과 정성이 어려 있었다."]], [["“And you in brown!” she said, indignantly turning to Mr.", "\"그리고 거기 갈색 옷 양반!\""], ["Lorry; “couldn’t you tell her what you had to tell her, without frightening her to death?", "그녀가 분개하여 로리 씨를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이 아가씨를 죽을 만큼 겁주지 않고는 할 말을 못 했어요?"], ["Look at her, with her pretty pale face and her cold hands.", "좀 봐요, 저 어여쁘고 창백한 얼굴이며 차가운 손을."], ["Do you call that being a Banker?”", "그러고도 당신이 은행원이라고요?\""]], [["Mr. Lorry was so exceedingly disconcerted by a question so hard to answer, that he could only look on, at a distance, with much feebler sympathy and humility, while the strong woman, having banished the inn servants under the mysterious penalty of “letting them know”", "로리 씨는 답하기가 너무도 어려운 그 질문에 어찌나 당황했던지, 멀찍이 서서 훨씬 기운 빠진 동정과 겸손함으로 지켜볼 수밖에 없었다."], ["something not mentioned if they stayed there, staring, recovered her charge by a regular series of gradations, and coaxed her to lay her drooping head upon her shoulder.", "그동안 그 억센 여자는, 여관 하인들이 거기 서서 빤히 쳐다보고만 있으면 입에 담지 않은 무언가를 \"가만 안 두겠다\"는 알 수 없는 협박으로 쫓아낸 뒤, 차근차근 단계를 밟아 자신이 맡은 사람을 회복시켰고, 축 늘어진 그녀의 머리를 자기 어깨에 기대도록 어르고 달랬다."]], [["“I hope she will do well now,”", "\"이제 괜찮아지겠지요.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“No thanks to you in brown, if she does.", "\"괜찮아진다 해도 거기 갈색 옷 양반 덕은 아니에요."], ["My darling pretty!”", "내 사랑스러운 아가씨!\""]], [["“I hope,” said Mr.", "\"바라건대,\""], ["Lorry, after another pause of feeble sympathy and humility, “that you accompany Miss Manette to France?”", "로리 씨가 다시 한 차례 기운 빠진 동정과 겸손함의 침묵 끝에 말했다. \"당신께서 매네뜨 양과 함께 프랑스로 가주시겠지요?\""]], [["“A likely thing, too!”", "\"그것참 그럴듯한 소리네요!\""], ["replied the strong woman.", "억센 여자가 대꾸했다."], ["“If it was ever intended that I should go across salt water, do you suppose Providence would have cast my lot in an island?”", "\"내가 짠 바닷물을 건너도록 점지되었다면, 신께서 내 운명을 섬에 던져놓으셨을 거라고 생각해요?\""]], [["This being another question hard to answer, Mr. Jarvis Lorry withdrew to consider it.", "이 또한 답하기 어려운 질문이었으므로, 자비스 로리 씨는 그것을 곰곰이 생각해보려 물러났다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. The Wine-shop", "# CHAPTER V. The Wine-shop"]], [["A large cask of wine had been dropped and broken, in the street.", "포도주가 가득 담긴 커다란 통 하나가 거리에 떨어져 깨졌다."], ["The accident had happened in getting it out of a cart; the cask had tumbled out with a run, the hoops had burst, and it lay on the stones just outside the door of the wine-shop, shattered like a walnut-shell.", "그 사고는 통을 수레에서 내리던 중에 일어났다. 통은 굴러떨어지면서 테가 터졌고, 술집 문 바로 앞 돌바닥에 호두 껍데기처럼 산산이 부서진 채 나뒹굴었다."]], [["All the people within reach had suspended their business, or their idleness, to run to the spot and drink the wine.", "가까이에 있던 사람들은 모두 하던 일이나 빈둥거림을 멈추고 그 자리로 달려와 포도주를 마셨다."], ["The rough, irregular stones of the street, pointing every way, and designed, one might have thought, expressly to lame all living creatures that approached them, had dammed it into little pools; these were surrounded, each by its own jostling group or crowd, according to its size.", "사방으로 뾰족하게 솟아, 가까이 다가오는 살아 있는 것이라면 무엇이든 절름발이로 만들려고 일부러 깔아놓은 것이 아닌가 싶은 거칠고 들쭉날쭉한 거리의 돌들이 포도주를 가두어 작은 웅덩이들을 만들었다. 그 웅덩이마다 크기에 따라 저마다 떼밀고 부대끼는 무리나 군중이 둘러쌌다."], ["Some men kneeled down, made scoops of their two hands joined, and sipped, or tried to help women, who bent over their shoulders, to sip, before the wine had all run out between their fingers.", "어떤 남자들은 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 모아 바가지를 만들어 홀짝였고, 또는 자기 어깨 너머로 몸을 숙인 여자들이 포도주가 손가락 사이로 다 흘러내리기 전에 홀짝이도록 도우려 했다."], ["Others, men and women, dipped in the puddles with little mugs of mutilated earthenware, or even with handkerchiefs from women’s heads, which were squeezed dry into infants’ mouths; others made small mud-embankments, to stem the wine as it ran; others, directed by lookers-on up at high windows, darted here and there, to cut off little streams of wine that started away in new directions; others devoted themselves to the sodden and lee-dyed pieces of the cask, licking, and even champing the moister wine-rotted fragments with eager relish.", "다른 사람들은, 남자든 여자든, 이가 빠진 작은 질그릇 잔으로, 또는 여자들의 머리에서 벗긴 손수건으로 웅덩이를 적셔, 그것을 짜내 갓난아기의 입에 넣어주었다. 또 어떤 사람들은 흘러내리는 포도주를 막으려고 작은 진흙 둑을 쌓았고, 또 어떤 사람들은 높은 창문에서 내려다보는 구경꾼들의 지시를 받아 새로운 방향으로 흘러가기 시작한 가느다란 포도주 줄기를 끊어내려고 이리저리 내달렸다."], ["There was no drainage to carry off the wine, and not only did it all get taken up, but so much mud got taken up along with it, that there might have been a scavenger in the street, if anybody acquainted with it could have believed in such a miraculous presence.", "또 어떤 사람들은 흠뻑 젖어 찌꺼기로 물든 통 조각에 매달려, 더 축축하게 포도주에 절은 부스러기들을 핥고 심지어 게걸스러운 입맛으로 우적우적 씹어댔다. 포도주를 흘려보낼 배수로가 없었으니, 포도주가 한 방울 남김없이 거두어졌을 뿐 아니라 진흙까지도 잔뜩 거두어져서, 거리에는 청소부가 다녀간 듯했다 — 그런 기적 같은 존재를 믿을 만한 사람이 그곳에 있었다면 말이다."]], [["A shrill sound of laughter and of amused voices--voices of men, women, and children--resounded in the street while this wine game lasted.", "이 포도주 놀음이 이어지는 동안, 날카로운 웃음소리와 흥겨워하는 목소리들 — 남자들과 여자들과 아이들의 목소리 — 이 거리에 울려 퍼졌다."], ["There was little roughness in the sport, and much playfulness.", "그 놀이에는 거친 구석이 별로 없었고 장난기가 가득했다."], ["There was a special companionship in it, an observable inclination on the part of every one to join some other one, which led, especially among the luckier or lighter-hearted, to frolicsome embraces, drinking of healths, shaking of hands, and even joining of hands and dancing, a dozen together.", "거기에는 어떤 특별한 동료애가 있었으니, 저마다 다른 누군가와 어울리려는 눈에 띄는 성향이 있어서, 특히 운이 좋거나 마음이 가벼운 이들 사이에서는 들뜬 포옹과 건배, 악수, 심지어 손을 맞잡고 열댓 명이 한데 어울려 춤추는 데까지 이어졌다. 포도주가 다 없어지고, 그것이 가장 흥건했던 자리들이 손가락으로 긁혀 석쇠 무늬가 되자, 이 떠들썩한 광경은 시작될 때처럼 갑작스레 멎었다."], ["When the wine was gone, and the places where it had been most abundant were raked into a gridiron-pattern by fingers, these demonstrations ceased, as suddenly as they had broken out.", "톱질하던 장작에 톱을 박아둔 채로 떠났던 남자는 다시 톱질을 시작했다. 자신의 굶주려 곱은 손가락과 발가락의, 또는 제 아이의 손발가락의 아픔을 누그러뜨리려고 뜨거운 잿가루가 담긴 작은 단지를 문간에 놓아두었던 여자는 그리로 돌아갔다."], ["The man who had left his saw sticking in the firewood he was cutting, set it in motion again; the women who had left on a door-step the little pot of hot ashes, at which she had been trying to soften the pain in her own starved fingers and toes, or in those of her child, returned to it; men with bare arms, matted locks, and cadaverous faces, who had emerged into the winter light from cellars, moved away, to descend again; and a gloom gathered on the scene that appeared more natural to it than sunshine.", "맨팔에 엉킨 머리, 시체 같은 얼굴을 한 채 지하실에서 겨울 햇빛 속으로 나왔던 남자들은 다시 내려가려고 자리를 떴다. 그리고 햇빛보다도 그곳에 더 어울려 보이는 음울함이 그 광경 위로 내려앉았다."]], [["The wine was red wine, and had stained the ground of the narrow street in the suburb of Saint Antoine, in Paris, where it was spilled.", "그 포도주는 붉은 포도주였고, 그것이 쏟아진 빠리의 쌩땅뚜안 변두리, 그 좁은 거리의 바닥을 붉게 물들였다."], ["It had stained many hands, too, and many faces, and many naked feet, and many wooden shoes.", "그것은 또 많은 손을, 많은 얼굴을, 많은 맨발을, 많은 나막신을 물들였다."], ["The hands of the man who sawed the wood, left red marks on the billets; and the forehead of the woman who nursed her baby, was stained with the stain of the old rag she wound about her head again.", "나무를 톱질하던 남자의 손은 장작에 붉은 자국을 남겼고, 아기에게 젖을 물리던 여자의 이마는 다시 머리에 둘러맨 낡은 헝겊의 얼룩으로 물들었다."], ["Those who had been greedy with the staves of the cask, had acquired a tigerish smear about the mouth; and one tall joker so besmirched, his head more out of a long squalid bag of a nightcap than in it, scrawled upon a wall with his finger dipped in muddy wine-lees--BLOOD.", "통의 널빤지를 게걸스레 핥던 자들은 입가에 호랑이 같은 자국이 묻었고, 그렇게 얼룩진 키 큰 익살꾼 하나는, 길고 더러운 자루 같은 잠자리 모자 속보다 바깥으로 머리를 더 내민 채, 진흙 섞인 포도주 찌꺼기에 손가락을 적셔 벽에다 갈겨썼다 — 피."]], [["The time was to come, when that wine too would be spilled on the street-stones, and when the stain of it would be red upon many there.", "그 포도주마저도 거리의 돌바닥에 쏟아지고, 그 얼룩이 그곳 많은 이들 위에 붉게 물들 때가 오게 되어 있었다."]], [["And now that the cloud settled on Saint Antoine, which a momentary gleam had driven from his sacred countenance, the darkness of it was heavy--cold, dirt, sickness, ignorance, and want, were the lords in waiting on the saintly presence--nobles of great power all of them; but, most especially the last.", "그리고 이제, 한순간의 빛줄기가 쌩땅뚜안의 거룩한 얼굴에서 몰아냈던 구름이 다시 그 위에 내려앉으니, 그 어둠은 무거웠다 — 추위와 더러움과 병과 무지와 궁핍이 그 성스러운 존재를 시중드는 영주들이었다 — 모두가 막강한 권세를 가진 귀족들이었으나, 그중에서도 특히 마지막 것이 그러했다."], ["Samples of a people that had undergone a terrible grinding and regrinding in the mill, and certainly not in the fabulous mill which ground old people young, shivered at every corner, passed in and out at every doorway, looked from every window, fluttered in every vestige of a garment that the wind shook.", "방앗간에서 무시무시하게 갈리고 또 갈린 — 그것도 늙은이를 젊게 갈아내는 전설 속의 방앗간에서 갈린 것이 결코 아닌 — 백성의 표본들이 길모퉁이마다 떨고 있었고, 문간마다 들고났으며, 창문마다 내다보았고, 바람에 흔들리는 누더기 옷의 자락마다 펄럭였다. 그들을 갈아내린 그 방앗간은 젊은이를 늙게 갈아내는 방앗간이었다."], ["The mill which had worked them down, was the mill that grinds young people old; the children had ancient faces and grave voices; and upon them, and upon the grown faces, and ploughed into every furrow of age and coming up afresh, was the sigh, Hunger.", "아이들은 늙은 얼굴에 무거운 목소리를 지녔다. 그들 위에, 그리고 어른들의 얼굴 위에, 나이의 주름마다 깊이 패고 또 새로이 솟아나는 것은 한숨, 곧 굶주림이었다."], ["It was prevalent everywhere.", "그것은 어디에나 만연했다. 굶주림은 높다란 집집마다 장대와 빨랫줄에 걸린 비참한 옷가지 속에서 비어져 나왔다."], ["Hunger was pushed out of the tall houses, in the wretched clothing that hung upon poles and lines; Hunger was patched into them with straw and rag and wood and paper; Hunger was repeated in every fragment of the small modicum of firewood that the man sawed off; Hunger stared down from the smokeless chimneys, and started up from the filthy street that had no offal, among its refuse, of anything to eat.", "굶주림은 짚과 헝겊과 나무와 종이로 그 옷에 덧대어졌다. 굶주림은 남자가 톱질해 낸 그 얼마 안 되는 장작 부스러기 한 조각 한 조각마다 되풀이되었다."], ["Hunger was the inscription on the baker’s shelves, written in every small loaf of his scanty stock of bad bread; at the sausage-shop, in every dead-dog preparation that was offered for sale.", "굶주림은 연기 없는 굴뚝에서 내려다보았고, 쓰레기 더미 속에 먹을 만한 찌꺼기 하나 없는 더러운 거리에서 솟구쳐 올랐다. 굶주림은 빵집 선반의 표어였으니, 변변찮은 재고로 쌓인 질 나쁜 빵의 작은 덩어리 하나하나에 쓰여 있었고, 소시지 가게에서는 죽은 개로 만든 듯한 모든 상품마다 적혀 있었다."], ["Hunger rattled its dry bones among the roasting chestnuts in the turned cylinder; Hunger was shred into atomics in every farthing porringer of husky chips of potato, fried with some reluctant drops of oil.", "굶주림은 돌아가는 원통 속 군밤들 사이에서 마른 뼈를 달그락거렸다. 굶주림은 마지못해 떨어뜨린 기름 몇 방울에 튀긴 푸석한 감자 조각이 담긴 한 푼짜리 죽 그릇마다 잘게 부스러져 있었다."]], [["Its abiding place was in all things fitted to it.", "그것이 깃든 곳은 모두 그것에 어울리는 것들 천지였다."], ["A narrow winding street, full of offence and stench, with other narrow winding streets diverging, all peopled by rags and nightcaps, and all smelling of rags and nightcaps, and all visible things with a brooding look upon them that looked ill.", "좁고 구불구불한 거리 하나가 더러움과 악취로 가득했고, 거기서 또 다른 좁고 구불구불한 거리들이 갈라져 나갔으니, 온통 누더기와 잠자리 모자를 쓴 사람들로 들어찼고, 온통 누더기와 잠자리 모자 냄새가 났으며, 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 병들어 보이는 음울한 기색을 띠고 있었다."], ["In the hunted air of the people there was yet some wild-beast thought of the possibility of turning at bay.", "사람들의 쫓기는 듯한 기색 속에는 그래도 막다른 곳에서 돌아서서 맞설 수도 있으리라는 어떤 야수 같은 생각이 남아 있었다."], ["Depressed and slinking though they were, eyes of fire were not wanting among them; nor compressed lips, white with what they suppressed; nor foreheads knitted into the likeness of the gallows-rope they mused about enduring, or inflicting.", "풀이 죽고 슬금슬금 피해 다니기는 했으나, 그들 가운데 불타는 눈빛이 없지 않았고, 억누른 것 때문에 새하얗게 질려 꼭 다문 입술도 없지 않았으며, 견뎌내거나 휘두를까 곰곰이 생각하는 교수대 밧줄을 닮은 모양으로 찌푸린 이마도 없지 않았다."], ["The trade signs (and they were almost as many as the shops) were, all, grim illustrations of Want.", "가게 간판들은 (가게 수만큼이나 많았는데) 하나같이 궁핍을 음산하게 그려 보였다."], ["The butcher and the porkman painted up, only the leanest scrags of meat; the baker, the coarsest of meagre loaves.", "정육점과 돼지고기 가게는 가장 빈약한 고기 부스러기만을 그려놓았고, 빵집은 가장 거칠고 메마른 빵 덩어리를 그려놓았다."], ["The people rudely pictured as drinking in the wine-shops, croaked over their scanty measures of thin wine and beer, and were gloweringly confidential together.", "술집에서 포도주를 마시는 모습으로 투박하게 그려진 사람들은 멀건 포도주와 맥주를 변변찮은 양으로 들이켜며 쉰 목소리로 투덜대고, 서로 노려보듯 은밀히 속닥거렸다."], ["Nothing was represented in a flourishing condition, save tools and weapons; but, the cutler’s knives and axes were sharp and bright, the smith’s hammers were heavy, and the gunmaker’s stock was murderous.", "연장과 무기 말고는 그 무엇도 번창하는 모습으로 그려진 것이 없었으나, 칼장수의 칼과 도끼는 날카롭고 번뜩였고, 대장장이의 망치는 묵직했으며, 총포상의 재고는 살벌했다."], ["The crippling stones of the pavement, with their many little reservoirs of mud and water, had no footways, but broke off abruptly at the doors.", "진흙과 물이 고인 작은 웅덩이가 무수히 패인, 사람을 절름발이로 만드는 포장도로의 돌들에는 인도가 따로 없이 문간에서 갑작스레 끝나버렸다."], ["The kennel, to make amends, ran down the middle of the street--when it ran at all: which was only after heavy rains, and then it ran, by many eccentric fits, into the houses.", "그 대신, 도랑이 거리 한복판으로 흘러내렸으니 — 하기야 흐를 때만 그랬다. 그것은 폭우가 쏟아진 뒤에야 그랬는데, 그럴 때면 갖가지 변덕스러운 발작을 일으키며 집 안으로 흘러들었다."], ["Across the streets, at wide intervals, one clumsy lamp was slung by a rope and pulley; at night, when the lamplighter had let these down, and lighted, and hoisted them again, a feeble grove of dim wicks swung in a sickly manner overhead, as if they were at sea.", "거리를 가로질러, 넓은 간격을 두고, 투박한 등 하나씩이 밧줄과 도르래에 매달려 있었다. 밤이면 점등부가 이것들을 내려 불을 켜고 다시 끌어올렸는데, 그러면 어슴푸레한 심지들이 이룬 빈약한 숲이 머리 위에서 병든 듯 흔들렸으니, 마치 바다 위에 떠 있는 것 같았다."], ["Indeed they were at sea, and the ship and crew were in peril of tempest.", "사실 그들은 바다 위에 있었고, 배와 선원은 폭풍의 위험에 처해 있었다."]], [["For, the time was to come, when the gaunt scarecrows of that region should have watched the lamplighter, in their idleness and hunger, so long, as to conceive the idea of improving on his method, and hauling up men by those ropes and pulleys, to flare upon the darkness of their condition.", "머지않아, 그 지역의 깡마른 허수아비들이 빈둥거림과 굶주림 속에서 점등부를 하도 오래 지켜본 끝에, 그의 방식을 개량해 그 밧줄과 도르래로 사람들을 끌어올려 자기네 처지의 어둠 위에 매달아 보여줄 생각을 떠올릴 때가 오게 되어 있었다. 그러나 그때는 아직 오지 않았다."], ["But, the time was not come yet; and every wind that blew over France shook the rags of the scarecrows in vain, for the birds, fine of song and feather, took no warning.", "프랑스 위로 불어대는 바람은 죄다 허수아비들의 누더기를 헛되이 흔들 뿐이었으니, 깃털 곱고 노래 좋은 새들은 아무런 경고도 알아채지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["The wine-shop was a corner shop, better than most others in its appearance and degree, and the master of the wine-shop had stood outside it, in a yellow waistcoat and green breeches, looking on at the struggle for the lost wine.", "그 술집은 모퉁이 가게로, 외양이나 격에서 다른 대부분의 가게보다 나았는데, 술집 주인은 노란 조끼에 초록 반바지를 입고 가게 밖에 서서 쏟아진 포도주를 두고 벌어지는 다툼을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["“It’s not my affair,”", "\"내 알 바 아니지.\""], ["said he, with a final shrug of the shoulders.", "그는 마지막으로 어깨를 한번 으쓱하며 말했다."], ["“The people from the market did it.", "\"시장 사람들이 저질러놓은 일이니까."], ["Let them bring another.”", "다른 통을 가져오라고 해.\""]], [["There, his eyes happening to catch the tall joker writing up his joke, he called to him across the way:", "거기서 그의 눈이 우연히 제 익살을 갈겨쓰고 있는 키 큰 익살꾼에게 가닿자, 그는 길 건너로 그를 불렀다."]], [["“Say, then, my Gaspard, what do you do there?”", "\"이봐, 가스빠르, 거기서 뭐 하는 거야?\""]], [["The fellow pointed to his joke with immense significance, as is often the way with his tribe.", "사내는 제 무리가 흔히 그러듯, 엄청나게 의미심장한 몸짓으로 자기 익살을 가리켰다."], ["It missed its mark, and completely failed, as is often the way with his tribe too.", "하지만 그것은 역시 제 무리가 흔히 그러듯, 과녁을 빗나가 완전히 실패하고 말았다."]], [["“What now?", "\"또 뭐야?"], ["Are you a subject for the mad hospital?”", "자네 미친 사람들 병원에나 들어갈 작정인가?\""], ["said the wine-shop keeper, crossing the road, and obliterating the jest with a handful of mud, picked up for the purpose, and smeared over it.", "술집 주인은 길을 건너가, 그러려고 한 줌 집어 든 진흙을 그 익살 위에 발라 문질러 지워버리며 말했다."], ["“Why do you write in the public streets?", "\"왜 공공연한 길거리에 글을 쓰는 거야?"], ["Is there--tell me thou--is there no other place to write such words in?”", "이봐, 말해 보게 — 그런 말을 쓸 다른 데가 없단 말인가?\""]], [["In his expostulation he dropped his cleaner hand (perhaps accidentally, perhaps not) upon the joker’s heart.", "그렇게 따지면서 그는 (어쩌면 우연히, 어쩌면 아니게) 더 깨끗한 손을 익살꾼의 가슴팍에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["The joker rapped it with his own, took a nimble spring upward, and came down in a fantastic dancing attitude, with one of his stained shoes jerked off his foot into his hand, and held out.", "익살꾼은 제 손으로 그 손을 탁 치고는 날렵하게 위로 뛰어올라, 얼룩진 신발 한 짝을 발에서 홱 벗겨 손에 쥐고 내밀며 기묘한 춤추는 자세로 내려섰다."], ["A joker of an extremely, not to say wolfishly practical character, he looked, under those circumstances.", "그런 상황에서 그는, 늑대 같다고까지는 못하더라도 지극히 실리적인 성격의 익살꾼으로 보였다."]], [["“Put it on, put it on,”", "\"신어, 신으라고.\""], ["said the other.", "상대가 말했다."], ["“Call wine, wine; and finish there.”", "\"포도주는 포도주라고 부르고, 거기서 끝내게.\" 그렇게 충고하고 나서 그는 그 익살꾼의 — 그래봤자 변변찮은 — 옷에다 더러워진 손을 닦았다."], ["With that advice, he wiped his soiled hand upon the joker’s dress, such as it was--quite deliberately, as having dirtied the hand on his account; and then recrossed the road and entered the wine-shop.", "그것도 아주 일부러, 익살꾼 때문에 손을 더럽혔다는 듯이 그랬다. 그러고는 다시 길을 건너 술집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["This wine-shop keeper was a bull-necked, martial-looking man of thirty, and he should have been of a hot temperament, for, although it was a bitter day, he wore no coat, but carried one slung over his shoulder.", "이 술집 주인은 황소처럼 목이 굵고 군인 같은 인상의 서른 살 사내였는데, 성미가 뜨거운 사람임에 틀림없었으니, 살을 에는 추운 날인데도 외투를 입지 않고 어깨에 둘러메고만 다녔기 때문이다. 셔츠 소매도 걷어 올려서, 갈색 팔이 팔꿈치까지 드러나 있었다."], ["His shirt-sleeves were rolled up, too, and his brown arms were bare to the elbows.", "머리에도 짧고 곱슬거리는 검은 머리카락 말고는 아무것도 쓰지 않았다."], ["Neither did he wear anything more on his head than his own crisply-curling short dark hair.", "그는 전체적으로 거무스름한 사내로, 눈매가 좋았고 그 눈 사이가 시원스레 넓었다."], ["He was a dark man altogether, with good eyes and a good bold breadth between them.", "대체로 쾌활해 보였으나, 또한 무자비해 보이기도 했다."], ["Good-humoured looking on the whole, but implacable-looking, too; evidently a man of a strong resolution and a set purpose; a man not desirable to be met, rushing down a narrow pass with a gulf on either side, for nothing would turn the man.", "분명 강인한 결단과 굳은 목적을 지닌 사내였다. 양옆이 낭떠러지인 좁은 길목을 내달릴 때 마주치고 싶지 않은 사내였으니, 그 무엇도 그를 돌려세우지 못할 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["Madame Defarge, his wife, sat in the shop behind the counter as he came in.", "그의 아내 마담 드파르주는 그가 들어왔을 때 가게 안 계산대 뒤에 앉아 있었다."], ["Madame Defarge was a stout woman of about his own age, with a watchful eye that seldom seemed to look at anything, a large hand heavily ringed, a steady face, strong features, and great composure of manner.", "마담 드파르주는 남편과 비슷한 나이의 건장한 여자로, 좀처럼 무엇을 바라보는 것 같지 않은 경계하는 눈, 반지를 잔뜩 낀 커다란 손, 흔들림 없는 얼굴, 강한 이목구비, 그리고 더없이 침착한 태도를 지녔다."], ["There was a character about Madame Defarge, from which one might have predicated that she did not often make mistakes against herself in any of the reckonings over which she presided.", "마담 드파르주에게는 어떤 됨됨이가 있어서, 그것을 보면 그녀가 자신이 맡아 처리하는 어떤 셈에서든 좀처럼 제 손해 보는 실수를 저지르지 않으리라고 점칠 수 있었다."], ["Madame Defarge being sensitive to cold, was wrapped in fur, and had a quantity of bright shawl twined about her head, though not to the concealment of her large earrings.", "마담 드파르주는 추위를 잘 타서 모피를 둘렀고, 머리에는 화사한 숄을 잔뜩 둘러 감았으나, 그래도 커다란 귀고리는 가려지지 않았다."], ["Her knitting was before her, but she had laid it down to pick her teeth with a toothpick.", "뜨개질거리가 앞에 놓여 있었지만, 그녀는 그것을 내려놓고 이쑤시개로 이를 쑤시고 있었다."], ["Thus engaged, with her right elbow supported by her left hand, Madame Defarge said nothing when her lord came in, but coughed just one grain of cough.", "그렇게 왼손으로 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 받친 채, 마담 드파르주는 그 주인이 들어와도 아무 말 없이 그저 기침을 딱 한 알갱이 했다."], ["This, in combination with the lifting of her darkly defined eyebrows over her toothpick by the breadth of a line, suggested to her husband that he would do well to look round the shop among the customers, for any new customer who had dropped in while he stepped over the way.", "이것은, 이쑤시개 너머로 짙게 그려진 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 치켜올린 것과 더불어, 남편에게 그가 길 건너로 가 있는 동안 들른 새 손님이 있는지 가게 안의 손님들을 한번 둘러보는 게 좋겠다는 뜻을 일러주었다."]], [["The wine-shop keeper accordingly rolled his eyes about, until they rested upon an elderly gentleman and a young lady, who were seated in a corner.", "이에 술집 주인은 눈동자를 굴려, 마침내 한쪽 구석에 앉아 있는 나이 지긋한 신사와 젊은 아가씨에게 시선을 멈추었다. 다른 손님들도 있었다."], ["Other company were there: two playing cards, two playing dominoes, three standing by the counter lengthening out a short supply of wine.", "둘은 카드놀이를, 둘은 도미노 놀이를 하고 있었고, 셋은 계산대에 기대서서 얼마 안 되는 포도주를 질질 끌며 마시고 있었다."], ["As he passed behind the counter, he took notice that the elderly gentleman said in a look to the young lady, “This is our man.”", "계산대 뒤를 지나가면서 그는, 그 나이 지긋한 신사가 젊은 아가씨에게 눈빛으로 \"이 사람이 우리가 찾던 자요\"라고 말하는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“What the devil do you do in that galley there?”", "\"대체 당신은 저기서 무슨 영문으로 그러고 있는 거지?\""], ["said Monsieur Defarge to himself; “I don’t know you.”", "무슈 드파르주는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"당신을 모르겠는데.\""]], [["But, he feigned not to notice the two strangers, and fell into discourse with the triumvirate of customers who were drinking at the counter.", "그러나 그는 두 낯선 사람을 알아채지 못한 척하며, 계산대에서 마시고 있던 세 손님의 삼두체와 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["“How goes it, Jacques?”", "\"어떻게 돼가나, 자끄?\""], ["said one of these three to Monsieur Defarge.", "그 셋 가운데 하나가 무슈 드파르주에게 물었다."], ["“Is all the spilt wine swallowed?”", "\"쏟아진 포도주는 다 들이켜졌나?\""]], [["“Every drop, Jacques,”", "\"한 방울도 남김없이, 자끄.\""], ["answered Monsieur Defarge.", "무슈 드파르주가 대답했다."]], [["When this interchange of Christian name was effected, Madame Defarge, picking her teeth with her toothpick, coughed another grain of cough, and raised her eyebrows by the breadth of another line.", "이렇게 세례명을 주고받고 나자, 마담 드파르주는 이쑤시개로 이를 쑤시면서 또 기침을 한 알갱이 하고, 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 더 치켜올렸다."]], [["“It is not often,” said the second of the three, addressing Monsieur Defarge, “that many of these miserable beasts know the taste of wine, or of anything but black bread and death.", "\"이런 가련한 짐승들이 포도주 맛을, 아니 검은 빵과 죽음 말고 다른 어떤 것의 맛을 알게 되는 일은 흔치 않지.\""], ["Is it not so, Jacques?”", "셋 가운데 둘째가 무슈 드파르주에게 말했다. \"안 그런가, 자끄?\""]], [["“It is so, Jacques,”", "\"그렇지, 자끄.\""], ["Monsieur Defarge returned.", "무슈 드파르주가 대꾸했다."]], [["At this second interchange of the Christian name, Madame Defarge, still using her toothpick with profound composure, coughed another grain of cough, and raised her eyebrows by the breadth of another line.", "이렇게 두 번째로 세례명을 주고받자, 마담 드파르주는 여전히 더없이 침착하게 이쑤시개를 놀리면서 또 기침을 한 알갱이 하고, 눈썹을 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼 더 치켜올렸다."]], [["The last of the three now said his say, as he put down his empty drinking vessel and smacked his lips.", "이제 셋 가운데 마지막 사람이 빈 술잔을 내려놓고 입맛을 다시며 제 할 말을 했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["So much the worse!", "그러니 더욱 딱하지!"], ["A bitter taste it is that such poor cattle always have in their mouths, and hard lives they live, Jacques.", "그런 불쌍한 가축들 입에는 늘 쓴맛이 감돌고, 사는 것도 고달프지, 자끄."], ["Am I right, Jacques?”", "내 말이 맞지, 자끄?\""]], [["“You are right, Jacques,”", "\"자네 말이 맞네, 자끄.\""], ["was the response of Monsieur Defarge.", "무슈 드파르주가 응답했다."]], [["This third interchange of the Christian name was completed at the moment when Madame Defarge put her toothpick by, kept her eyebrows up, and slightly rustled in her seat.", "이렇게 세 번째로 세례명을 주고받는 일이 끝나는 바로 그 순간, 마담 드파르주는 이쑤시개를 내려놓고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채로 자리에서 살짝 부스럭거렸다."]], [["“Hold then!", "\"가만!"], ["True!”", "옳지!\""], ["muttered her husband.", "그녀의 남편이 중얼거렸다."], ["“Gentlemen--my wife!”", "\"여러분 — 내 아내요!\""]], [["The three customers pulled off their hats to Madame Defarge, with three flourishes.", "세 손님은 세 번 멋을 부려 모자를 벗어 마담 드파르주에게 인사했다."], ["She acknowledged their homage by bending her head, and giving them a quick look.", "그녀는 고개를 숙이고 그들에게 재빠른 눈길을 한번 던지며 그 경의를 받아들였다."], ["Then she glanced in a casual manner round the wine-shop, took up her knitting with great apparent calmness and repose of spirit, and became absorbed in it.", "그러고는 무심한 듯 술집 안을 한번 둘러보고, 겉보기로 더없이 차분하고 평온한 마음으로 뜨개질거리를 집어 들어 거기에 몰두했다."]], [["“Gentlemen,”", "\"여러분,\""], ["said her husband, who had kept his bright eye observantly upon her, “good day.", "그동안 빛나는 눈으로 그녀를 유심히 지켜보고 있던 남편이 말했다."], ["The chamber, furnished bachelor-fashion, that you wished to see, and were inquiring for when I stepped out, is on the fifth floor.", "\"안녕히 가시오. 여러분이 보고 싶어하던, 내가 밖에 나가 있을 때 물어보던 그 독신자용으로 꾸민 방은 오 층에 있소."], ["The doorway of the staircase gives on the little courtyard close to the left here,”", "계단 입구는 여기 바로 왼쪽,\" 그는 손으로 가리켰다."], ["pointing with his hand, “near to the window of my establishment.", "\"내 가게 창문 근처의 작은 안뜰로 통하오."], ["But, now that I remember, one of you has already been there, and can show the way.", "한데 가만 보니, 그중 한 분은 이미 거기 가본 적이 있어 길을 안내할 수 있겠군."], ["Gentlemen, adieu!”", "여러분, 안녕히!\""]], [["They paid for their wine, and left the place.", "그들은 포도주값을 치르고 그곳을 떠났다."], ["The eyes of Monsieur Defarge were studying his wife at her knitting when the elderly gentleman advanced from his corner, and begged the favour of a word.", "무슈 드파르주의 눈이 뜨개질하는 아내를 살피고 있을 때, 나이 지긋한 신사가 구석에서 다가와 한마디 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 청했다."]], [["“Willingly, sir,”", "\"기꺼이 그러지요, 선생.\""], ["said Monsieur Defarge, and quietly stepped with him to the door.", "무슈 드파르주가 말하고는, 그와 함께 조용히 문가로 걸어갔다."]], [["Their conference was very short, but very decided.", "그들의 의논은 아주 짧았으나 아주 단호했다."], ["Almost at the first word, Monsieur Defarge started and became deeply attentive.", "거의 첫마디에 무슈 드파르주는 흠칫 놀라더니 깊이 귀를 기울였다."], ["It had not lasted a minute, when he nodded and went out.", "그것은 일 분도 채 이어지지 않았는데, 그는 고개를 끄덕이고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The gentleman then beckoned to the young lady, and they, too, went out.", "그러자 그 신사가 젊은 아가씨에게 손짓했고, 그들도 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Madame Defarge knitted with nimble fingers and steady eyebrows, and saw nothing.", "마담 드파르주는 날렵한 손놀림과 흔들림 없는 눈썹으로 뜨개질을 했고, 아무것도 보지 못했다."]], [["Mr. Jarvis Lorry and Miss Manette, emerging from the wine-shop thus, joined Monsieur Defarge in the doorway to which he had directed his own company just before.", "이렇게 술집에서 나온 자비스 로리 씨와 마네뜨 양은, 무슈 드파르주가 바로 조금 전에 자기 일행을 안내했던 그 문간에서 그와 합류했다."], ["It opened from a stinking little black courtyard, and was the general public entrance to a great pile of houses, inhabited by a great number of people.", "그 문은 악취 풍기는 작고 컴컴한 안뜰로 열려 있었고, 수많은 사람들이 사는 거대한 집 더미로 들어가는 공동 출입구였다."], ["In the gloomy tile-paved entry to the gloomy tile-paved staircase, Monsieur Defarge bent down on one knee to the child of his old master, and put her hand to his lips.", "음울하게 타일을 깐 입구에서 음울하게 타일을 깐 계단으로 이어지는 곳에서, 무슈 드파르주는 옛 주인의 딸 앞에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["It was a gentle action, but not at all gently done; a very remarkable transformation had come over him in a few seconds.", "그것은 정중한 행동이었으나, 결코 정중하게 행해지지는 않았다. 몇 초 사이에 그에게 아주 놀라운 변화가 일어났다."], ["He had no good-humour in his face, nor any openness of aspect left, but had become a secret, angry, dangerous man.", "그의 얼굴에는 쾌활함도, 솔직함도 더는 남아 있지 않았고, 그는 어느새 비밀스럽고 성나 있으며 위험한 사내가 되어 있었다."]], [["“It is very high; it is a little difficult.", "\"매우 높습니다. 좀 힘들지요."], ["Better to begin slowly.”", "천천히 시작하는 게 좋습니다.\""], ["Thus, Monsieur Defarge, in a stern voice, to Mr. Lorry, as they began ascending the stairs.", "계단을 오르기 시작하면서 무슈 드파르주가 로리 씨에게 엄한 목소리로 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Is he alone?”", "\"그가 혼자 있소?\""], ["the latter whispered.", "로리 씨가 속삭였다."]], [["“Alone!", "\"혼자라니!"], ["God help him, who should be with him!”", "신께서 그를 도우시길, 누가 그와 함께 있을 수 있겠소!\""], ["said the other, in the same low voice.", "상대가 똑같이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Is he always alone, then?”", "\"그러면 그는 늘 혼자요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Of his own desire?”", "\"제 뜻으로?\""]], [["“Of his own necessity.", "\"제 처지가 그래서요."], ["As he was, when I first saw him after they found me and demanded to know if I would take him, and, at my peril be discreet--as he was then, so he is now.”", "그들이 나를 찾아내, 내가 그를 맡아 위험을 무릅쓰고 입을 다물 수 있겠느냐고 물은 뒤에 내가 처음 그를 보았을 때 그랬듯이 — 그때 그랬던 그대로, 지금도 그렇소.\""]], [["“He is greatly changed?”", "\"그가 많이 변했소?\""]], [["“Changed!”", "\"변했냐고요!\""]], [["The keeper of the wine-shop stopped to strike the wall with his hand, and mutter a tremendous curse.", "술집 주인은 걸음을 멈추고 손으로 벽을 치며 끔찍한 저주를 중얼거렸다."], ["No direct answer could have been half so forcible. Mr.", "어떤 직접적인 대답도 그 절반만큼도 강렬하지 못했을 것이다."], ["Lorry’s spirits grew heavier and heavier, as he and his two companions ascended higher and higher.", "로리 씨와 두 동행이 점점 더 높이 올라갈수록, 로리 씨의 마음은 점점 더 무거워졌다."]], [["Such a staircase, with its accessories, in the older and more crowded parts of Paris, would be bad enough now; but, at that time, it was vile indeed to unaccustomed and unhardened senses.", "빠리의 더 오래되고 더 붐비는 구역에 있는 그런 계단은, 거기 딸린 것들까지 합쳐, 지금 봐도 충분히 고약할 것이다. 그러나 그 시절에는 익숙지 않고 단련되지 않은 감각에는 정말이지 끔찍했다."], ["Every little habitation within the great foul nest of one high building--that is to say, the room or rooms within every door that opened on the general staircase--left its own heap of refuse on its own landing, besides flinging other refuse from its own windows.", "그 높다란 한 채의 거대한 더러운 둥지 안에 있는 자그마한 거처들 — 다시 말해, 공동 계단으로 통하는 문마다 그 안에 있는 방 하나 또는 방들 — 은 저마다 제 쓰레기 더미를 제 층계참에 쌓아두었고, 그 외에도 다른 쓰레기들을 제 창문 밖으로 내던졌다."], ["The uncontrollable and hopeless mass of decomposition so engendered, would have polluted the air, even if poverty and deprivation had not loaded it with their intangible impurities; the two bad sources combined made it almost insupportable.", "그렇게 생겨난, 걷잡을 수 없고 어찌해볼 도리 없는 부패 덩어리는, 설령 가난과 궁핍이 거기에 그 형체 없는 더러움을 보태지 않았더라도 공기를 오염시켰을 것이다. 이 두 가지 나쁜 원천이 합쳐지니, 거의 견딜 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["Through such an atmosphere, by a steep dark shaft of dirt and poison, the way lay.", "그런 공기를 뚫고, 더러움과 독기로 이루어진 가파르고 어두운 수직 통로를 따라 길이 나 있었다."], ["Yielding to his own disturbance of mind, and to his young companion’s agitation, which became greater every instant, Mr. Jarvis Lorry twice stopped to rest.", "자기 마음의 동요에, 그리고 시시각각 더 커지는 젊은 동행의 격동에 못 이겨, 자비스 로리 씨는 두 번이나 멈춰 쉬었다."], ["Each of these stoppages was made at a doleful grating, by which any languishing good airs that were left uncorrupted, seemed to escape, and all spoilt and sickly vapours seemed to crawl in.", "이렇게 멈춘 곳은 매번 음울한 쇠창살 앞이었으니, 거기서 아직 썩지 않고 남아 있던 그나마 좋은 공기는 죄다 빠져나가는 듯했고, 온갖 상하고 병든 독기는 죄다 기어드는 듯했다."], ["Through the rusted bars, tastes, rather than glimpses, were caught of the jumbled neighbourhood; and nothing within range, nearer or lower than the summits of the two great towers of Notre-Dame, had any promise on it of healthy life or wholesome aspirations.", "녹슨 창살 너머로, 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉킨 이웃 동네가 눈에 들어온다기보다 차라리 맛으로 느껴졌다. 노트르담의 두 거대한 탑 꼭대기보다 가깝거나 낮은 곳에는, 시야가 닿는 범위 안에 건강한 삶이나 건전한 소망을 약속하는 것이라고는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["At last, the top of the staircase was gained, and they stopped for the third time.", "마침내 계단 꼭대기에 다다라, 그들은 세 번째로 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["There was yet an upper staircase, of a steeper inclination and of contracted dimensions, to be ascended, before the garret story was reached.", "다락방 층에 이르기까지는 아직 더 가파르고 폭이 좁은 위층 계단을 하나 더 올라가야 했다."], ["The keeper of the wine-shop, always going a little in advance, and always going on the side which Mr. Lorry took, as though he dreaded to be asked any question by the young lady, turned himself about here, and, carefully feeling in the pockets of the coat he carried over his shoulder, took out a key.", "늘 조금 앞서 가고, 늘 로리 씨가 선 쪽으로 가던 — 마치 젊은 아가씨에게서 무슨 질문이라도 받을까 두려워하는 듯이 — 술집 주인은, 여기서 몸을 돌려 어깨에 걸친 외투 주머니를 조심스레 더듬어 열쇠 하나를 꺼냈다."]], [["“The door is locked then, my friend?”", "\"그러면 문이 잠겨 있는 거요, 친구?\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, surprised.", "로리 씨가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“Ay. Yes,”", "\"그렇소."], ["was the grim reply of Monsieur Defarge.", "그렇소.\" 무슈 드파르주가 음울하게 대답했다."]], [["“You think it necessary to keep the unfortunate gentleman so retired?”", "\"그 불행한 신사를 그토록 숨겨둘 필요가 있다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["“I think it necessary to turn the key.”", "\"열쇠를 돌릴 필요가 있다고 생각하오.\""], ["Monsieur Defarge whispered it closer in his ear, and frowned heavily.", "무슈 드파르주는 그의 귀에 더 바싹 대고 속삭이며 심하게 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Why!", "\"왜냐고!"], ["Because he has lived so long, locked up, that he would be frightened--rave--tear himself to pieces--die--come to I know not what harm--if his door was left open.”", "그가 너무 오래 갇혀 지낸 탓에, 문을 열어두면 겁에 질려 — 미쳐 날뛰고 — 제 몸을 갈가리 찢고 — 죽고 — 무슨 해를 입을지 알 수 없기 때문이오.\""]], [["“Is it possible!”", "\"그럴 수가!\""], ["exclaimed Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Is it possible!”", "\"그럴 수가!\""], ["repeated Defarge, bitterly.", "드파르주가 씁쓸하게 되뇌었다."], ["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["And a beautiful world we live in, when it is possible, and when many other such things are possible, and not only possible, but done--done, see you!--under that sky there, every day.", "그런 일이 정말로 가능하고, 그런 다른 일들도 숱하게 가능하며, 가능할 뿐 아니라 실제로 행해지는 — 행해지는, 알겠소! — 저 하늘 아래서 날마다 행해지는, 그런 아름다운 세상에 우리가 살고 있는 거요."], ["Long live the Devil.", "악마 만세."], ["Let us go on.”", "그만 올라갑시다.\""]], [["This dialogue had been held in so very low a whisper, that not a word of it had reached the young lady’s ears.", "이 대화는 너무도 나직한 속삭임으로 오갔기에, 그 가운데 단 한마디도 젊은 아가씨의 귀에 닿지 않았다."], ["But, by this time she trembled under such strong emotion, and her face expressed such deep anxiety, and, above all, such dread and terror, that Mr. Lorry felt it incumbent on him to speak a word or two of reassurance.", "그러나 이때쯤 그녀는 너무도 격한 감정에 떨고 있었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 너무도 깊은 불안을, 무엇보다 너무도 큰 두려움과 공포를 드러내고 있어서, 로리 씨는 안심시키는 말을 한두 마디 건네야겠다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Courage, dear miss!", "\"용기를 내요, 아가씨!"], ["Courage!", "용기를!"], ["Business!", "일이오!"], ["The worst will be over in a moment; it is but passing the room-door, and the worst is over.", "최악은 곧 지나갈 거요. 그저 저 방문을 지나기만 하면 최악은 끝나오."], ["Then, all the good you bring to him, all the relief, all the happiness you bring to him, begin.", "그러면 당신이 그에게 가져다줄 온갖 좋은 것이, 온갖 위안이, 온갖 행복이 비로소 시작되는 거요."], ["Let our good friend here, assist you on that side.", "여기 우리의 좋은 친구가 그쪽에서 당신을 거들게 합시다."], ["That’s well, friend Defarge.", "됐소, 드파르주."], ["Come, now.", "자, 이제."], ["Business, business!”", "일이오, 일!\""]], [["They went up slowly and softly.", "그들은 천천히, 조용히 올라갔다."], ["The staircase was short, and they were soon at the top.", "계단은 짧았고, 그들은 곧 꼭대기에 이르렀다."], ["There, as it had an abrupt turn in it, they came all at once in sight of three men, whose heads were bent down close together at the side of a door, and who were intently looking into the room to which the door belonged, through some chinks or holes in the wall.", "거기서 계단이 급하게 꺾여 있던 탓에, 그들은 갑자기 세 남자를 보게 되었으니, 그자들은 어느 문 옆에 머리를 바싹 맞대고 숙인 채, 벽의 어떤 틈이나 구멍을 통해 그 문이 달린 방 안을 골똘히 들여다보고 있었다."], ["On hearing footsteps close at hand, these three turned, and rose, and showed themselves to be the three of one name who had been drinking in the wine-shop.", "바로 가까이서 들리는 발소리에 이 셋은 돌아서서 몸을 일으켰고, 술집에서 술을 마시던 한 이름의 세 사람임이 드러났다."]], [["“I forgot them in the surprise of your visit,”", "\"당신들이 찾아온 게 너무 놀라워 이 사람들을 깜빡했군요.\""], ["explained Monsieur Defarge.", "무슈 드파르주가 설명했다."], ["“Leave us, good boys; we have business here.”", "\"물러가게, 착한 친구들. 여기 볼일이 있으니.\""]], [["The three glided by, and went silently down.", "세 사람은 스쳐 지나가 소리 없이 내려갔다."]], [["There appearing to be no other door on that floor, and the keeper of the wine-shop going straight to this one when they were left alone, Mr. Lorry asked him in a whisper, with a little anger:", "그 층에는 다른 문이 없는 듯했고, 그들끼리만 남게 되자 술집 주인이 곧장 이 문으로 향하니, 로리 씨는 약간 화가 나서 그에게 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["“Do you make a show of Monsieur Manette?”", "\"무슈 마네뜨를 구경거리로 삼는 거요?\""]], [["“I show him, in the way you have seen, to a chosen few.”", "\"방금 보신 그 방식으로, 가려 뽑은 몇 사람에게만 보여주는 거요.\""]], [["“Is that well?”", "\"그게 잘하는 일이오?\""]], [["“I think it is well.”", "\"내 생각엔 잘하는 일이오.\""]], [["“Who are the few?", "\"그 몇 사람이란 누구요?"], ["How do you choose them?”", "어떻게 가려 뽑소?\""]], [["“I choose them as real men, of my name--Jacques is my name--to whom the sight is likely to do good.", "\"나와 같은 이름을 지닌 — 자끄가 내 이름이오 — 진짜 사내들로, 이 광경이 도움이 될 만한 자들을 뽑소. 됐소."], ["Enough; you are English; that is another thing.", "당신은 영국 사람이니, 그건 또 다른 문제요."], ["Stay there, if you please, a little moment.”", "부디 거기서 잠깐만 계시오.\""]], [["With an admonitory gesture to keep them back, he stooped, and looked in through the crevice in the wall.", "그들을 물러서 있게 하는 주의의 몸짓을 하고서, 그는 몸을 숙여 벽 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다."], ["Soon raising his head again, he struck twice or thrice upon the door--evidently with no other object than to make a noise there.", "이내 다시 머리를 들고는, 문을 두세 번 두드렸다 — 분명 거기서 소리를 내려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었다."], ["With the same intention, he drew the key across it, three or four times, before he put it clumsily into the lock, and turned it as heavily as he could.", "같은 의도로, 그는 열쇠를 문에 서너 번 긁어댄 다음에야 그것을 자물쇠에 어설프게 꽂아 넣고, 있는 힘껏 묵직하게 돌렸다."]], [["The door slowly opened inward under his hand, and he looked into the room and said something.", "문이 그의 손길에 따라 천천히 안쪽으로 열렸고, 그는 방 안을 들여다보며 뭐라고 말했다."], ["A faint voice answered something.", "희미한 목소리가 뭐라고 대답했다."], ["Little more than a single syllable could have been spoken on either side.", "양쪽 다 한 음절 남짓밖에 말하지 않았을 것이다."]], [["He looked back over his shoulder, and beckoned them to enter.", "그는 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 그들에게 들어오라고 손짓했다."], ["Mr. Lorry got his arm securely round the daughter’s waist, and held her; for he felt that she was sinking.", "로리 씨는 딸의 허리를 팔로 단단히 감싸 그녀를 부축했으니, 그녀가 주저앉으려 하는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["“A-a-a-business, business!”", "\"이-이-일이오, 일!\""], ["he urged, with a moisture that was not of business shining on his cheek.", "그는 일과는 무관한 물기를 뺨에 번뜩이며 재촉했다."], ["“Come in, come in!”", "\"들어가요, 들어가!\""]], [["“I am afraid of it,”", "\"무서워요.\""], ["she answered, shuddering.", "그녀가 몸을 떨며 대답했다."]], [["“Of it?", "\"그게?"], ["What?”", "무엇이?\""]], [["“I mean of him.", "\"제 말은 그분이요."], ["Of my father.”", "제 아버지가요.\""]], [["Rendered in a manner desperate, by her state and by the beckoning of their conductor, he drew over his neck the arm that shook upon his shoulder, lifted her a little, and hurried her into the room.", "그녀의 상태에, 그리고 안내자의 손짓에 어쩐지 다급해진 그는, 자기 어깨 위에서 떨리던 그녀의 팔을 자기 목에 두르고, 그녀를 살짝 들어 올려 서둘러 방 안으로 데리고 들어갔다."], ["He sat her down just within the door, and held her, clinging to him.", "그는 문 바로 안쪽에 그녀를 앉히고, 자기에게 매달리는 그녀를 붙들었다."]], [["Defarge drew out the key, closed the door, locked it on the inside, took out the key again, and held it in his hand.", "드파르주는 열쇠를 빼내 문을 닫고, 안쪽에서 잠근 다음, 다시 열쇠를 빼서 손에 쥐었다."], ["All this he did, methodically, and with as loud and harsh an accompaniment of noise as he could make.", "이 모든 것을 그는 차근차근, 그리고 낼 수 있는 한 크고 거친 소리를 내며 해냈다."], ["Finally, he walked across the room with a measured tread to where the window was.", "마침내 그는 절도 있는 걸음걸이로 방을 가로질러 창문이 있는 데로 갔다."], ["He stopped there, and faced round.", "그는 거기서 멈춰 서서 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["The garret, built to be a depository for firewood and the like, was dim and dark: for, the window of dormer shape, was in truth a door in the roof, with a little crane over it for the hoisting up of stores from the street: unglazed, and closing up the middle in two pieces, like any other door of French construction.", "장작 따위를 쟁여두는 곳으로 지어진 그 다락방은 어둑하고 캄캄했다. 지붕창 모양의 그 창문은 사실 지붕에 난 문으로, 그 위에는 거리에서 물건을 끌어올리는 작은 기중기가 달려 있었고, 유리가 없이, 다른 프랑스식 문이 다 그러하듯 가운데가 두 짝으로 닫히게 되어 있었다."], ["To exclude the cold, one half of this door was fast closed, and the other was opened but a very little way.", "추위를 막으려고 이 문의 한쪽은 단단히 닫혀 있었고, 다른 한쪽은 아주 조금만 열려 있었다."], ["Such a scanty portion of light was admitted through these means, that it was difficult, on first coming in, to see anything; and long habit alone could have slowly formed in any one, the ability to do any work requiring nicety in such obscurity.", "이런 식으로 들어오는 빛이 워낙 인색해서, 처음 들어와서는 무엇 하나 보기가 어려웠고, 오랜 습관만이 누군가에게 그런 어둠 속에서 정교함을 요하는 일을 해낼 능력을 더디게나마 길러줄 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Yet, work of that kind was being done in the garret; for, with his back towards the door, and his face towards the window where the keeper of the wine-shop stood looking at him, a white-haired man sat on a low bench, stooping forward and very busy, making shoes.", "그러나 그런 종류의 일이 그 다락방에서 행해지고 있었다. 문을 등지고, 술집 주인이 서서 자신을 바라보고 있는 창문 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌린 채, 백발의 한 남자가 낮은 의자에 앉아, 앞으로 몸을 숙이고 아주 분주하게, 신발을 만들고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. The Shoemaker", "# CHAPTER VI. The Shoemaker"]], [["“Good day!”", "“안녕하시오!”"], ["said Monsieur Defarge, looking down at the white head that bent low over the shoemaking.", "드파르주 씨가 구두 짓는 일에 깊이 수그러진 흰 머리를 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["It was raised for a moment, and a very faint voice responded to the salutation, as if it were at a distance:", "머리가 잠시 들렸고, 마치 멀리서 들려오는 듯 아주 희미한 목소리가 그 인사에 응답했다."]], [["“Good day!”", "“안녕하시오!”"]], [["“You are still hard at work, I see?”", "“아직도 일에 열중이시군요?”"]], [["After a long silence, the head was lifted for another moment, and the voice replied, “Yes--I am working.”", "긴 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 머리가 또 한번 잠시 들렸고, 목소리가 대답했다. “그렇소ㅡ나는 일하고 있소.”"], ["This time, a pair of haggard eyes had looked at the questioner, before the face had dropped again.", "이번에는 한 쌍의 초췌한 눈이 묻는 이를 바라본 다음에야 얼굴이 다시 떨구어졌다."]], [["The faintness of the voice was pitiable and dreadful.", "그 목소리의 희미함은 가련하고도 두려운 것이었다."], ["It was not the faintness of physical weakness, though confinement and hard fare no doubt had their part in it.", "그것은 육체적 쇠약에서 비롯된 희미함이 아니었으나, 감금과 거친 음식이 거기에 한몫한 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["Its deplorable peculiarity was, that it was the faintness of solitude and disuse.", "그 목소리의 가련한 특징은, 그것이 고독과 오랜 침묵에서 비롯된 희미함이라는 데 있었다."], ["It was like the last feeble echo of a sound made long and long ago.", "그것은 오래오래 전에 울렸던 어떤 소리의 마지막 가냘픈 메아리 같았다."], ["So entirely had it lost the life and resonance of the human voice, that it affected the senses like a once beautiful colour faded away into a poor weak stain.", "인간 목소리의 생기와 울림을 완전히 잃어버려서, 한때 아름다웠으나 이제는 보잘것없이 옅은 얼룩으로 바래버린 빛깔처럼 감각에 와닿았다."], ["So sunken and suppressed it was, that it was like a voice underground.", "그것은 너무도 가라앉고 억눌려 있어서 땅속에서 나오는 목소리 같았다."], ["So expressive it was, of a hopeless and lost creature, that a famished traveller, wearied out by lonely wandering in a wilderness, would have remembered home and friends in such a tone before lying down to die.", "그것은 희망 없이 길 잃은 어떤 존재를 너무도 잘 드러내고 있어서, 황야에서 외로이 헤매다 지쳐버린 굶주린 나그네라면 죽음을 앞두고 눕기 전에 그런 어조로 고향과 벗들을 떠올렸으리라."]], [["Some minutes of silent work had passed: and the haggard eyes had looked up again: not with any interest or curiosity, but with a dull mechanical perception, beforehand, that the spot where the only visitor they were aware of had stood, was not yet empty.", "몇 분간 말없는 작업이 이어졌다. 그러더니 초췌한 눈이 다시 위를 향했다. 어떤 관심이나 호기심에서가 아니라, 자신이 알고 있는 유일한 방문객이 서 있던 그 자리가 아직 비어 있지 않다는 것을, 둔하고 기계적인 지각으로 미리 알아챘기 때문이었다."]], [["“I want,” said Defarge, who had not removed his gaze from the shoemaker, “to let in a little more light here.", "“여기에 빛을 좀더 들이고 싶소만.”"], ["You can bear a little more?”", "구두장이에게서 시선을 떼지 않은 채 드파르주가 말했다. “조금 더 견딜 수 있겠소?”"]], [["The shoemaker stopped his work; looked with a vacant air of listening, at the floor on one side of him; then similarly, at the floor on the other side of him; then, upward at the speaker.", "구두장이가 일손을 멈추었다. 무언가에 귀 기울이는 듯한 멍한 표정으로 한쪽 바닥을 보더니, 같은 모양으로 다른 쪽 바닥을 보고, 그런 다음 위쪽의 말하는 이를 올려다보았다."]], [["“What did you say?”", "“뭐라고 했소?”"]], [["“You can bear a little more light?”", "“빛을 좀더 견딜 수 있겠소?”"]], [["“I must bear it, if you let it in.”", "“당신이 들인다면 견뎌야겠지.”"], ["(Laying the palest shadow of a stress upon the second word.)", "(두번째 낱말에 더없이 옅은 강세의 그림자를 드리우며.)"]], [["The opened half-door was opened a little further, and secured at that angle for the time.", "반쯤 열려 있던 문이 조금 더 열렸고, 잠시 그 각도로 고정되었다."], ["A broad ray of light fell into the garret, and showed the workman with an unfinished shoe upon his lap, pausing in his labour.", "넓은 빛줄기가 다락방으로 떨어져, 무릎 위에 미완성의 구두를 올려놓고 일손을 멈춘 일꾼의 모습을 드러냈다."], ["His few common tools and various scraps of leather were at his feet and on his bench.", "변변찮은 연장 몇 개와 갖가지 가죽 조각이 그의 발치와 작업대 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["He had a white beard, raggedly cut, but not very long, a hollow face, and exceedingly bright eyes.", "그는 거칠게 잘려 있으나 그리 길지는 않은 흰 수염을 기르고 있었고, 얼굴은 움푹 꺼졌으며, 두 눈은 유난히 빛났다."], ["The hollowness and thinness of his face would have caused them to look large, under his yet dark eyebrows and his confused white hair, though they had been really otherwise; but, they were naturally large, and looked unnaturally so.", "그 얼굴의 움푹함과 여윔 때문에, 아직은 검은 눈썹과 헝클어진 흰 머리칼 아래에서 그 눈은, 실제로는 그렇지 않았더라도 크게 보였을 것이다. 그러나 그 눈은 본래 컸고, 부자연스러울 만큼 크게 보였다."], ["His yellow rags of shirt lay open at the throat, and showed his body to be withered and worn.", "누렇게 바랜 누더기 셔츠는 목께가 벌어져, 시들고 닳은 그의 몸을 드러냈다."], ["He, and his old canvas frock, and his loose stockings, and all his poor tatters of clothes, had, in a long seclusion from direct light and air, faded down to such a dull uniformity of parchment-yellow, that it would have been hard to say which was which.", "그와, 그의 낡은 삼베 작업복과, 헐렁한 양말과, 가련한 누더기 옷가지들은, 직사광선과 바깥공기로부터 오래 격리된 탓에 양피지 같은 누런빛으로 한결같이 칙칙하게 바래어, 어느 것이 어느 것인지 분간하기 어려울 정도였다."]], [["He had put up a hand between his eyes and the light, and the very bones of it seemed transparent.", "그는 눈과 빛 사이에 한 손을 들어 올렸는데, 그 손의 뼈마디까지 투명해 보였다."], ["So he sat, with a steadfastly vacant gaze, pausing in his work.", "그렇게 그는 일손을 멈춘 채 한결같이 멍한 시선으로 앉아 있었다."], ["He never looked at the figure before him, without first looking down on this side of himself, then on that, as if he had lost the habit of associating place with sound; he never spoke, without first wandering in this manner, and forgetting to speak.", "그는 자기 앞의 모습을 바라볼 때면 언제나 먼저 자기 옆 이쪽을 내려다보고, 그다음 저쪽을 내려다보았는데, 마치 소리를 장소와 연결 짓는 습관을 잃어버린 듯했다. 그는 말을 할 때면 언제나 먼저 이런 식으로 헤매다가, 말하기를 잊어버리곤 했다."]], [["“Are you going to finish that pair of shoes to-day?”", "“오늘 그 구두 한 켤레를 마무리할 작정이오?”"], ["asked Defarge, motioning to Mr. Lorry to come forward.", "드파르주가 로리 씨에게 앞으로 나오라고 손짓하며 물었다."]], [["“What did you say?”", "“뭐라고 했소?”"]], [["“Do you mean to finish that pair of shoes to-day?”", "“오늘 그 구두 한 켤레를 마무리할 작정이냐고 했소.”"]], [["“I can’t say that I mean to.", "“작정이라고는 못하겠소."], ["I suppose so.", "그러려나 보오."], ["I don’t know.”", "모르겠소.”"]], [["But, the question reminded him of his work, and he bent over it again.", "그러나 그 물음이 그에게 자기 일을 일깨워주었고, 그는 다시 그 일 위로 수그러들었다."]], [["Mr. Lorry came silently forward, leaving the daughter by the door.", "로리 씨가 딸을 문가에 남겨둔 채 말없이 앞으로 나왔다."], ["When he had stood, for a minute or two, by the side of Defarge, the shoemaker looked up.", "그가 일이 분쯤 드파르주 곁에 서 있자, 구두장이가 올려다보았다."], ["He showed no surprise at seeing another figure, but the unsteady fingers of one of his hands strayed to his lips as he looked at it (his lips and his nails were of the same pale lead-colour), and then the hand dropped to his work, and he once more bent over the shoe.", "그는 또다른 모습을 보고도 놀라는 기색이 없었으나, 그것을 바라보는 동안 한 손의 떨리는 손가락들이 그의 입술께로 향했고(그의 입술과 손톱은 똑같이 창백한 납빛이었다), 그런 다음 그 손은 일감 위로 떨어졌고, 그는 또 한번 구두 위로 수그러들었다."], ["The look and the action had occupied but an instant.", "그 표정과 동작은 단지 한순간을 차지했을 뿐이었다."]], [["“You have a visitor, you see,”", "“보다시피 손님이 오셨소.”"], ["said Monsieur Defarge.", "드파르주 씨가 말했다."]], [["“What did you say?”", "“뭐라고 했소?”"]], [["“Here is a visitor.”", "“여기 손님이 오셨소.”"]], [["The shoemaker looked up as before, but without removing a hand from his work.", "구두장이는 아까처럼 올려다보았으나, 일감에서 손을 떼지는 않았다."]], [["“Come!”", "“이보시오!”"], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."], ["“Here is monsieur, who knows a well-made shoe when he sees one.", "“여기 이 신사분은 잘 만든 구두를 보면 알아보는 분이오."], ["Show him that shoe you are working at.", "당신이 짓고 있는 그 구두를 좀 보여드리시오."], ["Take it, monsieur.”", "신사분, 그것을 받으시오.”"]], [["Mr. Lorry took it in his hand.", "로리 씨가 그것을 손에 들었다."]], [["“Tell monsieur what kind of shoe it is, and the maker’s name.”", "“이게 어떤 종류의 구두인지, 또 만든 사람의 이름을 신사분께 말씀드리시오.”"]], [["There was a longer pause than usual, before the shoemaker replied:", "구두장이가 대답하기까지 평소보다 긴 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“I forget what it was you asked me.", "“당신이 내게 뭘 물었는지 잊었소."], ["What did you say?”", "뭐라고 했소?”"]], [["“I said, couldn’t you describe the kind of shoe, for monsieur’s information?”", "“신사분께 알려드리게, 이게 어떤 종류의 구두인지 설명해줄 수 없겠느냐고 했소.”"]], [["“It is a lady’s shoe.", "“이건 부인용 구두요."], ["It is a young lady’s walking-shoe.", "젊은 부인용 산책화요. 요즘 유행이지."], ["It is in the present mode. I never saw the mode.", "나는 그 유행을 본 적은 없소."], ["I have had a pattern in my hand.”", "본을 손에 쥐어본 적은 있소.”"], ["He glanced at the shoe with some little passing touch of pride.", "그는 스쳐 지나는 작은 자랑스러움이 깃든 눈길로 그 구두를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“And the maker’s name?”", "“그럼 만든 사람의 이름은?”"], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["Now that he had no work to hold, he laid the knuckles of the right hand in the hollow of the left, and then the knuckles of the left hand in the hollow of the right, and then passed a hand across his bearded chin, and so on in regular changes, without a moment’s intermission.", "이제 쥐고 있을 일감이 없어지자, 그는 오른손 마디를 왼손 오목한 곳에 놓았다가, 다시 왼손 마디를 오른손 오목한 곳에 놓았다가, 그런 다음 수염 난 턱을 한 손으로 쓸었고, 그렇게 한순간의 쉼도 없이 규칙적으로 동작을 바꾸었다."], ["The task of recalling him from the vagrancy into which he always sank when he had spoken, was like recalling some very weak person from a swoon, or endeavouring, in the hope of some disclosure, to stay the spirit of a fast-dying man.", "말을 마치고 나면 늘 빠져들곤 하는 그 정신의 방황으로부터 그를 불러내는 일은, 몹시 쇠약한 사람을 혼절 상태에서 깨우거나, 무언가를 털어놓게 하려는 바람에서 빠르게 죽어가는 사람의 영혼을 붙잡아두려 애쓰는 것과 같았다."]], [["“Did you ask me for my name?”", "“내 이름을 물었소?”"]], [["“Assuredly I did.”", "“그렇소, 분명히.”"]], [["“One Hundred and Five, North Tower.”", "“북쪽 탑, 백오번.”"]], [["“Is that all?”", "“그게 다요?”"]], [["“One Hundred and Five, North Tower.”", "“북쪽 탑, 백오번.”"]], [["With a weary sound that was not a sigh, nor a groan, he bent to work again, until the silence was again broken.", "한숨도 아니고 신음도 아닌 지친 소리를 내며 그는 다시 일감 위로 수그러들었고, 마침내 다시 침묵이 깨졌다."]], [["“You are not a shoemaker by trade?”", "“직업이 구두장이는 아니지요?”"], ["said Mr. Lorry, looking steadfastly at him.", "로리 씨가 그를 한결같이 바라보며 말했다."]], [["His haggard eyes turned to Defarge as if he would have transferred the question to him: but as no help came from that quarter, they turned back on the questioner when they had sought the ground.", "그의 초췌한 눈은 그 물음을 떠넘기려는 듯 드파르주 쪽으로 돌아갔다. 그러나 그쪽에서 아무 도움도 오지 않자, 그 눈은 바닥을 더듬은 뒤 다시 묻는 이에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“I am not a shoemaker by trade?", "“내 직업이 구두장이가 아니라고?"], ["No, I was not a shoemaker by trade.", "그렇소, 나는 직업이 구두장이가 아니었소."], ["I-I learnt it here.", "나는ㅡ나는 여기서 그것을 배웠소."], ["I taught myself.", "혼자 익혔지."], ["I asked leave to--”", "나는 허락을 청해서ㅡ”"]], [["He lapsed away, even for minutes, ringing those measured changes on his hands the whole time.", "그는, 그동안 내내 두 손으로 그 절도 있는 동작을 되풀이하면서, 몇 분 동안이나 다시 정신을 놓아버렸다."], ["His eyes came slowly back, at last, to the face from which they had wandered; when they rested on it, he started, and resumed, in the manner of a sleeper that moment awake, reverting to a subject of last night.", "마침내 그의 눈이 천천히, 그것이 떠나갔던 얼굴로 되돌아왔다. 그 얼굴에 눈길이 머물자, 그는 흠칫하더니, 방금 깨어난 잠든 이가 지난밤의 이야기로 되돌아가듯 말을 이었다."]], [["“I asked leave to teach myself, and I got it with much difficulty after a long while, and I have made shoes ever since.”", "“혼자 익히도록 허락해달라고 청했고, 오랜 시간이 지난 뒤 무척 어렵게 그 허락을 얻어, 그 뒤로 줄곧 구두를 지어왔소.”"]], [["As he held out his hand for the shoe that had been taken from him, Mr. Lorry said, still looking steadfastly in his face:", "빼앗겼던 구두를 받으려고 손을 내밀 때, 로리 씨가 여전히 그의 얼굴을 한결같이 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Monsieur Manette, do you remember nothing of me?”", "“마네뜨 씨, 나에 대해 아무것도 기억나는 게 없소?”"]], [["The shoe dropped to the ground, and he sat looking fixedly at the questioner.", "구두가 바닥으로 떨어졌고, 그는 묻는 이를 뚫어지게 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Monsieur Manette”; Mr.", "“마네뜨 씨.”"], ["Lorry laid his hand upon Defarge’s arm; “do you remember nothing of this man?", "로리 씨가 드파르주의 팔에 손을 얹었다. “이 사람에 대해서는 아무것도 기억나는 게 없소?"], ["Look at him.", "이 사람을 보시오."], ["Look at me.", "나를 보시오."], ["Is there no old banker, no old business, no old servant, no old time, rising in your mind, Monsieur Manette?”", "마네뜨 씨, 당신 마음속에 떠오르는 옛 은행가도, 옛 사업도, 옛 하인도, 옛 시절도 없단 말이오?”"]], [["As the captive of many years sat looking fixedly, by turns, at Mr.", "여러 해를 갇혀 지낸 그가 로리 씨와 드파르주를 번갈아 뚫어지게 바라보며 앉아 있는 동안, 이마 한가운데에 오래전 지워졌던, 활발하고 골똘했던 지성의 어떤 흔적들이, 그에게 드리워졌던 검은 안개를 뚫고 차츰 솟아올랐다."], ["Lorry and at Defarge, some long obliterated marks of an actively intent intelligence in the middle of the forehead, gradually forced themselves through the black mist that had fallen on him.", "그것들은 다시 구름에 가려졌고, 더 희미해졌으며, 사라졌다. 그러나 그것들은 거기 있었던 것이다."], ["They were overclouded again, they were fainter, they were gone; but they had been there.", "그리고 그 표정은, 벽을 따라 그를 볼 수 있는 지점까지 살금살금 다가가, 이제 거기 서서 그를 바라보고 있는 젊은 여인의 고운 얼굴 위에 똑같이 되풀이되었다."], ["And so exactly was the expression repeated on the fair young face of her who had crept along the wall to a point where she could see him, and where she now stood looking at him, with hands which at first had been only raised in frightened compassion, if not even to keep him off and shut out the sight of him, but which were now extending towards him, trembling with eagerness to lay the spectral face upon her warm young breast, and love it back to life and hope--so exactly was the expression repeated (though in stronger characters) on her fair young face, that it looked as though it had passed like a moving light, from him to her.", "그녀의 두 손은 처음에는, 그를 막고 그 모습을 가려 보이지 않게 하려는 것까지는 아니더라도, 겁에 질린 연민에서 그저 들어 올려졌을 뿐이었으나, 이제는 그 유령 같은 얼굴을 자신의 따뜻하고 젊은 가슴에 끌어안아 다시 생명과 희망으로 사랑해 되살리고 싶은 간절함에 떨면서 그를 향해 뻗어 있었다. 그 표정이 그녀의 고운 젊은 얼굴 위에 (더 강렬한 선으로이긴 했으나) 너무도 똑같이 되풀이되어서, 마치 그것이 움직이는 빛처럼 그에게서 그녀에게로 옮겨간 듯이 보였다."]], [["Darkness had fallen on him in its place.", "그 대신 어둠이 그에게 내려앉았다."], ["He looked at the two, less and less attentively, and his eyes in gloomy abstraction sought the ground and looked about him in the old way.", "그는 점점 덜 주의 깊게 두 사람을 바라보았고, 그의 눈은 침울한 멍함 속에서 바닥을 더듬다가 옛 방식대로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Finally, with a deep long sigh, he took the shoe up, and resumed his work.", "마침내 깊고 긴 한숨과 함께 그는 구두를 집어 들고 다시 일을 시작했다."]], [["“Have you recognised him, monsieur?”", "“그를 알아보셨소, 신사분?”"], ["asked Defarge in a whisper.", "드파르주가 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“Yes; for a moment.", "“그렇소, 한순간이지만."], ["At first I thought it quite hopeless, but I have unquestionably seen, for a single moment, the face that I once knew so well.", "처음엔 가망이 전혀 없다고 여겼는데, 한순간이나마 한때 그토록 잘 알았던 얼굴을 의심할 여지 없이 보았소."], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["Let us draw further back.", "좀더 뒤로 물러납시다."], ["Hush!”", "쉿!”"]], [["She had moved from the wall of the garret, very near to the bench on which he sat.", "그녀는 다락방의 벽에서 떨어져, 그가 앉아 있는 작업대 아주 가까이까지 다가가 있었다."], ["There was something awful in his unconsciousness of the figure that could have put out its hand and touched him as he stooped over his labour.", "일감 위로 수그린 그를, 손을 뻗으면 닿을 만한 곳까지 다가온 그 모습을 그가 전혀 알아채지 못한다는 데에는 무언가 두려운 것이 있었다."]], [["Not a word was spoken, not a sound was made.", "한마디 말도 오가지 않았고, 어떤 소리도 나지 않았다."], ["She stood, like a spirit, beside him, and he bent over his work.", "그녀는 망령처럼 그의 곁에 서 있었고, 그는 일감 위로 수그러들었다."]], [["It happened, at length, that he had occasion to change the instrument in his hand, for his shoemaker’s knife.", "마침내, 그가 손에 든 도구를 구두장이 칼로 바꾸어야 할 일이 생겼다."], ["It lay on that side of him which was not the side on which she stood.", "칼은 그녀가 서 있는 쪽이 아닌 반대편에 놓여 있었다."], ["He had taken it up, and was stooping to work again, when his eyes caught the skirt of her dress.", "그가 그것을 집어 들고 다시 일하려고 수그릴 때, 그의 눈에 그녀의 치맛자락이 들어왔다."], ["He raised them, and saw her face.", "그가 눈을 들어 그녀의 얼굴을 보았다."], ["The two spectators started forward, but she stayed them with a motion of her hand.", "두 구경꾼은 앞으로 달려 나갔으나, 그녀가 손짓 하나로 그들을 멈춰 세웠다."], ["She had no fear of his striking at her with the knife, though they had.", "그녀는 그가 칼로 자신을 내리칠까봐 두려워하지 않았다. 그들은 두려워했지만 말이다."]], [["He stared at her with a fearful look, and after a while his lips began to form some words, though no sound proceeded from them.", "그는 두려운 눈빛으로 그녀를 응시했고, 잠시 후 그의 입술이 무슨 말을 지어내기 시작했으나, 거기서는 아무 소리도 새어 나오지 않았다."], ["By degrees, in the pauses of his quick and laboured breathing, he was heard to say:", "가쁘고 힘겨운 숨결 사이의 멈춤 동안, 차츰 그가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다."]], [["“What is this?”", "“이게 어찌 된 일인가?”"]], [["With the tears streaming down her face, she put her two hands to her lips, and kissed them to him; then clasped them on her breast, as if she laid his ruined head there.", "눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내리는 가운데, 그녀는 두 손을 입술에 대고 그를 향해 입맞춤을 보냈다. 그런 다음 마치 그 부서진 머리를 거기에 누이는 듯 그 두 손을 자기 가슴에 모았다. “당신은 간수의 딸이 아니지?”"]], [["“You are not the gaoler’s daughter?”", "그녀는 “아니에요” 하고 한숨지었다."]], [["She sighed “No.”", "“당신은 누구지?”"]], [["“Who are you?” Not yet trusting the tones of her voice, she sat down on the bench beside him.", "자기 목소리의 어조를 아직 믿지 못한 채, 그녀는 그의 곁 작업대에 앉았다."], ["He recoiled, but she laid her hand upon his arm.", "그가 움찔 물러섰으나, 그녀가 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다."], ["A strange thrill struck him when she did so, and visibly passed over his frame; he laid the knife down softly, as he sat staring at her.", "그녀가 그렇게 하자 기이한 전율이 그를 덮쳐 그의 몸을 눈에 띄게 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 그녀를 응시한 채 앉아서 칼을 가만히 내려놓았다."]], [["Her golden hair, which she wore in long curls, had been hurriedly pushed aside, and fell down over her neck.", "긴 곱슬머리로 늘어뜨린 그녀의 금빛 머리칼이 황급히 옆으로 밀려 목 위로 흘러내려 있었다."], ["Advancing his hand by little and little, he took it up and looked at it.", "그가 조금씩 조금씩 손을 내밀어 그것을 집어 들고 바라보았다."], ["In the midst of the action he went astray, and, with another deep sigh, fell to work at his shoemaking.", "그러던 도중에 그는 정신을 놓았고, 또 한번 깊은 한숨과 함께 구두 짓는 일에 빠져들었다."]], [["But not for long.", "그러나 오래가지는 않았다."], ["Releasing his arm, she laid her hand upon his shoulder.", "그녀가 그의 팔을 놓아주고 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."], ["After looking doubtfully at it, two or three times, as if to be sure that it was really there, he laid down his work, put his hand to his neck, and took off a blackened string with a scrap of folded rag attached to it.", "정말 거기 있는 것이 맞는지 확인하려는 듯 두세 번 미심쩍게 그것을 바라본 뒤, 그는 일감을 내려놓고 손을 목으로 가져가, 접힌 헝겊 조각이 달린 거뭇한 끈 하나를 풀어냈다."], ["He opened this, carefully, on his knee, and it contained a very little quantity of hair: not more than one or two long golden hairs, which he had, in some old day, wound off upon his finger.", "그는 그것을 무릎 위에서 조심스레 펼쳤고, 그 안에는 아주 적은 양의 머리카락이 들어 있었다. 길고 금빛인 한두 올의 머리카락에 지나지 않았는데, 그가 언젠가 옛날에 손가락에 감아 두었던 것이었다."]], [["He took her hair into his hand again, and looked closely at it.", "그는 다시 그녀의 머리카락을 손에 쥐고 가까이서 들여다보았다. “똑같아."], ["“It is the same.", "어떻게 이럴 수가!"], ["How can it be!", "그게 언제였더라!"], ["When was it! How was it!”", "어떻게 된 거였더라!”"]], [["As the concentrated expression returned to his forehead, he seemed to become conscious that it was in hers too.", "골똘한 표정이 그의 이마로 되돌아오면서, 그는 그것이 그녀의 이마에도 있다는 것을 의식하는 듯했다."], ["He turned her full to the light, and looked at her.", "그는 그녀를 빛 쪽으로 온전히 돌려세우고 바라보았다."]], [["“She had laid her head upon my shoulder, that night when I was summoned out--she had a fear of my going, though I had none--and when I was brought to the North Tower they found these upon my sleeve.", "“그애는 내가 불려 나가던 그날 밤 내 어깨에 머리를 기댔지ㅡ나는 두렵지 않았는데 그애는 내가 가는 것을 두려워했어ㅡ그리고 내가 북쪽 탑으로 끌려갔을 때, 사람들이 내 소매에서 이것을 찾아냈지."], ["‘You will leave me them?", "‘이것을 내게 남겨주시겠소?"], ["They can never help me to escape in the body, though they may in the spirit.’ Those were the words I said.", "이것이 내 몸이 달아나는 데는 결코 도움이 못 되겠지만, 영혼이 달아나는 데는 도움이 될지 모르오.’ 그게 내가 한 말이었어."], ["I remember them very well.”", "나는 그 말을 아주 잘 기억하고 있어.”"]], [["He formed this speech with his lips many times before he could utter it.", "그는 그 말을 입 밖에 내기 전에 입술로 여러 번 그것을 빚어냈다."], ["But when he did find spoken words for it, they came to him coherently, though slowly.", "그러나 마침내 그것에 들어맞는 말을 찾아냈을 때, 그 말은 비록 느리게나마 조리 있게 그에게서 흘러나왔다."]], [["“How was this?--Was it you?”", "“이게 어찌 된 일인가?ㅡ당신이었나?”"]], [["Once more, the two spectators started, as he turned upon her with a frightful suddenness.", "그가 무시무시할 만큼 갑작스레 그녀를 향해 돌아서자, 또 한번 두 구경꾼이 흠칫했다."], ["But she sat perfectly still in his grasp, and only said, in a low voice, “I entreat you, good gentlemen, do not come near us, do not speak, do not move!”", "그러나 그녀는 그의 손아귀 안에서 미동도 없이 앉은 채, 낮은 목소리로 이렇게만 말했다. “선량하신 분들, 간청합니다, 우리에게 가까이 오지 마세요, 말씀하지 마세요, 움직이지 마세요!”"]], [["“Hark!”", "“가만!”"], ["he exclaimed.", "그가 외쳤다."], ["“Whose voice was that?”", "“저 목소리는 누구의 것이었지?”"]], [["His hands released her as he uttered this cry, and went up to his white hair, which they tore in a frenzy.", "이 외침을 내뱉으면서 그의 손이 그녀를 놓고 그의 흰 머리칼로 올라가, 광란하듯 그것을 쥐어뜯었다."], ["It died out, as everything but his shoemaking did die out of him, and he refolded his little packet and tried to secure it in his breast; but he still looked at her, and gloomily shook his head.", "그것은, 구두 짓는 일 말고는 그에게서 모든 것이 사라지듯, 사그라들었고, 그는 그 작은 꾸러미를 다시 접어 가슴속에 간직하려 했다. 그러나 그는 여전히 그녀를 바라보며 침울하게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“No, no, no; you are too young, too blooming.", "“아니, 아니, 아니야. 당신은 너무 젊고, 너무 한창때야."], ["It can’t be.", "그럴 리가 없어."], ["See what the prisoner is.", "이 죄수가 어떤 꼴인지 보라고."], ["These are not the hands she knew, this is not the face she knew, this is not a voice she ever heard. No, no.", "이건 그애가 알던 손이 아니고, 이건 그애가 알던 얼굴이 아니고, 이건 그애가 한 번도 들어본 적 없는 목소리야."], ["She was--and He was--before the slow years of the North Tower--ages ago.", "아니, 아니야. 그애는ㅡ그리고 그는ㅡ북쪽 탑의 더딘 세월 이전이지ㅡ아득한 옛날이야."], ["What is your name, my gentle angel?”", "당신 이름이 뭐요, 나의 다정한 천사여?”"]], [["Hailing his softened tone and manner, his daughter fell upon her knees before him, with her appealing hands upon his breast.", "부드러워진 그의 어조와 태도를 반기며, 그의 딸이 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 호소하는 두 손을 그의 가슴에 얹었다."]], [["“O, sir, at another time you shall know my name, and who my mother was, and who my father, and how I never knew their hard, hard history.", "“오, 아버지, 다른 때에 제 이름과, 제 어머니가 누구였는지, 제 아버지가 누구인지, 그리고 제가 그분들의 고되고도 고된 사연을 어떻게 한 번도 몰랐는지 아시게 될 거예요."], ["But I cannot tell you at this time, and I cannot tell you here.", "하지만 지금은 말씀드릴 수 없고, 여기서는 말씀드릴 수 없어요."], ["All that I may tell you, here and now, is, that I pray to you to touch me and to bless me.", "여기서 지금 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건, 부디 저를 어루만지고 축복해주십사 비는 것뿐이에요."], ["Kiss me, kiss me!", "입맞춰주세요, 입맞춰주세요!"], ["O my dear, my dear!”", "오, 사랑하는 분, 사랑하는 분!”"]], [["His cold white head mingled with her radiant hair, which warmed and lighted it as though it were the light of Freedom shining on him.", "그의 차갑고 흰 머리가 그녀의 빛나는 머리칼과 뒤섞였고, 그 머리칼은 마치 그에게 비추는 자유의 빛이기라도 한 듯 그것을 따뜻하게 데우고 환히 밝혔다."]], [["“If you hear in my voice--I don’t know that it is so, but I hope it is--if you hear in my voice any resemblance to a voice that once was sweet music in your ears, weep for it, weep for it!", "“제 목소리에서ㅡ정말 그런지는 모르겠지만, 그러기를 바라요ㅡ제 목소리에서, 한때 당신의 귓가에 감미로운 음악이었던 어떤 목소리와 닮은 데가 들리거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!"], ["If you touch, in touching my hair, anything that recalls a beloved head that lay on your breast when you were young and free, weep for it, weep for it!", "제 머리칼을 어루만지다가, 당신이 젊고 자유롭던 시절에 당신의 가슴에 기대었던 사랑스러운 머리를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 만져지거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!"], ["If, when I hint to you of a Home that is before us, where I will be true to you with all my duty and with all my faithful service, I bring back the remembrance of a Home long desolate, while your poor heart pined away, weep for it, weep for it!”", "제가 우리 앞에 놓인 보금자리에 대해, 제가 온 의무와 온 충실한 보살핌으로 당신께 진실할 그 보금자리에 대해 넌지시 말씀드릴 때, 당신의 가련한 마음이 시들어가는 동안 오래도록 황폐했던 보금자리의 기억을 제가 되살리거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!”"]], [["She held him closer round the neck, and rocked him on her breast like a child.", "그녀가 그의 목을 더 가까이 끌어안고, 어린아이처럼 그를 자기 가슴에 안고 어르었다."]], [["“If, when I tell you, dearest dear, that your agony is over, and that I have come here to take you from it, and that we go to England to be at peace and at rest, I cause you to think of your useful life laid waste, and of our native France so wicked to you, weep for it, weep for it!", "“가장 사랑하는 분, 당신의 고통은 끝났고, 제가 당신을 그 고통에서 데려가려고 여기 왔으며, 우리가 평화와 안식을 누리러 영국으로 간다고 제가 말씀드릴 때, 만일 제가 당신께 헛되이 버려진 당신의 쓸모 있던 삶을, 그리고 당신께 그토록 모질었던 우리 조국 프랑스를 떠올리게 하거든, 그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!"], ["And if, when I shall tell you of my name, and of my father who is living, and of my mother who is dead, you learn that I have to kneel to my honoured father, and implore his pardon for having never for his sake striven all day and lain awake and wept all night, because the love of my poor mother hid his torture from me, weep for it, weep for it!", "그리고 제가 제 이름과, 살아 계신 제 아버지와, 돌아가신 제 어머니에 대해 말씀드릴 때, 제가 존경하는 아버지께 무릎 꿇고, 그분을 위해 온종일 애쓰지도 못하고 밤새 깨어 울지도 못했던 일에 대해 용서를 빌어야 한다는 것을 당신이 아시게 되거든ㅡ제 가련한 어머니의 사랑이 그분의 고통을 제게서 숨겼던 탓이었지만요ㅡ그것을 위해 우세요, 그것을 위해 우세요!"], ["Weep for her, then, and for me!", "그렇거든 그분을 위해, 그리고 저를 위해 우세요!"], ["Good gentlemen, thank God!", "선량하신 분들, 하느님께 감사드려요!"], ["I feel his sacred tears upon my face, and his sobs strike against my heart.", "그분의 거룩한 눈물이 제 얼굴 위에 느껴지고, 그분의 흐느낌이 제 가슴을 두드려요. 오, 보세요!"], ["O, see! Thank God for us, thank God!”", "우리를 위해 하느님께 감사드려요, 하느님께 감사드려요!”"]], [["He had sunk in her arms, and his face dropped on her breast: a sight so touching, yet so terrible in the tremendous wrong and suffering which had gone before it, that the two beholders covered their faces.", "그가 그녀의 품에 무너져 내렸고, 그의 얼굴이 그녀의 가슴 위로 떨구어졌다. 그것은 너무도 가슴 저미는 광경이었으나, 그에 앞서 있었던 엄청난 부당함과 고통 때문에 또한 너무도 끔찍한 광경이어서, 두 구경꾼은 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["When the quiet of the garret had been long undisturbed, and his heaving breast and shaken form had long yielded to the calm that must follow all storms--emblem to humanity, of the rest and silence into which the storm called Life must hush at last--they came forward to raise the father and daughter from the ground.", "다락방의 고요가 오래도록 흐트러지지 않고, 그의 들먹이던 가슴과 떨리던 몸이 오래도록 가라앉아ㅡ삶이라는 폭풍이 마침내 잠잠해질 그 안식과 침묵을 인류에게 보여주는 표상인, 모든 폭풍 뒤에 반드시 찾아오는 그 고요에 자리를 내준 뒤에ㅡ그들이 앞으로 나와 아버지와 딸을 바닥에서 일으켜 세우려 했다."], ["He had gradually dropped to the floor, and lay there in a lethargy, worn out.", "그는 차츰 바닥으로 미끄러져 내려, 기진맥진한 채 무기력하게 누워 있었다."], ["She had nestled down with him, that his head might lie upon her arm; and her hair drooping over him curtained him from the light.", "그녀는 그의 머리가 자기 팔 위에 누이도록 그와 함께 웅크렸고, 그를 덮어 드리운 그녀의 머리칼이 그를 빛으로부터 가려주고 있었다."]], [["“If, without disturbing him,” she said, raising her hand to Mr.", "“만일 그분을 깨우지 않고서,”"], ["Lorry as he stooped over them, after repeated blowings of his nose, “all could be arranged for our leaving Paris at once, so that, from the very door, he could be taken away--”", "코를 거듭 푼 뒤 그들 위로 수그린 로리 씨를 향해 손을 들어 올리며 그녀가 말했다. “당장 빠리를 떠날 수 있도록 모든 채비를 갖춰서, 바로 이 문에서부터 그분을 모셔 갈 수 있다면ㅡ”"]], [["“But, consider.", "“하지만, 생각해보시오."], ["Is he fit for the journey?”", "그분이 여행을 견딜 만하겠소?”"], ["asked Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 물었다."]], [["“More fit for that, I think, than to remain in this city, so dreadful to him.”", "“그분에게 그토록 끔찍한 이 도시에 머무는 것보다는, 그편이 더 견딜 만하리라 생각해요.”"]], [["“It is true,”", "“맞는 말씀이오.”"], ["said Defarge, who was kneeling to look on and hear.", "무릎을 꿇고 지켜보며 듣고 있던 드파르주가 말했다."], ["“More than that; Monsieur Manette is, for all reasons, best out of France.", "“그뿐만이 아니오. 마네뜨 씨는 모든 면에서 프랑스를 떠나는 게 가장 좋소."], ["Say, shall I hire a carriage and post-horses?”", "자, 내가 마차와 역마를 빌려 올까요?”"]], [["“That’s business,” said Mr.", "“그건 사무적인 일이오.”"], ["Lorry, resuming on the shortest notice his methodical manners; “and if business is to be done, I had better do it.”", "로리 씨가 지체 없이 자신의 빈틈없는 태도를 되찾으며 말했다. “사무를 처리해야 한다면, 내가 처리하는 편이 낫겠소.”"]], [["“Then be so kind,”", "“그럼 부디,”"], ["urged Miss Manette, “as to leave us here.", "마네뜨 양이 청했다. “우리를 여기 남겨두고 가주세요."], ["You see how composed he has become, and you cannot be afraid to leave him with me now.", "그분이 얼마나 차분해지셨는지 보세요. 이제 저와 함께 그분을 두고 가셔도 두려워하실 일은 없어요."], ["Why should you be?", "어찌 두려워하시겠어요?"], ["If you will lock the door to secure us from interruption, I do not doubt that you will find him, when you come back, as quiet as you leave him.", "방해받지 않도록 문을 잠가주시면, 돌아오셨을 때 떠날 때와 다름없이 평온한 그분을 보시게 되리라는 걸 의심하지 않아요."], ["In any case, I will take care of him until you return, and then we will remove him straight.”", "어떻든 두 분이 돌아오실 때까지 제가 그분을 보살피겠고, 그런 다음 곧장 그분을 모셔 가겠어요.”"]], [["Both Mr. Lorry and Defarge were rather disinclined to this course, and in favour of one of them remaining.", "로리 씨도 드파르주도 이런 방식이 다소 내키지 않았고, 둘 중 하나는 남는 편을 바랐다."], ["But, as there were not only carriage and horses to be seen to, but travelling papers; and as time pressed, for the day was drawing to an end, it came at last to their hastily dividing the business that was necessary to be done, and hurrying away to do it.", "그러나 마차와 말뿐 아니라 여행 증명서도 챙겨야 했고, 하루가 저물어가서 시간이 촉박했으므로, 결국 그들은 처리해야 할 일을 황급히 나누어 맡고, 그것을 처리하러 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["Then, as the darkness closed in, the daughter laid her head down on the hard ground close at the father’s side, and watched him.", "이윽고 어둠이 닫혀 들자, 딸은 아버지 곁 딱딱한 바닥에 머리를 누이고 그를 지켜보았다."], ["The darkness deepened and deepened, and they both lay quiet, until a light gleamed through the chinks in the wall.", "어둠은 점점 더 짙어졌고, 벽 틈새로 불빛이 어른거릴 때까지 두 사람은 가만히 누워 있었다."]], [["Mr. Lorry and Monsieur Defarge had made all ready for the journey, and had brought with them, besides travelling cloaks and wrappers, bread and meat, wine, and hot coffee.", "로리 씨와 드파르주 씨는 여행 채비를 모두 갖추고서, 여행용 외투와 덮개 외에도 빵과 고기, 포도주, 그리고 뜨거운 커피를 가져왔다."], ["Monsieur Defarge put this provender, and the lamp he carried, on the shoemaker’s bench (there was nothing else in the garret but a pallet bed), and he and Mr. Lorry roused the captive, and assisted him to his feet.", "드파르주 씨는 이 먹을거리와 자신이 들고 온 등불을 구두장이의 작업대 위에 올려놓았고(다락방에는 짚자리 침상 말고는 아무것도 없었다), 그와 로리 씨가 갇혀 지냈던 이를 깨워 일으켜 세웠다."]], [["No human intelligence could have read the mysteries of his mind, in the scared blank wonder of his face.", "겁에 질린 멍한 경이로 가득한 그의 얼굴에서, 어떤 인간의 지력으로도 그의 마음속 신비를 읽어낼 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["Whether he knew what had happened, whether he recollected what they had said to him, whether he knew that he was free, were questions which no sagacity could have solved.", "그가 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 아는지, 그들이 자기에게 한 말을 기억하는지, 자신이 자유로워졌다는 것을 아는지, 그 어떤 명민함으로도 풀 수 없는 물음이었다."], ["They tried speaking to him; but, he was so confused, and so very slow to answer, that they took fright at his bewilderment, and agreed for the time to tamper with him no more.", "그들이 그에게 말을 걸어보았다. 그러나 그가 너무도 혼란스러워하고 대답이 너무도 더디어서, 그들은 그의 어리둥절함에 겁이 나, 당분간 그를 더 건드리지 않기로 뜻을 모았다."], ["He had a wild, lost manner of occasionally clasping his head in his hands, that had not been seen in him before; yet, he had some pleasure in the mere sound of his daughter’s voice, and invariably turned to it when she spoke.", "그에게는 이따금 두 손으로 자기 머리를 부여잡는, 이전에는 보이지 않던 거칠고 넋 나간 태도가 있었다. 그러면서도 그는 딸의 목소리가 들려오는 그 소리 자체에서 얼마간 기쁨을 느꼈고, 그녀가 말할 때면 어김없이 그쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["In the submissive way of one long accustomed to obey under coercion, he ate and drank what they gave him to eat and drink, and put on the cloak and other wrappings, that they gave him to wear.", "강압 아래 복종하는 데 오래 길든 이의 순순한 태도로, 그는 그들이 먹고 마시라고 준 것을 먹고 마셨으며, 그들이 입으라고 준 외투와 그밖의 덮개를 걸쳤다."], ["He readily responded to his daughter’s drawing her arm through his, and took--and kept--her hand in both his own.", "그는 딸이 자기 팔에 팔짱을 끼는 데 선뜻 응했고, 그녀의 손을 자신의 두 손에 쥐고ㅡ놓지 않았다."]], [["They began to descend; Monsieur Defarge going first with the lamp, Mr.", "그들은 내려가기 시작했다. 드파르주 씨가 등불을 들고 앞장섰고, 로리 씨가 그 작은 행렬의 맨 뒤를 맡았다."], ["Lorry closing the little procession. They had not traversed many steps of the long main staircase when he stopped, and stared at the roof and round at the walls.", "길고 큰 계단을 몇 걸음 채 내려가지 않아 그가 멈춰 서서, 천장을 응시하고 벽을 둘러보았다."]], [["“You remember the place, my father?", "“이곳을 기억하세요, 아버지?"], ["You remember coming up here?”", "여기 올라오던 것을 기억하세요?”"]], [["“What did you say?”", "“뭐라고 했지?”"]], [["But, before she could repeat the question, he murmured an answer as if she had repeated it.", "그러나 그녀가 그 물음을 되풀이하기도 전에, 그는 마치 그녀가 되풀이하기라도 한 듯 중얼거리며 대답했다."]], [["“Remember?", "“기억이라고?"], ["No, I don’t remember.", "아니, 기억나지 않아."], ["It was so very long ago.”", "너무도 오래전 일이야.”"]], [["That he had no recollection whatever of his having been brought from his prison to that house, was apparent to them.", "그가 감옥에서 그 집으로 끌려왔던 일을 전혀 기억하지 못한다는 것이 그들에게 분명해졌다."], ["They heard him mutter, “One Hundred and Five, North Tower;”", "그들은 그가 “북쪽 탑, 백오번” 하고 중얼거리는 것을 들었다."], ["and when he looked about him, it evidently was for the strong fortress-walls which had long encompassed him.", "그리고 그가 주위를 둘러볼 때, 그것은 분명 오래도록 그를 에워쌌던 견고한 요새의 벽을 찾는 것이었다."], ["On their reaching the courtyard he instinctively altered his tread, as being in expectation of a drawbridge; and when there was no drawbridge, and he saw the carriage waiting in the open street, he dropped his daughter’s hand and clasped his head again.", "안마당에 이르자 그는 도개교를 예상하는 듯 본능적으로 걸음걸이를 바꾸었다. 그리고 도개교가 없고, 트인 거리에 마차가 기다리고 있는 것을 보자, 그는 딸의 손을 놓고 다시 자기 머리를 부여잡았다."]], [["No crowd was about the door; no people were discernible at any of the many windows; not even a chance passerby was in the street.", "문 둘레에 사람은 모여 있지 않았다. 그 숱한 창문 어디에서도 사람을 알아볼 수 없었고, 거리에는 우연히 지나가는 행인조차 없었다."], ["An unnatural silence and desertion reigned there.", "그곳에는 부자연스러운 침묵과 적막이 감돌았다."], ["Only one soul was to be seen, and that was Madame Defarge--who leaned against the door-post, knitting, and saw nothing.", "단 하나의 인영만 보였는데, 그것은 드파르주 부인이었다. 그녀는 문기둥에 기대어 뜨개질을 하고 있었고, 아무것도 보지 않았다."]], [["The prisoner had got into a coach, and his daughter had followed him, when Mr. Lorry’s feet were arrested on the step by his asking, miserably, for his shoemaking tools and the unfinished shoes.", "죄수가 마차에 올랐고 딸이 그를 따라 올랐을 때, 발판에 올라서던 로리 씨의 발이 그 자리에 붙들렸으니, 그가 비참하게 자신의 구두 짓는 연장과 미완성의 구두를 찾았기 때문이었다."], ["Madame Defarge immediately called to her husband that she would get them, and went, knitting, out of the lamplight, through the courtyard.", "드파르주 부인이 즉시 남편에게 자기가 가져오겠노라 외치고는, 뜨개질을 하며 등불 빛을 벗어나 안마당을 가로질러 갔다."], ["She quickly brought them down and handed them in;--and immediately afterwards leaned against the door-post, knitting, and saw nothing.", "그녀는 그것들을 재빨리 들고 내려와 안으로 건네주었다.ㅡ그러고는 곧바로 문기둥에 기대어 뜨개질을 하며, 아무것도 보지 않았다."]], [["Defarge got upon the box, and gave the word “To the Barrier!”", "드파르주가 마부석에 올라 “관문으로!”라고 일렀다."], ["The postilion cracked his whip, and they clattered away under the feeble over-swinging lamps.", "마부가 채찍을 휘둘렀고, 그들은 머리 위에서 어슴푸레 흔들리는 등불들 아래로 덜커덩거리며 떠나갔다."]], [["Under the over-swinging lamps--swinging ever brighter in the better streets, and ever dimmer in the worse--and by lighted shops, gay crowds, illuminated coffee-houses, and theatre-doors, to one of the city gates.", "머리 위에서 흔들리는 등불들 아래로ㅡ나은 거리에서는 점점 밝게 흔들리고, 못한 거리에서는 점점 어둑하게 흔들리는 그 등불들 아래로ㅡ불 밝힌 상점들과 흥겨운 군중과 환히 빛나는 커피점과 극장 문들을 지나, 도시의 어느 성문 가운데 한 곳에 이르렀다."], ["Soldiers with lanterns, at the guard-house there.", "그곳 위병소에는 등롱을 든 병사들이 있었다."], ["“Your papers, travellers!”", "“증명서요, 여행자들!”"], ["“See here then, Monsieur the Officer,”", "“자, 이걸 보시오, 장교 나리.”"], ["said Defarge, getting down, and taking him gravely apart, “these are the papers of monsieur inside, with the white head.", "드파르주가 마차에서 내려, 그를 점잖게 한쪽으로 데리고 가며 말했다. “이건 안에 계신, 흰 머리의 신사분의 증명서요."], ["They were consigned to me, with him, at the--”", "이분과 함께 그분이ㅡ에서 내게 맡겨진 것이오.”"], ["He dropped his voice, there was a flutter among the military lanterns, and one of them being handed into the coach by an arm in uniform, the eyes connected with the arm looked, not an every day or an every night look, at monsieur with the white head.", "그가 목소리를 낮추자, 군대의 등롱들 사이에 동요가 일었고, 그중 하나가 제복 입은 팔에 들려 마차 안으로 들어갔으며, 그 팔에 딸린 두 눈이 흰 머리의 신사분을 바라보았으니, 그것은 여느 낮이나 여느 밤의 눈길이 아니었다. “좋소."], ["“It is well.", "출발하시오!”"], ["Forward!” from the uniform.", "제복 쪽에서 말했다."], ["“Adieu!”", "“안녕히!”"], ["from Defarge.", "드파르주 쪽에서 말했다."], ["And so, under a short grove of feebler and feebler over-swinging lamps, out under the great grove of stars.", "그리하여, 점점 더 어슴푸레하게 머리 위에서 흔들리는 등불들의 짤막한 숲을 지나, 별들의 거대한 숲 아래로 나아갔다."]], [["Beneath that arch of unmoved and eternal lights; some, so remote from this little earth that the learned tell us it is doubtful whether their rays have even yet discovered it, as a point in space where anything is suffered or done: the shadows of the night were broad and black.", "미동도 없는 영원한 빛들의 그 아치 아래에서ㅡ그중 어떤 것들은 이 작은 지구로부터 너무도 멀어서, 학자들은 그 빛이 무언가가 고통받거나 행해지는 공간의 한 점으로서 이 지구를 아직 발견하기나 했는지조차 의심스럽다고 우리에게 말한다ㅡ밤의 그림자들은 넓고 검었다."], ["All through the cold and restless interval, until dawn, they once more whispered in the ears of Mr.", "동이 트기까지 춥고 불안한 그 시간 내내, 그것들은 또 한번 자비스 로리 씨의 귓가에 속삭였다."], ["Jarvis Lorry--sitting opposite the buried man who had been dug out, and wondering what subtle powers were for ever lost to him, and what were capable of restoration--the old inquiry:", "파묻혀 있다가 파헤쳐진 그 사람을 맞은편에 두고 앉아, 그에게서 어떤 미묘한 능력들이 영영 사라져버렸으며 또 어떤 능력들이 회복될 수 있을지 헤아리는 그에게, 그 오래된 물음을."]], [["“I hope you care to be recalled to life?”", "“되살아나기를 바라시오?”"]], [["And the old answer:", "그리고 그 오래된 대답을."]], [["“I can’t say.”", "“모르겠소.”"]], [["# Book the Second--the Golden Thread CHAPTER I. Five Years Later", "# Book the Second--the Golden Thread CHAPTER I. Five Years Later"]], [["Tellson’s Bank by Temple Bar was an old-fashioned place, even in the year one thousand seven hundred and eighty.", "템플바 근처의 텔슨 은행은 1780년에도 이미 구식 건물이었다."], ["It was very small, very dark, very ugly, very incommodious.", "무척 비좁고, 무척 어둡고, 무척 흉하고, 무척 불편했다."], ["It was an old-fashioned place, moreover, in the moral attribute that the partners in the House were proud of its smallness, proud of its darkness, proud of its ugliness, proud of its incommodiousness.", "게다가 도덕적인 면에서도 구식이었으니, 그 은행의 동업자들은 그 비좁음을 자랑스러워했고, 그 어두움을 자랑스러워했고, 그 흉함을 자랑스러워했고, 그 불편함을 자랑스러워했다."], ["They were even boastful of its eminence in those particulars, and were fired by an express conviction that, if it were less objectionable, it would be less respectable.", "그들은 그런 점에서 자기네가 두드러진다는 것을 자랑하기까지 했으며, 만약 그것이 덜 거슬렸다면 덜 존경받았으리라는 명백한 확신에 불타올랐다."], ["This was no passive belief, but an active weapon which they flashed at more convenient places of business.", "이것은 수동적인 신념이 아니라 그들이 더 편리한 영업장들을 향해 번뜩이는 능동적인 무기였다."], ["Tellson’s (they said) wanted no elbow-room, Tellson’s wanted no light, Tellson’s wanted no embellishment.", "텔슨은 (그들 말로는) 팔꿈치를 펼 공간을 바라지 않았고, 텔슨은 빛을 바라지 않았고, 텔슨은 장식을 바라지 않았다."], ["Noakes and Co.’s might, or Snooks Brothers’ might; but Tellson’s, thank Heaven--!", "노우크스 상회는 그럴지 몰라도, 스누크스 형제 상회는 그럴지 몰라도, 텔슨은, 천만다행으로 — !"]], [["Any one of these partners would have disinherited his son on the question of rebuilding Tellson’s.", "이 동업자들 가운데 누구라도 텔슨을 다시 짓는 문제를 두고서라면 제 아들을 상속에서 폐했을 것이다."], ["In this respect the House was much on a par with the Country; which did very often disinherit its sons for suggesting improvements in laws and customs that had long been highly objectionable, but were only the more respectable.", "이 점에서 그 은행은 이 나라와 다를 바가 없었으니, 이 나라 역시 오래도록 몹시 거슬렸으나 그저 그래서 더욱 존경받기만 하던 법과 관습을 개선하자고 제안하는 아들들을 매우 자주 상속에서 폐하곤 했던 것이다."]], [["Thus it had come to pass, that Tellson’s was the triumphant perfection of inconvenience.", "이렇게 해서 텔슨은 불편함의 의기양양한 완성품이 되어 있었다."], ["After bursting open a door of idiotic obstinacy with a weak rattle in its throat, you fell into Tellson’s down two steps, and came to your senses in a miserable little shop, with two little counters, where the oldest of men made your cheque shake as if the wind rustled it, while they examined the signature by the dingiest of windows, which were always under a shower-bath of mud from Fleet-street, and which were made the dingier by their own iron bars proper, and the heavy shadow of Temple Bar.", "목구멍에서 가냘픈 덜컹 소리를 내는, 멍청하리만치 완강한 문을 박차고 들어가면, 두 계단 아래로 텔슨 안에 떨어졌고, 작은 계산대가 두 개 놓인 초라한 작은 가게 안에서 정신을 차리게 되었는데, 그곳에서는 가장 늙은 노인들이 가장 침침한 창문 곁에서 당신의 수표 서명을 검사했고, 그들이 들고 있는 수표는 마치 바람이 스치는 양 흔들렸으니, 그 창문들은 늘 플리트가에서 날아드는 진흙의 소나기를 뒤집어쓰고 있었고, 거기 박힌 철창과 템플바의 무거운 그림자 탓에 한층 더 침침했다."], ["If your business necessitated your seeing “the House,” you were put into a species of Condemned Hold at the back, where you meditated on a misspent life, until the House came with its hands in its pockets, and you could hardly blink at it in the dismal twilight.", "만약 볼일 때문에 \"은행 측\"을 만나야 한다면, 당신은 뒤편의 일종의 사형수 감방 같은 곳으로 끌려가, 은행 측이 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 나타날 때까지 헛되이 보낸 인생을 곱씹게 되는데, 그 음울한 어스름 속에서 당신은 좀처럼 눈도 깜박이기 어려웠다."], ["Your money came out of, or went into, wormy old wooden drawers, particles of which flew up your nose and down your throat when they were opened and shut.", "당신의 돈은 벌레 먹은 낡은 나무 서랍에서 나오거나 그 안으로 들어갔는데, 그것들을 여닫을 때면 부스러기가 당신의 코로 날아들고 목구멍으로 넘어갔다."], ["Your bank-notes had a musty odour, as if they were fast decomposing into rags again.", "당신의 지폐에서는 곰팡내가 났으니, 마치 다시 누더기로 빠르게 썩어가는 듯했다."], ["Your plate was stowed away among the neighbouring cesspools, and evil communications corrupted its good polish in a day or two.", "당신의 은그릇은 이웃한 오물 구덩이들 틈에 처박혀, 나쁜 교제가 그 고운 광택을 하루이틀 새 망쳐놓았다."], ["Your deeds got into extemporised strong-rooms made of kitchens and sculleries, and fretted all the fat out of their parchments into the banking-house air.", "당신의 증서들은 부엌과 설거지방을 임시변통으로 만든 금고실로 들어가, 그 양피지의 기름기를 죄다 은행 안 공기 속으로 졸여 보냈다."], ["Your lighter boxes of family papers went up-stairs into a Barmecide room, that always had a great dining-table in it and never had a dinner, and where, even in the year one thousand seven hundred and eighty, the first letters written to you by your old love, or by your little children, were but newly released from the horror of being ogled through the windows, by the heads exposed on Temple Bar with an insensate brutality and ferocity worthy of Abyssinia or Ashantee.", "가족 서류가 든 좀 더 가벼운 상자들은 위층의 바르메시드 방으로 올라갔는데, 거기엔 늘 커다란 식탁이 놓여 있었으나 한 번도 식사가 차려진 적은 없었고, 1780년에 이르러서도, 옛 연인이나 어린 자식들이 당신에게 처음 써 보낸 편지들이 창밖으로 노려보이던 공포에서 갓 풀려난 참이었으니, 그 창밖에서는 템플바에 효수된 머리들이, 아비시니아나 아샨티에나 어울릴 법한 무감각한 잔혹함과 흉포함으로 그것들을 곁눈질하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["But indeed, at that time, putting to death was a recipe much in vogue with all trades and professions, and not least of all with Tellson’s.", "* 바르메시드 방: 『아라비안나이트』에서 바르메시드 가문의 한 사람이 빈 접시로 거지에게 가짜 잔치를 베푼 이야기에서 유래한 표현으로, 실속 없이 형식만 갖춘 것을 뜻한다."], ["Death is Nature’s remedy for all things, and why not Legislation’s?", "그러나 실로 그 시절에는, 죽음에 처하는 것이 온갖 직종과 직업에서 크게 유행하던 처방이었고, 텔슨도 결코 예외가 아니었다."], ["Accordingly, the forger was put to Death; the utterer of a bad note was put to Death; the unlawful opener of a letter was put to Death; the purloiner of forty shillings and sixpence was put to Death; the holder of a horse at Tellson’s door, who made off with it, was put to Death; the coiner of a bad shilling was put to Death; the sounders of three-fourths of the notes in the whole gamut of Crime, were put to Death.", "죽음은 만물에 대한 자연의 치유책이거늘, 어찌 입법의 치유책이 되지 못하겠는가? 그리하여 위조범은 죽음에 처해졌고, 부정 어음을 유통한 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 불법으로 편지를 뜯은 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 사십 실링 육 펜스를 훔친 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 텔슨 문 앞에서 말을 맡아두었다가 그것을 끌고 달아난 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 가짜 실링을 만든 자는 죽음에 처해졌고, 범죄의 온 음계 가운데 사분의 삼에 해당하는 음을 울린 자들은 죽음에 처해졌다."], ["Not that it did the least good in the way of prevention--it might almost have been worth remarking that the fact was exactly the reverse--but, it cleared off (as to this world) the trouble of each particular case, and left nothing else connected with it to be looked after.", "그렇다고 그것이 예방의 면에서 조금이라도 효험이 있었던 것은 아니다 — 오히려 사실은 정반대였다고 짚어둘 만하기까지 했다 — 다만, 그것은 (이 세상의 일로 치자면) 각 사건의 골칫거리를 말끔히 치웠고, 그와 관련해 더 살펴야 할 다른 일은 아무것도 남기지 않았다."], ["Thus, Tellson’s, in its day, like greater places of business, its contemporaries, had taken so many lives, that, if the heads laid low before it had been ranged on Temple Bar instead of being privately disposed of, they would probably have excluded what little light the ground floor had, in a rather significant manner.", "이렇게 해서 텔슨은 그 시절에, 같은 시대의 더 큰 영업장들과 마찬가지로, 그토록 많은 목숨을 거두었으니, 만약 그 앞에서 베어진 머리들이 은밀히 처리되는 대신 템플바에 줄지어 걸렸더라면, 아마도 일층에 들던 그 얼마 안 되는 빛마저 꽤나 의미심장한 방식으로 가로막았을 것이다."]], [["Cramped in all kinds of dim cupboards and hutches at Tellson’s, the oldest of men carried on the business gravely.", "텔슨 안의 온갖 침침한 벽장과 칸막이 우리 속에 갇힌 채, 가장 늙은 노인들이 엄숙하게 영업을 이어갔다."], ["When they took a young man into Tellson’s London house, they hid him somewhere till he was old.", "텔슨의 런던 본점에 젊은이를 받아들일 때면, 그가 늙을 때까지 어딘가에 숨겨두었다."], ["They kept him in a dark place, like a cheese, until he had the full Tellson flavour and blue-mould upon him.", "그가 텔슨다운 풍미를 흠뻑 갖추고 그 위에 푸른곰팡이가 피어날 때까지, 마치 치즈처럼 어두운 곳에 보관해두었다."], ["Then only was he permitted to be seen, spectacularly poring over large books, and casting his breeches and gaiters into the general weight of the establishment.", "그제야 비로소 그는 사람들 눈에 띄도록 허락받아, 커다란 장부를 들여다보며 거창하게 일하고, 자신의 반바지와 각반을 그 은행의 전체 무게에 보태었다."]], [["Outside Tellson’s--never by any means in it, unless called in--was an odd-job-man, an occasional porter and messenger, who served as the live sign of the house.", "텔슨 밖에는 — 불려 들어가지 않는 한 결코 안으로는 들어가지 못했다 — 잡일꾼 하나가 있었으니, 이따금 짐꾼이며 심부름꾼 노릇을 하던 그는 그 은행의 살아 있는 간판 구실을 했다."], ["He was never absent during business hours, unless upon an errand, and then he was represented by his son: a grisly urchin of twelve, who was his express image.", "그는 심부름을 가지 않는 한 영업시간 동안 한 번도 자리를 비우지 않았고, 심부름을 갈 때면 제 아들이 그를 대신했으니, 그 아들은 열두 살 난 음산한 개구쟁이로 아비를 그대로 빼다 박은 모습이었다."], ["People understood that Tellson’s, in a stately way, tolerated the odd-job-man.", "사람들은 텔슨이 위엄 있는 태도로 그 잡일꾼을 너그러이 봐준다고 이해했다."], ["The house had always tolerated some person in that capacity, and time and tide had drifted this person to the post.", "그 은행은 늘 누군가를 그런 자격으로 너그러이 봐주어 왔고, 세월의 흐름이 이 사람을 그 자리로 떠밀어 온 것이었다."], ["His surname was Cruncher, and on the youthful occasion of his renouncing by proxy the works of darkness, in the easterly parish church of Hounsditch, he had received the added appellation of Jerry.", "그의 성은 크런처였고, 어린 시절 하운즈디치의 동쪽 교구 교회에서 대리인을 통해 어둠의 일들을 버리겠노라 서약하던 때에, 제리라는 이름을 더 얻었다."]], [["The scene was Mr.", "* 어둠의 일들을 버리겠노라 서약: 영국 국교회 세례식에서 대부·대모가 유아를 대신해 악마와 그 소행을 끊겠다고 맹세하는 절차를 가리킨다."], ["Cruncher’s private lodging in Hanging-sword-alley, Whitefriars: the time, half-past seven of the clock on a windy March morning, Anno Domini seventeen hundred and eighty.", "무대는 화이트프라이어스의 행잉스워드 골목에 있는 크런처 씨의 사사로운 거처였고, 때는 1780년 어느 바람 부는 삼월 아침 일곱시 반이었다."], ["(Mr. Cruncher himself always spoke of the year of our Lord as Anna Dominoes: apparently under the impression that the Christian era dated from the invention of a popular game, by a lady who had bestowed her name upon it.)", "(크런처 씨 본인은 주님의 해라는 말을 늘 \"주님의 도미노\"라고 했는데, 보아하니 그리스도교 기원이 어떤 부인이 자기 이름을 붙여 만든 인기 놀이의 발명에서 비롯되었다고 여기는 듯했다.)"]], [["Mr. Cruncher’s apartments were not in a savoury neighbourhood, and were but two in number, even if a closet with a single pane of glass in it might be counted as one.", "* 주님의 해: 라틴어 Anno Domini를 가리킨다."], ["But they were very decently kept.", "크런처는 이를 발음이 비슷한 Anna Dominoes(애나 도미노)로 잘못 알아들어 도미노 놀이와 연관 짓고 있다. 크런처 씨의 거처는 향기로운 동네에 있지 않았고, 유리 한 장 끼운 벽장을 하나로 친다 해도 방이 둘뿐이었다."], ["Early as it was, on the windy March morning, the room in which he lay abed was already scrubbed throughout; and between the cups and saucers arranged for breakfast, and the lumbering deal table, a very clean white cloth was spread.", "그러나 무척 단정하게 관리되고 있었다. 바람 부는 삼월 아침, 이른 시각인데도 그가 누워 있는 방은 이미 구석구석 닦여 있었고, 아침 식사를 위해 차려놓은 잔과 받침 사이에는, 그리고 투박한 전나무 식탁 위에는 무척 깨끗한 흰 식탁보가 펼쳐져 있었다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher reposed under a patchwork counterpane, like a Harlequin at home.", "크런처 씨는 마치 집에 있는 어릿광대처럼 조각보 이불 아래에 누워 쉬고 있었다."], ["At first, he slept heavily, but, by degrees, began to roll and surge in bed, until he rose above the surface, with his spiky hair looking as if it must tear the sheets to ribbons.", "처음에는 곤히 잠들었으나, 차츰 침대에서 몸을 굴리고 뒤척이기 시작하더니, 마침내 수면 위로 솟아올랐는데, 그 뻣뻣하게 곤두선 머리칼은 흡사 침대보를 갈가리 찢어놓을 듯했다."], ["At which juncture, he exclaimed, in a voice of dire exasperation:", "바로 그 순간 그는 끔찍하게 격분한 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Bust me, if she ain’t at it agin!”", "\"빌어먹을, 저 여편네가 또 저 짓을 하고 있잖아!\""]], [["A woman of orderly and industrious appearance rose from her knees in a corner, with sufficient haste and trepidation to show that she was the person referred to.", "정돈되고 부지런해 보이는 한 여인이 구석에서 무릎을 꿇고 있다가 일어섰는데, 그 황급하고 떨리는 기색만으로도 자신이 바로 그 지목된 사람임이 충분히 드러났다."]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, looking out of bed for a boot.", "크런처 씨가 부츠를 찾으려고 침대 밖을 내다보며 말했다."], ["“You’re at it agin, are you?”", "\"또 그 짓이지, 그렇지?\""]], [["After hailing the morn with this second salutation, he threw a boot at the woman as a third.", "이 두번째 인사로 아침을 맞이한 뒤, 그는 세번째 인사 삼아 여인에게 부츠 한 짝을 집어던졌다."], ["It was a very muddy boot, and may introduce the odd circumstance connected with Mr.", "그것은 진흙투성이 부츠였는데, 여기서 크런처 씨의 집안 살림과 얽힌 묘한 사정 하나를 소개할 만하다."], ["Cruncher’s domestic economy, that, whereas he often came home after banking hours with clean boots, he often got up next morning to find the same boots covered with clay.", "곧, 그는 은행 영업이 끝난 뒤 깨끗한 부츠를 신고 귀가하는 일이 흔했는데, 이튿날 아침에 일어나 보면 바로 그 부츠가 진흙범벅이 되어 있는 일이 흔했다는 것이다."]], [["“What,” said Mr.", "\"뭐,\""], ["Cruncher, varying his apostrophe after missing his mark--“what are you up to, Aggerawayter?”", "표적을 빗맞힌 뒤 호칭을 바꾸어 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"뭘 하는 거야, 이 악담쟁이야?\""]], [["“I was only saying my prayers.”", "\"그저 기도를 드리고 있었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Saying your prayers!", "\"기도를 드린다고!"], ["You’re a nice woman!", "잘난 여편네일세!"], ["What do you mean by flopping yourself down and praying agin me?”", "무슨 셈으로 털썩 엎드려서 나한테 맞서 기도를 한단 말이야?\""]], [["“I was not praying against you; I was praying for you.”", "\"당신한테 맞서 기도한 게 아니에요. 당신을 위해 기도했어요.\""]], [["“You weren’t.", "\"아니지."], ["And if you were, I won’t be took the liberty with.", "설령 그랬다 해도, 나는 그런 참견은 사양하겠어."], ["Here!", "야!"], ["your mother’s a nice woman, young Jerry, going a praying agin your father’s prosperity.", "네 어미가 잘난 여편네다, 어린 제리야, 글쎄 네 아비의 번영에 맞서 기도를 하고 있구나."], ["You’ve got a dutiful mother, you have, my son.", "너한테는 효심 깊은 어미가 있구나, 아들아."], ["You’ve got a religious mother, you have, my boy: going and flopping herself down, and praying that the bread-and-butter may be snatched out of the mouth of her only child.”", "너한테는 신앙심 깊은 어미가 있구나, 얘야. 글쎄 털썩 엎드려서, 하나뿐인 제 자식의 입에서 버터 바른 빵을 낚아채 가게 해달라고 기도를 하고 있다니.\""]], [["Master Cruncher (who was in his shirt) took this very ill, and, turning to his mother, strongly deprecated any praying away of his personal board.", "(셔츠 바람이던) 크런처 도련님은 이를 무척 언짢게 받아들이고는, 어머니 쪽으로 돌아서서 제 끼니가 기도로 날아가는 일에 강력히 반대했다."]], [["“And what do you suppose, you conceited female,” said Mr.", "\"그래 도대체 네 생각엔, 이 우쭐대는 여편네야,\""], ["Cruncher, with unconscious inconsistency, “that the worth of your prayers may be?", "크런처 씨가 무심결에 앞뒤 안 맞는 소리로 말했다. \"네 기도의 값어치가 얼마나 될 것 같으냐?"], ["Name the price that you put your prayers at!”", "네 기도에 매기는 값을 한번 대 봐!\""]], [["“They only come from the heart, Jerry.", "\"제 기도는 그저 마음에서 우러나는 것일 뿐이에요, 제리."], ["They are worth no more than that.”", "그 이상의 값어치는 없어요.\""]], [["“Worth no more than that,”", "\"그 이상의 값어치는 없다고,\""], ["repeated Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 따라 말했다."], ["“They ain’t worth much, then.", "\"그렇다면 별 값어치도 없겠군."], ["Whether or no, I won’t be prayed agin, I tell you.", "어찌 됐든, 내게 맞서는 기도는 받지 않겠어, 똑똑히 일러두는데."], ["I can’t afford it.", "나는 그럴 형편이 못 돼."], ["I’m not a going to be made unlucky by your sneaking.", "네 슬그머니 하는 짓 탓에 운수 사나워질 생각은 없단 말이야."], ["If you must go flopping yourself down, flop in favour of your husband and child, and not in opposition to ’em.", "굳이 털썩 엎드려야겠다면, 네 남편과 자식을 위해 엎드려라, 그들에게 맞서지 말고."], ["If I had had any but a unnat’ral wife, and this poor boy had had any but a unnat’ral mother, I might have made some money last week instead of being counter-prayed and countermined and religiously circumwented into the worst of luck.", "만약 내게 거꾸로 된 마누라만 아니었고, 이 가엾은 녀석한테 거꾸로 된 어미만 아니었더라면, 나는 지난주에 돈 좀 만질 수도 있었을 텐데, 맞기도질에 시달리고, 음모에 휘말리고, 신앙심에 에둘러져서 최악의 운수로 끌려가고 말았지."], ["B-u-u-ust me!” said Mr.", "빌-어-먹-을!\""], ["Cruncher, who all this time had been putting on his clothes, “if I ain’t, what with piety and one blowed thing and another, been choused this last week into as bad luck as ever a poor devil of a honest tradesman met with!", "이러는 내내 옷을 입고 있던 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"신앙이니 뭐니 별의별 빌어먹을 일들 탓에, 지난주에 정직한 장사꾼 가엾은 놈팡이가 일찍이 당해 본 적 없을 만큼 지독한 불운으로 내가 속아 넘어간 게 아니라면 말이지!"], ["Young Jerry, dress yourself, my boy, and while I clean my boots keep a eye upon your mother now and then, and if you see any signs of more flopping, give me a call. For, I tell you,”", "어린 제리야, 옷 입어라, 얘야, 그리고 내가 부츠를 닦는 동안 이따금 네 어미를 지켜보다가, 또 엎드릴 기미가 보이거든 나를 불러라."], ["here he addressed his wife once more, “I won’t be gone agin, in this manner.", "왜냐하면, 똑똑히 일러두는데,\" 여기서 그는 다시 한번 아내에게 말을 건넸다."], ["I am as rickety as a hackney-coach, I’m as sleepy as laudanum, my lines is strained to that degree that I shouldn’t know, if it wasn’t for the pain in ’em, which was me and which somebody else, yet I’m none the better for it in pocket; and it’s my suspicion that you’ve been at it from morning to night to prevent me from being the better for it in pocket, and I won’t put up with it, Aggerawayter, and what do you say now!”", "\"이런 식으로 또 당하지는 않겠어. 나는 삯마차처럼 삐걱거리고, 아편팅크처럼 졸리고, 살갗은 어찌나 당기는지, 그 아픔이 아니었더라면 어디까지가 나고 어디부터가 딴 사람인지도 모를 지경인데, 그래 봤자 주머니 사정은 조금도 나아진 게 없으니, 내가 의심하기로는 네가 아침부터 밤까지 그 짓을 해대며 내 주머니 사정이 나아지는 걸 막아 온 거야, 그러니 나는 그냥 참고 있지 않겠다, 이 악담쟁이야, 그래 이제 뭐라 할 테냐!\""]], [["Growling, in addition, such phrases as “Ah!", "여기에 더해 \"아! 그래!"], ["yes! You’re religious, too.", "너도 신앙심이 깊으시지."], ["You wouldn’t put yourself in opposition to the interests of your husband and child, would you?", "설마 네 남편과 자식의 이익에 맞서기야 하겠어, 안 그래?"], ["Not you!”", "천만에!\""], ["and throwing off other sarcastic sparks from the whirling grindstone of his indignation, Mr. Cruncher betook himself to his boot-cleaning and his general preparation for business.", "따위의 말을 으르렁대고, 빙빙 도는 제 분노의 숫돌에서 다른 비아냥의 불꽃들도 튀겨내면서, 크런처 씨는 부츠 닦기에, 그리고 그날의 영업을 위한 전반적인 채비에 들어갔다."], ["In the meantime, his son, whose head was garnished with tenderer spikes, and whose young eyes stood close by one another, as his father’s did, kept the required watch upon his mother.", "그동안, 좀 더 보드라운 가시 같은 머리칼로 머리가 장식되고, 아비처럼 두 어린 눈이 서로 바싹 붙어 있는 그 아들은, 어머니를 향해 시키는 대로 망을 보았다."], ["He greatly disturbed that poor woman at intervals, by darting out of his sleeping closet, where he made his toilet, with a suppressed cry of “You are going to flop, mother.", "그는 몸단장을 하던 잠자는 벽장에서 \"엎드리려 하시네, 어머니. — 이봐요, 아버지!\""], ["--Halloa, father!” and, after raising this fictitious alarm, darting in again with an undutiful grin.", "하고 억눌린 외침과 함께 불쑥 뛰쳐나와, 이 거짓 경보를 울린 뒤 불효막심한 히죽거림을 지으며 다시 뛰어들어가, 이따금 그 가엾은 여인을 크게 어지럽혔다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher’s temper was not at all improved when he came to his breakfast.", "크런처 씨의 기분은 아침 식사 자리에 이르러서도 조금도 나아지지 않았다."], ["He resented Mrs. Cruncher’s saying grace with particular animosity.", "그는 크런처 부인이 식전 기도를 올리는 것에 각별한 적의를 품고 분개했다."]], [["“Now, Aggerawayter!", "\"이봐, 악담쟁이야!"], ["What are you up to?", "뭘 하는 거야?"], ["At it again?”", "또 그 짓이야?\""]], [["His wife explained that she had merely “asked a blessing.”", "아내는 그저 \"축복을 빌었을\" 뿐이라고 설명했다."]], [["“Don’t do it!”", "\"하지 마!\""], ["said Mr. Crunches looking about, as if he rather expected to see the loaf disappear under the efficacy of his wife’s petitions.", "크런처 씨가 두리번거리며 말했는데, 마치 아내의 간청이 효험을 발휘해 빵 덩어리가 사라지기라도 할까 봐 살피는 듯했다."], ["“I ain’t a going to be blest out of house and home.", "\"나는 기도질에 집도 살림도 다 날릴 생각이 없어."], ["I won’t have my wittles blest off my table.", "내 음식이 식탁에서 축복으로 날아가게 두지 않겠어."], ["Keep still!”", "가만히 있어!\""]], [["Exceedingly red-eyed and grim, as if he had been up all night at a party which had taken anything but a convivial turn, Jerry Cruncher worried his breakfast rather than ate it, growling over it like any four-footed inmate of a menagerie.", "밤새도록 흥겨움과는 거리가 먼 방향으로 흘러간 어떤 잔치에 끼어 있기라도 했던 양 두 눈이 몹시 충혈되고 험상궂은 얼굴로, 제리 크런처는 아침을 먹는다기보다 들볶았으니, 동물원의 네발짐승처럼 그 위에서 으르렁댔다."], ["Towards nine o’clock he smoothed his ruffled aspect, and, presenting as respectable and business-like an exterior as he could overlay his natural self with, issued forth to the occupation of the day.", "아홉시가 가까워지자 그는 헝클어진 인상을 가다듬고, 타고난 제 모습 위에 덧입힐 수 있는 한 가장 존경스럽고 사무적인 외양을 차린 채, 그날의 일거리를 향해 나섰다."]], [["It could scarcely be called a trade, in spite of his favourite description of himself as “a honest tradesman.”", "스스로를 즐겨 \"정직한 장사꾼\"이라 일컫긴 했으나, 그것은 도무지 장사라 부르기 어려웠다."], ["His stock consisted of a wooden stool, made out of a broken-backed chair cut down, which stool, young Jerry, walking at his father’s side, carried every morning to beneath the banking-house window that was nearest Temple Bar: where, with the addition of the first handful of straw that could be gleaned from any passing vehicle to keep the cold and wet from the odd-job-man’s feet, it formed the encampment for the day.", "그의 밑천이라곤 등받이가 부러진 의자를 잘라 만든 나무 걸상 하나가 전부였는데, 그 걸상을 어린 제리가 아비 곁에서 걸으며 매일 아침 템플바에서 가장 가까운 은행 창문 아래로 날라다 놓았다. 거기에, 지나가는 아무 마차에서나 주워 모을 수 있는 첫 한 줌의 짚을 더해 잡일꾼의 발을 추위와 습기로부터 막으면, 그것이 그날의 진지가 되었다."], ["On this post of his, Mr. Cruncher was as well known to Fleet-street and the Temple, as the Bar itself,--and was almost as in-looking.", "이 자기 자리에서 크런처 씨는 플리트가와 템플 일대에 템플바 그 자체만큼이나 잘 알려져 있었고 — 그만큼이나 험상궂어 보였다."]], [["Encamped at a quarter before nine, in good time to touch his three-cornered hat to the oldest of men as they passed in to Tellson’s, Jerry took up his station on this windy March morning, with young Jerry standing by him, when not engaged in making forays through the Bar, to inflict bodily and mental injuries of an acute description on passing boys who were small enough for his amiable purpose.", "아홉시 십오분 전에 진지를 차려, 노인들이 텔슨으로 들어갈 때 그 삼각모에 손을 갖다 대 인사할 만큼 넉넉한 시간에, 제리는 바람 부는 이 삼월 아침의 자기 위치에 자리를 잡았고, 어린 제리도 그 곁에 서 있었으니, 다만 어린 제리는 템플바 안팎으로 출격해, 제 흐뭇한 목적에 알맞을 만큼 자그마한 지나가는 사내아이들에게 심신에 격렬한 상처를 입히지 않을 때만 그러했다."], ["Father and son, extremely like each other, looking silently on at the morning traffic in Fleet-street, with their two heads as near to one another as the two eyes of each were, bore a considerable resemblance to a pair of monkeys.", "서로 몹시 닮은 부자가, 저마다 두 눈이 서로 가까이 붙어 있듯 두 머리를 서로 바싹 맞대고서, 플리트가의 아침 통행을 말없이 바라보는 모습은 원숭이 한 쌍과 상당히 닮아 보였다."], ["The resemblance was not lessened by the accidental circumstance, that the mature Jerry bit and spat out straw, while the twinkling eyes of the youthful Jerry were as restlessly watchful of him as of everything else in Fleet-street.", "어른 제리가 짚을 씹다가 뱉어내고, 어린 제리의 반짝이는 두 눈이 플리트가의 온갖 것을 살피듯 그를 쉴 새 없이 살피던 우연한 사정도 그 닮음을 덜어주지 못했다."]], [["The head of one of the regular indoor messengers attached to Tellson’s establishment was put through the door, and the word was given:", "텔슨 본점에 딸린 정규 내부 심부름꾼 하나가 문틈으로 고개를 내밀고 일렀다."]], [["“Porter wanted!”", "\"짐꾼 부른다!\""]], [["“Hooray, father!", "\"만세, 아버지!"], ["Here’s an early job to begin with!”", "시작부터 이른 일거리예요!\""]], [["Having thus given his parent God speed, young Jerry seated himself on the stool, entered on his reversionary interest in the straw his father had been chewing, and cogitated.", "이렇게 아비에게 잘 다녀오라 빈 뒤, 어린 제리는 걸상에 걸터앉아, 아비가 씹던 짚에 대한 상속권을 행사하며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Al-ways rusty!", "\"늘-언제나 녹투성이야!"], ["His fingers is al-ways rusty!”", "아버지 손가락은 늘-언제나 녹투성이라니까!\""], ["muttered young Jerry.", "어린 제리가 중얼거렸다."], ["“Where does my father get all that iron rust from?", "\"우리 아버지는 대체 저 쇠 녹을 다 어디서 묻혀 오는 거지?"], ["He don’t get no iron rust here!”", "여기서는 쇠 녹이 묻을 데가 없는데!\""]], [["# CHAPTER II. A Sight", "# CHAPTER II. A Sight"]], [["“You know the Old Bailey well, no doubt?”", "\"올드 베일리는 잘 알고 있겠지, 분명히?\""], ["said one of the oldest of clerks to Jerry the messenger.", "가장 나이 든 사무원 가운데 하나가 심부름꾼 제리에게 말했다."]], [["“Ye-es, sir,”", "* 올드 베일리(Old Bailey): 런던의 중앙형사재판소."], ["returned Jerry, in something of a dogged manner.", "\"네에, 그렇습니다요.\" 제리가 다소 무뚝뚝한 투로 대꾸했다."], ["“I do know the Bailey.”", "\"베일리야 잘 알지요.\""]], [["“Just so.", "\"그래."], ["And you know Mr. Lorry.”", "그리고 로리 씨도 알지.\""]], [["“I know Mr.", "\"로리 씨는 베일리보다 훨씬 잘 압니다요, 나리."], ["Lorry, sir, much better than I know the Bailey. Much better,”", "정직한 장사꾼으로서 베일리를 알고 싶은 정도보다 훨씬 더 잘 알지요.\""], ["said Jerry, not unlike a reluctant witness at the establishment in question, “than I, as a honest tradesman, wish to know the Bailey.”", "제리가 마치 그 재판소에 끌려 나온 마지못한 증인이라도 되는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋아."], ["Find the door where the witnesses go in, and show the door-keeper this note for Mr.", "증인들이 들어가는 문을 찾아서, 문지기에게 로리 씨에게 보내는 이 쪽지를 보여주게."], ["Lorry. He will then let you in.”", "그럼 안으로 들여보내 줄 거야.\""]], [["“Into the court, sir?”", "\"법정 안으로 말입니까요, 나리?\""]], [["“Into the court.”", "\"법정 안으로.\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher’s eyes seemed to get a little closer to one another, and to interchange the inquiry, “What do you think of this?”", "크런처 씨의 두 눈은 서로 조금 가까워지면서, \"이걸 어떻게 생각해?\"라는 질문을 주고받는 듯했다."]], [["“Am I to wait in the court, sir?”", "\"법정 안에서 기다려야 합니까요, 나리?\""], ["he asked, as the result of that conference.", "그 협의의 결과로 그가 물었다."]], [["“I am going to tell you.", "\"이제 말해 주겠네."], ["The door-keeper will pass the note to Mr. Lorry, and do you make any gesture that will attract Mr.", "문지기가 쪽지를 로리 씨에게 전할 테니, 자네는 로리 씨의 주의를 끌 만한 몸짓을 해서 자네가 서 있는 곳을 알려주게."], ["Lorry’s attention, and show him where you stand. Then what you have to do, is, to remain there until he wants you.”", "그런 다음에 자네가 할 일은, 그분이 자네를 찾을 때까지 거기 그대로 있는 것일세.\""]], [["“Is that all, sir?”", "\"그게 전부입니까요, 나리?\""]], [["“That’s all.", "\"그게 전부야."], ["He wishes to have a messenger at hand.", "그분은 가까이에 심부름꾼을 두고 싶어 하셔."], ["This is to tell him you are there.”", "이건 자네가 거기 있다는 걸 알리려는 거야.\""]], [["As the ancient clerk deliberately folded and superscribed the note, Mr. Cruncher, after surveying him in silence until he came to the blotting-paper stage, remarked:", "나이 든 사무원이 천천히 쪽지를 접어 겉봉에 글씨를 쓰는 동안, 크런처 씨는 그가 압지를 대는 단계에 이를 때까지 잠자코 그를 살펴보다가 한마디 했다."]], [["“I suppose they’ll be trying Forgeries this morning?”", "\"오늘 아침엔 위조죄 재판을 하겠지요?\""]], [["“Treason!”", "\"반역죄야!\""]], [["“That’s quartering,”", "\"그건 사지를 찢는 거잖습니까.\""], ["said Jerry.", "제리가 말했다."], ["“Barbarous!”", "\"야만적이네요!\""]], [["“It is the law,”", "\"그게 법이야.\""], ["remarked the ancient clerk, turning his surprised spectacles upon him.", "나이 든 사무원이 놀란 안경알을 그에게 돌리며 말했다."], ["“It is the law.”", "\"그게 법이라고.\""]], [["“It’s hard in the law to spile a man, I think.", "\"사람을 그렇게 망쳐놓는 건 법으로도 너무하다 싶은데요."], ["It’s hard enough to kill him, but it’s wery hard to spile him, sir.”", "죽이는 것만 해도 충분히 가혹한데, 그렇게 망쳐놓는 건 정말 너무합니다요, 나리.\""]], [["“Not at all,”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않네.\""], ["retained the ancient clerk.", "나이 든 사무원이 받아쳤다."], ["“Speak well of the law.", "\"법을 좋게 말하게."], ["Take care of your chest and voice, my good friend, and leave the law to take care of itself.", "여보게, 자네 가슴과 목소리나 잘 챙기고, 법은 알아서 제 앞가림을 하도록 내버려 두게."], ["I give you that advice.”", "내가 그렇게 충고하겠네.\""]], [["“It’s the damp, sir, what settles on my chest and voice,”", "\"제 가슴과 목소리에 내려앉는 건 습기랍니다요, 나리.\""], ["said Jerry.", "제리가 말했다."], ["“I leave you to judge what a damp way of earning a living mine is.”", "\"제가 얼마나 습한 방식으로 벌어먹고 사는지는 나리께서 판단하시지요.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"자, 자.\""], ["said the old clerk; “we all have our various ways of gaining a livelihood.", "나이 든 사무원이 말했다. \"우리 모두 저마다 살아가는 방식이 다른 법이지."], ["Some of us have damp ways, and some of us have dry ways.", "누구는 습한 방식이고, 누구는 마른 방식이고."], ["Here is the letter.", "여기 편지 있네."], ["Go along.”", "어서 가게.\""]], [["Jerry took the letter, and, remarking to himself with less internal deference than he made an outward show of, “You are a lean old one, too,”", "제리는 편지를 받아 들고는, 겉으로 드러낸 것보다 속으로는 덜 공손하게 \"당신도 비쩍 마른 늙은이로군\""], ["made his bow, informed his son, in passing, of his destination, and went his way.", "하고 혼잣말을 하면서 인사를 하고, 지나가는 길에 아들에게 자기가 갈 곳을 알려준 뒤 제 길을 갔다."]], [["They hanged at Tyburn, in those days, so the street outside Newgate had not obtained one infamous notoriety that has since attached to it.", "그 시절에는 타이번에서 교수형을 집행했으므로, 뉴게이트 바깥의 거리는 훗날 그곳에 따라붙게 된 악명을 아직 얻지 않았다."], ["But, the gaol was a vile place, in which most kinds of debauchery and villainy were practised, and where dire diseases were bred, that came into court with the prisoners, and sometimes rushed straight from the dock at my Lord Chief Justice himself, and pulled him off the bench.", "그러나 그 감옥은 온갖 방탕과 악행이 자행되는 더러운 곳이었고, 그곳에서 무서운 질병들이 자라나 죄수들과 함께 법정으로 들어왔으며, 때로는 피고석에서 곧장 대법관 각하에게 달려들어 그를 자리에서 끌어내리기도 했다."], ["It had more than once happened, that the Judge in the black cap pronounced his own doom as certainly as the prisoner’s, and even died before him.", "검은 모자를 쓴 판사가 죄수의 운명을 선고하듯이 자신의 운명까지 확실하게 선고하고, 심지어 죄수보다 먼저 죽는 일이 한두 번 일어난 것이 아니었다."], ["For the rest, the Old Bailey was famous as a kind of deadly inn-yard, from which pale travellers set out continually, in carts and coaches, on a violent passage into the other world: traversing some two miles and a half of public street and road, and shaming few good citizens, if any.", "그밖에도 올드 베일리는 일종의 치명적인 여관 마당으로 유명했으니, 그곳에서 창백한 나그네들이 끊임없이 수레와 마차에 실려 저세상으로 가는 험한 여정을 떠났고, 그 길은 한 이 마일 반쯤 되는 공공 거리와 도로를 가로질렀으되, 설령 그 광경에 부끄러움을 느끼는 선량한 시민이 있다 한들 그 수는 거의 없었다."], ["So powerful is use, and so desirable to be good use in the beginning.", "그만큼 관습이란 강력한 것이며, 그러니 처음부터 좋은 관습이 되는 것이 그토록 바람직한 것이다."], ["It was famous, too, for the pillory, a wise old institution, that inflicted a punishment of which no one could foresee the extent; also, for the whipping-post, another dear old institution, very humanising and softening to behold in action; also, for extensive transactions in blood-money, another fragment of ancestral wisdom, systematically leading to the most frightful mercenary crimes that could be committed under Heaven.", "그곳은 또한 칼*로도 유명했으니, 그 누구도 그 형벌이 어느 정도에 이를지 예측할 수 없는 형벌을 가하는 지혜로운 옛 제도였고, 또한 태형 기둥으로도 유명했으니, 그것이 실제로 작동하는 모습을 보고 있노라면 사람을 무척이나 인간답고 부드럽게 만들어주는 또 하나의 정겨운 옛 제도였으며, 또한 피값을 거래하는 광범위한 흥정으로도 유명했으니, 그것은 하늘 아래에서 저질러질 수 있는 가장 끔찍한 돈벌이 범죄로 조직적으로 이어지는 또 한 조각의 조상 전래의 지혜였다."], ["Altogether, the Old Bailey, at that date, was a choice illustration of the precept, that “Whatever is is right;”", "통틀어 말하자면, 그 시절의 올드 베일리는 \"있는 것은 무엇이든 옳다\"는 격언의 더없이 좋은 본보기였으니, 이는 게으른 만큼이나 결정적인 경구였으되, 다만 일찍이 있었던 것은 무엇이든 그른 것이 아니었다는 골치 아픈 결론까지 포함하지 않았더라면 그러했을 것이다."], ["an aphorism that would be as final as it is lazy, did it not include the troublesome consequence, that nothing that ever was, was wrong.", "* 칼(pillory): 죄인의 목과 두 손을 끼워 묶어 두던 형틀."]], [["Making his way through the tainted crowd, dispersed up and down this hideous scene of action, with the skill of a man accustomed to make his way quietly, the messenger found out the door he sought, and handed in his letter through a trap in it. For, people then paid to see the play at the Old Bailey, just as they paid to see the play in Bedlam--only the former entertainment was much the dearer. Therefore, all the Old Bailey doors were well guarded--except, indeed, the social doors by which the criminals got there, and those were always left wide open.", "조용히 길을 헤쳐 나가는 데 익숙한 사람의 솜씨로, 이 끔찍한 활동 무대 위아래로 흩어져 있는 오염된 군중 사이를 뚫고 나아간 끝에, 심부름꾼은 찾던 문을 찾아내어 거기 달린 쪽문으로 편지를 들이밀었다. 그 시절 사람들은 베들럼*에서 구경거리를 보려고 돈을 냈듯이, 올드 베일리에서 구경거리를 보려고 돈을 냈던 것이다. 다만 전자의 오락거리가 훨씬 비쌌다. 그래서 올드 베일리의 모든 문은 철저히 지켜졌다. 물론 범죄자들이 그곳에 들어오게 되는 그 정겨운 문만은 예외였으니, 그 문들은 언제나 활짝 열려 있었다. * 베들럼(Bedlam): 런던의 정신병원. 당시 사람들이 돈을 내고 환자들을 구경하기도 했다."]], [["After some delay and demur, the door grudgingly turned on its hinges a very little way, and allowed Mr. Jerry Cruncher to squeeze himself into court.", "얼마간 지체와 실랑이 끝에, 문은 못마땅하다는 듯 경첩 위에서 아주 조금 돌아가며 제리 크런처 씨가 몸을 비집고 법정 안으로 들어가도록 허락했다."]], [["“What’s on?”", "\"무슨 일이 벌어지고 있소?\""], ["he asked, in a whisper, of the man he found himself next to.", "그는 어쩌다 곁에 서게 된 사람에게 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“Nothing yet.”", "\"아직 아무것도.\""]], [["“What’s coming on?”", "\"무슨 사건이 다뤄지오?\""]], [["“The Treason case.”", "\"반역죄 사건.\""]], [["“The quartering one, eh?”", "\"사지를 찢는 그거 말이오, 응?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["returned the man, with a relish; “he’ll be drawn on a hurdle to be half hanged, and then he’ll be taken down and sliced before his own face, and then his inside will be taken out and burnt while he looks on, and then his head will be chopped off, and he’ll be cut into quarters.", "그 사람이 입맛을 다시며 대꾸했다. \"썰매에 묶여 끌려 나와 반쯤 목이 매달렸다가, 끌어내려져 제 눈앞에서 살이 베이고, 그다음엔 제가 보는 가운데 내장이 끄집어내져 불태워지고, 그다음엔 목이 잘리고, 그다음엔 네 토막으로 잘릴 거요."], ["That’s the sentence.”", "그게 판결이오.\""]], [["“If he’s found Guilty, you mean to say?”", "\"유죄로 밝혀지면 말이지요?\""], ["Jerry added, by way of proviso.", "제리가 단서를 붙이듯 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["they’ll find him guilty,”", "유죄로 밝혀질 거요.\""], ["said the other.", "상대가 말했다."], ["“Don’t you be afraid of that.”", "\"그건 걱정 마쇼.\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher’s attention was here diverted to the door-keeper, whom he saw making his way to Mr.", "여기서 크런처 씨의 주의는 문지기에게로 쏠렸는데, 그가 쪽지를 손에 들고 로리 씨에게 다가가는 모습이 보였던 것이다."], ["Lorry, with the note in his hand. Mr.", "로리 씨는 가발을 쓴 신사들 사이의 탁자에 앉아 있었다."], ["Lorry sat at a table, among the gentlemen in wigs: not far from a wigged gentleman, the prisoner’s counsel, who had a great bundle of papers before him: and nearly opposite another wigged gentleman with his hands in his pockets, whose whole attention, when Mr. Cruncher looked at him then or afterwards, seemed to be concentrated on the ceiling of the court.", "자기 앞에 큰 서류 뭉치를 쌓아둔, 피고 측 변호인인 가발 쓴 신사로부터 멀지 않은 곳이었고, 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣은 또 다른 가발 쓴 신사와는 거의 마주 보는 자리였는데, 크런처 씨가 그때나 그 뒤에나 그를 볼 때면 그의 온 주의력은 법정 천장에 쏠려 있는 듯했다."], ["After some gruff coughing and rubbing of his chin and signing with his hand, Jerry attracted the notice of Mr. Lorry, who had stood up to look for him, and who quietly nodded and sat down again.", "몇 차례 거칠게 기침을 하고 턱을 문지르고 손으로 신호를 보낸 끝에, 제리는 그를 찾으려고 일어서 있던 로리 씨의 주의를 끌었고, 로리 씨는 조용히 고개를 끄덕인 뒤 다시 앉았다."]], [["“What’s he got to do with the case?”", "\"저 사람은 이 사건이랑 무슨 상관이오?\""], ["asked the man he had spoken with.", "그가 말을 나누었던 사람이 물었다."]], [["“Blest if I know,”", "\"낸들 알 게 뭐요.\""], ["said Jerry.", "제리가 말했다."]], [["“What have you got to do with it, then, if a person may inquire?”", "\"그럼 댁은 이 사건이랑 무슨 상관이오, 물어봐도 된다면?\""]], [["“Blest if I know that either,”", "\"그것도 낸들 알 게 뭐요.\""], ["said Jerry.", "제리가 말했다."]], [["The entrance of the Judge, and a consequent great stir and settling down in the court, stopped the dialogue.", "판사가 입정하고, 그에 따라 법정 안이 한바탕 술렁이다가 가라앉는 바람에 대화가 멈추었다."], ["Presently, the dock became the central point of interest.", "이내 피고석이 관심의 중심이 되었다."], ["Two gaolers, who had been standing there, went out, and the prisoner was brought in, and put to the bar.", "거기 서 있던 두 명의 간수가 나가고, 죄수가 끌려 들어와 피고석에 세워졌다."]], [["Everybody present, except the one wigged gentleman who looked at the ceiling, stared at him.", "천장을 바라보던 그 가발 쓴 신사 한 사람만 빼고, 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람이 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그곳에 있는 모든 인간의 숨결이 바다처럼, 바람처럼, 불길처럼 그에게로 밀려들었다."], ["All the human breath in the place, rolled at him, like a sea, or a wind, or a fire.", "열성적인 얼굴들이 그를 한번 보려고 기둥과 모퉁이 너머로 목을 빼고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["Eager faces strained round pillars and corners, to get a sight of him; spectators in back rows stood up, not to miss a hair of him; people on the floor of the court, laid their hands on the shoulders of the people before them, to help themselves, at anybody’s cost, to a view of him--stood a-tiptoe, got upon ledges, stood upon next to nothing, to see every inch of him.", "뒷줄의 구경꾼들은 그의 머리카락 한 올도 놓치지 않으려고 일어섰다. 법정 바닥에 있는 사람들은 누구의 희생을 무릅쓰고서라도 그를 보려고 앞사람의 어깨에 손을 얹고 몸을 일으켰고, 발끝으로 서고, 턱진 곳에 올라서고, 거의 아무것도 아닌 데에 발을 디디고서 그의 한 치 한 치를 다 보려 했다."], ["Conspicuous among these latter, like an animated bit of the spiked wall of Newgate, Jerry stood: aiming at the prisoner the beery breath of a whet he had taken as he came along, and discharging it to mingle with the waves of other beer, and gin, and tea, and coffee, and what not, that flowed at him, and already broke upon the great windows behind him in an impure mist and rain.", "이 뒤쪽 무리 가운데 단연 눈에 띄게, 마치 뉴게이트의 가시 박힌 담장이 살아 움직이는 한 조각이라도 되는 듯이, 제리가 서 있었다. 그는 오는 길에 한잔 걸친 맥주 냄새 나는 숨을 죄수에게로 내뿜었고, 그것은 그에게로 흘러드는 다른 맥주와 진과 차와 커피와 그밖에 온갖 것들의 물결과 뒤섞였으니, 그 물결은 이미 그의 등 뒤 커다란 창문들에 부딪쳐 탁한 안개와 비가 되어 흩어지고 있었다."]], [["The object of all this staring and blaring, was a young man of about five-and-twenty, well-grown and well-looking, with a sunburnt cheek and a dark eye.", "이 모든 응시와 떠들썩함의 대상은 스물다섯쯤 된 청년으로, 체격이 좋고 풍채가 훤했으며, 볕에 그을린 뺨과 검은 눈을 지니고 있었다."], ["His condition was that of a young gentleman.", "그의 처지는 젊은 신사의 처지였다."], ["He was plainly dressed in black, or very dark grey, and his hair, which was long and dark, was gathered in a ribbon at the back of his neck; more to be out of his way than for ornament.", "그는 수수하게 검은색, 혹은 아주 짙은 회색 옷을 입었고, 길고 검은 머리카락은 장식을 위해서라기보다 거치적거리지 않게 하려고 목덜미에서 리본으로 묶여 있었다."], ["As an emotion of the mind will express itself through any covering of the body, so the paleness which his situation engendered came through the brown upon his cheek, showing the soul to be stronger than the sun.", "마음의 동요가 몸을 가린 어떤 것을 통해서든 드러나기 마련이듯이, 그의 처지가 빚어낸 창백함이 뺨의 갈색을 뚫고 배어 나와, 영혼이 태양보다 강하다는 것을 보여주었다."], ["He was otherwise quite self-possessed, bowed to the Judge, and stood quiet.", "그밖에는 그는 꽤나 침착했고, 판사에게 인사를 하고는 조용히 서 있었다."]], [["The sort of interest with which this man was stared and breathed at, was not a sort that elevated humanity.", "이 사람이 응시와 숨결의 대상이 된 그 관심의 종류는 인간성을 고양하는 종류의 것이 아니었다."], ["Had he stood in peril of a less horrible sentence--had there been a chance of any one of its savage details being spared--by just so much would he have lost in his fascination.", "그가 덜 끔찍한 판결의 위험에 처해 있었더라면 — 그 야만적인 세부 사항 가운데 어느 하나라도 면제받을 가능성이 있었더라면 — 그만큼 그의 매력은 줄어들었을 것이다."], ["The form that was to be doomed to be so shamefully mangled, was the sight; the immortal creature that was to be so butchered and torn asunder, yielded the sensation.", "그토록 수치스럽게 갈가리 찢기도록 운명 지어진 그 육체가 바로 구경거리였고, 그토록 도살되고 사지가 찢길 그 불멸의 피조물이 그 짜릿함을 자아냈다."], ["Whatever gloss the various spectators put upon the interest, according to their several arts and powers of self-deceit, the interest was, at the root of it, Ogreish.", "여러 구경꾼들이 저마다의 기교와 자기기만의 능력에 따라 그 관심에 어떤 윤을 입히든, 그 관심은 뿌리부터 식인귀 같은 것이었다."]], [["Silence in the court!", "법정에 정숙을!"], ["Charles Darnay had yesterday pleaded Not Guilty to an indictment denouncing him (with infinite jingle and jangle) for that he was a false traitor to our serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, prince, our Lord the King, by reason of his having, on divers occasions, and by divers means and ways, assisted Lewis, the French King, in his wars against our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth; that was to say, by coming and going, between the dominions of our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, and those of the said French Lewis, and wickedly, falsely, traitorously, and otherwise evil-adverbiously, revealing to the said French Lewis what forces our said serene, illustrious, excellent, and so forth, had in preparation to send to Canada and North America.", "샤를 다네는 어제, (한없는 미사여구를 늘어놓으며) 그가 우리의 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 군주, 우리의 주군이신 국왕 폐하에 대한 거짓된 반역자라고 규탄하는 기소장에 대해 무죄를 주장한 바 있었다. 그 이유인즉, 그가 여러 차례에 걸쳐 여러 수단과 방법으로 프랑스 국왕 루이가 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분에 맞서 벌이는 전쟁을 도왔다는 것이었다."], ["This much, Jerry, with his head becoming more and more spiky as the law terms bristled it, made out with huge satisfaction, and so arrived circuitously at the understanding that the aforesaid, and over and over again aforesaid, Charles Darnay, stood there before him upon his trial; that the jury were swearing in; and that Mr.", "다시 말해, 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분의 영토와 위에서 말한 프랑스인 루이의 영토 사이를 오가면서, 사악하게, 거짓되게, 반역적으로, 그밖에 갖가지 사악한 부사적 방식으로, 위에서 말한 평온하고, 고명하고, 탁월하고, 등등하신 분이 캐나다와 북아메리카로 보내려고 준비해 둔 병력이 어떤 것인지를 위에서 말한 프랑스인 루이에게 누설했다는 것이었다."], ["Attorney-General was making ready to speak.", "법률 용어들이 그를 곤두서게 하는 통에 머리가 점점 더 뾰족해지는 가운데, 제리는 이만큼을 대단히 만족스럽게 알아들었고, 그리하여 빙 돌아서, 앞서 말했고 거듭거듭 앞서 말한 그 샤를 다네가 지금 자기 앞에 서서 재판을 받고 있다는 것, 배심원들이 선서를 하고 있다는 것, 그리고 검찰총장이 발언할 채비를 하고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["The accused, who was (and who knew he was) being mentally hanged, beheaded, and quartered, by everybody there, neither flinched from the situation, nor assumed any theatrical air in it.", "거기 있는 모든 사람들에게 마음속으로 목매달리고, 목이 잘리고, 사지가 찢기고 있는 피고는(그리고 자기가 그렇다는 것을 알고 있었던 피고는) 그 처지 앞에서 움츠러들지도, 거기에 어떤 연극적인 태도를 꾸미지도 않았다."], ["He was quiet and attentive; watched the opening proceedings with a grave interest; and stood with his hands resting on the slab of wood before him, so composedly, that they had not displaced a leaf of the herbs with which it was strewn.", "그는 조용하고 주의 깊었으며, 진지한 관심을 가지고 개정 절차를 지켜보았다. 그리고 두 손을 앞에 놓인 나무 판자 위에 올려놓고 어찌나 차분하게 서 있었던지, 그 판자 위에 흩뿌려진 약초의 잎사귀 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않았다."], ["The court was all bestrewn with herbs and sprinkled with vinegar, as a precaution against gaol air and gaol fever.", "법정에는 감옥의 공기와 감옥 열병을 막는 예방책으로 온통 약초가 흩뿌려지고 식초가 뿌려져 있었다."]], [["Over the prisoner’s head there was a mirror, to throw the light down upon him.", "죄수의 머리 위에는 빛을 그에게 내리비추기 위한 거울이 하나 있었다."], ["Crowds of the wicked and the wretched had been reflected in it, and had passed from its surface and this earth’s together.", "사악하고 비참한 자들의 무리가 거기에 비쳤다가, 그 표면과 이 세상에서 함께 사라져 갔다."], ["Haunted in a most ghastly manner that abominable place would have been, if the glass could ever have rendered back its reflections, as the ocean is one day to give up its dead.", "만약 그 유리가 언젠가 바다가 죽은 자들을 내어놓듯 자기가 비추었던 영상들을 되돌려 줄 수 있었더라면, 그 가증스러운 곳은 더없이 소름 끼치는 방식으로 유령에 시달렸을 것이다."], ["Some passing thought of the infamy and disgrace for which it had been reserved, may have struck the prisoner’s mind.", "그 거울이 어떤 오욕과 치욕을 위해 마련된 것인가 하는 생각이 문득 죄수의 마음을 스쳐 갔을지도 모른다."], ["Be that as it may, a change in his position making him conscious of a bar of light across his face, he looked up; and when he saw the glass his face flushed, and his right hand pushed the herbs away.", "어쨌든, 자세를 바꾸다가 빛줄기 하나가 자기 얼굴을 가로지르는 것을 의식하게 된 그는 위를 올려다보았고, 그 거울을 보자 얼굴이 붉어지면서 오른손으로 약초를 밀쳐냈다."]], [["It happened, that the action turned his face to that side of the court which was on his left.", "그 동작 때문에 그의 얼굴이 법정의 왼쪽에 있는 쪽으로 돌려지게 되었다."], ["About on a level with his eyes, there sat, in that corner of the Judge’s bench, two persons upon whom his look immediately rested; so immediately, and so much to the changing of his aspect, that all the eyes that were turned upon him, turned to them.", "그의 눈높이와 거의 같은 자리에, 판사석의 그 모퉁이에, 두 사람이 앉아 있었는데, 그의 시선이 곧바로 그들에게 머물렀다. 어찌나 곧바로, 또 어찌나 그의 표정을 바꾸어 놓았던지, 그에게 쏠려 있던 모든 눈길이 그들에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["The spectators saw in the two figures, a young lady of little more than twenty, and a gentleman who was evidently her father; a man of a very remarkable appearance in respect of the absolute whiteness of his hair, and a certain indescribable intensity of face: not of an active kind, but pondering and self-communing.", "구경꾼들은 그 두 사람에게서, 갓 스무 살 남짓한 젊은 숙녀와, 분명 그녀의 아버지로 보이는 신사를 보았다. 머리카락이 완전히 새하얗다는 점과, 뭐라 형언할 수 없는 얼굴의 어떤 강렬함이라는 점에서 무척 눈에 띄는 외모의 남자였는데, 그 강렬함은 활동적인 종류가 아니라 깊이 생각에 잠기고 스스로와 대화하는 종류의 것이었다."], ["When this expression was upon him, he looked as if he were old; but when it was stirred and broken up--as it was now, in a moment, on his speaking to his daughter--he became a handsome man, not past the prime of life.", "이 표정이 그에게 떠올라 있을 때면 그는 늙어 보였다. 그러나 그것이 흔들려 흩어질 때면 — 지금 그가 딸에게 말을 거는 순간 그러했듯이 — 그는 인생의 한창때를 아직 넘기지 않은 잘생긴 남자가 되었다."]], [["His daughter had one of her hands drawn through his arm, as she sat by him, and the other pressed upon it.", "그의 딸은 그의 곁에 앉아 한 손을 그의 팔에 끼우고, 다른 손으로 그 팔을 누르고 있었다."], ["She had drawn close to him, in her dread of the scene, and in her pity for the prisoner.", "그녀는 그 광경에 대한 두려움 때문에, 또 죄수에 대한 연민 때문에 그에게 바싹 다가앉아 있었다."], ["Her forehead had been strikingly expressive of an engrossing terror and compassion that saw nothing but the peril of the accused.", "그녀의 이마는 피고의 위험밖에는 아무것도 보지 못하는, 온통 마음을 사로잡는 공포와 동정을 놀랍도록 또렷하게 드러내고 있었다. 이것이 어찌나 두드러지게, 어찌나 강렬하고 자연스럽게 드러났던지, 그를 가엾게 여기지 않던 구경꾼들마저 그녀에게 마음이 움직였다."], ["This had been so very noticeable, so very powerfully and naturally shown, that starers who had had no pity for him were touched by her; and the whisper went about, “Who are they?”", "그리고 \"저들은 누구지?\" 하는 속삭임이 떠돌았다."]], [["Jerry, the messenger, who had made his own observations, in his own manner, and who had been sucking the rust off his fingers in his absorption, stretched his neck to hear who they were.", "심부름꾼 제리는, 제 나름의 방식으로 제 나름의 관찰을 해왔고, 골똘히 빠진 채 손가락의 녹을 빨아대고 있었는데, 그들이 누구인지 들으려고 목을 빼었다."], ["The crowd about him had pressed and passed the inquiry on to the nearest attendant, and from him it had been more slowly pressed and passed back; at last it got to Jerry:", "그를 둘러싼 군중은 그 질문을 가장 가까운 정리에게 밀어 넘겼고, 그에게서 그 질문은 더 천천히 밀려서 되돌아왔다. 마침내 그것이 제리에게 이르렀다."]], [["“Witnesses.”", "\"증인들이래.\""]], [["“For which side?”", "\"어느 편의?\""]], [["“Against.”", "\"반대편.\""]], [["“Against what side?”", "\"무엇에 반대하는 편 말이오?\""]], [["“The prisoner’s.”", "\"죄수에게.\""]], [["The Judge, whose eyes had gone in the general direction, recalled them, leaned back in his seat, and looked steadily at the man whose life was in his hand, as Mr. Attorney-General rose to spin the rope, grind the axe, and hammer the nails into the scaffold.", "판사는, 그쪽 방향으로 향했던 눈길을 거두어들이고는, 자리에 등을 기대고서, 검찰총장이 일어나 밧줄을 꼬고, 도끼를 갈고, 처형대에 못을 박기 시작하는 동안, 그 목숨이 자기 손에 달린 그 사람을 물끄러미 바라보았다."]], [["# CHAPTER III. A Disappointment", "# CHAPTER III. A Disappointment"]], [["Mr. Attorney-General had to inform the jury, that the prisoner before them, though young in years, was old in the treasonable practices which claimed the forfeit of his life.", "검찰총장은 배심원단에게 다음과 같이 알려야 했다."], ["That this correspondence with the public enemy was not a correspondence of to-day, or of yesterday, or even of last year, or of the year before.", "그들 앞에 선 피고인은 나이는 젊으나 그 목숨을 몰수당해 마땅한 반역 행위에 있어서는 노련한 자라고."], ["That, it was certain the prisoner had, for longer than that, been in the habit of passing and repassing between France and England, on secret business of which he could give no honest account.", "적국과 주고받은 이 내통은 오늘이나 어제, 심지어 작년이나 재작년의 일도 아니라고. 피고인이 그보다도 훨씬 오래전부터, 정직하게 해명할 수 없는 비밀 임무를 띠고 프랑스와 영국을 오가는 일을 습관으로 삼아왔음이 확실하다고."], ["That, if it were in the nature of traitorous ways to thrive (which happily it never was), the real wickedness and guilt of his business might have remained undiscovered.", "만일 반역의 길이 번창하는 것이 그 본성이었다면(다행히 결코 그렇지 않았으나) 그가 하는 일의 진짜 사악함과 죄과는 발각되지 않은 채 남았을지도 모른다고."], ["That Providence, however, had put it into the heart of a person who was beyond fear and beyond reproach, to ferret out the nature of the prisoner’s schemes, and, struck with horror, to disclose them to his Majesty’s Chief Secretary of State and most honourable Privy Council.", "그러나 섭리는 두려움도 비난도 초월한 어느 인물의 마음속에, 피고인이 꾸민 음모의 실체를 캐내고, 공포에 사로잡힌 채 그것을 폐하의 국무대신과 더없이 존귀한 추밀원에 폭로하도록 하는 뜻을 심어주었다고."], ["That, this patriot would be produced before them.", "이 애국자가 그들 앞에 출두할 것이라고."], ["That, his position and attitude were, on the whole, sublime.", "그의 입장과 태도는 대체로 숭고하다고."], ["That, he had been the prisoner’s friend, but, at once in an auspicious and an evil hour detecting his infamy, had resolved to immolate the traitor he could no longer cherish in his bosom, on the sacred altar of his country.", "그는 피고인의 친구였으나, 길하면서도 흉한 어느 한순간에 그의 파렴치함을 간파하고는, 더이상 가슴에 품을 수 없게 된 그 반역자를 조국의 신성한 제단에 제물로 바치기로 결심했다고."], ["That, if statues were decreed in Britain, as in ancient Greece and Rome, to public benefactors, this shining citizen would assuredly have had one.", "만일 영국에서도 고대 그리스와 로마에서처럼 공공의 은인들에게 동상을 세워주기로 정해져 있다면, 이 빛나는 시민에게도 틀림없이 하나가 세워졌으리라고."], ["That, as they were not so decreed, he probably would not have one.", "그러나 그렇게 정해져 있지 않으니, 그는 아마 동상을 얻지 못할 것이라고."], ["That, Virtue, as had been observed by the poets (in many passages which he well knew the jury would have, word for word, at the tips of their tongues; whereat the jury’s countenances displayed a guilty consciousness that they knew nothing about the passages), was in a manner contagious; more especially the bright virtue known as patriotism, or love of country.", "미덕이란, 시인들이 (그가 알기로 배심원단이 글자 하나 틀리지 않고 혀끝에 줄줄 욀 만한 수많은 구절 속에서 — 여기서 배심원단의 표정에는 그 구절들에 관해 아무것도 모른다는 죄스러운 자각이 드러났다) 노래한 바와 같이, 어떤 의미에서 전염성을 띤다고. 특히 애국심, 곧 조국애로 알려진 저 빛나는 미덕이 더욱 그러하다고."], ["That, the lofty example of this immaculate and unimpeachable witness for the Crown, to refer to whom however unworthily was an honour, had communicated itself to the prisoner’s servant, and had engendered in him a holy determination to examine his master’s table-drawers and pockets, and secrete his papers. That, he (Mr.", "변변치 못하게나마 언급하는 것만으로도 영광인, 이 흠 하나 없고 나무랄 데 없는 왕실 측 증인의 드높은 본보기가 피고인의 하인에게도 전해져, 그 하인의 마음속에 주인의 책상 서랍과 호주머니를 뒤지고 그 서류를 몰래 빼돌리겠다는 거룩한 결심을 낳았다고."], ["Attorney-General) was prepared to hear some disparagement attempted of this admirable servant; but that, in a general way, he preferred him to his (Mr. Attorney-General’s) brothers and sisters, and honoured him more than his (Mr.", "그(검찰총장)는 이 감탄스러운 하인을 깎아내리려는 시도가 다소 있으리라 각오하고 있으나, 대체로 그는 그 하인을 자신(검찰총장)의 형제자매보다 더 좋아하며, 자신(검찰총장)의 아버지와 어머니보다 더 존경한다고."], ["Attorney-General’s) father and mother. That, he called with confidence on the jury to come and do likewise.", "그는 배심원단도 나와서 똑같이 해주기를 자신 있게 촉구한다고."], ["That, the evidence of these two witnesses, coupled with the documents of their discovering that would be produced, would show the prisoner to have been furnished with lists of his Majesty’s forces, and of their disposition and preparation, both by sea and land, and would leave no doubt that he had habitually conveyed such information to a hostile power.", "이 두 증인의 증언은, 그들이 발견했으며 곧 제출될 문서들과 더불어, 피고인이 바다와 육지 양쪽에서의 폐하의 병력과 그 배치 및 준비 상황의 명단을 입수하고 있었음을 드러낼 것이고, 그가 그런 정보를 적대 세력에 상습적으로 넘겨왔음에 의심의 여지를 남기지 않을 것이라고. 이 명단들이 피고인의 필적이라고 입증할 수는 없으나, 그것은 다 마찬가지라고."], ["That, these lists could not be proved to be in the prisoner’s handwriting; but that it was all the same; that, indeed, it was rather the better for the prosecution, as showing the prisoner to be artful in his precautions.", "실로 그것은 피고인이 경계에 교활했음을 보여주므로 오히려 검찰 측에 더 유리하다고. 입증은 오년 전으로 거슬러 올라가, 영국군과 미국인 사이에 벌어진 바로 그 최초의 교전 날짜로부터 불과 몇 주 전에 이미 피고인이 이 해로운 임무에 종사하고 있었음을 보여줄 것이라고."], ["That, the proof would go back five years, and would show the prisoner already engaged in these pernicious missions, within a few weeks before the date of the very first action fought between the British troops and the Americans.", "이런 이유들로 인해, 충직한 배심원단인(그가 알기로 그들은 충직했다) 동시에 책임 있는 배심원단인(그들 자신이 알기로 그들은 책임이 있었다) 그들은, 좋든 싫든 반드시 피고인에게 유죄를 선고하여 그를 끝장내야 한다고."], ["That, for these reasons, the jury, being a loyal jury (as he knew they were), and being a responsible jury (as they knew they were), must positively find the prisoner Guilty, and make an end of him, whether they liked it or not.", "그들은 결코 자기네 베개에 머리를 누일 수 없으리라고. 그들은 결코 자기 아내가 베개에 머리를 누인다는 생각을 견딜 수 없으리라고."], ["That, they never could lay their heads upon their pillows; that, they never could tolerate the idea of their wives laying their heads upon their pillows; that, they never could endure the notion of their children laying their heads upon their pillows; in short, that there never more could be, for them or theirs, any laying of heads upon pillows at all, unless the prisoner’s head was taken off.", "그들은 결코 자기 자식들이 베개에 머리를 누인다는 관념을 참을 수 없으리라고. 요컨대 피고인의 머리를 베어내지 않는 한, 그들에게도 그들의 가족에게도 결코 다시는 베개에 머리를 누이는 일 따위는 있을 수 없으리라고."], ["That head Mr. Attorney-General concluded by demanding of them, in the name of everything he could think of with a round turn in it, and on the faith of his solemn asseveration that he already considered the prisoner as good as dead and gone.", "검찰총장은 자신이 생각해낼 수 있는, 단호한 매듭이 들어간 온갖 것의 이름을 걸고, 또 자신이 이미 피고인을 죽은 것이나 다름없는 자로 여긴다는 엄숙한 단언을 믿고서, 그들에게 바로 그 머리를 요구하며 말을 맺었다."]], [["When the Attorney-General ceased, a buzz arose in the court as if a cloud of great blue-flies were swarming about the prisoner, in anticipation of what he was soon to become.", "검찰총장이 말을 그치자, 마치 한 무리의 커다란 쉬파리떼가 곧 피고인이 될 그 무엇을 미리 내다보며 그의 둘레로 떼지어 몰려드는 듯이, 법정에 웅성거림이 일었다."], ["When toned down again, the unimpeachable patriot appeared in the witness-box.", "그것이 다시 가라앉자, 그 나무랄 데 없는 애국자가 증인석에 나타났다."]], [["Mr. Solicitor-General then, following his leader’s lead, examined the patriot: John Barsad, gentleman, by name.", "이어서 차장검사가 상관의 이끎을 좇아 그 애국자를 신문했으니, 이름은 존 바사드, 신사였다."], ["The story of his pure soul was exactly what Mr. Attorney-General had described it to be--perhaps, if it had a fault, a little too exactly.", "그 순결한 영혼의 이야기는 검찰총장이 묘사한 그대로였다 — 흠이라면 어쩌면 너무도 그대로라는 점이었다."], ["Having released his noble bosom of its burden, he would have modestly withdrawn himself, but that the wigged gentleman with the papers before him, sitting not far from Mr. Lorry, begged to ask him a few questions.", "자신의 고결한 가슴에서 짐을 덜어낸 그는 겸손하게 물러나려 했으나, 로리 씨에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 앉아 앞에 서류를 둔 가발 쓴 신사가 몇 가지 묻기를 청했다."], ["The wigged gentleman sitting opposite, still looking at the ceiling of the court.", "맞은편에 앉은 가발 쓴 신사는 여전히 법정 천장을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Had he ever been a spy himself?", "본인은 스파이였던 적이 있는가?"], ["No, he scorned the base insinuation.", "아니다, 그는 그런 비열한 암시를 경멸한다."], ["What did he live upon?", "무엇으로 생계를 꾸리는가?"], ["His property.", "자기 재산으로."], ["Where was his property?", "그 재산은 어디에 있는가?"], ["He didn’t precisely remember where it was.", "어디에 있는지 정확히는 기억나지 않는다."], ["What was it?", "어떤 재산인가?"], ["No business of anybody’s.", "누구의 상관할 바도 아니다."], ["Had he inherited it?", "물려받은 것인가?"], ["Yes, he had.", "그렇다, 물려받았다."], ["From whom?", "누구에게서?"], ["Distant relation.", "먼 친척에게서."], ["Very distant?", "아주 먼? 꽤."], ["Rather. Ever been in prison?", "감옥에 있은 적이 있는가?"], ["Certainly not.", "물론 없다."], ["Never in a debtors’ prison?", "채무자 감옥에 있은 적은 없는가?"], ["Didn’t see what that had to do with it.", "그게 이 사건과 무슨 상관인지 모르겠다."], ["Never in a debtors’ prison?--Come, once again.", "채무자 감옥에 있은 적이 없는가? — 자, 한번 더."], ["Never? Yes.", "결코 없는가?"], ["How many times?", "있다. 몇 번인가?"], ["Two or three times.", "두세 번."], ["Not five or six?", "대여섯 번은 아닌가?"], ["Perhaps.", "어쩌면."], ["Of what profession?", "직업은 무엇인가? 신사다."], ["Gentleman. Ever been kicked?", "발길질을 당한 적이 있는가?"], ["Might have been.", "그랬을지도 모른다."], ["Frequently?", "자주? 아니다."], ["No. Ever kicked downstairs?", "계단 아래로 걷어차인 적이 있는가?"], ["Decidedly not; once received a kick on the top of a staircase, and fell downstairs of his own accord.", "단연코 없다. 한번은 계단 꼭대기에서 발길질을 받고 제 발로 계단을 굴러 내려간 적이 있다."], ["Kicked on that occasion for cheating at dice?", "그때 주사위 노름에서 속임수를 썼다고 걷어차인 것인가?"], ["Something to that effect was said by the intoxicated liar who committed the assault, but it was not true.", "폭행을 저지른 그 술 취한 거짓말쟁이가 그 비슷한 말을 했으나, 사실이 아니다."], ["Swear it was not true?", "사실이 아니라고 맹세하는가?"], ["Positively.", "단언컨대."], ["Ever live by cheating at play?", "노름에서 속여 생계를 꾸린 적이 있는가? 결코 없다."], ["Never. Ever live by play?", "노름으로 생계를 꾸린 적은?"], ["Not more than other gentlemen do.", "다른 신사들보다 더하지는 않다."], ["Ever borrow money of the prisoner?", "피고인에게서 돈을 빌린 적이 있는가?"], ["Yes.", "있다."], ["Ever pay him?", "갚은 적은?"], ["No.", "없다."], ["Was not this intimacy with the prisoner, in reality a very slight one, forced upon the prisoner in coaches, inns, and packets?", "피고인과의 이 친분이란 실은 아주 보잘것없는 것으로, 마차와 여관과 연락선 안에서 피고인에게 억지로 떠맡겨진 것이 아닌가? 아니다."], ["No. Sure he saw the prisoner with these lists?", "피고인이 이 명단들을 가지고 있는 것을 분명히 보았는가?"], ["Certain.", "확실하다."], ["Knew no more about the lists? No.", "명단에 관해 더 아는 것은 없는가?"], ["Had not procured them himself, for instance?", "없다. 이를테면 그것들을 본인이 직접 구한 것은 아닌가?"], ["No.", "아니다."], ["Expect to get anything by this evidence?", "이 증언으로 무언가 얻기를 기대하는가? 아니다."], ["No. Not in regular government pay and employment, to lay traps?", "함정을 놓으라고 정부에 정식으로 고용되어 급료를 받는 것은 아닌가?"], ["Oh dear no.", "천만에, 아니다."], ["Or to do anything? Oh dear no.", "혹은 무슨 일이라도 하라고?"], ["Swear that?", "천만에, 아니다."], ["Over and over again.", "그것을 맹세하는가? 거듭거듭."], ["No motives but motives of sheer patriotism?", "순전한 애국심의 동기 말고 다른 동기는 없는가?"], ["None whatever.", "전혀 없다."]], [["The virtuous servant, Roger Cly, swore his way through the case at a great rate.", "그 덕망 높은 하인, 로저 클라이는 엄청난 속도로 사건을 헤치며 맹세를 늘어놓았다."], ["He had taken service with the prisoner, in good faith and simplicity, four years ago.", "그는 사년 전에 신의와 순박함으로 피고인의 고용살이에 들어갔다."], ["He had asked the prisoner, aboard the Calais packet, if he wanted a handy fellow, and the prisoner had engaged him.", "깔레행 연락선에서 그는 피고인에게 부릴 만한 사람이 필요한지 물었고, 피고인이 그를 고용했다."], ["He had not asked the prisoner to take the handy fellow as an act of charity--never thought of such a thing.", "그는 그 부릴 만한 사람을 자선 삼아 거두어달라고 청하지 않았다 — 그런 것은 생각해본 적도 없다."], ["He began to have suspicions of the prisoner, and to keep an eye upon him, soon afterwards.", "그는 머지않아 피고인을 의심하기 시작했고 그를 주시하게 되었다."], ["In arranging his clothes, while travelling, he had seen similar lists to these in the prisoner’s pockets, over and over again.", "여행 중에 옷을 정리하다가, 그는 피고인의 호주머니에서 이것들과 비슷한 명단을 거듭거듭 보았다. 그는 이 명단들을 피고인의 책상 서랍에서 꺼냈다."], ["He had taken these lists from the drawer of the prisoner’s desk.", "처음에 그것들을 그곳에 넣어둔 것은 그가 아니다."], ["He had not put them there first. He had seen the prisoner show these identical lists to French gentlemen at Calais, and similar lists to French gentlemen, both at Calais and Boulogne.", "그는 피고인이 깔레에서 프랑스 신사들에게 바로 이 명단들을 보여주는 것을, 또 깔레와 불로뉴 양쪽에서 프랑스 신사들에게 비슷한 명단들을 보여주는 것을 보았다."], ["He loved his country, and couldn’t bear it, and had given information.", "그는 조국을 사랑했고, 차마 견딜 수 없어 정보를 제공했다."], ["He had never been suspected of stealing a silver tea-pot; he had been maligned respecting a mustard-pot, but it turned out to be only a plated one.", "그는 은제 찻주전자를 훔쳤다는 혐의를 받은 적이 결코 없다. 겨자단지 일로 비방을 받은 적은 있으나, 그것은 도금한 것에 지나지 않은 것으로 밝혀졌다."], ["He had known the last witness seven or eight years; that was merely a coincidence.", "그는 앞서의 증인을 칠팔년 알고 지냈는데, 그것은 그저 우연의 일치였다."], ["He didn’t call it a particularly curious coincidence; most coincidences were curious.", "그는 그것을 딱히 기이한 우연의 일치라고 부르지는 않았다. 대부분의 우연의 일치란 기이한 법이다."], ["Neither did he call it a curious coincidence that true patriotism was his only motive too.", "또한 그는 진정한 애국심이 그에게도 유일한 동기라는 것을 기이한 우연의 일치라 부르지도 않았다."], ["He was a true Briton, and hoped there were many like him.", "그는 진정한 영국인이며, 자기 같은 사람이 많기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["The blue-flies buzzed again, and Mr. Attorney-General called Mr. Jarvis Lorry.", "쉬파리떼가 다시 윙윙거렸고, 검찰총장이 자비스 로리 씨를 불렀다."]], [["“Mr.", "\"자비스 로리 씨, 당신은 텔슨 은행의 직원입니까?\""], ["Jarvis Lorry, are you a clerk in Tellson’s bank?”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“I am.” “On a certain Friday night in November one thousand seven hundred and seventy-five, did business occasion you to travel between London and Dover by the mail?”", "\"천칠백칠십오년 십일월 어느 금요일 밤에, 볼일이 있어 우편마차로 런던과 도버 사이를 오갈 일이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“It did.”", "\"있었습니다.\""]], [["“Were there any other passengers in the mail?” “Two.”", "\"우편마차에 다른 승객이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“Did they alight on the road in the course of the night?”", "\"둘 있었습니다.\" \"그들은 밤중에 도중에 내렸습니까?\""]], [["“They did.”", "\"내렸습니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Lorry, look upon the prisoner.", "\"로리 씨, 피고인을 보십시오."], ["Was he one of those two passengers?”", "그가 그 두 승객 가운데 하나였습니까?\""]], [["“I cannot undertake to say that he was.”", "\"그렇다고 단언할 수는 없습니다.\""]], [["“Does he resemble either of these two passengers?”", "\"그가 그 두 승객 가운데 누군가와 닮았습니까?\""]], [["“Both were so wrapped up, and the night was so dark, and we were all so reserved, that I cannot undertake to say even that.”", "\"둘 다 너무 꽁꽁 싸매고 있었고, 밤은 너무 어두웠으며, 우리 모두 너무 말이 없었던 터라, 그것조차 단언할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Lorry, look again upon the prisoner.", "\"로리 씨, 피고인을 다시 보십시오."], ["Supposing him wrapped up as those two passengers were, is there anything in his bulk and stature to render it unlikely that he was one of them?”", "그가 그 두 승객처럼 몸을 싸매고 있었다고 가정한다면, 그의 덩치와 키에 그가 그들 가운데 하나였을 법하지 않다고 할 만한 점이 있습니까?\" \"없습니다.\""]], [["“You will not swear, Mr.", "\"로리 씨, 그가 그들 가운데 하나가 아니었다고 맹세하지는 않으시겠지요?\""], ["Lorry, that he was not one of them?”", "\"하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“No.” “So at least you say he may have been one of them?”", "\"그러면 적어도 그가 그들 가운데 하나였을 수도 있다고는 말씀하시는 거군요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["Except that I remember them both to have been--like myself--timorous of highwaymen, and the prisoner has not a timorous air.”", "다만 그 두 사람은 — 저와 마찬가지로 — 노상강도를 무서워했던 것으로 기억하는데, 피고인에게는 무서워하는 기색이 없다는 점만 빼면 말입니다.\""]], [["“Did you ever see a counterfeit of timidity, Mr. Lorry?”", "\"로리 씨, 두려움을 가장하는 것을 본 적이 있습니까?\""]], [["“I certainly have seen that.”", "\"분명 본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Lorry, look once more upon the prisoner.", "\"로리 씨, 피고인을 한번 더 보십시오."], ["Have you seen him, to your certain knowledge, before?”", "당신이 확실히 아는 한, 전에 그를 본 적이 있습니까?\" \"있습니다.\""]], [["“I have.” “When?”", "\"언제입니까?\""]], [["“I was returning from France a few days afterwards, and, at Calais, the prisoner came on board the packet-ship in which I returned, and made the voyage with me.”", "\"며칠 뒤에 제가 프랑스에서 돌아오고 있었는데, 깔레에서 피고인이 제가 타고 돌아오던 연락선에 올라, 저와 함께 항해를 했습니다.\""]], [["“At what hour did he come on board?”", "\"그가 몇 시에 배에 올랐습니까?\""]], [["“At a little after midnight.”", "\"자정이 조금 지나서였습니다.\""]], [["“In the dead of the night.", "\"한밤중에 말이군요."], ["Was he the only passenger who came on board at that untimely hour?”", "그 같은 늦은 시각에 배에 오른 승객은 그 사람뿐이었습니까?\""]], [["“He happened to be the only one.”", "\"공교롭게도 그가 유일한 승객이었습니다.\""]], [["“Never mind about ‘happening,’ Mr. Lorry.", "\"'공교롭게도'는 신경 쓰지 마십시오, 로리 씨."], ["He was the only passenger who came on board in the dead of the night?”", "한밤중에 배에 오른 승객은 그 사람뿐이었습니까?\""]], [["“He was.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“Were you travelling alone, Mr. Lorry, or with any companion?”", "\"로리 씨, 당신은 혼자 여행하고 있었습니까, 아니면 일행이 있었습니까?\""]], [["“With two companions.", "\"일행이 둘 있었습니다."], ["A gentleman and lady.", "신사 한 분과 숙녀 한 분이었지요."], ["They are here.”", "그분들은 여기 와 있습니다.\""]], [["“They are here.", "\"여기 와 있군요."], ["Had you any conversation with the prisoner?”", "당신은 피고인과 무슨 대화를 나누었습니까?\""]], [["“Hardly any.", "\"거의 없었습니다."], ["The weather was stormy, and the passage long and rough, and I lay on a sofa, almost from shore to shore.”", "날씨는 험악했고, 항해는 길고 거칠었으며, 저는 이쪽 기슭에서 저쪽 기슭에 이르도록 거의 내내 소파에 누워 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Miss Manette!”", "\"마네뜨 양!\""]], [["The young lady, to whom all eyes had been turned before, and were now turned again, stood up where she had sat.", "앞서 모든 눈길이 쏠렸다가 이제 다시 쏠리는 그 젊은 숙녀가 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["Her father rose with her, and kept her hand drawn through his arm.", "그녀의 아버지도 그녀와 함께 일어서서, 그녀의 손을 자기 팔에 끼운 채 잡고 있었다."]], [["“Miss Manette, look upon the prisoner.”", "\"마네뜨 양, 피고인을 보십시오.\""]], [["To be confronted with such pity, and such earnest youth and beauty, was far more trying to the accused than to be confronted with all the crowd.", "이런 연민, 이런 진실한 젊음과 아름다움과 마주하는 것은 피고인에게 군중 전체와 마주하는 것보다 훨씬 더 견디기 힘든 일이었다."], ["Standing, as it were, apart with her on the edge of his grave, not all the staring curiosity that looked on, could, for the moment, nerve him to remain quite still.", "말하자면 자기 무덤가에서 그녀와 따로 떨어져 서 있는 셈이었으니, 지켜보는 온갖 호기심 어린 시선을 다 합쳐도 그 순간 그가 꼼짝 않고 서 있도록 다잡아줄 수는 없었다."], ["His hurried right hand parcelled out the herbs before him into imaginary beds of flowers in a garden; and his efforts to control and steady his breathing shook the lips from which the colour rushed to his heart.", "그의 다급한 오른손은 앞에 놓인 약초들을 정원의 가상의 화단으로 나누어 심었고, 호흡을 억누르고 가다듬으려는 그의 안간힘에 입술이 떨렸으며 그 입술에서 핏기가 가슴으로 몰려갔다."], ["The buzz of the great flies was loud again.", "커다란 쉬파리들의 윙윙거림이 다시 요란해졌다."]], [["“Miss Manette, have you seen the prisoner before?”", "\"마네뜨 양, 전에 피고인을 본 적이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.” “Where?”", "\"네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“On board of the packet-ship just now referred to, sir, and on the same occasion.”", "\"어디서였습니까?\" \"방금 언급된 그 연락선 위에서, 같은 그 일이 있을 때였습니다.\""]], [["“You are the young lady just now referred to?”", "\"당신이 방금 언급된 그 젊은 숙녀입니까?\""]], [["“O!", "\"오!"], ["most unhappily, I am!”", "더없이 불행하게도, 그렇습니다!\""]], [["The plaintive tone of her compassion merged into the less musical voice of the Judge, as he said something fiercely: “Answer the questions put to you, and make no remark upon them.”", "그녀의 가엾어하는 애처로운 목소리는 판사의 덜 듣기 좋은 목소리에 묻혀버렸으니, 그가 사납게 무어라 말했던 것이다. \"당신에게 던져진 질문에 답하시오. 그에 관해 토를 달지 마시오.\""]], [["“Miss Manette, had you any conversation with the prisoner on that passage across the Channel?”", "\"마네뜨 양, 그 해협을 건너는 항해에서 피고인과 무슨 대화를 나누었습니까?\" \"네, 나누었습니다.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.” “Recall it.”", "\"그것을 되살려보시오.\""]], [["In the midst of a profound stillness, she faintly began: “When the gentleman came on board--”", "깊은 고요 속에서, 그녀는 가냘프게 입을 떼었다. \"그 신사분이 배에 올랐을 때―\""]], [["“Do you mean the prisoner?”", "\"피고인을 말하는 거요?\""], ["inquired the Judge, knitting his brows.", "판사가 눈썹을 찌푸리며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, my Lord.”", "\"네, 판사님.\""]], [["“Then say the prisoner.”", "\"그러면 피고인이라 하시오.\""]], [["“When the prisoner came on board, he noticed that my father,”", "\"피고인이 배에 올랐을 때, 그는 제 아버지가\""], ["turning her eyes lovingly to him as he stood beside her, “was much fatigued and in a very weak state of health.", "하고 그녀가 곁에 선 아버지에게 사랑스러운 눈길을 돌리며 말했다. \"몹시 지쳐 있고 건강이 매우 쇠약한 상태임을 알아차렸습니다."], ["My father was so reduced that I was afraid to take him out of the air, and I had made a bed for him on the deck near the cabin steps, and I sat on the deck at his side to take care of him.", "아버지는 너무도 쇠약해서, 저는 아버지를 바깥바람에서 안으로 모실 엄두가 나지 않아, 선실 계단 가까운 갑판에 잠자리를 마련해드리고, 아버지를 돌보려고 그 곁 갑판에 앉아 있었습니다."], ["There were no other passengers that night, but we four.", "그날 밤 다른 승객은 없었고, 우리 넷뿐이었습니다."], ["The prisoner was so good as to beg permission to advise me how I could shelter my father from the wind and weather, better than I had done.", "피고인은 친절하게도, 제가 했던 것보다 더 잘 아버지를 바람과 날씨로부터 막아드릴 방법을 일러주겠다고 청했습니다."], ["I had not known how to do it well, not understanding how the wind would set when we were out of the harbour.", "저는 그 일을 잘하는 법을 몰랐는데, 항구를 벗어나면 바람이 어느 쪽으로 불지 알지 못했기 때문입니다. 그가 저를 위해 그 일을 해주었습니다."], ["He did it for me. He expressed great gentleness and kindness for my father’s state, and I am sure he felt it.", "그는 제 아버지의 처지에 대해 깊은 다정함과 친절함을 보였고, 저는 그가 진심으로 그렇게 느꼈다고 확신합니다."], ["That was the manner of our beginning to speak together.”", "그렇게 해서 우리는 서로 이야기를 나누기 시작했습니다.\""]], [["“Let me interrupt you for a moment.", "\"잠깐 끼어들겠소. 그는 혼자 배에 올랐소?\""], ["Had he come on board alone?”", "\"아닙니다.\""]], [["“No.” “How many were with him?”", "\"몇 사람이 그와 함께 있었소?\""]], [["“Two French gentlemen.”", "\"프랑스 신사 두 분이었습니다.\""]], [["“Had they conferred together?”", "\"그들은 서로 의논을 했소?\""]], [["“They had conferred together until the last moment, when it was necessary for the French gentlemen to be landed in their boat.”", "\"그들은 마지막 순간까지 서로 의논을 했고, 그때 프랑스 신사분들은 자기네 보트로 내려 상륙해야 했습니다.\""]], [["“Had any papers been handed about among them, similar to these lists?”", "\"그들 사이에 이 명단들과 비슷한 서류가 오가지 않았소?\""]], [["“Some papers had been handed about among them, but I don’t know what papers.”", "\"그들 사이에 어떤 서류가 오가긴 했으나, 무슨 서류인지는 모릅니다.\""]], [["“Like these in shape and size?”", "\"이것들과 모양과 크기가 비슷한 것이었소?\""]], [["“Possibly, but indeed I don’t know, although they stood whispering very near to me: because they stood at the top of the cabin steps to have the light of the lamp that was hanging there; it was a dull lamp, and they spoke very low, and I did not hear what they said, and saw only that they looked at papers.”", "\"아마 그랬을 테지만, 정말이지 모르겠습니다. 그들이 제 아주 가까이에 서서 소곤거리기는 했어도, 그들은 거기 걸린 등불의 빛을 받으려고 선실 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었고, 그 등불은 침침했으며, 그들은 아주 낮게 말했고, 저는 그들이 하는 말을 듣지 못했으며, 그저 그들이 서류를 들여다보고 있었다는 것만 보았으니까요.\""]], [["“Now, to the prisoner’s conversation, Miss Manette.”", "\"이제, 피고인과의 대화로 넘어갑시다, 마네뜨 양.\""]], [["“The prisoner was as open in his confidence with me--which arose out of my helpless situation--as he was kind, and good, and useful to my father.", "\"피고인은 제 아버지에게 친절하고 자상하며 도움이 되었던 것과 마찬가지로, 저에게도 — 저의 무력한 처지에서 비롯된 것이지만 — 거리낌 없이 속내를 털어놓았습니다."], ["I hope,”", "저는 바라건대,\""], ["bursting into tears, “I may not repay him by doing him harm to-day.”", "하고 그녀가 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"오늘 그에게 해를 끼쳐 그 은혜를 갚는 일이 없기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Buzzing from the blue-flies.", "쉬파리떼의 윙윙거림."]], [["“Miss Manette, if the prisoner does not perfectly understand that you give the evidence which it is your duty to give--which you must give--and which you cannot escape from giving--with great unwillingness, he is the only person present in that condition.", "\"마네뜨 양, 만일 당신이 마지못해 — 당신이 마땅히 해야 하고, 반드시 해야 하며, 결코 피할 수 없는 — 그 증언을 매우 내키지 않은 마음으로 하고 있다는 것을 피고인이 온전히 이해하지 못한다면, 이 자리에서 그것을 이해하지 못하는 사람은 그 한 사람뿐일 것입니다."], ["Please to go on.”", "계속하십시오.\""]], [["“He told me that he was travelling on business of a delicate and difficult nature, which might get people into trouble, and that he was therefore travelling under an assumed name.", "\"그는 제게, 자신이 미묘하고 까다로운 성격의 일로 여행 중인데, 그 일이 사람들을 곤경에 빠뜨릴 수도 있어 그래서 가명으로 여행하고 있다고 말했습니다."], ["He said that this business had, within a few days, taken him to France, and might, at intervals, take him backwards and forwards between France and England for a long time to come.”", "그는 이 일 때문에 며칠 사이에 프랑스에 다녀왔으며, 앞으로도 오랫동안 이따금씩 프랑스와 영국 사이를 오가게 될지도 모른다고 말했습니다.\""]], [["“Did he say anything about America, Miss Manette?", "\"그가 미국에 관해 무슨 말을 했습니까, 마네뜨 양?"], ["Be particular.”", "자세히 말하시오.\""]], [["“He tried to explain to me how that quarrel had arisen, and he said that, so far as he could judge, it was a wrong and foolish one on England’s part.", "\"그는 그 분쟁이 어떻게 일어났는지 제게 설명하려 했고, 자기가 판단하기로는 그것이 영국 측의 그릇되고 어리석은 처사라고 말했습니다."], ["He added, in a jesting way, that perhaps George Washington might gain almost as great a name in history as George the Third.", "그는 농담조로, 어쩌면 조지 워싱턴이 조지 삼세에 거의 맞먹는 큰 이름을 역사에 남길지도 모른다고 덧붙였습니다."], ["But there was no harm in his way of saying this: it was said laughingly, and to beguile the time.”", "하지만 그가 그렇게 말한 데에는 아무런 악의도 없었습니다. 그것은 웃으며 한 말이었고, 무료한 시간을 달래려고 한 것이었습니다.\""]], [["Any strongly marked expression of face on the part of a chief actor in a scene of great interest to whom many eyes are directed, will be unconsciously imitated by the spectators.", "큰 관심을 끄는 장면에서 여러 눈길이 쏠리는 주역 인물이 짓는 어떤 뚜렷한 표정은, 구경꾼들에 의해 무의식중에 흉내내어지게 마련이다."], ["Her forehead was painfully anxious and intent as she gave this evidence, and, in the pauses when she stopped for the Judge to write it down, watched its effect upon the counsel for and against.", "그녀가 이 증언을 하는 동안 그녀의 이마는 고통스러우리만치 근심에 차고 골똘했으며, 판사가 그것을 받아 적느라 그녀가 말을 멈춘 짬마다, 그녀는 검찰 측과 변호 측 양쪽 변호인에게 그것이 미치는 영향을 살폈다."], ["Among the lookers-on there was the same expression in all quarters of the court; insomuch, that a great majority of the foreheads there, might have been mirrors reflecting the witness, when the Judge looked up from his notes to glare at that tremendous heresy about George Washington.", "구경꾼들 사이에서도 법정 사방에 똑같은 표정이 떠올라 있었으니, 판사가 조지 워싱턴에 관한 저 엄청난 이단을 노려보려고 필기에서 눈을 들었을 때, 그곳에 있던 이마들의 절대다수는 그 증인을 비추는 거울이라 해도 좋을 정도였다."]], [["Mr. Attorney-General now signified to my Lord, that he deemed it necessary, as a matter of precaution and form, to call the young lady’s father, Doctor Manette.", "이제 검찰총장은 판사에게, 신중을 기하고 형식을 갖추는 차원에서 그 젊은 숙녀의 아버지인 마네뜨 박사를 부르는 것이 필요하다고 여겨진다는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Who was called accordingly.", "그리하여 그가 호명되었다."]], [["“Doctor Manette, look upon the prisoner.", "\"마네뜨 박사, 피고인을 보십시오."], ["Have you ever seen him before?”", "전에 그를 본 적이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Once.", "\"한번 있습니다."], ["When he called at my lodgings in London.", "그가 런던의 제 거처로 찾아왔을 때입니다."], ["Some three years, or three years and a half ago.”", "한 삼년, 아니면 삼년 반쯤 전입니다.\""]], [["“Can you identify him as your fellow-passenger on board the packet, or speak to his conversation with your daughter?”", "\"피고인이 연락선에서 당신과 동승한 승객임을 알아볼 수 있습니까, 혹은 그가 당신의 따님과 나눈 대화에 대해 진술할 수 있습니까?\""]], [["“Sir, I can do neither.”", "\"판사님, 저는 그 어느 것도 할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Is there any particular and special reason for your being unable to do either?”", "\"그 어느 것도 하지 못하는 특별하고 각별한 이유가 있습니까?\""]], [["He answered, in a low voice, “There is.”", "그는 낮은 목소리로 답했다. \"있습니다.\""]], [["“Has it been your misfortune to undergo a long imprisonment, without trial, or even accusation, in your native country, Doctor Manette?”", "\"마네뜨 박사, 당신은 당신의 조국에서 재판도, 심지어 기소조차 없이 오랜 투옥을 겪는 불행을 당했습니까?\""]], [["He answered, in a tone that went to every heart, “A long imprisonment.”", "그는 모두의 가슴을 파고드는 어조로 답했다. \"오랜 투옥이었습니다.\""]], [["“Were you newly released on the occasion in question?”", "\"문제의 그때 당신은 갓 풀려난 참이었습니까?\""]], [["“They tell me so.”", "\"사람들이 그렇다고 합니다.\""]], [["“Have you no remembrance of the occasion?”", "\"그때 일을 전혀 기억하지 못합니까?\""]], [["“None.", "\"전혀요."], ["My mind is a blank, from some time--I cannot even say what time--when I employed myself, in my captivity, in making shoes, to the time when I found myself living in London with my dear daughter here.", "제 마음은, 어느 때부터 — 언제인지조차 말할 수 없는 어느 때부터 — 백지입니다. 갇혀 있는 동안 신발 만드는 일에 저 자신을 붙들어두던 그때부터, 여기 이 사랑하는 딸과 런던에서 살고 있는 저 자신을 발견한 그때까지가요."], ["She had become familiar to me, when a gracious God restored my faculties; but, I am quite unable even to say how she had become familiar.", "자비로운 하느님께서 제 정신을 되돌려주셨을 때 딸은 제게 낯익은 사람이 되어 있었습니다. 하지만 어떻게 그애가 낯익은 사람이 되었는지조차 도무지 말할 수가 없습니다."], ["I have no remembrance of the process.”", "그 과정에 대한 기억이 제겐 없습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Attorney-General sat down, and the father and daughter sat down together.", "검찰총장이 자리에 앉았고, 아버지와 딸도 함께 자리에 앉았다."]], [["A singular circumstance then arose in the case.", "그때 사건에 기묘한 정황이 떠올랐다."], ["The object in hand being to show that the prisoner went down, with some fellow-plotter untracked, in the Dover mail on that Friday night in November five years ago, and got out of the mail in the night, as a blind, at a place where he did not remain, but from which he travelled back some dozen miles or more, to a garrison and dockyard, and there collected information; a witness was called to identify him as having been at the precise time required, in the coffee-room of an hotel in that garrison-and-dockyard town, waiting for another person.", "당면한 과제는, 오년 전 십일월의 그 금요일 밤에 피고인이 행적이 추적되지 않은 어느 공모자와 함께 도버행 우편마차로 내려가, 눈속임으로 밤중에 어느 곳에서 우편마차를 내렸으되 그곳에 머물지 않고 거기서 다시 열두 마일 남짓을 되짚어 수비대와 조선소가 있는 곳으로 가서 거기서 정보를 수집했음을 보여주는 것이었다. 그래서 피고인이 바로 그 요구된 시각에 그 수비대 겸 조선소가 있는 도시의 한 호텔 다실에서 다른 사람을 기다리고 있었음을 확인해줄 증인이 호명되었다."], ["The prisoner’s counsel was cross-examining this witness with no result, except that he had never seen the prisoner on any other occasion, when the wigged gentleman who had all this time been looking at the ceiling of the court, wrote a word or two on a little piece of paper, screwed it up, and tossed it to him.", "피고인 측 변호인은 이 증인을 반대신문했으나, 다른 어느 경우에도 피고인을 본 적이 없다는 것 외에는 아무런 소득이 없었는데, 그때 줄곧 법정 천장을 바라보고 있던 그 가발 쓴 신사가 작은 종잇조각에 한두 마디를 적어 그것을 구겨 그에게 던졌다."], ["Opening this piece of paper in the next pause, the counsel looked with great attention and curiosity at the prisoner.", "다음번 짬에 이 종잇조각을 펼쳐본 변호인은 대단한 주의와 호기심을 띠고 피고인을 바라보았다."]], [["“You say again you are quite sure that it was the prisoner?”", "\"그것이 피고인이었음을 다시금 아주 확신한다고 말하는 거요?\""]], [["The witness was quite sure.", "증인은 아주 확신했다."]], [["“Did you ever see anybody very like the prisoner?”", "\"피고인과 아주 닮은 사람을 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["Not so like (the witness said) as that he could be mistaken.", "(증인이 말하기를) 잘못 볼 만큼 그렇게 닮은 사람은 없었다."]], [["“Look well upon that gentleman, my learned friend there,”", "\"저기 계신 저 신사, 저의 박식한 동료를 잘 보시오.\""], ["pointing to him who had tossed the paper over, “and then look well upon the prisoner.", "하고 종잇조각을 던진 사람을 가리키며 말했다. \"그런 다음 피고인을 잘 보시오."], ["How say you?", "어떻소?"], ["Are they very like each other?”", "두 사람이 서로 아주 닮지 않았소?\""]], [["Allowing for my learned friend’s appearance being careless and slovenly if not debauched, they were sufficiently like each other to surprise, not only the witness, but everybody present, when they were thus brought into comparison.", "박식한 동료의 행색이 방탕하다고까지는 못해도 단정치 못하고 허름하다는 점을 감안하더라도, 둘은 이렇게 비교해놓고 보니 증인뿐 아니라 그 자리에 있던 모든 사람을 놀라게 할 만큼 서로 닮아 있었다."], ["My Lord being prayed to bid my learned friend lay aside his wig, and giving no very gracious consent, the likeness became much more remarkable.", "판사에게 박식한 동료더러 가발을 벗으라 명해달라는 청이 올라갔고, 판사가 그리 흔쾌치 않게 동의하자, 그 닮은 정도는 훨씬 더 두드러졌다."], ["My Lord inquired of Mr. Stryver (the prisoner’s counsel), whether they were next to try Mr.", "판사가 스트라이버 씨(피고인 측 변호인)에게, 그렇다면 다음으로 카튼 씨(그 박식한 동료의 이름)를 반역죄로 재판하려는 것이냐고 물었다."], ["Carton (name of my learned friend) for treason? But, Mr.", "그러나 스트라이버 씨는 판사에게 아니라고 답했다."], ["Stryver replied to my Lord, no; but he would ask the witness to tell him whether what happened once, might happen twice; whether he would have been so confident if he had seen this illustration of his rashness sooner, whether he would be so confident, having seen it; and more.", "다만 그는 증인에게, 한번 일어난 일이 두번도 일어날 수 있는지, 만일 자신의 경솔함을 보여주는 이 실례를 좀더 일찍 보았더라면 그토록 확신했을 것인지, 그것을 본 지금도 그토록 확신하는지, 그리고 그밖의 것들을 묻겠다고 했다."], ["The upshot of which, was, to smash this witness like a crockery vessel, and shiver his part of the case to useless lumber.", "그 결과는, 이 증인을 질그릇 단지처럼 박살내어, 그가 맡은 사건의 몫을 쓸모없는 잡동사니로 산산이 부숴버린 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher had by this time taken quite a lunch of rust off his fingers in his following of the evidence.", "이때쯤 크런처 씨는 증언을 좇아 따라가느라 손가락에서 녹을 한 점심거리만큼이나 뜯어내고 있었다."], ["He had now to attend while Mr.", "이제 그는 스트라이버 씨가 마치 몸에 꼭 맞는 한 벌 옷을 입히듯이 피고인의 사건을 배심원단에게 끼워 맞추는 동안 지켜보아야 했다."], ["Stryver fitted the prisoner’s case on the jury, like a compact suit of clothes; showing them how the patriot, Barsad, was a hired spy and traitor, an unblushing trafficker in blood, and one of the greatest scoundrels upon earth since accursed Judas--which he certainly did look rather like.", "그 애국자 바사드가 어떻게 고용된 스파이이자 반역자이며, 낯빛 하나 붉히지 않고 피를 거래하는 자이고, 저주받은 유다 이후 지상에서 가장 큰 악당 가운데 하나인지를 — 사실 그는 다소 그렇게 보이기도 했다 — 보여주었다. 그 덕망 높은 하인 클라이가 어떻게 그의 친구이자 동업자였으며, 또 그럴 만한 자였는지."], ["How the virtuous servant, Cly, was his friend and partner, and was worthy to be; how the watchful eyes of those forgers and false swearers had rested on the prisoner as a victim, because some family affairs in France, he being of French extraction, did require his making those passages across the Channel--though what those affairs were, a consideration for others who were near and dear to him, forbade him, even for his life, to disclose.", "어떻게 그 위조꾼이자 위증꾼들의 빈틈없는 눈이 피고인을 제물로 점찍었는지를 — 프랑스 혈통인 피고인에게 프랑스에서의 어떤 집안일 때문에 그 해협을 건너는 일이 필요했던 까닭에 — 보여주었다. 다만 그 집안일이 무엇이었는지는, 그에게 가깝고 소중한 다른 이들에 대한 배려 때문에, 제 목숨이 걸렸더라도 밝힐 수 없었다."], ["How the evidence that had been warped and wrested from the young lady, whose anguish in giving it they had witnessed, came to nothing, involving the mere little innocent gallantries and politenesses likely to pass between any young gentleman and young lady so thrown together;--with the exception of that reference to George Washington, which was altogether too extravagant and impossible to be regarded in any other light than as a monstrous joke.", "어떻게 그 젊은 숙녀에게서 — 그녀가 그것을 진술하며 겪은 고뇌를 그들이 지켜본 바로 그 숙녀에게서 — 비틀고 쥐어짜낸 증언이 결국 아무것도 아닌 것으로 드러나, 그렇게 함께 어울리게 된 어느 젊은 신사와 젊은 숙녀 사이에 으레 오갈 법한 그저 사소하고 순진한 정중함과 예의에 지나지 않았는지를 보여주었다. 다만 저 조지 워싱턴에 관한 언급만은 예외였으니, 그것은 너무도 터무니없고 있을 법하지 않아 무시무시한 농담 외의 다른 어떤 것으로도 여길 수 없었다."], ["How it would be a weakness in the government to break down in this attempt to practise for popularity on the lowest national antipathies and fears, and therefore Mr.", "정부가 가장 저급한 국민적 반감과 두려움에 기대어 인기를 얻으려는 이 시도에서 무너지는 것은 정부의 약점을 드러내는 일이 될 것이므로, 검찰총장이 그것을 최대한 이용한 것이라고."], ["Attorney-General had made the most of it; how, nevertheless, it rested upon nothing, save that vile and infamous character of evidence too often disfiguring such cases, and of which the State Trials of this country were full.", "그럼에도 그것은, 이런 사건들을 너무도 흔히 흉하게 만드는, 그리고 이 나라의 국사범 재판이 가득 차 있는 저 비열하고 악명 높은 종류의 증언 외에는 아무것에도 근거하고 있지 않다고."], ["But, there my Lord interposed (with as grave a face as if it had not been true), saying that he could not sit upon that Bench and suffer those allusions.", "그러나 거기서 판사가 (그것이 사실이 아니기라도 한 양 자못 근엄한 얼굴로) 끼어들어, 자신은 저 법대에 앉아 그런 암시를 용납할 수 없노라고 말했다."]], [["Mr. Stryver then called his few witnesses, and Mr.", "이어서 스트라이버 씨가 자기 측 몇 안 되는 증인들을 불렀고, 다음으로 크런처 씨는 검찰총장이 스트라이버 씨가 배심원단에게 입혀놓은 옷 한 벌을 통째로 안팎을 뒤집는 동안 지켜보아야 했다."], ["Cruncher had next to attend while Mr. Attorney-General turned the whole suit of clothes Mr.", "검찰총장은 바사드와 클라이가 자기가 생각했던 것보다 백 배는 더 훌륭하고, 피고인은 백 배는 더 나쁜 자임을 보여주었다."], ["Stryver had fitted on the jury, inside out; showing how Barsad and Cly were even a hundred times better than he had thought them, and the prisoner a hundred times worse. Lastly, came my Lord himself, turning the suit of clothes, now inside out, now outside in, but on the whole decidedly trimming and shaping them into grave-clothes for the prisoner.", "마지막으로 판사 자신이 나서서, 그 옷 한 벌을 어느 때는 안팎을, 어느 때는 겉뒤를 뒤집었으나, 대체로 단연 피고인에게 입힐 수의로 손질하고 매만지는 쪽이었다."]], [["And now, the jury turned to consider, and the great flies swarmed again.", "이제 배심원단이 평의에 들어갔고, 커다란 쉬파리떼가 다시 떼지어 몰려들었다."]], [["Mr. Carton, who had so long sat looking at the ceiling of the court, changed neither his place nor his attitude, even in this excitement.", "그토록 오래 법정 천장만 바라보고 앉아 있던 카튼 씨는, 이 소동 속에서도 자리도 자세도 바꾸지 않았다."], ["While his learned friend, Mr. Stryver, massing his papers before him, whispered with those who sat near, and from time to time glanced anxiously at the jury; while all the spectators moved more or less, and grouped themselves anew; while even my Lord himself arose from his seat, and slowly paced up and down his platform, not unattended by a suspicion in the minds of the audience that his state was feverish; this one man sat leaning back, with his torn gown half off him, his untidy wig put on just as it had happened to light on his head after its removal, his hands in his pockets, and his eyes on the ceiling as they had been all day.", "그의 박식한 동료 스트라이버 씨가 앞에 서류를 모아 쌓으며 가까이 앉은 이들과 소곤거리고 때때로 근심스레 배심원단을 흘끔거리는 동안, 모든 구경꾼이 어느 정도씩 자리를 옮기며 새로이 무리를 이루는 동안, 심지어 판사 자신마저 자리에서 일어나 자기 단상을 천천히 오락가락하며 — 그의 상태가 흥분해 있는 것이 아닌가 하는 의심이 청중의 마음에 어리는 가운데 — 거니는 동안, 이 한 사람만은 등을 기댄 채 앉아, 찢어진 법복은 반쯤 흘러내려 있고, 흐트러진 가발은 벗어두었다가 머리에 떨어진 그대로 아무렇게나 얹혀 있으며, 두 손은 호주머니에 찌르고, 두 눈은 온종일 그래왔듯 천장에 둔 채였다."], ["Something especially reckless in his demeanour, not only gave him a disreputable look, but so diminished the strong resemblance he undoubtedly bore to the prisoner (which his momentary earnestness, when they were compared together, had strengthened), that many of the lookers-on, taking note of him now, said to one another they would hardly have thought the two were so alike. Mr.", "그의 태도에 깃든 무언가 유달리 무모한 데가 그에게 평판 사나운 인상을 줄 뿐 아니라, 그가 피고인에게 틀림없이 지닌 그 짙은 닮은꼴을 (둘을 함께 비교했을 때 그 순간의 진지함이 도드라지게 했던 그 닮은꼴을) 어찌나 흐려놓았던지, 이제 그를 눈여겨본 구경꾼들 가운데 많은 이들이 서로에게, 두 사람이 그렇게나 닮았다고는 도무지 생각하지 못했겠다고 말했다."], ["Cruncher made the observation to his next neighbour, and added, “I’d hold half a guinea that he don’t get no law-work to do.", "크런처 씨도 옆 사람에게 그렇게 말하고는 덧붙였다. \"내 반 기니를 걸겠수, 저 양반은 법률 일거리 하나 못 얻을 거요."], ["Don’t look like the sort of one to get any, do he?”", "그런 거 얻을 부류로는 안 보이잖수, 그렇잖수?\""]], [["Yet, this Mr.", "그래도 이 카튼 씨는 겉으로 보이는 것보다 그 장면의 세세한 부분을 더 많이 받아들이고 있었다."], ["Carton took in more of the details of the scene than he appeared to take in; for now, when Miss Manette’s head dropped upon her father’s breast, he was the first to see it, and to say audibly: “Officer!", "이제 마네뜨 양의 고개가 아버지의 가슴으로 떨구어지는 순간, 그것을 맨 먼저 알아채고 또렷이 소리내어 말한 것이 바로 그였으니 말이다. \"정리!"], ["look to that young lady.", "저 젊은 숙녀를 살피시오."], ["Help the gentleman to take her out.", "저 신사분이 그녀를 데리고 나가는 것을 도와드리시오."], ["Don’t you see she will fall!”", "그녀가 쓰러지려는 게 안 보이오!\""]], [["There was much commiseration for her as she was removed, and much sympathy with her father.", "그녀가 부축되어 나갈 때 많은 이들이 그녀를 가엾이 여겼고, 그녀의 아버지에게도 많은 동정을 보냈다."], ["It had evidently been a great distress to him, to have the days of his imprisonment recalled.", "투옥의 나날들을 떠올리는 것이 그에게는 분명 크나큰 괴로움이었다."], ["He had shown strong internal agitation when he was questioned, and that pondering or brooding look which made him old, had been upon him, like a heavy cloud, ever since.", "신문을 받을 때 그는 격렬한 내면의 동요를 드러냈고, 그를 늙어 보이게 하던 그 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴, 혹은 골똘히 시름하는 표정이 그때 이후 무거운 구름처럼 그에게 드리워 있었다."], ["As he passed out, the jury, who had turned back and paused a moment, spoke, through their foreman.", "그가 밖으로 나갈 때, 돌아서서 잠시 멈춰 섰던 배심원단이 그 대표를 통해 입을 열었다."]], [["They were not agreed, and wished to retire.", "그들은 의견이 일치하지 않아 퇴정하기를 바랐다."], ["My Lord (perhaps with George Washington on his mind) showed some surprise that they were not agreed, but signified his pleasure that they should retire under watch and ward, and retired himself.", "판사는 (어쩌면 조지 워싱턴이 머릿속에 있었던 듯) 그들의 의견이 일치하지 않는 것에 다소 놀라움을 드러냈으나, 감시와 보호하에 그들이 퇴정해도 좋다는 뜻을 밝히고 자신도 퇴정했다."], ["The trial had lasted all day, and the lamps in the court were now being lighted.", "재판은 온종일 이어졌고, 이제 법정의 등불에 불이 켜지고 있었다."], ["It began to be rumoured that the jury would be out a long while.", "배심원단이 오래도록 나와 있지 않으리라는 소문이 돌기 시작했다."], ["The spectators dropped off to get refreshment, and the prisoner withdrew to the back of the dock, and sat down.", "구경꾼들은 요기를 하러 하나둘 빠져나갔고, 피고인은 피고석 뒤쪽으로 물러나 앉았다."]], [["Mr.", "젊은 숙녀와 그녀의 아버지가 나갈 때 함께 나갔던 로리 씨가 이제 다시 나타나, 제리에게 손짓했다."], ["Lorry, who had gone out when the young lady and her father went out, now reappeared, and beckoned to Jerry: who, in the slackened interest, could easily get near him.", "제리는 관심이 느슨해진 틈에 어렵잖게 그에게 다가갈 수 있었다."]], [["“Jerry, if you wish to take something to eat, you can.", "\"제리, 뭐 좀 먹고 싶거든 그렇게 하게."], ["But, keep in the way.", "다만 가까운 데서 대기하고 있게나."], ["You will be sure to hear when the jury come in.", "배심원단이 들어오면 자네는 틀림없이 알게 될 걸세."], ["Don’t be a moment behind them, for I want you to take the verdict back to the bank.", "그들보다 한순간도 뒤처지지 말게. 평결을 은행에 가지고 돌아가는 일을 자네에게 맡기고 싶으니 말일세."], ["You are the quickest messenger I know, and will get to Temple Bar long before I can.”", "자네는 내가 아는 가장 빠른 심부름꾼이고, 나보다 한참 앞서 템플 바에 닿을 테니까.\""]], [["Jerry had just enough forehead to knuckle, and he knuckled it in acknowledgment of this communication and a shilling. Mr.", "제리는 손마디로 두드릴 만한 이마가 겨우 있을 뿐이었는데, 그 이마를 손마디로 두드려 이 전갈과 일 실링에 대한 답례로 삼았다."], ["Carton came up at the moment, and touched Mr. Lorry on the arm.", "바로 그때 카튼 씨가 다가와 로리 씨의 팔을 건드렸다."]], [["“How is the young lady?”", "\"그 젊은 숙녀는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["“She is greatly distressed; but her father is comforting her, and she feels the better for being out of court.”", "\"몹시 괴로워하고 있습니다. 하지만 아버지가 그녀를 달래주고 있고, 법정에서 나온 덕분에 한결 나아진 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“I’ll tell the prisoner so.", "\"내가 피고인에게 그렇게 전해주지요."], ["It won’t do for a respectable bank gentleman like you, to be seen speaking to him publicly, you know.”", "당신처럼 점잖은 은행의 신사가 그에게 공공연히 말을 거는 모습을 보여서야 안 될 일이니까요, 아시겠지만.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry reddened as if he were conscious of having debated the point in his mind, and Mr.", "로리 씨는 마음속으로 그 문제를 따져보고 있었음을 의식하기라도 한 듯 얼굴을 붉혔고, 카튼 씨는 난간 바깥쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["Carton made his way to the outside of the bar. The way out of court lay in that direction, and Jerry followed him, all eyes, ears, and spikes.", "법정 밖으로 나가는 길이 그쪽으로 나 있었던 터라, 제리도 온 신경을 곤두세운 채 그를 뒤따랐다."]], [["“Mr. Darnay!”", "\"다네이 씨!\""]], [["The prisoner came forward directly.", "피고인이 곧장 앞으로 나왔다."]], [["“You will naturally be anxious to hear of the witness, Miss Manette.", "\"증인 마네뜨 양에 대해 듣고 싶어 안달이 나 있겠지요."], ["She will do very well.", "그녀는 곧 괜찮아질 겁니다."], ["You have seen the worst of her agitation.”", "당신은 그녀가 가장 심하게 동요했을 때를 본 거니까요.\""]], [["“I am deeply sorry to have been the cause of it.", "\"내가 그 동요의 원인이 된 것이 깊이 송구합니다."], ["Could you tell her so for me, with my fervent acknowledgments?”", "저의 간곡한 사례와 함께, 그녀에게 그렇게 전해주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, I could.", "\"네, 그럴 수 있습니다."], ["I will, if you ask it.”", "당신이 청한다면, 그렇게 하지요.\""]], [["Mr. Carton’s manner was so careless as to be almost insolent.", "카튼 씨의 태도는 어찌나 무심한지 거의 무례할 지경이었다."], ["He stood, half turned from the prisoner, lounging with his elbow against the bar.", "그는 피고인에게서 몸을 반쯤 돌린 채, 팔꿈치를 난간에 괴고 비스듬히 기대 서 있었다."]], [["“I do ask it.", "\"청하는 바입니다."], ["Accept my cordial thanks.”", "진심 어린 감사를 받아주십시오.\""]], [["“What,”", "\"무엇을,\""], ["said Carton, still only half turned towards him, “do you expect, Mr.", "하고 카튼이 여전히 그를 향해 반쯤만 돌아선 채 말했다."], ["Darnay?”", "\"기대하고 있습니까, 다네이 씨?\""]], [["“The worst.”", "\"최악을요.\""]], [["“It’s the wisest thing to expect, and the likeliest.", "\"그게 기대하기에 가장 현명한 일이고, 또 가장 그럴 법한 일이지요."], ["But I think their withdrawing is in your favour.”", "하지만 그들이 퇴정한 것은 당신에게 유리하다고 봅니다.\""]], [["Loitering on the way out of court not being allowed, Jerry heard no more: but left them--so like each other in feature, so unlike each other in manner--standing side by side, both reflected in the glass above them.", "법정에서 나가는 길에 어정거리는 것은 허락되지 않았으므로, 제리는 더는 듣지 못했다. 다만 그들을 — 이목구비는 그토록 서로 닮았으되 태도는 그토록 서로 다른 두 사람을 — 나란히 서서 둘 다 머리 위의 유리에 비친 모습으로 남겨둔 채 떠났다."]], [["An hour and a half limped heavily away in the thief-and-rascal crowded passages below, even though assisted off with mutton pies and ale.", "도둑과 무뢰한이 들끓는 아래층 통로에서, 양고기 파이와 에일로 거든다 해도, 한 시간 반이 무겁게 절뚝거리며 흘러갔다."], ["The hoarse messenger, uncomfortably seated on a form after taking that refection, had dropped into a doze, when a loud murmur and a rapid tide of people setting up the stairs that led to the court, carried him along with them.", "그 쉰 목소리의 심부름꾼은 그 요기를 마친 뒤 긴 의자에 불편하게 걸터앉아 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 법정으로 이어지는 계단을 우르르 올라오는 요란한 웅성거림과 빠른 인파의 물결에 휩쓸려 함께 떠밀려 갔다."]], [["“Jerry!", "\"제리!"], ["Jerry!”", "제리!\""], ["Mr. Lorry was already calling at the door when he got there.", "그가 문에 이르렀을 때 로리 씨가 벌써 문간에서 부르고 있었다."]], [["“Here, sir!", "\"여기 있습니다, 나리!"], ["It’s a fight to get back again.", "다시 비집고 들어오기가 한바탕 싸움입니다요."], ["Here I am, sir!”", "저 여기 있습니다, 나리!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry handed him a paper through the throng.", "로리 씨가 인파를 헤치고 그에게 종이 한 장을 건넸다."], ["“Quick!", "\"어서!"], ["Have you got it?”", "받았나?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["Hastily written on the paper was the word “ACQUITTED.”", "종이에는 \"무죄 방면\"이라는 말이 급히 휘갈겨져 있었다."]], [["“If you had sent the message, ‘Recalled to Life,’ again,”", "\"또 '소생'이라는 전갈을 보내셨더라면,\""], ["muttered Jerry, as he turned, “I should have known what you meant, this time.”", "하고 제리가 돌아서며 중얼거렸다. \"이번엔 무슨 뜻인지 알아들었을 텐데 말이지.\""]], [["He had no opportunity of saying, or so much as thinking, anything else, until he was clear of the Old Bailey; for, the crowd came pouring out with a vehemence that nearly took him off his legs, and a loud buzz swept into the street as if the baffled blue-flies were dispersing in search of other carrion.", "그는 올드베일리를 벗어날 때까지 다른 무엇을 말할, 아니 생각할 겨를조차 없었다. 군중이 어찌나 거센 기세로 쏟아져 나오는지 하마터면 그를 넘어뜨릴 뻔했고, 좌절한 쉬파리떼가 다른 썩은 고기를 찾아 흩어지기라도 하듯 요란한 윙윙거림이 거리로 휩쓸려 나갔기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV. Congratulatory", "# CHAPTER IV. Congratulatory"]], [["From the dimly-lighted passages of the court, the last sediment of the human stew that had been boiling there all day, was straining off, when Doctor Manette, Lucie Manette, his daughter, Mr. Lorry, the solicitor for the defence, and its counsel, Mr. Stryver, stood gathered round Mr. Charles Darnay--just released--congratulating him on his escape from death.", "법정의 어슴푸레한 통로에서, 온종일 그곳에서 끓어오르던 인간 잡탕의 마지막 찌끼가 빠져나가고 있을 무렵, 마네뜨 박사와 그의 딸 루시 마네뜨, 로리 씨, 변호인 측 사무 변호사, 그리고 그 변호사 스트라이버 씨가, 방금 풀려난 찰스 다네이 씨 주위에 모여 서서, 죽음을 면한 그를 축하하고 있었다."]], [["It would have been difficult by a far brighter light, to recognise in Doctor Manette, intellectual of face and upright of bearing, the shoemaker of the garret in Paris.", "훨씬 밝은 빛 아래에서라도, 얼굴에 지성이 어리고 자세가 꼿꼿한 마네뜨 박사에게서 빠리의 다락방 구두장이를 알아보기란 어려웠을 것이다."], ["Yet, no one could have looked at him twice, without looking again: even though the opportunity of observation had not extended to the mournful cadence of his low grave voice, and to the abstraction that overclouded him fitfully, without any apparent reason.", "그러나 누구든 그를 한번 보면 다시 보지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 관찰의 기회가 그의 낮고 무거운 목소리의 구슬픈 억양에까지, 그리고 뚜렷한 까닭도 없이 이따금 그를 뒤덮는 그 멍한 상태에까지 미치지 못했다 하더라도 말이다."], ["While one external cause, and that a reference to his long lingering agony, would always--as on the trial--evoke this condition from the depths of his soul, it was also in its nature to arise of itself, and to draw a gloom over him, as incomprehensible to those unacquainted with his story as if they had seen the shadow of the actual Bastille thrown upon him by a summer sun, when the substance was three hundred miles away.", "한가지 외부적인 원인, 곧 그의 오래 끌어온 고통에 대한 언급은, 재판에서 그러했듯이 언제나 그의 영혼 깊은 곳에서 이 상태를 불러일으키곤 했지만, 그것은 또한 본디 제 스스로 떠올라 그에게 음울함을 드리우기도 했으니, 그의 사연을 모르는 이들에게는 그것이 마치 실제 바스띠유의 그림자가 — 그 실체는 삼백 마일 떨어져 있는데도 — 여름 햇살에 그의 위로 드리워진 것을 본 것만큼이나 이해할 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["Only his daughter had the power of charming this black brooding from his mind.", "오직 그의 딸만이 이 검은 우수를 그의 마음에서 걷어낼 힘을 지녔다."], ["She was the golden thread that united him to a Past beyond his misery, and to a Present beyond his misery: and the sound of her voice, the light of her face, the touch of her hand, had a strong beneficial influence with him almost always.", "그녀는 그를 그의 불행 너머의 과거와, 그의 불행 너머의 현재로 이어주는 황금실이었다. 그녀의 목소리, 그녀의 얼굴빛, 그녀의 손길은 거의 언제나 그에게 강력하고도 이로운 영향을 미쳤다."], ["Not absolutely always, for she could recall some occasions on which her power had failed; but they were few and slight, and she believed them over.", "절대적으로 언제나 그런 것은 아니었으니, 그녀는 자신의 힘이 듣지 않았던 몇몇 경우를 떠올릴 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그런 경우는 드물고 사소했으며, 그녀는 그것이 다 지나갔다고 믿었다."]], [["Mr. Darnay had kissed her hand fervently and gratefully, and had turned to Mr. Stryver, whom he warmly thanked. Mr. Stryver, a man of little more than thirty, but looking twenty years older than he was, stout, loud, red, bluff, and free from any drawback of delicacy, had a pushing way of shouldering himself (morally and physically) into companies and conversations, that argued well for his shouldering his way up in life.", "다네이 씨는 그녀의 손에 열렬하고 고마운 마음으로 입을 맞춘 뒤, 스트라이버 씨에게 돌아서서 따뜻하게 감사를 표했다. 스트라이버 씨는 서른을 갓 넘긴 사내였으나 제 나이보다 스무 살은 더 들어 보였고, 몸집이 우람하고 목소리가 크며 얼굴이 붉고 무뚝뚝했으며 섬세함이라고는 도무지 찾아볼 수 없는 약점이 있었는데, (도덕적으로든 신체적으로든) 무리와 대화 속으로 어깨를 들이밀며 밀고 들어가는 저돌적인 방식을 지녔으니, 이는 그가 인생에서도 어깨로 밀치며 출세해 나가리라는 것을 능히 짐작케 했다."]], [["He still had his wig and gown on, and he said, squaring himself at his late client to that degree that he squeezed the innocent Mr.", "그는 아직도 가발과 법복을 걸친 채, 자신의 방금 전 의뢰인을 향해 어찌나 떡 버티고 섰던지 죄 없는 로리 씨를 무리에서 말끔히 밀어내 버리며 이렇게 말했다."], ["Lorry clean out of the group: “I am glad to have brought you off with honour, Mr. Darnay.", "\"명예롭게 당신을 빼내드려 기쁩니다, 다네이 씨."], ["It was an infamous prosecution, grossly infamous; but not the less likely to succeed on that account.”", "그것은 파렴치한 기소였습니다, 지독히도 파렴치한 기소였지요. 그렇다고 해서 성공할 가능성이 덜한 것도 아니었지만 말입니다.\""]], [["“You have laid me under an obligation to you for life--in two senses,”", "\"당신은 두가지 의미에서 평생 갚을 신세를 제게 지우셨습니다.\""], ["said his late client, taking his hand.", "방금 전 그의 의뢰인이 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다."]], [["“I have done my best for you, Mr.", "\"당신을 위해 최선을 다했습니다, 다네이 씨."], ["Darnay; and my best is as good as another man’s, I believe.”", "그리고 제 최선은 다른 누구의 최선 못지않다고 믿습니다.\""]], [["It clearly being incumbent on some one to say, “Much better,” Mr.", "여기서 누군가가 \"훨씬 더 낫지요\"라고 말해야 마땅했으므로, 로리 씨가 그 말을 했다."], ["Lorry said it; perhaps not quite disinterestedly, but with the interested object of squeezing himself back again.", "어쩌면 전혀 사심이 없었던 것은 아니고, 도로 자기를 비집고 들어가려는 사심 어린 목적에서였을 테지만 말이다."]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 생각하시오?\""], ["said Mr. Stryver.", "스트라이버 씨가 말했다."], ["“Well!", "\"음!"], ["you have been present all day, and you ought to know.", "당신은 온종일 자리에 계셨으니 잘 아시겠지요."], ["You are a man of business, too.”", "당신 또한 사업가이시니까.\""]], [["“And as such,” quoth Mr.", "\"그리고 사업가로서,\""], ["Lorry, whom the counsel learned in the law had now shouldered back into the group, just as he had previously shouldered him out of it--“as such I will appeal to Doctor Manette, to break up this conference and order us all to our homes.", "법에 정통한 그 변호사가 앞서 무리에서 그를 밀어냈던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 이제 다시 무리 안으로 밀어 넣어준 로리 씨가 입을 열었다. \"사업가로서 저는 마네뜨 박사께 호소하여 이 모임을 끝내고 우리 모두를 각자의 집으로 보내자고 명해 주시길 청하겠습니다."], ["Miss Lucie looks ill, Mr. Darnay has had a terrible day, we are worn out.”", "루시 양은 안색이 좋지 않고, 다네이 씨는 끔찍한 하루를 보냈으며, 우리는 지칠 대로 지쳤으니까요.\""]], [["“Speak for yourself, Mr. Lorry,”", "\"당신 사정이나 말씀하시오, 로리 씨.\""], ["said Stryver; “I have a night’s work to do yet.", "스트라이버가 말했다. \"나는 아직 밤새 해야 할 일이 있소."], ["Speak for yourself.”", "당신 사정이나 말씀하시오.\""]], [["“I speak for myself,” answered Mr.", "\"제 사정을 말하는 겁니다.\""], ["Lorry, “and for Mr.", "로리 씨가 대답했다."], ["Darnay, and for Miss Lucie, and--Miss Lucie, do you not think I may speak for us all?”", "\"그리고 다네이 씨와 루시 양을 위해서도 말입니다. 그리고 — 루시 양, 제가 우리 모두를 대신해 말해도 되지 않겠습니까?\""], ["He asked her the question pointedly, and with a glance at her father.", "그는 그녀에게 또렷이 그 질문을 던지며, 그녀의 아버지를 흘끗 바라보았다."]], [["His face had become frozen, as it were, in a very curious look at Darnay: an intent look, deepening into a frown of dislike and distrust, not even unmixed with fear.", "그의 얼굴은 다네이를 향한 자못 기이한 표정으로, 말하자면 얼어붙어 있었다. 골똘한 그 눈길은 혐오와 불신의 찌푸림으로 깊어졌고, 거기엔 두려움마저 섞여 있었다."], ["With this strange expression on him his thoughts had wandered away.", "그 야릇한 표정을 띤 채 그의 생각은 어딘가로 떠돌아가 있었다."]], [["“My father,”", "\"아버지.\""], ["said Lucie, softly laying her hand on his.", "루시가 그의 손 위에 가만히 자신의 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["He slowly shook the shadow off, and turned to her.", "그는 천천히 그 그림자를 떨쳐내고 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“Shall we go home, my father?”", "\"집으로 갈까요, 아버지?\""]], [["With a long breath, he answered “Yes.”", "긴 숨을 내쉬며 그는 \"그래\"라고 대답했다."]], [["The friends of the acquitted prisoner had dispersed, under the impression--which he himself had originated--that he would not be released that night.", "무죄 방면된 죄수의 친구들은, 그가 그날 밤에는 풀려나지 못하리라는 인상을 받고 — 그 인상은 다네이 자신이 퍼뜨린 것이었다 — 이미 흩어진 뒤였다."], ["The lights were nearly all extinguished in the passages, the iron gates were being closed with a jar and a rattle, and the dismal place was deserted until to-morrow morning’s interest of gallows, pillory, whipping-post, and branding-iron, should repeople it.", "통로의 불은 거의 다 꺼졌고, 철문은 삐걱대고 덜컹대는 소리와 함께 닫히고 있었으며, 그 음산한 곳은 이튿날 아침 교수대와 칼, 채찍 기둥과 낙인 인두에 쏠릴 관심이 다시 사람들을 불러 모을 때까지 텅 비어 있을 터였다."], ["Walking between her father and Mr. Darnay, Lucie Manette passed into the open air.", "아버지와 다네이 씨 사이를 걸어, 루시 마네뜨는 바깥 공기 속으로 나섰다."], ["A hackney-coach was called, and the father and daughter departed in it.", "삯마차가 불려 왔고, 아버지와 딸은 그것을 타고 떠났다."]], [["Mr. Stryver had left them in the passages, to shoulder his way back to the robing-room.", "스트라이버 씨는 통로에서 그들과 헤어져, 어깨로 밀치며 법복실로 되돌아갔다."], ["Another person, who had not joined the group, or interchanged a word with any one of them, but who had been leaning against the wall where its shadow was darkest, had silently strolled out after the rest, and had looked on until the coach drove away. He now stepped up to where Mr.", "그 무리에 끼지도, 그들 가운데 누구와도 말 한마디 나누지도 않은 채, 그림자가 가장 짙게 드리운 벽에 기대어 서 있던 또 한 사람이, 나머지 사람들의 뒤를 따라 소리 없이 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 나와, 마차가 떠나갈 때까지 지켜보았다."], ["Lorry and Mr. Darnay stood upon the pavement.", "그는 이제 로리 씨와 다네이 씨가 보도 위에 서 있는 곳으로 다가섰다."]], [["“So, Mr. Lorry!", "\"자, 로리 씨!"], ["Men of business may speak to Mr. Darnay now?”", "이제 사업가들이 다네이 씨께 말을 걸어도 되겠지요?\""]], [["Nobody had made any acknowledgment of Mr. Carton’s part in the day’s proceedings; nobody had known of it.", "그날의 재판 과정에서 카턴 씨가 맡은 몫을 인정해 준 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["He was unrobed, and was none the better for it in appearance.", "아무도 그것을 알지 못했다. 그는 법복을 벗고 있었는데, 그렇다고 겉모습이 더 나아 보이지도 않았다."]], [["“If you knew what a conflict goes on in the business mind, when the business mind is divided between good-natured impulse and business appearances, you would be amused, Mr. Darnay.”", "\"마음씨 좋은 충동과 사업가의 체면 사이에서 사업가의 마음이 갈라질 때, 그 안에서 어떤 갈등이 벌어지는지 아신다면, 다네이 씨, 재미있어하실 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry reddened, and said, warmly, “You have mentioned that before, sir.", "로리 씨는 얼굴을 붉히며 발끈해서 말했다."], ["We men of business, who serve a House, are not our own masters.", "\"그 말씀은 전에도 하셨소이다, 선생. 우리 사업가들은, 어느 상회를 섬기는 몸이라 제 마음대로 할 수 없는 처지요."], ["We have to think of the House more than ourselves.”", "우리는 우리 자신보다 상회를 더 생각해야 하는 사람들이오.\""]], [["“I know, I know,” rejoined Mr.", "\"나도 알아요, 나도 압니다.\""], ["Carton, carelessly.", "카턴 씨가 무심하게 대꾸했다."], ["“Don’t be nettled, Mr. Lorry.", "\"발끈하지 마시오, 로리 씨."], ["You are as good as another, I have no doubt: better, I dare say.”", "당신은 다른 누구 못지않게 훌륭한 사람이오, 의심할 바 없이. 감히 말하건대, 더 낫지요.\""]], [["“And indeed, sir,” pursued Mr.", "\"그리고 정말이지, 선생,\""], ["Lorry, not minding him, “I really don’t know what you have to do with the matter.", "로리 씨가 그에게 개의치 않고 말을 이었다. \"이 일과 당신이 무슨 상관인지 도무지 모르겠소."], ["If you’ll excuse me, as very much your elder, for saying so, I really don’t know that it is your business.”", "당신보다 한참 연장자로서 이런 말을 하는 것을 양해해 주신다면, 정말이지 이게 당신이 관여할 일인지 모르겠단 말이오.\""]], [["“Business!", "\"관여할 일이라!"], ["Bless you, I have no business,”", "원 별말씀을, 나야 관여할 일이라곤 아무것도 없소이다.\""], ["said Mr. Carton.", "카턴 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It is a pity you have not, sir.”", "\"그것 참 안된 일이군요, 선생.\""]], [["“I think so, too.”", "\"나도 그렇게 생각하오.\""]], [["“If you had,” pursued Mr.", "\"관여할 일이 있었더라면,\""], ["Lorry, “perhaps you would attend to it.”", "로리 씨가 말을 이었다. \"아마 그 일에 신경을 쓰셨겠지요.\""]], [["“Lord love you, no!--I shouldn’t,”", "\"맙소사, 천만에요! 그러지 않았을 겁니다.\""], ["said Mr. Carton.", "카턴 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Well, sir!” cried Mr.", "\"이거 참, 선생!\""], ["Lorry, thoroughly heated by his indifference, “business is a very good thing, and a very respectable thing.", "그의 무관심에 완전히 달아오른 로리 씨가 소리쳤다. \"일이란 아주 좋은 것이고, 아주 존경할 만한 것이오."], ["And, sir, if business imposes its restraints and its silences and impediments, Mr. Darnay as a young gentleman of generosity knows how to make allowance for that circumstance.", "그리고 선생, 만일 일이 그 나름의 제약과 침묵과 방해를 강요한다 해도, 다네이 씨는 너그러운 젊은 신사로서 그런 사정을 어떻게 헤아려야 하는지 아실 거요."], ["Mr. Darnay, good night, God bless you, sir!", "다네이 씨, 안녕히 가십시오, 신의 가호가 함께하기를!"], ["I hope you have been this day preserved for a prosperous and happy life.--Chair there!”", "오늘 당신이 번창하고 행복한 삶을 위해 살아남으셨기를 바랍니다. — 가마 여기로!\""]], [["Perhaps a little angry with himself, as well as with the barrister, Mr. Lorry bustled into the chair, and was carried off to Tellson’s.", "어쩌면 그 변호사뿐 아니라 자기 자신에게도 조금 화가 났던지, 로리 씨는 허둥지둥 가마에 올라 텔슨 상회로 실려 갔다."], ["Carton, who smelt of port wine, and did not appear to be quite sober, laughed then, and turned to Darnay:", "포도주 냄새를 풍기며 도무지 멀쩡히 깨어 있는 것 같지 않은 카턴은, 그제야 웃음을 터뜨리며 다네이를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“This is a strange chance that throws you and me together.", "\"당신과 나를 한자리에 던져놓다니, 이것 참 묘한 우연이군요."], ["This must be a strange night to you, standing alone here with your counterpart on these street stones?”", "이 거리의 돌바닥 위에 당신의 분신과 단둘이 서 있다니, 당신에게는 참 묘한 밤이겠습니다?\""]], [["“I hardly seem yet,”", "\"아직은 도무지,\""], ["returned Charles Darnay, “to belong to this world again.”", "찰스 다네이가 대답했다. \"이 세상에 다시 속한 것 같지가 않습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t wonder at it; it’s not so long since you were pretty far advanced on your way to another.", "\"놀랄 것도 없지요. 당신이 또 다른 세상으로 가는 길에 꽤 멀리까지 나아갔던 게 그리 오래된 일도 아니니까요."], ["You speak faintly.”", "목소리에 기운이 없군요.\""]], [["“I begin to think I am faint.”", "\"제가 정말 기운이 없는 모양이라는 생각이 들기 시작하는군요.\""]], [["“Then why the devil don’t you dine?", "\"그럼 도대체 왜 식사를 안 하시오?"], ["I dined, myself, while those numskulls were deliberating which world you should belong to--this, or some other.", "나는, 저 멍청이들이 당신이 이 세상에 속할지 다른 세상에 속할지를 따지고 있는 동안에 혼자 식사를 했소."], ["Let me show you the nearest tavern to dine well at.”", "잘 먹을 수 있는 가장 가까운 선술집을 알려드리리다.\""]], [["Drawing his arm through his own, he took him down Ludgate-hill to Fleet-street, and so, up a covered way, into a tavern.", "그는 다네이의 팔을 자기 팔에 끼고는, 그를 데리고 러드게이트 언덕을 내려가 플리트 거리로, 그리고 지붕 덮인 통로를 따라 올라가 어느 선술집으로 들어갔다."], ["Here, they were shown into a little room, where Charles Darnay was soon recruiting his strength with a good plain dinner and good wine: while Carton sat opposite to him at the same table, with his separate bottle of port before him, and his fully half-insolent manner upon him.", "거기서 그들은 작은 방으로 안내되었고, 찰스 다네이는 곧 변변한 정식과 좋은 포도주로 기력을 되찾고 있었다. 그동안 카턴은 같은 식탁의 맞은편에 앉아, 제 몫의 포트와인 병을 앞에 두고, 절반쯤은 거만한 태도를 잔뜩 부린 채였다."]], [["“Do you feel, yet, that you belong to this terrestrial scheme again, Mr. Darnay?”", "\"이제 다시 이 지상의 질서에 속해 있다는 게 실감이 나시오, 다네이 씨?\""]], [["“I am frightfully confused regarding time and place; but I am so far mended as to feel that.”", "\"시간과 장소에 관해서는 끔찍하게 혼란스럽습니다. 그래도 그 정도는 느낄 만큼은 회복되었습니다.\""]], [["“It must be an immense satisfaction!”", "\"그거야말로 엄청난 만족이겠소이다!\""]], [["He said it bitterly, and filled up his glass again: which was a large one.", "그는 그 말을 씁쓸하게 내뱉고는 다시 잔을 채웠다. 큼직한 잔이었다."]], [["“As to me, the greatest desire I have, is to forget that I belong to it.", "\"나로 말하면, 내가 가진 가장 큰 소망은 내가 이 세상에 속해 있다는 사실을 잊는 것이오."], ["It has no good in it for me--except wine like this--nor I for it.", "이 세상에는 나를 위한 좋은 것이라곤 없소 — 이런 포도주를 빼면 말이오 — 나 또한 이 세상을 위한 좋은 것이 못 되고."], ["So we are not much alike in that particular.", "그러니 그 점에서 우리는 별로 닮지 않았소."], ["Indeed, I begin to think we are not much alike in any particular, you and I.”", "사실, 당신과 나는 그 어떤 점에서도 별로 닮지 않았다는 생각이 들기 시작하는구려.\""]], [["Confused by the emotion of the day, and feeling his being there with this Double of coarse deportment, to be like a dream, Charles Darnay was at a loss how to answer; finally, answered not at all.", "그날의 격정에 혼란스러운데다, 거동이 거친 이 분신과 이 자리에 함께 있는 것이 마치 꿈만 같아서, 찰스 다네이는 무어라 대답해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했고, 끝내 아무 대답도 하지 못했다."]], [["“Now your dinner is done,”", "\"이제 식사를 마치셨으니,\""], ["Carton presently said, “why don’t you call a health, Mr.", "카턴이 이윽고 말했다. \"건배 한번 청하시는 게 어떻소, 다네이 씨."], ["Darnay; why don’t you give your toast?”", "축배를 드는 게 어떻소?\""]], [["“What health?", "\"무엇을 위한 건배요?"], ["What toast?”", "무슨 축배 말이오?\""]], [["“Why, it’s on the tip of your tongue.", "\"이런, 그건 당신 혀끝에 맴돌고 있잖소."], ["It ought to be, it must be, I’ll swear it’s there.”", "그래야 마땅하고, 분명히 그럴 테고, 맹세코 거기 있을 거요.\""]], [["“Miss Manette, then!”", "\"그럼, 마네뜨 양을 위하여!\""]], [["“Miss Manette, then!”", "\"그럼, 마네뜨 양을 위하여!\""]], [["Looking his companion full in the face while he drank the toast, Carton flung his glass over his shoulder against the wall, where it shivered to pieces; then, rang the bell, and ordered in another.", "축배를 들이켜는 동안 일행의 얼굴을 정면으로 바라보던 카턴은, 잔을 어깨 너머로 벽에다 내던졌고, 잔은 산산조각이 났다. 그러고는 종을 울려 또 한 잔을 가져오라 일렀다."]], [["“That’s a fair young lady to hand to a coach in the dark, Mr. Darnay!”", "\"어둠 속에서 마차에 부축해 태워줄 만한 어여쁜 아가씨더군요, 다네이 씨!\""], ["he said, filling his new goblet.", "그는 새 술잔을 채우며 말했다."]], [["A slight frown and a laconic “Yes,” were the answer.", "가벼운 찌푸림과 짤막한 \"그렇소\"가 그 대답이었다."]], [["“That’s a fair young lady to be pitied by and wept for by!", "\"동정받고 눈물 흘려질 만한 어여쁜 아가씨라니!"], ["How does it feel?", "기분이 어떻소?"], ["Is it worth being tried for one’s life, to be the object of such sympathy and compassion, Mr. Darnay?”", "그런 동정과 연민의 대상이 되는 것이, 목숨을 걸고 재판을 받을 만한 가치가 있는 일이오, 다네이 씨?\""]], [["Again Darnay answered not a word.", "다네이는 또다시 한마디도 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“She was mightily pleased to have your message, when I gave it her.", "\"내가 당신 전갈을 전해주었을 때, 그녀는 그걸 받고 무척이나 기뻐하더군요."], ["Not that she showed she was pleased, but I suppose she was.”", "기뻐하는 기색을 내보인 건 아니지만, 기뻐했으리라 짐작하오.\""]], [["The allusion served as a timely reminder to Darnay that this disagreeable companion had, of his own free will, assisted him in the strait of the day.", "그 언급은 이 불쾌한 일행이 그날의 위기에서 제 발로 자청해 자신을 도와주었다는 사실을, 다네이에게 때맞춰 일깨워 주었다."], ["He turned the dialogue to that point, and thanked him for it.", "그는 대화를 그 점으로 돌려, 그에게 그 일에 대해 감사를 표했다."]], [["“I neither want any thanks, nor merit any,”", "\"나는 감사 따위 바라지도 않고, 받을 자격도 없소.\""], ["was the careless rejoinder.", "무심한 대꾸였다."], ["“It was nothing to do, in the first place; and I don’t know why I did it, in the second.", "\"우선 그건 아무것도 아닌 일이었고, 둘째로 내가 왜 그랬는지도 모르겠소."], ["Mr. Darnay, let me ask you a question.”", "다네이 씨, 한가지 물어봅시다.\""]], [["“Willingly, and a small return for your good offices.”", "\"기꺼이요. 당신의 호의에 대한 작은 보답이 되겠지요.\""]], [["“Do you think I particularly like you?”", "\"내가 당신을 특별히 좋아한다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["“Really, Mr. Carton,”", "\"솔직히, 카턴 씨,\""], ["returned the other, oddly disconcerted, “I have not asked myself the question.”", "묘하게 당혹스러워하며 상대가 대답했다. \"그런 질문을 제 자신에게 던져본 적이 없소이다.\""]], [["“But ask yourself the question now.”", "\"그럼 지금 자신에게 그 질문을 던져보시오.\""]], [["“You have acted as if you do; but I don’t think you do.”", "\"좋아하는 것처럼 행동하셨소. 하지만 좋아하시는 것 같지는 않습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t think I do,”", "\"나도 좋아하는 것 같지 않소.\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["“I begin to have a very good opinion of your understanding.”", "\"당신의 이해력에 대해 아주 좋게 보기 시작하는구려.\""]], [["“Nevertheless,”", "\"그렇더라도,\""], ["pursued Darnay, rising to ring the bell, “there is nothing in that, I hope, to prevent my calling the reckoning, and our parting without ill-blood on either side.”", "다네이가 종을 울리려고 일어서며 말을 이었다. \"그 때문에 제가 셈을 치르고, 양쪽 다 악감정 없이 헤어지지 못할 까닭은 없겠지요.\""]], [["Carton rejoining, “Nothing in life!” Darnay rang.", "카턴이 \"결단코 없소이다!\"라고 받자 다네이가 종을 울렸다."], ["“Do you call the whole reckoning?”", "\"계산을 통째로 치르시려오?\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["On his answering in the affirmative, “Then bring me another pint of this same wine, drawer, and come and wake me at ten.”", "그가 그렇다고 대답하자, \"그럼 같은 포도주로 한 파인트 더 가져오게, 종업원, 그리고 열시에 와서 나를 깨워주게.\""]], [["The bill being paid, Charles Darnay rose and wished him good night.", "계산을 마치고, 찰스 다네이는 일어서서 그에게 잘 자라는 인사를 건넸다."], ["Without returning the wish, Carton rose too, with something of a threat of defiance in his manner, and said, “A last word, Mr.", "그 인사에 답하지도 않은 채, 카턴 역시 어딘가 반항적인 위협의 기색을 풍기며 일어서서 말했다. \"마지막으로 한마디 하리다, 다네이 씨."], ["Darnay: you think I am drunk?”", "내가 취했다고 생각하시오?\""]], [["“I think you have been drinking, Mr. Carton.”", "\"술을 드신 것 같습니다, 카턴 씨.\""]], [["“Think?", "\"생각이라?"], ["You know I have been drinking.”", "내가 술을 마셨다는 걸 당신은 알고 있잖소.\""]], [["“Since I must say so, I know it.”", "\"꼭 말해야 한다면, 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Then you shall likewise know why.", "\"그렇다면 그 까닭도 알아두시오."], ["I am a disappointed drudge, sir.", "나는 실의에 빠진 막일꾼이오, 선생."], ["I care for no man on earth, and no man on earth cares for me.”", "나는 이 세상 어느 누구도 마음에 두지 않고, 이 세상 어느 누구도 나를 마음에 두지 않소.\""]], [["“Much to be regretted.", "\"몹시 안타까운 일이군요."], ["You might have used your talents better.”", "당신의 재능을 더 잘 쓰실 수도 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["“May be so, Mr.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요, 다네이 씨."], ["Darnay; may be not.", "아닐지도 모르고."], ["Don’t let your sober face elate you, however; you don’t know what it may come to.", "하지만 당신의 그 멀쩡한 얼굴로 우쭐대지는 마시오. 그게 어떻게 될지 당신은 모르니까."], ["Good night!”", "잘 가시오!\""]], [["When he was left alone, this strange being took up a candle, went to a glass that hung against the wall, and surveyed himself minutely in it.", "홀로 남게 되자, 이 기이한 사내는 촛불을 집어 들고 벽에 걸린 거울로 다가가, 그 안의 제 모습을 찬찬히 뜯어보았다."]], [["“Do you particularly like the man?”", "\"너는 저 사내를 특별히 좋아하느냐?\""], ["he muttered, at his own image; “why should you particularly like a man who resembles you?", "그는 거울에 비친 제 모습을 향해 중얼거렸다. \"어째서 너를 닮은 사내를 특별히 좋아해야 한단 말이냐?"], ["There is nothing in you to like; you know that.", "네 안에는 좋아할 만한 구석이라곤 없잖느냐."], ["Ah, confound you!", "너도 그건 알 테지."], ["What a change you have made in yourself!", "아, 빌어먹을 놈! 너 스스로를 얼마나 망쳐놓았느냐!"], ["A good reason for taking to a man, that he shows you what you have fallen away from, and what you might have been!", "어떤 사내에게 마음이 끌릴 만한 그럴듯한 이유로구나, 그가 네가 어디서부터 굴러떨어졌는지, 네가 어떤 사람이 될 수도 있었는지를 보여준다는 것이!"], ["Change places with him, and would you have been looked at by those blue eyes as he was, and commiserated by that agitated face as he was?", "저자와 처지를 바꿔보아라, 그러면 너도 그가 그러했듯 저 푸른 눈동자의 바라봄을 받고, 저 떨리는 얼굴의 연민을 받았겠느냐?"], ["Come on, and have it out in plain words!", "자, 솔직한 말로 결판을 내보아라!"], ["You hate the fellow.”", "너는 저 녀석이 미운 게다.\""]], [["He resorted to his pint of wine for consolation, drank it all in a few minutes, and fell asleep on his arms, with his hair straggling over the table, and a long winding-sheet in the candle dripping down upon him.", "그는 위안을 얻으려 한 파인트의 포도주에 의지해, 몇 분 만에 그것을 죄다 들이켜고는, 두 팔에 엎드려 잠들어 버렸다. 머리카락이 식탁 위로 흐트러져 있었고, 촛농이 길게 늘어진 수의처럼 그의 위로 뚝뚝 떨어져 내렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. The Jackal", "# CHAPTER V. The Jackal"]], [["Those were drinking days, and most men drank hard.", "술을 마셔대는 시절이었고, 대부분의 사람들이 독하게 마셨다."], ["So very great is the improvement Time has brought about in such habits, that a moderate statement of the quantity of wine and punch which one man would swallow in the course of a night, without any detriment to his reputation as a perfect gentleman, would seem, in these days, a ridiculous exaggeration.", "그런 습관에서 세월이 가져다준 개선이 어찌나 큰지, 완벽한 신사로서의 명성에 아무런 손상도 입지 않은 채 한 사람이 하룻밤 사이에 들이켤 수 있던 포도주와 펀치의 양을 적당히 진술하기만 해도, 요즘 사람들에게는 터무니없는 과장으로 보일 것이다."], ["The learned profession of the law was certainly not behind any other learned profession in its Bacchanalian propensities; neither was Mr.", "법이라는 학식 있는 직업도 바커스적 기질에서는 그 어떤 학식 있는 직업에도 결코 뒤지지 않았다."], ["Stryver, already fast shouldering his way to a large and lucrative practice, behind his compeers in this particular, any more than in the drier parts of the legal race.", "또한 이미 크고 수지맞는 변론 업무를 향해 빠르게 어깨로 밀치며 나아가고 있던 스트라이버 씨도, 법조계 경주의 더 메마른 부분에서와 마찬가지로 이 점에서도 자기 동료들에게 뒤지지 않았다."]], [["A favourite at the Old Bailey, and eke at the Sessions, Mr.", "* 바커스적: 술과 향락의 신 바커스(디오니소스)에서 유래한 표현으로, 흥청대며 술을 마시는 성향을 가리킨다."], ["Stryver had begun cautiously to hew away the lower staves of the ladder on which he mounted.", "올드 베일리에서 인기를 끌었고 순회재판에서도 마찬가지였던 스트라이버 씨는, 자신이 올라선 사다리의 아래쪽 가로대를 조심스레 잘라내기 시작했다."], ["Sessions and Old Bailey had now to summon their favourite, specially, to their longing arms; and shouldering itself towards the visage of the Lord Chief Justice in the Court of King’s Bench, the florid countenance of Mr. Stryver might be daily seen, bursting out of the bed of wigs, like a great sunflower pushing its way at the sun from among a rank garden-full of flaring companions.", "이제 순회재판과 올드 베일리는 그들의 총아를 특별히, 갈망하는 두 팔로 불러들여야 했으며, 왕좌재판소에서 대법관의 얼굴을 향해 어깨로 밀치고 나아가는 스트라이버 씨의 불그레한 얼굴은, 무성한 정원 가득 화려하게 피어난 동료 꽃들 사이에서 해를 향해 솟아오르는 커다란 해바라기처럼, 가발의 화단을 뚫고 솟구치는 모습을 날마다 볼 수 있었다."]], [["It had once been noted at the Bar, that while Mr.", "* 올드 베일리: 런던의 중앙형사재판소."], ["Stryver was a glib man, and an unscrupulous, and a ready, and a bold, he had not that faculty of extracting the essence from a heap of statements, which is among the most striking and necessary of the advocate’s accomplishments.", "법조계에서 한때 주목된 바, 스트라이버 씨는 말솜씨 좋고, 거리낌 없으며, 재빠르고, 대담한 사람이었으나, 변호사의 가장 두드러지고 가장 필요한 자질에 속하는, 진술 더미에서 핵심을 뽑아내는 능력만은 갖추지 못했다."], ["But, a remarkable improvement came upon him as to this.", "그러나 이 점에서 그에게 놀라운 진전이 찾아왔다."], ["The more business he got, the greater his power seemed to grow of getting at its pith and marrow; and however late at night he sat carousing with Sydney Carton, he always had his points at his fingers’ ends in the morning.", "일을 더 많이 맡을수록 그 골수와 정수를 파고드는 그의 능력은 점점 더 커지는 듯했으며, 밤늦도록 시드니 카턴과 흥청거리며 앉아 있었어도 아침이면 늘 요점을 손끝에 쥐고 있었다."]], [["Sydney Carton, idlest and most unpromising of men, was Stryver’s great ally.", "사람들 가운데 가장 게으르고 가장 가망 없는 자인 시드니 카턴은 스트라이버의 든든한 동맹이었다."], ["What the two drank together, between Hilary Term and Michaelmas, might have floated a king’s ship.", "두 사람이 힐러리 개정기와 미카엘 축일 사이에 함께 마셔댄 술이라면 왕의 군함이라도 띄울 수 있었을 것이다. 스트라이버가 어디서든 사건을 맡을 때면 카턴이 늘 거기 있었으니, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣은 채 법정 천장을 응시하고 있었다."], ["Stryver never had a case in hand, anywhere, but Carton was there, with his hands in his pockets, staring at the ceiling of the court; they went the same Circuit, and even there they prolonged their usual orgies late into the night, and Carton was rumoured to be seen at broad day, going home stealthily and unsteadily to his lodgings, like a dissipated cat.", "둘은 같은 순회재판을 돌았고, 거기서도 늘 하던 흥청망청을 밤늦도록 이어갔으며, 카턴은 환한 대낮에 타락한 고양이처럼 휘청거리며 슬그머니 하숙집으로 돌아가는 모습이 목격된다는 소문이 돌았다. 마침내 이 일에 관심 있는 자들 사이에서는, 시드니 카턴이 결코 사자는 되지 못하겠지만 놀랍도록 훌륭한 자칼이며, 그 미천한 자격으로 스트라이버에게 봉사와 시중을 든다는 말이 퍼지기 시작했다."], ["At last, it began to get about, among such as were interested in the matter, that although Sydney Carton would never be a lion, he was an amazingly good jackal, and that he rendered suit and service to Stryver in that humble capacity.", "* 힐러리 개정기와 미카엘 축일: 영국 법정의 개정기를 나타내는 구분. 힐러리 개정기는 겨울철, 미카엘 축일은 9월 29일로 가을철 개정기를 가리킨다."]], [["“Ten o’clock, sir,”", "\"열시입니다, 손님.\""], ["said the man at the tavern, whom he had charged to wake him--“ten o’clock, sir.”", "술집에서 그가 깨워달라고 일러둔 남자가 말했다. \"열시입니다, 손님.\""]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["“Ten o’clock, sir.”", "\"열시입니다, 손님.\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["Ten o’clock at night?”", "밤 열시라고?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"네, 손님."], ["Your honour told me to call you.”", "깨워달라고 하셨잖습니까.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I remember.", "생각났어."], ["Very well, very well.”", "됐어, 됐어.\""]], [["After a few dull efforts to get to sleep again, which the man dexterously combated by stirring the fire continuously for five minutes, he got up, tossed his hat on, and walked out.", "다시 잠들어 보려는 멍한 시도를 몇번 했으나, 남자가 오 분 동안 끊임없이 불을 들쑤시며 능숙하게 막아냈고, 그는 일어나 모자를 휙 눌러쓰고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["He turned into the Temple, and, having revived himself by twice pacing the pavements of King’s Bench-walk and Paper-buildings, turned into the Stryver chambers.", "그는 템플로 접어들어, 왕좌재판소 보도와 페이퍼 빌딩의 포도(鋪道)를 두 차례 거닐며 정신을 추스른 뒤, 스트라이버의 사무실로 들어섰다."]], [["The Stryver clerk, who never assisted at these conferences, had gone home, and the Stryver principal opened the door.", "이런 회합에는 한번도 거든 적 없는 스트라이버의 서기는 집으로 돌아갔고, 주인장 스트라이버가 문을 열었다."], ["He had his slippers on, and a loose bed-gown, and his throat was bare for his greater ease.", "그는 슬리퍼를 신고 헐렁한 실내복을 걸쳤으며, 한결 편안하라고 목을 풀어헤치고 있었다."], ["He had that rather wild, strained, seared marking about the eyes, which may be observed in all free livers of his class, from the portrait of Jeffries downward, and which can be traced, under various disguises of Art, through the portraits of every Drinking Age.", "그의 눈가에는 다소 사납고, 긴장되고, 그을린 듯한 자국이 있었는데, 이는 제프리스의 초상화 이래로 그와 같은 부류의 모든 방탕한 자들에게서 볼 수 있는 것이며, 온갖 예술적 분장 아래서도 모든 음주 시대의 초상화마다 더듬어 찾아낼 수 있는 것이었다. * 제프리스: 17세기 영국의 악명 높은 판사 조지 제프리스를 가리킨다."]], [["“You are a little late, Memory,”", "\"좀 늦었군, 기억력 양반.\""], ["said Stryver.", "스트라이버가 말했다."]], [["“About the usual time; it may be a quarter of an hour later.”", "\"늘 오던 시간쯤이지. 십오 분쯤 늦었을지도.\""]], [["They went into a dingy room lined with books and littered with papers, where there was a blazing fire.", "그들은 책으로 둘러싸이고 서류가 어질러진 침침한 방으로 들어갔는데, 거기에는 활활 타오르는 불이 있었다."], ["A kettle steamed upon the hob, and in the midst of the wreck of papers a table shone, with plenty of wine upon it, and brandy, and rum, and sugar, and lemons.", "화로 선반 위에서 주전자가 김을 뿜었고, 서류 잔해 한가운데 탁자가 번들거렸으니, 그 위에는 포도주와 브랜디와 럼과 설탕과 레몬이 그득했다."]], [["“You have had your bottle, I perceive, Sydney.”", "\"보아하니 자네 술을 한병 비웠군, 시드니.\""]], [["“Two to-night, I think.", "\"오늘 밤엔 두 병인 것 같은데. 오늘 의뢰인과 저녁을 먹었거든."], ["I have been dining with the day’s client; or seeing him dine--it’s all one!”", "아니, 그가 먹는 걸 지켜봤다고나 할까. 그게 그거지!\""]], [["“That was a rare point, Sydney, that you brought to bear upon the identification.", "\"오늘 신원 확인에 자네가 끌어댄 그 논점은 보기 드문 것이었어, 시드니."], ["How did you come by it?", "그걸 어떻게 떠올렸나?"], ["When did it strike you?”", "언제 생각이 났어?\""]], [["“I thought he was rather a handsome fellow, and I thought I should have been much the same sort of fellow, if I had had any luck.”", "\"그자가 꽤 잘생긴 친구라고 생각했고, 나도 운이 좀 따랐다면 그와 별반 다르지 않은 부류가 되었으리라고 생각했지.\""]], [["Mr. Stryver laughed till he shook his precocious paunch.", "스트라이버 씨는 일찌감치 나온 똥배가 출렁이도록 웃어댔다."]], [["“You and your luck, Sydney!", "\"자네하고 자네 운이라니, 시드니!"], ["Get to work, get to work.”", "일이나 하게, 일이나 해.\""]], [["Sullenly enough, the jackal loosened his dress, went into an adjoining room, and came back with a large jug of cold water, a basin, and a towel or two.", "자칼은 시무룩하게 옷매무새를 풀고, 옆방으로 가서 찬물이 가득 든 커다란 항아리와 대야와 수건 한두 장을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["Steeping the towels in the water, and partially wringing them out, he folded them on his head in a manner hideous to behold, sat down at the table, and said, “Now I am ready!”", "수건을 물에 적셔 대충 짜낸 뒤, 보기에 끔찍한 모양새로 머리에 둘둘 감고는, 탁자에 앉아 말했다. \"자, 준비됐어!\""]], [["“Not much boiling down to be done to-night, Memory,” said Mr.", "\"오늘 밤엔 졸여낼 게 그리 많지 않아, 기억력 양반.\""], ["Stryver, gaily, as he looked among his papers.", "스트라이버 씨가 서류를 뒤적이며 유쾌하게 말했다."]], [["“How much?”", "\"얼마나 되는데?\""]], [["“Only two sets of them.”", "\"두 묶음뿐이야.\""]], [["“Give me the worst first.”", "\"제일 고약한 것부터 줘.\""]], [["“There they are, Sydney.", "\"여기 있네, 시드니."], ["Fire away!”", "자, 시작!\""]], [["The lion then composed himself on his back on a sofa on one side of the drinking-table, while the jackal sat at his own paper-bestrewn table proper, on the other side of it, with the bottles and glasses ready to his hand.", "그러고 나서 사자는 술 탁자 한쪽에 놓인 소파에 등을 대고 자리를 잡았고, 자칼은 그 맞은편에 놓인 자기 서류투성이 탁자에 앉았는데, 술병과 잔들을 손 닿는 곳에 갖춰두고 있었다. 둘 다 거리낌 없이 술 탁자에 의지했으나, 저마다 방식이 달랐다."], ["Both resorted to the drinking-table without stint, but each in a different way; the lion for the most part reclining with his hands in his waistband, looking at the fire, or occasionally flirting with some lighter document; the jackal, with knitted brows and intent face, so deep in his task, that his eyes did not even follow the hand he stretched out for his glass--which often groped about, for a minute or more, before it found the glass for his lips.", "사자는 대개 두 손을 허리띠에 찔러넣은 채 비스듬히 기대어 불을 바라보거나, 이따금 가벼운 문서 따위를 건성으로 들춰보았다. 자칼은 미간을 찌푸린 채 골똘한 얼굴로 일에 얼마나 깊이 빠져 있던지, 잔을 향해 뻗은 손조차 눈으로 좇지 않았으니, 그 손은 종종 입에 댈 잔을 찾기까지 일 분 남짓 더듬거리곤 했다."], ["Two or three times, the matter in hand became so knotty, that the jackal found it imperative on him to get up, and steep his towels anew.", "두세 번쯤 다루던 문제가 어찌나 까다로워지던지, 자칼은 일어나 수건을 새로 적셔야만 한다고 느꼈다."], ["From these pilgrimages to the jug and basin, he returned with such eccentricities of damp headgear as no words can describe; which were made the more ludicrous by his anxious gravity.", "항아리와 대야로의 이 순례에서 그는 말로는 도저히 형용할 수 없을 만큼 기괴한 모양으로 축축한 머리쓰개를 두르고 돌아왔는데, 그 진지하고 안달하는 표정 때문에 한층 더 우스꽝스러웠다."]], [["At length the jackal had got together a compact repast for the lion, and proceeded to offer it to him.", "마침내 자칼은 사자를 위한 알찬 한 끼 식사를 차려내어 그에게 내놓았다."], ["The lion took it with care and caution, made his selections from it, and his remarks upon it, and the jackal assisted both.", "사자는 그것을 조심스럽고 신중하게 받아들어, 그 가운데서 골라내고 그것에 대해 논평했으며, 자칼은 그 두 가지를 다 거들었다."], ["When the repast was fully discussed, the lion put his hands in his waistband again, and lay down to meditate.", "그 식사가 충분히 검토되자, 사자는 다시 두 손을 허리띠에 찔러넣고 누워 깊이 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The jackal then invigorated himself with a bumper for his throttle, and a fresh application to his head, and applied himself to the collection of a second meal; this was administered to the lion in the same manner, and was not disposed of until the clocks struck three in the morning.", "그러자 자칼은 목구멍을 위해 한 잔 가득 들이켜고 머리에 다시 한번 물을 적셔 기운을 차린 뒤, 두번째 식사를 차리는 일에 매달렸다. 이것 역시 같은 방식으로 사자에게 제공되었고, 새벽 세시를 알리는 종이 울릴 때까지 처리되지 않았다."]], [["“And now we have done, Sydney, fill a bumper of punch,”", "\"자, 이제 끝났으니, 시드니, 펀치를 한 잔 가득 채우게.\""], ["said Mr. Stryver.", "스트라이버 씨가 말했다."]], [["The jackal removed the towels from his head, which had been steaming again, shook himself, yawned, shivered, and complied.", "자칼은 다시 김을 뿜고 있던 수건을 머리에서 벗겨내고, 몸을 한번 떨고, 하품을 하고, 부르르 진저리를 치고는, 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["“You were very sound, Sydney, in the matter of those crown witnesses to-day.", "\"오늘 그 검찰측 증인들 건에서 자네는 아주 빈틈없었어, 시드니."], ["Every question told.”", "질문마다 먹혀들었지.\""]], [["“I always am sound; am I not?”", "\"난 늘 빈틈없잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["“I don’t gainsay it.", "\"부정하진 않겠네."], ["What has roughened your temper?", "무엇이 자네 성미를 거칠게 했나?"], ["Put some punch to it and smooth it again.”", "펀치를 좀 부어 다시 매끄럽게 가라앉히게.\""]], [["With a deprecatory grunt, the jackal again complied.", "자칼은 못마땅한 듯 투덜대며 또다시 시키는 대로 했다."]], [["“The old Sydney Carton of old Shrewsbury School,”", "\"옛 슈루즈베리 학교 시절의 옛 시드니 카턴이로군.\""], ["said Stryver, nodding his head over him as he reviewed him in the present and the past, “the old seesaw Sydney.", "스트라이버는 그를 현재와 과거 속에서 찬찬히 살피며 머리 위로 고개를 끄덕였다. \"그 옛 시소 같은 시드니."], ["Up one minute and down the next; now in spirits and now in despondency!”", "금세 올라갔다가 다음 순간엔 내려가고, 한때는 기운이 넘쳤다가 또 한때는 풀이 죽고!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["returned the other, sighing: “yes!", "상대가 한숨을 쉬며 대꾸했다. \"그래!"], ["The same Sydney, with the same luck.", "똑같은 시드니에, 똑같은 운이지."], ["Even then, I did exercises for other boys, and seldom did my own.”", "그때도 난 다른 애들 숙제를 대신 해주면서 정작 내 것은 좀처럼 하지 않았어.\""]], [["“And why not?”", "\"그건 또 왜?\""]], [["“God knows.", "\"누가 알겠나."], ["It was my way, I suppose.”", "그게 내 방식이었던 모양이지.\""]], [["He sat, with his hands in his pockets and his legs stretched out before him, looking at the fire.", "그는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러넣고 두 다리를 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 채 앉아, 불을 바라보았다."]], [["“Carton,”", "\"카턴.\""], ["said his friend, squaring himself at him with a bullying air, as if the fire-grate had been the furnace in which sustained endeavour was forged, and the one delicate thing to be done for the old Sydney Carton of old Shrewsbury School was to shoulder him into it, “your way is, and always was, a lame way.", "그의 친구가 윽박지르는 태도로 그를 향해 자세를 곧추세우며 말했는데, 마치 그 난로가 꾸준한 노력을 벼려내는 용광로이고, 옛 슈루즈베리 학교 시절의 옛 시드니 카턴을 위해 해줄 단 하나의 섬세한 일이란 그를 그 안으로 떠밀어 넣는 것이기라도 한 듯했다. \"자네 방식은 절름발이 같은 방식이고, 늘 그래왔어."], ["You summon no energy and purpose.", "자넨 도무지 기력도 목적도 끌어모으질 못해."], ["Look at me.”", "나를 보게.\""]], [["“Oh, botheration!”", "\"아, 성가셔 죽겠군!\""], ["returned Sydney, with a lighter and more good-humoured laugh, “don’t you be moral!”", "시드니는 한결 가볍고 더 너그러운 웃음으로 대꾸했다. \"제발 자네가 도덕군자 노릇 좀 하지 마!\""]], [["“How have I done what I have done?”", "\"내가 어떻게 지금의 이걸 이뤘겠나?\""], ["said Stryver; “how do I do what I do?”", "스트라이버가 말했다. \"내가 어떻게 지금의 이 일을 해내겠어?\""]], [["“Partly through paying me to help you, I suppose.", "\"내게 돈을 주어 자네를 돕게 한 덕도 얼마간 있겠지."], ["But it’s not worth your while to apostrophise me, or the air, about it; what you want to do, you do.", "하지만 그 일로 나를, 아니면 허공을 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓을 가치는 없어. 자넨 하고 싶은 일은 하고 마는 사람이야."], ["You were always in the front rank, and I was always behind.”", "자넨 늘 맨 앞줄에 있었고, 난 늘 뒤에 있었지.\""]], [["“I had to get into the front rank; I was not born there, was I?”", "\"난 맨 앞줄로 비집고 들어가야만 했네. 거기서 태어난 게 아니잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["“I was not present at the ceremony; but my opinion is you were,”", "\"그 의식에 내가 참석한 건 아니지만, 내 생각엔 자넨 거기서 태어났어.\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["At this, he laughed again, and they both laughed.", "이 말에 그는 다시 웃었고, 둘 다 웃었다."]], [["“Before Shrewsbury, and at Shrewsbury, and ever since Shrewsbury,”", "\"슈루즈베리 이전에도, 슈루즈베리에서도, 슈루즈베리 이후로도 줄곧.\""], ["pursued Carton, “you have fallen into your rank, and I have fallen into mine.", "카턴이 말을 이었다. \"자넨 자네 자리로 떨어졌고, 난 내 자리로 떨어졌어."], ["Even when we were fellow-students in the Student-Quarter of Paris, picking up French, and French law, and other French crumbs that we didn’t get much good of, you were always somewhere, and I was always nowhere.”", "우리가 빠리의 학생 구역에서 동급생으로 지내며 프랑스어와 프랑스 법, 그밖에 별 쓸모도 없던 프랑스의 부스러기들을 주워 모으던 시절에조차, 자넨 늘 어딘가에 있었고, 난 늘 아무 데도 없었지.\""]], [["“And whose fault was that?”", "\"그게 누구 탓이었나?\""]], [["“Upon my soul, I am not sure that it was not yours.", "\"맹세코, 그게 자네 탓이 아니라고는 장담 못하겠네."], ["You were always driving and riving and shouldering and passing, to that restless degree that I had no chance for my life but in rust and repose.", "자넨 늘 밀어붙이고 헤집고 어깨로 밀치고 앞질러 나갔는데, 그 쉴 줄 모르는 정도가 어찌나 심하던지, 나로선 녹슬어 가라앉아 쉬는 것 말고는 평생 기회가 없었어."], ["It’s a gloomy thing, however, to talk about one’s own past, with the day breaking.", "하지만 날이 밝아오는데 자기 과거를 이야기하는 건 음울한 일이야."], ["Turn me in some other direction before I go.”", "가기 전에 나를 다른 쪽으로 돌려놓게.\""]], [["“Well then!", "\"좋아, 그럼!"], ["Pledge me to the pretty witness,”", "그 어여쁜 증인을 위해 나와 함께 잔을 들지.\""], ["said Stryver, holding up his glass.", "스트라이버가 잔을 치켜들며 말했다."], ["“Are you turned in a pleasant direction?”", "\"이제 좀 유쾌한 쪽으로 돌아섰나?\""]], [["Apparently not, for he became gloomy again.", "아무래도 그렇지 못한 듯, 그는 다시 음울해졌다."]], [["“Pretty witness,”", "\"어여쁜 증인이라.\""], ["he muttered, looking down into his glass.", "그가 잔 속을 들여다보며 중얼거렸다."], ["“I have had enough of witnesses to-day and to-night; who’s your pretty witness?”", "\"오늘 낮과 밤에 증인이라면 신물이 나도록 봤어. 자네의 어여쁜 증인이 누구야?\""]], [["“The picturesque doctor’s daughter, Miss Manette.”", "\"그 그림 같은 의사의 딸, 마네뜨 양 말일세.\""]], [["“She pretty?”", "\"그 여자가 어여쁘다고?\""]], [["“Is she not?” “No.”", "\"어여쁘지 않은가?\""]], [["“Why, man alive, she was the admiration of the whole Court!”", "\"아니.\" \"이런, 이 사람아, 그 여자는 법정 전체의 찬탄을 받았다고!\""]], [["“Rot the admiration of the whole Court!", "\"법정 전체의 찬탄이라니 썩어빠질!"], ["Who made the Old Bailey a judge of beauty?", "누가 올드 베일리를 미모의 심판관으로 세웠나?"], ["She was a golden-haired doll!”", "그 여잔 금발 머리 인형이었어!\""]], [["“Do you know, Sydney,” said Mr.", "\"이보게, 시드니.\""], ["Stryver, looking at him with sharp eyes, and slowly drawing a hand across his florid face: “do you know, I rather thought, at the time, that you sympathised with the golden-haired doll, and were quick to see what happened to the golden-haired doll?”", "스트라이버 씨가 날카로운 눈으로 그를 바라보며, 불그레한 얼굴 위로 한 손을 천천히 쓸어내리며 말했다. \"이보게, 난 그때 어쩐지 자네가 그 금발 머리 인형에게 동정심을 품었고, 그 금발 머리 인형에게 무슨 일이 일어나는지 재빨리 알아챘다고 생각했네만?\""]], [["“Quick to see what happened!", "\"무슨 일이 일어나는지 재빨리 알아챘다고!"], ["If a girl, doll or no doll, swoons within a yard or two of a man’s nose, he can see it without a perspective-glass.", "한 처녀가, 인형이건 아니건, 사내 코앞 한두 야드 안에서 기절하면, 그야 망원경 없이도 보이는 법이지."], ["I pledge you, but I deny the beauty.", "자네와 잔은 들겠지만, 그 미모만은 인정 못해."], ["And now I’ll have no more drink; I’ll get to bed.”", "그리고 이제 술은 그만 마시겠어. 잠자리에 들어야겠네.\""]], [["When his host followed him out on the staircase with a candle, to light him down the stairs, the day was coldly looking in through its grimy windows.", "주인장이 그를 계단 아래로 비춰주려고 촛불을 들고 층계참까지 뒤따라 나왔을 때, 날은 때 낀 창문을 통해 싸늘하게 들여다보고 있었다."], ["When he got out of the house, the air was cold and sad, the dull sky overcast, the river dark and dim, the whole scene like a lifeless desert.", "그가 집에서 나오자 공기는 차갑고 서글펐으며, 흐릿한 하늘은 잔뜩 흐렸고, 강물은 어둡고 침침했으며, 온 풍경이 생기 없는 사막 같았다."], ["And wreaths of dust were spinning round and round before the morning blast, as if the desert-sand had risen far away, and the first spray of it in its advance had begun to overwhelm the city.", "그리고 아침 돌풍 앞에서 먼지 소용돌이가 빙글빙글 맴돌았으니, 마치 저 멀리서 사막의 모래가 일어나 그 진군의 첫 물보라가 도시를 뒤덮기 시작하기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["Waste forces within him, and a desert all around, this man stood still on his way across a silent terrace, and saw for a moment, lying in the wilderness before him, a mirage of honourable ambition, self-denial, and perseverance.", "안에는 허비된 힘을, 사방에는 사막을 둔 채, 이 사내는 적막한 광장을 가로질러 가던 길에 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 한순간 자기 앞의 황야에 누운, 명예로운 야망과 극기와 인내의 신기루를 보았다."], ["In the fair city of this vision, there were airy galleries from which the loves and graces looked upon him, gardens in which the fruits of life hung ripening, waters of Hope that sparkled in his sight.", "이 환상 속의 아름다운 도시에는 사랑과 우아함이 그를 내려다보는 허공의 회랑들이 있었고, 삶의 열매가 무르익어 매달린 정원들이 있었으며, 그의 눈앞에서 반짝이는 희망의 물결이 있었다."], ["A moment, and it was gone.", "한순간, 그것은 사라졌다."], ["Climbing to a high chamber in a well of houses, he threw himself down in his clothes on a neglected bed, and its pillow was wet with wasted tears.", "빼곡한 집들로 이루어진 우물 같은 곳의 높다란 방으로 올라가, 그는 옷을 입은 채로 방치된 침대에 몸을 던졌고, 그 베개는 헛되이 흘린 눈물로 젖었다."]], [["Sadly, sadly, the sun rose; it rose upon no sadder sight than the man of good abilities and good emotions, incapable of their directed exercise, incapable of his own help and his own happiness, sensible of the blight on him, and resigning himself to let it eat him away.", "서글프게, 서글프게 해가 떠올랐다. 그 해가 비추는 그 어떤 광경도, 훌륭한 재능과 훌륭한 감정을 지녔으나 그것을 제대로 발휘할 줄 모르고, 스스로를 돕지도 스스로 행복해지지도 못하며, 제 몸을 좀먹는 황폐를 느끼면서도 그것이 자신을 갉아먹도록 체념해 내맡긴 이 사내보다 더 서글프지는 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI. Hundreds of People", "# CHAPTER VI. Hundreds of People"]], [["The quiet lodgings of Doctor Manette were in a quiet street-corner not far from Soho-square.", "마네뜨 박사의 조용한 거처는 소호 광장에서 멀지 않은 어느 조용한 길모퉁이에 있었다."], ["On the afternoon of a certain fine Sunday when the waves of four months had rolled over the trial for treason, and carried it, as to the public interest and memory, far out to sea, Mr. Jarvis Lorry walked along the sunny streets from Clerkenwell where he lived, on his way to dine with the Doctor.", "반역죄 재판이 있은 지 넉 달이라는 파도가 흘러가, 대중의 관심과 기억 속에서 그 사건을 저 먼 바다로 실어내 보낸 어느 화창한 일요일 오후, 자비스 로리 씨는 자신이 사는 클러큰웰에서 햇살 가득한 거리를 따라 박사와 만찬을 함께하러 가는 길을 걷고 있었다."], ["After several relapses into business-absorption, Mr. Lorry had become the Doctor’s friend, and the quiet street-corner was the sunny part of his life.", "몇 차례 다시 업무에 파묻혔다 빠져나온 끝에, 로리 씨는 박사의 친구가 되어 있었고, 그 조용한 길모퉁이는 그의 삶에서 햇살이 비치는 부분이었다."]], [["On this certain fine Sunday, Mr. Lorry walked towards Soho, early in the afternoon, for three reasons of habit.", "이 화창한 일요일에, 로리 씨는 이른 오후부터 습관에서 비롯된 세 가지 이유로 소호를 향해 걸었다."], ["Firstly, because, on fine Sundays, he often walked out, before dinner, with the Doctor and Lucie; secondly, because, on unfavourable Sundays, he was accustomed to be with them as the family friend, talking, reading, looking out of window, and generally getting through the day; thirdly, because he happened to have his own little shrewd doubts to solve, and knew how the ways of the Doctor’s household pointed to that time as a likely time for solving them.", "첫째, 화창한 일요일이면 그는 만찬에 앞서 박사 및 루씨와 자주 산책을 나갔기 때문이고, 둘째, 날씨가 궂은 일요일에는 집안의 친구로서 그들과 함께 머물며 이야기하고, 책을 읽고, 창밖을 내다보면서 대체로 하루를 보내곤 했기 때문이며, 셋째, 마침 자신만의 자그마한 예리한 의문을 풀어야 했고, 박사 집안의 일상이 그 시간이야말로 그것을 풀기에 적당한 때임을 어떻게 가리키는지 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["A quainter corner than the corner where the Doctor lived, was not to be found in London.", "박사가 사는 모퉁이보다 더 정취 있는 모퉁이는 런던에서 찾아볼 수 없었다."], ["There was no way through it, and the front windows of the Doctor’s lodgings commanded a pleasant little vista of street that had a congenial air of retirement on it.", "그곳을 가로지르는 길은 없었고, 박사 거처의 정면 창문에서는 한적한 분위기가 어우러진, 정겹고 자그마한 거리 풍경이 한눈에 들어왔다."], ["There were few buildings then, north of the Oxford-road, and forest-trees flourished, and wild flowers grew, and the hawthorn blossomed, in the now vanished fields.", "그 무렵 옥스퍼드 거리 북쪽에는 건물이 거의 없었고, 지금은 사라진 들판에서는 숲의 나무들이 무성했고, 들꽃이 자랐으며, 산사나무가 꽃을 피웠다."], ["As a consequence, country airs circulated in Soho with vigorous freedom, instead of languishing into the parish like stray paupers without a settlement; and there was many a good south wall, not far off, on which the peaches ripened in their season.", "그 결과, 시골 공기가 거처할 곳 없는 떠돌이 빈민처럼 교구 안으로 흘러들어 시들어버리는 대신, 소호에는 활기차고 거침없이 감돌았다. 게다가 그리 멀지 않은 곳에는 제철이면 복숭아가 익어가는 좋은 남향 담장이 여럿 있었다."]], [["The summer light struck into the corner brilliantly in the earlier part of the day; but, when the streets grew hot, the corner was in shadow, though not in shadow so remote but that you could see beyond it into a glare of brightness.", "여름 햇살은 하루의 이른 시간에는 그 모퉁이를 눈부시게 비추었으나, 거리가 달아오를 무렵이면 모퉁이는 그늘에 잠겼다. 그렇다고 그 그늘 너머로 환한 빛의 광채가 보이지 않을 만큼 외진 그늘은 아니었다."], ["It was a cool spot, staid but cheerful, a wonderful place for echoes, and a very harbour from the raging streets.", "그곳은 서늘한 곳이었고, 차분하면서도 명랑했으며, 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 곳이었고, 사납게 들끓는 거리로부터 피할 수 있는 바로 그런 항구였다."]], [["There ought to have been a tranquil bark in such an anchorage, and there was.", "그런 정박지에는 마땅히 평온한 거룻배 한 척이 있어야 했고, 실제로 있었다."], ["The Doctor occupied two floors of a large stiff house, where several callings purported to be pursued by day, but whereof little was audible any day, and which was shunned by all of them at night.", "박사는 크고 딱딱한 건물의 두 층을 차지하고 있었는데, 그 건물에서는 낮 동안 여러 생업이 이루어진다고들 했으나 어느 날이고 들리는 소리는 거의 없었고, 밤이면 그들 모두가 그곳을 멀리했다."], ["In a building at the back, attainable by a courtyard where a plane-tree rustled its green leaves, church-organs claimed to be made, and silver to be chased, and likewise gold to be beaten by some mysterious giant who had a golden arm starting out of the wall of the front hall--as if he had beaten himself precious, and menaced a similar conversion of all visitors.", "뒤편 건물 하나는 플라타너스 한 그루가 푸른 잎을 바스락거리는 안뜰을 지나야 닿을 수 있었는데, 그곳에서는 교회 오르간을 만든다고 했고, 은을 새긴다고 했으며, 마찬가지로 금을 두드린다고 했다. 그 일을 하는 이는 정면 현관의 벽에서 금빛 팔 하나가 튀어나와 있는 어느 신비로운 거인이었으니, 마치 자기 자신을 두드려 귀하게 만들어놓고는 찾아오는 모든 이들에게도 그와 비슷한 변모를 가하겠다고 위협하는 듯했다."], ["Very little of these trades, or of a lonely lodger rumoured to live up-stairs, or of a dim coach-trimming maker asserted to have a counting-house below, was ever heard or seen.", "이런 생업들이나, 위층에 산다고 소문난 외로운 하숙인이나, 아래층에 사무실을 두고 있다고들 하는 어렴풋한 마차 장식 제조업자에 관해서 들리거나 보이는 것은 거의 없었다."], ["Occasionally, a stray workman putting his coat on, traversed the hall, or a stranger peered about there, or a distant clink was heard across the courtyard, or a thump from the golden giant.", "이따금씩 길 잃은 일꾼 하나가 외투를 걸치며 현관을 가로지르거나, 낯선 사람 하나가 그곳을 기웃거리거나, 안뜰 너머로 멀리서 짤그랑거리는 소리가 들리거나, 금빛 거인이 쾅 두드리는 소리가 들리곤 했다."], ["These, however, were only the exceptions required to prove the rule that the sparrows in the plane-tree behind the house, and the echoes in the corner before it, had their own way from Sunday morning unto Saturday night.", "그러나 이런 것들은, 집 뒤편 플라타너스의 참새들과 집 앞 모퉁이의 메아리들이 일요일 아침부터 토요일 밤까지 제멋대로 지낸다는 규칙을 입증하기 위해 필요한 예외들에 지나지 않았다."]], [["Doctor Manette received such patients here as his old reputation, and its revival in the floating whispers of his story, brought him.", "마네뜨 박사는 이곳에서, 그의 오랜 명성과 그의 이야기를 떠도는 속삭임 속에서 그 명성이 되살아남으로써 찾아오는 환자들을 받았다."], ["His scientific knowledge, and his vigilance and skill in conducting ingenious experiments, brought him otherwise into moderate request, and he earned as much as he wanted.", "그의 과학적 지식, 그리고 기발한 실험을 수행하는 그의 빈틈없음과 솜씨는 또 다른 방면에서 그에게 적당한 청을 불러들였고, 그는 자신이 바라는 만큼은 벌었다."]], [["These things were within Mr. Jarvis Lorry’s knowledge, thoughts, and notice, when he rang the door-bell of the tranquil house in the corner, on the fine Sunday afternoon.", "이 화창한 일요일 오후, 모퉁이의 그 평온한 집의 초인종을 눌렀을 때, 이런 것들이 자비스 로리 씨가 알고, 생각하고, 마음에 두고 있는 바였다."]], [["“Doctor Manette at home?”", "\"마네뜨 박사 댁에 계신가?\""]], [["Expected home.", "곧 돌아오실 예정입니다."]], [["“Miss Lucie at home?”", "\"루씨 아가씨 댁에 계신가?\""]], [["Expected home.", "곧 돌아오실 예정입니다."]], [["“Miss Pross at home?”", "\"프로스 양 댁에 계신가?\""]], [["Possibly at home, but of a certainty impossible for handmaid to anticipate intentions of Miss Pross, as to admission or denial of the fact.", "아마 계실 테지만, 그분의 출입을 허락하실지 거절하실지 그 의중을 하녀로서 미리 짐작하기란 도무지 불가능했다."]], [["“As I am at home myself,”", "\"나야 제집처럼 드나드는 사람이니,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, “I’ll go upstairs.”", "로리 씨가 말했다, \"위층으로 올라가겠네.\""]], [["Although the Doctor’s daughter had known nothing of the country of her birth, she appeared to have innately derived from it that ability to make much of little means, which is one of its most useful and most agreeable characteristics.", "박사의 딸은 자신이 태어난 나라에 관해 아무것도 알지 못했지만, 그 나라에서 가장 유용하고 가장 흐뭇한 특성 가운데 하나인, 적은 것으로 많은 것을 이루어내는 그 능력만큼은 천부적으로 물려받은 듯했다."], ["Simple as the furniture was, it was set off by so many little adornments, of no value but for their taste and fancy, that its effect was delightful.", "가구는 소박했으나, 그 자체로는 별 가치가 없고 다만 취향과 운치만으로 빛나는 자그마한 장식들이 그토록 많이 곁들여져 있어, 그 효과가 흐뭇했다."], ["The disposition of everything in the rooms, from the largest object to the least; the arrangement of colours, the elegant variety and contrast obtained by thrift in trifles, by delicate hands, clear eyes, and good sense; were at once so pleasant in themselves, and so expressive of their originator, that, as Mr. Lorry stood looking about him, the very chairs and tables seemed to ask him, with something of that peculiar expression which he knew so well by this time, whether he approved?", "가장 큰 것에서 가장 작은 것에 이르기까지 방안 모든 물건의 배치, 색의 조화, 자질구레한 데서 아끼는 알뜰함과 섬세한 손과 맑은 눈과 분별로 얻어낸 우아한 변화와 대조, 그 모든 것이 그 자체로 너무도 흐뭇하고 또 그것을 빚어낸 이를 너무도 잘 드러내고 있어서, 로리 씨가 서서 주위를 둘러보고 있노라니, 의자며 탁자며 하는 것들마저 이제는 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그 독특한 표정 같은 것을 띠고서, 마음에 드시느냐고 그에게 묻는 듯했다."]], [["There were three rooms on a floor, and, the doors by which they communicated being put open that the air might pass freely through them all, Mr.", "한 층에는 방이 셋 있었고, 공기가 그 모든 방을 자유로이 통하도록 그것들을 잇는 문이 죄다 열려 있어서, 로리 씨는 사방에서 자신이 알아챈 그 기발한 닮음을 빙긋이 눈여겨보며 이 방에서 저 방으로 걸어 다녔다."], ["Lorry, smilingly observant of that fanciful resemblance which he detected all around him, walked from one to another.", "첫번째 방은 가장 좋은 방이었는데, 그곳에는 루씨의 새들과 꽃들과 책들과 책상과 작업대와 수채물감 상자가 있었다."], ["The first was the best room, and in it were Lucie’s birds, and flowers, and books, and desk, and work-table, and box of water-colours; the second was the Doctor’s consulting-room, used also as the dining-room; the third, changingly speckled by the rustle of the plane-tree in the yard, was the Doctor’s bedroom, and there, in a corner, stood the disused shoemaker’s bench and tray of tools, much as it had stood on the fifth floor of the dismal house by the wine-shop, in the suburb of Saint Antoine in Paris.", "두번째 방은 박사의 진료실로, 식당으로도 쓰였다. 세번째 방은 안뜰의 플라타너스가 바스락거리며 시시각각 얼룩무늬를 드리우는 박사의 침실이었는데, 그곳 한구석에는 이제는 쓰지 않는 구두장이의 작업대와 연장 쟁반이, 빠리의 쌩땅뚜안 변두리에 있던 그 음울한 집, 술집 곁 그 집 오층에 놓여 있던 그대로 거의 변함없이 놓여 있었다."]], [["“I wonder,”", "\"이상하군,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, pausing in his looking about, “that he keeps that reminder of his sufferings about him!”", "로리 씨가 둘러보던 것을 멈추고 말했다, \"그가 자기 고난의 저런 기억거리를 곁에 두고 있다니!\""]], [["“And why wonder at that?”", "\"그게 뭐가 이상한가요?\""], ["was the abrupt inquiry that made him start.", "그를 흠칫 놀라게 한 불쑥 던진 질문이었다."]], [["It proceeded from Miss Pross, the wild red woman, strong of hand, whose acquaintance he had first made at the Royal George Hotel at Dover, and had since improved.", "그것은 프로스 양에게서 나온 것이었으니, 손아귀 힘이 센 그 거칠고 불그레한 여인은 로리 씨가 도버의 로열 조지 호텔에서 처음 알게 되어 그 뒤로 친분을 쌓아온 사이였다."]], [["“I should have thought--”", "\"나는 생각하기를—\""], ["Mr. Lorry began.", "로리 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Pooh!", "\"흥!"], ["You’d have thought!”", "생각하셨겠지요!\""], ["said Miss Pross; and Mr. Lorry left off.", "프로스 양이 말하자, 로리 씨는 입을 다물었다."]], [["“How do you do?”", "\"그래, 안녕하셨어요?\""], ["inquired that lady then--sharply, and yet as if to express that she bore him no malice.", "그러더니 그 부인이 물었다—퉁명스럽게, 그러나 그에게 악의는 없다는 것을 나타내려는 듯이."]], [["“I am pretty well, I thank you,” answered Mr.", "\"덕분에 잘 지냅니다,\""], ["Lorry, with meekness; “how are you?”", "로리 씨가 온순하게 대답했다. \"당신은 어떻습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing to boast of,”", "\"내세울 거 하나 없어요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그런가요?\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["indeed!”", "그렇다니까요!\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["“I am very much put out about my Ladybird.”", "\"우리 무당벌레 때문에 속이 영 상해서요.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그런가요?\""]], [["“For gracious sake say something else besides ‘indeed,’ or you’ll fidget me to death,”", "\"제발 '그런가요' 말고 다른 말 좀 해보세요, 안 그러면 답답해 죽겠어요,\""], ["said Miss Pross: whose character (dissociated from stature) was shortness.", "프로스 양이 말했다. 그녀의 성격은 (키와는 따로 떼어놓고 보자면) 무뚝뚝함이었다."]], [["“Really, then?” said Mr.", "\"그래, 정말이요?\""], ["Lorry, as an amendment.", "로리 씨가 고쳐 말했다."]], [["“Really, is bad enough,”", "\"'정말'도 충분히 못마땅하지만,\""], ["returned Miss Pross, “but better.", "프로스 양이 받았다, \"그래도 낫네요."], ["Yes, I am very much put out.”", "그래요, 속이 영 상했어요.\""]], [["“May I ask the cause?”", "\"그 까닭을 물어봐도 될까요?\""]], [["“I don’t want dozens of people who are not at all worthy of Ladybird, to come here looking after her,”", "\"무당벌레한테 도무지 어울리지도 않는 사람들이 수십 명씩 여기로 그애를 보러 찾아오는 게 싫어요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Do dozens come for that purpose?”", "\"정말로 수십 명이 그러려고 온다는 말입니까?\""]], [["“Hundreds,”", "\"수백 명이요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["It was characteristic of this lady (as of some other people before her time and since) that whenever her original proposition was questioned, she exaggerated it.", "이 부인의 특징은 (그녀 이전과 이후의 어떤 사람들도 그렇듯이) 자신의 처음 주장이 의심받을 때마다 그것을 과장한다는 것이었다."]], [["“Dear me!” said Mr.", "\"이런, 저런!\""], ["Lorry, as the safest remark he could think of.", "로리 씨가, 떠올릴 수 있는 가장 무난한 말로 응수했다."]], [["“I have lived with the darling--or the darling has lived with me, and paid me for it; which she certainly should never have done, you may take your affidavit, if I could have afforded to keep either myself or her for nothing--since she was ten years old.", "\"나는 그 귀한 애와 함께 살아왔어요—아니, 그애가 나와 함께 살면서 그 값을 치러주었지요. 내가 나든 그애든 거저 먹여 살릴 형편만 되었다면 그애는 절대로 그래선 안 됐던 거예요, 맹세코 그래요—그애가 열 살 때부터 말이에요."], ["And it’s really very hard,”", "그런데 이건 정말 너무 힘든 일이라고요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["Not seeing with precision what was very hard, Mr.", "무엇이 그토록 힘든 일인지 정확히는 알 수 없었으므로, 로리 씨는 고개를 가로저었다."], ["Lorry shook his head; using that important part of himself as a sort of fairy cloak that would fit anything.", "그는 자신의 그 중요한 부위를, 무엇에든 어울리는 요술 망토처럼 써먹었다."]], [["“All sorts of people who are not in the least degree worthy of the pet, are always turning up,”", "\"그 귀염둥이한테는 조금도 어울리지 않는 온갖 부류의 사람들이 늘 나타나요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["“When you began it--”", "\"당신이 시작한 일이니까—\""]], [["“I began it, Miss Pross?”", "\"내가요, 프로스 양?\""]], [["“Didn’t you?", "\"아니었나요?"], ["Who brought her father to life?”", "그애 아버지를 되살려놓은 게 누구죠?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["If that was beginning it--”", "그게 시작한 일이라면—\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“It wasn’t ending it, I suppose?", "\"그게 끝낸 일은 아니었을 테죠?"], ["I say, when you began it, it was hard enough; not that I have any fault to find with Doctor Manette, except that he is not worthy of such a daughter, which is no imputation on him, for it was not to be expected that anybody should be, under any circumstances.", "내 말은, 당신이 시작했을 때, 그것만으로도 충분히 힘들었다는 거예요. 마네뜨 박사한테 나무랄 데가 있다는 게 아니에요, 그런 딸한테 어울리지 않는다는 것만 빼면요."], ["But it really is doubly and trebly hard to have crowds and multitudes of people turning up after him (I could have forgiven him), to take Ladybird’s affections away from me.”", "하지만 그건 그분 탓이 아니지요, 어떤 처지에서든 누구도 그애한테 어울릴 거라고는 기대할 수 없으니까요. 그렇지만 그분을 뒤따라 사람들이 떼로 무리로 나타나서 (그분이야 용서할 수 있었지요) 무당벌레의 정을 내게서 앗아가려 드는 건 정말이지 두 배 세 배로 힘든 일이라고요.\""]], [["Mr.", "로리 씨는 프로스 양이 몹시 질투심이 강하다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 이제는 그녀가 그 별난 행동의 이면에 자리한 사심 없는 존재들 가운데 하나—오직 여인들에게서만 볼 수 있는 부류—라는 것 또한 알고 있었다."], ["Lorry knew Miss Pross to be very jealous, but he also knew her by this time to be, beneath the service of her eccentricity, one of those unselfish creatures--found only among women--who will, for pure love and admiration, bind themselves willing slaves, to youth when they have lost it, to beauty that they never had, to accomplishments that they were never fortunate enough to gain, to bright hopes that never shone upon their own sombre lives.", "그런 이들은 순수한 사랑과 흠모에서, 이미 잃어버린 젊음에, 한 번도 가져보지 못한 아름다움에, 다행히 얻어본 적 없는 재주에, 자신들의 어두운 삶에는 한 번도 비춘 적 없는 밝은 희망에, 기꺼이 자신을 노예로 옭아매는 법이다. 그는 세상물정을 충분히 알았기에, 세상에 마음에서 우러나는 충실한 헌신보다 더 나은 것은 없음을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew enough of the world to know that there is nothing in it better than the faithful service of the heart; so rendered and so free from any mercenary taint, he had such an exalted respect for it, that in the retributive arrangements made by his own mind--we all make such arrangements, more or less--he stationed Miss Pross much nearer to the lower Angels than many ladies immeasurably better got up both by Nature and Art, who had balances at Tellson’s.", "그처럼 바쳐지고 그처럼 어떤 타산의 때도 묻지 않은 그 헌신에 그가 얼마나 드높은 경의를 품었던지, 자기 마음속에서 행한 응보의 배치에서—우리는 누구나 많든 적든 그런 배치를 한다—그는 프로스 양을, 자연과 기교 양쪽으로 비할 데 없이 잘 차려입었고 텔슨 은행에 잔고를 둔 수많은 귀부인들보다도 한층 더 낮은 천사들 가까이에 자리매김해 두었다."]], [["“There never was, nor will be, but one man worthy of Ladybird,”", "\"무당벌레한테 어울리는 남자는 단 한 사람뿐이었고 앞으로도 그럴 거예요,\""], ["said Miss Pross; “and that was my brother Solomon, if he hadn’t made a mistake in life.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"그건 우리 오빠 솔로몬이었어요, 인생에서 실수만 저지르지 않았더라면 말이지요.\""]], [["Here again: Mr. Lorry’s inquiries into Miss Pross’s personal history had established the fact that her brother Solomon was a heartless scoundrel who had stripped her of everything she possessed, as a stake to speculate with, and had abandoned her in her poverty for evermore, with no touch of compunction.", "여기서 또: 프로스 양의 개인사에 관한 로리 씨의 탐문은, 그녀의 오빠 솔로몬이라는 자가 투기 밑천으로 삼겠다며 그녀가 가진 모든 것을 빼앗아가고는, 양심의 가책이라곤 한 점 없이 가난에 빠진 그녀를 영영 내버린 무정한 악당이라는 사실을 밝혀낸 바 있었다."], ["Miss Pross’s fidelity of belief in Solomon (deducting a mere trifle for this slight mistake) was quite a serious matter with Mr. Lorry, and had its weight in his good opinion of her.", "솔로몬에 대한 프로스 양의 변함없는 믿음은 (이 사소한 실수에 대해 자그마한 것만 깎아낸다면) 로리 씨에게는 자못 진지한 문제였고, 그녀에 대한 그의 호의 어린 평가에서도 적잖은 무게를 지녔다."]], [["“As we happen to be alone for the moment, and are both people of business,”", "\"마침 우리 둘만 잠시 있게 됐고, 또 우리 둘 다 사무적인 사람들이니,\""], ["he said, when they had got back to the drawing-room and had sat down there in friendly relations, “let me ask you--does the Doctor, in talking with Lucie, never refer to the shoemaking time, yet?”", "그들이 응접실로 돌아와 다정한 사이로 그곳에 앉았을 때 그가 말했다, \"한 가지 물읍시다—박사는 루씨와 이야기할 때, 아직도 구두 짓던 시절을 한 번도 언급하지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"한 번도요.\""]], [["“And yet keeps that bench and those tools beside him?”", "\"그런데도 저 작업대와 저 연장들은 곁에 두고요?\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["returned Miss Pross, shaking her head.", "프로스 양이 고개를 가로저으며 받았다."], ["“But I don’t say he don’t refer to it within himself.”", "\"하지만 그분이 마음속으로도 그 생각을 안 한다고는 말 안 해요.\""]], [["“Do you believe that he thinks of it much?”", "\"그가 그 생각을 많이 한다고 보십니까?\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Do you imagine--” Mr.", "\"혹시 상상하시기로는—\""], ["Lorry had begun, when Miss Pross took him up short with:", "로리 씨가 입을 떼자, 프로스 양이 그의 말을 짧게 끊었다."]], [["“Never imagine anything.", "\"아무것도 상상하지 마세요."], ["Have no imagination at all.”", "상상력 따위는 아예 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I stand corrected; do you suppose--you go so far as to suppose, sometimes?”", "\"제 말이 잘못됐군요. 그럼 짐작하시기로는—이따금 짐작쯤은 하시지요?\""]], [["“Now and then,”", "\"가끔은요,\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Do you suppose,”", "\"짐작하시기로는,\""], ["Mr. Lorry went on, with a laughing twinkle in his bright eye, as it looked kindly at her, “that Doctor Manette has any theory of his own, preserved through all those years, relative to the cause of his being so oppressed; perhaps, even to the name of his oppressor?”", "로리 씨가, 그녀를 다정하게 바라보는 그 빛나는 눈에 웃음기를 반짝이며 말을 이었다, \"마네뜨 박사가, 자신이 그토록 억눌리게 된 까닭에 관해서—어쩌면 자신을 억누른 자의 이름에 관해서까지도—그 모든 세월 내내 간직해온 자기 나름의 어떤 견해를 갖고 있다고 보십니까?\""]], [["“I don’t suppose anything about it but what Ladybird tells me.”", "\"무당벌레가 내게 말해주는 것 말고는 그 일에 관해 아무것도 짐작 안 해요.\""]], [["“And that is--?”", "\"그게 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“Now don’t be angry at my asking all these questions; because I am a mere dull man of business, and you are a woman of business.”", "\"그애는 그분이 그런 견해를 갖고 있다고 생각한다는 거예요.\" \"이런 질문들을 다 해댄다고 화내지는 마세요. 나야 그저 둔한 사무가일 뿐이고, 당신은 사무에 밝은 여인이니까요.\""]], [["“Dull?”", "\"둔하다고요?\""], ["Miss Pross inquired, with placidity.", "프로스 양이 차분하게 물었다."]], [["Rather wishing his modest adjective away, Mr.", "자신의 그 겸손한 형용사를 거두어들이고 싶어진 로리 씨가 대답했다."], ["Lorry replied, “No, no, no.", "\"아니, 아니, 아닙니다."], ["Surely not.", "결코 그렇지 않지요."], ["To return to business:--Is it not remarkable that Doctor Manette, unquestionably innocent of any crime as we are all well assured he is, should never touch upon that question?", "다시 본론으로 돌아가서—우리 모두가 그렇게 굳게 믿고 있다시피 마네뜨 박사는 어떤 죄도 짓지 않은 게 분명한데, 그가 그 문제를 결코 입에 올리지 않는다는 게 주목할 만하지 않습니까?"], ["I will not say with me, though he had business relations with me many years ago, and we are now intimate; I will say with the fair daughter to whom he is so devotedly attached, and who is so devotedly attached to him?", "나한테 그렇다는 게 아닙니다, 비록 그가 여러 해 전에 나와 사무적인 관계를 맺었고 지금은 우리가 가까운 사이이긴 하지만요. 그가 그토록 헌신적으로 아끼고, 또 그를 그토록 헌신적으로 아끼는 그 어여쁜 딸에게도 그렇다는 말입니다."], ["Believe me, Miss Pross, I don’t approach the topic with you, out of curiosity, but out of zealous interest.”", "믿어주세요, 프로스 양, 나는 호기심에서 당신에게 이 화제를 꺼내는 게 아니라, 간절한 관심에서 그러는 거예요.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"글쎄요!"], ["To the best of my understanding, and bad’s the best, you’ll tell me,”", "내가 이해하는 한에서, 그게 시원찮은 이해이긴 하지만, 들어보세요,\""], ["said Miss Pross, softened by the tone of the apology, “he is afraid of the whole subject.”", "사과의 어조에 누그러진 프로스 양이 말했다, \"그분은 그 문제 전체를 두려워하는 거예요.\" \"두려워한다고요?\""]], [["“Afraid?” “It’s plain enough, I should think, why he may be.", "\"왜 그럴 만한지는 충분히 뻔하다고 생각해요."], ["It’s a dreadful remembrance.", "끔찍한 기억이니까요."], ["Besides that, his loss of himself grew out of it.", "그뿐 아니라, 그분이 자기 자신을 잃어버린 것도 거기서 비롯됐지요."], ["Not knowing how he lost himself, or how he recovered himself, he may never feel certain of not losing himself again.", "어떻게 자기 자신을 잃었는지, 또 어떻게 되찾았는지 모르니, 다시 자기 자신을 잃지 않으리라고 결코 확신하지 못할 거예요."], ["That alone wouldn’t make the subject pleasant, I should think.”", "그것 하나만으로도 그 문제가 유쾌할 리 없다고 생각해요.\""]], [["It was a profounder remark than Mr.", "그것은 로리 씨가 기대했던 것보다 한층 깊이 있는 말이었다."], ["Lorry had looked for.", "\"맞는 말입니다,\""], ["“True,” said he, “and fearful to reflect upon.", "그가 말했다, \"생각만 해도 두려운 일이지요."], ["Yet, a doubt lurks in my mind, Miss Pross, whether it is good for Doctor Manette to have that suppression always shut up within him.", "하지만 프로스 양, 내 마음속에는 한 가지 의문이 도사리고 있어요, 마네뜨 박사가 그 억눌린 것을 늘 속에 가둬두는 것이 과연 그에게 좋은 일일까 하는 거지요."], ["Indeed, it is this doubt and the uneasiness it sometimes causes me that has led me to our present confidence.”", "사실, 바로 이 의문과, 그것이 이따금 내게 불러일으키는 불안 때문에 우리가 지금 이렇게 속내를 털어놓게 된 겁니다.\""]], [["“Can’t be helped,”", "\"어쩔 수 없어요,\""], ["said Miss Pross, shaking her head.", "프로스 양이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["“Touch that string, and he instantly changes for the worse.", "\"그 줄을 건드리면, 그분은 당장 더 나쁘게 변해요."], ["Better leave it alone.", "그냥 내버려두는 게 나아요."], ["In short, must leave it alone, like or no like.", "한마디로, 좋든 싫든 내버려둘 수밖에 없어요."], ["Sometimes, he gets up in the dead of the night, and will be heard, by us overhead there, walking up and down, walking up and down, in his room.", "가끔, 그분은 한밤중에 일어나서는, 위층에 있는 우리한테, 방안을 이리저리, 이리저리 거니는 소리가 들려요."], ["Ladybird has learnt to know then that his mind is walking up and down, walking up and down, in his old prison.", "무당벌레는 그럴 때면 그분의 마음이 옛 감옥 안을 이리저리, 이리저리 거닐고 있다는 걸 알아차리게 됐지요."], ["She hurries to him, and they go on together, walking up and down, walking up and down, until he is composed.", "그애는 서둘러 그분한테 가고, 둘이 함께 이리저리, 이리저리, 그분이 진정될 때까지 거닐어요."], ["But he never says a word of the true reason of his restlessness, to her, and she finds it best not to hint at it to him.", "하지만 그분은 자신이 안절부절못하는 진짜 까닭은 그애한테 한마디도 하지 않고, 그애도 그것을 그분한테 넌지시 비추지 않는 게 가장 좋다는 걸 알지요."], ["In silence they go walking up and down together, walking up and down together, till her love and company have brought him to himself.”", "말없이 둘은 함께 이리저리, 이리저리 거닐어요, 함께 이리저리, 이리저리, 그애의 사랑과 함께함이 그분을 제정신으로 돌려놓을 때까지요.\""]], [["Notwithstanding Miss Pross’s denial of her own imagination, there was a perception of the pain of being monotonously haunted by one sad idea, in her repetition of the phrase, walking up and down, which testified to her possessing such a thing.", "자신에게는 상상력이 없다는 프로스 양의 부인에도 불구하고, 이리저리 거닌다는 그 구절을 거듭 되풀이하는 데에는 한 가지 슬픈 생각에 단조롭게 시달리는 그 고통에 대한 감지가 깃들어 있었으니, 그것이 그녀가 그런 것을 지니고 있음을 입증했다."]], [["The corner has been mentioned as a wonderful corner for echoes; it had begun to echo so resoundingly to the tread of coming feet, that it seemed as though the very mention of that weary pacing to and fro had set it going.", "그 모퉁이는 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 모퉁이라고 앞서 말한 바 있다. 다가오는 발소리에 그곳이 어찌나 쩌렁쩌렁 울리기 시작했던지, 마치 이리저리 오가는 그 고단한 거닒을 입에 올린 것만으로 그 메아리가 일어나기 시작한 듯했다."]], [["“Here they are!”", "\"오셨네요!\""], ["said Miss Pross, rising to break up the conference; “and now we shall have hundreds of people pretty soon!”", "프로스 양이 대화를 끝맺으려 일어서며 말했다. \"이제 곧 수백 명의 사람들이 들이닥치겠군요!\""]], [["It was such a curious corner in its acoustical properties, such a peculiar Ear of a place, that as Mr. Lorry stood at the open window, looking for the father and daughter whose steps he heard, he fancied they would never approach.", "그곳은 그 음향적 특성에 있어 어찌나 묘한 모퉁이였던지, 어찌나 독특한 귀를 가진 장소였던지, 로리 씨가 열린 창가에 서서 자신이 그 발소리를 듣고 있는 아버지와 딸을 찾고 있노라니, 그들이 결코 다가오지 않으리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["Not only would the echoes die away, as though the steps had gone; but, echoes of other steps that never came would be heard in their stead, and would die away for good when they seemed close at hand.", "마치 발소리가 멀어져버린 듯 메아리가 잦아들 뿐만 아니라, 그 대신 결코 오지 않는 다른 발소리의 메아리가 들려오다가, 그것이 바로 가까이 다가온 듯싶을 때 영영 잦아들곤 했다."], ["However, father and daughter did at last appear, and Miss Pross was ready at the street door to receive them.", "그러나 아버지와 딸은 마침내 모습을 드러냈고, 프로스 양은 그들을 맞이하러 길로 난 문 앞에 채비를 하고 서 있었다."]], [["Miss Pross was a pleasant sight, albeit wild, and red, and grim, taking off her darling’s bonnet when she came up-stairs, and touching it up with the ends of her handkerchief, and blowing the dust off it, and folding her mantle ready for laying by, and smoothing her rich hair with as much pride as she could possibly have taken in her own hair if she had been the vainest and handsomest of women.", "프로스 양은, 비록 거칠고 불그레하고 무뚝뚝하긴 해도, 보기 좋은 광경이었다. 그녀의 귀한 애가 위층으로 올라오자 그애의 보닛을 벗겨주고, 손수건 끄트머리로 그것을 매만지고, 먼지를 후후 불어내고, 치워둘 채비로 외투를 개고, 그애의 풍성한 머리칼을 쓸어 넘기는 그 모습은, 그녀가 만일 세상에서 가장 허영심 많고 가장 아름다운 여인이었더라도 자기 머리칼에 그러했을 만큼이나 큰 자부심을 담고 있었다."], ["Her darling was a pleasant sight too, embracing her and thanking her, and protesting against her taking so much trouble for her--which last she only dared to do playfully, or Miss Pross, sorely hurt, would have retired to her own chamber and cried.", "그녀의 귀한 애 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 그녀를 끌어안고 고마워하며, 자기 때문에 그토록 수고를 들이는 것에 항의했는데—이 마지막 것은 짐짓 장난스럽게밖에는 감히 하지 못했으니, 안 그러면 프로스 양이 몹시 마음 상해 자기 방으로 물러가 울어버렸을 터였다."], ["The Doctor was a pleasant sight too, looking on at them, and telling Miss Pross how she spoilt Lucie, in accents and with eyes that had as much spoiling in them as Miss Pross had, and would have had more if it were possible.", "박사 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 그 둘을 바라보면서, 루씨를 어찌나 오냐오냐하느냐고 프로스 양에게 일렀는데, 그 어조와 눈빛에는 프로스 양 못지않은 응석받이의 정이, 가능하기만 했다면 그보다 더한 정이 담겨 있었다."], ["Mr. Lorry was a pleasant sight too, beaming at all this in his little wig, and thanking his bachelor stars for having lighted him in his declining years to a Home.", "로리 씨 또한 보기 좋은 광경이었으니, 자그마한 가발을 쓴 채 이 모든 것에 환히 미소 지으며, 노년에 이르러 한 가정으로 자신을 인도해준 독신자의 별들에게 감사했다."], ["But, no Hundreds of people came to see the sights, and Mr. Lorry looked in vain for the fulfilment of Miss Pross’s prediction.", "그러나 그 광경을 보러 수백 명의 사람들이 오는 일은 없었고, 로리 씨는 프로스 양의 예언이 이루어지기를 기다렸으나 헛일이었다."]], [["Dinner-time, and still no Hundreds of people.", "만찬 시간이 되었으나 여전히 수백 명의 사람들은 없었다."], ["In the arrangements of the little household, Miss Pross took charge of the lower regions, and always acquitted herself marvellously.", "이 자그마한 집안의 운영에서, 프로스 양은 아래층 영역을 맡았는데, 언제나 놀랍도록 제 몫을 해냈다."], ["Her dinners, of a very modest quality, were so well cooked and so well served, and so neat in their contrivances, half English and half French, that nothing could be better.", "매우 소박한 그녀의 만찬은 어찌나 잘 요리되고 어찌나 잘 차려졌는지, 또 반은 영국식이고 반은 프랑스식인 그 솜씨가 어찌나 깔끔했는지, 그보다 더 나을 수는 없었다."], ["Miss Pross’s friendship being of the thoroughly practical kind, she had ravaged Soho and the adjacent provinces, in search of impoverished French, who, tempted by shillings and half-crowns, would impart culinary mysteries to her.", "프로스 양의 우정은 철저히 실용적인 종류여서, 그녀는 가난해진 프랑스 사람들을 찾아 소호와 그 인근 지방을 샅샅이 휘젓고 다녔는데, 그들은 몇 실링이나 반 크라운에 혹해서 그녀에게 요리의 비법을 전수해주곤 했다."], ["From these decayed sons and daughters of Gaul, she had acquired such wonderful arts, that the woman and girl who formed the staff of domestics regarded her as quite a Sorceress, or Cinderella’s Godmother: who would send out for a fowl, a rabbit, a vegetable or two from the garden, and change them into anything she pleased.", "이 몰락한 골족의 아들딸들로부터 그녀는 어찌나 놀라운 솜씨를 익혔던지, 하인의 진용을 이루는 여인과 처녀는 그녀를 영락없는 마녀, 혹은 신데렐라의 대모쯤으로 여겼으니, 닭 한 마리, 토끼 한 마리, 정원에서 난 채소 한두 가지를 시켜다가, 그것들을 제 마음에 드는 무엇으로든 바꿔놓는 사람이었던 것이다."]], [["On Sundays, Miss Pross dined at the Doctor’s table, but on other days persisted in taking her meals at unknown periods, either in the lower regions, or in her own room on the second floor--a blue chamber, to which no one but her Ladybird ever gained admittance.", "일요일이면 프로스 양은 박사의 식탁에서 만찬을 들었으나, 다른 날에는 알 수 없는 시각에, 아래층 영역에서든 이층 자기 방에서든 식사하기를 고집했다—그 방은 푸른 방으로, 그녀의 무당벌레 말고는 누구도 들어가본 적 없는 곳이었다."], ["On this occasion, Miss Pross, responding to Ladybird’s pleasant face and pleasant efforts to please her, unbent exceedingly; so the dinner was very pleasant, too.", "이번에는, 자신을 기쁘게 하려는 무당벌레의 정겨운 얼굴과 정겨운 노력에 화답하여, 프로스 양이 더없이 누그러졌으니, 만찬 또한 매우 즐거웠다."]], [["It was an oppressive day, and, after dinner, Lucie proposed that the wine should be carried out under the plane-tree, and they should sit there in the air.", "찌는 듯한 날이었던지라, 만찬 뒤에 루씨는 포도주를 플라타너스 아래로 가지고 나가, 바깥바람을 쐬며 거기 앉자고 제안했다."], ["As everything turned upon her, and revolved about her, they went out under the plane-tree, and she carried the wine down for the special benefit of Mr.", "모든 것이 그녀를 중심으로 돌아가고 그녀를 축으로 움직였으므로, 그들은 플라타너스 아래로 나갔고, 그녀는 특별히 로리 씨를 위해 포도주를 내려 가져갔다."], ["Lorry. She had installed herself, some time before, as Mr.", "얼마 전부터 그녀는 스스로 로리 씨의 술 따르는 시종 노릇을 자처해온 터였다."], ["Lorry’s cup-bearer; and while they sat under the plane-tree, talking, she kept his glass replenished.", "그래서 그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 이야기를 나누는 동안, 그녀는 그의 잔을 계속 채워주었다."], ["Mysterious backs and ends of houses peeped at them as they talked, and the plane-tree whispered to them in its own way above their heads.", "그들이 이야기하는 동안 신비로운 집들의 뒷면과 끄트머리가 그들을 엿보았고, 플라타너스는 그들 머리 위에서 제 나름의 방식으로 그들에게 속삭였다."]], [["Still, the Hundreds of people did not present themselves.", "여전히, 수백 명의 사람들은 나타나지 않았다."], ["Mr. Darnay presented himself while they were sitting under the plane-tree, but he was only One.", "그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있는 동안 다네이 씨가 나타나기는 했으나, 그는 단 한 사람일 뿐이었다."]], [["Doctor Manette received him kindly, and so did Lucie.", "마네뜨 박사는 그를 다정하게 맞이했고, 루씨 또한 그러했다."], ["But, Miss Pross suddenly became afflicted with a twitching in the head and body, and retired into the house.", "그러나 프로스 양은 별안간 머리와 몸을 씰룩거리는 발작에 시달려 집안으로 물러갔다."], ["She was not unfrequently the victim of this disorder, and she called it, in familiar conversation, “a fit of the jerks.”", "그녀는 드물지 않게 이 병의 희생자가 되곤 했는데, 허물없는 대화에서 그것을 \"씰룩증 발작\"이라고 불렀다."]], [["The Doctor was in his best condition, and looked specially young.", "박사는 더없이 좋은 상태였고, 유난히 젊어 보였다."], ["The resemblance between him and Lucie was very strong at such times, and as they sat side by side, she leaning on his shoulder, and he resting his arm on the back of her chair, it was very agreeable to trace the likeness.", "그와 루씨가 닮은 점은 그런 때면 매우 두드러졌고, 그들이 나란히 앉아, 그녀가 그의 어깨에 기대고 그가 그녀의 의자 등받이에 팔을 얹고 있노라니, 그 닮음을 짚어보는 것이 무척 흐뭇했다."]], [["He had been talking all day, on many subjects, and with unusual vivacity.", "그는 온종일, 여러 주제에 관해, 유난한 활기를 띠고 이야기해온 터였다. \"마네뜨 박사님,\""], ["“Pray, Doctor Manette,” said Mr.", "그들이 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있을 때 다네이 씨가 말했다—그가 그 말을 한 것은, 마침 화제에 오른 런던의 오래된 건물들에 관해 자연스럽게 이야기를 이어가던 차였다—\"탑은 많이 둘러보셨습니까?\""], ["Darnay, as they sat under the plane-tree--and he said it in the natural pursuit of the topic in hand, which happened to be the old buildings of London--“have you seen much of the Tower?”", "* 탑: 런던 탑(Tower of London). 감옥으로도 쓰였다."]], [["“Lucie and I have been there; but only casually.", "\"루씨와 나도 가본 적은 있지만, 그저 지나는 길에 들른 정도였소."], ["We have seen enough of it, to know that it teems with interest; little more.”", "흥미로 가득한 곳이라는 걸 알 만큼은 보았지요. 그 이상은 거의 못 보았소.\""]], [["“I have been there, as you remember,”", "\"저는, 기억하시겠지만, 거기 가본 적이 있지요,\""], ["said Darnay, with a smile, though reddening a little angrily, “in another character, and not in a character that gives facilities for seeing much of it.", "다네이가, 살짝 성난 듯 얼굴을 붉히면서도 미소 지으며 말했다, \"다른 처지로 말입니다, 그것도 그곳을 많이 둘러볼 만한 편의를 주는 처지는 아니었지요."], ["They told me a curious thing when I was there.”", "거기 있을 때 묘한 이야기를 하나 들었습니다.\""]], [["“What was that?”", "\"그게 뭐였나요?\""], ["Lucie asked.", "루씨가 물었다."]], [["“In making some alterations, the workmen came upon an old dungeon, which had been, for many years, built up and forgotten.", "\"무슨 개축 공사를 하다가, 인부들이 오래된 지하 감방 하나를 발견했답니다. 여러 해 동안 벽으로 막혀 잊혀 있던 곳이었지요."], ["Every stone of its inner wall was covered by inscriptions which had been carved by prisoners--dates, names, complaints, and prayers.", "그 안쪽 벽은 돌마다 죄수들이 새겨놓은 글귀들로 뒤덮여 있었답니다—날짜며, 이름이며, 하소연이며, 기도며 하는 것들이었지요."], ["Upon a corner stone in an angle of the wall, one prisoner, who seemed to have gone to execution, had cut as his last work, three letters.", "벽 모서리의 어느 귓돌에는, 처형장으로 끌려간 듯한 죄수 하나가 마지막 작업으로 글자 셋을 새겨놓았더랍니다."], ["They were done with some very poor instrument, and hurriedly, with an unsteady hand.", "매우 변변찮은 도구로, 떨리는 손으로, 황급히 새긴 것이었지요. 처음에는 그것을 D."], ["At first, they were read as D.", "I. C.로 읽었답니다."], ["I. C.; but, on being more carefully examined, the last letter was found to be G.", "그러나 더 꼼꼼히 살펴보니, 마지막 글자가 G임이 드러났지요."], ["There was no record or legend of any prisoner with those initials, and many fruitless guesses were made what the name could have been.", "그런 머리글자를 가진 죄수에 관한 기록이나 전설은 없었고, 그 이름이 무엇이었을지 부질없는 추측이 숱하게 오갔답니다."], ["At length, it was suggested that the letters were not initials, but the complete word, DIG.", "마침내, 그 글자들은 머리글자가 아니라 완전한 한 단어, 즉 DIG(파라)였으리라는 의견이 나왔지요."], ["The floor was examined very carefully under the inscription, and, in the earth beneath a stone, or tile, or some fragment of paving, were found the ashes of a paper, mingled with the ashes of a small leathern case or bag.", "그 글귀 아래 바닥을 매우 꼼꼼히 살펴보니, 어떤 돌이나 타일이나 포석 조각 밑의 흙 속에서, 종이 한 장의 재가, 자그마한 가죽 상자인지 주머니인지의 재와 뒤섞인 채 발견되었답니다."], ["What the unknown prisoner had written will never be read, but he had written something, and hidden it away to keep it from the gaoler.”", "그 무명의 죄수가 무엇을 썼는지는 영영 읽을 수 없겠지만, 그는 무언가를 썼고, 그것을 간수에게서 지키려고 숨겨두었던 거지요.\""]], [["“My father,”", "\"아버지,\""], ["exclaimed Lucie, “you are ill!”", "루씨가 외쳤다, \"편찮으세요!\""]], [["He had suddenly started up, with his hand to his head.", "그가 별안간 손을 머리에 짚으며 벌떡 일어섰던 것이다."], ["His manner and his look quite terrified them all.", "그의 거동과 표정에 그들 모두가 적잖이 놀랐다."]], [["“No, my dear, not ill.", "\"아니다, 얘야, 편찮은 게 아니야."], ["There are large drops of rain falling, and they made me start.", "굵은 빗방울이 떨어지는구나, 그게 나를 흠칫하게 했어."], ["We had better go in.”", "들어가는 게 좋겠다.\""]], [["He recovered himself almost instantly.", "그는 거의 곧바로 평정을 되찾았다."], ["Rain was really falling in large drops, and he showed the back of his hand with rain-drops on it.", "정말로 굵은 빗방울이 떨어지고 있었고, 그는 빗방울이 맺힌 손등을 내보였다."], ["But, he said not a single word in reference to the discovery that had been told of, and, as they went into the house, the business eye of Mr. Lorry either detected, or fancied it detected, on his face, as it turned towards Charles Darnay, the same singular look that had been upon it when it turned towards him in the passages of the Court House.", "그러나 앞서 들려준 그 발견에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 그들이 집안으로 들어갈 때, 로리 씨의 사무가다운 눈은, 그의 얼굴이 찰스 다네이 쪽으로 돌려질 때 그 위에서, 법원 복도에서 그것이 자신 쪽으로 돌려졌을 때 어려 있던 바로 그 기이한 표정을 알아챘거나, 알아챘다고 여겼다."]], [["He recovered himself so quickly, however, that Mr. Lorry had doubts of his business eye.", "그러나 그가 어찌나 빨리 평정을 되찾았던지, 로리 씨는 자신의 사무가다운 눈을 의심하게 되었다."], ["The arm of the golden giant in the hall was not more steady than he was, when he stopped under it to remark to them that he was not yet proof against slight surprises (if he ever would be), and that the rain had startled him.", "그가 현관의 금빛 거인 아래 멈춰 서서, 자신은 아직 사소한 놀람에도 끄떡없을 만큼은 못 되며 (앞으로 그렇게 될지는 몰라도) 비가 자신을 흠칫하게 했다고 그들에게 말했을 때, 그는 그 금빛 거인의 팔 못지않게 의연했다."]], [["Tea-time, and Miss Pross making tea, with another fit of the jerks upon her, and yet no Hundreds of people.", "차 마시는 시간이 되어, 프로스 양은 또 한 차례 씰룩증 발작에 시달리며 차를 끓였고, 그래도 여전히 수백 명의 사람들은 없었다."], ["Mr. Carton had lounged in, but he made only Two.", "카튼 씨가 어슬렁어슬렁 들어오기는 했으나, 그로써 겨우 두 사람일 뿐이었다."]], [["The night was so very sultry, that although they sat with doors and windows open, they were overpowered by heat.", "밤이 어찌나 후텁지근했던지, 그들이 문과 창을 열어둔 채 앉아 있는데도 더위에 압도되었다."], ["When the tea-table was done with, they all moved to one of the windows, and looked out into the heavy twilight.", "찻상을 물리고 나서, 그들은 모두 창가 하나로 옮겨가, 무겁게 내려앉은 황혼 속을 내다보았다."], ["Lucie sat by her father; Darnay sat beside her; Carton leaned against a window.", "루씨는 아버지 곁에 앉았고, 다네이는 그녀 옆에 앉았으며, 카튼은 창에 기대섰다."], ["The curtains were long and white, and some of the thunder-gusts that whirled into the corner, caught them up to the ceiling, and waved them like spectral wings.", "커튼은 길고 희었는데, 모퉁이로 휘몰아쳐 들어온 천둥 바람의 돌풍 몇 가닥이 그것을 천장까지 끌어올려, 유령 같은 날개처럼 펄럭이게 했다."]], [["“The rain-drops are still falling, large, heavy, and few,”", "\"빗방울이 아직도 떨어지는군요, 굵고, 무겁고, 드문드문.\""], ["said Doctor Manette.", "마네뜨 박사가 말했다."], ["“It comes slowly.”", "\"천천히 오는군요.\""]], [["“It comes surely,”", "\"하지만 틀림없이 옵니다,\""], ["said Carton.", "카튼이 말했다."]], [["They spoke low, as people watching and waiting mostly do; as people in a dark room, watching and waiting for Lightning, always do.", "그들은 나직이 이야기했으니, 지켜보며 기다리는 사람들이 대개 그러하듯이, 어두운 방에서 번개를 지켜보며 기다리는 사람들이 언제나 그러하듯이."]], [["There was a great hurry in the streets of people speeding away to get shelter before the storm broke; the wonderful corner for echoes resounded with the echoes of footsteps coming and going, yet not a footstep was there.", "거리에는 폭풍우가 몰아치기 전에 비를 피할 곳을 찾아 서둘러 달아나는 사람들로 큰 소란이 일었다. 메아리가 울리기에 놀라운 그 모퉁이는 오가는 발소리의 메아리로 쩌렁쩌렁 울렸으나, 정작 거기에 발소리 하나 없었다."]], [["“A multitude of people, and yet a solitude!”", "\"사람의 무리, 그런데도 고독이라니!\""], ["said Darnay, when they had listened for a while.", "한동안 귀 기울이던 끝에 다네이가 말했다."]], [["“Is it not impressive, Mr. Darnay?”", "\"인상적이지 않나요, 다네이 씨?\""], ["asked Lucie.", "루씨가 물었다."], ["“Sometimes, I have sat here of an evening, until I have fancied--but even the shade of a foolish fancy makes me shudder to-night, when all is so black and solemn--”", "\"이따금, 저는 저녁이면 여기 앉아 있다가, 그만 상상하게 되곤 했어요—하지만 이렇게 온통 캄캄하고 엄숙한 오늘 밤엔, 어리석은 상상의 그림자만으로도 몸서리가 쳐지네요—\""]], [["“Let us shudder too.", "\"우리도 함께 몸서리치게 해주십시오."], ["We may know what it is.”", "그게 무엇인지 알 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“It will seem nothing to you.", "\"당신께는 아무것도 아닌 것처럼 여겨질 거예요."], ["Such whims are only impressive as we originate them, I think; they are not to be communicated.", "그런 변덕스러운 생각이란 우리가 스스로 그것을 빚어낼 때에만 인상적인 것 같아요."], ["I have sometimes sat alone here of an evening, listening, until I have made the echoes out to be the echoes of all the footsteps that are coming by-and-bye into our lives.”", "남에게 전해질 수 있는 게 아니지요. 저는 가끔 저녁이면 여기 홀로 앉아 귀 기울이다가, 그 메아리가 머지않아 우리 삶 속으로 들어오게 될 모든 발소리의 메아리라고 여기게 되곤 했어요.\""]], [["“There is a great crowd coming one day into our lives, if that be so,”", "\"정말 그렇다면, 언젠가 큰 무리가 우리 삶 속으로 들어오게 되겠군요.\""], ["Sydney Carton struck in, in his moody way.", "시드니 카튼이 그 침울한 투로 끼어들었다."]], [["The footsteps were incessant, and the hurry of them became more and more rapid.", "발소리는 끊임이 없었고, 그 다급함은 점점 더 빨라졌다. 모퉁이는 발소리에 메아리치고 또 메아리쳤다."], ["The corner echoed and re-echoed with the tread of feet; some, as it seemed, under the windows; some, as it seemed, in the room; some coming, some going, some breaking off, some stopping altogether; all in the distant streets, and not one within sight.", "어떤 것은 창문 아래에서인 듯, 어떤 것은 방안에서인 듯, 어떤 것은 다가오고, 어떤 것은 멀어지고, 어떤 것은 뚝 끊기고, 어떤 것은 아주 멎어버렸다. 모두가 먼 거리에서 나는 것이었고, 시야에 든 것은 하나도 없었다."]], [["“Are all these footsteps destined to come to all of us, Miss Manette, or are we to divide them among us?”", "\"이 모든 발소리가 우리 모두에게 오기로 정해진 걸까요, 마네뜨 양, 아니면 우리가 그것들을 우리끼리 나눠 가지게 될까요?\""]], [["“I don’t know, Mr.", "\"모르겠어요, 다네이 씨."], ["Darnay; I told you it was a foolish fancy, but you asked for it.", "어리석은 상상이라고 말씀드렸잖아요, 그래도 청하시기에."], ["When I have yielded myself to it, I have been alone, and then I have imagined them the footsteps of the people who are to come into my life, and my father’s.”", "제가 거기에 몸을 내맡기곤 했을 때는, 저 혼자였고, 그럴 때면 그것들을 제 삶과 제 아버지의 삶 속으로 들어올 사람들의 발소리라고 상상하곤 했어요.\""]], [["“I take them into mine!”", "\"저는 그것들을 제 삶 속으로 받아들이겠습니다!\""], ["said Carton.", "카튼이 말했다."], ["“I ask no questions and make no stipulations.", "\"저야 아무것도 묻지 않고 아무 조건도 달지 않지요."], ["There is a great crowd bearing down upon us, Miss Manette, and I see them--by the Lightning.”", "큰 무리가 우리를 향해 밀려오고 있어요, 마네뜨 양, 그리고 저는 그들이 보입니다—번갯불로요.\""], ["He added the last words, after there had been a vivid flash which had shown him lounging in the window.", "그는 그를 창가에 어슬렁어슬렁 기댄 모습으로 비춘 한 줄기 선명한 섬광이 지나간 뒤에 이 마지막 말을 덧붙였다."]], [["“And I hear them!”", "\"그리고 그들의 소리가 들립니다!\""], ["he added again, after a peal of thunder.", "그가 천둥소리가 한바탕 지나간 뒤 다시 덧붙였다."], ["“Here they come, fast, fierce, and furious!”", "\"여기 그들이 옵니다, 빠르고, 사납고, 맹렬하게!\""]], [["It was the rush and roar of rain that he typified, and it stopped him, for no voice could be heard in it.", "그것은 그가 빗대어 표현한 비의 돌진과 포효였으니, 그것이 그의 말을 멈추게 했다. 그 속에서는 어떤 목소리도 들릴 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["A memorable storm of thunder and lightning broke with that sweep of water, and there was not a moment’s interval in crash, and fire, and rain, until after the moon rose at midnight.", "그 한바탕 물줄기와 더불어 천둥과 번개의 잊지 못할 폭풍우가 몰아쳤고, 자정에 달이 떠오른 뒤까지, 천둥 치고, 불 번득이고, 비 쏟아지는 데 한순간의 틈도 없었다."]], [["The great bell of Saint Paul’s was striking one in the cleared air, when Mr. Lorry, escorted by Jerry, high-booted and bearing a lantern, set forth on his return-passage to Clerkenwell.", "대기가 맑게 갠 가운데 쎄인트폴 대성당의 큰 종이 한 시를 칠 무렵, 로리 씨는 긴 장화를 신고 등불을 든 제리의 호위를 받으며, 클러큰웰로 돌아가는 길에 나섰다."], ["There were solitary patches of road on the way between Soho and Clerkenwell, and Mr.", "소호와 클러큰웰 사이의 길에는 외진 구간이 군데군데 있었으므로, 로리 씨는 노상강도를 염두에 두고 늘 이 일에 제리를 거느렸다."], ["Lorry, mindful of foot-pads, always retained Jerry for this service: though it was usually performed a good two hours earlier.", "다만 평소에는 이보다 족히 두 시간은 일찍 그 일이 치러졌다."]], [["“What a night it has been!", "\"참 대단한 밤이었네!"], ["Almost a night, Jerry,”", "거의, 제리,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, “to bring the dead out of their graves.”", "로리 씨가 말했다, \"죽은 자들을 무덤에서 불러낼 만한 밤이었어.\""]], [["“I never see the night myself, master--nor yet I don’t expect to--what would do that,”", "\"저야 그런 밤을 본 적이 없습죠, 나리—그럴 일도 없을 것 같고요—그런 일을 하실 밤 말입죠,\""], ["answered Jerry.", "제리가 대답했다."]], [["“Good night, Mr. Carton,”", "\"잘 가게, 카튼 씨,\""], ["said the man of business.", "사무가가 말했다."], ["“Good night, Mr. Darnay.", "\"잘 가시오, 다네이 씨."], ["Shall we ever see such a night again, together!”", "우리가 함께 이런 밤을 또 보게 될까요!\""]], [["Perhaps.", "어쩌면."], ["Perhaps, see the great crowd of people with its rush and roar, bearing down upon them, too.", "어쩌면, 그 돌진과 포효와 더불어 그들을 향해 밀려오는 사람들의 큰 무리 또한 보게 될지도."]], [["# CHAPTER VII. Monseigneur in Town", "# CHAPTER VII. Monseigneur in Town"]], [["Monseigneur, one of the great lords in power at the Court, held his fortnightly reception in his grand hotel in Paris.", "궁정에서 권세를 누리는 대영주들 가운데 하나인 영주는 빠리에 있는 자신의 화려한 저택에서 격주로 접견을 베풀었다."], ["Monseigneur was in his inner room, his sanctuary of sanctuaries, the Holiest of Holiests to the crowd of worshippers in the suite of rooms without.", "영주는 안쪽 방, 그의 성소 중의 성소, 바깥 여러 방에 모인 숭배자 무리에게는 지성소와도 같은 곳에 들어가 있었다."], ["Monseigneur was about to take his chocolate.", "영주는 막 초콜릿을 들려는 참이었다."], ["Monseigneur could swallow a great many things with ease, and was by some few sullen minds supposed to be rather rapidly swallowing France; but, his morning’s chocolate could not so much as get into the throat of Monseigneur, without the aid of four strong men besides the Cook.", "영주는 수많은 것들을 손쉽게 삼킬 수 있었고, 몇몇 시무룩한 사람들은 그가 프랑스마저 꽤 빠른 속도로 삼키고 있다고 여겼다. 그러나 영주의 아침 초콜릿은 요리사 외에 건장한 사내 넷의 도움이 없이는 영주의 목구멍으로 넘어갈 수조차 없었다."]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["It took four men, all four ablaze with gorgeous decoration, and the Chief of them unable to exist with fewer than two gold watches in his pocket, emulative of the noble and chaste fashion set by Monseigneur, to conduct the happy chocolate to Monseigneur’s lips.", "그 행복한 초콜릿을 영주의 입술까지 모셔가는 데에는 네 사람이 필요했으니, 넷 모두 호화로운 장식으로 번쩍였고, 그들의 우두머리는 영주가 정해놓은 고귀하고 정숙한 유행을 본받아 주머니에 금시계 두 개 미만으로는 도무지 존재할 수 없는 자였다."], ["One lacquey carried the chocolate-pot into the sacred presence; a second, milled and frothed the chocolate with the little instrument he bore for that function; a third, presented the favoured napkin; a fourth (he of the two gold watches), poured the chocolate out.", "한 하인은 초콜릿 주전자를 신성한 어전으로 받들어 들고 갔고, 두번째 하인은 그 일에 쓰려고 지니고 다니는 작은 도구로 초콜릿을 휘저어 거품을 냈으며, 세번째 하인은 영광스러운 냅킨을 바쳤고, 네번째 하인(금시계 두 개를 지닌 그자)은 초콜릿을 따랐다."], ["It was impossible for Monseigneur to dispense with one of these attendants on the chocolate and hold his high place under the admiring Heavens.", "영주가 초콜릿 시중을 드는 이 시종들 가운데 하나라도 없이 지내면서, 흠모하는 하늘 아래 그 드높은 자리를 지킨다는 것은 불가능한 일이었다."], ["Deep would have been the blot upon his escutcheon if his chocolate had been ignobly waited on by only three men; he must have died of two.", "만약 그의 초콜릿이 단지 세 사람의 비천한 시중밖에 받지 못했다면 그의 가문 문장에는 깊은 오점이 남았으리라. 둘뿐이었다면 그는 죽어버렸을 것이 분명했다."]], [["Monseigneur had been out at a little supper last night, where the Comedy and the Grand Opera were charmingly represented.", "영주는 어젯밤 어느 소박한 만찬에 다녀왔는데, 그곳에서는 희극과 그랜드 오페라가 매혹적으로 펼쳐졌다."], ["Monseigneur was out at a little supper most nights, with fascinating company.", "영주는 거의 매일 밤 매혹적인 친구들과 더불어 소박한 만찬에 나갔다."], ["So polite and so impressible was Monseigneur, that the Comedy and the Grand Opera had far more influence with him in the tiresome articles of state affairs and state secrets, than the needs of all France.", "영주는 어찌나 점잖고 또 감수성이 예민한지, 따분한 국사며 국가 기밀의 문제에 있어서는 희극과 그랜드 오페라가 프랑스 전체의 필요보다 그에게 훨씬 더 큰 영향을 미쳤다."], ["A happy circumstance for France, as the like always is for all countries similarly favoured!--always was for England (by way of example), in the regretted days of the merry Stuart who sold it.", "프랑스로서는 행복한 일이었으니, 비슷한 은총을 입은 모든 나라가 늘 그러하듯이! 일례로, 나라를 팔아넘긴 저 유쾌한 스튜어트 왕가의 안타까운 시절 영국이 늘 그러했듯이."]], [["Monseigneur had one truly noble idea of general public business, which was, to let everything go on in its own way; of particular public business, Monseigneur had the other truly noble idea that it must all go his way--tend to his own power and pocket.", "영주는 일반적인 공무에 관해 참으로 고귀한 생각 하나를 품고 있었으니, 모든 것이 제멋대로 굴러가게 내버려두자는 것이었다. 특정한 공무에 관해서는 모든 일이 자신의 뜻대로 풀려야 한다는, 즉 자신의 권력과 주머니에 보탬이 되어야 한다는 또 하나의 참으로 고귀한 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["Of his pleasures, general and particular, Monseigneur had the other truly noble idea, that the world was made for them.", "일반적이든 특정한 것이든 자신의 쾌락에 관해서는, 세상이 그 쾌락을 위해 만들어졌다는 또 하나의 참으로 고귀한 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["The text of his order (altered from the original by only a pronoun, which is not much) ran: “The earth and the fulness thereof are mine, saith Monseigneur.”", "그가 내린 명령의 본문은 (원문에서 대명사 하나만 바꾼 것이니, 대단한 일은 아니다) 이러했다. \"땅과 거기 가득 찬 것이 다 내 것이로다, 영주께서 이르시니라.\""]], [["Yet, Monseigneur had slowly found that vulgar embarrassments crept into his affairs, both private and public; and he had, as to both classes of affairs, allied himself perforce with a Farmer-General.", "그러나 영주는 사적으로나 공적으로나 자신의 일에 천박한 곤경이 스며들고 있음을 차츰 깨닫게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 두 부류의 일에 관해 부득이 어느 징세청부업자와 손을 잡았다."], ["As to finances public, because Monseigneur could not make anything at all of them, and must consequently let them out to somebody who could; as to finances private, because Farmer-Generals were rich, and Monseigneur, after generations of great luxury and expense, was growing poor.", "공적인 재정에 관해서는, 영주가 그것을 도무지 어찌해볼 수 없었던 까닭에 그럴 수 있는 누군가에게 도급으로 넘겨야 했기 때문이고, 사적인 재정에 관해서는, 징세청부업자들이 부유한 반면, 영주는 여러 세대에 걸친 대단한 사치와 낭비 끝에 점점 가난해지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Hence Monseigneur had taken his sister from a convent, while there was yet time to ward off the impending veil, the cheapest garment she could wear, and had bestowed her as a prize upon a very rich Farmer-General, poor in family.", "그리하여 영주는 자신의 누이를, 닥쳐오는 베일 — 그녀가 걸칠 수 있는 가장 값싼 옷이었다 — 을 막아낼 시간이 아직 남아 있을 때 수녀원에서 데려와, 가문은 보잘것없으나 매우 부유한 어느 징세청부업자에게 상으로 하사했다."], ["Which Farmer-General, carrying an appropriate cane with a golden apple on the top of it, was now among the company in the outer rooms, much prostrated before by mankind--always excepting superior mankind of the blood of Monseigneur, who, his own wife included, looked down upon him with the loftiest contempt.", "그 징세청부업자는 꼭대기에 황금 사과가 달린 어울리는 지팡이를 짚고서, 지금 바깥 방에 모인 손님들 가운데 끼어 있었으며, 사람들 앞에서 한껏 굽실거림을 받고 있었다. 다만 영주의 핏줄을 이은 우월한 사람들만은 예외였으니, 그자의 아내까지 포함해서, 그들은 더없이 도도한 경멸로 그를 내려다보았다."]], [["A sumptuous man was the Farmer-General.", "징세청부업자는 호사스러운 인물이었다."], ["Thirty horses stood in his stables, twenty-four male domestics sat in his halls, six body-women waited on his wife.", "그의 마구간에는 말 서른 마리가 있었고, 그의 홀에는 스물네 명의 남자 하인이 앉아 있었으며, 여섯 명의 시녀가 그의 아내의 시중을 들었다."], ["As one who pretended to do nothing but plunder and forage where he could, the Farmer-General--howsoever his matrimonial relations conduced to social morality--was at least the greatest reality among the personages who attended at the hotel of Monseigneur that day.", "닥치는 대로 약탈하고 노략질하는 것 말고는 아무 일도 하는 체하지 않는 자로서, 징세청부업자는 — 그의 혼인 관계가 사회의 도덕에 어떤 식으로 이바지했든 간에 — 적어도 그날 영주의 저택에 모인 인물들 가운데 가장 큰 실체였다."]], [["For, the rooms, though a beautiful scene to look at, and adorned with every device of decoration that the taste and skill of the time could achieve, were, in truth, not a sound business; considered with any reference to the scarecrows in the rags and nightcaps elsewhere (and not so far off, either, but that the watching towers of Notre Dame, almost equidistant from the two extremes, could see them both), they would have been an exceedingly uncomfortable business--if that could have been anybody’s business, at the house of Monseigneur.", "방들은 보기에 아름다운 풍경이었고, 당대의 취향과 기교가 이룰 수 있는 온갖 장식의 묘를 다해 꾸며져 있었으나, 사실 그것은 건전한 거래가 못 되었다. 다른 곳에서 누더기와 잠자리 모자를 걸치고 있는 허수아비들에 견주어 본다면 (게다가 그리 멀지도 않은 곳이어서, 그 양극단으로부터 거의 같은 거리에 있는 노트르담의 망루들은 양쪽을 다 내려다볼 수 있었다) 그것은 지극히 불편한 거래였으리라 — 영주의 저택에서 그것이 누군가의 관심사가 될 수 있었다면 말이다."], ["Military officers destitute of military knowledge; naval officers with no idea of a ship; civil officers without a notion of affairs; brazen ecclesiastics, of the worst world worldly, with sensual eyes, loose tongues, and looser lives; all totally unfit for their several callings, all lying horribly in pretending to belong to them, but all nearly or remotely of the order of Monseigneur, and therefore foisted on all public employments from which anything was to be got; these were to be told off by the score and the score.", "군사 지식이라곤 없는 군 장교들, 배에 대한 개념이 없는 해군 장교들, 사무라곤 모르는 행정 관리들, 세속 중의 세속에 물든 뻔뻔한 성직자들, 음탕한 눈빛과 헤픈 혀와 더 헤픈 삶을 지닌 자들, 모두 저마다의 소임에 전혀 어울리지 않으면서도 거기에 속한 척 끔찍하게 거짓말을 늘어놓는 자들, 그러나 모두가 가깝게든 멀게든 영주의 부류에 속하여, 그리하여 무엇이든 얻어낼 것이 있는 온갖 공직마다 슬그머니 끼어든 자들, 이런 자들이 수십 명씩 또 수십 명씩 헤아려졌다."], ["People not immediately connected with Monseigneur or the State, yet equally unconnected with anything that was real, or with lives passed in travelling by any straight road to any true earthly end, were no less abundant.", "영주나 국가와 직접적인 관련이 없으면서도, 실재하는 그 무엇과도, 또는 어떤 참된 지상의 목적을 향해 곧은길로 나아가며 보낸 삶과도 똑같이 무관한 사람들 또한 그에 못지않게 넘쳐났다."], ["Doctors who made great fortunes out of dainty remedies for imaginary disorders that never existed, smiled upon their courtly patients in the ante-chambers of Monseigneur.", "결코 존재한 적도 없는 가상의 질병에 대한 고상한 치료법으로 큰 재산을 모은 의사들이 영주의 대기실에서 자기네 궁정풍 환자들에게 미소를 지었다."], ["Projectors who had discovered every kind of remedy for the little evils with which the State was touched, except the remedy of setting to work in earnest to root out a single sin, poured their distracting babble into any ears they could lay hold of, at the reception of Monseigneur.", "단 하나의 악이라도 진심으로 뿌리 뽑으려 일에 나서는 치료법만 빼고, 국가가 시달리는 자잘한 폐단 하나하나마다 온갖 치료법을 발견한 기획가들이, 영주의 접견에서 붙잡을 수 있는 모든 귀에 대고 정신 사나운 헛소리를 쏟아부었다."], ["Unbelieving Philosophers who were remodelling the world with words, and making card-towers of Babel to scale the skies with, talked with Unbelieving Chemists who had an eye on the transmutation of metals, at this wonderful gathering accumulated by Monseigneur.", "말로써 세상을 다시 빚어내고, 하늘에 닿으려고 바벨의 카드탑을 쌓는 불신의 철학자들이, 금속의 변성에 눈독을 들이는 불신의 화학자들과 더불어, 영주가 그러모은 이 놀라운 모임에서 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Exquisite gentlemen of the finest breeding, which was at that remarkable time--and has been since--to be known by its fruits of indifference to every natural subject of human interest, were in the most exemplary state of exhaustion, at the hotel of Monseigneur.", "가장 빼어난 가문 출신의 세련된 신사들 — 그 시절, 그 주목할 만한 때에, 그리고 그 이후로도, 인간의 관심을 끄는 모든 자연스러운 주제에 대한 무관심이라는 열매로 그 정체를 알아볼 수 있는 자들 — 이 영주의 저택에서 더없이 모범적인 권태의 상태에 빠져 있었다."], ["Such homes had these various notabilities left behind them in the fine world of Paris, that the spies among the assembled devotees of Monseigneur--forming a goodly half of the polite company--would have found it hard to discover among the angels of that sphere one solitary wife, who, in her manners and appearance, owned to being a Mother.", "이 다양한 명사들이 빠리의 화려한 사교계에 남겨두고 온 가정이라는 것이 어떠했는가 하면, 영주를 떠받드는 신도 무리에 섞인 첩자들이 — 그 점잖은 손님들의 족히 절반은 차지했다 — 그 영역의 천사들 가운데서 그 몸가짐과 차림새로 보아 어머니임을 자처하는 단 한 명의 아내라도 찾아내기가 어려울 정도였다."], ["Indeed, except for the mere act of bringing a troublesome creature into this world--which does not go far towards the realisation of the name of mother--there was no such thing known to the fashion.", "사실, 성가신 생명 하나를 이 세상에 데려오는 단순한 행위를 빼면 — 이것만으로는 어머니라는 이름을 실현하는 데 그다지 보탬이 되지 못한다 — 그런 것은 유행에 알려진 바가 없었다."], ["Peasant women kept the unfashionable babies close, and brought them up, and charming grandmammas of sixty dressed and supped as at twenty.", "농촌 아낙들이 유행에 뒤떨어진 갓난아기들을 곁에 두고 길렀고, 매력적인 예순 살 할머니들은 스무 살처럼 차려입고 만찬을 들었다."]], [["The leprosy of unreality disfigured every human creature in attendance upon Monseigneur.", "비현실이라는 나병이 영주의 시중을 드는 모든 인간을 흉하게 일그러뜨렸다."], ["In the outermost room were half a dozen exceptional people who had had, for a few years, some vague misgiving in them that things in general were going rather wrong.", "가장 바깥쪽 방에는 몇 해 전부터, 세상만사가 어쩐지 잘못되어 가고 있다는 막연한 불안을 품어온 예외적인 인물 여섯이 있었다."], ["As a promising way of setting them right, half of the half-dozen had become members of a fantastic sect of Convulsionists, and were even then considering within themselves whether they should foam, rage, roar, and turn cataleptic on the spot--thereby setting up a highly intelligible finger-post to the Future, for Monseigneur’s guidance.", "그것을 바로잡을 유망한 방도라며, 그 여섯 중 절반은 어느 기괴한 경련파의 일원이 되어, 바로 그 자리에서 거품을 물고 미쳐 날뛰고 울부짖으며 강경증에 빠져들지 말지를, 그리하여 영주의 길잡이가 될 미래로 향하는 더없이 알기 쉬운 이정표를 세울지 말지를 그때까지도 마음속으로 궁리하고 있었다."], ["Besides these Dervishes, were other three who had rushed into another sect, which mended matters with a jargon about “the Centre of Truth:”", "이 데르비시들 외에 다른 세 사람은 또 다른 종파로 뛰어들었는데, 그들은 \"진리의 중심\"에 관한 횡설수설로 사태를 바로잡으려 했다."], ["holding that Man had got out of the Centre of Truth--which did not need much demonstration--but had not got out of the Circumference, and that he was to be kept from flying out of the Circumference, and was even to be shoved back into the Centre, by fasting and seeing of spirits.", "그들이 주장하기를, 인간은 진리의 중심에서 벗어났으나 — 이건 굳이 증명할 것도 없었다 — 그 원주 밖으로까지 나가지는 않았으며, 단식과 영혼을 보는 것으로써 인간이 원주 밖으로 날아가지 못하게 막고, 나아가 중심으로 도로 밀어 넣어야 한다는 것이었다."], ["Among these, accordingly, much discoursing with spirits went on--and it did a world of good which never became manifest.", "그리하여 이들 사이에서는 영혼들과 나누는 많은 대화가 오갔으나 — 그것은 결코 드러난 적 없는 무량한 선을 베풀었다."]], [["But, the comfort was, that all the company at the grand hotel of Monseigneur were perfectly dressed.", "그러나 위안이 되는 것은, 영주의 화려한 저택에 모인 손님들이 모두 완벽하게 차려입었다는 사실이었다."], ["If the Day of Judgment had only been ascertained to be a dress day, everybody there would have been eternally correct.", "최후의 심판일이 정장을 갖춰야 하는 날로 확정되기만 했다면, 그곳의 모든 이가 영원히 흠잡을 데 없었으리라."], ["Such frizzling and powdering and sticking up of hair, such delicate complexions artificially preserved and mended, such gallant swords to look at, and such delicate honour to the sense of smell, would surely keep anything going, for ever and ever.", "그토록 곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고 위로 빗어 올린 머리, 그토록 정교하게 인위적으로 보존하고 손질한 섬세한 안색, 그토록 보기에 늠름한 검들, 그토록 후각에 섬세한 경의를 표하는 향내라면, 분명 무엇이든 영원토록 굴러가게 할 수 있으리라."], ["The exquisite gentlemen of the finest breeding wore little pendent trinkets that chinked as they languidly moved; these golden fetters rang like precious little bells; and what with that ringing, and with the rustle of silk and brocade and fine linen, there was a flutter in the air that fanned Saint Antoine and his devouring hunger far away.", "가장 빼어난 가문 출신의 세련된 신사들은 나른하게 움직일 때마다 짤랑이는 작은 장신구들을 매달고 다녔으니, 이 황금 족쇄는 값진 작은 종처럼 울렸다. 그렇게 울리는 소리와 더불어 비단과 금란과 고운 아마포가 사각거리는 소리에, 공기 중에 일렁임이 일어나 생땅뚜안과 그곳을 집어삼키는 굶주림을 저 멀리로 부채질해 보냈다."]], [["Dress was the one unfailing talisman and charm used for keeping all things in their places.", "복식이야말로 모든 것을 제자리에 붙들어두는 데 쓰이는 단 하나의 어김없는 부적이자 마력이었다."], ["Everybody was dressed for a Fancy Ball that was never to leave off.", "누구나 결코 끝나지 않을 가장무도회를 위해 차려입고 있었다."], ["From the Palace of the Tuileries, through Monseigneur and the whole Court, through the Chambers, the Tribunals of Justice, and all society (except the scarecrows), the Fancy Ball descended to the Common Executioner: who, in pursuance of the charm, was required to officiate “frizzled, powdered, in a gold-laced coat, pumps, and white silk stockings.”", "뛸르리 궁전으로부터, 영주와 궁정 전체를 거쳐, 의회와 사법재판소를 거쳐, 그리고 (허수아비들을 뺀) 사회 전체를 거쳐, 이 가장무도회는 사형 집행인에게까지 내려갔으니, 그 또한 마력에 따라 \"곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고, 금실로 수놓은 외투에 단화와 흰 비단 양말 차림으로\" 직무를 수행하도록 요구받았다."], ["At the gallows and the wheel--the axe was a rarity--Monsieur Paris, as it was the episcopal mode among his brother Professors of the provinces, Monsieur Orleans, and the rest, to call him, presided in this dainty dress.", "교수대와 형차에서 — 도끼는 드물었다 — 무슈 빠리는, 지방의 동료 집행인들인 무슈 오를레앙 등과 마찬가지로 그를 그렇게 주교식으로 부르는 것이 관례였는데, 이 우아한 차림으로 의식을 주재했다."], ["And who among the company at Monseigneur’s reception in that seventeen hundred and eightieth year of our Lord, could possibly doubt, that a system rooted in a frizzled hangman, powdered, gold-laced, pumped, and white-silk stockinged, would see the very stars out!", "주님의 해 1780년에 영주의 접견에 모인 손님들 가운데, 곱슬곱슬 지지고 분을 바르고 금실 수를 놓고 단화에 흰 비단 양말을 신은 사형 집행인에 뿌리내린 체제가 별들이 다 사그라드는 날까지 살아남으리라는 것을 의심할 자가 어디 있었으랴!"]], [["Monseigneur having eased his four men of their burdens and taken his chocolate, caused the doors of the Holiest of Holiests to be thrown open, and issued forth.", "영주가 네 사내의 짐을 덜어주고 초콜릿을 든 뒤, 지성소의 문을 활짝 열게 하고서 밖으로 나섰다."], ["Then, what submission, what cringing and fawning, what servility, what abject humiliation!", "그러자 그 얼마나 굽실거림이며, 그 얼마나 비굴한 알랑거림이며, 그 얼마나 비루한 비굴함이며, 그 얼마나 천한 굴종이었던가!"], ["As to bowing down in body and spirit, nothing in that way was left for Heaven--which may have been one among other reasons why the worshippers of Monseigneur never troubled it.", "몸과 마음을 다해 머리를 조아리는 것으로 말하자면, 하늘에 바칠 몫이라곤 그런 식으로는 하나도 남아 있지 않았다 — 이것이야말로 영주의 숭배자들이 결코 하늘을 번거롭게 하지 않은 여러 이유들 가운데 하나였을 것이다."]], [["Bestowing a word of promise here and a smile there, a whisper on one happy slave and a wave of the hand on another, Monseigneur affably passed through his rooms to the remote region of the Circumference of Truth.", "여기서는 약속의 말 한마디, 저기서는 미소 한번, 어느 행복한 노예에게는 속삭임 한마디, 또 다른 노예에게는 손짓 한번을 베풀면서, 영주는 상냥하게 여러 방을 지나 진리의 원주 끄트머리 영역까지 나아갔다."], ["There, Monseigneur turned, and came back again, and so in due course of time got himself shut up in his sanctuary by the chocolate sprites, and was seen no more.", "그곳에서 영주는 돌아서서 다시 되돌아왔고, 그리하여 때가 되자 초콜릿 요정들의 손에 이끌려 자신의 성소에 갇혀버리니, 다시는 모습을 보이지 않았다."]], [["The show being over, the flutter in the air became quite a little storm, and the precious little bells went ringing downstairs.", "구경거리가 끝나자 공기 중의 일렁임은 자못 작은 폭풍이 되었고, 값진 작은 종들은 짤랑거리며 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["There was soon but one person left of all the crowd, and he, with his hat under his arm and his snuff-box in his hand, slowly passed among the mirrors on his way out.", "이윽고 그 많은 무리 가운데 단 한 사람만이 남았는데, 그는 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 코담뱃갑을 손에 든 채, 거울들 사이를 천천히 지나 밖으로 향했다."]], [["“I devote you,”", "\"네놈을 바치노라.\""], ["said this person, stopping at the last door on his way, and turning in the direction of the sanctuary, “to the Devil!”", "그 사람이 나가는 길의 마지막 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 성소 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"악마에게!\""]], [["With that, he shook the snuff from his fingers as if he had shaken the dust from his feet, and quietly walked downstairs.", "그 말과 함께, 그는 마치 발에서 먼지를 털어내듯 손가락에 묻은 코담배를 털고는 조용히 아래층으로 걸어 내려갔다."]], [["He was a man of about sixty, handsomely dressed, haughty in manner, and with a face like a fine mask.", "그는 예순 살가량의 남자로, 잘 차려입었고, 거동이 거만했으며, 정교한 가면 같은 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["A face of a transparent paleness; every feature in it clearly defined; one set expression on it.", "투명하리만치 창백한 얼굴, 이목구비 하나하나가 또렷이 새겨진 얼굴, 그 위에 굳어 있는 단 하나의 표정."], ["The nose, beautifully formed otherwise, was very slightly pinched at the top of each nostril.", "그밖에는 아름답게 빚어진 코는 양쪽 콧구멍 위가 아주 살짝 오므라져 있었다."], ["In those two compressions, or dints, the only little change that the face ever showed, resided.", "그 두 군데 죈 자국, 곧 옴폭한 곳에 그 얼굴이 보이는 유일하게 자그마한 변화가 깃들어 있었다."], ["They persisted in changing colour sometimes, and they would be occasionally dilated and contracted by something like a faint pulsation; then, they gave a look of treachery, and cruelty, to the whole countenance.", "그것들은 이따금 끈질기게 빛깔을 바꾸었고, 때때로 희미한 맥박 같은 무언가에 따라 벌어졌다 오므라들었다. 그러면 얼굴 전체에 음흉함과 잔인함의 기색이 어렸다."], ["Examined with attention, its capacity of helping such a look was to be found in the line of the mouth, and the lines of the orbits of the eyes, being much too horizontal and thin; still, in the effect of the face made, it was a handsome face, and a remarkable one.", "주의 깊게 살펴보면, 그런 표정을 거드는 그 능력은 입매와 두 눈구멍의 윤곽이 지나치게 수평적이고 가느다란 데서 비롯됨을 알 수 있었다. 그래도 얼굴이 자아내는 인상으로 보자면 그것은 잘생긴 얼굴이었고, 또한 비범한 얼굴이었다."]], [["Its owner went downstairs into the courtyard, got into his carriage, and drove away.", "그 임자는 아래층 안뜰로 내려가 마차에 올라 떠나갔다."], ["Not many people had talked with him at the reception; he had stood in a little space apart, and Monseigneur might have been warmer in his manner.", "접견에서 그와 이야기를 나눈 사람은 많지 않았으니, 그는 조금 떨어진 좁은 자리에 서 있었고, 영주도 그를 좀 더 따뜻하게 대할 수 있었으련만 그러지 않았다."], ["It appeared, under the circumstances, rather agreeable to him to see the common people dispersed before his horses, and often barely escaping from being run down.", "이런 상황에서, 자신의 말들 앞으로 평민들이 흩어지고 종종 가까스로 깔려 죽기를 면하는 광경을 보는 것이 그에게는 자못 흐뭇한 일인 듯했다."], ["His man drove as if he were charging an enemy, and the furious recklessness of the man brought no check into the face, or to the lips, of the master.", "그의 마부는 적진으로 돌격하듯 마차를 몰았고, 마부의 그 사납고 무모한 짓에도 주인의 얼굴이나 입가에는 아무런 제지의 기색이 떠오르지 않았다."], ["The complaint had sometimes made itself audible, even in that deaf city and dumb age, that, in the narrow streets without footways, the fierce patrician custom of hard driving endangered and maimed the mere vulgar in a barbarous manner.", "이따금, 그 귀먹은 도시와 벙어리 같은 시대에서조차, 인도조차 없는 좁은 거리에서 험하게 마차를 모는 귀족들의 사나운 관습이 한낱 천민들을 야만스럽게 위태롭게 하고 불구로 만든다는 불평이 들려오곤 했다."], ["But, few cared enough for that to think of it a second time, and, in this matter, as in all others, the common wretches were left to get out of their difficulties as they could.", "그러나 그 일을 두 번 생각할 만큼 마음 쓰는 이는 거의 없었고, 이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 그 가련한 비참한 이들은 알아서 곤경을 헤쳐 나가도록 내버려졌다."]], [["With a wild rattle and clatter, and an inhuman abandonment of consideration not easy to be understood in these days, the carriage dashed through streets and swept round corners, with women screaming before it, and men clutching each other and clutching children out of its way.", "요즈음에는 좀처럼 이해하기 어려운, 거칠게 덜컹대고 쩔걱대는 소리와 함께, 그리고 비인간적으로 배려심이라곤 내팽개친 채, 마차는 거리를 내달리며 모퉁이를 휙휙 돌았고, 그 앞에서 여자들은 비명을 질렀으며 남자들은 서로를 부둥켜안고 아이들을 그 길에서 낚아챘다."], ["At last, swooping at a street corner by a fountain, one of its wheels came to a sickening little jolt, and there was a loud cry from a number of voices, and the horses reared and plunged.", "마침내 어느 샘가 길모퉁이를 덮치듯 돌아드는데, 바퀴 하나가 속이 메스꺼울 만큼 작게 덜컹였고, 여러 목소리에서 큰 외침이 터져 나왔으며, 말들이 앞발을 쳐들고 날뛰었다."]], [["But for the latter inconvenience, the carriage probably would not have stopped; carriages were often known to drive on, and leave their wounded behind, and why not?", "이 마지막 사달이 아니었더라면 마차는 아마 멈추지 않았으리라. 마차들이 그대로 달려가 부상자를 뒤에 남겨두고 가는 일은 흔히 알려진 바였으니, 안 될 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["But the frightened valet had got down in a hurry, and there were twenty hands at the horses’ bridles.", "그러나 겁에 질린 시종이 황급히 내려왔고, 말들의 고삐에는 스무 개의 손이 매달려 있었다."]], [["“What has gone wrong?”", "\"무슨 일이 잘못됐느냐?\""], ["said Monsieur, calmly looking out.", "무슈가 차분히 내다보며 말했다."]], [["A tall man in a nightcap had caught up a bundle from among the feet of the horses, and had laid it on the basement of the fountain, and was down in the mud and wet, howling over it like a wild animal.", "잠자리 모자를 쓴 키 큰 남자가 말들의 발치에서 보따리 하나를 집어 올려 샘의 받침대 위에 뉘어놓고는, 진흙과 물구덩이에 엎드린 채 짐승처럼 그 위에서 울부짖고 있었다."]], [["“Pardon, Monsieur the Marquis!”", "\"용서하십시오, 후작 나리!\""], ["said a ragged and submissive man, “it is a child.”", "누더기를 걸친 비굴한 남자가 말했다. \"아이입니다.\""]], [["“Why does he make that abominable noise?", "\"왜 저놈은 저 끔찍한 소리를 내느냐?"], ["Is it his child?”", "저자의 아이냐?\""]], [["“Excuse me, Monsieur the Marquis--it is a pity--yes.”", "\"송구합니다, 후작 나리 — 가엾게도 — 그렇습니다.\""]], [["The fountain was a little removed; for the street opened, where it was, into a space some ten or twelve yards square.", "샘은 조금 떨어져 있었으니, 그곳에서는 거리가 트여 사방 열에서 열두 야드쯤 되는 공터로 이어졌다."], ["As the tall man suddenly got up from the ground, and came running at the carriage, Monsieur the Marquis clapped his hand for an instant on his sword-hilt.", "키 큰 남자가 갑자기 땅바닥에서 일어나 마차를 향해 달려오자, 후작은 한순간 검 자루에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Killed!”", "\"죽었어!\""], ["shrieked the man, in wild desperation, extending both arms at their length above his head, and staring at him.", "남자가 사납게 절망하여 두 팔을 머리 위로 한껏 뻗고 그를 노려보며 비명을 질렀다."], ["“Dead!”", "\"죽었다고!\""]], [["The people closed round, and looked at Monsieur the Marquis.", "사람들이 둘러서서 후작을 바라보았다."], ["There was nothing revealed by the many eyes that looked at him but watchfulness and eagerness; there was no visible menacing or anger.", "그를 바라보는 그 숱한 눈에서 드러나는 것은 오로지 경계심과 간절함뿐, 눈에 보이는 위협이나 분노는 없었다."], ["Neither did the people say anything; after the first cry, they had been silent, and they remained so.", "사람들은 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 처음의 그 외침 이후로 그들은 입을 다물었고, 그대로 침묵을 지켰다."], ["The voice of the submissive man who had spoken, was flat and tame in its extreme submission.", "아까 말을 한 비굴한 남자의 목소리는 그 극진한 굴종 속에서 밋밋하고 맥이 없었다."], ["Monsieur the Marquis ran his eyes over them all, as if they had been mere rats come out of their holes.", "후작은 그들 모두를, 마치 제 굴에서 기어 나온 한낱 쥐들인 양 눈으로 훑었다."]], [["He took out his purse.", "그는 지갑을 꺼냈다."]], [["“It is extraordinary to me,”", "\"나로서는 참 별일이다.\""], ["said he, “that you people cannot take care of yourselves and your children.", "그가 말했다. \"너희 같은 것들이 제 한 몸과 제 자식 하나 건사하지 못한다는 것이."], ["One or the other of you is for ever in the way.", "너희 중 누구 하나는 늘 길을 막고 있단 말이다."], ["How do I know what injury you have done my horses.", "너희가 내 말들에 무슨 상해를 입혔는지 내 어찌 알겠느냐."], ["See!", "옜다!"], ["Give him that.”", "저자에게 이걸 주어라.\""]], [["He threw out a gold coin for the valet to pick up, and all the heads craned forward that all the eyes might look down at it as it fell.", "그는 시종이 줍도록 금화 한 닢을 던졌고, 그것이 떨어질 때 모든 눈이 그것을 내려다보려고 모든 머리가 앞으로 쭉 빼졌다."], ["The tall man called out again with a most unearthly cry, “Dead!”", "키 큰 남자는 다시 더없이 섬뜩한 비명으로 외쳤다. \"죽었어!\""]], [["He was arrested by the quick arrival of another man, for whom the rest made way.", "또 다른 남자가 잽싸게 다가오는 바람에 그는 외침을 멈추었고, 나머지 사람들은 그에게 길을 비켜주었다."], ["On seeing him, the miserable creature fell upon his shoulder, sobbing and crying, and pointing to the fountain, where some women were stooping over the motionless bundle, and moving gently about it.", "그를 보자 그 가엾은 사내는 그의 어깨에 쓰러져 흐느끼며 울었고, 샘 쪽을 가리켰는데, 그곳에서는 몇몇 여자들이 미동도 없는 보따리 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 그것을 조심스럽게 매만지고 있었다."], ["They were as silent, however, as the men.", "그러나 그 여자들 또한 남자들처럼 말이 없었다."]], [["“I know all, I know all,”", "\"다 알아, 다 안다네.\""], ["said the last comer.", "마지막에 온 남자가 말했다."], ["“Be a brave man, my Gaspard!", "\"용감한 사람이 되게, 내 친구 가스빠르!"], ["It is better for the poor little plaything to die so, than to live.", "저 가엾은 어린것에게는 저렇게 사느니 죽는 편이 낫다네."], ["It has died in a moment without pain.", "고통 없이 한순간에 죽었어."], ["Could it have lived an hour as happily?”", "한 시간인들 저렇게 행복하게 살 수 있었겠나?\""]], [["“You are a philosopher, you there,”", "\"이봐, 거기 자네, 자네는 철학자로군.\""], ["said the Marquis, smiling.", "후작이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“How do they call you?”", "\"이름이 무엇인가?\""]], [["“They call me Defarge.”", "\"드파르주라고 합니다.\""]], [["“Of what trade?”", "\"무슨 일을 하는가?\""]], [["“Monsieur the Marquis, vendor of wine.”", "\"후작 나리, 포도주 장수입니다.\""]], [["“Pick up that, philosopher and vendor of wine,”", "\"그것을 집어라, 철학자이자 포도주 장수여.\""], ["said the Marquis, throwing him another gold coin, “and spend it as you will.", "후작이 그에게 또 다른 금화 한 닢을 던지며 말했다. \"그러고는 좋을 대로 써버려라."], ["The horses there; are they right?”", "거기 말들은, 괜찮으냐?\""]], [["Without deigning to look at the assemblage a second time, Monsieur the Marquis leaned back in his seat, and was just being driven away with the air of a gentleman who had accidentally broke some common thing, and had paid for it, and could afford to pay for it; when his ease was suddenly disturbed by a coin flying into his carriage, and ringing on its floor.", "후작은 다시 한번 그 무리를 거들떠보지도 않은 채 좌석에 등을 기댔고, 어쩌다 흔해빠진 물건 하나를 깨뜨리고 그 값을 치렀으며 또 능히 치를 수 있는 신사의 태도로 막 마차를 몰아 떠나려는 참이었다. 그때 동전 한 닢이 그의 마차 안으로 날아들어 바닥에 쨍그랑 울리는 바람에 그의 평온이 별안간 깨졌다."]], [["“Hold!”", "\"멈춰라!\""], ["said Monsieur the Marquis.", "후작이 말했다."], ["“Hold the horses!", "\"말들을 멈춰라!"], ["Who threw that?”", "누가 저것을 던졌느냐?\""]], [["He looked to the spot where Defarge the vendor of wine had stood, a moment before; but the wretched father was grovelling on his face on the pavement in that spot, and the figure that stood beside him was the figure of a dark stout woman, knitting.", "그는 조금 전까지 포도주 장수 드파르주가 서 있던 자리를 바라보았다. 그러나 그 비참한 아버지는 바로 그 자리 포석 위에 얼굴을 박고 엎드려 있었고, 그 곁에 서 있는 모습은 뜨개질을 하는, 가무잡잡하고 다부진 여자의 모습이었다."]], [["“You dogs!”", "\"이 개 같은 것들!\""], ["said the Marquis, but smoothly, and with an unchanged front, except as to the spots on his nose: “I would ride over any of you very willingly, and exterminate you from the earth.", "후작이 말했으나, 코 위의 그 자국만은 예외로, 매끄럽게, 흐트러짐 없는 표정으로 말했다. \"너희 중 누구라도 기꺼이 깔아뭉개서 이 땅에서 쓸어버리고 싶구나."], ["If I knew which rascal threw at the carriage, and if that brigand were sufficiently near it, he should be crushed under the wheels.”", "어느 놈이 마차에 던졌는지 안다면, 그리고 그 도적놈이 충분히 가까이 있다면, 바퀴 아래 짓이겨놓을 텐데.\""]], [["So cowed was their condition, and so long and hard their experience of what such a man could do to them, within the law and beyond it, that not a voice, or a hand, or even an eye was raised.", "그들의 처지는 그토록 주눅 들어 있었고, 그런 사내가 법의 테두리 안에서든 밖에서든 그들에게 무슨 짓을 할 수 있는지 오래도록 모질게 겪어왔던 터라, 목소리 하나, 손 하나, 심지어 눈길 하나조차 들지 않았다. 남자들 가운데는 단 하나도 없었다."], ["Among the men, not one.", "그러나 서서 뜨개질을 하던 여자만은 흔들림 없이 고개를 들어 후작의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["But the woman who stood knitting looked up steadily, and looked the Marquis in the face.", "그것을 알은체하는 것은 그의 체면에 어울리지 않는 일이었다. 그의 경멸에 찬 눈길은 그녀를, 그리고 다른 모든 쥐들을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["It was not for his dignity to notice it; his contemptuous eyes passed over her, and over all the other rats; and he leaned back in his seat again, and gave the word “Go on!”", "그러고는 다시 좌석에 등을 기대고 \"가자!\" 하고 명령을 내렸다."]], [["He was driven on, and other carriages came whirling by in quick succession; the Minister, the State-Projector, the Farmer-General, the Doctor, the Lawyer, the Ecclesiastic, the Grand Opera, the Comedy, the whole Fancy Ball in a bright continuous flow, came whirling by.", "마차는 다시 내달렸고, 다른 마차들이 잇따라 빠르게 휙휙 지나갔으니, 장관, 국가 기획가, 징세청부업자, 의사, 변호사, 성직자, 그랜드 오페라, 희극, 그 가장무도회 전체가 환하게 끊임없이 흐르며 휙휙 지나갔다."], ["The rats had crept out of their holes to look on, and they remained looking on for hours; soldiers and police often passing between them and the spectacle, and making a barrier behind which they slunk, and through which they peeped.", "쥐들은 제 굴에서 기어 나와 구경을 했고, 몇 시간이고 구경을 계속했다. 군인들과 경찰이 자주 그들과 그 구경거리 사이를 오가며 장벽을 만들었고, 그들은 그 뒤로 슬그머니 숨어 그 사이로 엿보았다."], ["The father had long ago taken up his bundle and bidden himself away with it, when the women who had tended the bundle while it lay on the base of the fountain, sat there watching the running of the water and the rolling of the Fancy Ball--when the one woman who had stood conspicuous, knitting, still knitted on with the steadfastness of Fate.", "아버지는 이미 오래전에 제 보따리를 거두어 그것을 안고 어딘가로 숨어버렸고, 보따리가 샘의 받침대 위에 놓여 있는 동안 그것을 돌보던 여자들은 그곳에 앉아 흐르는 물과 굴러가는 가장무도회를 지켜보았다 — 눈에 띄게 서서 뜨개질을 하던 그 한 여자는 여전히 운명의 한결같음으로 뜨개질을 계속했다."], ["The water of the fountain ran, the swift river ran, the day ran into evening, so much life in the city ran into death according to rule, time and tide waited for no man, the rats were sleeping close together in their dark holes again, the Fancy Ball was lighted up at supper, all things ran their course.", "샘물이 흘렀고, 빠른 강물이 흘렀고, 낮은 흘러 저녁이 되었고, 도시의 그 많은 생명이 정해진 이치대로 죽음으로 흘러갔으며, 세월과 물때는 그 누구도 기다려주지 않았고, 쥐들은 다시 어두운 굴속에 옹기종기 모여 잠들었으며, 가장무도회는 만찬에 환히 불을 밝혔고, 만물이 제 갈 길을 갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. Monseigneur in the Country", "# CHAPTER VIII. Monseigneur in the Country"]], [["A beautiful landscape, with the corn bright in it, but not abundant.", "옥수수가 환히 빛나지만 그리 풍성하지는 않은 아름다운 풍경."], ["Patches of poor rye where corn should have been, patches of poor peas and beans, patches of most coarse vegetable substitutes for wheat.", "옥수수가 자라야 할 곳에는 메마른 호밀이 듬성듬성, 메마른 완두와 콩이 듬성듬성, 밀을 대신할 가장 거친 채소들이 듬성듬성 들어차 있었다."], ["On inanimate nature, as on the men and women who cultivated it, a prevalent tendency towards an appearance of vegetating unwillingly--a dejected disposition to give up, and wither away.", "생명 없는 자연 위에도, 그것을 일구는 남자와 여자들 위에서와 마찬가지로, 마지못해 겨우 자라나는 듯한 기색이 만연해 있었다 — 포기하고 시들어버리고 싶다는 풀죽은 성정이."]], [["Monsieur the Marquis in his travelling carriage (which might have been lighter), conducted by four post-horses and two postilions, fagged up a steep hill.", "후작 나리는 여행용 마차에 올라 — 좀더 가벼웠어도 좋았을 마차였다 — 네 마리 역마와 두 명의 마부에게 이끌려 가파른 언덕을 힘겹게 올라갔다."], ["A blush on the countenance of Monsieur the Marquis was no impeachment of his high breeding; it was not from within; it was occasioned by an external circumstance beyond his control--the setting sun.", "후작 나리의 얼굴에 떠오른 홍조는 그의 고귀한 혈통을 흠잡을 만한 것이 못 되었으니, 그것은 안에서 비롯된 것이 아니라 그가 어찌할 수 없는 외부의 사정 — 지는 해 — 에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["The sunset struck so brilliantly into the travelling carriage when it gained the hill-top, that its occupant was steeped in crimson.", "마차가 언덕 꼭대기에 이르렀을 때 노을이 어찌나 찬란하게 여행용 마차 안으로 비쳐들었던지, 그 안에 탄 사람이 진홍빛에 흠뻑 잠겼다."], ["“It will die out,”", "\"곧 사그라지겠지.\""], ["said Monsieur the Marquis, glancing at his hands, “directly.”", "후작 나리가 자기 두 손을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"당장에.\""]], [["In effect, the sun was so low that it dipped at the moment.", "실제로 해가 어찌나 낮게 걸려 있었던지 바로 그 순간 가라앉아버렸다."], ["When the heavy drag had been adjusted to the wheel, and the carriage slid down hill, with a cinderous smell, in a cloud of dust, the red glow departed quickly; the sun and the Marquis going down together, there was no glow left when the drag was taken off.", "묵직한 제동장치가 바퀴에 맞춰지고 마차가 잿내를 풍기며 흙먼지 구름 속에서 언덕을 미끄러져 내려갈 무렵, 붉은 광채는 빠르게 사라졌다. 해와 후작이 함께 저물어, 제동장치가 풀렸을 때는 아무런 광채도 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["But, there remained a broken country, bold and open, a little village at the bottom of the hill, a broad sweep and rise beyond it, a church-tower, a windmill, a forest for the chase, and a crag with a fortress on it used as a prison.", "그러나 험준한 땅이, 가파르고 탁 트인 채로 남아 있었고, 언덕 아래에는 작은 마을이 있었으며, 그 너머로는 넓게 펼쳐져 솟아오른 비탈과 교회의 탑, 풍차, 사냥터로 쓰이는 숲, 그리고 감옥으로 쓰이는 요새가 얹힌 바위 절벽이 있었다."], ["Round upon all these darkening objects as the night drew on, the Marquis looked, with the air of one who was coming near home.", "밤이 다가오면서 점점 어두워지는 이 모든 것들을, 후작은 집에 가까워지는 사람의 기색으로 둘러보았다."]], [["The village had its one poor street, with its poor brewery, poor tannery, poor tavern, poor stable-yard for relays of post-horses, poor fountain, all usual poor appointments.", "마을에는 초라한 거리가 하나 있었고, 거기에는 초라한 양조장, 초라한 무두질 작업장, 초라한 선술집, 역마를 갈아매는 초라한 마구간 마당, 초라한 샘이 있었으니, 모두가 으레 있을 법한 초라한 시설이었다."], ["It had its poor people too.", "마을에는 초라한 사람들도 있었다."], ["All its people were poor, and many of them were sitting at their doors, shredding spare onions and the like for supper, while many were at the fountain, washing leaves, and grasses, and any such small yieldings of the earth that could be eaten.", "마을 사람들은 모두 가난했고, 그들 가운데 많은 이들이 저녁거리로 쓸 자투리 양파 따위를 다듬으며 문간에 앉아 있었고, 또 많은 이들은 샘가에서 먹을 수 있는 잎사귀며 풀이며 땅에서 거둔 자잘한 소출 따위를 씻고 있었다."], ["Expressive signs of what made them poor, were not wanting; the tax for the state, the tax for the church, the tax for the lord, tax local and tax general, were to be paid here and to be paid there, according to solemn inscription in the little village, until the wonder was, that there was any village left unswallowed.", "무엇이 그들을 가난하게 만들었는지를 또렷이 드러내는 표지가 모자라지 않았으니, 국가에 바치는 세금, 교회에 바치는 세금, 영주에게 바치는 세금, 지방세와 일반세를 이곳에서도 저곳에서도 내야 한다는 것이 그 작은 마을에 엄숙히 새겨져 있었고, 마침내 통째로 삼켜지지 않고 남아난 마을이 있다는 것이 오히려 놀라울 지경이었다."]], [["Few children were to be seen, and no dogs.", "아이들은 거의 보이지 않았고, 개는 한 마리도 없었다."], ["As to the men and women, their choice on earth was stated in the prospect--Life on the lowest terms that could sustain it, down in the little village under the mill; or captivity and Death in the dominant prison on the crag.", "남자와 여자들로 말하자면, 이 세상에서 그들이 택할 수 있는 길은 눈앞의 전망 속에 다 드러나 있었으니 — 방앗간 아래 작은 마을에서 목숨을 부지할 수 있는 최저한의 조건으로 사는 삶, 아니면 바위 절벽 위에 군림하는 감옥에서의 감금과 죽음이었다."]], [["Heralded by a courier in advance, and by the cracking of his postilions’ whips, which twined snake-like about their heads in the evening air, as if he came attended by the Furies, Monsieur the Marquis drew up in his travelling carriage at the posting-house gate.", "앞서 달려가는 전령과, 마치 복수의 여신들을 거느리고 오기라도 하는 듯 저녁 공기 속에서 뱀처럼 머리 위로 휘감기며 울리는 마부들의 채찍 소리에 알려져, 후작 나리는 여행용 마차를 역참 문 앞에 세웠다."], ["It was hard by the fountain, and the peasants suspended their operations to look at him.", "그곳은 샘 바로 곁이었고, 농부들은 하던 일을 멈추고 그를 바라보았다."], ["He looked at them, and saw in them, without knowing it, the slow sure filing down of misery-worn face and figure, that was to make the meagreness of Frenchmen an English superstition which should survive the truth through the best part of a hundred years.", "그도 그들을 바라보았으나, 자신은 알지 못한 채, 그들에게서 비참함에 닳고 닳은 얼굴과 몸이 더디지만 틀림없이 깎여나가는 것을 보았으니, 그것은 프랑스인의 깡마름을 영국인의 미신으로 만들어, 백 년의 대부분 동안 그 진실을 넘어 살아남게 할 바로 그것이었다."]], [["Monsieur the Marquis cast his eyes over the submissive faces that drooped before him, as the like of himself had drooped before Monseigneur of the Court--only the difference was, that these faces drooped merely to suffer and not to propitiate--when a grizzled mender of the roads joined the group.", "후작 나리는 자기 앞에 고개 숙인 순종적인 얼굴들 위로 눈길을 던졌으니, 그 자신과 같은 부류가 궁정의 영주 앞에 고개 숙였던 것과 같은 모습이었다 — 다만 다른 점이라면, 이 얼굴들은 그저 견디려고 고개를 숙였을 뿐, 비위를 맞추려는 것이 아니었다 — 그때 머리가 희끗희끗한 도로 보수공 하나가 그 무리에 끼어들었다."]], [["“Bring me hither that fellow!”", "\"저자를 이리로 데려오너라!\""], ["said the Marquis to the courier.", "후작이 전령에게 말했다."]], [["The fellow was brought, cap in hand, and the other fellows closed round to look and listen, in the manner of the people at the Paris fountain.", "그 사내가 모자를 손에 든 채 끌려왔고, 다른 사내들은 빠리의 샘가에서 사람들이 그러했던 것과 같은 모양새로 보고 들으려고 빙 둘러섰다."]], [["“I passed you on the road?”", "\"내가 길에서 너를 지나쳤지?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, it is true.", "\"나리, 그렇습니다."], ["I had the honour of being passed on the road.”", "길에서 지나쳐주시는 영광을 입었습니다.\""]], [["“Coming up the hill, and at the top of the hill, both?”", "\"언덕을 올라올 때, 그리고 언덕 꼭대기에서, 둘 다였더냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, it is true.”", "\"나리, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“What did you look at, so fixedly?”", "\"무엇을 그토록 뚫어지게 보고 있었느냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, I looked at the man.”", "\"나리, 저는 그 사내를 보고 있었습니다.\""]], [["He stooped a little, and with his tattered blue cap pointed under the carriage.", "그는 몸을 약간 숙이고는 너덜너덜한 파란 모자로 마차 밑을 가리켰다."], ["All his fellows stooped to look under the carriage.", "그의 동료들도 모두 몸을 숙여 마차 밑을 들여다보았다."]], [["“What man, pig?", "\"무슨 사내냐, 이 돼지 같은 놈아?"], ["And why look there?”", "그리고 왜 거기를 보느냐?\""]], [["“Pardon, Monseigneur; he swung by the chain of the shoe--the drag.”", "\"용서하십시오, 나리. 그자가 신발의 사슬에 — 그 제동장치에 매달려 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Who?”", "\"누가?\""], ["demanded the traveller.", "여행객이 다그쳤다."]], [["“Monseigneur, the man.”", "\"나리, 그 사내가요.\""]], [["“May the Devil carry away these idiots!", "\"이 멍청이들을 악마가 데려가버렸으면!"], ["How do you call the man?", "그 사내를 뭐라고 부르느냐?"], ["You know all the men of this part of the country.", "너는 이 고장 사람들을 다 알 텐데."], ["Who was he?”", "그자가 누구였느냐?\""]], [["“Your clemency, Monseigneur!", "\"자비를 베푸소서, 나리!"], ["He was not of this part of the country.", "그자는 이 고장 사람이 아니었습니다."], ["Of all the days of my life, I never saw him.”", "제 평생 단 한 번도 본 적 없는 자입니다.\""]], [["“Swinging by the chain?", "\"사슬에 매달려서?"], ["To be suffocated?”", "질식이라도 하려고?\""]], [["“With your gracious permission, that was the wonder of it, Monseigneur.", "\"황송하오나, 바로 그게 이상한 점이었습니다, 나리."], ["His head hanging over--like this!”", "그자가 머리를 이렇게 늘어뜨린 채로 — 이렇게요!\""]], [["He turned himself sideways to the carriage, and leaned back, with his face thrown up to the sky, and his head hanging down; then recovered himself, fumbled with his cap, and made a bow.", "그는 몸을 옆으로 돌려 마차 쪽을 향하더니, 얼굴을 하늘로 쳐든 채 머리를 아래로 늘어뜨리고 뒤로 젖혔다. 그러고는 다시 몸을 바로 세우고 모자를 만지작거리며 절을 했다."]], [["“What was he like?”", "\"그자가 어떻게 생겼더냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, he was whiter than the miller.", "\"나리, 그자는 방앗간 주인보다도 더 하얬습니다."], ["All covered with dust, white as a spectre, tall as a spectre!”", "온통 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고, 유령처럼 하얗고, 유령처럼 키가 컸습니다!\""]], [["The picture produced an immense sensation in the little crowd; but all eyes, without comparing notes with other eyes, looked at Monsieur the Marquis.", "이 묘사는 작은 무리 가운데 엄청난 반향을 일으켰다. 그러나 모든 눈길이, 서로 의견을 견주어보지도 않고, 후작 나리를 바라보았다."], ["Perhaps, to observe whether he had any spectre on his conscience.", "아마도 그의 양심에 어떤 유령이라도 걸려 있는지 살펴보려는 것이었으리라."]], [["“Truly, you did well,”", "\"참으로 잘했구나.\""], ["said the Marquis, felicitously sensible that such vermin were not to ruffle him, “to see a thief accompanying my carriage, and not open that great mouth of yours.", "후작이, 그런 벌레 같은 것들 따위로는 자신이 동요하지 않으리라는 것을 흡족하게 깨달으며 말했다. \"도둑놈 하나가 내 마차에 따라붙는 것을 보고도 그 큰 입을 열지 않다니."], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["Put him aside, Monsieur Gabelle!”", "저자를 치워버려라, 가벨 씨!\""]], [["Monsieur Gabelle was the Postmaster, and some other taxing functionary united; he had come out with great obsequiousness to assist at this examination, and had held the examined by the drapery of his arm in an official manner.", "가벨 씨는 역참 관리인이자 다른 징세 관리직을 겸한 자였다. 그는 이 심문을 거들려고 잔뜩 굽실거리며 나와서, 심문받는 자의 옷소매를 관에서 하듯 붙잡고 있었다."]], [["“Bah!", "\"흥!"], ["Go aside!”", "비켜라!\""], ["said Monsieur Gabelle.", "가벨 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Lay hands on this stranger if he seeks to lodge in your village to-night, and be sure that his business is honest, Gabelle.”", "\"이 낯선 자가 오늘 밤 네 마을에 묵으려 하거든 붙잡아 두고, 그자의 용무가 정당한 것인지 반드시 확인하라, 가벨.\""]], [["“Monseigneur, I am flattered to devote myself to your orders.”", "\"나리, 분부에 제 몸을 바칠 수 있다니 영광입니다.\""]], [["“Did he run away, fellow?--where is that Accursed?”", "\"그자가 달아났느냐, 이놈아? — 그 빌어먹을 놈은 어디 있느냐?\""]], [["The accursed was already under the carriage with some half-dozen particular friends, pointing out the chain with his blue cap.", "그 빌어먹을 놈은 이미 여섯 명쯤 되는 막역한 친구들과 함께 마차 밑에 들어가, 파란 모자로 사슬을 가리키고 있었다."], ["Some half-dozen other particular friends promptly hauled him out, and presented him breathless to Monsieur the Marquis.", "또다른 여섯 명쯤 되는 막역한 친구들이 잽싸게 그를 끌어내, 숨을 헐떡이는 그를 후작 나리 앞에 대령했다."]], [["“Did the man run away, Dolt, when we stopped for the drag?”", "\"우리가 제동장치 때문에 멈춰 섰을 때 그 사내가 달아났느냐, 이 둔한 놈아?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, he precipitated himself over the hill-side, head first, as a person plunges into the river.”", "\"나리, 그자는 사람이 강물에 뛰어들 듯이 머리부터 언덕 비탈 너머로 곤두박질쳤습니다.\""]], [["“See to it, Gabelle.", "\"잘 처리하라, 가벨."], ["Go on!”", "출발!\""]], [["The half-dozen who were peering at the chain were still among the wheels, like sheep; the wheels turned so suddenly that they were lucky to save their skins and bones; they had very little else to save, or they might not have been so fortunate.", "사슬을 들여다보던 그 여섯 명은 양 떼처럼 여전히 바퀴들 사이에 있었다. 바퀴가 어찌나 갑작스레 돌아갔던지, 그들이 살가죽과 뼈를 건사한 것만도 다행이었다. 그밖에는 건사할 것이 거의 없었으니, 아니었다면 그토록 운이 좋지는 못했을지도 모른다."]], [["The burst with which the carriage started out of the village and up the rise beyond, was soon checked by the steepness of the hill.", "마차가 마을을 빠져나와 그 너머의 비탈을 오르며 내달린 기세는, 언덕의 가파름 탓에 곧 꺾이고 말았다."], ["Gradually, it subsided to a foot pace, swinging and lumbering upward among the many sweet scents of a summer night.", "마차는 점차 걸음걸이 속도로 잦아들어, 여름밤의 갖가지 달콤한 향기 사이를 흔들거리며 덜컹덜컹 올라갔다."], ["The postilions, with a thousand gossamer gnats circling about them in lieu of the Furies, quietly mended the points to the lashes of their whips; the valet walked by the horses; the courier was audible, trotting on ahead into the dull distance.", "마부들은 복수의 여신들 대신 천 마리의 아른거리는 각다귀들에게 둘러싸인 채, 채찍 끝의 매듭을 조용히 손보았다. 시종은 말들 곁에서 걸었고, 전령이 흐릿한 먼 곳으로 종종걸음 쳐 나아가는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["At the steepest point of the hill there was a little burial-ground, with a Cross and a new large figure of Our Saviour on it; it was a poor figure in wood, done by some inexperienced rustic carver, but he had studied the figure from the life--his own life, maybe--for it was dreadfully spare and thin.", "언덕에서 가장 가파른 지점에 작은 묘지가 있었는데, 거기에는 십자가와 새로 만든 커다란 구세주 상이 세워져 있었다. 그것은 경험이 부족한 어느 시골 조각가가 만든 초라한 나무 상이었으나, 그는 실물을 — 어쩌면 자기 자신의 삶을 — 본떠 그 상을 빚은 듯했으니, 그것이 무시무시하게 깡마르고 여위어 있었기 때문이다."]], [["To this distressful emblem of a great distress that had long been growing worse, and was not at its worst, a woman was kneeling.", "오래도록 점점 더 나빠져왔으나 아직 최악에는 이르지 않은 큰 고난을 나타내는 이 비통한 표상 앞에, 한 여인이 무릎 꿇고 있었다."], ["She turned her head as the carriage came up to her, rose quickly, and presented herself at the carriage-door.", "마차가 다가오자 그녀는 고개를 돌려 재빨리 일어서더니 마차 문 앞으로 다가섰다."]], [["“It is you, Monseigneur!", "\"나리시군요!"], ["Monseigneur, a petition.”", "나리, 청이 하나 있습니다.\""]], [["With an exclamation of impatience, but with his unchangeable face, Monseigneur looked out.", "참다못한 탄성을 내뱉으면서도 변함없는 얼굴로, 영주는 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["“How, then!", "\"또 뭐냐!"], ["What is it?", "무슨 일이냐?"], ["Always petitions!”", "늘 청원이로구나!\""]], [["“Monseigneur.", "\"나리."], ["For the love of the great God!", "위대하신 하느님의 사랑을 걸고!"], ["My husband, the forester.”", "제 남편, 그 산지기 말입니다.\""]], [["“What of your husband, the forester?", "\"네 남편, 그 산지기가 어쨌다는 것이냐?"], ["Always the same with you people.", "너희들은 늘 똑같구나."], ["He cannot pay something?”", "무언가를 못 내겠다는 게냐?\""]], [["“He has paid all, Monseigneur.", "\"그이는 다 냈습니다, 나리."], ["He is dead.”", "죽었으니까요.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"음!"], ["He is quiet.", "그러면 편히 쉬고 있겠구나."], ["Can I restore him to you?”", "내가 그를 네게 되돌려줄 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“Alas, no, Monseigneur!", "\"아아, 그건 안 되지요, 나리!"], ["But he lies yonder, under a little heap of poor grass.”", "하지만 그이는 저기, 보잘것없는 풀이 작게 쌓인 무더기 밑에 누워 있습니다.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, there are so many little heaps of poor grass?”", "\"나리, 보잘것없는 풀이 작게 쌓인 무더기가 너무도 많습니다.\""]], [["“Again, well?”", "\"그래서, 또?\""]], [["She looked an old woman, but was young.", "그녀는 노파처럼 보였으나 젊었다."], ["Her manner was one of passionate grief; by turns she clasped her veinous and knotted hands together with wild energy, and laid one of them on the carriage-door--tenderly, caressingly, as if it had been a human breast, and could be expected to feel the appealing touch.", "그녀의 태도에는 격정적인 슬픔이 어려 있었다. 그녀는 핏줄이 불거지고 마디진 두 손을 사납게 마주 그러쥐었다가, 그중 한 손을 마차 문에 얹기를 거듭했으니 — 마치 그것이 사람의 가슴이기라도 한 듯, 그래서 그 호소하는 손길을 느낄 수라도 있는 듯, 부드럽고 어루만지듯이."]], [["“Monseigneur, hear me!", "\"나리, 제 말을 들어주십시오!"], ["Monseigneur, hear my petition!", "나리, 제 청을 들어주십시오!"], ["My husband died of want; so many die of want; so many more will die of want.”", "제 남편은 굶주려 죽었습니다. 그토록 많은 이들이 굶주려 죽고, 앞으로 더 많은 이들이 굶주려 죽을 것입니다.\""]], [["“Again, well?", "\"그래서, 또?"], ["Can I feed them?”", "내가 그들을 먹여 살리기라도 하란 말이냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, the good God knows; but I don’t ask it.", "\"나리, 선하신 하느님께서는 아십니다. 하지만 저는 그것을 청하는 게 아닙니다."], ["My petition is, that a morsel of stone or wood, with my husband’s name, may be placed over him to show where he lies.", "제 청은, 제 남편의 이름을 새긴 돌이나 나무 조각을 그이 위에 세워 그가 어디 누워 있는지 표시해 달라는 것입니다."], ["Otherwise, the place will be quickly forgotten, it will never be found when I am dead of the same malady, I shall be laid under some other heap of poor grass.", "그렇지 않으면 그 자리는 금세 잊힐 것이고, 제가 같은 병으로 죽고 나면 결코 찾을 수 없게 될 것이며, 저 또한 보잘것없는 풀이 쌓인 또다른 무더기 밑에 묻히게 될 것입니다."], ["Monseigneur, they are so many, they increase so fast, there is so much want. Monseigneur!", "나리, 그런 무더기가 너무 많고, 너무 빨리 늘어나고, 너무도 큰 결핍이 있습니다."], ["Monseigneur!”", "나리! 나리!\""]], [["The valet had put her away from the door, the carriage had broken into a brisk trot, the postilions had quickened the pace, she was left far behind, and Monseigneur, again escorted by the Furies, was rapidly diminishing the league or two of distance that remained between him and his chateau.", "시종이 그녀를 문에서 떼어놓았고, 마차는 활기찬 속보로 내달리기 시작했으며, 마부들이 속도를 높이자 그녀는 멀찍이 뒤에 남겨졌다. 다시 복수의 여신들을 거느린 영주는, 그와 그의 성 사이에 남은 일이 리외쯤의 거리를 빠르게 좁혀가고 있었다."]], [["The sweet scents of the summer night rose all around him, and rose, as the rain falls, impartially, on the dusty, ragged, and toil-worn group at the fountain not far away; to whom the mender of roads, with the aid of the blue cap without which he was nothing, still enlarged upon his man like a spectre, as long as they could bear it.", "여름밤의 달콤한 향기가 그를 에워싸고 피어올랐고, 그것은 비가 내리듯 공평하게, 멀지 않은 샘가에서 먼지투성이가 되어 누더기를 걸친 채 노동에 닳아빠진 무리 위에도 피어올랐다. 그들 곁에서 도로 보수공은, 그것이 없으면 아무것도 아닌 자신의 파란 모자의 도움을 받아, 그들이 견딜 수 있는 한 줄곧 유령 같은 그 사내에 대해 늘어놓았다."], ["By degrees, as they could bear no more, they dropped off one by one, and lights twinkled in little casements; which lights, as the casements darkened, and more stars came out, seemed to have shot up into the sky instead of having been extinguished.", "더는 견딜 수 없게 되자 그들은 차츰 하나둘 떨어져 나갔고, 작은 여닫이창들에 불빛이 깜박였다. 그 불빛들은, 여닫이창이 어두워지고 더 많은 별이 돋아남에 따라, 꺼졌다기보다는 하늘로 쏘아 올려진 듯이 보였다."]], [["The shadow of a large high-roofed house, and of many over-hanging trees, was upon Monsieur the Marquis by that time; and the shadow was exchanged for the light of a flambeau, as his carriage stopped, and the great door of his chateau was opened to him.", "그 무렵 후작 나리 위로는 지붕이 높고 커다란 집의 그림자와 휘늘어진 수많은 나무들의 그림자가 드리워 있었다. 그리고 그 그림자는 그의 마차가 멈추고 성의 큰 문이 그를 맞아 열리면서 횃불의 빛으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“Monsieur Charles, whom I expect; is he arrived from England?”", "\"내가 기다리는 샤를 씨, 영국에서 도착했느냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, not yet.”", "\"나리, 아직입니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER IX. The Gorgon’s Head", "# CHAPTER IX. The Gorgon’s Head"]], [["It was a heavy mass of building, that chateau of Monsieur the Marquis, with a large stone courtyard before it, and two stone sweeps of staircase meeting in a stone terrace before the principal door.", "후작 나리의 그 성관은 묵직한 건물 덩어리였다. 앞에는 너른 돌마당이 있었고, 두 줄기 돌계단이 반원을 그리며 올라가 정문 앞 돌 테라스에서 만났다."], ["A stony business altogether, with heavy stone balustrades, and stone urns, and stone flowers, and stone faces of men, and stone heads of lions, in all directions.", "어디를 보나 온통 돌투성이여서, 묵직한 돌난간이며 돌항아리며 돌꽃이며 사람의 돌얼굴이며 사자의 돌머리가 사방에 널려 있었다."], ["As if the Gorgon’s head had surveyed it, when it was finished, two centuries ago.", "마치 이백 년 전 그것이 완성되었을 때 고르곤의 머리가 그것을 둘러본 것만 같았다."]], [["Up the broad flight of shallow steps, Monsieur the Marquis, flambeau preceded, went from his carriage, sufficiently disturbing the darkness to elicit loud remonstrance from an owl in the roof of the great pile of stable building away among the trees.", "후작 나리는 횃불을 앞세우고, 폭이 넓고 낮은 계단을 밟고 올라가 마차에서 내렸다. 그 소란은 어둠을 어지간히 흔들어, 나무들 사이 멀찍이 자리한 거대한 마구간 건물 지붕에서 부엉이 한 마리가 요란하게 항의하도록 만들기에 충분했다."], ["All else was so quiet, that the flambeau carried up the steps, and the other flambeau held at the great door, burnt as if they were in a close room of state, instead of being in the open night-air.", "다른 모든 것은 어찌나 고요한지, 계단 위로 들고 올라온 횃불도 정문 앞에 들고 선 또 다른 횃불도, 탁 트인 밤공기 속이 아니라 닫힌 어전(御殿)의 방 안에 있는 것처럼 타올랐다."], ["Other sound than the owl’s voice there was none, save the falling of a fountain into its stone basin; for, it was one of those dark nights that hold their breath by the hour together, and then heave a long low sigh, and hold their breath again.", "부엉이의 울음소리 말고는, 분수가 돌 수반으로 떨어지는 소리 외에 아무 소리도 없었다. 그날 밤은 한 시간씩 통째로 숨을 죽이고 있다가 낮고 긴 한숨을 토하고는 다시 숨을 죽이는, 그런 캄캄한 밤들 가운데 하나였기 때문이다."]], [["The great door clanged behind him, and Monsieur the Marquis crossed a hall grim with certain old boar-spears, swords, and knives of the chase; grimmer with certain heavy riding-rods and riding-whips, of which many a peasant, gone to his benefactor Death, had felt the weight when his lord was angry.", "육중한 문이 그의 등 뒤에서 쩌렁 닫혔고, 후작 나리는 묵은 멧돼지 창과 칼과 사냥용 단검 따위로 음산한 홀을 가로질렀다. 그보다 더 음산한 것은 묵직한 승마용 채찍과 가죽 채찍들이었으니, 그의 영주가 노여워할 때 그것들의 무게를 직접 느껴본 농부가, 자신의 은인인 죽음에게로 떠나간 농부가 한둘이 아니었다."]], [["Avoiding the larger rooms, which were dark and made fast for the night, Monsieur the Marquis, with his flambeau-bearer going on before, went up the staircase to a door in a corridor.", "밤을 맞아 어둡고 단단히 잠긴 더 큰 방들을 피해, 후작 나리는 횃불잡이를 앞세우고 계단을 올라 어느 복도의 문 앞에 이르렀다."], ["This thrown open, admitted him to his own private apartment of three rooms: his bed-chamber and two others.", "그 문이 활짝 열리자 그는 세 칸으로 된 자신만의 사적인 거처로 들어섰다. 침실과 다른 두 방이었다."], ["High vaulted rooms with cool uncarpeted floors, great dogs upon the hearths for the burning of wood in winter time, and all luxuries befitting the state of a marquis in a luxurious age and country.", "높은 둥근 천장에 양탄자 없는 서늘한 바닥, 겨울철 장작을 태우기 위한 큼직한 장작받침이 난로마다 놓여 있었고, 사치스러운 시대와 나라에서 후작의 신분에 걸맞은 온갖 호사가 갖추어져 있었다."], ["The fashion of the last Louis but one, of the line that was never to break--the fourteenth Louis--was conspicuous in their rich furniture; but, it was diversified by many objects that were illustrations of old pages in the history of France.", "결코 끊어지지 않을 그 혈통의, 마지막 루이에서 하나 앞선 루이 — 즉 루이 14세 — 의 양식이 호화로운 가구마다 두드러졌다. 그러나 거기에는 프랑스 역사의 옛 장면들을 보여주는 숱한 물건들이 한데 섞여 변화를 주고 있었다."]], [["A supper-table was laid for two, in the third of the rooms; a round room, in one of the chateau’s four extinguisher-topped towers.", "세 방 가운데 셋째 방에 두 사람을 위한 저녁 식탁이 차려져 있었다. 성관의 네 모퉁이에 솟은, 촛불 끄개를 얹은 듯한 탑들 가운데 하나에 자리한 둥근 방이었다."], ["A small lofty room, with its window wide open, and the wooden jalousie-blinds closed, so that the dark night only showed in slight horizontal lines of black, alternating with their broad lines of stone colour.", "작고 천장이 높은 방으로, 창문은 활짝 열려 있었으나 나무로 된 발 모양 덧창이 닫혀 있어서, 캄캄한 밤은 그저 검은빛의 가느다란 가로줄로만, 넓은 돌빛 가로줄과 번갈아 가며 어렴풋이 비칠 뿐이었다."]], [["“My nephew,”", "\"내 조카 말이다.\""], ["said the Marquis, glancing at the supper preparation; “they said he was not arrived.”", "저녁 차림을 흘긋 보며 후작이 말했다. \"그 애가 아직 도착하지 않았다고들 하던데.\""]], [["Nor was he; but, he had been expected with Monseigneur.", "과연 그는 도착하지 않았다. 그러나 나리와 함께 오리라 예상되고 있었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["It is not probable he will arrive to-night; nevertheless, leave the table as it is.", "오늘 밤 안에 그 애가 도착할 것 같지는 않구나. 그래도 식탁은 그대로 두어라."], ["I shall be ready in a quarter of an hour.”", "나는 십오 분 뒤에 준비가 될 것이다.\""]], [["In a quarter of an hour Monseigneur was ready, and sat down alone to his sumptuous and choice supper.", "십오 분 뒤 나리는 준비가 되어, 호화롭고 정갈한 저녁상 앞에 홀로 앉았다."], ["His chair was opposite to the window, and he had taken his soup, and was raising his glass of Bordeaux to his lips, when he put it down.", "그의 의자는 창문을 마주 보고 있었는데, 수프를 비우고 보르도 포도주잔을 입술로 가져가다가 그것을 내려놓았다."]], [["“What is that?”", "\"저게 무엇이냐?\""], ["he calmly asked, looking with attention at the horizontal lines of black and stone colour.", "그는 검은빛과 돌빛의 가로줄들을 유심히 바라보며 차분하게 물었다."]], [["“Monseigneur?", "\"나리?"], ["That?”", "무엇 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“Outside the blinds.", "\"덧창 바깥 말이다."], ["Open the blinds.”", "덧창을 열어라.\""]], [["It was done.", "분부대로 되었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떠냐?\""]], [["“Monseigneur, it is nothing.", "\"나리, 아무것도 없습니다."], ["The trees and the night are all that are here.”", "여기에는 나무와 밤뿐입니다.\""]], [["The servant who spoke, had thrown the blinds wide, had looked out into the vacant darkness, and stood with that blank behind him, looking round for instructions.", "말을 한 하인은 덧창을 활짝 젖히고 텅 빈 어둠 속을 내다보았으며, 그 공허를 등진 채 서서 지시를 기다리느라 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Good,”", "\"좋다.\""], ["said the imperturbable master.", "동요 없는 주인이 말했다."], ["“Close them again.”", "\"다시 닫아라.\""]], [["That was done too, and the Marquis went on with his supper.", "그 또한 분부대로 되었고, 후작은 저녁 식사를 계속했다."], ["He was half way through it, when he again stopped with his glass in his hand, hearing the sound of wheels.", "절반쯤 먹었을 때, 그는 다시 손에 잔을 든 채 멈췄으니, 바퀴 소리가 들렸기 때문이다."], ["It came on briskly, and came up to the front of the chateau.", "그 소리는 경쾌하게 다가와 성관 정면에 이르렀다."]], [["“Ask who is arrived.”", "\"누가 도착했는지 물어보아라.\""]], [["It was the nephew of Monseigneur.", "나리의 조카였다."], ["He had been some few leagues behind Monseigneur, early in the afternoon.", "그날 이른 오후에 그는 나리보다 몇 리그 뒤처져 있었다."], ["He had diminished the distance rapidly, but not so rapidly as to come up with Monseigneur on the road.", "그는 그 거리를 빠르게 좁혔으나, 도중에 나리를 따라잡을 만큼 빠르지는 못했다."], ["He had heard of Monseigneur, at the posting-houses, as being before him.", "그는 역참에서 나리가 앞서 가고 있다는 소식을 들었던 것이다."]], [["He was to be told (said Monseigneur) that supper awaited him then and there, and that he was prayed to come to it.", "(나리가 분부하기를) 그에게 전하라, 저녁상이 지금 여기서 그를 기다리고 있으니 와서 들기를 청한다고."], ["In a little while he came.", "잠시 뒤 그가 들어왔다."], ["He had been known in England as Charles Darnay.", "그는 영국에서 찰스 다네이라는 이름으로 알려져 있었다."]], [["Monseigneur received him in a courtly manner, but they did not shake hands.", "나리는 정중하게 그를 맞이했으나, 두 사람은 악수하지 않았다."]], [["“You left Paris yesterday, sir?”", "\"어제 빠리를 떠나셨습니까, 나리?\""], ["he said to Monseigneur, as he took his seat at table.", "식탁에 자리를 잡으며 그가 나리에게 말했다."]], [["“Yesterday.", "\"어제."], ["And you?”", "너는?\""]], [["“I come direct.”", "\"저는 곧장 왔습니다.\""]], [["“From London?”", "\"런던에서?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“You have been a long time coming,”", "\"오는 데 오래 걸렸구나.\""], ["said the Marquis, with a smile.", "후작이 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“On the contrary; I come direct.”", "\"그 반대입니다. 저는 곧장 왔습니다.\""]], [["“Pardon me!", "\"미안하구나!"], ["I mean, not a long time on the journey; a long time intending the journey.”", "내 말은, 여정이 오래 걸렸다는 게 아니라, 여정에 나서기까지 오래 걸렸다는 뜻이다.\""]], [["“I have been detained by”--the nephew stopped a moment in his answer--“various business.”", "\"여러 가지……\" 조카는 대답하다가 잠시 멈췄다. \"여러 가지 일에 발이 묶여 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Without doubt,”", "\"그야 의심할 바 없겠지.\""], ["said the polished uncle.", "세련된 숙부가 말했다."]], [["So long as a servant was present, no other words passed between them.", "하인이 곁에 있는 동안에는 두 사람 사이에 다른 말이 오가지 않았다."], ["When coffee had been served and they were alone together, the nephew, looking at the uncle and meeting the eyes of the face that was like a fine mask, opened a conversation.", "커피가 나오고 단둘이 남게 되자, 조카는 숙부를 바라보며, 잘 빚은 가면 같은 그 얼굴의 눈과 마주치고는 입을 열었다."]], [["“I have come back, sir, as you anticipate, pursuing the object that took me away.", "\"짐작하시는 대로, 저는 저를 떠나게 했던 그 목적을 좇아 돌아왔습니다."], ["It carried me into great and unexpected peril; but it is a sacred object, and if it had carried me to death I hope it would have sustained me.”", "그것은 저를 크고 뜻밖의 위험으로 몰아넣었습니다. 그러나 그것은 신성한 목적이며, 설령 그것이 저를 죽음으로 이끌었다 해도 그것이 저를 지탱해주리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Not to death,”", "\"죽음이라니.\""], ["said the uncle; “it is not necessary to say, to death.”", "숙부가 말했다. \"죽음 운운할 것까지야 없지.\""]], [["“I doubt, sir,”", "\"의심스럽습니다, 나리.\""], ["returned the nephew, “whether, if it had carried me to the utmost brink of death, you would have cared to stop me there.”", "조카가 응수했다. \"설령 그것이 저를 죽음의 벼랑 끝까지 몰고 갔다 한들, 나리께서 거기서 저를 막아주려 하셨을지 말입니다.\""]], [["The deepened marks in the nose, and the lengthening of the fine straight lines in the cruel face, looked ominous as to that; the uncle made a graceful gesture of protest, which was so clearly a slight form of good breeding that it was not reassuring.", "콧등에 깊이 패인 자국과, 그 잔혹한 얼굴에 곧고 가느다란 선들이 길어지는 모양새가, 그 점에 관해서는 불길해 보였다. 숙부는 우아하게 항의하는 몸짓을 했는데, 그것이 너무도 분명하게 가벼운 교양의 형식에 지나지 않아서 도무지 안심이 되지 않았다."]], [["“Indeed, sir,”", "\"실은, 나리.\""], ["pursued the nephew, “for anything I know, you may have expressly worked to give a more suspicious appearance to the suspicious circumstances that surrounded me.”", "조카가 말을 이었다. \"제가 알기로는, 저를 둘러싼 의심스러운 정황에 한층 더 의심스러운 빛을 더하려고 나리께서 일부러 손을 쓰셨을 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“No, no, no,”", "\"아니다, 아니야, 아니지.\""], ["said the uncle, pleasantly.", "숙부가 유쾌하게 말했다."]], [["“But, however that may be,”", "\"하나, 그것이 어찌 되었든 간에,\""], ["resumed the nephew, glancing at him with deep distrust, “I know that your diplomacy would stop me by any means, and would know no scruple as to means.”", "깊은 불신의 눈으로 그를 흘긋 보며 조카가 다시 말했다. \"나리의 수완이라면 어떤 수단을 써서라도 저를 막으려 들 것이며, 수단에 관해서는 일말의 거리낌도 없으리라는 것을 저는 압니다.\""]], [["“My friend, I told you so,”", "\"이 사람아, 내가 그렇게 말하지 않았더냐.\""], ["said the uncle, with a fine pulsation in the two marks.", "두 줄기 자국을 곱게 떨며 숙부가 말했다."], ["“Do me the favour to recall that I told you so, long ago.”", "\"내가 오래전에 그렇게 말했다는 것을 기억해주는 호의를 베풀어다오.\""]], [["“I recall it.”", "\"기억하고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙구나.\""], ["said the Marquis--very sweetly indeed.", "후작이 말했다 — 참으로 더없이 다정하게."]], [["His tone lingered in the air, almost like the tone of a musical instrument.", "그의 어조는 거의 악기의 음색처럼 공중에 여운을 남겼다."]], [["“In effect, sir,”", "\"사실은, 나리.\""], ["pursued the nephew, “I believe it to be at once your bad fortune, and my good fortune, that has kept me out of a prison in France here.”", "조카가 말을 이었다. \"저를 이곳 프랑스의 감옥 밖에 머물러 있게 한 것은, 나리의 불운인 동시에 저의 행운이라고 믿습니다.\""]], [["“I do not quite understand,”", "\"잘 이해가 가지 않는구나.\""], ["returned the uncle, sipping his coffee.", "숙부가 커피를 홀짝이며 응수했다."], ["“Dare I ask you to explain?”", "\"설명을 부탁드려도 되겠느냐?\""]], [["“I believe that if you were not in disgrace with the Court, and had not been overshadowed by that cloud for years past, a letter de cachet would have sent me to some fortress indefinitely.”", "\"제 믿음으로는, 나리께서 궁정의 미움을 사지 않으셨고, 지난 여러 해 동안 그 먹구름에 가려지지 않으셨더라면, 봉인장 한 장이면 저를 어느 요새엔가 기약 없이 처넣었을 겁니다.\""]], [["“It is possible,”", "\"가능한 일이지.\""], ["said the uncle, with great calmness.", "숙부가 더없이 침착하게 말했다."], ["“For the honour of the family, I could even resolve to incommode you to that extent.", "\"가문의 명예를 위해서라면, 나는 그 정도까지 너를 불편하게 할 결심도 할 수 있을 게다."], ["Pray excuse me!”", "부디 너그러이 보아다오!\""]], [["“I perceive that, happily for me, the Reception of the day before yesterday was, as usual, a cold one,” observed the nephew.", "* 봉인장(lettre de cachet): 프랑스 국왕의 명의로 발부되어, 재판 없이 특정인을 무기한 투옥하거나 추방할 수 있게 한 봉인된 영장."]], [["“I would not say happily, my friend,”", "\"저로서는 다행스럽게도, 그저께의 그 접견이 늘 그렇듯 냉랭했음을 알겠습니다.\" 조카가 한마디 했다."], ["returned the uncle, with refined politeness; “I would not be sure of that.", "\"다행이라고는 하지 않으련다, 이 사람아.\" 숙부가 세련된 정중함으로 응수했다."], ["A good opportunity for consideration, surrounded by the advantages of solitude, might influence your destiny to far greater advantage than you influence it for yourself.", "\"나라면 그것을 장담하지 않겠다. 고독이라는 이점에 둘러싸여 곰곰이 생각해볼 좋은 기회가, 네 스스로 네 운명을 좌우하는 것보다 훨씬 더 이롭게 네 운명을 좌우할 수도 있으니 말이다."], ["But it is useless to discuss the question.", "하나 이 문제를 논해봐야 부질없지."], ["I am, as you say, at a disadvantage.", "네 말마따나 나는 불리한 처지에 있구나."], ["These little instruments of correction, these gentle aids to the power and honour of families, these slight favours that might so incommode you, are only to be obtained now by interest and importunity.", "이 자그마한 교정 도구들, 가문의 권세와 명예를 돕는 이 점잖은 수단들, 너를 그토록 불편하게 할 수 있는 이 사소한 은전들은, 이제 연줄과 끈질긴 청원 없이는 얻을 수가 없게 되었다. 워낙 많은 이들이 그것을 구하고, (상대적으로) 워낙 소수에게만 그것이 주어지니!"], ["They are sought by so many, and they are granted (comparatively) to so few!", "예전에는 그렇지 않았다만, 프랑스는 이런 일이라면 죄다 더 나쁜 쪽으로 변하고 말았다."], ["It used not to be so, but France in all such things is changed for the worse.", "그리 멀지 않은 우리 조상들은 주위의 천한 것들에 대해 생사여탈의 권리를 쥐고 있었다."], ["Our not remote ancestors held the right of life and death over the surrounding vulgar.", "이 방에서, 그런 개 같은 자들이 숱하게 끌려 나가 교수형을 당했지."], ["From this room, many such dogs have been taken out to be hanged; in the next room (my bedroom), one fellow, to our knowledge, was poniarded on the spot for professing some insolent delicacy respecting his daughter--his daughter?", "옆방 — 내 침실 — 에서는, 우리가 아는 바로는, 한 녀석이 제 딸에 관해 무엄하게도 어쩌고 하는 까탈을 부렸다는 이유로 그 자리에서 칼에 찔려 죽었다. 제 딸이라니?"], ["We have lost many privileges; a new philosophy has become the mode; and the assertion of our station, in these days, might (I do not go so far as to say would, but might) cause us real inconvenience.", "우리는 숱한 특권을 잃었다. 새로운 철학이 유행이 되었으니, 요즈음 우리의 신분을 내세우는 것은 (정말로 그러리라고까지는 말하지 않겠다만) 우리에게 실질적인 불편을 끼칠 수도 있다."], ["All very bad, very bad!”", "다 아주 고약한 일이야, 아주 고약해!\""]], [["The Marquis took a gentle little pinch of snuff, and shook his head; as elegantly despondent as he could becomingly be of a country still containing himself, that great means of regeneration.", "후작은 코담배를 점잖게 한 자밤 집어넣고는 고개를 저었다. 자기 자신이라는, 그 위대한 재생의 수단을 아직 품고 있는 나라를 두고 보여줄 수 있는 한 가장 우아하게 낙담한 모습으로."]], [["“We have so asserted our station, both in the old time and in the modern time also,”", "\"우리는 옛 시절에도 또 요즈음에도 우리의 신분을 어찌나 내세웠던지,\""], ["said the nephew, gloomily, “that I believe our name to be more detested than any name in France.”", "조카가 침울하게 말했다. \"저는 우리 이름이 프랑스의 그 어떤 이름보다도 더 혐오받고 있으리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["“Let us hope so,”", "\"그렇기를 바라자.\""], ["said the uncle.", "숙부가 말했다."], ["“Detestation of the high is the involuntary homage of the low.”", "\"높은 자에 대한 혐오는 낮은 자의 마지못한 경의이니라.\""]], [["“There is not,”", "\"이 일대의 온 나라를 둘러보아도,\""], ["pursued the nephew, in his former tone, “a face I can look at, in all this country round about us, which looks at me with any deference on it but the dark deference of fear and slavery.”", "조카가 앞서의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"제가 마주 볼 수 있는 얼굴 가운데, 두려움과 노예근성에서 비롯된 어두운 굴종 말고 다른 경의를 담아 저를 바라보는 얼굴은 하나도 없습니다.\""]], [["“A compliment,”", "\"그것은,\""], ["said the Marquis, “to the grandeur of the family, merited by the manner in which the family has sustained its grandeur.", "후작이 말했다. \"가문의 위세에 대한 찬사이며, 그 위세를 떠받쳐온 가문의 방식에 마땅히 따르는 것이지."], ["Hah!”", "하!\""], ["And he took another gentle little pinch of snuff, and lightly crossed his legs.", "그러고는 다시 코담배를 점잖게 한 자밤 집어넣고는 가볍게 다리를 꼬았다."]], [["But, when his nephew, leaning an elbow on the table, covered his eyes thoughtfully and dejectedly with his hand, the fine mask looked at him sideways with a stronger concentration of keenness, closeness, and dislike, than was comportable with its wearer’s assumption of indifference.", "그러나 그의 조카가 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 눈을 가린 채 깊은 생각에 잠겨 의기소침해 있을 때, 그 잘 빚은 가면은 그것을 쓴 자가 짐짓 꾸민 무관심과는 어울리지 않을 만큼 더욱 강렬하게 응축된 날카로움과 음험함과 혐오를 담아 곁눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Repression is the only lasting philosophy.", "\"억압이야말로 유일하게 오래가는 철학이다."], ["The dark deference of fear and slavery, my friend,”", "두려움과 노예근성에서 비롯된 어두운 굴종은, 이 사람아,\""], ["observed the Marquis, “will keep the dogs obedient to the whip, as long as this roof,”", "후작이 한마디 했다. \"이 지붕이,\""], ["looking up to it, “shuts out the sky.”", "그것을 올려다보며, \"하늘을 가려주는 한, 개들을 채찍에 순종하게 만들어줄 것이다.\""]], [["That might not be so long as the Marquis supposed.", "그것은 후작이 생각하는 만큼 오래가지 않을 수도 있었다."], ["If a picture of the chateau as it was to be a very few years hence, and of fifty like it as they too were to be a very few years hence, could have been shown to him that night, he might have been at a loss to claim his own from the ghastly, fire-charred, plunder-wrecked rains.", "불과 몇 해 뒤의 이 성관의 모습이, 그리고 그것과 마찬가지로 불과 몇 해 뒤가 될 비슷한 성관 쉰 채의 모습이 그날 밤 그에게 보여졌더라면, 그는 그 끔찍하게 불에 그을리고 약탈로 폐허가 된 잔해들 가운데서 자기 것을 가려내느라 난감해했을지도 모른다."], ["As for the roof he vaunted, he might have found that shutting out the sky in a new way--to wit, for ever, from the eyes of the bodies into which its lead was fired, out of the barrels of a hundred thousand muskets.", "그가 으스대던 지붕으로 말하자면, 그는 그것이 새로운 방식으로 하늘을 가려주는 것을 보았을지도 모른다 — 다시 말해, 십만 자루의 머스킷 총신에서 발사된 그 지붕의 납덩이가 박혀버린 시신들의 눈에서, 영원히 하늘을 가려주는 것을 말이다."]], [["“Meanwhile,”", "\"그동안,\""], ["said the Marquis, “I will preserve the honour and repose of the family, if you will not.", "후작이 말했다. \"네가 하지 않겠다면 나는 가문의 명예와 안녕을 지키겠다."], ["But you must be fatigued.", "그러나 너는 피곤하겠구나."], ["Shall we terminate our conference for the night?”", "오늘 밤 우리의 담화는 이쯤에서 끝낼까?\""]], [["“A moment more.”", "\"잠시만 더.\""]], [["“An hour, if you please.”", "\"한 시간이라도 좋다.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"나리,\""], ["said the nephew, “we have done wrong, and are reaping the fruits of wrong.”", "조카가 말했다. \"우리는 잘못을 저질렀고, 그 잘못의 열매를 거두고 있습니다.\""]], [["“We have done wrong?”", "\"우리가 잘못을 저질렀다고?\""], ["repeated the Marquis, with an inquiring smile, and delicately pointing, first to his nephew, then to himself.", "후작이 묻는 듯한 미소를 띠고, 먼저 조카를, 다음에 자기 자신을 섬세하게 가리키며 되뇌었다."]], [["“Our family; our honourable family, whose honour is of so much account to both of us, in such different ways.", "\"우리 가문, 우리의 명예로운 가문 말입니다. 그 명예가 우리 둘 모두에게 — 비록 서로 너무나 다른 방식으로이지만 — 그토록 중요한 그 가문 말입니다."], ["Even in my father’s time, we did a world of wrong, injuring every human creature who came between us and our pleasure, whatever it was.", "제 아버지의 시절에도 우리는 한없이 많은 잘못을 저질렀으니, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 우리와 우리의 쾌락 사이에 끼어든 모든 인간을 해쳤습니다."], ["Why need I speak of my father’s time, when it is equally yours?", "그것이 똑같이 나리의 시절이기도 한데, 제 아버지의 시절을 굳이 말할 필요가 있겠습니까?"], ["Can I separate my father’s twin-brother, joint inheritor, and next successor, from himself?”", "제 아버지의 쌍둥이 형제이자, 공동 상속자이며, 바로 다음 계승자인 나리를 제가 아버지와 따로 떼어 생각할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Death has done that!”", "\"죽음이 그렇게 만들어주었지!\""], ["said the Marquis.", "후작이 말했다."]], [["“And has left me,”", "\"그리고 죽음은 저를,\" 조카가 대답했다."], ["answered the nephew, “bound to a system that is frightful to me, responsible for it, but powerless in it; seeking to execute the last request of my dear mother’s lips, and obey the last look of my dear mother’s eyes, which implored me to have mercy and to redress; and tortured by seeking assistance and power in vain.”", "\"저로서는 끔찍하기 짝이 없는 어떤 체제에 옭아맨 채로, 그것에 대한 책임은 지우되 그 안에서는 무력하기만 한 채로 남겨놓았습니다. 자비를 베풀고 바로잡으라고 간청하셨던, 제 사랑하는 어머니의 입술이 남긴 마지막 부탁을 이행하려, 또 제 사랑하는 어머니의 눈이 남긴 마지막 눈길에 따르려 애쓰면서, 그러나 도움과 힘을 헛되이 구하느라 고통받으면서 말입니다.\""]], [["“Seeking them from me, my nephew,”", "\"그것을 나에게서 구하다니, 내 조카야,\""], ["said the Marquis, touching him on the breast with his forefinger--they were now standing by the hearth--“you will for ever seek them in vain, be assured.”", "후작이 집게손가락으로 그의 가슴을 건드리며 말했다 — 두 사람은 이제 난로 옆에 서 있었다 — \"두고 보아라, 너는 그것을 영원히 헛되이 구하게 될 것이다.\""]], [["Every fine straight line in the clear whiteness of his face, was cruelly, craftily, and closely compressed, while he stood looking quietly at his nephew, with his snuff-box in his hand.", "그가 손에 코담뱃갑을 든 채 조용히 조카를 바라보며 서 있는 동안, 그 맑고 새하얀 얼굴의 곧고 가느다란 선들은 하나하나 잔혹하게, 음흉하게, 빈틈없이 오므라들었다."], ["Once again he touched him on the breast, as though his finger were the fine point of a small sword, with which, in delicate finesse, he ran him through the body, and said,", "그는 다시 한번 그의 가슴을 건드렸으니, 마치 그 손가락이 작은 칼의 가느다란 칼끝이어서, 섬세한 술책으로 그 칼을 조카의 몸에 꿰뚫어 박기라도 하는 것 같았다. 그러고는 말했다."]], [["“My friend, I will die, perpetuating the system under which I have lived.”", "\"이 사람아, 나는 내가 살아온 그 체제를 길이 이어가며 죽을 것이다.\""]], [["When he had said it, he took a culminating pinch of snuff, and put his box in his pocket.", "이렇게 말하고 나서 그는 코담배를 한 자밤 마지막으로 집어넣고는 갑을 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["“Better to be a rational creature,”", "\"이성적인 인간이 되는 편이 낫지.\""], ["he added then, after ringing a small bell on the table, “and accept your natural destiny.", "그는 식탁 위의 작은 종을 울리고 나서 덧붙였다. \"그리고 네 타고난 운명을 받아들이는 편이."], ["But you are lost, Monsieur Charles, I see.”", "그러나 너는 가망이 없는 게로구나, 찰스, 보아하니.\""]], [["“This property and France are lost to me,”", "\"이 재산도 프랑스도 제게는 잃어버린 것입니다.\""], ["said the nephew, sadly; “I renounce them.”", "조카가 서글프게 말했다. \"저는 그것들을 버립니다.\""]], [["“Are they both yours to renounce?", "\"그 둘 다 네가 버릴 만큼 네 것이더냐?"], ["France may be, but is the property?", "프랑스는 그럴지 모르나, 재산이 그러하더냐?"], ["It is scarcely worth mentioning; but, is it yet?”", "입에 올릴 가치도 거의 없다만, 아직 네 것이더냐?\""]], [["“I had no intention, in the words I used, to claim it yet.", "\"제가 한 말로 그것을 지금 제 것이라 주장하려던 의도는 없었습니다."], ["If it passed to me from you, to-morrow--”", "만약 그것이 나리에게서 제게 넘어온다면, 내일이라도—\""]], [["“Which I have the vanity to hope is not probable.”", "\"내가 허영으로나마 바라건대, 그럴 것 같지는 않다만.\""]], [["“--or twenty years hence--”", "\"—혹은 이십 년 뒤에라도—\""]], [["“You do me too much honour,”", "\"내게 과분한 영예로구나.\""], ["said the Marquis; “still, I prefer that supposition.”", "후작이 말했다. \"그래도 나는 그쪽 가정이 더 마음에 드는구나.\""]], [["“--I would abandon it, and live otherwise and elsewhere.", "\"—저는 그것을 내버리고, 다른 곳에서 다르게 살겠습니다."], ["It is little to relinquish.", "단념하기에 그것은 하찮은 것입니다."], ["What is it but a wilderness of misery and ruin!”", "그것이 비참과 폐허의 황무지 말고 무엇이겠습니까!\""]], [["“Hah!”", "\"하!\""], ["said the Marquis, glancing round the luxurious room.", "후작이 호화로운 방을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“To the eye it is fair enough, here; but seen in its integrity, under the sky, and by the daylight, it is a crumbling tower of waste, mismanagement, extortion, debt, mortgage, oppression, hunger, nakedness, and suffering.”", "\"여기서는, 눈으로 보기에 그럴듯해 보입니다. 그러나 하늘 아래 대낮의 빛으로 그 전모를 보면, 그것은 낭비와 실정과 강탈과 부채와 저당과 압제와 굶주림과 헐벗음과 고통으로 이루어진, 허물어져가는 탑입니다.\""]], [["“Hah!”", "\"하!\""], ["said the Marquis again, in a well-satisfied manner.", "후작이 흡족한 투로 다시 말했다."]], [["“If it ever becomes mine, it shall be put into some hands better qualified to free it slowly (if such a thing is possible) from the weight that drags it down, so that the miserable people who cannot leave it and who have been long wrung to the last point of endurance, may, in another generation, suffer less; but it is not for me.", "\"만약 그것이 언젠가 제 것이 된다면, 그것을 짓누르는 그 무게로부터 (그럴 수만 있다면) 그것을 서서히 풀어줄 더 나은 자격을 갖춘 손에 맡길 것입니다. 그리하여 그곳을 떠날 수 없고 오래도록 견딤의 마지막 한계까지 쥐어짜인 그 가련한 사람들이, 다음 세대에는 덜 고통받게 말입니다."], ["There is a curse on it, and on all this land.”", "그러나 그것은 제가 할 일이 아닙니다. 그것에는, 그리고 이 온 땅에는 저주가 내려 있으니까요.\""]], [["“And you?”", "\"그러면 너는?\""], ["said the uncle.", "숙부가 말했다."], ["“Forgive my curiosity; do you, under your new philosophy, graciously intend to live?”", "\"내 호기심을 용서해다오. 너는 그 새로운 철학에 따라, 황송하게도 살아갈 작정이냐?\""]], [["“I must do, to live, what others of my countrymen, even with nobility at their backs, may have to do some day--work.”", "\"살아가려면, 저는 다른 동포들이, 비록 등 뒤에 귀족 신분을 두고 있는 자들조차, 언젠가는 하게 될지도 모를 일을 해야 합니다 — 일 말입니다.\""]], [["“In England, for example?”", "\"가령, 영국에서?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"예."], ["The family honour, sir, is safe from me in this country.", "가문의 명예는, 나리, 이 나라에서는 저로 인해 안전합니다."], ["The family name can suffer from me in no other, for I bear it in no other.”", "가문의 이름은 다른 어떤 나라에서도 저로 인해 손상될 일이 없으니, 다른 어떤 나라에서도 제가 그 이름을 쓰지 않기 때문입니다.\""]], [["The ringing of the bell had caused the adjoining bed-chamber to be lighted.", "종을 울린 탓에 옆방 침실에 불이 밝혀졌다."], ["It now shone brightly, through the door of communication.", "이제 그 불빛이 사이를 잇는 문을 통해 환하게 비쳐 들었다."], ["The Marquis looked that way, and listened for the retreating step of his valet.", "후작은 그쪽을 바라보며, 시종이 물러가는 발소리에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“England is very attractive to you, seeing how indifferently you have prospered there,”", "\"네가 영국에서 그토록 시원찮게 형편을 폈던 것을 보면, 영국이 너에게는 무척 매력적인 모양이로구나.\""], ["he observed then, turning his calm face to his nephew with a smile.", "그러고서 그는 차분한 얼굴을 미소와 함께 조카에게로 돌리며 한마디 했다."]], [["“I have already said, that for my prospering there, I am sensible I may be indebted to you, sir.", "\"이미 말씀드렸듯이, 제가 그곳에서 형편을 편 것에 대해서는 나리께 신세를 졌을지도 모른다는 것을 잘 알고 있습니다."], ["For the rest, it is my Refuge.”", "그 나머지로 말하자면, 그곳은 저의 피난처입니다.\""]], [["“They say, those boastful English, that it is the Refuge of many.", "\"잘난 체하는 그 영국인들이 말하기를, 그곳은 숱한 이들의 피난처라더구나."], ["You know a compatriot who has found a Refuge there?", "그곳에서 피난처를 찾은 동포 한 사람을 알고 있느냐?"], ["A Doctor?”", "어떤 의사 말이다.\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“With a daughter?”", "\"딸을 둔?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"예.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said the Marquis.", "후작이 말했다."], ["“You are fatigued.", "\"너는 피곤하겠구나."], ["Good night!”", "잘 자거라!\""]], [["As he bent his head in his most courtly manner, there was a secrecy in his smiling face, and he conveyed an air of mystery to those words, which struck the eyes and ears of his nephew forcibly.", "그가 더없이 정중한 태도로 고개를 숙일 때, 그 미소 띤 얼굴에는 무언가 은밀함이 어려 있었고, 그는 그 말에 알 수 없는 분위기를 실어 보냈으니, 그것이 조카의 눈과 귀를 강하게 때렸다."], ["At the same time, the thin straight lines of the setting of the eyes, and the thin straight lips, and the markings in the nose, curved with a sarcasm that looked handsomely diabolic.", "동시에, 눈가의 가느다랗고 곧은 선들과, 가느다랗고 곧은 입술과, 콧등의 자국들이, 곱상하게 악마적으로 보이는 빈정거림으로 휘어졌다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["repeated the Marquis.", "후작이 되뇌었다."], ["“A Doctor with a daughter.", "\"딸을 둔 의사라. 그래."], ["Yes. So commences the new philosophy!", "새로운 철학은 이렇게 시작되는 게로구나!"], ["You are fatigued.", "너는 피곤하겠다."], ["Good night!”", "잘 자거라!\""]], [["It would have been of as much avail to interrogate any stone face outside the chateau as to interrogate that face of his.", "성관 바깥의 그 어떤 돌얼굴을 캐묻는 것이나, 그의 그 얼굴을 캐묻는 것이나 매한가지로 부질없을 터였다."], ["The nephew looked at him, in vain, in passing on to the door.", "조카는 문 쪽으로 걸어가면서 그를 바라보았으나, 헛일이었다."]], [["“Good night!”", "\"잘 자거라!\""], ["said the uncle.", "숙부가 말했다."], ["“I look to the pleasure of seeing you again in the morning.", "\"아침에 너를 다시 보는 즐거움을 고대하마."], ["Good repose!", "편히 쉬어라!"], ["Light Monsieur my nephew to his chamber there!--And burn Monsieur my nephew in his bed, if you will,”", "거기 내 조카 나리를 침소로 안내해 불을 밝혀드려라! — 그리고 좋다면, 내 조카 나리를 침대째 태워버려라.\""], ["he added to himself, before he rang his little bell again, and summoned his valet to his own bedroom.", "그는 다시 작은 종을 울려 시종을 자기 침실로 부르기에 앞서, 혼잣말로 덧붙였다."]], [["The valet come and gone, Monsieur the Marquis walked to and fro in his loose chamber-robe, to prepare himself gently for sleep, that hot still night.", "시종이 왔다 가고 나서, 후작 나리는 그 무덥고 고요한 밤에 헐렁한 실내복 차림으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며, 잠을 청하려 천천히 채비를 했다."], ["Rustling about the room, his softly-slippered feet making no noise on the floor, he moved like a refined tiger:--looked like some enchanted marquis of the impenitently wicked sort, in story, whose periodical change into tiger form was either just going off, or just coming on.", "부드러운 슬리퍼를 신은 그의 발이 바닥에 아무 소리도 내지 않은 채, 그는 방 안을 사부작거리며, 마치 세련된 호랑이처럼 움직였다. 이야기 속에 나오는, 회개를 모르는 사악한 부류의 어느 마법에 걸린 후작 같았으니, 주기적으로 호랑이 형상으로 바뀌는 변신이 막 끝나가는 참이거나 막 시작되려는 참인 그런 후작 말이다."]], [["He moved from end to end of his voluptuous bedroom, looking again at the scraps of the day’s journey that came unbidden into his mind; the slow toil up the hill at sunset, the setting sun, the descent, the mill, the prison on the crag, the little village in the hollow, the peasants at the fountain, and the mender of roads with his blue cap pointing out the chain under the carriage.", "그는 자신의 관능적인 침실을 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 거닐며, 청하지도 않았는데 마음속에 떠오르는 그날 여정의 단편들을 다시 떠올렸다. 해 질 녘 언덕을 더디게 오르던 일, 지던 해, 내리막길, 물방앗간, 바위 위의 감옥, 우묵한 곳의 작은 마을, 분수가의 농부들, 그리고 푸른 모자를 쓴 채 마차 밑의 쇠사슬을 가리키던 도로 보수공."], ["That fountain suggested the Paris fountain, the little bundle lying on the step, the women bending over it, and the tall man with his arms up, crying, “Dead!”", "그 분수는 빠리의 분수를, 계단에 놓여 있던 작은 보따리를, 그 위로 몸을 굽히던 여인들을, 그리고 두 팔을 치켜든 채 \"죽었어!\" 하고 외치던 키 큰 사내를 떠올리게 했다."]], [["“I am cool now,”", "\"이제 마음이 가라앉았으니,\""], ["said Monsieur the Marquis, “and may go to bed.”", "후작 나리가 말했다. \"잠자리에 들어도 되겠구나.\""]], [["So, leaving only one light burning on the large hearth, he let his thin gauze curtains fall around him, and heard the night break its silence with a long sigh as he composed himself to sleep.", "그리하여 큰 난로에 등불 하나만을 켜둔 채, 그는 얇은 망사 휘장을 둘레로 드리우고, 잠을 청하려 몸을 누일 때 밤이 긴 한숨으로 그 정적을 깨는 소리를 들었다."]], [["The stone faces on the outer walls stared blindly at the black night for three heavy hours; for three heavy hours, the horses in the stables rattled at their racks, the dogs barked, and the owl made a noise with very little resemblance in it to the noise conventionally assigned to the owl by men-poets.", "바깥벽의 돌얼굴들은 무거운 세 시간 동안 캄캄한 밤을 맹목적으로 응시했다. 무거운 세 시간 동안, 마구간의 말들은 여물통에 대고 덜그럭거렸고, 개들은 짖었으며, 부엉이는 사람 시인들이 관습적으로 부엉이에게 부여한 소리와는 거의 닮지 않은 소리를 냈다."], ["But it is the obstinate custom of such creatures hardly ever to say what is set down for them.", "그러나 자기들에게 정해진 대로 좀처럼 말하는 법이 없는 것이, 그런 짐승들의 고집스러운 습성이다."]], [["For three heavy hours, the stone faces of the chateau, lion and human, stared blindly at the night.", "무거운 세 시간 동안, 성관의 돌얼굴들은, 사자며 사람이며, 밤을 맹목적으로 응시했다."], ["Dead darkness lay on all the landscape, dead darkness added its own hush to the hushing dust on all the roads.", "죽은 듯한 어둠이 온 풍경 위에 깔렸고, 죽은 듯한 어둠은 온 길 위에 내려앉아 잠재우는 먼지에 제 나름의 고요를 더했다."], ["The burial-place had got to the pass that its little heaps of poor grass were undistinguishable from one another; the figure on the Cross might have come down, for anything that could be seen of it.", "묘지는 빈약한 풀더미들을 서로 분간할 수 없는 지경에 이르렀고, 십자가 위의 형상은, 눈에 보이는 한에서는, 내려와버렸을 법도 했다."], ["In the village, taxers and taxed were fast asleep.", "마을에서는 세금을 매기는 자와 세금을 무는 자가 깊이 잠들어 있었다."], ["Dreaming, perhaps, of banquets, as the starved usually do, and of ease and rest, as the driven slave and the yoked ox may, its lean inhabitants slept soundly, and were fed and freed.", "굶주린 자들이 흔히 그러하듯 잔칫상을 꿈꾸고, 내몰린 노예와 멍에 멘 황소가 그러할 법하듯 안락과 휴식을 꿈꾸며, 그 비쩍 마른 주민들은 곤히 잠들어, 배불리 먹고 자유를 누렸다."]], [["The fountain in the village flowed unseen and unheard, and the fountain at the chateau dropped unseen and unheard--both melting away, like the minutes that were falling from the spring of Time--through three dark hours.", "마을의 분수는 보이지도 들리지도 않게 흘렀고, 성관의 분수는 보이지도 들리지도 않게 떨어졌다 — 둘 다, 세월의 샘에서 떨어지는 시간들처럼 녹아 사라지며 — 캄캄한 세 시간을 통과해서."], ["Then, the grey water of both began to be ghostly in the light, and the eyes of the stone faces of the chateau were opened.", "이윽고 양쪽의 잿빛 물이 빛 속에서 유령처럼 어렴풋해지기 시작했고, 성관의 돌얼굴들의 눈이 떠졌다."]], [["Lighter and lighter, until at last the sun touched the tops of the still trees, and poured its radiance over the hill.", "점점 더 밝아져, 마침내 해가 고요한 나무들의 우듬지에 닿아 그 광휘를 언덕 위로 쏟아부었다."], ["In the glow, the water of the chateau fountain seemed to turn to blood, and the stone faces crimsoned.", "그 광채 속에서, 성관 분수의 물은 핏빛으로 변하는 듯했고, 돌얼굴들은 진홍빛으로 물들었다."], ["The carol of the birds was loud and high, and, on the weather-beaten sill of the great window of the bed-chamber of Monsieur the Marquis, one little bird sang its sweetest song with all its might.", "새들의 지저귐이 드높이 울려 퍼졌고, 후작 나리의 침실 큰 창문의 비바람에 닳은 창턱에서, 작은 새 한 마리가 온 힘을 다해 더없이 고운 노래를 불렀다."], ["At this, the nearest stone face seemed to stare amazed, and, with open mouth and dropped under-jaw, looked awe-stricken.", "이에 가장 가까운 돌얼굴은 놀라 멍하니 바라보는 듯했고, 입을 벌리고 아래턱을 늘어뜨린 채 경외에 사로잡힌 모습이었다."]], [["Now, the sun was full up, and movement began in the village.", "이제 해가 완전히 떠올랐고, 마을에 움직임이 일기 시작했다."], ["Casement windows opened, crazy doors were unbarred, and people came forth shivering--chilled, as yet, by the new sweet air.", "여닫이창들이 열리고, 부서질 듯한 문들의 빗장이 풀렸으며, 사람들이 — 새로 든 달콤한 공기에 아직 으슬으슬 떨면서 — 밖으로 나왔다."], ["Then began the rarely lightened toil of the day among the village population.", "그러고는 마을 사람들 사이에 좀처럼 누그러질 줄 모르는 그날의 노역이 시작되었다."], ["Some, to the fountain; some, to the fields; men and women here, to dig and delve; men and women there, to see to the poor live stock, and lead the bony cows out, to such pasture as could be found by the roadside.", "더러는 분수가로, 더러는 들판으로. 여기서는 남자와 여자가 땅을 파고 일구러, 저기서는 남자와 여자가 가련한 가축을 보살피고 뼈만 앙상한 소들을 끌어내 길가에서 찾아낼 수 있는 만큼의 풀밭으로 데려가러."], ["In the church and at the Cross, a kneeling figure or two; attendant on the latter prayers, the led cow, trying for a breakfast among the weeds at its foot.", "교회에서, 또 십자가 앞에서, 무릎 꿇은 형상이 한둘. 후자의 기도에 시중드는 것은, 끌려온 그 소였으니, 발치의 잡초들 사이에서 아침거리를 찾고 있었다."]], [["The chateau awoke later, as became its quality, but awoke gradually and surely.", "성관은 그 신분에 걸맞게 더 늦게 깨어났으나, 차츰차츰 그러나 어김없이 깨어났다. 먼저, 외따로 걸린 멧돼지 창과 사냥용 단검들이 예전처럼 붉게 물들었다."], ["First, the lonely boar-spears and knives of the chase had been reddened as of old; then, had gleamed trenchant in the morning sunshine; now, doors and windows were thrown open, horses in their stables looked round over their shoulders at the light and freshness pouring in at doorways, leaves sparkled and rustled at iron-grated windows, dogs pulled hard at their chains, and reared impatient to be loosed.", "그러고는 아침 햇살 속에 날카롭게 번득였다. 이제, 문과 창문이 활짝 열렸고, 마구간의 말들은 어깨너머로 문간으로 쏟아져 드는 빛과 신선함을 돌아보았으며, 쇠창살 창에서는 나뭇잎들이 반짝이며 바스락거렸고, 개들은 쇠사슬을 힘껏 잡아당기며 풀려나고 싶어 안달하며 곧추섰다."]], [["All these trivial incidents belonged to the routine of life, and the return of morning.", "이 모든 사소한 일들은 삶의 일상에, 그리고 아침의 돌아옴에 속하는 것이었다."], ["Surely, not so the ringing of the great bell of the chateau, nor the running up and down the stairs; nor the hurried figures on the terrace; nor the booting and tramping here and there and everywhere, nor the quick saddling of horses and riding away?", "그러나 성관의 큰 종이 울리는 것이며, 계단을 오르내리며 뛰어다니는 것이며, 테라스 위를 서두르는 형상들이며, 여기저기 사방에서 구둣발을 구르며 쿵쾅거리는 것이며, 황급히 말에 안장을 얹고 달려나가는 것이, 설마 그러할 리는 없지 않은가?"]], [["What winds conveyed this hurry to the grizzled mender of roads, already at work on the hill-top beyond the village, with his day’s dinner (not much to carry) lying in a bundle that it was worth no crow’s while to peck at, on a heap of stones?", "어떤 바람이 이 소란을, 마을 너머 언덕 꼭대기에서 이미 일하고 있던 그 희끗희끗한 도로 보수공에게 실어 날랐을까? 그날의 점심밥은 (가져갈 것도 별로 없어) 돌무더기 위에 보따리째 놓여 있어, 까마귀가 쪼아볼 만한 값어치도 없었다."], ["Had the birds, carrying some grains of it to a distance, dropped one over him as they sow chance seeds?", "새들이 그 낟알 몇 알을 멀리 나르다가, 우연한 씨앗을 뿌리듯 한 알을 그의 머리 위로 떨어뜨리기라도 했을까?"], ["Whether or no, the mender of roads ran, on the sultry morning, as if for his life, down the hill, knee-high in dust, and never stopped till he got to the fountain.", "그랬든 안 그랬든, 도로 보수공은 그 무더운 아침에 마치 목숨이라도 걸린 듯, 무릎까지 빠지는 먼지를 헤치며 언덕을 달려 내려가, 분수가에 이르기까지 한 번도 멈추지 않았다."]], [["All the people of the village were at the fountain, standing about in their depressed manner, and whispering low, but showing no other emotions than grim curiosity and surprise.", "마을 사람들이 죄다 분수가에 나와, 그 풀 죽은 모습으로 서성이며 낮게 소곤거렸으나, 음울한 호기심과 놀라움 말고 다른 감정은 드러내지 않았다."], ["The led cows, hastily brought in and tethered to anything that would hold them, were looking stupidly on, or lying down chewing the cud of nothing particularly repaying their trouble, which they had picked up in their interrupted saunter.", "황급히 끌려와 붙들어 맬 수 있는 아무것에나 매어둔 소들은, 멍하니 바라보거나, 끊긴 어슬렁거림 중에 주워 먹은, 수고에 별로 보답이 되지도 않을 그 무엇도 아닌 것을 되새김질하며 누워 있었다."], ["Some of the people of the chateau, and some of those of the posting-house, and all the taxing authorities, were armed more or less, and were crowded on the other side of the little street in a purposeless way, that was highly fraught with nothing.", "성관 사람들 몇과 역참 사람들 몇, 그리고 세금을 매기는 관리들 모두가 어지간히 무장한 채, 작은 길 건너편에 아무 목적도 없는 모양새로 몰려 있었는데, 그 모양새는 더없이 아무 의미도 없는 것으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["Already, the mender of roads had penetrated into the midst of a group of fifty particular friends, and was smiting himself in the breast with his blue cap.", "도로 보수공은 어느새 쉰 명쯤 되는 절친한 벗들 무리 한복판에 파고들어, 푸른 모자로 제 가슴을 치고 있었다."], ["What did all this portend, and what portended the swift hoisting-up of Monsieur Gabelle behind a servant on horseback, and the conveying away of the said Gabelle (double-laden though the horse was), at a gallop, like a new version of the German ballad of Leonora?", "이 모든 것은 무엇을 예고하는 것이며, 가벨 씨가 말에 탄 어느 하인 뒤로 잽싸게 끌어올려지는 것은, 또 (말이 두 사람을 짊어졌는데도) 그 가벨 씨가 마치 「레오노라」라는 독일 발라드의 새로운 판본처럼 질주하여 실려 가는 것은 무엇을 예고하는 것이었던가?"]], [["It portended that there was one stone face too many, up at the chateau.", "그것은 저 위 성관에 돌얼굴이 하나 더 많아졌음을 예고하는 것이었다."]], [["The Gorgon had surveyed the building again in the night, and had added the one stone face wanting; the stone face for which it had waited through about two hundred years.", "고르곤이 밤사이 그 건물을 다시 한번 둘러보고는, 모자라던 그 돌얼굴 하나를 보태놓았던 것이다. 그것이 이백 년 가까이 기다려온 그 돌얼굴을."]], [["It lay back on the pillow of Monsieur the Marquis.", "그것은 후작 나리의 베개를 베고 누워 있었다."], ["It was like a fine mask, suddenly startled, made angry, and petrified.", "그것은 잘 빚은 가면 같았으니, 별안간 흠칫 놀라, 노여워하다가, 그대로 굳어버린 가면이었다."], ["Driven home into the heart of the stone figure attached to it, was a knife.", "거기에 딸린 돌 형상의 심장 깊숙이 박혀 있는 것은, 칼이었다."], ["Round its hilt was a frill of paper, on which was scrawled:", "그 칼자루 둘레에는 종잇조각이 주름져 감겨 있었고, 거기에는 이렇게 휘갈겨 쓰여 있었다."]], [["“Drive him fast to his tomb.", "\"이자를 어서 무덤으로 몰아가라."], ["This, from Jacques.”", "자끄가.\""]], [["# CHAPTER X. Two Promises", "# CHAPTER X. Two Promises"]], [["More months, to the number of twelve, had come and gone, and Mr. Charles Darnay was established in England as a higher teacher of the French language who was conversant with French literature.", "더 많은 달들이, 그 수가 열두 달에 이르도록 오고 갔으며, 찰스 다네이 씨는 프랑스 문학에 정통한 프랑스어 고급 교사로서 영국에 자리를 잡았다."], ["In this age, he would have been a Professor; in that age, he was a Tutor.", "이 시대였다면 그는 교수가 되었을 테고, 그 시대에 그는 가정교사였다."], ["He read with young men who could find any leisure and interest for the study of a living tongue spoken all over the world, and he cultivated a taste for its stores of knowledge and fancy.", "그는 전세계에서 두루 쓰이는 살아 있는 언어를 공부할 만한 여가와 흥미를 지닌 젊은이들과 함께 책을 읽었고, 그 언어가 담은 지식과 상상의 보고를 음미하는 취향을 길렀다."], ["He could write of them, besides, in sound English, and render them into sound English.", "게다가 그는 그것들에 관해 훌륭한 영어로 글을 쓸 수 있었고, 그것들을 훌륭한 영어로 옮겨낼 수 있었다."], ["Such masters were not at that time easily found; Princes that had been, and Kings that were to be, were not yet of the Teacher class, and no ruined nobility had dropped out of Tellson’s ledgers, to turn cooks and carpenters.", "그런 스승은 그 시절 쉬이 찾을 수 있는 존재가 아니었다. 한때 왕자였던 자들도, 장차 왕이 될 자들도 아직 교사 계층은 아니었고, 텔슨 은행의 장부에서 떨어져나와 요리사나 목수로 전락한 몰락 귀족도 아직은 없었다."], ["As a tutor, whose attainments made the student’s way unusually pleasant and profitable, and as an elegant translator who brought something to his work besides mere dictionary knowledge, young Mr. Darnay soon became known and encouraged.", "그 학식이 학생의 길을 유달리 즐겁고 유익하게 만들어주는 가정교사로서, 또 단순한 사전적 지식을 넘어서는 무언가를 자기 일에 불어넣는 우아한 번역가로서, 젊은 다네이 씨는 곧 이름이 알려지고 격려를 받았다."], ["He was well acquainted, more-over, with the circumstances of his country, and those were of ever-growing interest.", "더욱이 그는 제 조국의 사정에 밝았는데, 그 사정은 갈수록 더 큰 관심을 끄는 것이었다."], ["So, with great perseverance and untiring industry, he prospered.", "그리하여 그는 대단한 끈기와 지칠 줄 모르는 근면으로 번창했다."]], [["In London, he had expected neither to walk on pavements of gold, nor to lie on beds of roses; if he had had any such exalted expectation, he would not have prospered.", "런던에서 그는 황금의 포도 위를 걷거나 장미꽃 침대에 눕기를 기대하지 않았다. 만일 그가 그토록 드높은 기대를 품었더라면, 그는 번창하지 못했을 것이다."], ["He had expected labour, and he found it, and did it and made the best of it.", "그는 노고를 기대했고, 그것을 찾아냈으며, 그것을 해냈고, 그것을 최대한 활용했다."], ["In this, his prosperity consisted.", "바로 그 점에 그의 번영이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["A certain portion of his time was passed at Cambridge, where he read with undergraduates as a sort of tolerated smuggler who drove a contraband trade in European languages, instead of conveying Greek and Latin through the Custom-house.", "그의 시간 가운데 일정 부분은 케임브리지에서 보내졌는데, 그곳에서 그는 그리스어와 라틴어를 세관으로 운반하는 대신 유럽 언어로 밀무역을 벌이는, 일종의 묵인받는 밀수꾼처럼 학부생들과 함께 책을 읽었다."], ["The rest of his time he passed in London.", "나머지 시간은 런던에서 보냈다."]], [["Now, from the days when it was always summer in Eden, to these days when it is mostly winter in fallen latitudes, the world of a man has invariably gone one way--Charles Darnay’s way--the way of the love of a woman.", "자, 에덴이 늘 여름이던 시절부터, 타락한 위도에서 대개 겨울인 이 시절에 이르기까지, 한 남자의 세계는 변함없이 한 방향으로 — 찰스 다네이의 방향으로, 한 여자의 사랑이라는 방향으로 — 흘러왔다."]], [["He had loved Lucie Manette from the hour of his danger.", "그는 위기에 처했던 순간부터 루시 마네뜨를 사랑했다."], ["He had never heard a sound so sweet and dear as the sound of her compassionate voice; he had never seen a face so tenderly beautiful, as hers when it was confronted with his own on the edge of the grave that had been dug for him.", "그는 그녀의 연민에 찬 목소리만큼 달콤하고 정겨운 소리를 들어본 적이 없었다. 그는 그를 위해 파놓은 무덤의 가장자리에서 그의 얼굴과 마주했을 때의 그녀 얼굴만큼 부드럽게 아름다운 얼굴을 본 적이 없었다."], ["But, he had not yet spoken to her on the subject; the assassination at the deserted chateau far away beyond the heaving water and the long, long, dusty roads--the solid stone chateau which had itself become the mere mist of a dream--had been done a year, and he had never yet, by so much as a single spoken word, disclosed to her the state of his heart.", "그러나 그는 아직 그 일에 관해 그녀에게 말하지 않았다. 일렁이는 바다와 길고도 긴 먼지투성이 길을 한참 건너간 저 멀리 버려진 성에서 벌어진 그 암살 사건은 — 그 자체가 한낱 꿈의 안개가 되어버린 그 견고한 석조 성에서 벌어진 일은 — 일 년 전 일이었건만, 그는 아직 단 한 마디 말로도 그녀에게 제 마음 상태를 털어놓은 적이 없었다."]], [["That he had his reasons for this, he knew full well.", "이렇게 하는 데에 자기 나름의 이유가 있음을 그는 잘 알았다."], ["It was again a summer day when, lately arrived in London from his college occupation, he turned into the quiet corner in Soho, bent on seeking an opportunity of opening his mind to Doctor Manette.", "다시 여름날이었는데, 대학 일을 마치고 막 런던에 돌아온 그가 마네뜨 박사에게 마음을 열 기회를 찾으려는 마음으로 소호의 고요한 모퉁이로 접어들었다."], ["It was the close of the summer day, and he knew Lucie to be out with Miss Pross.", "여름날이 저물 무렵이었고, 그는 루시가 프로스 양과 함께 외출했음을 알고 있었다."]], [["He found the Doctor reading in his arm-chair at a window.", "그는 박사가 창가의 안락의자에 앉아 책을 읽고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The energy which had at once supported him under his old sufferings and aggravated their sharpness, had been gradually restored to him.", "한때 그가 옛 고난을 견디게 해주는 동시에 그 고통의 날카로움을 더하기도 했던 그 활력이 차츰차츰 그에게 되돌아와 있었다."], ["He was now a very energetic man indeed, with great firmness of purpose, strength of resolution, and vigour of action.", "이제 그는 참으로 정력적인 사람이었고, 목적의 굳건함과 결의의 힘과 행동의 기운이 넘쳤다."], ["In his recovered energy he was sometimes a little fitful and sudden, as he had at first been in the exercise of his other recovered faculties; but, this had never been frequently observable, and had grown more and more rare.", "되찾은 활력 속에서 그는 이따금 다소 변덕스럽고 갑작스러웠는데, 다른 능력들을 되찾아 발휘하기 시작했던 처음에 그랬던 것과 마찬가지였다. 그러나 이런 일은 결코 자주 눈에 띈 적이 없었고, 점점 더 드물어졌다."]], [["He studied much, slept little, sustained a great deal of fatigue with ease, and was equably cheerful.", "그는 많이 공부했고, 잠을 적게 잤으며, 상당한 피로를 거뜬히 견뎌냈고, 한결같이 명랑했다."], ["To him, now entered Charles Darnay, at sight of whom he laid aside his book and held out his hand.", "그런 그에게 이제 찰스 다네이가 들어섰고, 그를 보자 그는 책을 옆으로 치우고 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Charles Darnay!", "\"찰스 다네이!"], ["I rejoice to see you.", "자네를 보니 기쁘구먼."], ["We have been counting on your return these three or four days past.", "지난 사나흘 동안 우리는 자네가 돌아오기만 기다리고 있었다네."], ["Mr. Stryver and Sydney Carton were both here yesterday, and both made you out to be more than due.”", "스트라이버 씨와 시드니 카턴이 어제 둘 다 여기 왔었는데, 둘 다 자네가 진작 돌아왔어야 했다더군.\""]], [["“I am obliged to them for their interest in the matter,”", "\"그 일에 관심을 가져주셔서 그분들께 감사할 따름입니다.\""], ["he answered, a little coldly as to them, though very warmly as to the Doctor.", "그는 그들에 대해서는 다소 냉랭하게, 그러나 박사에 대해서는 매우 따뜻하게 대답했다."], ["“Miss Manette--”", "\"마네뜨 양은—\""]], [["“Is well,”", "\"잘 있다네.\""], ["said the Doctor, as he stopped short, “and your return will delight us all.", "그가 말을 끊자 박사가 말했다. \"그리고 자네가 돌아왔으니 우리 모두 기뻐할 걸세."], ["She has gone out on some household matters, but will soon be home.”", "그애는 집안일 때문에 나갔지만, 곧 돌아올 거야.\""]], [["“Doctor Manette, I knew she was from home.", "\"마네뜨 박사님, 저는 그녀가 집에 없는 걸 알고 있었습니다."], ["I took the opportunity of her being from home, to beg to speak to you.”", "그녀가 집에 없는 틈을 타서, 박사님께 드릴 말씀이 있어 청을 드리려고 합니다.\""]], [["There was a blank silence.", "텅 빈 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래?\""], ["said the Doctor, with evident constraint.", "박사가 뚜렷한 거북함을 띠고 말했다."], ["“Bring your chair here, and speak on.”", "\"의자를 이리 가져와서 말해보게.\""]], [["He complied as to the chair, but appeared to find the speaking on less easy.", "그는 의자에 관해서는 순순히 따랐으나, 말을 이어나가는 일은 그만큼 쉽지 않은 듯했다."]], [["“I have had the happiness, Doctor Manette, of being so intimate here,”", "\"마네뜨 박사님, 저는 이곳에서 이토록 친밀하게 지내는 행복을 누려왔는데,\""], ["so he at length began, “for some year and a half, that I hope the topic on which I am about to touch may not--”", "마침내 그가 입을 열었다. \"어느덧 일 년 반쯤 되었으니, 제가 이제 꺼내려는 화제가 부디—\""]], [["He was stayed by the Doctor’s putting out his hand to stop him.", "박사가 손을 내밀어 그를 멈추게 하는 바람에 그는 말을 멈추었다."], ["When he had kept it so a little while, he said, drawing it back:", "잠시 그렇게 손을 든 채로 있다가, 박사는 그 손을 거두며 말했다."]], [["“Is Lucie the topic?”", "\"루시가 그 화제인가?\""]], [["“She is.”", "\"그렇습니다.\""]], [["“It is hard for me to speak of her at any time.", "\"나로서는 언제든 그애에 관해 말하기가 힘들다네."], ["It is very hard for me to hear her spoken of in that tone of yours, Charles Darnay.”", "그애가 자네의 그런 어조로 거론되는 걸 듣는 건 더더욱 힘들고, 찰스 다네이.\""]], [["“It is a tone of fervent admiration, true homage, and deep love, Doctor Manette!”", "\"이건 열렬한 흠모와 진정한 경의, 그리고 깊은 사랑의 어조입니다, 마네뜨 박사님!\""], ["he said deferentially.", "그가 공손하게 말했다."]], [["There was another blank silence before her father rejoined:", "그녀의 아버지가 다시 입을 열기까지 또 한 번 텅 빈 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“I believe it.", "\"믿네."], ["I do you justice; I believe it.”", "자네를 공정하게 보고 있어. 믿는다네.\""]], [["His constraint was so manifest, and it was so manifest, too, that it originated in an unwillingness to approach the subject, that Charles Darnay hesitated.", "그의 거북함은 너무도 분명했고, 그것이 그 화제에 다가서기를 꺼리는 데서 비롯되었다는 것 또한 너무도 분명했기에, 찰스 다네이는 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“Shall I go on, sir?”", "\"계속할까요, 선생님?\""]], [["Another blank.", "또 한 번 침묵."]], [["“Yes, go on.”", "\"그래, 계속하게.\""]], [["“You anticipate what I would say, though you cannot know how earnestly I say it, how earnestly I feel it, without knowing my secret heart, and the hopes and fears and anxieties with which it has long been laden.", "\"박사님께서는 제가 하려는 말을 미리 짐작하고 계십니다. 다만 제 은밀한 마음과, 그 마음에 오래도록 실려 있던 희망과 두려움과 불안을 모르신 채로는, 제가 얼마나 간절히 이 말을 하는지, 얼마나 간절히 이것을 느끼는지는 아실 수 없겠지요."], ["Dear Doctor Manette, I love your daughter fondly, dearly, disinterestedly, devotedly.", "사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님, 저는 박사님의 따님을 다정하게, 애틋하게, 사심 없이, 헌신적으로 사랑합니다."], ["If ever there were love in the world, I love her.", "이 세상에 사랑이라는 것이 있었던 적이 있다면, 저는 그녀를 사랑합니다."], ["You have loved yourself; let your old love speak for me!”", "박사님께서도 사랑을 해보셨으니, 부디 박사님의 옛 사랑이 저를 대신해 말하게 해주십시오!\""]], [["The Doctor sat with his face turned away, and his eyes bent on the ground.", "박사는 얼굴을 돌리고 눈을 바닥에 떨군 채 앉아 있었다."], ["At the last words, he stretched out his hand again, hurriedly, and cried:", "그 마지막 말에 그는 다시 황급히 손을 뻗으며 외쳤다."]], [["“Not that, sir!", "\"그것만은 안 되네, 여보게!"], ["Let that be!", "그건 내버려두게!"], ["I adjure you, do not recall that!”", "부탁하건대, 그것만은 떠올리게 하지 말아주게!\""]], [["His cry was so like a cry of actual pain, that it rang in Charles Darnay’s ears long after he had ceased.", "그 외침은 실제 고통의 외침과 너무도 흡사해서, 그가 외침을 멈춘 뒤로도 오래도록 찰스 다네이의 귓전에 울렸다."], ["He motioned with the hand he had extended, and it seemed to be an appeal to Darnay to pause.", "그는 뻗었던 손으로 무언가 몸짓을 했는데, 다네이에게 잠시 멈춰달라는 호소처럼 보였다."], ["The latter so received it, and remained silent.", "다네이는 그렇게 받아들이고 잠자코 있었다."]], [["“I ask your pardon,”", "\"용서하게.\""], ["said the Doctor, in a subdued tone, after some moments.", "잠시 후 박사가 가라앉은 어조로 말했다."], ["“I do not doubt your loving Lucie; you may be satisfied of it.”", "\"자네가 루시를 사랑한다는 걸 의심하는 건 아니야. 그 점은 안심해도 좋네.\""]], [["He turned towards him in his chair, but did not look at him, or raise his eyes.", "그는 의자에 앉은 채 그를 향해 몸을 돌렸으나, 그를 쳐다보지도 눈을 들지도 않았다."], ["His chin dropped upon his hand, and his white hair overshadowed his face:", "턱이 손 위로 떨어지고, 백발이 그의 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["“Have you spoken to Lucie?”", "\"루시에게 말한 적이 있나?\" \"아닙니다.\""]], [["“No.” “Nor written?”", "\"편지를 쓴 적도?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["“It would be ungenerous to affect not to know that your self-denial is to be referred to your consideration for her father.", "\"자네의 자제가 그애 아버지를 배려한 데서 비롯된 것임을 모르는 척하는 건 너그럽지 못한 일이겠지."], ["Her father thanks you.”", "그애 아버지가 자네에게 고맙다네.\""]], [["He offered his hand; but his eyes did not go with it.", "그는 손을 내밀었으나, 그 눈길은 손과 함께 가지 않았다."]], [["“I know,”", "\"저는 압니다.\""], ["said Darnay, respectfully, “how can I fail to know, Doctor Manette, I who have seen you together from day to day, that between you and Miss Manette there is an affection so unusual, so touching, so belonging to the circumstances in which it has been nurtured, that it can have few parallels, even in the tenderness between a father and child.", "다네이가 정중하게 말했다. \"어찌 모를 수 있겠습니까, 마네뜨 박사님."], ["I know, Doctor Manette--how can I fail to know--that, mingled with the affection and duty of a daughter who has become a woman, there is, in her heart, towards you, all the love and reliance of infancy itself.", "날마다 두 분이 함께 계신 모습을 보아온 제가, 박사님과 마네뜨 양 사이에 너무도 보기 드물고, 너무도 가슴 뭉클하며, 그 애정이 자라난 환경과 너무도 깊이 맞닿아 있어서, 부녀간의 다정함 가운데서조차 견줄 만한 예가 거의 없는 그런 애정이 있다는 것을 말입니다."], ["I know that, as in her childhood she had no parent, so she is now devoted to you with all the constancy and fervour of her present years and character, united to the trustfulness and attachment of the early days in which you were lost to her.", "저는 압니다, 마네뜨 박사님 — 어찌 모를 수 있겠습니까 — 여인이 된 딸의 애정과 의무에 더하여, 그녀의 마음속에는 박사님을 향한, 유아기 그 자체의 온갖 사랑과 의지가 깃들어 있음을 말입니다. 어린 시절 그녀에게 부모가 없었던 것처럼, 그녀가 이제 현재의 나이와 성품이 지닌 온갖 한결같음과 열정으로, 그리고 박사님을 잃었던 어린 날들의 신뢰와 애착이 어우러진 채 박사님께 헌신하고 있음을 저는 압니다."], ["I know perfectly well that if you had been restored to her from the world beyond this life, you could hardly be invested, in her sight, with a more sacred character than that in which you are always with her.", "만일 박사님이 이승 너머의 세계에서 그녀에게 돌아오셨다 한들, 그녀의 눈에 박사님이 지금 늘 그녀 곁에 계신 모습 이상으로 신성한 성격을 지닌 존재로 여겨질 수는 없으리라는 것을 저는 완벽하게 압니다."], ["I know that when she is clinging to you, the hands of baby, girl, and woman, all in one, are round your neck.", "그녀가 박사님께 매달릴 때면, 갓난아기와 소녀와 여인의 손이 하나가 되어 박사님의 목을 감싸고 있음을 저는 압니다."], ["I know that in loving you she sees and loves her mother at her own age, sees and loves you at my age, loves her mother broken-hearted, loves you through your dreadful trial and in your blessed restoration.", "박사님을 사랑하면서 그녀가 제 어머니를 자기 또래의 나이로 보고 사랑하며, 박사님을 제 나이의 모습으로 보고 사랑하고, 가슴이 찢어진 어머니를 사랑하며, 그 끔찍한 시련을 겪는 박사님과 축복받은 회복 속의 박사님을 사랑함을 저는 압니다."], ["I have known this, night and day, since I have known you in your home.”", "박사님 댁에서 박사님을 알게 된 이래로, 저는 밤낮으로 이것을 알아왔습니다.\""]], [["Her father sat silent, with his face bent down.", "그녀의 아버지는 얼굴을 떨군 채 잠자코 앉아 있었다."], ["His breathing was a little quickened; but he repressed all other signs of agitation.", "그의 숨결이 조금 빨라졌으나, 그는 동요의 다른 모든 기색을 억눌렀다."]], [["“Dear Doctor Manette, always knowing this, always seeing her and you with this hallowed light about you, I have forborne, and forborne, as long as it was in the nature of man to do it.", "\"사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님, 늘 이것을 알았기에, 늘 박사님과 그녀를 그 신성한 빛에 둘러싸인 모습으로 보아왔기에, 저는 사람의 본성으로 견딜 수 있는 한 참고 또 참았습니다."], ["I have felt, and do even now feel, that to bring my love--even mine--between you, is to touch your history with something not quite so good as itself.", "저는 제 사랑을 — 그것이 제 사랑일지라도 — 두 분 사이에 들이미는 것은 박사님의 역사를 그 자체만큼 좋지는 못한 무언가로 건드리는 일이라고 느껴왔고, 지금도 그리 느낍니다."], ["But I love her.", "그러나 저는 그녀를 사랑합니다."], ["Heaven is my witness that I love her!”", "제가 그녀를 사랑함을 하늘이 증언해줍니다!\""]], [["“I believe it,”", "\"믿네.\""], ["answered her father, mournfully.", "그녀의 아버지가 구슬프게 대답했다."], ["“I have thought so before now.", "\"전부터 그리 생각해왔어."], ["I believe it.”", "믿는다네.\""]], [["“But, do not believe,”", "\"그러나 믿지는 말아주십시오,\""], ["said Darnay, upon whose ear the mournful voice struck with a reproachful sound, “that if my fortune were so cast as that, being one day so happy as to make her my wife, I must at any time put any separation between her and you, I could or would breathe a word of what I now say.", "그 구슬픈 목소리가 책망하는 소리처럼 그의 귀를 친 다네이가 말했다. \"만일 제 운명이 그녀를 아내로 맞는 행복을 어느 날 누리게 되면서도 언제든 그녀와 박사님 사이에 어떤 이별을 들이밀어야 하는 식으로 정해진 것이라면, 제가 지금 드리는 이 말을 한 마디라도 입에 담을 수 있거나 입에 담으리라고는 믿지 말아주십시오."], ["Besides that I should know it to be hopeless, I should know it to be a baseness.", "그것이 가망 없는 일임을 알기에 앞서, 그것이 비열한 짓임을 저는 알 것입니다."], ["If I had any such possibility, even at a remote distance of years, harboured in my thoughts, and hidden in my heart--if it ever had been there--if it ever could be there--I could not now touch this honoured hand.”", "만일 제가 그런 가능성을, 설령 아주 먼 훗날의 일로라도, 제 생각 속에 품고 제 마음속에 숨겨두었더라면 — 그것이 한 번이라도 그 안에 있었더라면 — 그것이 한 번이라도 그 안에 있을 수 있었더라면 — 저는 지금 이 존경하는 손을 잡을 수 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["He laid his own upon it as he spoke.", "그는 말하면서 자기 손을 그 위에 얹었다."]], [["“No, dear Doctor Manette.", "\"아닙니다, 사랑하는 마네뜨 박사님."], ["Like you, a voluntary exile from France; like you, driven from it by its distractions, oppressions, and miseries; like you, striving to live away from it by my own exertions, and trusting in a happier future; I look only to sharing your fortunes, sharing your life and home, and being faithful to you to the death.", "박사님처럼 프랑스에서 스스로 망명해온 자로서, 박사님처럼 그 나라의 혼란과 억압과 비참에 떠밀려 그곳을 떠나온 자로서, 박사님처럼 제 힘으로 그곳을 벗어나 살아가려 애쓰며 더 행복한 미래를 믿는 자로서, 저는 오직 박사님의 운명을 함께 나누고, 박사님의 삶과 가정을 함께 나누며, 죽을 때까지 박사님께 충실하기만을 바랍니다."], ["Not to divide with Lucie her privilege as your child, companion, and friend; but to come in aid of it, and bind her closer to you, if such a thing can be.”", "박사님의 자식이자 동반자이자 벗으로서 루시가 누리는 특권을 그녀와 나누어 갖자는 것이 아니라, 그 특권을 돕고, 그럴 수만 있다면 그녀를 박사님께 더욱 가까이 묶어두자는 것입니다.\""]], [["His touch still lingered on her father’s hand.", "그의 손길은 여전히 그녀 아버지의 손 위에 머물러 있었다."], ["Answering the touch for a moment, but not coldly, her father rested his hands upon the arms of his chair, and looked up for the first time since the beginning of the conference.", "잠시 그 손길에 응답하면서도 냉랭하지는 않게, 그녀의 아버지는 두 손을 의자의 팔걸이에 얹고는, 이 대화가 시작된 이래 처음으로 고개를 들었다."], ["A struggle was evidently in his face; a struggle with that occasional look which had a tendency in it to dark doubt and dread.", "그의 얼굴에는 분명 어떤 싸움이 어려 있었다. 어두운 의심과 두려움으로 기우는 경향을 지닌 그 이따금의 표정과의 싸움이었다."]], [["“You speak so feelingly and so manfully, Charles Darnay, that I thank you with all my heart, and will open all my heart--or nearly so.", "\"자네는 너무도 진심 어리게, 또 너무도 사내답게 말하는군, 찰스 다네이. 그래서 진심으로 자네에게 고맙고, 내 마음을 죄다 — 아니, 거의 죄다 — 열어 보이겠네."], ["Have you any reason to believe that Lucie loves you?”", "루시가 자네를 사랑한다고 믿을 만한 어떤 근거라도 있나?\""]], [["“None.", "\"없습니다."], ["As yet, none.”", "아직은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Is it the immediate object of this confidence, that you may at once ascertain that, with my knowledge?”", "\"내가 알고 있다는 것을 등에 업고 당장 그것을 확인하려는 게 이 고백의 당면한 목적인가?\""]], [["“Not even so.", "\"그렇지도 않습니다."], ["I might not have the hopefulness to do it for weeks; I might (mistaken or not mistaken) have that hopefulness to-morrow.”", "저는 몇 주가 지나도 그럴 만한 희망을 못 가질 수도 있고, (잘못 생각한 것이든 아니든) 내일 당장 그런 희망을 가질 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["“Do you seek any guidance from me?”", "\"내게서 무슨 인도라도 구하는 건가?\""]], [["“I ask none, sir.", "\"구하지 않습니다, 선생님."], ["But I have thought it possible that you might have it in your power, if you should deem it right, to give me some.”", "다만 박사님께서 옳다고 여기신다면, 제게 어떤 인도를 베푸실 힘이 박사님께 있을지도 모른다는 생각은 해보았습니다.\""]], [["“Do you seek any promise from me?”", "\"내게서 무슨 약속이라도 구하는 건가?\""]], [["“I do seek that.”", "\"그건 구합니다.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 무엇인가?\""]], [["“I well understand that, without you, I could have no hope.", "\"박사님이 안 계시면 제게 아무런 희망도 없으리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다."], ["I well understand that, even if Miss Manette held me at this moment in her innocent heart--do not think I have the presumption to assume so much--I could retain no place in it against her love for her father.”", "설령 이 순간 마네뜨 양이 자신의 순결한 마음속에 저를 품고 있다 한들 — 제가 감히 그만큼을 넘겨짚을 만큼 주제넘다고는 여기지 말아주십시오 — 그녀가 아버지를 향해 품은 사랑에 맞서서는 제가 그 마음속에 자리를 지킬 수 없으리라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다.\""]], [["“If that be so, do you see what, on the other hand, is involved in it?”", "\"그게 그렇다면, 한편으로 거기에 무엇이 얽혀 있는지 알겠나?\""]], [["“I understand equally well, that a word from her father in any suitor’s favour, would outweigh herself and all the world.", "\"마찬가지로 잘 압니다. 어떤 구혼자든 그를 두둔하는 아버지의 한 마디 말이면, 그녀 자신과 온 세상보다도 더 무게가 나가리라는 것을 말입니다."], ["For which reason, Doctor Manette,”", "그렇기 때문에, 마네뜨 박사님,\""], ["said Darnay, modestly but firmly, “I would not ask that word, to save my life.”", "다네이가 겸손하지만 단호하게 말했다. \"저는 제 목숨을 구하기 위해서라도 그 한 마디 말을 청하지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“I am sure of it.", "\"그건 나도 확신하네."], ["Charles Darnay, mysteries arise out of close love, as well as out of wide division; in the former case, they are subtle and delicate, and difficult to penetrate.", "찰스 다네이, 비밀이란 넓은 단절에서뿐 아니라 가까운 사랑에서도 생겨나는 법이야. 후자의 경우, 그 비밀은 미묘하고 섬세하여 꿰뚫어보기가 어렵지."], ["My daughter Lucie is, in this one respect, such a mystery to me; I can make no guess at the state of her heart.”", "내 딸 루시는 바로 이 한 가지 점에서 내게 그런 비밀이라네. 나는 그애 마음의 상태를 도무지 짐작할 수가 없어.\""]], [["“May I ask, sir, if you think she is--”", "\"여쭤도 될까요, 선생님, 그녀가 혹시—\""], ["As he hesitated, her father supplied the rest.", "그가 머뭇거리자, 그녀의 아버지가 나머지를 채워주었다."]], [["“Is sought by any other suitor?”", "\"다른 구혼자가 구애하고 있느냐고?\""]], [["“It is what I meant to say.”", "\"제가 하려던 말이 그것입니다.\""]], [["Her father considered a little before he answered:", "그녀의 아버지는 대답하기 전에 잠시 생각했다."]], [["“You have seen Mr. Carton here, yourself.", "\"자네도 카턴 씨가 여기 오는 걸 직접 보았지."], ["Mr. Stryver is here too, occasionally.", "스트라이버 씨도 가끔 여기 온다네."], ["If it be at all, it can only be by one of these.”", "만일 그런 일이 있다면, 이 둘 중 하나일 수밖에 없겠지.\""]], [["“Or both,”", "\"아니면 둘 다이거나요.\""], ["said Darnay.", "다네이가 말했다."]], [["“I had not thought of both; I should not think either, likely.", "\"둘 다라고는 생각지 못했네. 어느 쪽도 그럴 법하다고는 여기지 않아."], ["You want a promise from me.", "자네는 내게서 약속을 원하는군."], ["Tell me what it is.”", "그게 무엇인지 말해보게.\""]], [["“It is, that if Miss Manette should bring to you at any time, on her own part, such a confidence as I have ventured to lay before you, you will bear testimony to what I have said, and to your belief in it.", "\"이런 것입니다. 만일 마네뜨 양이 언제든 그녀 자신의 입으로, 제가 감히 박사님께 털어놓은 것과 같은 고백을 박사님께 가져온다면, 제가 한 말과, 그 말에 대한 박사님의 믿음을 증언해주십사 하는 것입니다."], ["I hope you may be able to think so well of me, as to urge no influence against me.", "부디 박사님께서 저를 좋게 보아주셔서, 제게 불리한 어떤 영향력도 행사하지 않으시기를 바랍니다."], ["I say nothing more of my stake in this; this is what I ask.", "이 일에 걸린 제 몫에 관해서는 더 말하지 않겠습니다. 이것이 제가 청하는 바입니다."], ["The condition on which I ask it, and which you have an undoubted right to require, I will observe immediately.”", "제가 그것을 청하는 조건, 그리고 박사님께서 요구하실 의심할 바 없는 권리를 지니신 그 조건은, 제가 곧바로 지키겠습니다.\""]], [["“I give the promise,”", "\"약속하겠네.\""], ["said the Doctor, “without any condition.", "박사가 말했다. \"아무런 조건 없이 말일세."], ["I believe your object to be, purely and truthfully, as you have stated it.", "자네의 목적이 자네가 말한 그대로, 순수하고 진실하다고 믿네."], ["I believe your intention is to perpetuate, and not to weaken, the ties between me and my other and far dearer self.", "자네의 의도가 나와, 또다른 나, 나보다 훨씬 더 소중한 나 사이의 끈을 약하게 하는 것이 아니라 영원토록 이어가려는 것이라 믿어."], ["If she should ever tell me that you are essential to her perfect happiness, I will give her to you.", "만일 그애가 언제고 자네가 자기의 온전한 행복에 없어서는 안 될 사람이라고 내게 말한다면, 나는 그애를 자네에게 주겠네."], ["If there were--Charles Darnay, if there were--”", "만일 — 찰스 다네이, 만일 —\""]], [["The young man had taken his hand gratefully; their hands were joined as the Doctor spoke:", "젊은이는 고맙게 그의 손을 잡았고, 박사가 말하는 동안 두 사람의 손은 맞잡혀 있었다."]], [["“--any fancies, any reasons, any apprehensions, anything whatsoever, new or old, against the man she really loved--the direct responsibility thereof not lying on his head--they should all be obliterated for her sake.", "\"— 그애가 진정으로 사랑하는 사람에 맞서는 어떤 환상이든, 어떤 이유든, 어떤 불안이든, 새롭든 오래되었든 그 무엇이든 — 그 직접적인 책임이 그 사람의 머리 위에 있지 않은 한 — 그것은 모두 그애를 위해 지워져야 하네."], ["She is everything to me; more to me than suffering, more to me than wrong, more to me--Well!", "그애는 내게 전부일세. 내게는 고통보다도 더하고, 부당함보다도 더하며, 더 — 자, 됐네!"], ["This is idle talk.”", "이건 부질없는 얘기야.\""]], [["So strange was the way in which he faded into silence, and so strange his fixed look when he had ceased to speak, that Darnay felt his own hand turn cold in the hand that slowly released and dropped it.", "그가 침묵 속으로 잦아드는 모양새가 어찌나 기이했던지, 또 말을 멈추었을 때 그 고정된 눈빛이 어찌나 기이했던지, 다네이는 천천히 자기 손을 놓아 떨어뜨리는 그 손 안에서 제 손이 차갑게 식는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“You said something to me,”", "\"자네가 내게 무슨 말을 했지.\""], ["said Doctor Manette, breaking into a smile.", "마네뜨 박사가 빙긋 웃음을 지으며 말했다."], ["“What was it you said to me?”", "\"내게 한 말이 무엇이었나?\""]], [["He was at a loss how to answer, until he remembered having spoken of a condition.", "그는 어떻게 대답해야 할지 막막했으나, 어떤 조건에 관해 말했던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Relieved as his mind reverted to that, he answered:", "그쪽으로 생각이 돌아가며 마음이 놓인 그가 대답했다."]], [["“Your confidence in me ought to be returned with full confidence on my part.", "\"저에 대한 박사님의 신뢰에는 제 쪽에서도 온전한 신뢰로 보답해야 마땅합니다."], ["My present name, though but slightly changed from my mother’s, is not, as you will remember, my own.", "지금의 제 이름은, 비록 어머니의 이름에서 조금 바뀐 것에 지나지 않지만, 기억하시다시피 제 본래 이름이 아닙니다."], ["I wish to tell you what that is, and why I am in England.”", "그것이 무엇인지, 그리고 제가 왜 영국에 와 있는지를 말씀드리고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈추게!\""], ["said the Doctor of Beauvais.", "보베의 의사가 말했다. * 보베: 마네뜨 박사가 한때 의사로 일하던 프랑스 북부의 도시."]], [["“I wish it, that I may the better deserve your confidence, and have no secret from you.”", "\"그래야 박사님의 신뢰를 더 잘 받을 자격을 갖추고, 박사님께 아무런 비밀도 없게 될 것 같아 바라는 일입니다.\""]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈추게!\""]], [["For an instant, the Doctor even had his two hands at his ears; for another instant, even had his two hands laid on Darnay’s lips.", "한순간, 박사는 두 손을 제 귀에 갖다 대기까지 했다. 또 한순간에는 두 손을 다네이의 입술에 얹기까지 했다."]], [["“Tell me when I ask you, not now.", "\"내가 묻거든 그때 말해주게, 지금이 아니라."], ["If your suit should prosper, if Lucie should love you, you shall tell me on your marriage morning.", "만일 자네의 구애가 잘 풀려서, 루시가 자네를 사랑하게 된다면, 자네 혼인날 아침에 내게 말해주게."], ["Do you promise?”", "약속하겠나?\""]], [["“Willingly.", "\"기꺼이 그러겠습니다.\""]], [["“Give me your hand.", "\"손을 주게."], ["She will be home directly, and it is better she should not see us together to-night.", "그애가 곧 돌아올 텐데, 오늘밤은 우리 둘이 함께 있는 걸 보지 않는 편이 낫겠어. 가게!"], ["Go! God bless you!”", "신의 가호가 함께하길!\""]], [["It was dark when Charles Darnay left him, and it was an hour later and darker when Lucie came home; she hurried into the room alone--for Miss Pross had gone straight up-stairs--and was surprised to find his reading-chair empty.", "찰스 다네이가 그를 떠날 때는 날이 어두웠고, 루시가 집에 돌아왔을 때는 그보다 한 시간 뒤라 더 어두웠다. 그녀는 혼자 방으로 급히 들어왔는데 — 프로스 양은 곧장 위층으로 올라갔기 때문이다 — 그의 독서용 의자가 비어 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."]], [["“My father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["she called to him.", "그녀가 그를 불렀다."], ["“Father dear!”", "\"아버지!\""]], [["Nothing was said in answer, but she heard a low hammering sound in his bedroom.", "대답으로 아무 말도 들리지 않았으나, 그녀는 그의 침실에서 낮게 망치 두드리는 소리를 들었다."], ["Passing lightly across the intermediate room, she looked in at his door and came running back frightened, crying to herself, with her blood all chilled, “What shall I do!", "그 사이의 방을 가볍게 가로질러 그의 방문 안을 들여다본 그녀는 겁에 질려 달려 돌아왔고, 온몸의 피가 싸늘하게 식은 채 혼잣말로 외쳤다. \"어떡하지!"], ["What shall I do!”", "어떡하지!\""]], [["Her uncertainty lasted but a moment; she hurried back, and tapped at his door, and softly called to him.", "그녀의 망설임은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 서둘러 돌아가 그의 방문을 두드리고 부드럽게 그를 불렀다."], ["The noise ceased at the sound of her voice, and he presently came out to her, and they walked up and down together for a long time.", "그녀의 목소리가 들리자 그 소리는 멎었고, 그가 이내 그녀에게로 나왔으며, 두 사람은 오래도록 함께 거닐었다."]], [["She came down from her bed, to look at him in his sleep that night.", "그날 밤 그녀는 잠든 그를 보려고 침대에서 내려왔다."], ["He slept heavily, and his tray of shoemaking tools, and his old unfinished work, were all as usual.", "그는 깊이 잠들어 있었고, 구두 만드는 연장들이 담긴 쟁반과, 미처 끝내지 못한 그의 옛 작업물은 모두 평소 그대로였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI. A Companion Picture", "# CHAPTER XI. A Companion Picture"]], [["“Sydney,”", "“시드니,”"], ["said Mr.", "바로 그날 밤, 아니 새벽에, 스트라이버 씨가 자기 하수인에게 말했다."], ["Stryver, on that self-same night, or morning, to his jackal; “mix another bowl of punch; I have something to say to you.”", "“펀치를 한 사발 더 타게. 자네한테 할 말이 있어.”"]], [["Sydney had been working double tides that night, and the night before, and the night before that, and a good many nights in succession, making a grand clearance among Mr. Stryver’s papers before the setting in of the long vacation.", "시드니는 그날 밤도, 그 전날 밤도, 또 그 전날 밤도, 그리고 연이은 여러 밤을 곱절로 일했으니, 긴 휴정기가 시작되기 전에 스트라이버 씨의 서류들을 대대적으로 정리해두기 위함이었다."], ["The clearance was effected at last; the Stryver arrears were handsomely fetched up; everything was got rid of until November should come with its fogs atmospheric, and fogs legal, and bring grist to the mill again.", "그 정리는 마침내 끝났다. 스트라이버의 밀린 일들은 말끔하게 처리되었고, 십일월이 대기의 안개와 법정의 안개를 몰고 와 방앗간에 다시 빻을 곡식을 가져올 때까지 모든 것이 처분되었다."]], [["Sydney was none the livelier and none the soberer for so much application.", "시드니는 그토록 매달려 일하고도 더 활기차지도, 더 말짱하지도 않았다."], ["It had taken a deal of extra wet-towelling to pull him through the night; a correspondingly extra quantity of wine had preceded the towelling; and he was in a very damaged condition, as he now pulled his turban off and threw it into the basin in which he had steeped it at intervals for the last six hours.", "그 밤을 버텨내느라 적신 수건을 어지간히 많이 동원해야 했고, 그 수건질에 앞서 그에 걸맞게 술도 많이 들이켰던 터라, 그는 무척 망가진 상태였다. 그는 이제 머리에 두르고 있던 터번을 벗어, 지난 여섯 시간 동안 틈틈이 적셔두었던 대야 속에 던져 넣었다."]], [["“Are you mixing that other bowl of punch?”", "“그 펀치 한 사발 더 타고 있는 건가?”"], ["said Stryver the portly, with his hands in his waistband, glancing round from the sofa where he lay on his back.", "등을 대고 누워 있던 소파에서 고개를 돌리며, 허리춤에 두 손을 찔러 넣은 채 풍채 좋은 스트라이버가 말했다. “타고 있네.”"]], [["“I am.” “Now, look here!", "“자, 이걸 보게!"], ["I am going to tell you something that will rather surprise you, and that perhaps will make you think me not quite as shrewd as you usually do think me.", "자네를 좀 놀라게 할, 그리고 어쩌면 자네가 늘 여기던 것만큼 내가 빈틈없는 사람은 아니라고 생각하게 만들지도 모를 이야기를 하나 하려네."], ["I intend to marry.”", "나 결혼할 작정이야.”"]], [["“Do you?”", "“정말인가?”"]], [["“Yes.", "“그래."], ["And not for money.", "돈 때문도 아니지."], ["What do you say now?”", "이제 뭐라고 하겠나?”"]], [["“I don’t feel disposed to say much.", "“딱히 할 말이 떠오르지 않는군."], ["Who is she?”", "그 여자가 누군가?”"]], [["“Guess.”", "“맞혀보게.”"]], [["“Do I know her?”", "“내가 아는 사람인가?”"]], [["“Guess.”", "“맞혀보게.”"]], [["“I am not going to guess, at five o’clock in the morning, with my brains frying and sputtering in my head.", "“새벽 다섯시에, 머릿속이 지글지글 끓고 튀고 있는데 나더러 맞히라고."], ["If you want me to guess, you must ask me to dinner.”", "정 맞히기를 바란다면 저녁 식사에라도 초대해야지.”"]], [["“Well then, I’ll tell you,”", "“그럼 좋아, 내가 말해주지.”"], ["said Stryver, coming slowly into a sitting posture.", "스트라이버가 천천히 일어나 앉으며 말했다."], ["“Sydney, I rather despair of making myself intelligible to you, because you are such an insensible dog.”", "“시드니, 자네한테 내 뜻을 알아듣게 하기란 어지간히 가망 없는 일이야, 자네가 그렇게나 무딘 인간이니 말이지.”"]], [["“And you,”", "“그리고 자네는,”"], ["returned Sydney, busy concocting the punch, “are such a sensitive and poetical spirit--”", "펀치를 만드느라 분주한 시드니가 받아넘겼다, “그토록 섬세하고 시적인 영혼이고—”"]], [["“Come!”", "“이보게!”"], ["rejoined Stryver, laughing boastfully, “though I don’t prefer any claim to being the soul of Romance (for I hope I know better), still I am a tenderer sort of fellow than you.”", "스트라이버가 으스대며 웃으며 응수했다. “내가 낭만의 화신이라고 주장하는 건 아니지만(난 그 정도는 분별할 줄 안다고 믿으니까), 그래도 나는 자네보다는 다정한 부류의 사내거든.”"]], [["“You are a luckier, if you mean that.”", "“그게 운이 더 좋다는 뜻이라면 그렇겠지.”"]], [["“I don’t mean that.", "“그런 뜻이 아니야."], ["I mean I am a man of more--more--”", "내가 더—더—그런 사내라는 거지.”"]], [["“Say gallantry, while you are about it,”", "“이왕 말이 나온 김에 풍류라고 해두게.”"], ["suggested Carton.", "카턴이 거들었다."]], [["“Well!", "“좋아!"], ["I’ll say gallantry.", "풍류라고 하지."], ["My meaning is that I am a man,”", "내 말은,”"], ["said Stryver, inflating himself at his friend as he made the punch, “who cares more to be agreeable, who takes more pains to be agreeable, who knows better how to be agreeable, in a woman’s society, than you do.”", "펀치를 만드는 친구를 향해 스스로를 부풀리며 스트라이버가 말했다, “나는 여자들과 어울릴 때 호감을 사는 데 더 마음을 쓰고, 호감을 사려고 더 공을 들이고, 호감을 살 줄 더 잘 아는 사내라는 거지, 자네보다 말이야.”"]], [["“Go on,”", "“계속하게.”"], ["said Sydney Carton.", "시드니 카턴이 말했다."]], [["“No; but before I go on,”", "“아니지, 계속하기 전에,”"], ["said Stryver, shaking his head in his bullying way, “I’ll have this out with you.", "스트라이버가 윽박지르듯 고개를 가로저으며 말했다, “이건 자네하고 분명히 짚고 넘어가야겠어."], ["You’ve been at Doctor Manette’s house as much as I have, or more than I have.", "자네도 나만큼이나, 아니 나보다 더 자주 마네뜨 박사 댁에 드나들었지."], ["Why, I have been ashamed of your moroseness there!", "아니, 그 댁에서 보인 자네의 그 시무룩한 꼴이 부끄러울 지경이었어!"], ["Your manners have been of that silent and sullen and hangdog kind, that, upon my life and soul, I have been ashamed of you, Sydney!”", "자네의 그 태도란 게 어찌나 말없고 뚱하고 풀죽은 꼴이던지, 정말이지 내 목숨과 영혼을 걸고 말하건대, 시드니, 난 자네가 부끄러웠다네!”"]], [["“It should be very beneficial to a man in your practice at the bar, to be ashamed of anything,”", "“법정에서 일하는 사람이 무언가를 부끄러워한다는 건 자네에게 무척 이로운 일일 텐데.”"], ["returned Sydney; “you ought to be much obliged to me.”", "시드니가 받아넘겼다. “자네는 내게 크게 고마워해야 해.”"]], [["“You shall not get off in that way,”", "“그런 식으로 빠져나가게 둘 순 없지.”"], ["rejoined Stryver, shouldering the rejoinder at him; “no, Sydney, it’s my duty to tell you--and I tell you to your face to do you good--that you are a devilish ill-conditioned fellow in that sort of society.", "스트라이버가 어깨로 떠밀듯 응수했다. “안 돼, 시드니, 자네한테 이 말을 해주는 게 내 의무야—그리고 자네 잘되라고 면전에 대고 말하는 거야—자네는 그런 자리에서는 지독히도 심사 사나운 작자라는 걸세."], ["You are a disagreeable fellow.”", "자네는 불쾌한 작자라고.”"]], [["Sydney drank a bumper of the punch he had made, and laughed.", "시드니는 자기가 만든 펀치를 한 잔 가득 들이켜고는 웃었다."]], [["“Look at me!”", "“나를 보게!”"], ["said Stryver, squaring himself; “I have less need to make myself agreeable than you have, being more independent in circumstances.", "스트라이버가 어깨를 펴며 말했다. “나는 자네보다 호감을 살 필요가 덜한 사람이야, 형편이 더 넉넉하니 말이지."], ["Why do I do it?”", "그런데도 내가 왜 그러겠나?”"]], [["“I never saw you do it yet,”", "“아직 한 번도 자네가 그러는 걸 본 적이 없네만.”"], ["muttered Carton.", "카턴이 중얼거렸다."]], [["“I do it because it’s politic; I do it on principle.", "“그게 처세에 이로우니까 하는 거지. 신조에 따라 하는 거야."], ["And look at me!", "그리고 나를 보게!"], ["I get on.”", "나는 잘나가고 있잖아.”"]], [["“You don’t get on with your account of your matrimonial intentions,”", "“자네 결혼 계획에 대한 이야기는 잘 나아가지 못하고 있네만.”"], ["answered Carton, with a careless air; “I wish you would keep to that.", "카턴이 무심한 태도로 대꾸했다. “그 얘기나 계속하면 좋겠군."], ["As to me--will you never understand that I am incorrigible?”", "나로 말할 것 같으면—내가 구제 불능이라는 걸 자네는 영영 모르겠나?”"]], [["He asked the question with some appearance of scorn.", "그는 어딘지 경멸하는 기색으로 그 물음을 던졌다."]], [["“You have no business to be incorrigible,”", "“자네가 구제 불능일 까닭이 없지.”"], ["was his friend’s answer, delivered in no very soothing tone.", "친구의 대답은 그다지 다독이는 어조가 아니었다."]], [["“I have no business to be, at all, that I know of,”", "“내가 알기로는 무엇이 될 까닭조차 통 없다네.”"], ["said Sydney Carton.", "시드니 카턴이 말했다."], ["“Who is the lady?”", "“그 숙녀가 누군가?”"]], [["“Now, don’t let my announcement of the name make you uncomfortable, Sydney,” said Mr.", "“자, 내가 그 이름을 밝힌다고 해서 거북해하지는 말게, 시드니.”"], ["Stryver, preparing him with ostentatious friendliness for the disclosure he was about to make, “because I know you don’t mean half you say; and if you meant it all, it would be of no importance.", "스트라이버 씨가 곧 털어놓을 사실을 앞두고 요란스레 다정한 태도로 그를 어르며 말했다. “자네가 하는 말의 절반도 진심이 아니란 걸 내가 아니까."], ["I make this little preface, because you once mentioned the young lady to me in slighting terms.”", "설령 전부 진심이라 해도 대수롭지 않을 테고. 이렇게 짤막한 서두를 다는 건, 언젠가 자네가 그 젊은 숙녀를 두고 깔보는 투로 말한 적이 있어서야.”"]], [["“Certainly; and in these chambers.”", "“분명히 그랬지, 바로 이 방에서.”"]], [["Sydney Carton looked at his punch and looked at his complacent friend; drank his punch and looked at his complacent friend.", "시드니 카턴은 자기 펀치를 바라보다가, 흡족해하는 친구를 바라보았다. 펀치를 들이켜고는 다시 흡족해하는 친구를 바라보았다."]], [["“You made mention of the young lady as a golden-haired doll.", "“자네는 그 젊은 숙녀를 금발 인형이라고 했지."], ["The young lady is Miss Manette.", "그 젊은 숙녀가 바로 마네뜨 양이라네."], ["If you had been a fellow of any sensitiveness or delicacy of feeling in that kind of way, Sydney, I might have been a little resentful of your employing such a designation; but you are not.", "만일 자네가 그런 방면으로 조금이라도 예민하고 섬세한 감정을 지닌 사내였다면, 시드니, 자네가 그런 호칭을 쓴 것에 내가 약간은 분개했을지도 모르지. 하지만 자네는 그렇지 않으니까."], ["You want that sense altogether; therefore I am no more annoyed when I think of the expression, than I should be annoyed by a man’s opinion of a picture of mine, who had no eye for pictures: or of a piece of music of mine, who had no ear for music.”", "자네는 그런 감각이 통째로 없으니까. 그러니 그 표현을 떠올린다 해도 나는 조금도 거슬리지 않아, 그림을 볼 줄 모르는 자가 내 그림에 대해 늘어놓는 평이나, 음악을 들을 귀가 없는 자가 내 음악에 대해 늘어놓는 평에 내가 거슬려할 까닭이 없는 것처럼 말이지.”"]], [["Sydney Carton drank the punch at a great rate; drank it by bumpers, looking at his friend.", "시드니 카턴은 엄청난 속도로 펀치를 들이켰다. 친구를 바라보며 잔째로 들이켰다."]], [["“Now you know all about it, Syd,”", "“이제 자네도 전부 알았네, 시드.” 스트라이버 씨가 말했다."], ["said Mr. Stryver.", "“나는 재산 따위는 개의치 않아."], ["“I don’t care about fortune: she is a charming creature, and I have made up my mind to please myself: on the whole, I think I can afford to please myself.", "그 여자는 매력적인 사람이고, 나는 내 마음에 흡족할 대로 하기로 작정했어. 대체로 보아 내 마음에 흡족하게 굴 만한 여유는 있다고 보네."], ["She will have in me a man already pretty well off, and a rapidly rising man, and a man of some distinction: it is a piece of good fortune for her, but she is worthy of good fortune.", "그 여자는 나라는, 이미 제법 형편이 좋고, 빠르게 출세하는 중이며, 어느 정도 명망도 있는 사내를 얻게 되는 거지. 그 여자에게는 더없는 행운이지만, 그 여자도 행운을 누릴 만한 사람이지."], ["Are you astonished?”", "놀랐나?”"]], [["Carton, still drinking the punch, rejoined, “Why should I be astonished?”", "여전히 펀치를 들이켜며 카턴이 받았다. “내가 왜 놀라야 하나?”"]], [["“You approve?”", "“찬성하나?”"]], [["Carton, still drinking the punch, rejoined, “Why should I not approve?”", "여전히 펀치를 들이켜며 카턴이 받았다. “내가 왜 찬성하지 않겠나?”"]], [["“Well!”", "“음!” 친구 스트라이버가 말했다."], ["said his friend Stryver, “you take it more easily than I fancied you would, and are less mercenary on my behalf than I thought you would be; though, to be sure, you know well enough by this time that your ancient chum is a man of a pretty strong will.", "“자네는 내가 생각했던 것보다 한결 담담하게 받아들이고, 내 입장에서 보면 내가 생각했던 것보다 한결 욕심 없이 구는군. 하기야 이쯤 되면 자네도 자네의 그 오랜 벗이 제법 의지가 굳센 사내라는 걸 잘 알겠지."], ["Yes, Sydney, I have had enough of this style of life, with no other as a change from it; I feel that it is a pleasant thing for a man to have a home when he feels inclined to go to it (when he doesn’t, he can stay away), and I feel that Miss Manette will tell well in any station, and will always do me credit.", "그래, 시드니, 이런 식의 생활은 이제 지긋지긋해, 달리 기분 전환거리도 없이 말이야. 사내라면 마음 내킬 때 돌아갈 가정이 있다는 게 흐뭇한 일이라고 느끼게 됐어(내키지 않으면 안 가도 그만이지)."], ["So I have made up my mind.", "그리고 마네뜨 양은 어떤 처지에서든 잘 어울릴 것이며, 늘 내 체면을 세워주리라고 느끼네."], ["And now, Sydney, old boy, I want to say a word to you about your prospects.", "그래서 마음을 굳혔지. 자, 그리고 시드니, 이 친구야, 이제 자네의 앞날에 대해 한마디 하고 싶네."], ["You are in a bad way, you know; you really are in a bad way.", "자네는 지금 처지가 안 좋아, 알겠나. 정말이지 처지가 안 좋다고."], ["You don’t know the value of money, you live hard, you’ll knock up one of these days, and be ill and poor; you really ought to think about a nurse.”", "자네는 돈의 가치를 모르고, 험하게 살고, 머잖아 어느 날 쓰러져서 병들고 가난해질 걸세. 자네야말로 자네를 돌봐줄 사람을 생각해봐야 해.”"]], [["The prosperous patronage with which he said it, made him look twice as big as he was, and four times as offensive.", "번창하는 자의 생색을 내며 그렇게 말하니, 그는 실제보다 두 배는 커 보였고, 네 배는 거슬려 보였다."]], [["“Now, let me recommend you,”", "“자, 내가 자네한테 권하건대,”"], ["pursued Stryver, “to look it in the face.", "스트라이버가 말을 이었다. “현실을 똑바로 마주하게."], ["I have looked it in the face, in my different way; look it in the face, you, in your different way.", "나는 내 나름의 방식으로 현실을 똑바로 마주했어. 자네도 자네 나름의 방식으로 현실을 똑바로 마주하라고."], ["Marry.", "결혼하게."], ["Provide somebody to take care of you.", "자네를 보살펴줄 누군가를 마련하라고."], ["Never mind your having no enjoyment of women’s society, nor understanding of it, nor tact for it.", "자네가 여자들과 어울리는 걸 즐기지도 못하고, 이해하지도 못하고, 그럴 요령도 없다는 건 괘념치 말게."], ["Find out somebody.", "누군가를 찾아내."], ["Find out some respectable woman with a little property--somebody in the landlady way, or lodging-letting way--and marry her, against a rainy day.", "약간의 재산이 있는, 점잖은 여자를 찾아내라고—집주인이라든가, 하숙을 치는 부류의 여자 말이지—그리고 그 여자랑 결혼해, 비 오는 날에 대비해서 말이야."], ["That’s the kind of thing for you.", "그게 자네에게 어울리는 거야."], ["Now think of it, Sydney.”", "자, 그걸 생각해보게, 시드니.”"]], [["“I’ll think of it,”", "“생각해보지.”"], ["said Sydney.", "시드니가 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. The Fellow of Delicacy", "# CHAPTER XII. The Fellow of Delicacy"]], [["Mr. Stryver having made up his mind to that magnanimous bestowal of good fortune on the Doctor’s daughter, resolved to make her happiness known to her before he left town for the Long Vacation.", "스트라이버 씨는 박사의 딸에게 행운을 베푸는 그 관대한 처분을 내리기로 마음을 굳혔으니, 장기 휴정에 들어가 도시를 떠나기 전에 그녀에게 행복을 알려주리라 결심했다."], ["After some mental debating of the point, he came to the conclusion that it would be as well to get all the preliminaries done with, and they could then arrange at their leisure whether he should give her his hand a week or two before Michaelmas Term, or in the little Christmas vacation between it and Hilary.", "그 점을 두고 머릿속으로 얼마간 논의한 끝에, 그는 예비 절차를 모조리 끝내두는 편이 좋겠다는 결론에 이르렀고, 그러고 나면 미가엘 축일 학기 시작 한두 주 전에 그녀에게 손을 내밀 것인지, 아니면 그 학기와 힐러리 학기 사이의 짧은 성탄 휴가 동안 그리할 것인지를 두 사람이 한가로이 정할 수 있으리라 보았다. * 미가엘 축일 학기·힐러리 학기: 영국 법조계와 대학의 학기 구분으로, 미가엘 축일 학기는 가을, 힐러리 학기는 겨울에 시작된다."]], [["As to the strength of his case, he had not a doubt about it, but clearly saw his way to the verdict.", "자기 쪽 논거의 견실함에 대해서는 그는 한 점 의심도 없었고, 평결로 가는 길이 또렷이 보였다."], ["Argued with the jury on substantial worldly grounds--the only grounds ever worth taking into account--it was a plain case, and had not a weak spot in it.", "오직 고려할 가치가 있는 유일한 근거인 실질적이고 세속적인 근거를 들어 배심원단을 상대로 변론한다면, 이는 명백한 사건이었고 약점이라곤 하나도 없었다."], ["He called himself for the plaintiff, there was no getting over his evidence, the counsel for the defendant threw up his brief, and the jury did not even turn to consider.", "그는 원고로서 자신을 불러 세웠고, 그 증언은 도무지 반박할 길이 없었으며, 피고측 변호인은 사건 적요서를 내던졌고, 배심원단은 심의하려고 돌아앉을 필요조차 없었다."], ["After trying it, Stryver, C. J., was satisfied that no plainer case could be.", "한번 따져본 끝에, 수석 재판관 스트라이버는 이보다 더 명백한 사건은 있을 수 없다고 흡족해했다."]], [["Accordingly, Mr.", "그리하여 스트라이버 씨는 마네뜨 양을 복스홀 가든으로 데려가겠노라는 격식 차린 제안으로 장기 휴정을 열기로 했다."], ["Stryver inaugurated the Long Vacation with a formal proposal to take Miss Manette to Vauxhall Gardens; that failing, to Ranelagh; that unaccountably failing too, it behoved him to present himself in Soho, and there declare his noble mind.", "그것이 실패하면 래닐러로, 그것마저 영문 모르게 실패하면, 그가 직접 소호에 모습을 드러내어 거기서 자신의 고결한 뜻을 선언해야 마땅했다."]], [["Towards Soho, therefore, Mr. Stryver shouldered his way from the Temple, while the bloom of the Long Vacation’s infancy was still upon it.", "그리하여 스트라이버 씨는 장기 휴정의 첫머리에 어린 꽃이 아직 피어 있을 무렵, 사람들을 헤치며 템플에서 소호 쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["Anybody who had seen him projecting himself into Soho while he was yet on Saint Dunstan’s side of Temple Bar, bursting in his full-blown way along the pavement, to the jostlement of all weaker people, might have seen how safe and strong he was.", "그가 아직 템플 바의 세인트 던스턴 쪽에 있으면서 소호를 향해 자신을 내던지듯, 한껏 부푼 기세로 보도를 따라 돌진하며 더 약한 사람들을 모조리 떠밀어대는 모습을 본 사람이라면, 그가 얼마나 안전하고 강건한지 알아보았을 것이다."]], [["His way taking him past Tellson’s, and he both banking at Tellson’s and knowing Mr. Lorry as the intimate friend of the Manettes, it entered Mr.", "가는 길이 텔슨 은행을 지나게 되어 있었고, 그 자신이 텔슨 은행과 거래하는 데다 로리 씨를 마네뜨 가의 막역한 친구로 알고 있었으므로, 스트라이버 씨는 은행에 들러 로리 씨에게 소호 지평선의 환한 빛을 알려주어야겠다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Stryver’s mind to enter the bank, and reveal to Mr. Lorry the brightness of the Soho horizon.", "그래서 그는 목구멍에서 힘없이 덜그럭거리는 소리가 나는 문을 밀어젖히고, 두 계단을 비틀거리며 내려가, 늙은 출납계원 둘을 지나쳐, 곰팡내 나는 안쪽 골방으로 어깨를 들이밀고 들어갔다."], ["So, he pushed open the door with the weak rattle in its throat, stumbled down the two steps, got past the two ancient cashiers, and shouldered himself into the musty back closet where Mr. Lorry sat at great books ruled for figures, with perpendicular iron bars to his window as if that were ruled for figures too, and everything under the clouds were a sum.", "거기서는 로리 씨가 숫자를 기입하도록 줄이 그어진 커다란 장부들을 앞에 놓고 앉아 있었는데, 창문에는 마치 그것도 숫자를 기입하도록 줄이 그어진 양, 그리고 하늘 아래 모든 것이 하나의 계산인 양, 수직의 쇠창살이 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Halloa!”", "\"여어!\""], ["said Mr. Stryver.", "스트라이버 씨가 말했다."], ["“How do you do?", "\"안녕하시오?"], ["I hope you are well!”", "무탈하시길 바라오!\""]], [["It was Stryver’s grand peculiarity that he always seemed too big for any place, or space.", "언제나 어떤 장소나 공간에도 너무 크게 보인다는 것이 스트라이버의 대단한 특징이었다."], ["He was so much too big for Tellson’s, that old clerks in distant corners looked up with looks of remonstrance, as though he squeezed them against the wall.", "그는 텔슨 은행에는 너무도 컸으므로, 먼 구석의 늙은 직원들이 마치 그가 자기들을 벽에 짓눌러대기라도 하는 듯 항의하는 눈빛으로 고개를 들었다."], ["The House itself, magnificently reading the paper quite in the far-off perspective, lowered displeased, as if the Stryver head had been butted into its responsible waistcoat.", "멀찍한 원경에서 위풍당당하게 신문을 읽고 있던 은행 본체조차, 마치 스트라이버의 머리가 그 책임 막중한 조끼를 들이받기라도 한 듯 못마땅하게 낯을 찌푸렸다."]], [["The discreet Mr. Lorry said, in a sample tone of the voice he would recommend under the circumstances, “How do you do, Mr.", "신중한 로리 씨는 그런 상황에서라면 권하고 싶은 목소리의 본보기 같은 어조로, \"안녕하시오, 스트라이버 씨?"], ["Stryver? How do you do, sir?”", "안녕하시오, 선생?\""], ["and shook hands.", "하고 말하며 악수를 했다."], ["There was a peculiarity in his manner of shaking hands, always to be seen in any clerk at Tellson’s who shook hands with a customer when the House pervaded the air.", "그의 악수하는 방식에는 한 가지 특징이 있었으니, 은행 본체의 기운이 공기 중에 가득할 때 고객과 악수하는 텔슨 은행의 직원이라면 누구에게서나 늘 볼 수 있는 것이었다."], ["He shook in a self-abnegating way, as one who shook for Tellson and Co.", "그는 자신을 지워버리는 듯한 태도로, 마치 텔슨 상회를 대신해 악수하는 사람처럼 손을 흔들었다."]], [["“Can I do anything for you, Mr. Stryver?”", "\"제가 무얼 도와드릴까요, 스트라이버 씨?\""], ["asked Mr. Lorry, in his business character.", "로리 씨가 업무용 인격으로 물었다."]], [["“Why, no, thank you; this is a private visit to yourself, Mr.", "\"아니, 됐소, 고맙소. 이건 당신을 찾아온 사사로운 방문이오, 로리 씨."], ["Lorry; I have come for a private word.”", "사적인 이야기를 좀 하러 왔소.\""]], [["“Oh indeed!” said Mr.", "\"오, 그렇군요!\""], ["Lorry, bending down his ear, while his eye strayed to the House afar off.", "로리 씨는 한쪽 귀를 기울이면서도 눈길은 저 멀리 은행 본체 쪽으로 흘려보내며 말했다."]], [["“I am going,” said Mr.", "\"실은 말이오,\""], ["Stryver, leaning his arms confidentially on the desk: whereupon, although it was a large double one, there appeared to be not half desk enough for him: “I am going to make an offer of myself in marriage to your agreeable little friend, Miss Manette, Mr.", "스트라이버 씨가 은밀하게 책상에 두 팔을 괴며 말했는데, 그러자 그 책상은 큼직한 이인용이었는데도 그에게는 반쪽도 모자라 보였다."], ["Lorry.”", "\"당신의 그 상냥한 어린 친구 마네뜨 양에게, 나 자신을 결혼 상대로 청해볼 작정이오, 로리 씨.\""]], [["“Oh dear me!”", "\"오, 저런!\""], ["cried Mr. Lorry, rubbing his chin, and looking at his visitor dubiously.", "로리 씨가 턱을 문지르며, 미심쩍은 눈으로 손님을 바라보며 외쳤다."]], [["“Oh dear me, sir?”", "\"오, 저런이라니, 선생?\""], ["repeated Stryver, drawing back.", "스트라이버가 몸을 뒤로 빼며 되받았다."], ["“Oh dear you, sir?", "\"오, 당신께 저런이라니, 선생?"], ["What may your meaning be, Mr. Lorry?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이오, 로리 씨?\""]], [["“My meaning,”", "\"제 뜻은,\""], ["answered the man of business, “is, of course, friendly and appreciative, and that it does you the greatest credit, and--in short, my meaning is everything you could desire. But--really, you know, Mr.", "그 업무의 사나이가 대답했다, \"물론 우호적이고 높이 평가하는 것이며, 당신께 더없는 영예가 된다는 것이고, 그러니까—한마디로, 제 뜻은 당신이 바랄 수 있는 모든 것입니다."], ["Stryver--” Mr.", "그렇지만—정말이지, 그게 말이오, 스트라이버 씨—\""], ["Lorry paused, and shook his head at him in the oddest manner, as if he were compelled against his will to add, internally, “you know there really is so much too much of you!”", "로리 씨는 말을 멈추고는 더없이 기묘한 태도로 그를 향해 고개를 저었으니, 마치 자기 의지와는 무관하게, 속으로 \"당신은 정말이지 너무 과하다는 걸 아셔야 한다오!\" 하고 덧붙이도록 떠밀리기라도 한 것 같았다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"이거 참!\""], ["said Stryver, slapping the desk with his contentious hand, opening his eyes wider, and taking a long breath, “if I understand you, Mr.", "스트라이버가 시비조의 손으로 책상을 철썩 치고, 눈을 더 크게 뜨고, 긴 숨을 들이쉬며 말했다."], ["Lorry, I’ll be hanged!”", "\"내가 당신 말을 제대로 알아들었다면, 내 목을 매달아도 좋소!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry adjusted his little wig at both ears as a means towards that end, and bit the feather of a pen.", "로리 씨는 그 목적을 위한 방편이라는 듯 작은 가발을 양쪽 귀에서 매만지고는, 펜의 깃을 깨물었다."]], [["“D--n it all, sir!”", "\"이런 빌어먹을, 선생!\""], ["said Stryver, staring at him, “am I not eligible?”", "스트라이버가 그를 빤히 노려보며 말했다. \"내가 자격이 없단 말이오?\""]], [["“Oh dear yes!", "\"오, 천만에! 있고말고요."], ["Yes. Oh yes, you’re eligible!”", "오, 그럼요, 자격이 있으시지요!\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“If you say eligible, you are eligible.”", "\"자격이 있다고 하시면, 자격이 있는 거지요.\""]], [["“Am I not prosperous?”", "\"내가 번창하지 않았소?\""], ["asked Stryver.", "스트라이버가 물었다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["if you come to prosperous, you are prosperous,”", "번창을 따지신다면, 번창하셨지요.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“And advancing?”", "\"그리고 출세하고 있지 않소?\""]], [["“If you come to advancing you know,” said Mr.", "\"출세를 따지신다면 말이지요,\""], ["Lorry, delighted to be able to make another admission, “nobody can doubt that.”", "로리 씨가 또 한 가지 인정할 거리가 생겨 기뻐하며 말했다. \"그건 아무도 의심할 수 없지요.\""]], [["“Then what on earth is your meaning, Mr.", "\"그렇다면 도대체 무슨 뜻이오, 로리 씨?\""], ["Lorry?” demanded Stryver, perceptibly crestfallen.", "스트라이버가 눈에 띄게 풀이 죽어 따져 물었다."]], [["“Well!", "\"글쎄요!"], ["I--Were you going there now?”", "저는—지금 그리로 가시려던 참이었습니까?\""], ["asked Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Straight!”", "\"곧장!\""], ["said Stryver, with a plump of his fist on the desk.", "스트라이버가 주먹을 책상에 쿵 내리치며 말했다."]], [["“Then I think I wouldn’t, if I was you.”", "\"그렇다면 제가 당신이라면 안 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["said Stryver.", "스트라이버가 말했다."], ["“Now, I’ll put you in a corner,”", "\"자, 내가 당신을 궁지로 몰아넣겠소,\""], ["forensically shaking a forefinger at him.", "하고는 변론하듯 그를 향해 집게손가락을 흔들었다."], ["“You are a man of business and bound to have a reason.", "\"당신은 업무의 사나이이니, 마땅히 이유가 있을 거요."], ["State your reason.", "그 이유를 대시오."], ["Why wouldn’t you go?”", "왜 안 가겠다는 거요?\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, “I wouldn’t go on such an object without having some cause to believe that I should succeed.”", "로리 씨가 말했다, \"저라면 성공하리라고 믿을 만한 어떤 근거도 없이 그런 일을 하러 가지는 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“D--n me!”", "\"이런 빌어먹을!\""], ["cried Stryver, “but this beats everything.”", "스트라이버가 외쳤다. \"이건 정말 모든 걸 능가하는군.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry glanced at the distant House, and glanced at the angry Stryver.", "로리 씨는 멀리 있는 은행 본체를 흘끗 보고, 화가 난 스트라이버를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Here’s a man of business--a man of years--a man of experience--in a Bank,”", "\"여기 한 업무의 사나이가 있소—연륜이 있는 사람이—경험이 있는 사람이—은행 안에 있단 말이오,\""], ["said Stryver; “and having summed up three leading reasons for complete success, he says there’s no reason at all!", "스트라이버가 말했다. \"그런데 완전한 성공의 주된 이유를 셋이나 합산해놓고도, 이유가 도무지 없다고 하다니!"], ["Says it with his head on!” Mr.", "그것도 머리를 멀쩡히 달고 그런 말을 하다니!\""], ["Stryver remarked upon the peculiarity as if it would have been infinitely less remarkable if he had said it with his head off.", "스트라이버 씨는 그 기이함을 마치, 머리를 떼어놓고 그런 말을 했다면 한없이 덜 기이했으리라는 듯이 지적했다."]], [["“When I speak of success, I speak of success with the young lady; and when I speak of causes and reasons to make success probable, I speak of causes and reasons that will tell as such with the young lady.", "\"제가 성공을 말할 때는, 그 아가씨와의 성공을 말하는 겁니다. 그리고 제가 성공을 가능케 할 원인과 이유를 말할 때는, 그 아가씨에게 그렇게 작용할 원인과 이유를 말하는 겁니다."], ["The young lady, my good sir,” said Mr.", "그 아가씨 말입니다, 선생,\""], ["Lorry, mildly tapping the Stryver arm, “the young lady.", "로리 씨가 스트라이버의 팔을 부드럽게 두드리며 말했다. \"그 아가씨 말이오."], ["The young lady goes before all.”", "그 아가씨가 무엇보다 우선입니다.\""]], [["“Then you mean to tell me, Mr. Lorry,”", "\"그렇다면 당신은 내게 이렇게 말하려는 거요, 로리 씨,\""], ["said Stryver, squaring his elbows, “that it is your deliberate opinion that the young lady at present in question is a mincing Fool?”", "스트라이버가 양 팔꿈치를 떡 벌리며 말했다. \"지금 문제가 되고 있는 그 아가씨가 새침을 떠는 바보라는 게 당신의 신중한 견해라고 말이오?\""]], [["“Not exactly so.", "\"꼭 그런 건 아닙니다."], ["I mean to tell you, Mr.", "제가 당신께 말하려는 것은, 스트라이버 씨,\""], ["Stryver,” said Mr.", "로리 씨가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Lorry, reddening, “that I will hear no disrespectful word of that young lady from any lips; and that if I knew any man--which I hope I do not--whose taste was so coarse, and whose temper was so overbearing, that he could not restrain himself from speaking disrespectfully of that young lady at this desk, not even Tellson’s should prevent my giving him a piece of my mind.”", "\"그 어떤 입에서 나온 것이든 그 아가씨에 대한 불경한 말은 한마디도 듣지 않겠다는 것입니다. 그리고 만일 제가 어떤 사람을—그런 자를 안다고는 생각하고 싶지 않습니다만—취향이 그토록 천하고 성정이 그토록 오만하여, 이 책상 앞에서 그 아가씨를 두고 불경하게 말하는 것을 스스로 자제하지 못하는 자를 안다면, 텔슨 은행이라 해도 제가 그자에게 한 소리 해주는 것을 막지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["The necessity of being angry in a suppressed tone had put Mr. Stryver’s blood-vessels into a dangerous state when it was his turn to be angry; Mr. Lorry’s veins, methodical as their courses could usually be, were in no better state now it was his turn.", "화를 내야 할 차례가 되었을 때 억눌린 어조로 화를 내야 한다는 필요는 스트라이버 씨의 혈관을 위태로운 지경에 몰아넣었었는데, 평소에는 더없이 규칙적으로 흐를 수 있는 로리 씨의 혈관도 이제 그의 차례가 되자 그보다 나을 게 없는 상태가 되었다."]], [["“That is what I mean to tell you, sir,”", "\"그것이 제가 당신께 말하려던 바입니다, 선생,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Pray let there be no mistake about it.”", "\"부디 이 점에 오해가 없기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr. Stryver sucked the end of a ruler for a little while, and then stood hitting a tune out of his teeth with it, which probably gave him the toothache.", "스트라이버 씨는 잠시 자 끝을 빨다가, 이번에는 그걸로 이를 두드려 곡조를 뽑아내며 서 있었는데, 아마 그 바람에 이가 시큰거렸을 것이다."], ["He broke the awkward silence by saying:", "그가 어색한 침묵을 깨고 말했다."]], [["“This is something new to me, Mr. Lorry.", "\"이건 내게 처음 듣는 소리요, 로리 씨."], ["You deliberately advise me not to go up to Soho and offer myself--myself, Stryver of the King’s Bench bar?”", "당신은 신중히 생각한 끝에, 내게 소호로 올라가 나 자신을—이 나, 왕좌부 법정의 스트라이버를—내놓지 말라고 충고하는 거요?\""]], [["“Do you ask me for my advice, Mr. Stryver?”", "\"제 충고를 구하시는 겁니까, 스트라이버 씨?\""]], [["“Yes, I do.”", "\"그렇소, 구하는 거요.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"좋습니다."], ["Then I give it, and you have repeated it correctly.”", "그러면 충고를 드리는 거고, 당신은 그걸 정확히 되풀이하신 겁니다.\""]], [["“And all I can say of it is,”", "\"그리고 그에 대해 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤,\""], ["laughed Stryver with a vexed laugh, “that this--ha, ha!--beats everything past, present, and to come.”", "스트라이버가 분한 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"이것이—하, 하!—과거와 현재와 미래의 모든 걸 능가한다는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“Now understand me,”", "\"자, 제 말을 이해해주십시오,\""], ["pursued Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["“As a man of business, I am not justified in saying anything about this matter, for, as a man of business, I know nothing of it.", "\"업무의 사나이로서 저는 이 문제에 대해 무어라 말할 자격이 없습니다. 업무의 사나이로서 저는 이 일에 대해 아무것도 모르니까요."], ["But, as an old fellow, who has carried Miss Manette in his arms, who is the trusted friend of Miss Manette and of her father too, and who has a great affection for them both, I have spoken.", "그렇지만 마네뜨 양을 두 팔에 안아 키웠고, 마네뜨 양과 그 부친 양쪽 모두의 신임받는 친구이며, 두 사람을 깊이 아끼는 늙은이로서, 저는 이렇게 말한 겁니다. 이 비밀스러운 이야기는 제가 청한 것이 아님을 기억해주십시오."], ["The confidence is not of my seeking, recollect. Now, you think I may not be right?”", "자, 당신은 제 말이 옳지 않을지도 모른다고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Not I!”", "\"천만에요!\""], ["said Stryver, whistling.", "스트라이버가 휘파람을 불며 말했다."], ["“I can’t undertake to find third parties in common sense; I can only find it for myself.", "\"나로서는 다른 사람들에게서 상식을 찾아낼 재간이 없소. 나 자신에게서나 찾을 수 있을 뿐이지."], ["I suppose sense in certain quarters; you suppose mincing bread-and-butter nonsense.", "나는 어떤 부류에게 분별이 있으리라 짐작하는데, 당신은 새침떠는 빵과 버터 같은 헛소리를 짐작하는군."], ["It’s new to me, but you are right, I dare say.”", "내게는 처음 듣는 얘기지만, 당신 말이 옳을 거요, 아마도.\""]], [["“What I suppose, Mr. Stryver, I claim to characterise for myself--And understand me, sir,”", "\"제가 무얼 짐작하는지는, 스트라이버 씨, 저 스스로 규정하겠습니다—그리고 제 말을 이해해주십시오, 선생,\""], ["said Mr.", "로리 씨가 다시 얼른 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Lorry, quickly flushing again, “I will not--not even at Tellson’s--have it characterised for me by any gentleman breathing.”", "\"저는 그것을—텔슨 은행에서라 해도—숨 쉬는 그 어떤 신사가 저 대신 규정하도록 두지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“There!", "\"이런!"], ["I beg your pardon!”", "미안하오!\""], ["said Stryver.", "스트라이버가 말했다."]], [["“Granted. Thank you.", "\"받아들이겠습니다."], ["Well, Mr.", "고맙습니다."], ["Stryver, I was about to say:--it might be painful to you to find yourself mistaken, it might be painful to Doctor Manette to have the task of being explicit with you, it might be very painful to Miss Manette to have the task of being explicit with you.", "자, 스트라이버 씨, 제가 말하려던 것은 이겁니다—당신이 잘못 짚었음을 알게 되는 것은 당신께 고통스러울 수 있고, 마네뜨 박사가 당신께 분명히 밝혀야 하는 임무를 떠맡는 것은 박사께 고통스러울 수 있으며, 마네뜨 양이 당신께 분명히 밝혀야 하는 임무를 떠맡는 것은 마네뜨 양에게 매우 고통스러울 수 있습니다."], ["You know the terms upon which I have the honour and happiness to stand with the family.", "제가 그 가족과 어떤 사이로 영광스럽고 행복하게 지내는지 당신도 아실 겁니다."], ["If you please, committing you in no way, representing you in no way, I will undertake to correct my advice by the exercise of a little new observation and judgment expressly brought to bear upon it.", "괜찮으시다면, 당신을 어떤 식으로도 연루시키지 않고, 어떤 식으로도 대변하지 않으면서, 제가 이 문제에 일부러 적용한 약간의 새로운 관찰과 판단을 통해 제 충고를 바로잡는 일을 떠맡겠습니다."], ["If you should then be dissatisfied with it, you can but test its soundness for yourself; if, on the other hand, you should be satisfied with it, and it should be what it now is, it may spare all sides what is best spared.", "만일 그러고도 당신이 그 충고에 불만이라면, 당신 스스로 그 타당성을 시험해보실 수밖에 없겠지요. 반대로 당신이 그 충고에 만족한다면, 그리고 그것이 지금과 같은 내용이라면, 모두에게 가장 아끼는 게 좋은 것을 아낄 수 있을 겁니다."], ["What do you say?”", "어떠십니까?\""]], [["“How long would you keep me in town?”", "\"나를 얼마나 오래 도시에 붙들어두려는 거요?\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["It is only a question of a few hours.", "그저 몇 시간의 문제일 뿐입니다."], ["I could go to Soho in the evening, and come to your chambers afterwards.”", "제가 저녁에 소호로 가서, 그 뒤에 당신 사무실로 들르면 됩니다.\""]], [["“Then I say yes,”", "\"그럼 좋다고 하겠소,\" 스트라이버가 말했다."], ["said Stryver: “I won’t go up there now, I am not so hot upon it as that comes to; I say yes, and I shall expect you to look in to-night.", "\"지금 거기 올라가지 않겠소. 그렇게 안달이 난 것도 아니니까."], ["Good morning.”", "좋다고 하겠고, 당신이 오늘밤 들러주기를 기대하겠소. 안녕히 가시오.\""]], [["Then Mr. Stryver turned and burst out of the Bank, causing such a concussion of air on his passage through, that to stand up against it bowing behind the two counters, required the utmost remaining strength of the two ancient clerks.", "그러고 나서 스트라이버 씨는 돌아서서 은행을 박차고 나갔는데, 그가 지나가며 일으킨 공기의 충격이 어찌나 컸던지, 두 카운터 뒤에서 허리를 굽힌 채 그 바람에 맞서 버티려면 두 늙은 직원에게 남아 있는 온 힘이 다 들 정도였다."], ["Those venerable and feeble persons were always seen by the public in the act of bowing, and were popularly believed, when they had bowed a customer out, still to keep on bowing in the empty office until they bowed another customer in.", "그 점잖고 노쇠한 사람들은 손님들 눈에 늘 허리를 굽히는 모습으로만 보였고, 한 손님을 굽실거리며 배웅하고 나면 또 다른 손님을 굽실거리며 맞아들일 때까지 텅 빈 사무실에서도 줄곧 허리를 굽히고 있으리라고 사람들은 으레 믿었다."]], [["The barrister was keen enough to divine that the banker would not have gone so far in his expression of opinion on any less solid ground than moral certainty.", "그 법정 변호사는, 은행가가 도덕적 확신만큼 견실한 근거가 아니고서야 그토록 멀리까지 자기 의견을 표명하지는 않았으리라는 것을 꿰뚫어볼 만큼 예리했다."], ["Unprepared as he was for the large pill he had to swallow, he got it down.", "삼켜야 할 그 큰 알약에 미처 대비하지 못했으면서도, 그는 그것을 넘겨버렸다."], ["“And now,” said Mr.", "\"자, 이제,\""], ["Stryver, shaking his forensic forefinger at the Temple in general, when it was down, “my way out of this, is, to put you all in the wrong.”", "그것을 넘기고 나자 스트라이버 씨가 법정에서처럼 집게손가락을 템플 전체를 향해 흔들며 말했다. \"여기서 빠져나갈 내 방도는, 너희 모두를 잘못한 쪽으로 만드는 것이다.\""]], [["It was a bit of the art of an Old Bailey tactician, in which he found great relief.", "그것은 올드 베일리 전술가의 기술 한 토막이었으니, 그는 거기서 큰 위안을 얻었다."], ["“You shall not put me in the wrong, young lady,” said Mr.", "\"당신이 나를 잘못한 쪽으로 만들지는 못할 거요, 아가씨,\""], ["Stryver; “I’ll do that for you.”", "스트라이버 씨가 말했다. \"그건 당신을 위해 내가 해드리지.\""]], [["Accordingly, when Mr. Lorry called that night as late as ten o’clock, Mr. Stryver, among a quantity of books and papers littered out for the purpose, seemed to have nothing less on his mind than the subject of the morning. He even showed surprise when he saw Mr. Lorry, and was altogether in an absent and preoccupied state.", "그리하여 그날 밤 로리 씨가 열 시나 되어 늦게 찾아왔을 때, 스트라이버 씨는 일부러 늘어놓은 잔뜩 쌓인 책과 서류에 둘러싸인 채, 그 아침의 화제 따위는 도무지 안중에도 없는 것처럼 보였다. 그는 로리 씨를 보고는 놀라는 기색마저 보였고, 전체적으로 멍하니 딴 데 정신이 팔린 상태였다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["said that good-natured emissary, after a full half-hour of bootless attempts to bring him round to the question.", "그 마음씨 좋은 사절이, 그를 그 문제로 다시 끌어들이려는 부질없는 시도로 꼬박 반시간을 보낸 뒤에 말했다."], ["“I have been to Soho.”", "\"제가 소호에 다녀왔습니다.\""]], [["“To Soho?”", "\"소호에요?\""], ["repeated Mr. Stryver, coldly.", "스트라이버 씨가 차갑게 되뇌었다."], ["“Oh, to be sure!", "\"오, 그렇지!"], ["What am I thinking of!”", "내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거람!\""]], [["“And I have no doubt,” said Mr.", "\"그리고 저는 추호도 의심하지 않습니다,\""], ["Lorry, “that I was right in the conversation we had.", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"우리가 나눈 대화에서 제가 옳았다는 것을요."], ["My opinion is confirmed, and I reiterate my advice.”", "제 의견은 확인되었고, 저는 제 충고를 거듭 말씀드립니다.\""]], [["“I assure you,” returned Mr.", "\"분명히 말하건대,\""], ["Stryver, in the friendliest way, “that I am sorry for it on your account, and sorry for it on the poor father’s account.", "스트라이버 씨가 더없이 다정한 투로 대꾸했다. \"당신을 봐서도 그 일이 안됐고, 그 가엾은 부친을 봐서도 안됐소."], ["I know this must always be a sore subject with the family; let us say no more about it.”", "나도 이게 그 가족에게는 늘 아픈 화제일 수밖에 없다는 걸 아오. 더는 그 얘기를 하지 맙시다.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I dare say not,”", "\"모르실 테지요,\""], ["rejoined Stryver, nodding his head in a smoothing and final way; “no matter, no matter.”", "스트라이버가 무마하듯, 결론을 짓듯 고개를 끄덕이며 받았다. \"괜찮소, 괜찮아요.\""]], [["“But it does matter,”", "\"하지만 괜찮지 않습니다.\""], ["Mr. Lorry urged.", "로리 씨가 다그쳤다."]], [["“No it doesn’t; I assure you it doesn’t.", "\"아니, 괜찮소. 분명히 말하건대 괜찮소."], ["Having supposed that there was sense where there is no sense, and a laudable ambition where there is not a laudable ambition, I am well out of my mistake, and no harm is done.", "분별이 없는 데 분별이 있으리라, 칭찬할 만한 야심이 없는 데 칭찬할 만한 야심이 있으리라 짐작했던 것인데, 이제 그 착각에서 깨끗이 벗어났으니, 아무 해도 없는 셈이오. 젊은 여자들은 전에도 흔히 그와 비슷한 어리석은 짓을 저질렀고, 전에도 흔히 가난과 무명 속에서 그것을 뉘우쳤소."], ["Young women have committed similar follies often before, and have repented them in poverty and obscurity often before.", "사심 없는 관점에서 보자면, 이 일이 그만둬진 것이 안타깝소. 세속적인 관점에서 내게 나쁜 일이었을 테니까."], ["In an unselfish aspect, I am sorry that the thing is dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view; in a selfish aspect, I am glad that the thing has dropped, because it would have been a bad thing for me in a worldly point of view--it is hardly necessary to say I could have gained nothing by it.", "사심 있는 관점에서 보자면, 이 일이 그만둬진 것이 기쁘오. 세속적인 관점에서 내게 나쁜 일이었을 테니까—내가 그걸로 아무것도 얻을 게 없었으리라는 건 굳이 말할 필요도 없겠지."], ["There is no harm at all done.", "아무런 해도 없소."], ["I have not proposed to the young lady, and, between ourselves, I am by no means certain, on reflection, that I ever should have committed myself to that extent. Mr.", "나는 그 아가씨에게 청혼하지 않았고, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 곰곰이 따져보면, 내가 과연 그 지경까지 나 자신을 옭아맸을지 결코 확신할 수 없소."], ["Lorry, you cannot control the mincing vanities and giddinesses of empty-headed girls; you must not expect to do it, or you will always be disappointed.", "로리 씨, 머리가 텅 빈 처녀들의 새침떠는 허영과 들뜸을 당신은 어쩌지 못하오. 그걸 어쩌리라 기대해서도 안 되오."], ["Now, pray say no more about it.", "그러면 늘 실망만 할 테니까. 자, 부디 더는 그 얘기를 하지 맙시다."], ["I tell you, I regret it on account of others, but I am satisfied on my own account.", "다시 말하지만, 나는 남들을 봐서는 그게 안타깝지만, 나 자신을 봐서는 만족스럽소. 그리고 내가 당신을 떠볼 수 있게 해주고, 충고를 해준 것에 대해 정말 대단히 고맙게 생각하오."], ["And I am really very much obliged to you for allowing me to sound you, and for giving me your advice; you know the young lady better than I do; you were right, it never would have done.”", "당신은 나보다 그 아가씨를 더 잘 아니, 당신 말이 옳았소. 절대 될 일이 아니었던 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry was so taken aback, that he looked quite stupidly at Mr.", "로리 씨는 어찌나 어안이 벙벙했던지, 스트라이버가 자신의 그릇된 머리 위로 관대함과 아량과 호의를 마구 뿌려대는 시늉을 하며 그를 문 쪽으로 떠미는 것을 그저 멍하니 바라볼 뿐이었다."], ["Stryver shouldering him towards the door, with an appearance of showering generosity, forbearance, and goodwill, on his erring head.", "\"마음 편히 받아들이시오, 친애하는 선생,\" 스트라이버가 말했다."], ["“Make the best of it, my dear sir,”", "\"더는 그 얘기를 마시오."], ["said Stryver; “say no more about it; thank you again for allowing me to sound you; good night!”", "내가 당신을 떠볼 수 있게 해준 것에 대해 다시 한번 고맙소. 안녕히 가시오!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry was out in the night, before he knew where he was.", "로리 씨는 자기가 어디 있는지 미처 깨닫기도 전에 밤거리로 나와 있었다."], ["Mr. Stryver was lying back on his sofa, winking at his ceiling.", "스트라이버 씨는 소파에 등을 기댄 채, 천장을 향해 눈을 끔벅이고 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. The Fellow of No Delicacy", "# CHAPTER XIII. The Fellow of No Delicacy"]], [["If Sydney Carton ever shone anywhere, he certainly never shone in the house of Doctor Manette.", "시드니 카턴이 어디선가 빛난 적이 있었다 해도, 마네뜨 박사의 집에서만큼은 결코 빛난 적이 없었다."], ["He had been there often, during a whole year, and had always been the same moody and morose lounger there.", "그는 꼬박 한 해 동안 그곳을 자주 드나들었지만, 언제나 변함없이 침울하고 무뚝뚝한 빈둥거리는 자였다."], ["When he cared to talk, he talked well; but, the cloud of caring for nothing, which overshadowed him with such a fatal darkness, was very rarely pierced by the light within him.", "마음 내켜 말할 때면 말솜씨가 좋았으나, 무엇에도 마음 쓰지 않는 그 구름은 치명적인 어둠으로 그를 뒤덮어, 그의 내면의 빛이 그것을 뚫고 나오는 일은 좀처럼 없었다."]], [["And yet he did care something for the streets that environed that house, and for the senseless stones that made their pavements.", "그래도 그는 그 집을 둘러싼 거리들에, 그리고 그 포장도로를 이룬 무심한 돌들에 얼마간 마음을 두었다."], ["Many a night he vaguely and unhappily wandered there, when wine had brought no transitory gladness to him; many a dreary daybreak revealed his solitary figure lingering there, and still lingering there when the first beams of the sun brought into strong relief, removed beauties of architecture in spires of churches and lofty buildings, as perhaps the quiet time brought some sense of better things, else forgotten and unattainable, into his mind.", "술이 그에게 덧없는 즐거움조차 가져다주지 못한 숱한 밤, 그는 막연하고 불행한 심정으로 그곳을 떠돌았다. 음울한 여명이 그 홀로 선 모습을 그곳에서 서성이며 드러낸 날도 여러 날이었으니, 첫 햇살이 교회 첨탑과 우뚝 솟은 건물들의 사라졌던 건축미를 또렷이 돋보이게 할 때에도 그는 여전히 그곳에 머물러 있었고, 어쩌면 그 고요한 시각이 다른 때라면 잊히고 도달할 수 없는 더 나은 것들에 대한 어떤 감각을 그의 마음속으로 불러들였는지도 모른다."], ["Of late, the neglected bed in the Temple Court had known him more scantily than ever; and often when he had thrown himself upon it no longer than a few minutes, he had got up again, and haunted that neighbourhood.", "근래 들어 템플 코트의 방치된 침대는 그 어느 때보다 그를 드물게 맞았으니, 그가 거기에 몸을 던지고 몇 분도 채 지나지 않아 다시 일어나 그 동네를 배회하는 일이 잦았다."]], [["On a day in August, when Mr. Stryver (after notifying to his jackal that “he had thought better of that marrying matter”) had carried his delicacy into Devonshire, and when the sight and scent of flowers in the City streets had some waifs of goodness in them for the worst, of health for the sickliest, and of youth for the oldest, Sydney’s feet still trod those stones.", "8월의 어느 날, 스트라이버 씨가 (자기 자칼에게 \"그 결혼 문제는 다시 생각해보니 그만두기로 했네\"라고 통보한 뒤) 자신의 섬세함을 데번셔로 실어 가버렸을 때, 그리고 도시의 거리에 핀 꽃들의 모습과 향기가 가장 못된 자에게는 한 가닥 선함을, 가장 병약한 자에게는 한 가닥 건강함을, 가장 늙은 자에게는 한 가닥 젊음을 떠도는 부스러기로나마 품고 있을 무렵, 시드니의 발은 여전히 그 돌들을 밟고 있었다."], ["From being irresolute and purposeless, his feet became animated by an intention, and, in the working out of that intention, they took him to the Doctor’s door.", "우유부단하고 목적 없던 그의 발은 어떤 의도로 활기를 띠게 되었고, 그 의도를 이행하는 과정에서 그를 박사의 문 앞으로 데려갔다."]], [["He was shown up-stairs, and found Lucie at her work, alone.", "그는 위층으로 안내되었고, 루시가 혼자 일감을 붙들고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She had never been quite at her ease with him, and received him with some little embarrassment as he seated himself near her table.", "그녀는 그와 함께 있을 때 한 번도 완전히 편안한 적이 없었기에, 그가 그녀의 탁자 가까이에 자리를 잡고 앉자 약간의 어색함을 띠며 그를 맞이했다."], ["But, looking up at his face in the interchange of the first few common-places, she observed a change in it.", "그러나 처음 몇 마디 의례적인 인사를 주고받으며 그의 얼굴을 올려다본 그녀는 거기에 변화가 일어난 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I fear you are not well, Mr. Carton!”", "\"몸이 편찮으신 게 아닌가 걱정스럽네요, 카턴 씨!\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닙니다."], ["But the life I lead, Miss Manette, is not conducive to health.", "하지만 제가 사는 삶이란 게, 마네뜨 양, 건강에 이로울 게 없지요."], ["What is to be expected of, or by, such profligates?”", "저 같은 방탕한 자에게서, 혹은 그런 자에게 무엇을 기대할 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Is it not--forgive me; I have begun the question on my lips--a pity to live no better life?”", "\"더 나은 삶을 살지 못하는 것이―용서하세요, 입에 담은 김에 끝까지 묻겠어요―안타까운 일이 아닐까요?\""]], [["“God knows it is a shame!”", "\"하느님은 그것이 부끄러운 일임을 아십니다!\""]], [["“Then why not change it?”", "\"그렇다면 어째서 바꾸지 않으세요?\""]], [["Looking gently at him again, she was surprised and saddened to see that there were tears in his eyes.", "다시 그를 부드럽게 바라보던 그녀는 그의 눈에 눈물이 고여 있는 것을 보고 놀라며 마음 아파했다."], ["There were tears in his voice too, as he answered:", "그가 대답할 때 그의 목소리에도 눈물이 어려 있었다."]], [["“It is too late for that.", "\"그러기엔 너무 늦었습니다."], ["I shall never be better than I am.", "저는 지금의 저보다 결코 나아지지 못할 겁니다."], ["I shall sink lower, and be worse.”", "저는 더 낮은 데로 가라앉고, 더 나빠질 겁니다.\""]], [["He leaned an elbow on her table, and covered his eyes with his hand.", "그는 그녀의 탁자에 한쪽 팔꿈치를 괴고 손으로 두 눈을 가렸다."], ["The table trembled in the silence that followed.", "뒤이은 침묵 속에서 탁자가 떨렸다."]], [["She had never seen him softened, and was much distressed.", "그녀는 그가 누그러진 모습을 한 번도 본 적이 없었기에 몹시 괴로웠다."], ["He knew her to be so, without looking at her, and said:", "그는 그녀를 바라보지 않고도 그녀가 그러함을 알고서 말했다."]], [["“Pray forgive me, Miss Manette.", "\"부디 용서해주십시오, 마네뜨 양."], ["I break down before the knowledge of what I want to say to you.", "당신에게 하고 싶은 말을 떠올리니 무너지고 맙니다."], ["Will you hear me?”", "제 말을 들어주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“If it will do you any good, Mr. Carton, if it would make you happier, it would make me very glad!”", "\"그것이 당신에게 조금이라도 도움이 된다면, 카턴 씨, 그것이 당신을 더 행복하게 한다면, 저는 무척 기쁠 거예요!\""]], [["“God bless you for your sweet compassion!”", "\"당신의 그 다정한 연민에 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["He unshaded his face after a little while, and spoke steadily.", "그는 잠시 후 얼굴을 가린 손을 거두고 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid to hear me.", "\"제 말을 듣기를 두려워하지 마십시오."], ["Don’t shrink from anything I say.", "제가 하는 어떤 말에도 움츠러들지 마십시오."], ["I am like one who died young.", "저는 젊어서 죽은 사람과 같습니다."], ["All my life might have been.”", "제 삶 전부가 그러했을 수도 있었지요.\""]], [["“No, Mr. Carton.", "\"아니에요, 카턴 씨."], ["I am sure that the best part of it might still be; I am sure that you might be much, much worthier of yourself.”", "저는 그중 가장 좋은 부분이 아직 가능하다고 확신해요. 당신이 스스로에게 훨씬, 훨씬 더 떳떳한 사람이 될 수 있다고 확신해요.\""]], [["“Say of you, Miss Manette, and although I know better--although in the mystery of my own wretched heart I know better--I shall never forget it!”", "\"당신에게 떳떳한 사람이라고 말씀하시지요, 마네뜨 양, 그리고 저는 그렇지 않음을 더 잘 알지만―제 비참한 가슴속 깊은 곳에서 더 잘 알지만―저는 결코 그 말을 잊지 못할 겁니다!\""]], [["She was pale and trembling.", "그녀는 창백해진 채 떨고 있었다."], ["He came to her relief with a fixed despair of himself which made the interview unlike any other that could have been holden.", "그는 스스로에 대한 굳어진 절망을 품고 그녀를 구하러 나섰으니, 그것이 이 만남을 다른 어떤 만남과도 다른 것으로 만들었다."]], [["“If it had been possible, Miss Manette, that you could have returned the love of the man you see before yourself--flung away, wasted, drunken, poor creature of misuse as you know him to be--he would have been conscious this day and hour, in spite of his happiness, that he would bring you to misery, bring you to sorrow and repentance, blight you, disgrace you, pull you down with him. I know very well that you can have no tenderness for me; I ask for none; I am even thankful that it cannot be.”", "\"만약, 마네뜨 양, 당신이 지금 당신 앞에 있는 이 사내의 사랑에―당신이 알다시피 내던져지고, 허비되고, 술에 절고, 잘못 쓰인 가련한 존재인 이 사내의 사랑에―보답할 수 있는 일이 가능했다 하더라도, 그는 자신의 행복에도 불구하고 바로 오늘 이 순간에 알고 있었을 겁니다. 자신이 당신을 불행으로, 슬픔과 후회로 이끌고, 당신을 시들게 하고, 당신을 욕되게 하고, 당신을 자신과 함께 끌어내리리라는 것을 말입니다. 당신이 제게 어떤 애틋함도 품을 수 없음을 저는 아주 잘 압니다. 저는 그것을 청하지도 않습니다. 그것이 가능하지 않다는 사실에 저는 오히려 감사할 따름입니다.\""]], [["“Without it, can I not save you, Mr. Carton?", "\"그것 없이는, 제가 당신을 구할 수 없을까요, 카턴 씨?"], ["Can I not recall you--forgive me again!--to a better course?", "제가 당신을―다시 한번 용서하세요!―더 나은 길로 불러낼 수 없을까요?"], ["Can I in no way repay your confidence?", "제가 당신의 신뢰에 어떻게도 보답할 수 없을까요?"], ["I know this is a confidence,”", "저는 이것이 신뢰임을 알아요.\""], ["she modestly said, after a little hesitation, and in earnest tears, “I know you would say this to no one else.", "그녀는 잠시 머뭇거린 끝에, 진심에서 우러난 눈물을 흘리며 겸손하게 말했다. \"당신이 이런 말을 다른 누구에게도 하지 않으리라는 것을 알아요."], ["Can I turn it to no good account for yourself, Mr. Carton?”", "제가 이것을 당신을 위한 어떤 좋은 결실로 바꿀 수 없을까요, 카턴 씨?\""]], [["He shook his head.", "그는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“To none. No, Miss Manette, to none.", "\"아무것으로도 바꿀 수 없습니다."], ["If you will hear me through a very little more, all you can ever do for me is done.", "아니요, 마네뜨 양, 아무것으로도. 조금만 더 제 말을 끝까지 들어주신다면, 당신이 저를 위해 해줄 수 있는 모든 일은 이미 다 한 셈이 됩니다."], ["I wish you to know that you have been the last dream of my soul.", "저는 당신이 제 영혼의 마지막 꿈이었음을 알아주시기를 바랍니다."], ["In my degradation I have not been so degraded but that the sight of you with your father, and of this home made such a home by you, has stirred old shadows that I thought had died out of me.", "제 타락 속에서도 저는, 당신의 아버지와 함께 있는 당신의 모습과 당신이 그토록 가정답게 만든 이 가정을 보고서, 제게서 죽어 사라졌다고 여겼던 옛 그림자들이 일렁였을 만큼은 완전히 타락하지는 않았습니다."], ["Since I knew you, I have been troubled by a remorse that I thought would never reproach me again, and have heard whispers from old voices impelling me upward, that I thought were silent for ever.", "당신을 안 뒤로 저는, 다시는 저를 책망하지 않으리라 여겼던 회한에 시달렸고, 영원히 잠잠해졌다고 여겼던 옛 목소리들이 저를 위로 떠밀며 속삭이는 것을 들었습니다."], ["I have had unformed ideas of striving afresh, beginning anew, shaking off sloth and sensuality, and fighting out the abandoned fight.", "저는 새로이 분발하고, 다시 시작하고, 나태와 향락을 떨쳐버리고, 내던졌던 싸움을 끝까지 싸워보겠다는 어렴풋한 생각들을 품었습니다."], ["A dream, all a dream, that ends in nothing, and leaves the sleeper where he lay down, but I wish you to know that you inspired it.”", "한낱 꿈, 모든 것이 꿈이었으니, 아무것에도 이르지 못한 채 끝나고 잠든 자를 누웠던 그 자리에 그대로 남겨둘 따름이지만, 저는 당신이 그 꿈을 불러일으켰음을 알아주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["“Will nothing of it remain?", "\"그중 아무것도 남지 않을까요?"], ["O Mr. Carton, think again!", "오, 카턴 씨, 다시 생각해보세요!"], ["Try again!”", "다시 한번 해보세요!\""]], [["“No, Miss Manette; all through it, I have known myself to be quite undeserving.", "\"아니요, 마네뜨 양. 그 모든 과정에서 저는 제 자신이 전혀 자격이 없음을 알았습니다."], ["And yet I have had the weakness, and have still the weakness, to wish you to know with what a sudden mastery you kindled me, heap of ashes that I am, into fire--a fire, however, inseparable in its nature from myself, quickening nothing, lighting nothing, doing no service, idly burning away.”", "그런데도 저는, 잿더미에 불과한 제가 당신으로 인해 얼마나 갑작스러운 위세로 불붙었는지를 당신에게 알리고 싶은 약함을 품었고, 지금도 그 약함을 품고 있습니다. 비록 그 불은 그 본성상 저 자신과 떼어놓을 수 없어, 아무것도 살리지 못하고, 아무것도 밝히지 못하며, 아무 쓸모도 없이 헛되이 타들어갈 뿐이지만 말입니다.\""]], [["“Since it is my misfortune, Mr. Carton, to have made you more unhappy than you were before you knew me--”", "\"제가 당신을 알기 전보다 당신을 더 불행하게 만든 것이 제 불운이라면, 카턴 씨―\""]], [["“Don’t say that, Miss Manette, for you would have reclaimed me, if anything could.", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 마네뜨 양. 무언가가 저를 되살릴 수 있었다면 그것은 바로 당신이었을 테니까요."], ["You will not be the cause of my becoming worse.”", "당신은 제가 더 나빠지는 원인이 되지 않을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Since the state of your mind that you describe, is, at all events, attributable to some influence of mine--this is what I mean, if I can make it plain--can I use no influence to serve you?", "\"당신이 말씀하시는 그 마음의 상태가, 어쨌든, 저의 어떤 영향에서 비롯된 것이라면―제 말을 분명히 할 수 있다면, 제 뜻은 이런 거예요―제가 당신을 위해 그 영향을 쓸 수는 없을까요?"], ["Have I no power for good, with you, at all?”", "제게는 당신에게 좋은 일을 할 힘이 조금도 없는 걸까요?\""]], [["“The utmost good that I am capable of now, Miss Manette, I have come here to realise.", "\"제가 지금 할 수 있는 가장 좋은 일을, 마네뜨 양, 저는 이루기 위해 이곳에 왔습니다."], ["Let me carry through the rest of my misdirected life, the remembrance that I opened my heart to you, last of all the world; and that there was something left in me at this time which you could deplore and pity.”", "잘못 이끌어온 제 남은 삶 동안, 제가 온 세상에서 가장 마지막으로 당신에게 마음을 열었다는 것, 그리고 이때의 제 안에 당신이 애석해하고 가엾이 여길 만한 무언가가 남아 있었다는 기억을 간직하게 해주십시오.\""]], [["“Which I entreated you to believe, again and again, most fervently, with all my heart, was capable of better things, Mr. Carton!”", "\"제가 거듭거듭, 더없이 간절하게, 온 마음을 다해 더 나은 것을 이룰 수 있다고 믿어달라 애원했던 그 무언가 말이에요, 카턴 씨!\""]], [["“Entreat me to believe it no more, Miss Manette.", "\"더는 그것을 믿어달라 애원하지 마십시오, 마네뜨 양."], ["I have proved myself, and I know better.", "저는 제 자신을 시험해보았고, 더 잘 압니다."], ["I distress you; I draw fast to an end.", "제가 당신을 괴롭히고 있군요."], ["Will you let me believe, when I recall this day, that the last confidence of my life was reposed in your pure and innocent breast, and that it lies there alone, and will be shared by no one?”", "저는 빠르게 끝을 향해 가고 있습니다. 제가 이날을 떠올릴 때, 제 삶의 마지막 신뢰가 당신의 순수하고 결백한 가슴에 맡겨졌다는 것, 그리고 그것이 오직 그곳에만 깃들어 누구와도 나뉘지 않으리라는 것을 믿게 해주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“If that will be a consolation to you, yes.”", "\"그것이 당신에게 위안이 된다면, 그러겠어요.\""]], [["“Not even by the dearest one ever to be known to you?”", "\"앞으로 당신에게 가장 소중해질 사람과도 나누지 않으시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Mr.", "\"카턴 씨.\""], ["Carton,”", "그녀는 동요하며 잠시 멈춘 뒤 대답했다."], ["she answered, after an agitated pause, “the secret is yours, not mine; and I promise to respect it.”", "\"그 비밀은 당신의 것이지 저의 것이 아니에요. 저는 그것을 지키겠다고 약속하겠어요.\""]], [["“Thank you.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["And again, God bless you.”", "그리고 다시 한번, 하느님의 축복이 당신과 함께하기를.\""]], [["He put her hand to his lips, and moved towards the door.", "그는 그녀의 손을 자기 입술에 가져다 댄 뒤 문 쪽으로 발을 옮겼다."]], [["“Be under no apprehension, Miss Manette, of my ever resuming this conversation by so much as a passing word.", "\"걱정하지 마십시오, 마네뜨 양. 제가 이 대화를 지나가는 말 한마디로라도 다시 꺼내는 일은 결코 없을 겁니다."], ["I will never refer to it again.", "저는 두 번 다시 그것을 입에 담지 않겠습니다."], ["If I were dead, that could not be surer than it is henceforth.", "제가 죽는다 해도 이보다 더 확실할 수는 없을 만큼, 이제부터 그렇습니다."], ["In the hour of my death, I shall hold sacred the one good remembrance--and shall thank and bless you for it--that my last avowal of myself was made to you, and that my name, and faults, and miseries were gently carried in your heart.", "제가 죽는 순간에, 저는 단 하나의 좋은 기억을 신성하게 간직할 것이며―그것으로 당신에게 감사하고 당신을 축복할 것입니다―제 자신에 대한 마지막 고백이 당신에게 바쳐졌다는 것, 그리고 제 이름과 허물과 비참함이 당신의 가슴속에 다정히 품어졌다는 그 기억을 말입니다."], ["May it otherwise be light and happy!”", "그 밖의 모든 것은 가볍고 행복하기를!\""]], [["He was so unlike what he had ever shown himself to be, and it was so sad to think how much he had thrown away, and how much he every day kept down and perverted, that Lucie Manette wept mournfully for him as he stood looking back at her.", "그는 여태껏 자신이 보여온 모습과 너무도 달랐고, 그가 얼마나 많은 것을 내던져버렸는지, 그리고 날마다 얼마나 많은 것을 짓누르고 비뚤어지게 만드는지를 생각하니 너무도 서글퍼, 그가 멈춰 서서 그녀를 돌아보는 동안 루시 마네뜨는 그를 위해 애처롭게 울었다."]], [["“Be comforted!”", "\"위로받으십시오!\""], ["he said, “I am not worth such feeling, Miss Manette.", "그가 말했다. \"저는 그런 마음을 받을 가치가 없는 자입니다, 마네뜨 양."], ["An hour or two hence, and the low companions and low habits that I scorn but yield to, will render me less worth such tears as those, than any wretch who creeps along the streets.", "한두 시간 뒤면, 제가 경멸하면서도 굴복하고 마는 저급한 동료들과 저급한 습관들이 저를, 거리를 기어다니는 그 어떤 가련한 자보다도 그런 눈물을 받을 자격이 없는 자로 만들어버릴 겁니다."], ["Be comforted!", "위로받으십시오!"], ["But, within myself, I shall always be, towards you, what I am now, though outwardly I shall be what you have heretofore seen me.", "하지만 제 안에서는, 당신을 향한 저는, 겉으로 당신이 지금껏 보아온 모습 그대로일지언정, 언제나 지금의 저로 남아 있을 겁니다."], ["The last supplication but one I make to you, is, that you will believe this of me.”", "제가 당신에게 청하는 마지막에서 두 번째 간청은, 이것을 저에 대한 진실로 믿어달라는 것입니다.\""]], [["“I will, Mr. Carton.”", "\"그러겠어요, 카턴 씨.\""]], [["“My last supplication of all, is this; and with it, I will relieve you of a visitor with whom I well know you have nothing in unison, and between whom and you there is an impassable space.", "\"제 마지막 간청은 이것입니다. 그리고 이것으로, 당신과 조금도 통하는 바가 없고 당신과의 사이에 건널 수 없는 거리가 놓인 한 방문객을 당신에게서 물러나게 하겠습니다."], ["It is useless to say it, I know, but it rises out of my soul.", "부질없는 말인 줄 알지만, 이것은 제 영혼에서 우러난 것입니다."], ["For you, and for any dear to you, I would do anything.", "당신을 위해서라면, 그리고 당신에게 소중한 누구를 위해서라면, 저는 무엇이든 하겠습니다."], ["If my career were of that better kind that there was any opportunity or capacity of sacrifice in it, I would embrace any sacrifice for you and for those dear to you.", "제 삶이, 희생할 기회나 역량이 조금이라도 있는 그런 더 나은 부류였다면, 저는 당신과 당신에게 소중한 이들을 위해 어떤 희생이든 기꺼이 받아들였을 겁니다."], ["Try to hold me in your mind, at some quiet times, as ardent and sincere in this one thing.", "어느 고요한 때에, 이 한 가지 일에서만큼은 제가 뜨겁고 진실했다고 마음에 새겨주려 애써주십시오. 때가 올 겁니다."], ["The time will come, the time will not be long in coming, when new ties will be formed about you--ties that will bind you yet more tenderly and strongly to the home you so adorn--the dearest ties that will ever grace and gladden you.", "그 때가 오는 데 오래 걸리지 않을 겁니다. 당신 곁에 새로운 인연들이 맺어질 때가―당신이 그토록 빛내는 그 가정에 당신을 한층 더 다정하고 굳게 묶어줄 인연들이, 당신을 영원히 빛내고 기쁘게 할 가장 소중한 인연들이 맺어질 때가 말입니다."], ["O Miss Manette, when the little picture of a happy father’s face looks up in yours, when you see your own bright beauty springing up anew at your feet, think now and then that there is a man who would give his life, to keep a life you love beside you!”", "오, 마네뜨 양, 행복한 아버지의 얼굴을 한 작은 그림 같은 것이 당신을 올려다볼 때, 당신 자신의 환한 아름다움이 발치에서 새로이 피어나는 것을 볼 때, 때때로 떠올려주십시오. 당신이 사랑하는 한 생명을 당신 곁에 지키기 위해서라면 제 목숨을 바칠 사내가 있었다는 것을!\""]], [["He said, “Farewell!” said a last “God bless you!” and left her.", "그는 \"안녕히 계십시오!\"라고 말하고, 마지막으로 \"하느님의 축복이 함께하기를!\"이라고 말한 뒤, 그녀를 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV. The Honest Tradesman", "# CHAPTER XIV. The Honest Tradesman"]], [["To the eyes of Mr. Jeremiah Cruncher, sitting on his stool in Fleet-street with his grisly urchin beside him, a vast number and variety of objects in movement were every day presented.", "제리마이어 크런처 씨가 플리트가에서 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 곁에 그 험상궂은 사내아이를 데리고 있는 그의 눈앞에는, 움직이는 온갖 사물들이 날마다 헤아릴 수 없이 많고도 다양하게 펼쳐졌다."], ["Who could sit upon anything in Fleet-street during the busy hours of the day, and not be dazed and deafened by two immense processions, one ever tending westward with the sun, the other ever tending eastward from the sun, both ever tending to the plains beyond the range of red and purple where the sun goes down!", "한낮의 분주한 시간에 플리트가에서 무엇인가 위에 앉아 있으면서, 두 개의 거대한 행렬에 어찔하고 귀먹먹해지지 않을 수 있는 자가 어디 있겠는가. 하나는 해를 따라 끊임없이 서쪽으로 향하고, 다른 하나는 해로부터 끊임없이 동쪽으로 향하니, 둘 다 해가 지는 붉고 자줏빛인 저 산마루 너머의 평원을 향해 끝없이 흘러가는 것이었다!"]], [["With his straw in his mouth, Mr. Cruncher sat watching the two streams, like the heathen rustic who has for several centuries been on duty watching one stream--saving that Jerry had no expectation of their ever running dry.", "크런처 씨는 입에 지푸라기를 문 채, 마치 여러 세기 동안 하나의 물줄기를 지켜보는 임무를 맡아온 이교도 시골뜨기처럼 두 흐름을 바라보며 앉아 있었다 — 다만 제리는 그 흐름이 언젠가 말라붙으리라고는 결코 기대하지 않았다는 점이 달랐다."], ["Nor would it have been an expectation of a hopeful kind, since a small part of his income was derived from the pilotage of timid women (mostly of a full habit and past the middle term of life) from Tellson’s side of the tides to the opposite shore.", "그것은 희망적인 종류의 기대도 못 되었을 것이니, 그의 수입 가운데 작은 몫이 겁 많은 여인들(대개 살집이 풍만하고 중년을 넘긴 이들)을 텔슨 은행 쪽 강기슭에서 맞은편 기슭으로 안내해주는 데서 나왔기 때문이다."], ["Brief as such companionship was in every separate instance, Mr. Cruncher never failed to become so interested in the lady as to express a strong desire to have the honour of drinking her very good health.", "그런 동행은 매번 짧기 그지없었으나, 크런처 씨는 한 번도 빠짐없이 그 부인에게 깊은 관심을 보이며 그녀의 건강을 위해 한잔할 영광을 누리고 싶다는 강한 소망을 표명하곤 했다."], ["And it was from the gifts bestowed upon him towards the execution of this benevolent purpose, that he recruited his finances, as just now observed.", "그리고 바로 그 자비로운 목적을 이루기 위해 그에게 내려진 선물들로부터, 방금 말한 대로 그는 재정을 보충했던 것이다."]], [["Time was, when a poet sat upon a stool in a public place, and mused in the sight of men. Mr.", "한때는 어느 시인이 공공장소의 의자에 앉아 사람들의 눈앞에서 사색에 잠기던 시절도 있었다."], ["Cruncher, sitting on a stool in a public place, but not being a poet, mused as little as possible, and looked about him.", "크런처 씨는 공공장소의 의자에 앉아 있었으나 시인이 아니었기에 되도록 사색을 적게 하고 주위를 두리번거렸다."]], [["It fell out that he was thus engaged in a season when crowds were few, and belated women few, and when his affairs in general were so unprosperous as to awaken a strong suspicion in his breast that Mrs.", "그가 이렇게 시간을 보내던 무렵은 마침 군중도 드물고 때늦은 여인들도 드물었으며, 그의 사업 형편이 전반적으로 너무도 불황이라 그의 가슴속에 크런처 부인이 또 어떤 노골적인 방식으로 \"엎드려 빌고\""], ["Cruncher must have been “flopping” in some pointed manner, when an unusual concourse pouring down Fleet-street westward, attracted his attention.", "있었음이 틀림없다는 강한 의심을 불러일으킬 정도였는데, 바로 그때 플리트가를 따라 서쪽으로 쏟아져 내려오는 예사롭지 않은 무리가 그의 주의를 끌었다."], ["Looking that way, Mr. Cruncher made out that some kind of funeral was coming along, and that there was popular objection to this funeral, which engendered uproar.", "그쪽을 바라보던 크런처 씨는 어떤 종류의 장례 행렬이 다가오고 있으며, 그 장례에 대한 민중의 반감이 소동을 일으키고 있음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Young Jerry,”", "\"어린 제리야,\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, turning to his offspring, “it’s a buryin’.”", "크런처 씨가 자기 자식을 돌아보며 말했다, \"저건 장사 행렬이다.\""]], [["“Hooroar, father!”", "\"만세, 아버지!\""], ["cried Young Jerry.", "어린 제리가 외쳤다."]], [["The young gentleman uttered this exultant sound with mysterious significance.", "이 어린 신사는 알 수 없는 의미를 담아 환호성을 질렀다."], ["The elder gentleman took the cry so ill, that he watched his opportunity, and smote the young gentleman on the ear.", "나이 든 신사는 그 외침을 몹시 못마땅하게 여긴 나머지, 기회를 엿보다가 그 어린 신사의 귀싸대기를 후려쳤다."]], [["“What d’ye mean?", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이냐?"], ["What are you hooroaring at?", "뭣 땜에 만세를 부르는 거야?"], ["What do you want to conwey to your own father, you young Rip?", "친아비한테 대체 뭘 전하려는 게냐, 이 망나니 녀석아?"], ["This boy is a getting too many for me!”", "이놈이 나한테 너무 까불기 시작하는구나!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, surveying him.", "크런처 씨가 아이를 노려보며 말했다."], ["“Him and his hooroars!", "\"이놈하고 이놈의 만세 타령이라니!"], ["Don’t let me hear no more of you, or you shall feel some more of me.", "다시는 그런 소리 듣게 하지 마라, 안 그러면 내 맛을 더 보게 될 테니."], ["D’ye hear?”", "알아들었어?\""]], [["“I warn’t doing no harm,”", "\"난 아무 해코지도 안 했단 말예요,\""], ["Young Jerry protested, rubbing his cheek.", "어린 제리가 뺨을 문지르며 항변했다."]], [["“Drop it then,” said Mr.", "\"그럼 집어치워,\""], ["Cruncher; “I won’t have none of your no harms.", "크런처 씨가 말했다. \"난 네 해코지인지 아닌지는 알 바 아니다."], ["Get a top of that there seat, and look at the crowd.”", "저기 저 자리 위에 올라가서 군중이나 봐라.\""]], [["His son obeyed, and the crowd approached; they were bawling and hissing round a dingy hearse and dingy mourning coach, in which mourning coach there was only one mourner, dressed in the dingy trappings that were considered essential to the dignity of the position.", "그의 아들은 시키는 대로 했고, 군중이 다가왔다. 그들은 칙칙한 영구차와 칙칙한 상주 마차를 둘러싸고 야유하고 쉿쉿거리고 있었는데, 그 상주 마차 안에는 상주가 단 한 사람뿐이었고, 그는 그 자리의 품위에 필수적이라 여겨지는 칙칙한 상복 차림이었다."], ["The position appeared by no means to please him, however, with an increasing rabble surrounding the coach, deriding him, making grimaces at him, and incessantly groaning and calling out: “Yah!", "그러나 그 자리가 그에게는 도무지 마음에 들지 않는 듯했으니, 점점 불어나는 천한 무리가 마차를 둘러싼 채 그를 조롱하고 얼굴을 찡그려 보이며 끊임없이 신음하듯 소리쳐댔기 때문이다. \"야!"], ["Spies!", "첩자!"], ["Tst!", "쳇!"], ["Yaha!", "야하!"], ["Spies!”", "첩자!\""], ["with many compliments too numerous and forcible to repeat.", "게다가 다시 옮기기엔 너무 많고도 격한 온갖 찬사까지 곁들였다."]], [["Funerals had at all times a remarkable attraction for Mr.", "장례 행렬은 언제나 크런처 씨에게 각별한 매력을 지녔다."], ["Cruncher; he always pricked up his senses, and became excited, when a funeral passed Tellson’s.", "텔슨 은행 앞으로 장례가 지나갈 때면 그는 늘 감각을 곤두세우고 흥분하곤 했다."], ["Naturally, therefore, a funeral with this uncommon attendance excited him greatly, and he asked of the first man who ran against him:", "그러니 당연하게도, 이처럼 보기 드문 수행을 거느린 장례는 그를 크게 흥분시켰고, 그는 자기와 부딪힌 첫번째 사내에게 물었다."]], [["“What is it, brother?", "\"무슨 일이오, 형씨?"], ["What’s it about?”", "뭐 때문에 이러는 거요?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"나야 모르지,\""], ["said the man.", "그 사내가 말했다."], ["“Spies!", "\"첩자!"], ["Yaha!", "야하!"], ["Tst!", "쳇!"], ["Spies!”", "첩자!\""]], [["He asked another man.", "그는 또 다른 사내에게 물었다."], ["“Who is it?”", "\"누구 장례요?\""]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"나야 모르지,\""], ["returned the man, clapping his hands to his mouth nevertheless, and vociferating in a surprising heat and with the greatest ardour, “Spies!", "그 사내가 대답하면서도 두 손을 입에 모으고 놀라운 열기와 더할 나위 없는 열성으로 고래고래 소리쳤다. \"첩자!"], ["Yaha!", "야하!"], ["Tst, tst!", "쳇, 쳇!"], ["Spi--ies!”", "처어업자!\""]], [["At length, a person better informed on the merits of the case, tumbled against him, and from this person he learned that the funeral was the funeral of one Roger Cly.", "마침내 이 사건의 내막을 좀 더 잘 아는 사람이 그에게 부딪쳐왔고, 이 사람으로부터 그는 그 장례가 로저 클라이라는 자의 장례임을 알게 되었다."]], [["“Was he a spy?”", "\"그자가 첩자였소?\""], ["asked Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Old Bailey spy,”", "\"올드 베일리 첩자였지,\""], ["returned his informant.", "그 정보 제공자가 대답했다."], ["“Yaha!", "\"야하!"], ["Tst!", "쳇!"], ["Yah!", "야!"], ["Old Bailey Spi--i--ies!”", "올드 베일리 처업자!\""]], [["“Why, to be sure!”", "\"아니, 그러고 보니!\""], ["exclaimed Jerry, recalling the Trial at which he had assisted.", "제리는 자기가 입회했던 그 재판을 떠올리며 소리쳤다."], ["“I’ve seen him.", "\"그자를 본 적이 있지."], ["Dead, is he?”", "죽었단 말이오?\""]], [["“Dead as mutton,”", "\"양고기처럼 죽었지,\""], ["returned the other, “and can’t be too dead.", "상대가 대답했다. \"아무리 죽어도 모자랄 놈이야."], ["Have ’em out, there!", "끌어내라, 거기!"], ["Spies!", "첩자다!"], ["Pull ’em out, there!", "끌어내라, 거기!"], ["Spies!”", "첩자야!\""]], [["The idea was so acceptable in the prevalent absence of any idea, that the crowd caught it up with eagerness, and loudly repeating the suggestion to have ’em out, and to pull ’em out, mobbed the two vehicles so closely that they came to a stop.", "이렇다 할 어떤 생각도 통 없던 판국이라 그 발상은 어찌나 그럴듯했던지, 군중은 그것을 열렬히 받아들여 끌어내라, 끄집어내라는 제안을 큰 소리로 되풀이하면서 두 대의 마차를 어찌나 바짝 에워쌌던지 마차가 멈춰서고 말았다."], ["On the crowd’s opening the coach doors, the one mourner scuffled out by himself and was in their hands for a moment; but he was so alert, and made such good use of his time, that in another moment he was scouring away up a bye-street, after shedding his cloak, hat, long hatband, white pocket-handkerchief, and other symbolical tears.", "군중이 마차 문을 열어젖히자 단 한 사람인 상주가 혼자서 허둥지둥 기어 나왔고 잠시 그들 손아귀에 잡혔다. 하지만 그는 어찌나 날쌔고 시간을 잘 활용했던지, 다음 순간 망토와 모자와 긴 모자 띠와 흰 손수건과 그 밖의 상징적인 눈물들을 벗어 던지고 어느 옆골목으로 쏜살같이 내빼고 있었다."]], [["These, the people tore to pieces and scattered far and wide with great enjoyment, while the tradesmen hurriedly shut up their shops; for a crowd in those times stopped at nothing, and was a monster much dreaded.", "이것들을 사람들은 갈가리 찢어 신나게 사방으로 흩뿌렸고, 그 사이 상인들은 부랴부랴 가게 문을 닫았다. 그 시절의 군중은 못 할 짓이 없었고, 몹시 두려운 괴물이었기 때문이다."], ["They had already got the length of opening the hearse to take the coffin out, when some brighter genius proposed instead, its being escorted to its destination amidst general rejoicing.", "그들은 이미 관을 꺼내려고 영구차를 열어젖히던 참이었는데, 그때 좀 더 명민한 천재 하나가 그 대신 관을 목적지까지 모두의 환호 속에 호위해 가자고 제안했다."], ["Practical suggestions being much needed, this suggestion, too, was received with acclamation, and the coach was immediately filled with eight inside and a dozen out, while as many people got on the roof of the hearse as could by any exercise of ingenuity stick upon it.", "실용적인 제안이 몹시 아쉬운 터라 이 제안 역시 갈채를 받았고, 마차에는 즉시 안에 여덟 명, 밖에 열두 명이 가득 들어찼으며, 영구차 지붕 위에도 갖은 재간을 부려 매달릴 수 있는 만큼 많은 사람이 올라탔다."], ["Among the first of these volunteers was Jerry Cruncher himself, who modestly concealed his spiky head from the observation of Tellson’s, in the further corner of the mourning coach.", "이 지원자들 가운데 가장 먼저 나선 이가 바로 제리 크런처 본인이었으니, 그는 상주 마차의 가장 안쪽 구석에 자기 가시 같은 머리를 텔슨 은행의 눈에 띄지 않도록 얌전히 숨겼다."]], [["The officiating undertakers made some protest against these changes in the ceremonies; but, the river being alarmingly near, and several voices remarking on the efficacy of cold immersion in bringing refractory members of the profession to reason, the protest was faint and brief.", "장례를 집전하던 장의사들이 이렇게 의식이 바뀌는 것에 다소 항의했으나, 강이 위태로울 만큼 가까이 있었고, 또 여럿이 그 업종에서 말 안 듣는 자들을 정신 차리게 하는 데는 찬물에 담가버리는 것이 효험 있다고 떠들어대자, 그 항의는 미약하고 짧았다."], ["The remodelled procession started, with a chimney-sweep driving the hearse--advised by the regular driver, who was perched beside him, under close inspection, for the purpose--and with a pieman, also attended by his cabinet minister, driving the mourning coach.", "개편된 행렬이 출발했으니, 영구차는 굴뚝 청소부가 몰았는데 — 바로 그 목적으로 곁에 바짝 붙어 앉아 감시하는 본래 마부의 조언을 받으며 몰았다 — 상주 마차는 파이 장수가, 역시 자기 측근 대신을 거느리고 몰았다."], ["A bear-leader, a popular street character of the time, was impressed as an additional ornament, before the cavalcade had gone far down the Strand; and his bear, who was black and very mangy, gave quite an Undertaking air to that part of the procession in which he walked.", "그 시절 인기 있던 길거리 인물인 곰 부리는 사내도 추가 장식으로 징발되었으니, 행렬이 스트랜드가를 멀리 내려가기도 전의 일이었다. 그리고 그가 끌던 곰은 검고 옴이 잔뜩 올라 있었는데, 그가 걷는 행렬의 그 부분에 자못 장의사다운 분위기를 더해주었다."]], [["Thus, with beer-drinking, pipe-smoking, song-roaring, and infinite caricaturing of woe, the disorderly procession went its way, recruiting at every step, and all the shops shutting up before it.", "이렇게, 맥주를 마시고 담배를 피우고 노래를 고래고래 부르며 슬픔을 한없이 희화화하면서, 그 무질서한 행렬은 발걸음마다 사람을 불려가며 나아갔고, 그 앞에서는 모든 가게가 문을 닫았다."], ["Its destination was the old church of Saint Pancras, far off in the fields.", "행렬의 목적지는 들판 멀리 떨어진 세인트 팽크라스의 옛 교회였다."], ["It got there in course of time; insisted on pouring into the burial-ground; finally, accomplished the interment of the deceased Roger Cly in its own way, and highly to its own satisfaction.", "행렬은 때맞춰 거기 다다랐고, 묘지 안으로 몰려들어가겠다고 우겨댔으며, 마침내 고인 로저 클라이의 매장을 제 나름의 방식으로, 더없이 흡족하게 치러냈다."]], [["The dead man disposed of, and the crowd being under the necessity of providing some other entertainment for itself, another brighter genius (or perhaps the same) conceived the humour of impeaching casual passers-by, as Old Bailey spies, and wreaking vengeance on them.", "죽은 자를 처리하고 나자 군중은 스스로 또 다른 놀이를 마련해야 할 처지가 되었고, 또 다른 명민한 천재(어쩌면 같은 자였을지도)가 지나가는 행인들을 올드 베일리 첩자로 몰아세워 그들에게 복수하는 익살을 떠올렸다."], ["Chase was given to some scores of inoffensive persons who had never been near the Old Bailey in their lives, in the realisation of this fancy, and they were roughly hustled and maltreated.", "이 기발한 생각을 실현하느라 평생 올드 베일리 근처에도 가본 적 없는 무고한 사람 수십 명이 쫓기게 되었고, 거칠게 떠밀리고 학대당했다."], ["The transition to the sport of window-breaking, and thence to the plundering of public-houses, was easy and natural.", "거기서 유리창 깨기 놀이로, 다시 술집 약탈로 넘어가는 것은 쉽고도 자연스러웠다."], ["At last, after several hours, when sundry summer-houses had been pulled down, and some area-railings had been torn up, to arm the more belligerent spirits, a rumour got about that the Guards were coming.", "마침내 몇 시간이 지나, 여기저기 정자가 헐리고 좀 더 호전적인 무리를 무장시키려고 몇몇 지하실 난간이 뜯겨나간 뒤에, 근위대가 오고 있다는 소문이 퍼졌다."], ["Before this rumour, the crowd gradually melted away, and perhaps the Guards came, and perhaps they never came, and this was the usual progress of a mob.", "이 소문 앞에서 군중은 차츰 흩어졌고, 어쩌면 근위대가 왔을 수도 있고 어쩌면 끝내 오지 않았을 수도 있으니, 이것이 폭도가 으레 밟는 수순이었다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher did not assist at the closing sports, but had remained behind in the churchyard, to confer and condole with the undertakers.", "크런처 씨는 그 마무리 놀이에는 가담하지 않고 교회 묘지에 남아 장의사들과 이야기를 나누며 위로를 건넸다."], ["The place had a soothing influence on him.", "그곳은 그에게 마음을 가라앉히는 영향을 미쳤다."], ["He procured a pipe from a neighbouring public-house, and smoked it, looking in at the railings and maturely considering the spot.", "그는 이웃 술집에서 파이프를 하나 얻어 와 피우며, 난간 안을 들여다보고 그 자리를 곰곰이 살펴보았다."]], [["“Jerry,” said Mr.", "\"제리,\""], ["Cruncher, apostrophising himself in his usual way, “you see that there Cly that day, and you see with your own eyes that he was a young ’un and a straight made ’un.”", "크런처 씨가 늘 하던 식으로 자기 자신에게 말을 걸었다. \"그날 저 클라이를 봤지, 그리고 네 두 눈으로 그자가 젊은 놈이고 몸이 곧게 빠진 놈이라는 걸 봤지.\""]], [["Having smoked his pipe out, and ruminated a little longer, he turned himself about, that he might appear, before the hour of closing, on his station at Tellson’s.", "파이프를 다 피우고 좀 더 곱씹은 뒤, 그는 폐점 시간 전에 텔슨 은행의 제자리에 모습을 보이려고 발길을 돌렸다."], ["Whether his meditations on mortality had touched his liver, or whether his general health had been previously at all amiss, or whether he desired to show a little attention to an eminent man, is not so much to the purpose, as that he made a short call upon his medical adviser--a distinguished surgeon--on his way back.", "죽음에 대한 그의 명상이 간을 건드렸는지, 아니면 평소에 건강이 어딘가 좋지 않았던 것인지, 아니면 어느 저명한 인물에게 약간의 경의를 표하고 싶었던 것인지는, 그가 돌아가는 길에 자기 주치의 — 어느 저명한 외과의사 — 를 잠시 들렀다는 사실만큼 중요한 것은 아니다."]], [["Young Jerry relieved his father with dutiful interest, and reported No job in his absence.", "어린 제리는 의무에 충실한 관심으로 아버지와 교대했고, 부재중에 일거리가 없었다고 보고했다."], ["The bank closed, the ancient clerks came out, the usual watch was set, and Mr. Cruncher and his son went home to tea.", "은행이 문을 닫고, 늙은 직원들이 나오고, 늘 하던 대로 경비가 세워졌으며, 크런처 씨와 그의 아들은 차를 마시러 집으로 갔다."]], [["“Now, I tell you where it is!”", "\"자, 내가 일러두겠는데!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher to his wife, on entering.", "크런처 씨가 집에 들어서며 아내에게 말했다."], ["“If, as a honest tradesman, my wenturs goes wrong to-night, I shall make sure that you’ve been praying again me, and I shall work you for it just the same as if I seen you do it.”", "\"정직한 장사꾼인 내가 오늘 밤 일을 그르치게 되면, 난 당신이 또 나한테 맞서서 빌었다고 확신할 거고, 마치 당신이 그러는 걸 내 눈으로 본 것처럼 똑같이 당신을 손봐줄 거요.\""]], [["The dejected Mrs. Cruncher shook her head.", "풀이 죽은 크런처 부인은 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Why, you’re at it afore my face!” said Mr.", "\"아니, 내 면전에서 그 짓을 하고 있군!\""], ["Cruncher, with signs of angry apprehension.", "크런처 씨가 성난 의심의 기색을 보이며 말했다."]], [["“I am saying nothing.”", "\"난 아무 말도 안 하고 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, then; don’t meditate nothing.", "\"흥, 그럼 아무 명상도 하지 마."], ["You might as well flop as meditate.", "명상하느니 차라리 엎드려 비는 게 낫겠어."], ["You may as well go again me one way as another.", "이러나저러나 나한테 맞서기는 마찬가지지."], ["Drop it altogether.”", "아예 다 집어치워.\""]], [["“Yes, Jerry.”", "\"네, 제리.\""]], [["“Yes, Jerry,”", "\"네, 제리,\""], ["repeated Mr. Cruncher sitting down to tea.", "크런처 씨가 차를 마시려 앉으며 되뇌었다."], ["“Ah!", "\"허!"], ["It is yes, Jerry.", "그래, 네, 제리렷다."], ["That’s about it.", "바로 그거지."], ["You may say yes, Jerry.”", "네, 제리라고 말할 만하지.\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher had no particular meaning in these sulky corroborations, but made use of them, as people not unfrequently do, to express general ironical dissatisfaction.", "크런처 씨는 이 뚱한 맞장구에 무슨 특별한 의미를 둔 것이 아니라, 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 그저 막연한 반어적 불만을 토로하려고 그것을 써먹은 것이었다."]], [["“You and your yes, Jerry,” said Mr.", "\"당신하고 당신의 네, 제리라니,\""], ["Cruncher, taking a bite out of his bread-and-butter, and seeming to help it down with a large invisible oyster out of his saucer.", "크런처 씨가 버터 바른 빵을 한입 베어 물고는, 받침접시에서 커다란 보이지 않는 굴이라도 꺼내 그것을 넘기는 듯한 시늉을 하며 말했다. \"허!"], ["“Ah! I think so.", "그래, 그럴 테지."], ["I believe you.”", "내 당신을 믿어주지.\""]], [["“You are going out to-night?”", "\"오늘 밤에 나가시려고요?\""], ["asked his decent wife, when he took another bite.", "그가 또 한입 베어 물자 그의 점잖은 아내가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I am.”", "\"그래, 나갈 거요.\""]], [["“May I go with you, father?”", "\"저도 같이 가도 돼요, 아버지?\""], ["asked his son, briskly.", "그의 아들이 활기차게 물었다."]], [["“No, you mayn’t.", "\"안 된다."], ["I’m a going--as your mother knows--a fishing.", "난 가는 거다 — 네 어미도 알다시피 — 낚시하러."], ["That’s where I’m going to.", "거기로 가는 거다."], ["Going a fishing.”", "낚시하러 가는 거야.\""]], [["“Your fishing-rod gets rayther rusty; don’t it, father?”", "\"아버지 낚싯대는 좀 녹이 슬었던데요, 안 그래요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Never you mind.”", "\"네가 상관할 바 아니다.\""]], [["“Shall you bring any fish home, father?”", "\"물고기 좀 집에 가져오실 거예요, 아버지?\""]], [["“If I don’t, you’ll have short commons, to-morrow,”", "\"못 가져오면 너희는 내일 끼니가 부실할 게다,\" 그 신사가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["returned that gentleman, shaking his head; “that’s questions enough for you; I ain’t a going out, till you’ve been long abed.”", "\"질문은 그쯤이면 충분하다. 난 네가 잠자리에 든 지 한참 지나기 전엔 안 나갈 거야.\""]], [["He devoted himself during the remainder of the evening to keeping a most vigilant watch on Mrs. Cruncher, and sullenly holding her in conversation that she might be prevented from meditating any petitions to his disadvantage.", "그는 그날 저녁 남은 시간 내내 크런처 부인을 더없이 빈틈없이 감시하고, 그녀가 자기에게 불리한 어떤 청원도 명상하지 못하도록 뚱한 얼굴로 줄곧 대화에 붙들어두는 데 전념했다."], ["With this view, he urged his son to hold her in conversation also, and led the unfortunate woman a hard life by dwelling on any causes of complaint he could bring against her, rather than he would leave her for a moment to her own reflections.", "이런 속셈으로 그는 아들에게도 어미를 대화에 붙들어두라고 다그쳤고, 그 불운한 여인이 단 한순간이라도 혼자만의 생각에 빠지게 두느니, 그녀에게 들이댈 수 있는 온갖 불평거리를 늘어놓으며 그녀를 고달프게 만들었다."], ["The devoutest person could have rendered no greater homage to the efficacy of an honest prayer than he did in this distrust of his wife.", "아무리 독실한 사람이라도 정직한 기도의 효험에, 이 사내가 자기 아내를 이렇게 불신하며 바친 것보다 더 큰 경의를 바칠 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["It was as if a professed unbeliever in ghosts should be frightened by a ghost story.", "그것은 마치 유령을 믿지 않는다고 공언하던 자가 유령 이야기에 겁먹는 격이었다."]], [["“And mind you!”", "\"그리고 명심해!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 말했다."], ["“No games to-morrow!", "\"내일은 무슨 수작도 부리지 마!"], ["If I, as a honest tradesman, succeed in providing a jinte of meat or two, none of your not touching of it, and sticking to bread.", "정직한 장사꾼인 내가 고기 한두 토막을 마련하는 데 성공하면, 손도 안 대고 빵만 고집하는 짓일랑 하지 마."], ["If I, as a honest tradesman, am able to provide a little beer, none of your declaring on water.", "정직한 장사꾼인 내가 맥주를 좀 마련할 수 있게 되면, 물만 마시겠다고 선언하는 짓일랑 하지 마."], ["When you go to Rome, do as Rome does.", "로마에 가면 로마법을 따르랬어."], ["Rome will be a ugly customer to you, if you don’t.", "안 그러면 로마가 당신한테 고약한 상대가 될 거야."], ["I’m your Rome, you know.”", "내가 당신의 로마라고, 알겠어.\""]], [["Then he began grumbling again:", "그러더니 그는 다시 투덜대기 시작했다."]], [["“With your flying into the face of your own wittles and drink!", "\"제 먹을 것과 마실 것한테 대드는 꼴이라니!"], ["I don’t know how scarce you mayn’t make the wittles and drink here, by your flopping tricks and your unfeeling conduct.", "당신이 엎드려 비는 수작과 인정머리 없는 행실로, 여기 먹을 것과 마실 것을 얼마나 귀하게 만들어놓을지 난 모르겠소."], ["Look at your boy: he is your’n, ain’t he?", "당신 애를 봐. 저게 당신 애 맞지, 안 그래?"], ["He’s as thin as a lath.", "막대기처럼 비쩍 말랐잖아."], ["Do you call yourself a mother, and not know that a mother’s first duty is to blow her boy out?”", "당신이 어미라면서, 어미의 첫째 의무가 제 자식을 토실토실하게 살찌우는 거란 걸 모른단 말이야?\""]], [["This touched Young Jerry on a tender place; who adjured his mother to perform her first duty, and, whatever else she did or neglected, above all things to lay especial stress on the discharge of that maternal function so affectingly and delicately indicated by his other parent.", "이 말은 어린 제리의 아픈 데를 건드렸다. 그는 어머니에게 첫째 의무를 다하라고 간청하면서, 다른 무엇을 하든 안 하든, 무엇보다도 다른 한쪽 어버이가 그토록 가슴 뭉클하고 섬세하게 일러준 그 어미로서의 직분을 다하는 데 특별히 힘써달라고 호소했다."]], [["Thus the evening wore away with the Cruncher family, until Young Jerry was ordered to bed, and his mother, laid under similar injunctions, obeyed them. Mr.", "이렇게 크런처 가족에게 저녁이 흘러갔고, 마침내 어린 제리는 잠자리에 들라는 명을 받았으며, 그의 어머니도 비슷한 지시에 따랐다."], ["Cruncher beguiled the earlier watches of the night with solitary pipes, and did not start upon his excursion until nearly one o’clock.", "크런처 씨는 밤의 이른 시각을 홀로 피우는 파이프로 달래며 보냈고, 거의 한 시가 다 되어서야 외출에 나섰다."], ["Towards that small and ghostly hour, he rose up from his chair, took a key out of his pocket, opened a locked cupboard, and brought forth a sack, a crowbar of convenient size, a rope and chain, and other fishing tackle of that nature.", "그 작고 으스스한 시각이 다가오자, 그는 의자에서 일어나 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼내 잠긴 찬장을 열고는, 자루 하나와 적당한 크기의 쇠지렛대 하나, 밧줄과 사슬, 그리고 그런 따위의 다른 낚시 도구들을 꺼냈다."], ["Disposing these articles about him in skilful manner, he bestowed a parting defiance on Mrs. Cruncher, extinguished the light, and went out.", "이 물건들을 능숙한 솜씨로 몸에 갖춰 넣은 뒤, 그는 크런처 부인에게 작별의 으름장을 한 번 던지고는 불을 끄고 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Young Jerry, who had only made a feint of undressing when he went to bed, was not long after his father.", "잠자리에 들 때 옷을 벗는 시늉만 했던 어린 제리는 아버지 뒤를 곧바로 따라나섰다."], ["Under cover of the darkness he followed out of the room, followed down the stairs, followed down the court, followed out into the streets.", "어둠을 틈타 그는 방을 나와 따라가고, 계단을 내려가 따라가고, 안마당을 지나 따라가고, 거리로 나와 따라갔다."], ["He was in no uneasiness concerning his getting into the house again, for it was full of lodgers, and the door stood ajar all night.", "집에 다시 들어가는 일은 걱정할 것이 없었으니, 집에 하숙인이 가득해 문이 밤새 빠끔히 열려 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Impelled by a laudable ambition to study the art and mystery of his father’s honest calling, Young Jerry, keeping as close to house fronts, walls, and doorways, as his eyes were close to one another, held his honoured parent in view.", "아버지의 정직한 생업의 기술과 비밀을 배우고자 하는 갸륵한 야심에 이끌려, 어린 제리는 두 눈이 서로 바짝 붙어 있는 만큼이나 집 정면과 담벼락과 문간에 바짝 붙어, 자기의 존경하는 어버이를 시야에서 놓치지 않았다."], ["The honoured parent steering Northward, had not gone far, when he was joined by another disciple of Izaak Walton, and the two trudged on together.", "북쪽으로 방향을 잡은 그 존경하는 어버이가 얼마 가지 않았을 때, 또 다른 아이작 월턴의 제자가 그에게 합류했고, 둘은 함께 터벅터벅 걸어갔다."]], [["Within half an hour from the first starting, they were beyond the winking lamps, and the more than winking watchmen, and were out upon a lonely road.", "* 아이작 월턴: 17세기 영국의 작가로, 낚시에 관한 고전 「조어대전(釣魚大全)」의 저자. 여기서 '아이작 월턴의 제자'란 낚시꾼을 뜻하는 익살스러운 표현이다."], ["Another fisherman was picked up here--and that so silently, that if Young Jerry had been superstitious, he might have supposed the second follower of the gentle craft to have, all of a sudden, split himself into two.", "처음 출발한 지 반 시간도 안 되어, 그들은 깜박이는 가로등과 깜박임이나 다름없는 야경꾼들을 지나, 인적 없는 길로 나섰다. 여기서 또 다른 낚시꾼이 합류했는데 — 그것이 어찌나 소리 없이 이루어졌던지, 어린 제리가 미신을 믿는 아이였다면 이 점잖은 업종의 두 번째 추종자가 갑자기 둘로 쪼개졌다고 여겼을 법했다."]], [["The three went on, and Young Jerry went on, until the three stopped under a bank overhanging the road.", "셋은 계속 나아갔고, 어린 제리도 계속 나아갔으니, 마침내 셋이 길 위로 비스듬히 솟은 둑 아래에서 멈출 때까지 그랬다."], ["Upon the top of the bank was a low brick wall, surmounted by an iron railing.", "둑 꼭대기에는 낮은 벽돌담이 있었고 그 위에 쇠 난간이 둘려 있었다."], ["In the shadow of bank and wall the three turned out of the road, and up a blind lane, of which the wall--there, risen to some eight or ten feet high--formed one side.", "둑과 담의 그늘 속에서 셋은 길에서 벗어나, 그 담이 — 거기서는 여덟 자에서 열 자쯤 높이로 솟아 있었는데 — 한쪽 면을 이루는 막다른 골목으로 올라갔다."], ["Crouching down in a corner, peeping up the lane, the next object that Young Jerry saw, was the form of his honoured parent, pretty well defined against a watery and clouded moon, nimbly scaling an iron gate.", "한 모퉁이에 웅크리고 앉아 골목 위쪽을 훔쳐보던 어린 제리가 그다음으로 본 것은, 물기 어린 흐린 달을 배경으로 제법 또렷하게 윤곽이 드러난 채 쇠문을 날렵하게 기어오르는 그의 존경하는 어버이의 모습이었다."], ["He was soon over, and then the second fisherman got over, and then the third.", "그는 곧 넘어갔고, 이어 두 번째 낚시꾼이 넘어갔으며, 그다음 세 번째도 넘어갔다."], ["They all dropped softly on the ground within the gate, and lay there a little--listening perhaps.", "그들은 모두 문 안쪽 땅 위로 사뿐히 내려앉아 잠시 그곳에 엎드려 있었다 — 아마도 귀를 기울이는 듯했다."], ["Then, they moved away on their hands and knees.", "그러더니 그들은 두 손과 두 무릎으로 기어 멀어져 갔다."]], [["It was now Young Jerry’s turn to approach the gate: which he did, holding his breath.", "이제 어린 제리가 문에 다가갈 차례였고, 그는 숨을 죽인 채 그렇게 했다."], ["Crouching down again in a corner there, and looking in, he made out the three fishermen creeping through some rank grass!", "거기 다시 한 모퉁이에 웅크리고 앉아 안을 들여다보던 그는, 세 낚시꾼이 우거진 풀숲을 헤치며 기어가는 모습을 알아보았다!"], ["and all the gravestones in the churchyard--it was a large churchyard that they were in--looking on like ghosts in white, while the church tower itself looked on like the ghost of a monstrous giant.", "그리고 묘지의 모든 묘비들이 — 그들이 들어가 있는 곳은 커다란 묘지였다 — 흰옷 입은 유령들처럼 지켜보았고, 교회의 탑 그 자체는 거대한 거인의 유령처럼 내려다보았다."], ["They did not creep far, before they stopped and stood upright.", "그들은 멀리 기어가지 않아 멈춰서서 똑바로 일어섰다."], ["And then they began to fish.", "그러더니 낚시를 시작했다."]], [["They fished with a spade, at first.", "그들은 처음에 삽으로 낚시를 했다."], ["Presently the honoured parent appeared to be adjusting some instrument like a great corkscrew.", "이윽고 그 존경하는 어버이가 커다란 코르크 마개뽑이 같은 어떤 도구를 맞추는 듯했다."], ["Whatever tools they worked with, they worked hard, until the awful striking of the church clock so terrified Young Jerry, that he made off, with his hair as stiff as his father’s.", "그들이 무슨 연장으로 일하든, 그들은 열심히 일했으니, 마침내 교회 시계가 무시무시하게 울리는 바람에 어린 제리는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 머리카락을 아버지 것만큼이나 빳빳하게 곤두세운 채 줄행랑을 쳤다."]], [["But, his long-cherished desire to know more about these matters, not only stopped him in his running away, but lured him back again.", "그러나 이런 일들에 대해 더 알고 싶다는 그의 오래 간직해온 소망은, 그를 달아나다 멈춰 세웠을 뿐 아니라 다시 그곳으로 꾀어 돌아오게 했다."], ["They were still fishing perseveringly, when he peeped in at the gate for the second time; but, now they seemed to have got a bite.", "그가 두 번째로 문 안을 들여다보았을 때 그들은 여전히 끈질기게 낚시를 하고 있었다. 하지만 이제는 입질이 온 듯했다."], ["There was a screwing and complaining sound down below, and their bent figures were strained, as if by a weight.", "저 아래쪽에서 무언가 비틀어 돌리는 소리와 삐걱대는 소리가 났고, 그들의 구부린 몸은 마치 무게에 짓눌린 듯 잔뜩 긴장해 있었다."], ["By slow degrees the weight broke away the earth upon it, and came to the surface.", "그 무게가 차츰차츰 그 위의 흙을 밀어내고 지면으로 떠올랐다."], ["Young Jerry very well knew what it would be; but, when he saw it, and saw his honoured parent about to wrench it open, he was so frightened, being new to the sight, that he made off again, and never stopped until he had run a mile or more.", "어린 제리는 그것이 무엇일지 잘 알고 있었으나, 막상 그것을 보고, 자기의 존경하는 어버이가 그것을 비틀어 열려는 것을 보자, 그런 광경이 처음인지라 어찌나 겁이 났던지 다시 줄행랑을 쳐서, 일 마일 남짓 달리고 나서야 비로소 멈춰 섰다."]], [["He would not have stopped then, for anything less necessary than breath, it being a spectral sort of race that he ran, and one highly desirable to get to the end of.", "숨을 쉬는 것보다 덜 절박한 일이었다면 그는 그때도 멈추지 않았을 것이니, 그가 달린 것은 유령에게 쫓기는 듯한 종류의 질주였고, 어떻게든 그 끝에 다다르고 싶은 질주였기 때문이다."], ["He had a strong idea that the coffin he had seen was running after him; and, pictured as hopping on behind him, bolt upright, upon its narrow end, always on the point of overtaking him and hopping on at his side--perhaps taking his arm--it was a pursuer to shun.", "그는 자기가 본 그 관이 자기 뒤를 쫓아오고 있다는 강한 느낌에 사로잡혔다. 그 관이 좁은 한쪽 끝으로 꼿꼿이 선 채 그의 뒤에서 깡충깡충 따라오며, 언제라도 그를 따라잡아 그의 옆구리에 붙어 깡충거릴 — 어쩌면 그의 팔짱을 낄 — 태세인 모습이 그려지니, 그것은 피해야 할 추격자였다."], ["It was an inconsistent and ubiquitous fiend too, for, while it was making the whole night behind him dreadful, he darted out into the roadway to avoid dark alleys, fearful of its coming hopping out of them like a dropsical boy’s kite without tail and wings.", "게다가 그것은 종잡을 수 없고 어디에나 있는 악귀였으니, 그것이 그의 등 뒤로 온 밤을 무시무시하게 만드는 동안, 그는 어두운 골목을 피하려고 큰길로 튀어나갔으니, 그것이 꼬리도 날개도 없는 부어오른 사내아이의 연처럼 골목에서 깡충거리며 튀어나올까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["It hid in doorways too, rubbing its horrible shoulders against doors, and drawing them up to its ears, as if it were laughing.", "그것은 문간에도 숨어 있어, 그 끔찍한 어깨를 문에 비벼대며 마치 웃기라도 하듯 문을 제 귀까지 끌어올렸다."], ["It got into shadows on the road, and lay cunningly on its back to trip him up.", "그것은 길 위의 그늘 속으로 들어가, 그를 걸어 넘어뜨리려고 교활하게 등을 대고 누워 있었다."], ["All this time it was incessantly hopping on behind and gaining on him, so that when the boy got to his own door he had reason for being half dead.", "그러는 내내 그것은 끊임없이 등 뒤에서 깡충거리며 그를 따라잡고 있었으니, 그 소년이 자기 집 문에 다다랐을 때 반쯤 죽을 만한 까닭이 있었던 것이다."], ["And even then it would not leave him, but followed him upstairs with a bump on every stair, scrambled into bed with him, and bumped down, dead and heavy, on his breast when he fell asleep.", "그러고 나서도 그것은 그를 놓아주지 않고, 계단마다 쿵쿵 부딪히며 그를 따라 위층까지 올라와, 그와 함께 침대로 기어들었고, 그가 잠들자 죽은 듯 무겁게 그의 가슴 위로 쿵 내려앉았다."]], [["From his oppressed slumber, Young Jerry in his closet was awakened after daybreak and before sunrise, by the presence of his father in the family room.", "벽장 속의 어린 제리는 새벽이 지나고 해가 뜨기 전, 짓눌린 잠에서 가족 방에 들어온 아버지의 기척에 깨어났다. 아버지에게 무언가 잘못된 일이 있었다."], ["Something had gone wrong with him; at least, so Young Jerry inferred, from the circumstance of his holding Mrs. Cruncher by the ears, and knocking the back of her head against the head-board of the bed.", "적어도 어린 제리는 아버지가 크런처 부인의 귀를 붙들고 그녀의 뒤통수를 침대 머리판에 찧어대고 있는 정황으로 미루어 그렇게 짐작했다."]], [["“I told you I would,” said Mr.", "\"내가 그러겠다고 했지,\""], ["Cruncher, “and I did.”", "크런처 씨가 말했다. \"그래서 했고.\""]], [["“Jerry, Jerry, Jerry!”", "\"제리, 제리, 제리!\""], ["his wife implored.", "그의 아내가 애원했다."]], [["“You oppose yourself to the profit of the business,”", "\"당신은 사업의 이익에 맞서고 있어,\" 제리가 말했다."], ["said Jerry, “and me and my partners suffer.", "\"그래서 나와 내 동업자들이 손해를 보잖아."], ["You was to honour and obey; why the devil don’t you?”", "당신은 남편을 공경하고 따르기로 했어. 그런데 빌어먹을, 왜 안 하는 거야?\""]], [["“I try to be a good wife, Jerry,”", "\"난 좋은 아내가 되려고 애쓰고 있어요, 제리,\""], ["the poor woman protested, with tears.", "그 가엾은 여인이 눈물을 흘리며 항변했다."]], [["“Is it being a good wife to oppose your husband’s business?", "\"남편의 사업에 맞서는 게 좋은 아내야?"], ["Is it honouring your husband to dishonour his business?", "남편의 사업에 먹칠을 하는 게 남편을 공경하는 거야?"], ["Is it obeying your husband to disobey him on the wital subject of his business?”", "남편 사업이라는 중대한 문제에서 남편을 거역하는 게 남편을 따르는 거냔 말이야?\""]], [["“You hadn’t taken to the dreadful business then, Jerry.”", "\"그땐 당신이 그 끔찍한 사업을 하지 않았잖아요, 제리.\""]], [["“It’s enough for you,”", "\"당신은,\""], ["retorted Mr. Cruncher, “to be the wife of a honest tradesman, and not to occupy your female mind with calculations when he took to his trade or when he didn’t.", "크런처 씨가 쏘아붙였다, \"정직한 장사꾼의 아내라는 걸로 충분하지, 그자가 언제 그 생업을 시작했는지 안 했는지 따위 계산으로 그 여자 머리를 채울 게 아니야."], ["A honouring and obeying wife would let his trade alone altogether.", "남편을 공경하고 따르는 아내라면 그자의 생업 따위 아예 건드리지 않을 거라고."], ["Call yourself a religious woman?", "자기가 독실한 여자라고?"], ["If you’re a religious woman, give me a irreligious one!", "당신이 독실한 여자라면, 차라리 불경한 여자를 내게 다오!"], ["You have no more nat’ral sense of duty than the bed of this here Thames river has of a pile, and similarly it must be knocked into you.”", "당신은 여기 이 템스강 바닥이 말뚝에 대해 타고난 의무감을 갖고 있는 정도밖에 의무감이 없어, 그래서 마찬가지로 그걸 두들겨 박아 넣어야 하는 거지.\""]], [["The altercation was conducted in a low tone of voice, and terminated in the honest tradesman’s kicking off his clay-soiled boots, and lying down at his length on the floor.", "이 언쟁은 낮은 목소리로 진행되었고, 결국 그 정직한 장사꾼이 진흙투성이 장화를 걷어차 벗고 바닥에 큰 대자로 드러눕는 것으로 끝났다."], ["After taking a timid peep at him lying on his back, with his rusty hands under his head for a pillow, his son lay down too, and fell asleep again.", "녹슨 두 손을 베개 삼아 머리 밑에 괴고 등을 대고 누운 아버지를 겁먹은 듯 슬쩍 엿본 뒤, 그의 아들도 자리에 누워 다시 잠이 들었다."]], [["There was no fish for breakfast, and not much of anything else. Mr.", "아침 식사에는 물고기도 없었고 다른 것도 별로 없었다."], ["Cruncher was out of spirits, and out of temper, and kept an iron pot-lid by him as a projectile for the correction of Mrs. Cruncher, in case he should observe any symptoms of her saying Grace.", "크런처 씨는 기운도 없고 심기도 사나웠으며, 크런처 부인이 식전 기도를 올릴 기미라도 보이면 그것을 바로잡으려고 던질 무기로 쇠 냄비 뚜껑을 곁에 두었다."], ["He was brushed and washed at the usual hour, and set off with his son to pursue his ostensible calling.", "그는 여느 때 시각에 솔질을 하고 세수를 했으며, 표면상의 생업에 나서려고 아들과 함께 길을 떠났다."]], [["Young Jerry, walking with the stool under his arm at his father’s side along sunny and crowded Fleet-street, was a very different Young Jerry from him of the previous night, running home through darkness and solitude from his grim pursuer.", "햇볕이 내리쬐고 붐비는 플리트가를 따라 의자를 옆구리에 끼고 아버지 곁에서 걷는 어린 제리는, 어둠과 적막 속을 그 무시무시한 추격자에게 쫓겨 집으로 달려가던 전날 밤의 어린 제리와는 사뭇 다른 어린 제리였다."], ["His cunning was fresh with the day, and his qualms were gone with the night--in which particulars it is not improbable that he had compeers in Fleet-street and the City of London, that fine morning.", "그의 약삭빠름은 낮과 함께 새로워졌고, 그의 거리낌은 밤과 함께 사라졌으니 — 이런 점에서라면 그 화창한 아침에 플리트가와 런던 시내에 그와 같은 동류들이 있었다 해도 이상할 것은 없었다."]], [["“Father,”", "\"아버지,\" 어린 제리가 길을 걸으며 말했다."], ["said Young Jerry, as they walked along: taking care to keep at arm’s length and to have the stool well between them: “what’s a Resurrection-Man?”", "팔 하나 길이만큼 거리를 두고 둘 사이에 의자를 잘 끼워 넣도록 조심하면서 말했다. \"부활업자가 뭐예요?\""]], [["Mr.", "크런처 씨는 대답하기 전에 보도 위에 멈춰 섰다."], ["Cruncher came to a stop on the pavement before he answered, “How should I know?”", "\"내가 그걸 어떻게 아냐?\""]], [["“I thought you knowed everything, father,”", "\"아버지는 뭐든 다 아시는 줄 알았는데요,\""], ["said the artless boy.", "그 천진한 소년이 말했다."]], [["“Hem!", "\"흠!"], ["Well,” returned Mr.", "그래,\""], ["Cruncher, going on again, and lifting off his hat to give his spikes free play, “he’s a tradesman.”", "크런처 씨가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 가시 같은 머리털이 마음껏 놀도록 모자를 들어 올리면서 대답했다. \"그건 장사꾼이다.\""]], [["“What’s his goods, father?”", "\"그 사람 물건은 뭐예요, 아버지?\""], ["asked the brisk Young Jerry.", "활기찬 어린 제리가 물었다."]], [["“His goods,” said Mr.", "\"그자 물건은,\""], ["Cruncher, after turning it over in his mind, “is a branch of Scientific goods.”", "크런처 씨가 머릿속에서 곰곰이 굴려본 뒤에 말했다, \"과학용 물건의 한 갈래지.\""]], [["“Persons’ bodies, ain’t it, father?”", "\"사람 몸뚱이죠, 그렇죠, 아버지?\""], ["asked the lively boy.", "그 발랄한 소년이 물었다."]], [["“I believe it is something of that sort,”", "\"그런 비슷한 거지,\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, father, I should so like to be a Resurrection-Man when I’m quite growed up!”", "\"오, 아버지, 저는 다 자라면 꼭 부활업자가 되고 싶어요!\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher was soothed, but shook his head in a dubious and moral way.", "크런처 씨는 흐뭇해했으나, 미심쩍고 도덕적인 투로 고개를 저었다."], ["“It depends upon how you dewelop your talents.", "\"그건 네가 네 재능을 어떻게 갈고닦느냐에 달렸지."], ["Be careful to dewelop your talents, and never to say no more than you can help to nobody, and there’s no telling at the present time what you may not come to be fit for.”", "네 재능을 갈고닦도록 조심하고, 어느 누구한테도 안 해도 될 말은 한마디도 하지 마라, 그러면 네가 지금으로선 장차 무엇이 될 자격을 갖추게 될지 알 수 없는 거야.\""], ["As Young Jerry, thus encouraged, went on a few yards in advance, to plant the stool in the shadow of the Bar, Mr.", "이렇게 격려를 받은 어린 제리가 의자를 바 의 그늘에 세워두려고 몇 야드 앞서 나아가자, 크런처 씨는 혼잣말로 덧붙였다."], ["Cruncher added to himself: “Jerry, you honest tradesman, there’s hopes wot that boy will yet be a blessing to you, and a recompense to you for his mother!”", "\"제리야, 이 정직한 장사꾼아, 저 아이가 언젠가 너에게 축복이 되고, 제 어미 몫까지 너에게 보답이 될 거라는 희망이 있구나!\" * 바(Bar): 런던 플리트가와 스트랜드가 사이에 있던 옛 시문(市門) '템플 바'를 가리킨다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV. Knitting", "# CHAPTER XV. Knitting"]], [["There had been earlier drinking than usual in the wine-shop of Monsieur Defarge.", "드파르주 씨의 술집에서는 평소보다 이른 시간부터 술이 오갔다."], ["As early as six o’clock in the morning, sallow faces peeping through its barred windows had descried other faces within, bending over measures of wine.", "아침 여섯시밖에 안 된 시각인데도, 창살 친 창문 너머로 들여다보던 누런 얼굴들은 안에서 술잔에 머리를 숙이고 있는 다른 얼굴들을 알아보았다."], ["Monsieur Defarge sold a very thin wine at the best of times, but it would seem to have been an unusually thin wine that he sold at this time.", "드파르주 씨는 형편이 가장 좋을 때에도 몹시 묽은 술을 팔았지만, 이맘때 그가 팔던 술은 유난히 더 묽은 듯했다."], ["A sour wine, moreover, or a souring, for its influence on the mood of those who drank it was to make them gloomy.", "게다가 시큼한 술, 혹은 시어가는 술이었으니, 그것을 마시는 자들의 기분에 미치는 영향은 그들을 음울하게 만드는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["No vivacious Bacchanalian flame leaped out of the pressed grape of Monsieur Defarge: but, a smouldering fire that burnt in the dark, lay hidden in the dregs of it.", "드파르주 씨가 짜낸 포도에서 흥겨운 바쿠스의 불길은 튀어 오르지 않았다. 다만 어둠 속에서 타들어 가는 음울한 불씨가 그 찌꺼기 속에 숨어 있었다."]], [["This had been the third morning in succession, on which there had been early drinking at the wine-shop of Monsieur Defarge.", "드파르주 씨의 술집에서 이른 아침부터 술이 오간 것은 이날로 사흘째 연속이었다."], ["It had begun on Monday, and here was Wednesday come.", "그것은 월요일에 시작되었고, 이제 수요일이 되었다."], ["There had been more of early brooding than drinking; for, many men had listened and whispered and slunk about there from the time of the opening of the door, who could not have laid a piece of money on the counter to save their souls.", "술보다는 이른 시간부터 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는 일이 더 많았다. 문이 열리는 시각부터 그곳에서 귀를 기울이고 소곤거리며 몰래 드나드는 자들이 많았지만, 그들은 제 영혼을 구하기 위해서라도 계산대에 동전 한 닢 올려놓을 수 없는 처지였기 때문이다."], ["These were to the full as interested in the place, however, as if they could have commanded whole barrels of wine; and they glided from seat to seat, and from corner to corner, swallowing talk in lieu of drink, with greedy looks.", "그렇지만 그들은 술통을 통째로 부릴 수 있기라도 한 듯이 그곳에 한껏 관심을 쏟았다. 그들은 자리에서 자리로, 구석에서 구석으로 미끄러지듯 옮겨 다니며, 술 대신 이야기를 게걸스러운 눈빛으로 집어삼켰다."]], [["Notwithstanding an unusual flow of company, the master of the wine-shop was not visible.", "손님들이 유난히 들끓는데도, 술집 주인의 모습은 보이지 않았다. 그가 없다고 아쉬워하는 사람도 없었다."], ["He was not missed; for, nobody who crossed the threshold looked for him, nobody asked for him, nobody wondered to see only Madame Defarge in her seat, presiding over the distribution of wine, with a bowl of battered small coins before her, as much defaced and beaten out of their original impress as the small coinage of humanity from whose ragged pockets they had come.", "문지방을 넘어 들어온 누구도 그를 찾지 않았고, 누구도 그를 찾아 묻지 않았으며, 드파르주 부인이 홀로 자리를 지키며 술 분배를 주관하는 것을 보고도 이상하게 여기는 이가 없었다. 그녀 앞에는 닳고 두들겨 맞아 본래의 새김이 다 지워진 잔돈 동전들이 사발에 담겨 있었는데, 그것은 그 돈이 흘러나온 누더기 주머니 임자들, 곧 인류라는 잔돈만큼이나 형체를 잃은 것이었다."]], [["A suspended interest and a prevalent absence of mind, were perhaps observed by the spies who looked in at the wine-shop, as they looked in at every place, high and low, from the king’s palace to the criminal’s gaol.", "술집을 들여다보는 첩자들의 눈에는 — 그들은 왕의 궁전에서 죄수의 감옥에 이르기까지 높은 곳이든 낮은 곳이든 어디나 들여다보았으니 — 멈춰버린 관심과 만연한 멍한 기색이 어쩌면 포착되었을지도 모른다."], ["Games at cards languished, players at dominoes musingly built towers with them, drinkers drew figures on the tables with spilt drops of wine, Madame Defarge herself picked out the pattern on her sleeve with her toothpick, and saw and heard something inaudible and invisible a long way off.", "카드놀이는 시들했고, 도미노를 하던 자들은 골똘히 그것으로 탑을 쌓았으며, 술꾼들은 흘린 술방울로 탁자 위에 그림을 그렸고, 드파르주 부인 자신은 이쑤시개로 소맷자락의 무늬를 콕콕 짚으면서, 멀리 떨어진 곳의 들리지도 보이지도 않는 무언가를 보고 들었다."]], [["Thus, Saint Antoine in this vinous feature of his, until midday.", "이렇듯 쌩땅뚜안은 술집의 이런 면모를 띤 채 한낮까지 이어졌다."], ["It was high noontide, when two dusty men passed through his streets and under his swinging lamps: of whom, one was Monsieur Defarge: the other a mender of roads in a blue cap.", "먼지투성이 사내 둘이 그곳 거리들을 지나, 흔들거리는 등불 아래를 통과한 것은 한낮이 절정에 이른 때였다. 그중 하나는 드파르주 씨였고, 다른 하나는 푸른 모자를 쓴 도로 보수공이었다."], ["All adust and athirst, the two entered the wine-shop.", "온통 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 목이 탄 두 사람은 술집으로 들어섰다."], ["Their arrival had lighted a kind of fire in the breast of Saint Antoine, fast spreading as they came along, which stirred and flickered in flames of faces at most doors and windows.", "그들의 도착은 쌩땅뚜안의 가슴속에 일종의 불을 지폈으니, 그것은 그들이 다가옴에 따라 빠르게 번지며, 거의 모든 문과 창에서 얼굴이라는 불꽃으로 일렁이고 너울거렸다."], ["Yet, no one had followed them, and no man spoke when they entered the wine-shop, though the eyes of every man there were turned upon them.", "그러나 아무도 그들을 뒤따르지 않았고, 그들이 술집에 들어섰을 때 입을 여는 사람은 없었으니, 그곳의 모든 사내들의 눈이 그들에게 쏠려 있었음에도 그러했다."]], [["“Good day, gentlemen!”", "\"안녕하시오, 여러분!\""], ["said Monsieur Defarge.", "드파르주 씨가 말했다."]], [["It may have been a signal for loosening the general tongue.", "그것은 다들 입을 풀라는 신호였는지도 모른다."], ["It elicited an answering chorus of “Good day!”", "\"안녕하시오!\" 하는 화답의 합창이 터져 나왔다."]], [["“It is bad weather, gentlemen,”", "\"날이 궂군요, 여러분.\""], ["said Defarge, shaking his head.", "드파르주가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["Upon which, every man looked at his neighbour, and then all cast down their eyes and sat silent.", "그러자 사내들은 저마다 옆 사람을 바라보았고, 이윽고 모두 눈을 내리깔고 잠자코 앉아 있었다."], ["Except one man, who got up and went out.", "다만 한 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“My wife,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said Defarge aloud, addressing Madame Defarge: “I have travelled certain leagues with this good mender of roads, called Jacques.", "드파르주가 큰 소리로 드파르주 부인을 향해 말했다. \"내가 자끄라고 불리는 이 착한 도로 보수공과 함께 몇 리외를 걸어왔소."], ["I met him--by accident--a day and half’s journey out of Paris.", "그를 만난 건 — 우연히 — 빠리에서 하루 반 거리 떨어진 곳이었지."], ["He is a good child, this mender of roads, called Jacques.", "자끄라고 불리는 이 도로 보수공은 착한 친구요."], ["Give him to drink, my wife!”", "마실 것을 좀 주구려, 여보!\""]], [["A second man got up and went out.", "두번째 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Madame Defarge set wine before the mender of roads called Jacques, who doffed his blue cap to the company, and drank.", "드파르주 부인은 자끄라고 불리는 도로 보수공 앞에 술을 놓았고, 그는 일동에게 푸른 모자를 벗어 인사하고 마셨다."], ["In the breast of his blouse he carried some coarse dark bread; he ate of this between whiles, and sat munching and drinking near Madame Defarge’s counter.", "그의 작업복 가슴께에는 거칠고 검은 빵이 들어 있었는데, 그는 틈틈이 그것을 먹으며 드파르주 부인의 계산대 가까이에 앉아 우물우물 씹으며 마셨다."], ["A third man got up and went out.", "세번째 사내가 일어서서 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Defarge refreshed himself with a draught of wine--but, he took less than was given to the stranger, as being himself a man to whom it was no rarity--and stood waiting until the countryman had made his breakfast.", "드파르주는 술 한 모금으로 기운을 차렸으나 — 손님에게 따라준 것보다는 적게 마셨으니, 그 자신에게는 술이 드문 것이 아니었기 때문이다 — 시골 사람이 아침 식사를 마칠 때까지 서서 기다렸다."], ["He looked at no one present, and no one now looked at him; not even Madame Defarge, who had taken up her knitting, and was at work.", "그는 그 자리의 누구도 바라보지 않았고, 이제 누구도 그를 바라보지 않았다. 뜨개질감을 집어 들고 일에 몰두하고 있던 드파르주 부인조차도 그러했다."]], [["“Have you finished your repast, friend?”", "\"식사를 마쳤소, 친구?\""], ["he asked, in due season.", "그가 때맞춰 물었다."]], [["“Yes, thank you.”", "\"네, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["“Come, then!", "\"그럼 이리 오시오!"], ["You shall see the apartment that I told you you could occupy.", "당신이 묵을 수 있다고 했던 그 방을 보여주겠소."], ["It will suit you to a marvel.”", "당신에게 더없이 잘 맞을 거요.\""]], [["Out of the wine-shop into the street, out of the street into a courtyard, out of the courtyard up a steep staircase, out of the staircase into a garret--formerly the garret where a white-haired man sat on a low bench, stooping forward and very busy, making shoes.", "술집에서 거리로, 거리에서 안마당으로, 안마당에서 가파른 계단을 올라, 계단에서 다락방으로 — 예전에 백발의 남자가 낮은 걸상에 앉아 몸을 앞으로 숙인 채 매우 바쁘게 구두를 만들던 바로 그 다락방으로."]], [["No white-haired man was there now; but, the three men were there who had gone out of the wine-shop singly.", "이제 그곳에 백발의 남자는 없었다. 다만 술집에서 하나씩 따로 나간 세 사내가 거기 있었다."], ["And between them and the white-haired man afar off, was the one small link, that they had once looked in at him through the chinks in the wall.", "그리고 그들과 멀리 떨어진 백발의 남자 사이에는, 그들이 한때 벽 틈으로 그를 들여다본 적이 있다는 작은 연결고리 하나가 있었다."]], [["Defarge closed the door carefully, and spoke in a subdued voice:", "드파르주는 조심스럽게 문을 닫고, 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques Three!", "\"자끄 하나, 자끄 둘, 자끄 셋!"], ["This is the witness encountered by appointment, by me, Jacques Four.", "이 사람이 나, 자끄 넷이 약속을 잡아 만난 증인이오."], ["He will tell you all.", "그가 여러분에게 모든 걸 말해줄 거요."], ["Speak, Jacques Five!”", "말하시오, 자끄 다섯!\""]], [["The mender of roads, blue cap in hand, wiped his swarthy forehead with it, and said, “Where shall I commence, monsieur?”", "도로 보수공은 손에 든 푸른 모자로 거무스름한 이마를 닦으며 말했다. \"어디서부터 시작할까요, 나리?\""]], [["“Commence,”", "\"시작하시오,\""], ["was Monsieur Defarge’s not unreasonable reply, “at the commencement.”", "드파르주 씨가 더없이 합당하게 대답했다. \"처음부터.\""]], [["“I saw him then, messieurs,”", "\"그러니까 제가 그를 본 건, 여러분,\""], ["began the mender of roads, “a year ago this running summer, underneath the carriage of the Marquis, hanging by the chain.", "도로 보수공이 입을 열었다. \"이번 여름이면 꼭 한해 전이었는데, 후작님의 마차 밑에서 쇠사슬에 매달려 있었습죠."], ["Behold the manner of it.", "그 모양이 어땠는지 보십시오."], ["I leaving my work on the road, the sun going to bed, the carriage of the Marquis slowly ascending the hill, he hanging by the chain--like this.”", "저는 도로에서 일을 마치는 참이었고, 해는 잠자리에 들고 있었고, 후작님의 마차는 천천히 언덕을 오르고 있었고, 그자는 쇠사슬에 매달려 있었는데 — 이렇게요.\""]], [["Again the mender of roads went through the whole performance; in which he ought to have been perfect by that time, seeing that it had been the infallible resource and indispensable entertainment of his village during a whole year.", "도로 보수공은 다시 한번 그 모든 동작을 처음부터 끝까지 해 보였다. 그때쯤이면 그 동작에 완벽해졌을 법도 했으니, 그것이 한해 내내 그의 마을에서 결코 빗나가지 않는 화제거리이자 없어서는 안 될 여흥이었기 때문이다."]], [["Jacques One struck in, and asked if he had ever seen the man before?", "자끄 하나가 끼어들어, 전에 그 사내를 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Never,”", "\"한번도 없습죠.\""], ["answered the mender of roads, recovering his perpendicular.", "도로 보수공이 다시 똑바로 서며 대답했다."]], [["Jacques Three demanded how he afterwards recognised him then?", "자끄 셋이 그렇다면 나중에 어떻게 그를 알아보았느냐고 따져 물었다."]], [["“By his tall figure,”", "\"키가 크다는 걸로요.\""], ["said the mender of roads, softly, and with his finger at his nose.", "도로 보수공이 손가락을 코에 댄 채 나직이 말했다."], ["“When Monsieur the Marquis demands that evening, ‘Say, what is he like?’ I make response, ‘Tall as a spectre.’”", "\"후작님이 그날 저녁에 '어디, 그자가 어떻게 생겼더냐?' 하고 물으셨을 때, 저는 '유령처럼 키가 컸습니다'라고 대답했습죠.\""]], [["“You should have said, short as a dwarf,”", "\"난쟁이처럼 작다고 말했어야지.\""], ["returned Jacques Two.", "자끄 둘이 받아쳤다."]], [["“But what did I know?", "\"하지만 제가 뭘 알았겠습니까?"], ["The deed was not then accomplished, neither did he confide in me.", "그땐 아직 그 일이 저질러지지도 않았고, 그자가 제게 속내를 털어놓지도 않았는걸요."], ["Observe!", "보십시오!"], ["Under those circumstances even, I do not offer my testimony.", "그런 상황에서조차 저는 제 증언을 내놓지 않았습죠."], ["Monsieur the Marquis indicates me with his finger, standing near our little fountain, and says, ‘To me!", "후작님이 우리 마을 작은 샘 가까이 서서 손가락으로 저를 가리키며 '내게로! 저 놈을 끌고 오너라!'"], ["Bring that rascal!’ My faith, messieurs, I offer nothing.”", "하셨을 때도, 정말이지 여러분, 저는 아무것도 내놓지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“He is right there, Jacques,”", "\"저 사람 말이 옳소, 자끄.\""], ["murmured Defarge, to him who had interrupted.", "드파르주가 끼어들었던 자에게 나직이 말했다."], ["“Go on!”", "\"계속하시오!\""]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋습니다!\""], ["said the mender of roads, with an air of mystery.", "도로 보수공이 신비스러운 태도로 말했다."], ["“The tall man is lost, and he is sought--how many months?", "\"키 큰 사내는 종적을 감추었고, 사람들이 그를 찾았습죠 — 몇 달 동안이었을까요?"], ["Nine, ten, eleven?”", "아홉 달, 열 달, 열한 달?\""]], [["“No matter, the number,”", "\"몇 달인지는 중요치 않소.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."], ["“He is well hidden, but at last he is unluckily found.", "\"그는 잘 숨어 있었지만, 결국 운 나쁘게 발견되었지."], ["Go on!”", "계속하시오!\""]], [["“I am again at work upon the hill-side, and the sun is again about to go to bed.", "\"저는 또다시 언덕배기에서 일하고 있었고, 해는 또다시 잠자리에 들려는 참이었습죠."], ["I am collecting my tools to descend to my cottage down in the village below, where it is already dark, when I raise my eyes, and see coming over the hill six soldiers.", "저 아래 마을에 있는 제 오두막으로 내려가려고 — 거긴 이미 어둑했지요 — 연장을 챙기던 참에 눈을 들어 보니, 군인 여섯이 언덕을 넘어오는 게 보였습니다."], ["In the midst of them is a tall man with his arms bound--tied to his sides--like this!”", "그 한가운데에 두 팔이 묶인 키 큰 사내가 있었는데 — 양옆구리에 붙들어 매여 있었습죠 — 이렇게요!\""]], [["With the aid of his indispensable cap, he represented a man with his elbows bound fast at his hips, with cords that were knotted behind him.", "그는 없어서는 안 될 모자의 도움을 빌려, 등 뒤에서 매듭지은 끈으로 두 팔꿈치를 허리께에 단단히 묶인 사내를 흉내 냈다."]], [["“I stand aside, messieurs, by my heap of stones, to see the soldiers and their prisoner pass (for it is a solitary road, that, where any spectacle is well worth looking at), and at first, as they approach, I see no more than that they are six soldiers with a tall man bound, and that they are almost black to my sight--except on the side of the sun going to bed, where they have a red edge, messieurs.", "\"저는 한쪽으로 물러서서, 여러분, 제 돌무더기 옆에 서서, 군인들과 그 죄수가 지나가는 걸 보았습죠 (그곳은 외딴 길이라, 무슨 구경거리든 볼만한 가치가 있거든요). 처음에 그들이 다가올 때는, 군인 여섯에 묶인 키 큰 사내 하나라는 것, 그리고 그들이 제 눈에는 거의 새카맣게 보인다는 것 — 다만 해가 지는 쪽으로는 가장자리가 붉었습죠, 여러분 — 그것 말고는 더 보이지 않았습니다."], ["Also, I see that their long shadows are on the hollow ridge on the opposite side of the road, and are on the hill above it, and are like the shadows of giants.", "또 보니, 그들의 긴 그림자가 길 건너편의 움푹 꺼진 등성이에 드리워지고, 그 위 언덕에도 드리워져, 마치 거인들의 그림자 같았습니다."], ["Also, I see that they are covered with dust, and that the dust moves with them as they come, tramp, tramp!", "또 보니, 그들은 먼지를 뒤집어쓰고 있었고, 그들이 다가옴에 따라 그 먼지도 함께 움직여, 저벅, 저벅!"], ["But when they advance quite near to me, I recognise the tall man, and he recognises me.", "하지만 그들이 제게 아주 가까이 다가왔을 때, 저는 그 키 큰 사내를 알아보았고, 그자도 저를 알아보았습죠."], ["Ah, but he would be well content to precipitate himself over the hill-side once again, as on the evening when he and I first encountered, close to the same spot!”", "아아, 그러나 그자는 저와 처음 마주쳤던 그 저녁처럼, 바로 그 자리 가까이에서 다시 한번 언덕배기로 몸을 던지고 싶었을 겁니다!\""]], [["He described it as if he were there, and it was evident that he saw it vividly; perhaps he had not seen much in his life.", "그는 마치 자기가 그 자리에 있는 듯이 그것을 묘사했고, 그것을 생생하게 떠올리고 있음이 분명했다. 어쩌면 그는 살면서 본 것이 별로 없었는지도 모른다."]], [["“I do not show the soldiers that I recognise the tall man; he does not show the soldiers that he recognises me; we do it, and we know it, with our eyes.", "\"저는 군인들에게 그 키 큰 사내를 알아본 티를 내지 않았고, 그자도 군인들에게 저를 알아본 티를 내지 않았습죠. 우리는 눈으로 그 짓을 했고, 눈으로 그걸 알았습니다."], ["‘Come on!’ says the chief of that company, pointing to the village, ‘bring him fast to his tomb!’ and they bring him faster.", "'가자!' 그 무리의 대장이 마을을 가리키며 말했습죠."], ["I follow.", "'저놈을 어서 무덤으로 데려가라!'"], ["His arms are swelled because of being bound so tight, his wooden shoes are large and clumsy, and he is lame.", "그러고는 그를 더 빨리 끌고 갔습니다. 저는 뒤따랐죠."], ["Because he is lame, and consequently slow, they drive him with their guns--like this!”", "그의 두 팔은 어찌나 꽉 묶였던지 퉁퉁 부어 있었고, 그의 나막신은 크고 투박했고, 그는 절름발이였습니다. 절름발이라 느릴 수밖에 없으니, 그들은 총으로 그를 몰아댔습죠 — 이렇게요!\""]], [["He imitated the action of a man’s being impelled forward by the butt-ends of muskets.", "그는 머스킷 총 개머리판으로 사람을 앞으로 떠미는 동작을 흉내 냈다."]], [["“As they descend the hill like madmen running a race, he falls.", "\"미친 사람들이 경주라도 하듯 언덕을 내달리며 내려가니, 그가 쓰러집니다. 그들은 웃으며 그를 다시 일으켜 세웁니다."], ["They laugh and pick him up again.", "그의 얼굴은 피투성이에 먼지투성이지만, 그는 손을 댈 수가 없으니, 그러자 그들은 또 웃습니다."], ["His face is bleeding and covered with dust, but he cannot touch it; thereupon they laugh again.", "그들은 그를 마을로 끌고 들어옵니다. 온 마을이 보려고 달려나오고요."], ["They bring him into the village; all the village runs to look; they take him past the mill, and up to the prison; all the village sees the prison gate open in the darkness of the night, and swallow him--like this!”", "그들은 그를 방앗간을 지나 감옥까지 데려갑니다. 온 마을은 그 밤의 어둠 속에서 감옥 문이 열려 그를 삼키는 걸 봅니다 — 이렇게요!\""]], [["He opened his mouth as wide as he could, and shut it with a sounding snap of his teeth.", "그는 입을 한껏 크게 벌렸다가 이를 딱 소리 나게 부딪치며 다물었다."], ["Observant of his unwillingness to mar the effect by opening it again, Defarge said, “Go on, Jacques.”", "다시 입을 벌려 그 효과를 망치고 싶어하지 않는 것을 알아채고, 드파르주가 말했다. \"계속하시오, 자끄.\""]], [["“All the village,” pursued the mender of roads, on tiptoe and in a low voice, “withdraws; all the village whispers by the fountain; all the village sleeps; all the village dreams of that unhappy one, within the locks and bars of the prison on the crag, and never to come out of it, except to perish. In the morning, with my tools upon my shoulder, eating my morsel of black bread as I go, I make a circuit by the prison, on my way to my work. There I see him, high up, behind the bars of a lofty iron cage, bloody and dusty as last night, looking through. He has no hand free, to wave to me; I dare not call to him; he regards me like a dead man.”", "\"온 마을이,\" 도로 보수공이 발끝으로 서서 낮은 목소리로 이어갔다. \"물러갑니다. 온 마을이 샘가에서 소곤거리고요. 온 마을이 잠듭니다. 온 마을이 그 불행한 자의 꿈을 꿉니다, 바위산 위 감옥의 자물쇠와 빗장 안에 갇혀, 죽지 않고서는 결코 그곳에서 나오지 못할 그자의 꿈을요. 아침이 되어, 어깨에 연장을 메고 가는 길에 검은 빵 부스러기를 뜯어 먹으면서, 저는 일터로 가는 길에 감옥을 빙 둘러 갑니다. 거기서 저는 그를 봅니다, 높은 곳, 높다란 철창 우리의 창살 뒤에서, 간밤처럼 피투성이에 먼지투성이로, 밖을 내다보고 있는 그를요. 그에겐 제게 흔들어 보일 자유로운 손 하나 없습니다. 저는 감히 그를 부르지 못합니다. 그는 죽은 사람처럼 저를 바라봅니다.\""]], [["Defarge and the three glanced darkly at one another. The looks of all of them were dark, repressed, and revengeful, as they listened to the countryman’s story; the manner of all of them, while it was secret, was authoritative too. They had the air of a rough tribunal; Jacques One and Two sitting on the old pallet-bed, each with his chin resting on his hand, and his eyes intent on the road-mender; Jacques Three, equally intent, on one knee behind them, with his agitated hand always gliding over the network of fine nerves about his mouth and nose; Defarge standing between them and the narrator, whom he had stationed in the light of the window, by turns looking from him to them, and from them to him.", "드파르주와 세 사내는 어두운 눈빛으로 서로를 흘긋 보았다. 시골 사람의 이야기에 귀 기울이는 그들 모두의 표정은 어둡고, 억눌려 있고, 복수심에 차 있었다. 그들 모두의 태도는 은밀하면서도 위엄이 있었다. 그들에게는 거친 법정 같은 분위기가 감돌았다. 자끄 하나와 둘은 낡은 간이침대에 앉아, 저마다 손으로 턱을 괸 채 도로 보수공에게 눈길을 고정하고 있었다. 자끄 셋은 그에 못지않게 골똘히, 그들 뒤에서 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 안절부절못하는 손을 입과 코 언저리의 가느다란 신경 다발 위로 끊임없이 미끄러뜨리고 있었다. 드파르주는 그들과 이야기꾼 사이에 서서, 창문의 빛 속에 세워둔 그를 번갈아 보았으니, 그에게서 그들에게로, 그들에게서 그에게로 시선을 옮겼다."]], [["“Go on, Jacques,”", "\"계속하시오, 자끄.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“He remains up there in his iron cage some days.", "\"그는 며칠 동안 그 위 철창 우리에 갇혀 있습니다."], ["The village looks at him by stealth, for it is afraid.", "마을은 두려운 나머지 몰래 그를 바라봅니다."], ["But it always looks up, from a distance, at the prison on the crag; and in the evening, when the work of the day is achieved and it assembles to gossip at the fountain, all faces are turned towards the prison.", "하지만 마을은 늘 멀찍이서 바위산 위의 감옥을 올려다봅죠. 그리고 저녁이 되어, 하루의 일을 마치고 샘가에 모여 이야기를 나눌 때면, 모든 얼굴이 감옥 쪽을 향합니다."], ["Formerly, they were turned towards the posting-house; now, they are turned towards the prison.", "예전에는 역참 쪽을 향했지만, 이제는 감옥 쪽을 향하지요. 사람들은 샘가에서 소곤거립니다, 그자가 사형 선고를 받긴 했어도 처형되지는 않을 거라고요."], ["They whisper at the fountain, that although condemned to death he will not be executed; they say that petitions have been presented in Paris, showing that he was enraged and made mad by the death of his child; they say that a petition has been presented to the King himself.", "빠리에 탄원서가 제출되었는데, 그자가 제 자식의 죽음 때문에 격분해서 미쳐버렸다는 내용이라고들 합니다. 국왕 폐하께 직접 탄원서가 올라갔다고도 하고요."], ["What do I know?", "제가 뭘 알겠습니까?"], ["It is possible.", "그럴 수도 있겠죠."], ["Perhaps yes, perhaps no.”", "그럴지도, 아닐지도.\""]], [["“Listen then, Jacques,”", "\"그렇다면 들어보시오, 자끄.\""], ["Number One of that name sternly interposed.", "그 이름을 가진 첫번째 사내가 엄하게 끼어들었다."], ["“Know that a petition was presented to the King and Queen.", "\"국왕과 왕비에게 탄원서가 제출되었다는 걸 알아두시오."], ["All here, yourself excepted, saw the King take it, in his carriage in the street, sitting beside the Queen.", "당신만 빼고 여기 있는 모두가, 국왕이 거리에서 마차 안에 왕비 곁에 앉아 그것을 받는 걸 보았소."], ["It is Defarge whom you see here, who, at the hazard of his life, darted out before the horses, with the petition in his hand.”", "목숨을 걸고 말들 앞으로 뛰쳐나가 그 탄원서를 손에 든 사람이, 바로 여기 보고 있는 이 드파르주요.\""]], [["“And once again listen, Jacques!”", "\"그리고 다시 한번 들어보시오, 자끄!\" 무릎 꿇은 세번째 사내가 말했다."], ["said the kneeling Number Three: his fingers ever wandering over and over those fine nerves, with a strikingly greedy air, as if he hungered for something--that was neither food nor drink; “the guard, horse and foot, surrounded the petitioner, and struck him blows.", "그의 손가락들은 마치 무언가를 — 먹을 것도 마실 것도 아닌 무언가를 — 갈망하기라도 하듯, 놀랍도록 게걸스러운 기색으로 그 가느다란 신경 위를 끊임없이 더듬고 또 더듬었다. \"기마병과 보병으로 이루어진 호위대가 탄원자를 둘러싸고 두들겨 팼소."], ["You hear?”", "듣고 있소?\""]], [["“I hear, messieurs.”", "\"듣고 있습니다, 여러분.\""]], [["“Go on then,”", "\"그럼 계속하시오.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“Again; on the other hand, they whisper at the fountain,”", "\"또요, 다른 한편으로는, 사람들이 샘가에서 소곤거립니다.\""], ["resumed the countryman, “that he is brought down into our country to be executed on the spot, and that he will very certainly be executed.", "시골 사람이 다시 이어갔다. \"그자가 우리 고장으로 끌려 내려온 건 그 자리에서 처형하기 위해서이고, 그자는 틀림없이 처형될 거라고요."], ["They even whisper that because he has slain Monseigneur, and because Monseigneur was the father of his tenants--serfs--what you will--he will be executed as a parricide.", "그자가 영주님을 죽였고, 영주님은 그자의 소작인들 — 농노들 — 뭐라 부르든 간에 — 그들의 아버지였으니, 그자는 친부 살해범으로 처형될 거라고까지 소곤거립니다."], ["One old man says at the fountain, that his right hand, armed with the knife, will be burnt off before his face; that, into wounds which will be made in his arms, his breast, and his legs, there will be poured boiling oil, melted lead, hot resin, wax, and sulphur; finally, that he will be torn limb from limb by four strong horses.", "어떤 노인이 샘가에서 그러는데, 그자의 오른손, 곧 칼을 쥐었던 손이 그자의 얼굴 앞에서 불에 태워질 거라고요. 그자의 두 팔과 가슴과 다리에 상처를 내고, 거기에 끓는 기름과 녹인 납과 뜨거운 송진과 밀랍과 유황을 부을 거라고요."], ["That old man says, all this was actually done to a prisoner who made an attempt on the life of the late King, Louis Fifteen.", "마지막에는 힘센 말 네 마리가 그자의 사지를 갈가리 찢어놓을 거라고 합니다. 그 노인 말이, 이 모든 게 선왕이신 루이 십오세의 목숨을 노렸던 어느 죄수에게 실제로 행해졌다는군요."], ["But how do I know if he lies?", "하지만 그 노인이 거짓말하는 건지 제가 어찌 알겠습니까?"], ["I am not a scholar.”", "저는 학자가 아니걸요.\""]], [["“Listen once again then, Jacques!”", "\"그렇다면 다시 한번 들어보시오, 자끄!\""], ["said the man with the restless hand and the craving air.", "쉴 새 없는 손과 갈망하는 기색의 사내가 말했다."], ["“The name of that prisoner was Damiens, and it was all done in open day, in the open streets of this city of Paris; and nothing was more noticed in the vast concourse that saw it done, than the crowd of ladies of quality and fashion, who were full of eager attention to the last--to the last, Jacques, prolonged until nightfall, when he had lost two legs and an arm, and still breathed!", "\"그 죄수의 이름은 다미앵이었고, 그 모든 게 백주 대낮에, 이 빠리 시의 탁 트인 거리에서 행해졌소. 그리고 그것을 지켜본 어마어마한 군중 가운데 가장 눈길을 끈 것은, 끝까지 — 끝까지요, 자끄, 그자가 두 다리와 한 팔을 잃고도 여전히 숨이 붙어 있던 밤까지 늘어진 그 끝까지 — 열렬한 관심으로 가득 차 있던 지체 높고 멋들어진 귀부인들의 무리였소!"], ["And it was done--why, how old are you?”", "그리고 그 일이 행해진 게 — 그래, 당신은 나이가 어떻게 되오?\""]], [["“Thirty-five,”", "\"서른다섯입니다.\""], ["said the mender of roads, who looked sixty.", "예순은 되어 보이는 도로 보수공이 말했다."]], [["“It was done when you were more than ten years old; you might have seen it.”", "\"당신이 열 살이 넘었을 때 행해진 일이오. 당신도 그걸 봤을지 모르지.\""]], [["“Enough!”", "\"그만하면 됐소!\""], ["said Defarge, with grim impatience.", "드파르주가 험상궂게 안달하며 말했다."], ["“Long live the Devil!", "\"악마 만세!"], ["Go on.”", "계속하시오.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["Some whisper this, some whisper that; they speak of nothing else; even the fountain appears to fall to that tune.", "누구는 이걸 소곤거리고, 누구는 저걸 소곤거립니다. 사람들은 온통 그 얘기뿐이지요."], ["At length, on Sunday night when all the village is asleep, come soldiers, winding down from the prison, and their guns ring on the stones of the little street.", "샘물까지도 그 가락에 맞춰 흐르는 것 같습니다. 마침내 일요일 밤, 온 마을이 잠든 사이에 군인들이 와서, 감옥에서부터 구불구불 내려오는데, 그들의 총이 좁은 거리의 돌바닥에 부딪쳐 쟁그랑 소리를 냅니다."], ["Workmen dig, workmen hammer, soldiers laugh and sing; in the morning, by the fountain, there is raised a gallows forty feet high, poisoning the water.”", "일꾼들이 땅을 파고, 일꾼들이 망치질을 하고, 군인들이 웃고 노래합니다. 아침에 샘가에는 마흔 자 높이의 교수대가 솟아올라, 물을 더럽힙니다.\""]], [["The mender of roads looked through rather than at the low ceiling, and pointed as if he saw the gallows somewhere in the sky.", "도로 보수공은 낮은 천장을 바라보았다기보다 그것을 꿰뚫어 보았고, 마치 하늘 어딘가에 교수대가 보이기라도 하는 듯 손가락질을 했다."]], [["“All work is stopped, all assemble there, nobody leads the cows out, the cows are there with the rest.", "\"모든 일이 멈추고, 모두가 그곳에 모입니다. 아무도 소를 끌고 나가지 않으니, 소들도 다른 사람들과 함께 거기 있습니다."], ["At midday, the roll of drums.", "한낮이 되자, 북소리가 울립니다."], ["Soldiers have marched into the prison in the night, and he is in the midst of many soldiers.", "군인들이 밤사이 감옥으로 행군해 들어가 있었고, 그자는 수많은 군인들 한가운데에 있습니다."], ["He is bound as before, and in his mouth there is a gag--tied so, with a tight string, making him look almost as if he laughed.”", "그는 전처럼 묶여 있고, 그의 입에는 재갈이 물려 있는데 — 팽팽한 끈으로 이렇게 묶어서, 거의 웃고 있는 것처럼 보이게 했습니다.\""], ["He suggested it, by creasing his face with his two thumbs, from the corners of his mouth to his ears.", "그는 두 엄지를 입꼬리에서 귀까지 가져가 얼굴을 일그러뜨리며 그 모습을 흉내 냈다."], ["“On the top of the gallows is fixed the knife, blade upwards, with its point in the air.", "\"교수대 꼭대기에는 칼이 고정되어 있는데, 날이 위를 향하고 칼끝이 허공을 찌르고 있습니다."], ["He is hanged there forty feet high--and is left hanging, poisoning the water.”", "그는 거기 마흔 자 높이에 매달립니다 — 그러고는 매달린 채로 버려져, 물을 더럽힙니다.\""]], [["They looked at one another, as he used his blue cap to wipe his face, on which the perspiration had started afresh while he recalled the spectacle.", "그가 푸른 모자로 얼굴을 닦는 동안 — 그 구경거리를 떠올리느라 다시 땀이 솟아난 얼굴이었다 — 그들은 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["“It is frightful, messieurs.", "\"끔찍합니다, 여러분."], ["How can the women and the children draw water!", "여자들과 아이들이 어떻게 물을 길을 수 있겠습니까!"], ["Who can gossip of an evening, under that shadow!", "누가 저녁에 그 그림자 아래서 한담을 나눌 수 있겠습니까!"], ["Under it, have I said?", "그 아래서라고 했던가요?"], ["When I left the village, Monday evening as the sun was going to bed, and looked back from the hill, the shadow struck across the church, across the mill, across the prison--seemed to strike across the earth, messieurs, to where the sky rests upon it!”", "제가 월요일 저녁, 해가 잠자리에 들 무렵 마을을 떠나면서 언덕에서 돌아보니, 그 그림자가 교회를 가로지르고, 방앗간을 가로지르고, 감옥을 가로질러 — 마치 땅을 가로질러, 여러분, 하늘이 땅에 닿아 있는 데까지 뻗치는 것 같았습니다!\""]], [["The hungry man gnawed one of his fingers as he looked at the other three, and his finger quivered with the craving that was on him.", "그 굶주린 사내는 다른 세 사람을 바라보며 손가락 하나를 깨물었고, 그의 손가락은 그를 사로잡은 갈망으로 떨렸다."]], [["“That’s all, messieurs.", "\"그게 다입니다, 여러분."], ["I left at sunset (as I had been warned to do), and I walked on, that night and half next day, until I met (as I was warned I should) this comrade.", "저는 (그러라는 일러둠을 받은 대로) 해 질 녘에 떠났고, 그날 밤과 이튿날 한낮까지 걸어, (만나게 될 거라는 일러둠을 받은 대로) 이 동지를 만났습니다."], ["With him, I came on, now riding and now walking, through the rest of yesterday and through last night.", "그와 함께, 때로는 타고 때로는 걸어서, 어제 남은 시간과 간밤 내내 줄곧 왔습니다."], ["And here you see me!”", "그래서 여기 이렇게 와 있는 거지요!\""]], [["After a gloomy silence, the first Jacques said, “Good!", "음울한 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 첫번째 자끄가 말했다. \"좋소!"], ["You have acted and recounted faithfully.", "당신은 충실하게 행동했고 충실하게 이야기했소."], ["Will you wait for us a little, outside the door?”", "문밖에서 잠시 우리를 기다려주겠소?\""]], [["“Very willingly,”", "\"기꺼이요.\""], ["said the mender of roads.", "도로 보수공이 말했다."], ["Whom Defarge escorted to the top of the stairs, and, leaving seated there, returned.", "드파르주는 그를 계단 꼭대기까지 바래다주고, 거기 앉혀둔 채 돌아왔다."]], [["The three had risen, and their heads were together when he came back to the garret.", "그가 다락방으로 돌아왔을 때, 세 사람은 일어서서 머리를 맞대고 있었다."]], [["“How say you, Jacques?”", "\"어떻게 생각하시오, 자끄?\""], ["demanded Number One.", "첫번째가 물었다."], ["“To be registered?”", "\"등록할까요?\""]], [["“To be registered, as doomed to destruction,”", "\"멸망이 정해진 자로 등록합시다.\""], ["returned Defarge.", "드파르주가 대답했다."]], [["“Magnificent!”", "\"훌륭하군!\""], ["croaked the man with the craving.", "갈망하는 기색의 사내가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“The chateau, and all the race?”", "\"성도, 그 일족 전부도?\""], ["inquired the first.", "첫번째가 물었다."]], [["“The chateau and all the race,”", "\"성도, 그 일족 전부도.\""], ["returned Defarge.", "드파르주가 대답했다."], ["“Extermination.”", "\"몰살이오.\""]], [["The hungry man repeated, in a rapturous croak, “Magnificent!”", "그 굶주린 사내는 황홀한 쉰 목소리로 \"훌륭하군!\""], ["and began gnawing another finger.", "하고 되뇌고는, 또 다른 손가락을 깨물기 시작했다."]], [["“Are you sure,”", "\"분명하오?\""], ["asked Jacques Two, of Defarge, “that no embarrassment can arise from our manner of keeping the register?", "자끄 둘이 드파르주에게 물었다. \"우리가 명부를 보관하는 방식 때문에 곤란한 일이 생기지 않으리라는 게 확실하오?"], ["Without doubt it is safe, for no one beyond ourselves can decipher it; but shall we always be able to decipher it--or, I ought to say, will she?”", "의심할 여지 없이 안전하지요, 우리 말고는 누구도 그걸 해독할 수 없으니까요. 하지만 우리가 언제까지나 그걸 해독할 수 있겠소 — 아니, 이렇게 말해야겠군, 부인은 그럴 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Jacques,”", "\"자끄,\""], ["returned Defarge, drawing himself up, “if madame my wife undertook to keep the register in her memory alone, she would not lose a word of it--not a syllable of it.", "드파르주가 몸을 곧추세우며 대답했다. \"내 아내가 그 명부를 오로지 기억만으로 간직하겠다고 나선다 해도, 그녀는 한마디도 — 한 음절도 — 놓치지 않을 거요."], ["Knitted, in her own stitches and her own symbols, it will always be as plain to her as the sun.", "그녀 자신의 코와 그녀 자신의 기호로 떠 넣은 것이니, 그녀에게는 언제까지나 해처럼 환할 거요."], ["Confide in Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인을 믿으시오."], ["It would be easier for the weakest poltroon that lives, to erase himself from existence, than to erase one letter of his name or crimes from the knitted register of Madame Defarge.”", "세상에 둘도 없는 비겁한 겁쟁이라도 제 존재 자체를 지워 없애는 편이, 제 이름이나 죄목의 글자 하나를 드파르주 부인의 뜨개질 명부에서 지워내는 것보다 더 쉬울 거요.\""]], [["There was a murmur of confidence and approval, and then the man who hungered, asked: “Is this rustic to be sent back soon?", "신뢰와 동의의 웅성거림이 일었고, 이윽고 그 굶주린 사내가 물었다. \"이 촌사람은 곧 돌려보낼 거요?"], ["I hope so.", "그러길 바라오."], ["He is very simple; is he not a little dangerous?”", "저자는 아주 단순해. 좀 위험하지 않겠소?\""]], [["“He knows nothing,”", "\"저자는 아무것도 모르오.\""], ["said Defarge; “at least nothing more than would easily elevate himself to a gallows of the same height.", "드파르주가 말했다. \"적어도 저 자신을 같은 높이의 교수대로 손쉽게 올려보낼 만한 것 이상은 아무것도."], ["I charge myself with him; let him remain with me; I will take care of him, and set him on his road.", "저자는 내가 맡겠소. 나와 함께 있게 하시오."], ["He wishes to see the fine world--the King, the Queen, and Court; let him see them on Sunday.”", "내가 그를 돌보아, 길을 떠나게 하겠소. 저자는 화려한 세상 — 국왕과 왕비와 궁정 — 을 보고 싶어하니, 일요일에 그것들을 보게 합시다.\""]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["exclaimed the hungry man, staring.", "굶주린 사내가 빤히 쳐다보며 외쳤다."], ["“Is it a good sign, that he wishes to see Royalty and Nobility?”", "\"왕족과 귀족을 보고 싶어한다는 게 좋은 징조란 말이오?\""]], [["“Jacques,”", "\"자끄,\""], ["said Defarge; “judiciously show a cat milk, if you wish her to thirst for it.", "드파르주가 말했다. \"고양이가 우유를 탐내게 하고 싶거든, 우유를 솜씨 좋게 보여주시오."], ["Judiciously show a dog his natural prey, if you wish him to bring it down one day.”", "개가 언젠가 제 먹잇감을 쓰러뜨리게 하고 싶거든, 타고난 먹잇감을 솜씨 좋게 보여주시오.\""]], [["Nothing more was said, and the mender of roads, being found already dozing on the topmost stair, was advised to lay himself down on the pallet-bed and take some rest.", "더 이상 말은 없었고, 도로 보수공은 어느새 맨 위 계단에서 졸고 있는 것이 발견되어, 간이침대에 누워 좀 쉬라는 권유를 받았다."], ["He needed no persuasion, and was soon asleep.", "그는 설득할 필요도 없이 곧 잠들었다."]], [["Worse quarters than Defarge’s wine-shop, could easily have been found in Paris for a provincial slave of that degree.", "그 정도 신분의 시골 노예에게는, 빠리에서 드파르주의 술집보다 더 못한 거처도 얼마든지 찾을 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Saving for a mysterious dread of madame by which he was constantly haunted, his life was very new and agreeable.", "끊임없이 그를 따라다니는, 부인에 대한 알 수 없는 두려움만 빼면, 그의 생활은 무척 새롭고 유쾌했다."], ["But, madame sat all day at her counter, so expressly unconscious of him, and so particularly determined not to perceive that his being there had any connection with anything below the surface, that he shook in his wooden shoes whenever his eye lighted on her.", "그러나 부인은 온종일 계산대에 앉아, 어찌나 노골적으로 그를 의식하지 않는 척하는지, 또 그가 그곳에 있는 것이 겉으로 드러나지 않는 그 무엇과도 관련이 있다는 것을 어찌나 한사코 알아채지 않으려 하는지, 그는 그녀에게 눈길이 닿을 때마다 나막신 속에서 부들부들 떨었다."], ["For, he contended with himself that it was impossible to foresee what that lady might pretend next; and he felt assured that if she should take it into her brightly ornamented head to pretend that she had seen him do a murder and afterwards flay the victim, she would infallibly go through with it until the play was played out.", "저 부인이 다음번엔 또 무슨 시늉을 할지 도무지 예측할 수 없다고 그는 속으로 되뇌었기 때문이다. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그 화려하게 꾸민 머릿속에서, 자기가 그를 보았다는 — 그가 살인을 저지르고 그 후에 희생자의 가죽을 벗기는 것을 보았다는 — 시늉을 하기로 마음먹는다면, 그녀는 그 연극이 다 끝날 때까지 틀림없이 끝까지 밀고 나갈 거라고 그는 확신했다."]], [["Therefore, when Sunday came, the mender of roads was not enchanted (though he said he was) to find that madame was to accompany monsieur and himself to Versailles.", "그리하여 일요일이 되자, 도로 보수공은 부인이 주인 양반과 자기와 함께 베르사유에 동행하리라는 것을 알고 (좋다고 말은 했지만) 마냥 신이 나지는 않았다."], ["It was additionally disconcerting to have madame knitting all the way there, in a public conveyance; it was additionally disconcerting yet, to have madame in the crowd in the afternoon, still with her knitting in her hands as the crowd waited to see the carriage of the King and Queen.", "게다가 부인이 공공 마차 안에서 가는 내내 뜨개질을 하고 있는 것이 한층 더 마음을 어수선하게 했다. 한층 더 마음을 어수선하게 한 것은, 오후에 군중이 국왕과 왕비의 마차를 보려고 기다리는 동안, 부인이 여전히 손에 뜨개질감을 든 채 군중 속에 끼어 있다는 사실이었다."]], [["“You work hard, madame,”", "\"열심히 일하시는군요, 부인.\""], ["said a man near her.", "그녀 곁의 한 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Madame Defarge; “I have a good deal to do.”", "드파르주 부인이 대답했다. \"할 일이 많아서요.\""]], [["“What do you make, madame?”", "\"무얼 만드십니까, 부인?\""]], [["“Many things.”", "\"여러가지요.\""]], [["“For instance--”", "\"이를테면 —\""]], [["“For instance,”", "\"이를테면,\""], ["returned Madame Defarge, composedly, “shrouds.”", "드파르주 부인이 침착하게 대답했다. \"수의(壽衣) 같은 거요.\""]], [["The man moved a little further away, as soon as he could, and the mender of roads fanned himself with his blue cap: feeling it mightily close and oppressive.", "사내는 할 수 있는 한 빨리 조금 더 멀찍이 물러났고, 도로 보수공은 푸른 모자로 부채질을 했다. 몹시 후텁지근하고 숨이 막히는 것 같았기 때문이다."], ["If he needed a King and Queen to restore him, he was fortunate in having his remedy at hand; for, soon the large-faced King and the fair-faced Queen came in their golden coach, attended by the shining Bull’s Eye of their Court, a glittering multitude of laughing ladies and fine lords; and in jewels and silks and powder and splendour and elegantly spurning figures and handsomely disdainful faces of both sexes, the mender of roads bathed himself, so much to his temporary intoxication, that he cried Long live the King, Long live the Queen, Long live everybody and everything!", "그가 기운을 되찾으려면 국왕과 왕비가 필요했다면, 그는 그 처방을 바로 곁에 두고 있는 셈이니 운이 좋았다. 이내 얼굴이 큰 국왕과 얼굴이 고운 왕비가 황금 마차를 타고 나타났는데, 빛나는 궁정의 측근들이 — 웃어대는 귀부인들과 멋진 영주들의 반짝이는 무리가 — 뒤따랐다."], ["as if he had never heard of ubiquitous Jacques in his time.", "보석과 비단과 분과 화려함, 그리고 우아하게 발길질하는 자태와 점잖게 거들먹거리는 양성의 얼굴들 속에서, 도로 보수공은 한껏 멱을 감았다."], ["Then, there were gardens, courtyards, terraces, fountains, green banks, more King and Queen, more Bull’s Eye, more lords and ladies, more Long live they all!", "그것이 어찌나 그를 일시적으로 도취시켰던지, 그는 국왕 만세, 왕비 만세, 만인 만세, 만물 만세! 하고 외쳤으니, 마치 그가 살면서 어디에나 있는 자끄에 대해 들어본 적도 없는 듯했다."], ["until he absolutely wept with sentiment.", "이어서 정원과 안마당과 테라스와 분수와 푸른 둔덕이 있었고, 또다른 국왕과 왕비가, 또다른 측근들이, 또다른 영주와 귀부인들이, 또다시 그들 모두 만세!"], ["During the whole of this scene, which lasted some three hours, he had plenty of shouting and weeping and sentimental company, and throughout Defarge held him by the collar, as if to restrain him from flying at the objects of his brief devotion and tearing them to pieces.", "하는 외침이 이어졌으니, 마침내 그는 감격에 겨워 그야말로 눈물을 흘렸다. 세 시간쯤 이어진 이 광경 내내, 그에게는 함께 소리치고 울고 감격하는 동무들이 넘쳐났고, 그동안 드파르주는 마치 그가 잠시 동안의 숭배 대상에게 달려들어 갈가리 찢어놓지 못하게 막기라도 하듯 그의 멱살을 붙잡고 있었다."]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"잘했네!\""], ["said Defarge, clapping him on the back when it was over, like a patron; “you are a good boy!”", "일이 끝나자 드파르주가 후원자처럼 그의 등을 두드리며 말했다. \"자넨 착한 친구야!\""]], [["The mender of roads was now coming to himself, and was mistrustful of having made a mistake in his late demonstrations; but no.", "도로 보수공은 이제 정신이 들었고, 방금 자기가 한 짓에 무슨 잘못이라도 저지른 게 아닌가 미심쩍어했다. 그러나 그렇지 않았다."]], [["“You are the fellow we want,”", "\"자넨 우리가 원하는 바로 그런 사람일세.\""], ["said Defarge, in his ear; “you make these fools believe that it will last for ever.", "드파르주가 그의 귀에 대고 말했다. \"자넨 저 어리석은 것들에게 이게 영원히 이어질 거라고 믿게 만들지."], ["Then, they are the more insolent, and it is the nearer ended.”", "그러면 저들은 더욱 거만해지고, 그럴수록 끝이 더 가까워지는 거야.\""]], [["“Hey!”", "\"이런!\""], ["cried the mender of roads, reflectively; “that’s true.”", "도로 보수공이 곰곰이 생각하며 외쳤다. \"그게 참말이군요.\""]], [["“These fools know nothing.", "\"저 어리석은 것들은 아무것도 몰라."], ["While they despise your breath, and would stop it for ever and ever, in you or in a hundred like you rather than in one of their own horses or dogs, they only know what your breath tells them.", "저들은 자네 숨결을 경멸하면서, 제 말이나 개 한 마리보다도 자네나 자네 같은 백 명의 숨을 영영 끊어놓으려 하지만, 저들이 아는 건 오직 자네 숨결이 일러주는 것뿐이지."], ["Let it deceive them, then, a little longer; it cannot deceive them too much.”", "그러니 그 숨결로 저들을 조금 더 속이게나. 아무리 속여도 지나치지 않을 테니.\""]], [["Madame Defarge looked superciliously at the client, and nodded in confirmation.", "드파르주 부인이 거만한 눈초리로 그 손님을 바라보며 동의한다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“As to you,”", "\"당신으로 말하자면,\""], ["said she, “you would shout and shed tears for anything, if it made a show and a noise.", "그녀가 말했다. \"볼거리가 있고 떠들썩하기만 하면, 당신은 무엇에든 소리치고 눈물을 흘릴 사람이야."], ["Say!", "말해봐!"], ["Would you not?”", "안 그래?\""]], [["“Truly, madame, I think so.", "\"정말이지, 부인, 제 생각에도 그렇습니다."], ["For the moment.”", "그 순간만큼은요.\""]], [["“If you were shown a great heap of dolls, and were set upon them to pluck them to pieces and despoil them for your own advantage, you would pick out the richest and gayest. Say!", "\"인형이 한 무더기 쌓여 있는 걸 당신에게 보여주고, 당신더러 그것들을 갈가리 뜯어내 당신 잇속을 차리라고 풀어놓는다면, 당신은 가장 값지고 가장 화려한 걸 골라내겠지."], ["Would you not?”", "말해봐! 안 그래?\""]], [["“Truly yes, madame.”", "\"정말 그렇습니다, 부인.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["And if you were shown a flock of birds, unable to fly, and were set upon them to strip them of their feathers for your own advantage, you would set upon the birds of the finest feathers; would you not?”", "그리고 날지 못하는 새 떼를 당신에게 보여주고, 당신더러 당신 잇속을 차리려고 그 깃털을 뽑으라고 풀어놓는다면, 당신은 가장 고운 깃털을 가진 새들에게 달려들겠지. 안 그래?\""]], [["“It is true, madame.”", "\"맞습니다, 부인.\""]], [["“You have seen both dolls and birds to-day,”", "\"당신은 오늘 인형도 새도 다 보았어.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, with a wave of her hand towards the place where they had last been apparent; “now, go home!”", "드파르주 부인이 그것들이 마지막으로 보였던 곳을 향해 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"자, 이제 집으로 돌아가!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XVI. Still Knitting", "# CHAPTER XVI. Still Knitting"]], [["Madame Defarge and monsieur her husband returned amicably to the bosom of Saint Antoine, while a speck in a blue cap toiled through the darkness, and through the dust, and down the weary miles of avenue by the wayside, slowly tending towards that point of the compass where the chateau of Monsieur the Marquis, now in his grave, listened to the whispering trees.", "드파르주 부인과 그녀의 남편 되는 무슈는 사이좋게 쌩땅뚜안의 품으로 돌아갔고, 그러는 동안 파란 모자를 쓴 점 하나가 어둠을 뚫고, 먼지를 뚫고, 길가의 가로수 길을 따라 지친 발걸음으로 이정표를 헤아리며, 이제 무덤에 누운 후작 무슈의 성관이 속삭이는 나무들의 소리에 귀 기울이고 있는 그 방위를 향해 천천히 나아갔다."], ["Such ample leisure had the stone faces, now, for listening to the trees and to the fountain, that the few village scarecrows who, in their quest for herbs to eat and fragments of dead stick to burn, strayed within sight of the great stone courtyard and terrace staircase, had it borne in upon their starved fancy that the expression of the faces was altered.", "이제 그 돌 얼굴들에게는 나무와 분수의 소리에 귀 기울일 시간이 넉넉히 주어졌으니, 먹을 풀과 태울 마른 나뭇가지 조각을 찾아 헤매던 몇 안 되는 마을의 허수아비 같은 사람들이 그 거대한 돌 안뜰과 테라스 계단이 보이는 곳까지 흘러들어 왔다가, 그 굶주린 상상 속에서 얼굴들의 표정이 달라졌다는 느낌을 받았다."], ["A rumour just lived in the village--had a faint and bare existence there, as its people had--that when the knife struck home, the faces changed, from faces of pride to faces of anger and pain; also, that when that dangling figure was hauled up forty feet above the fountain, they changed again, and bore a cruel look of being avenged, which they would henceforth bear for ever.", "마을 안에는 하나의 소문이 가까스로 살아 있었으니 — 그곳 사람들이 그러하듯 희미하고 헐벗은 존재로 — 칼이 그 깊숙이 박혔을 때 얼굴들이 변하여, 오만한 얼굴에서 분노와 고통의 얼굴로 바뀌었다는 것이었고, 또한 그 매달린 형상이 분수 위 사십 피트 높이로 끌어 올려졌을 때 다시 한번 변하여, 복수를 이루었다는 잔혹한 표정을 띠었으며, 이후로 그 표정을 영원히 지니게 되었다는 것이었다."], ["In the stone face over the great window of the bed-chamber where the murder was done, two fine dints were pointed out in the sculptured nose, which everybody recognised, and which nobody had seen of old; and on the scarce occasions when two or three ragged peasants emerged from the crowd to take a hurried peep at Monsieur the Marquis petrified, a skinny finger would not have pointed to it for a minute, before they all started away among the moss and leaves, like the more fortunate hares who could find a living there.", "살인이 저질러진 침실의 큰 창문 위 돌 얼굴에는, 조각된 코에 두 줄기 미세한 자국이 나 있었으니, 누구나 그것을 알아보았으나 예전에는 아무도 본 적이 없는 자국이었다. 그리고 누더기를 걸친 농부 두셋이 군중 속에서 빠져나와 돌처럼 굳어버린 후작 무슈를 황급히 훔쳐보던 드문 경우에, 앙상한 손가락이 그것을 가리킨 지 채 일 분도 지나지 않아, 그들은 모두 이끼와 나뭇잎 사이로 달아나버렸으니, 그곳에서 먹고살 길을 찾을 수 있던 좀더 운 좋은 산토끼들과 다를 바 없었다."]], [["Chateau and hut, stone face and dangling figure, the red stain on the stone floor, and the pure water in the village well--thousands of acres of land--a whole province of France--all France itself--lay under the night sky, concentrated into a faint hair-breadth line.", "성관과 오두막, 돌 얼굴과 매달린 형상, 돌바닥의 붉은 얼룩, 그리고 마을 우물의 맑은 물 — 수천 에이커의 땅 — 프랑스의 한 지방 전체 — 프랑스 그 자체가 모두 — 밤하늘 아래 놓여, 머리카락 한 올 너비의 희미한 선 안으로 응축되었다."], ["So does a whole world, with all its greatnesses and littlenesses, lie in a twinkling star.", "마찬가지로 온 세계가 그 모든 위대함과 하찮음을 안고 반짝이는 별 하나 속에 놓여 있다."], ["And as mere human knowledge can split a ray of light and analyse the manner of its composition, so, sublimer intelligences may read in the feeble shining of this earth of ours, every thought and act, every vice and virtue, of every responsible creature on it.", "그리고 한낱 인간의 지식이 빛줄기 하나를 쪼개어 그것이 어떻게 이루어졌는지를 분석할 수 있듯이, 더 숭고한 지성이라면 우리네 이 지상의 미약한 빛 속에서, 그 위에 있는 책임 있는 모든 존재의 온갖 생각과 행위, 온갖 악덕과 미덕을 읽어낼 수 있으리라."]], [["The Defarges, husband and wife, came lumbering under the starlight, in their public vehicle, to that gate of Paris whereunto their journey naturally tended.", "드파르주 부부는 별빛 아래 그들의 합승마차를 타고 덜컹거리며, 그들의 여정이 자연히 향하게 마련인 빠리의 그 성문에 다다랐다."], ["There was the usual stoppage at the barrier guardhouse, and the usual lanterns came glancing forth for the usual examination and inquiry.", "거기에는 늘 그렇듯 검문소에서의 정차가 있었고, 늘 그렇듯 등불들이 늘 그렇듯 검문과 심문을 위해 흔들리며 다가왔다."], ["Monsieur Defarge alighted; knowing one or two of the soldiery there, and one of the police.", "드파르주 무슈가 마차에서 내렸으니, 거기 군인 한둘과 경찰 한 명을 알고 있었던 것이다."], ["The latter he was intimate with, and affectionately embraced.", "후자와는 친한 사이여서, 다정하게 끌어안았다."]], [["When Saint Antoine had again enfolded the Defarges in his dusky wings, and they, having finally alighted near the Saint’s boundaries, were picking their way on foot through the black mud and offal of his streets, Madame Defarge spoke to her husband:", "쌩땅뚜안이 다시 한번 그 거뭇한 날개로 드파르주 부부를 감싸 안았을 때, 그리고 그들이 마침내 그 성인의 경계 부근에서 내려, 그 거리의 시커먼 진창과 오물을 헤치며 걸어서 길을 찾아갈 때, 드파르주 부인이 남편에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Say then, my friend; what did Jacques of the police tell thee?”", "\"그래서, 여보, 경찰의 자끄가 당신한테 뭐라고 했어요?\""]], [["“Very little to-night, but all he knows.", "\"오늘 밤엔 별로 없지만, 그가 아는 건 다 말해줬소."], ["There is another spy commissioned for our quarter.", "우리 구역에 또다른 끄나풀이 임명됐다더군."], ["There may be many more, for all that he can say, but he knows of one.”", "그가 말할 수 있는 한에서는 더 많을지도 모르지만, 그가 아는 건 한 명이오.\""]], [["“Eh well!”", "\"좋아요!\""], ["said Madame Defarge, raising her eyebrows with a cool business air.", "드파르주 부인이 차분하고 사무적인 태도로 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다."], ["“It is necessary to register him.", "\"그자를 등록해두는 게 필요하겠군요."], ["How do they call that man?”", "그 사내를 뭐라고 부른답니까?\""]], [["“He is English.”", "\"영국인이오.\""]], [["“So much the better.", "\"그럴수록 더 좋지요."], ["His name?”", "이름은?\""]], [["“Barsad,”", "\"바사드요.\""], ["said Defarge, making it French by pronunciation.", "드파르주가 발음하여 프랑스식으로 만들며 말했다."], ["But, he had been so careful to get it accurately, that he then spelt it with perfect correctness.", "그러나 그는 그것을 정확히 알아두려고 무척 신경을 쓴 터라, 이어 한 치의 오차도 없이 철자를 댔다."]], [["“Barsad,”", "\"바사드라.\""], ["repeated madame.", "부인이 되뇌었다."], ["“Good.", "\"좋아요."], ["Christian name?”", "세례명은?\""]], [["“John.”", "\"존이오.\""]], [["“John Barsad,”", "\"존 바사드.\""], ["repeated madame, after murmuring it once to herself.", "부인이 혼잣말로 한 번 중얼거린 뒤 되뇌었다. \"좋아요."], ["“Good. His appearance; is it known?”", "그자의 생김새는, 알려져 있나요?\""]], [["“Age, about forty years; height, about five feet nine; black hair; complexion dark; generally, rather handsome visage; eyes dark, face thin, long, and sallow; nose aquiline, but not straight, having a peculiar inclination towards the left cheek; expression, therefore, sinister.”", "\"나이는 마흔 안팎, 키는 오 피트 구 인치쯤, 검은 머리에 가무잡잡한 피부, 대체로 제법 잘생긴 얼굴, 짙은 눈, 가늘고 길며 누르스름한 얼굴, 매부리코지만 곧지 않고 왼쪽 뺨 쪽으로 묘하게 기울어져 있어, 그 때문에 표정이 음흉하오.\""]], [["“Eh my faith.", "\"이런, 맙소사."], ["It is a portrait!”", "이건 초상화로군요!\""], ["said madame, laughing.", "부인이 웃으며 말했다."], ["“He shall be registered to-morrow.”", "\"내일 그자를 등록해두겠어요.\""]], [["They turned into the wine-shop, which was closed (for it was midnight), and where Madame Defarge immediately took her post at her desk, counted the small moneys that had been taken during her absence, examined the stock, went through the entries in the book, made other entries of her own, checked the serving man in every possible way, and finally dismissed him to bed.", "그들은 술집으로 들어섰는데, (자정이었으므로) 문이 닫혀 있었다. 드파르주 부인은 곧장 계산대의 자기 자리에 앉아, 자리를 비운 사이 들어온 잔돈을 세고, 재고를 살피고, 장부의 기재 사항을 훑어보고, 자기 나름의 기재 사항을 적어 넣고, 가능한 모든 방식으로 점원을 점검한 뒤, 마침내 그를 잠자리로 보냈다."], ["Then she turned out the contents of the bowl of money for the second time, and began knotting them up in her handkerchief, in a chain of separate knots, for safe keeping through the night.", "그러고는 두 번째로 돈 그릇의 내용물을 쏟아, 밤새 안전하게 간수하려고 손수건에다 따로따로 매듭을 지어 사슬처럼 묶기 시작했다."], ["All this while, Defarge, with his pipe in his mouth, walked up and down, complacently admiring, but never interfering; in which condition, indeed, as to the business and his domestic affairs, he walked up and down through life.", "그러는 내내 드파르주는 파이프를 입에 문 채 위아래로 거닐며, 흐뭇하게 감탄하면서도 결코 참견하지 않았으니, 실로 장사와 집안일에 관한 한, 그는 평생을 그러한 상태로 거닐며 살아왔던 것이다."]], [["The night was hot, and the shop, close shut and surrounded by so foul a neighbourhood, was ill-smelling.", "밤은 무더웠고, 꽉 닫힌 채 그토록 더러운 동네에 둘러싸인 술집에서는 고약한 냄새가 났다."], ["Monsieur Defarge’s olfactory sense was by no means delicate, but the stock of wine smelt much stronger than it ever tasted, and so did the stock of rum and brandy and aniseed.", "드파르주 무슈의 후각은 결코 예민하지 않았으나, 재어둔 포도주는 그 맛보다 훨씬 강한 냄새를 풍겼고, 럼주와 브랜디와 아니스 술도 마찬가지였다."], ["He whiffed the compound of scents away, as he put down his smoked-out pipe.", "그는 다 피운 파이프를 내려놓으며, 그 뒤섞인 냄새들을 후 불어 날려버렸다."]], [["“You are fatigued,”", "\"피곤하신가봐요.\""], ["said madame, raising her glance as she knotted the money.", "부인이 돈을 묶다가 시선을 들며 말했다."], ["“There are only the usual odours.”", "\"여느 때 나는 냄새일 뿐인데요.\""]], [["“I am a little tired,”", "\"좀 피곤하군.\""], ["her husband acknowledged.", "남편이 인정했다."]], [["“You are a little depressed, too,”", "\"좀 우울하시기도 하고요.\""], ["said madame, whose quick eyes had never been so intent on the accounts, but they had had a ray or two for him.", "부인이 말했는데, 그 날카로운 눈은 장부에 그토록 골똘히 박혀 있던 적이 없었으나, 그래도 남편에게 한두 줄기 시선을 던지고 있었다."], ["“Oh, the men, the men!”", "\"아이고, 남자들이란, 남자들이란!\""]], [["“But my dear!”", "\"하지만 여보!\""], ["began Defarge.", "드파르주가 입을 열었다."]], [["“But my dear!”", "\"하지만 여보!\""], ["repeated madame, nodding firmly; “but my dear!", "부인이 단호하게 고개를 끄덕이며 되뇌었다. \"하지만 여보라니!"], ["You are faint of heart to-night, my dear!”", "당신 오늘 밤 마음이 약해졌어요, 여보!\""]], [["“Well, then,”", "\"그래, 그렇다면,\""], ["said Defarge, as if a thought were wrung out of his breast, “it is a long time.”", "드파르주가 마치 가슴속에서 어떤 생각이 짜내어지듯 말했다. \"이건 오랜 시간이 걸리는 일이야.\""]], [["“It is a long time,”", "\"오랜 시간이 걸리는 일이지요.\""], ["repeated his wife; “and when is it not a long time?", "그의 아내가 되뇌었다. \"그런데 언제는 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않았나요?"], ["Vengeance and retribution require a long time; it is the rule.”", "복수와 응징에는 오랜 시간이 걸려요. 그게 법칙이에요.\""]], [["“It does not take a long time to strike a man with Lightning,”", "\"벼락으로 사람을 내려치는 데에는 오랜 시간이 걸리지 않잖소.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“How long,”", "\"그 벼락을 만들어 쌓아두는 데에는,\""], ["demanded madame, composedly, “does it take to make and store the lightning?", "부인이 침착하게 물었다. \"얼마나 오래 걸리지요?"], ["Tell me.”", "말해보세요.\""]], [["Defarge raised his head thoughtfully, as if there were something in that too.", "드파르주는 거기에도 무언가 있다는 듯, 생각에 잠겨 고개를 들었다."]], [["“It does not take a long time,”", "\"지진이 도시 하나를 삼키는 데에는,\""], ["said madame, “for an earthquake to swallow a town.", "부인이 말했다. \"오랜 시간이 걸리지 않아요."], ["Eh well!", "그렇지요!"], ["Tell me how long it takes to prepare the earthquake?”", "그럼 그 지진을 준비하는 데에는 얼마나 오래 걸리는지 말해보세요.\""]], [["“A long time, I suppose,”", "\"오래 걸리겠지, 아마.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“But when it is ready, it takes place, and grinds to pieces everything before it.", "\"하지만 일단 준비가 되면, 그것은 일어나서, 앞에 놓인 모든 것을 산산이 부숴버려요."], ["In the meantime, it is always preparing, though it is not seen or heard.", "그러는 동안에도 그것은 늘 준비되고 있지요, 비록 보이지도 들리지도 않지만. 그게 당신에게 위안이에요."], ["That is your consolation. Keep it.”", "그 위안을 간직해두세요.\""]], [["She tied a knot with flashing eyes, as if it throttled a foe.", "그녀는 마치 적의 목을 조르듯, 번득이는 눈으로 매듭 하나를 묶었다."]], [["“I tell thee,”", "\"내 당신에게 말하지만,\""], ["said madame, extending her right hand, for emphasis, “that although it is a long time on the road, it is on the road and coming.", "부인이 강조하느라 오른손을 뻗으며 말했다. \"비록 그것이 오는 길이 멀다 해도, 그것은 길 위에 있고 오고 있어요."], ["I tell thee it never retreats, and never stops. I tell thee it is always advancing.", "내 당신에게 말하지만, 그것은 결코 물러서지 않고, 결코 멈추지 않아요."], ["Look around and consider the lives of all the world that we know, consider the faces of all the world that we know, consider the rage and discontent to which the Jacquerie addresses itself with more and more of certainty every hour.", "내 당신에게 말하지만, 그것은 늘 전진하고 있어요. 주위를 둘러보고 우리가 아는 온 세상 사람들의 삶을 생각해보세요, 우리가 아는 온 세상 사람들의 얼굴을 생각해보세요, 자끄리가 시시각각 점점 더 확실하게 호소하고 있는 그 분노와 불만을 생각해보세요."], ["Can such things last?", "이런 일들이 오래갈 수 있을까요?"], ["Bah!", "흥!"], ["I mock you.”", "당신을 비웃어주겠어요.\""]], [["“My brave wife,”", "\"내 용감한 아내여,\""], ["returned Defarge, standing before her with his head a little bent, and his hands clasped at his back, like a docile and attentive pupil before his catechist, “I do not question all this.", "드파르주가 고개를 약간 숙이고 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡은 채, 마치 교리문답 선생 앞에 선 온순하고 주의 깊은 학생처럼 그녀 앞에 서서 대답했다. \"나는 이 모든 걸 의심하지 않소."], ["But it has lasted a long time, and it is possible--you know well, my wife, it is possible--that it may not come, during our lives.”", "하지만 이건 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 또 가능한 일이오 — 당신도 잘 알다시피, 여보, 가능한 일이오 — 그것이 우리 생애 동안에는 오지 않을 수도 있다는 게.\""]], [["“Eh well! How then?”", "\"그래요!"], ["demanded madame, tying another knot, as if there were another enemy strangled.", "그러면 어떻단 말이에요?\" 부인이 마치 또다른 적의 목을 조르듯 또 하나의 매듭을 묶으며 물었다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"글쎄!\""], ["said Defarge, with a half complaining and half apologetic shrug.", "드파르주가 반쯤은 불평하고 반쯤은 변명하는 투로 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."], ["“We shall not see the triumph.”", "\"우리는 그 승리를 보지 못할 거요.\""]], [["“We shall have helped it,”", "\"우리는 거기에 보탬이 되었을 거예요.\""], ["returned madame, with her extended hand in strong action.", "부인이 뻗은 손을 힘차게 놀리며 대답했다."], ["“Nothing that we do, is done in vain.", "\"우리가 하는 일은 그 어느 것도 헛되지 않아요."], ["I believe, with all my soul, that we shall see the triumph.", "나는 온 영혼을 다해, 우리가 그 승리를 보리라고 믿어요."], ["But even if not, even if I knew certainly not, show me the neck of an aristocrat and tyrant, and still I would--”", "하지만 설령 그렇지 못한다 해도, 설령 내가 분명히 못 보리라는 걸 안다 해도, 귀족과 폭군의 목덜미를 내게 보여주기만 하면, 나는 그래도 —\""]], [["Then madame, with her teeth set, tied a very terrible knot indeed.", "그러고 나서 부인은 이를 악물고, 실로 무시무시한 매듭 하나를 묶었다."]], [["“Hold!”", "\"그만!\""], ["cried Defarge, reddening a little as if he felt charged with cowardice; “I too, my dear, will stop at nothing.”", "드파르주가 마치 비겁하다는 비난을 받은 듯 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다. \"나도, 여보, 어떤 것에도 멈추지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Yes!", "\"그래요!"], ["But it is your weakness that you sometimes need to see your victim and your opportunity, to sustain you.", "하지만 가끔 자신을 지탱하기 위해 자기 희생자와 자기 기회를 봐야만 한다는 게 당신의 약점이에요."], ["Sustain yourself without that.", "그런 것 없이도 자신을 지탱하세요."], ["When the time comes, let loose a tiger and a devil; but wait for the time with the tiger and the devil chained--not shown--yet always ready.”", "때가 오면, 호랑이와 악마를 풀어놓으세요. 하지만 그때가 오기를 기다리는 동안에는 호랑이와 악마를 사슬에 묶어두세요 — 드러내지 말되 — 그러나 늘 준비된 채로.\""]], [["Madame enforced the conclusion of this piece of advice by striking her little counter with her chain of money as if she knocked its brains out, and then gathering the heavy handkerchief under her arm in a serene manner, and observing that it was time to go to bed.", "부인은 마치 그것의 골을 박살내듯 자신의 작은 계산대를 돈 사슬로 내리치며 이 충고의 결론을 강조한 뒤, 차분한 태도로 그 묵직한 손수건을 팔 밑에 챙겨 들고는, 이제 잘 시간이라고 말했다."]], [["Next noontide saw the admirable woman in her usual place in the wine-shop, knitting away assiduously.", "이튿날 정오, 그 훌륭한 여인은 술집의 늘 그렇듯 제자리에서 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있었다."], ["A rose lay beside her, and if she now and then glanced at the flower, it was with no infraction of her usual preoccupied air.", "그녀 곁에는 장미 한 송이가 놓여 있었는데, 이따금 그 꽃을 흘끗 보더라도, 그것은 평소의 골똘한 태도를 조금도 흩뜨리지 않았다."], ["There were a few customers, drinking or not drinking, standing or seated, sprinkled about.", "손님 몇이 술을 마시거나 마시지 않거나, 서 있거나 앉아 있거나 하며 여기저기 흩어져 있었다."], ["The day was very hot, and heaps of flies, who were extending their inquisitive and adventurous perquisitions into all the glutinous little glasses near madame, fell dead at the bottom.", "그날은 몹시 무더웠고, 떼 지은 파리들이 부인 가까이에 놓인 끈적한 작은 잔마다 호기심 어린 모험심으로 탐사를 넓혀 가다가, 그 바닥에서 죽어 나자빠졌다."], ["Their decease made no impression on the other flies out promenading, who looked at them in the coolest manner (as if they themselves were elephants, or something as far removed), until they met the same fate.", "그것들의 죽음은 산책하러 나온 다른 파리들에게는 아무런 감흥도 주지 못했으니, 그것들은 마치 자기네가 코끼리거나 그만큼이나 동떨어진 무엇이라도 되는 양 더없이 태연하게 죽은 것들을 바라보다가, 결국 자기들도 같은 운명을 맞았다."], ["Curious to consider how heedless flies are!--perhaps they thought as much at Court that sunny summer day.", "파리들이 얼마나 무심한지를 생각하면 묘하다! — 아마 그 햇살 좋은 여름날 궁정에서도 다들 그만큼이나 생각했을 터이다."]], [["A figure entering at the door threw a shadow on Madame Defarge which she felt to be a new one.", "문으로 들어선 어떤 형상이 드파르주 부인에게 그림자를 드리웠는데, 그녀는 그것이 낯선 그림자임을 느꼈다."], ["She laid down her knitting, and began to pin her rose in her head-dress, before she looked at the figure.", "그녀는 뜨개질감을 내려놓고, 그 형상을 보기에 앞서, 머리쓰개에 장미를 꽂기 시작했다."]], [["It was curious.", "기이한 일이었다."], ["The moment Madame Defarge took up the rose, the customers ceased talking, and began gradually to drop out of the wine-shop.", "드파르주 부인이 장미를 집어 든 바로 그 순간, 손님들은 이야기를 멈추고, 차츰 술집에서 빠져나가기 시작했다."]], [["“Good day, madame,”", "\"안녕하시오, 부인.\""], ["said the new-comer.", "새로 들어온 자가 말했다."]], [["“Good day, monsieur.”", "\"안녕하세요, 무슈.\""]], [["She said it aloud, but added to herself, as she resumed her knitting: “Hah!", "그녀는 소리 내어 그렇게 말했으나, 뜨개질을 다시 시작하면서 속으로 덧붙였다."], ["Good day, age about forty, height about five feet nine, black hair, generally rather handsome visage, complexion dark, eyes dark, thin, long and sallow face, aquiline nose but not straight, having a peculiar inclination towards the left cheek which imparts a sinister expression!", "\"하! 안녕하시오, 나이는 마흔 안팎, 키는 오 피트 구 인치쯤, 검은 머리, 대체로 제법 잘생긴 얼굴, 가무잡잡한 피부, 짙은 눈, 가늘고 길며 누르스름한 얼굴, 매부리코지만 곧지 않고 왼쪽 뺨 쪽으로 묘하게 기울어져 음흉한 표정을 자아내는 자!"], ["Good day, one and all!”", "안녕하시오, 하나도 빠짐없이!\""]], [["“Have the goodness to give me a little glass of old cognac, and a mouthful of cool fresh water, madame.”", "\"오래된 꼬냑 한 잔하고, 시원하고 신선한 물 한 모금 베풀어주시면 고맙겠소, 부인.\""]], [["Madame complied with a polite air.", "부인이 정중한 태도로 응했다."]], [["“Marvellous cognac this, madame!”", "\"이 꼬냑, 기막히군요, 부인!\""]], [["It was the first time it had ever been so complimented, and Madame Defarge knew enough of its antecedents to know better.", "그것이 그토록 칭찬받은 것은 처음 있는 일이었고, 드파르주 부인은 그 내력을 잘 알고 있던 터라 그렇지 않다는 걸 알고 있었다."], ["She said, however, that the cognac was flattered, and took up her knitting.", "그래도 그녀는 꼬냑이 과찬을 받았다고 말한 뒤, 뜨개질감을 집어 들었다."], ["The visitor watched her fingers for a few moments, and took the opportunity of observing the place in general.", "손님은 잠시 그녀의 손가락을 지켜보다가, 그 틈을 타 가게 안 전체를 살폈다."]], [["“You knit with great skill, madame.”", "\"솜씨가 대단하시군요, 부인.\""]], [["“I am accustomed to it.”", "\"익숙해서요.\""]], [["“A pretty pattern too!”", "\"무늬도 예쁘고요!\""]], [["“You think so?”", "\"그렇게 보이세요?\""], ["said madame, looking at him with a smile.", "부인이 미소 지으며 그를 바라보고 말했다."]], [["“Decidedly.", "\"틀림없이."], ["May one ask what it is for?”", "무엇에 쓰는 건지 여쭤도 될까요?\""]], [["“Pastime,”", "\"심심풀이지요.\""], ["said madame, still looking at him with a smile while her fingers moved nimbly.", "부인이 손가락을 날렵하게 놀리면서도 여전히 미소 지으며 그를 바라보고 말했다."]], [["“Not for use?”", "\"쓸 데가 있는 건 아니고요?\""]], [["“That depends.", "\"그건 모를 일이지요."], ["I may find a use for it one day.", "언젠가 쓸 데를 찾을지도요."], ["If I do--Well,”", "그렇게 되면 — 글쎄,\""], ["said madame, drawing a breath and nodding her head with a stern kind of coquetry, “I’ll use it!”", "부인이 숨을 들이쉬고 준엄한 교태 같은 것으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그땐 쓸 거예요!\""]], [["It was remarkable; but, the taste of Saint Antoine seemed to be decidedly opposed to a rose on the head-dress of Madame Defarge.", "기이한 일이었으나, 쌩땅뚜안의 취향은 드파르주 부인의 머리쓰개에 꽂힌 장미에 확연히 반대하는 듯했다."], ["Two men had entered separately, and had been about to order drink, when, catching sight of that novelty, they faltered, made a pretence of looking about as if for some friend who was not there, and went away.", "두 사내가 따로따로 들어와 술을 주문하려다가, 그 새로운 물건을 발견하고는 머뭇거리며, 마치 거기 있지도 않은 친구를 찾는 척하며 두리번거리다가, 가버렸다."], ["Nor, of those who had been there when this visitor entered, was there one left.", "또한 이 손님이 들어왔을 때 거기 있던 자들 가운데, 남아 있는 자는 하나도 없었다."], ["They had all dropped off.", "그들은 모두 빠져나갔다."], ["The spy had kept his eyes open, but had been able to detect no sign.", "끄나풀은 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었으나, 아무런 낌새도 알아채지 못했다."], ["They had lounged away in a poverty-stricken, purposeless, accidental manner, quite natural and unimpeachable.", "그들은 궁색하고, 목적 없고, 우연한 태도로, 지극히 자연스럽고 흠잡을 데 없이 어슬렁어슬렁 빠져나갔던 것이다."]], [["“John,”", "'존이라.'"], ["thought madame, checking off her work as her fingers knitted, and her eyes looked at the stranger.", "부인은 손가락으로 뜨개질을 하면서 자기 일감을 점검하고, 눈으로는 그 낯선 자를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["“Stay long enough, and I shall knit ‘BARSAD’ before you go.”", "'오래 머물러봐, 그럼 네가 가기 전에 내가 「바사드」를 떠 넣어줄 테니.'"]], [["“You have a husband, madame?”", "\"부군이 있으시오, 부인?\""]], [["“I have.”", "\"있어요.\""]], [["“Children?”", "\"자녀는?\""]], [["“No children.”", "\"자녀는 없어요.\""]], [["“Business seems bad?”", "\"장사가 신통치 않은가보군요?\""]], [["“Business is very bad; the people are so poor.”", "\"장사는 아주 신통치 않아요. 사람들이 워낙 가난해서요.\""]], [["“Ah, the unfortunate, miserable people!", "\"아, 불운하고, 비참한 사람들이지요!"], ["So oppressed, too--as you say.”", "게다가 그토록 억압받고 — 당신 말마따나.\""]], [["“As you say,”", "\"당신 말마따나지요.\""], ["madame retorted, correcting him, and deftly knitting an extra something into his name that boded him no good.", "부인이 그를 바로잡으며 받아쳤고, 그의 이름에다 그에게 이로울 게 없는 무언가를 한 코 더 능숙하게 떠 넣었다."]], [["“Pardon me; certainly it was I who said so, but you naturally think so.", "\"미안하오. 분명히 그렇게 말한 건 나였지만, 당신도 당연히 그리 생각하겠지요."], ["Of course.”", "물론이오.\""]], [["“I think?”", "\"내가 생각한다고요?\""], ["returned madame, in a high voice.", "부인이 높은 목소리로 받았다."], ["“I and my husband have enough to do to keep this wine-shop open, without thinking.", "\"나하고 내 남편은 생각할 겨를도 없이 이 술집을 열어두는 것만으로도 벅차요."], ["All we think, here, is how to live.", "여기서 우리가 생각하는 거라곤 어떻게 먹고사느냐 하는 것뿐이에요."], ["That is the subject we think of, and it gives us, from morning to night, enough to think about, without embarrassing our heads concerning others.", "그게 우리가 생각하는 주제이고, 그것만으로도 아침부터 밤까지 머릿속을 남들 일로 번거롭게 할 것도 없이 생각할 거리가 차고 넘쳐요. 내가 남들 생각을 한다고요?"], ["I think for others? No, no.”", "아니, 아니에요.\""]], [["The spy, who was there to pick up any crumbs he could find or make, did not allow his baffled state to express itself in his sinister face; but, stood with an air of gossiping gallantry, leaning his elbow on Madame Defarge’s little counter, and occasionally sipping his cognac.", "끄나풀은 찾아내거나 만들어낼 수 있는 부스러기라면 무엇이든 주워 모으려고 거기 와 있던 터라, 자신의 낭패한 상태를 그 음흉한 얼굴에 드러내지 않았다. 다만, 드파르주 부인의 작은 계산대에 팔꿈치를 괴고서, 한담을 나누는 듯한 정중한 태도로 서서, 이따금 꼬냑을 홀짝였다."]], [["“A bad business this, madame, of Gaspard’s execution.", "\"가스빠르의 처형 말인데, 그건 안된 일이었소, 부인."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["the poor Gaspard!”", "가엾은 가스빠르!\""], ["With a sigh of great compassion.", "깊은 연민의 한숨과 함께였다."]], [["“My faith!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["returned madame, coolly and lightly, “if people use knives for such purposes, they have to pay for it.", "부인이 차갑고 가볍게 받았다. \"사람들이 그런 목적으로 칼을 쓴다면, 그 대가를 치러야지요."], ["He knew beforehand what the price of his luxury was; he has paid the price.”", "그자는 자기 사치의 값이 무엇인지 미리 알고 있었어요. 그 값을 치른 거고요.\""]], [["“I believe,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["said the spy, dropping his soft voice to a tone that invited confidence, and expressing an injured revolutionary susceptibility in every muscle of his wicked face: “I believe there is much compassion and anger in this neighbourhood, touching the poor fellow?", "끄나풀이 부드러운 목소리를 더욱 낮춰 신뢰를 자아내는 어조로, 그 사악한 얼굴 근육 하나하나에 상처 입은 혁명가의 감수성을 드러내며 말했다. \"내 생각엔 이 동네에 그 가엾은 친구를 두고 연민과 분노가 많은 것 같소만?"], ["Between ourselves.”", "우리끼리 얘기지만.\""]], [["“Is there?”", "\"그런가요?\""], ["asked madame, vacantly.", "부인이 멍하니 물었다."]], [["“Is there not?”", "\"그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“--Here is my husband!”", "\"— 마침 내 남편이 오는군요!\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."]], [["As the keeper of the wine-shop entered at the door, the spy saluted him by touching his hat, and saying, with an engaging smile, “Good day, Jacques!”", "술집 주인이 문으로 들어서자, 끄나풀이 모자에 손을 대며 인사하고는, 호감을 사려는 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"안녕하시오, 자끄!\""], ["Defarge stopped short, and stared at him.", "드파르주는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그를 응시했다."]], [["“Good day, Jacques!”", "\"안녕하시오, 자끄!\""], ["the spy repeated; with not quite so much confidence, or quite so easy a smile under the stare.", "끄나풀이 되뇌었으나, 그 응시 아래서 그리 자신만만하지도, 그리 편안한 미소도 짓지 못했다."]], [["“You deceive yourself, monsieur,”", "\"착각하셨소, 무슈.\" 술집 주인이 받았다."], ["returned the keeper of the wine-shop.", "\"나를 다른 사람으로 잘못 보셨군요."], ["“You mistake me for another.", "그건 내 이름이 아니오."], ["That is not my name. I am Ernest Defarge.”", "나는 에르네스뜨 드파르주요.\""]], [["“It is all the same,”", "\"그게 그거지요.\""], ["said the spy, airily, but discomfited too: “good day!”", "끄나풀이 가볍게 말했으나 당황한 기색도 있었다. \"안녕하시오!\""]], [["“Good day!”", "\"안녕하시오!\""], ["answered Defarge, drily.", "드파르주가 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["“I was saying to madame, with whom I had the pleasure of chatting when you entered, that they tell me there is--and no wonder!--much sympathy and anger in Saint Antoine, touching the unhappy fate of poor Gaspard.”", "\"당신이 들어왔을 때 부인과 즐겁게 얘기를 나누던 참인데, 부인께 말하던 게 있소. 사람들 말로는 — 당연한 일이지만! — 쌩땅뚜안에 가엾은 가스빠르의 불행한 운명을 두고 동정과 분노가 많다더군요.\""]], [["“No one has told me so,”", "\"나한테는 아무도 그런 말을 하지 않았소.\""], ["said Defarge, shaking his head.", "드파르주가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["“I know nothing of it.”", "\"나는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르오.\""]], [["Having said it, he passed behind the little counter, and stood with his hand on the back of his wife’s chair, looking over that barrier at the person to whom they were both opposed, and whom either of them would have shot with the greatest satisfaction.", "이렇게 말한 뒤, 그는 작은 계산대 뒤로 돌아가, 아내의 의자 등받이에 손을 얹고 서서, 그 칸막이 너머로 그들 두 사람이 다 같이 적대하는 자를, 그리고 둘 중 누구라도 더없이 흡족하게 쏘아 죽였을 법한 자를 바라보았다."]], [["The spy, well used to his business, did not change his unconscious attitude, but drained his little glass of cognac, took a sip of fresh water, and asked for another glass of cognac.", "자기 일에 잘 단련된 끄나풀은 그 무심한 태도를 바꾸지 않고, 작은 꼬냑 잔을 비우고, 신선한 물을 한 모금 마신 뒤, 꼬냑을 한 잔 더 청했다."], ["Madame Defarge poured it out for him, took to her knitting again, and hummed a little song over it.", "드파르주 부인은 그것을 따라주고, 다시 뜨개질감을 집어 들고는, 그 위에 대고 짧은 노래를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["“You seem to know this quarter well; that is to say, better than I do?”", "\"이 구역을 잘 아시는 것 같군요. 다시 말해, 나보다 더 잘?\""], ["observed Defarge.", "드파르주가 한마디 했다."]], [["“Not at all, but I hope to know it better.", "\"천만에요, 하지만 더 잘 알고 싶소."], ["I am so profoundly interested in its miserable inhabitants.”", "나는 이곳의 비참한 주민들에게 깊은 관심이 있으니까요.\""]], [["“Hah!”", "\"하!\""], ["muttered Defarge.", "드파르주가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“The pleasure of conversing with you, Monsieur Defarge, recalls to me,”", "\"당신과 이야기를 나누는 즐거움을 누리니 떠오르는 게 있소, 드파르주 무슈.\""], ["pursued the spy, “that I have the honour of cherishing some interesting associations with your name.”", "끄나풀이 말을 이었다. \"당신 이름과 더불어 흥미로운 인연을 소중히 간직하는 영광을 누리고 있다는 거 말이오.\""]], [["“Indeed!”", "\"그래요!\""], ["said Defarge, with much indifference.", "드파르주가 자못 무심하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed.", "\"그래요, 정말로."], ["When Doctor Manette was released, you, his old domestic, had the charge of him, I know.", "마네뜨 박사가 풀려났을 때, 그의 옛 하인이던 당신이 그를 맡았다고 내가 알고 있소."], ["He was delivered to you.", "그가 당신에게 인도되었지요."], ["You see I am informed of the circumstances?”", "보다시피 나는 그 정황을 알고 있다오?\""]], [["“Such is the fact, certainly,”", "\"분명히 그건 사실이오.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."], ["He had had it conveyed to him, in an accidental touch of his wife’s elbow as she knitted and warbled, that he would do best to answer, but always with brevity.", "그는 아내가 뜨개질을 하며 노래를 흥얼거리다가 팔꿈치로 우연인 듯 슬쩍 건드린 것을 통해, 대답하되 늘 짤막하게 하는 것이 상책이라는 뜻을 전달받았다."]], [["“It was to you,” said the spy, “that his daughter came; and it was from your care that his daughter took him, accompanied by a neat brown monsieur; how is he called?--in a little wig--Lorry--of the bank of Tellson and Company--over to England.”", "\"그의 딸이 찾아온 것도 당신에게였고,\" 끄나풀이 말했다. \"그의 딸이 그를 데려간 것도 당신의 보살핌으로부터였소. 깔끔한 갈색 옷차림의 무슈가 동행했는데, 그를 뭐라고 부르더라? — 작은 가발을 쓴 — 로리 — 뗄슨 상회의 — 영국으로 데려갔지요.\""]], [["“Such is the fact,”", "\"그건 사실이오.\""], ["repeated Defarge.", "드파르주가 되뇌었다."]], [["“Very interesting remembrances!”", "\"무척 흥미로운 추억이지요!\""], ["said the spy.", "끄나풀이 말했다."], ["“I have known Doctor Manette and his daughter, in England.”", "\"나는 영국에서 마네뜨 박사와 그 딸을 알게 됐소.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“You don’t hear much about them now?”", "\"요즘은 그들 소식을 별로 못 들으시오?\""], ["said the spy.", "끄나풀이 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"못 듣소.\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."]], [["“In effect,”", "\"사실인즉,\""], ["madame struck in, looking up from her work and her little song, “we never hear about them.", "부인이 일감과 짧은 노래에서 눈을 들며 끼어들었다. \"우리는 그들 소식을 전혀 못 들어요."], ["We received the news of their safe arrival, and perhaps another letter, or perhaps two; but, since then, they have gradually taken their road in life--we, ours--and we have held no correspondence.”", "그들이 무사히 도착했다는 소식을 받았고, 아마 편지를 한 통, 어쩌면 두 통쯤 더 받았지요. 하지만 그 뒤로는 그들이 차츰 자기네 삶의 길을 갔고 — 우리는 우리의 길을 갔고 — 서로 아무 연락도 없었어요.\""]], [["“Perfectly so, madame,”", "\"바로 그렇겠지요, 부인.\""], ["replied the spy.", "끄나풀이 대답했다."], ["“She is going to be married.”", "\"그 딸이 결혼한다더군요.\""]], [["“Going?” echoed madame.", "\"한다고요?\""], ["“She was pretty enough to have been married long ago.", "부인이 되받았다. \"예쁘기로는 진작에 결혼했을 만한데요."], ["You English are cold, it seems to me.”", "영국 사람들은 냉랭한가봐요, 내 보기엔.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You know I am English.”", "내가 영국인인 걸 아시는군요.\""]], [["“I perceive your tongue is,”", "\"당신 혀가 영국 혀라는 건 알겠어요.\""], ["returned madame; “and what the tongue is, I suppose the man is.”", "부인이 받았다. \"그리고 혀가 그렇다면, 사람도 그러하겠지요.\""]], [["He did not take the identification as a compliment; but he made the best of it, and turned it off with a laugh.", "그는 그 단정을 칭찬으로 받아들이지는 않았으나, 되도록 좋게 넘기려고 웃음으로 흘려버렸다."], ["After sipping his cognac to the end, he added:", "꼬냑을 끝까지 홀짝인 뒤, 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, Miss Manette is going to be married.", "\"그렇소, 마네뜨 양이 결혼한다오."], ["But not to an Englishman; to one who, like herself, is French by birth.", "하지만 영국인하고는 아니고, 자기처럼 태생이 프랑스인인 자하고요."], ["And speaking of Gaspard (ah, poor Gaspard!", "그리고 가스빠르 얘기가 나왔으니 말이지만 (아, 가엾은 가스빠르!"], ["It was cruel, cruel!), it is a curious thing that she is going to marry the nephew of Monsieur the Marquis, for whom Gaspard was exalted to that height of so many feet; in other words, the present Marquis.", "그건 잔혹한, 잔혹한 일이었소!), 묘한 일은, 그녀가 후작 무슈의 조카하고 결혼한다는 거요. 가스빠르가 그토록 여러 피트 높이로 매달려 떠받들어졌던 바로 그 후작 말이오."], ["But he lives unknown in England, he is no Marquis there; he is Mr.", "다시 말해, 지금의 후작이지요. 하지만 그자는 영국에서 신분을 감춘 채 살고 있어, 거기서는 후작이 아니라오."], ["Charles Darnay.", "그자는 샤를 다네 씨요."], ["D’Aulnais is the name of his mother’s family.”", "도네는 그자 어머니 집안의 성이오.\""]], [["Madame Defarge knitted steadily, but the intelligence had a palpable effect upon her husband.", "드파르주 부인은 꾸준히 뜨개질을 했으나, 그 정보는 그녀의 남편에게는 뚜렷한 효과를 미쳤다."], ["Do what he would, behind the little counter, as to the striking of a light and the lighting of his pipe, he was troubled, and his hand was not trustworthy.", "작은 계산대 뒤에서 불을 켜고 파이프에 불을 붙이는 일을 하면서도, 그는 아무리 애를 써도 마음이 흔들렸고, 손은 미덥지 못했다."], ["The spy would have been no spy if he had failed to see it, or to record it in his mind.", "끄나풀이 그것을 못 보았거나, 마음속에 새겨두지 못했다면 끄나풀이 아니었을 것이다."]], [["Having made, at least, this one hit, whatever it might prove to be worth, and no customers coming in to help him to any other, Mr.", "적어도 이 한 방은 — 그것이 무슨 값어치가 있다고 판명되든 간에 — 먹였고, 다른 어떤 한 방을 거들어줄 손님도 들어오지 않았으므로, 바사드 씨는 마신 값을 치르고 자리를 떴다."], ["Barsad paid for what he had drunk, and took his leave: taking occasion to say, in a genteel manner, before he departed, that he looked forward to the pleasure of seeing Monsieur and Madame Defarge again.", "떠나기에 앞서, 점잖은 태도로, 드파르주 무슈와 부인을 다시 만나는 즐거움을 고대한다고 말할 기회를 잡았다."], ["For some minutes after he had emerged into the outer presence of Saint Antoine, the husband and wife remained exactly as he had left them, lest he should come back.", "그가 쌩땅뚜안의 바깥 세계로 나선 뒤로 몇 분 동안, 부부는 그가 되돌아올까봐 그가 떠날 때 그대로의 모습으로 가만히 있었다."]], [["“Can it be true,”", "\"사실일 수 있을까,\""], ["said Defarge, in a low voice, looking down at his wife as he stood smoking with his hand on the back of her chair: “what he has said of Ma’amselle Manette?”", "드파르주가 아내의 의자 등받이에 손을 얹고 담배를 피우며 서서 그녀를 내려다보고 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"그자가 마네뜨 양에 대해 한 말 말이오?\""]], [["“As he has said it,”", "\"그자가 한 말이고 보면,\""], ["returned madame, lifting her eyebrows a little, “it is probably false.", "부인이 눈썹을 약간 치켜올리며 받았다. \"아마 거짓일 거예요."], ["But it may be true.”", "하지만 사실일 수도 있지요.\""]], [["“If it is--”", "\"만약 사실이라면 —\""], ["Defarge began, and stopped.", "드파르주가 입을 열다가 멈췄다."]], [["“If it is?”", "\"사실이라면?\""], ["repeated his wife.", "그의 아내가 되뇌었다."]], [["“--And if it does come, while we live to see it triumph--I hope, for her sake, Destiny will keep her husband out of France.”", "\"— 그리고 만약 그것이 오게 된다면, 우리가 그 승리를 보도록 살아 있는 동안에 — 나는, 그녀를 위해서라도, 운명이 그녀 남편을 프랑스 밖에 두기를 바라오.\""]], [["“Her husband’s destiny,”", "\"그녀 남편의 운명은,\""], ["said Madame Defarge, with her usual composure, “will take him where he is to go, and will lead him to the end that is to end him.", "드파르주 부인이 여느 때의 침착함으로 말했다. \"그가 가야 할 곳으로 그를 데려갈 것이고, 그를 끝장낼 종말로 그를 이끌어 갈 거예요."], ["That is all I know.”", "내가 아는 건 그게 다예요.\""]], [["“But it is very strange--now, at least, is it not very strange”--said Defarge, rather pleading with his wife to induce her to admit it, “that, after all our sympathy for Monsieur her father, and herself, her husband’s name should be proscribed under your hand at this moment, by the side of that infernal dog’s who has just left us?”", "\"하지만 참 이상하잖소 — 적어도 지금으로선, 참 이상하지 않소\" — 드파르주가 아내에게 인정하도록 거의 사정하다시피 말했다. \"그녀 아버지 무슈와 그녀 자신을 향한 우리의 그 모든 동정에도 불구하고, 그녀 남편의 이름이 바로 이 순간 당신 손으로, 우리를 막 떠난 그 지긋지긋한 개 같은 자의 이름 곁에 등록되어야 한다는 게 말이오.\""]], [["“Stranger things than that will happen when it does come,”", "\"그것이 오게 되면 그보다 더 이상한 일들도 일어날 거예요.\" 부인이 대답했다."], ["answered madame.", "\"나는 그 둘을 다 여기 분명히 가지고 있어요."], ["“I have them both here, of a certainty; and they are both here for their merits; that is enough.”", "그리고 둘 다 제 죗값으로 여기 있는 거고요. 그거면 충분해요.\""]], [["She rolled up her knitting when she had said those words, and presently took the rose out of the handkerchief that was wound about her head.", "그 말을 마친 뒤 그녀는 뜨개질감을 둘둘 말았고, 이윽고 머리에 두른 손수건에서 장미를 빼냈다."], ["Either Saint Antoine had an instinctive sense that the objectionable decoration was gone, or Saint Antoine was on the watch for its disappearance; howbeit, the Saint took courage to lounge in, very shortly afterwards, and the wine-shop recovered its habitual aspect.", "쌩땅뚜안이 그 거슬리는 장식이 사라졌다는 것을 본능적으로 알아챘든, 아니면 쌩땅뚜안이 그것이 사라지기를 망보고 있었든, 어쨌든 그 성인은 곧 용기를 내어 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔고, 술집은 평소의 모습을 되찾았다."]], [["In the evening, at which season of all others Saint Antoine turned himself inside out, and sat on door-steps and window-ledges, and came to the corners of vile streets and courts, for a breath of air, Madame Defarge with her work in her hand was accustomed to pass from place to place and from group to group: a Missionary--there were many like her--such as the world will do well never to breed again.", "저녁이면, 다른 어느 때보다도 바로 그 시간에 쌩땅뚜안은 속을 죄다 뒤집어내듯 하여, 사람들이 바람 한 줄기 쐬려고 문간 계단과 창턱에 걸터앉고 더러운 거리와 안뜰 모퉁이로 나왔는데, 드파르주 부인은 일감을 손에 들고 이곳에서 저곳으로, 이 무리에서 저 무리로 옮겨 다니곤 했다. 한 사람의 전도사였으니 — 그녀 같은 이는 많았다 — 세상으로서는 다시는 길러내지 않는 편이 좋을 그런 부류였다."], ["All the women knitted.", "여인들은 모두 뜨개질을 했다."], ["They knitted worthless things; but, the mechanical work was a mechanical substitute for eating and drinking; the hands moved for the jaws and the digestive apparatus: if the bony fingers had been still, the stomachs would have been more famine-pinched.", "그들은 쓸모없는 것들을 떴으나, 그 기계적인 노동은 먹고 마시는 일의 기계적인 대용물이었다. 손은 턱과 소화기관을 위해 움직였으니, 만일 그 앙상한 손가락이 멈춰 있었다면, 위장은 기아로 더욱 옥죄였을 것이다."]], [["But, as the fingers went, the eyes went, and the thoughts.", "그러나 손가락이 움직이는 대로, 눈도 움직였고, 생각도 움직였다."], ["And as Madame Defarge moved on from group to group, all three went quicker and fiercer among every little knot of women that she had spoken with, and left behind.", "그리고 드파르주 부인이 이 무리에서 저 무리로 옮겨 가는 동안, 그녀가 말을 건넨 뒤 뒤에 남겨두고 떠난 여인들의 작은 매듭마다, 그 셋은 모두 더 빠르고 더 사납게 움직였다."]], [["Her husband smoked at his door, looking after her with admiration.", "그녀의 남편은 자기 집 문간에서 담배를 피우며, 감탄의 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["“A great woman,”", "\"대단한 여자야.\""], ["said he, “a strong woman, a grand woman, a frightfully grand woman!”", "그가 말했다. \"강한 여자, 위대한 여자, 무섭도록 위대한 여자라고!\""]], [["Darkness closed around, and then came the ringing of church bells and the distant beating of the military drums in the Palace Courtyard, as the women sat knitting, knitting.", "어둠이 사방으로 내려앉았고, 이윽고 교회 종소리와 궁정 안뜰에서 울리는 군악대 북소리가 멀리서 들려왔다. 여인들은 앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 했다."], ["Darkness encompassed them.", "어둠이 그들을 에워쌌다."], ["Another darkness was closing in as surely, when the church bells, then ringing pleasantly in many an airy steeple over France, should be melted into thundering cannon; when the military drums should be beating to drown a wretched voice, that night all potent as the voice of Power and Plenty, Freedom and Life.", "또 하나의 어둠이 그만큼이나 어김없이 다가오고 있었으니, 그때가 되면 프랑스 곳곳의 수많은 가뿐한 첨탑에서 지금은 흥겹게 울리는 그 교회 종들이 녹아내려 천둥 치는 대포가 될 것이며, 그 군악대 북은 그 밤에는 권세와 풍요, 자유와 생명의 목소리처럼 더없이 강력한 어느 비참한 목소리를 묻어버리려고 두드려댈 것이었다."], ["So much was closing in about the women who sat knitting, knitting, that they their very selves were closing in around a structure yet unbuilt, where they were to sit knitting, knitting, counting dropping heads.", "앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 하던 그 여인들 주위로 그토록 많은 것이 다가들고 있었으니, 그들 자신이야말로 아직 세워지지도 않은 어떤 구조물을 에워싸고 다가들고 있었던 것이다. 그곳에서 그들은 앉아서 뜨개질을, 뜨개질을 하며, 떨어지는 머리를 헤아리게 될 터였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII. One Night", "# CHAPTER XVII. One Night"]], [["Never did the sun go down with a brighter glory on the quiet corner in Soho, than one memorable evening when the Doctor and his daughter sat under the plane-tree together.", "의사와 그의 딸이 함께 플라타너스 아래 앉아 있던 어느 잊지 못할 저녁만큼, 소호의 그 조용한 모퉁이 위로 태양이 그토록 찬란한 영광을 지니고 저문 적은 없었다."], ["Never did the moon rise with a milder radiance over great London, than on that night when it found them still seated under the tree, and shone upon their faces through its leaves.", "그날 밤, 그들이 여전히 나무 아래 앉아 있는 것을 발견하고 잎사귀 사이로 그들의 얼굴을 비추던 그때만큼, 거대한 런던 위로 달이 그토록 부드러운 광채를 띠고 떠오른 적도 없었다."]], [["Lucie was to be married to-morrow.", "루시는 내일 결혼할 예정이었다."], ["She had reserved this last evening for her father, and they sat alone under the plane-tree.", "그녀는 이 마지막 저녁을 아버지를 위해 남겨두었고, 그래서 두 사람은 플라타너스 아래 단둘이 앉아 있었다."]], [["“You are happy, my dear father?”", "\"행복하세요, 아버지?\""]], [["“Quite, my child.”", "\"그럼, 아주 행복하단다.\""]], [["They had said little, though they had been there a long time.", "오랜 시간 그곳에 있었지만 그들은 말이 거의 없었다."], ["When it was yet light enough to work and read, she had neither engaged herself in her usual work, nor had she read to him.", "아직 일하거나 책을 읽을 만큼 빛이 남아 있었을 때에도, 그녀는 평소처럼 일감을 손에 쥐지도, 아버지에게 책을 읽어드리지도 않았다."], ["She had employed herself in both ways, at his side under the tree, many and many a time; but, this time was not quite like any other, and nothing could make it so.", "그녀는 나무 아래 그의 곁에서 그 두 가지로 시간을 보낸 적이 여러 번, 아주 여러 번 있었지만, 이번은 그 어느 때와도 사뭇 달랐고, 무엇으로도 그렇게 만들 수는 없었다."]], [["“And I am very happy to-night, dear father.", "\"그리고 저는 오늘 밤 무척 행복해요, 아버지."], ["I am deeply happy in the love that Heaven has so blessed--my love for Charles, and Charles’s love for me.", "하늘이 이토록 축복해주신 사랑 안에서 저는 깊이 행복합니다 — 샤를을 향한 제 사랑과, 저를 향한 샤를의 사랑이요."], ["But, if my life were not to be still consecrated to you, or if my marriage were so arranged as that it would part us, even by the length of a few of these streets, I should be more unhappy and self-reproachful now than I can tell you.", "하지만 만약 제 인생이 더 이상 아버지께 바쳐지지 않는 것이라면, 또는 제 결혼이 우리를 이 거리 몇 개의 길이만큼이라도 갈라놓도록 정해진 것이라면, 저는 지금 이루 말할 수 없을 만큼 더 불행하고 자책에 시달릴 거예요."], ["Even as it is--”", "지금 이대로도—\""]], [["Even as it was, she could not command her voice.", "지금 이대로도, 그녀는 자신의 목소리를 다스릴 수 없었다."]], [["In the sad moonlight, she clasped him by the neck, and laid her face upon his breast.", "슬픈 달빛 속에서 그녀는 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 그의 가슴에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["In the moonlight which is always sad, as the light of the sun itself is--as the light called human life is--at its coming and its going.", "태양 그 자체의 빛이 그러하듯이 — 인간의 삶이라 불리는 빛이 그러하듯이 — 그 오고 감에 있어 언제나 슬픈 그 달빛 속에서."]], [["“Dearest dear!", "\"사랑하는 아버지!"], ["Can you tell me, this last time, that you feel quite, quite sure, no new affections of mine, and no new duties of mine, will ever interpose between us?", "이 마지막으로 제게 말씀해주실 수 있나요, 제 어떤 새로운 애정도, 제 어떤 새로운 의무도 결코 우리 사이를 가로막지 않으리라고 정말로, 정말로 확신하신다고요?"], ["I know it well, but do you know it?", "저는 그것을 잘 알아요, 하지만 아버지께서도 아시나요?"], ["In your own heart, do you feel quite certain?”", "아버지 마음속으로, 정말 확실하다고 느끼세요?\""]], [["Her father answered, with a cheerful firmness of conviction he could scarcely have assumed, “Quite sure, my darling!", "아버지는 그가 좀처럼 꾸며낼 수 없을 법한 명랑하고 굳건한 확신을 담아 대답했다. \"정말 확실하단다, 내 사랑!"], ["More than that,”", "그뿐만이 아니야,\""], ["he added, as he tenderly kissed her: “my future is far brighter, Lucie, seen through your marriage, than it could have been--nay, than it ever was--without it.”", "그가 그녀에게 다정하게 입맞추며 덧붙였다. \"네 결혼을 통해 바라보는 내 미래는, 루시야, 그것이 없었을 때보다 — 아니, 지금껏 한 번도 그랬던 적이 없을 만큼 — 훨씬 더 밝단다.\""]], [["“If I could hope that, my father!--”", "\"그렇게 바랄 수만 있다면요, 아버지!—\""]], [["“Believe it, love!", "\"믿으렴, 얘야!"], ["Indeed it is so.", "정말로 그러하니까."], ["Consider how natural and how plain it is, my dear, that it should be so.", "그것이 마땅히 그래야 한다는 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 얼마나 명백한지 생각해보렴."], ["You, devoted and young, cannot fully appreciate the anxiety I have felt that your life should not be wasted--”", "헌신적이고 젊은 너는 네 인생이 헛되이 버려지지 않기를 바라며 내가 느껴온 그 근심을 온전히 헤아릴 수 없을 거다—\""]], [["She moved her hand towards his lips, but he took it in his, and repeated the word.", "그녀가 손을 그의 입술 쪽으로 가져갔지만, 그는 그 손을 자신의 손으로 잡고는 그 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“--wasted, my child--should not be wasted, struck aside from the natural order of things--for my sake.", "\"—헛되이, 내 아이야 — 헛되이 버려지지 않기를, 사물의 자연스러운 질서에서 — 나 때문에 — 비껴나지 않기를 말이다."], ["Your unselfishness cannot entirely comprehend how much my mind has gone on this; but, only ask yourself, how could my happiness be perfect, while yours was incomplete?”", "네 이타심으로는 내 마음이 이 문제에 얼마나 매달려왔는지 온전히 이해할 수 없겠지. 하지만 스스로 한번 물어보렴, 네 행복이 불완전한데 어떻게 내 행복이 완전할 수 있겠느냐?\""]], [["“If I had never seen Charles, my father, I should have been quite happy with you.”", "\"제가 만약 샤를을 한 번도 보지 못했더라면요, 아버지, 저는 아버지와 함께 아주 행복했을 거예요.\""]], [["He smiled at her unconscious admission that she would have been unhappy without Charles, having seen him; and replied:", "샤를을 본 이상 그가 없었다면 자신이 불행했으리라는, 그녀의 무심결의 고백에 그는 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“My child, you did see him, and it is Charles.", "\"내 아이야, 너는 그를 보았고, 그가 바로 샤를이지."], ["If it had not been Charles, it would have been another.", "만약 그가 샤를이 아니었다면, 다른 사람이었을 게다."], ["Or, if it had been no other, I should have been the cause, and then the dark part of my life would have cast its shadow beyond myself, and would have fallen on you.”", "또는, 다른 누구도 아니었다면, 그건 내가 원인이었을 테고, 그러면 내 인생의 어두운 부분이 그 그림자를 나 자신 너머로 드리워 네게 떨어졌겠지.\""]], [["It was the first time, except at the trial, of her ever hearing him refer to the period of his suffering.", "재판 때를 제외하고, 그녀가 그가 고통받던 시절을 언급하는 것을 들은 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["It gave her a strange and new sensation while his words were in her ears; and she remembered it long afterwards.", "그의 말이 귓가에 머무는 동안 그것은 그녀에게 기이하고 새로운 감각을 주었다. 그리고 그녀는 그 일을 오래도록 기억했다."]], [["“See!”", "\"저것 보렴!\""], ["said the Doctor of Beauvais, raising his hand towards the moon.", "보베의 의사가 손을 달을 향해 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["“I have looked at her from my prison-window, when I could not bear her light.", "\"나는 감옥 창문으로 저 달을 바라보았지, 그 빛을 견딜 수 없을 때에도."], ["I have looked at her when it has been such torture to me to think of her shining upon what I had lost, that I have beaten my head against my prison-walls.", "내가 잃어버린 것 위로 달이 비치고 있다는 생각이 어찌나 고통스러웠던지, 감옥 벽에 머리를 찧어가며 달을 바라본 적도 있었다."], ["I have looked at her, in a state so dull and lethargic, that I have thought of nothing but the number of horizontal lines I could draw across her at the full, and the number of perpendicular lines with which I could intersect them.”", "너무도 멍하고 무기력한 상태로 달을 바라보면서, 보름달일 때 달을 가로지르며 그을 수 있는 수평선의 수와, 그것들과 교차시켜 그을 수 있는 수직선의 수 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 못한 적도 있었지.\""], ["He added in his inward and pondering manner, as he looked at the moon, “It was twenty either way, I remember, and the twentieth was difficult to squeeze in.”", "그는 달을 바라보며 속으로 곱씹는 듯한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"어느 쪽이든 스무 개였던 게 기억나는구나, 그리고 스무번째 선을 비집어 넣기가 어려웠지.\""]], [["The strange thrill with which she heard him go back to that time, deepened as he dwelt upon it; but, there was nothing to shock her in the manner of his reference.", "그가 그 시절로 돌아가는 것을 들으며 느낀 기이한 전율은 그가 그 일을 곱씹을수록 더욱 깊어졌다. 하지만 그가 그것을 언급하는 태도에는 그녀를 놀라게 할 만한 것이 전혀 없었다."], ["He only seemed to contrast his present cheerfulness and felicity with the dire endurance that was over.", "그는 다만 지금의 명랑함과 행복을, 이제는 지나간 그 끔찍한 인내와 견주어보는 듯할 뿐이었다."]], [["“I have looked at her, speculating thousands of times upon the unborn child from whom I had been rent.", "\"나는 달을 바라보며, 나에게서 떨어져 나간, 아직 태어나지 않은 아이에 대해 수천 번을 헤아려보았다."], ["Whether it was alive.", "그 아이가 살아 있을까."], ["Whether it had been born alive, or the poor mother’s shock had killed it.", "살아서 태어났을까, 아니면 가엾은 어미의 충격이 그 아이를 죽였을까."], ["Whether it was a son who would some day avenge his father.", "언젠가 제 아비의 원수를 갚아줄 아들일까."], ["(There was a time in my imprisonment, when my desire for vengeance was unbearable.) Whether it was a son who would never know his father’s story; who might even live to weigh the possibility of his father’s having disappeared of his own will and act.", "(감옥에 갇혀 있는 동안 복수에 대한 내 갈망이 견딜 수 없을 만큼 컸던 때가 있었지.) 제 아비의 이야기를 결코 알지 못할 아들일까. 제 아비가 제 스스로의 뜻과 행동으로 사라졌을 가능성을 저울질하며 살아갈 수도 있는 아들일까."], ["Whether it was a daughter who would grow to be a woman.”", "자라서 여인이 될 딸일까.\""]], [["She drew closer to him, and kissed his cheek and his hand.", "그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 다가가 그의 뺨과 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“I have pictured my daughter, to myself, as perfectly forgetful of me--rather, altogether ignorant of me, and unconscious of me.", "\"나는 내 딸이 나를 완전히 잊은 모습을 — 아니, 나를 전혀 모르고, 나의 존재조차 의식하지 못하는 모습을 그려보았다."], ["I have cast up the years of her age, year after year.", "나는 그 아이의 나이를 한 해 한 해 헤아려보았지."], ["I have seen her married to a man who knew nothing of my fate.", "내 운명에 대해 아무것도 모르는 남자와 결혼한 그 아이를 보았다."], ["I have altogether perished from the remembrance of the living, and in the next generation my place was a blank.”", "나는 산 자들의 기억에서 완전히 사라졌고, 다음 세대에서 내 자리는 텅 빈 공백이었지.\""]], [["“My father!", "\"아버지!"], ["Even to hear that you had such thoughts of a daughter who never existed, strikes to my heart as if I had been that child.”", "한 번도 존재한 적 없는 딸에 대해 그런 생각을 하셨다는 말을 듣는 것만으로도, 마치 제가 그 아이였던 것처럼 제 가슴이 미어집니다.\""]], [["“You, Lucie?", "\"네가 말이냐, 루시?"], ["It is out of the Consolation and restoration you have brought to me, that these remembrances arise, and pass between us and the moon on this last night.--What did I say just now?”", "이런 기억들이 떠오르는 것은, 그리고 이 마지막 밤에 우리와 달 사이를 지나가는 것은, 바로 네가 내게 가져다준 위안과 회복에서 비롯된 것이란다.—내가 방금 무어라 말했더라?\""]], [["“She knew nothing of you.", "\"그 아이가 아버지에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다고요."], ["She cared nothing for you.”", "아버지를 조금도 마음에 두지 않았다고요.\""]], [["“So!", "\"그래!"], ["But on other moonlight nights, when the sadness and the silence have touched me in a different way--have affected me with something as like a sorrowful sense of peace, as any emotion that had pain for its foundations could--I have imagined her as coming to me in my cell, and leading me out into the freedom beyond the fortress.", "하지만 다른 달밤이면, 슬픔과 고요가 사뭇 다른 방식으로 나를 어루만질 때면 — 고통을 그 바탕에 둔 어떤 감정이 그럴 수 있는 한도 내에서, 슬픔 어린 평화의 느낌 같은 것으로 나를 적실 때면 — 나는 그 아이가 내 감방으로 찾아와 나를 그 요새 너머의 자유로 이끌어 내는 모습을 상상하곤 했단다. 지금 내가 너를 보듯이, 나는 달빛 속에서 그 아이의 모습을 자주 보았지."], ["I have seen her image in the moonlight often, as I now see you; except that I never held her in my arms; it stood between the little grated window and the door.", "다만 한 번도 그 아이를 내 품에 안아본 적은 없었다. 그것은 작은 쇠창살 창과 문 사이에 서 있었지."], ["But, you understand that that was not the child I am speaking of?”", "하지만 그것이 내가 지금 말하는 그 아이가 아니라는 것을, 너는 이해하겠지?\""]], [["“The figure was not; the--the--image; the fancy?”", "\"그 형상이 아니라요. 그—그—영상, 그 환상이요?\""]], [["“No.", "\"그래."], ["That was another thing.", "그것은 다른 것이었다."], ["It stood before my disturbed sense of sight, but it never moved.", "그것은 어지러워진 내 시각 앞에 서 있었지만, 결코 움직이지 않았어."], ["The phantom that my mind pursued, was another and more real child.", "내 마음이 좇던 그 환영은 또 다른, 더 실재하는 아이였단다."], ["Of her outward appearance I know no more than that she was like her mother.", "그 아이의 겉모습에 대해 내가 아는 것이라곤 제 어미를 닮았다는 것뿐이지."], ["The other had that likeness too--as you have--but was not the same.", "다른 그것도 그 닮음을 지니고 있었어 — 네가 그러하듯이 — 하지만 같은 것은 아니었다."], ["Can you follow me, Lucie?", "내 말을 따라올 수 있겠느냐, 루시?"], ["Hardly, I think?", "어렵겠지, 그렇지?"], ["I doubt you must have been a solitary prisoner to understand these perplexed distinctions.”", "이 뒤얽힌 구분을 이해하려면 너도 외로운 수인이 되어보았어야 할 것 같구나.\""]], [["His collected and calm manner could not prevent her blood from running cold, as he thus tried to anatomise his old condition.", "침착하고 차분한 그의 태도도, 그가 이렇게 자신의 옛 처지를 해부하려 들 때 그녀의 피가 차갑게 식는 것을 막지는 못했다."]], [["“In that more peaceful state, I have imagined her, in the moonlight, coming to me and taking me out to show me that the home of her married life was full of her loving remembrance of her lost father.", "\"그 더 평온한 상태에서, 나는 그 아이가 달빛 속에서 내게로 와, 나를 데리고 나가, 그 아이가 결혼해 꾸린 가정이 잃어버린 아비에 대한 사랑스러운 기억으로 가득하다는 것을 보여주는 모습을 상상했다."], ["My picture was in her room, and I was in her prayers.", "내 초상화가 그 아이의 방에 걸려 있었고, 나는 그 아이의 기도 속에 있었지."], ["Her life was active, cheerful, useful; but my poor history pervaded it all.”", "그 아이의 삶은 활기차고, 명랑하고, 쓸모 있었다. 하지만 나의 가엾은 사연이 그 모든 것에 스며들어 있었어.\""]], [["“I was that child, my father, I was not half so good, but in my love that was I.”", "\"제가 그 아이였어요, 아버지. 저는 그 절반만큼도 착하지 못했지만, 제 사랑에 있어서는 제가 바로 그 아이였어요.\""]], [["“And she showed me her children,”", "\"그리고 그 아이는 내게 자기 아이들을 보여주었지,\""], ["said the Doctor of Beauvais, “and they had heard of me, and had been taught to pity me.", "보베의 의사가 말했다. \"그 아이들은 내 이야기를 들어 알고 있었고, 나를 가엾이 여기도록 가르침받았지."], ["When they passed a prison of the State, they kept far from its frowning walls, and looked up at its bars, and spoke in whispers.", "그들은 국가의 감옥을 지날 때면 그 험상궂은 벽에서 멀찍이 떨어져, 창살을 올려다보며 속삭이듯 이야기했단다."], ["She could never deliver me; I imagined that she always brought me back after showing me such things.", "그 아이는 결코 나를 구해낼 수 없었어. 나는 그 아이가 그런 것들을 보여준 뒤에 늘 나를 다시 데려다놓는다고 상상했지."], ["But then, blessed with the relief of tears, I fell upon my knees, and blessed her.”", "하지만 그러고 나면, 눈물이라는 위안의 축복을 받아, 나는 무릎을 꿇고 그 아이를 축복했단다.\""]], [["“I am that child, I hope, my father.", "\"제가 그 아이이기를 바라요, 아버지."], ["O my dear, my dear, will you bless me as fervently to-morrow?”", "오 사랑하는 아버지, 사랑하는 아버지, 내일도 그렇게 간절하게 저를 축복해주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Lucie, I recall these old troubles in the reason that I have to-night for loving you better than words can tell, and thanking God for my great happiness.", "\"루시야, 내가 이 오래된 괴로움들을 떠올리는 것은, 오늘 밤 내가 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 너를 더욱 사랑하고, 내 크나큰 행복에 대해 하느님께 감사드릴 까닭이 있기 때문이란다."], ["My thoughts, when they were wildest, never rose near the happiness that I have known with you, and that we have before us.”", "내 생각이 가장 거칠었던 때에도, 그것은 내가 너와 함께 알게 된 행복, 그리고 우리 앞에 놓인 그 행복 근처에는 결코 다다르지 못했지.\""]], [["He embraced her, solemnly commended her to Heaven, and humbly thanked Heaven for having bestowed her on him.", "그는 그녀를 끌어안고, 엄숙하게 그녀를 하늘에 의탁하며, 그녀를 자신에게 내려주신 하늘에 겸허히 감사드렸다."], ["By-and-bye, they went into the house.", "이윽고 두 사람은 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["There was no one bidden to the marriage but Mr.", "결혼식에 청해진 사람은 로리 씨뿐이었다."], ["Lorry; there was even to be no bridesmaid but the gaunt Miss Pross.", "신부 들러리조차 수척한 프로스 양 외에는 없을 터였다."], ["The marriage was to make no change in their place of residence; they had been able to extend it, by taking to themselves the upper rooms formerly belonging to the apocryphal invisible lodger, and they desired nothing more.", "결혼으로 그들의 거처가 바뀌지는 않을 것이었다. 그들은 예전에 그 정체 모를 보이지 않는 세입자의 것이었던 위층 방들을 차지함으로써 거처를 넓힐 수 있었고, 그 이상은 아무것도 바라지 않았다."]], [["Doctor Manette was very cheerful at the little supper.", "마네뜨 의사는 그 조촐한 저녁식사 자리에서 무척 명랑했다."], ["They were only three at table, and Miss Pross made the third.", "식탁에는 단 세 사람뿐이었고, 프로스 양이 그 셋 중 하나였다."], ["He regretted that Charles was not there; was more than half disposed to object to the loving little plot that kept him away; and drank to him affectionately.", "그는 샤를이 그 자리에 없는 것을 아쉬워했고, 그를 떼어놓은 그 사랑스러운 작은 모의에 반쯤 넘게 항의할 마음이 들었으며, 그를 위해 다정하게 잔을 들었다."]], [["So, the time came for him to bid Lucie good night, and they separated.", "그렇게, 그가 루시에게 잘 자라고 인사할 시간이 되어, 두 사람은 헤어졌다."], ["But, in the stillness of the third hour of the morning, Lucie came downstairs again, and stole into his room; not free from unshaped fears, beforehand.", "그러나 새벽 세 시의 고요 속에서, 루시는 다시 아래층으로 내려와 그의 방으로 몰래 들어갔다. 미리부터 형체 없는 두려움에서 자유롭지 못한 채였다."]], [["All things, however, were in their places; all was quiet; and he lay asleep, his white hair picturesque on the untroubled pillow, and his hands lying quiet on the coverlet. She put her needless candle in the shadow at a distance, crept up to his bed, and put her lips to his; then, leaned over him, and looked at him.", "그러나 모든 것이 제자리에 있었다. 모든 것이 고요했다. 그리고 그는 잠들어 누워 있었으니, 그의 백발이 평온한 베개 위에 그림처럼 놓여 있었고, 두 손은 이불 위에 가지런히 놓여 있었다. 그녀는 필요 없게 된 촛불을 멀찍이 그늘 속에 내려놓고, 그의 침대로 살금살금 다가가, 그의 입술에 자신의 입술을 갖다 댔다. 그러고는 그에게 몸을 숙여 그를 바라보았다."]], [["Into his handsome face, the bitter waters of captivity had worn; but, he covered up their tracks with a determination so strong, that he held the mastery of them even in his sleep.", "그의 잘생긴 얼굴에는 갇혀 지낸 세월의 쓰디쓴 물결이 닳아 새겨놓은 흔적이 있었다. 하지만 그는 그 자취를 어찌나 강한 결의로 덮어버렸던지, 잠들어 있는 와중에도 그것을 다스리고 있었다."], ["A more remarkable face in its quiet, resolute, and guarded struggle with an unseen assailant, was not to be beheld in all the wide dominions of sleep, that night.", "그날 밤, 보이지 않는 공격자와 벌이는 조용하고 단호하며 경계심 어린 그 싸움 속에서, 그보다 더 주목할 만한 얼굴은 잠이라는 광대한 영토 어디에서도 볼 수 없었다."]], [["She timidly laid her hand on his dear breast, and put up a prayer that she might ever be as true to him as her love aspired to be, and as his sorrows deserved.", "그녀는 조심스레 그의 사랑하는 가슴에 손을 얹고, 자신의 사랑이 바라는 만큼, 그리고 그의 슬픔이 마땅히 받아야 할 만큼, 그에게 영원히 진실하기를 기도했다."], ["Then, she withdrew her hand, and kissed his lips once more, and went away.", "그러고는 손을 거두고, 그의 입술에 다시 한 번 입맞춘 뒤, 떠나갔다."], ["So, the sunrise came, and the shadows of the leaves of the plane-tree moved upon his face, as softly as her lips had moved in praying for him.", "그렇게 해돋이가 찾아왔고, 플라타너스 잎사귀의 그림자가 그의 얼굴 위에서, 그를 위해 기도하던 그녀의 입술이 움직이던 것만큼이나 부드럽게 일렁였다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII. Nine Days", "# CHAPTER XVIII. Nine Days"]], [["The marriage-day was shining brightly, and they were ready outside the closed door of the Doctor’s room, where he was speaking with Charles Darnay.", "결혼식 날은 환하게 빛나고 있었고, 그들은 의사 선생의 방 닫힌 문 밖에서 준비를 마치고 있었다."], ["They were ready to go to church; the beautiful bride, Mr.", "그 방에서 의사 선생은 찰스 다네이와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Lorry, and Miss Pross--to whom the event, through a gradual process of reconcilement to the inevitable, would have been one of absolute bliss, but for the yet lingering consideration that her brother Solomon should have been the bridegroom.", "그들은 교회로 갈 채비가 되어 있었으니, 아름다운 신부와 로리 씨, 그리고 프로스 양이었다. 프로스 양으로 말하자면, 피할 수 없는 일을 점차 받아들이는 과정을 거치며 이 사건은 그녀에게 더할 나위 없는 기쁨이 되었을 터이나, 신랑이 마땅히 자기 오라비 솔로몬이었어야 한다는 미련이 아직도 가시지 않아 그렇지는 못했다."]], [["“And so,” said Mr.", "\"그러니까,\""], ["Lorry, who could not sufficiently admire the bride, and who had been moving round her to take in every point of her quiet, pretty dress; “and so it was for this, my sweet Lucie, that I brought you across the Channel, such a baby!", "신부를 아무리 보아도 감탄이 가시지 않았던 로리 씨가 신부의 단정하고 어여쁜 옷차림을 구석구석 눈에 담으려 그 주위를 빙빙 돌면서 말했다. \"그러니까 이렇게 되려고, 나의 사랑스러운 루시, 내가 너를 그토록 어린 아기였을 적에 해협 건너로 데려왔던 게로구나!"], ["Lord bless me!", "맙소사!"], ["How little I thought what I was doing!", "내가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지 어찌 그리 몰랐을까!"], ["How lightly I valued the obligation I was conferring on my friend Mr. Charles!”", "내 친구 찰스 군에게 베푸는 그 은혜를 어쩌면 그리 가볍게 여겼을까!\""]], [["“You didn’t mean it,”", "\"일부러 그러신 게 아니잖아요,\""], ["remarked the matter-of-fact Miss Pross, “and therefore how could you know it?", "현실적인 프로스 양이 한마디 했다. \"그러니 어떻게 아실 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Nonsense!”", "말도 안 되는 소리!\""]], [["“Really?", "\"그런가요?"], ["Well; but don’t cry,”", "그래도, 울지는 말아요,\""], ["said the gentle Mr. Lorry.", "점잖은 로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I am not crying,”", "\"전 울지 않아요,\""], ["said Miss Pross; “you are.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"우는 건 어르신이죠.\""]], [["“I, my Pross?” (By this time, Mr.", "\"내가, 나의 프로스 양?\""], ["Lorry dared to be pleasant with her, on occasion.)", "(이 무렵 로리 씨는 때때로 그녀에게 농을 건넬 만큼 대담해져 있었다.)"]], [["“You were, just now; I saw you do it, and I don’t wonder at it.", "\"방금 우셨어요. 제 눈으로 봤는걸요."], ["Such a present of plate as you have made ’em, is enough to bring tears into anybody’s eyes.", "그도 그럴 만하지요. 어르신이 저들에게 선물한 그 은식기 한 벌만 봐도 누구든 눈물이 날 만하다고요."], ["There’s not a fork or a spoon in the collection,”", "그 한 벌 가운데 포크 하나, 숟가락 하나 빠짐없이,\""], ["said Miss Pross, “that I didn’t cry over, last night after the box came, till I couldn’t see it.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"어젯밤 상자가 도착한 뒤로 더는 안 보일 때까지 제가 울지 않은 게 없는걸요.\""]], [["“I am highly gratified,” said Mr.", "\"참으로 흐뭇하구려,\""], ["Lorry, “though, upon my honour, I had no intention of rendering those trifling articles of remembrance invisible to any one.", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"허나 내 명예를 걸고 말하건대, 그 보잘것없는 기념품들을 누구의 눈에도 안 보이게 할 의도는 전혀 없었소."], ["Dear me!", "이런!"], ["This is an occasion that makes a man speculate on all he has lost.", "이런 날이면 사내란 자신이 잃어버린 모든 것을 떠올리게 되는 법이지."], ["Dear, dear, dear!", "저런, 저런, 저런!"], ["To think that there might have been a Mrs. Lorry, any time these fifty years almost!”", "지난 오십년 가까운 세월 가운데 어느 때쯤이라도 로리 부인이라는 사람이 있었을지도 모른다고 생각하면 말이오!\""]], [["“Not at all!”", "\"천만의 말씀!\""], ["From Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 받았다."]], [["“You think there never might have been a Mrs. Lorry?”", "\"로리 부인이라는 사람이 도무지 있을 수 없었으리라 생각하시오?\""], ["asked the gentleman of that name.", "그 이름의 신사가 물었다."]], [["“Pooh!”", "\"흥!\""], ["rejoined Miss Pross; “you were a bachelor in your cradle.”", "프로스 양이 응수했다. \"어르신은 요람 속에서부터 독신자였는걸요.\""]], [["“Well!” observed Mr.", "\"그렇구려!\""], ["Lorry, beamingly adjusting his little wig, “that seems probable, too.”", "로리 씨가 작은 가발을 흐뭇하게 매만지며 말했다. \"그것도 그럴듯하군.\""]], [["“And you were cut out for a bachelor,”", "\"게다가 요람에 눕혀지기도 전에,\""], ["pursued Miss Pross, “before you were put in your cradle.”", "프로스 양이 계속했다. \"독신자로 점지되셨고요.\""]], [["“Then, I think,” said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 내 생각엔,\""], ["Lorry, “that I was very unhandsomely dealt with, and that I ought to have had a voice in the selection of my pattern.", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"나는 참으로 박하게 대접받은 셈이고, 내 본을 고르는 데에는 내 발언권도 있었어야 마땅하오."], ["Enough!", "그만하면 됐소!"], ["Now, my dear Lucie,”", "자, 나의 사랑하는 루시,\" 그가 달래듯 한 팔을 그녀의 허리에 두르며 말했다."], ["drawing his arm soothingly round her waist, “I hear them moving in the next room, and Miss Pross and I, as two formal folks of business, are anxious not to lose the final opportunity of saying something to you that you wish to hear.", "\"옆방에서 그들이 움직이는 소리가 들리는구나. 프로스 양과 나는 격식을 차리는 두 사무가로서, 네가 듣고 싶어 할 말을 전할 마지막 기회를 놓치고 싶지 않단다."], ["You leave your good father, my dear, in hands as earnest and as loving as your own; he shall be taken every conceivable care of; during the next fortnight, while you are in Warwickshire and thereabouts, even Tellson’s shall go to the wall (comparatively speaking) before him.", "얘야, 너는 너의 훌륭한 아버님을 너 자신만큼이나 진심 어리고 다정한 손에 맡기고 떠나는 것이란다. 아버님은 생각할 수 있는 온갖 보살핌을 받으실 것이야."], ["And when, at the fortnight’s end, he comes to join you and your beloved husband, on your other fortnight’s trip in Wales, you shall say that we have sent him to you in the best health and in the happiest frame.", "네가 워릭셔며 그 인근에 머무는 앞으로 두 주 동안, 텔슨 은행마저도 (견주어 말하자면) 아버님 다음으로 밀려나게 될 게다. 그리고 두 주가 끝날 무렵 아버님이 너와 네 사랑하는 남편을 만나러 와서 웨일스로 떠나는 또 다른 두 주의 여행에 합류하실 때면, 우리가 더없이 좋은 건강과 더없이 행복한 마음으로 아버님을 네게 보내드렸다고 너는 말하게 될 게다."], ["Now, I hear Somebody’s step coming to the door.", "자, 누군가 문으로 걸어오는 발소리가 들리는구나."], ["Let me kiss my dear girl with an old-fashioned bachelor blessing, before Somebody comes to claim his own.”", "누군가 자기 사람을 데리러 오기 전에, 이 늙은 독신자가 옛 방식으로 축복하며 나의 사랑하는 아가씨에게 입 맞추게 해다오.\""]], [["For a moment, he held the fair face from him to look at the well-remembered expression on the forehead, and then laid the bright golden hair against his little brown wig, with a genuine tenderness and delicacy which, if such things be old-fashioned, were as old as Adam.", "그는 잠시 그 고운 얼굴을 자기 앞에 떼어 두고 익히 기억하는 이마의 표정을 들여다보았고, 이윽고 그 빛나는 금빛 머리칼을 자신의 작은 갈색 가발에 가져다 대었으니, 그 진실한 다정함과 섬세함은, 만일 그런 것들이 구식이라 한다면, 아담만큼이나 오래된 것이었다."]], [["The door of the Doctor’s room opened, and he came out with Charles Darnay.", "의사 선생의 방문이 열리고, 그가 찰스 다네이와 함께 나왔다."], ["He was so deadly pale--which had not been the case when they went in together--that no vestige of colour was to be seen in his face.", "그는 너무도 새파랗게 질려 있어서 — 함께 들어갔을 때만 해도 그렇지 않았건만 — 그 얼굴에서는 핏기라곤 한 점도 찾아볼 수 없었다."], ["But, in the composure of his manner he was unaltered, except that to the shrewd glance of Mr. Lorry it disclosed some shadowy indication that the old air of avoidance and dread had lately passed over him, like a cold wind.", "그러나 거동의 침착함만은 변함이 없었으니, 다만 로리 씨의 날카로운 눈에는 회피와 두려움이라는 옛 기색이 차가운 바람처럼 최근 그를 스치고 지나갔다는 어렴풋한 그림자가 어렴풋이 비쳤을 뿐이었다."]], [["He gave his arm to his daughter, and took her down-stairs to the chariot which Mr. Lorry had hired in honour of the day.", "그는 딸에게 팔을 내주고, 로리 씨가 이날을 기려 빌린 마차로 그녀를 데리고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["The rest followed in another carriage, and soon, in a neighbouring church, where no strange eyes looked on, Charles Darnay and Lucie Manette were happily married.", "나머지 사람들은 다른 마차로 뒤따랐고, 머지않아 낯선 눈길이 지켜보는 이 없는 이웃 교회에서, 찰스 다네이와 루시 마네뜨는 행복하게 부부의 연을 맺었다."]], [["Besides the glancing tears that shone among the smiles of the little group when it was done, some diamonds, very bright and sparkling, glanced on the bride’s hand, which were newly released from the dark obscurity of one of Mr. Lorry’s pockets.", "식이 끝났을 때 그 작은 무리의 미소 사이로 반짝인 눈물 말고도, 매우 밝고 반짝이는 다이아몬드 몇 알이 신부의 손에서 반짝였으니, 그것은 로리 씨의 호주머니 가운데 하나의 어두운 구석에서 막 풀려난 것이었다."], ["They returned home to breakfast, and all went well, and in due course the golden hair that had mingled with the poor shoemaker’s white locks in the Paris garret, were mingled with them again in the morning sunlight, on the threshold of the door at parting.", "그들은 아침 식사를 하러 집으로 돌아왔고, 모든 일이 잘 풀렸으며, 이윽고 빠리의 다락방에서 가련한 구두장이의 흰 머리칼과 뒤섞였던 그 금빛 머리칼이, 헤어지는 문턱 위에서 아침 햇살을 받으며 다시 그 흰 머리칼과 뒤섞였다."]], [["It was a hard parting, though it was not for long.", "비록 오래는 아니었으나 힘겨운 작별이었다."], ["But her father cheered her, and said at last, gently disengaging himself from her enfolding arms, “Take her, Charles!", "그러나 그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 다독였고, 마침내 자신을 끌어안은 그녀의 팔에서 부드럽게 몸을 빼며 말했다. \"이 아이를 데려가게, 찰스!"], ["She is yours!”", "이 아이는 자네 사람이야!\""]], [["And her agitated hand waved to them from a chaise window, and she was gone.", "그리고 떨리는 그녀의 손이 사륜마차의 창에서 그들을 향해 흔들렸고, 그녀는 떠나갔다."]], [["The corner being out of the way of the idle and curious, and the preparations having been very simple and few, the Doctor, Mr.", "길모퉁이는 한가한 자들과 호기심 많은 자들의 발길에서 비켜나 있었고, 준비도 매우 단출하고 적었던 터라, 의사 선생과 로리 씨, 그리고 프로스 양만이 덩그러니 남았다."], ["Lorry, and Miss Pross, were left quite alone. It was when they turned into the welcome shade of the cool old hall, that Mr.", "그들이 서늘하고 오래된 현관의 반가운 그늘로 접어들었을 때, 로리 씨는 의사 선생에게 커다란 변화가 닥쳤음을 알아차렸다."], ["Lorry observed a great change to have come over the Doctor; as if the golden arm uplifted there, had struck him a poisoned blow.", "마치 그곳에 치켜올려진 금빛 팔이 그에게 독을 묻힌 일격을 가하기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["He had naturally repressed much, and some revulsion might have been expected in him when the occasion for repression was gone.", "그는 본디 많은 것을 억눌러 왔던 터라, 억누를 일이 사라지고 나면 어떤 반동이 그에게 일어나리라 짐작할 만도 했다."], ["But, it was the old scared lost look that troubled Mr.", "그러나 로리 씨를 불안케 한 것은 옛날의 그 겁먹고 길 잃은 듯한 표정이었다."], ["Lorry; and through his absent manner of clasping his head and drearily wandering away into his own room when they got up-stairs, Mr. Lorry was reminded of Defarge the wine-shop keeper, and the starlight ride.", "그리고 그들이 위층으로 올라갔을 때 멍하니 머리를 감싸 쥐고 음울하게 자기 방으로 걸어 들어가는 그 모습에서, 로리 씨는 포도주 가게 주인 드파르주와 별빛 아래의 마차길을 떠올렸다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["he whispered to Miss Pross, after anxious consideration, “I think we had best not speak to him just now, or at all disturb him.", "그가 근심스레 곰곰이 생각한 끝에 프로스 양에게 속삭였다. \"내 생각엔 지금은 그에게 말을 걸지도, 그를 조금이라도 방해하지도 않는 게 상책일 듯하오."], ["I must look in at Tellson’s; so I will go there at once and come back presently.", "나는 텔슨 은행에 잠깐 들러야 하니, 곧장 거기 다녀와서 이내 돌아오겠소."], ["Then, we will take him a ride into the country, and dine there, and all will be well.”", "그런 다음 우리가 그를 시골로 데리고 가 마차를 태우고 거기서 식사를 하면, 만사가 잘될 거요.\""]], [["It was easier for Mr. Lorry to look in at Tellson’s, than to look out of Tellson’s.", "로리 씨에게는 텔슨 은행에 잠깐 들르는 것보다 텔슨 은행에서 빠져나오는 것이 더 어려웠다."], ["He was detained two hours.", "그는 두 시간이나 발이 묶였다."], ["When he came back, he ascended the old staircase alone, having asked no question of the servant; going thus into the Doctor’s rooms, he was stopped by a low sound of knocking.", "돌아왔을 때 그는 하인에게 아무것도 묻지 않은 채 홀로 오래된 계단을 올랐다. 그렇게 의사 선생의 방으로 들어가던 그는 나직이 두드리는 소리에 발걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Good God!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["he said, with a start.", "그가 흠칫 놀라며 말했다."], ["“What’s that?”", "\"저게 무슨 소리지?\""]], [["Miss Pross, with a terrified face, was at his ear.", "프로스 양이 겁에 질린 얼굴로 그의 귓전에 다가와 있었다."], ["“O me, O me! All is lost!”", "\"아이고, 어쩌나, 어쩌나!"], ["cried she, wringing her hands.", "다 틀렸어요!\" 그녀가 두 손을 비틀며 외쳤다."], ["“What is to be told to Ladybird?", "\"이 일을 어떻게 우리 귀여운 아기씨에게 알리지요?"], ["He doesn’t know me, and is making shoes!”", "저를 알아보지도 못하고, 구두를 만들고 계세요!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry said what he could to calm her, and went himself into the Doctor’s room.", "로리 씨는 그녀를 진정시키려 할 수 있는 말을 한 뒤, 몸소 의사 선생의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["The bench was turned towards the light, as it had been when he had seen the shoemaker at his work before, and his head was bent down, and he was very busy.", "작업대는 빛이 드는 쪽으로 돌려져 있었으니, 전에 그 구두장이가 일하는 모습을 보았을 때와 똑같았고, 그는 고개를 숙인 채 몹시 분주했다."]], [["“Doctor Manette.", "\"마네뜨 선생."], ["My dear friend, Doctor Manette!”", "나의 친애하는 벗, 마네뜨 선생!\""]], [["The Doctor looked at him for a moment--half inquiringly, half as if he were angry at being spoken to--and bent over his work again.", "의사 선생은 잠시 그를 바라보았다 — 반은 캐묻는 듯, 반은 말을 걸어온 것에 노한 듯 — 그러고는 다시 일거리 위로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["He had laid aside his coat and waistcoat; his shirt was open at the throat, as it used to be when he did that work; and even the old haggard, faded surface of face had come back to him.", "그는 외투와 조끼를 벗어 두고 있었다. 셔츠는 그 일을 하던 시절 늘 그랬듯 목께가 풀려 있었고, 심지어 핼쑥하고 빛바랜 옛 얼굴빛마저 그에게 되돌아와 있었다."], ["He worked hard--impatiently--as if in some sense of having been interrupted.", "그는 열심히 — 조바심을 내며 — 마치 어떤 의미로든 방해받았다는 듯이 일했다."]], [["Mr. Lorry glanced at the work in his hand, and observed that it was a shoe of the old size and shape.", "로리 씨는 그의 손에 들린 일거리를 흘긋 보고는, 그것이 옛 치수와 모양의 구두임을 알아차렸다."], ["He took up another that was lying by him, and asked what it was.", "그는 그의 곁에 놓인 또 다른 구두를 집어 들고는 무엇이냐고 물었다."]], [["“A young lady’s walking shoe,”", "\"젊은 아가씨가 신을 산책용 구두지요.\""], ["he muttered, without looking up.", "그가 고개도 들지 않은 채 중얼거렸다."], ["“It ought to have been finished long ago.", "\"벌써 한참 전에 끝냈어야 하는 것을."], ["Let it be.”", "그냥 두시오.\""]], [["“But, Doctor Manette.", "\"하지만, 마네뜨 선생."], ["Look at me!”", "날 좀 보시오!\""]], [["He obeyed, in the old mechanically submissive manner, without pausing in his work.", "그는 일손을 멈추지 않은 채, 옛날의 그 기계적으로 순종하는 태도로 그 말에 따랐다."]], [["“You know me, my dear friend?", "\"나를 알아보겠소, 나의 친애하는 벗?"], ["Think again.", "다시 생각해보시오."], ["This is not your proper occupation.", "이건 당신에게 맞는 일이 아니오."], ["Think, dear friend!”", "생각해보시오, 친애하는 벗!\""]], [["Nothing would induce him to speak more.", "무엇으로도 그가 더는 말을 하게 할 수 없었다."], ["He looked up, for an instant at a time, when he was requested to do so; but, no persuasion would extract a word from him.", "그는 그렇게 해달라는 청을 받으면 한 번에 한순간씩 고개를 들었으나, 어떤 설득으로도 그에게서 한마디조차 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["He worked, and worked, and worked, in silence, and words fell on him as they would have fallen on an echoless wall, or on the air.", "그는 일하고, 또 일하고, 또 일했으니, 묵묵히 그러했고, 말들은 메아리 없는 벽에 떨어지듯, 혹은 허공에 떨어지듯 그에게 떨어졌다."], ["The only ray of hope that Mr. Lorry could discover, was, that he sometimes furtively looked up without being asked.", "로리 씨가 찾아낼 수 있었던 단 하나의 희망의 빛은, 그가 이따금 청하지도 않았는데 슬그머니 고개를 든다는 것이었다."], ["In that, there seemed a faint expression of curiosity or perplexity--as though he were trying to reconcile some doubts in his mind.", "그 안에는 호기심이나 당혹스러움의 희미한 기색이 어려 있는 듯했다 — 마치 마음속 어떤 의혹을 풀어보려 애쓰기라도 하는 듯이."]], [["Two things at once impressed themselves on Mr.", "두 가지가 한꺼번에 무엇보다도 중요한 일로 로리 씨의 머릿속에 새겨졌다."], ["Lorry, as important above all others; the first, that this must be kept secret from Lucie; the second, that it must be kept secret from all who knew him.", "첫째는 이 일을 루시에게 비밀로 해야 한다는 것이었고, 둘째는 그를 아는 모든 이에게 비밀로 해야 한다는 것이었다."], ["In conjunction with Miss Pross, he took immediate steps towards the latter precaution, by giving out that the Doctor was not well, and required a few days of complete rest.", "그는 프로스 양과 손을 맞잡고 후자의 방비를 위해 즉시 조치를 취하였으니, 의사 선생이 몸이 좋지 않아 며칠간 완전한 휴식이 필요하다고 둘러댔다."], ["In aid of the kind deception to be practised on his daughter, Miss Pross was to write, describing his having been called away professionally, and referring to an imaginary letter of two or three hurried lines in his own hand, represented to have been addressed to her by the same post.", "그의 딸에게 행할 그 다정한 속임수를 거들기 위해, 프로스 양이 편지를 써서 그가 직업상 불려 갔다고 적고, 그가 직접 쓴 급히 갈겨쓴 두세 줄짜리 가상의 편지를 언급하기로 했으니, 그 편지는 같은 우편으로 그녀에게 부쳐진 것처럼 꾸며질 터였다."]], [["These measures, advisable to be taken in any case, Mr. Lorry took in the hope of his coming to himself.", "어떤 경우에든 취해 두는 편이 나을 이러한 조치들을, 로리 씨는 그가 제정신으로 돌아오기를 바라는 마음에서 취했다."], ["If that should happen soon, he kept another course in reserve; which was, to have a certain opinion that he thought the best, on the Doctor’s case.", "만일 그 일이 곧 일어난다면, 그는 따로 마련해 둔 또 다른 방책이 있었으니, 그것은 의사 선생의 증상에 관해 자신이 최선이라 여기는 어떤 의견을 구하는 것이었다."]], [["In the hope of his recovery, and of resort to this third course being thereby rendered practicable, Mr. Lorry resolved to watch him attentively, with as little appearance as possible of doing so.", "그가 회복되어 이 세 번째 방책에 의지하는 것이 가능해지기를 바라는 마음에서, 로리 씨는 가능한 한 그러는 티를 내지 않으면서 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보기로 결심했다."], ["He therefore made arrangements to absent himself from Tellson’s for the first time in his life, and took his post by the window in the same room.", "그리하여 그는 평생 처음으로 텔슨 은행을 비우도록 일을 꾸며 두고, 같은 방의 창가에 자기 자리를 잡았다."]], [["He was not long in discovering that it was worse than useless to speak to him, since, on being pressed, he became worried.", "그는 오래지 않아 그에게 말을 거는 것이 쓸데없을 뿐 아니라 오히려 해롭다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 다그치면 그가 안절부절못했기 때문이다."], ["He abandoned that attempt on the first day, and resolved merely to keep himself always before him, as a silent protest against the delusion into which he had fallen, or was falling.", "그는 첫날에 그 시도를 단념하고, 그저 늘 그의 앞에 자신을 두기로만 마음먹었으니, 그가 빠져들었거나 빠져들고 있는 그 망상에 대한 말 없는 항의로서였다."], ["He remained, therefore, in his seat near the window, reading and writing, and expressing in as many pleasant and natural ways as he could think of, that it was a free place.", "그리하여 그는 창가의 자기 자리에 머물며 책을 읽고 글을 썼고, 자신이 떠올릴 수 있는 한 갖가지 유쾌하고 자연스러운 방식으로, 이곳이 자유로운 자리임을 표현했다."]], [["Doctor Manette took what was given him to eat and drink, and worked on, that first day, until it was too dark to see--worked on, half an hour after Mr. Lorry could not have seen, for his life, to read or write.", "마네뜨 선생은 차려준 먹을 것과 마실 것을 받아들고는, 첫날 내내 너무 어두워져 보이지 않을 때까지 계속 일했다 — 로리 씨가 무슨 수를 써도 읽거나 쓸 수 없게 된 뒤로도 반 시간을 더 일했다."], ["When he put his tools aside as useless, until morning, Mr. Lorry rose and said to him:", "그가 연장들을 아침까지 쓸모없다며 옆으로 치워 놓자, 로리 씨가 일어나 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Will you go out?”", "\"밖에 나가시겠소?\""]], [["He looked down at the floor on either side of him in the old manner, looked up in the old manner, and repeated in the old low voice:", "그는 옛날 방식 그대로 자기 양옆의 바닥을 내려다보고, 옛날 방식 그대로 고개를 들고는, 옛날의 그 나직한 목소리로 되뇌었다."]], [["“Out?”", "\"밖에?\""]], [["“Yes; for a walk with me.", "\"그렇소. 나와 함께 산책하러요."], ["Why not?”", "어떻소?\""]], [["He made no effort to say why not, and said not a word more. But, Mr.", "그는 어떻소라고 하는지 말하려는 노력조차 하지 않았고, 더는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["Lorry thought he saw, as he leaned forward on his bench in the dusk, with his elbows on his knees and his head in his hands, that he was in some misty way asking himself, “Why not?”", "그러나 로리 씨는 어스름 속에서 그가 작업대 위로 몸을 기울여 두 팔꿈치를 무릎에 얹고 두 손으로 머리를 감싼 채, 어떤 어렴풋한 방식으로 \"어떻소?\" 하고 스스로에게 묻고 있다고 본 듯했다."], ["The sagacity of the man of business perceived an advantage here, and determined to hold it.", "사무가의 명민함이 여기서 한 가닥 가망을 알아챘고, 그것을 붙잡아 두기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Miss Pross and he divided the night into two watches, and observed him at intervals from the adjoining room.", "프로스 양과 그는 밤을 두 차례의 당번으로 나누어, 옆방에서 틈틈이 그를 지켜보았다."], ["He paced up and down for a long time before he lay down; but, when he did finally lay himself down, he fell asleep.", "그는 자리에 눕기 전 오랫동안 이리저리 거닐었으나, 마침내 자리에 눕고 나서는 잠이 들었다."], ["In the morning, he was up betimes, and went straight to his bench and to work.", "아침이면 그는 일찍 일어나 곧장 작업대로, 일거리로 갔다."]], [["On this second day, Mr. Lorry saluted him cheerfully by his name, and spoke to him on topics that had been of late familiar to them.", "이 둘째 날에 로리 씨는 그의 이름을 부르며 밝게 인사했고, 근래 그들에게 익숙해진 화제들로 그에게 말을 걸었다."], ["He returned no reply, but it was evident that he heard what was said, and that he thought about it, however confusedly. This encouraged Mr.", "그는 아무런 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 하는 말을 듣고 있고, 비록 혼란스럽게나마 그것을 곱씹고 있음은 분명했다."], ["Lorry to have Miss Pross in with her work, several times during the day; at those times, they quietly spoke of Lucie, and of her father then present, precisely in the usual manner, and as if there were nothing amiss.", "이에 힘을 얻은 로리 씨는 그날 낮 동안 여러 차례 프로스 양을 일감과 함께 안으로 불러들였다. 그럴 때면 그들은 루시에 관해, 그리고 그 자리에 있는 그녀의 아버지에 관해, 꼭 평소와 다름없는 방식으로, 마치 아무 탈도 없다는 듯이 조용히 이야기를 나누었다."], ["This was done without any demonstrative accompaniment, not long enough, or often enough to harass him; and it lightened Mr.", "이는 어떤 요란한 곁들임도 없이 행해졌고, 그를 괴롭힐 만큼 길지도 잦지도 않았다."], ["Lorry’s friendly heart to believe that he looked up oftener, and that he appeared to be stirred by some perception of inconsistencies surrounding him.", "그가 더 자주 고개를 드는 듯하고, 자신을 둘러싼 어떤 어긋남을 알아챈 기색에 흔들리는 듯해 보인다고 믿게 되니, 로리 씨의 다정한 마음이 한결 가벼워졌다."]], [["When it fell dark again, Mr. Lorry asked him as before:", "다시 어둠이 내리자, 로리 씨는 전처럼 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Dear Doctor, will you go out?”", "\"친애하는 선생, 밖에 나가시겠소?\""]], [["As before, he repeated, “Out?”", "전처럼 그가 되뇌었다. \"밖에?\""]], [["“Yes; for a walk with me.", "\"그렇소. 나와 함께 산책하러요."], ["Why not?”", "어떻소?\""]], [["This time, Mr. Lorry feigned to go out when he could extract no answer from him, and, after remaining absent for an hour, returned.", "이번에 로리 씨는 그에게서 아무 대답도 끌어낼 수 없자 밖에 나가는 척하고는, 한 시간 동안 자리를 비웠다가 돌아왔다."], ["In the meanwhile, the Doctor had removed to the seat in the window, and had sat there looking down at the plane-tree; but, on Mr. Lorry’s return, he slipped away to his bench.", "그사이 의사 선생은 창가 자리로 옮겨 앉아 플라타너스 나무를 내려다보고 있었으나, 로리 씨가 돌아오자 슬그머니 자기 작업대로 자리를 옮겼다."]], [["The time went very slowly on, and Mr. Lorry’s hope darkened, and his heart grew heavier again, and grew yet heavier and heavier every day.", "시간은 더디게 흘러갔고, 로리 씨의 희망은 어두워졌으며, 그의 마음은 다시 무거워졌고, 날마다 더욱더 무거워졌다."], ["The third day came and went, the fourth, the fifth.", "셋째 날이 오고 갔으며, 넷째 날, 다섯째 날도 그러했다."], ["Five days, six days, seven days, eight days, nine days.", "닷새, 엿새, 이레, 여드레, 아흐레."]], [["With a hope ever darkening, and with a heart always growing heavier and heavier, Mr. Lorry passed through this anxious time.", "갈수록 어두워지는 희망과 늘 더욱더 무거워지는 마음을 안고, 로리 씨는 이 근심스러운 시간을 견뎌냈다."], ["The secret was well kept, and Lucie was unconscious and happy; but he could not fail to observe that the shoemaker, whose hand had been a little out at first, was growing dreadfully skilful, and that he had never been so intent on his work, and that his hands had never been so nimble and expert, as in the dusk of the ninth evening.", "비밀은 잘 지켜졌고, 루시는 아무것도 모른 채 행복했다. 그러나 그는, 처음에는 손이 다소 서툴렀던 그 구두장이가 무섭도록 능숙해져 가고 있음을, 그리고 아흐렛날 저녁의 어스름 속에서만큼 그가 일에 골몰한 적도, 그 손이 그토록 날래고 능란했던 적도 없었음을 알아차리지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX. An Opinion", "# CHAPTER XIX. An Opinion"]], [["Worn out by anxious watching, Mr. Lorry fell asleep at his post.", "불안한 간호로 지친 나머지, 로리 씨는 자기 자리에서 잠이 들고 말았다."], ["On the tenth morning of his suspense, he was startled by the shining of the sun into the room where a heavy slumber had overtaken him when it was dark night.", "마음 졸이며 보낸 열흘째 되는 아침, 그는 깊은 밤 짙은 잠에 빠졌던 그 방으로 햇살이 비쳐드는 바람에 깜짝 놀라 깨어났다."]], [["He rubbed his eyes and roused himself; but he doubted, when he had done so, whether he was not still asleep.", "그는 눈을 비비고 정신을 차렸으나, 그러고 나서도 자신이 여전히 잠들어 있는 게 아닌지 의심스러웠다."], ["For, going to the door of the Doctor’s room and looking in, he perceived that the shoemaker’s bench and tools were put aside again, and that the Doctor himself sat reading at the window.", "박사의 방문으로 가서 안을 들여다보니, 제화공의 작업대와 연장이 다시 한쪽으로 치워져 있고, 박사 본인은 창가에 앉아 책을 읽고 있었던 것이다."], ["He was in his usual morning dress, and his face (which Mr. Lorry could distinctly see), though still very pale, was calmly studious and attentive.", "그는 평소의 아침 차림이었고, 그 얼굴은(로리 씨가 또렷이 볼 수 있었다) 여전히 몹시 창백하긴 했으나 차분하게 무언가에 몰두한 채 정신을 집중하고 있었다."]], [["Even when he had satisfied himself that he was awake, Mr.", "자신이 깨어 있음을 확인하고 나서도, 로리 씨는 몇 순간 동안 지난번의 제화일이 그저 자신의 어지러운 꿈은 아니었나 하는 아찔한 불확실함에 휩싸였다."], ["Lorry felt giddily uncertain for some few moments whether the late shoemaking might not be a disturbed dream of his own; for, did not his eyes show him his friend before him in his accustomed clothing and aspect, and employed as usual; and was there any sign within their range, that the change of which he had so strong an impression had actually happened?", "눈앞에 보이는 그의 친구가 익숙한 옷차림과 모습으로, 여느 때처럼 일에 몰두해 있지 않은가. 그토록 강렬한 인상으로 남은 그 변화가 실제로 일어났다는 어떤 흔적이 시야 안에 있단 말인가."]], [["It was but the inquiry of his first confusion and astonishment, the answer being obvious.", "그러나 그것은 처음의 혼란과 경악에서 나온 물음일 뿐, 답은 명백했다."], ["If the impression were not produced by a real corresponding and sufficient cause, how came he, Jarvis Lorry, there?", "만약 그 인상이 실재하는, 그에 상응하고 충분한 원인에서 나온 것이 아니라면, 어떻게 자비스 로리 자신이 그곳에 있단 말인가."], ["How came he to have fallen asleep, in his clothes, on the sofa in Doctor Manette’s consulting-room, and to be debating these points outside the Doctor’s bedroom door in the early morning?", "어떻게 옷을 입은 채로 머네뜨 박사의 진찰실 소파에서 잠이 들었으며, 이른 아침에 박사의 침실 문 밖에서 이런 점들을 따지고 있단 말인가."]], [["Within a few minutes, Miss Pross stood whispering at his side.", "몇 분도 지나지 않아, 프로스 양이 그의 곁에 서서 속삭이고 있었다."], ["If he had had any particle of doubt left, her talk would of necessity have resolved it; but he was by that time clear-headed, and had none.", "만약 그에게 한 점의 의심이라도 남아 있었다면 그녀의 말이 어김없이 그것을 풀어주었겠지만, 그때쯤 그는 머리가 맑았고 의심이라곤 없었다."], ["He advised that they should let the time go by until the regular breakfast-hour, and should then meet the Doctor as if nothing unusual had occurred.", "그는 평소의 아침식사 시간이 될 때까지 그냥 시간을 흘려보낸 뒤, 아무런 별일도 없었다는 듯이 박사를 대하자고 제안했다."], ["If he appeared to be in his customary state of mind, Mr. Lorry would then cautiously proceed to seek direction and guidance from the opinion he had been, in his anxiety, so anxious to obtain.", "만약 그가 평소의 정신 상태로 보인다면, 로리 씨는 그제야 조심스럽게, 그가 그토록 애타게 얻고 싶어했던 의견으로부터 방향과 인도를 구해보려는 것이었다."]], [["Miss Pross, submitting herself to his judgment, the scheme was worked out with care.", "프로스 양이 그의 판단에 따르기로 하면서, 그 계획은 신중하게 실행되었다."], ["Having abundance of time for his usual methodical toilette, Mr. Lorry presented himself at the breakfast-hour in his usual white linen, and with his usual neat leg.", "평소의 꼼꼼한 몸단장을 할 시간이 넉넉했으므로, 로리 씨는 아침식사 시간에 여느 때처럼 흰 리넨 차림에 여느 때처럼 단정한 종아리를 하고서 나타났다."], ["The Doctor was summoned in the usual way, and came to breakfast.", "박사는 평소처럼 불려나와 아침식사 자리로 왔다."]], [["So far as it was possible to comprehend him without overstepping those delicate and gradual approaches which Mr. Lorry felt to be the only safe advance, he at first supposed that his daughter’s marriage had taken place yesterday.", "로리 씨가 유일하게 안전한 접근이라고 여긴 그 미묘하고 점진적인 접근의 선을 넘지 않으면서 그를 헤아릴 수 있는 한에서 보자면, 박사는 처음에 자기 딸의 결혼이 어제 있었던 것으로 알고 있는 듯했다."], ["An incidental allusion, purposely thrown out, to the day of the week, and the day of the month, set him thinking and counting, and evidently made him uneasy.", "일부러 슬쩍 던진, 무슨 요일이고 며칠인가에 대한 우연한 언급에 그는 생각하고 헤아리기 시작했고, 분명히 불안해했다."], ["In all other respects, however, he was so composedly himself, that Mr. Lorry determined to have the aid he sought.", "그러나 그 밖의 모든 점에서는 그토록 차분하게 제정신이었으므로, 로리 씨는 그가 구하던 도움을 받기로 결심했다."], ["And that aid was his own.", "그리고 그 도움이란 곧 박사 자신이었다."]], [["Therefore, when the breakfast was done and cleared away, and he and the Doctor were left together, Mr. Lorry said, feelingly:", "그리하여 아침식사가 끝나 식탁이 치워지고 그와 박사 둘만 남게 되자, 로리 씨는 진심을 담아 말했다."]], [["“My dear Manette, I am anxious to have your opinion, in confidence, on a very curious case in which I am deeply interested; that is to say, it is very curious to me; perhaps, to your better information it may be less so.”", "\"머네뜨 박사, 내가 깊이 마음 쓰고 있는 아주 묘한 사례에 대해 자네의 의견을 은밀히 듣고 싶네. 그러니까, 나에게는 아주 묘한 사례인데, 자네의 더 나은 식견으로 보면 덜 그럴지도 모르지.\""]], [["Glancing at his hands, which were discoloured by his late work, the Doctor looked troubled, and listened attentively.", "지난번 일로 변색된 자기 두 손을 흘끗 보면서, 박사는 괴로운 표정을 짓더니 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."], ["He had already glanced at his hands more than once.", "그는 이미 한 번 넘게 자기 손을 흘끗거린 터였다."]], [["“Doctor Manette,” said Mr.", "\"머네뜨 박사.\""], ["Lorry, touching him affectionately on the arm, “the case is the case of a particularly dear friend of mine.", "로리 씨가 다정하게 그의 팔을 어루만지며 말했다. \"이 사례는 특별히 소중한 내 친구의 사례라네."], ["Pray give your mind to it, and advise me well for his sake--and above all, for his daughter’s--his daughter’s, my dear Manette.”", "부디 마음을 기울여, 그를 위해—그리고 무엇보다도 그의 딸을 위해—그의 딸을 위해서 좋은 조언을 해주게, 친애하는 머네뜨.\""]], [["“If I understand,”", "\"내가 제대로 이해했다면,\" 박사가 낮게 가라앉은 어조로 말했다."], ["said the Doctor, in a subdued tone, “some mental shock--?”", "\"어떤 정신적 충격을—?\" \"그렇다네!\""]], [["“Yes!” “Be explicit,”", "\"분명하게 말해주게.\""], ["said the Doctor.", "박사가 말했다."], ["“Spare no detail.”", "\"어떤 세부도 빼놓지 말고.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry saw that they understood one another, and proceeded.", "로리 씨는 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음을 알아차리고 말을 이어갔다."]], [["“My dear Manette, it is the case of an old and a prolonged shock, of great acuteness and severity to the affections, the feelings, the--the--as you express it--the mind.", "\"친애하는 머네뜨, 이것은 오래되고 오래 지속된 충격의 사례라네. 애정과 감정, 그리고—그—자네 표현대로—그 정신에 대단히 날카롭고 혹독했던 충격이지."], ["The mind.", "그 정신 말일세."], ["It is the case of a shock under which the sufferer was borne down, one cannot say for how long, because I believe he cannot calculate the time himself, and there are no other means of getting at it.", "그 충격에 짓눌려 시달린 사례인데, 얼마나 오래였는지는 말할 수 없네. 그 본인도 그 시간을 헤아릴 수 없으리라고 보며, 달리 알아낼 방도도 없기 때문이지."], ["It is the case of a shock from which the sufferer recovered, by a process that he cannot trace himself--as I once heard him publicly relate in a striking manner.", "그것은 그 사람 자신도 그 과정을 더듬어볼 수 없는 식으로 그 충격에서 회복한 사례라네—언젠가 그가 사람들 앞에서 인상적으로 그 이야기를 하는 것을 내가 들은 적이 있지."], ["It is the case of a shock from which he has recovered, so completely, as to be a highly intelligent man, capable of close application of mind, and great exertion of body, and of constantly making fresh additions to his stock of knowledge, which was already very large.", "그것은 그가 너무도 완전히 회복하여, 정신을 면밀히 집중하고 몸을 크게 쓸 줄 알며, 이미 매우 방대했던 자기 지식의 곳간에 끊임없이 새로운 것을 보태나가는, 대단히 명민한 사람이 된 그런 사례라네. 그런데, 불행히도,\""], ["But, unfortunately, there has been,”", "그는 말을 멈추고 깊이 숨을 들이쉬었다."], ["he paused and took a deep breath--“a slight relapse.”", "\"가벼운 재발이 있었다네.\""]], [["The Doctor, in a low voice, asked, “Of how long duration?”", "박사가 낮은 목소리로 물었다. \"얼마나 오래 지속되었나?\""]], [["“Nine days and nights.”", "\"아흐레 밤낮일세.\""]], [["“How did it show itself?", "\"어떤 식으로 나타났나?"], ["I infer,”", "짐작건대,\""], ["glancing at his hands again, “in the resumption of some old pursuit connected with the shock?”", "다시 자기 손을 흘끗 보며, \"그 충격과 관련된 어떤 옛 일거리를 다시 시작한 것이겠지?\""]], [["“That is the fact.”", "\"바로 그렇다네.\""]], [["“Now, did you ever see him,”", "\"그런데, 자네는 그가,\" 박사가 또렷하고 침착하게, 그러나 여전히 낮은 목소리로 물었다."], ["asked the Doctor, distinctly and collectedly, though in the same low voice, “engaged in that pursuit originally?” “Once.”", "\"처음에 그 일에 매달려 있는 것을 본 적이 있나?\""]], [["“And when the relapse fell on him, was he in most respects--or in all respects--as he was then?”", "\"한 번 있네.\" \"그리고 재발이 그를 덮쳤을 때, 그는 대부분의 점에서—아니, 모든 점에서—그때와 같았나?\""]], [["“I think in all respects.”", "\"모든 점에서 그랬다고 보네.\""]], [["“You spoke of his daughter.", "\"자네가 그의 딸 이야기를 했지."], ["Does his daughter know of the relapse?”", "그 딸은 재발에 대해 아나?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닐세."], ["It has been kept from her, and I hope will always be kept from her.", "그녀에게는 숨겨왔고, 앞으로도 늘 숨길 수 있기를 바라네."], ["It is known only to myself, and to one other who may be trusted.”", "오직 나 자신과, 믿을 만한 또 한 사람만이 알고 있지.\""]], [["The Doctor grasped his hand, and murmured, “That was very kind.", "박사는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고 중얼거렸다. \"참으로 친절한 일이군."], ["That was very thoughtful!” Mr.", "참으로 사려 깊은 일이야!\""], ["Lorry grasped his hand in return, and neither of the two spoke for a little while.", "로리 씨도 그 손을 마주 쥐었고, 두 사람은 잠시 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Now, my dear Manette,” said Mr.", "\"그런데, 친애하는 머네뜨,\""], ["Lorry, at length, in his most considerate and most affectionate way, “I am a mere man of business, and unfit to cope with such intricate and difficult matters.", "마침내 로리 씨가 더없이 신중하고 더없이 다정한 태도로 말했다. \"나는 그저 사무를 보는 사람일 뿐이라, 이렇게 복잡하고 어려운 일에 맞설 만한 위인이 못 되네."], ["I do not possess the kind of information necessary; I do not possess the kind of intelligence; I want guiding.", "나는 그런 데 필요한 종류의 지식도 없고, 그런 종류의 분별력도 없어서, 인도가 필요하다네."], ["There is no man in this world on whom I could so rely for right guidance, as on you.", "이 세상에 올바른 인도를 받기 위해 자네만큼 의지할 수 있는 사람은 없네."], ["Tell me, how does this relapse come about?", "말해주게, 이 재발은 어떻게 일어나는 것인가?"], ["Is there danger of another?", "또다시 일어날 위험이 있는가?"], ["Could a repetition of it be prevented?", "재발을 막을 수 있는가?"], ["How should a repetition of it be treated?", "재발은 어떻게 다루어야 하는가?"], ["How does it come about at all?", "도대체 어떻게 일어나는 것인가?"], ["What can I do for my friend?", "내 친구를 위해 내가 무엇을 할 수 있겠나?"], ["No man ever can have been more desirous in his heart to serve a friend, than I am to serve mine, if I knew how.", "친구를 돕고자 하는 마음으로 따지자면, 만약 그 방법을 알기만 한다면 나만큼 진심으로 내 친구를 돕고 싶어한 사람은 일찍이 없었을 걸세."]], [["“But I don’t know how to originate, in such a case.", "\"하지만 이런 사례에서 어디서부터 시작해야 할지를 모르겠네."], ["If your sagacity, knowledge, and experience, could put me on the right track, I might be able to do so much; unenlightened and undirected, I can do so little.", "자네의 명민함과 지식과 경험이 나를 올바른 길로 들여놓아준다면, 나는 많은 것을 할 수 있을지도 모르네. 깨우침도 인도도 없으니 내가 할 수 있는 것이 너무도 적다네."], ["Pray discuss it with me; pray enable me to see it a little more clearly, and teach me how to be a little more useful.”", "부디 나와 함께 이 일을 의논해주게. 부디 내가 이것을 조금 더 분명히 볼 수 있게 해주고, 내가 조금이라도 더 쓸모 있게 되는 법을 가르쳐주게.\""]], [["Doctor Manette sat meditating after these earnest words were spoken, and Mr. Lorry did not press him.", "이 간곡한 말이 끝나고 머네뜨 박사는 앉은 채로 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼고, 로리 씨는 그를 재촉하지 않았다."]], [["“I think it probable,”", "\"내 생각에는 아마,\""], ["said the Doctor, breaking silence with an effort, “that the relapse you have described, my dear friend, was not quite unforeseen by its subject.”", "박사가 애써 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"친애하는 친구여, 자네가 묘사한 그 재발이 그 당사자에게 전혀 예견되지 않았던 것은 아닌 듯하네.\""]], [["“Was it dreaded by him?” Mr.", "\"그가 그것을 두려워했단 말인가?\""], ["Lorry ventured to ask.", "로리 씨가 조심스레 물었다."]], [["“Very much.”", "\"무척이나.\""], ["He said it with an involuntary shudder.", "그는 자기도 모르게 몸서리치며 그렇게 말했다."], ["“You have no idea how such an apprehension weighs on the sufferer’s mind, and how difficult--how almost impossible--it is, for him to force himself to utter a word upon the topic that oppresses him.”", "\"그런 두려움이 시달리는 사람의 마음을 얼마나 짓누르는지, 그리고 자기를 짓누르는 그 화제에 대해 한마디라도 억지로 입 밖에 내는 것이 그에게 얼마나 어려운—얼마나 거의 불가능한—일인지 자네는 짐작도 못 할 걸세.\""]], [["“Would he,” asked Mr.", "\"그렇다면 그가,\""], ["Lorry, “be sensibly relieved if he could prevail upon himself to impart that secret brooding to any one, when it is on him?”", "로리 씨가 물었다. \"그 은밀한 번민이 자신을 사로잡고 있을 때, 누군가에게 그것을 털어놓도록 스스로를 다잡을 수만 있다면 눈에 띄게 가벼워질 수 있을까?\""]], [["“I think so.", "\"그럴 걸세."], ["But it is, as I have told you, next to impossible.", "하지만 그것은, 내가 말했듯이, 거의 불가능한 일이라네."], ["I even believe it--in some cases--to be quite impossible.”", "나는 심지어 그것이—어떤 경우에는—전혀 불가능하다고 믿네.\""]], [["“Now,” said Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["Lorry, gently laying his hand on the Doctor’s arm again, after a short silence on both sides, “to what would you refer this attack?”", "양쪽이 잠시 침묵한 뒤에 로리 씨가 다시 부드럽게 박사의 팔에 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"이번 발작의 원인을 자네는 무엇으로 보겠나?\""]], [["“I believe,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["returned Doctor Manette, “that there had been a strong and extraordinary revival of the train of thought and remembrance that was the first cause of the malady.", "머네뜨 박사가 대답했다. \"그 병의 첫번째 원인이었던 일련의 생각과 기억이 강렬하고도 예사롭지 않게 되살아난 것이라네."], ["Some intense associations of a most distressing nature were vividly recalled, I think.", "더없이 괴로운 종류의 어떤 강렬한 연상들이 생생하게 되살아난 것이라고 보네."], ["It is probable that there had long been a dread lurking in his mind, that those associations would be recalled--say, under certain circumstances--say, on a particular occasion.", "아마 그의 마음속에는 그런 연상들이 되살아나리라는 두려움이 오랫동안—이를테면 어떤 상황에서, 이를테면 어떤 특정한 순간에—도사리고 있었을 걸세."], ["He tried to prepare himself in vain; perhaps the effort to prepare himself made him less able to bear it.”", "그는 헛되이 마음의 준비를 하려 애썼고, 어쩌면 그렇게 대비하려는 노력 자체가 그것을 견뎌낼 힘을 더 약하게 만들었는지도 모르지.\""]], [["“Would he remember what took place in the relapse?”", "\"그가 재발 중에 일어난 일을 기억할까?\""], ["asked Mr. Lorry, with natural hesitation.", "로리 씨가 자연스레 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["The Doctor looked desolately round the room, shook his head, and answered, in a low voice, “Not at all.”", "박사는 황량한 눈빛으로 방 안을 둘러보고, 고개를 저으며 낮은 목소리로 답했다. \"전혀 못 하네.\""]], [["“Now, as to the future,”", "\"그럼, 앞날에 대해서는,\""], ["hinted Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 넌지시 운을 뗐다."]], [["“As to the future,”", "\"앞날에 대해서라면,\""], ["said the Doctor, recovering firmness, “I should have great hope.", "박사가 단호함을 되찾으며 말했다. \"나는 크게 희망을 품겠네."], ["As it pleased Heaven in its mercy to restore him so soon, I should have great hope.", "하늘이 자비를 베푸시어 그를 이토록 빨리 회복시켜주셨으니, 나는 크게 희망을 품겠네."], ["He, yielding under the pressure of a complicated something, long dreaded and long vaguely foreseen and contended against, and recovering after the cloud had burst and passed, I should hope that the worst was over.”", "오랫동안 두려워하고 오랫동안 어렴풋이 예견하며 맞서 싸워온 어떤 복잡한 것의 압박에 굴복했다가, 그 먹구름이 터지고 지나간 뒤 회복한 것이니, 나는 최악의 고비는 지났기를 바라네.\""]], [["“Well, well!", "\"그렇군, 그래!"], ["That’s good comfort.", "그것 참 다행한 위안일세."], ["I am thankful!”", "고맙네!\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I am thankful!”", "\"감사한 일이지!\""], ["repeated the Doctor, bending his head with reverence.", "박사가 경건하게 고개를 숙이며 따라 말했다."]], [["“There are two other points,” said Mr.", "\"두 가지 더 짚고 싶은 점이 있네.\""], ["Lorry, “on which I am anxious to be instructed.", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"그 점에 대해 자네의 가르침을 꼭 받고 싶은데."], ["I may go on?”", "계속해도 되겠나?\""]], [["“You cannot do your friend a better service.”", "\"자네 친구에게 그보다 더 좋은 도움을 줄 수는 없을 걸세.\""], ["The Doctor gave him his hand.", "박사가 그에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“To the first, then.", "\"그럼 첫번째 점일세."], ["He is of a studious habit, and unusually energetic; he applies himself with great ardour to the acquisition of professional knowledge, to the conducting of experiments, to many things.", "그는 공부하는 습성이 있고, 유달리 정력적이라네. 그는 대단한 열정으로 직업적 지식을 쌓고, 실험을 수행하고, 여러 일에 매달리지."], ["Now, does he do too much?”", "그런데, 그가 너무 무리하는 것은 아닐까?\""]], [["“I think not.", "\"그렇지는 않다고 보네."], ["It may be the character of his mind, to be always in singular need of occupation.", "늘 유난히 일거리를 필요로 하는 것이 그 사람 정신의 본성일지도 모르지."], ["That may be, in part, natural to it; in part, the result of affliction.", "그것은 일부는 타고난 것이고, 일부는 시련의 결과일 걸세."], ["The less it was occupied with healthy things, the more it would be in danger of turning in the unhealthy direction.", "건전한 일에 덜 매달릴수록, 불건전한 방향으로 돌아설 위험이 더 커질 테지."], ["He may have observed himself, and made the discovery.”", "어쩌면 그 자신이 스스로를 관찰해서 그 사실을 알아냈는지도 모르네.\""]], [["“You are sure that he is not under too great a strain?”", "\"그가 너무 큰 부담을 받고 있는 것이 아니라고 확신하나?\""]], [["“I think I am quite sure of it.”", "\"꽤 확신한다고 보네.\""]], [["“My dear Manette, if he were overworked now--”", "\"친애하는 머네뜨, 만약 그가 지금 과로하고 있다면—\""]], [["“My dear Lorry, I doubt if that could easily be.", "\"친애하는 로리, 그런 일은 쉽게 일어나기 어려우리라고 보네."], ["There has been a violent stress in one direction, and it needs a counterweight.”", "한 방향으로 격렬한 압박이 있었으니, 거기에는 평형추가 필요하다네.\""]], [["“Excuse me, as a persistent man of business.", "\"끈질긴 사무가의 입장에서 미안하네만."], ["Assuming for a moment, that he was overworked; it would show itself in some renewal of this disorder?”", "잠시 그가 정말로 과로하고 있다고 가정해보세. 그러면 이 장애가 어떤 식으로든 다시 나타나는 형태로 드러날까?\""]], [["“I do not think so.", "\"그렇지는 않다고 보네."], ["I do not think,”", "나는,\""], ["said Doctor Manette with the firmness of self-conviction, “that anything but the one train of association would renew it.", "머네뜨 박사가 스스로 확신하는 단호함으로 말했다. \"오직 그 하나의 연상의 흐름 말고는 어떤 것도 그것을 다시 일으키지 못하리라고 보네."], ["I think that, henceforth, nothing but some extraordinary jarring of that chord could renew it.", "이제부터는 그 현(絃)을 예사롭지 않게 뒤흔드는 무언가가 아니고서는 그것을 되살리지 못하리라고 보네."], ["After what has happened, and after his recovery, I find it difficult to imagine any such violent sounding of that string again.", "일어났던 일과 그의 회복 이후로는, 그 줄이 다시 그토록 격렬하게 울리는 일은 상상하기 어렵다네."], ["I trust, and I almost believe, that the circumstances likely to renew it are exhausted.”", "나는 그것을 되살릴 만한 상황들이 다 소진되었다고 믿으며, 거의 그렇게 확신한다네.\""]], [["He spoke with the diffidence of a man who knew how slight a thing would overset the delicate organisation of the mind, and yet with the confidence of a man who had slowly won his assurance out of personal endurance and distress.", "그는 정신이라는 섬세한 조직을 무너뜨리는 데 얼마나 하찮은 것으로도 충분한지를 아는 사람의 조심스러움으로, 그러면서도 몸소 겪은 인내와 고통 속에서 천천히 확신을 얻어낸 사람의 자신감으로 말했다."], ["It was not for his friend to abate that confidence.", "그 친구로서는 그 자신감을 꺾을 일이 아니었다."], ["He professed himself more relieved and encouraged than he really was, and approached his second and last point.", "로리 씨는 실제보다 더 안심하고 더 고무된 척하면서, 두번째이자 마지막 점으로 다가갔다."], ["He felt it to be the most difficult of all; but, remembering his old Sunday morning conversation with Miss Pross, and remembering what he had seen in the last nine days, he knew that he must face it.", "그는 그것이 무엇보다 가장 어려운 문제임을 느꼈으나, 프로스 양과 나누었던 지난 일요일 아침의 대화를 떠올리고, 지난 아흐레 동안 자신이 본 것을 떠올리며, 그것을 정면으로 마주해야 한다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“The occupation resumed under the influence of this passing affliction so happily recovered from,” said Mr.", "\"이번에 그토록 다행히 회복된 일시적 시련의 영향으로 다시 시작된 그 일을,\""], ["Lorry, clearing his throat, “we will call--Blacksmith’s work, Blacksmith’s work.", "로리 씨가 목을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"우리 대장간 일이라고 부르세, 대장간 일이라고."], ["We will say, to put a case and for the sake of illustration, that he had been used, in his bad time, to work at a little forge.", "비유 삼아 한 가지 경우를 들자면, 그가 좋지 않던 시절에 자그마한 화덕에서 일하는 데 익숙했다고 해보세."], ["We will say that he was unexpectedly found at his forge again.", "그가 뜻밖에도 그 화덕 앞에 다시 있는 것이 발견되었다고 해보지."], ["Is it not a pity that he should keep it by him?”", "그가 그것을 곁에 두는 것은 안타까운 일이 아닐까?\""]], [["The Doctor shaded his forehead with his hand, and beat his foot nervously on the ground.", "박사는 손으로 이마를 가리고, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 두드렸다."]], [["“He has always kept it by him,” said Mr.", "\"그는 늘 그것을 곁에 두어왔네.\""], ["Lorry, with an anxious look at his friend.", "로리 씨가 친구를 불안하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Now, would it not be better that he should let it go?”", "\"그런데, 그것을 치워버리는 편이 더 낫지 않겠나?\""]], [["Still, the Doctor, with shaded forehead, beat his foot nervously on the ground.", "여전히 박사는 이마를 가린 채로, 초조하게 발로 바닥을 두드렸다."]], [["“You do not find it easy to advise me?”", "\"내게 조언하는 것이 쉽지 않은 모양이군?\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“I quite understand it to be a nice question.", "\"이것이 까다로운 문제임을 나도 충분히 알겠네."], ["And yet I think--”", "그래도 내 생각에는—\""], ["And there he shook his head, and stopped.", "그러더니 그는 고개를 젓고 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You see,”", "\"보게나,\" 머네뜨 박사가 거북한 침묵 끝에 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["said Doctor Manette, turning to him after an uneasy pause, “it is very hard to explain, consistently, the innermost workings of this poor man’s mind.", "\"이 가엾은 사람의 마음 가장 깊은 곳의 움직임을 앞뒤가 맞게 설명하기란 매우 어렵다네. 그는 한때 그 일거리를 그토록 무섭게 갈망했고, 그것이 찾아왔을 때 그토록 반가워했지."], ["He once yearned so frightfully for that occupation, and it was so welcome when it came; no doubt it relieved his pain so much, by substituting the perplexity of the fingers for the perplexity of the brain, and by substituting, as he became more practised, the ingenuity of the hands, for the ingenuity of the mental torture; that he has never been able to bear the thought of putting it quite out of his reach.", "의심할 바 없이 그것은 머릿속의 혼란을 손가락의 혼란으로 대신하게 하고, 또 그가 점점 익숙해짐에 따라 정신적 고문의 교묘함을 손의 교묘함으로 대신하게 함으로써, 그의 고통을 그토록 덜어주었던 것이지. 그래서 그는 그것을 자기 손 닿지 않는 곳에 아주 치워버린다는 생각을 결코 견뎌내지 못했네."], ["Even now, when I believe he is more hopeful of himself than he has ever been, and even speaks of himself with a kind of confidence, the idea that he might need that old employment, and not find it, gives him a sudden sense of terror, like that which one may fancy strikes to the heart of a lost child.”", "그가 그 어느 때보다 자신에 대해 더 희망을 품고 있고, 심지어 일종의 자신감을 갖고 스스로를 이야기하기까지 한다고 내가 믿는 지금에도, 그 옛 일거리가 필요해질지 모르는데 그것을 찾지 못할지도 모른다는 생각은 그에게 별안간 공포감을 안겨준다네. 길 잃은 아이의 가슴에 사무친다고 상상해봄 직한 그런 공포 말일세.\""]], [["He looked like his illustration, as he raised his eyes to Mr. Lorry’s face.", "그가 로리 씨의 얼굴로 눈을 들어 올렸을 때, 그 모습은 자신이 든 그 비유 그대로였다."]], [["“But may not--mind!", "\"하지만—잘 듣게!"], ["I ask for information, as a plodding man of business who only deals with such material objects as guineas, shillings, and bank-notes--may not the retention of the thing involve the retention of the idea?", "나는 기니화와 실링화와 지폐 같은 그런 물질적인 것만 다루는, 한 우직한 사무가로서 알고 싶어 묻는 것이네만—그 물건을 간직하는 것이 곧 그 생각을 간직하는 것을 수반하지는 않겠나?"], ["If the thing were gone, my dear Manette, might not the fear go with it?", "그 물건이 사라진다면, 친애하는 머네뜨, 그 두려움도 함께 사라지지 않겠나?"], ["In short, is it not a concession to the misgiving, to keep the forge?”", "요컨대, 그 화덕을 간직한다는 것은 그 불안에 굴복하는 것이 아니겠나?\""]], [["There was another silence.", "또 한 번 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“You see, too,”", "\"게다가 자네도 알다시피,\""], ["said the Doctor, tremulously, “it is such an old companion.”", "박사가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그것은 그토록 오래된 동무라네.\""]], [["“I would not keep it,” said Mr.", "\"나라면 그것을 간직하지 않겠네.\""], ["Lorry, shaking his head; for he gained in firmness as he saw the Doctor disquieted.", "로리 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. 박사가 불안해하는 것을 보면서 그는 오히려 단호해졌던 것이다."], ["“I would recommend him to sacrifice it.", "\"나라면 그것을 희생시키라고 권하겠네."], ["I only want your authority.", "나는 다만 자네의 승인을 바랄 뿐이야."], ["I am sure it does no good. Come!", "그것이 아무 도움도 안 된다고 나는 확신하네."], ["Give me your authority, like a dear good man.", "자, 어서! 마음씨 고운 사람답게 내게 승인을 주게."], ["For his daughter’s sake, my dear Manette!”", "그의 딸을 위해서 말일세, 친애하는 머네뜨!\""]], [["Very strange to see what a struggle there was within him!", "그의 내면에 얼마나 큰 갈등이 일고 있었는지를 보는 것은 참으로 묘한 일이었다!"]], [["“In her name, then, let it be done; I sanction it.", "\"그렇다면 그녀의 이름으로 그렇게 하게나. 내가 그것을 승인하네."], ["But, I would not take it away while he was present.", "다만, 그가 있을 때 그것을 가져가지는 않겠네."], ["Let it be removed when he is not there; let him miss his old companion after an absence.”", "그가 없을 때 치워주게. 한동안 자리를 비웠다 돌아와서야 그 옛 동무가 없어진 것을 그가 알아채도록 하게.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry readily engaged for that, and the conference was ended.", "로리 씨는 기꺼이 그렇게 하기로 약속했고, 그것으로 의논은 끝났다."], ["They passed the day in the country, and the Doctor was quite restored.", "그들은 그날 하루를 시골에서 보냈고, 박사는 완전히 회복되었다."], ["On the three following days he remained perfectly well, and on the fourteenth day he went away to join Lucie and her husband.", "이어진 사흘 동안 그는 더없이 건강했고, 열나흘째 되는 날 루씨와 그녀의 남편에게 가기 위해 길을 떠났다."], ["The precaution that had been taken to account for his silence, Mr. Lorry had previously explained to him, and he had written to Lucie in accordance with it, and she had no suspicions.", "그의 침묵을 둘러대기 위해 취해둔 조치에 대해서는 로리 씨가 미리 그에게 설명해두었고, 그는 그에 맞추어 루씨에게 편지를 써두었으므로, 그녀는 아무런 의심도 하지 않았다."]], [["On the night of the day on which he left the house, Mr. Lorry went into his room with a chopper, saw, chisel, and hammer, attended by Miss Pross carrying a light.", "그가 집을 떠난 날 밤, 로리 씨는 도끼와 톱과 끌과 망치를 들고 그의 방으로 들어갔고, 프로스 양이 등불을 들고 함께 했다."], ["There, with closed doors, and in a mysterious and guilty manner, Mr. Lorry hacked the shoemaker’s bench to pieces, while Miss Pross held the candle as if she were assisting at a murder--for which, indeed, in her grimness, she was no unsuitable figure.", "거기서 문을 닫아건 채로, 은밀하고 죄지은 듯한 태도로, 로리 씨는 제화공의 작업대를 산산조각으로 쪼개버렸고, 그동안 프로스 양은 마치 살인을 거드는 사람처럼 촛불을 들고 있었다—실로 그 험상궂은 모습으로 보아 그녀는 그 일에 어울리지 않는 인물도 아니었다."], ["The burning of the body (previously reduced to pieces convenient for the purpose) was commenced without delay in the kitchen fire; and the tools, shoes, and leather, were buried in the garden.", "(그 목적에 알맞게 미리 토막 낸) 그 몸뚱이를 태우는 일은 지체 없이 부엌 아궁이에서 시작되었고, 연장과 신발과 가죽은 정원에 묻었다."], ["So wicked do destruction and secrecy appear to honest minds, that Mr. Lorry and Miss Pross, while engaged in the commission of their deed and in the removal of its traces, almost felt, and almost looked, like accomplices in a horrible crime.", "정직한 마음에는 파괴와 은폐가 어찌나 사악해 보이는지, 로리 씨와 프로스 양은 그 일을 저지르고 그 흔적을 없애는 동안 거의, 어떤 끔찍한 범죄의 공범처럼 느꼈고 또 그렇게 보이기까지 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XX. A Plea", "# CHAPTER XX. A Plea"]], [["When the newly-married pair came home, the first person who appeared, to offer his congratulations, was Sydney Carton.", "신혼부부가 집으로 돌아왔을 때, 축하 인사를 건네려고 가장 먼저 나타난 사람은 시드니 카턴이었다."], ["They had not been at home many hours, when he presented himself.", "부부가 집에 돌아온 지 몇 시간 지나지 않아 그가 모습을 드러냈다."], ["He was not improved in habits, or in looks, or in manner; but there was a certain rugged air of fidelity about him, which was new to the observation of Charles Darnay.", "그는 습관에서도, 외모에서도, 태도에서도 나아진 데가 없었다. 그러나 그에게는 어딘가 거칠지만 진실한 분위기가 감돌았는데, 그것은 찰스 다네이의 관찰에는 새로운 것이었다."]], [["He watched his opportunity of taking Darnay aside into a window, and of speaking to him when no one overheard.", "그는 다네이를 창가로 따로 데려가, 아무도 엿듣지 않을 때 그에게 말할 기회를 엿보았다."]], [["“Mr. Darnay,”", "\"다네이 씨,\""], ["said Carton, “I wish we might be friends.”", "카턴이 말했다. \"우리가 친구가 될 수 있으면 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["“We are already friends, I hope.”", "\"우리는 이미 친구가 아닙니까, 그러길 바랍니다만.\""]], [["“You are good enough to say so, as a fashion of speech; but, I don’t mean any fashion of speech.", "\"그렇게 말씀해주시니 고맙습니다, 인사치레로 하는 말이라도. 하지만 저는 인사치레로 하는 말이 아닙니다."], ["Indeed, when I say I wish we might be friends, I scarcely mean quite that, either.”", "사실, 우리가 친구가 될 수 있으면 좋겠다고 말할 때도, 저는 그저 그것만을 뜻하는 것도 아닙니다.\""]], [["Charles Darnay--as was natural--asked him, in all good-humour and good-fellowship, what he did mean?", "찰스 다네이는 — 당연한 일이지만 — 더없이 명랑하고 우애로운 태도로, 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다."]], [["“Upon my life,”", "\"맹세코,\""], ["said Carton, smiling, “I find that easier to comprehend in my own mind, than to convey to yours.", "카턴이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그게 제 마음속에서 이해하기는 쉬워도 당신께 전하기는 더 어렵군요."], ["However, let me try.", "그래도, 한번 해보지요."], ["You remember a certain famous occasion when I was more drunk than--than usual?”", "제가 — 평소보다도 — 더 취해 있던 어떤 유명한 일이 있었던 걸 기억하십니까?\""]], [["“I remember a certain famous occasion when you forced me to confess that you had been drinking.”", "\"당신이 술을 마셨다는 걸 제게 인정하라고 강요했던 어떤 유명한 일이 있었던 건 기억합니다.\""]], [["“I remember it too.", "\"저도 그걸 기억합니다."], ["The curse of those occasions is heavy upon me, for I always remember them.", "그런 일들의 저주가 제게 무겁게 얹혀 있지요, 저는 늘 그것들을 기억하니까요."], ["I hope it may be taken into account one day, when all days are at an end for me!", "언젠가, 제게 모든 날이 끝나는 날, 그것이 참작되기를 바랍니다!"], ["Don’t be alarmed; I am not going to preach.”", "놀라지 마세요, 설교를 늘어놓으려는 건 아니니까요.\""]], [["“I am not at all alarmed.", "\"전혀 놀라지 않습니다."], ["Earnestness in you, is anything but alarming to me.”", "당신의 진지함이라면, 제게는 결코 놀랄 일이 아닙니다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Carton, with a careless wave of his hand, as if he waved that away.", "카턴은 그것을 떨쳐버리려는 듯 손을 무심하게 내저으며 말했다."], ["“On the drunken occasion in question (one of a large number, as you know), I was insufferable about liking you, and not liking you.", "\"문제의 그 취했던 일에서 (아시다시피 수많은 일들 가운데 하나지요), 저는 당신을 좋아한다느니 좋아하지 않는다느니 하면서 참을 수 없을 정도로 굴었습니다."], ["I wish you would forget it.”", "그걸 잊어주셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["“I forgot it long ago.”", "\"저는 오래전에 잊었습니다.\""]], [["“Fashion of speech again!", "\"또 인사치레로군요!"], ["But, Mr. Darnay, oblivion is not so easy to me, as you represent it to be to you.", "하지만 다네이 씨, 망각이란 당신이 말씀하시는 만큼 제게는 쉽지가 않습니다."], ["I have by no means forgotten it, and a light answer does not help me to forget it.”", "저는 결코 그걸 잊지 못했고, 가벼운 대답은 제가 그걸 잊는 데 도움이 되지 않습니다.\""]], [["“If it was a light answer,”", "\"그것이 가벼운 대답이었다면,\""], ["returned Darnay, “I beg your forgiveness for it.", "다네이가 대답했다. \"그 점 용서를 빕니다."], ["I had no other object than to turn a slight thing, which, to my surprise, seems to trouble you too much, aside.", "저는 그저, 놀랍게도 당신을 너무 괴롭히는 듯한 사소한 일을 한쪽으로 치워버리려는 것 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었습니다."], ["I declare to you, on the faith of a gentleman, that I have long dismissed it from my mind.", "신사의 신의를 걸고 분명히 말씀드리는데, 저는 오래전에 그걸 마음에서 떨쳐버렸습니다."], ["Good Heaven, what was there to dismiss!", "맙소사, 떨쳐버릴 게 뭐가 있었겠습니까!"], ["Have I had nothing more important to remember, in the great service you rendered me that day?”", "그날 당신이 제게 베풀어준 그 큰 도움에서, 제가 기억해야 할 더 중요한 것이 없었겠습니까?\""]], [["“As to the great service,”", "\"그 큰 도움이라는 것에 관해서라면,\" 카턴이 말했다."], ["said Carton, “I am bound to avow to you, when you speak of it in that way, that it was mere professional claptrap, I don’t know that I cared what became of you, when I rendered it.--Mind!", "\"당신이 그런 식으로 말씀하시니, 솔직히 털어놓아야겠는데, 그건 한낱 직업적인 허풍이었을 뿐입니다. 그걸 베풀었을 때, 제가 당신이 어떻게 되든 신경을 썼는지조차 모르겠습니다."], ["I say when I rendered it; I am speaking of the past.”", "— 잘 들으세요! 베풀었을 때라고 말씀드린 겁니다, 저는 과거를 이야기하고 있는 거예요.\""]], [["“You make light of the obligation,”", "\"당신은 그 은혜를 가볍게 여기시는군요,\""], ["returned Darnay, “but I will not quarrel with your light answer.”", "다네이가 대답했다. \"하지만 당신의 가벼운 대답을 두고 다투지는 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“Genuine truth, Mr. Darnay, trust me!", "\"진실한 진심입니다, 다네이 씨, 저를 믿어주세요!"], ["I have gone aside from my purpose; I was speaking about our being friends.", "제가 본론에서 벗어났군요. 저는 우리가 친구가 되는 것에 대해 말하고 있었습니다."], ["Now, you know me; you know I am incapable of all the higher and better flights of men.", "자, 당신은 저를 아십니다. 제가 사람들이 도달하는 더 높고 더 나은 경지에는 도무지 이를 수 없는 자라는 걸 아시지요."], ["If you doubt it, ask Stryver, and he’ll tell you so.”", "의심스러우시면 스트라이버에게 물어보세요, 그가 그렇다고 말해줄 겁니다.\""]], [["“I prefer to form my own opinion, without the aid of his.”", "\"저는 그의 도움 없이 제 나름의 견해를 세우는 편이 좋습니다.\""]], [["“Well!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["At any rate you know me as a dissolute dog, who has never done any good, and never will.”", "어쨌든 당신은 저를 방탕한 개 같은 놈으로, 좋은 일이라고는 한 적도 없고 앞으로도 결코 하지 않을 자로 알고 계시지요.\""]], [["“I don’t know that you ‘never will.’”", "\"당신이 '앞으로도 결코 하지 않을' 거라는 건 모르겠는데요.\""]], [["“But I do, and you must take my word for it.", "\"하지만 저는 압니다, 제 말을 믿으셔야 합니다."], ["Well!", "좋습니다!"], ["If you could endure to have such a worthless fellow, and a fellow of such indifferent reputation, coming and going at odd times, I should ask that I might be permitted to come and go as a privileged person here; that I might be regarded as an useless (and I would add, if it were not for the resemblance I detected between you and me, an unornamental) piece of furniture, tolerated for its old service, and taken no notice of.", "만약 이렇게 쓸모없는 자, 이렇게 변변찮은 평판을 지닌 자가 이따금 드나드는 걸 견딜 수 있으시다면, 저는 이곳에 특권을 가진 사람으로 드나드는 것을 허락받기를 청하고 싶습니다. 옛 봉사를 봐서 너그러이 봐주는, 누구도 눈여겨보지 않는, 쓸모없는 (그리고 당신과 저 사이에서 제가 발견한 닮은 점이 아니었다면, 볼품없는, 이라고 덧붙이고 싶군요) 가구 한 점쯤으로 여겨지기를 청합니다."], ["I doubt if I should abuse the permission.", "제가 그 허락을 남용할 것 같지는 않습니다."], ["It is a hundred to one if I should avail myself of it four times in a year.", "일년에 네 번이라도 그걸 이용한다면 그건 백에 하나 있을까 말까 한 일이지요."], ["It would satisfy me, I dare say, to know that I had it.”", "단언컨대, 그저 그런 허락을 받았다는 걸 아는 것만으로도 저는 만족할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Will you try?”", "\"한번 해보시겠습니까?\""]], [["“That is another way of saying that I am placed on the footing I have indicated.", "\"그건 제가 말씀드린 그런 입장에 놓였다는 걸 달리 표현하는 말씀이군요."], ["I thank you, Darnay.", "고맙습니다, 다네이."], ["I may use that freedom with your name?”", "당신의 이름을 그렇게 격의 없이 불러도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“I think so, Carton, by this time.”", "\"이제는 그래도 될 것 같습니다, 카턴.\""]], [["They shook hands upon it, and Sydney turned away.", "그들은 그 일을 두고 악수를 나누었고, 시드니는 돌아섰다."], ["Within a minute afterwards, he was, to all outward appearance, as unsubstantial as ever.", "그러고는 일분도 채 지나지 않아, 겉으로 보기에 그는 여느 때처럼 실속 없는 사람으로 되돌아가 있었다."]], [["When he was gone, and in the course of an evening passed with Miss Pross, the Doctor, and Mr. Lorry, Charles Darnay made some mention of this conversation in general terms, and spoke of Sydney Carton as a problem of carelessness and recklessness.", "그가 떠난 뒤, 프로스 양과 박사와 로리 씨와 함께 보낸 저녁 동안, 찰스 다네이는 이 대화를 대략적으로 언급하면서, 시드니 카턴을 무신경과 무모함의 문제로 이야기했다."], ["He spoke of him, in short, not bitterly or meaning to bear hard upon him, but as anybody might who saw him as he showed himself.", "요컨대 그는 카턴을 두고 신랄하게 말하거나 그를 모질게 깎아내리려는 것이 아니라, 그가 스스로 드러내 보이는 모습 그대로 그를 본 사람이라면 누구라도 했을 법한 식으로 말했다."]], [["He had no idea that this could dwell in the thoughts of his fair young wife; but, when he afterwards joined her in their own rooms, he found her waiting for him with the old pretty lifting of the forehead strongly marked.", "그는 이것이 어여쁘고 젊은 아내의 생각에 머물러 있으리라고는 미처 생각지 못했다. 그러나 나중에 그가 자기네 방에서 아내와 합류했을 때, 그는 예의 그 어여쁘게 이마를 치켜올리는 표정을 뚜렷이 지은 채 자신을 기다리는 그녀를 보았다."]], [["“We are thoughtful to-night!”", "\"오늘 밤 우리는 생각이 많은가 보군!\""], ["said Darnay, drawing his arm about her.", "다네이가 그녀에게 팔을 두르며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, dearest Charles,”", "\"네, 사랑하는 찰스,\""], ["with her hands on his breast, and the inquiring and attentive expression fixed upon him; “we are rather thoughtful to-night, for we have something on our mind to-night.”", "그의 가슴에 두 손을 얹고 묻는 듯 골똘한 표정을 그에게 고정한 채 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 우리는 좀 생각이 많아요, 오늘 밤 우리 마음에 무언가 걸리는 게 있거든요.\""]], [["“What is it, my Lucie?”", "\"그게 뭐요, 나의 루시?\""]], [["“Will you promise not to press one question on me, if I beg you not to ask it?”", "\"제가 묻지 말아달라고 청하면, 그 한 가지 질문을 제게 강요하지 않겠다고 약속해주시겠어요?\""]], [["“Will I promise?", "\"약속하겠냐고?"], ["What will I not promise to my Love?”", "내 사랑에게 무엇인들 약속하지 못하겠소?\""]], [["What, indeed, with his hand putting aside the golden hair from the cheek, and his other hand against the heart that beat for him!", "정말이지, 한 손으로는 그녀의 뺨에서 금빛 머리카락을 쓸어 넘기고, 다른 한 손은 자신을 위해 뛰는 그 가슴에 댄 채로 무엇인들!"]], [["“I think, Charles, poor Mr. Carton deserves more consideration and respect than you expressed for him to-night.”", "\"제 생각엔 말이에요, 찰스, 가엾은 카턴 씨는 당신이 오늘 밤 그에 대해 표현한 것보다 더 많은 배려와 존중을 받을 만한 분이에요.\""]], [["“Indeed, my own?", "\"정말 그렇소, 내 사랑?"], ["Why so?”", "어째서 그렇소?\""]], [["“That is what you are not to ask me.", "\"그게 바로 제게 물으시면 안 되는 거예요."], ["But I think--I know--he does.”", "하지만 저는 생각해요 — 알아요 — 그분이 그렇다는 걸.\""]], [["“If you know it, it is enough.", "\"당신이 안다면, 그걸로 충분하오."], ["What would you have me do, my Life?”", "내가 어떻게 하길 바라오, 내 생명?\""]], [["“I would ask you, dearest, to be very generous with him always, and very lenient on his faults when he is not by.", "\"청하고 싶어요, 여보, 늘 그분에게 아주 너그럽게 대하시고, 그분이 곁에 없을 때 그분의 잘못을 아주 관대하게 봐주시기를요."], ["I would ask you to believe that he has a heart he very, very seldom reveals, and that there are deep wounds in it.", "그분에게는 아주, 아주 드물게만 드러내는 마음이 있고, 그 마음에 깊은 상처가 있다는 걸 믿어주시기를 청하고 싶어요."], ["My dear, I have seen it bleeding.”", "여보, 저는 그것이 피 흘리는 것을 보았어요.\""]], [["“It is a painful reflection to me,”", "\"내가 그에게 무슨 잘못을 했을지도 모른다는 건,\""], ["said Charles Darnay, quite astounded, “that I should have done him any wrong.", "찰스 다네이가 몹시 놀라며 말했다. \"내게는 고통스러운 생각이오."], ["I never thought this of him.”", "나는 그에 대해 그런 생각은 한 번도 해본 적이 없소.\""]], [["“My husband, it is so.", "\"여보, 정말 그래요."], ["I fear he is not to be reclaimed; there is scarcely a hope that anything in his character or fortunes is reparable now.", "저는 그분이 구제될 수 없을까 봐 두려워요. 이제 그분의 성품이나 처지에서 무언가 바로잡힐 수 있으리라는 희망은 거의 없어요."], ["But, I am sure that he is capable of good things, gentle things, even magnanimous things.”", "하지만 저는 그분이 선한 일, 다정한 일, 심지어 고결한 일까지도 해낼 수 있는 분이라고 확신해요.\""]], [["She looked so beautiful in the purity of her faith in this lost man, that her husband could have looked at her as she was for hours.", "이 길 잃은 남자에 대한 자신의 믿음이 지닌 순결함 속에서 그녀가 어찌나 아름다워 보였던지, 그녀의 남편은 그 모습 그대로의 그녀를 몇 시간이라도 바라볼 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["“And, O my dearest Love!”", "\"그리고, 오 사랑하는 나의 임이여!\""], ["she urged, clinging nearer to him, laying her head upon his breast, and raising her eyes to his, “remember how strong we are in our happiness, and how weak he is in his misery!”", "그녀는 그에게 더 가까이 매달리며, 그의 가슴에 머리를 얹고, 두 눈을 들어 그를 올려다보며 간청했다. \"우리가 우리의 행복 속에서 얼마나 강한지, 그리고 그분이 그분의 불행 속에서 얼마나 약한지를 기억해주세요!\""]], [["The supplication touched him home.", "그 간청이 그의 마음 깊이 와닿았다."], ["“I will always remember it, dear Heart!", "\"언제나 기억하리다, 사랑하는 그대여!"], ["I will remember it as long as I live.”", "살아 있는 동안 내내 기억하리다.\""]], [["He bent over the golden head, and put the rosy lips to his, and folded her in his arms.", "그는 금빛 머리 위로 몸을 굽혀, 장밋빛 입술에 자신의 입술을 대고, 그녀를 두 팔로 감싸안았다."], ["If one forlorn wanderer then pacing the dark streets, could have heard her innocent disclosure, and could have seen the drops of pity kissed away by her husband from the soft blue eyes so loving of that husband, he might have cried to the night--and the words would not have parted from his lips for the first time--", "만약 그때 어두운 거리를 거닐던 어느 쓸쓸한 방랑자가 그녀의 순결한 고백을 들을 수 있었더라면, 그리고 그 남편을 그토록 사랑하는 부드러운 푸른 눈에서 남편이 입맞춤으로 닦아내는 연민의 눈물방울을 볼 수 있었더라면, 그는 밤을 향해 외쳤을지도 모른다 — 그리고 그 말이 그의 입술에서 떠나는 것은 처음이 아니었으리라 —"]], [["“God bless her for her sweet compassion!”", "\"그녀의 그 다정한 연민에 신의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["# CHAPTER XXI. Echoing Footsteps", "# CHAPTER XXI. Echoing Footsteps"]], [["A wonderful corner for echoes, it has been remarked, that corner where the Doctor lived.", "의사가 살았던 그 모퉁이는 메아리가 울리기에 더없이 멋진 곳이라고들 했다."], ["Ever busily winding the golden thread which bound her husband, and her father, and herself, and her old directress and companion, in a life of quiet bliss, Lucie sat in the still house in the tranquilly resounding corner, listening to the echoing footsteps of years.", "남편과 아버지, 자기 자신, 그리고 자신의 옛 보호자이자 동무를 고요한 행복의 삶 속으로 묶어주는 황금실을 늘 부지런히 감으며, 루시는 잔잔히 메아리치는 그 모퉁이의 고요한 집에 앉아, 세월의 메아리치는 발소리에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["At first, there were times, though she was a perfectly happy young wife, when her work would slowly fall from her hands, and her eyes would be dimmed.", "처음에는, 그녀가 더할 나위 없이 행복한 젊은 아내였음에도, 손에서 일감이 천천히 떨어지고 두 눈이 흐려지는 때가 있었다."], ["For, there was something coming in the echoes, something light, afar off, and scarcely audible yet, that stirred her heart too much.", "메아리 속에서 무언가가 다가오고 있었기 때문이다. 가볍고, 아득히 멀고, 아직 거의 들리지 않는 무언가가 그녀의 마음을 너무도 뒤흔들었다."], ["Fluttering hopes and doubts--hopes, of a love as yet unknown to her: doubts, of her remaining upon earth, to enjoy that new delight--divided her breast.", "나풀거리는 희망과 의혹이 — 아직 그녀가 알지 못하는 사랑에 대한 희망, 그리고 그 새로운 기쁨을 누리며 지상에 남아 있을 수 있을지에 대한 의혹이 — 그녀의 가슴을 갈라놓았다."], ["Among the echoes then, there would arise the sound of footsteps at her own early grave; and thoughts of the husband who would be left so desolate, and who would mourn for her so much, swelled to her eyes, and broke like waves.", "그러면 그 메아리 속에서 그녀 자신의 이른 무덤가에 들려오는 발소리가 일어났고, 그토록 쓸쓸히 홀로 남아 그녀를 그토록 깊이 애도할 남편을 떠올리는 생각이 두 눈에 차올라 파도처럼 부서졌다."]], [["That time passed, and her little Lucie lay on her bosom.", "그 시절은 지나갔고, 어린 루시가 그녀의 품에 안겼다."], ["Then, among the advancing echoes, there was the tread of her tiny feet and the sound of her prattling words.", "그러자 다가오는 메아리 속에는 그 작은 발의 디딤 소리와 옹알거리는 말소리가 섞여 있었다."], ["Let greater echoes resound as they would, the young mother at the cradle side could always hear those coming.", "더 큰 메아리들이 제멋대로 울려대도, 요람 곁의 젊은 어머니는 언제나 그 다가오는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["They came, and the shady house was sunny with a child’s laugh, and the Divine friend of children, to whom in her trouble she had confided hers, seemed to take her child in his arms, as He took the child of old, and made it a sacred joy to her.", "그 소리가 다가오면, 그늘진 집은 아이의 웃음으로 환해졌고, 그녀가 어려움 속에서 자기 아이를 맡겼던 어린아이들의 거룩한 친구가 옛날에 어린아이를 안았듯이 그녀의 아이를 품에 안아, 그것을 그녀에게 성스러운 기쁨으로 만들어주는 듯했다."]], [["Ever busily winding the golden thread that bound them all together, weaving the service of her happy influence through the tissue of all their lives, and making it predominate nowhere, Lucie heard in the echoes of years none but friendly and soothing sounds.", "그들 모두를 함께 묶어주는 황금실을 늘 부지런히 감으며, 자신의 행복한 영향력의 봉사를 그들 삶의 직물 전체에 짜 넣으면서도 그것이 어느 곳에서도 두드러지지 않게 하면서, 루시는 세월의 메아리 속에서 다정하고 위안을 주는 소리밖에 듣지 못했다."], ["Her husband’s step was strong and prosperous among them; her father’s firm and equal.", "남편의 발걸음은 그 속에서 굳세고 번창했으며, 아버지의 발걸음은 단단하고 한결같았다."], ["Lo, Miss Pross, in harness of string, awakening the echoes, as an unruly charger, whip-corrected, snorting and pawing the earth under the plane-tree in the garden!", "보라, 프로스 양은 끈으로 된 마구를 차고서, 채찍질에 길든 거친 군마처럼 콧김을 내뿜고 정원의 플라타너스 아래 땅을 앞발로 긁으며 메아리를 깨우고 있었다!"]], [["Even when there were sounds of sorrow among the rest, they were not harsh nor cruel.", "다른 소리들 가운데 슬픔의 소리가 섞여 있을 때조차, 그것들은 거칠지도 잔혹하지도 않았다."], ["Even when golden hair, like her own, lay in a halo on a pillow round the worn face of a little boy, and he said, with a radiant smile, “Dear papa and mamma, I am very sorry to leave you both, and to leave my pretty sister; but I am called, and I must go!”", "그녀 자신의 것과 같은 황금빛 머리카락이 어느 어린 사내아이의 야윈 얼굴을 후광처럼 두르며 베개 위에 펼쳐져 있고, 그 아이가 환한 미소를 지으며 \"사랑하는 아빠 엄마, 두 분을 떠나고 예쁜 누이를 떠나게 되어 정말 슬퍼요."], ["those were not tears all of agony that wetted his young mother’s cheek, as the spirit departed from her embrace that had been entrusted to it.", "하지만 저는 부름을 받았으니, 가야만 해요!\"라고 말했을 때조차도, 그 영혼이 그것을 맡았던 그녀의 품에서 떠나가는 동안 젊은 어머니의 뺨을 적신 그 눈물이 온통 고통의 눈물만은 아니었다."], ["Suffer them and forbid them not.", "어린아이들이 내게 오는 것을 용납하고 막지 말라."], ["They see my Father’s face.", "그들은 내 아버지의 얼굴을 보느니라."], ["O Father, blessed words!", "오, 아버지, 복된 말씀이여!"]], [["Thus, the rustling of an Angel’s wings got blended with the other echoes, and they were not wholly of earth, but had in them that breath of Heaven.", "이렇게 천사의 날개가 스치는 소리가 다른 메아리들과 어우러졌고, 그것들은 온전히 지상의 것이 아니라 그 속에 천국의 숨결을 품고 있었다."], ["Sighs of the winds that blew over a little garden-tomb were mingled with them also, and both were audible to Lucie, in a hushed murmur--like the breathing of a summer sea asleep upon a sandy shore--as the little Lucie, comically studious at the task of the morning, or dressing a doll at her mother’s footstool, chattered in the tongues of the Two Cities that were blended in her life.", "어느 작은 정원 무덤 위로 부는 바람의 한숨도 그것들과 뒤섞였으니, 그 둘 모두가 루시에게는 — 모래 해변에서 잠든 여름 바다의 숨결처럼 — 나직한 속삭임으로 들려왔고, 그러는 동안 어린 루시는 우스꽝스러우리만치 진지하게 아침 공부에 몰두하거나, 어머니의 발치 의자에서 인형에게 옷을 입히면서, 자기 삶에 한데 섞여 있는 두 도시의 언어로 재잘거렸다."]], [["The Echoes rarely answered to the actual tread of Sydney Carton.", "그 메아리들은 시드니 카턴의 실제 발소리에 좀처럼 응답하지 않았다."], ["Some half-dozen times a year, at most, he claimed his privilege of coming in uninvited, and would sit among them through the evening, as he had once done often.", "그는 기껏해야 일 년에 대여섯 번쯤 초대받지 않고 찾아드는 자신의 특권을 내세웠고, 한때 자주 그랬듯이 저녁 내내 그들 가운데 앉아 있곤 했다."], ["He never came there heated with wine.", "그는 술에 달아오른 채로 그곳에 온 적이 없었다."], ["And one other thing regarding him was whispered in the echoes, which has been whispered by all true echoes for ages and ages.", "그리고 그에 관한 또 한 가지가 메아리 속에서 속삭여졌는데, 그것은 진실한 모든 메아리가 오랜 세월 동안 속삭여온 것이었다."]], [["No man ever really loved a woman, lost her, and knew her with a blameless though an unchanged mind, when she was a wife and a mother, but her children had a strange sympathy with him--an instinctive delicacy of pity for him.", "진정으로 한 여자를 사랑했고, 그녀를 잃었으며, 그녀가 아내가 되고 어머니가 된 뒤에도 변함없으면서도 흠 없는 마음으로 그녀를 알고 지낸 남자치고, 그 아이들이 그에게 묘한 공감을 — 그를 향한 본능적이고도 섬세한 연민을 — 품지 않은 경우는 없었다."], ["What fine hidden sensibilities are touched in such a case, no echoes tell; but it is so, and it was so here.", "그런 경우에 어떤 고운 숨은 감수성이 건드려지는지는 어떤 메아리도 말해주지 않지만, 어쨌든 그러하며, 이곳에서도 그러했다."], ["Carton was the first stranger to whom little Lucie held out her chubby arms, and he kept his place with her as she grew.", "카턴은 어린 루시가 통통한 팔을 내민 첫 낯선 사람이었고, 아이가 자라는 동안에도 그녀 곁에서 자기 자리를 지켰다."], ["The little boy had spoken of him, almost at the last.", "그 어린 사내아이도 거의 마지막 순간에 그에 관해 말했다."], ["“Poor Carton!", "\"불쌍한 카턴 아저씨!"], ["Kiss him for me!”", "저 대신 입맞춰주세요!\""]], [["Mr. Stryver shouldered his way through the law, like some great engine forcing itself through turbid water, and dragged his useful friend in his wake, like a boat towed astern.", "스트라이버 씨는 마치 탁한 물살을 헤치며 나아가는 어떤 거대한 기관처럼 어깨로 밀어붙여 법조계를 헤쳐나갔고, 자신의 쓸모 있는 친구를 뒤에 매단 거룻배처럼 끌고 다녔다."], ["As the boat so favoured is usually in a rough plight, and mostly under water, so, Sydney had a swamped life of it.", "그렇게 끌려가는 배가 대개는 험한 처지에 놓여 대부분 물속에 잠겨 있듯이, 시드니의 삶도 물에 잠긴 삶이었다."], ["But, easy and strong custom, unhappily so much easier and stronger in him than any stimulating sense of desert or disgrace, made it the life he was to lead; and he no more thought of emerging from his state of lion’s jackal, than any real jackal may be supposed to think of rising to be a lion.", "그러나 안일하고 끈질긴 습관이 — 불행하게도 그에게는 공적이나 치욕에 대한 그 어떤 자극적 의식보다 훨씬 더 안일하고 끈질긴 그 습관이 — 그것을 그가 살아갈 삶으로 만들어버렸으니, 그는 진짜 자칼이 사자로 올라설 생각을 하지 않듯이, 자신의 사자의 자칼이라는 처지에서 벗어날 생각을 더는 하지 않았다."], ["Stryver was rich; had married a florid widow with property and three boys, who had nothing particularly shining about them but the straight hair of their dumpling heads.", "스트라이버는 부유했다. 재산과 세 아들을 가진 혈색 좋은 과부와 결혼했는데, 그 아들들이라야 만두처럼 둥근 머리에 곧게 뻗은 머리카락 말고는 특별히 빛나는 구석이 없었다."]], [["These three young gentlemen, Mr. Stryver, exuding patronage of the most offensive quality from every pore, had walked before him like three sheep to the quiet corner in Soho, and had offered as pupils to Lucie’s husband: delicately saying “Halloa!", "스트라이버 씨는 모공마다 더없이 불쾌한 종류의 생색을 풍기며, 이 세 젊은 신사를 마치 양 세 마리처럼 앞세워 소호의 그 조용한 모퉁이로 데려가서는, 루시의 남편에게 제자로 받아달라고 내밀었다."], ["here are three lumps of bread-and-cheese towards your matrimonial picnic, Darnay!”", "\"이보게! 자네 결혼 소풍에 보태라고 빵과 치즈 덩어리 셋을 가져왔네, 다네이!\"라고 점잖게 말하면서 말이다."], ["The polite rejection of the three lumps of bread-and-cheese had quite bloated Mr. Stryver with indignation, which he afterwards turned to account in the training of the young gentlemen, by directing them to beware of the pride of Beggars, like that tutor-fellow.", "그 빵과 치즈 덩어리 셋이 정중히 거절당하자 스트라이버 씨는 분노로 잔뜩 부풀어 올랐는데, 나중에 그는 그것을 젊은 신사들의 교육에 써먹어, 그 가정교사 같은 작자처럼 거지의 자존심을 조심하라고 그들에게 일렀다."], ["He was also in the habit of declaiming to Mrs. Stryver, over his full-bodied wine, on the arts Mrs.", "또한 그는 묵직한 포도주를 들이켜며 스트라이버 부인에게, 다네이 부인이 한때 자기를 \"낚으려고\""], ["Darnay had once put in practice to “catch”", "부려먹은 수작에 관하여, 그리고 자신을 \"낚이지 않게\""], ["him, and on the diamond-cut-diamond arts in himself, madam, which had rendered him “not to be caught.”", "만들어준 자기 안의, 부인, 강철로 강철을 깎는 듯한 수완에 관하여 일장 연설을 늘어놓는 버릇이 있었다."], ["Some of his King’s Bench familiars, who were occasionally parties to the full-bodied wine and the lie, excused him for the latter by saying that he had told it so often, that he believed it himself--which is surely such an incorrigible aggravation of an originally bad offence, as to justify any such offender’s being carried off to some suitably retired spot, and there hanged out of the way.", "이따금 그 묵직한 포도주와 그 거짓말에 동석하곤 했던 그의 왕좌재판소 친구들 가운데 몇몇은, 그가 하도 자주 그 말을 하다 보니 스스로도 그것을 믿게 된 거라고 말하며 그 후자를 변명해주었는데 — 이는 본디도 못된 죄를 도무지 어찌해볼 수 없을 만큼 악화시키는 짓이어서, 그런 죄인이라면 어딘가 알맞게 외진 곳으로 끌려가 거치적거리지 않게 목매달려 마땅하다 할 만했다."]], [["These were among the echoes to which Lucie, sometimes pensive, sometimes amused and laughing, listened in the echoing corner, until her little daughter was six years old.", "이런 것들이, 어린 딸이 여섯 살이 될 때까지 루시가 그 메아리치는 모퉁이에서 — 때로는 생각에 잠겨, 때로는 즐거워하며 웃으면서 — 귀 기울인 메아리들 가운데 끼어 있었다."], ["How near to her heart the echoes of her child’s tread came, and those of her own dear father’s, always active and self-possessed, and those of her dear husband’s, need not be told.", "아이의 발소리 메아리가, 그리고 늘 활기차고 침착한 자기 아버지의 발소리 메아리가, 또 사랑하는 남편의 발소리 메아리가 그녀의 마음에 얼마나 가까이 다가왔는지는 굳이 말할 필요가 없다."], ["Nor, how the lightest echo of their united home, directed by herself with such a wise and elegant thrift that it was more abundant than any waste, was music to her.", "어떤 낭비보다도 더 풍요로울 만큼 현명하고 우아한 절약으로 그녀 자신이 꾸려가는 그 화목한 가정의 가장 가벼운 메아리가 그녀에게 얼마나 음악 같았는지도 굳이 말할 필요가 없다."], ["Nor, how there were echoes all about her, sweet in her ears, of the many times her father had told her that he found her more devoted to him married (if that could be) than single, and of the many times her husband had said to her that no cares and duties seemed to divide her love for him or her help to him, and asked her “What is the magic secret, my darling, of your being everything to all of us, as if there were only one of us, yet never seeming to be hurried, or to have too much to do?”", "또한 그녀의 귀에 감미롭게 들려오는 메아리들이 그녀 둘레에 가득했으니, 아버지가 그녀에게 미혼일 때보다 결혼한 뒤에 (그럴 수만 있다면) 자신에게 더 헌신적인 것 같다고 여러 번 말했던 일, 남편이 그녀에게 그 어떤 근심과 의무도 자신을 향한 그녀의 사랑이나 자신에 대한 그녀의 도움을 갈라놓는 것 같지 않다고 여러 번 말하며 \"여보, 우리가 단 한 사람뿐인 것처럼 당신이 우리 모두에게 그 모든 것이 되어주면서도, 결코 서두르거나 할 일이 너무 많은 것처럼 보이지 않는, 그 마법 같은 비결이 대체 뭐요?\"라고 물었던 일에 관한 메아리들이 그러했다는 것도, 굳이 말할 필요가 없다."]], [["But, there were other echoes, from a distance, that rumbled menacingly in the corner all through this space of time.", "그러나 이 모든 세월 동안, 멀리서부터 그 모퉁이에 위협적으로 우르릉거리는 다른 메아리들이 있었다."], ["And it was now, about little Lucie’s sixth birthday, that they began to have an awful sound, as of a great storm in France with a dreadful sea rising.", "그리고 어린 루시의 여섯번째 생일 무렵인 바로 지금, 그것들이 무시무시한 소리를 — 무서운 바다가 솟구치는 프랑스의 거대한 폭풍 같은 소리를 — 내기 시작했다."]], [["On a night in mid-July, one thousand seven hundred and eighty-nine, Mr. Lorry came in late, from Tellson’s, and sat himself down by Lucie and her husband in the dark window.", "천칠백팔십구년 칠월 중순의 어느 밤, 로리 씨가 텔슨 은행에서 늦게 돌아와, 어두운 창가에 앉은 루시와 그녀의 남편 곁에 자리를 잡았다."], ["It was a hot, wild night, and they were all three reminded of the old Sunday night when they had looked at the lightning from the same place.", "무덥고 거친 밤이었고, 세 사람 모두 예전에 같은 자리에서 번개를 바라보았던 그 옛 일요일 밤을 떠올렸다."]], [["“I began to think,” said Mr.", "\"오늘 밤은 텔슨에서 지새워야 하나 싶은 생각이 들기 시작했답니다.\""], ["Lorry, pushing his brown wig back, “that I should have to pass the night at Tellson’s.", "로리 씨가 갈색 가발을 뒤로 밀어 올리며 말했다."], ["We have been so full of business all day, that we have not known what to do first, or which way to turn.", "\"하루 종일 일이 어찌나 밀려들었는지, 뭘 먼저 해야 할지, 어느 쪽으로 손을 대야 할지 알 수가 없었어요."], ["There is such an uneasiness in Paris, that we have actually a run of confidence upon us!", "빠리가 어찌나 불안한지, 우리한테 실제로 예금이 밀려들고 있다니까요!"], ["Our customers over there, seem not to be able to confide their property to us fast enough.", "저쪽 우리 고객들이 자기 재산을 우리한테 맡기지 못해 안달인 것 같아요."], ["There is positively a mania among some of them for sending it to England.”", "그걸 영국으로 보내려는 광적인 열기가 그들 가운데 일부에게는 분명히 일고 있어요.\""]], [["“That has a bad look,”", "\"그거 안 좋은 조짐이로군요.\""], ["said Darnay--", "다네이가 말했다."]], [["“A bad look, you say, my dear Darnay?", "\"안 좋은 조짐이라고요, 친애하는 다네이?"], ["Yes, but we don’t know what reason there is in it.", "그렇소, 하지만 그 안에 무슨 까닭이 있는지는 우리도 모르오."], ["People are so unreasonable!", "사람들이란 어찌나 비합리적인지!"], ["Some of us at Tellson’s are getting old, and we really can’t be troubled out of the ordinary course without due occasion.”", "우리 텔슨 사람들 가운데 몇몇은 늙어가고 있어서, 정당한 이유 없이는 통상적인 일과를 벗어나는 수고를 정말 견디기 어렵다오.\""]], [["“Still,”", "\"그래도,\""], ["said Darnay, “you know how gloomy and threatening the sky is.”", "다네이가 말했다. \"하늘이 얼마나 음울하고 위협적인지는 아시잖습니까.\""]], [["“I know that, to be sure,” assented Mr.", "\"그야 물론 알지요.\""], ["Lorry, trying to persuade himself that his sweet temper was soured, and that he grumbled, “but I am determined to be peevish after my long day’s botheration.", "로리 씨가 동의하면서, 자신의 온화한 성미가 틀어졌다는 둥, 자신이 투덜대고 있다는 둥 스스로를 설득하려 애쓰며 말했다. \"하지만 나는 하루 종일 시달린 끝이니 좀 까다롭게 굴기로 작정했소이다."], ["Where is Manette?”", "마네뜨는 어디 있소?\""]], [["“Here he is,”", "\"여기 있습니다.\""], ["said the Doctor, entering the dark room at the moment.", "바로 그 순간 어두운 방으로 들어서며 의사가 말했다."]], [["“I am quite glad you are at home; for these hurries and forebodings by which I have been surrounded all day long, have made me nervous without reason.", "\"댁에 계시다니 참 다행이오. 하루 종일 나를 둘러싼 이 부산함과 불길한 예감 탓에, 까닭 없이 신경이 곤두서 있었거든요."], ["You are not going out, I hope?”", "외출하실 건 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“No; I am going to play backgammon with you, if you like,”", "\"아닙니다. 괜찮으시다면 함께 주사위놀이나 할까 합니다.\""], ["said the Doctor.", "의사가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t think I do like, if I may speak my mind.", "\"솔직히 말해도 된다면, 별로 내키지 않는구려."], ["I am not fit to be pitted against you to-night.", "오늘 밤은 당신과 겨룰 만한 상태가 못 되오."], ["Is the teaboard still there, Lucie?", "차 쟁반이 아직 거기 있나, 루시?"], ["I can’t see.”", "잘 안 보이는구나.\""]], [["“Of course, it has been kept for you.”", "\"물론이지요, 그대로 두었답니다.\""]], [["“Thank ye, my dear.", "\"고맙구나, 얘야."], ["The precious child is safe in bed?”", "그 귀한 아이는 잠자리에 잘 들었느냐?\""]], [["“And sleeping soundly.”", "\"새근새근 잘 자고 있어요.\""]], [["“That’s right; all safe and well!", "\"그래, 됐다. 다들 무사하고 평안하니!"], ["I don’t know why anything should be otherwise than safe and well here, thank God; but I have been so put out all day, and I am not as young as I was!", "하느님 덕분에, 어째서 여기 무엇 하나라도 무사하고 평안하지 않을 까닭이 있겠느냐만, 종일 어찌나 심란했는지, 게다가 나도 예전만큼 젊지가 않으니!"], ["My tea, my dear!", "내 차 좀 주렴, 얘야!"], ["Thank ye.", "고맙구나."], ["Now, come and take your place in the circle, and let us sit quiet, and hear the echoes about which you have your theory.”", "자, 와서 다 같이 둘러앉으세나, 가만히 앉아서, 자네가 그 이론을 펼친다는 메아리나 들어봅시다.\""]], [["“Not a theory; it was a fancy.”", "\"이론이 아니에요. 공상이었지요.\""]], [["“A fancy, then, my wise pet,”", "\"그럼 공상이라 합시다, 내 슬기로운 아가씨.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, patting her hand.", "로리 씨가 그녀의 손을 토닥이며 말했다."], ["“They are very numerous and very loud, though, are they not?", "\"그래도 그 메아리들이 무척 많고 무척 시끄럽지 않소?"], ["Only hear them!”", "저것 좀 들어봐요!\""]], [["Headlong, mad, and dangerous footsteps to force their way into anybody’s life, footsteps not easily made clean again if once stained red, the footsteps raging in Saint Antoine afar off, as the little circle sat in the dark London window.", "누군가의 삶 속으로 억지로 밀고 들어오려는, 무모하고 광기 어리고 위험한 발소리들, 일단 붉게 물들면 다시 깨끗하게 되기 어려운 발소리들, 그 작은 무리가 어두운 런던의 창가에 앉아 있는 동안, 저 멀리 쌩땅뚜안에서 광란하는 발소리들."]], [["Saint Antoine had been, that morning, a vast dusky mass of scarecrows heaving to and fro, with frequent gleams of light above the billowy heads, where steel blades and bayonets shone in the sun.", "쌩땅뚜안은 그날 아침, 이리저리 출렁이는 허수아비 떼의 거대하고 어슴푸레한 덩어리였으니, 물결치는 머리들 위로 강철 칼날과 총검이 햇빛에 번득이며 자주 빛을 뿜었다."], ["A tremendous roar arose from the throat of Saint Antoine, and a forest of naked arms struggled in the air like shrivelled branches of trees in a winter wind: all the fingers convulsively clutching at every weapon or semblance of a weapon that was thrown up from the depths below, no matter how far off.", "쌩땅뚜안의 목구멍에서 어마어마한 포효가 솟아올랐고, 헐벗은 팔들의 숲이 겨울바람 속에 쪼그라든 나뭇가지처럼 허공에서 몸부림쳤으니, 모든 손가락이 저 아래 깊은 곳에서 던져 올려지는 온갖 무기나 무기 비슷한 것이라면 아무리 멀리 있어도 가리지 않고 발작하듯 움켜쥐었다."]], [["Who gave them out, whence they last came, where they began, through what agency they crookedly quivered and jerked, scores at a time, over the heads of the crowd, like a kind of lightning, no eye in the throng could have told; but, muskets were being distributed--so were cartridges, powder, and ball, bars of iron and wood, knives, axes, pikes, every weapon that distracted ingenuity could discover or devise.", "누가 그것들을 나누어 주었는지, 그것들이 마지막으로 어디서 왔는지, 어디서 시작되었는지, 무슨 작용으로 그것들이 일종의 번개처럼 한 번에 수십 개씩 군중의 머리 위로 비뚜로 떨리며 홱홱 움직였는지, 그 무리 가운데 어느 누구의 눈도 말해줄 수 없었으리라. 그러나 머스킷 총이 나누어지고 있었고, 탄약통과 화약과 탄환, 쇠막대와 나무막대, 칼과 도끼와 창, 광란하는 독창성이 찾아내거나 고안해낼 수 있는 온갖 무기가 그렇게 나누어지고 있었다."], ["People who could lay hold of nothing else, set themselves with bleeding hands to force stones and bricks out of their places in walls.", "다른 아무것도 손에 쥘 수 없던 사람들은 피 흘리는 손으로 벽에서 돌과 벽돌을 억지로 뽑아내는 일에 매달렸다."], ["Every pulse and heart in Saint Antoine was on high-fever strain and at high-fever heat.", "쌩땅뚜안의 모든 맥박과 심장이 고열의 긴장 속에, 고열의 열기 속에 있었다."], ["Every living creature there held life as of no account, and was demented with a passionate readiness to sacrifice it.", "그곳의 살아 있는 모든 존재가 목숨을 하찮게 여겼고, 그것을 바치려는 격정적인 각오로 미쳐 있었다."]], [["As a whirlpool of boiling waters has a centre point, so, all this raging circled round Defarge’s wine-shop, and every human drop in the caldron had a tendency to be sucked towards the vortex where Defarge himself, already begrimed with gunpowder and sweat, issued orders, issued arms, thrust this man back, dragged this man forward, disarmed one to arm another, laboured and strove in the thickest of the uproar.", "펄펄 끓는 물의 소용돌이에 중심점이 있듯이, 이 모든 광란은 드파르주의 술집을 중심으로 소용돌이쳤고, 그 가마솥 속 인간의 물방울 하나하나가 그 소용돌이의 중심으로 빨려드는 경향이 있었으니, 거기서 드파르주 자신은 이미 화약과 땀으로 시커멓게 된 채, 명령을 내리고 무기를 나누어 주고, 이 사람을 뒤로 밀치고 저 사람을 앞으로 끌어당기고, 한 사람을 무장 해제시켜 다른 사람을 무장시키며, 그 아우성의 한복판에서 애쓰고 분투했다."]], [["“Keep near to me, Jacques Three,”", "\"내 곁에 붙어 있게, 자끄 셋.\""], ["cried Defarge; “and do you, Jacques One and Two, separate and put yourselves at the head of as many of these patriots as you can.", "드파르주가 외쳤다. \"그리고 자끄 하나와 둘, 자네들은 갈라져서 이 애국자들을 되도록 많이 거느리고 선두에 서게."], ["Where is my wife?”", "내 아내는 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Eh, well!", "\"아니, 뭐예요!"], ["Here you see me!”", "여기 내가 있잖아요!\""], ["said madame, composed as ever, but not knitting to-day.", "마담이 여느 때처럼 침착하게 말했으나, 오늘은 뜨개질을 하고 있지 않았다."], ["Madame’s resolute right hand was occupied with an axe, in place of the usual softer implements, and in her girdle were a pistol and a cruel knife.", "마담의 단호한 오른손에는 평소의 부드러운 도구 대신 도끼가 들려 있었고, 허리띠에는 권총과 잔혹한 칼이 꽂혀 있었다."]], [["“Where do you go, my wife?”", "\"어디로 갈 거요, 여보?\""]], [["“I go,”", "\"나는,\""], ["said madame, “with you at present.", "마담이 말했다. \"지금은 당신과 함께 가요."], ["You shall see me at the head of women, by-and-bye.”", "이따가 여자들의 선두에 선 나를 보게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Come, then!”", "\"그럼, 갑시다!\""], ["cried Defarge, in a resounding voice.", "드파르주가 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Patriots and friends, we are ready!", "\"애국자들이여, 동지들이여, 우리는 준비됐다!"], ["The Bastille!”", "바스띠유로!\""]], [["With a roar that sounded as if all the breath in France had been shaped into the detested word, the living sea rose, wave on wave, depth on depth, and overflowed the city to that point.", "마치 프랑스의 온 숨결이 그 가증스러운 한마디로 빚어진 듯한 포효와 함께, 살아 있는 바다가 물결 위에 물결로, 깊이 위에 깊이로 솟구쳐 올라, 그 지점을 향해 도시를 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["Alarm-bells ringing, drums beating, the sea raging and thundering on its new beach, the attack began.", "경종이 울리고, 북이 울리고, 바다가 새로운 해변에서 광란하며 천둥처럼 부딪히는 가운데, 공격이 시작되었다."]], [["Deep ditches, double drawbridge, massive stone walls, eight great towers, cannon, muskets, fire and smoke.", "깊은 해자, 이중의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기."], ["Through the fire and through the smoke--in the fire and in the smoke, for the sea cast him up against a cannon, and on the instant he became a cannonier--Defarge of the wine-shop worked like a manful soldier, Two fierce hours.", "그 불을 뚫고 그 연기를 뚫고 — 그 불 속에서 그 연기 속에서, 바다가 그를 어느 대포 앞으로 떠밀어 던졌고 그 순간 그는 포수가 되었으므로 — 술집의 드파르주는 사나운 두 시간 동안 용감한 병사처럼 일했다."]], [["Deep ditch, single drawbridge, massive stone walls, eight great towers, cannon, muskets, fire and smoke.", "깊은 해자, 단 하나의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기."], ["One drawbridge down!", "도개교 하나가 내려졌다!"], ["“Work, comrades all, work!", "\"일하라, 동지들이여 모두, 일하라!"], ["Work, Jacques One, Jacques Two, Jacques One Thousand, Jacques Two Thousand, Jacques Five-and-Twenty Thousand; in the name of all the Angels or the Devils--which you prefer--work!”", "일하라, 자끄 하나, 자끄 둘, 자끄 천, 자끄 이천, 자끄 이만 오천이여. 모든 천사들의 이름으로든 악마들의 이름으로든 — 어느 쪽이든 좋을 대로 — 일하라!\""], ["Thus Defarge of the wine-shop, still at his gun, which had long grown hot.", "술집의 드파르주는 진작부터 뜨겁게 달아오른 자기 대포 곁에서 그렇게 외쳤다."]], [["“To me, women!”", "\"내게로, 여인들이여!\""], ["cried madame his wife.", "그의 아내인 마담이 외쳤다."], ["“What!", "\"뭐예요!"], ["We can kill as well as the men when the place is taken!”", "일단 함락되면 우리도 남자들 못지않게 죽일 수 있어요!\""], ["And to her, with a shrill thirsty cry, trooping women variously armed, but all armed alike in hunger and revenge.", "그러자 그녀에게로, 날카롭고 굶주린 외침과 함께, 가지각색으로 무장한, 그러나 모두가 한결같이 굶주림과 복수로 무장한 여인들이 떼 지어 몰려들었다."]], [["Cannon, muskets, fire and smoke; but, still the deep ditch, the single drawbridge, the massive stone walls, and the eight great towers.", "대포, 머스킷 총, 불과 연기. 그러나 여전히 깊은 해자, 단 하나의 도개교, 육중한 돌벽, 그리고 여덟 개의 거대한 탑."], ["Slight displacements of the raging sea, made by the falling wounded.", "쓰러지는 부상자들로 인해 광란하는 바다가 조금씩 자리를 옮겼다."], ["Flashing weapons, blazing torches, smoking waggonloads of wet straw, hard work at neighbouring barricades in all directions, shrieks, volleys, execrations, bravery without stint, boom smash and rattle, and the furious sounding of the living sea; but, still the deep ditch, and the single drawbridge, and the massive stone walls, and the eight great towers, and still Defarge of the wine-shop at his gun, grown doubly hot by the service of Four fierce hours.", "번득이는 무기들, 활활 타오르는 횃불들, 젖은 짚을 가득 실어 연기를 뿜는 수레들, 사방의 이웃 바리케이드에서 벌어지는 고된 작업, 비명, 일제 사격, 저주, 끝없는 용맹, 쾅 하고 부서지고 덜그럭거리는 소리, 그리고 살아 있는 바다의 격렬한 울림. 그러나 여전히 깊은 해자, 그리고 단 하나의 도개교, 그리고 육중한 돌벽, 그리고 여덟 개의 거대한 탑, 그리고 여전히 술집의 드파르주는, 사나운 네 시간의 노역으로 갑절이나 뜨거워진 자기 대포 곁에 있었다."]], [["A white flag from within the fortress, and a parley--this dimly perceptible through the raging storm, nothing audible in it--suddenly the sea rose immeasurably wider and higher, and swept Defarge of the wine-shop over the lowered drawbridge, past the massive stone outer walls, in among the eight great towers surrendered!", "요새 안에서 백기가 오르고, 협상이 벌어졌으나 — 광란하는 폭풍 사이로 이것이 어렴풋이 보였을 뿐, 그 안에서 들리는 것은 아무것도 없었다 — 갑자기 바다가 헤아릴 수 없이 더 넓고 더 높이 솟구쳐, 술집의 드파르주를 내려진 도개교 너머로, 육중한 돌 외벽 너머로, 항복한 여덟 개의 거대한 탑 한가운데로 쓸어 넣었다!"]], [["So resistless was the force of the ocean bearing him on, that even to draw his breath or turn his head was as impracticable as if he had been struggling in the surf at the South Sea, until he was landed in the outer courtyard of the Bastille.", "그를 떠밀고 가는 그 대양의 힘이 어찌나 거역할 수 없었던지, 숨을 쉬거나 고개를 돌리는 것조차, 마치 남쪽 바다의 파도 속에서 발버둥치기라도 하는 것처럼 불가능했으니, 그는 마침내 바스띠유의 바깥뜰에 내려놓였다."], ["There, against an angle of a wall, he made a struggle to look about him.", "거기서 그는 어느 벽 모퉁이에 기대어 둘레를 둘러보려고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["Jacques Three was nearly at his side; Madame Defarge, still heading some of her women, was visible in the inner distance, and her knife was in her hand.", "자끄 셋이 거의 그의 곁에 있었다. 여전히 자기 여인들 가운데 일부를 이끄는 마담 드파르주가 안쪽 멀찍이 보였고, 그녀의 손에는 칼이 들려 있었다."], ["Everywhere was tumult, exultation, deafening and maniacal bewilderment, astounding noise, yet furious dumb-show.", "사방이 소동과 환희, 귀가 먹먹하고 광기 어린 혼란, 어마어마한 소음, 그러면서도 격렬한 무언극이었다."]], [["“The Prisoners!”", "\"죄수들을!\""]], [["“The Records!”", "\"기록들을!\""]], [["“The secret cells!”", "\"비밀 독방들을!\""]], [["“The instruments of torture!”", "\"고문 도구들을!\""]], [["“The Prisoners!”", "\"죄수들을!\""]], [["Of all these cries, and ten thousand incoherences, “The Prisoners!” was the cry most taken up by the sea that rushed in, as if there were an eternity of people, as well as of time and space.", "이 모든 외침과 만 가지 두서없는 소리 가운데, \"죄수들을!\"이 밀려드는 바다가 가장 많이 받아 외친 외침이었으니, 마치 시간과 공간뿐 아니라 사람도 영원무궁한 듯했다."], ["When the foremost billows rolled past, bearing the prison officers with them, and threatening them all with instant death if any secret nook remained undisclosed, Defarge laid his strong hand on the breast of one of these men--a man with a grey head, who had a lighted torch in his hand--separated him from the rest, and got him between himself and the wall.", "맨 앞의 물결이 교도소 관리들을 떠밀고서 굴러 지나가며, 만약 드러나지 않은 비밀 구석이라도 남아 있으면 모조리 즉시 죽이겠다고 그들 모두를 위협할 때, 드파르주는 그 사람들 가운데 하나의 — 백발에, 손에 불 켜진 횃불을 든 사람의 — 가슴에 억센 손을 얹어, 나머지에게서 떼어내고 자기와 벽 사이에 끼워 넣었다."]], [["“Show me the North Tower!”", "\"북쪽 탑을 보여다오!\""], ["said Defarge.", "드파르주가 말했다."], ["“Quick!”", "\"빨리!\""]], [["“I will faithfully,”", "\"성심껏 그러지요.\""], ["replied the man, “if you will come with me.", "그 사람이 대답했다. \"저와 함께 가신다면요."], ["But there is no one there.”", "하지만 거기엔 아무도 없습니다.\""]], [["“What is the meaning of One Hundred and Five, North Tower?”", "\"북쪽 탑 백오 호가 무슨 뜻이냐?\" 드파르주가 물었다."], ["asked Defarge. “Quick!”", "\"빨리!\""]], [["“The meaning, monsieur?”", "\"뜻이라니요, 무슈?\""]], [["“Does it mean a captive, or a place of captivity?", "\"그게 죄수를 뜻하느냐, 아니면 감금하는 장소를 뜻하느냐?"], ["Or do you mean that I shall strike you dead?”", "아니면 내가 너를 쳐 죽이기라도 바라는 거냐?\""]], [["“Kill him!”", "\"죽여버려요!\""], ["croaked Jacques Three, who had come close up.", "가까이 다가온 자끄 셋이 쉰 목소리로 내뱉었다."]], [["“Monsieur, it is a cell.”", "\"무슈, 그것은 독방입니다.\""]], [["“Show it me!”", "\"내게 보여다오!\""]], [["“Pass this way, then.”", "\"그럼 이쪽으로 오시지요.\""]], [["Jacques Three, with his usual craving on him, and evidently disappointed by the dialogue taking a turn that did not seem to promise bloodshed, held by Defarge’s arm as he held by the turnkey’s.", "자끄 셋은 여느 때의 갈망에 사로잡혀, 유혈을 약속할 것 같지 않은 쪽으로 대화가 흘러가는 데 명백히 실망한 채, 드파르주가 간수의 팔을 붙들듯이 드파르주의 팔을 붙들었다."], ["Their three heads had been close together during this brief discourse, and it had been as much as they could do to hear one another, even then: so tremendous was the noise of the living ocean, in its irruption into the Fortress, and its inundation of the courts and passages and staircases.", "이 짧은 대화 동안 세 사람의 머리는 바짝 맞붙어 있었으나, 그렇게 했는데도 서로의 말을 알아듣기가 여간 힘든 게 아니었다. 요새 안으로 난입하여 안뜰과 통로와 계단을 범람하는 살아 있는 대양의 소음이 그토록 어마어마했기 때문이다."], ["All around outside, too, it beat the walls with a deep, hoarse roar, from which, occasionally, some partial shouts of tumult broke and leaped into the air like spray.", "바깥에서도 사방으로, 바다는 깊고 쉰 포효로 벽을 두드렸으니, 이따금 그 속에서 부분적인 소동의 함성이 터져 나와 물보라처럼 허공으로 솟구쳤다."]], [["Through gloomy vaults where the light of day had never shone, past hideous doors of dark dens and cages, down cavernous flights of steps, and again up steep rugged ascents of stone and brick, more like dry waterfalls than staircases, Defarge, the turnkey, and Jacques Three, linked hand and arm, went with all the speed they could make.", "햇빛이 한 번도 비친 적 없는 음침한 지하실들을 지나고, 어두운 굴과 우리의 흉측한 문들을 지나, 동굴 같은 계단을 한참 내려갔다가, 다시 계단이라기보다는 메마른 폭포에 가까운 돌과 벽돌의 가파르고 험한 오르막을 올라, 드파르주와 간수와 자끄 셋은 서로 손과 팔을 맞잡고 낼 수 있는 한 빠른 속도로 나아갔다."], ["Here and there, especially at first, the inundation started on them and swept by; but when they had done descending, and were winding and climbing up a tower, they were alone.", "여기저기서, 특히 처음에는, 범람한 물결이 그들에게 덮쳐와 지나갔다. 그러나 다 내려가서 어느 탑을 휘돌아 오를 때쯤에는 그들뿐이었다."], ["Hemmed in here by the massive thickness of walls and arches, the storm within the fortress and without was only audible to them in a dull, subdued way, as if the noise out of which they had come had almost destroyed their sense of hearing.", "여기서는 육중한 두께의 벽과 아치에 둘러싸여, 요새 안팎의 폭풍이 그들에게는 둔하고 잦아든 소리로만 들렸으니, 마치 그들이 빠져나온 그 소음이 그들의 청각을 거의 망가뜨려놓기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["The turnkey stopped at a low door, put a key in a clashing lock, swung the door slowly open, and said, as they all bent their heads and passed in:", "간수가 어느 낮은 문 앞에서 멈춰, 철커덕거리는 자물쇠에 열쇠를 꽂아 문을 천천히 밀어 열고는, 그들 모두가 머리를 숙이고 들어설 때 말했다."]], [["“One hundred and five, North Tower!”", "\"북쪽 탑, 백오 호!\""]], [["There was a small, heavily-grated, unglazed window high in the wall, with a stone screen before it, so that the sky could be only seen by stooping low and looking up.", "벽 높은 곳에 작고, 쇠창살이 촘촘하고, 유리도 끼우지 않은 창이 하나 있었는데, 그 앞에 돌 가림막이 있어서 하늘은 몸을 잔뜩 굽혀 올려다보아야만 보였다."], ["There was a small chimney, heavily barred across, a few feet within.", "안쪽으로 몇 자 들어간 곳에 작은 굴뚝이 하나 있었는데, 가로로 쇠창살이 촘촘히 박혀 있었다."], ["There was a heap of old feathery wood-ashes on the hearth.", "화덕에는 깃털처럼 묵은 나무재가 한 무더기 쌓여 있었다."], ["There was a stool, and table, and a straw bed.", "걸상 하나와 탁자 하나, 짚으로 된 침상 하나가 있었다."], ["There were the four blackened walls, and a rusted iron ring in one of them.", "시커멓게 그을린 네 벽이 있었고, 그중 한 벽에는 녹슨 쇠고리 하나가 박혀 있었다."]], [["“Pass that torch slowly along these walls, that I may see them,”", "\"저 횃불을 이 벽들을 따라 천천히 비춰다오, 내가 볼 수 있게.\""], ["said Defarge to the turnkey.", "드파르주가 간수에게 말했다."]], [["The man obeyed, and Defarge followed the light closely with his eyes.", "그 사람이 시키는 대로 했고, 드파르주는 그 불빛을 두 눈으로 바짝 좇았다."]], [["“Stop!--Look here, Jacques!”", "\"멈춰라! 여기 보게, 자끄!\""]], [["“A. M.!”", "\"아, 엠!\""], ["croaked Jacques Three, as he read greedily.", "자끄 셋이 게걸스럽게 읽으며 쉰 목소리로 내뱉었다."]], [["“Alexandre Manette,”", "\"알렉상드르 마네뜨.\""], ["said Defarge in his ear, following the letters with his swart forefinger, deeply engrained with gunpowder.", "화약이 깊이 배어든 거뭇한 집게손가락으로 글자를 따라가며 드파르주가 그의 귀에 대고 말했다."], ["“And here he wrote ‘a poor physician.’ And it was he, without doubt, who scratched a calendar on this stone.", "\"그리고 여기 그는 '가련한 의사'라고 썼군. 그리고 이 돌에 달력을 새겨놓은 것도 틀림없이 그 사람일 거야."], ["What is that in your hand?", "자네 손에 든 게 뭔가?"], ["A crowbar?", "쇠지레?"], ["Give it me!”", "그걸 이리 주게!\""]], [["He had still the linstock of his gun in his own hand.", "그는 아직 자기 대포의 점화봉을 손에 들고 있었다."], ["He made a sudden exchange of the two instruments, and turning on the worm-eaten stool and table, beat them to pieces in a few blows.", "그는 그 두 도구를 갑자기 맞바꾸더니, 벌레 먹은 걸상과 탁자로 돌아서서 몇 번 내리쳐 산산조각 냈다."]], [["“Hold the light higher!”", "\"횃불을 더 높이 들어!\""], ["he said, wrathfully, to the turnkey.", "그가 간수에게 노기 띤 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Look among those fragments with care, Jacques.", "\"저 조각들 사이를 잘 살펴보게, 자끄."], ["And see!", "그리고 봐!"], ["Here is my knife,”", "여기 내 칼이 있네.\""], ["throwing it to him; “rip open that bed, and search the straw.", "그것을 그에게 던지며 말했다. \"저 침상을 째서 짚을 뒤져보게."], ["Hold the light higher, you!”", "횃불을 더 높이 들라니까, 자네!\""]], [["With a menacing look at the turnkey he crawled upon the hearth, and, peering up the chimney, struck and prised at its sides with the crowbar, and worked at the iron grating across it.", "간수를 위협적으로 한 번 쏘아본 뒤 그는 화덕 위로 기어 올라가, 굴뚝 안을 들여다보며 쇠지레로 그 옆벽을 치고 비집었으며, 굴뚝을 가로지른 쇠창살에 매달려 일했다."], ["In a few minutes, some mortar and dust came dropping down, which he averted his face to avoid; and in it, and in the old wood-ashes, and in a crevice in the chimney into which his weapon had slipped or wrought itself, he groped with a cautious touch.", "몇 분 만에 회반죽과 먼지가 조금 떨어져 내렸고, 그는 그것을 피하려고 얼굴을 돌렸다. 그러고는 그 속에서, 그리고 묵은 나무재 속에서, 또 그의 연장이 미끄러져 들어갔거나 비집어 들어간 굴뚝의 틈새 속에서, 조심스러운 손길로 더듬었다."]], [["“Nothing in the wood, and nothing in the straw, Jacques?”", "\"나무에도 아무것도 없고, 짚에도 아무것도 없나, 자끄?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무것도 없습니다.\""]], [["“Let us collect them together, in the middle of the cell.", "\"그것들을 한데 모으세, 독방 한가운데에. 그렇지!"], ["So! Light them, you!”", "거기에 불을 붙이게, 자네!\""]], [["The turnkey fired the little pile, which blazed high and hot.", "간수가 그 작은 무더기에 불을 댕기자, 높고 뜨겁게 타올랐다."], ["Stooping again to come out at the low-arched door, they left it burning, and retraced their way to the courtyard; seeming to recover their sense of hearing as they came down, until they were in the raging flood once more.", "다시 몸을 숙여 그 낮은 아치문으로 나서며, 그들은 그것을 타게 내버려둔 채 안뜰로 가는 길을 되짚어갔다. 내려가는 동안 청각이 되살아나는 듯하더니, 마침내 다시 한번 광란하는 홍수 속에 들어섰다."]], [["They found it surging and tossing, in quest of Defarge himself.", "그들은 그것이 드파르주 자신을 찾아 출렁이며 들끓고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Saint Antoine was clamorous to have its wine-shop keeper foremost in the guard upon the governor who had defended the Bastille and shot the people.", "쌩땅뚜안은 바스띠유를 지키며 민중에게 총을 쏘았던 그 사령관을 호위하는 일에 자기네 술집 주인이 선두에 서야 한다고 시끄럽게 외쳐댔다."], ["Otherwise, the governor would not be marched to the Hotel de Ville for judgment.", "그러지 않으면 그 사령관은 재판을 받으러 시청으로 끌려가지 않을 것이었다."], ["Otherwise, the governor would escape, and the people’s blood (suddenly of some value, after many years of worthlessness) be unavenged.", "그러지 않으면 그 사령관은 달아날 것이고, 민중의 피는 (여러 해 동안 무가치하다가 갑자기 얼마간의 가치를 띠게 된 그 피는) 복수받지 못할 것이었다."]], [["In the howling universe of passion and contention that seemed to encompass this grim old officer conspicuous in his grey coat and red decoration, there was but one quite steady figure, and that was a woman’s.", "* 시청(Hotel de Ville): 빠리 시청사. 혁명기에 봉기의 거점이자 즉결 재판이 이루어지던 곳."], ["“See, there is my husband!”", "회색 외투와 붉은 훈장으로 눈에 띄는 이 험상궂은 늙은 장교를 에워싼 듯한, 격정과 다툼이 울부짖는 그 세계 속에서, 단 하나 아주 침착한 형체가 있었으니, 그것은 한 여자의 모습이었다. \"보라, 저기 내 남편이 있다!\""], ["she cried, pointing him out.", "그녀가 그를 가리키며 외쳤다. \"드파르주를 보라!\""], ["“See Defarge!”", "그녀는 그 험상궂은 늙은 장교 바로 곁에 꼼짝 않고 서 있었고, 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었다."], ["She stood immovable close to the grim old officer, and remained immovable close to him; remained immovable close to him through the streets, as Defarge and the rest bore him along; remained immovable close to him when he was got near his destination, and began to be struck at from behind; remained immovable close to him when the long-gathering rain of stabs and blows fell heavy; was so close to him when he dropped dead under it, that, suddenly animated, she put her foot upon his neck, and with her cruel knife--long ready--hewed off his head.", "드파르주와 나머지 사람들이 그를 끌고 거리를 지나가는 동안 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었고, 그가 목적지 가까이 끌려가 뒤에서 얻어맞기 시작했을 때에도 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었으며, 오래 쌓여온 칼질과 매질의 비가 무겁게 쏟아져 내릴 때에도 그의 곁에 꼼짝 않고 있었다. 그가 그 아래 죽어 쓰러졌을 때에도 그의 곁에 어찌나 바짝 붙어 있었던지, 그녀는 갑자기 활기를 띠며 그의 목에 발을 올려놓고, 진작부터 준비해둔 그 잔혹한 칼로 그의 머리를 베어냈다."]], [["The hour was come, when Saint Antoine was to execute his horrible idea of hoisting up men for lamps to show what he could be and do.", "쌩땅뚜안이 인간을 등불처럼 매달아 올려, 자기가 무엇이 될 수 있고 무엇을 할 수 있는지를 보여주겠다는 그 끔찍한 발상을 실행할 때가 온 것이다."], ["Saint Antoine’s blood was up, and the blood of tyranny and domination by the iron hand was down--down on the steps of the Hotel de Ville where the governor’s body lay--down on the sole of the shoe of Madame Defarge where she had trodden on the body to steady it for mutilation.", "쌩땅뚜안의 피가 끓어올랐고, 쇠 같은 손으로 휘두르던 폭정과 지배의 피는 떨어졌으니 — 사령관의 시신이 누운 시청 계단 위로 떨어졌고, 절단을 위해 시신을 고정하려고 그것을 밟았던 마담 드파르주의 신발 밑창 위로 떨어졌다."], ["“Lower the lamp yonder!”", "\"저기 등불을 내려라!\""], ["cried Saint Antoine, after glaring round for a new means of death; “here is one of his soldiers to be left on guard!”", "새로운 죽음의 수단을 찾아 사방을 노려보던 쌩땅뚜안이 외쳤다. \"여기 보초로 세워둘 그의 병사가 하나 있다!\""], ["The swinging sentinel was posted, and the sea rushed on.", "그 흔들리는 보초가 세워졌고, 바다는 계속 밀려들었다."]], [["The sea of black and threatening waters, and of destructive upheaving of wave against wave, whose depths were yet unfathomed and whose forces were yet unknown.", "검고 위협적인 물의 바다, 물결이 물결을 향해 파괴적으로 솟구치는 그 바다, 그 깊이는 아직 헤아려지지 않았고 그 힘은 아직 알려지지 않은 그 바다."], ["The remorseless sea of turbulently swaying shapes, voices of vengeance, and faces hardened in the furnaces of suffering until the touch of pity could make no mark on them.", "격렬하게 출렁이는 형체들, 복수의 목소리들, 그리고 고통의 용광로 속에서 단단해져 연민의 손길로도 흔적 하나 남길 수 없게 된 얼굴들의, 무자비한 바다."]], [["But, in the ocean of faces where every fierce and furious expression was in vivid life, there were two groups of faces--each seven in number--so fixedly contrasting with the rest, that never did sea roll which bore more memorable wrecks with it.", "그러나 사납고 격렬한 표정이 하나하나 생생히 살아 있는 그 얼굴의 대양 속에, 두 무리의 얼굴이 — 각각 일곱씩인 — 나머지와 너무도 확연히 대조를 이루며 있었으니, 그보다 더 기억할 만한 난파선을 싣고 굽이친 바다는 일찍이 없었다."], ["Seven faces of prisoners, suddenly released by the storm that had burst their tomb, were carried high overhead: all scared, all lost, all wondering and amazed, as if the Last Day were come, and those who rejoiced around them were lost spirits.", "그들의 무덤을 터뜨려 갑자기 풀려난 일곱 죄수의 얼굴이 머리 위 높이 떠받들려 갔으니, 모두가 겁에 질리고, 모두가 넋이 나가고, 모두가 의아해하며 놀라워했으니, 마치 최후의 날이 왔고 자기들 둘레에서 환호하는 자들이 지옥에 떨어진 영혼들이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Other seven faces there were, carried higher, seven dead faces, whose drooping eyelids and half-seen eyes awaited the Last Day.", "또 다른 일곱 얼굴이 더 높이 떠받들려 갔으니, 일곱 개의 죽은 얼굴이었으며, 그 처진 눈꺼풀과 반쯤 드러난 눈은 최후의 날을 기다리고 있었다."], ["Impassive faces, yet with a suspended--not an abolished--expression on them; faces, rather, in a fearful pause, as having yet to raise the dropped lids of the eyes, and bear witness with the bloodless lips, “THOU DIDST IT!”", "무표정한 얼굴들, 그러나 그 위에는 — 사라진 것이 아니라 — 유예된 표정이 어려 있었다. 차라리, 두려운 멈춤 속에 있는 얼굴들이었으니, 아직 처진 눈꺼풀을 들어 올려, 핏기 없는 입술로 \"네가 그 짓을 했노라!\"라고 증언해야 할 것만 같았다."]], [["Seven prisoners released, seven gory heads on pikes, the keys of the accursed fortress of the eight strong towers, some discovered letters and other memorials of prisoners of old time, long dead of broken hearts,--such, and such--like, the loudly echoing footsteps of Saint Antoine escort through the Paris streets in mid-July, one thousand seven hundred and eighty-nine.", "풀려난 일곱 죄수, 창끝에 꽂힌 일곱 개의 피투성이 머리, 여덟 개의 굳센 탑을 가진 그 저주받은 요새의 열쇠들, 오래전에 상심하여 죽은 옛 시절 죄수들의 발견된 편지 몇 통과 다른 유품들 — 이런 것들이, 또 이런 것들이, 천칠백팔십구년 칠월 중순 빠리의 거리를 지나며 쌩땅뚜안이 요란하게 메아리치는 발소리로 호위해 가는 것들이었다."], ["Now, Heaven defeat the fancy of Lucie Darnay, and keep these feet far out of her life!", "자, 하늘이여, 루시 다네이의 공상을 무찔러, 이 발들이 그녀의 삶에서 멀리 떨어져 있게 하소서!"], ["For, they are headlong, mad, and dangerous; and in the years so long after the breaking of the cask at Defarge’s wine-shop door, they are not easily purified when once stained red.", "그것들은 무모하고, 광기 어리고, 위험하니, 드파르주의 술집 문간에서 술통이 깨진 그토록 오랜 세월 뒤에도, 일단 붉게 물들면 좀처럼 깨끗해지지 않기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII. The Sea Still Rises", "# CHAPTER XXII. The Sea Still Rises"]], [["Haggard Saint Antoine had had only one exultant week, in which to soften his modicum of hard and bitter bread to such extent as he could, with the relish of fraternal embraces and congratulations, when Madame Defarge sat at her counter, as usual, presiding over the customers.", "초췌한 쌩땅뜨완은 형제애의 포옹과 축하라는 양념으로 자기 몫의 딱딱하고 쓴 빵을 할 수 있는 만큼 부드럽게 누릴 수 있었던 단 한 주의 환희에 찬 시간을 보냈을 뿐이었는데, 그동안 마담 드파르주는 여느 때처럼 계산대에 앉아 손님들을 주재하고 있었다."], ["Madame Defarge wore no rose in her head, for the great brotherhood of Spies had become, even in one short week, extremely chary of trusting themselves to the saint’s mercies.", "마담 드파르주는 머리에 장미를 꽂고 있지 않았으니, 첩자들의 거대한 형제단이 단 한 주 만에도 이 성자의 자비에 자기 몸을 맡기기를 극도로 꺼리게 되었기 때문이다."], ["The lamps across his streets had a portentously elastic swing with them.", "그의 거리 곳곳에 걸린 등잔들은 불길하리만큼 탄력 있게 흔들거렸다."]], [["Madame Defarge, with her arms folded, sat in the morning light and heat, contemplating the wine-shop and the street.", "마담 드파르주는 팔짱을 낀 채 아침의 빛과 열기 속에 앉아, 술집과 거리를 바라보았다."], ["In both, there were several knots of loungers, squalid and miserable, but now with a manifest sense of power enthroned on their distress.", "양쪽 모두에 빈둥거리는 자들이 몇 무리씩 있었는데, 누추하고 비참했지만 이제는 자신들의 고난 위에 권력이 자리 잡았다는 명백한 감각을 지니고 있었다."], ["The raggedest nightcap, awry on the wretchedest head, had this crooked significance in it: “I know how hard it has grown for me, the wearer of this, to support life in myself; but do you know how easy it has grown for me, the wearer of this, to destroy life in you?”", "가장 가련한 머리에 비뚜름하게 얹힌 가장 너덜너덜한 잠모자에는 이런 비뚤어진 의미가 담겨 있었다. \"이것을 쓴 내가 내 안의 목숨을 부지하기가 얼마나 힘들어졌는지 나는 안다."], ["Every lean bare arm, that had been without work before, had this work always ready for it now, that it could strike.", "그러나 이것을 쓴 내가 네 안의 목숨을 끊어버리기가 얼마나 쉬워졌는지 너는 아느냐?\" 전에는 일거리 없이 놀던 앙상하고 헐벗은 팔마다 이제는 늘 준비된 일거리가 있었으니, 곧 후려칠 수 있다는 것이었다."], ["The fingers of the knitting women were vicious, with the experience that they could tear.", "뜨개질하는 여자들의 손가락은, 찢어발길 수 있다는 경험으로 인해 사악했다."], ["There was a change in the appearance of Saint Antoine; the image had been hammering into this for hundreds of years, and the last finishing blows had told mightily on the expression.", "쌩땅뜨완의 모습에는 변화가 있었다. 그 형상은 수백 년에 걸쳐 망치로 두들겨져 빚어졌는데, 마지막 마무리 일격들이 그 표정에 강력하게 새겨진 것이다."]], [["Madame Defarge sat observing it, with such suppressed approval as was to be desired in the leader of the Saint Antoine women.", "마담 드파르주는 그것을 지켜보며 앉아 있었으니, 쌩땅뜨완 여인들의 지도자에게서 바랄 법한 그런 억눌린 인정이 그 안에 담겨 있었다."], ["One of her sisterhood knitted beside her.", "그 자매단 중 하나가 그녀 곁에서 뜨개질을 했다."], ["The short, rather plump wife of a starved grocer, and the mother of two children withal, this lieutenant had already earned the complimentary name of The Vengeance.", "굶주린 식료품상의 키 작고 다소 통통한 아내이자 두 아이의 어머니이기도 한 이 부관은 이미 '복수'라는 칭송의 이름을 얻은 터였다."]], [["“Hark!”", "\"들어봐!\""], ["said The Vengeance.", "하고 복수가 말했다."], ["“Listen, then!", "\"그러니까 들어봐!"], ["Who comes?”", "누가 오는 거지?\""]], [["As if a train of powder laid from the outermost bound of Saint Antoine Quarter to the wine-shop door, had been suddenly fired, a fast-spreading murmur came rushing along.", "쌩땅뜨완 구역의 가장 바깥 경계에서 술집 문까지 깔아놓은 화약 도화선에 갑자기 불이 붙기라도 한 듯, 빠르게 번지는 웅성거림이 밀려왔다."]], [["“It is Defarge,”", "\"드파르주예요.\""], ["said madame.", "하고 마담이 말했다."], ["“Silence, patriots!”", "\"조용히, 동지들!\""]], [["Defarge came in breathless, pulled off a red cap he wore, and looked around him!", "드파르주가 숨을 헐떡이며 들어와, 쓰고 있던 붉은 모자를 벗고 주위를 둘러보았다!"], ["“Listen, everywhere!”", "\"어디서나, 들어요!\""], ["said madame again.", "하고 마담이 다시 말했다."], ["“Listen to him!”", "\"이 사람 말을 들어요!\""], ["Defarge stood, panting, against a background of eager eyes and open mouths, formed outside the door; all those within the wine-shop had sprung to their feet.", "드파르주는 헐떡이며 서 있었고, 그 배경으로 문밖에는 간절한 눈빛과 벌어진 입들이 모여들어 있었다. 술집 안에 있던 자들은 모두 벌떡 일어나 있었다."]], [["“Say then, my husband.", "\"그러니까 말해봐요, 여보."], ["What is it?”", "무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["“News from the other world!”", "\"저세상에서 온 소식이오!\""]], [["“How, then?”", "\"그게 무슨 말이에요?\""], ["cried madame, contemptuously.", "하고 마담이 경멸하듯 외쳤다."], ["“The other world?”", "\"저세상이라니?\""]], [["“Does everybody here recall old Foulon, who told the famished people that they might eat grass, and who died, and went to Hell?”", "\"여기 다들 늙은 풀롱을 기억하지? 굶주린 사람들에게 풀이나 뜯어 먹으라고 한 그자, 죽어서 지옥에 갔다던 그자 말이오.\""]], [["“Everybody!”", "\"다들 기억하지!\""], ["from all throats.", "모든 목구멍에서 터져 나왔다."]], [["“The news is of him.", "\"바로 그자에 관한 소식이오."], ["He is among us!”", "그가 우리 가운데 있소!\""]], [["“Among us!”", "\"우리 가운데!\""], ["from the universal throat again.", "다시 한목소리로 터져 나왔다."], ["“And dead?”", "\"그리고 죽었다며?\""]], [["“Not dead!", "\"죽지 않았소!"], ["He feared us so much--and with reason--that he caused himself to be represented as dead, and had a grand mock-funeral.", "그는 우리를 너무도 두려워한 나머지—그럴 만한 이유가 있었지—자신을 죽은 것으로 꾸미고 성대한 가짜 장례까지 치렀던 거요."], ["But they have found him alive, hiding in the country, and have brought him in.", "그런데 시골에 숨어 살아 있는 그자를 찾아내 끌고 왔소."], ["I have seen him but now, on his way to the Hotel de Ville, a prisoner.", "방금 그자가 죄수의 몸으로 시청으로 끌려가는 걸 내 눈으로 보았소."], ["I have said that he had reason to fear us.", "그가 우리를 두려워할 이유가 있었다고 내가 말했지. 다들 말해보시오!"], ["Say all! Had he reason?”", "그자에게 정말로 그럴 이유가 있었소?\""]], [["Wretched old sinner of more than threescore years and ten, if he had never known it yet, he would have known it in his heart of hearts if he could have heard the answering cry.", "칠십을 넘긴 가련한 늙은 죄인은, 설사 지금껏 한 번도 그것을 알지 못했다 해도, 그 응답하는 함성을 들을 수 있었다면 마음 깊은 곳에서 절로 깨달았을 것이다."]], [["A moment of profound silence followed.", "깊은 침묵의 순간이 뒤따랐다."], ["Defarge and his wife looked steadfastly at one another.", "드파르주와 그의 아내는 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["The Vengeance stooped, and the jar of a drum was heard as she moved it at her feet behind the counter.", "복수는 몸을 굽혔고, 그녀가 계산대 뒤 발치에서 그것을 옮길 때 북이 덜그럭거리는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Patriots!”", "\"동지들!\""], ["said Defarge, in a determined voice, “are we ready?”", "하고 드파르주가 단호한 목소리로 말했다. \"우리는 준비가 됐소?\""]], [["Instantly Madame Defarge’s knife was in her girdle; the drum was beating in the streets, as if it and a drummer had flown together by magic; and The Vengeance, uttering terrific shrieks, and flinging her arms about her head like all the forty Furies at once, was tearing from house to house, rousing the women.", "순식간에 마담 드파르주의 칼은 그녀의 허리띠에 꽂혔다. 마치 북과 북 치는 자가 마법으로 함께 날아오기라도 한 듯 거리에서는 북소리가 울리고 있었다. 그리고 복수는 무시무시한 비명을 지르며 마치 사십 명의 복수의 여신을 한꺼번에 합쳐놓은 것처럼 머리 위로 두 팔을 휘둘러대면서 이 집 저 집을 휘젓고 다니며 여자들을 깨우고 있었다."]], [["The men were terrible, in the bloody-minded anger with which they looked from windows, caught up what arms they had, and came pouring down into the streets; but, the women were a sight to chill the boldest.", "남자들은 창밖을 내다보고 손에 잡히는 무기를 집어 들고 거리로 쏟아져 나오는 그 피에 굶주린 분노로 끔찍했다. 그러나 여자들은 아무리 대담한 자라도 오싹하게 할 광경이었다."], ["From such household occupations as their bare poverty yielded, from their children, from their aged and their sick crouching on the bare ground famished and naked, they ran out with streaming hair, urging one another, and themselves, to madness with the wildest cries and actions.", "적나라한 가난이 허락한 정도의 가사 노동에서, 자기 아이들에게서, 헐벗고 굶주린 채 맨땅에 웅크린 노인과 병자에게서 벗어나, 그들은 머리카락을 흩날리며 뛰쳐나와 서로를, 그리고 자신들을 가장 거친 비명과 행동으로 광기로 몰아갔다."], ["Villain Foulon taken, my sister!", "악당 풀롱이 잡혔어, 언니!"], ["Old Foulon taken, my mother!", "늙은 풀롱이 잡혔어, 엄마!"], ["Miscreant Foulon taken, my daughter!", "비열한 풀롱이 잡혔어, 얘야!"], ["Then, a score of others ran into the midst of these, beating their breasts, tearing their hair, and screaming, Foulon alive!", "그러자 또 다른 스무 명쯤이 이들 한가운데로 달려들며 가슴을 치고 머리를 쥐어뜯고 비명을 질렀다, 풀롱이 살아 있다!"], ["Foulon who told the starving people they might eat grass!", "굶주린 사람들에게 풀이나 뜯어 먹으라고 한 풀롱이!"], ["Foulon who told my old father that he might eat grass, when I had no bread to give him!", "내가 드릴 빵이 없을 때 내 늙은 아버지에게 풀이나 드시라고 한 풀롱이!"], ["Foulon who told my baby it might suck grass, when these breasts were dry with want!", "이 젖가슴이 굶주려 말라붙었을 때 내 아기에게 풀이나 빨라고 한 풀롱이!"], ["O mother of God, this Foulon! O Heaven our suffering!", "오 하느님의 어머니시여, 이 풀롱 놈을!"], ["Hear me, my dead baby and my withered father: I swear on my knees, on these stones, to avenge you on Foulon!", "오 하늘이시여, 우리의 고통을! 들어다오, 죽은 내 아기야, 시들어버린 내 아버지야."], ["Husbands, and brothers, and young men, Give us the blood of Foulon, Give us the head of Foulon, Give us the heart of Foulon, Give us the body and soul of Foulon, Rend Foulon to pieces, and dig him into the ground, that grass may grow from him!", "나는 무릎을 꿇고, 이 돌바닥 위에서 맹세한다, 너희를 위해 풀롱에게 복수하겠다고! 남편들이여, 형제들이여, 젊은이들이여, 우리에게 풀롱의 피를 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 머리를 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 심장을 다오, 우리에게 풀롱의 몸과 영혼을 다오, 풀롱을 갈가리 찢어 땅에 파묻어라, 그놈에게서 풀이 자라나도록!"], ["With these cries, numbers of the women, lashed into blind frenzy, whirled about, striking and tearing at their own friends until they dropped into a passionate swoon, and were only saved by the men belonging to them from being trampled under foot.", "이런 외침과 함께, 수많은 여자들이 눈먼 광란에 휘몰려 빙빙 돌며 제 친구들을 후려치고 쥐어뜯다가 격렬한 실신 상태로 쓰러졌고, 자신들에게 딸린 남자들이 아니었더라면 발에 짓밟히지 않을 도리가 없었다."]], [["Nevertheless, not a moment was lost; not a moment!", "그럼에도 단 한순간도 허비되지 않았다."], ["This Foulon was at the Hotel de Ville, and might be loosed.", "단 한순간도! 이 풀롱은 시청에 있었고, 풀려날지도 몰랐다."], ["Never, if Saint Antoine knew his own sufferings, insults, and wrongs!", "쌩땅뜨완이 자신의 고통과 모욕과 부당함을 안다면, 결코 그럴 수 없었다!"], ["Armed men and women flocked out of the Quarter so fast, and drew even these last dregs after them with such a force of suction, that within a quarter of an hour there was not a human creature in Saint Antoine’s bosom but a few old crones and the wailing children.", "무장한 남자들과 여자들이 어찌나 빠르게 구역에서 쏟아져 나왔는지, 그리고 그 마지막 찌꺼기들까지 어찌나 강한 흡인력으로 끌어당겼는지, 십오 분도 채 못 되어 쌩땅뜨완의 품 안에는 몇몇 늙은 노파들과 울부짖는 아이들 말고는 사람이라곤 하나도 남지 않았다."]], [["No.", "그렇다."], ["They were all by that time choking the Hall of Examination where this old man, ugly and wicked, was, and overflowing into the adjacent open space and streets.", "그들은 그 무렵 추하고 사악한 이 늙은이가 있던 심문장을 가득 메워 숨 막히게 하고, 인접한 빈터와 거리로 넘쳐흐르고 있었다."], ["The Defarges, husband and wife, The Vengeance, and Jacques Three, were in the first press, and at no great distance from him in the Hall.", "부부인 드파르주 내외와 복수와 자끄 쓰리는 가장 앞쪽 무리에 끼어 있었고, 심문장 안에서 그자와 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 있었다."]], [["“See!”", "\"보라고!\""], ["cried madame, pointing with her knife.", "하고 마담이 칼로 가리키며 외쳤다."], ["“See the old villain bound with ropes.", "\"저 늙은 악당이 밧줄로 묶여 있는 걸 봐."], ["That was well done to tie a bunch of grass upon his back.", "그자 등에 풀 한 다발을 묶어놓은 건 정말 잘한 일이야."], ["Ha, ha!", "하, 하!"], ["That was well done.", "정말 잘했어."], ["Let him eat it now!”", "이제 그놈더러 먹으라고 해!\""], ["Madame put her knife under her arm, and clapped her hands as at a play.", "마담은 칼을 겨드랑이에 끼고, 마치 연극을 보듯 두 손뼉을 쳤다."]], [["The people immediately behind Madame Defarge, explaining the cause of her satisfaction to those behind them, and those again explaining to others, and those to others, the neighbouring streets resounded with the clapping of hands.", "마담 드파르주 바로 뒤에 있던 사람들이 그녀가 흡족해하는 까닭을 그 뒤의 사람들에게 설명하고, 그들이 다시 다른 이들에게, 그들이 또 다른 이들에게 설명하니, 인근 거리들은 손뼉 소리로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Similarly, during two or three hours of drawl, and the winnowing of many bushels of words, Madame Defarge’s frequent expressions of impatience were taken up, with marvellous quickness, at a distance: the more readily, because certain men who had by some wonderful exercise of agility climbed up the external architecture to look in from the windows, knew Madame Defarge well, and acted as a telegraph between her and the crowd outside the building.", "마찬가지로, 두세 시간에 걸쳐 늘어지는 동안, 그리고 여러 부셸이나 되는 말을 까부르는 동안, 마담 드파르주가 자주 내비치던 초조함의 표시들이 놀라운 속도로 멀리까지 받아들여졌다. 더욱 손쉽게 그리되었으니, 어떤 놀라운 민첩성으로 건물 외부 구조물을 타고 올라가 창문으로 들여다보던 몇몇 남자들이 마담 드파르주를 잘 알고 있어서, 그녀와 건물 바깥 군중 사이의 전신기 노릇을 했기 때문이다."]], [["At length the sun rose so high that it struck a kindly ray as of hope or protection, directly down upon the old prisoner’s head.", "마침내 해가 어찌나 높이 솟았던지, 희망이나 보호처럼 자애로운 한 줄기 빛이 늙은 죄수의 머리 바로 위로 곧장 내리쬐었다."], ["The favour was too much to bear; in an instant the barrier of dust and chaff that had stood surprisingly long, went to the winds, and Saint Antoine had got him!", "그 은총은 견디기에 너무도 과했다. 한순간에, 놀랍도록 오래 버티고 있던 먼지와 검불의 장벽이 바람결에 흩어졌고, 쌩땅뜨완은 그를 손에 넣었다!"]], [["It was known directly, to the furthest confines of the crowd.", "그 사실은 곧장 군중의 가장 먼 끝까지 알려졌다."], ["Defarge had but sprung over a railing and a table, and folded the miserable wretch in a deadly embrace--Madame Defarge had but followed and turned her hand in one of the ropes with which he was tied--The Vengeance and Jacques Three were not yet up with them, and the men at the windows had not yet swooped into the Hall, like birds of prey from their high perches--when the cry seemed to go up, all over the city, “Bring him out!", "드파르주가 난간 하나와 탁자 하나를 뛰어넘어 그 가련한 자를 죽음의 포옹으로 감싸 안았을 뿐이고—마담 드파르주가 그저 뒤따라가 그자를 묶고 있던 밧줄 중 하나에 손을 비틀어 넣었을 뿐이고—복수와 자끄 쓰리는 아직 그들에게 미치지 못했고, 창가의 남자들도 아직 높은 횃대에서 내리덮치는 맹금처럼 심문장 안으로 덮쳐들지 않았을 때—온 도시에 함성이 솟아오르는 듯했다, \"그놈을 끌어내라!"], ["Bring him to the lamp!”", "그놈을 등잔대로 끌고 가라!\""]], [["Down, and up, and head foremost on the steps of the building; now, on his knees; now, on his feet; now, on his back; dragged, and struck at, and stifled by the bunches of grass and straw that were thrust into his face by hundreds of hands; torn, bruised, panting, bleeding, yet always entreating and beseeching for mercy; now full of vehement agony of action, with a small clear space about him as the people drew one another back that they might see; now, a log of dead wood drawn through a forest of legs; he was hauled to the nearest street corner where one of the fatal lamps swung, and there Madame Defarge let him go--as a cat might have done to a mouse--and silently and composedly looked at him while they made ready, and while he besought her: the women passionately screeching at him all the time, and the men sternly calling out to have him killed with grass in his mouth. Once, he went aloft, and the rope broke, and they caught him shrieking; twice, he went aloft, and the rope broke, and they caught him shrieking; then, the rope was merciful, and held him, and his head was soon upon a pike, with grass enough in the mouth for all Saint Antoine to dance at the sight of.", "내려갔다가, 올라왔다가, 건물 계단에서 머리부터 곤두박질치고, 어느 때는 무릎을 꿇고, 어느 때는 두 발로 서고, 어느 때는 등을 대고 자빠진 채, 끌려가고, 얻어맞고, 수백 개의 손이 얼굴에 처박는 풀과 짚 다발에 숨이 막히고, 찢기고, 멍들고, 헐떡이고, 피 흘리면서도 줄곧 자비를 애원하고 빌었다. 어느 때는 격렬한 몸부림의 고통에 가득 차서, 사람들이 보려고 서로를 뒤로 끌어당기는 통에 그의 주위로 작은 빈 공간이 생겼고, 어느 때는 다리들의 숲 사이로 끌려가는 죽은 통나무 토막이었다. 그는 치명적인 등잔대 하나가 흔들거리는 가장 가까운 길모퉁이로 끌려갔고, 거기서 마담 드파르주는 그를 놓아주었다—고양이가 쥐를 가지고 놀듯—그러고는 그들이 준비하는 동안, 그리고 그가 그녀에게 애원하는 동안 말없이 침착하게 그를 바라보았다. 그동안 여자들은 줄곧 그에게 격렬하게 악을 써댔고, 남자들은 그놈의 입에 풀을 물린 채 죽이라고 매섭게 소리쳤다. 한번은 그가 위로 올라갔으나 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 그들은 비명을 지르는 그를 받아냈다. 두번째도 그가 위로 올라갔으나 밧줄이 끊어졌고, 그들은 비명을 지르는 그를 받아냈다. 그러더니 밧줄이 자비를 베풀어 그를 붙들었고, 그의 머리는 곧 창끝에 꽂혔으며, 입에는 쌩땅뜨완 전체가 그 광경을 보고 춤을 출 만큼 충분한 풀이 물려 있었다."]], [["Nor was this the end of the day’s bad work, for Saint Antoine so shouted and danced his angry blood up, that it boiled again, on hearing when the day closed in that the son-in-law of the despatched, another of the people’s enemies and insulters, was coming into Paris under a guard five hundred strong, in cavalry alone.", "이것이 그날의 끔찍한 소행의 끝은 아니었으니, 쌩땅뜨완이 어찌나 소리치고 춤추며 분노의 피를 끓어오르게 했던지, 날이 저물 무렵 처형된 자의 사위가, 또 한 명의 백성의 적이자 능멸자가, 기병만 오백 명에 이르는 호위대에 둘러싸여 빠리로 들어오고 있다는 소식을 듣자 그 피가 다시 끓어올랐다."], ["Saint Antoine wrote his crimes on flaring sheets of paper, seized him--would have torn him out of the breast of an army to bear Foulon company--set his head and heart on pikes, and carried the three spoils of the day, in Wolf-procession through the streets.", "쌩땅뜨완은 그의 죄목을 활활 타오르는 종이에 적어 그를 붙잡았고—풀롱과 짝을 지어주려고 군대의 품 안에서라도 그를 잡아 뜯어냈을 것이며—그의 머리와 심장을 창끝에 꽂아, 그날의 세 가지 전리품을 늑대의 행렬로 거리거리 들고 다녔다."]], [["Not before dark night did the men and women come back to the children, wailing and breadless.", "칠흑 같은 밤이 되어서야 남자들과 여자들은 울부짖으며 빵 한 조각 없는 아이들에게 돌아왔다."], ["Then, the miserable bakers’ shops were beset by long files of them, patiently waiting to buy bad bread; and while they waited with stomachs faint and empty, they beguiled the time by embracing one another on the triumphs of the day, and achieving them again in gossip.", "그러고 나서, 그 비참한 빵집들 앞에는 형편없는 빵을 사려고 참을성 있게 기다리는 긴 줄이 늘어섰다. 그들은 어지럽고 텅 빈 위장으로 기다리는 동안, 그날의 승리에 대해 서로를 끌어안고, 그것들을 잡담으로 다시 이루어내면서 시간을 달랬다."], ["Gradually, these strings of ragged people shortened and frayed away; and then poor lights began to shine in high windows, and slender fires were made in the streets, at which neighbours cooked in common, afterwards supping at their doors.", "차츰 이 누더기 차림 사람들의 줄은 짧아지고 풀어져 흩어졌다. 그러고 나서 높은 창문들에 초라한 불빛이 비치기 시작했고, 거리에는 가냘픈 불이 피워졌으며, 그 곁에서 이웃들이 함께 음식을 지어 나중에 제 집 문간에서 저녁을 먹었다."]], [["Scanty and insufficient suppers those, and innocent of meat, as of most other sauce to wretched bread.", "빈약하고 모자란 저녁 식사였으니, 비참한 빵에 곁들일 다른 대다수 양념은 물론이요 고기라곤 한 점도 없었다."], ["Yet, human fellowship infused some nourishment into the flinty viands, and struck some sparks of cheerfulness out of them.", "그래도 인간의 동료애가 그 돌처럼 딱딱한 음식에 약간의 자양분을 불어넣었고, 거기서 약간의 명랑한 불꽃을 튀겨냈다."], ["Fathers and mothers who had had their full share in the worst of the day, played gently with their meagre children; and lovers, with such a world around them and before them, loved and hoped.", "그날의 가장 험한 일에 제 몫을 톡톡히 치렀던 아버지와 어머니들은 야윈 아이들과 다정하게 놀아주었고, 연인들은 그런 세상을 주위에, 그리고 앞에 두고도 사랑하고 희망을 품었다."]], [["It was almost morning, when Defarge’s wine-shop parted with its last knot of customers, and Monsieur Defarge said to madame his wife, in husky tones, while fastening the door:", "거의 아침이 다 되어서야 드파르주의 술집은 마지막 손님 무리를 떠나보냈고, 무슈 드파르주는 문을 잠그면서 아내인 마담에게 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“At last it is come, my dear!”", "\"마침내 그날이 왔구려, 여보!\""]], [["“Eh well!”", "\"글쎄요!\""], ["returned madame.", "하고 마담이 대답했다."], ["“Almost.”", "\"거의 다 왔지요.\""]], [["Saint Antoine slept, the Defarges slept: even The Vengeance slept with her starved grocer, and the drum was at rest.", "쌩땅뜨완은 잠들었고, 드파르주 내외도 잠들었다. 복수마저 굶주린 식료품상 남편과 함께 잠들었고, 북도 쉬고 있었다."], ["The drum’s was the only voice in Saint Antoine that blood and hurry had not changed.", "피와 소동이 바꿔놓지 못한 쌩땅뜨완의 유일한 목소리는 북소리였다."], ["The Vengeance, as custodian of the drum, could have wakened him up and had the same speech out of him as before the Bastille fell, or old Foulon was seized; not so with the hoarse tones of the men and women in Saint Antoine’s bosom.", "북의 관리자인 복수는 그것을 깨워 바스띠유가 함락되기 전이나 늙은 풀롱이 잡히기 전과 똑같은 말을 시킬 수 있었으리라. 그러나 쌩땅뜨완의 품 안에 있는 남자들과 여자들의 쉰 목소리는 그렇지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII. Fire Rises", "# CHAPTER XXIII. Fire Rises"]], [["There was a change on the village where the fountain fell, and where the mender of roads went forth daily to hammer out of the stones on the highway such morsels of bread as might serve for patches to hold his poor ignorant soul and his poor reduced body together.", "분수가 떨어지던 그 마을, 그리고 도로 보수공이 날마다 나가 큰길의 돌에서 자신의 가련하고 무지한 영혼과 가련하게 쪼그라든 몸뚱이를 한데 붙들어 맬 만한 빵 조각이나마 망치로 두드려 내던 그 마을에, 어떤 변화가 일어났다. 바위 위의 감옥은 예전만큼 위압적이지 않았다."], ["The prison on the crag was not so dominant as of yore; there were soldiers to guard it, but not many; there were officers to guard the soldiers, but not one of them knew what his men would do--beyond this: that it would probably not be what he was ordered.", "그것을 지키는 병사들이 있었으나 많지 않았고, 그 병사들을 지키는 장교들이 있었으나, 그 가운데 누구도 자기 부하들이 무슨 짓을 할지 알지 못했다. 다만 그것이 자신이 명령한 것과는 아마도 다르리라는 것만은 알았다."]], [["Far and wide lay a ruined country, yielding nothing but desolation.", "멀리까지 폐허가 된 땅이 펼쳐져 있었고, 그 땅은 황폐함 말고는 아무것도 내지 못했다."], ["Every green leaf, every blade of grass and blade of grain, was as shrivelled and poor as the miserable people.", "푸른 잎새 하나하나, 풀잎과 곡식 줄기 하나하나가 저 비참한 사람들만큼이나 시들고 빈약했다."], ["Everything was bowed down, dejected, oppressed, and broken.", "모든 것이 고개를 숙이고, 의기소침하고, 짓눌리고, 부서져 있었다."], ["Habitations, fences, domesticated animals, men, women, children, and the soil that bore them--all worn out.", "거처도, 울타리도, 가축들도, 남자들과 여자들과 아이들도, 그리고 그들을 길러낸 흙마저도 — 모두가 닳아 빠져 있었다."]], [["Monseigneur (often a most worthy individual gentleman) was a national blessing, gave a chivalrous tone to things, was a polite example of luxurious and shining life, and a great deal more to equal purpose; nevertheless, Monseigneur as a class had, somehow or other, brought things to this.", "영주님은(흔히 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 개별 신사이기도 했다) 국가적 축복이었고, 만사에 기사도적 기품을 부여했으며, 사치스럽고 찬란한 삶의 정중한 본보기였고, 그밖에도 같은 취지의 많은 것을 의미했다. 그럼에도 하나의 계급으로서 영주님은 어찌어찌하여 사태를 이 지경으로 몰고 왔다."], ["Strange that Creation, designed expressly for Monseigneur, should be so soon wrung dry and squeezed out!", "오로지 영주님을 위해 마련된 천지창조가 이토록 빨리 쥐어짜이고 말라붙어 버리다니 기이한 일이었다!"], ["There must be something short-sighted in the eternal arrangements, surely!", "영원한 섭리 안에 분명 무언가 근시안적인 데가 있었던 것이리라!"], ["Thus it was, however; and the last drop of blood having been extracted from the flints, and the last screw of the rack having been turned so often that its purchase crumbled, and it now turned and turned with nothing to bite, Monseigneur began to run away from a phenomenon so low and unaccountable.", "그러나 사실이 그러했다. 그리하여 부싯돌에서 마지막 핏방울까지 짜내고, 고문대의 나사가 하도 여러 번 돌려진 나머지 물려 있던 자리가 으스러져 이제는 아무것도 물지 못한 채 헛돌고 또 헛돌게 되자, 영주님은 그토록 비천하고 설명할 수 없는 현상으로부터 달아나기 시작했다."]], [["But, this was not the change on the village, and on many a village like it.", "그러나 이것이 그 마을과, 그와 비슷한 수많은 마을에 일어난 변화는 아니었다."], ["For scores of years gone by, Monseigneur had squeezed it and wrung it, and had seldom graced it with his presence except for the pleasures of the chase--now, found in hunting the people; now, found in hunting the beasts, for whose preservation Monseigneur made edifying spaces of barbarous and barren wilderness.", "지난 수십 년 동안 영주님은 마을을 쥐어짜고 비틀었으되, 사냥의 즐거움을 위해서가 아니고서는 좀처럼 그곳을 자신의 행차로 빛내는 일이 없었다. 어떤 때는 사람들을 사냥하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았고, 어떤 때는 짐승들을 사냥하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았으니, 영주님은 그 짐승들의 보존을 위해 야만적이고 메마른 황무지를 교화적인 공간으로 마련해 두었다."], ["No.", "아니다."], ["The change consisted in the appearance of strange faces of low caste, rather than in the disappearance of the high caste, chiselled, and otherwise beautified and beautifying features of Monseigneur.", "그 변화란, 깎아 다듬어진 데다 여러모로 아름답게 꾸며지고 또 주위를 아름답게 하던 영주님의 높은 신분의 이목구비가 사라진 데 있다기보다는, 낮은 신분의 낯선 얼굴들이 나타난 데 있었다."]], [["For, in these times, as the mender of roads worked, solitary, in the dust, not often troubling himself to reflect that dust he was and to dust he must return, being for the most part too much occupied in thinking how little he had for supper and how much more he would eat if he had it--in these times, as he raised his eyes from his lonely labour, and viewed the prospect, he would see some rough figure approaching on foot, the like of which was once a rarity in those parts, but was now a frequent presence. As it advanced, the mender of roads would discern without surprise, that it was a shaggy-haired man, of almost barbarian aspect, tall, in wooden shoes that were clumsy even to the eyes of a mender of roads, grim, rough, swart, steeped in the mud and dust of many highways, dank with the marshy moisture of many low grounds, sprinkled with the thorns and leaves and moss of many byways through woods.", "이 시절에는, 도로 보수공이 먼지 속에서 홀로 일하면서, 자신이 흙에서 났으니 흙으로 돌아가야 한다는 사실을 곰곰이 되새기느라 마음 쓰는 일은 드물었다. 대개는 저녁거리가 얼마나 보잘것없는지, 그것이 있다면 얼마나 더 먹어 치울 수 있을지를 궁리하느라 너무 바빴기 때문이다. 그런 시절에, 그가 외로운 노동에서 눈을 들어 앞을 바라보면, 어떤 거친 형체가 걸어서 다가오는 것을 보곤 했다. 한때 그 일대에서는 좀처럼 보기 드물던 그런 모습이 이제는 흔히 나타나게 된 것이다. 그것이 가까이 다가오면, 도로 보수공은 놀라는 기색도 없이 알아보았다. 그것은 봉두난발에 거의 야만인 같은 풍모를 한 키 큰 사내로, 도로 보수공의 눈에도 투박해 보이는 나막신을 신고 있었으며, 험상궂고 거칠며 가무잡잡하고, 숱한 큰길의 진흙과 먼지에 절어 있었고, 숱한 저지대의 습기로 축축했으며, 숲을 지나는 숱한 샛길의 가시와 잎과 이끼를 뒤집어쓰고 있었다."]], [["Such a man came upon him, like a ghost, at noon in the July weather, as he sat on his heap of stones under a bank, taking such shelter as he could get from a shower of hail.", "그런 사내 하나가, 7월 어느 한낮에 유령처럼 그에게 나타났다. 그가 우박을 피해 둑 아래 돌무더기 위에 앉아 그나마 얻을 수 있는 만큼의 비바람막이를 삼고 있던 때였다."]], [["The man looked at him, looked at the village in the hollow, at the mill, and at the prison on the crag.", "사내는 그를 바라보았고, 골짜기의 마을을, 물방앗간을, 그리고 바위 위의 감옥을 바라보았다."], ["When he had identified these objects in what benighted mind he had, he said, in a dialect that was just intelligible:", "그가 어둑한 머릿속으로 이 대상들을 가려낸 뒤, 겨우 알아들을 만한 사투리로 말했다."]], [["“How goes it, Jacques?”", "\"어떻게 지내나, 자끄?\""]], [["“All well, Jacques.”", "\"다 잘 지내네, 자끄.\""]], [["“Touch then!”", "\"그럼, 손!\""]], [["They joined hands, and the man sat down on the heap of stones.", "그들은 손을 맞잡았고, 사내는 돌무더기 위에 앉았다."]], [["“No dinner?”", "\"점심은 없나?\""]], [["“Nothing but supper now,”", "\"이젠 저녁밖에 없다네.\""], ["said the mender of roads, with a hungry face.", "도로 보수공이 굶주린 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["“It is the fashion,”", "\"그게 요즘 유행이지.\""], ["growled the man.", "사내가 으르렁대듯 말했다."], ["“I meet no dinner anywhere.”", "\"어디를 가도 점심이라곤 만날 수가 없으니.\""]], [["He took out a blackened pipe, filled it, lighted it with flint and steel, pulled at it until it was in a bright glow: then, suddenly held it from him and dropped something into it from between his finger and thumb, that blazed and went out in a puff of smoke.", "그는 거뭇해진 파이프를 꺼내 담배를 채우고, 부싯돌과 쇳조각으로 불을 붙여, 환히 타오를 때까지 빨아들였다. 그러더니 별안간 파이프를 입에서 떼어, 엄지와 검지 사이에 쥐고 있던 무언가를 그 안에 떨어뜨렸는데, 그것은 확 타오르더니 한 줄기 연기 속에 꺼져 버렸다."]], [["“Touch then.”", "\"그럼, 손.\""], ["It was the turn of the mender of roads to say it this time, after observing these operations.", "이번에는 이 동작들을 지켜본 도로 보수공이 그렇게 말할 차례였다."], ["They again joined hands.", "그들은 다시 손을 맞잡았다."]], [["“To-night?”", "\"오늘 밤인가?\""], ["said the mender of roads.", "도로 보수공이 말했다."]], [["“To-night,”", "\"오늘 밤이지.\""], ["said the man, putting the pipe in his mouth.", "사내가 파이프를 입에 물며 말했다."]], [["“Where?”", "\"어디서?\""]], [["“Here.”", "\"여기서.\""]], [["He and the mender of roads sat on the heap of stones looking silently at one another, with the hail driving in between them like a pigmy charge of bayonets, until the sky began to clear over the village.", "그와 도로 보수공은 돌무더기 위에 앉아 말없이 서로를 바라보았고, 그동안 우박이 마치 난쟁이 총검대의 돌격처럼 둘 사이로 들이쳤다. 그러다 마을 위로 하늘이 개기 시작했다."]], [["“Show me!”", "\"보여주게!\""], ["said the traveller then, moving to the brow of the hill.", "그러자 나그네가 언덕 마루로 옮겨가며 말했다."]], [["“See!”", "\"보게나!\""], ["returned the mender of roads, with extended finger.", "도로 보수공이 손가락을 펴 가리키며 대답했다."], ["“You go down here, and straight through the street, and past the fountain--”", "\"여기로 내려가서, 거리를 곧장 가로질러, 분수를 지나면—\""]], [["“To the Devil with all that!”", "\"그딴 건 다 악마한테나 가버리라지!\""], ["interrupted the other, rolling his eye over the landscape.", "상대가 풍경 위로 눈길을 굴리며 말을 끊었다."], ["“I go through no streets and past no fountains.", "\"나는 거리도 가로지르지 않고 분수도 지나지 않아."], ["Well?”", "그래서?\""]], [["“Well!", "\"그래서!"], ["About two leagues beyond the summit of that hill above the village.”", "마을 위 저 언덕 꼭대기에서 두 리외쯤 더 간 곳일세.\""]], [["“Good.", "\"좋아."], ["When do you cease to work?”", "자네는 언제 일을 마치나?\""]], [["“At sunset.”", "\"해질녘에.\""]], [["“Will you wake me, before departing?", "\"떠나기 전에 나를 깨워 주겠나?"], ["I have walked two nights without resting.", "이틀 밤을 쉬지 않고 걸었네."], ["Let me finish my pipe, and I shall sleep like a child.", "파이프를 다 피우게 해주면, 어린애처럼 잘 거야."], ["Will you wake me?”", "깨워 주겠나?\""]], [["“Surely.”", "\"물론이지.\""]], [["The wayfarer smoked his pipe out, put it in his breast, slipped off his great wooden shoes, and lay down on his back on the heap of stones.", "나그네는 파이프를 다 피우고 나서 그것을 품에 넣고, 커다란 나막신을 벗어 던지고는, 돌무더기 위에 벌렁 드러누웠다."], ["He was fast asleep directly.", "그는 곧장 깊이 잠들었다."]], [["As the road-mender plied his dusty labour, and the hail-clouds, rolling away, revealed bright bars and streaks of sky which were responded to by silver gleams upon the landscape, the little man (who wore a red cap now, in place of his blue one) seemed fascinated by the figure on the heap of stones.", "도로 보수공이 먼지투성이 일을 계속하는 동안, 우박을 머금은 구름이 굴러가며 밝은 띠와 줄무늬 같은 하늘을 드러냈고, 그에 화답하듯 풍경 위로 은빛 광채가 어렸다. 그 작은 사내는(이제는 파란 모자 대신 빨간 모자를 쓰고 있었다) 돌무더기 위의 형체에 홀린 듯했다."], ["His eyes were so often turned towards it, that he used his tools mechanically, and, one would have said, to very poor account.", "그의 눈은 하도 자주 그쪽으로 향한 나머지, 그는 연장을 기계적으로 놀렸으니, 누가 보든 일은 형편없이 진행되는 셈이었다."], ["The bronze face, the shaggy black hair and beard, the coarse woollen red cap, the rough medley dress of home-spun stuff and hairy skins of beasts, the powerful frame attenuated by spare living, and the sullen and desperate compression of the lips in sleep, inspired the mender of roads with awe.", "청동빛 얼굴, 봉두난발의 검은 머리칼과 수염, 거친 양모로 짠 빨간 모자, 손으로 짠 천과 짐승의 털가죽이 뒤섞인 투박한 옷차림, 변변찮은 끼니로 야위어 버린 강건한 골격, 그리고 잠결에도 부루퉁하고 결연하게 다물린 입술은 도로 보수공에게 외경심을 불러일으켰다."], ["The traveller had travelled far, and his feet were footsore, and his ankles chafed and bleeding; his great shoes, stuffed with leaves and grass, had been heavy to drag over the many long leagues, and his clothes were chafed into holes, as he himself was into sores.", "나그네는 멀리서부터 걸어온 터라 발이 부르트고, 발목은 쓸려서 피가 났다. 잎과 풀로 채운 그의 커다란 신발은 그 숱한 긴 리외를 끌고 오기에 무거웠고, 그의 옷은 쓸려서 구멍이 났으니, 그 자신이 헐어 상처가 난 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["Stooping down beside him, the road-mender tried to get a peep at secret weapons in his breast or where not; but, in vain, for he slept with his arms crossed upon him, and set as resolutely as his lips.", "도로 보수공은 그의 곁에 몸을 굽히고, 그의 품속이나 어딘가에 감춰진 무기를 슬쩍 엿보려 했으나 헛수고였으니, 사내는 두 팔을 가슴 위에 엇갈려 얹은 채, 그 입술만큼이나 단호하게 잠들어 있었다."], ["Fortified towns with their stockades, guard-houses, gates, trenches, and drawbridges, seemed to the mender of roads, to be so much air as against this figure.", "도로 보수공이 보기에, 방책과 위병소와 성문과 참호와 도개교를 갖춘 요새 도시도 이 형체에 견주면 한낱 허공에 지나지 않을 듯했다."], ["And when he lifted his eyes from it to the horizon and looked around, he saw in his small fancy similar figures, stopped by no obstacle, tending to centres all over France.", "그가 그 형체에서 지평선으로 눈을 들어 사방을 둘러보자, 그의 작은 상상 속에서, 어떤 장애물에도 멈추지 않고 프랑스 곳곳의 중심지들을 향해 나아가는 비슷한 형체들이 보였다."]], [["The man slept on, indifferent to showers of hail and intervals of brightness, to sunshine on his face and shadow, to the paltering lumps of dull ice on his body and the diamonds into which the sun changed them, until the sun was low in the west, and the sky was glowing.", "사내는 계속 잠들어 있었으니, 우박이 쏟아지든 잠깐씩 날이 개든, 얼굴에 햇살이 내리든 그늘이 지든, 둔탁한 얼음덩이가 몸 위로 후두둑 떨어지든 햇빛이 그것을 다이아몬드로 바꾸어 놓든, 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["Then, the mender of roads having got his tools together and all things ready to go down into the village, roused him.", "그러다 해가 서편으로 낮게 기울고 하늘이 붉게 타오를 무렵, 도로 보수공은 연장을 챙기고 마을로 내려갈 채비를 다 마친 뒤 그를 깨웠다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋아!\""], ["said the sleeper, rising on his elbow.", "잠자던 사내가 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키며 말했다."], ["“Two leagues beyond the summit of the hill?” “About.”", "\"언덕 꼭대기에서 두 리외 더 간 곳이라고?\""]], [["“About. Good!”", "\"대략 그쯤이지.\""]], [["The mender of roads went home, with the dust going on before him according to the set of the wind, and was soon at the fountain, squeezing himself in among the lean kine brought there to drink, and appearing even to whisper to them in his whispering to all the village.", "\"대략 그쯤. 좋아!\""], ["When the village had taken its poor supper, it did not creep to bed, as it usually did, but came out of doors again, and remained there.", "도로 보수공은 바람결 따라 앞서 날리는 먼지를 거느리고 집으로 돌아가, 이내 분수에 이르렀고, 물을 마시러 그곳에 끌려온 비쩍 마른 소떼 틈에 끼어들었으며, 마을 전체에 속삭이듯 그 소들에게도 속삭이는 듯 보였다."], ["A curious contagion of whispering was upon it, and also, when it gathered together at the fountain in the dark, another curious contagion of looking expectantly at the sky in one direction only.", "마을이 그 보잘것없는 저녁을 먹고 난 뒤, 여느 때처럼 슬며시 잠자리로 기어들지 않고, 다시 문밖으로 나와 거기 머물러 있었다. 기묘한 속삭임의 전염이 마을에 번졌고, 또한 어둠 속 분수가에 모여들었을 때는, 오직 한 방향으로만 기대에 차서 하늘을 바라보는 또 하나의 기묘한 전염이 번졌다."], ["Monsieur Gabelle, chief functionary of the place, became uneasy; went out on his house-top alone, and looked in that direction too; glanced down from behind his chimneys at the darkening faces by the fountain below, and sent word to the sacristan who kept the keys of the church, that there might be need to ring the tocsin by-and-bye.", "그곳의 우두머리 관리인 가벨 씨는 불안해졌다. 홀로 자기 집 지붕에 올라가 그 역시 그쪽을 바라보았고, 굴뚝 뒤에서 아래쪽 분수가의 어두워져 가는 얼굴들을 내려다보았으며, 교회 열쇠를 지닌 성당지기에게 머지않아 경종을 울려야 할지도 모른다고 전갈을 보냈다."]], [["The night deepened.", "밤이 깊어 갔다."], ["The trees environing the old chateau, keeping its solitary state apart, moved in a rising wind, as though they threatened the pile of building massive and dark in the gloom.", "외따로 떨어진 그 적막한 상태를 지키던 옛 성을 둘러싼 나무들이, 점점 거세지는 바람결에 흔들리며, 마치 어둠 속에 육중하고 검게 솟은 그 건물 더미를 위협하는 듯했다."], ["Up the two terrace flights of steps the rain ran wildly, and beat at the great door, like a swift messenger rousing those within; uneasy rushes of wind went through the hall, among the old spears and knives, and passed lamenting up the stairs, and shook the curtains of the bed where the last Marquis had slept.", "두 단의 테라스 계단을 따라 빗물이 사납게 흘러내려 큰 문을 두드렸으니, 마치 안에 있는 자들을 깨우려는 잰 사자(使者) 같았다. 불안한 바람이 휙휙 홀을 가로질러 낡은 창과 칼들 사이를 지나, 탄식하듯 계단을 타고 올라가, 마지막 후작이 잠들었던 침대의 휘장을 흔들었다."], ["East, West, North, and South, through the woods, four heavy-treading, unkempt figures crushed the high grass and cracked the branches, striding on cautiously to come together in the courtyard.", "동, 서, 남, 북으로, 숲을 지나, 무겁게 발을 디디는 봉두난발의 형체 넷이 키 큰 풀을 짓밟고 나뭇가지를 부러뜨리며, 안마당에서 한데 모이려고 조심스레 성큼성큼 나아갔다."], ["Four lights broke out there, and moved away in different directions, and all was black again.", "거기서 불빛 넷이 일더니, 제각기 다른 방향으로 옮겨갔고, 다시 온통 캄캄해졌다."]], [["But, not for long.", "그러나 오래가지는 않았다."], ["Presently, the chateau began to make itself strangely visible by some light of its own, as though it were growing luminous.", "이윽고 성은 무언가 제 안의 빛으로, 마치 스스로 환히 빛을 내듯 기이하게 모습을 드러내기 시작했다."], ["Then, a flickering streak played behind the architecture of the front, picking out transparent places, and showing where balustrades, arches, and windows were.", "그러더니 깜박이는 한 줄기 빛이 정면 건축물 뒤에서 어른거리며, 빛이 투과되는 곳들을 짚어내어, 난간과 아치와 창문이 어디 있는지를 드러냈다."], ["Then it soared higher, and grew broader and brighter.", "그러더니 그 빛은 더 높이 솟아오르며, 더 넓고 더 밝아졌다."], ["Soon, from a score of the great windows, flames burst forth, and the stone faces awakened, stared out of fire.", "이내 스무남은 개의 큰 창문에서 불길이 터져 나왔고, 돌로 된 얼굴들이 깨어나, 불 속에서 노려보았다."]], [["A faint murmur arose about the house from the few people who were left there, and there was a saddling of a horse and riding away.", "거기 남아 있던 몇 안 되는 사람들로부터 집 주위로 희미한 웅성거림이 일었고, 말 한 마리에 안장을 얹어 타고 달려 나가는 일이 벌어졌다."], ["There was spurring and splashing through the darkness, and bridle was drawn in the space by the village fountain, and the horse in a foam stood at Monsieur Gabelle’s door.", "어둠을 뚫고 박차를 가하며 첨벙거리는 소리가 났고, 마을 분수가의 공터에서 고삐가 당겨졌으며, 거품을 문 말이 가벨 씨의 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["“Help, Gabelle!", "\"도와주시오, 가벨!"], ["Help, every one!”", "모두들 도와주시오!\""], ["The tocsin rang impatiently, but other help (if that were any) there was none.", "경종이 다급하게 울렸으나, 다른 도움은(그것이 도움이라 할 수 있다면) 전혀 없었다."], ["The mender of roads, and two hundred and fifty particular friends, stood with folded arms at the fountain, looking at the pillar of fire in the sky.", "도로 보수공과 이백쉰 명의 각별한 벗들은 팔짱을 낀 채 분수가에 서서, 하늘로 치솟는 불기둥을 바라보았다."], ["“It must be forty feet high,”", "\"마흔 자는 되겠군.\""], ["said they, grimly; and never moved.", "그들은 험상궂게 말했고, 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."]], [["The rider from the chateau, and the horse in a foam, clattered away through the village, and galloped up the stony steep, to the prison on the crag.", "성에서 온 기수와 거품을 문 말은 마을을 지나 요란하게 달려, 돌투성이 비탈을 질주해 올라가, 바위 위의 감옥에 이르렀다."], ["At the gate, a group of officers were looking at the fire; removed from them, a group of soldiers.", "성문에서는 한 무리의 장교들이 불을 바라보고 있었고, 그들로부터 떨어진 곳에 한 무리의 병사들이 있었다."], ["“Help, gentlemen--officers!", "\"도와주십시오, 장교 나리들!"], ["The chateau is on fire; valuable objects may be saved from the flames by timely aid!", "성에 불이 났습니다. 제때에 손을 쓰면 값진 물건들을 불길에서 건질 수 있습니다!"], ["Help, help!”", "도와주십시오, 도와주십시오!\""], ["The officers looked towards the soldiers who looked at the fire; gave no orders; and answered, with shrugs and biting of lips, “It must burn.”", "장교들은 불을 바라보는 병사들 쪽으로 시선을 돌렸고, 아무런 명령도 내리지 않았으며, 어깨를 으쓱하고 입술을 깨물며 대답했다. \"타게 내버려 두는 수밖에.\""]], [["As the rider rattled down the hill again and through the street, the village was illuminating.", "기수가 다시 언덕을 덜그럭거리며 내려와 거리를 지날 무렵, 마을은 환하게 밝혀지고 있었다."], ["The mender of roads, and the two hundred and fifty particular friends, inspired as one man and woman by the idea of lighting up, had darted into their houses, and were putting candles in every dull little pane of glass.", "도로 보수공과 그 이백쉰 명의 각별한 벗들은, 불을 밝히자는 생각에 마치 한 사람의 남자와 한 사람의 여자처럼 의기투합하여, 저마다 집으로 달려 들어가, 흐릿한 유리창마다 촛불을 꽂고 있었다."], ["The general scarcity of everything, occasioned candles to be borrowed in a rather peremptory manner of Monsieur Gabelle; and in a moment of reluctance and hesitation on that functionary’s part, the mender of roads, once so submissive to authority, had remarked that carriages were good to make bonfires with, and that post-horses would roast.", "만물이 두루 모자라던 터라, 촛불은 가벨 씨에게서 다소 강압적인 방식으로 빌려 왔다. 그 관리가 마지못해 머뭇거리던 순간, 한때 그토록 권위에 고분고분하던 도로 보수공이, 마차란 모닥불 피우기에 좋고, 역마(驛馬)란 구워 먹기에 좋겠다고 한마디 던졌다."]], [["The chateau was left to itself to flame and burn.", "성은 제멋대로 타오르고 불사르도록 내버려졌다."], ["In the roaring and raging of the conflagration, a red-hot wind, driving straight from the infernal regions, seemed to be blowing the edifice away.", "활활 타오르며 사납게 미쳐 날뛰는 불길 속에서, 지옥에서 곧장 불어오는 듯한 시뻘겋게 달아오른 바람이 그 건물을 통째로 날려 보내는 듯했다."], ["With the rising and falling of the blaze, the stone faces showed as if they were in torment.", "화염이 솟구쳤다 가라앉음에 따라, 돌로 된 얼굴들은 마치 고통에 시달리는 것처럼 보였다."], ["When great masses of stone and timber fell, the face with the two dints in the nose became obscured: anon struggled out of the smoke again, as if it were the face of the cruel Marquis, burning at the stake and contending with the fire.", "커다란 돌덩이와 목재가 우르르 무너져 내릴 때면, 코에 두 군데 움푹 팬 자국이 있는 그 얼굴이 가려졌다가, 이윽고 다시 연기 속에서 헤쳐 나왔으니, 마치 화형대에서 불타며 불길과 다투는 잔인한 후작의 얼굴 같았다."]], [["The chateau burned; the nearest trees, laid hold of by the fire, scorched and shrivelled; trees at a distance, fired by the four fierce figures, begirt the blazing edifice with a new forest of smoke.", "성이 불탔다. 가장 가까운 나무들은 불길에 사로잡혀 그슬리고 오그라들었고, 멀리 있는 나무들은 사나운 형체 넷에 의해 불붙어, 타오르는 건물을 새로운 연기의 숲으로 에워쌌다."], ["Molten lead and iron boiled in the marble basin of the fountain; the water ran dry; the extinguisher tops of the towers vanished like ice before the heat, and trickled down into four rugged wells of flame.", "녹은 납과 쇠가 분수의 대리석 수반 안에서 끓어올랐고, 물은 말라 버렸으며, 탑 꼭대기의 소화구(消火具) 모양 첨탑들은 열기 앞에서 얼음처럼 사라져, 우락부락한 화염의 우물 넷으로 녹아 흘러내렸다."], ["Great rents and splits branched out in the solid walls, like crystallisation; stupefied birds wheeled about and dropped into the furnace; four fierce figures trudged away, East, West, North, and South, along the night-enshrouded roads, guided by the beacon they had lighted, towards their next destination.", "단단한 벽에는 마치 결정(結晶)이 자라나듯 커다란 균열과 틈새가 갈라져 뻗어 나갔고, 얼이 빠진 새들은 빙빙 맴돌다 화덕 속으로 떨어졌다. 사나운 형체 넷은 동, 서, 남, 북으로, 밤에 휩싸인 길을 따라 터벅터벅 떠나갔으니, 자기네가 밝혀 놓은 봉화의 인도를 받아 다음 목적지를 향했다."], ["The illuminated village had seized hold of the tocsin, and, abolishing the lawful ringer, rang for joy.", "환하게 밝혀진 마을은 경종을 거머쥐었고, 합법적인 종지기를 내쫓고서, 기쁨에 겨워 종을 울렸다."]], [["Not only that; but the village, light-headed with famine, fire, and bell-ringing, and bethinking itself that Monsieur Gabelle had to do with the collection of rent and taxes--though it was but a small instalment of taxes, and no rent at all, that Gabelle had got in those latter days--became impatient for an interview with him, and, surrounding his house, summoned him to come forth for personal conference.", "그뿐이 아니었다. 굶주림과 불길과 종소리에 들떠 어질어질해진 마을은, 가벨 씨가 지대와 세금 징수에 관여한다는 데 생각이 미치자—그가 근래에 거둬들인 것이라곤 세금의 일부에 지나지 않았고, 지대는 전혀 없었지만—그와의 면담을 안달하며 갈망하게 되었고, 그의 집을 에워싸고는 몸소 나와 대면하라고 그를 불러댔다."], ["Whereupon, Monsieur Gabelle did heavily bar his door, and retire to hold counsel with himself.", "그러자 가벨 씨는 자기 집 문을 단단히 빗장 지르고 물러나, 혼자서 궁리를 거듭했다."], ["The result of that conference was, that Gabelle again withdrew himself to his housetop behind his stack of chimneys; this time resolved, if his door were broken in (he was a small Southern man of retaliative temperament), to pitch himself head foremost over the parapet, and crush a man or two below.", "그 숙고의 결과, 가벨은 다시 굴뚝 무더기 뒤편 지붕으로 물러났는데, 이번에는 만일 문이 부서져 들어오면(그는 보복심이 강한 기질의 자그마한 남부 사람이었다), 난간 너머로 머리부터 곤두박질쳐서 아래에 있는 한두 사람을 깔아뭉개기로 작심한 터였다."]], [["Probably, Monsieur Gabelle passed a long night up there, with the distant chateau for fire and candle, and the beating at his door, combined with the joy-ringing, for music; not to mention his having an ill-omened lamp slung across the road before his posting-house gate, which the village showed a lively inclination to displace in his favour.", "아마 가벨 씨는 그 위에서 긴 밤을 지새웠을 것이다. 멀리 보이는 성을 불이자 촛불로 삼고, 문을 두드려대는 소리에 환희의 종소리가 어우러진 것을 음악으로 삼은 채로 말이다."], ["A trying suspense, to be passing a whole summer night on the brink of the black ocean, ready to take that plunge into it upon which Monsieur Gabelle had resolved!", "게다가 그의 역참 문 앞 길 위에는 불길한 등이 가로질러 매달려 있었으니, 마을은 그를 위해 친히 그것을 옮겨 주려는 발랄한 의향을 드러내 보였다."], ["But, the friendly dawn appearing at last, and the rush-candles of the village guttering out, the people happily dispersed, and Monsieur Gabelle came down bringing his life with him for that while.", "검은 바다의 벼랑 끝에서 여름밤 내내, 가벨 씨가 결심한 그 몸 던지기를 언제라도 감행할 채비를 한 채 버틴다는 것은, 참으로 견디기 힘든 마음 졸임이었으리라! 그러나 마침내 정다운 새벽이 밝아 오고, 마을의 골풀 촛불들이 깜박거리며 꺼져가자, 사람들은 다행히도 흩어졌고, 가벨 씨는 그 한동안 제 목숨을 부지한 채 지붕에서 내려왔다."]], [["Within a hundred miles, and in the light of other fires, there were other functionaries less fortunate, that night and other nights, whom the rising sun found hanging across once-peaceful streets, where they had been born and bred; also, there were other villagers and townspeople less fortunate than the mender of roads and his fellows, upon whom the functionaries and soldiery turned with success, and whom they strung up in their turn.", "백 마일 안쪽에서, 다른 불빛 아래에서는, 그날 밤과 다른 밤들에 그만큼 운이 좋지 못한 다른 관리들이 있었으니, 떠오르는 해는 자기들이 나고 자란 한때 평화롭던 거리에 매달려 있는 그들을 발견했다. 또한 도로 보수공과 그 동료들만큼 운이 좋지 못한 다른 마을 사람들과 읍내 사람들도 있었으니, 관리들과 군대가 그들에게 성공적으로 맞서, 이번에는 그들을 줄줄이 매달았다."], ["But, the fierce figures were steadily wending East, West, North, and South, be that as it would; and whosoever hung, fire burned.", "그러나 사나운 형체들은 어찌 되었든 한결같이 동, 서, 남, 북으로 나아가고 있었다. 그리고 누가 매달리든 간에, 불은 타올랐다."], ["The altitude of the gallows that would turn to water and quench it, no functionary, by any stretch of mathematics, was able to calculate successfully.", "어떤 관리도, 수학을 아무리 끌어다 써본들, 그 불을 물로 바꾸어 꺼버릴 만한 교수대의 높이를 끝내 계산해 낼 수 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXIV. Drawn to the Loadstone Rock", "# CHAPTER XXIV. Drawn to the Loadstone Rock"]], [["In such risings of fire and risings of sea--the firm earth shaken by the rushes of an angry ocean which had now no ebb, but was always on the flow, higher and higher, to the terror and wonder of the beholders on the shore--three years of tempest were consumed.", "이렇게 불길이 치솟고 바다가 솟구치는 가운데 — 굳건한 대지가, 이제는 썰물이라곤 없이 늘 밀물로만, 점점 더 높이 차오르며 해변에서 지켜보는 이들에게 공포와 경이를 안기던 성난 대양의 거센 돌격에 흔들리는 가운데 — 세 해의 폭풍이 다 타들어 갔다."], ["Three more birthdays of little Lucie had been woven by the golden thread into the peaceful tissue of the life of her home.", "어린 루씨의 생일 세 번이 또 황금실에 엮여 그 집안의 평화로운 삶의 직물 속으로 짜여 들어갔다."]], [["Many a night and many a day had its inmates listened to the echoes in the corner, with hearts that failed them when they heard the thronging feet.", "수많은 밤과 수많은 낮 동안 그 집의 식구들은 모퉁이의 메아리에 귀를 기울였고, 떼 지어 몰려드는 발소리를 들을 때면 가슴이 내려앉았다."], ["For, the footsteps had become to their minds as the footsteps of a people, tumultuous under a red flag and with their country declared in danger, changed into wild beasts, by terrible enchantment long persisted in.", "그 발소리가 이제는 그들의 마음속에서, 붉은 깃발 아래 소란을 떨며 조국이 위기에 처했다고 선포된 가운데, 오래도록 끈질기게 이어진 끔찍한 마법에 걸려 야수로 변해버린 한 민족의 발소리처럼 여겨졌기 때문이다."]], [["Monseigneur, as a class, had dissociated himself from the phenomenon of his not being appreciated: of his being so little wanted in France, as to incur considerable danger of receiving his dismissal from it, and this life together.", "므슈* 영주들은 하나의 계급으로서, 자신들이 평가받지 못하는 현상으로부터 발을 떼어버렸다. 프랑스에서 자신들이 너무도 쓸모없어진 나머지, 그곳으로부터, 그리고 이 삶 자체로부터 해고당할 상당한 위험에 처해 있다는 현상으로부터 말이다."], ["Like the fabled rustic who raised the Devil with infinite pains, and was so terrified at the sight of him that he could ask the Enemy no question, but immediately fled; so, Monseigneur, after boldly reading the Lord’s Prayer backwards for a great number of years, and performing many other potent spells for compelling the Evil One, no sooner beheld him in his terrors than he took to his noble heels.", "무한한 노고를 들여 악마를 불러냈으나 정작 그 모습을 보자 너무도 겁에 질려 적에게 아무런 질문도 하지 못한 채 곧장 도망쳐버렸다는 우화 속의 그 시골뜨기처럼, 므슈 영주들도 여러 해 동안 대담하게 주기도문을 거꾸로 외고 사악한 자를 불러내려 다른 온갖 강력한 주문을 행하다가, 막상 그 무시무시한 모습을 보자마자 고귀한 두 발로 줄행랑을 쳤다."]], [["The shining Bull’s Eye of the Court was gone, or it would have been the mark for a hurricane of national bullets.", "궁정의 빛나는 황소 눈*은 사라졌으니, 그렇지 않았다면 국민이 쏘아대는 총탄의 폭풍 속 표적이 되었으리라."], ["It had never been a good eye to see with--had long had the mote in it of Lucifer’s pride, Sardanapalus’s luxury, and a mole’s blindness--but it had dropped out and was gone.", "그것은 결코 잘 보는 눈이었던 적이 없었으니 — 오래도록 루씨퍼의 오만과 사르다나팔루스의 사치와 두더지의 맹목이라는 티가 박혀 있었다 — 그러나 그것은 빠져나가 사라져버렸다."], ["The Court, from that exclusive inner circle to its outermost rotten ring of intrigue, corruption, and dissimulation, was all gone together.", "궁정은, 그 폐쇄적인 내부 핵심으로부터 음모와 부패와 위선으로 이루어진 가장 바깥의 썩어빠진 고리에 이르기까지, 모조리 함께 사라져버렸다."], ["Royalty was gone; had been besieged in its Palace and “suspended,” when the last tidings came over.", "왕권도 사라졌으니, 마지막 소식이 전해졌을 때는 궁전에서 포위된 채 \"정지\"되어 있었다."]], [["The August of the year one thousand seven hundred and ninety-two was come, and Monseigneur was by this time scattered far and wide.", "* 황소 눈: 천장의 둥근 채광창을 가리키는 동시에 궁정의 권력자들을 비유한 표현. 1792년의 8월이 왔고, 므슈 영주들은 이 무렵 멀리멀리 흩어져 있었다."]], [["As was natural, the head-quarters and great gathering-place of Monseigneur, in London, was Tellson’s Bank.", "당연한 일이지만, 런던에서 므슈 영주들의 본부이자 큰 집결지는 뗄슨 은행이었다."], ["Spirits are supposed to haunt the places where their bodies most resorted, and Monseigneur without a guinea haunted the spot where his guineas used to be.", "영혼은 그 육신이 가장 자주 드나들던 장소를 떠돈다고들 하는데, 한 닢의 기니도 없는 므슈 영주들은 자신들의 기니가 놓여 있던 그 자리를 떠돌았다."], ["Moreover, it was the spot to which such French intelligence as was most to be relied upon, came quickest.", "게다가 그곳은 가장 믿을 만한 프랑스 소식이 가장 빨리 당도하는 자리이기도 했다."], ["Again: Tellson’s was a munificent house, and extended great liberality to old customers who had fallen from their high estate.", "또한 뗄슨 은행은 후한 곳이어서, 높은 지위에서 추락한 옛 고객들에게 넉넉한 아량을 베풀었다."], ["Again: those nobles who had seen the coming storm in time, and anticipating plunder or confiscation, had made provident remittances to Tellson’s, were always to be heard of there by their needy brethren.", "또한 다가오는 폭풍을 제때 알아채고 약탈이나 몰수를 예견하여 미리 뗄슨에 송금해둔 귀족들의 소식은, 궁핍해진 동료들이 언제든 그곳에서 들을 수 있었다."], ["To which it must be added that every new-comer from France reported himself and his tidings at Tellson’s, almost as a matter of course.", "거기에 덧붙여, 프랑스에서 오는 모든 새 인물은 거의 당연한 일처럼 뗄슨에서 자신의 신원과 소식을 알렸다."], ["For such variety of reasons, Tellson’s was at that time, as to French intelligence, a kind of High Exchange; and this was so well known to the public, and the inquiries made there were in consequence so numerous, that Tellson’s sometimes wrote the latest news out in a line or so and posted it in the Bank windows, for all who ran through Temple Bar to read.", "이렇듯 다양한 이유로, 뗄슨은 그 무렵 프랑스 소식에 관한 한 일종의 거대한 거래소였다. 그리고 이것은 대중에게 너무도 잘 알려져 있어서 그곳에 들어오는 문의가 그만큼 빈번했던 까닭에, 뗄슨은 이따금 최신 소식을 한두 줄로 적어 은행 창문에 게시하여, 템플 바를 지나는 모든 이가 읽을 수 있게 했다."]], [["On a steaming, misty afternoon, Mr. Lorry sat at his desk, and Charles Darnay stood leaning on it, talking with him in a low voice.", "후텁지근하고 안개 낀 어느 오후, 로리 씨는 책상 앞에 앉아 있었고 찰스 다네이는 거기에 기대선 채 낮은 목소리로 그와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The penitential den once set apart for interviews with the House, was now the news-Exchange, and was filled to overflowing.", "한때 은행과의 면담을 위해 따로 마련되었던 그 참회의 골방은 이제 소식의 거래소가 되어 넘칠 듯이 가득 차 있었다."], ["It was within half an hour or so of the time of closing.", "마감 시각까지는 반 시간 남짓 남아 있었다."]], [["“But, although you are the youngest man that ever lived,”", "\"하지만, 비록 세상에 없을 만큼 젊으신 분이라 해도,\""], ["said Charles Darnay, rather hesitating, “I must still suggest to you--”", "찰스 다네이가 다소 망설이며 말했다, \"저는 그래도 말씀드려야겠습니다 —\""]], [["“I understand.", "\"알겠네."], ["That I am too old?”", "내가 너무 늙었다는 거지?\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Unsettled weather, a long journey, uncertain means of travelling, a disorganised country, a city that may not be even safe for you.”", "\"날씨도 변덕스럽고, 여정은 멀고, 이동 수단은 불확실하고, 나라는 혼란에 빠졌고, 도시는 어르신께 안전하지조차 않을지 모릅니다.\""]], [["“My dear Charles,”", "\"이보게 찰스,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, with cheerful confidence, “you touch some of the reasons for my going: not for my staying away.", "로리 씨가 쾌활한 확신을 담아 말했다, \"자네가 짚은 건 내가 가야 하는 이유들이지, 멀리 떨어져 있어야 하는 이유가 아닐세."], ["It is safe enough for me; nobody will care to interfere with an old fellow of hard upon fourscore when there are so many people there much better worth interfering with.", "나한테는 충분히 안전해. 거기에 훨씬 더 간섭할 만한 가치가 있는 사람이 그렇게 많은데, 누가 여든을 코앞에 둔 늙은이를 굳이 건드리려 하겠나."], ["As to its being a disorganised city, if it were not a disorganised city there would be no occasion to send somebody from our House here to our House there, who knows the city and the business, of old, and is in Tellson’s confidence.", "혼란에 빠진 도시라는 점에 대해서라면, 혼란에 빠진 도시가 아니라면 그 도시와 사정을 예전부터 잘 알고 뗄슨의 신임을 받는 누군가를 여기 우리 은행에서 그곳 우리 은행으로 보낼 필요도 없겠지."], ["As to the uncertain travelling, the long journey, and the winter weather, if I were not prepared to submit myself to a few inconveniences for the sake of Tellson’s, after all these years, who ought to be?”", "불확실한 이동이며 먼 여정이며 겨울 날씨에 대해서라면, 이 오랜 세월이 흐른 마당에 뗄슨을 위해 약간의 불편을 감수할 각오가 안 된다면, 대체 누가 그래야 한단 말인가?\""]], [["“I wish I were going myself,”", "\"제가 직접 갈 수 있다면 좋으련만,\""], ["said Charles Darnay, somewhat restlessly, and like one thinking aloud.", "찰스 다네이가 다소 안절부절못하며, 마치 혼잣말을 입 밖에 내는 사람처럼 말했다."]], [["“Indeed!", "\"이런!"], ["You are a pretty fellow to object and advise!”", "반대하고 충고하기 좋아하는 양반일세!\""], ["exclaimed Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“You wish you were going yourself?", "\"자네가 직접 가고 싶다고?"], ["And you a Frenchman born?", "프랑스 태생인 자네가?"], ["You are a wise counsellor.”", "자네야말로 현명한 조언자로구먼.\""]], [["“My dear Mr. Lorry, it is because I am a Frenchman born, that the thought (which I did not mean to utter here, however) has passed through my mind often.", "\"이보세요 로리 씨, 바로 제가 프랑스 태생이기 때문에 그 생각이(물론 여기서 입 밖에 낼 생각은 없었습니다만) 종종 머릿속을 스쳐갔던 겁니다."], ["One cannot help thinking, having had some sympathy for the miserable people, and having abandoned something to them,”", "저 비참한 사람들에게 얼마간 연민을 느꼈고, 그들에게 무언가를 내려놓은 사람으로서,\""], ["he spoke here in his former thoughtful manner, “that one might be listened to, and might have the power to persuade to some restraint.", "그는 여기서 예전의 사색적인 태도로 돌아가 말했다, \"내가 가면 사람들이 귀를 기울여줄지도 모르고, 어느 정도 자제하도록 설득할 힘이 내게 있을지도 모른다는 생각을 하지 않을 수 없지요."], ["Only last night, after you had left us, when I was talking to Lucie--”", "바로 어젯밤에도, 어르신께서 우리 곁을 떠나신 뒤에 제가 루씨와 이야기하다가 —\""]], [["“When you were talking to Lucie,” Mr.", "\"자네가 루씨와 이야기하다가,\""], ["Lorry repeated. “Yes.", "로리 씨가 그 말을 되받았다."], ["I wonder you are not ashamed to mention the name of Lucie!", "\"그렇지. 자네가 루씨라는 이름을 입에 올리면서 부끄럽지 않다니 놀랍군!"], ["Wishing you were going to France at this time of day!”", "이 시국에 프랑스로 가고 싶다고 하다니!\""]], [["“However, I am not going,”", "\"하지만 저는 가지 않습니다,\""], ["said Charles Darnay, with a smile.", "찰스 다네이가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“It is more to the purpose that you say you are.”", "\"어르신께서 가신다고 하시는 게 더 중요하지요.\""]], [["“And I am, in plain reality.", "\"가고말고, 솔직히 말해 정말로 가네."], ["The truth is, my dear Charles,”", "사실은 말이지, 이보게 찰스,\""], ["Mr. Lorry glanced at the distant House, and lowered his voice, “you can have no conception of the difficulty with which our business is transacted, and of the peril in which our books and papers over yonder are involved.", "로리 씨는 멀리 있는 은행 쪽을 흘끗 보고는 목소리를 낮추었다, \"우리 업무가 얼마나 어렵게 처리되고 있는지, 또 저쪽에 있는 우리 장부와 서류가 얼마나 위태로운 처지에 놓여 있는지, 자네는 짐작도 못 할 걸세."], ["The Lord above knows what the compromising consequences would be to numbers of people, if some of our documents were seized or destroyed; and they might be, at any time, you know, for who can say that Paris is not set afire to-day, or sacked to-morrow!", "우리 문서 가운데 일부라도 압수되거나 파기된다면 수많은 사람들에게 얼마나 위험한 결과가 닥칠지는 하느님만이 아실 일이야. 게다가 그런 일은 언제든 일어날 수 있어, 알다시피, 빠리가 오늘 불바다가 되지 않으리라고, 내일 약탈당하지 않으리라고 누가 장담하겠나!"], ["Now, a judicious selection from these with the least possible delay, and the burying of them, or otherwise getting of them out of harm’s way, is within the power (without loss of precious time) of scarcely any one but myself, if any one.", "이제 그것들 가운데서 가능한 한 지체 없이 신중하게 골라내어 묻어버리든가, 아니면 다른 방법으로 화를 면하게 하는 일은 (귀중한 시간을 허비하지 않고서) 나 말고는 거의 아무도, 있다 한들 거의 아무도 해낼 수 없는 일이야."], ["And shall I hang back, when Tellson’s knows this and says this--Tellson’s, whose bread I have eaten these sixty years--because I am a little stiff about the joints?", "뗄슨이 이 사실을 알고 이렇게 말하는데 — 내가 육십 년 동안 빵을 얻어먹은 뗄슨이 말일세 — 관절이 좀 뻣뻣하다고 해서 내가 뒷걸음질 쳐야겠나?"], ["Why, I am a boy, sir, to half a dozen old codgers here!”", "원, 여기 늙다리 영감 대여섯에 비하면 나는 애송이일세!\""]], [["“How I admire the gallantry of your youthful spirit, Mr. Lorry.”", "\"그 젊디젊은 기개가 어찌나 존경스러운지요, 로리 씨.\""]], [["“Tut!", "\"쯧!"], ["Nonsense, sir!--And, my dear Charles,”", "허튼소리 말게!— 그리고 이보게 찰스,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, glancing at the House again, “you are to remember, that getting things out of Paris at this present time, no matter what things, is next to an impossibility.", "로리 씨가 다시 은행 쪽을 흘끗 보며 말했다, \"기억해두게나, 지금 이 시국에 빠리에서 무언가를 빼내 오는 일은, 그게 무엇이든 거의 불가능에 가깝다는 걸 말일세."], ["Papers and precious matters were this very day brought to us here (I speak in strict confidence; it is not business-like to whisper it, even to you), by the strangest bearers you can imagine, every one of whom had his head hanging on by a single hair as he passed the Barriers.", "바로 오늘도 서류며 귀중품이 여기 우리에게 도착했는데(엄히 비밀로 하는 얘길세, 자네한테조차 귓속말로 흘리는 건 사업가답지 못한 일이지), 그걸 가져온 자들은 자네가 상상도 못 할 만큼 기이한 사람들이었고, 하나같이 관문을 지날 때 목이 머리카락 한 올에 달랑 매달린 처지였다네."], ["At another time, our parcels would come and go, as easily as in business-like Old England; but now, everything is stopped.”", "다른 때 같으면 우리 소포가 사업가다운 옛 잉글랜드에서처럼 손쉽게 오갔겠지만, 지금은 모든 게 막혀버렸어.\""]], [["“And do you really go to-night?”", "\"그래서 정말로 오늘 밤에 떠나십니까?\""]], [["“I really go to-night, for the case has become too pressing to admit of delay.”", "\"정말로 오늘 밤에 떠나네, 사정이 너무 다급해져서 지체할 수가 없게 되었으니.\""]], [["“And do you take no one with you?”", "\"그래서 아무도 데려가지 않으십니까?\""]], [["“All sorts of people have been proposed to me, but I will have nothing to say to any of them.", "\"온갖 사람을 추천받았지만, 그중 누구와도 상관하고 싶지 않네."], ["I intend to take Jerry.", "제리를 데려갈 작정이야."], ["Jerry has been my bodyguard on Sunday nights for a long time past and I am used to him.", "제리는 오랫동안 일요일 밤마다 내 경호원 노릇을 해왔으니 나도 그에게 익숙하지."], ["Nobody will suspect Jerry of being anything but an English bull-dog, or of having any design in his head but to fly at anybody who touches his master.”", "제리를 두고 영국 불도그가 아닌 다른 무엇이라고, 또는 제 주인을 건드리는 자에게 달려드는 것 말고 다른 속셈을 품고 있다고 의심할 사람은 아무도 없을 걸세.\""]], [["“I must say again that I heartily admire your gallantry and youthfulness.”", "\"다시 말씀드리지만, 어르신의 그 기개와 젊음에 진심으로 감탄합니다.\""]], [["“I must say again, nonsense, nonsense!", "\"다시 말하지만, 허튼소리, 허튼소리야!"], ["When I have executed this little commission, I shall, perhaps, accept Tellson’s proposal to retire and live at my ease.", "이 자그마한 임무를 끝내고 나면, 어쩌면 은퇴해서 편히 살라는 뗄슨의 제안을 받아들일지도 모르지."], ["Time enough, then, to think about growing old.”", "그때 가서 늙어가는 일을 생각해도 늦지 않아.\""]], [["This dialogue had taken place at Mr. Lorry’s usual desk, with Monseigneur swarming within a yard or two of it, boastful of what he would do to avenge himself on the rascal-people before long.", "이 대화는 로리 씨의 늘 쓰는 책상에서 오갔는데, 한두 야드 안쪽으로는 므슈 영주들이 떼 지어 들끓으며 머지않아 그 악당 같은 민중에게 어떻게 복수할 것인지를 큰소리로 떠벌리고 있었다."], ["It was too much the way of Monseigneur under his reverses as a refugee, and it was much too much the way of native British orthodoxy, to talk of this terrible Revolution as if it were the only harvest ever known under the skies that had not been sown--as if nothing had ever been done, or omitted to be done, that had led to it--as if observers of the wretched millions in France, and of the misused and perverted resources that should have made them prosperous, had not seen it inevitably coming, years before, and had not in plain words recorded what they saw.", "망명자로서 역경에 처한 므슈 영주들의 방식이란 너무도 한결같았고, 잉글랜드 토박이의 정통 보수주의의 방식이란 한층 더 한결같았으니, 이 끔찍한 혁명을 마치 하늘 아래 씨 뿌리지 않고도 거둔 유일한 수확이라도 되는 양 — 마치 그것을 초래한 어떤 일도 행해진 적 없고 빠뜨린 적도 없다는 양 — 마치 프랑스의 그 비참한 수백만 군중과, 그들을 번영케 했어야 마땅했으나 오용되고 망가진 자원을 지켜본 이들이 몇 해 전부터 그 일이 불가피하게 다가옴을 보지 못했고, 자신들이 본 바를 명백한 말로 기록해두지 않았다는 양 떠들어댔다."], ["Such vapouring, combined with the extravagant plots of Monseigneur for the restoration of a state of things that had utterly exhausted itself, and worn out Heaven and earth as well as itself, was hard to be endured without some remonstrance by any sane man who knew the truth.", "그런 허풍은, 이미 스스로 완전히 소진되어 하늘과 땅까지 자신과 함께 닳아버린 상태를 복원하겠다는 므슈 영주들의 터무니없는 음모와 뒤섞여, 진실을 아는 제정신의 사람이라면 누구든 얼마간 항변하지 않고서는 견디기 힘든 것이었다."], ["And it was such vapouring all about his ears, like a troublesome confusion of blood in his own head, added to a latent uneasiness in his mind, which had already made Charles Darnay restless, and which still kept him so.", "그리고 사방에서 귓전을 울리는 그런 허풍이, 마치 제 머릿속에서 피가 거슬러 솟구치는 듯한 성가신 혼란처럼, 마음속에 잠복해 있던 불안에 더해져, 찰스 다네이를 이미 안절부절못하게 만들었고 여전히 그를 그렇게 붙들어두고 있었다."]], [["Among the talkers, was Stryver, of the King’s Bench Bar, far on his way to state promotion, and, therefore, loud on the theme: broaching to Monseigneur, his devices for blowing the people up and exterminating them from the face of the earth, and doing without them: and for accomplishing many similar objects akin in their nature to the abolition of eagles by sprinkling salt on the tails of the race.", "떠드는 무리 가운데에는 왕좌 법정 변호사인 스트라이버가 있었는데, 그는 국가의 요직으로 한참 출세 가도를 달리고 있던 터라 이 주제에 대해 목청을 높였다. 그는 므슈 영주들에게 민중을 폭파시켜 지상에서 절멸시키고 그들 없이 해나갈 자신의 방안을, 그리고 독수리 떼의 꼬리에 소금을 뿌려 그 종족을 없애겠다는 발상과 본질적으로 비슷한 여러 목표를 이룰 방안을 늘어놓았다."], ["Him, Darnay heard with a particular feeling of objection; and Darnay stood divided between going away that he might hear no more, and remaining to interpose his word, when the thing that was to be, went on to shape itself out.", "다네이는 특별한 거부감을 느끼며 그의 말을 들었다. 그러고는 더 듣지 않으려 자리를 떠야 할지, 아니면 남아서 한마디 끼어들어야 할지 갈피를 못 잡고 서 있는데, 마침 일어나기로 되어 있던 그 일이 점차 모습을 갖추어 나갔다."]], [["The House approached Mr. Lorry, and laying a soiled and unopened letter before him, asked if he had yet discovered any traces of the person to whom it was addressed?", "은행 측이 로리 씨에게 다가와 더럽혀진 채 개봉되지 않은 편지 한 통을 그 앞에 내려놓으며, 수신인의 행방에 대해 아직 어떤 단서도 찾지 못했는지 물었다."], ["The House laid the letter down so close to Darnay that he saw the direction--the more quickly because it was his own right name.", "은행 측이 편지를 다네이 바로 가까이에 내려놓았기에 그는 수신처를 보았는데 — 그것이 바로 자신의 본명이었던 까닭에 더욱 재빨리 알아보았다."], ["The address, turned into English, ran:", "그 주소를 영어로 옮기면 이러했다."]], [["“Very pressing.", "\"매우 긴급함."], ["To Monsieur heretofore the Marquis St. Evrémonde, of France.", "프랑스의 전(前) 후작 므슈 쌩떼브레몽드 귀하."], ["Confided to the cares of Messrs. Tellson and Co., Bankers, London, England.”", "잉글랜드 런던 소재 뗄슨 상회 은행가 제씨에게 위탁함.\""]], [["On the marriage morning, Doctor Manette had made it his one urgent and express request to Charles Darnay, that the secret of this name should be--unless he, the Doctor, dissolved the obligation--kept inviolate between them.", "결혼식 날 아침, 마네뜨 의사는 찰스 다네이에게, 자신 즉 의사가 그 의무를 풀어주지 않는 한, 이 이름의 비밀을 두 사람 사이에서 절대로 지켜달라는 것을 단 하나의 절박하고 명백한 요청으로 삼았다. 그것이 그의 이름임을 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["Nobody else knew it to be his name; his own wife had no suspicion of the fact; Mr. Lorry could have none.", "그의 아내조차 그 사실을 의심하지 못했고, 로리 씨로서도 알 도리가 없었다."]], [["“No,” said Mr.", "\"못 찾았네,\""], ["Lorry, in reply to the House; “I have referred it, I think, to everybody now here, and no one can tell me where this gentleman is to be found.”", "로리 씨가 은행 측에 답하여 말했다. \"지금 여기 있는 모든 사람에게 물어본 것 같은데, 이 신사를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 아무도 말해주지 못하는군.\""]], [["The hands of the clock verging upon the hour of closing the Bank, there was a general set of the current of talkers past Mr.", "은행 마감 시각이 다가오면서 시곗바늘이 그 시각에 가까워지자, 떠들던 무리의 흐름이 대체로 로리 씨의 책상 곁을 지나갔다."], ["Lorry’s desk. He held the letter out inquiringly; and Monseigneur looked at it, in the person of this plotting and indignant refugee; and Monseigneur looked at it in the person of that plotting and indignant refugee; and This, That, and The Other, all had something disparaging to say, in French or in English, concerning the Marquis who was not to be found.", "그가 묻듯이 편지를 들어 보이자, 음모를 꾸미며 분개하는 이 망명자의 모습으로 므슈 영주가 그것을 들여다보았고, 음모를 꾸미며 분개하는 저 망명자의 모습으로 또 므슈 영주가 그것을 들여다보았으며, 이 사람 저 사람 또 다른 사람 할 것 없이 모두가 행방을 알 수 없는 그 후작에 관하여 프랑스어로든 영어로든 무언가 헐뜯을 말을 했다."]], [["“Nephew, I believe--but in any case degenerate successor--of the polished Marquis who was murdered,”", "\"내 짐작에 그 조카지 — 어쨌든 타락한 후계자야 — 살해당한 그 세련된 후작의 말일세,\" 한 사람이 말했다."], ["said one. “Happy to say, I never knew him.”", "\"다행스럽게도 나는 그를 알지 못했다네.\""]], [["“A craven who abandoned his post,”", "\"제 자리를 내팽개친 겁쟁이지,\""], ["said another--this Monseigneur had been got out of Paris, legs uppermost and half suffocated, in a load of hay--“some years ago.”", "또 한 사람이 말했다 — 이 므슈 영주는 다리를 위로 향한 채 반쯤 숨이 막혀가며 건초 더미에 실려 몇 해 전 빠리를 빠져나온 자였다 — \"몇 해 전 일이지.\""]], [["“Infected with the new doctrines,”", "\"새로운 사상에 물든 자야,\""], ["said a third, eyeing the direction through his glass in passing; “set himself in opposition to the last Marquis, abandoned the estates when he inherited them, and left them to the ruffian herd.", "지나가며 외알 안경 너머로 그 수신처를 훑어본 세 번째 사람이 말했다. \"선대 후작에게 맞서 들고일어났고, 영지를 물려받자 그것을 내팽개쳐 그 불한당 무리에게 넘겨버렸지."], ["They will recompense him now, I hope, as he deserves.”", "이제 그들이 그가 받아 마땅한 대로 보답해주기를 바라네.\""]], [["“Hey?”", "\"엥?\""], ["cried the blatant Stryver.", "떠벌이 스트라이버가 외쳤다."], ["“Did he though?", "\"정말 그랬다고?"], ["Is that the sort of fellow?", "그런 부류의 작자란 말이지?"], ["Let us look at his infamous name.", "그 더러운 이름이나 한번 봅시다."], ["D--n the fellow!”", "빌어먹을 작자 같으니!\""]], [["Darnay, unable to restrain himself any longer, touched Mr. Stryver on the shoulder, and said:", "더는 자제할 수 없었던 다네이가 스트라이버 씨의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“I know the fellow.”", "\"나는 그 사람을 압니다.\""]], [["“Do you, by Jupiter?”", "\"이런, 자네가? 정말로?\""], ["said Stryver.", "스트라이버가 말했다."], ["“I am sorry for it.”", "\"안됐구먼.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Why, Mr. Darnay?", "\"어째서라니요, 다네이 씨?"], ["D’ye hear what he did?", "그가 무슨 짓을 했는지 못 들으셨소?"], ["Don’t ask, why, in these times.” “But I do ask why?”", "이런 시국에 어째서냐고 묻지 마시오.\""]], [["“Then I tell you again, Mr. Darnay, I am sorry for it.", "\"하지만 저는 어째서냐고 묻습니다.\""], ["I am sorry to hear you putting any such extraordinary questions.", "\"그렇다면 다시 말하지요, 다네이 씨, 안됐습니다. 그런 별난 질문을 던지는 걸 들으니 안타깝군요."], ["Here is a fellow, who, infected by the most pestilent and blasphemous code of devilry that ever was known, abandoned his property to the vilest scum of the earth that ever did murder by wholesale, and you ask me why I am sorry that a man who instructs youth knows him?", "여기 한 작자가 있는데, 일찍이 알려진 가장 흉악하고 신성모독적인 악마의 율법에 물들어, 일찍이 닥치는 대로 살인을 저지른 지상에서 가장 비열한 쓰레기들에게 제 재산을 내던져버렸소. 그런데 젊은이들을 가르치는 사람이 그런 작자를 안다는데 내가 어찌 안타까워하느냐고 묻는 거요?"], ["Well, but I’ll answer you.", "좋소, 답해드리지요."], ["I am sorry because I believe there is contamination in such a scoundrel.", "그런 악당에게는 오염이 깃들어 있다고 믿기에 안타까운 거요."], ["That’s why.”", "그래서요.\""]], [["Mindful of the secret, Darnay with great difficulty checked himself, and said: “You may not understand the gentleman.”", "비밀을 의식한 다네이는 무진 애를 써서 자제하며 말했다. \"그 신사를 잘못 이해하셨을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["“I understand how to put you in a corner, Mr.", "\"내가 잘 아는 건 당신을 궁지로 몰아넣는 방법이오, 다네이 씨,\""], ["Darnay,” said Bully Stryver, “and I’ll do it.", "으름장꾼 스트라이버가 말했다, \"그리고 그렇게 할 작정이오."], ["If this fellow is a gentleman, I don’t understand him.", "그 작자가 신사라면 나는 그를 이해하지 못하는 거요."], ["You may tell him so, with my compliments.", "내 인사와 함께 그에게 그리 전하시구려."], ["You may also tell him, from me, that after abandoning his worldly goods and position to this butcherly mob, I wonder he is not at the head of them.", "또 내가 하는 말이라고 전하시오, 그 백정 같은 폭도들에게 제 재산과 지위를 내던져놓고서도 정작 그들의 우두머리 자리에 앉아 있지 않다니 의아하다고 말이오."], ["But, no, gentlemen,”", "하지만 천만에요, 여러분,\""], ["said Stryver, looking all round, and snapping his fingers, “I know something of human nature, and I tell you that you’ll never find a fellow like this fellow, trusting himself to the mercies of such precious protégés.", "스트라이버가 사방을 둘러보며 손가락을 탁 튀기고는 말했다, \"나는 인간 본성을 좀 아는데, 단언컨대 여러분은 그런 귀하신 피보호자들의 자비에 자기 몸을 내맡기는 그런 작자는 결코 보지 못할 거요."], ["No, gentlemen; he’ll always show ’em a clean pair of heels very early in the scuffle, and sneak away.”", "천만에요, 여러분. 그런 작자는 싸움이 벌어지면 늘 아주 일찌감치 줄행랑을 쳐서 슬그머니 빠져나갈 겁니다.\""]], [["With those words, and a final snap of his fingers, Mr. Stryver shouldered himself into Fleet-street, amidst the general approbation of his hearers.", "그 말과 함께 마지막으로 손가락을 탁 튀기고는, 스트라이버 씨는 청중의 전반적인 갈채를 받으며 어깨로 사람들을 헤치고 플리트 스트리트로 나섰다."], ["Mr. Lorry and Charles Darnay were left alone at the desk, in the general departure from the Bank.", "사람들이 모두 은행을 빠져나가는 가운데 로리 씨와 찰스 다네이만이 책상에 남았다."]], [["“Will you take charge of the letter?”", "\"그 편지를 맡아주겠나?\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“You know where to deliver it?”", "\"어디로 전달할지 알지?\""]], [["“I do.”", "\"압니다.\""]], [["“Will you undertake to explain, that we suppose it to have been addressed here, on the chance of our knowing where to forward it, and that it has been here some time?”", "\"우리가 전달할 곳을 알지도 모른다는 짐작에 이리로 부쳐온 것이라 여긴다는 점과, 그것이 여기 온 지 얼마쯤 되었다는 점을 설명해주겠나?\""]], [["“I will do so.", "\"그렇게 하지요."], ["Do you start for Paris from here?”", "빠리로는 여기서 출발하십니까?\""]], [["“From here, at eight.”", "\"여기서, 여덟 시에.\""]], [["“I will come back, to see you off.”", "\"제가 다시 와서 배웅해 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["Very ill at ease with himself, and with Stryver and most other men, Darnay made the best of his way into the quiet of the Temple, opened the letter, and read it.", "자신에 대해서도, 또 스트라이버며 다른 대부분의 사람들에 대해서도 몹시 언짢아진 다네이는 되도록 빨리 템플의 고요 속으로 들어가 편지를 뜯어 읽었다."], ["These were its contents:", "그 내용은 이러했다."]], [["“Prison of the Abbaye, Paris.", "\"빠리 아베이 감옥에서."]], [["“After having long been in danger of my life at the hands of the village, I have been seized, with great violence and indignity, and brought a long journey on foot to Paris.", "\"오랫동안 마을 사람들의 손에 목숨을 위협받아오다가, 저는 극심한 폭력과 모욕을 당하며 붙잡혀, 빠리까지 먼 길을 걸어서 끌려왔습니다."], ["On the road I have suffered a great deal.", "도중에 저는 무척이나 고초를 겪었습니다."], ["Nor is that all; my house has been destroyed--razed to the ground.", "그뿐이 아닙니다. 제 집은 파괴되었으니 — 땅바닥까지 헐려버렸습니다."]], [["“The crime for which I am imprisoned, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, and for which I shall be summoned before the tribunal, and shall lose my life (without your so generous help), is, they tell me, treason against the majesty of the people, in that I have acted against them for an emigrant.", "\"제가 갇힌 죄목, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 그리고 그 죄목으로 제가 법정에 소환되어 (당신의 그토록 너그러운 도움 없이는) 목숨을 잃게 될 그 죄목은, 그들의 말로는, 민중의 존엄에 대한 반역이라고 합니다. 제가 한 망명자를 위하여 민중에게 맞서 행동했다는 것이지요."], ["It is in vain I represent that I have acted for them, and not against, according to your commands.", "제가 당신의 명령에 따라 민중에게 맞선 것이 아니라 그들을 위하여 행동했다고 항변해도 소용없습니다."], ["It is in vain I represent that, before the sequestration of emigrant property, I had remitted the imposts they had ceased to pay; that I had collected no rent; that I had had recourse to no process.", "망명자 재산이 몰수되기 전에, 제가 그들이 더는 내지 못하게 된 부과금을 면제해주었고, 소작료를 한 푼도 거두지 않았으며, 어떤 법적 절차에도 호소하지 않았다고 항변해도 소용없습니다."], ["The only response is, that I have acted for an emigrant, and where is that emigrant?", "돌아오는 대답은 오직, 내가 한 망명자를 위해 행동했다는 것, 그리고 그 망명자는 어디 있느냐는 것뿐입니다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["most gracious Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, where is that emigrant?", "너무도 자비로우신 전(前) 후작 므슈, 그 망명자는 어디 있습니까?"], ["I cry in my sleep where is he?", "저는 잠결에도 그가 어디 있느냐고 부르짖습니다."], ["I demand of Heaven, will he not come to deliver me? No answer.", "하늘에 따져 묻습니다, 그가 와서 나를 구해주지 않으려는가?"], ["Ah Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, I send my desolate cry across the sea, hoping it may perhaps reach your ears through the great bank of Tilson known at Paris!", "아무 대답이 없습니다. 아 전(前) 후작 므슈, 저는 이 황량한 부르짖음을 바다 건너로 보내며, 빠리에 알려진 저 거대한 틸슨 은행을 통해 어쩌면 그것이 당신의 귀에 가닿기를 바랍니다!"]], [["“For the love of Heaven, of justice, of generosity, of the honour of your noble name, I supplicate you, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, to succour and release me.", "\"하늘에 대한 사랑으로, 정의에 대한 사랑으로, 너그러움에 대한 사랑으로, 당신의 고귀한 이름의 명예에 대한 사랑으로, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 간청합니다, 저를 구원하여 풀어주십시오."], ["My fault is, that I have been true to you.", "제 잘못은 당신께 충실했다는 것입니다."], ["Oh Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, I pray you be you true to me!", "오 전(前) 후작 므슈, 부디 당신도 제게 충실해 주시기를 빕니다!"]], [["“From this prison here of horror, whence I every hour tend nearer and nearer to destruction, I send you, Monsieur heretofore the Marquis, the assurance of my dolorous and unhappy service.", "\"매시간 점점 더 파멸에 가까워지는 이 공포의 감옥에서, 전(前) 후작 므슈, 저는 당신께 비통하고 불행한 저의 충정을 다짐드립니다."]], [["“Your afflicted,", "\"고통받는 당신의 사람,"]], [["“Gabelle.”", "\"가벨.\""]], [["The latent uneasiness in Darnay’s mind was roused to vigourous life by this letter.", "다네이의 마음속에 잠복해 있던 불안이 이 편지로 말미암아 격렬하게 되살아났다."], ["The peril of an old servant and a good one, whose only crime was fidelity to himself and his family, stared him so reproachfully in the face, that, as he walked to and fro in the Temple considering what to do, he almost hid his face from the passersby.", "그를 향한, 그리고 그 가문을 향한 충성 말고는 아무 죄도 없는 한 늙은 하인, 그것도 충직한 하인이 처한 위험이 너무도 책망하듯 그의 얼굴을 정면으로 노려보았기에, 그는 어찌해야 할지 궁리하며 템플 안을 이리저리 거니는 동안 지나가는 사람들에게서 거의 얼굴을 가리다시피 했다."]], [["He knew very well, that in his horror of the deed which had culminated the bad deeds and bad reputation of the old family house, in his resentful suspicions of his uncle, and in the aversion with which his conscience regarded the crumbling fabric that he was supposed to uphold, he had acted imperfectly.", "그는 잘 알고 있었다. 그 오래된 가문 저택의 악행과 악명을 정점으로 끌어올린 그 행위에 대한 공포 속에서, 또 숙부에 대한 원망 섞인 의혹 속에서, 또 자신이 지탱하기로 되어 있던 그 무너져가는 구조물을 양심이 혐오스럽게 바라보던 가운데, 자신이 불완전하게 처신했음을 말이다."], ["He knew very well, that in his love for Lucie, his renunciation of his social place, though by no means new to his own mind, had been hurried and incomplete.", "그는 잘 알고 있었다. 루씨에 대한 사랑 속에서, 자신의 사회적 지위를 포기한 일이, 비록 그것이 자기 마음속에서 결코 새로운 것은 아니었다 해도, 서두른 채 미완으로 남았음을 말이다."], ["He knew that he ought to have systematically worked it out and supervised it, and that he had meant to do it, and that it had never been done.", "그는 알고 있었다, 그것을 체계적으로 마무리하고 관리했어야 했고, 또 그러려고 마음먹었으면서도 끝내 그렇게 하지 못했음을 말이다."]], [["The happiness of his own chosen English home, the necessity of being always actively employed, the swift changes and troubles of the time which had followed on one another so fast, that the events of this week annihilated the immature plans of last week, and the events of the week following made all new again; he knew very well, that to the force of these circumstances he had yielded:--not without disquiet, but still without continuous and accumulating resistance.", "스스로 택한 잉글랜드 가정의 행복, 늘 활발하게 일에 매여 있어야 할 필요, 그리고 너무도 빠르게 잇따라 닥쳐서 이번 주의 사건이 지난주의 설익은 계획을 무너뜨리고 그다음 주의 사건이 모든 것을 다시 새롭게 만들어버리던 시대의 급격한 변화와 혼란 — 그는 잘 알고 있었다, 자신이 이러한 정황의 힘에 굴복했음을, 불안이 없지는 않았으되 그래도 끈질기게 쌓여가는 저항 없이 그리했음을 말이다."], ["That he had watched the times for a time of action, and that they had shifted and struggled until the time had gone by, and the nobility were trooping from France by every highway and byway, and their property was in course of confiscation and destruction, and their very names were blotting out, was as well known to himself as it could be to any new authority in France that might impeach him for it.", "그가 행동에 나설 때를 기다리며 시국을 지켜보는 동안, 시국은 자꾸만 변하고 요동치다가 마침내 때가 지나가 버렸고, 귀족들은 큰길로 샛길로 떼 지어 프랑스를 떠나고 있었으며, 그들의 재산은 몰수와 파괴의 과정에 있었고, 그들의 이름조차 지워지고 있었으니 — 이 사실은, 그 일로 자신을 탄핵할지 모를 프랑스의 어떤 새로운 권력만큼이나 그 자신도 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["But, he had oppressed no man, he had imprisoned no man; he was so far from having harshly exacted payment of his dues, that he had relinquished them of his own will, thrown himself on a world with no favour in it, won his own private place there, and earned his own bread.", "그러나 그는 누구도 억압한 적 없었고, 누구도 가둔 적 없었다. 자기 몫의 대가를 가혹하게 거두기는커녕, 그것을 제 의지로 내려놓고서, 호의라곤 없는 세상에 자신을 내던져 그곳에서 자기만의 자리를 일구고 제 손으로 빵을 벌어왔다."], ["Monsieur Gabelle had held the impoverished and involved estate on written instructions, to spare the people, to give them what little there was to give--such fuel as the heavy creditors would let them have in the winter, and such produce as could be saved from the same grip in the summer--and no doubt he had put the fact in plea and proof, for his own safety, so that it could not but appear now.", "가벨 므슈는 궁핍하고 얽혀버린 영지를, 민중을 아끼고 줄 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 것이나마 — 겨울에 빚 독촉이 심한 채권자들이 그들에게 남겨줄 만한 땔감과, 여름에 그 같은 손아귀에서 건져낼 수 있는 약간의 소출을 — 그들에게 내어주라는 서면 지시에 따라 관리해왔으니, 의심할 바 없이 그는 제 안전을 위해 그 사실을 항변과 증거로 제시해두었을 터, 그러니 지금 그것이 드러나지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["This favoured the desperate resolution Charles Darnay had begun to make, that he would go to Paris.", "이러한 사정이, 찰스 다네이가 굳히기 시작한 필사적인 결심, 곧 빠리로 가겠다는 결심을 부추겼다."]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["Like the mariner in the old story, the winds and streams had driven him within the influence of the Loadstone Rock, and it was drawing him to itself, and he must go.", "옛이야기 속의 그 뱃사람처럼, 바람과 물살이 그를 자석 바위*의 영향권 안으로 몰아넣었고, 그것은 그를 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었으니, 그는 가야만 했다."], ["Everything that arose before his mind drifted him on, faster and faster, more and more steadily, to the terrible attraction.", "그의 마음속에 떠오르는 모든 것이 그를, 점점 더 빠르게, 점점 더 한결같이, 그 무시무시한 인력 쪽으로 떠밀었다."], ["His latent uneasiness had been, that bad aims were being worked out in his own unhappy land by bad instruments, and that he who could not fail to know that he was better than they, was not there, trying to do something to stay bloodshed, and assert the claims of mercy and humanity.", "그의 잠복한 불안은, 사악한 도구들에 의해 사악한 목적이 그의 불행한 조국에서 이루어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 자신이 그들보다 낫다는 것을 모를 리 없는 그가 정작 그곳에 가지 않고 있다는 것, 유혈을 막고 자비와 인류애의 주장을 내세우려 무언가 애써보지 않고 있다는 것이었다."], ["With this uneasiness half stifled, and half reproaching him, he had been brought to the pointed comparison of himself with the brave old gentleman in whom duty was so strong; upon that comparison (injurious to himself) had instantly followed the sneers of Monseigneur, which had stung him bitterly, and those of Stryver, which above all were coarse and galling, for old reasons.", "반쯤 억눌리고 반쯤 그를 책망하는 이 불안과 더불어, 그는 의무감이 그토록 강했던 저 용감한 노신사와 자신을 날카롭게 견주는 지경에 이르렀다. 그리고 (자신에게 불리한) 그 비교에 곧바로 므슈 영주들의 비웃음이 뒤따라 그를 쓰라리게 찔렀고, 무엇보다도 거칠고 속을 긁는, 오래된 이유에서 비롯된 스트라이버의 비웃음이 뒤따랐다."], ["Upon those, had followed Gabelle’s letter: the appeal of an innocent prisoner, in danger of death, to his justice, honour, and good name.", "그러한 것들에 가벨의 편지가 뒤따랐으니 — 죽음의 위험에 처한 무고한 죄수가 그의 정의와 명예와 명성에 호소하는 외침이었다. * 자석 바위: 옛 항해 전설에서 지나가는 배의 쇠붙이를 끌어당겨 난파시킨다고 전해지는 바위."]], [["His resolution was made.", "결심은 섰다."], ["He must go to Paris.", "그는 빠리로 가야 했다."]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["The Loadstone Rock was drawing him, and he must sail on, until he struck.", "자석 바위가 그를 끌어당기고 있었으니, 그는 부딪칠 때까지 항해해 나아가야 했다."], ["He knew of no rock; he saw hardly any danger.", "그는 어떤 바위도 알지 못했고, 어떤 위험도 거의 보지 못했다."], ["The intention with which he had done what he had done, even although he had left it incomplete, presented it before him in an aspect that would be gratefully acknowledged in France on his presenting himself to assert it.", "비록 미완으로 남겼다 해도, 그가 행한 일에 담겼던 그 의도는, 자신이 직접 나서서 그것을 내세운다면 프랑스에서 감사히 인정받으리라는 모습으로 그의 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["Then, that glorious vision of doing good, which is so often the sanguine mirage of so many good minds, arose before him, and he even saw himself in the illusion with some influence to guide this raging Revolution that was running so fearfully wild.", "그러자, 선한 일을 한다는 그 영광스러운 환영 — 그토록 많은 선한 마음들에게 그토록 자주 찾아드는 낙관의 신기루 — 이 그의 앞에 떠올랐고, 그는 그 환상 속에서, 그토록 무섭게 미쳐 날뛰는 이 광란의 혁명을 이끌어갈 얼마간의 영향력을 지닌 자기 자신의 모습마저 보았다."]], [["As he walked to and fro with his resolution made, he considered that neither Lucie nor her father must know of it until he was gone.", "결심을 굳힌 채 이리저리 거니는 동안, 그는 자신이 떠나기 전까지는 루씨도 그녀의 아버지도 이 일을 알아서는 안 된다고 생각했다."], ["Lucie should be spared the pain of separation; and her father, always reluctant to turn his thoughts towards the dangerous ground of old, should come to the knowledge of the step, as a step taken, and not in the balance of suspense and doubt.", "루씨에게는 이별의 고통을 면하게 해주어야 했고, 늘 위태로운 옛 기억의 영역으로 생각을 돌리기를 꺼리던 그녀의 아버지에게는, 이 결행이 망설임과 의심 속에 저울질되는 것이 아니라 이미 내디뎌진 한 걸음으로서 알려져야 했다."], ["How much of the incompleteness of his situation was referable to her father, through the painful anxiety to avoid reviving old associations of France in his mind, he did not discuss with himself.", "자신이 처한 상황의 미완성이 얼마나 그녀의 아버지에게서, 곧 프랑스의 옛 기억을 그의 마음속에 되살리지 않으려는 고통스러운 염려에서 비롯되었는지를 그는 스스로 따져 묻지 않았다."], ["But, that circumstance too, had had its influence in his course.", "그러나 그 정황 역시 그의 행로에 나름의 영향을 미쳤다."]], [["He walked to and fro, with thoughts very busy, until it was time to return to Tellson’s and take leave of Mr. Lorry.", "그는 갖가지 생각으로 머릿속이 분주한 채, 뗄슨으로 돌아가 로리 씨에게 작별을 고할 시간이 될 때까지 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["As soon as he arrived in Paris he would present himself to this old friend, but he must say nothing of his intention now.", "빠리에 도착하는 대로 이 옛 친구를 찾아갈 작정이었으나, 지금은 자신의 의도에 대해 아무 말도 해서는 안 되었다."]], [["A carriage with post-horses was ready at the Bank door, and Jerry was booted and equipped.", "역마를 맨 마차 한 대가 은행 문 앞에 대기하고 있었고, 제리는 장화를 신고 채비를 갖추고 있었다."]], [["“I have delivered that letter,”", "\"그 편지를 전달했습니다,\""], ["said Charles Darnay to Mr. Lorry.", "찰스 다네이가 로리 씨에게 말했다."], ["“I would not consent to your being charged with any written answer, but perhaps you will take a verbal one?”", "\"어르신께서 서면 답장을 떠맡으시는 데는 동의할 수 없습니다만, 어쩌면 구두로 한마디 전해주실 수는 있겠지요?\""]], [["“That I will, and readily,”", "\"그러고말고, 기꺼이,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, “if it is not dangerous.”", "로리 씨가 말했다, \"위험한 일만 아니라면 말일세.\""]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀 위험하지 않습니다."], ["Though it is to a prisoner in the Abbaye.”", "비록 아베이 감옥에 갇힌 죄수에게 보내는 것이긴 합니다만.\""]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"이름이 뭔가?\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, with his open pocket-book in his hand.", "수첩을 펼쳐 든 채 로리 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Gabelle.”", "\"가벨입니다.\""]], [["“Gabelle.", "\"가벨이라."], ["And what is the message to the unfortunate Gabelle in prison?”", "그래, 감옥에 갇힌 그 가엾은 가벨에게 전할 말이 무엇인가?\""]], [["“Simply, ‘that he has received the letter, and will come.’”", "\"그저, '편지를 받았으며, 가겠다'고만요.\""]], [["“Any time mentioned?”", "\"시일은 언급할까?\""]], [["“He will start upon his journey to-morrow night.”", "\"내일 밤에 여정에 오를 겁니다.\""]], [["“Any person mentioned?”", "\"누구 이름은 언급할까?\""]], [["He helped Mr.", "\"아니요.\""], ["Lorry to wrap himself in a number of coats and cloaks, and went out with him from the warm atmosphere of the old Bank, into the misty air of Fleet-street.", "그는 로리 씨가 외투와 망토 여러 벌로 몸을 감싸는 것을 거들고는, 오래된 은행의 따뜻한 공기 속에서 그와 함께 나와 안개 낀 플리트 스트리트로 들어섰다."], ["“My love to Lucie, and to little Lucie,”", "\"루씨와 어린 루씨에게 내 사랑을 전해주게,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry at parting, “and take precious care of them till I come back.”", "헤어지며 로리 씨가 말했다, \"그리고 내가 돌아올 때까지 그들을 소중히 지켜주게나.\""], ["Charles Darnay shook his head and doubtfully smiled, as the carriage rolled away.", "마차가 굴러 멀어져가는 가운데, 찰스 다네이는 고개를 가로저으며 미심쩍게 미소 지었다."]], [["That night--it was the fourteenth of August--he sat up late, and wrote two fervent letters; one was to Lucie, explaining the strong obligation he was under to go to Paris, and showing her, at length, the reasons that he had, for feeling confident that he could become involved in no personal danger there; the other was to the Doctor, confiding Lucie and their dear child to his care, and dwelling on the same topics with the strongest assurances.", "그날 밤 — 8월 14일이었다 — 그는 늦도록 깨어 앉아 간곡한 편지 두 통을 썼다. 하나는 루씨에게 쓴 것으로, 자신이 빠리로 가야 하는 강한 의무를 설명하고, 그곳에서 자신이 어떤 개인적 위험에도 휘말리지 않으리라 확신하는 이유를 그녀에게 상세히 밝혔다."], ["To both, he wrote that he would despatch letters in proof of his safety, immediately after his arrival.", "다른 하나는 의사에게 쓴 것으로, 루씨와 그들의 사랑하는 아이를 그의 보살핌에 맡기며, 같은 주제들을 더없이 강한 다짐과 함께 거듭 짚었다. 두 사람 모두에게, 그는 도착하는 즉시 자신의 안전을 입증할 편지를 부치겠노라고 적었다."]], [["It was a hard day, that day of being among them, with the first reservation of their joint lives on his mind.", "그날, 그들 공동의 삶에서 처음으로 숨기는 것을 마음에 품은 채 그들 곁에 있던 그날은 힘겨운 하루였다."], ["It was a hard matter to preserve the innocent deceit of which they were profoundly unsuspicious.", "그들이 도무지 의심하지 못하는 그 순결한 기만을 유지하기란 힘든 일이었다."], ["But, an affectionate glance at his wife, so happy and busy, made him resolute not to tell her what impended (he had been half moved to do it, so strange it was to him to act in anything without her quiet aid), and the day passed quickly.", "그러나 그토록 행복하고 분주한 아내를 다정하게 한 번 바라보자, 그는 닥쳐오는 일을 그녀에게 말하지 않으리라 굳게 마음먹었고(차분한 그녀의 도움 없이 무엇이든 행한다는 것이 그에게는 너무도 낯선 일이라, 반쯤은 털어놓을 마음이 동했던 터였다), 그리하여 하루는 빠르게 지나갔다."], ["Early in the evening he embraced her, and her scarcely less dear namesake, pretending that he would return by-and-bye (an imaginary engagement took him out, and he had secreted a valise of clothes ready), and so he emerged into the heavy mist of the heavy streets, with a heavier heart.", "초저녁에 그는 아내를, 그리고 못지않게 사랑스러운 그 이름을 물려받은 아이를 끌어안으며, 곧 돌아오겠노라고 둘러댔고(가공의 약속을 핑계로 밖으로 나섰으며, 옷가지를 챙긴 여행 가방을 미리 숨겨두었다), 그리하여 한층 무거운 마음으로 무겁게 가라앉은 거리의 짙은 안개 속으로 나아갔다."]], [["The unseen force was drawing him fast to itself, now, and all the tides and winds were setting straight and strong towards it.", "보이지 않는 힘이 이제 그를 빠르게 제 쪽으로 끌어당기고 있었고, 모든 조류와 바람이 곧장 거세게 그쪽으로 향하고 있었다."], ["He left his two letters with a trusty porter, to be delivered half an hour before midnight, and no sooner; took horse for Dover; and began his journey.", "그는 두 통의 편지를, 자정 삼십 분 전에, 그보다 일찍은 말고 전달하라며 믿을 만한 짐꾼에게 맡겼다. 그러고는 도버행 말에 올라 여정을 시작했다."], ["“For the love of Heaven, of justice, of generosity, of the honour of your noble name!”", "\"하늘에 대한 사랑으로, 정의에 대한 사랑으로, 너그러움에 대한 사랑으로, 당신의 고귀한 이름의 명예에 대한 사랑으로!\""], ["was the poor prisoner’s cry with which he strengthened his sinking heart, as he left all that was dear on earth behind him, and floated away for the Loadstone Rock. The end of the second book.", "— 이것은 그 가엾은 죄수의 부르짖음이었으니, 지상에서 소중한 모든 것을 등 뒤에 남겨두고 자석 바위를 향해 떠밀려 가는 동안, 그는 그 외침으로 가라앉는 마음을 다잡았다."]], [["# Book the Third--the Track of a Storm CHAPTER I. In Secret", "# Book the Third--the Track of a Storm CHAPTER I. In Secret"]], [["The traveller fared slowly on his way, who fared towards Paris from England in the autumn of the year one thousand seven hundred and ninety-two.", "천칠백구십이년 가을에 영국에서 빠리로 향하던 그 나그네는 더디게 길을 나아갔다."], ["More than enough of bad roads, bad equipages, and bad horses, he would have encountered to delay him, though the fallen and unfortunate King of France had been upon his throne in all his glory; but, the changed times were fraught with other obstacles than these.", "몰락하여 불운에 처한 프랑스 왕이 온갖 영화를 누리며 왕좌에 앉아 있었다 해도, 그는 가는 길을 더디게 만드는 험한 길과 형편없는 마차와 형편없는 말을 충분히, 아니 넘치도록 만났을 것이다. 그러나 변해버린 시절은 그런 것들 말고도 다른 장애물들로 가득 차 있었다."], ["Every town-gate and village taxing-house had its band of citizen-patriots, with their national muskets in a most explosive state of readiness, who stopped all comers and goers, cross-questioned them, inspected their papers, looked for their names in lists of their own, turned them back, or sent them on, or stopped them and laid them in hold, as their capricious judgment or fancy deemed best for the dawning Republic One and Indivisible, of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death.", "도시의 성문마다, 마을의 세관마다 시민 애국자 무리가 진을 치고 있었는데, 그들은 당장이라도 터질 듯한 준비태세를 갖춘 국민군 머스킷 총을 들고 오가는 모든 이를 멈춰 세우고, 캐묻고, 서류를 검사하고, 그들 나름의 명단에서 이름을 찾아보고는, 되돌려보내거나, 통과시키거나, 붙들어 가두었으니, 자유와 평등과 박애, 그렇지 않으면 죽음이라는, 막 동터오는 단일하고도 불가분한 공화국을 위해 그들의 변덕스러운 판단이나 기분에 가장 좋다고 여겨지는 대로 처분했다."]], [["A very few French leagues of his journey were accomplished, when Charles Darnay began to perceive that for him along these country roads there was no hope of return until he should have been declared a good citizen at Paris.", "여정 가운데 몇 프랑스 리외도 채 가기 전에, 샤를 다네는 이 시골길을 따라가는 자신에게는 빠리에서 선량한 시민으로 선언되기 전까지 돌아올 가망이 없다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."], ["Whatever might befall now, he must on to his journey’s end.", "이제 무슨 일이 닥치든 그는 여정의 끝까지 나아가야 했다."], ["Not a mean village closed upon him, not a common barrier dropped across the road behind him, but he knew it to be another iron door in the series that was barred between him and England.", "그의 등 뒤로 닫히는 하찮은 마을 하나하나가, 길 위로 내려지는 평범한 차단봉 하나하나가, 자신과 영국 사이에 빗장으로 잠긴 일련의 또다른 쇠문임을 그는 알았다."], ["The universal watchfulness so encompassed him, that if he had been taken in a net, or were being forwarded to his destination in a cage, he could not have felt his freedom more completely gone.", "어디서나 감시가 그를 에워싸고 있어, 그물에 사로잡혔거나 우리에 갇혀 목적지로 호송되는 신세라 해도 자신의 자유가 그토록 완전히 사라졌다고 느끼지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["This universal watchfulness not only stopped him on the highway twenty times in a stage, but retarded his progress twenty times in a day, by riding after him and taking him back, riding before him and stopping him by anticipation, riding with him and keeping him in charge.", "* 리외: 옛 프랑스의 거리 단위로 약 4킬로미터에 해당한다. 이 어디서나 펼쳐지는 감시는 한 구간에서 그를 스무 번씩 큰길 위에서 멈춰 세웠을 뿐 아니라, 하루에 스무 번씩 그의 행로를 더디게 했으니, 그의 뒤를 쫓아 와서 되돌려보내고, 앞질러 달려가 미리 막아서고, 함께 말을 달리며 그를 감시했다."], ["He had been days upon his journey in France alone, when he went to bed tired out, in a little town on the high road, still a long way from Paris.", "그는 프랑스에서만도 여러 날을 길에서 보냈고, 빠리까지는 아직 한참 먼 큰길가의 작은 마을에서 녹초가 된 채 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["Nothing but the production of the afflicted Gabelle’s letter from his prison of the Abbaye would have got him on so far.", "비탄에 잠긴 가벨이 아바이 감옥에서 보낸 편지를 내보이지 않았다면 그는 여기까지 결코 오지 못했을 것이다."], ["His difficulty at the guard-house in this small place had been such, that he felt his journey to have come to a crisis.", "이 작은 마을의 위병소에서 겪은 어려움이 어찌나 컸던지, 그는 자신의 여정이 고비에 다다랐다고 느꼈다."], ["And he was, therefore, as little surprised as a man could be, to find himself awakened at the small inn to which he had been remitted until morning, in the middle of the night.", "그러므로 아침까지 머물도록 보내진 그 작은 여인숙에서 한밤중에 잠을 깨우는 것을 발견했을 때도, 그는 사람으로서 더없이 놀라지 않는 만큼만 놀랐다."]], [["Awakened by a timid local functionary and three armed patriots in rough red caps and with pipes in their mouths, who sat down on the bed.", "소심한 그 고장 관리 하나와, 거친 붉은 모자를 쓰고 입에 파이프를 문 무장한 애국자 셋이 그를 깨웠는데, 그들은 침대 위에 걸터앉았다."]], [["“Emigrant,”", "\"이민자여,\""], ["said the functionary, “I am going to send you on to Paris, under an escort.”", "관리가 말했다. \"당신을 호송대를 붙여 빠리로 보내려 하오.\""]], [["“Citizen, I desire nothing more than to get to Paris, though I could dispense with the escort.”", "\"시민이여, 나는 빠리에 가는 것보다 더 바라는 것이 없소. 호송대는 없어도 되겠지만 말이오.\""]], [["“Silence!”", "\"닥쳐!\""], ["growled a red-cap, striking at the coverlet with the butt-end of his musket.", "붉은 모자 하나가 머스킷 총 개머리판으로 이불을 내리치며 으르렁거렸다."], ["“Peace, aristocrat!”", "\"조용히 해, 귀족 놈아!\""]], [["“It is as the good patriot says,”", "\"선량한 애국자가 하는 말 그대로요,\""], ["observed the timid functionary.", "소심한 관리가 말했다."], ["“You are an aristocrat, and must have an escort--and must pay for it.”", "\"당신은 귀족이니 호송대를 붙여야 하고, 그 값을 치러야 하오.\""]], [["“I have no choice,”", "\"내겐 선택의 여지가 없군요,\""], ["said Charles Darnay.", "샤를 다네가 말했다."]], [["“Choice!", "\"선택이라니!"], ["Listen to him!”", "저 말 좀 들어보게!\""], ["cried the same scowling red-cap.", "그 인상 쓰던 붉은 모자가 외쳤다."], ["“As if it was not a favour to be protected from the lamp-iron!”", "\"마치 가로등 쇠막대에서 보호받는 것이 은혜가 아니라는 듯이!\""]], [["“It is always as the good patriot says,”", "\"늘 선량한 애국자가 하는 말 그대로요,\""], ["observed the functionary.", "관리가 말했다."], ["“Rise and dress yourself, emigrant.”", "\"일어나서 옷을 입으시오, 이민자여.\""]], [["Darnay complied, and was taken back to the guard-house, where other patriots in rough red caps were smoking, drinking, and sleeping, by a watch-fire.", "다네는 시키는 대로 했고, 위병소로 도로 끌려갔는데, 그곳에서는 거친 붉은 모자를 쓴 또다른 애국자들이 화톳불가에서 담배를 피우고, 술을 마시고, 잠을 자고 있었다."], ["Here he paid a heavy price for his escort, and hence he started with it on the wet, wet roads at three o’clock in the morning.", "여기서 그는 호송대에 대한 값을 비싸게 치렀고, 새벽 세시에 그 호송대와 함께 흠뻑 젖고 또 젖은 길 위로 출발했다."]], [["The escort were two mounted patriots in red caps and tri-coloured cockades, armed with national muskets and sabres, who rode one on either side of him.", "호송대는 붉은 모자에 삼색 모표를 단 기마 애국자 둘로, 국민군 머스킷 총과 군도로 무장한 채 그의 양옆에 하나씩 붙어 말을 달렸다."]], [["The escorted governed his own horse, but a loose line was attached to his bridle, the end of which one of the patriots kept girded round his wrist.", "호송되는 자는 자기 말을 직접 몰았으나, 그의 고삐에는 느슨한 줄이 매여 있었고, 그 줄 끝을 애국자 가운데 하나가 손목에 감고 있었다."], ["In this state they set forth with the sharp rain driving in their faces: clattering at a heavy dragoon trot over the uneven town pavement, and out upon the mire-deep roads.", "이런 상태로 그들은 얼굴에 매섭게 들이치는 빗줄기를 맞으며 출발했으니, 울퉁불퉁한 마을 포석 위를 묵직한 기병대식 속보로 덜그럭거리며 달려 진창처럼 깊은 길로 빠져나갔다."], ["In this state they traversed without change, except of horses and pace, all the mire-deep leagues that lay between them and the capital.", "이런 상태로, 말과 속도가 바뀐 것 말고는 아무 변화 없이, 그들과 수도 사이에 놓인 진창처럼 깊은 길의 모든 리외를 가로질러 갔다."]], [["They travelled in the night, halting an hour or two after daybreak, and lying by until the twilight fell.", "그들은 밤에 이동했고, 동이 트고 한두 시간 뒤에 멈춰 어스름이 내릴 때까지 누워 있었다."], ["The escort were so wretchedly clothed, that they twisted straw round their bare legs, and thatched their ragged shoulders to keep the wet off.", "호송대는 어찌나 비참하게 옷을 걸쳤던지, 맨다리에 짚을 둘둘 감고 비를 막으려고 누더기 어깨에 짚을 덮었다."], ["Apart from the personal discomfort of being so attended, and apart from such considerations of present danger as arose from one of the patriots being chronically drunk, and carrying his musket very recklessly, Charles Darnay did not allow the restraint that was laid upon him to awaken any serious fears in his breast; for, he reasoned with himself that it could have no reference to the merits of an individual case that was not yet stated, and of representations, confirmable by the prisoner in the Abbaye, that were not yet made.", "그토록 호위받는 데서 오는 개인적인 불편함은 차치하고, 애국자 가운데 하나가 만성적으로 취해 머스킷 총을 매우 무모하게 다루는 데서 비롯되는 당장의 위험에 대한 우려도 차치하고서, 샤를 다네는 자신에게 가해진 구속이 가슴속에 어떤 심각한 두려움도 불러일으키도록 내버려두지 않았다. 그는 그것이 아직 진술되지도 않은 한 개인 사건의 정당성과는, 그리고 아바이 감옥의 죄수가 확인해줄 수 있으나 아직 제기되지도 않은 진술들과는 아무 관련도 없을 거라고 스스로 따져보았기 때문이다."]], [["But when they came to the town of Beauvais--which they did at eventide, when the streets were filled with people--he could not conceal from himself that the aspect of affairs was very alarming.", "그러나 그들이 보베라는 마을에 다다랐을 때 — 거리가 사람들로 가득 찬 저녁 무렵에 그곳에 들어섰는데 — 그는 사태의 양상이 매우 불온하다는 것을 스스로에게 숨길 수 없었다."], ["An ominous crowd gathered to see him dismount of the posting-yard, and many voices called out loudly, “Down with the emigrant!”", "그가 역참 마당에서 말에서 내리는 것을 보려고 불길한 군중이 모여들었고, 많은 목소리가 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"이민자를 타도하라!\""]], [["He stopped in the act of swinging himself out of his saddle, and, resuming it as his safest place, said:", "그는 안장에서 몸을 내리려다 멈추고는, 그것이 가장 안전한 자리라 여겨 다시 올라앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Emigrant, my friends!", "\"이민자라니, 친구들이여!"], ["Do you not see me here, in France, of my own will?”", "내가 여기, 프랑스에, 제 발로 와 있는 게 보이지 않소?\""]], [["“You are a cursed emigrant,”", "\"넌 빌어먹을 이민자야,\""], ["cried a farrier, making at him in a furious manner through the press, hammer in hand; “and you are a cursed aristocrat!”", "어느 편자공이 망치를 손에 들고 인파를 헤치며 사납게 그에게 달려들면서 외쳤다. \"그리고 넌 빌어먹을 귀족 놈이야!\""]], [["The postmaster interposed himself between this man and the rider’s bridle (at which he was evidently making), and soothingly said, “Let him be; let him be!", "역참지기가 이 사내와 말 탄 자의 고삐(그가 분명 노리고 있던) 사이로 끼어들며 달래듯 말했다. \"내버려둬, 내버려둬!"], ["He will be judged at Paris.”", "이자는 빠리에서 재판받을 거야.\""]], [["“Judged!”", "\"재판이라고!\""], ["repeated the farrier, swinging his hammer.", "편자공이 망치를 휘두르며 되뇌었다. \"그래!"], ["“Ay! and condemned as a traitor.”", "반역자로 단죄될 테지.\""], ["At this the crowd roared approval.", "이 말에 군중이 와르르 환호했다."]], [["Checking the postmaster, who was for turning his horse’s head to the yard (the drunken patriot sat composedly in his saddle looking on, with the line round his wrist), Darnay said, as soon as he could make his voice heard:", "말 머리를 마당으로 돌리려던 역참지기를 만류하며(취한 애국자는 손목에 줄을 감은 채 안장에 태연히 앉아 구경하고 있었다), 다네는 자기 목소리가 들릴 만해지자 곧바로 말했다."]], [["“Friends, you deceive yourselves, or you are deceived.", "\"친구들이여, 그대들은 스스로를 속이고 있거나, 아니면 누군가에게 속고 있소."], ["I am not a traitor.”", "나는 반역자가 아니오.\""]], [["“He lies!” cried the smith.", "\"저놈이 거짓말을 하네!\""], ["“He is a traitor since the decree.", "대장장이가 외쳤다. \"법령이 내린 뒤로 저놈은 반역자야."], ["His life is forfeit to the people.", "저놈 목숨은 인민에게 몰수당했어."], ["His cursed life is not his own!”", "저 빌어먹을 목숨은 제 것이 아니라고!\""]], [["At the instant when Darnay saw a rush in the eyes of the crowd, which another instant would have brought upon him, the postmaster turned his horse into the yard, the escort rode in close upon his horse’s flanks, and the postmaster shut and barred the crazy double gates.", "다네가 군중의 눈에서 한순간만 더 있으면 자신에게 덮쳐올 돌진의 기미를 본 바로 그 찰나, 역참지기가 말을 마당으로 돌려세웠고, 호송대가 그의 말 옆구리에 바짝 붙어 따라 들어왔으며, 역참지기는 삐걱대는 두짝문을 닫고 빗장을 질렀다."], ["The farrier struck a blow upon them with his hammer, and the crowd groaned; but, no more was done.", "편자공이 망치로 그 문을 한 번 내리쳤고 군중은 신음을 토했으나, 그 이상은 아무 일도 없었다."]], [["“What is this decree that the smith spoke of?”", "\"그 대장장이가 말한 법령이라는 게 뭐요?\""], ["Darnay asked the postmaster, when he had thanked him, and stood beside him in the yard.", "다네는 역참지기에게 고마움을 표하고 마당에서 그의 곁에 선 뒤에 물었다."]], [["“Truly, a decree for selling the property of emigrants.”", "\"실은 이민자의 재산을 매각하라는 법령이오.\""]], [["“When passed?”", "\"언제 통과됐소?\""]], [["“On the fourteenth.”", "\"십사일에요.\""]], [["“The day I left England!”", "\"내가 영국을 떠난 날이군!\""]], [["“Everybody says it is but one of several, and that there will be others--if there are not already--banishing all emigrants, and condemning all to death who return.", "\"다들 그게 여럿 가운데 하나일 뿐이고, 다른 것들도 나올 거라고 — 이미 나오지 않았다면 — 합디다. 모든 이민자를 추방하고, 돌아오는 자는 모두 사형에 처하는 법령 말이오."], ["That is what he meant when he said your life was not your own.”", "저자가 당신 목숨이 당신 것이 아니라고 한 게 바로 그 뜻이오.\""]], [["“But there are no such decrees yet?”", "\"하지만 아직 그런 법령들은 없는 거잖소?\""]], [["“What do I know!”", "\"내가 뭘 알겠소!\""], ["said the postmaster, shrugging his shoulders; “there may be, or there will be.", "역참지기가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"있을 수도 있고, 곧 생길 수도 있지요."], ["It is all the same.", "다 마찬가지요."], ["What would you have?”", "어쩌겠소?\""]], [["They rested on some straw in a loft until the middle of the night, and then rode forward again when all the town was asleep.", "그들은 한밤중까지 다락의 짚더미 위에서 쉬었다가, 온 마을이 잠들었을 때 다시 앞으로 말을 달렸다."], ["Among the many wild changes observable on familiar things which made this wild ride unreal, not the least was the seeming rarity of sleep.", "이 거친 질주를 비현실적으로 만든, 익숙한 것들에서 관찰되는 숱한 거친 변화들 가운데서도 결코 사소하지 않은 것이 잠이 드물어 보이는 점이었다."], ["After long and lonely spurring over dreary roads, they would come to a cluster of poor cottages, not steeped in darkness, but all glittering with lights, and would find the people, in a ghostly manner in the dead of the night, circling hand in hand round a shrivelled tree of Liberty, or all drawn up together singing a Liberty song.", "황량한 길 위를 길고 외롭게 박차를 가하며 달리다 보면, 그들은 어둠에 잠겨 있기는커녕 온통 불빛으로 반짝이는 초라한 오두막 무리에 이르곤 했고, 죽은 듯한 한밤중에 사람들이 유령처럼 손에 손을 잡고 시들어버린 자유의 나무 둘레를 돌거나, 다 함께 늘어서서 자유의 노래를 부르고 있는 것을 보곤 했다."], ["Happily, however, there was sleep in Beauvais that night to help them out of it and they passed on once more into solitude and loneliness: jingling through the untimely cold and wet, among impoverished fields that had yielded no fruits of the earth that year, diversified by the blackened remains of burnt houses, and by the sudden emergence from ambuscade, and sharp reining up across their way, of patriot patrols on the watch on all the roads.", "그러나 다행히도 그날 밤 보베에는 잠이 있어 그들이 거기서 빠져나오도록 도와주었고, 그들은 다시 한번 적막과 고독 속으로 나아갔다. 철 이른 추위와 빗속을 짤랑거리며 지나갔으니, 그해 땅의 열매를 하나도 내지 못한 헐벗은 들판 사이로, 불탄 집들의 거뭇해진 잔해와, 매복에서 불쑥 튀어나와 그들의 앞길을 가로막고 매섭게 고삐를 당기는, 온갖 길목을 지키는 애국자 순찰대들로 변화를 더하며 지나갔다."]], [["Daylight at last found them before the wall of Paris.", "마침내 동이 트자 그들은 빠리의 성벽 앞에 이르렀다."], ["The barrier was closed and strongly guarded when they rode up to it.", "그들이 말을 몰아 다가갔을 때 성문은 닫혀 있었고 삼엄하게 지켜지고 있었다."]], [["“Where are the papers of this prisoner?”", "\"이 죄수의 서류는 어디 있나?\""], ["demanded a resolute-looking man in authority, who was summoned out by the guard.", "위병에게 불려 나온, 단호해 보이는 권위 있는 사내가 물었다."]], [["Naturally struck by the disagreeable word, Charles Darnay requested the speaker to take notice that he was a free traveller and French citizen, in charge of an escort which the disturbed state of the country had imposed upon him, and which he had paid for.", "그 불쾌한 단어에 자연히 충격을 받은 샤를 다네는, 말한 자에게 자신은 자유로운 나그네이자 프랑스 시민이며, 어수선한 나라 사정 때문에 떠안게 되어 그 값까지 치른 호송대를 거느리고 있을 뿐임을 유의하라고 청했다."]], [["“Where,”", "\"이 죄수의 서류는,\""], ["repeated the same personage, without taking any heed of him whatever, “are the papers of this prisoner?”", "같은 인물이 그에게는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 되풀이했다. \"어디 있느냔 말이다?\""]], [["The drunken patriot had them in his cap, and produced them.", "취한 애국자가 모자 속에 서류를 넣어두었다가 꺼냈다."], ["Casting his eyes over Gabelle’s letter, the same personage in authority showed some disorder and surprise, and looked at Darnay with a close attention.", "가벨의 편지를 훑어보던 그 권위 있는 인물은 약간 동요하며 놀란 기색을 보였고, 다네를 유심히 살펴보았다."]], [["He left escort and escorted without saying a word, however, and went into the guard-room; meanwhile, they sat upon their horses outside the gate.", "그러나 그는 한마디도 없이 호송대와 호송되는 자를 남겨두고 위병소로 들어갔다. 그동안 그들은 성문 밖에서 말 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["Looking about him while in this state of suspense, Charles Darnay observed that the gate was held by a mixed guard of soldiers and patriots, the latter far outnumbering the former; and that while ingress into the city for peasants’ carts bringing in supplies, and for similar traffic and traffickers, was easy enough, egress, even for the homeliest people, was very difficult.", "이렇게 마음 졸이는 상태에서 주위를 둘러보던 샤를 다네는, 성문이 군인과 애국자가 뒤섞인 위병들에게 지켜지고 있으며 후자가 전자보다 훨씬 많다는 것을, 그리고 보급품을 실어 오는 농민의 짐수레나 그와 비슷한 통행과 통행인의 도성 진입은 충분히 쉬운 반면, 가장 보잘것없는 사람의 진출조차 매우 어렵다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["A numerous medley of men and women, not to mention beasts and vehicles of various sorts, was waiting to issue forth; but, the previous identification was so strict, that they filtered through the barrier very slowly.", "짐승과 갖가지 탈것은 말할 것도 없고, 무수히 뒤섞인 남녀가 빠져나가려고 기다리고 있었다. 그러나 사전 신원 확인이 어찌나 엄격한지, 그들은 매우 느릿느릿 차단문을 통해 걸러져 나갔다."], ["Some of these people knew their turn for examination to be so far off, that they lay down on the ground to sleep or smoke, while others talked together, or loitered about.", "이 사람들 가운데 일부는 자기 차례가 검사받기까지 한참 멀었음을 알고 땅바닥에 드러누워 잠을 자거나 담배를 피웠고, 다른 이들은 서로 이야기를 나누거나 어슬렁거렸다."], ["The red cap and tri-colour cockade were universal, both among men and women.", "붉은 모자와 삼색 모표는 남자에게나 여자에게나 어디서나 볼 수 있었다."]], [["When he had sat in his saddle some half-hour, taking note of these things, Darnay found himself confronted by the same man in authority, who directed the guard to open the barrier.", "다네가 안장에 앉아 이런 것들을 눈여겨보며 반시간쯤 보냈을 때, 그는 또다시 그 권위 있는 사내와 마주하게 되었는데, 사내는 위병에게 차단문을 열라고 지시했다."], ["Then he delivered to the escort, drunk and sober, a receipt for the escorted, and requested him to dismount.", "그러고는 취한 자와 멀쩡한 자, 그 호송대에게 호송된 자에 대한 영수증을 건네고는 다네에게 말에서 내리라고 청했다."], ["He did so, and the two patriots, leading his tired horse, turned and rode away without entering the city.", "그는 그렇게 했고, 두 애국자는 지친 그의 말을 끌고 도성에는 들어가지 않은 채 몸을 돌려 말을 타고 떠나갔다."]], [["He accompanied his conductor into a guard-room, smelling of common wine and tobacco, where certain soldiers and patriots, asleep and awake, drunk and sober, and in various neutral states between sleeping and waking, drunkenness and sobriety, were standing and lying about.", "그는 자신을 안내하는 자를 따라 위병소로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서는 흔한 포도주와 담배 냄새가 났으며, 자는 자와 깨어 있는 자, 취한 자와 멀쩡한 자, 그리고 잠과 깸, 취함과 맑은 정신 사이의 갖가지 중간 상태에 있는 군인들과 애국자들이 서 있거나 여기저기 누워 있었다."], ["The light in the guard-house, half derived from the waning oil-lamps of the night, and half from the overcast day, was in a correspondingly uncertain condition.", "위병소의 빛은, 절반은 밤새 사위어가는 기름등잔에서, 절반은 잔뜩 흐린 한낮에서 나온 것이어서, 그에 걸맞게 어정쩡한 상태였다."], ["Some registers were lying open on a desk, and an officer of a coarse, dark aspect, presided over these.", "책상 위에는 명부 몇 권이 펼쳐져 있었고, 거칠고 거무튀튀한 인상의 한 장교가 그것들을 관장하고 있었다."]], [["“Citizen Defarge,”", "\"드파르주 시민,\""], ["said he to Darnay’s conductor, as he took a slip of paper to write on.", "그가 적을 종잇조각 하나를 집어 들며 다네를 안내한 자에게 말했다."], ["“Is this the emigrant Evrémonde?”", "\"이자가 그 이민자 에브레몽드인가?\""]], [["“This is the man.”", "\"이 사람이오.\""]], [["“Your age, Evrémonde?”", "\"나이가 어떻게 되나, 에브레몽드?\""]], [["“Thirty-seven.”", "\"서른일곱이오.\""]], [["“Married, Evrémonde?”", "\"결혼은 했나, 에브레몽드?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"했소.\""]], [["“Where married?”", "\"어디서 했지?\""]], [["“In England.”", "\"영국에서요.\""]], [["“Without doubt.", "\"그렇겠지."], ["Where is your wife, Evrémonde?”", "아내는 어디 있나, 에브레몽드?\""]], [["“In England.”", "\"영국에 있소.\""]], [["“Without doubt.", "\"그렇겠지."], ["You are consigned, Evrémonde, to the prison of La Force.”", "에브레몽드, 당신은 라포르스 감옥으로 보내진다.\""]], [["“Just Heaven!”", "\"하느님 맙소사!\""], ["exclaimed Darnay.", "다네가 외쳤다."], ["“Under what law, and for what offence?”", "\"무슨 법으로, 무슨 죄목으로 말이오?\""]], [["The officer looked up from his slip of paper for a moment.", "장교가 잠깐 종잇조각에서 눈을 들었다."]], [["“We have new laws, Evrémonde, and new offences, since you were here.”", "\"당신이 여기 있던 이후로, 에브레몽드, 우리에겐 새 법이 생겼고 새 죄목이 생겼지.\""], ["He said it with a hard smile, and went on writing.", "그는 모진 미소를 지으며 그렇게 말하고는 계속 써 내려갔다."]], [["“I entreat you to observe that I have come here voluntarily, in response to that written appeal of a fellow-countryman which lies before you.", "\"내가 여기 자진해서 왔다는 것을 유의해주기 바라오. 당신 앞에 놓인, 동포가 써 보낸 그 호소문에 응해서 말이오."], ["I demand no more than the opportunity to do so without delay.", "나는 지체 없이 그렇게 할 기회 외에는 아무것도 요구하지 않소."], ["Is not that my right?”", "그것이 내 권리가 아니오?\""]], [["“Emigrants have no rights, Evrémonde,”", "\"이민자에게는 권리가 없다, 에브레몽드.\""], ["was the stolid reply.", "무뚝뚝한 대답이 돌아왔다."], ["The officer wrote until he had finished, read over to himself what he had written, sanded it, and handed it to Defarge, with the words “In secret.”", "장교는 다 쓸 때까지 써 내려가더니, 자신이 쓴 것을 스스로 읽어보고, 모래를 뿌려 잉크를 말린 뒤, \"비밀 감금\"이라는 말과 함께 그것을 드파르주에게 건넸다."]], [["Defarge motioned with the paper to the prisoner that he must accompany him.", "드파르주는 그 종이로 죄수에게 자신을 따라와야 한다는 몸짓을 했다."], ["The prisoner obeyed, and a guard of two armed patriots attended them.", "죄수는 따랐고, 무장한 애국자 둘로 이루어진 위병이 그들을 수행했다."]], [["“Is it you,”", "\"당신이,\""], ["said Defarge, in a low voice, as they went down the guardhouse steps and turned into Paris, “who married the daughter of Doctor Manette, once a prisoner in the Bastille that is no more?”", "위병소 계단을 내려가 빠리 시내로 접어들면서 드파르주가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"지금은 사라지고 없는 바스띠유의 죄수였던 마네뜨 박사의 딸과 결혼한 사람이오?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["replied Darnay, looking at him with surprise.", "다네가 놀라며 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“My name is Defarge, and I keep a wine-shop in the Quarter Saint Antoine.", "\"내 이름은 드파르주요, 쌩땅뚜안 지구에서 포도주 가게를 하고 있소."], ["Possibly you have heard of me.”", "아마 내 이야기를 들어봤을 거요.\""]], [["“My wife came to your house to reclaim her father?", "\"내 아내가 아버지를 도로 모셔 가려고 당신 집에 갔었지요?"], ["Yes!”", "맞소!\""]], [["The word “wife”", "\"아내\"라는 말이 드파르주에게 음울한 기억을 떠올리게 한 듯, 그는 별안간 짜증을 내며 말했다."], ["seemed to serve as a gloomy reminder to Defarge, to say with sudden impatience, “In the name of that sharp female newly-born, and called La Guillotine, why did you come to France?”", "\"새로 태어나 라기요띤이라 불리는 그 매서운 여자의 이름으로 묻겠소, 당신은 어째서 프랑스에 왔소?\""]], [["“You heard me say why, a minute ago.", "\"방금 그 까닭을 말하는 것을 당신도 들었잖소."], ["Do you not believe it is the truth?”", "그게 진실이라는 걸 믿지 않는 거요?\""]], [["“A bad truth for you,”", "\"당신에게는 나쁜 진실이지,\""], ["said Defarge, speaking with knitted brows, and looking straight before him.", "드파르주가 미간을 찌푸린 채 똑바로 앞을 보며 말했다."]], [["“Indeed I am lost here.", "\"정말이지 나는 여기서 길을 잃었소."], ["All here is so unprecedented, so changed, so sudden and unfair, that I am absolutely lost.", "여기 모든 것이 너무도 전례 없고, 너무도 변했고, 너무도 갑작스럽고 부당해서, 나는 완전히 길을 잃었소."], ["Will you render me a little help?”", "내게 약간의 도움을 베풀어주겠소?\""]], [["“None.”", "\"안 되오.\""], ["Defarge spoke, always looking straight before him.", "드파르주는 줄곧 똑바로 앞을 보며 말했다."]], [["“Will you answer me a single question?”", "\"단 하나의 질문에라도 답해주겠소?\""]], [["“Perhaps.", "\"아마도."], ["According to its nature.", "그 성격에 따라서 말이오."], ["You can say what it is.”", "무엇인지 말해보시오.\""]], [["“In this prison that I am going to so unjustly, shall I have some free communication with the world outside?”", "\"내가 이토록 부당하게 끌려가는 이 감옥에서, 바깥세상과 자유로이 연락할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“You will see.”", "\"두고 보면 알 거요.\""]], [["“I am not to be buried there, prejudged, and without any means of presenting my case?”", "\"나는 미리 단죄당한 채, 내 사정을 호소할 아무런 수단도 없이 거기 파묻혀버리는 거요?\""]], [["“You will see.", "\"두고 보면 알 거요."], ["But, what then?", "하지만 그렇다 한들 어떻소?"], ["Other people have been similarly buried in worse prisons, before now.”", "다른 사람들도 지금껏 더 나쁜 감옥에 그렇게 파묻혀왔으니 말이오.\""]], [["“But never by me, Citizen Defarge.”", "\"하지만 내 손으로 그런 적은 결코 없소, 드파르주 시민.\""]], [["Defarge glanced darkly at him for answer, and walked on in a steady and set silence.", "드파르주는 대답 대신 어둡게 그를 흘끗 보고는, 한결같이 굳은 침묵 속에서 걸어 나아갔다."], ["The deeper he sank into this silence, the fainter hope there was--or so Darnay thought--of his softening in any slight degree.", "이 침묵에 깊이 가라앉을수록, 그가 조금이라도 누그러질 가망은 더욱 희미해졌다 — 적어도 다네는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["He, therefore, made haste to say:", "그래서 그는 서둘러 말했다."]], [["“It is of the utmost importance to me (you know, Citizen, even better than I, of how much importance), that I should be able to communicate to Mr.", "\"내게는 더없이 중요한 일이오(시민이여, 당신은 나보다도 그게 얼마나 중요한지 더 잘 알 거요)."], ["Lorry of Tellson’s Bank, an English gentleman who is now in Paris, the simple fact, without comment, that I have been thrown into the prison of La Force.", "지금 빠리에 와 있는 영국 신사이자 뗄슨 은행의 로리 씨에게, 내가 라포르스 감옥에 갇혔다는 단순한 사실만이라도, 아무 부연 없이 전할 수 있다면 말이오."], ["Will you cause that to be done for me?”", "나를 위해 그 일을 해주겠소?\""]], [["“I will do,”", "\"나는,\""], ["Defarge doggedly rejoined, “nothing for you.", "드파르주가 완강하게 응수했다. \"당신을 위해서는 아무것도 하지 않겠소."], ["My duty is to my country and the People.", "내 의무는 내 조국과 인민에 대한 것이오."], ["I am the sworn servant of both, against you.", "나는 그 둘 모두에게 맹세한 종이며, 당신에게는 맞서는 사람이오."], ["I will do nothing for you.”", "나는 당신을 위해 아무것도 하지 않겠소.\""]], [["Charles Darnay felt it hopeless to entreat him further, and his pride was touched besides.", "샤를 다네는 그에게 더 간청해봐야 소용없다고 느꼈고, 게다가 자존심도 상했다."], ["As they walked on in silence, he could not but see how used the people were to the spectacle of prisoners passing along the streets.", "그들이 말없이 걸어가는 동안, 그는 사람들이 죄수가 거리를 끌려가는 광경에 얼마나 익숙해져 있는지 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The very children scarcely noticed him.", "아이들조차 그에게 거의 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["A few passers turned their heads, and a few shook their fingers at him as an aristocrat; otherwise, that a man in good clothes should be going to prison, was no more remarkable than that a labourer in working clothes should be going to work.", "행인 몇이 고개를 돌렸고, 몇은 귀족이라며 그를 향해 손가락질을 했다. 그밖에는, 좋은 옷을 입은 사람이 감옥에 끌려가는 것은 작업복을 입은 노동자가 일하러 가는 것이나 다를 바 없이 별스러운 일이 아니었다."], ["In one narrow, dark, and dirty street through which they passed, an excited orator, mounted on a stool, was addressing an excited audience on the crimes against the people, of the king and the royal family.", "그들이 지나가는 어느 좁고 어둡고 더러운 거리에서는, 흥분한 연설가 하나가 걸상에 올라서서 흥분한 청중을 향해 왕과 왕족이 인민에게 저지른 죄에 대해 일장연설을 하고 있었다."], ["The few words that he caught from this man’s lips, first made it known to Charles Darnay that the king was in prison, and that the foreign ambassadors had one and all left Paris.", "이 사내의 입에서 그가 주워들은 몇 마디 말로, 샤를 다네는 비로소 왕이 감옥에 갇혔다는 것과, 외국 대사들이 하나같이 빠리를 떠났다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["On the road (except at Beauvais) he had heard absolutely nothing.", "길 위에서(보베에서를 빼고는) 그는 정말이지 아무것도 듣지 못했다."], ["The escort and the universal watchfulness had completely isolated him.", "호송대와 어디서나 펼쳐지는 감시가 그를 완전히 고립시켰던 것이다."]], [["That he had fallen among far greater dangers than those which had developed themselves when he left England, he of course knew now.", "자신이 영국을 떠날 때 모습을 드러냈던 위험들보다 훨씬 더 큰 위험 속에 빠졌다는 것을, 그는 이제 물론 알았다."], ["That perils had thickened about him fast, and might thicken faster and faster yet, he of course knew now.", "위험이 자기 주위로 빠르게 짙어졌고, 앞으로 더욱더 빠르게 짙어질지 모른다는 것을, 그는 이제 물론 알았다."], ["He could not but admit to himself that he might not have made this journey, if he could have foreseen the events of a few days.", "며칠 뒤의 사건들을 미리 내다볼 수 있었더라면 이 여정을 떠나지 않았을지도 모른다는 것을, 그는 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["And yet his misgivings were not so dark as, imagined by the light of this later time, they would appear.", "그렇지만 그의 불안은, 훗날의 빛에 비추어 상상되는 것만큼 그리 어둡지는 않았다."], ["Troubled as the future was, it was the unknown future, and in its obscurity there was ignorant hope.", "미래가 아무리 어수선하다 해도 그것은 알 수 없는 미래였고, 그 어둠 속에는 무지한 희망이 깃들어 있었다."], ["The horrible massacre, days and nights long, which, within a few rounds of the clock, was to set a great mark of blood upon the blessed garnering time of harvest, was as far out of his knowledge as if it had been a hundred thousand years away.", "며칠 밤낮을 이어진 그 끔찍한 학살, 시곗바늘이 몇 바퀴 돌기도 전에 축복받은 수확의 거둠철 위에 거대한 핏자국을 남기게 될 그 학살은, 십만 년쯤 떨어진 일이기라도 한 듯 그의 앎에서 까마득히 멀었다."], ["The “sharp female newly-born, and called La Guillotine,” was hardly known to him, or to the generality of people, by name.", "\"새로 태어나 라기요띤이라 불리는 그 매서운 여자\"는 그에게도, 또 대다수 사람들에게도 그 이름조차 거의 알려져 있지 않았다."], ["The frightful deeds that were to be soon done, were probably unimagined at that time in the brains of the doers.", "머지않아 저질러질 그 무시무시한 짓들은, 아마 그 무렵 그것을 저지를 자들의 머릿속에서조차 떠올려지지 않았을 것이다."], ["How could they have a place in the shadowy conceptions of a gentle mind?", "그러니 그것들이 어찌 온화한 마음의 어렴풋한 상념 속에 자리할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["Of unjust treatment in detention and hardship, and in cruel separation from his wife and child, he foreshadowed the likelihood, or the certainty; but, beyond this, he dreaded nothing distinctly.", "구금과 고초 속에서의 부당한 처우, 그리고 아내와 아이와의 모진 이별에 대해서는, 그는 그럴 법함을, 아니 그 확실함을 어렴풋이 내다보았다. 그러나 그 너머로는 무엇 하나 또렷이 두려워하지 않았다."], ["With this on his mind, which was enough to carry into a dreary prison courtyard, he arrived at the prison of La Force.", "음울한 감옥 마당으로 들고 들어가기에 충분한 이런 생각을 품은 채, 그는 라포르스 감옥에 다다랐다."]], [["A man with a bloated face opened the strong wicket, to whom Defarge presented “The Emigrant Evrémonde.”", "부풀어오른 얼굴을 한 사내가 튼튼한 쪽문을 열었고, 드파르주는 그에게 \"이민자 에브레몽드\"를 인계했다."]], [["“What the Devil!", "\"이런 빌어먹을!"], ["How many more of them!”", "이런 게 대체 얼마나 더 오는 거야!\""], ["exclaimed the man with the bloated face.", "부풀어오른 얼굴의 사내가 외쳤다."]], [["Defarge took his receipt without noticing the exclamation, and withdrew, with his two fellow-patriots.", "드파르주는 그 외침에 아랑곳하지 않고 영수증을 받아 들고는, 두 동료 애국자와 함께 물러갔다."]], [["“What the Devil, I say again!”", "\"빌어먹을, 다시 말하지만!\""], ["exclaimed the gaoler, left with his wife.", "아내와 함께 남겨진 간수가 외쳤다."], ["“How many more!”", "\"얼마나 더 온단 말이야!\""]], [["The gaoler’s wife, being provided with no answer to the question, merely replied, “One must have patience, my dear!”", "간수의 아내는 그 물음에 내놓을 답이 없어, 그저 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"참아야지요, 여보!\""], ["Three turnkeys who entered responsive to a bell she rang, echoed the sentiment, and one added, “For the love of Liberty;”", "그녀가 울린 종소리에 응해 들어온 옥지기 셋이 그 말에 맞장구쳤고, 하나는 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"자유를 위해서 말이오.\""], ["which sounded in that place like an inappropriate conclusion.", "그것은 그곳에서 어울리지 않는 마무리처럼 들렸다."]], [["The prison of La Force was a gloomy prison, dark and filthy, and with a horrible smell of foul sleep in it.", "라포르스 감옥은 음울한 감옥이었으니, 어둡고 더러웠으며, 더러운 잠에서 풍기는 끔찍한 냄새가 배어 있었다."], ["Extraordinary how soon the noisome flavour of imprisoned sleep, becomes manifest in all such places that are ill cared for!", "제대로 돌보지 않는 그런 곳마다 갇힌 잠의 역겨운 냄새가 얼마나 빨리 또렷이 드러나는지, 참으로 놀라운 일이다!"]], [["“In secret, too,”", "\"게다가 비밀 감금이라니,\""], ["grumbled the gaoler, looking at the written paper.", "간수가 그 쓰인 종이를 보며 투덜거렸다."], ["“As if I was not already full to bursting!”", "\"이미 터져나갈 만큼 꽉 찼는데 말이야!\""]], [["He stuck the paper on a file, in an ill-humour, and Charles Darnay awaited his further pleasure for half an hour: sometimes, pacing to and fro in the strong arched room: sometimes, resting on a stone seat: in either case detained to be imprinted on the memory of the chief and his subordinates.", "그는 언짢은 기분으로 종이를 서류꽂이에 꽂았고, 샤를 다네는 반시간 동안 그가 마음 내키기를 기다렸다. 때로는 튼튼한 아치형 방 안을 이리저리 거닐면서, 때로는 돌의자에 앉아 쉬면서. 어느 쪽이든 그는 우두머리와 그 부하들의 기억에 새겨지도록 붙들려 있었다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"가자!\""], ["said the chief, at length taking up his keys, “come with me, emigrant.”", "마침내 우두머리가 열쇠를 집어 들며 말했다. \"나를 따라와, 이민자여.\""]], [["Through the dismal prison twilight, his new charge accompanied him by corridor and staircase, many doors clanging and locking behind them, until they came into a large, low, vaulted chamber, crowded with prisoners of both sexes.", "음울한 감옥의 어스름을 지나, 그가 새로 떠맡은 자는 복도와 계단을 따라 그를 뒤따랐고, 그들 뒤로 숱한 문들이 쾅 닫히며 잠겼다. 마침내 그들은 넓고 천장이 낮은 둥근 천장의 방에 이르렀는데, 그곳은 남녀 죄수들로 붐볐다."], ["The women were seated at a long table, reading and writing, knitting, sewing, and embroidering; the men were for the most part standing behind their chairs, or lingering up and down the room.", "여자들은 긴 탁자에 앉아 책을 읽거나 글을 쓰고, 뜨개질을 하고, 바느질을 하고, 수를 놓고 있었다. 남자들은 대부분 의자 뒤에 서 있거나, 방 안을 오락가락 서성이고 있었다."]], [["In the instinctive association of prisoners with shameful crime and disgrace, the new-comer recoiled from this company.", "죄수를 부끄러운 범죄나 치욕과 본능적으로 결부시키는 탓에, 새로 온 자는 이 무리에게서 움찔 물러섰다."], ["But the crowning unreality of his long unreal ride, was, their all at once rising to receive him, with every refinement of manner known to the time, and with all the engaging graces and courtesies of life.", "그러나 그토록 길고 비현실적이었던 그 질주의 절정을 이루는 비현실은, 그들이 일제히 일어서서 당대에 알려진 온갖 세련된 예법과, 삶의 온갖 매력적인 우아함과 정중함으로 그를 맞이한 것이었다."]], [["So strangely clouded were these refinements by the prison manners and gloom, so spectral did they become in the inappropriate squalor and misery through which they were seen, that Charles Darnay seemed to stand in a company of the dead. Ghosts all!", "이 세련됨이 감옥의 행색과 음울함으로 어찌나 기이하게 흐려졌던지, 그것들이 보이는 어울리지 않는 누추함과 비참함 속에서 어찌나 유령 같아졌던지, 샤를 다네는 마치 죽은 자들의 무리 가운데 서 있는 듯했다."], ["The ghost of beauty, the ghost of stateliness, the ghost of elegance, the ghost of pride, the ghost of frivolity, the ghost of wit, the ghost of youth, the ghost of age, all waiting their dismissal from the desolate shore, all turning on him eyes that were changed by the death they had died in coming there.", "모두가 유령이었다! 아름다움의 유령, 위엄의 유령, 우아함의 유령, 자긍심의 유령, 경박함의 유령, 재치의 유령, 젊음의 유령, 노년의 유령, 모두가 그 황량한 기슭에서 풀려나기를 기다리며, 이곳에 오는 길에 그들이 겪은 죽음으로 달라진 눈을 그에게로 돌리고 있었다."]], [["It struck him motionless.", "그것에 그는 옴짝달싹 못한 채 굳어버렸다."], ["The gaoler standing at his side, and the other gaolers moving about, who would have been well enough as to appearance in the ordinary exercise of their functions, looked so extravagantly coarse contrasted with sorrowing mothers and blooming daughters who were there--with the apparitions of the coquette, the young beauty, and the mature woman delicately bred--that the inversion of all experience and likelihood which the scene of shadows presented, was heightened to its utmost.", "그의 곁에 선 간수와 주위를 오가는 다른 간수들은, 평소의 직무를 수행하는 모습이라면 외양이 어지간했을 테지만, 그곳에 있는 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니들과 활짝 피어난 딸들과 견주어보면 — 아리따운 여인과 젊은 미녀와 곱게 자란 원숙한 여인의 환영들과 견주어보면 — 어찌나 터무니없이 상스러워 보였던지, 이 그림자들의 광경이 펼쳐 보이는 온갖 경험과 개연성의 역전이 더할 나위 없이 고조되었다. 분명, 모두가 유령이었다."], ["Surely, ghosts all. Surely, the long unreal ride some progress of disease that had brought him to these gloomy shades!", "분명, 그 길고 비현실적이었던 질주는 그를 이 음울한 망령들에게로 데려온 어떤 병의 진행이었던 것이다!"]], [["“In the name of the assembled companions in misfortune,”", "\"불운 속에 모인 동료들의 이름으로,\""], ["said a gentleman of courtly appearance and address, coming forward, “I have the honour of giving you welcome to La Force, and of condoling with you on the calamity that has brought you among us.", "궁정풍의 외모와 말씨를 지닌 한 신사가 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"라포르스에 오신 당신을 환영하고, 당신을 우리 가운데로 데려온 그 재난에 대해 위로의 말을 전하는 영광을 누리겠습니다."], ["May it soon terminate happily!", "부디 그것이 머지않아 행복하게 끝나기를!"], ["It would be an impertinence elsewhere, but it is not so here, to ask your name and condition?”", "다른 곳에서라면 무례한 일이겠으나, 여기서는 그렇지 않으니, 당신의 이름과 처지를 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["Charles Darnay roused himself, and gave the required information, in words as suitable as he could find.", "샤를 다네는 정신을 가다듬고, 찾을 수 있는 한 가장 알맞은 말로 요청받은 정보를 알려주었다."]], [["“But I hope,”", "\"하지만 바라건대,\""], ["said the gentleman, following the chief gaoler with his eyes, who moved across the room, “that you are not in secret?”", "방을 가로질러 가는 우두머리 간수를 눈으로 좇으며 신사가 말했다. \"당신은 비밀 감금은 아니겠지요?\""]], [["“I do not understand the meaning of the term, but I have heard them say so.”", "\"그 말이 무슨 뜻인지는 모르겠습니다만, 그들이 그렇게 말하는 것을 들었습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, what a pity!", "\"아, 참으로 안된 일이군요!"], ["We so much regret it!", "우리도 무척 유감입니다!"], ["But take courage; several members of our society have been in secret, at first, and it has lasted but a short time.”", "하지만 용기를 내세요. 우리 무리 가운데 여럿이 처음엔 비밀 감금이었으나, 그것은 잠깐밖에 가지 않았습니다.\""], ["Then he added, raising his voice, “I grieve to inform the society--in secret.”", "그러고는 목소리를 높여 덧붙였다. \"여러분께 알리게 되어 가슴 아프오만 — 비밀 감금이랍니다.\""]], [["There was a murmur of commiseration as Charles Darnay crossed the room to a grated door where the gaoler awaited him, and many voices--among which, the soft and compassionate voices of women were conspicuous--gave him good wishes and encouragement.", "샤를 다네가 간수가 기다리는 격자문으로 방을 가로질러 갈 때 동정의 웅성거림이 일었고, 많은 목소리가 — 그 가운데서도 여인들의 부드럽고 자비로운 목소리가 두드러졌다 — 그에게 행운과 격려를 빌어주었다."], ["He turned at the grated door, to render the thanks of his heart; it closed under the gaoler’s hand; and the apparitions vanished from his sight forever.", "그는 격자문 앞에서 몸을 돌려 진심에서 우러난 감사를 전하려 했다. 문은 간수의 손 아래 닫혔고, 그 환영들은 그의 시야에서 영영 사라져버렸다."]], [["The wicket opened on a stone staircase, leading upward.", "쪽문은 위로 이어지는 돌계단으로 열렸다."], ["When they had ascended forty steps (the prisoner of half an hour already counted them), the gaoler opened a low black door, and they passed into a solitary cell.", "마흔 계단을 올라갔을 때(이미 반시간째 죄수인 그는 그것을 세고 있었다), 간수가 낮고 검은 문을 열었고, 그들은 외딴 독방으로 들어섰다."], ["It struck cold and damp, but was not dark.", "그곳은 춥고 축축한 기운이 끼쳐왔으나, 어둡지는 않았다."]], [["“Yours,”", "\"당신 거요,\""], ["said the gaoler.", "간수가 말했다."]], [["“Why am I confined alone?”", "\"왜 나를 혼자 가두는 거요?\""]], [["“How do I know!”", "\"내가 어찌 알겠소!\""]], [["“I can buy pen, ink, and paper?”", "\"펜과 잉크와 종이를 살 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Such are not my orders.", "\"그건 내 지시에 없는 일이오."], ["You will be visited, and can ask then.", "면회를 받게 될 테니 그때 물어보시오."], ["At present, you may buy your food, and nothing more.”", "지금으로선 음식을 살 수 있고, 그 이상은 안 되오.\""]], [["There were in the cell, a chair, a table, and a straw mattress.", "독방에는 의자 하나와 탁자 하나, 그리고 짚 매트리스가 있었다."], ["As the gaoler made a general inspection of these objects, and of the four walls, before going out, a wandering fancy wandered through the mind of the prisoner leaning against the wall opposite to him, that this gaoler was so unwholesomely bloated, both in face and person, as to look like a man who had been drowned and filled with water.", "간수가 나가기 전에 이 물건들과 네 벽을 두루 살펴보는 동안, 그의 맞은편 벽에 기대선 죄수의 마음속으로 떠도는 상념 하나가 떠돌았으니, 이 간수가 얼굴이며 몸이며 어찌나 병적으로 부풀어 있는지 마치 물에 빠져 죽어 몸속이 물로 가득 찬 사람처럼 보인다는 것이었다."], ["When the gaoler was gone, he thought in the same wandering way, “Now am I left, as if I were dead.”", "간수가 떠나자, 그는 그 똑같이 떠도는 식으로 생각했다. \"이제 나는 마치 죽은 것처럼 남겨졌구나.\""], ["Stopping then, to look down at the mattress, he turned from it with a sick feeling, and thought, “And here in these crawling creatures is the first condition of the body after death.”", "그러고는 멈춰 서서 매트리스를 내려다보다가, 메스꺼운 느낌에 그것에서 몸을 돌리며 생각했다. \"그리고 여기 이 기어 다니는 것들 속에, 죽은 뒤 몸이 맞는 첫 상태가 있구나.\""]], [["“Five paces by four and a half, five paces by four and a half, five paces by four and a half.”", "\"다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반, 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반, 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반.\""], ["The prisoner walked to and fro in his cell, counting its measurement, and the roar of the city arose like muffled drums with a wild swell of voices added to them.", "죄수는 독방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 그 치수를 세었고, 도시의 굉음이 거기에 거친 함성의 너울을 보태며 둔탁한 북소리처럼 일었다."], ["“He made shoes, he made shoes, he made shoes.”", "\"그는 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지.\""], ["The prisoner counted the measurement again, and paced faster, to draw his mind with him from that latter repetition.", "죄수는 다시 치수를 세었고, 그 뒤의 되뇜에서 자기 마음을 끌어내려고 더 빠르게 걸었다."], ["“The ghosts that vanished when the wicket closed.", "\"쪽문이 닫혔을 때 사라진 그 유령들."], ["There was one among them, the appearance of a lady dressed in black, who was leaning in the embrasure of a window, and she had a light shining upon her golden hair, and she looked like * * * * Let us ride on again, for God’s sake, through the illuminated villages with the people all awake!", "그 가운데 검은 옷을 입은 한 여인의 모습이 있었지, 창문의 후미진 곳에 기대선 채, 황금빛 머리카락 위로 빛이 내리비치는 그 여인은 마치 ×××× 같았어. 제발, 우리 다시 말을 달리자, 사람들이 모두 깨어 있는 불 밝힌 마을들을 가로질러!"], ["* * * * He made shoes, he made shoes, he made shoes.", "×××× 그는 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지, 신을 지었지."], ["* * * * Five paces by four and a half.”", "×××× 다섯 걸음에 네 걸음 반.\""], ["With such scraps tossing and rolling upward from the depths of his mind, the prisoner walked faster and faster, obstinately counting and counting; and the roar of the city changed to this extent--that it still rolled in like muffled drums, but with the wail of voices that he knew, in the swell that rose above them.", "마음 깊은 데서 이런 토막들이 떠올라 뒹굴고 휘도는 가운데, 죄수는 점점 더 빠르게 걸으며 고집스레 세고 또 세었다. 그리고 도시의 굉음은 이만큼 변했으니 — 여전히 둔탁한 북소리처럼 밀려들었으나, 그 위로 솟구치는 너울 속에는 그가 아는 목소리들의 울부짖음이 섞여 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER II. The Grindstone", "# CHAPTER II. The Grindstone"]], [["Tellson’s Bank, established in the Saint Germain Quarter of Paris, was in a wing of a large house, approached by a courtyard and shut off from the street by a high wall and a strong gate.", "빠리의 쌩제르맹 구역에 자리 잡은 텔슨 은행은 어느 큰 저택의 한쪽 별채에 들어 있었는데, 안마당을 거쳐 드나들도록 되어 있었고 높은 담과 튼튼한 대문으로 길과 차단되어 있었다."], ["The house belonged to a great nobleman who had lived in it until he made a flight from the troubles, in his own cook’s dress, and got across the borders.", "그 저택은 어느 대귀족의 소유였으니, 그는 이 집에서 살다가 자기 집 요리사의 옷을 입고 그 소동을 피해 달아나 국경을 넘어버린 사람이었다."], ["A mere beast of the chase flying from hunters, he was still in his metempsychosis no other than the same Monseigneur, the preparation of whose chocolate for whose lips had once occupied three strong men besides the cook in question.", "사냥꾼에게 쫓겨 달아나는 한낱 사냥감 짐승에 지나지 않았지만, 그는 윤회를 겪고서도 여전히 다름 아닌 바로 그 영주님이었으니, 한때 그의 입에 들일 초콜릿을 준비하는 데 문제의 그 요리사 말고도 건장한 사내 셋이 매달렸던 그 영주님이었다."]], [["Monseigneur gone, and the three strong men absolving themselves from the sin of having drawn his high wages, by being more than ready and willing to cut his throat on the altar of the dawning Republic one and indivisible of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, Monseigneur’s house had been first sequestrated, and then confiscated.", "영주님은 떠나버렸고, 그의 높은 봉급을 받아왔다는 죄를 씻으려고 그 건장한 사내 셋은 자유·평등·우애, 그도 아니면 죽음의 하나이자 나뉠 수 없는 동트는 공화국의 제단 위에서 그 영주님의 목을 베어버릴 만반의 채비가 되어 있고도 남았으며, 영주님의 저택은 처음에는 압류되었다가 이내 몰수되었다."], ["For, all things moved so fast, and decree followed decree with that fierce precipitation, that now upon the third night of the autumn month of September, patriot emissaries of the law were in possession of Monseigneur’s house, and had marked it with the tri-colour, and were drinking brandy in its state apartments.", "모든 일이 어찌나 빠르게 움직이고 포고령이 또 포고령을 따라 그토록 사납게 줄을 이었던지, 이제 가을철 구월의 사흘째 밤에 이르러 법의 애국적 사절들이 영주님의 저택을 차지하고는 삼색기로 표시를 해두었으며, 그 호화로운 거실에서 브랜디를 마시고 있었다."]], [["A place of business in London like Tellson’s place of business in Paris, would soon have driven the House out of its mind and into the Gazette.", "런던에 있는 영업소가 빠리의 텔슨 영업소 같았더라면, 그 은행은 이내 미쳐버려 파산 공고에 이름을 올리고 말았을 것이다."], ["For, what would staid British responsibility and respectability have said to orange-trees in boxes in a Bank courtyard, and even to a Cupid over the counter?", "점잖은 영국식 책임감과 품위가, 은행 안마당의 상자에 심긴 오렌지나무를, 게다가 계산대 위의 큐피드를 보았더라면 무어라고 했겠는가? 그러나 그런 것들이 버젓이 있었다."], ["Yet such things were. Tellson’s had whitewashed the Cupid, but he was still to be seen on the ceiling, in the coolest linen, aiming (as he very often does) at money from morning to night.", "텔슨 은행은 그 큐피드를 회칠로 덮어버렸으나, 큐피드는 여전히 천장에서, 더없이 시원한 아마포 차림으로 (늘 그러하듯이) 아침부터 밤까지 돈을 겨누고 있는 모습으로 보였다."], ["Bankruptcy must inevitably have come of this young Pagan, in Lombard-street, London, and also of a curtained alcove in the rear of the immortal boy, and also of a looking-glass let into the wall, and also of clerks not at all old, who danced in public on the slightest provocation.", "런던 롬바드 거리에서라면 이 어린 이교도 신상 탓에, 또 그 불멸의 소년 뒤편에 휘장을 친 벽감 탓에, 또 벽에 박아 넣은 거울 탓에, 또 아주 사소한 자극에도 사람들 앞에서 춤을 추는, 결코 늙지도 않은 사무원들 탓에 파산을 면치 못했을 것이다."], ["Yet, a French Tellson’s could get on with these things exceedingly well, and, as long as the times held together, no man had taken fright at them, and drawn out his money.", "그러나 프랑스의 텔슨 은행은 이런 것들과 더없이 잘 지낼 수 있었으니, 시국이 그럭저럭 버텨주는 한, 그것들을 보고 겁을 집어먹어 돈을 빼간 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["What money would be drawn out of Tellson’s henceforth, and what would lie there, lost and forgotten; what plate and jewels would tarnish in Tellson’s hiding-places, while the depositors rusted in prisons, and when they should have violently perished; how many accounts with Tellson’s never to be balanced in this world, must be carried over into the next; no man could have said, that night, any more than Mr.", "앞으로 텔슨 은행에서 얼마나 많은 돈이 빠져나갈 것이며, 얼마나 많은 돈이 잃어버린 채 잊힌 채로 거기 잠겨 있을 것인지. 예금주들이 감옥에서 녹슬어가는 동안, 그리고 그들이 무참히 죽임을 당하고 난 뒤에, 얼마나 많은 식기와 보석이 텔슨 은행의 은닉처에서 빛을 잃어갈 것인지."], ["Jarvis Lorry could, though he thought heavily of these questions.", "텔슨 은행과의 거래 가운데 얼마나 많은 것이 이승에서는 결산되지 못한 채 저승으로 넘어가야 할 것인지."], ["He sat by a newly-lighted wood fire (the blighted and unfruitful year was prematurely cold), and on his honest and courageous face there was a deeper shade than the pendent lamp could throw, or any object in the room distortedly reflect--a shade of horror.", "그것은, 자비스 로리 씨가 그러했듯, 그날 밤 누구도 말할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그가 이런 물음들을 무겁게 생각하긴 했어도 그러했다. 그는 갓 지핀 장작불 곁에 앉아 있었는데(시들고 결실 없는 그해는 일찌감치 추웠다), 그의 정직하고 용감한 얼굴에는 늘어뜨린 등불이 드리울 수 있는 것보다도, 방 안의 그 어떤 물건이 일그러뜨려 비출 수 있는 것보다도 더 짙은 그늘이 어려 있었다 — 공포의 그늘이었다."]], [["He occupied rooms in the Bank, in his fidelity to the House of which he had grown to be a part, like strong root-ivy.", "그는 은행 안에 방을 두고 지냈으니, 굵은 뿌리내린 담쟁이덩굴처럼 한 부분이 되어버린 그 은행에 대한 충심에서였다."], ["It chanced that they derived a kind of security from the patriotic occupation of the main building, but the true-hearted old gentleman never calculated about that.", "마침 본관을 애국자들이 차지하고 있던 덕분에 일종의 안전이 보장되긴 했으나, 진실한 마음을 지닌 그 노신사는 그런 것을 결코 셈하지 않았다."], ["All such circumstances were indifferent to him, so that he did his duty.", "그가 자기 임무를 다할 수만 있다면, 그러한 사정은 모두 그에게 아무래도 상관없는 일이었다."], ["On the opposite side of the courtyard, under a colonnade, was extensive standing--for carriages--where, indeed, some carriages of Monseigneur yet stood.", "안마당 맞은편, 열주랑 아래에는 마차를 세워두는 넓은 자리가 있었는데, 거기에는 정말로 영주님의 마차 몇 대가 아직 서 있었다."], ["Against two of the pillars were fastened two great flaring flambeaux, and in the light of these, standing out in the open air, was a large grindstone: a roughly mounted thing which appeared to have hurriedly been brought there from some neighbouring smithy, or other workshop.", "두 기둥에는 활활 타오르는 커다란 횃불 두 개가 매여 있었고, 그 불빛 속에, 노천에 뚜렷이 드러나 있는 것은 커다란 숫돌이었다 — 인근의 어느 대장간이나 다른 작업장에서 황급히 가져온 듯 거칠게 받침대에 올려놓은 물건이었다."], ["Rising and looking out of window at these harmless objects, Mr. Lorry shivered, and retired to his seat by the fire.", "로리 씨는 일어나 창밖으로 이 무해한 물건들을 내다보다가 몸을 떨었고, 불가의 자기 자리로 물러났다."], ["He had opened, not only the glass window, but the lattice blind outside it, and he had closed both again, and he shivered through his frame.", "그는 유리창뿐 아니라 그 바깥의 격자 덧문까지 열어두었었는데, 두 가지를 다시 닫고 나서 온몸을 부르르 떨었다."]], [["From the streets beyond the high wall and the strong gate, there came the usual night hum of the city, with now and then an indescribable ring in it, weird and unearthly, as if some unwonted sounds of a terrible nature were going up to Heaven.", "높은 담과 튼튼한 대문 너머의 거리에서, 도시의 여느 밤 같은 웅성거림이 들려왔는데, 이따금 그 속에는 형언할 수 없는 울림이 섞여 있었으니, 기괴하고 이 세상 것 같지 않아서, 마치 끔찍한 본성을 지닌 어떤 심상찮은 소리들이 하늘로 솟구쳐 올라가는 것만 같았다."]], [["“Thank God,” said Mr.", "\"하느님, 감사합니다.\""], ["Lorry, clasping his hands, “that no one near and dear to me is in this dreadful town to-night.", "로리 씨가 두 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"오늘 밤 이 무서운 도시에 제게 가깝고 소중한 사람이 아무도 없으니."], ["May He have mercy on all who are in danger!”", "위험에 처한 모든 이들에게 자비를 베푸소서!\""]], [["Soon afterwards, the bell at the great gate sounded, and he thought, “They have come back!”", "곧이어 대문의 종이 울렸고, 그는 \"그들이 돌아왔구나!\" 하고 생각하며 앉아서 귀를 기울였다."], ["and sat listening. But, there was no loud irruption into the courtyard, as he had expected, and he heard the gate clash again, and all was quiet.", "그러나 그가 예상했던 것처럼 안마당으로 요란하게 밀려드는 소리는 없었고, 그는 대문이 다시 철커덩 닫히는 소리를 들었으며, 모든 것이 잠잠해졌다."]], [["The nervousness and dread that were upon him inspired that vague uneasiness respecting the Bank, which a great change would naturally awaken, with such feelings roused.", "그를 사로잡은 초조함과 두려움은 은행에 대한 막연한 불안을 불러일으켰으니, 그런 감정이 일깨워진 마당에 큰 변화가 자연스레 자아낼 법한 불안이었다."], ["It was well guarded, and he got up to go among the trusty people who were watching it, when his door suddenly opened, and two figures rushed in, at sight of which he fell back in amazement.", "은행은 잘 지켜지고 있었고, 그는 그곳을 지키는 믿음직한 사람들 사이로 가보려고 일어섰는데, 바로 그때 그의 방문이 갑자기 열리며 두 사람이 뛰어들어왔고, 그 모습에 그는 놀라 뒷걸음질 쳤다."]], [["Lucie and her father!", "루시와 그녀의 아버지였다!"], ["Lucie with her arms stretched out to him, and with that old look of earnestness so concentrated and intensified, that it seemed as though it had been stamped upon her face expressly to give force and power to it in this one passage of her life.", "루시는 그를 향해 두 팔을 뻗고 있었고, 그 한결같은 간절한 표정은 어찌나 응축되고 격해졌던지, 마치 그녀 생애의 이 한 대목에서 그 표정에 힘과 위력을 실어주려고 일부러 그녀의 얼굴에 찍어놓은 것만 같았다."]], [["“What is this?”", "\"이게 무슨 일이오?\""], ["cried Mr. Lorry, breathless and confused.", "로리 씨는 숨도 못 쉬고 어쩔 줄 모르며 외쳤다."], ["“What is the matter?", "\"무슨 일이오? 루시!"], ["Lucie! Manette!", "머네뜨 박사님!"], ["What has happened?", "무슨 일이 일어난 거요?"], ["What has brought you here?", "무엇 때문에 여기 온 거요?"], ["What is it?”", "도대체 무슨 일이오?\""]], [["With the look fixed upon him, in her paleness and wildness, she panted out in his arms, imploringly, “O my dear friend!", "그를 응시한 채, 창백하고 격앙된 모습으로, 그녀는 그의 품에 안겨 애원하듯 헐떡이며 말했다. \"오, 친애하는 친구분!"], ["My husband!”", "제 남편이!\""]], [["“Your husband, Lucie?”", "\"당신 남편이라니, 루시?\""]], [["“Charles.”", "\"샤를이.\""]], [["“What of Charles?” “Here.", "\"샤를이 어떻다는 거요?\""]], [["“Here, in Paris?”", "\"여기 있어요.\""]], [["“Has been here some days--three or four--I don’t know how many--I can’t collect my thoughts.", "\"여기, 빠리에?\" \"며칠째 여기 있어요 — 사나흘 — 며칠인지 모르겠어요 — 정신을 차릴 수가 없어요."], ["An errand of generosity brought him here unknown to us; he was stopped at the barrier, and sent to prison.”", "너그러운 일을 하려고 우리도 모르게 여기 온 거예요. 그이는 관문에서 붙들려 감옥으로 보내졌어요.\""]], [["The old man uttered an irrepressible cry.", "노인은 억누를 수 없는 비명을 내질렀다."], ["Almost at the same moment, the bell of the great gate rang again, and a loud noise of feet and voices came pouring into the courtyard.", "거의 동시에 대문의 종이 다시 울렸고, 발소리와 목소리가 요란하게 안마당으로 쏟아져 들어왔다."]], [["“What is that noise?”", "\"저 소리는 무엇이오?\""], ["said the Doctor, turning towards the window.", "박사가 창 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Don’t look!”", "\"보지 마십시오!\""], ["cried Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 외쳤다."], ["“Don’t look out!", "\"내다보지 마십시오!"], ["Manette, for your life, don’t touch the blind!”", "머네뜨 박사님, 목숨이 걸렸으니, 덧문에 손대지 마십시오!\""]], [["The Doctor turned, with his hand upon the fastening of the window, and said, with a cool, bold smile:", "박사는 창의 걸쇠에 손을 얹은 채 돌아서서, 침착하고 대담한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“My dear friend, I have a charmed life in this city. I have been a Bastille prisoner.", "\"친애하는 친구여, 나는 이 도시에서 마법에 걸린 듯 보호받는 목숨이라오."], ["There is no patriot in Paris--in Paris?", "나는 바스띠유의 죄수였소. 빠리에서 — 빠리뿐이겠소?"], ["In France--who, knowing me to have been a prisoner in the Bastille, would touch me, except to overwhelm me with embraces, or carry me in triumph.", "프랑스 전역에서 — 내가 바스띠유의 죄수였음을 아는 애국자라면, 나를 끌어안아 압도하거나 의기양양하게 떠메고 다니려는 것이 아니고서야 나를 건드릴 자는 없소."], ["My old pain has given me a power that has brought us through the barrier, and gained us news of Charles there, and brought us here.", "내 오랜 고통이 내게 힘을 주어, 그 힘이 우리를 관문으로 통과시켰고, 거기서 샤를의 소식을 알아내게 해주었으며, 우리를 여기까지 데려왔소. 나는 그러리란 걸 알고 있었소."], ["I knew it would be so; I knew I could help Charles out of all danger; I told Lucie so.--What is that noise?”", "샤를을 어떤 위험에서든 구해낼 수 있음을 알고 있었소. 루시에게도 그렇게 말했고 — 저 소리는 무엇이오?\""], ["His hand was again upon the window.", "그의 손은 다시 창에 가 있었다."]], [["“Don’t look!”", "\"보지 마십시오!\""], ["cried Mr. Lorry, absolutely desperate.", "로리 씨가 그야말로 절박하게 외쳤다."], ["“No, Lucie, my dear, nor you!”", "\"안 됩니다, 루시, 아가야, 너도 마찬가지다!\""], ["He got his arm round her, and held her.", "그는 한 팔로 그녀를 감싸 붙들었다."], ["“Don’t be so terrified, my love.", "\"그렇게 무서워하지 마라, 얘야."], ["I solemnly swear to you that I know of no harm having happened to Charles; that I had no suspicion even of his being in this fatal place.", "내가 엄숙히 맹세하건대, 샤를에게 무슨 해가 닥쳤다는 이야기는 나는 전혀 아는 바 없으며, 그가 이 끔찍한 곳에 와 있으리라곤 의심조차 해본 적이 없단다."], ["What prison is he in?” “La Force!”", "그가 어느 감옥에 있느냐?\""]], [["“La Force!", "\"라포르스 감옥이에요!\""], ["Lucie, my child, if ever you were brave and serviceable in your life--and you were always both--you will compose yourself now, to do exactly as I bid you; for more depends upon it than you can think, or I can say.", "\"라포르스라니! 루시, 얘야, 네가 평생 단 한번이라도 용감하고 쓸모 있게 처신한 적이 있다면 — 너는 늘 그 두 가지 다 그러했지만 — 이제 네 마음을 가다듬어, 내가 시키는 대로 똑같이 하거라."], ["There is no help for you in any action on your part to-night; you cannot possibly stir out.", "네가 생각하는 것보다, 또 내가 말할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것이 거기에 달려 있으니까. 오늘 밤 네가 무슨 행동을 한들 도움이 될 것은 전혀 없다."], ["I say this, because what I must bid you to do for Charles’s sake, is the hardest thing to do of all.", "너는 결코 밖으로 나갈 수 없어. 이렇게 말하는 것은, 샤를을 위해 내가 너에게 시켜야 하는 일이 무엇보다도 가장 하기 어려운 일이기 때문이다."], ["You must instantly be obedient, still, and quiet.", "너는 당장 순순히 따라야 하고, 가만히, 조용히 있어야 한다."], ["You must let me put you in a room at the back here.", "내가 너를 이 뒤쪽 방에 들여보내도록 해다오."], ["You must leave your father and me alone for two minutes, and as there are Life and Death in the world you must not delay.”", "너는 두 사람만 — 네 아버지와 나만 — 이 분 동안 남겨두어야 한다. 세상에 삶과 죽음이 있는 만큼, 너는 지체해서는 안 된다.\""]], [["“I will be submissive to you.", "\"시키는 대로 따를게요."], ["I see in your face that you know I can do nothing else than this.", "제가 이것 말고는 달리 아무것도 할 수 없다는 걸 아저씨가 아신다는 게 얼굴에 보여요."], ["I know you are true.”", "아저씨가 진실하다는 걸 알아요.\""]], [["The old man kissed her, and hurried her into his room, and turned the key; then, came hurrying back to the Doctor, and opened the window and partly opened the blind, and put his hand upon the Doctor’s arm, and looked out with him into the courtyard.", "노인은 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 서둘러 그녀를 자기 방으로 들여보낸 뒤 열쇠를 돌려 잠갔다. 그러고는 급히 박사에게 돌아와 창을 열고 덧문을 반쯤 연 다음, 박사의 팔에 손을 얹고 그와 함께 안마당을 내다보았다."]], [["Looked out upon a throng of men and women: not enough in number, or near enough, to fill the courtyard: not more than forty or fifty in all.", "남자들과 여자들의 무리를 내다보았다 — 안마당을 가득 메울 만큼 수가 많지도, 그만큼 가까이 있지도 않았으니, 다 합쳐 마흔이나 쉰을 넘지 않았다."], ["The people in possession of the house had let them in at the gate, and they had rushed in to work at the grindstone; it had evidently been set up there for their purpose, as in a convenient and retired spot.", "저택을 차지한 사람들이 대문으로 그들을 들여보냈고, 그들은 숫돌에서 일하려고 몰려든 것이었다. 숫돌은 분명 그들의 용도에 맞게, 편리하고 외진 자리인 양 거기 설치되어 있었다."]], [["But, such awful workers, and such awful work!", "그러나 그 얼마나 끔찍한 일꾼들이며, 그 얼마나 끔찍한 일이었던가!"]], [["The grindstone had a double handle, and, turning at it madly were two men, whose faces, as their long hair flapped back when the whirlings of the grindstone brought their faces up, were more horrible and cruel than the visages of the wildest savages in their most barbarous disguise.", "숫돌에는 손잡이가 둘 달려 있었고, 그것을 미친 듯이 돌리고 있는 것은 두 사내였는데, 숫돌이 빙빙 돌면서 그들의 얼굴을 위로 들어 올릴 때 긴 머리카락이 뒤로 휘날리는 그 얼굴은, 가장 야만적으로 분장한 가장 사나운 미개인의 상판보다도 더 무시무시하고 잔인했다."], ["False eyebrows and false moustaches were stuck upon them, and their hideous countenances were all bloody and sweaty, and all awry with howling, and all staring and glaring with beastly excitement and want of sleep.", "그들에게는 가짜 눈썹과 가짜 콧수염이 붙어 있었고, 그 흉측한 낯짝은 온통 피와 땀투성이였으며, 고함을 질러대느라 온통 일그러져 있었고, 짐승 같은 흥분과 수면 부족으로 온통 눈을 부릅뜨고 번뜩이고 있었다."], ["As these ruffians turned and turned, their matted locks now flung forward over their eyes, now flung backward over their necks, some women held wine to their mouths that they might drink; and what with dropping blood, and what with dropping wine, and what with the stream of sparks struck out of the stone, all their wicked atmosphere seemed gore and fire.", "이 무뢰한들이 돌리고 또 돌리는 동안, 엉겨 붙은 머리채가 어떤 때는 앞으로 쏟아져 눈을 덮고 어떤 때는 뒤로 넘어가 목을 덮었는데, 몇몇 여자들이 그들의 입에 포도주를 대주어 마시게 했다. 떨어지는 피와, 떨어지는 포도주와, 돌에서 튀어나오는 불꽃의 줄기로 말미암아, 그들을 둘러싼 사악한 기운은 온통 핏빛과 불빛으로 보였다."], ["The eye could not detect one creature in the group free from the smear of blood.", "그 무리에서 핏자국 하나 묻지 않은 자는 눈을 씻고 보아도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Shouldering one another to get next at the sharpening-stone, were men stripped to the waist, with the stain all over their limbs and bodies; men in all sorts of rags, with the stain upon those rags; men devilishly set off with spoils of women’s lace and silk and ribbon, with the stain dyeing those trifles through and through.", "서로 어깨를 밀치며 갈이돌에 먼저 다가가려는 것은, 허리까지 벌거벗은 채 팔다리와 몸뚱이에 온통 핏자국이 묻은 사내들이었고, 온갖 누더기를 걸친 채 그 누더기에 핏자국이 묻은 사내들이었으며, 여자들에게서 약탈한 레이스와 비단과 리본으로 악마처럼 차려입은 채 그 자질구레한 장식들에 핏자국이 속속들이 물들어버린 사내들이었다."], ["Hatchets, knives, bayonets, swords, all brought to be sharpened, were all red with it.", "갈려고 가져온 손도끼, 칼, 총검, 검은 죄다 피로 시뻘겼다."], ["Some of the hacked swords were tied to the wrists of those who carried them, with strips of linen and fragments of dress: ligatures various in kind, but all deep of the one colour.", "날이 이 빠진 검들 가운데 더러는 아마포 조각과 옷가지 자투리로 그것을 든 자들의 손목에 동여매여 있었으니 — 종류는 가지각색이었으나 한결같이 한 가지 색으로 짙게 물든 끈들이었다."], ["And as the frantic wielders of these weapons snatched them from the stream of sparks and tore away into the streets, the same red hue was red in their frenzied eyes;--eyes which any unbrutalised beholder would have given twenty years of life, to petrify with a well-directed gun.", "그리고 이 무기를 휘두르는 광란의 무리가 불꽃의 줄기에서 무기를 낚아채 거리로 내달릴 때, 똑같은 핏빛이 그들의 광기 어린 눈 속에서도 시뻘겋게 타올랐다 — 짐승으로 전락하지 않은 사람이라면 누구든 제대로 겨눈 총 한 방으로 그 눈을 굳혀버릴 수만 있다면 제 목숨에서 이십 년이라도 내놓았을 그런 눈이었다."]], [["All this was seen in a moment, as the vision of a drowning man, or of any human creature at any very great pass, could see a world if it were there.", "이 모든 것은, 물에 빠진 사람의 환영처럼, 혹은 절체절명의 고비에 놓인 어떤 인간이든 만약 거기 한 세상이 있다면 그것을 볼 수 있듯이, 한순간에 눈에 들어왔다."], ["They drew back from the window, and the Doctor looked for explanation in his friend’s ashy face.", "두 사람은 창에서 물러섰고, 박사는 친구의 잿빛이 된 얼굴에서 설명을 구했다."]], [["“They are,”", "\"저들은,\""], ["Mr. Lorry whispered the words, glancing fearfully round at the locked room, “murdering the prisoners.", "로리 씨가 잠긴 방 쪽을 두려운 눈으로 흘끔거리며 그 말을 속삭였다, \"죄수들을 학살하고 있는 겁니다."], ["If you are sure of what you say; if you really have the power you think you have--as I believe you have--make yourself known to these devils, and get taken to La Force.", "당신이 한 말에 확신이 있다면, 당신이 지녔다고 생각하는 그 힘을 정말로 지녔다면 — 나는 당신이 그것을 지녔다고 믿습니다만 — 저 악마들에게 당신이 누구인지 알리고 라포르스로 데려가달라고 하십시오."], ["It may be too late, I don’t know, but let it not be a minute later!”", "너무 늦었을지도 모릅니다, 나도 모르지만, 단 일 분이라도 더 늦지 않게 하십시오!\""]], [["Doctor Manette pressed his hand, hastened bareheaded out of the room, and was in the courtyard when Mr. Lorry regained the blind.", "머네뜨 박사는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐고, 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 서둘러 방을 나섰고, 로리 씨가 덧문가로 돌아왔을 때는 이미 안마당에 나가 있었다."]], [["His streaming white hair, his remarkable face, and the impetuous confidence of his manner, as he put the weapons aside like water, carried him in an instant to the heart of the concourse at the stone.", "흩날리는 백발과, 범상치 않은 얼굴과, 무기를 물처럼 헤치며 나아가는 그 거침없이 당당한 태도가, 그를 순식간에 숫돌 둘레로 모여든 군중의 한복판으로 데려갔다."], ["For a few moments there was a pause, and a hurry, and a murmur, and the unintelligible sound of his voice; and then Mr.", "잠시 멈춤이 있었고, 술렁임이 있었고, 웅성거림이 있었고, 알아들을 수 없는 그의 목소리가 들렸다."], ["Lorry saw him, surrounded by all, and in the midst of a line of twenty men long, all linked shoulder to shoulder, and hand to shoulder, hurried out with cries of--“Live the Bastille prisoner!", "그러고는 로리 씨는, 그가 모두에게 에워싸인 채, 어깨와 어깨를, 손과 어깨를 맞물려 잇댄 스무 명 남짓한 사내들의 행렬 한가운데에서, 다음과 같은 외침과 함께 황급히 끌려 나가는 것을 보았다 — \"바스띠유의 죄수 만세!"], ["Help for the Bastille prisoner’s kindred in La Force!", "라포르스에 있는 바스띠유 죄수의 가족을 도와라!"], ["Room for the Bastille prisoner in front there!", "저 앞쪽에 바스띠유의 죄수가 지날 길을 비켜라!"], ["Save the prisoner Evrémonde at La Force!”", "라포르스의 죄수 에브레몽드를 구하라!\""], ["and a thousand answering shouts.", "그리고 이에 화답하는 천 갈래의 함성이 일었다."]], [["He closed the lattice again with a fluttering heart, closed the window and the curtain, hastened to Lucie, and told her that her father was assisted by the people, and gone in search of her husband.", "그는 두근거리는 가슴으로 격자 덧문을 다시 닫고, 창과 커튼을 닫은 뒤, 서둘러 루시에게 가서, 그녀의 아버지가 사람들의 도움을 받아 남편을 찾으러 갔다고 일러주었다."], ["He found her child and Miss Pross with her; but, it never occurred to him to be surprised by their appearance until a long time afterwards, when he sat watching them in such quiet as the night knew.", "그는 그녀 곁에 그녀의 아이와 프로스 양이 함께 있는 것을 보았으나, 그들이 거기 와 있는 것에 놀라야겠다는 생각은 그에게 떠오르지 않았으니, 한참 뒤에 밤이 허락하는 그 정도의 고요 속에서 그들을 지켜보며 앉아 있을 때에야 비로소 그러했다."]], [["Lucie had, by that time, fallen into a stupor on the floor at his feet, clinging to his hand.", "그 무렵 루시는 바닥에 쓰러져, 그의 발치에서 그의 손에 매달린 채 혼절해 있었다."], ["Miss Pross had laid the child down on his own bed, and her head had gradually fallen on the pillow beside her pretty charge.", "프로스 양은 아이를 그의 침대에 눕혀놓았는데, 그녀의 머리는 어느새 그 사랑스러운 아이 곁의 베개 위로 떨궈져 있었다."], ["O the long, long night, with the moans of the poor wife!", "오, 가련한 아내의 신음 소리와 함께한 그 길고 긴 밤이여!"], ["And O the long, long night, with no return of her father and no tidings!", "오, 그녀의 아버지도 돌아오지 않고 아무 소식도 없던 그 길고 긴 밤이여!"]], [["Twice more in the darkness the bell at the great gate sounded, and the irruption was repeated, and the grindstone whirled and spluttered.", "어둠 속에서 대문의 종이 두 번 더 울렸고, 무리가 밀려드는 일이 되풀이되었으며, 숫돌이 빙빙 돌며 불꽃을 튀겼다."], ["“What is it?”", "\"저게 뭐예요?\""], ["cried Lucie, affrighted.", "루시가 겁에 질려 외쳤다."], ["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["The soldiers’ swords are sharpened there,”", "군인들의 검을 저기서 가는 거란다.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“The place is national property now, and used as a kind of armoury, my love.”", "\"이제 저곳은 국가 재산이 되어서, 일종의 무기고처럼 쓰이고 있단다, 얘야.\""]], [["Twice more in all; but, the last spell of work was feeble and fitful.", "다 합쳐 두 번 더였다. 그러나 마지막으로 일하던 동안은 그 기세가 미약하고 띄엄띄엄했다."], ["Soon afterwards the day began to dawn, and he softly detached himself from the clasping hand, and cautiously looked out again.", "곧이어 동이 트기 시작했고, 그는 매달린 손에서 살그머니 몸을 떼어, 조심스레 다시 밖을 내다보았다."], ["A man, so besmeared that he might have been a sorely wounded soldier creeping back to consciousness on a field of slain, was rising from the pavement by the side of the grindstone, and looking about him with a vacant air.", "어떤 사내 하나가, 어찌나 피범벅이 되었던지 시체가 널린 들판에서 의식을 되찾으려 기어 나오는 중상 입은 병사라 해도 믿을 만한 자가, 숫돌 옆 포석에서 몸을 일으켜 멍한 표정으로 주위를 둘러보고 있었다."], ["Shortly, this worn-out murderer descried in the imperfect light one of the carriages of Monseigneur, and, staggering to that gorgeous vehicle, climbed in at the door, and shut himself up to take his rest on its dainty cushions.", "이내 이 기진맥진한 살인자는 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 영주님의 마차 한 대를 발견하고는, 그 호화로운 탈것으로 비틀비틀 다가가 문으로 기어 올라가, 그 고운 방석 위에서 쉬려고 안에 틀어박혔다."]], [["The great grindstone, Earth, had turned when Mr. Lorry looked out again, and the sun was red on the courtyard.", "로리 씨가 다시 밖을 내다보았을 때, 그 거대한 숫돌인 지구가 돌아가 있었고, 안마당에는 붉은 해가 비치고 있었다."], ["But, the lesser grindstone stood alone there in the calm morning air, with a red upon it that the sun had never given, and would never take away.", "그러나 그보다 작은 숫돌은 잔잔한 아침 공기 속에 홀로 서 있었으니, 그 위에는 해가 결코 입혀준 적도 없고 결코 거두어가지도 못할 붉은빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III. The Shadow", "# CHAPTER III. The Shadow"]], [["One of the first considerations which arose in the business mind of Mr.", "업무 시간이 다시 돌아오자 로리 씨의 사무적인 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각 가운데 하나는 이것이었다."], ["Lorry when business hours came round, was this:--that he had no right to imperil Tellson’s by sheltering the wife of an emigrant prisoner under the Bank roof.", "망명자 죄수의 아내를 은행 지붕 아래 숨겨 둠으로써 텔슨을 위태롭게 할 권리가 자신에게는 없다는 것이었다."], ["His own possessions, safety, life, he would have hazarded for Lucie and her child, without a moment’s demur; but the great trust he held was not his own, and as to that business charge he was a strict man of business.", "자기 자신의 재산이며 안전이며 목숨이라면 한순간의 망설임도 없이 루시와 그 아이를 위해 내걸었을 테지만, 그가 맡고 있는 그 막중한 신탁은 그의 것이 아니었으며, 그 업무상의 책임에 관한 한 그는 엄정한 사무가였다."]], [["At first, his mind reverted to Defarge, and he thought of finding out the wine-shop again and taking counsel with its master in reference to the safest dwelling-place in the distracted state of the city.", "처음에 그의 마음은 드파르주에게로 돌아갔고, 다시 그 포도주 가게를 찾아내어 어지러운 도시 형편 속에서 가장 안전한 거처에 관해 그 주인과 상의해 볼까 생각했다."], ["But, the same consideration that suggested him, repudiated him; he lived in the most violent Quarter, and doubtless was influential there, and deep in its dangerous workings.", "그러나 그를 떠올리게 했던 바로 그 고려가 또한 그를 물리쳤다. 그는 가장 험악한 구역에 살고 있었고, 그곳에서 영향력이 큰 인물임에 틀림없었으며, 그 위험한 움직임에 깊이 발을 담그고 있었다."]], [["Noon coming, and the Doctor not returning, and every minute’s delay tending to compromise Tellson’s, Mr. Lorry advised with Lucie.", "정오가 되었는데도 의사는 돌아오지 않았고, 일분 일분 지체될 때마다 텔슨이 위태로워질 형편이라, 로리 씨는 루시와 의논했다."], ["She said that her father had spoken of hiring a lodging for a short term, in that Quarter, near the Banking-house.", "그녀는 아버지가 그 구역의 은행 근처에 잠시 머무를 셋방을 얻겠다는 말을 했다고 일러 주었다."], ["As there was no business objection to this, and as he foresaw that even if it were all well with Charles, and he were to be released, he could not hope to leave the city, Mr. Lorry went out in quest of such a lodging, and found a suitable one, high up in a removed by-street where the closed blinds in all the other windows of a high melancholy square of buildings marked deserted homes.", "이에 대해서는 업무상 거리낄 것이 없었고, 또 설령 샤를에게 모든 일이 잘 풀려 그가 풀려난다 하더라도 도시를 떠나기는 바랄 수 없으리라는 것을 그가 내다보았으므로, 로리 씨는 그런 셋방을 찾아 나섰고, 외진 골목 높직한 곳에서 적당한 곳을 하나 찾아냈는데, 그 음울한 사각형 건물군의 다른 모든 창문에 닫힌 덧문들이 버려진 집들을 표시하고 있었다."]], [["To this lodging he at once removed Lucie and her child, and Miss Pross: giving them what comfort he could, and much more than he had himself.", "그는 곧장 이 셋방으로 루시와 그 아이, 그리고 프로스 양을 옮겨 주었다. 자신이 줄 수 있는 만큼, 자기 자신이 누리는 것보다 훨씬 많은 위안을 그들에게 베풀었다."], ["He left Jerry with them, as a figure to fill a doorway that would bear considerable knocking on the head, and returned to his own occupations.", "그는 제리를 그들 곁에 남겨 두었는데, 어지간히 머리를 두들겨 맞아도 견딜 만한, 출입구를 채우기에 알맞은 인물이었기 때문이다. 그러고는 자신의 일로 돌아왔다."], ["A disturbed and doleful mind he brought to bear upon them, and slowly and heavily the day lagged on with him.", "어지럽고 침울한 마음을 안고 그 일에 매달렸으며, 그날 하루는 더디고 무겁게 그와 함께 흘러갔다."]], [["It wore itself out, and wore him out with it, until the Bank closed.", "하루는 스스로 닳아 가고, 그와 더불어 그를 닳게 하다가, 마침내 은행이 문을 닫았다."], ["He was again alone in his room of the previous night, considering what to do next, when he heard a foot upon the stair.", "그는 다시금 전날 밤의 그 방에 홀로 있으면서 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 궁리하던 차에, 계단을 오르는 발소리를 들었다."], ["In a few moments, a man stood in his presence, who, with a keenly observant look at him, addressed him by his name.", "잠시 뒤, 한 남자가 그의 앞에 서서 날카롭게 살피는 눈길로 그를 보더니 그의 이름을 불렀다."]], [["“Your servant,”", "\"무엇을 도와드릴까요.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Do you know me?”", "\"저를 아십니까?\""]], [["He was a strongly made man with dark curling hair, from forty-five to fifty years of age.", "그는 검은 곱슬머리에 다부지게 생긴, 마흔다섯에서 쉰 살가량의 남자였다."], ["For answer he repeated, without any change of emphasis, the words:", "대답 대신 그는 어조에 조금도 변화를 주지 않고 그 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“Do you know me?”", "\"저를 아십니까?\""]], [["“I have seen you somewhere.”", "\"어디선가 뵌 적이 있군요.\""]], [["“Perhaps at my wine-shop?”", "\"혹시 제 포도주 가게에서?\""]], [["Much interested and agitated, Mr.", "크게 흥미를 느끼며 동요한 로리 씨가 말했다."], ["Lorry said: “You come from Doctor Manette?”", "\"마네뜨 의사 선생께서 보내서 오셨습니까?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["I come from Doctor Manette.”", "마네뜨 의사 선생께서 보내서 왔습니다.\""]], [["“And what says he?", "\"그분이 뭐라 하십니까?"], ["What does he send me?”", "저에게 무엇을 보내셨습니까?\""]], [["Defarge gave into his anxious hand, an open scrap of paper.", "드파르주는 애가 타서 내미는 그의 손에 펼쳐진 종잇조각 하나를 건넸다."], ["It bore the words in the Doctor’s writing:", "거기에는 의사의 필체로 이런 말이 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Charles is safe, but I cannot safely leave this place yet.", "\"샤를은 무사하나, 나는 아직 이곳을 안전하게 떠날 수가 없소."], ["I have obtained the favour that the bearer has a short note from Charles to his wife.", "이 사람이 샤를에게서 그 아내에게 보내는 짧은 글을 지니고 가도록 허락을 얻었소."], ["Let the bearer see his wife.”", "이 사람이 그 아내를 만나게 해 주시오.\""]], [["It was dated from La Force, within an hour.", "라포르스에서 한 시간 안에 쓴 날짜가 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Will you accompany me,” said Mr.", "\"이분 아내가 사는 곳까지,\""], ["Lorry, joyfully relieved after reading this note aloud, “to where his wife resides?”", "이 쪽지를 소리 내어 읽고 나서 기쁘게 안도한 로리 씨가 말했다. \"저와 함께 가시겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그러지요.\""], ["returned Defarge.", "드파르주가 대답했다."]], [["Scarcely noticing as yet, in what a curiously reserved and mechanical way Defarge spoke, Mr. Lorry put on his hat and they went down into the courtyard.", "드파르주가 얼마나 묘하게 절제된, 기계적인 태도로 말하는지 아직 거의 눈치채지 못한 채, 로리 씨는 모자를 쓰고 그들은 안마당으로 내려갔다."], ["There, they found two women; one, knitting.", "거기서 그들은 두 여자를 만났는데, 그중 하나는 뜨개질을 하고 있었다."]], [["“Madame Defarge, surely!” said Mr.", "\"드파르주 부인이로군요, 틀림없이!\""], ["Lorry, who had left her in exactly the same attitude some seventeen years ago.", "로리 씨가 말했다. 그는 십칠 년쯤 전에 바로 똑같은 자세로 있던 그녀를 두고 떠났던 것이다."]], [["“It is she,”", "\"그녀가 맞소.\""], ["observed her husband.", "그녀의 남편이 말했다."]], [["“Does Madame go with us?”", "\"부인께서도 우리와 함께 가십니까?\""], ["inquired Mr. Lorry, seeing that she moved as they moved.", "그녀가 자기들 움직임을 따라 움직이는 것을 보고 로리 씨가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["That she may be able to recognise the faces and know the persons.", "얼굴들을 알아보고 사람들을 익혀 두기 위해서요."], ["It is for their safety.”", "그들의 안전을 위한 것이오.\""]], [["Beginning to be struck by Defarge’s manner, Mr. Lorry looked dubiously at him, and led the way.", "드파르주의 태도에 점차 마음이 쓰이기 시작한 로리 씨는 미심쩍은 눈으로 그를 보고는 앞장섰다."], ["Both the women followed; the second woman being The Vengeance.", "두 여자가 모두 뒤를 따랐는데, 두번째 여자는 복수의 여신이었다."]], [["They passed through the intervening streets as quickly as they might, ascended the staircase of the new domicile, were admitted by Jerry, and found Lucie weeping, alone. She was thrown into a transport by the tidings Mr.", "그들은 그 사이의 거리들을 되도록 빨리 지나, 새 거처의 계단을 올라갔고, 제리의 안내를 받아 들어가, 홀로 울고 있는 루시를 발견했다."], ["Lorry gave her of her husband, and clasped the hand that delivered his note--little thinking what it had been doing near him in the night, and might, but for a chance, have done to him.", "그녀는 로리 씨가 전해 준 남편의 소식에 환희에 휩싸였고, 그 쪽지를 건네준 손을 부여잡았다. 그 손이 그날 밤 그의 곁에서 무슨 일을 했었는지, 그리고 어쩌다 한순간의 우연이 아니었던들 그에게 무슨 일을 했을지는 까맣게 모른 채."]], [["“DEAREST,--Take courage.", "\"사랑하는 그대여, 용기를 내시오."], ["I am well, and your father has influence around me.", "나는 잘 있고, 그대의 아버지가 내 주위에 영향력을 가지고 있소."], ["You cannot answer this.", "이것에 답장할 수는 없소."], ["Kiss our child for me.”", "나 대신 우리 아이에게 입맞춰 주시오.\""]], [["That was all the writing.", "적혀 있는 것은 그것이 전부였다."], ["It was so much, however, to her who received it, that she turned from Defarge to his wife, and kissed one of the hands that knitted.", "그러나 그것을 받아 든 그녀에게는 너무도 큰 것이어서, 그녀는 드파르주에게서 그 아내에게로 몸을 돌려, 뜨개질하는 그 손 가운데 하나에 입을 맞추었다."], ["It was a passionate, loving, thankful, womanly action, but the hand made no response--dropped cold and heavy, and took to its knitting again.", "그것은 격정적이고 사랑스럽고 감사에 찬, 여성스러운 행동이었으나, 그 손은 아무런 응답이 없었다. 차갑고 묵직하게 떨어지더니, 다시 뜨개질을 시작했다."]], [["There was something in its touch that gave Lucie a check.", "그 손이 닿는 감촉에는 루시를 멈칫하게 하는 무언가가 있었다."], ["She stopped in the act of putting the note in her bosom, and, with her hands yet at her neck, looked terrified at Madame Defarge.", "그녀는 쪽지를 품에 넣으려다가 동작을 멈추었고, 손을 아직 목께에 댄 채로, 겁에 질린 얼굴로 드파르주 부인을 바라보았다."], ["Madame Defarge met the lifted eyebrows and forehead with a cold, impassive stare.", "드파르주 부인은 치켜올라간 눈썹과 이마를 차갑고 무표정한 응시로 맞받았다."]], [["“My dear,”", "\"부인,\" 로리 씨가 끼어들어 설명했다."], ["said Mr.", "\"거리에서는 폭동이 자주 일어납니다."], ["Lorry, striking in to explain; “there are frequent risings in the streets; and, although it is not likely they will ever trouble you, Madame Defarge wishes to see those whom she has the power to protect at such times, to the end that she may know them--that she may identify them.", "그리고 그런 일들이 부인을 괴롭힐 것 같지는 않지만, 드파르주 부인께서는 그러한 때에 자신이 보호할 권한이 있는 이들을 보아 두기를 바라십니다. 그들을 알아 두기 위해서요, 그들을 식별할 수 있도록 말입니다."], ["I believe,” said Mr.", "제 생각에는,\""], ["Lorry, rather halting in his reassuring words, as the stony manner of all the three impressed itself upon him more and more, “I state the case, Citizen Defarge?”", "세 사람 모두의 돌덩이 같은 태도가 점점 더 강하게 그를 짓누르는 통에, 그는 안심시키려는 말을 다소 더듬거리며 말했다. \"제가 사정을 제대로 말씀드린 것 같은데요, 드파르주 시민?\""]], [["Defarge looked gloomily at his wife, and gave no other answer than a gruff sound of acquiescence.", "드파르주는 음울한 눈으로 자기 아내를 보았고, 무뚝뚝하게 동의하는 소리 외에 다른 대답은 하지 않았다."]], [["“You had better, Lucie,” said Mr.", "\"루시, 사랑하는 그 아이를,\""], ["Lorry, doing all he could to propitiate, by tone and manner, “have the dear child here, and our good Pross.", "어조와 태도로 할 수 있는 한 그들의 비위를 맞추려 애쓰며 로리 씨가 말했다. \"이리로 데려오는 게 좋겠소."], ["Our good Pross, Defarge, is an English lady, and knows no French.”", "그리고 우리의 착한 프로스도. 우리의 착한 프로스는, 드파르주, 영국 부인이고 프랑스어를 전혀 모릅니다.\""]], [["The lady in question, whose rooted conviction that she was more than a match for any foreigner, was not to be shaken by distress and, danger, appeared with folded arms, and observed in English to The Vengeance, whom her eyes first encountered, “Well, I am sure, Boldface!", "문제의 그 부인은, 어떤 외국인이라도 자기가 능히 당해 낼 수 있다는 뿌리 깊은 확신을 품고 있었고, 고통과 위험에도 흔들리지 않은 채, 팔짱을 끼고 나타나서는, 눈이 처음으로 마주친 복수의 여신을 향해 영어로 한마디 했다. \"흥, 정말이지, 뻔뻔한 양반아!"], ["I hope you are pretty well!”", "너야 아주 잘 지내시겠지!\""], ["She also bestowed a British cough on Madame Defarge; but, neither of the two took much heed of her.", "그녀는 또 드파르주 부인에게는 영국식 기침을 한 번 선사했다. 그러나 두 여자 가운데 누구도 그녀에게 별 신경을 쓰지 않았다."]], [["“Is that his child?”", "\"저 아이가 그의 자식인가?\""], ["said Madame Defarge, stopping in her work for the first time, and pointing her knitting-needle at little Lucie as if it were the finger of Fate.", "드파르주 부인이 처음으로 손을 멈추고, 마치 운명의 손가락이기라도 한 듯 뜨개바늘을 어린 루시에게 겨누며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, madame,” answered Mr.", "\"그렇습니다, 부인.\""], ["Lorry; “this is our poor prisoner’s darling daughter, and only child.”", "로리 씨가 대답했다. \"이 아이가 우리 가엾은 죄수의 사랑하는 딸이자 외동입니다.\""]], [["The shadow attendant on Madame Defarge and her party seemed to fall so threatening and dark on the child, that her mother instinctively kneeled on the ground beside her, and held her to her breast.", "드파르주 부인과 그 일행에게 따라붙은 그림자가 그 아이 위로 어찌나 위협적이고 어둡게 드리우는 듯했던지, 아이의 어머니는 본능적으로 그 곁 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 아이를 가슴에 끌어안았다."], ["The shadow attendant on Madame Defarge and her party seemed then to fall, threatening and dark, on both the mother and the child.", "그러자 드파르주 부인과 그 일행에게 따라붙은 그림자는 위협적이고 어둡게, 어머니와 아이 양쪽 위로 드리우는 듯했다."]], [["“It is enough, my husband,”", "\"이만하면 됐소, 여보.\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."], ["“I have seen them.", "\"그들을 봤소."], ["We may go.”", "우리는 가도 되오.\""]], [["But, the suppressed manner had enough of menace in it--not visible and presented, but indistinct and withheld--to alarm Lucie into saying, as she laid her appealing hand on Madame Defarge’s dress:", "그러나 그 억눌린 태도에는 — 눈에 보이게 내세운 것이 아니라 어렴풋이 감추어진 것이었으나 — 충분한 위협이 깃들어 있어서, 루시는 불안에 떨며 드파르주 부인의 옷에 호소하듯 손을 얹고 말했다."]], [["“You will be good to my poor husband.", "\"제 가엾은 남편에게 잘해 주시겠지요."], ["You will do him no harm.", "그이를 해치지 않으시겠지요."], ["You will help me to see him if you can?”", "할 수 있으시다면 제가 그이를 만나도록 도와주시겠지요?\""]], [["“Your husband is not my business here,”", "\"당신 남편은 여기서 내가 상관할 바가 아니오.\""], ["returned Madame Defarge, looking down at her with perfect composure.", "드파르주 부인이 더없이 차분하게 그녀를 내려다보며 대답했다."], ["“It is the daughter of your father who is my business here.”", "\"내가 여기서 상관할 바는 당신 아버지의 딸이오.\""]], [["“For my sake, then, be merciful to my husband.", "\"그러면 저를 보아서라도, 제 남편에게 자비를 베풀어 주세요."], ["For my child’s sake!", "제 아이를 보아서라도요!"], ["She will put her hands together and pray you to be merciful.", "이 아이가 두 손을 모아 자비를 베풀어 달라고 빌 거예요."], ["We are more afraid of you than of these others.”", "우리는 다른 누구보다도 당신이 두렵습니다.\""]], [["Madame Defarge received it as a compliment, and looked at her husband.", "드파르주 부인은 그것을 칭찬으로 받아들이고는 자기 남편을 바라보았다."], ["Defarge, who had been uneasily biting his thumb-nail and looking at her, collected his face into a sterner expression.", "불안하게 엄지손톱을 깨물며 그녀를 보고 있던 드파르주는, 자기 얼굴을 한층 더 엄한 표정으로 다잡았다."]], [["“What is it that your husband says in that little letter?”", "\"당신 남편이 그 짤막한 편지에서 뭐라고 한 거요?\""], ["asked Madame Defarge, with a lowering smile.", "드파르주 부인이 음울하게 미소 지으며 물었다."], ["“Influence; he says something touching influence?”", "\"영향력 — 영향력에 관해 무어라고 쓴 모양인데?\""]], [["“That my father,”", "\"제 아버지가,\""], ["said Lucie, hurriedly taking the paper from her breast, but with her alarmed eyes on her questioner and not on it, “has much influence around him.”", "루시가 황급히 품에서 그 종이를 꺼냈지만, 그것이 아니라 묻는 이에게 겁에 질린 눈길을 둔 채로 말했다. \"그이 주위에서 큰 영향력을 갖고 있다고요.\""]], [["“Surely it will release him!”", "\"그렇다면 분명 그를 풀어 주겠군!\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."], ["“Let it do so.”", "\"그렇게 되도록 내버려 두지.\""]], [["“As a wife and mother,”", "\"아내이자 어머니로서,\""], ["cried Lucie, most earnestly, “I implore you to have pity on me and not to exercise any power that you possess, against my innocent husband, but to use it in his behalf.", "루시가 더없이 간절하게 외쳤다. \"저를 가엾이 여기시어, 당신이 가진 어떤 힘도 제 죄 없는 남편에게 거슬러 쓰지 마시고, 그이를 위해 써 주시기를 애원합니다."], ["O sister-woman, think of me.", "오, 같은 여인이여, 저를 생각해 주세요."], ["As a wife and mother!”", "아내이자 어머니로서요!\""]], [["Madame Defarge looked, coldly as ever, at the suppliant, and said, turning to her friend The Vengeance:", "드파르주 부인은 여전히 차가운 눈으로 그 애원하는 이를 바라보더니, 자기 친구인 복수의 여신을 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“The wives and mothers we have been used to see, since we were as little as this child, and much less, have not been greatly considered?", "\"우리가 이 아이만 했을 때부터, 아니 훨씬 더 어렸을 때부터 보아 온 그 아내들과 어머니들이 별로 배려받기나 했던가?"], ["We have known their husbands and fathers laid in prison and kept from them, often enough?", "우리는 그네들의 남편과 아버지가 감옥에 갇혀 그들에게서 떼어 놓이는 꼴을 숱하게 보지 않았던가?"], ["All our lives, we have seen our sister-women suffer, in themselves and in their children, poverty, nakedness, hunger, thirst, sickness, misery, oppression and neglect of all kinds?”", "평생토록 우리는, 같은 여인들이 제 몸으로, 또 제 자식들로, 가난과 헐벗음과 굶주림과 목마름과 병과 비참과 억압과 온갖 방치를 겪는 것을 보아 오지 않았던가?\""]], [["“We have seen nothing else,”", "\"우리는 그것 말고 다른 것은 본 적이 없지.\""], ["returned The Vengeance.", "복수의 여신이 대답했다."]], [["“We have borne this a long time,”", "\"우리는 이것을 오랫동안 견뎌 왔어.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, turning her eyes again upon Lucie. “Judge you!", "드파르주 부인이 다시 루시에게로 눈길을 돌리며 말했다."], ["Is it likely that the trouble of one wife and mother would be much to us now?”", "\"당신이 판단해 보시오! 아내이자 어머니 하나의 괴로움이 이제 와서 우리에게 무슨 대수겠소?\""]], [["She resumed her knitting and went out.", "그녀는 다시 뜨개질을 시작하며 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The Vengeance followed.", "복수의 여신이 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Defarge went last, and closed the door.", "드파르주는 맨 마지막에 나가서 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Courage, my dear Lucie,”", "\"용기를 내요, 사랑하는 루시.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry, as he raised her.", "로리 씨가 그녀를 일으키며 말했다."], ["“Courage, courage!", "\"용기를, 용기를!"], ["So far all goes well with us--much, much better than it has of late gone with many poor souls.", "지금까지는 우리에게 모든 일이 잘되어 가고 있소. 근래에 수많은 가엾은 사람들에게 닥친 것보다 훨씬, 훨씬 나은 형편이오."], ["Cheer up, and have a thankful heart.”", "기운을 내고, 감사하는 마음을 가져요.\""]], [["“I am not thankless, I hope, but that dreadful woman seems to throw a shadow on me and on all my hopes.”", "\"저도 감사할 줄 모르는 사람은 아닐 거예요. 하지만 저 무서운 여자가 저에게, 그리고 제 모든 희망 위에 그림자를 드리우는 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“Tut, tut!” said Mr.", "\"쯧, 쯧!\""], ["Lorry; “what is this despondency in the brave little breast?", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"용감한 작은 가슴속에 이 무슨 낙담이오?"], ["A shadow indeed!", "그림자라니!"], ["No substance in it, Lucie.”", "거기에는 실체가 없소, 루시.\""]], [["But the shadow of the manner of these Defarges was dark upon himself, for all that, and in his secret mind it troubled him greatly.", "그러나 그 드파르주 부부의 태도가 드리운 그림자는, 그럼에도 불구하고 그 자신에게도 어둡게 드리워 있었고, 마음속 깊은 곳에서 그를 크게 괴롭혔다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV. Calm in Storm", "# CHAPTER IV. Calm in Storm"]], [["Doctor Manette did not return until the morning of the fourth day of his absence.", "「마네뜨」 박사는 집을 떠난 지 나흘째 되는 날 아침에야 돌아왔다."], ["So much of what had happened in that dreadful time as could be kept from the knowledge of Lucie was so well concealed from her, that not until long afterwards, when France and she were far apart, did she know that eleven hundred defenceless prisoners of both sexes and all ages had been killed by the populace; that four days and nights had been darkened by this deed of horror; and that the air around her had been tainted by the slain.", "그 끔찍한 시간 동안 일어난 일들 가운데 「루시」에게 알리지 않을 수 있는 것은 그녀에게 어찌나 철저히 감춰졌던지, 한참 뒤에 「프랑스」와 그녀가 멀리 떨어지고 나서야 그녀는 비로소 알게 되었다. 남녀노소를 가리지 않은, 무방비 상태의 죄수 천백 명이 군중에게 살해당했다는 것을, 그 공포의 만행으로 나흘 밤낮이 어둠에 잠겼다는 것을, 그리고 그녀를 둘러싼 공기가 살해된 자들로 인해 오염되었다는 것을."], ["She only knew that there had been an attack upon the prisons, that all political prisoners had been in danger, and that some had been dragged out by the crowd and murdered.", "그녀가 아는 것이라고는 감옥을 향한 습격이 있었다는 것, 모든 정치범이 위험에 처해 있었다는 것, 그리고 일부는 군중에게 끌려 나가 살해되었다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["To Mr.", "「로리」"], ["Lorry, the Doctor communicated under an injunction of secrecy on which he had no need to dwell, that the crowd had taken him through a scene of carnage to the prison of La Force.", "씨에게는, 박사가 굳이 강조할 필요도 없는 비밀 엄수의 다짐 아래 이렇게 털어놓았다. 군중이 그를 학살의 현장을 지나 「라포르스」"], ["That, in the prison he had found a self-appointed Tribunal sitting, before which the prisoners were brought singly, and by which they were rapidly ordered to be put forth to be massacred, or to be released, or (in a few cases) to be sent back to their cells.", "감옥으로 데려갔다는 것을. 그 감옥에서 그는 스스로 임명한 재판소가 열려 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그 앞으로 죄수들이 한 사람씩 끌려 나오면, 그 재판소는 신속하게 그들을 끌어내 학살하라거나, 석방하라거나, (몇몇 경우에는) 다시 감방으로 돌려보내라고 명령했다는 것을."], ["That, presented by his conductors to this Tribunal, he had announced himself by name and profession as having been for eighteen years a secret and unaccused prisoner in the Bastille; that, one of the body so sitting in judgment had risen and identified him, and that this man was Defarge.", "그를 데려온 자들에 의해 이 재판소 앞에 서게 되자, 그는 자신이 「바스띠유」에서 십팔 년간 기소도 없이 비밀리에 갇혀 있던 죄수였다며 이름과 신분을 밝혔다는 것을. 그렇게 자리에 앉아 심판을 내리던 무리 가운데 하나가 일어나 그를 알아보았는데, 그 사람이 바로 「드파르주」였다는 것을."]], [["That, hereupon he had ascertained, through the registers on the table, that his son-in-law was among the living prisoners, and had pleaded hard to the Tribunal--of whom some members were asleep and some awake, some dirty with murder and some clean, some sober and some not--for his life and liberty.", "이 일로 그는 탁자 위의 명부를 통해 자신의 사위가 살아 있는 죄수들 가운데 있음을 확인하고는, 재판소를 향해 그의 목숨과 자유를 위해 간절히 호소했다는 것을 — 그 재판소의 구성원들 가운데 일부는 졸고 있고 일부는 깨어 있었으며, 일부는 살인으로 더럽혀져 있고 일부는 깨끗했으며, 일부는 술에 취해 있지 않고 일부는 취해 있었다."], ["That, in the first frantic greetings lavished on himself as a notable sufferer under the overthrown system, it had been accorded to him to have Charles Darnay brought before the lawless Court, and examined.", "무너진 체제 아래서 고초를 겪은 저명한 수난자로서 그에게 쏟아진 처음의 광적인 환대 속에서, 「샤를 다네」를 그 무법의 법정 앞으로 끌어내 심문하도록 허락받았다는 것을."], ["That, he seemed on the point of being at once released, when the tide in his favour met with some unexplained check (not intelligible to the Doctor), which led to a few words of secret conference.", "그가 곧바로 석방될 듯한 찰나에, 그에게 유리하던 흐름이 (박사로서는 이해할 수 없는) 어떤 설명 없는 제동에 부딪혔고, 그것이 몇 마디 은밀한 협의로 이어졌다는 것을."], ["That, the man sitting as President had then informed Doctor Manette that the prisoner must remain in custody, but should, for his sake, be held inviolate in safe custody.", "그러자 의장으로 앉아 있던 사람이 「마네뜨」 박사에게, 죄수는 계속 구금되어야 하지만, 박사를 봐서 안전한 구금 상태에서 침해받지 않도록 보호하겠다고 알렸다는 것을."], ["That, immediately, on a signal, the prisoner was removed to the interior of the prison again; but, that he, the Doctor, had then so strongly pleaded for permission to remain and assure himself that his son-in-law was, through no malice or mischance, delivered to the concourse whose murderous yells outside the gate had often drowned the proceedings, that he had obtained the permission, and had remained in that Hall of Blood until the danger was over.", "곧바로 신호가 떨어지자 죄수는 다시 감옥 안쪽으로 끌려갔으나, 박사 자신은 그곳에 남아 자기 사위가 어떤 악의나 불운으로 인해, 문밖에서 살기 어린 고함으로 종종 재판 진행 소리마저 덮어버리던 그 무리에게 넘겨지지 않았음을 직접 확인하게 해달라고 어찌나 강하게 호소했던지, 마침내 허락을 얻어 위험이 지나갈 때까지 그 피의 홀에 남아 있었다는 것을."]], [["The sights he had seen there, with brief snatches of food and sleep by intervals, shall remain untold.", "그가 거기서 간간이 짧게 음식을 먹고 잠을 자며 목격한 광경들은 입에 담지 않은 채 두기로 하자."], ["The mad joy over the prisoners who were saved, had astounded him scarcely less than the mad ferocity against those who were cut to pieces.", "목숨을 건진 죄수들을 향한 광적인 환희는, 갈가리 찢긴 자들을 향한 광적인 잔인함 못지않게 그를 경악케 했다."], ["One prisoner there was, he said, who had been discharged into the street free, but at whom a mistaken savage had thrust a pike as he passed out.", "그가 말하기를, 한 죄수가 자유의 몸이 되어 거리로 풀려났는데, 그가 빠져나가는 순간 어떤 착각한 야만인이 그에게 창을 찔렀다고 했다."], ["Being besought to go to him and dress the wound, the Doctor had passed out at the same gate, and had found him in the arms of a company of Samaritans, who were seated on the bodies of their victims.", "그에게 가서 상처를 치료해달라는 간청을 받고 박사는 같은 문으로 빠져나갔는데, 그가 자기네 희생자들의 시체 위에 걸터앉은 한 무리의 사마리아인들 품에 안겨 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["With an inconsistency as monstrous as anything in this awful nightmare, they had helped the healer, and tended the wounded man with the gentlest solicitude--had made a litter for him and escorted him carefully from the spot--had then caught up their weapons and plunged anew into a butchery so dreadful, that the Doctor had covered his eyes with his hands, and swooned away in the midst of it.", "이 끔찍한 악몽 속의 그 무엇 못지않게 기괴한 모순으로, 그들은 그 치료자를 돕고 더할 나위 없이 다정한 보살핌으로 부상자를 돌보았으며 — 그를 위해 들것을 만들고 그 자리에서 조심스레 호위해 데려가더니 — 그러고는 다시 무기를 집어 들고, 박사가 두 손으로 눈을 가린 채 그 한복판에서 정신을 잃을 만큼 끔찍한 도살에 새로이 뛰어들었다."]], [["As Mr.", "「로리」"], ["Lorry received these confidences, and as he watched the face of his friend now sixty-two years of age, a misgiving arose within him that such dread experiences would revive the old danger.", "씨는 이러한 비밀스러운 이야기를 전해 들으며, 이제 예순두 살이 된 친구의 얼굴을 지켜보는 동안, 그런 무서운 경험들이 옛 위험을 되살리지나 않을까 하는 불안이 마음속에 일었다."]], [["But, he had never seen his friend in his present aspect: he had never at all known him in his present character.", "그러나 그는 지금 같은 모습의 친구를 한 번도 본 적이 없었으니, 지금 같은 성품의 그를 도무지 알지 못했던 것이다."], ["For the first time the Doctor felt, now, that his suffering was strength and power.", "박사는 이제 처음으로, 자신의 고난이 곧 힘이요 권능임을 느꼈다."], ["For the first time he felt that in that sharp fire, he had slowly forged the iron which could break the prison door of his daughter’s husband, and deliver him.", "그는 처음으로, 그 매서운 불길 속에서 자기 딸의 남편을 가둔 감옥의 문을 부수고 그를 구해낼 수 있는 무쇠를 천천히 벼려왔음을 느꼈다."], ["“It all tended to a good end, my friend; it was not mere waste and ruin.", "\"이 모든 것이 좋은 결말로 이어졌다네, 친구. 그것은 한낱 낭비와 파멸이 아니었어."], ["As my beloved child was helpful in restoring me to myself, I will be helpful now in restoring the dearest part of herself to her; by the aid of Heaven I will do it!”", "내 사랑하는 아이가 나를 나 자신에게 되돌려놓는 데 도움을 주었듯이, 이제 나는 그 아이에게서 가장 소중한 부분을 그 아이에게 되돌려놓는 데 도움이 될 걸세. 하늘의 도우심으로 내 그것을 해내고 말겠네!\""], ["Thus, Doctor Manette.", "이렇게 「마네뜨」"], ["And when Jarvis Lorry saw the kindled eyes, the resolute face, the calm strong look and bearing of the man whose life always seemed to him to have been stopped, like a clock, for so many years, and then set going again with an energy which had lain dormant during the cessation of its usefulness, he believed.", "박사는 말했다. 그리고 「자비스 로리」가, 그토록 오랜 세월 시계처럼 멈춰 있다가 그 쓸모가 멎어 있는 동안 잠들어 있던 기운으로 다시 돌아가기 시작한 것만 같던 그 사람의, 불타오르는 눈과 결연한 얼굴, 침착하고 강인한 표정과 태도를 보았을 때, 그는 믿었다."]], [["Greater things than the Doctor had at that time to contend with, would have yielded before his persevering purpose.", "그 무렵 박사가 맞서야 했던 것보다 더 거대한 일들이라도 그의 끈질긴 결의 앞에서는 굴복했을 것이다. 그는 신분이 매인 자든 자유로운 자든, 부유한 자든 가난한 자든, 악한 자든 선한 자든 온갖 부류의 사람들을 상대하는 의사로서 자기 자리를 지키면서, 그 개인적 영향력을 어찌나 현명하게 활용했던지, 머지않아 그는 세 곳의 감옥을 감독하는 의사가 되었고, 그 가운데 「라포르스」도 있었다."], ["While he kept himself in his place, as a physician, whose business was with all degrees of mankind, bond and free, rich and poor, bad and good, he used his personal influence so wisely, that he was soon the inspecting physician of three prisons, and among them of La Force.", "그는 이제 「루시」에게 그녀의 남편이 더는 혼자 갇혀 있지 않고 일반 죄수들 무리에 섞여 있다고 안심시킬 수 있었다. 그는 매주 그녀의 남편을 만났고, 그의 입에서 곧장 나온 다정한 전갈을 그녀에게 전해주었다."], ["He could now assure Lucie that her husband was no longer confined alone, but was mixed with the general body of prisoners; he saw her husband weekly, and brought sweet messages to her, straight from his lips; sometimes her husband himself sent a letter to her (though never by the Doctor’s hand), but she was not permitted to write to him: for, among the many wild suspicions of plots in the prisons, the wildest of all pointed at emigrants who were known to have made friends or permanent connections abroad.", "때로는 그녀의 남편이 직접 그녀에게 편지를 보내기도 했지만 (결코 박사의 손을 거치지는 않았다), 그녀는 그에게 편지를 쓰는 것이 허락되지 않았다. 감옥 안의 음모에 대한 숱한 거친 의혹들 가운데서도 가장 거친 것은 해외에서 친구를 사귀거나 영속적인 연줄을 맺은 것으로 알려진 망명자들을 겨냥하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["This new life of the Doctor’s was an anxious life, no doubt; still, the sagacious Mr.", "박사의 이 새로운 삶은 의심할 바 없이 불안한 삶이었다. 그래도 사려 깊은 「로리」"], ["Lorry saw that there was a new sustaining pride in it.", "씨는 그 안에 그를 지탱해주는 새로운 자부심이 있음을 보았다."], ["Nothing unbecoming tinged the pride; it was a natural and worthy one; but he observed it as a curiosity.", "그 자부심에는 어울리지 않는 빛깔이라곤 조금도 섞이지 않았으니, 그것은 자연스럽고 떳떳한 것이었다. 다만 그는 그것을 진기한 것으로 눈여겨보았다."], ["The Doctor knew, that up to that time, his imprisonment had been associated in the minds of his daughter and his friend, with his personal affliction, deprivation, and weakness.", "박사는 알고 있었다. 그때까지 자신의 투옥이 딸과 친구의 마음속에서 자신의 개인적 고통과 박탈과 나약함과 결부되어 있었음을."], ["Now that this was changed, and he knew himself to be invested through that old trial with forces to which they both looked for Charles’s ultimate safety and deliverance, he became so far exalted by the change, that he took the lead and direction, and required them as the weak, to trust to him as the strong.", "이제 그것이 바뀌어, 그 옛 시련을 통해 자신이 어떤 힘을 부여받았으며, 두 사람 모두 「샤를」의 궁극적 안전과 구출을 위해 바로 그 힘에 기대고 있음을 알게 되자, 그는 그 변화로 어찌나 고양되었던지 앞장서서 이끌고 지시하기에 이르렀고, 약한 자인 두 사람에게 강한 자인 자신을 믿으라고 요구했다."], ["The preceding relative positions of himself and Lucie were reversed, yet only as the liveliest gratitude and affection could reverse them, for he could have had no pride but in rendering some service to her who had rendered so much to him.", "자신과 「루시」의 이전 위치는 뒤바뀌었으나, 그것은 오직 더없이 생생한 감사와 애정만이 뒤바꿀 수 있는 방식의 뒤바뀜이었으니, 자신에게 그토록 많은 것을 베풀어준 그녀에게 어떤 봉사라도 하는 것 외에 그가 가질 수 있는 자부심이란 없었기 때문이다. \"다 진기하게 볼 만한 일이지.\""], ["“All curious to see,”", "「로리」 씨는 그 다정하고도 빈틈없는 방식으로 생각했다."], ["thought Mr.", "\"하지만 다 자연스럽고 옳은 일이야."], ["Lorry, in his amiably shrewd way, “but all natural and right; so, take the lead, my dear friend, and keep it; it couldn’t be in better hands.”", "그러니 앞장서게, 친애하는 친구여, 그리고 그 자리를 지키게. 이보다 더 좋은 손에 맡겨질 수는 없을 테니.\""]], [["But, though the Doctor tried hard, and never ceased trying, to get Charles Darnay set at liberty, or at least to get him brought to trial, the public current of the time set too strong and fast for him. The new era began; the king was tried, doomed, and beheaded; the Republic of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, declared for victory or death against the world in arms; the black flag waved night and day from the great towers of Notre Dame; three hundred thousand men, summoned to rise against the tyrants of the earth, rose from all the varying soils of France, as if the dragon’s teeth had been sown broadcast, and had yielded fruit equally on hill and plain, on rock, in gravel, and alluvial mud, under the bright sky of the South and under the clouds of the North, in fell and forest, in the vineyards and the olive-grounds and among the cropped grass and the stubble of the corn, along the fruitful banks of the broad rivers, and in the sand of the sea-shore. What private solicitude could rear itself against the deluge of the Year One of Liberty--the deluge rising from below, not falling from above, and with the windows of Heaven shut, not opened!", "그러나 박사가 「샤를 다네」를 풀어주거나, 적어도 재판에 회부되도록 하려고 무던히 애쓰며 결코 그 노력을 멈추지 않았음에도, 그 시대의 공적 흐름은 그에게 맞서기에는 너무도 강하고 빨랐다. 새로운 시대가 시작되었다. 왕은 재판에 부쳐졌고, 사형을 선고받았으며, 참수되었다. 자유, 평등, 형제애, 그렇지 않으면 죽음을 내건 공화국이 무장한 세계를 상대로 승리 아니면 죽음을 선포했다. 검은 깃발이 노트르담의 거대한 탑들에서 밤낮없이 나부꼈다. 지상의 폭군들에 맞서 일어나라는 소집을 받은 삼십만 명이, 마치 용의 이빨이 사방에 뿌려져 언덕에서나 평원에서나, 바위에서나 자갈밭에서나 충적토에서나, 남쪽의 밝은 하늘 아래서나 북쪽의 구름 아래서나, 황야와 숲에서나, 포도밭과 올리브밭에서나, 베어낸 풀밭과 곡식 그루터기 사이에서나, 너른 강의 기름진 둑을 따라서나, 바닷가 모래밭에서나 똑같이 열매를 맺기라도 한 듯이, 「프랑스」의 온갖 다채로운 토양에서 떨쳐 일어났다. 어떤 개인적 염려가 자유의 원년이라는 대홍수에 맞서 스스로를 일으켜 세울 수 있었으랴 — 위에서 쏟아져 내리는 것이 아니라 아래에서 차올라오는, 하늘의 창문이 열린 것이 아니라 닫힌 그 대홍수에!"]], [["There was no pause, no pity, no peace, no interval of relenting rest, no measurement of time.", "멈춤도, 연민도, 평화도, 누그러지는 안식의 틈도, 시간을 가늠할 길도 없었다."], ["Though days and nights circled as regularly as when time was young, and the evening and morning were the first day, other count of time there was none.", "시간이 젊었던 시절처럼 밤과 낮이 규칙적으로 돌고, 저녁과 아침이 첫째 날을 이루었음에도, 그 밖의 다른 시간의 셈이란 없었다."], ["Hold of it was lost in the raging fever of a nation, as it is in the fever of one patient.", "한 환자의 열병 속에서 그러하듯, 한 나라의 미쳐 날뛰는 열병 속에서 시간에 대한 감각은 사라져버렸다."], ["Now, breaking the unnatural silence of a whole city, the executioner showed the people the head of the king--and now, it seemed almost in the same breath, the head of his fair wife which had had eight weary months of imprisoned widowhood and misery, to turn it grey.", "이제 온 도시의 부자연스러운 침묵을 깨뜨리며 처형인이 사람들에게 왕의 머리를 보여주었고 — 그러더니 거의 같은 숨결에, 갇힌 채 과부로 지낸 여덟 달의 고단함과 비참함이 머리칼을 잿빛으로 물들여놓은 그 고운 아내의 머리를 보여주는 듯했다."]], [["And yet, observing the strange law of contradiction which obtains in all such cases, the time was long, while it flamed by so fast.", "그런데도, 그런 모든 경우에 적용되는 모순의 기이한 법칙을 따라, 시간은 그토록 빠르게 불꽃처럼 스쳐 가면서도 더디기만 했다. 수도에는 혁명재판소가 하나, 그리고 온 나라에는 사오만 개에 이르는 혁명위원회가 있었다."], ["A revolutionary tribunal in the capital, and forty or fifty thousand revolutionary committees all over the land; a law of the Suspected, which struck away all security for liberty or life, and delivered over any good and innocent person to any bad and guilty one; prisons gorged with people who had committed no offence, and could obtain no hearing; these things became the established order and nature of appointed things, and seemed to be ancient usage before they were many weeks old.", "자유나 생명에 대한 모든 안전을 앗아가고, 선량하고 무고한 사람을 누구든 악하고 죄 있는 자에게 넘겨버리는 용의자법이 있었다. 아무 죄도 짓지 않았으나 심리조차 받지 못하는 사람들로 감옥은 가득 찼다."], ["Above all, one hideous figure grew as familiar as if it had been before the general gaze from the foundations of the world--the figure of the sharp female called La Guillotine.", "이런 것들이 정해진 질서이자 마땅한 사물의 본성이 되어, 생겨난 지 몇 주도 채 되지 않았는데도 마치 오래된 관습인 양 여겨졌다. 무엇보다도, 마치 세상의 기초가 놓이던 때부터 만인의 시선 앞에 있어 온 것처럼 친숙해진 하나의 흉측한 형상이 자라났으니 — 「라기요띤」이라 불리는 그 날카로운 여인의 형상이었다."]], [["It was the popular theme for jests; it was the best cure for headache, it infallibly prevented the hair from turning grey, it imparted a peculiar delicacy to the complexion, it was the National Razor which shaved close: who kissed La Guillotine, looked through the little window and sneezed into the sack.", "그것은 농담의 단골 소재였다. 그것은 두통에 가장 잘 듣는 치료제였고, 머리칼이 잿빛으로 세는 것을 어김없이 막아주었으며, 안색에 독특한 섬세함을 더해주었고, 바짝 깎아주는 국민의 면도칼이었다."], ["It was the sign of the regeneration of the human race.", "「라기요띤」에게 입을 맞추는 자는 그 작은 창문으로 내다보다가 자루 속으로 재채기를 했다."], ["It superseded the Cross.", "그것은 인류가 새로 태어났다는 표지였다."], ["Models of it were worn on breasts from which the Cross was discarded, and it was bowed down to and believed in where the Cross was denied.", "그것은 십자가를 대신했다. 십자가를 내던진 가슴마다 그것의 모형을 달았고, 십자가가 부정된 곳에서 그것을 향해 머리를 조아리며 그것을 믿었다."]], [["It sheared off heads so many, that it, and the ground it most polluted, were a rotten red.", "그것은 어찌나 많은 머리를 베어냈던지, 그것과 그것이 가장 더럽힌 땅이 썩은 붉은빛으로 물들었다."], ["It was taken to pieces, like a toy-puzzle for a young Devil, and was put together again when the occasion wanted it.", "그것은 어린 악마를 위한 장난감 퍼즐처럼 분해되었다가, 필요한 때가 오면 다시 조립되었다."], ["It hushed the eloquent, struck down the powerful, abolished the beautiful and good.", "그것은 웅변가의 입을 다물게 했고, 권세 있는 자를 거꾸러뜨렸으며, 아름답고 선한 것을 없애버렸다."], ["Twenty-two friends of high public mark, twenty-one living and one dead, it had lopped the heads off, in one morning, in as many minutes.", "어느 날 아침 단 몇 분 만에, 그것은 세간에 이름 높은 친구 스물두 명의 — 산 자가 스물하나요 죽은 자가 하나인 — 머리를 베어냈다."], ["The name of the strong man of Old Scripture had descended to the chief functionary who worked it; but, so armed, he was stronger than his namesake, and blinder, and tore away the gates of God’s own Temple every day.", "그것을 다루는 우두머리 일꾼에게는 옛 성서에 나오는 장사의 이름이 붙여졌다. 그러나 그렇게 무장한 그는 자신과 이름이 같은 그 장사보다 더 강했고, 더 눈멀었으며, 날마다 하느님의 성전 문짝을 뜯어냈다."]], [["Among these terrors, and the brood belonging to them, the Doctor walked with a steady head: confident in his power, cautiously persistent in his end, never doubting that he would save Lucie’s husband at last.", "이 공포들과, 그것들에 딸린 무리들 한가운데서 박사는 흔들림 없는 머리로 걸어다녔다. 자신의 힘을 확신하고, 자신의 목적을 향해 신중하게 끈질겼으며, 끝내 「루시」의 남편을 구해내리라는 것을 결코 의심하지 않았다."], ["Yet the current of the time swept by, so strong and deep, and carried the time away so fiercely, that Charles had lain in prison one year and three months when the Doctor was thus steady and confident.", "그러나 그 시대의 흐름은 어찌나 강하고 깊게 휩쓸어 가며 어찌나 사납게 시간을 앗아갔던지, 박사가 이토록 흔들림 없이 확신에 차 있는 동안 「샤를」은 일 년 삼 개월째 감옥에 갇혀 있었다."], ["So much more wicked and distracted had the Revolution grown in that December month, that the rivers of the South were encumbered with the bodies of the violently drowned by night, and prisoners were shot in lines and squares under the southern wintry sun.", "그 십이월 한 달 동안 혁명은 그만큼 더 사악하고 광란에 빠져, 남쪽의 강들은 밤사이 폭력적으로 익사당한 자들의 시체로 막혔고, 죄수들은 남쪽의 겨울 햇살 아래 줄줄이, 무리지어 총살당했다."], ["Still, the Doctor walked among the terrors with a steady head.", "그래도 박사는 그 공포들 한가운데를 흔들림 없는 머리로 걸어다녔다."], ["No man better known than he, in Paris at that day; no man in a stranger situation.", "그 시절 「파리」에서 그보다 더 잘 알려진 사람은 없었고, 그보다 더 기이한 처지에 놓인 사람도 없었다."], ["Silent, humane, indispensable in hospital and prison, using his art equally among assassins and victims, he was a man apart.", "과묵하고, 인정 많고, 병원과 감옥에 없어서는 안 될 존재였으며, 암살자들 사이에서나 희생자들 사이에서나 똑같이 자신의 의술을 베푸는 그는, 따로 떨어진 사람이었다."], ["In the exercise of his skill, the appearance and the story of the Bastille Captive removed him from all other men.", "그 의술을 펼치는 데서, 「바스띠유」 수인의 풍모와 사연은 그를 다른 모든 사람들로부터 구별지었다."], ["He was not suspected or brought in question, any more than if he had indeed been recalled to life some eighteen years before, or were a Spirit moving among mortals.", "그는 마치 십팔 년쯤 전에 정말로 되살아난 사람이거나 인간들 사이를 거니는 영혼이기라도 한 듯, 의심받거나 의문에 부쳐지는 일이 없었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V. The Wood-Sawyer", "# CHAPTER V. The Wood-Sawyer"]], [["One year and three months.", "일년 삼개월."], ["During all that time Lucie was never sure, from hour to hour, but that the Guillotine would strike off her husband’s head next day.", "그 기간 내내 루시는 단 한순간도, 다음날이면 단두대가 남편의 목을 베어버리지나 않을까 하는 불안에서 벗어나지 못했다."], ["Every day, through the stony streets, the tumbrils now jolted heavily, filled with Condemned.", "날마다, 돌투성이 거리를 가로질러, 이제는 사형수들을 가득 실은 호송 수레가 묵직하게 덜컹거리며 지나갔다."], ["Lovely girls; bright women, brown-haired, black-haired, and grey; youths; stalwart men and old; gentle born and peasant born; all red wine for La Guillotine, all daily brought into light from the dark cellars of the loathsome prisons, and carried to her through the streets to slake her devouring thirst.", "사랑스러운 처녀들, 활기찬 여인들, 갈색 머리도 검은 머리도 흰 머리도, 젊은이들, 건장한 사내들과 노인들, 귀하게 태어난 자들과 천하게 태어난 자들 — 모두가 라기요면을 위한 붉은 포도주였고, 모두가 날마다 그 혐오스러운 감옥의 어두운 지하실에서 밝은 데로 끌려 나와, 거리를 지나 그녀에게로 옮겨져, 게걸스럽게 삼키는 그녀의 갈증을 적셔주었다."], ["Liberty, equality, fraternity, or death;--the last, much the easiest to bestow, O Guillotine!", "자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음 — 그 마지막 것이야말로 베풀기에 가장 쉬운 것이었으니, 오 단두대여!"]], [["If the suddenness of her calamity, and the whirling wheels of the time, had stunned the Doctor’s daughter into awaiting the result in idle despair, it would but have been with her as it was with many.", "만일 갑작스럽게 닥친 재난과 빙글빙글 돌아가는 시대의 수레바퀴가 박사의 딸을 멍하게 만들어 부질없는 절망 속에 결과만을 기다리게 했다면, 그녀의 처지도 그저 많은 이들과 다르지 않았으리라."], ["But, from the hour when she had taken the white head to her fresh young bosom in the garret of Saint Antoine, she had been true to her duties.", "그러나 쌩땅뚜안의 다락방에서 그 백발의 머리를 자신의 싱싱하고 젊은 가슴에 끌어안았던 그 순간부터, 그녀는 자신의 의무에 충실했다."], ["She was truest to them in the season of trial, as all the quietly loyal and good will always be.", "조용히 충직하고 선량한 모든 이들이 늘 그러하듯, 그녀 역시 시련의 시기에 가장 충실했다."]], [["As soon as they were established in their new residence, and her father had entered on the routine of his avocations, she arranged the little household as exactly as if her husband had been there.", "새 거처에 자리를 잡고 아버지가 일상의 직무에 들어서자마자, 그녀는 마치 남편이 거기 있기라도 한 듯 그 작은 살림을 정확하게 꾸려나갔다."], ["Everything had its appointed place and its appointed time.", "모든 것에는 정해진 자리와 정해진 시간이 있었다."], ["Little Lucie she taught, as regularly, as if they had all been united in their English home.", "어린 루시는, 마치 그들 모두가 영국의 보금자리에 함께 있기라도 한 듯, 규칙적으로 가르쳤다."], ["The slight devices with which she cheated herself into the show of a belief that they would soon be reunited--the little preparations for his speedy return, the setting aside of his chair and his books--these, and the solemn prayer at night for one dear prisoner especially, among the many unhappy souls in prison and the shadow of death--were almost the only outspoken reliefs of her heavy mind.", "그들이 곧 다시 만날 것이라는 믿음을 꾸며내어 스스로를 달래던 사소한 방편들 — 그의 빠른 귀환을 위한 자그마한 준비, 그의 의자와 책을 따로 챙겨두는 일 — 그리고 옥에 갇히고 죽음의 그림자에 덮인 수많은 불행한 영혼들 가운데서도 특별히 한 사람의 사랑하는 죄수를 위해 밤마다 올리는 엄숙한 기도, 이런 것들이 무거운 그녀의 마음을 풀어주는 거의 유일한 출구였다."]], [["She did not greatly alter in appearance.", "겉모습은 크게 변하지 않았다."], ["The plain dark dresses, akin to mourning dresses, which she and her child wore, were as neat and as well attended to as the brighter clothes of happy days.", "그녀와 아이가 입은, 상복에 가까운 수수하고 어두운 옷들은, 행복하던 시절의 밝은 옷들 못지않게 단정하고 정갈하게 손질되어 있었다."], ["She lost her colour, and the old and intent expression was a constant, not an occasional, thing; otherwise, she remained very pretty and comely.", "혈색은 잃었고, 예전의 골똘한 표정이 이제는 이따금이 아니라 늘 자리잡고 있었지만, 그밖에는 여전히 무척 곱고 어여뻤다."], ["Sometimes, at night on kissing her father, she would burst into the grief she had repressed all day, and would say that her sole reliance, under Heaven, was on him.", "이따금 밤에 아버지에게 입을 맞추다가, 하루 종일 억눌렀던 슬픔이 북받쳐 올라, 하늘 아래 자신이 의지할 분은 오직 아버지뿐이라고 말하곤 했다. 그러면 그는 언제나 단호하게 대답했다."], ["He always resolutely answered: “Nothing can happen to him without my knowledge, and I know that I can save him, Lucie.”", "\"내가 모르는 사이에 그애에게 무슨 일이 일어날 리 없고, 나는 그애를 구할 수 있다는 걸 알고 있단다, 루시야.\""]], [["They had not made the round of their changed life many weeks, when her father said to her, on coming home one evening:", "달라진 생활을 시작한 지 몇 주 지나지 않은 어느 저녁, 집으로 돌아온 아버지가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“My dear, there is an upper window in the prison, to which Charles can sometimes gain access at three in the afternoon.", "\"얘야, 감옥에 위층 창문이 하나 있는데, 샤를이 이따금 오후 세시쯤 거기로 갈 수가 있단다."], ["When he can get to it--which depends on many uncertainties and incidents--he might see you in the street, he thinks, if you stood in a certain place that I can show you.", "그애가 거기 닿을 수 있을 때면 — 여러가지 불확실한 사정과 우연에 달려 있긴 하다만 — 그애 생각에, 네가 내가 일러주는 어느 자리에 서 있으면 거리에 있는 너를 볼 수 있을 거라는구나."], ["But you will not be able to see him, my poor child, and even if you could, it would be unsafe for you to make a sign of recognition.”", "하지만 너는 그애를 볼 수 없을 게다, 가엾은 것아. 설령 볼 수 있다 해도 알아보는 기색을 내는 건 네게 위험한 일이란다.\""]], [["“O show me the place, my father, and I will go there every day.”", "\"오, 그 자리를 알려주세요, 아버지. 매일 거기 가겠어요.\""]], [["From that time, in all weathers, she waited there two hours.", "그때부터, 날씨가 어떻든, 그녀는 거기서 두 시간씩 기다렸다."], ["As the clock struck two, she was there, and at four she turned resignedly away.", "시계가 두시를 치면 그녀는 거기 있었고, 네시가 되면 체념한 듯 발길을 돌렸다."], ["When it was not too wet or inclement for her child to be with her, they went together; at other times she was alone; but, she never missed a single day.", "아이를 데리고 다닐 수 없을 만큼 비가 많이 오거나 날씨가 사납지 않을 때면 둘이 함께 갔고, 그렇지 않을 때면 혼자였지만, 하루도 거르는 법이 없었다."]], [["It was the dark and dirty corner of a small winding street.", "그곳은 작고 구불구불한 거리의 어둡고 지저분한 모퉁이였다."], ["The hovel of a cutter of wood into lengths for burning, was the only house at that end; all else was wall.", "그쪽 끝에 있는 집이라고는 땔감 길이로 나무를 자르는 톱장이의 오두막 한 채뿐이었고, 나머지는 온통 담벼락이었다."], ["On the third day of her being there, he noticed her.", "그녀가 거기 다닌 지 사흘째 되는 날, 그가 그녀를 알아보았다."]], [["“Good day, citizeness.”", "\"안녕하시오, 여성 시민.\""]], [["“Good day, citizen.”", "\"안녕하세요, 시민.\""]], [["This mode of address was now prescribed by decree.", "이런 호칭 방식은 이제 법령으로 규정되어 있었다."], ["It had been established voluntarily some time ago, among the more thorough patriots; but, was now law for everybody.", "얼마 전 더 철저한 애국자들 사이에서 자발적으로 자리잡은 것이었지만, 이제는 모두에게 법이 되어 있었다."]], [["“Walking here again, citizeness?”", "\"또 여기 거니시오, 여성 시민?\""]], [["“You see me, citizen!”", "\"보시는 대로예요, 시민!\""]], [["The wood-sawyer, who was a little man with a redundancy of gesture (he had once been a mender of roads), cast a glance at the prison, pointed at the prison, and putting his ten fingers before his face to represent bars, peeped through them jocosely.", "몸짓이 유난히 많은 자그마한 사내였던 톱장이는(한때 도로 보수공이었다) 감옥 쪽을 힐끗 보더니 감옥을 손가락질하고는, 열 손가락을 얼굴 앞에 갖다 대 창살 모양을 만들어 그 사이로 익살스럽게 들여다보았다."]], [["“But it’s not my business,”", "\"하긴 내 알 바 아니지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["And went on sawing his wood.", "그러고는 다시 나무를 켜기 시작했다."]], [["Next day he was looking out for her, and accosted her the moment she appeared.", "이튿날 그는 그녀를 살피고 있다가, 그녀가 나타나자마자 말을 걸었다."]], [["“What?", "\"아니?"], ["Walking here again, citizeness?”", "또 여기 거니시오, 여성 시민?\""]], [["“Yes, citizen.”", "\"네, 시민.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["A child too!", "아이까지!"], ["Your mother, is it not, my little citizeness?”", "댁 어머니지, 그렇지, 우리 꼬마 여성 시민?\""]], [["“Do I say yes, mamma?”", "\"네, 그렇다고 말할까요, 엄마?\""], ["whispered little Lucie, drawing close to her.", "어린 루시가 그녀에게 바싹 붙으며 속삭였다."]], [["“Yes, dearest.”", "\"그래, 우리 아가.\""]], [["“Yes, citizen.”", "\"네, 시민.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But it’s not my business.", "하지만 내 알 바 아니지."], ["My work is my business.", "내 일은 내 일이니까."], ["See my saw!", "내 톱 좀 보시오!"], ["I call it my Little Guillotine.", "나는 이걸 내 꼬마 단두대라고 부른다오."], ["La, la, la; La, la, la!", "라, 라, 라, 라, 라, 라!"], ["And off his head comes!”", "그러면 목이 댕강!\""]], [["The billet fell as he spoke, and he threw it into a basket.", "그가 말하는 사이에 통나무 토막이 떨어졌고, 그는 그것을 바구니에 던져 넣었다."]], [["“I call myself the Samson of the firewood guillotine.", "\"나는 나 자신을 땔감 단두대의 쌍송이라고 부르지."], ["See here again!", "자, 여기 또 보시오!"], ["Loo, loo, loo; Loo, loo, loo!", "루, 루, 루, 루, 루, 루!"], ["And off her head comes!", "그러면 그년 목이 댕강!"], ["Now, a child.", "자, 이번엔 아이."], ["Tickle, tickle; Pickle, pickle!", "간질간질, 콕콕!"], ["And off its head comes.", "그러면 그놈 목이 댕강."], ["All the family!”", "온 가족이 다!\""]], [["Lucie shuddered as he threw two more billets into his basket, but it was impossible to be there while the wood-sawyer was at work, and not be in his sight.", "그가 통나무 토막 두 개를 더 바구니에 던져 넣자 루시는 몸서리쳤지만, 톱장이가 일하는 동안 거기 있으면서 그의 눈에 띄지 않기란 불가능했다."], ["Thenceforth, to secure his good will, she always spoke to him first, and often gave him drink-money, which he readily received.", "그때부터 그의 호의를 사두려고, 그녀는 늘 먼저 그에게 말을 걸었고, 이따금 술값을 쥐여주었는데, 그는 그것을 선뜻 받았다."]], [["He was an inquisitive fellow, and sometimes when she had quite forgotten him in gazing at the prison roof and grates, and in lifting her heart up to her husband, she would come to herself to find him looking at her, with his knee on his bench and his saw stopped in its work.", "그는 호기심 많은 사내여서, 그녀가 감옥의 지붕과 쇠창살을 올려다보며 남편에게로 마음을 끌어올리느라 그를 까맣게 잊고 있다가 정신을 차려보면, 그가 작업대에 한쪽 무릎을 올린 채 톱질을 멈추고 자기를 바라보고 있는 일이 더러 있었다."], ["“But it’s not my business!”", "\"하긴 내 알 바 아니지!\""], ["he would generally say at those times, and would briskly fall to his sawing again.", "그럴 때면 그는 으레 이렇게 말하고는 다시 재빨리 톱질에 매달리곤 했다."]], [["In all weathers, in the snow and frost of winter, in the bitter winds of spring, in the hot sunshine of summer, in the rains of autumn, and again in the snow and frost of winter, Lucie passed two hours of every day at this place; and every day on leaving it, she kissed the prison wall.", "날씨가 어떻든, 겨울의 눈과 서리 속에서도, 봄의 매서운 바람 속에서도, 여름의 뜨거운 햇볕 속에서도, 가을의 빗속에서도, 그리고 다시 겨울의 눈과 서리 속에서도, 루시는 날마다 그곳에서 두 시간을 보냈다. 그리고 그곳을 떠날 때면 날마다 감옥의 담벼락에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Her husband saw her (so she learned from her father) it might be once in five or six times: it might be twice or thrice running: it might be, not for a week or a fortnight together.", "남편이 그녀를 보는 것은(아버지에게 들은 바로는) 다섯번이나 여섯번에 한번쯤일 수도 있었고, 연거푸 두세번일 수도 있었으며, 한 주나 두 주 동안 한번도 없을 수도 있었다."], ["It was enough that he could and did see her when the chances served, and on that possibility she would have waited out the day, seven days a week.", "기회가 닿을 때 그가 그녀를 볼 수 있고 또 보았다는 것만으로도 충분했으니, 그 가능성 하나에 그녀는 한 주 내내, 하루 종일이라도 기다렸을 것이다."]], [["These occupations brought her round to the December month, wherein her father walked among the terrors with a steady head.", "이런 나날을 보내는 사이에 그녀는 십이월에 이르렀고, 그 무렵 아버지는 공포의 한가운데를 침착한 머리로 누비고 다녔다."], ["On a lightly-snowing afternoon she arrived at the usual corner.", "눈발이 가볍게 날리는 어느 오후, 그녀는 늘 가던 모퉁이에 다다랐다."], ["It was a day of some wild rejoicing, and a festival.", "그날은 한바탕 미친 듯한 환희가 벌어지는 축제일이었다."], ["She had seen the houses, as she came along, decorated with little pikes, and with little red caps stuck upon them; also, with tricoloured ribbons; also, with the standard inscription (tricoloured letters were the favourite), Republic One and Indivisible.", "그녀는 오는 길에, 작은 창들과 그 위에 꽂힌 작은 붉은 모자로 장식된 집들을 보았다. 또 삼색 리본으로, 또 정해진 문구(삼색 글자가 가장 인기였다)인 '하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국."], ["Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death!", "자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음!'으로 꾸며진 집들도 보았다."]], [["The miserable shop of the wood-sawyer was so small, that its whole surface furnished very indifferent space for this legend.", "톱장이의 초라한 가게는 너무 작아서, 그 표면 전체로도 이 문구를 적을 자리가 변변치 않았다."], ["He had got somebody to scrawl it up for him, however, who had squeezed Death in with most inappropriate difficulty.", "그래도 그는 누군가에게 부탁해 그것을 갈겨 써두었는데, 그 사람은 '죽음'이라는 단어를 더없이 어울리지 않게 끼워 넣느라 애를 먹은 모양이었다."], ["On his house-top, he displayed pike and cap, as a good citizen must, and in a window he had stationed his saw inscribed as his “Little Sainte Guillotine”--for the great sharp female was by that time popularly canonised.", "그는 선량한 시민이라면 마땅히 그래야 하듯 지붕 위에 창과 모자를 내걸었고, 창문에는 '꼬마 성녀 단두대'라고 적은 자기 톱을 세워두었으니 — 그 무렵에는 그 거대하고 날카로운 여인이 이미 세간에서 성녀로 추앙받고 있었기 때문이다."], ["His shop was shut and he was not there, which was a relief to Lucie, and left her quite alone.", "가게는 닫혀 있고 그도 거기 없었는데, 이는 루시에게 다행한 일이어서 그녀를 온전히 혼자 있게 해주었다."]], [["But, he was not far off, for presently she heard a troubled movement and a shouting coming along, which filled her with fear.", "그러나 그는 멀리 있지 않았으니, 이내 어수선한 움직임과 고함 소리가 다가오는 것이 들려와 그녀를 두려움에 휩싸이게 했다."], ["A moment afterwards, and a throng of people came pouring round the corner by the prison wall, in the midst of whom was the wood-sawyer hand in hand with The Vengeance.", "잠시 후, 감옥 담벼락 옆 모퉁이를 돌아 한 무리의 사람들이 쏟아져 나왔고, 그 한가운데에는 톱장이가 '복수의 여신'과 손을 맞잡고 있었다."], ["There could not be fewer than five hundred people, and they were dancing like five thousand demons.", "오백 명은 족히 되어 보였고, 그들은 오천 마리 악령처럼 춤을 추고 있었다."], ["There was no other music than their own singing.", "그들 자신의 노랫소리 말고는 다른 음악이 없었다."], ["They danced to the popular Revolution song, keeping a ferocious time that was like a gnashing of teeth in unison.", "그들은 그 인기 있는 혁명가에 맞춰, 마치 일제히 이를 가는 듯한 사나운 박자를 지키며 춤을 추었다."], ["Men and women danced together, women danced together, men danced together, as hazard had brought them together.", "남자와 여자가 함께 춤추고, 여자들끼리 춤추고, 남자들끼리 춤추니, 우연히 한데 모이는 대로였다."], ["At first, they were a mere storm of coarse red caps and coarse woollen rags; but, as they filled the place, and stopped to dance about Lucie, some ghastly apparition of a dance-figure gone raving mad arose among them.", "처음에는 그저 거친 붉은 모자들과 거친 모직 누더기들의 폭풍에 지나지 않았다. 그러나 그들이 그곳을 가득 메우고 멈춰 서서 루시를 둘러싸고 춤추자, 그들 사이에서 미쳐 날뛰는 무도의 형상이라 할 어떤 섬뜩한 환영이 솟아올랐다."], ["They advanced, retreated, struck at one another’s hands, clutched at one another’s heads, spun round alone, caught one another and spun round in pairs, until many of them dropped.", "그들은 앞으로 나아가고 뒤로 물러서며, 서로의 손을 치고, 서로의 머리를 움켜쥐고, 혼자서 빙글빙글 돌다가, 서로를 붙잡고 둘씩 짝지어 빙글빙글 돌더니, 마침내 여럿이 쓰러졌다."], ["While those were down, the rest linked hand in hand, and all spun round together: then the ring broke, and in separate rings of two and four they turned and turned until they all stopped at once, began again, struck, clutched, and tore, and then reversed the spin, and all spun round another way.", "그자들이 쓰러져 있는 동안, 나머지는 손에 손을 맞잡고 다 함께 빙글빙글 돌았다. 그러다 고리가 풀어지면, 둘씩 넷씩 따로 고리를 이루어 돌고 또 돌다가, 일제히 한꺼번에 멈추고는 다시 시작하여, 치고, 움켜쥐고, 잡아당기고, 그러다 도는 방향을 뒤집어 모두가 반대편으로 빙글빙글 돌았다."], ["Suddenly they stopped again, paused, struck out the time afresh, formed into lines the width of the public way, and, with their heads low down and their hands high up, swooped screaming off.", "갑자기 그들은 다시 멈추어 잠시 쉬더니, 새로 박자를 두드리고는, 한길 너비만큼 줄을 지어, 머리는 낮게 숙이고 손은 높이 치켜든 채, 비명을 지르며 휙 몰려 사라졌다."], ["No fight could have been half so terrible as this dance.", "그 어떤 싸움도 이 춤의 반만큼도 끔찍할 수 없었으리라."], ["It was so emphatically a fallen sport--a something, once innocent, delivered over to all devilry--a healthy pastime changed into a means of angering the blood, bewildering the senses, and steeling the heart.", "그것은 그야말로 타락한 놀이였으니 — 한때는 순결했던 것이 온갖 악마성에 내맡겨진 것이요 — 건강한 오락이 피를 끓게 하고 정신을 어지럽히며 마음을 무디게 하는 수단으로 변해버린 것이었다."], ["Such grace as was visible in it, made it the uglier, showing how warped and perverted all things good by nature were become.", "거기서 엿보이는 우아함이 도리어 그것을 더 추악하게 만들어, 본래 선한 모든 것이 얼마나 뒤틀리고 왜곡되었는지를 드러내 보였다."], ["The maidenly bosom bared to this, the pretty almost-child’s head thus distracted, the delicate foot mincing in this slough of blood and dirt, were types of the disjointed time.", "처녀의 가슴이 이런 짓에 드러나고, 거의 어린아이라 할 어여쁜 머리가 이렇게 미쳐 날뛰며, 가녀린 발이 이 피와 진흙의 수렁 속에서 종종거리는 모습은, 산산이 어그러진 시대의 표상이었다."]], [["This was the Carmagnole.", "이것이 까르마뇰이었다."], ["As it passed, leaving Lucie frightened and bewildered in the doorway of the wood-sawyer’s house, the feathery snow fell as quietly and lay as white and soft, as if it had never been.", "그것이 지나가고, 루시를 톱장이 집 문간에 겁에 질려 어찌할 바를 모른 채 남겨둔 뒤, 깃털 같은 눈은 마치 처음부터 그런 일이 없었다는 듯 고요히 내려와 희고 부드럽게 쌓였다."]], [["“O my father!”", "\"오, 아버지!\""], ["for he stood before her when she lifted up the eyes she had momentarily darkened with her hand; “such a cruel, bad sight.”", "그녀가 잠시 손으로 가렸던 눈을 들어보니 아버지가 그녀 앞에 서 있었다. \"참으로 잔인하고 끔찍한 광경이었어요.\""]], [["“I know, my dear, I know.", "\"안다, 얘야, 알아."], ["I have seen it many times.", "나도 여러 번 보았단다."], ["Don’t be frightened!", "무서워 마라!"], ["Not one of them would harm you.”", "저들 중 누구도 너를 해치지 않을 게다.\""]], [["“I am not frightened for myself, my father.", "\"제 자신이 무서운 게 아니에요, 아버지."], ["But when I think of my husband, and the mercies of these people--”", "하지만 제 남편을, 그리고 저 사람들의 자비를 생각하면—\""]], [["“We will set him above their mercies very soon.", "\"우리는 곧 그애를 저들의 자비 위에 올려놓을 게다."], ["I left him climbing to the window, and I came to tell you.", "그애가 창문으로 기어오르는 걸 두고, 너한테 알려주려고 왔단다."], ["There is no one here to see. You may kiss your hand towards that highest shelving roof.”", "여기는 보는 사람이 아무도 없으니, 저 가장 높은 비탈진 지붕을 향해 손에 입을 맞추어 보내려무나.\""]], [["“I do so, father, and I send him my Soul with it!”", "\"그렇게 할게요, 아버지. 거기에 제 영혼을 실어 보낼게요!\""]], [["“You cannot see him, my poor dear?”", "\"그애가 보이지 않느냐, 가엾은 것아?\""]], [["“No, father,”", "\"네, 아버지.\""], ["said Lucie, yearning and weeping as she kissed her hand, “no.”", "루시가 손에 입을 맞추며 그리움에 젖어 흐느꼈다. \"보이지 않아요.\""]], [["A footstep in the snow.", "눈 위에 발소리."], ["Madame Defarge.", "마담 드파르주였다."], ["“I salute you, citizeness,”", "\"안녕하시오, 여성 시민.\""], ["from the Doctor.", "박사가 인사했다."], ["“I salute you, citizen.”", "\"안녕하세요, 시민.\""], ["This in passing. Nothing more.", "지나치는 길에 건넨 인사였다."], ["Madame Defarge gone, like a shadow over the white road.", "그뿐이었다. 마담 드파르주는 흰 길 위로 드리운 그림자처럼 사라졌다."]], [["“Give me your arm, my love.", "\"내 팔을 잡으렴, 얘야."], ["Pass from here with an air of cheerfulness and courage, for his sake.", "그애를 위해서, 명랑하고 용기 있는 기색으로 여기를 떠나자."], ["That was well done;”", "잘했다.\""], ["they had left the spot; “it shall not be in vain.", "그들은 그 자리를 떠났다. \"헛되지 않을 게다."], ["Charles is summoned for to-morrow.”", "샤를이 내일 소환된단다.\""]], [["“For to-morrow!”", "\"내일이라고요!\""]], [["“There is no time to lose.", "\"지체할 시간이 없다."], ["I am well prepared, but there are precautions to be taken, that could not be taken until he was actually summoned before the Tribunal.", "나는 만반의 준비가 되어 있다만, 그애가 실제로 재판소에 소환되기 전에는 취할 수 없는 조치들이 남아 있단다."], ["He has not received the notice yet, but I know that he will presently be summoned for to-morrow, and removed to the Conciergerie; I have timely information.", "그애는 아직 통지를 받지 못했지만, 곧 내일자로 소환되어 콩시에르주리로 옮겨지리라는 걸 나는 안다. 때맞춰 소식을 들었으니까."], ["You are not afraid?”", "무섭지 않으냐?\""]], [["She could scarcely answer, “I trust in you.”", "그녀는 가까스로 대답했다. \"아버지를 믿어요.\""]], [["“Do so, implicitly.", "\"그래, 절대적으로 믿으렴."], ["Your suspense is nearly ended, my darling; he shall be restored to you within a few hours; I have encompassed him with every protection.", "네 마음 졸이는 시간도 거의 끝나간다, 아가야. 그애는 몇 시간 안에 네게 돌아올 게다."], ["I must see Lorry.”", "나는 온갖 방비로 그애를 둘러쌌단다. 로리 씨를 만나봐야겠구나.\""]], [["He stopped.", "그가 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["There was a heavy lumbering of wheels within hearing.", "들리는 거리 안에서 수레바퀴의 묵직한 덜컹거림이 있었다."], ["They both knew too well what it meant. One.", "두 사람 모두 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 너무도 잘 알았다."], ["Two.", "하나."], ["Three.", "둘. 셋."], ["Three tumbrils faring away with their dread loads over the hushing snow.", "호송 수레 세 대가 그 무시무시한 짐을 싣고 소리를 죽이는 눈 위를 지나 멀어져 갔다."]], [["“I must see Lorry,”", "\"로리 씨를 만나봐야겠다.\""], ["the Doctor repeated, turning her another way.", "박사가 그녀를 다른 길로 돌려세우며 거듭 말했다."]], [["The staunch old gentleman was still in his trust; had never left it.", "이 충직한 노신사는 여전히 자신의 직무를 맡고 있었으니, 단 한 번도 그것을 떠난 적이 없었다."], ["He and his books were in frequent requisition as to property confiscated and made national.", "그와 그의 장부는 몰수되어 국유화된 재산을 두고 빈번히 호출되었다."], ["What he could save for the owners, he saved.", "그는 원래 주인들을 위해 지킬 수 있는 것이라면 지켜냈다."], ["No better man living to hold fast by what Tellson’s had in keeping, and to hold his peace.", "텔슨 은행이 맡아둔 것을 굳건히 지키고 또 입을 다무는 데에 그보다 나은 사람은 세상에 없었다."]], [["A murky red and yellow sky, and a rising mist from the Seine, denoted the approach of darkness.", "거무튀튀한 붉고 누런 하늘과 쎈 강에서 피어오르는 안개가 어둠이 다가옴을 알렸다."], ["It was almost dark when they arrived at the Bank.", "은행에 다다랐을 때는 거의 어두워져 있었다."], ["The stately residence of Monseigneur was altogether blighted and deserted.", "무슈 영주의 위풍당당하던 저택은 온통 황폐해진 채 버려져 있었다."], ["Above a heap of dust and ashes in the court, ran the letters: National Property.", "안마당의 흙먼지와 잿더미 위로는 이런 글자들이 적혀 있었다. '국유 재산."], ["Republic One and Indivisible.", "하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국."], ["Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death!", "자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음!'"]], [["Who could that be with Mr. Lorry--the owner of the riding-coat upon the chair--who must not be seen?", "로리 씨와 함께 있던 그 사람 — 의자 위에 걸쳐진 승마용 외투의 주인 — 남의 눈에 띄어서는 안 될 그 사람은 누구였을까?"], ["From whom newly arrived, did he come out, agitated and surprised, to take his favourite in his arms?", "새로 도착한 누구에게서, 그는 그토록 동요하고 놀란 채 나와 자신이 아끼는 사람을 품에 끌어안았던 것일까?"], ["To whom did he appear to repeat her faltering words, when, raising his voice and turning his head towards the door of the room from which he had issued, he said: “Removed to the Conciergerie, and summoned for to-morrow?”", "누구를 향해, 그는 그녀가 더듬더듬 했던 말을 되풀이하는 듯 목소리를 높이고는, 자신이 막 나온 방의 문 쪽으로 고개를 돌리며 이렇게 말했던 것일까. \"콩시에르주리로 옮겨져, 내일 소환된답니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER VI. Triumph", "# CHAPTER VI. Triumph"]], [["The dread tribunal of five Judges, Public Prosecutor, and determined Jury, sat every day.", "다섯명의 재판관과 검사, 그리고 단호한 배심원으로 이루어진 무시무시한 법정은 날마다 열렸다."], ["Their lists went forth every evening, and were read out by the gaolers of the various prisons to their prisoners.", "그들의 명단은 저녁마다 나돌았고, 여러 감옥의 간수들이 죄수들에게 그것을 읽어주었다."], ["The standard gaoler-joke was, “Come out and listen to the Evening Paper, you inside there!”", "간수들이 으레 던지는 농담은 이러했다. \"이리 나와서 석간을 들어보시지, 거기 안에 있는 양반!\""]], [["“Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay!”", "\"샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이!\""]], [["So at last began the Evening Paper at La Force.", "라포르스에서 마침내 석간이 그렇게 시작되었다."]], [["When a name was called, its owner stepped apart into a spot reserved for those who were announced as being thus fatally recorded.", "이름이 불리면, 그 주인은 따로 떨어져 나가 이렇게 치명적으로 기록된 자들을 위해 마련된 자리로 들어섰다."], ["Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay, had reason to know the usage; he had seen hundreds pass away so.", "샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이는 그 관례를 알 만한 이유가 있었으니, 수백명이 그렇게 사라져가는 것을 보아왔던 것이다."]], [["His bloated gaoler, who wore spectacles to read with, glanced over them to assure himself that he had taken his place, and went through the list, making a similar short pause at each name.", "읽을 때 안경을 쓰는 그 비대한 간수는, 그가 자리를 잡았는지 확인하려고 안경 너머로 흘긋 보고는, 이름마다 비슷하게 잠깐씩 멈추어가며 명단을 훑어 내려갔다."], ["There were twenty-three names, but only twenty were responded to; for one of the prisoners so summoned had died in gaol and been forgotten, and two had already been guillotined and forgotten.", "이름은 스물세개였지만, 대답한 사람은 스무명뿐이었다. 그렇게 호명된 죄수 가운데 하나는 감옥에서 죽어 잊혔고, 둘은 이미 단두대에서 처형되어 잊혔기 때문이다."], ["The list was read, in the vaulted chamber where Darnay had seen the associated prisoners on the night of his arrival.", "명단은 다네이가 도착하던 밤에 함께 갇힌 죄수들을 보았던 그 둥근 천장의 방에서 낭독되었다."], ["Every one of those had perished in the massacre; every human creature he had since cared for and parted with, had died on the scaffold.", "그들은 하나같이 학살로 죽어 사라졌고, 그가 그 뒤로 마음을 쓰고 헤어졌던 모든 인간 존재들은 단두대 위에서 죽었다."]], [["There were hurried words of farewell and kindness, but the parting was soon over.", "서둘러 작별과 친절의 말이 오갔으나, 이별은 곧 끝났다."], ["It was the incident of every day, and the society of La Force were engaged in the preparation of some games of forfeits and a little concert, for that evening.", "그것은 날마다 있는 일이었고, 라포르스의 사람들은 그날 저녁을 위해 벌금놀이 몇가지와 작은 음악회 준비에 매달려 있었다."], ["They crowded to the grates and shed tears there; but, twenty places in the projected entertainments had to be refilled, and the time was, at best, short to the lock-up hour, when the common rooms and corridors would be delivered over to the great dogs who kept watch there through the night.", "그들은 쇠창살로 몰려들어 거기서 눈물을 흘렸으나, 마련된 여흥에서 스무개의 빈자리를 다시 채워야 했고, 시간은 기껏해야 폐쇄 시각까지 짧을 뿐이었으니, 그때가 되면 공동실과 복도는 밤새 그곳을 지키는 커다란 개들에게 넘겨질 터였다."], ["The prisoners were far from insensible or unfeeling; their ways arose out of the condition of the time.", "죄수들이 결코 무감각하거나 무정했던 것은 아니었다. 그들의 방식은 시대의 상황에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["Similarly, though with a subtle difference, a species of fervour or intoxication, known, without doubt, to have led some persons to brave the guillotine unnecessarily, and to die by it, was not mere boastfulness, but a wild infection of the wildly shaken public mind.", "마찬가지로, 미묘한 차이는 있으나, 일종의 열정 혹은 도취가 있었으니, 그것이 어떤 이들로 하여금 불필요하게 단두대에 맞서게 하고 그 칼날에 죽게 했음은 의심할 여지가 없는데, 그것은 단순한 허세가 아니라, 사납게 뒤흔들린 민심의 거친 전염이었다."], ["In seasons of pestilence, some of us will have a secret attraction to the disease--a terrible passing inclination to die of it.", "역병의 시절에는 우리 가운데 누군가는 그 병에 은밀히 끌리게 마련이니, 그것에 걸려 죽고 싶다는 끔찍하고 일시적인 충동에 사로잡힌다."], ["And all of us have like wonders hidden in our breasts, only needing circumstances to evoke them.", "그리고 우리 모두에게는 그와 같은 기이한 것들이 가슴속에 숨어 있어, 그저 그것들을 불러낼 상황만을 필요로 할 뿐이다."]], [["The passage to the Conciergerie was short and dark; the night in its vermin-haunted cells was long and cold.", "콩시에르주리로 가는 통로는 짧고 어두웠고, 해충이 들끓는 그곳 감방의 밤은 길고 추웠다."], ["Next day, fifteen prisoners were put to the bar before Charles Darnay’s name was called.", "이튿날, 샤를 다네이의 이름이 불리기 전에 죄수 열다섯명이 피고석에 세워졌다."], ["All the fifteen were condemned, and the trials of the whole occupied an hour and a half.", "열다섯명 모두 유죄 판결을 받았고, 전체 재판에 한시간 반이 걸렸다."]], [["“Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay,” was at length arraigned.", "\"샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이\"가 마침내 기소되었다."]], [["His judges sat upon the Bench in feathered hats; but the rough red cap and tricoloured cockade was the head-dress otherwise prevailing.", "그의 재판관들은 깃털 달린 모자를 쓰고 재판석에 앉아 있었으나, 그 밖에는 거친 붉은 모자와 삼색 표장이 두루 쓰인 머리 장식이었다."], ["Looking at the Jury and the turbulent audience, he might have thought that the usual order of things was reversed, and that the felons were trying the honest men.", "배심원들과 소란스러운 방청객들을 바라보노라면, 그는 평소의 질서가 뒤바뀌어 중죄인들이 정직한 사람들을 재판하고 있다고 여겼을 법도 했다."], ["The lowest, cruelest, and worst populace of a city, never without its quantity of low, cruel, and bad, were the directing spirits of the scene: noisily commenting, applauding, disapproving, anticipating, and precipitating the result, without a check.", "한 도시에서 가장 천하고, 가장 잔인하고, 가장 못된 백성들이 — 천하고 잔인하고 나쁜 자들이 늘 일정량은 있게 마련인 그들이 — 이 장면을 이끄는 정신이었다. 그들은 시끄럽게 논평하고, 갈채를 보내고, 못마땅해하고, 결과를 미리 점치고, 아무런 제지 없이 그 결과를 재촉했다."], ["Of the men, the greater part were armed in various ways; of the women, some wore knives, some daggers, some ate and drank as they looked on, many knitted.", "남자들은 대부분 갖가지 방식으로 무장하고 있었고, 여자들 가운데 더러는 칼을, 더러는 단검을 지녔으며, 더러는 구경하면서 먹고 마셨고, 많은 이들이 뜨개질을 했다."], ["Among these last, was one, with a spare piece of knitting under her arm as she worked.", "이 마지막 부류 가운데, 일하는 동안 팔 밑에 여분의 뜨개질감을 끼고 있는 여자가 하나 있었다."], ["She was in a front row, by the side of a man whom he had never seen since his arrival at the Barrier, but whom he directly remembered as Defarge.", "그녀는 앞줄에 앉아 있었는데, 그 옆에는 그가 관문에 도착한 이래 한번도 보지 못한 한 남자가 있었으나, 그는 그를 드파르주라고 곧장 기억해냈다."], ["He noticed that she once or twice whispered in his ear, and that she seemed to be his wife; but, what he most noticed in the two figures was, that although they were posted as close to himself as they could be, they never looked towards him.", "그는 그녀가 한두번 그의 귀에 속삭이는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 그의 아내인 듯한 것을 알아차렸다. 그러나 그가 그 두 사람에게서 가장 눈여겨본 것은, 그들이 그에게 닿을 수 있는 한 가까이 자리 잡고 있으면서도 결코 그를 향해 눈길을 주지 않는다는 점이었다."], ["They seemed to be waiting for something with a dogged determination, and they looked at the Jury, but at nothing else.", "그들은 끈질긴 결의를 품고 무언가를 기다리는 듯했고, 배심원들을 바라볼 뿐 그밖에는 아무것도 보지 않았다."], ["Under the President sat Doctor Manette, in his usual quiet dress.", "재판장 아래에는 마네뜨 박사가 평소의 차분한 옷차림으로 앉아 있었다."], ["As well as the prisoner could see, he and Mr. Lorry were the only men there, unconnected with the Tribunal, who wore their usual clothes, and had not assumed the coarse garb of the Carmagnole.", "피고가 보기에, 그와 로리 씨가 그곳에서 법정과 무관하게 평소의 옷을 입고 있는, 까르마뇰의 거친 복장을 걸치지 않은 유일한 사람들이었다."]], [["Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay, was accused by the public prosecutor as an emigrant, whose life was forfeit to the Republic, under the decree which banished all emigrants on pain of Death.", "샤를 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이는 망명자라는 죄목으로 검사에게 고발되었는데, 모든 망명자를 사형으로써 추방한다는 포고령에 따라 그의 목숨은 공화국에 몰수된 것이었다."], ["It was nothing that the decree bore date since his return to France.", "그 포고령이 그가 프랑스로 돌아온 뒤의 날짜로 되어 있다는 것은 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["There he was, and there was the decree; he had been taken in France, and his head was demanded.", "그가 거기 있었고, 포고령이 거기 있었다. 그는 프랑스에서 체포되었고, 그의 목이 요구되었다."]], [["“Take off his head!”", "\"저자의 목을 쳐라!\""], ["cried the audience.", "방청객들이 외쳤다."], ["“An enemy to the Republic!”", "\"공화국의 적이다!\""]], [["The President rang his bell to silence those cries, and asked the prisoner whether it was not true that he had lived many years in England?", "재판장은 그 외침들을 잠재우려고 종을 울리고는, 피고에게 영국에서 여러 해 살았던 것이 사실이 아니냐고 물었다."]], [["Undoubtedly it was.", "틀림없는 사실이었다."]], [["Was he not an emigrant then?", "그렇다면 그는 망명자가 아닌가?"], ["What did he call himself?", "그는 자신을 무엇이라 일컫는가?"]], [["Not an emigrant, he hoped, within the sense and spirit of the law.", "법의 취지와 정신 안에서라면, 망명자가 아니기를 그는 바랐다."]], [["Why not?", "어째서 아닌가?"], ["the President desired to know.", "재판장은 알고 싶어 했다."]], [["Because he had voluntarily relinquished a title that was distasteful to him, and a station that was distasteful to him, and had left his country--he submitted before the word emigrant in the present acceptation by the Tribunal was in use--to live by his own industry in England, rather than on the industry of the overladen people of France.", "그는 자신에게 거슬리는 작위와, 자신에게 거슬리는 지위를 자발적으로 내려놓고, 자기 나라를 떠나 — 그는 법정에서 현재 통용되는 의미의 망명자라는 말이 쓰이기 전에 떠났다고 진술했다 — 짓눌린 프랑스 국민의 노동에 기대기보다는 영국에서 자신의 노동으로 살아가고자 했기 때문이었다."]], [["What proof had he of this?", "이에 대해 그에게 무슨 증거가 있는가?"]], [["He handed in the names of two witnesses; Theophile Gabelle, and Alexandre Manette.", "그는 두 증인의 이름을 제출했다. 떼오필 가벨과 알렉상드르 마네뜨였다."]], [["But he had married in England?", "그러나 그는 영국에서 결혼하지 않았는가?"], ["the President reminded him.", "재판장이 그에게 일깨웠다."]], [["True, but not an English woman.", "사실이었으나, 영국 여자는 아니었다."]], [["A citizeness of France?", "프랑스 여성 시민인가?"]], [["Yes.", "그렇다."], ["By birth.", "태생부터."]], [["Her name and family?", "그녀의 이름과 가문은?"]], [["“Lucie Manette, only daughter of Doctor Manette, the good physician who sits there.”", "\"뤼시 마네뜨, 저기 앉아 계신 훌륭한 의사이신 마네뜨 박사의 외동딸입니다.\""]], [["This answer had a happy effect upon the audience.", "이 대답은 방청객들에게 행복한 효과를 일으켰다."], ["Cries in exaltation of the well-known good physician rent the hall.", "잘 알려진 그 훌륭한 의사를 찬양하는 외침이 법정을 갈라놓았다."], ["So capriciously were the people moved, that tears immediately rolled down several ferocious countenances which had been glaring at the prisoner a moment before, as if with impatience to pluck him out into the streets and kill him.", "사람들이 어찌나 변덕스럽게 움직였던지, 조금 전까지 피고를 길거리로 끌어내어 죽이지 못해 안달하듯 그를 노려보던 몇몇 사나운 얼굴에서 곧바로 눈물이 굴러내렸다."]], [["On these few steps of his dangerous way, Charles Darnay had set his foot according to Doctor Manette’s reiterated instructions.", "자신의 위험한 길의 이 몇 걸음에서, 샤를 다네이는 마네뜨 박사가 거듭 일러준 지시에 따라 발을 디뎌놓았다."], ["The same cautious counsel directed every step that lay before him, and had prepared every inch of his road.", "똑같이 신중한 그 조언이 그의 앞에 놓인 모든 발걸음을 인도했고, 그의 길을 한치 한치 마련해두었던 것이다."]], [["The President asked, why had he returned to France when he did, and not sooner?", "재판장은 물었다. 어째서 그는 더 일찍 돌아오지 않고 그제야 프랑스로 돌아왔는가?"]], [["He had not returned sooner, he replied, simply because he had no means of living in France, save those he had resigned; whereas, in England, he lived by giving instruction in the French language and literature.", "더 일찍 돌아오지 않은 것은, 그가 포기한 것 말고는 프랑스에서 살아갈 수단이 전혀 없었기 때문이며, 반면에 영국에서는 프랑스어와 프랑스 문학을 가르치며 살았기 때문이라고 그는 대답했다."], ["He had returned when he did, on the pressing and written entreaty of a French citizen, who represented that his life was endangered by his absence.", "그가 그제야 돌아온 것은, 자신의 부재로 인해 목숨이 위태로워졌다고 호소하는 한 프랑스 시민의 절박하고 서면으로 된 간청 때문이었다."], ["He had come back, to save a citizen’s life, and to bear his testimony, at whatever personal hazard, to the truth.", "그는 한 시민의 목숨을 구하고, 어떤 개인적 위험을 무릅쓰더라도 진실을 증언하기 위해 돌아온 것이었다."], ["Was that criminal in the eyes of the Republic?", "그것이 공화국의 눈에 죄가 되는가?"]], [["The populace cried enthusiastically, “No!” and the President rang his bell to quiet them.", "백성들이 열광하여 \"아니다!\"라고 외쳤고, 재판장은 그들을 진정시키려고 종을 울렸다."], ["Which it did not, for they continued to cry “No!” until they left off, of their own will.", "그러나 종은 그러지 못했으니, 그들은 제 뜻으로 그칠 때까지 \"아니다!\"라고 계속 외쳤기 때문이다."]], [["The President required the name of that citizen.", "재판장은 그 시민의 이름을 요구했다."], ["The accused explained that the citizen was his first witness.", "피고는 그 시민이 자신의 첫번째 증인이라고 설명했다."], ["He also referred with confidence to the citizen’s letter, which had been taken from him at the Barrier, but which he did not doubt would be found among the papers then before the President.", "그는 또한 관문에서 자신에게서 압수된 그 시민의 편지를 자신있게 언급하며, 그것이 지금 재판장 앞에 놓인 서류들 가운데 있으리라는 것을 의심하지 않았다."]], [["The Doctor had taken care that it should be there--had assured him that it would be there--and at this stage of the proceedings it was produced and read.", "박사는 그것이 거기 있도록 조처해두었고 — 그것이 거기 있으리라고 그에게 확언해두었으며 — 절차의 이 단계에서 그것이 제출되어 낭독되었다."], ["Citizen Gabelle was called to confirm it, and did so.", "시민 가벨이 그것을 확인하라는 부름을 받았고, 그렇게 했다."], ["Citizen Gabelle hinted, with infinite delicacy and politeness, that in the pressure of business imposed on the Tribunal by the multitude of enemies of the Republic with which it had to deal, he had been slightly overlooked in his prison of the Abbaye--in fact, had rather passed out of the Tribunal’s patriotic remembrance--until three days ago; when he had been summoned before it, and had been set at liberty on the Jury’s declaring themselves satisfied that the accusation against him was answered, as to himself, by the surrender of the citizen Evrémonde, called Darnay.", "시민 가벨은 한없는 섬세함과 정중함으로 넌지시 말하기를, 법정이 상대해야 할 공화국의 적들이 워낙 많아 그 업무의 압박 속에서 자신이 아베이 감옥에서 약간 간과되었다고 — 실은 법정의 애국적 기억에서 좀 빠져 있었다고 — 사흘 전까지는 그러했는데, 그때 그가 법정 앞에 불려나갔고, 배심원들이 그에 대한 고발은 시민 에브레몽드, 일명 다네이의 자수로써 그 자신에 관한 한 답이 되었다고 만족을 선언함에 따라 풀려났다고 했다."]], [["Doctor Manette was next questioned.", "다음으로 마네뜨 박사가 신문을 받았다."], ["His high personal popularity, and the clearness of his answers, made a great impression; but, as he proceeded, as he showed that the Accused was his first friend on his release from his long imprisonment; that, the accused had remained in England, always faithful and devoted to his daughter and himself in their exile; that, so far from being in favour with the Aristocrat government there, he had actually been tried for his life by it, as the foe of England and friend of the United States--as he brought these circumstances into view, with the greatest discretion and with the straightforward force of truth and earnestness, the Jury and the populace became one.", "그의 높은 개인적 인기와 답변의 명료함은 큰 인상을 남겼다. 그러나 그가 말을 이어가면서, 피고가 자신이 오랜 투옥에서 풀려났을 때 처음으로 사귄 친구였다는 것, 피고가 영국에 머물며 망명 중인 자신의 딸과 자신에게 늘 충실하고 헌신적이었다는 것, 그곳의 귀족 정부에게 총애를 받기는커녕 실은 영국의 적이자 합중국의 벗이라 하여 그 정부에 의해 목숨을 걸고 재판받았다는 것을 밝히자 — 그가 더없는 신중함과 진실하고 진지한 진솔한 힘으로 이런 정황들을 드러내자, 배심원들과 백성들은 하나가 되었다."], ["At last, when he appealed by name to Monsieur Lorry, an English gentleman then and there present, who, like himself, had been a witness on that English trial and could corroborate his account of it, the Jury declared that they had heard enough, and that they were ready with their votes if the President were content to receive them.", "마침내 그가 그 자리에 함께 있던 영국 신사 로리 씨를 이름으로 호명하며, 그 역시 자신처럼 그 영국 재판에서 증인이었고 자신의 진술을 입증할 수 있다고 호소하자, 배심원들은 충분히 들었으며 재판장이 받아들이기를 원한다면 표결할 준비가 되었다고 선언했다."]], [["At every vote (the Jurymen voted aloud and individually), the populace set up a shout of applause.", "표가 던져질 때마다 (배심원들은 각자 소리내어 표결했다) 백성들은 갈채의 함성을 질렀다."], ["All the voices were in the prisoner’s favour, and the President declared him free.", "모든 목소리가 피고에게 유리했고, 재판장은 그를 자유의 몸이라고 선언했다."]], [["Then, began one of those extraordinary scenes with which the populace sometimes gratified their fickleness, or their better impulses towards generosity and mercy, or which they regarded as some set-off against their swollen account of cruel rage.", "그러자 백성들이 때로 자신들의 변덕을, 또는 관대함과 자비를 향한 더 나은 충동을 만족시키거나, 혹은 부풀어오른 잔혹한 분노의 계산서에 대한 일종의 상쇄로 여기던 그 비범한 장면 가운데 하나가 시작되었다."], ["No man can decide now to which of these motives such extraordinary scenes were referable; it is probable, to a blending of all the three, with the second predominating.", "그 비범한 장면들이 이 동기들 가운데 어느 것에 속하는지는 이제 아무도 단정할 수 없으니, 아마도 세가지 모두가 뒤섞인 것이되 두번째가 우세했으리라."], ["No sooner was the acquittal pronounced, than tears were shed as freely as blood at another time, and such fraternal embraces were bestowed upon the prisoner by as many of both sexes as could rush at him, that after his long and unwholesome confinement he was in danger of fainting from exhaustion; none the less because he knew very well, that the very same people, carried by another current, would have rushed at him with the very same intensity, to rend him to pieces and strew him over the streets.", "무죄가 선고되기가 무섭게, 다른 때에 피가 흐르듯 자유로이 눈물이 흘렀고, 그에게 달려들 수 있는 만큼의 남녀가 피고에게 그토록 형제애 넘치는 포옹을 퍼부었던 까닭에, 그는 길고 해로운 감금을 겪은 뒤라 탈진하여 기절할 위험에 처했다. 그가 너무도 잘 알고 있는 사실, 곧 바로 그 똑같은 사람들이 다른 흐름에 실리면 똑같은 격렬함으로 그에게 달려들어 그를 갈가리 찢어 거리에 뿌렸으리라는 것을 알았기에 더더욱 그러했다."]], [["His removal, to make way for other accused persons who were to be tried, rescued him from these caresses for the moment.", "재판받을 다른 피고인들에게 자리를 내주기 위해 그가 옮겨지면서, 그는 잠시 이러한 애무에서 벗어났다."], ["Five were to be tried together, next, as enemies of the Republic, forasmuch as they had not assisted it by word or deed.", "다음으로 다섯 사람이 공화국의 적으로서 함께 재판받을 참이었으니, 말로든 행동으로든 공화국을 돕지 않았다는 이유에서였다."], ["So quick was the Tribunal to compensate itself and the nation for a chance lost, that these five came down to him before he left the place, condemned to die within twenty-four hours.", "법정은 놓친 기회를 자신과 국가에 보상하는 데 어찌나 빨랐던지, 그가 그곳을 떠나기도 전에 그 다섯 사람이 스물네시간 안에 죽으라는 선고를 받고 그에게로 내려왔다."], ["The first of them told him so, with the customary prison sign of Death--a raised finger--and they all added in words, “Long live the Republic!”", "그들 가운데 첫번째 사람이 죽음을 뜻하는 감옥의 관습적인 신호로 — 손가락 하나를 치켜올리며 — 그에게 그 사실을 알렸고, 그들 모두가 말로 덧붙였다. \"공화국 만세!\""]], [["The five had had, it is true, no audience to lengthen their proceedings, for when he and Doctor Manette emerged from the gate, there was a great crowd about it, in which there seemed to be every face he had seen in Court--except two, for which he looked in vain.", "사실 그 다섯 사람에게는 절차를 길게 끌어줄 방청객이 없었으니, 그와 마네뜨 박사가 문을 나섰을 때 그 둘레에 커다란 군중이 있었기 때문이다. 그 안에는 법정에서 보았던 모든 얼굴이 다 있는 듯했다 — 두 사람만 빼고, 그는 그 둘을 찾았으나 헛수고였다."], ["On his coming out, the concourse made at him anew, weeping, embracing, and shouting, all by turns and all together, until the very tide of the river on the bank of which the mad scene was acted, seemed to run mad, like the people on the shore.", "그가 나오자 군중이 새로이 그에게 몰려들어, 울고, 끌어안고, 외쳤으니, 모두 번갈아가며 그리고 모두 한꺼번에 그러했고, 마침내 이 미친 장면이 펼쳐지던 강기슭의 그 강물조차 강가의 사람들처럼 미쳐 날뛰는 것만 같았다."]], [["They put him into a great chair they had among them, and which they had taken either out of the Court itself, or one of its rooms or passages.", "그들은 그들 가운데 가지고 있던 커다란 의자에 그를 앉혔는데, 그것은 법정 자체에서, 아니면 그 방이나 복도 가운데 하나에서 꺼내온 것이었다."], ["Over the chair they had thrown a red flag, and to the back of it they had bound a pike with a red cap on its top.", "그들은 의자 위에 붉은 깃발을 던져 덮었고, 그 등받이에는 꼭대기에 붉은 모자를 씌운 창을 묶어두었다."], ["In this car of triumph, not even the Doctor’s entreaties could prevent his being carried to his home on men’s shoulders, with a confused sea of red caps heaving about him, and casting up to sight from the stormy deep such wrecks of faces, that he more than once misdoubted his mind being in confusion, and that he was in the tumbril on his way to the Guillotine.", "이 개선의 수레에서, 박사의 간청조차도 그가 사람들의 어깨에 실려 자기 집으로 옮겨지는 것을 막을 수 없었으니, 어지러운 붉은 모자의 바다가 그를 둘러싸고 출렁이며, 그 폭풍 같은 심연에서 사람 얼굴의 그런 잔해들을 눈앞에 떠올려놓았던 까닭에, 그는 한두번이 아니라 자기 정신이 혼란에 빠진 것이며 자신이 단두대로 끌려가는 호송 수레에 타고 있다고 의심했다."]], [["In wild dreamlike procession, embracing whom they met and pointing him out, they carried him on.", "미친 듯 꿈결 같은 행렬을 이루어, 마주치는 이를 끌어안고 그를 가리키며, 그들은 그를 실어 갔다."], ["Reddening the snowy streets with the prevailing Republican colour, in winding and tramping through them, as they had reddened them below the snow with a deeper dye, they carried him thus into the courtyard of the building where he lived.", "구불구불 돌고 발을 구르며 지나가는 눈 덮인 거리를 두루 퍼진 공화국의 색으로 붉게 물들이며 — 그들이 일찍이 눈 밑을 더 짙은 물감으로 붉게 물들였던 것처럼 — 그들은 그를 그렇게 그가 살던 건물의 안뜰로 실어 갔다."], ["Her father had gone on before, to prepare her, and when her husband stood upon his feet, she dropped insensible in his arms.", "그녀의 아버지가 그녀에게 알리려고 앞서 가 있었고, 그녀의 남편이 두 발로 서자 그녀는 정신을 잃고 그의 품에 쓰러졌다."]], [["As he held her to his heart and turned her beautiful head between his face and the brawling crowd, so that his tears and her lips might come together unseen, a few of the people fell to dancing.", "그가 그녀를 가슴에 안고, 자신의 눈물과 그녀의 입술이 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 만날 수 있도록 그녀의 아름다운 머리를 자기 얼굴과 떠들썩한 군중 사이로 돌려놓는데, 몇몇 사람들이 춤을 추기 시작했다."], ["Instantly, all the rest fell to dancing, and the courtyard overflowed with the Carmagnole.", "곧바로 나머지 사람들도 모두 춤을 추기 시작했고, 안뜰은 까르마뇰로 넘쳐흘렀다."], ["Then, they elevated into the vacant chair a young woman from the crowd to be carried as the Goddess of Liberty, and then swelling and overflowing out into the adjacent streets, and along the river’s bank, and over the bridge, the Carmagnole absorbed them every one and whirled them away.", "그러더니 그들은 빈 의자 위에 군중 가운데서 젊은 여자 하나를 올려 자유의 여신으로 떠받쳐 실었고, 인근 거리로, 강기슭을 따라, 다리 너머로 부풀어 넘쳐흐르며, 까르마뇰은 그들 모두를 빨아들여 휩쓸어 갔다."]], [["After grasping the Doctor’s hand, as he stood victorious and proud before him; after grasping the hand of Mr. Lorry, who came panting in breathless from his struggle against the waterspout of the Carmagnole; after kissing little Lucie, who was lifted up to clasp her arms round his neck; and after embracing the ever zealous and faithful Pross who lifted her; he took his wife in his arms, and carried her up to their rooms.", "그는 자기 앞에 승리에 차서 자랑스럽게 서 있는 박사의 손을 움켜쥐고 나서, 까르마뇰의 물기둥과 맞서 싸우느라 숨이 차서 헐떡이며 들어온 로리 씨의 손을 움켜쥐고 나서, 들어올려져 그의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안은 어린 뤼시에게 입을 맞추고 나서, 그 아이를 들어올린 늘 열성적이고 충실한 프로스를 끌어안고 나서, 아내를 두 팔로 안아 그들의 방으로 데리고 올라갔다."]], [["“Lucie!", "\"뤼시!"], ["My own!", "내 사랑!"], ["I am safe.”", "나는 무사하오.\""]], [["“O dearest Charles, let me thank God for this on my knees as I have prayed to Him.”", "\"오, 가장 사랑하는 샤를, 제가 그분께 기도드렸던 것처럼 무릎 꿇고 이 일을 하느님께 감사드리게 해주세요.\""]], [["They all reverently bowed their heads and hearts.", "그들은 모두 경건하게 머리와 마음을 숙였다."], ["When she was again in his arms, he said to her:", "그녀가 다시 그의 품에 안기자, 그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“And now speak to your father, dearest.", "\"그리고 이제 당신 아버님께 말씀드리시오, 여보."], ["No other man in all this France could have done what he has done for me.”", "이 온 프랑스의 그 누구도 그분이 나를 위해 해주신 일을 해내지 못했을 거요.\""]], [["She laid her head upon her father’s breast, as she had laid his poor head on her own breast, long, long ago.", "그녀는 오래오래 전에 그의 가엾은 머리를 자기 가슴에 누였던 것처럼, 자기 머리를 아버지의 가슴에 기댔다."], ["He was happy in the return he had made her, he was recompensed for his suffering, he was proud of his strength.", "그는 그녀에게 베푼 보답으로 행복했고, 자신의 고통을 보상받았으며, 자신의 강인함을 자랑스러워했다."], ["“You must not be weak, my darling,”", "\"약해지면 안 된다, 내 사랑아.\""], ["he remonstrated; “don’t tremble so.", "그가 타일렀다. \"그렇게 떨지 마라."], ["I have saved him.”", "내가 그를 구했단다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER VII. A Knock at the Door", "# CHAPTER VII. A Knock at the Door"]], [["“I have saved him.”", "“내가 그를 구했다.”"], ["It was not another of the dreams in which he had often come back; he was really here.", "이것은 그가 자주 돌아오곤 하던 그 꿈들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 그는 정말로 여기 있었다."], ["And yet his wife trembled, and a vague but heavy fear was upon her.", "그런데도 그의 아내는 떨었고, 막연하지만 무거운 두려움이 그녀를 짓눌렀다."]], [["All the air round was so thick and dark, the people were so passionately revengeful and fitful, the innocent were so constantly put to death on vague suspicion and black malice, it was so impossible to forget that many as blameless as her husband and as dear to others as he was to her, every day shared the fate from which he had been clutched, that her heart could not be as lightened of its load as she felt it ought to be.", "주위의 공기는 온통 너무도 답답하고 어두웠으며, 사람들은 너무도 격정적으로 복수심에 차 변덕스러웠고, 무고한 이들이 막연한 의심과 시커먼 악의 때문에 너무도 끊임없이 처형당했으며, 자기 남편만큼이나 죄 없고 자기에게 그가 소중한 만큼 다른 이들에게 소중한 많은 사람들이 그가 가까스로 벗어난 운명을 날마다 함께 겪고 있다는 사실을 잊기란 너무도 불가능해서, 그녀의 마음은 마땅히 가벼워져야 한다고 느끼는 만큼 그 짐을 덜어내지 못했다."], ["The shadows of the wintry afternoon were beginning to fall, and even now the dreadful carts were rolling through the streets.", "겨울 오후의 그림자가 내려앉기 시작했고, 바로 지금 이 순간에도 그 무시무시한 수레들이 거리를 굴러가고 있었다."], ["Her mind pursued them, looking for him among the Condemned; and then she clung closer to his real presence and trembled more.", "그녀의 마음은 그 수레들을 뒤쫓으며 사형수들 가운데서 그를 찾았다. 그러고는 실재하는 그의 존재에 더욱 매달리며 더욱 떨었다."]], [["Her father, cheering her, showed a compassionate superiority to this woman’s weakness, which was wonderful to see.", "그녀의 아버지는 그녀를 다독이며, 이 여자다운 연약함을 향해 가엾이 여기는 우월감을 드러냈는데, 그 모습은 보기에 놀라웠다."], ["No garret, no shoemaking, no One Hundred and Five, North Tower, now!", "이제는 다락방도 없고, 구두 짓기도 없고, 북쪽 탑 백오호도 없다!"], ["He had accomplished the task he had set himself, his promise was redeemed, he had saved Charles.", "그는 스스로에게 부과한 과업을 이루어냈고, 그의 약속은 지켜졌으며, 그는 샤를을 구했다."], ["Let them all lean upon him.", "모두 그에게 기대게 하라."]], [["Their housekeeping was of a very frugal kind: not only because that was the safest way of life, involving the least offence to the people, but because they were not rich, and Charles, throughout his imprisonment, had had to pay heavily for his bad food, and for his guard, and towards the living of the poorer prisoners.", "그들의 살림은 매우 검소했다. 그것이 사람들의 반감을 가장 적게 사며 가장 안전하게 사는 길이었기 때문만은 아니라, 그들이 부유하지 않았기 때문이기도 했다."], ["Partly on this account, and partly to avoid a domestic spy, they kept no servant; the citizen and citizeness who acted as porters at the courtyard gate, rendered them occasional service; and Jerry (almost wholly transferred to them by Mr.", "게다가 샤를은 갇혀 있는 동안 내내 형편없는 음식값과 간수값, 그리고 더 가난한 죄수들의 생활비까지 비싸게 치러야 했던 것이다. 한편으로는 이런 이유로, 또 한편으로는 집 안의 첩자를 피하기 위해 그들은 하인을 두지 않았다."], ["Lorry) had become their daily retainer, and had his bed there every night.", "안마당 문지기 노릇을 하는 시민 남자와 시민 여자가 이따금 잡일을 해주었고, (로리 씨가 거의 통째로 그들에게 넘겨준) 제리는 그들의 날품 시중꾼이 되어 밤마다 그곳에서 잠자리를 얻었다."]], [["It was an ordinance of the Republic One and Indivisible of Liberty, Equality, Fraternity, or Death, that on the door or doorpost of every house, the name of every inmate must be legibly inscribed in letters of a certain size, at a certain convenient height from the ground. Mr.", "자유, 평등, 박애, 아니면 죽음의 하나이며 나뉠 수 없는 공화국의 법령에 따르면, 모든 집의 문이나 문기둥에는 거주자 전원의 이름을 일정한 크기의 글자로, 땅에서 일정하게 적당한 높이에, 또렷이 새겨 넣어야 했다."], ["Jerry Cruncher’s name, therefore, duly embellished the doorpost down below; and, as the afternoon shadows deepened, the owner of that name himself appeared, from overlooking a painter whom Doctor Manette had employed to add to the list the name of Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay.", "그리하여 제리 크런처 씨의 이름이 아래쪽 문기둥을 정식으로 장식하고 있었다. 그리고 오후의 그림자가 짙어질 무렵, 그 이름의 임자가 몸소 나타났는데, 그는 마네뜨 박사가 고용해 그 명단에 다네라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드의 이름을 덧붙이게 한 칠장이를 지켜보고 오는 길이었다."]], [["In the universal fear and distrust that darkened the time, all the usual harmless ways of life were changed.", "그 시절을 어둡게 물들인 보편적인 두려움과 불신 속에서, 일상의 무해한 생활 방식이 죄다 바뀌었다."], ["In the Doctor’s little household, as in very many others, the articles of daily consumption that were wanted were purchased every evening, in small quantities and at various small shops.", "박사의 작은 살림집에서도 다른 수많은 집들과 마찬가지로, 필요한 일용품들을 매일 저녁 적은 양으로 여러 작은 가게에서 사들였다."], ["To avoid attracting notice, and to give as little occasion as possible for talk and envy, was the general desire.", "남의 눈에 띄지 않고, 입길에 오르내리거나 시샘을 살 빌미를 되도록 적게 주는 것이 모두의 바람이었다."]], [["For some months past, Miss Pross and Mr. Cruncher had discharged the office of purveyors; the former carrying the money; the latter, the basket.", "지난 몇 달 동안 프로스 양과 크런처 씨가 물품 조달 임무를 맡아왔다."], ["Every afternoon at about the time when the public lamps were lighted, they fared forth on this duty, and made and brought home such purchases as were needful.", "프로스 양은 돈을, 크런처 씨는 바구니를 들었다. 오후마다 거리의 가로등에 불이 켜질 무렵이면 그들은 이 일을 하러 길을 나서, 필요한 물건들을 사서 집으로 가져왔다."], ["Although Miss Pross, through her long association with a French family, might have known as much of their language as of her own, if she had had a mind, she had no mind in that direction; consequently she knew no more of that “nonsense”", "프로스 양은 프랑스인 가족과 오래 어울려 지냈으니 마음만 먹었다면 그들의 말을 제 모국어만큼이나 알 수도 있었겠지만, 그녀에게는 그쪽으로 마음이 없었다."], ["(as she was pleased to call it) than Mr. Cruncher did.", "따라서 그녀는 그 “허튼소리”(그녀는 즐겨 그렇게 불렀다)에 대해 크런처 씨보다 더 아는 게 없었다."], ["So her manner of marketing was to plump a noun-substantive at the head of a shopkeeper without any introduction in the nature of an article, and, if it happened not to be the name of the thing she wanted, to look round for that thing, lay hold of it, and hold on by it until the bargain was concluded.", "그래서 그녀가 장을 보는 방식은, 관사 따위로 운을 떼는 일도 없이 가게 주인의 머리에다 대고 명사 하나를 툭 던지는 것이었고, 그것이 마침 자기가 원하는 물건의 이름이 아닐 경우에는 그 물건을 둘러보며 찾아내 움켜쥐고는 흥정이 끝날 때까지 붙잡고 놓지 않는 것이었다."], ["She always made a bargain for it, by holding up, as a statement of its just price, one finger less than the merchant held up, whatever his number might be.", "그녀는 언제나 흥정을 했는데, 상인이 손가락 몇개를 펴 보이든 그보다 하나 적은 손가락을 정당한 값이라는 표시로 펴 보이는 식이었다."]], [["“Now, Mr.", "“자, 크런처 씨,”"], ["Cruncher,”", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Pross, whose eyes were red with felicity; “if you are ready, I am.”", "그녀의 두 눈은 행복으로 붉어져 있었다. “당신이 준비됐으면, 나도 됐어요.”"]], [["Jerry hoarsely professed himself at Miss Pross’s service.", "제리는 쉰 목소리로 프로스 양에게 봉사할 채비가 되어 있다고 밝혔다."], ["He had worn all his rust off long ago, but nothing would file his spiky head down.", "그는 오래전에 녹을 죄다 벗겨냈지만, 그 어떤 것도 그의 뾰족한 머리만은 다듬어 내릴 수 없었다."]], [["“There’s all manner of things wanted,”", "“온갖 것들이 다 필요해요,”"], ["said Miss Pross, “and we shall have a precious time of it.", "프로스 양이 말했다. “우리는 한바탕 진땀을 빼게 될 거예요."], ["We want wine, among the rest.", "그중에서도 포도주가 필요하지요."], ["Nice toasts these Redheads will be drinking, wherever we buy it.”", "우리가 어디서 사든, 그 빨강머리들이 멋들어진 건배를 하겠군요.”"]], [["“It will be much the same to your knowledge, miss, I should think,”", "“제 생각으론, 아가씨, 그자들이 아가씨의 건강을 위해 마시든 영감님을 위해 마시든 아가씨가 알기엔 별 차이 없을 것 같습니다만,”"], ["retorted Jerry, “whether they drink your health or the Old Un’s.”", "제리가 받아쳤다."]], [["“Who’s he?”", "“그 영감님이 누군데요?”"], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher, with some diffidence, explained himself as meaning “Old Nick’s.”", "크런처 씨는 다소 머뭇거리며, 자기가 말한 것은 “늙은 닉”을 뜻한다고 설명했다."]], [["“Ha!”", "* 늙은 닉(Old Nick): 악마를 가리키는 영어 속어."], ["said Miss Pross, “it doesn’t need an interpreter to explain the meaning of these creatures.", "“하!” 프로스 양이 말했다."], ["They have but one, and it’s Midnight Murder, and Mischief.”", "“저 족속들의 속내를 풀이하는 데는 통역이 필요 없어요. 저들에겐 단 하나뿐이고, 그건 한밤중의 살인과 행패니까요.”"]], [["“Hush, dear!", "“쉿, 그러지 마세요!"], ["Pray, pray, be cautious!”", "제발, 제발 조심하세요!”"], ["cried Lucie.", "루씨가 외쳤다."]], [["“Yes, yes, yes, I’ll be cautious,”", "“그래요, 그래, 그래, 조심할게요,”"], ["said Miss Pross; “but I may say among ourselves, that I do hope there will be no oniony and tobaccoey smotherings in the form of embracings all round, going on in the streets.", "프로스 양이 말했다. “하지만 우리끼리니까 말인데, 거리에서 서로 끌어안는답시고 양파 냄새, 담배 냄새 풍기며 숨 막히게 하는 짓거리가 없었으면 정말 좋겠어요."], ["Now, Ladybird, never you stir from that fire till I come back!", "자, 무당벌레, 내가 돌아올 때까지 그 난롯가에서 한 발짝도 움직이지 마요!"], ["Take care of the dear husband you have recovered, and don’t move your pretty head from his shoulder as you have it now, till you see me again!", "되찾은 소중한 남편을 잘 돌보고, 나를 다시 볼 때까지 지금처럼 그 예쁜 머리를 남편 어깨에서 떼지 말아요!"], ["May I ask a question, Doctor Manette, before I go?”", "박사님, 가기 전에 한 가지 여쭤봐도 될까요?”"]], [["“I think you may take that liberty,”", "“그런 자유쯤은 누리셔도 될 것 같습니다,”"], ["the Doctor answered, smiling.", "박사가 미소 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“For gracious sake, don’t talk about Liberty; we have quite enough of that,”", "“제발 자유 얘기는 마세요. 그건 지긋지긋하게 많으니까요,”"], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["“Hush, dear! Again?”", "“쉿, 그러지 마세요!"], ["Lucie remonstrated.", "또요?” 루씨가 타일렀다."]], [["“Well, my sweet,”", "“그래, 얘야,” 프로스 양이 힘주어 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["said Miss Pross, nodding her head emphatically, “the short and the long of it is, that I am a subject of His Most Gracious Majesty King George the Third;”", "“요컨대 짧게 말하자면, 나는 자비로우신 국왕 조지 삼세 폐하의 신민이라는 거야.” 프로스 양은 그 이름에 무릎을 굽혀 절을 했다."], ["Miss Pross curtseyed at the name; “and as such, my maxim is, Confound their politics, Frustrate their knavish tricks, On him our hopes we fix, God save the King!”", "“그러니 내 좌우명은 이거지. 저들의 음모를 혼란케 하시고, 저들의 간악한 술수를 좌절케 하시며, 그분께 우리의 희망을 거나니, 하느님이여 국왕을 지키소서!”"]], [["Mr. Cruncher, in an access of loyalty, growlingly repeated the words after Miss Pross, like somebody at church.", "크런처 씨는 충성심이 북받쳐, 교회에 온 사람처럼 프로스 양을 따라 그 말을 우물우물 되뇌었다."]], [["“I am glad you have so much of the Englishman in you, though I wish you had never taken that cold in your voice,”", "“당신 안에 그렇게 영국인다운 데가 많다니 기쁘군요. 다만 그 목소리에 그런 감기가 든 적이 없었다면 좋았을 텐데요,”"], ["said Miss Pross, approvingly.", "프로스 양이 흐뭇해하며 말했다."], ["“But the question, Doctor Manette.", "“그건 그렇고 박사님, 그 질문 말인데요."], ["Is there”--it was the good creature’s way to affect to make light of anything that was a great anxiety with them all, and to come at it in this chance manner--“is there any prospect yet, of our getting out of this place?”", "혹시”—그들 모두에게 큰 근심거리인 무언가를 짐짓 대수롭지 않은 척하며 이렇듯 지나가는 투로 꺼내는 것이 이 선량한 사람의 방식이었다—“혹시 우리가 이곳을 벗어날 가망이 아직은 좀 보이나요?”"]], [["“I fear not yet.", "“아직은 아닐 듯합니다."], ["It would be dangerous for Charles yet.”", "아직 샤를에게는 위험한 일일 테니까요.”"]], [["“Heigh-ho-hum!”", "“에휴, 저런!”"], ["said Miss Pross, cheerfully repressing a sigh as she glanced at her darling’s golden hair in the light of the fire, “then we must have patience and wait: that’s all.", "프로스 양은 난롯불 빛 속에 빛나는 사랑하는 아씨의 금빛 머리칼을 흘끗 보며 한숨을 명랑하게 억누른 채 말했다. “그렇다면 우린 참고 기다려야겠군요."], ["We must hold up our heads and fight low, as my brother Solomon used to say.", "별수 없죠. 우리 오라비 솔로몬이 늘 말하던 대로, 고개를 높이 들고 낮게 싸워야 해요."], ["Now, Mr. Cruncher!--Don’t you move, Ladybird!”", "자, 크런처 씨!—무당벌레, 움직이지 마요!”"]], [["They went out, leaving Lucie, and her husband, her father, and the child, by a bright fire. Mr.", "그들은 루씨와 그녀의 남편, 그녀의 아버지, 그리고 아이를 환한 난롯가에 남겨둔 채 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Lorry was expected back presently from the Banking House.", "로리 씨는 곧 은행에서 돌아오기로 되어 있었다."], ["Miss Pross had lighted the lamp, but had put it aside in a corner, that they might enjoy the fire-light undisturbed.", "프로스 양은 등불을 켜두었지만, 그들이 방해받지 않고 난롯불 빛을 즐길 수 있도록 한쪽 구석에 치워두었다."], ["Little Lucie sat by her grandfather with her hands clasped through his arm: and he, in a tone not rising much above a whisper, began to tell her a story of a great and powerful Fairy who had opened a prison-wall and let out a captive who had once done the Fairy a service.", "어린 루씨는 할아버지 곁에 앉아 두 손을 그의 팔에 끼워 맞잡고 있었다. 그리고 그는 속삭임보다 그리 높지 않은 어조로, 감옥 벽을 열어 한때 자신에게 은혜를 베푼 포로를 풀어준 위대하고 강력한 요정의 이야기를 그 아이에게 들려주기 시작했다."], ["All was subdued and quiet, and Lucie was more at ease than she had been.", "모든 것이 가라앉고 고요했으며, 루씨는 전보다 한결 마음이 놓였다."]], [["“What is that?”", "“저게 뭐죠?”"], ["she cried, all at once.", "그녀가 별안간 외쳤다."]], [["“My dear!”", "“얘야!”"], ["said her father, stopping in his story, and laying his hand on hers, “command yourself.", "그녀의 아버지가 이야기를 멈추고 그녀의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. “마음을 다잡으렴."], ["What a disordered state you are in!", "어쩌면 이리도 어지러운 상태에 있느냐!"], ["The least thing--nothing--startles you!", "아주 사소한 것에—아무것도 아닌 것에—깜짝깜짝 놀라다니!"], ["You, your father’s daughter!”", "다른 사람도 아니고 네가, 네 아버지의 딸이!”"]], [["“I thought, my father,”", "“아버지, 저는,”"], ["said Lucie, excusing herself, with a pale face and in a faltering voice, “that I heard strange feet upon the stairs.”", "루씨가 창백한 얼굴로 더듬더듬 변명하며 말했다. “계단에서 낯선 발소리가 들린 것 같았어요.”"]], [["“My love, the staircase is as still as Death.”", "“얘야, 계단은 죽음처럼 고요하기만 하구나.”"]], [["As he said the word, a blow was struck upon the door.", "그가 그 말을 하는 순간, 문에 일격이 가해졌다."]], [["“Oh father, father.", "“오 아버지, 아버지."], ["What can this be!", "이게 무슨 일일까요!"], ["Hide Charles.", "샤를을 숨겨요."], ["Save him!”", "그이를 구해줘요!”"]], [["“My child,”", "“얘야,”"], ["said the Doctor, rising, and laying his hand upon her shoulder, “I have saved him.", "박사가 일어서며 그녀의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다. “나는 이미 그를 구했단다."], ["What weakness is this, my dear!", "이 무슨 연약함이냐, 얘야!"], ["Let me go to the door.”", "내가 문으로 나가보마.”"]], [["He took the lamp in his hand, crossed the two intervening outer rooms, and opened it.", "그는 손에 등불을 들고 사이에 놓인 바깥쪽 두 방을 가로질러 문을 열었다."], ["A rude clattering of feet over the floor, and four rough men in red caps, armed with sabres and pistols, entered the room.", "바닥을 거칠게 울리는 발소리가 들리더니, 붉은 모자를 쓰고 군도와 권총으로 무장한 사나운 사내 넷이 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“The Citizen Evrémonde, called Darnay,”", "“다네라 불리는 시민 에브레몽드,”"], ["said the first.", "첫번째 사내가 말했다."]], [["“Who seeks him?”", "“누가 그를 찾소?”"], ["answered Darnay.", "다네가 대답했다."]], [["“I seek him. We seek him.", "“내가 그를 찾는다."], ["I know you, Evrémonde; I saw you before the Tribunal to-day.", "우리가 그를 찾는다. 나는 당신을 안다, 에브레몽드."], ["You are again the prisoner of the Republic.”", "오늘 재판정에서 당신을 봤다. 당신은 다시 공화국의 죄수다.”"]], [["The four surrounded him, where he stood with his wife and child clinging to him.", "넷은 아내와 아이가 매달려 있는 그를 둘러쌌다."]], [["“Tell me how and why am I again a prisoner?”", "“내가 어떻게 해서, 왜 다시 죄수가 됐는지 말해주시오.”"]], [["“It is enough that you return straight to the Conciergerie, and will know to-morrow.", "“당신이 콩시에르주리로 곧장 돌아가면 그것으로 충분하고, 내일이면 알게 될 것이다."], ["You are summoned for to-morrow.”", "당신은 내일 소환됐다.”"]], [["Doctor Manette, whom this visitation had so turned into stone, that he stood with the lamp in his hand, as if he were a statue made to hold it, moved after these words were spoken, put the lamp down, and confronting the speaker, and taking him, not ungently, by the loose front of his red woollen shirt, said:", "이 방문에 그토록 돌처럼 굳어버려, 마치 등불을 들도록 만들어진 조각상이라도 된 듯 손에 등불을 든 채 서 있던 마네뜨 박사는, 이 말이 다 떨어지자 움직여 등불을 내려놓고는, 말한 자와 마주 서서 그의 붉은 모직 셔츠 앞섶을 거칠지 않게 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["“You know him, you have said.", "“당신은 그를 안다고 했소."], ["Do you know me?”", "나도 아시오?”"]], [["“Yes, I know you, Citizen Doctor.”", "“그렇소, 당신을 압니다, 시민 박사.”"]], [["“We all know you, Citizen Doctor,”", "“우리는 다들 당신을 알지요, 시민 박사,”"], ["said the other three.", "나머지 셋이 말했다."]], [["He looked abstractedly from one to another, and said, in a lower voice, after a pause:", "그는 멍하니 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보다가, 잠시 사이를 두고 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Will you answer his question to me then?", "“그렇다면 그가 내게 한 질문에 답해주겠소?"], ["How does this happen?”", "어떻게 이런 일이 벌어진 거요?”"]], [["“Citizen Doctor,”", "“시민 박사,”"], ["said the first, reluctantly, “he has been denounced to the Section of Saint Antoine.", "첫번째 사내가 마지못해 말했다. “그는 쌩땅뚜안 구역에 고발당했소."], ["This citizen,”", "이 시민이,”"], ["pointing out the second who had entered, “is from Saint Antoine.”", "그러면서 두번째로 들어온 자를 가리켰다. “쌩땅뚜안 사람이오.”"]], [["The citizen here indicated nodded his head, and added:", "여기서 지목된 시민이 고개를 끄덕이며 덧붙였다."]], [["“He is accused by Saint Antoine.”", "“그는 쌩땅뚜안의 고발을 받았소.”"]], [["“Of what?”", "“무슨 죄로?”"], ["asked the Doctor.", "박사가 물었다."]], [["“Citizen Doctor,”", "“시민 박사,”"], ["said the first, with his former reluctance, “ask no more.", "첫번째 사내가 아까처럼 마지못해하며 말했다. “더는 묻지 마시오."], ["If the Republic demands sacrifices from you, without doubt you as a good patriot will be happy to make them.", "공화국이 당신에게 희생을 요구한다면, 의심할 바 없이 당신은 훌륭한 애국자로서 기꺼이 그것을 바칠 것이오."], ["The Republic goes before all.", "공화국이 모든 것에 앞서니까."], ["The People is supreme.", "인민이 지고하니까."], ["Evrémonde, we are pressed.”", "에브레몽드, 우린 시간이 촉박하오.”"]], [["“One word,”", "“한마디만,”"], ["the Doctor entreated.", "박사가 간청했다."], ["“Will you tell me who denounced him?”", "“누가 그를 고발했는지 말해주겠소?”"]], [["“It is against rule,”", "“그건 규칙에 어긋나오,”"], ["answered the first; “but you can ask Him of Saint Antoine here.”", "첫번째 사내가 대답했다. “하지만 여기 쌩땅뚜안 사람에게 물어볼 수는 있겠지요.”"]], [["The Doctor turned his eyes upon that man.", "박사는 그 사내에게 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["Who moved uneasily on his feet, rubbed his beard a little, and at length said:", "그자는 불안하게 발을 옮기며 수염을 잠시 문지르다가, 마침내 입을 열었다."]], [["“Well!", "“글쎄!"], ["Truly it is against rule.", "정말이지 이건 규칙에 어긋나오."], ["But he is denounced--and gravely--by the Citizen and Citizeness Defarge.", "하지만 그는 고발당했소—그것도 무겁게—시민 드파르주와 시민 드파르주 부인에게서 말이오."], ["And by one other.”", "그리고 또 한 사람에게서.”"]], [["“What other?”", "“또 한 사람이라니?”"]], [["“Do you ask, Citizen Doctor?”", "“시민 박사, 당신이 그걸 묻는 거요?”"]], [["“Yes.”", "“그렇소.”"]], [["“Then,”", "“그렇다면,”"], ["said he of Saint Antoine, with a strange look, “you will be answered to-morrow.", "쌩땅뚜안의 사내가 묘한 표정으로 말했다. “내일이면 답을 듣게 될 거요."], ["Now, I am dumb!”", "지금 나는 입을 다물겠소!”"]], [["# CHAPTER VIII. A Hand at Cards", "# CHAPTER VIII. A Hand at Cards"]], [["Happily unconscious of the new calamity at home, Miss Pross threaded her way along the narrow streets and crossed the river by the bridge of the Pont-Neuf, reckoning in her mind the number of indispensable purchases she had to make.", "집의 새로운 재앙을 다행히 알지 못한 채, 프로스 양은 좁은 길을 헤치며 나아가 뽕네프 다리를 건너 강을 가로질렀고, 마음속으로는 꼭 사야 할 물건들의 수를 헤아리고 있었다."], ["Mr. Cruncher, with the basket, walked at her side.", "크런처 씨는 바구니를 들고 그녀 곁에서 걸었다."], ["They both looked to the right and to the left into most of the shops they passed, had a wary eye for all gregarious assemblages of people, and turned out of their road to avoid any very excited group of talkers.", "두 사람은 지나치는 가게 대부분을 좌우로 들여다보았고, 무리지어 모인 사람들 전부를 경계의 눈초리로 살폈으며, 몹시 흥분해 떠드는 무리가 있으면 길을 비켜 피해 갔다."], ["It was a raw evening, and the misty river, blurred to the eye with blazing lights and to the ear with harsh noises, showed where the barges were stationed in which the smiths worked, making guns for the Army of the Republic.", "으슬으슬한 저녁이었고, 안개 낀 강은 타오르는 불빛으로 눈앞이 흐릿하고 거친 소음으로 귀가 어지러웠는데, 그 너머에 대장장이들이 일하며 공화국 군대를 위한 총을 만드는 거룻배들이 정박해 있었다."], ["Woe to the man who played tricks with that Army, or got undeserved promotion in it!", "그 군대를 상대로 농간을 부리거나, 그 안에서 분에 넘치는 진급을 얻은 자에게는 화가 있으리라!"], ["Better for him that his beard had never grown, for the National Razor shaved him close.", "그런 자에게는 차라리 수염이 자라지 않았던 편이 나았으니, 국민 면도칼이 그를 바싹 깎아버렸기 때문이다."]], [["Having purchased a few small articles of grocery, and a measure of oil for the lamp, Miss Pross bethought herself of the wine they wanted.", "* 국민 면도칼: 단두대를 가리키는 당시의 별칭. 식료품 몇 가지와 등잔에 쓸 기름 한 됫박을 산 뒤, 프로스 양은 자신들이 필요로 하는 포도주를 떠올렸다."], ["After peeping into several wine-shops, she stopped at the sign of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, not far from the National Palace, once (and twice) the Tuileries, where the aspect of things rather took her fancy.", "여러 술집을 기웃거린 끝에, 그녀는 한때(또 두번째로) 뛸르리 궁이었던 국민 궁에서 멀지 않은 곳에서, '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'라는 간판 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그곳의 분위기가 자못 그녀의 마음에 들었던 것이다."], ["It had a quieter look than any other place of the same description they had passed, and, though red with patriotic caps, was not so red as the rest.", "그곳은 지나온 같은 부류의 어느 곳보다도 조용해 보였고, 애국적인 모자들로 붉기는 했으나 다른 곳들만큼 붉지는 않았다."], ["Sounding Mr. Cruncher, and finding him of her opinion, Miss Pross resorted to the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, attended by her cavalier.", "크런처 씨의 의향을 떠보고 그도 자기와 같은 생각임을 알아낸 프로스 양은, 호위 기사를 거느린 채 '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'로 향했다."]], [["Slightly observant of the smoky lights; of the people, pipe in mouth, playing with limp cards and yellow dominoes; of the one bare-breasted, bare-armed, soot-begrimed workman reading a journal aloud, and of the others listening to him; of the weapons worn, or laid aside to be resumed; of the two or three customers fallen forward asleep, who in the popular high-shouldered shaggy black spencer looked, in that attitude, like slumbering bears or dogs; the two outlandish customers approached the counter, and showed what they wanted.", "연기 자욱한 불빛을, 입에 파이프를 문 채 너덜너덜한 카드와 누런 도미노를 가지고 노는 사람들을, 가슴과 팔을 드러내고 검댕을 뒤집어쓴 채 신문을 소리 내어 읽는 노동자 한 명과 그에게 귀 기울이는 다른 이들을, 몸에 차고 있거나 다시 집어들려고 한쪽에 놓아둔 무기들을, 앞으로 고꾸라져 잠든 두세 명의 손님들 — 그 자세로 보면 유행하는 어깨가 높고 텁수룩한 검은 스펜서 차림이어서 졸고 있는 곰이나 개처럼 보였다 — 을 가볍게 둘러보며, 두 이방인 손님은 계산대로 다가가 원하는 것을 보여주었다."]], [["As their wine was measuring out, a man parted from another man in a corner, and rose to depart.", "그들의 포도주가 계량되고 있을 때, 한 사내가 구석에서 다른 사내와 헤어져 떠나려고 일어섰다."], ["In going, he had to face Miss Pross.", "가던 길에 그는 프로스 양과 마주 보게 되었다."], ["No sooner did he face her, than Miss Pross uttered a scream, and clapped her hands.", "그가 그녀와 마주하기가 무섭게, 프로스 양은 비명을 지르며 두 손을 마주쳤다."]], [["In a moment, the whole company were on their feet.", "순식간에 가게 안의 모든 사람들이 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["That somebody was assassinated by somebody vindicating a difference of opinion was the likeliest occurrence.", "누군가가 의견 차이를 따지려는 누군가에게 살해당한 것이 가장 있음직한 일이었다."], ["Everybody looked to see somebody fall, but only saw a man and a woman standing staring at each other; the man with all the outward aspect of a Frenchman and a thorough Republican; the woman, evidently English.", "다들 누군가가 쓰러지는 모습을 보려고 했으나, 한 남자와 한 여자가 서로를 노려보며 서 있는 것만 보였다. 남자는 겉모습이 영락없이 프랑스인이자 철저한 공화주의자였고, 여자는 분명히 영국인이었다."]], [["What was said in this disappointing anti-climax, by the disciples of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, except that it was something very voluble and loud, would have been as so much Hebrew or Chaldean to Miss Pross and her protector, though they had been all ears.", "이 실망스러운 반전 속에서, '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'의 추종자들이 무어라 말했든 — 매우 거침없고 큰 소리였다는 것 말고는 — 프로스 양과 그녀의 보호자에게는, 설령 온통 귀를 곤두세웠다 한들 히브리어나 칼데아어나 마찬가지였을 것이다."], ["But, they had no ears for anything in their surprise.", "그러나 두 사람은 놀란 나머지 무엇에도 귀 기울일 여유가 없었다."], ["For, it must be recorded, that not only was Miss Pross lost in amazement and agitation, but, Mr. Cruncher--though it seemed on his own separate and individual account--was in a state of the greatest wonder.", "기록해두어야 할 것은, 프로스 양만 경악과 동요에 빠진 것이 아니라, 크런처 씨 또한 — 그것은 그 자신의 별개의 개인적인 이유 때문인 듯했지만 — 더할 나위 없는 경악 상태에 빠져 있었다는 점이다."]], [["“What is the matter?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["said the man who had caused Miss Pross to scream; speaking in a vexed, abrupt voice (though in a low tone), and in English.", "프로스 양으로 하여금 비명을 지르게 만든 사내가, 짜증스럽고 퉁명스러운 목소리로(다만 낮은 어조로), 그것도 영어로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Solomon, dear Solomon!”", "\"오, 솔로몬, 사랑하는 솔로몬!\""], ["cried Miss Pross, clapping her hands again.", "프로스 양이 다시 두 손을 마주치며 외쳤다."], ["“After not setting eyes upon you or hearing of you for so long a time, do I find you here!”", "\"이토록 오랜 세월 너를 한 번도 보지도 못하고 소식조차 듣지도 못하다가, 여기서 너를 만나다니!\""]], [["“Don’t call me Solomon.", "\"날 솔로몬이라고 부르지 마."], ["Do you want to be the death of me?”", "날 죽이려는 거요?\""], ["asked the man, in a furtive, frightened way.", "사내가 은밀하고 겁먹은 투로 물었다."]], [["“Brother, brother!”", "\"동생아, 동생아!\""], ["cried Miss Pross, bursting into tears.", "프로스 양이 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["“Have I ever been so hard with you that you ask me such a cruel question?”", "\"내가 너한테 그렇게 모질게 군 적이 있어서 그런 잔인한 질문을 하는 거니?\""]], [["“Then hold your meddlesome tongue,”", "\"그럼 그 참견 잘하는 혀를 좀 가만두고,\""], ["said Solomon, “and come out, if you want to speak to me.", "솔로몬이 말했다, \"나한테 할 말이 있거든 밖으로 나와."], ["Pay for your wine, and come out.", "포도주값 치르고 나오라고."], ["Who’s this man?”", "이 사람은 누구요?\""]], [["Miss Pross, shaking her loving and dejected head at her by no means affectionate brother, said through her tears, “Mr.", "프로스 양은 결코 다정하지 않은 동생을 향해 사랑스럽고 풀 죽은 고개를 흔들며, 눈물 사이로 말했다."], ["Cruncher.”", "\"크런처 씨야.\""]], [["“Let him come out too,”", "\"이 사람도 같이 나오라고 해,\""], ["said Solomon.", "솔로몬이 말했다."], ["“Does he think me a ghost?”", "\"나를 유령으로 아나?\""]], [["Apparently, Mr. Cruncher did, to judge from his looks.", "겉보기로 판단하건대, 크런처 씨는 분명 그렇게 여기는 듯했다."], ["He said not a word, however, and Miss Pross, exploring the depths of her reticule through her tears with great difficulty paid for her wine.", "그러나 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 프로스 양은 눈물 사이로 가까스로 손가방 속을 뒤져 포도주값을 치렀다."], ["As she did so, Solomon turned to the followers of the Good Republican Brutus of Antiquity, and offered a few words of explanation in the French language, which caused them all to relapse into their former places and pursuits.", "그녀가 그러는 동안, 솔로몬은 '고대의 선량한 공화주의자 브루투스'의 추종자들을 향해 프랑스어로 몇 마디 해명을 했고, 그 덕에 그들 모두 본래의 자리와 하던 일로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\""], ["said Solomon, stopping at the dark street corner, “what do you want?”", "어두운 길모퉁이에서 멈춰 서며 솔로몬이 말했다, \"원하는 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“How dreadfully unkind in a brother nothing has ever turned my love away from!”", "\"무엇으로도 내 사랑을 한 번도 돌려놓지 못한 동생이 어쩌면 이리도 끔찍하게 무정할 수가!\""], ["cried Miss Pross, “to give me such a greeting, and show me no affection.”", "프로스 양이 외쳤다. \"나한테 이런 인사를 하고, 애정이라곤 보여주지 않다니.\""]], [["“There.", "\"자."], ["Confound it!", "빌어먹을!"], ["There,”", "자,\""], ["said Solomon, making a dab at Miss Pross’s lips with his own.", "솔로몬이 자기 입술을 프로스 양의 입술에 가볍게 갖다대며 말했다."], ["“Now are you content?”", "\"이제 됐소?\""]], [["Miss Pross only shook her head and wept in silence.", "프로스 양은 그저 고개를 흔들며 말없이 흐느꼈다."]], [["“If you expect me to be surprised,”", "\"내가 놀라기를 바란다면,\""], ["said her brother Solomon, “I am not surprised; I knew you were here; I know of most people who are here.", "동생 솔로몬이 말했다, \"난 놀라지 않았어. 누나가 여기 있는 걸 알고 있었으니까."], ["If you really don’t want to endanger my existence--which I half believe you do--go your ways as soon as possible, and let me go mine.", "여기 있는 사람들 대부분을 난 알고 있다고. 정말로 내 목숨을 위태롭게 하고 싶지 않다면 — 절반쯤은 그러고 싶어하는 것 같지만 — 되도록 빨리 누나 갈 길을 가고, 나는 내 길을 가게 해줘."], ["I am busy.", "난 바쁜 몸이야."], ["I am an official.”", "관리란 말이야.\""]], [["“My English brother Solomon,”", "\"내 영국인 동생 솔로몬,\""], ["mourned Miss Pross, casting up her tear-fraught eyes, “that had the makings in him of one of the best and greatest of men in his native country, an official among foreigners, and such foreigners!", "프로스 양이 눈물 가득한 눈을 치켜뜨며 탄식했다, \"고국에서라면 가장 훌륭하고 위대한 사람 가운데 하나가 될 자질을 지녔던 애가, 외국인들 틈에서, 그것도 그런 외국인들 틈에서 관리 노릇을 하다니!"], ["I would almost sooner have seen the dear boy lying in his--”", "차라리 이 사랑하는 아이가 제……에 누워 있는 꼴을 보는 게 거의 더 나을 뻔했어……\""]], [["“I said so!”", "\"내 그럴 줄 알았다니까!\""], ["cried her brother, interrupting.", "동생이 말을 가로채며 외쳤다."], ["“I knew it.", "\"그럴 줄 알았어."], ["You want to be the death of me.", "누나는 날 죽이려는 거야."], ["I shall be rendered Suspected, by my own sister.", "난 친누나 때문에 '용의자'가 되겠어."], ["Just as I am getting on!”", "이제 막 자리를 잡아가는 마당에!\""]], [["“The gracious and merciful Heavens forbid!”", "\"자비롭고 인자하신 하늘이 그것만은 막아주시기를!\""], ["cried Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 외쳤다."], ["“Far rather would I never see you again, dear Solomon, though I have ever loved you truly, and ever shall.", "\"차라리 너를 다시는 보지 않는 편이 훨씬 낫겠다, 사랑하는 솔로몬, 비록 내가 늘 진심으로 너를 사랑해왔고 앞으로도 늘 그러겠지만 말이야."], ["Say but one affectionate word to me, and tell me there is nothing angry or estranged between us, and I will detain you no longer.”", "나한테 다정한 말 한마디만 해다오, 우리 사이에 노여움이나 멀어진 마음 같은 건 없다고 말해다오, 그러면 더는 너를 붙잡지 않으마.\""]], [["Good Miss Pross!", "선량한 프로스 양이여!"], ["As if the estrangement between them had come of any culpability of hers.", "마치 두 사람 사이의 소원함이 그녀의 어떤 잘못에서 비롯되기라도 한 것처럼."], ["As if Mr. Lorry had not known it for a fact, years ago, in the quiet corner in Soho, that this precious brother had spent her money and left her!", "마치 로리 씨가 여러 해 전 쏘호의 그 조용한 모퉁이에서, 이 소중한 동생이 누나의 돈을 다 써버리고 그녀를 버렸다는 사실을 알지 못하기라도 했던 것처럼!"]], [["He was saying the affectionate word, however, with a far more grudging condescension and patronage than he could have shown if their relative merits and positions had been reversed (which is invariably the case, all the world over), when Mr.", "그러나 그는 그 다정한 말을 하고 있었는데, 만약 두 사람의 상대적 자질과 처지가 뒤바뀌었다면(이런 일은 온 세상 어디서나 한결같이 일어나는 법이다) 보였을 법한 것보다 훨씬 더 마지못한 생색과 거드름을 부리며 그렇게 하고 있었다."], ["Cruncher, touching him on the shoulder, hoarsely and unexpectedly interposed with the following singular question:", "그때 크런처 씨가 그의 어깨를 건드리며, 쉰 목소리로 느닷없이 다음과 같은 기묘한 질문을 끼워넣었다."]], [["“I say!", "\"이보쇼!"], ["Might I ask the favour?", "한 가지 부탁 좀 해도 되겠소?"], ["As to whether your name is John Solomon, or Solomon John?”", "당신 이름이 존 솔로몬이오, 아니면 솔로몬 존이오?\""]], [["The official turned towards him with sudden distrust.", "그 관리는 갑작스러운 불신을 품고 그를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["He had not previously uttered a word.", "그는 그전까지 한마디도 하지 않았던 것이다."]], [["“Come!”", "\"자!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 말했다."], ["“Speak out, you know.”", "\"분명히 말해보쇼, 알겠소.\""], ["(Which, by the way, was more than he could do himself.) “John Solomon, or Solomon John?", "(덧붙이자면, 정작 자신은 분명히 말하지 못하는 처지였다.) \"존 솔로몬이오, 솔로몬 존이오?"], ["She calls you Solomon, and she must know, being your sister.", "누이가 당신을 솔로몬이라 부르니, 누이라면 알 게 아니오."], ["And I know you’re John, you know.", "그리고 나는 당신이 존이라는 걸 알지, 알겠소."], ["Which of the two goes first?", "둘 중 어느 게 앞에 오는 거요?"], ["And regarding that name of Pross, likewise.", "그리고 프로스라는 그 성씨에 대해서도 마찬가지요."], ["That warn’t your name over the water.”", "그건 바다 건너에서 쓰던 당신 이름이 아니었소.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 소리요?\""]], [["“Well, I don’t know all I mean, for I can’t call to mind what your name was, over the water.”", "\"글쎄, 무슨 뜻인지 다 알지는 못하오, 바다 건너에서 당신 이름이 뭐였는지 도통 떠오르질 않으니 말이오.\" \"안 떠오른다?\""]], [["“No?” “No.", "\"안 떠오르오."], ["But I’ll swear it was a name of two syllables.”", "하지만 그게 두 음절짜리 이름이었다는 건 맹세할 수 있소.\""]], [["“Indeed?”", "\"그래요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["T’other one’s was one syllable.", "다른 자의 이름은 한 음절이었지."], ["I know you.", "난 당신을 알아."], ["You was a spy--witness at the Bailey.", "당신은 첩자였소 — 베일리 법정의 증인이었지."], ["What, in the name of the Father of Lies, own father to yourself, was you called at that time?”", "거짓의 아비요 당신 자신의 친아비 이름을 걸고 묻는데, 그때 당신은 뭐라고 불렸소?\""]], [["“Barsad,”", "\"바사드,\""], ["said another voice, striking in.", "또다른 목소리가 끼어들며 말했다."]], [["“That’s the name for a thousand pound!”", "\"그게 천 파운드짜리 이름이군!\""], ["cried Jerry.", "제리가 외쳤다."]], [["The speaker who struck in, was Sydney Carton.", "끼어든 화자는 시드니 카튼이었다."], ["He had his hands behind him under the skirts of his riding-coat, and he stood at Mr. Cruncher’s elbow as negligently as he might have stood at the Old Bailey itself.", "그는 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌려 승마용 외투 자락 아래에 넣은 채, 마치 올드 베일리 법정에 서 있기라도 한 양 무심한 태도로 크런처 씨의 팔꿈치 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["“Don’t be alarmed, my dear Miss Pross.", "\"놀라지 마십시오, 친애하는 프로스 양."], ["I arrived at Mr.", "저는 어제 저녁에 로리 씨 댁에 도착해 그를 놀라게 했지요."], ["Lorry’s, to his surprise, yesterday evening; we agreed that I would not present myself elsewhere until all was well, or unless I could be useful; I present myself here, to beg a little talk with your brother.", "우리는 모든 일이 잘 풀리거나, 아니면 제가 쓸모가 있을 경우가 아니면 다른 곳에 모습을 드러내지 않기로 합의했습니다. 저는 여기 당신 동생분과 잠시 이야기를 나누고자 모습을 드러낸 겁니다."], ["I wish you had a better employed brother than Mr.", "당신께 바사드 씨보다 더 나은 일에 종사하는 동생이 있었으면 좋았을 텐데요."], ["Barsad. I wish for your sake Mr.", "당신을 위해서라도 바사드 씨가 감옥의 '양'이 아니었더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""], ["Barsad was not a Sheep of the Prisons.”", "* 양: 당시 간수 밑에서 일하는 첩자를 가리키던 은어."]], [["Sheep was a cant word of the time for a spy, under the gaolers.", "'양'은 간수들 밑에서 일하는 첩자를 가리키는 당시의 은어였다."], ["The spy, who was pale, turned paler, and asked him how he dared--", "본래 창백했던 그 첩자는 더욱 창백해지며, 무슨 근거로 감히 그런 말을 하느냐고 물었다 —"]], [["“I’ll tell you,”", "\"말해드리지요,\""], ["said Sydney.", "시드니가 말했다."], ["“I lighted on you, Mr. Barsad, coming out of the prison of the Conciergerie while I was contemplating the walls, an hour or more ago.", "\"바사드 씨, 한 시간쯤 전에 제가 성벽을 바라보고 있는데, 당신이 콩시에르주리 감옥에서 나오는 걸 우연히 보게 되었습니다."], ["You have a face to be remembered, and I remember faces well.", "당신은 기억에 남을 만한 얼굴을 가졌고, 저는 얼굴을 잘 기억하지요."], ["Made curious by seeing you in that connection, and having a reason, to which you are no stranger, for associating you with the misfortunes of a friend now very unfortunate, I walked in your direction.", "그런 상황에서 당신을 본 데다, 당신도 모르지 않을 어떤 이유로 — 지금 몹시 불행한 처지에 놓인 한 친구의 불운과 당신을 연결지을 만한 이유로 — 호기심이 일어, 저는 당신 쪽을 향해 걸어갔습니다."], ["I walked into the wine-shop here, close after you, and sat near you.", "당신 바로 뒤를 따라 이곳 술집으로 들어와, 당신 가까이에 앉았지요."], ["I had no difficulty in deducing from your unreserved conversation, and the rumour openly going about among your admirers, the nature of your calling.", "당신의 거리낌 없는 대화와, 당신을 흠모하는 자들 사이에 공공연히 떠도는 소문으로부터, 저는 당신의 직업이 어떤 것인지 어렵잖게 추론해냈습니다."], ["And gradually, what I had done at random, seemed to shape itself into a purpose, Mr. Barsad.”", "그리고 차츰, 제가 닥치는 대로 했던 일이 하나의 목적으로 형태를 갖추어가는 것 같았습니다, 바사드 씨.\""]], [["“What purpose?”", "\"무슨 목적 말이오?\""], ["the spy asked.", "첩자가 물었다."]], [["“It would be troublesome, and might be dangerous, to explain in the street.", "\"길거리에서 설명하기엔 번거롭고, 어쩌면 위험할 수도 있겠지요."], ["Could you favour me, in confidence, with some minutes of your company--at the office of Tellson’s Bank, for instance?” “Under a threat?”", "비밀스럽게, 잠시 동행해주시는 호의를 베풀어주시겠습니까 — 이를테면 텔슨 은행 사무소에서 말입니다?\""]], [["“Oh! Did I say that?”", "\"협박을 받으면서 말이오?\""]], [["“Then, why should I go there?”", "\"이런! 제가 그런 말을 했던가요?\""]], [["“Really, Mr. Barsad, I can’t say, if you can’t.”", "\"그렇다면, 내가 왜 거기 가야 하오?\""]], [["“Do you mean that you won’t say, sir?” the spy irresolutely asked.", "\"정말이지, 바사드 씨, 당신이 모르겠다면 저로서도 알 수 없는 노릇이지요.\""]], [["“You apprehend me very clearly, Mr.", "\"말하지 않겠다는 뜻이오, 선생?\""], ["Barsad. I won’t.”", "첩자가 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["Carton’s negligent recklessness of manner came powerfully in aid of his quickness and skill, in such a business as he had in his secret mind, and with such a man as he had to do with.", "\"제 말을 아주 분명히 알아들으셨군요, 바사드 씨. 저는 말하지 않겠습니다.\""], ["His practised eye saw it, and made the most of it.", "카튼의 무심하고 거리낌 없는 태도는, 그가 마음속에 은밀히 품은 그런 종류의 일에서, 그리고 그가 상대해야 하는 그런 종류의 인간을 다룸에 있어, 그의 민첩함과 솜씨를 강력하게 뒷받침해주었다. 그의 노련한 눈이 이를 알아차리고 최대한 활용했다."]], [["“Now, I told you so,”", "\"거봐, 내가 뭐라 그랬어,\""], ["said the spy, casting a reproachful look at his sister; “if any trouble comes of this, it’s your doing.”", "첩자가 누나에게 나무라는 눈길을 던지며 말했다. \"이 일로 무슨 곤란한 일이 생기면, 그건 누나 탓이야.\""]], [["“Come, come, Mr. Barsad!”", "\"이런, 이런, 바사드 씨!\""], ["exclaimed Sydney.", "시드니가 소리쳤다."], ["“Don’t be ungrateful.", "\"은혜를 모르시는군요."], ["But for my great respect for your sister, I might not have led up so pleasantly to a little proposal that I wish to make for our mutual satisfaction.", "제가 당신 누님을 크게 존경하지 않았더라면, 우리 서로의 만족을 위해 제가 제안하고 싶은 작은 제안으로 이토록 기분 좋게 이끌어가지 않았을 텐데요."], ["Do you go with me to the Bank?”", "저와 함께 은행으로 가시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I’ll hear what you have got to say.", "\"당신이 할 말이 뭔지 들어보지."], ["Yes, I’ll go with you.”", "그래, 같이 가겠소.\""]], [["“I propose that we first conduct your sister safely to the corner of her own street.", "\"먼저 당신 누님을 그분 댁이 있는 거리 모퉁이까지 안전하게 모셔다드릴 것을 제안합니다."], ["Let me take your arm, Miss Pross.", "제 팔을 잡으시지요, 프로스 양."], ["This is not a good city, at this time, for you to be out in, unprotected; and as your escort knows Mr.", "지금 이 시기에 이 도시는 당신이 보호도 없이 밖에 나다닐 만한 좋은 곳이 못 됩니다."], ["Barsad, I will invite him to Mr. Lorry’s with us.", "그리고 당신의 호위인이 바사드 씨를 아는 사이이니, 그도 우리와 함께 로리 씨 댁으로 청하겠습니다."], ["Are we ready?", "다들 준비됐습니까?"], ["Come then!”", "그럼 갑시다!\""]], [["Miss Pross recalled soon afterwards, and to the end of her life remembered, that as she pressed her hands on Sydney’s arm and looked up in his face, imploring him to do no hurt to Solomon, there was a braced purpose in the arm and a kind of inspiration in the eyes, which not only contradicted his light manner, but changed and raised the man.", "프로스 양은 얼마 지나지 않아 떠올렸고, 또 평생토록 기억했다. 그녀가 두 손을 시드니의 팔에 얹은 채 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 솔로몬을 해치지 말아달라고 애원했을 때, 그 팔에는 단단히 다잡은 결의가, 그 두 눈에는 일종의 영감 같은 것이 깃들어 있었으니, 그것은 그의 가벼운 태도와 모순될 뿐 아니라 그 사람을 변모시키고 고양시켰다는 것을."], ["She was too much occupied then with fears for the brother who so little deserved her affection, and with Sydney’s friendly reassurances, adequately to heed what she observed.", "그러나 그때 그녀는 자신의 애정을 그토록 누릴 자격이 없는 동생을 향한 두려움과, 시드니의 다정한 위로에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 자신이 본 것을 제대로 헤아릴 겨를이 없었다."]], [["They left her at the corner of the street, and Carton led the way to Mr. Lorry’s, which was within a few minutes’ walk.", "그들은 거리 모퉁이에서 그녀를 내려놓았고, 카튼은 몇 분 걸으면 닿는 로리 씨 댁으로 앞장서 갔다."], ["John Barsad, or Solomon Pross, walked at his side.", "존 바사드, 다시 말해 솔로몬 프로스가 그의 곁에서 걸었다."]], [["Mr. Lorry had just finished his dinner, and was sitting before a cheery little log or two of fire--perhaps looking into their blaze for the picture of that younger elderly gentleman from Tellson’s, who had looked into the red coals at the Royal George at Dover, now a good many years ago.", "로리 씨는 막 저녁식사를 끝내고, 기분 좋게 타오르는 자그마한 장작 한두 개 앞에 앉아 있었는데 — 어쩌면 그 불꽃 속을 들여다보며, 여러 해 전 도버의 로열 조지 여관에서 빨갛게 단 석탄을 들여다보던, 텔슨 은행의 그 더 젊었던 노신사의 모습을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다."], ["He turned his head as they entered, and showed the surprise with which he saw a stranger.", "그는 그들이 들어오자 고개를 돌렸고, 낯선 사람을 보고 놀라는 기색을 드러냈다."]], [["“Miss Pross’s brother, sir,”", "\"프로스 양의 동생입니다, 어르신,\""], ["said Sydney.", "시드니가 말했다."], ["“Mr. Barsad.”", "\"바사드 씨이지요.\""]], [["“Barsad?”", "\"바사드?\""], ["repeated the old gentleman, “Barsad?", "노신사가 되뇌었다, \"바사드?"], ["I have an association with the name--and with the face.”", "그 이름에 — 그리고 그 얼굴에 — 떠오르는 게 있는데.\""]], [["“I told you you had a remarkable face, Mr. Barsad,”", "\"제가 당신이 눈에 띄는 얼굴을 가졌다고 했잖습니까, 바사드 씨,\""], ["observed Carton, coolly.", "카튼이 냉정하게 말했다."], ["“Pray sit down.”", "\"앉으시지요.\""]], [["As he took a chair himself, he supplied the link that Mr.", "그는 자신도 의자에 앉으면서, 로리 씨가 필요로 하던 연결고리를 찌푸린 얼굴로 일러주었다."], ["Lorry wanted, by saying to him with a frown, “Witness at that trial.” Mr.", "\"그 재판의 증인 말입니다.\""], ["Lorry immediately remembered, and regarded his new visitor with an undisguised look of abhorrence.", "로리 씨는 즉시 기억해냈고, 새로 온 손님을 가리는 기색 없는 혐오의 눈길로 바라보았다."]], [["“Mr. Barsad has been recognised by Miss Pross as the affectionate brother you have heard of,”", "\"바사드 씨는 프로스 양에게 어르신께서 익히 들으셨던 그 다정한 동생으로 알아보였고,\""], ["said Sydney, “and has acknowledged the relationship.", "시드니가 말했다, \"그 관계를 인정했습니다."], ["I pass to worse news.", "이제 더 나쁜 소식으로 넘어가지요."], ["Darnay has been arrested again.”", "다네이가 다시 체포되었습니다.\""]], [["Struck with consternation, the old gentleman exclaimed, “What do you tell me!", "경악에 사로잡힌 노신사가 외쳤다. \"무슨 소릴 하는 건가!"], ["I left him safe and free within these two hours, and am about to return to him!”", "두 시간도 안 돼 그를 무사히, 자유로운 몸으로 두고 왔고, 막 그에게 돌아가려던 참인데!\""]], [["“Arrested for all that.", "\"그런데도 체포되었습니다."], ["When was it done, Mr. Barsad?”", "언제 그 일이 벌어졌습니까, 바사드 씨?\""]], [["“Just now, if at all.”", "\"방금 전이오, 일이 벌어졌다면 말이지만.\""]], [["“Mr. Barsad is the best authority possible, sir,”", "\"바사드 씨는 누구보다 확실한 정보통입니다, 어르신,\""], ["said Sydney, “and I have it from Mr.", "시드니가 말했다."], ["Barsad’s communication to a friend and brother Sheep over a bottle of wine, that the arrest has taken place.", "\"그리고 저는 바사드 씨가 포도주 한 병을 놓고 동료 '양'인 친구에게 전한 이야기로부터, 체포가 이루어졌다는 사실을 들었습니다."], ["He left the messengers at the gate, and saw them admitted by the porter.", "그는 전령들을 문간에 남겨두고, 문지기가 그들을 들여보내는 것을 보았다더군요."], ["There is no earthly doubt that he is retaken.”", "그가 다시 잡혀갔다는 데는 추호의 의심도 없습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry’s business eye read in the speaker’s face that it was loss of time to dwell upon the point.", "로리 씨의 사무적인 눈이 화자의 얼굴에서, 이 점을 두고 더 따져보아야 시간 낭비라는 것을 읽어냈다."], ["Confused, but sensible that something might depend on his presence of mind, he commanded himself, and was silently attentive.", "혼란스러웠으나, 무언가가 자신의 침착함에 달려 있을지도 모른다는 것을 알아차린 그는, 마음을 다잡고 말없이 주의를 기울였다."]], [["“Now, I trust,”", "\"자, 저는 믿습니다,\""], ["said Sydney to him, “that the name and influence of Doctor Manette may stand him in as good stead to-morrow--you said he would be before the Tribunal again to-morrow, Mr.", "시드니가 그에게 말했다, \"마네뜨 박사의 이름과 영향력이 내일도 그에게 오늘만큼이나 큰 도움이 되어주리라고 — 바사드 씨, 당신은 그가 내일 다시 재판소에 서게 될 거라고 했지요?"], ["Barsad?--”", "—\""]], [["“Yes; I believe so.”", "\"그렇소, 그렇게 알고 있소.\""]], [["“--In as good stead to-morrow as to-day. But it may not be so.", "\"— 내일도 오늘만큼이나 큰 도움이 되어주기를요."], ["I own to you, I am shaken, Mr.", "하지만 그렇지 않을 수도 있습니다."], ["Lorry, by Doctor Manette’s not having had the power to prevent this arrest.”", "솔직히 말씀드리면, 저는 마네뜨 박사가 이 체포를 막을 힘이 없었다는 데 흔들리고 있습니다, 로리 씨.\""]], [["“He may not have known of it beforehand,”", "\"미리 알지 못했을 수도 있지,\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“But that very circumstance would be alarming, when we remember how identified he is with his son-in-law.”", "\"하지만 그가 사위와 얼마나 한 몸처럼 여겨지는지를 생각하면, 바로 그 사정이 우려스러운 것이지요.\""]], [["“That’s true,” Mr.", "\"그건 맞는 말일세,\""], ["Lorry acknowledged, with his troubled hand at his chin, and his troubled eyes on Carton.", "로리 씨가 근심 어린 손을 턱에 댄 채, 근심 어린 눈을 카튼에게 둔 채로 수긍했다."]], [["“In short,”", "\"요컨대,\""], ["said Sydney, “this is a desperate time, when desperate games are played for desperate stakes.", "시드니가 말했다, \"지금은 절박한 판돈을 걸고 절박한 노름이 벌어지는 절박한 시국입니다."], ["Let the Doctor play the winning game; I will play the losing one.", "박사께서는 이기는 노름을 하시게 두지요. 지는 노름은 제가 하겠습니다."], ["No man’s life here is worth purchase.", "여기서는 그 누구의 목숨도 사들일 값어치가 없습니다."], ["Any one carried home by the people to-day, may be condemned tomorrow.", "오늘 사람들에게 떠받들려 집에 실려온 자라도, 내일이면 단죄될 수 있지요."], ["Now, the stake I have resolved to play for, in case of the worst, is a friend in the Conciergerie.", "그래서, 최악의 경우에 대비해 제가 걸기로 결심한 판돈은, 콩시에르주리 안에 있는 친구 하나입니다."], ["And the friend I purpose to myself to win, is Mr. Barsad.”", "그리고 제가 따내려고 마음먹은 그 친구는, 바로 바사드 씨이지요.\""]], [["“You need have good cards, sir,”", "\"좋은 패를 들고 계셔야 할 텐데요, 선생,\""], ["said the spy.", "첩자가 말했다."]], [["“I’ll run them over. I’ll see what I hold,--Mr.", "\"한번 훑어보지요."], ["Lorry, you know what a brute I am; I wish you’d give me a little brandy.”", "내가 무슨 패를 쥐고 있는지 살펴보겠습니다 — 로리 씨, 제가 얼마나 거친 인간인지 아시지요. 브랜디를 좀 주셨으면 합니다만.\""]], [["It was put before him, and he drank off a glassful--drank off another glassful--pushed the bottle thoughtfully away.", "브랜디가 그의 앞에 놓였고, 그는 한 잔을 들이켰다 — 또 한 잔을 들이켰다 — 그러고는 생각에 잠긴 채 술병을 밀쳐놓았다."]], [["“Mr. Barsad,”", "\"바사드 씨,\""], ["he went on, in the tone of one who really was looking over a hand at cards: “Sheep of the prisons, emissary of Republican committees, now turnkey, now prisoner, always spy and secret informer, so much the more valuable here for being English that an Englishman is less open to suspicion of subornation in those characters than a Frenchman, represents himself to his employers under a false name.", "그가 정말로 손에 쥔 카드 패를 훑어보는 사람의 어조로 말을 이었다. \"감옥의 양, 공화파 위원회의 밀정, 어떤 때는 간수, 어떤 때는 죄수, 언제나 첩자이자 비밀 밀고자 — 영국인이기에 이곳에서 그만큼 더 값어치가 있으니, 그런 역할을 맡았을 때 영국인은 프랑스인보다 매수의 의심을 덜 받기 때문이지요 — 그가 고용주들에게 자신을 가짜 이름으로 소개합니다."], ["That’s a very good card. Mr.", "그건 아주 좋은 패입니다."], ["Barsad, now in the employ of the republican French government, was formerly in the employ of the aristocratic English government, the enemy of France and freedom. That’s an excellent card.", "지금은 공화파 프랑스 정부에 고용된 바사드 씨가, 전에는 프랑스와 자유의 적인 귀족적 영국 정부에 고용되어 있었지요."], ["Inference clear as day in this region of suspicion, that Mr.", "그건 빼어난 패입니다."], ["Barsad, still in the pay of the aristocratic English government, is the spy of Pitt, the treacherous foe of the Republic crouching in its bosom, the English traitor and agent of all mischief so much spoken of and so difficult to find.", "이런 의심의 영역에서는 추론이 대낮처럼 명백하니, 곧 바사드 씨가 여전히 귀족적 영국 정부의 봉급을 받으면서, 공화국의 품속에 웅크린 배신자 적, 피트의 첩자, 그토록 많이 입에 오르내리지만 찾아내기 어려운 영국인 반역자이자 온갖 해악의 첩자라는 것이지요. 그건 누구도 이길 수 없는 패입니다."], ["That’s a card not to be beaten.", "제 패를 따라오셨습니까, 바사드 씨?\""], ["Have you followed my hand, Mr. Barsad?”", "* 피트: 당시 영국 총리 윌리엄 피트(소피트)를 가리킨다."]], [["“Not to understand your play,”", "\"당신 노름이 뭔지는 모르겠소만,\""], ["returned the spy, somewhat uneasily.", "첩자가 다소 불안해하며 받았다."]], [["“I play my Ace, Denunciation of Mr. Barsad to the nearest Section Committee.", "\"저는 제 으뜸패를 내겠습니다 — 가장 가까운 구역 위원회에 바사드 씨를 고발하는 것이지요."], ["Look over your hand, Mr. Barsad, and see what you have.", "당신 패를 훑어보십시오, 바사드 씨, 무얼 들고 있는지 보세요."], ["Don’t hurry.”", "서두르지 마시고요.\""]], [["He drew the bottle near, poured out another glassful of brandy, and drank it off.", "그는 술병을 가까이 끌어와, 브랜디를 또 한 잔 따라서 들이켰다."], ["He saw that the spy was fearful of his drinking himself into a fit state for the immediate denunciation of him.", "그는 첩자가, 자신이 술에 취해 당장이라도 자기를 고발할 상태가 될까 봐 두려워하는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Seeing it, he poured out and drank another glassful.", "그것을 알아채고는, 그는 또 한 잔을 따라 들이켰다."]], [["“Look over your hand carefully, Mr.", "\"당신 패를 잘 살펴보세요, 바사드 씨."], ["Barsad. Take time.”", "천천히 하시고요.\""]], [["It was a poorer hand than he suspected. Mr.", "그것은 그가 짐작한 것보다 더 보잘것없는 패였다."], ["Barsad saw losing cards in it that Sydney Carton knew nothing of.", "바사드 씨는 그 패 속에서, 시드니 카튼이 전혀 알지 못하는 지는 패들을 보았다."], ["Thrown out of his honourable employment in England, through too much unsuccessful hard swearing there--not because he was not wanted there; our English reasons for vaunting our superiority to secrecy and spies are of very modern date--he knew that he had crossed the Channel, and accepted service in France: first, as a tempter and an eavesdropper among his own countrymen there: gradually, as a tempter and an eavesdropper among the natives.", "영국에서 너무 많은 위증을 했으나 끝내 성공하지 못한 탓에 명예로운 직업에서 쫓겨났는데 — 그곳에서 그가 필요 없어서가 아니라, 비밀 첩자에 대한 우리의 우월성을 자랑하는 영국식 명분이라는 게 지극히 근래에 생긴 것이기 때문이다 — 그는 자신이 해협을 건너 프랑스에서 일자리를 받아들였음을 알고 있었다. 처음에는 그곳의 자기 동포들 사이에서 유혹자이자 엿듣는 자로, 차츰 그 나라 사람들 사이에서 유혹자이자 엿듣는 자로 말이다."], ["He knew that under the overthrown government he had been a spy upon Saint Antoine and Defarge’s wine-shop; had received from the watchful police such heads of information concerning Doctor Manette’s imprisonment, release, and history, as should serve him for an introduction to familiar conversation with the Defarges; and tried them on Madame Defarge, and had broken down with them signally.", "그는 전복된 정부 아래서, 자신이 쌩 땅뚜안과 드파르주의 술집을 감시하는 첩자였음을, 마네뜨 박사의 투옥과 석방, 그 내력에 관한 정보의 요점들을 빈틈없는 경찰로부터 받아내어 드파르주 부부와 허물없는 대화로 들어가는 실마리로 삼으려 했음을, 그래서 그것들을 드파르주 부인에게 시험해보았다가 보기 좋게 실패했음을 알고 있었다."], ["He always remembered with fear and trembling, that that terrible woman had knitted when he talked with her, and had looked ominously at him as her fingers moved.", "그는 그 무시무시한 여인이 자신이 그녀와 이야기하는 동안 뜨개질을 하면서, 손가락을 움직이며 불길하게 자신을 바라보던 것을 늘 두려움에 떨며 기억했다."], ["He had since seen her, in the Section of Saint Antoine, over and over again produce her knitted registers, and denounce people whose lives the guillotine then surely swallowed up.", "그는 그 이후로 쌩 땅뚜안 구역에서, 그녀가 거듭거듭 자신의 뜨개질로 짠 명부를 꺼내, 그 목숨이 그러고 나면 단두대에 어김없이 삼켜지는 사람들을 고발하는 것을 보았다."], ["He knew, as every one employed as he was did, that he was never safe; that flight was impossible; that he was tied fast under the shadow of the axe; and that in spite of his utmost tergiversation and treachery in furtherance of the reigning terror, a word might bring it down upon him.", "그는, 그처럼 일하는 사람이라면 누구나 그렇듯, 자신이 결코 안전하지 못하다는 것을, 도망은 불가능하다는 것을, 자신이 도끼의 그림자 아래 단단히 묶여 있다는 것을, 그리고 횡행하는 공포정치에 부역하느라 온갖 변절과 배신을 다 부렸음에도, 말 한마디면 그것이 자기 머리 위로 떨어질 수 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Once denounced, and on such grave grounds as had just now been suggested to his mind, he foresaw that the dreadful woman of whose unrelenting character he had seen many proofs, would produce against him that fatal register, and would quash his last chance of life.", "일단 고발당하기만 하면, 그것도 방금 그의 머릿속에 제시된 그런 중대한 근거로 고발당하기만 하면, 그 매정한 성품을 여러 증거로 익히 보아온 그 끔찍한 여인이 자신을 상대로 그 치명적인 명부를 꺼내, 자신의 마지막 목숨의 기회마저 짓이겨버리리라는 것을 그는 내다보았다."], ["Besides that all secret men are men soon terrified, here were surely cards enough of one black suit, to justify the holder in growing rather livid as he turned them over.", "게다가 비밀스러운 일을 하는 자들은 누구나 쉬이 겁먹는 법인데, 여기 같은 검은 패가 충분히 쌓여 있으니, 그 패를 든 자가 그것을 한 장 한 장 넘기며 점점 새파랗게 질려가는 것도 무리가 아니었다."]], [["“You scarcely seem to like your hand,”", "\"당신 패가 영 마음에 들지 않으시는 모양이군요,\""], ["said Sydney, with the greatest composure.", "시드니가 더할 나위 없는 침착함으로 말했다."], ["“Do you play?”", "\"노름하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I think, sir,” said the spy, in the meanest manner, as he turned to Mr.", "\"제 생각엔, 선생,\""], ["Lorry, “I may appeal to a gentleman of your years and benevolence, to put it to this other gentleman, so much your junior, whether he can under any circumstances reconcile it to his station to play that Ace of which he has spoken.", "첩자가 로리 씨를 향해 돌아서며 더없이 비굴한 태도로 말했다, \"선생 같으신 연륜과 자비를 갖추신 분께 호소해서, 선생보다 한참 젊으신 이 다른 분께 한번 여쭤봐주십사 하고 싶군요. 과연 어떤 상황에서든 저 양반이 방금 말씀하신 그 으뜸패를 내는 일을 자신의 신분과 양립시킬 수 있겠는지를요."], ["I admit that I am a spy, and that it is considered a discreditable station--though it must be filled by somebody; but this gentleman is no spy, and why should he so demean himself as to make himself one?”", "저야 첩자라는 걸 인정합니다, 그리고 그게 떳떳지 못한 처지로 여겨진다는 것도요 — 누군가는 맡아야 하는 일이긴 하지만 말이지요. 하지만 이 양반은 첩자가 아닌데, 어째서 스스로 첩자 노릇을 하는 데까지 자신을 떨어뜨리려 하는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I play my Ace, Mr. Barsad,”", "\"제 으뜸패를 내겠습니다, 바사드 씨,\""], ["said Carton, taking the answer on himself, and looking at his watch, “without any scruple, in a very few minutes.”", "카튼이 그 답을 자기가 떠맡고 회중시계를 보며 말했다, \"아무런 거리낌 없이, 아주 잠시 후에 말이지요.\""]], [["“I should have hoped, gentlemen both,”", "\"두 분 다 부디,\""], ["said the spy, always striving to hook Mr. Lorry into the discussion, “that your respect for my sister--”", "첩자가 줄곧 로리 씨를 논의로 끌어들이려 애쓰며 말했다, \"제 누이에 대한 존중을 생각해서라도 —\""]], [["“I could not better testify my respect for your sister than by finally relieving her of her brother,”", "\"제가 당신 누님에 대한 존중을 보여드릴 더 나은 방법은, 결국 그분에게서 동생을 떼어내드리는 것밖에 없겠지요,\""], ["said Sydney Carton.", "시드니 카튼이 말했다."]], [["“You think not, sir?”", "\"그렇게 생각하지 않으시오, 선생?\""]], [["“I have thoroughly made up my mind about it.”", "\"저는 그 점에 대해 마음을 단단히 굳혔습니다.\""]], [["The smooth manner of the spy, curiously in dissonance with his ostentatiously rough dress, and probably with his usual demeanour, received such a check from the inscrutability of Carton,--who was a mystery to wiser and honester men than he,--that it faltered here and failed him.", "첩자의 매끄러운 태도는, 일부러 거칠게 차려입은 그의 옷차림과, 또 필시 그의 평소 행동거지와 묘하게 어긋나는 것이었는데, 카튼의 헤아릴 수 없음 — 그는 자기보다 더 현명하고 정직한 사람들에게도 수수께끼였다 — 앞에서 그토록 제동이 걸린 나머지, 여기서 흔들리며 그를 저버렸다."], ["While he was at a loss, Carton said, resuming his former air of contemplating cards:", "그가 어찌할 바를 모르는 사이에, 카튼은 카드 패를 들여다보던 아까의 태도로 돌아가 말했다."]], [["“And indeed, now I think again, I have a strong impression that I have another good card here, not yet enumerated.", "\"그러고 보니, 이제 다시 생각해보니, 아직 헤아리지 않은 또 하나의 좋은 패를 여기 쥐고 있다는 강한 인상이 드는군요."], ["That friend and fellow-Sheep, who spoke of himself as pasturing in the country prisons; who was he?”", "시골 감옥에서 풀을 뜯고 있다고 자기 입으로 말하던 그 친구이자 동료 양 말입니다. 그자는 누구지요?\""]], [["“French.", "\"프랑스인이오."], ["You don’t know him,”", "당신은 모르는 자요,\""], ["said the spy, quickly.", "첩자가 재빨리 말했다."]], [["“French, eh?”", "\"프랑스인이라, 응?\""], ["repeated Carton, musing, and not appearing to notice him at all, though he echoed his word.", "카튼이 그의 말을 따라 되뇌면서도 그를 전혀 의식하지 않는 듯 골똘히 생각하며 되뇌었다."], ["“Well; he may be.”", "\"글쎄요, 그럴 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“Is, I assure you,”", "\"틀림없소, 장담하리다,\""], ["said the spy; “though it’s not important.”", "첩자가 말했다, \"중요한 일은 아니지만 말이오.\""]], [["“Though it’s not important,”", "\"중요한 일은 아니다,\""], ["repeated Carton, in the same mechanical way--“though it’s not important--No, it’s not important.", "카튼이 똑같이 기계적인 투로 되뇌었다 — \"중요한 일은 아니다 — 그래요, 중요한 일은 아니지요. 그래요."], ["No. Yet I know the face.”", "그런데 그 얼굴을 내가 알아요.\""]], [["“I think not.", "\"아닐 거요."], ["I am sure not.", "틀림없이 아닐 거요."], ["It can’t be,”", "그럴 리 없소,\""], ["said the spy.", "첩자가 말했다."]], [["“It-can’t-be,”", "\"그-럴-리-없다,\""], ["muttered Sydney Carton, retrospectively, and idling his glass (which fortunately was a small one) again.", "시드니 카튼이 옛 기억을 더듬으며 중얼거리고는, (다행히 작은 잔이었던) 자기 잔을 다시 만지작거렸다."], ["“Can’t-be.", "\"그럴-리-없다."], ["Spoke good French.", "프랑스어를 잘했지."], ["Yet like a foreigner, I thought?”", "그런데도 외국인 같았어, 안 그랬나?\""]], [["“Provincial,”", "\"지방 출신이오,\""], ["said the spy.", "첩자가 말했다."]], [["“No. Foreign!”", "\"아니야."], ["cried Carton, striking his open hand on the table, as a light broke clearly on his mind.", "외국인이었어!\" 카튼이 펼친 손바닥으로 탁자를 내리치며 외쳤으니, 한 줄기 빛이 그의 머릿속에 또렷이 비쳐든 것이다."], ["“Cly!", "\"클라이!"], ["Disguised, but the same man.", "변장은 했지만, 같은 자야."], ["We had that man before us at the Old Bailey.”", "우리는 올드 베일리에서 그자를 앞에 두고 있었어.\""]], [["“Now, there you are hasty, sir,”", "\"이런, 선생, 거기선 너무 성급하시오,\""], ["said Barsad, with a smile that gave his aquiline nose an extra inclination to one side; “there you really give me an advantage over you.", "바사드가 매부리코를 한쪽으로 한층 더 기울게 만드는 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"거기선 정말이지 당신이 나한테 유리한 패를 쥐여주는 셈이오."], ["Cly (who I will unreservedly admit, at this distance of time, was a partner of mine) has been dead several years.", "클라이는 (이만큼 세월이 흘렀으니 그가 내 동업자였다는 건 거리낌 없이 인정하겠소) 죽은 지 여러 해 됐소."], ["I attended him in his last illness.", "내가 그의 임종을 지켰지."], ["He was buried in London, at the church of Saint Pancras-in-the-Fields.", "그는 런던의 쌩 팽크라스인더필즈 교회에 묻혔소."], ["His unpopularity with the blackguard multitude at the moment prevented my following his remains, but I helped to lay him in his coffin.”", "당시 그 불한당 같은 군중들 사이에서 인심을 잃은 탓에 내가 운구를 따라가지는 못했지만, 그를 관에 눕히는 건 거들었소.\""]], [["Here, Mr. Lorry became aware, from where he sat, of a most remarkable goblin shadow on the wall.", "이때 로리 씨는 자기가 앉은 자리에서, 벽에 비친 더없이 기이한 도깨비 그림자를 알아차렸다."], ["Tracing it to its source, he discovered it to be caused by a sudden extraordinary rising and stiffening of all the risen and stiff hair on Mr. Cruncher’s head.", "그 근원을 더듬어 올라가던 그는, 그것이 크런처 씨 머리에 곤두서 뻣뻣하던 머리카락이 죄다 갑자기 비상하게 솟구쳐 뻣뻣해진 탓임을 알아냈다."]], [["“Let us be reasonable,”", "\"우리 이성적으로 굽시다,\""], ["said the spy, “and let us be fair.", "첩자가 말했다, \"그리고 공정하게 합시다."], ["To show you how mistaken you are, and what an unfounded assumption yours is, I will lay before you a certificate of Cly’s burial, which I happened to have carried in my pocket-book,”", "당신이 얼마나 잘못 짚었는지, 당신 추측이 얼마나 근거 없는 것인지 보여드리기 위해, 클라이의 매장 증명서를 당신 앞에 내놓으리다. 마침 내 수첩에 넣고 다녔지,\""], ["with a hurried hand he produced and opened it, “ever since.", "그는 황급한 손놀림으로 그것을 꺼내 펼쳤다, \"그 이후로 줄곧 말이오."], ["There it is.", "자, 여기 있소."], ["Oh, look at it, look at it!", "오, 봐요, 보라고요!"], ["You may take it in your hand; it’s no forgery.”", "손에 들고 보셔도 좋소. 위조한 게 아니니까.\""]], [["Here, Mr. Lorry perceived the reflection on the wall to elongate, and Mr.", "이때 로리 씨는 벽에 비친 그림자가 길게 늘어나는 것을 알아차렸고, 크런처 씨가 일어나 앞으로 나섰다."], ["Cruncher rose and stepped forward.", "'잭이 지은 집'"], ["His hair could not have been more violently on end, if it had been that moment dressed by the Cow with the crumpled horn in the house that Jack built.", "속 뿔이 구부러진 암소가 바로 그 순간 그의 머리를 매만져주었다 한들, 그의 머리카락이 더 사납게 곤두서지는 못했을 것이다. * 잭이 지은 집: 영국의 누적식 동요 〈This is the house that Jack built〉에 나오는 구절을 가리킨다."]], [["Unseen by the spy, Mr. Cruncher stood at his side, and touched him on the shoulder like a ghostly bailiff.", "첩자가 알아채지 못하는 사이에, 크런처 씨는 그의 곁에 서서, 마치 유령 같은 집행관처럼 그의 어깨를 건드렸다."]], [["“That there Roger Cly, master,” said Mr.", "\"그 로저 클라이 말이오, 나리,\""], ["Cruncher, with a taciturn and iron-bound visage.", "크런처 씨가 말없이 굳어 무쇠 같은 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“So you put him in his coffin?”", "\"그러니까 당신이 그를 관에 눕혔다고?\""]], [["“I did.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Who took him out of it?”", "\"누가 그를 거기서 꺼냈소?\""]], [["Barsad leaned back in his chair, and stammered, “What do you mean?”", "바사드는 의자에 등을 기대며 더듬거렸다. \"무슨 소리요?\""]], [["“I mean,”", "\"내 말은,\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, “that he warn’t never in it.", "크런처 씨가 말했다, \"그가 거기 들어 있던 적이 도통 없었다는 거요."], ["No! Not he!", "천만에!"], ["I’ll have my head took off, if he was ever in it.”", "그자는 아니었어! 그가 거기 들어 있었다면, 내 목을 떼어가도 좋소.\""]], [["The spy looked round at the two gentlemen; they both looked in unspeakable astonishment at Jerry.", "첩자는 두 신사를 둘러보았고, 두 사람 다 이루 말할 수 없는 경악에 차서 제리를 바라보았다."]], [["“I tell you,”", "\"내 말하지,\""], ["said Jerry, “that you buried paving-stones and earth in that there coffin.", "제리가 말했다, \"당신은 그 관에 포석 돌멩이하고 흙을 묻은 거야."], ["Don’t go and tell me that you buried Cly.", "클라이를 묻었다고 나한테 와서 말하지 마쇼."], ["It was a take in.", "그건 속임수였어."], ["Me and two more knows it.”", "나하고 다른 두 사람이 그걸 안다고.\""]], [["“How do you know it?”", "\"그걸 어떻게 아시오?\""]], [["“What’s that to you? Ecod!”", "\"그게 당신하고 무슨 상관이오?"], ["growled Mr.", "빌어먹을!\""], ["Cruncher, “it’s you I have got a old grudge again, is it, with your shameful impositions upon tradesmen!", "크런처 씨가 으르렁거렸다, \"내가 묵은 원한을 진 건 바로 당신이지, 안 그렇소, 그 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 장사꾼들한테 사기 친 짓거리 말이오!"], ["I’d catch hold of your throat and choke you for half a guinea.”", "반 기니만 주면 당신 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버릴 수도 있소.\""]], [["Sydney Carton, who, with Mr. Lorry, had been lost in amazement at this turn of the business, here requested Mr. Cruncher to moderate and explain himself.", "이 일의 전환에 로리 씨와 더불어 경악에 빠져 있던 시드니 카튼이, 이때 크런처 씨에게 진정하고 스스로 설명해보라고 청했다."]], [["“At another time, sir,”", "\"다음에 합시다, 선생,\""], ["he returned, evasively, “the present time is ill-conwenient for explainin’.", "그가 얼버무리며 받았다, \"지금은 설명하기에 영 거시기한 때라서요."], ["What I stand to, is, that he knows well wot that there Cly was never in that there coffin.", "내가 분명히 말하는 건, 저자가 그 클라이가 그 관 속에 도통 들어 있지 않았다는 걸 잘 안다는 거요."], ["Let him say he was, in so much as a word of one syllable, and I’ll either catch hold of his throat and choke him for half a guinea;”", "저자가 그자가 들어 있었다고 한 음절짜리 말이라도 하면, 난 반 기니에 저자 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버리든가,\""], ["Mr. Cruncher dwelt upon this as quite a liberal offer; “or I’ll out and announce him.”", "크런처 씨는 이것을 자못 후한 제안인 양 강조했다, \"아니면 나가서 저자를 까발리든가 하겠소.\""]], [["“Humph!", "\"흠!"], ["I see one thing,” said Carton.", "한 가지는 알겠군요,\""], ["“I hold another card, Mr. Barsad.", "카튼이 말했다."], ["Impossible, here in raging Paris, with Suspicion filling the air, for you to outlive denunciation, when you are in communication with another aristocratic spy of the same antecedents as yourself, who, moreover, has the mystery about him of having feigned death and come to life again!", "\"저는 또다른 패를 쥐고 있습니다, 바사드 씨. 의심이 공기를 가득 채운 이 광란의 빠리에서, 당신이 당신과 똑같은 전력을 지닌, 게다가 죽은 척했다가 되살아났다는 수수께끼까지 두른 또다른 귀족적 첩자와 내통하는 마당에, 당신이 고발을 면하고 살아남기란 불가능하지요!"], ["A plot in the prisons, of the foreigner against the Republic.", "감옥에서 외국인이 공화국에 맞서 꾸민 음모."], ["A strong card--a certain Guillotine card!", "강력한 패입니다 — 단두대행이 확실한 패지요!"], ["Do you play?”", "노름하시겠습니까?\""]], [["“No!” returned the spy.", "\"안 하오!\""], ["“I throw up.", "첩자가 받았다."], ["I confess that we were so unpopular with the outrageous mob, that I only got away from England at the risk of being ducked to death, and that Cly was so ferreted up and down, that he never would have got away at all but for that sham.", "\"패를 던지겠소. 솔직히 우리는 그 난폭한 폭도들 사이에서 어찌나 인심을 잃었던지, 나는 물에 처박혀 죽을 위험을 무릅쓰고서야 겨우 영국을 빠져나왔고, 클라이는 어찌나 사방으로 쫓겨다녔던지 그 위장극이 아니었더라면 도무지 빠져나오지 못했을 거요."], ["Though how this man knows it was a sham, is a wonder of wonders to me.”", "다만 이 사람이 그게 위장극이었다는 걸 어떻게 아는지는, 나한텐 그야말로 불가사의 중의 불가사의지만 말이오.\""]], [["“Never you trouble your head about this man,”", "\"이 사람 일로는 머리 쓸 거 없소,\""], ["retorted the contentious Mr. Cruncher; “you’ll have trouble enough with giving your attention to that gentleman.", "시비조의 크런처 씨가 받아쳤다, \"저 양반한테 신경 쓰는 것만으로도 충분히 골치 아플 테니까."], ["And look here!", "그리고 이거 보쇼!"], ["Once more!”--Mr.", "한 번 더 말하지!\""], ["Cruncher could not be restrained from making rather an ostentatious parade of his liberality--“I’d catch hold of your throat and choke you for half a guinea.”", "— 크런처 씨는 자신의 후한 인심을 자못 거창하게 늘어놓고픈 충동을 억누르지 못했다 — \"반 기니만 주면 당신 목을 움켜쥐고 졸라버리겠소.\""]], [["The Sheep of the prisons turned from him to Sydney Carton, and said, with more decision, “It has come to a point.", "감옥의 양은 그에게서 시드니 카튼 쪽으로 돌아서며, 한층 단호하게 말했다. \"이제 결판을 낼 때가 됐군."], ["I go on duty soon, and can’t overstay my time.", "나는 곧 근무에 들어가야 해서, 시간을 넘길 수 없소."], ["You told me you had a proposal; what is it?", "당신은 제안이 있다고 했는데, 그게 뭐요?"], ["Now, it is of no use asking too much of me.", "자, 나한테 너무 많은 걸 요구해봐야 소용없소."], ["Ask me to do anything in my office, putting my head in great extra danger, and I had better trust my life to the chances of a refusal than the chances of consent.", "내 직무에서 무슨 일을 하라고, 내 목을 큰 추가 위험에 빠뜨리는 일을 하라고 요구한다면, 차라리 거절의 위험에 내 목숨을 거는 편이 승낙의 위험에 거는 편보다 낫소."], ["In short, I should make that choice.", "요컨대 난 그 쪽을 택하겠다는 거요."], ["You talk of desperation. We are all desperate here.", "절박함을 말하는데, 여기선 우리 모두가 절박하오."], ["Remember!", "명심하쇼!"], ["I may denounce you if I think proper, and I can swear my way through stone walls, and so can others.", "내가 적당하다고 여기면 당신을 고발할 수도 있고, 난 돌벽도 뚫고 나갈 만큼 위증할 수 있으며, 다른 자들도 그럴 수 있소."], ["Now, what do you want with me?”", "자, 나한테 원하는 게 뭐요?\""]], [["“Not very much.", "\"그리 대단한 건 아닙니다."], ["You are a turnkey at the Conciergerie?”", "당신은 콩시에르주리의 간수이지요?\""]], [["“I tell you once for all, there is no such thing as an escape possible,”", "\"단번에 못박아 두지만, 탈옥 같은 건 불가능한 일이오,\""], ["said the spy, firmly.", "첩자가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Why need you tell me what I have not asked?", "\"제가 묻지도 않은 걸 무엇하러 말씀하십니까?"], ["You are a turnkey at the Conciergerie?”", "당신은 콩시에르주리의 간수이지요?\""]], [["“I am sometimes.”", "\"가끔은 그렇소.\""]], [["“You can be when you choose?”", "\"마음만 먹으면 될 수 있고요?\""]], [["“I can pass in and out when I choose.”", "\"마음만 먹으면 드나들 수 있소.\""]], [["Sydney Carton filled another glass with brandy, poured it slowly out upon the hearth, and watched it as it dropped.", "시드니 카튼은 또다른 잔에 브랜디를 채워, 벽난로 위에 천천히 부으며, 그것이 떨어지는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["It being all spent, he said, rising:", "그것이 다 쏟아지자, 그는 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“So far, we have spoken before these two, because it was as well that the merits of the cards should not rest solely between you and me.", "\"여기까지는, 이 두 분 앞에서 이야기했습니다. 패의 우열이 오로지 당신과 저 사이에만 머물지 않는 편이 좋았기 때문이지요."], ["Come into the dark room here, and let us have one final word alone.”", "이쪽 어두운 방으로 들어갑시다, 그리고 단둘이서 마지막 한마디를 나누지요.\""]], [["# CHAPTER IX. The Game Made", "# CHAPTER IX. The Game Made"]], [["While Sydney Carton and the Sheep of the prisons were in the adjoining dark room, speaking so low that not a sound was heard, Mr.", "시드니 카턴과 감옥의 양이 옆방의 어두운 곳에서, 아무 소리도 들리지 않을 만큼 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누는 동안, 로리 씨는 적잖은 의심과 불신을 품은 채 제리를 바라보았다."], ["Lorry looked at Jerry in considerable doubt and mistrust.", "그 정직한 장사꾼이 그 시선을 받아들이는 태도는 신뢰를 불러일으키지 못했다."], ["That honest tradesman’s manner of receiving the look, did not inspire confidence; he changed the leg on which he rested, as often as if he had fifty of those limbs, and were trying them all; he examined his finger-nails with a very questionable closeness of attention; and whenever Mr. Lorry’s eye caught his, he was taken with that peculiar kind of short cough requiring the hollow of a hand before it, which is seldom, if ever, known to be an infirmity attendant on perfect openness of character.", "그는 마치 그런 다리가 쉰 개쯤 되어 그것들을 모조리 시험해보려는 듯이 몸을 기댄 다리를 자꾸 바꾸었고, 매우 미심쩍을 만큼 골똘하게 자기 손톱을 들여다보았으며, 로리 씨의 눈길이 제 눈과 마주칠 때마다 손바닥을 입 앞에 갖다 대야 하는 그 독특한 종류의 짧은 기침에 사로잡혔는데, 그것은 완전히 솔직한 성품에 따르는 병이라고는 좀처럼, 아니 결코 알려진 바 없는 것이었다."]], [["“Jerry,”", "\"제리.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Come here.”", "\"이리 오게.\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher came forward sideways, with one of his shoulders in advance of him.", "크런처 씨는 한쪽 어깨를 앞세운 채 옆으로 다가왔다."]], [["“What have you been, besides a messenger?”", "\"자네, 심부름꾼 말고 무슨 일을 했나?\""]], [["After some cogitation, accompanied with an intent look at his patron, Mr. Cruncher conceived the luminous idea of replying, “Agicultooral character.”", "후원자를 골똘히 바라보며 한참 궁리한 끝에, 크런처 씨는 \"농사 일을 하는 사람\"이라고 대답한다는 기발한 착상을 떠올렸다."]], [["“My mind misgives me much,” said Mr.", "\"내 마음에 몹시 걸리는 것이 있어.\""], ["Lorry, angrily shaking a forefinger at him, “that you have used the respectable and great house of Tellson’s as a blind, and that you have had an unlawful occupation of an infamous description.", "로리 씨가 그를 향해 집게손가락을 성난 듯이 흔들며 말했다. \"자네가 점잖고 큰 텔슨 상회를 눈가림으로 이용했고, 입에 담기도 부끄러운 종류의 불법적인 일을 해왔다는 의심 말일세."], ["If you have, don’t expect me to befriend you when you get back to England.", "만일 그랬다면, 자네가 영국으로 돌아갔을 때 내가 자네 편이 되어주리라고는 기대하지 말게."], ["If you have, don’t expect me to keep your secret.", "만일 그랬다면, 내가 자네 비밀을 지켜주리라고도 기대하지 말게."], ["Tellson’s shall not be imposed upon.”", "텔슨 상회는 결코 기만당하지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["“I hope, sir,”", "\"바라건대 나리,\""], ["pleaded the abashed Mr.", "무안해진 크런처 씨가 사정하듯 말했다."], ["Cruncher, “that a gentleman like yourself wot I’ve had the honour of odd jobbing till I’m grey at it, would think twice about harming of me, even if it wos so--I don’t say it is, but even if it wos.", "\"제가 머리가 셀 때까지 영광스럽게도 자질구레한 일을 해드린 나리 같은 신사분이라면, 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 — 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만, 그렇다손 치더라도 — 저를 해치는 일을 두번은 생각해주실 거라고 봅니다요."], ["And which it is to be took into account that if it wos, it wouldn’t, even then, be all o’ one side.", "그리고 또 한 가지 따져볼 게 있는데요, 설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 그조차 죄다 한쪽만의 일은 아닐 겝니다."], ["There’d be two sides to it.", "거기엔 양쪽이 다 있는 거지요."], ["There might be medical doctors at the present hour, a picking up their guineas where a honest tradesman don’t pick up his fardens--fardens!", "지금 이 시각에도 정직한 장사꾼이 동전 한닢 — 동전 한닢이라굽쇼!"], ["no, nor yet his half fardens--half fardens!", "아니죠, 반푼도, 반푼이라굽쇼!"], ["no, nor yet his quarter--a banking away like smoke at Tellson’s, and a cocking their medical eyes at that tradesman on the sly, a going in and going out to their own carriages--ah!", "아니죠, 반의반도 못 줍는 곳에서 기니 금화를 주워 담는 의사 선생들이 있을 겁니다요. 텔슨 상회에 연기처럼 돈을 쌓아두고서, 그 장사꾼한테 슬그머니 의사 눈을 부라리며, 제 마차에 타고 내리고 — 아!"], ["equally like smoke, if not more so.", "그 또한 연기처럼, 아니 그보다 더하면 더했지요."], ["Well, that ’ud be imposing, too, on Tellson’s.", "자, 그것도 텔슨 상회를 기만하는 일이올시다."], ["For you cannot sarse the goose and not the gander. And here’s Mrs.", "거위한테 양념을 치면 수거위한테도 쳐야 하는 법 아닙니까요."], ["Cruncher, or leastways wos in the Old England times, and would be to-morrow, if cause given, a floppin’ again the business to that degree as is ruinating--stark ruinating!", "그리고 여기 크런처 부인은, 적어도 옛 영국 시절에는 그랬고, 빌미만 주어지면 내일이라도 그럴 텐데요, 장사를 망칠 지경으로 — 아주 거덜 낼 지경으로 — 엎드려 기도질을 해댑니다요!"], ["Whereas them medical doctors’ wives don’t flop--catch ’em at it!", "그런데 그 의사 선생들 마누라는 엎드려 기도질을 안 해요 — 그러는 꼴을 한번 봤어야지요!"], ["Or, if they flop, their floppings goes in favour of more patients, and how can you rightly have one without t’other?", "아니, 한다손 치더라도, 그 기도질은 환자가 더 늘어나는 쪽으로 가는 거지요. 그러니 한쪽 없이 다른 쪽을 어찌 제대로 가질 수 있겠습니까요?"], ["Then, wot with undertakers, and wot with parish clerks, and wot with sextons, and wot with private watchmen (all awaricious and all in it), a man wouldn’t get much by it, even if it wos so.", "게다가 장의사다, 교구 서기다, 묘지기다, 사설 야경꾼이다 — 죄다 욕심꾸러기에 죄다 한통속인데 — 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 사람이 그걸로 별로 벌이도 못 해요."], ["And wot little a man did get, would never prosper with him, Mr. Lorry.", "그리고 사람이 벌어들인 쥐꼬리만 한 것도 결코 잘 풀리지가 않습니다, 로리 나리."], ["He’d never have no good of it; he’d want all along to be out of the line, if he, could see his way out, being once in--even if it wos so.”", "그걸로 좋은 일이라곤 절대 없을 겁니다요. 그 일에 한번 들어선 다음에야 빠져나갈 길만 보이면 줄곧 그 짓에서 손을 떼고 싶어 할 겝니다 — 설령 그렇다손 치더라도 말이지요.\""]], [["“Ugh!” cried Mr.", "\"으흠!\""], ["Lorry, rather relenting, nevertheless, “I am shocked at the sight of you.”", "로리 씨가 외쳤지만, 그래도 다소 누그러진 채였다. \"자네 꼴을 보니 충격일세.\""]], [["“Now, what I would humbly offer to you, sir,” pursued Mr.", "\"이제, 나리, 제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀은요,\""], ["Cruncher, “even if it wos so, which I don’t say it is--”", "크런처 씨가 말을 이었다. \"설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만—\""]], [["“Don’t prevaricate,”", "\"얼버무리지 말게.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."]], [["“No, I will not, sir,” returned Mr.", "\"아니죠, 절대 안 그럽니다요, 나리.\""], ["Crunches as if nothing were further from his thoughts or practice--“which I don’t say it is--wot I would humbly offer to you, sir, would be this.", "크런처 씨는 그런 생각이나 행실과는 거리가 멀다는 듯이 대꾸했다. \"— 그렇다는 건 아닙니다만 — 제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀은 이겝니다요."], ["Upon that there stool, at that there Bar, sets that there boy of mine, brought up and growed up to be a man, wot will errand you, message you, general-light-job you, till your heels is where your head is, if such should be your wishes.", "저기 저 의자, 저기 저 칸막이 자리에 제 자식놈이 앉아 있는데, 사내로 잘 키워 다 컸으니, 나리께서 바라시기만 하면 발꿈치가 머리 있는 데로 갈 때까지 심부름이고 전갈이고 온갖 잔심부름이고 다 해드릴 겁니다요."], ["If it wos so, which I still don’t say it is (for I will not prewaricate to you, sir), let that there boy keep his father’s place, and take care of his mother; don’t blow upon that boy’s father--do not do it, sir--and let that father go into the line of the reg’lar diggin’, and make amends for what he would have undug--if it wos so--by diggin’ of ’em in with a will, and with conwictions respectin’ the futur’ keepin’ of ’em safe.", "만일 그렇다손 치더라도, 그렇다는 건 여전히 아닙니다만 (나리께 절대 얼버무리지 않을 테니까요), 저 자식놈이 제 아비 자리를 물려받아 제 어미를 돌보게 해주십쇼. 그 애 아비를 고발하지 말아주십쇼 — 그러지 말아주십쇼, 나리 — 그리고 그 아비가 떳떳하게 땅 파는 일에 들어서서, 자기가 파내지 않았어야 할 것들을 — 설령 그랬다손 치더라도 — 열심히 도로 파묻어주고, 앞으로 그것들을 잘 지키겠다는 신념으로 보상하게 해주십쇼."], ["That, Mr. Lorry,”", "이것이, 로리 나리,\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, wiping his forehead with his arm, as an announcement that he had arrived at the peroration of his discourse, “is wot I would respectfully offer to you, sir.", "크런처 씨는 팔로 이마를 닦으며, 자기 연설의 마무리에 이르렀음을 알리듯 말했다."], ["A man don’t see all this here a goin’ on dreadful round him, in the way of Subjects without heads, dear me, plentiful enough fur to bring the price down to porterage and hardly that, without havin’ his serious thoughts of things.", "\"제가 황송하게나마 나리께 드리고 싶은 말씀입니다요. 사람이 제 둘레에서 이런 끔찍한 일들이 벌어지는 걸, 그러니까 머리 없는 시체들이 — 저런, 값이 짐꾼 삯, 아니 그조차도 안 될 만큼 흔해빠지게 — 굴러다니는 걸 보고서, 그 일들에 대해 진지하게 생각하지 않을 수가 없는 거지요."], ["And these here would be mine, if it wos so, entreatin’ of you fur to bear in mind that wot I said just now, I up and said in the good cause when I might have kep’ it back.”", "그리고 설령 그렇다손 치더라도, 이게 제 생각일 겁니다요. 부디 명심해주십쇼, 제가 방금 한 말은, 도로 숨길 수도 있었는데 좋은 뜻에서 떨치고 나서서 한 말이라는 걸 말입니다요.\""]], [["“That at least is true,”", "\"적어도 그건 사실이군.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Say no more now.", "\"이제 그만하게."], ["It may be that I shall yet stand your friend, if you deserve it, and repent in action--not in words.", "자네가 그럴 자격이 있고, 말이 아니라 행동으로 뉘우친다면, 내가 아직 자네 편이 되어줄지도 모르지."], ["I want no more words.”", "더는 말이 필요 없네.\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher knuckled his forehead, as Sydney Carton and the spy returned from the dark room.", "시드니 카턴과 첩자가 어두운 방에서 돌아오자, 크런처 씨는 주먹 마디로 이마를 문질렀다."], ["“Adieu, Mr. Barsad,”", "\"안녕히 가시오, 바사드 씨.\""], ["said the former; “our arrangement thus made, you have nothing to fear from me.”", "앞쪽 사람이 말했다. \"이렇게 합의가 된 이상, 당신은 나한테서 두려워할 게 아무것도 없소.\""]], [["He sat down in a chair on the hearth, over against Mr. Lorry.", "그는 로리 씨와 마주 보이는, 난롯가 의자에 앉았다."], ["When they were alone, Mr. Lorry asked him what he had done?", "둘만 남게 되자 로리 씨는 그에게 무슨 일을 했느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Not much.", "\"별것 아닙니다."], ["If it should go ill with the prisoner, I have ensured access to him, once.”", "죄수에게 일이 나쁘게 돌아갈 경우, 한번은 그에게 접근할 수 있도록 확보해두었습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry’s countenance fell.", "로리 씨의 안색이 어두워졌다."]], [["“It is all I could do,”", "\"그게 제가 할 수 있는 전부였습니다.\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["“To propose too much, would be to put this man’s head under the axe, and, as he himself said, nothing worse could happen to him if he were denounced.", "\"너무 많은 걸 요구하는 건 이자의 목을 도끼 아래 들이미는 꼴이 됐을 겁니다. 게다가 그자 말마따나, 고발당한다 해도 그보다 더 나쁜 일이 일어날 수는 없을 테고요."], ["It was obviously the weakness of the position.", "분명 그게 우리 처지의 약점이었죠."], ["There is no help for it.”", "어쩔 도리가 없습니다.\""]], [["“But access to him,” said Mr.", "\"하지만 그에게 접근하는 것으로는,\""], ["Lorry, “if it should go ill before the Tribunal, will not save him.”", "로리 씨가 말했다. \"재판소에서 일이 나쁘게 돌아간다 해도 그를 구할 수 없잖나.\""]], [["“I never said it would.”", "\"제가 구할 수 있다고는 말한 적 없습니다.\""]], [["Mr.", "로리 씨의 눈길이 차츰 불을 찾아갔다."], ["Lorry’s eyes gradually sought the fire; his sympathy with his darling, and the heavy disappointment of his second arrest, gradually weakened them; he was an old man now, overborne with anxiety of late, and his tears fell.", "사랑하는 이에 대한 연민과, 그가 다시 체포된 데서 온 무거운 실망감이 차츰 그 눈을 약하게 만들었다. 그는 이제 늙은이였고, 근래의 불안에 짓눌려 있었으며, 그의 눈물이 떨어졌다."]], [["“You are a good man and a true friend,”", "\"당신은 선한 분이고 진실한 벗입니다.\""], ["said Carton, in an altered voice.", "카턴이 달라진 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Forgive me if I notice that you are affected.", "\"당신이 마음 아파하시는 걸 제가 알아챘다 해도 용서하십시오."], ["I could not see my father weep, and sit by, careless.", "저는 제 아버지가 우시는 걸 보면서 무심히 곁에 앉아 있을 수 없었을 겁니다."], ["And I could not respect your sorrow more, if you were my father.", "그리고 당신이 제 아버지였다 해도 당신의 슬픔을 지금보다 더 존중할 수는 없었겠지요."], ["You are free from that misfortune, however.”", "하지만 당신은 그런 불행에서 벗어나 있으시군요.\""]], [["Though he said the last words, with a slip into his usual manner, there was a true feeling and respect both in his tone and in his touch, that Mr. Lorry, who had never seen the better side of him, was wholly unprepared for.", "마지막 말은 평소의 태도로 슬쩍 돌아가 하긴 했지만, 그의 어조에도 손길에도 진정한 감정과 존경이 담겨 있어서, 그의 더 나은 면모를 한번도 본 적 없던 로리 씨는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["He gave him his hand, and Carton gently pressed it.", "그가 손을 내밀자, 카턴은 그 손을 부드럽게 쥐었다."]], [["“To return to poor Darnay,”", "\"가엾은 다네이 이야기로 돌아가자면,\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["“Don’t tell Her of this interview, or this arrangement.", "\"이번 면담이나 이번 합의에 대해 그녀에게 말하지 마십시오."], ["It would not enable Her to go to see him.", "그렇다고 그녀가 그를 면회하러 갈 수 있게 되는 것도 아니니까요."], ["She might think it was contrived, in case of the worse, to convey to him the means of anticipating the sentence.”", "그녀는 이게 최악의 경우에 대비해, 그에게 형 집행을 앞당길 방법을 전하려고 꾸민 일이라고 여길지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry had not thought of that, and he looked quickly at Carton to see if it were in his mind.", "로리 씨는 그런 생각을 미처 하지 못했던 터라, 그것이 그의 마음속에 있는지 보려고 재빨리 카턴을 바라보았다."], ["It seemed to be; he returned the look, and evidently understood it.", "그런 듯했다. 카턴이 그 시선을 되받았고, 분명히 그 뜻을 알아차렸다."]], [["“She might think a thousand things,”", "\"그녀는 천 가지 생각을 할지도 모릅니다.\""], ["Carton said, “and any of them would only add to her trouble.", "카턴이 말했다. \"그리고 그 어느 것이든 그녀의 괴로움만 더할 뿐이겠지요."], ["Don’t speak of me to her.", "그녀에게 제 이야기는 하지 마십시오."], ["As I said to you when I first came, I had better not see her.", "처음 왔을 때 말씀드렸듯이, 저는 그녀를 만나지 않는 편이 낫습니다."], ["I can put my hand out, to do any little helpful work for her that my hand can find to do, without that.", "그러지 않고도 제 손이 찾아낼 수 있는 자질구레한 도움이라면 무엇이든 그녀를 위해 손을 내밀 수 있으니까요."], ["You are going to her, I hope?", "이제 그녀에게 가시겠지요?"], ["She must be very desolate to-night.”", "오늘 밤 그녀는 무척 외로울 겁니다.\""]], [["“I am going now, directly.”", "\"지금 곧장 갈 참일세.\""]], [["“I am glad of that.", "\"그것 참 다행입니다."], ["She has such a strong attachment to you and reliance on you.", "그녀는 당신에게 그토록 깊이 의지하고 기댑니다."], ["How does she look?”", "그녀는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["“Anxious and unhappy, but very beautiful.”", "\"불안하고 비통해하지만, 무척 아름답네.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""]], [["It was a long, grieving sound, like a sigh--almost like a sob.", "그것은 한숨 같은 — 거의 흐느낌 같은 — 길고 비탄에 찬 소리였다."], ["It attracted Mr. Lorry’s eyes to Carton’s face, which was turned to the fire.", "그 소리에 로리 씨의 눈길이 불 쪽으로 돌려진 카턴의 얼굴로 끌렸다."], ["A light, or a shade (the old gentleman could not have said which), passed from it as swiftly as a change will sweep over a hill-side on a wild bright day, and he lifted his foot to put back one of the little flaming logs, which was tumbling forward.", "빛인지 그늘인지 (그 노신사는 어느 쪽이라고 말할 수 없었으리라) 그의 얼굴에서, 거칠고 밝은 날 산비탈을 휩쓸고 지나가는 변화처럼 재빨리 스쳐 지나갔다. 그는 발을 들어, 앞으로 굴러떨어지려는 작은 장작 하나를 도로 밀어 넣었다."], ["He wore the white riding-coat and top-boots, then in vogue, and the light of the fire touching their light surfaces made him look very pale, with his long brown hair, all untrimmed, hanging loose about him.", "그는 당시 유행하던 흰 승마복에 목 긴 장화를 신고 있었고, 그 밝은 표면에 닿는 불빛 때문에 그는 무척 창백해 보였으며, 다듬지 않은 긴 갈색 머리카락이 헝클어진 채 그의 둘레로 늘어져 있었다."], ["His indifference to fire was sufficiently remarkable to elicit a word of remonstrance from Mr.", "불에 대한 그의 무관심은 로리 씨가 한마디 나무랄 만큼 두드러진 것이었다."], ["Lorry; his boot was still upon the hot embers of the flaming log, when it had broken under the weight of his foot.", "발의 무게에 눌려 불붙은 장작이 부러졌는데도 그의 장화는 여전히 그 뜨거운 잿불 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["“I forgot it,”", "\"깜빡했군요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Mr. Lorry’s eyes were again attracted to his face.", "로리 씨의 눈길이 다시 그의 얼굴로 끌렸다."], ["Taking note of the wasted air which clouded the naturally handsome features, and having the expression of prisoners’ faces fresh in his mind, he was strongly reminded of that expression.", "본래 잘생긴 이목구비를 흐리고 있는 초췌한 기색에 주목하고, 죄수들의 얼굴 표정을 마음속에 생생히 떠올리고 있던 그는, 바로 그 표정을 강렬하게 떠올렸다."]], [["“And your duties here have drawn to an end, sir?”", "\"이곳에서 맡으신 일은 다 끝나셨습니까, 나리?\""], ["said Carton, turning to him.", "카턴이 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇다네."], ["As I was telling you last night when Lucie came in so unexpectedly, I have at length done all that I can do here.", "어젯밤 루시가 그토록 뜻밖에 들어왔을 때 자네에게 말했듯이, 마침내 여기서 내가 할 수 있는 일은 다 해냈네."], ["I hoped to have left them in perfect safety, and then to have quitted Paris.", "그들을 완전히 안전한 데 두고서 빠리를 떠날 수 있기를 바랐지."], ["I have my Leave to Pass.", "통행 허가증도 가지고 있고."], ["I was ready to go.”", "떠날 채비가 되어 있었네.\""]], [["They were both silent.", "둘 다 말이 없었다."]], [["“Yours is a long life to look back upon, sir?”", "\"나리의 삶은 돌아보기에 긴 세월이겠지요?\""], ["said Carton, wistfully.", "카턴이 아쉬운 듯 말했다."]], [["“I am in my seventy-eighth year.”", "\"일흔여덟번째 해를 맞고 있다네.\""]], [["“You have been useful all your life; steadily and constantly occupied; trusted, respected, and looked up to?”", "\"평생 쓸모 있게 사셨고, 한결같이 꾸준히 일에 매달리셨으며, 신뢰받고 존경받고 우러름을 받으셨겠지요?\""]], [["“I have been a man of business, ever since I have been a man.", "\"나는 어른이 된 이후로 줄곧 사업가였네."], ["Indeed, I may say that I was a man of business when a boy.”", "사실, 어릴 적에도 사업가였다고 할 수 있지.\""]], [["“See what a place you fill at seventy-eight.", "\"보십시오, 일흔여덟에 얼마나 큰 자리를 차지하고 계신지."], ["How many people will miss you when you leave it empty!”", "나리께서 그 자리를 비우고 떠나면 얼마나 많은 사람이 나리를 그리워하겠습니까!\""]], [["“A solitary old bachelor,”", "\"외로운 늙은 독신자일 뿐일세.\""], ["answered Mr. Lorry, shaking his head.", "로리 씨가 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["“There is nobody to weep for me.”", "\"나를 위해 울어줄 사람은 아무도 없네.\""]], [["“How can you say that?", "\"어찌 그런 말씀을 하십니까?"], ["Wouldn’t She weep for you?", "그녀가 나리를 위해 울지 않겠습니까?"], ["Wouldn’t her child?”", "그녀의 아이가 울지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, thank God.", "\"그래, 그래, 하느님께 감사하지."], ["I didn’t quite mean what I said.”", "내 말이 꼭 그런 뜻은 아니었네.\""]], [["“It is a thing to thank God for; is it not?”", "\"그건 정말이지 하느님께 감사할 일이지요. 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Surely, surely.”", "\"그렇고말고, 그렇고말고.\""]], [["“If you could say, with truth, to your own solitary heart, to-night, ‘I have secured to myself the love and attachment, the gratitude or respect, of no human creature; I have won myself a tender place in no regard; I have done nothing good or serviceable to be remembered by!’ your seventy-eight years would be seventy-eight heavy curses; would they not?”", "\"만일 나리께서 오늘 밤 나리의 외로운 가슴에 대고 진심으로 이렇게 말할 수 있다면 — '나는 그 어떤 인간의 사랑도 애정도, 감사도 존경도 내 것으로 확보하지 못했다. 나는 그 누구의 마음속에도 다정한 자리 하나 얻지 못했다. 나는 기억될 만한 선한 일도 쓸모 있는 일도 하나 하지 못했다!' — 나리의 일흔여덟 해는 일흔여덟 가지 무거운 저주가 되겠지요. 그렇지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“You say truly, Mr.", "\"옳은 말일세, 카턴 씨."], ["Carton; I think they would be.”", "정말 그러리라 생각하네.\""]], [["Sydney turned his eyes again upon the fire, and, after a silence of a few moments, said:", "시드니는 다시 불 쪽으로 눈길을 돌렸고, 몇 순간의 침묵 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“I should like to ask you:--Does your childhood seem far off?", "\"여쭙고 싶은 게 있습니다. 나리의 어린 시절이 멀게 느껴지십니까?"], ["Do the days when you sat at your mother’s knee, seem days of very long ago?”", "어머니 무릎에 앉았던 날들이 아주 오래전 일처럼 느껴지십니까?\""]], [["Responding to his softened manner, Mr. Lorry answered:", "그의 부드러워진 태도에 응하여, 로리 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Twenty years back, yes; at this time of my life, no.", "\"이십 년 전이라면 그랬을 테지. 하지만 내 삶의 이 시점에서는 아니라네."], ["For, as I draw closer and closer to the end, I travel in the circle, nearer and nearer to the beginning.", "끝에 점점 더 가까이 다가갈수록, 나는 그 둘레를 돌아 시작점에 점점 더 가까이 가게 되거든."], ["It seems to be one of the kind smoothings and preparings of the way.", "그건 길을 부드럽게 다듬고 채비해주는 어떤 자비로운 손길 가운데 하나인 듯싶네."], ["My heart is touched now, by many remembrances that had long fallen asleep, of my pretty young mother (and I so old!), and by many associations of the days when what we call the World was not so real with me, and my faults were not confirmed in me.”", "지금 내 마음은, 오래 잠들어 있던 수많은 기억들에 닿아 흔들린다네. 내 예쁘고 젊은 어머니에 대한 (이렇게나 늙은 나에게!), 그리고 우리가 세상이라 부르는 것이 나에게 그토록 실감 나지 않았고 내 결점들이 내 안에 굳어지지 않았던 날들에 얽힌 수많은 추억들에 말일세.\""]], [["“I understand the feeling!”", "\"그 심정 이해합니다!\""], ["exclaimed Carton, with a bright flush.", "카턴이 환하게 얼굴을 붉히며 외쳤다."], ["“And you are the better for it?”", "\"그리고 그 덕에 더 나아지셨군요?\""]], [["“I hope so.”", "\"그러기를 바라네.\""]], [["Carton terminated the conversation here, by rising to help him on with his outer coat; “But you,”", "카턴은 자리에서 일어나 그가 겉옷 입는 것을 도와주며 여기서 대화를 끝맺었다."], ["said Mr.", "\"하지만 자네는,\""], ["Lorry, reverting to the theme, “you are young.”", "로리 씨가 그 화제로 돌아가며 말했다. \"자네는 젊지 않은가.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇지요.\""], ["said Carton.", "카턴이 말했다."], ["“I am not old, but my young way was never the way to age.", "\"저는 늙지 않았습니다만, 제 젊은 시절은 결코 늙어가는 길이 아니었지요."], ["Enough of me.”", "제 이야기는 그만하겠습니다.\""]], [["“And of me, I am sure,”", "\"내 이야기도 그렇네, 분명히.\""], ["said Mr. Lorry.", "로리 씨가 말했다."], ["“Are you going out?”", "\"자네 나가려는가?\""]], [["“I’ll walk with you to her gate.", "\"그녀의 집 문까지 함께 걷지요."], ["You know my vagabond and restless habits.", "제 떠돌고 안절부절못하는 버릇은 아시지 않습니까."], ["If I should prowl about the streets a long time, don’t be uneasy; I shall reappear in the morning.", "제가 오래도록 거리를 어슬렁거리더라도 걱정하지 마십시오. 아침이면 다시 나타날 테니까요."], ["You go to the Court to-morrow?”", "내일 법정에 가십니까?\""]], [["“Yes, unhappily.”", "\"그렇다네, 불행히도.\""]], [["“I shall be there, but only as one of the crowd.", "\"저도 거기 있을 겁니다. 하지만 군중 가운데 한 사람으로서일 뿐이지요."], ["My Spy will find a place for me.", "제 첩자가 자리를 마련해줄 겁니다."], ["Take my arm, sir.”", "제 팔을 잡으시지요, 나리.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry did so, and they went down-stairs and out in the streets.", "로리 씨가 그렇게 했고, 그들은 계단을 내려가 거리로 나섰다."], ["A few minutes brought them to Mr. Lorry’s destination.", "몇 분 만에 로리 씨의 목적지에 다다랐다."], ["Carton left him there; but lingered at a little distance, and turned back to the gate again when it was shut, and touched it.", "카턴은 거기서 그와 헤어졌으나, 조금 떨어진 곳에서 머뭇거리다가, 문이 닫힌 뒤 다시 그 문으로 돌아가 그것을 어루만졌다."], ["He had heard of her going to the prison every day.", "그는 그녀가 날마다 감옥에 간다는 이야기를 들은 터였다."], ["“She came out here,”", "\"그녀는 여기로 나왔겠지.\""], ["he said, looking about him, “turned this way, must have trod on these stones often.", "그는 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"이쪽으로 돌아서, 이 돌들을 자주 밟았을 거야."], ["Let me follow in her steps.”", "그녀의 발자취를 따라가 보자.\""]], [["It was ten o’clock at night when he stood before the prison of La Force, where she had stood hundreds of times.", "그녀가 수백 번 서 있었던 라포르스 감옥 앞에 그가 섰을 때는 밤 열 시였다."], ["A little wood-sawyer, having closed his shop, was smoking his pipe at his shop-door.", "자그마한 톱장이 하나가 가게 문을 닫고서 가게 문간에서 파이프 담배를 피우고 있었다."]], [["“Good night, citizen,”", "\"안녕하시오, 시민.\""], ["said Sydney Carton, pausing in going by; for, the man eyed him inquisitively.", "시드니 카턴은 지나가다 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. 그 사람이 캐묻듯이 그를 쳐다보았기 때문이다."]], [["“Good night, citizen.”", "\"안녕하시오, 시민.\""]], [["“How goes the Republic?”", "\"공화국은 어떻게 돌아가고 있소?\""]], [["“You mean the Guillotine.", "\"단두대 말이겠지."], ["Not ill.", "나쁘지 않소."], ["Sixty-three to-day.", "오늘 예순셋이었거든."], ["We shall mount to a hundred soon.", "머잖아 백까지 올라갈 거요."], ["Samson and his men complain sometimes, of being exhausted.", "쌍송이랑 그 부하들은 가끔 기진맥진하다고 투덜대지. 하하하!"], ["Ha, ha, ha! He is so droll, that Samson.", "어찌나 우스운지, 그 쌍송이란 작자가."], ["Such a Barber!”", "대단한 이발사라니까!\""]], [["“Do you often go to see him--”", "\"그자를 자주 보러 가시오—\""]], [["“Shave?", "\"면도하는 거?"], ["Always.", "늘 가지."], ["Every day.", "매일같이."], ["What a barber!", "대단한 이발사라니까!"], ["You have seen him at work?”", "일하는 걸 본 적 있소?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"없소.\""]], [["“Go and see him when he has a good batch.", "\"한 무더기 잘 모였을 때 가서 보시구려."], ["Figure this to yourself, citizen; he shaved the sixty-three to-day, in less than two pipes!", "자, 한번 그려보슈, 시민. 그자가 오늘 예순셋을 파이프 두 대도 안 피우는 사이에 면도해버렸다니까!"], ["Less than two pipes.", "두 대도 안 되게."], ["Word of honour!”", "정말이라니까!\""]], [["As the grinning little man held out the pipe he was smoking, to explain how he timed the executioner, Carton was so sensible of a rising desire to strike the life out of him, that he turned away.", "씩 웃는 그 자그마한 사내가 자기가 사형 집행인의 솜씨를 어떻게 쟀는지 설명하려고 피우던 파이프를 내밀었을 때, 카턴은 그자의 목숨을 쳐서 끊어버리고 싶은 충동이 솟구치는 것을 너무도 또렷이 느낀 나머지, 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“But you are not English,”", "\"한데 당신은 영국인이 아니지요?\""], ["said the wood-sawyer, “though you wear English dress?”", "톱장이가 말했다. \"영국 옷을 입고 있긴 하지만.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞소.\""], ["said Carton, pausing again, and answering over his shoulder.", "카턴은 다시 걸음을 멈추고 어깨 너머로 대답했다."]], [["“You speak like a Frenchman.”", "\"프랑스인처럼 말하는데.\""]], [["“I am an old student here.”", "\"여기서 오래 공부한 사람이오.\""]], [["“Aha, a perfect Frenchman!", "\"아하, 완벽한 프랑스인이로군!"], ["Good night, Englishman.”", "잘 가시오, 영국인.\""]], [["“Good night, citizen.”", "\"잘 있으시오, 시민.\""]], [["“But go and see that droll dog,”", "\"하지만 그 우스운 작자를 꼭 보러 가구려.\""], ["the little man persisted, calling after him.", "자그마한 사내가 끈질기게 그의 등 뒤에 대고 외쳤다."], ["“And take a pipe with you!”", "\"파이프도 챙겨 가시고!\""]], [["Sydney had not gone far out of sight, when he stopped in the middle of the street under a glimmering lamp, and wrote with his pencil on a scrap of paper.", "시드니는 시야에서 멀리 벗어나기도 전에, 거리 한복판 가물거리는 등불 아래 멈춰 서서 종잇조각에 연필로 무언가를 적었다."], ["Then, traversing with the decided step of one who remembered the way well, several dark and dirty streets--much dirtier than usual, for the best public thoroughfares remained uncleansed in those times of terror--he stopped at a chemist’s shop, which the owner was closing with his own hands.", "그러고는 길을 잘 기억하는 사람의 단호한 걸음걸이로, 여러 어둡고 더러운 거리를 — 공포의 시절이라 가장 번화한 큰길조차 청소가 안 되어 평소보다 훨씬 더 더러운 거리를 — 가로질러, 주인이 손수 문을 닫고 있는 어느 약방 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["A small, dim, crooked shop, kept in a tortuous, up-hill thoroughfare, by a small, dim, crooked man.", "자그맣고 침침하고 구부정한 사내가 운영하는, 구불구불 비탈진 거리에 자리한 자그맣고 침침하고 비뚤어진 가게였다."]], [["Giving this citizen, too, good night, as he confronted him at his counter, he laid the scrap of paper before him.", "계산대에서 마주한 이 시민에게도 잘 있으라고 인사한 뒤, 그는 종잇조각을 그 앞에 내려놓았다."], ["“Whew!”", "\"휘유!\""], ["the chemist whistled softly, as he read it.", "약제사는 그것을 읽으며 나직이 휘파람을 불었다. \"이런!"], ["“Hi!", "이런!"], ["hi! hi!”", "이런!\""]], [["Sydney Carton took no heed, and the chemist said:", "시드니 카턴은 아랑곳하지 않았고, 약제사가 말했다."]], [["“For you, citizen?”", "\"당신이 쓸 거요, 시민?\""], ["“For me.”", "\"내가 쓸 거요.\""]], [["“You will be careful to keep them separate, citizen?", "\"이것들을 따로따로 보관하도록 조심할 거지요, 시민?"], ["You know the consequences of mixing them?”", "섞었을 때 어떻게 되는지 아시오?\""]], [["“Perfectly.”", "\"잘 아오.\""]], [["Certain small packets were made and given to him.", "작은 꾸러미 몇 개가 만들어져 그에게 건네졌다."], ["He put them, one by one, in the breast of his inner coat, counted out the money for them, and deliberately left the shop.", "그는 그것들을 하나씩 안쪽 외투의 가슴께에 넣고, 값을 헤아려 치른 뒤, 천천히 가게를 나섰다."], ["“There is nothing more to do,”", "\"더 할 일은 아무것도 없군.\""], ["said he, glancing upward at the moon, “until to-morrow.", "그는 달을 올려다보며 말했다. \"내일까지는."], ["I can’t sleep.”", "잠을 이룰 수가 없어.\""]], [["It was not a reckless manner, the manner in which he said these words aloud under the fast-sailing clouds, nor was it more expressive of negligence than defiance.", "빠르게 흘러가는 구름 아래에서 그가 이 말을 소리 내어 한 태도에는 무모함이라곤 없었고, 무관심이라기보다는 차라리 결연함을 드러내고 있었다."], ["It was the settled manner of a tired man, who had wandered and struggled and got lost, but who at length struck into his road and saw its end.", "그것은 헤매고 발버둥치다 길을 잃었으나, 마침내 제 길로 들어서서 그 끝을 본 지친 사람의 차분한 태도였다."]], [["Long ago, when he had been famous among his earliest competitors as a youth of great promise, he had followed his father to the grave.", "오래전, 그가 가장 이른 시절의 경쟁자들 사이에서 장래가 촉망되는 청년으로 이름났을 때, 그는 아버지를 무덤까지 따라간 적이 있었다."], ["His mother had died, years before.", "어머니는 그보다 여러 해 앞서 세상을 떠났다."], ["These solemn words, which had been read at his father’s grave, arose in his mind as he went down the dark streets, among the heavy shadows, with the moon and the clouds sailing on high above him.", "아버지의 무덤가에서 낭독되었던 그 엄숙한 말씀이, 그가 머리 위로 달과 구름이 흘러가는 가운데 짙은 그림자들 사이로 어두운 거리를 지나가는 동안 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다."], ["“I am the resurrection and the life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me, shall never die.”", "\"주께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\""]], [["In a city dominated by the axe, alone at night, with natural sorrow rising in him for the sixty-three who had been that day put to death, and for to-morrow’s victims then awaiting their doom in the prisons, and still of to-morrow’s and to-morrow’s, the chain of association that brought the words home, like a rusty old ship’s anchor from the deep, might have been easily found.", "도끼가 지배하는 도시에서, 밤에 홀로, 그날 처형당한 예순셋과, 그때 감옥에서 운명을 기다리던 내일의 희생자들과, 그리고 또 내일의, 또 내일의 희생자들에 대한 자연스러운 슬픔이 그의 안에서 솟구치는 가운데, 깊은 바다에서 끌어올린 녹슨 낡은 닻처럼 그 말씀을 마음에 와닿게 한 연상의 사슬은 쉽게 찾아볼 수 있었으리라."], ["He did not seek it, but repeated them and went on.", "그는 그것을 찾지 않았으나, 그 말씀을 되뇌며 걸어갔다."]], [["With a solemn interest in the lighted windows where the people were going to rest, forgetful through a few calm hours of the horrors surrounding them; in the towers of the churches, where no prayers were said, for the popular revulsion had even travelled that length of self-destruction from years of priestly impostors, plunderers, and profligates; in the distant burial-places, reserved, as they wrote upon the gates, for Eternal Sleep; in the abounding gaols; and in the streets along which the sixties rolled to a death which had become so common and material, that no sorrowful story of a haunting Spirit ever arose among the people out of all the working of the Guillotine; with a solemn interest in the whole life and death of the city settling down to its short nightly pause in fury; Sydney Carton crossed the Seine again for the lighter streets.", "자기들을 에워싼 공포를 몇 시간의 평온 속에 잊은 채 잠자리에 들려는 사람들의 불 켜진 창문들 — 기도 소리 하나 들리지 않는 교회의 탑들, 사제 노릇을 한 사기꾼과 약탈자와 난봉꾼들에 대한 수년간의 반감이 그 자멸의 지경에까지 이른 탓이었다 — 정문에 그들이 적어놓았듯 영원한 잠을 위해 마련된 멀리 떨어진 매장지들 — 넘쳐나는 감옥들 — 그리고 너무도 흔하고 무미건조해져서, 단두대의 그 온갖 작업을 두고 사람들 사이에 떠도는 유령 이야기 하나 나오지 않을 만큼 예순 명씩 죽음을 향해 굴러가던 거리들 — 분노에 찬 그 짧은 밤의 멈춤 속에 가라앉아가는 이 도시의 삶과 죽음 전체에 대해 엄숙한 관심을 품은 채, 시드니 카턴은 더 밝은 거리들을 향해 다시 쎈 강을 건넜다."]], [["Few coaches were abroad, for riders in coaches were liable to be suspected, and gentility hid its head in red nightcaps, and put on heavy shoes, and trudged.", "거리에 나다니는 마차는 거의 없었다. 마차를 탄 자들은 의심받기 십상이었기에, 양반네들은 붉은 잠모자 아래 고개를 숨기고 투박한 신을 신은 채 터덜터덜 걸었다."], ["But, the theatres were all well filled, and the people poured cheerfully out as he passed, and went chatting home.", "하지만 극장들은 모두 만원이었고, 그가 지나갈 때 사람들이 즐겁게 쏟아져 나와 떠들썩하게 이야기하며 집으로 갔다."], ["At one of the theatre doors, there was a little girl with a mother, looking for a way across the street through the mud.", "어느 극장 문 앞에, 어머니와 함께 있던 어린 소녀 하나가 진흙탕 너머로 길을 건널 방법을 찾고 있었다."], ["He carried the child over, and before the timid arm was loosed from his neck asked her for a kiss.", "그는 아이를 안아 건네주었고, 머뭇거리던 그 팔이 목에서 풀리기 전에 입맞춤을 청했다."]], [["“I am the resurrection and the life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me, shall never die.”", "\"주께서 이르시되, 나는 부활이요 생명이니, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\""]], [["Now, that the streets were quiet, and the night wore on, the words were in the echoes of his feet, and were in the air.", "이제 거리가 고요해지고 밤이 깊어가자, 그 말씀이 그의 발걸음 메아리 속에 있었고, 또 공기 속에 있었다."], ["Perfectly calm and steady, he sometimes repeated them to himself as he walked; but, he heard them always.", "더없이 차분하고 흔들림 없는 채로, 그는 걸으면서 이따금 그 말씀을 혼잣말로 되뇌었으나, 늘 그 소리를 들었다."]], [["The night wore out, and, as he stood upon the bridge listening to the water as it splashed the river-walls of the Island of Paris, where the picturesque confusion of houses and cathedral shone bright in the light of the moon, the day came coldly, looking like a dead face out of the sky.", "밤이 다 지나갔고, 그가 다리 위에 서서, 그림처럼 어수선한 집들과 대성당이 달빛을 받아 환히 빛나는 빠리 섬의 강변 벽에 물이 철썩이는 소리를 듣고 있을 때, 하늘에서 죽은 얼굴처럼 보이는 날이 차갑게 밝아왔다."], ["Then, the night, with the moon and the stars, turned pale and died, and for a little while it seemed as if Creation were delivered over to Death’s dominion.", "그러자 달과 별을 거느린 밤이 창백해지더니 죽어갔고, 잠시 동안은 마치 천지창조가 죽음의 권세에 넘겨진 듯이 보였다."]], [["But, the glorious sun, rising, seemed to strike those words, that burden of the night, straight and warm to his heart in its long bright rays.", "그러나 영광스러운 해가 떠오르면서, 밤의 그 짐이었던 그 말씀을, 그 길고 밝은 빛줄기로 곧장 따스하게 그의 가슴에 와닿게 하는 듯했다."], ["And looking along them, with reverently shaded eyes, a bridge of light appeared to span the air between him and the sun, while the river sparkled under it.", "그리고 경건하게 손으로 눈을 가린 채 그 빛줄기를 따라 바라보니, 빛의 다리가 그와 해 사이의 허공에 걸린 듯했고, 그 아래로 강물이 반짝였다."]], [["The strong tide, so swift, so deep, and certain, was like a congenial friend, in the morning stillness.", "그토록 빠르고 깊고 확실한 거센 물살은, 아침의 고요 속에서 마음 맞는 벗과도 같았다."], ["He walked by the stream, far from the houses, and in the light and warmth of the sun fell asleep on the bank.", "그는 집들에서 멀리 떨어져 강줄기를 따라 걸었고, 햇빛과 온기 속에서 강둑에 잠들었다."], ["When he awoke and was afoot again, he lingered there yet a little longer, watching an eddy that turned and turned purposeless, until the stream absorbed it, and carried it on to the sea.--“Like me.”", "잠에서 깨어 다시 일어났을 때, 그는 거기서 잠시 더 머물며, 목적 없이 돌고 또 도는 소용돌이가 마침내 강물에 휩쓸려 바다로 실려 가는 것을 지켜보았다. \"나처럼.\""]], [["A trading-boat, with a sail of the softened colour of a dead leaf, then glided into his view, floated by him, and died away.", "그때 죽은 잎사귀의 바랜 빛깔 같은 돛을 단 장삿배 한 척이 그의 시야에 미끄러져 들어와, 그의 곁을 떠가다가 사라져갔다."], ["As its silent track in the water disappeared, the prayer that had broken up out of his heart for a merciful consideration of all his poor blindnesses and errors, ended in the words, “I am the resurrection and the life.”", "물 위에 남은 그 고요한 자취가 사라질 무렵, 자신의 그 모든 가련한 눈멀음과 과오를 자비롭게 헤아려달라고 그의 가슴에서 솟구쳐 나온 그 기도는, \"나는 부활이요 생명이니\"라는 말씀으로 끝맺었다."]], [["Mr. Lorry was already out when he got back, and it was easy to surmise where the good old man was gone.", "그가 돌아왔을 때 로리 씨는 이미 나가고 없었고, 그 선한 노인이 어디로 갔는지는 짐작하기 어렵지 않았다."], ["Sydney Carton drank nothing but a little coffee, ate some bread, and, having washed and changed to refresh himself, went out to the place of trial.", "시드니 카턴은 커피 한 잔만 마시고 빵을 좀 먹은 뒤, 몸을 씻고 옷을 갈아입어 기운을 차리고는, 재판이 열리는 곳으로 나갔다."]], [["The court was all astir and a-buzz, when the black sheep--whom many fell away from in dread--pressed him into an obscure corner among the crowd.", "법정은 온통 술렁이며 웅성거리고 있었고, 그 검은 양은 — 두려워서 많은 이들이 그에게서 멀어졌다 — 그를 군중 속 어느 외진 구석으로 밀어 넣었다."], ["Mr. Lorry was there, and Doctor Manette was there.", "로리 씨가 거기 있었고, 마네뜨 박사도 거기 있었다."], ["She was there, sitting beside her father.", "그녀도 거기, 아버지 곁에 앉아 있었다."]], [["When her husband was brought in, she turned a look upon him, so sustaining, so encouraging, so full of admiring love and pitying tenderness, yet so courageous for his sake, that it called the healthy blood into his face, brightened his glance, and animated his heart.", "그녀의 남편이 끌려 들어왔을 때, 그녀는 그에게 시선을 보냈는데, 어찌나 떠받쳐주고, 어찌나 북돋아주고, 어찌나 흠모하는 사랑과 가엾어하는 다정함으로 가득하면서도, 그를 위해 어찌나 용감한 시선이었던지, 그것은 그의 얼굴에 건강한 핏기를 불러일으키고, 그의 눈빛을 밝히며, 그의 마음에 생기를 불어넣었다."], ["If there had been any eyes to notice the influence of her look, on Sydney Carton, it would have been seen to be the same influence exactly.", "만일 그녀의 시선이 시드니 카턴에게 미친 영향을 알아챌 눈이 있었다면, 그것이 정확히 똑같은 영향임을 알아볼 수 있었으리라."]], [["Before that unjust Tribunal, there was little or no order of procedure, ensuring to any accused person any reasonable hearing.", "그 부당한 재판소 앞에는, 그 어떤 피고에게도 합당한 심리를 보장하는 절차의 질서라곤 거의, 아니 전혀 없었다."], ["There could have been no such Revolution, if all laws, forms, and ceremonies, had not first been so monstrously abused, that the suicidal vengeance of the Revolution was to scatter them all to the winds.", "만일 모든 법과 형식과 의례가 먼저 그토록 끔찍하게 남용되지 않았더라면, 그래서 혁명의 자멸적인 복수가 그것들을 모조리 바람에 흩날려 버리려 들지 않았더라면, 그런 혁명은 결코 일어날 수 없었으리라."]], [["Every eye was turned to the jury.", "모든 눈이 배심원단을 향했다."], ["The same determined patriots and good republicans as yesterday and the day before, and to-morrow and the day after.", "어제도 그제도, 그리고 내일도 모레도 마찬가지일 그 단호한 애국자들이자 선량한 공화주의자들이었다."], ["Eager and prominent among them, one man with a craving face, and his fingers perpetually hovering about his lips, whose appearance gave great satisfaction to the spectators.", "그들 가운데 열성적이고 두드러진 사람으로, 갈망하는 얼굴에 손가락을 끊임없이 입가에 가져다 대는 한 사내가 있었는데, 그의 모습은 구경꾼들에게 큰 만족을 주었다."], ["A life-thirsting, cannibal-looking, bloody-minded juryman, the Jacques Three of St.", "생명에 굶주린, 식인귀 같은 인상에, 피에 굶주린 그 배심원은 쌩땅뚜안의 자끄 쓰리였다."], ["Antoine. The whole jury, as a jury of dogs empannelled to try the deer.", "배심원단 전체가, 사슴을 재판하라고 소집된 개들의 배심원단 같았다."]], [["Every eye then turned to the five judges and the public prosecutor.", "이어 모든 눈이 다섯 명의 판사와 검사에게로 향했다."], ["No favourable leaning in that quarter to-day.", "오늘 그쪽에는 호의적인 기색이라곤 없었다."], ["A fell, uncompromising, murderous business-meaning there.", "거기엔 무자비하고 타협 없는, 살의를 띤 단호한 의도가 서려 있었다."], ["Every eye then sought some other eye in the crowd, and gleamed at it approvingly; and heads nodded at one another, before bending forward with a strained attention.", "이어 모든 눈이 군중 속에서 다른 누군가의 눈을 찾아, 그것을 향해 동의하듯 번득였다. 그리고 긴장한 듯 골똘히 앞으로 몸을 숙이기 전에 서로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay.", "다네이라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드."], ["Released yesterday.", "어제 석방됨."], ["Reaccused and retaken yesterday.", "어제 다시 고발되어 다시 체포됨."], ["Indictment delivered to him last night.", "어젯밤 기소장이 전달됨."], ["Suspected and Denounced enemy of the Republic, Aristocrat, one of a family of tyrants, one of a race proscribed, for that they had used their abolished privileges to the infamous oppression of the people.", "공화국의 적으로 의심받고 고발된 자, 귀족, 폭군 일가의 일원, 폐지된 특권을 백성에 대한 파렴치한 압제에 이용했다는 이유로 추방당한 가문의 일원."], ["Charles Evrémonde, called Darnay, in right of such proscription, absolutely Dead in Law.", "다네이라 불리는 샤를 에브레몽드는 그러한 추방의 이유로, 법적으로 완전히 사망한 자였다."]], [["To this effect, in as few or fewer words, the Public Prosecutor.", "이러한 취지로, 그만큼의, 아니 그보다 더 적은 말로 검사가 진술했다."]], [["The President asked, was the Accused openly denounced or secretly?", "재판장이 물었다. 피고는 공공연히 고발되었는가, 아니면 은밀히 고발되었는가?"]], [["“Openly, President.”", "\"공공연히 고발되었습니다, 재판장님.\""]], [["“By whom?”", "\"누구에 의해?\""]], [["“Three voices.", "\"세 사람의 목소리에 의해서입니다."], ["Ernest Defarge, wine-vendor of St. Antoine.”", "쌩땅뚜안의 포도주 상인 에르네스뜨 드파르주.\""]], [["“Good.”", "\"좋소.\""]], [["“Thérèse Defarge, his wife.”", "\"그의 아내, 떼레즈 드파르주.\""]], [["“Good.”", "\"좋소.\""]], [["“Alexandre Manette, physician.”", "\"의사, 알렉상드르 마네뜨.\""]], [["A great uproar took place in the court, and in the midst of it, Doctor Manette was seen, pale and trembling, standing where he had been seated.", "법정에 큰 소동이 일었고, 그 한가운데서, 창백하게 떨며 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어선 마네뜨 박사가 보였다."]], [["“President, I indignantly protest to you that this is a forgery and a fraud.", "\"재판장, 나는 이것이 위조요 사기임을 분개하여 항의하는 바이오."], ["You know the accused to be the husband of my daughter.", "당신도 피고가 내 딸의 남편임을 알지 않소."], ["My daughter, and those dear to her, are far dearer to me than my life.", "내 딸과 그녀가 사랑하는 이들은 내 목숨보다 훨씬 더 소중하오."], ["Who and where is the false conspirator who says that I denounce the husband of my child!”", "내가 내 자식의 남편을 고발했다고 말하는 그 거짓 음모자는 대체 누구이며 어디 있단 말이오!\""]], [["“Citizen Manette, be tranquil.", "\"마네뜨 시민, 진정하시오."], ["To fail in submission to the authority of the Tribunal would be to put yourself out of Law.", "재판소의 권위에 복종하지 않는 것은 스스로를 법 밖에 두는 일이오."], ["As to what is dearer to you than life, nothing can be so dear to a good citizen as the Republic.”", "당신에게 목숨보다 소중한 것이 무엇이냐 하면, 선량한 시민에게 공화국보다 더 소중한 것은 있을 수 없소.\""]], [["Loud acclamations hailed this rebuke.", "이 질책에 우렁찬 갈채가 쏟아졌다."], ["The President rang his bell, and with warmth resumed.", "재판장은 종을 울리고는 열을 올리며 말을 이었다."]], [["“If the Republic should demand of you the sacrifice of your child herself, you would have no duty but to sacrifice her.", "\"만일 공화국이 당신에게 당신의 자식을 희생하라고 요구한다면, 당신에게는 그녀를 희생하는 것 말고 다른 의무가 없을 것이오."], ["Listen to what is to follow.", "이어지는 말을 들으시오."], ["In the meanwhile, be silent!”", "그동안은 잠자코 계시오!\""]], [["Frantic acclamations were again raised.", "또다시 광적인 갈채가 터져 나왔다."], ["Doctor Manette sat down, with his eyes looking around, and his lips trembling; his daughter drew closer to him.", "마네뜨 박사는 두리번거리는 눈에 입술을 떨며 자리에 앉았다. 그의 딸이 그에게 더 바짝 다가갔다."], ["The craving man on the jury rubbed his hands together, and restored the usual hand to his mouth.", "배심원단의 그 갈망하는 사내는 두 손을 비비더니, 늘 하던 손을 다시 입가로 가져갔다."]], [["Defarge was produced, when the court was quiet enough to admit of his being heard, and rapidly expounded the story of the imprisonment, and of his having been a mere boy in the Doctor’s service, and of the release, and of the state of the prisoner when released and delivered to him.", "법정이 그의 말을 들을 수 있을 만큼 조용해지자 드파르주가 불려 나왔고, 그는 그 투옥에 관한 이야기와, 자신이 한낱 소년으로 박사의 집에서 일했던 일, 그리고 석방에 관한 이야기, 또 석방되어 자신에게 넘겨졌을 때의 죄수 상태를 빠르게 설명했다."], ["This short examination followed, for the court was quick with its work.", "이어 짧은 심문이 뒤따랐으니, 법정은 일을 신속하게 처리했기 때문이다."]], [["“You did good service at the taking of the Bastille, citizen?”", "\"바스띠유 함락 때 큰 공을 세웠소, 시민?\""]], [["“I believe so.”", "\"그렇다고 생각합니다.\""]], [["Here, an excited woman screeched from the crowd: “You were one of the best patriots there.", "이때, 흥분한 여자 하나가 군중 속에서 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"당신은 거기서 가장 훌륭한 애국자 중 하나였잖소."], ["Why not say so?", "왜 그렇게 말하지 않는 거요?"], ["You were a cannonier that day there, and you were among the first to enter the accursed fortress when it fell.", "그날 거기서 당신은 포수였고, 그 저주받은 요새가 함락됐을 때 가장 먼저 들어간 사람들 중 하나였소."], ["Patriots, I speak the truth!”", "애국자들이여, 내 말이 진실이오!\""]], [["It was The Vengeance who, amidst the warm commendations of the audience, thus assisted the proceedings.", "청중의 따뜻한 칭찬 속에서 이렇게 절차를 거든 것은 라벵죙스였다."], ["The President rang his bell; but, The Vengeance, warming with encouragement, shrieked, “I defy that bell!”", "재판장이 종을 울렸으나, 라벵죙스는 격려에 한껏 신이 나서 새된 소리로 외쳤다. \"그 종 따위 두렵지 않다!\""], ["wherein she was likewise much commended.", "이에 그녀는 또한 큰 칭찬을 받았다."]], [["“Inform the Tribunal of what you did that day within the Bastille, citizen.”", "\"그날 바스띠유 안에서 당신이 한 일을 재판소에 알리시오, 시민.\""]], [["“I knew,”", "\"나는 알았소.\""], ["said Defarge, looking down at his wife, who stood at the bottom of the steps on which he was raised, looking steadily up at him; “I knew that this prisoner, of whom I speak, had been confined in a cell known as One Hundred and Five, North Tower.", "드파르주는 자기가 올라선 계단 아래에 서서 그를 똑바로 올려다보고 있는 아내를 내려다보며 말했다. \"내가 말하는 이 죄수가 북탑 백오호로 알려진 감방에 갇혀 있었다는 것을 나는 알았소."], ["I knew it from himself.", "그자에게서 직접 들어 알았소."], ["He knew himself by no other name than One Hundred and Five, North Tower, when he made shoes under my care.", "그자는 내 보살핌 아래 구두를 만들 때, 자신을 북탑 백오호 말고는 다른 어떤 이름으로도 몰랐소."], ["As I serve my gun that day, I resolve, when the place shall fall, to examine that cell.", "그날 대포를 다루면서, 나는 이곳이 함락되면 그 감방을 살펴보리라 결심했소."], ["It falls.", "함락되었소."], ["I mount to the cell, with a fellow-citizen who is one of the Jury, directed by a gaoler.", "나는 배심원단의 일원인 동료 시민 하나와 함께, 간수의 안내를 받아 그 감방으로 올라갔소."], ["I examine it, very closely.", "나는 그곳을 아주 면밀히 살폈소."], ["In a hole in the chimney, where a stone has been worked out and replaced, I find a written paper.", "굴뚝의 한 구멍, 돌 하나가 빼냈다가 도로 끼워진 곳에서, 나는 글이 적힌 종이 한 장을 발견했소."], ["This is that written paper.", "이것이 그 글이 적힌 종이요."], ["I have made it my business to examine some specimens of the writing of Doctor Manette.", "나는 마네뜨 박사의 필적 몇 가지를 살펴보는 것을 내 일로 삼아왔소."], ["This is the writing of Doctor Manette.", "이것은 마네뜨 박사의 필적이오."], ["I confide this paper, in the writing of Doctor Manette, to the hands of the President.”", "나는 마네뜨 박사의 필적으로 된 이 종이를 재판장의 손에 맡기는 바이오.\""]], [["“Let it be read.”", "\"낭독하도록 하시오.\""]], [["In a dead silence and stillness--the prisoner under trial looking lovingly at his wife, his wife only looking from him to look with solicitude at her father, Doctor Manette keeping his eyes fixed on the reader, Madame Defarge never taking hers from the prisoner, Defarge never taking his from his feasting wife, and all the other eyes there intent upon the Doctor, who saw none of them--the paper was read, as follows.", "죽은 듯한 정적과 고요 속에서 — 재판받는 죄수는 아내를 사랑스럽게 바라보고, 그의 아내는 오직 그에게서 눈을 떼어 근심스레 제 아버지를 바라볼 뿐이며, 마네뜨 박사는 낭독자에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 드파르주 부인은 결코 죄수에게서 눈을 떼지 않으며, 드파르주는 결코 흡족해하는 제 아내에게서 눈을 떼지 않고, 그곳의 다른 모든 눈은 정작 그들 가운데 누구도 보지 못하는 박사에게 골똘히 쏠려 있는 가운데 — 그 종이가 다음과 같이 낭독되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER X. The Substance of the Shadow", "# CHAPTER X. The Substance of the Shadow"]], [["“I, Alexandre Manette, unfortunate physician, native of Beauvais, and afterwards resident in Paris, write this melancholy paper in my doleful cell in the Bastille, during the last month of the year, 1767.", "「나, 알렉상드르 마네뜨, 보베 태생의 불행한 의사이며 후일 빠리에 거주한 자는, 1767년 마지막 달에 바스띠유의 음울한 감방에서 이 비통한 글을 쓴다."], ["I write it at stolen intervals, under every difficulty.", "나는 온갖 어려움을 무릅쓰고 틈틈이 몰래 이 글을 쓴다."], ["I design to secrete it in the wall of the chimney, where I have slowly and laboriously made a place of concealment for it.", "굴뚝의 벽 안에 숨겨둘 작정인데, 그곳에 나는 더디고 고된 노력으로 이것을 감출 자리를 마련해두었다."], ["Some pitying hand may find it there, when I and my sorrows are dust.", "나와 나의 슬픔이 한 줌 흙으로 돌아갔을 때, 어느 가엾이 여기는 손길이 그것을 거기서 발견할지도 모른다."]], [["“These words are formed by the rusty iron point with which I write with difficulty in scrapings of soot and charcoal from the chimney, mixed with blood, in the last month of the tenth year of my captivity.", "이 글자들은, 내가 갇혀 지낸 지 열 해째 되는 해의 마지막 달에, 굴뚝에서 긁어낸 검댕과 숯가루를 피와 섞어 어렵사리 써내려가는, 녹슨 쇠끝으로 새긴 것이다."], ["Hope has quite departed from my breast.", "희망은 내 가슴에서 완전히 떠나버렸다."], ["I know from terrible warnings I have noted in myself that my reason will not long remain unimpaired, but I solemnly declare that I am at this time in the possession of my right mind--that my memory is exact and circumstantial--and that I write the truth as I shall answer for these my last recorded words, whether they be ever read by men or not, at the Eternal Judgment-seat.", "내가 스스로에게서 알아챈 무서운 징후들로 미루어, 내 이성이 오래 온전하게 남아 있지는 못하리라는 것을 안다. 그러나 나는 엄숙히 선언하노니, 나는 지금 이 순간 제정신을 온전히 지니고 있으며 — 내 기억은 정확하고 상세하며 — 사람들이 이 글을 언젠가 읽든 읽지 않든, 나는 영원한 심판의 자리에서 마지막으로 기록하는 이 말들에 대해 책임지듯이 진실을 쓴다."]], [["“One cloudy moonlight night, in the third week of December (I think the twenty-second of the month) in the year 1757, I was walking on a retired part of the quay by the Seine for the refreshment of the frosty air, at an hour’s distance from my place of residence in the Street of the School of Medicine, when a carriage came along behind me, driven very fast.", "1757년 12월 셋째 주의 어느 흐린 달밤(아마 그달 22일이었던 듯하다), 나는 의과대학 거리에 있던 내 거처에서 한 시간쯤 떨어진, 쎈 강가의 인적 드문 부두를 차가운 공기로 머리를 식히려고 걷고 있었다. 그때 마차 한 대가 매우 빠르게 내 뒤를 따라왔다."], ["As I stood aside to let that carriage pass, apprehensive that it might otherwise run me down, a head was put out at the window, and a voice called to the driver to stop.", "자칫하면 치일까 염려되어 그 마차를 지나가게 하려고 비켜섰을 때, 창밖으로 머리 하나가 나오더니 어떤 목소리가 마부에게 멈추라고 외쳤다."]], [["“The carriage stopped as soon as the driver could rein in his horses, and the same voice called to me by my name.", "마부가 말고삐를 당길 수 있게 되자마자 마차는 멈췄고, 같은 목소리가 내 이름을 불렀다."], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했다."], ["The carriage was then so far in advance of me that two gentlemen had time to open the door and alight before I came up with it.", "그때 마차가 나보다 한참 앞서 있었던 터라, 내가 다가가기도 전에 두 신사가 문을 열고 내릴 시간이 있었다."]], [["“I observed that they were both wrapped in cloaks, and appeared to conceal themselves.", "나는 그들이 둘 다 망토를 두르고 있으며 신분을 감추려는 듯한 것을 알아챘다."], ["As they stood side by side near the carriage door, I also observed that they both looked of about my own age, or rather younger, and that they were greatly alike, in stature, manner, voice, and (as far as I could see) face too.", "그들이 마차 문 근처에 나란히 섰을 때, 나는 또한 그들이 둘 다 나와 비슷한 나이로, 아니 오히려 더 젊어 보였으며, 체격과 거동, 목소리, 그리고 (내가 볼 수 있는 한) 얼굴까지 무척 닮았다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“‘You are Doctor Manette?’ said one.", "'당신이 마네뜨 의사요?'"], ["“I am.”", "한 사람이 말했다."]], [["“‘Doctor Manette, formerly of Beauvais,’ said the other; ‘the young physician, originally an expert surgeon, who within the last year or two has made a rising reputation in Paris?’", "'그렇습니다.' '전에 보베에 살던 마네뜨 의사 말이오.' 다른 사람이 말했다. '본래 뛰어난 외과의였다가 근래 한두 해 사이에 빠리에서 명성이 높아진 젊은 의사 말이오?'"]], [["“‘Gentlemen,’ I returned, ‘I am that Doctor Manette of whom you speak so graciously.’", "'두 분.' 나는 대답했다. '제가 바로 두 분께서 그토록 정중하게 말씀하시는 그 마네뜨 의사입니다.'"]], [["“‘We have been to your residence,’ said the first, ‘and not being so fortunate as to find you there, and being informed that you were probably walking in this direction, we followed, in the hope of overtaking you.", "'우리는 당신 거처에 갔었소.' 첫번째 사람이 말했다."], ["Will you please to enter the carriage?’", "'그러나 운이 좋지 못해 거기서 당신을 만나지 못했고, 당신이 아마 이쪽으로 산책을 갔을 거라는 말을 듣고서, 당신을 따라잡을까 하여 뒤쫓아왔소. 마차에 좀 오르시겠소?'"]], [["“The manner of both was imperious, and they both moved, as these words were spoken, so as to place me between themselves and the carriage door.", "두 사람의 태도는 거만했고, 이 말이 오가는 동안 둘 다 나를 자신들과 마차 문 사이에 두도록 움직였다. 그들은 무장하고 있었다."], ["They were armed. I was not.", "나는 그렇지 못했다."]], [["“‘Gentlemen,’ said I, ‘pardon me; but I usually inquire who does me the honour to seek my assistance, and what is the nature of the case to which I am summoned.’", "'두 분.' 내가 말했다. '실례합니다만, 저는 으레 누가 영광스럽게도 제 도움을 청하는지, 그리고 제가 불려가는 환자의 상태가 어떤 것인지 여쭙곤 합니다.'"]], [["“The reply to this was made by him who had spoken second.", "이에 대한 대답은 두번째로 말했던 사람이 했다."], ["‘Doctor, your clients are people of condition.", "'의사 양반, 당신의 의뢰인은 지체 높은 사람들이오."], ["As to the nature of the case, our confidence in your skill assures us that you will ascertain it for yourself better than we can describe it. Enough.", "환자의 상태에 관해서는, 당신의 솜씨를 믿는 우리로서는 우리가 설명하는 것보다 당신 스스로 더 잘 알아낼 거라고 확신하오."], ["Will you please to enter the carriage?’", "그것으로 충분하오. 마차에 좀 오르시겠소?'"]], [["“I could do nothing but comply, and I entered it in silence.", "나는 따르는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었고, 잠자코 마차에 올랐다."], ["They both entered after me--the last springing in, after putting up the steps.", "두 사람도 내 뒤를 따라 올라탔는데 — 마지막 사람은 발판을 거둬들인 뒤 뛰어들었다."], ["The carriage turned about, and drove on at its former speed.", "마차는 방향을 돌려 아까의 속도로 달려갔다."]], [["“I repeat this conversation exactly as it occurred.", "나는 이 대화를 일어난 그대로 정확히 옮겨 적는다."], ["I have no doubt that it is, word for word, the same.", "한마디 한마디가 그대로라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["I describe everything exactly as it took place, constraining my mind not to wander from the task.", "나는 모든 일을 일어난 그대로 정확히 묘사하며, 내 정신이 이 일에서 벗어나지 않도록 다잡는다."], ["Where I make the broken marks that follow here, I leave off for the time, and put my paper in its hiding-place.", "여기 이어지는 끊어진 표시를 해둘 때, 나는 한동안 글쓰기를 멈추고 종이를 숨긴 곳에 넣어둔다."]], [["“The carriage left the streets behind, passed the North Barrier, and emerged upon the country road.", "마차는 거리를 뒤로하고, 북쪽 관문을 지나, 시골길로 나섰다."], ["At two-thirds of a league from the Barrier--I did not estimate the distance at that time, but afterwards when I traversed it--it struck out of the main avenue, and presently stopped at a solitary house, We all three alighted, and walked, by a damp soft footpath in a garden where a neglected fountain had overflowed, to the door of the house.", "관문에서 삼분의 이 리외쯤 떨어진 곳에서 — 당시에는 거리를 가늠하지 못했으나 후일 그 길을 지날 때 알았다 — 마차는 큰길에서 벗어나, 이윽고 외딴집 앞에 멈췄다. 우리 셋은 모두 내려, 버려진 분수가 넘쳐흐른 정원의 축축하고 무른 오솔길을 따라 집 문 앞까지 걸어갔다."], ["It was not opened immediately, in answer to the ringing of the bell, and one of my two conductors struck the man who opened it, with his heavy riding glove, across the face.", "종을 울렸으나 곧바로 문이 열리지 않았고, 나를 데려온 두 사람 중 하나가 문을 연 남자의 얼굴을 두꺼운 승마용 장갑으로 후려쳤다."]], [["“There was nothing in this action to attract my particular attention, for I had seen common people struck more commonly than dogs.", "이 행동에는 내 특별한 주의를 끌 만한 것이 없었으니, 나는 평민들이 개보다 더 예사로 얻어맞는 것을 보아왔기 때문이다."], ["But, the other of the two, being angry likewise, struck the man in like manner with his arm; the look and bearing of the brothers were then so exactly alike, that I then first perceived them to be twin brothers.", "그러나 다른 한 사람도 마찬가지로 화가 났는지 같은 식으로 팔을 휘둘러 그 남자를 쳤다. 그때 두 형제의 표정과 거동이 어찌나 똑같던지, 나는 비로소 그들이 쌍둥이 형제임을 알아챘다."]], [["“From the time of our alighting at the outer gate (which we found locked, and which one of the brothers had opened to admit us, and had relocked), I had heard cries proceeding from an upper chamber.", "바깥문(잠겨 있던 것을 형제 중 하나가 우리를 들이려고 열었다가 다시 잠갔다)에서 내린 때부터, 나는 위층 방에서 새어 나오는 비명을 듣고 있었다."], ["I was conducted to this chamber straight, the cries growing louder as we ascended the stairs, and I found a patient in a high fever of the brain, lying on a bed.", "나는 곧장 그 방으로 안내되었고, 계단을 올라갈수록 비명이 더 커졌으며, 나는 침대에 누운 채 뇌에 고열을 앓는 환자를 발견했다."]], [["“The patient was a woman of great beauty, and young; assuredly not much past twenty.", "환자는 무척 아름답고 젊은 여인으로, 분명 스무 살을 크게 넘기지 않았다."], ["Her hair was torn and ragged, and her arms were bound to her sides with sashes and handkerchiefs.", "머리카락은 쥐어뜯겨 헝클어져 있었고, 두 팔은 띠와 손수건으로 양옆에 묶여 있었다."], ["I noticed that these bonds were all portions of a gentleman’s dress.", "나는 이 묶은 것들이 모두 어느 신사의 의복 일부임을 알아챘다."], ["On one of them, which was a fringed scarf for a dress of ceremony, I saw the armorial bearings of a Noble, and the letter E.", "그중 하나는 예식용 의상에 두르는 술 달린 스카프였는데, 거기서 나는 어느 귀족의 문장(紋章)과 E라는 글자를 보았다."]], [["“I saw this, within the first minute of my contemplation of the patient; for, in her restless strivings she had turned over on her face on the edge of the bed, had drawn the end of the scarf into her mouth, and was in danger of suffocation.", "나는 환자를 살피기 시작한 지 한 일 분도 안 되어 이것을 보았다. 그녀가 불안하게 몸부림치다가 침대 가장자리에서 엎드린 자세로 돌아누우면서 스카프 끝을 입 안으로 끌어들여 질식할 위험에 처해 있었기 때문이다."], ["My first act was to put out my hand to relieve her breathing; and in moving the scarf aside, the embroidery in the corner caught my sight.", "내가 맨 처음 한 일은 그녀의 숨을 틔워주려고 손을 뻗은 것이었고, 스카프를 치우다가 그 한쪽 귀퉁이의 자수가 내 눈에 들어왔다."]], [["“I turned her gently over, placed my hands upon her breast to calm her and keep her down, and looked into her face. Her eyes were dilated and wild, and she constantly uttered piercing shrieks, and repeated the words, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ and then counted up to twelve, and said, ‘Hush!’ For an instant, and no more, she would pause to listen, and then the piercing shrieks would begin again, and she would repeat the cry, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ and would count up to twelve, and say, ‘Hush!’ There was no variation in the order, or the manner. There was no cessation, but the regular moment’s pause, in the utterance of these sounds.", "나는 그녀를 가만히 돌려 눕히고, 진정시켜 누워 있게 하려고 그녀의 가슴에 두 손을 얹고서 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그녀의 눈은 크게 벌어진 채 사납게 번득였고, 그녀는 쉴 새 없이 날카로운 비명을 지르며 '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 말을 되풀이하더니, 열둘까지 세고는 '쉿!' 하고 말했다. 그녀는 한순간, 그 이상은 아니게, 귀를 기울이려고 멈췄다가 다시 날카로운 비명을 지르기 시작했고, '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 외침을 되풀이하더니, 열둘까지 세고는 '쉿!' 하고 말했다. 그 순서나 방식에는 아무런 변화가 없었다. 규칙적인 한순간의 멈춤 말고는, 이 소리들의 발설에 끊김이 없었다."]], [["“‘How long,’ I asked, ‘has this lasted?’", "'이게 얼마나 오래되었습니까?' 나는 물었다."]], [["“To distinguish the brothers, I will call them the elder and the younger; by the elder, I mean him who exercised the most authority.", "두 형제를 구별하기 위해, 나는 그들을 형과 동생이라 부르겠다. 형이라 함은 가장 권위를 휘두른 자를 뜻한다."], ["It was the elder who replied, ‘Since about this hour last night.’", "대답한 것은 형이었다. '어젯밤 이맘때쯤부터요.'"]], [["“‘She has a husband, a father, and a brother?’", "'이 여인에게 남편과 아버지와 오라비가 있습니까?'"]], [["“‘A brother.’", "'오라비요.'"]], [["“‘I do not address her brother?’", "'제가 그 오라비분께 말씀드리는 게 아니지요?'"]], [["“He answered with great contempt, ‘No.’", "그는 몹시 경멸하는 투로 대답했다. '아니오.'"]], [["“‘She has some recent association with the number twelve?’", "'이 여인이 최근에 열둘이라는 숫자와 관련된 무슨 일을 겪었습니까?'"]], [["“The younger brother impatiently rejoined, ‘With twelve o’clock?’", "동생이 짜증스럽게 받아쳤다. '열두시 말이오?'"]], [["“‘See, gentlemen,’ said I, still keeping my hands upon her breast, ‘how useless I am, as you have brought me!", "'보십시오, 두 분.' 여전히 그녀의 가슴에 손을 얹은 채 내가 말했다."], ["If I had known what I was coming to see, I could have come provided.", "'두 분께서 저를 이렇게 데려오시니 제가 얼마나 쓸모없겠습니까! 제가 무엇을 보러 오는지 알았더라면 채비를 갖추고 왔을 겁니다."], ["As it is, time must be lost.", "이래서는 시간만 허비하게 됩니다."], ["There are no medicines to be obtained in this lonely place.’", "이 외딴곳에서는 약을 구할 수가 없습니다.'"]], [["“The elder brother looked to the younger, who said haughtily, ‘There is a case of medicines here;’ and brought it from a closet, and put it on the table.", "형이 동생을 바라보자, 동생이 거만하게 말했다. '여기 약상자가 하나 있소.' 그러고는 벽장에서 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["“I opened some of the bottles, smelt them, and put the stoppers to my lips.", "나는 병 몇 개를 열어 냄새를 맡고, 마개를 입술에 대보았다."], ["If I had wanted to use anything save narcotic medicines that were poisons in themselves, I would not have administered any of those.", "그 자체로 독이나 다름없는 마취제 말고 다른 무엇을 쓰려 했다면, 나는 그것들 중 어느 것도 쓰지 않았을 것이다."]], [["“‘Do you doubt them?’ asked the younger brother.", "'그것들을 의심하시오?' 동생이 물었다."]], [["“‘You see, monsieur, I am going to use them,’ I replied, and said no more.", "'보시다시피, 무슈, 저는 이것들을 쓰려는 참입니다.' 나는 대답하고, 더는 말하지 않았다."]], [["“I made the patient swallow, with great difficulty, and after many efforts, the dose that I desired to give.", "나는 무척 애를 쓰며 여러 차례 시도한 끝에, 내가 주고자 한 약을 환자에게 삼키게 했다."], ["As I intended to repeat it after a while, and as it was necessary to watch its influence, I then sat down by the side of the bed.", "얼마 후 다시 먹일 작정이었고, 그 약효를 지켜볼 필요가 있었으므로, 나는 그러고 나서 침대 곁에 앉았다."], ["There was a timid and suppressed woman in attendance (wife of the man down-stairs), who had retreated into a corner.", "시중드는 여자(아래층 남자의 아내)가 하나 있었는데, 소심하고 주눅 든 그녀는 한쪽 구석으로 물러나 있었다."], ["The house was damp and decayed, indifferently furnished--evidently, recently occupied and temporarily used.", "집은 축축하고 낡았으며, 가구도 변변치 않게 갖춰져 있어 — 누가 봐도 최근에 사람이 들어와 임시로 쓰는 곳이었다."], ["Some thick old hangings had been nailed up before the windows, to deaden the sound of the shrieks.", "비명 소리를 죽이려고 두꺼운 낡은 휘장 몇 개를 창문 앞에 못으로 박아두었다."], ["They continued to be uttered in their regular succession, with the cry, ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!’ the counting up to twelve, and ‘Hush!’ The frenzy was so violent, that I had not unfastened the bandages restraining the arms; but, I had looked to them, to see that they were not painful.", "비명은 여전히 규칙적으로 이어졌으니, '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!'라는 외침과, 열둘까지 세는 것과, '쉿!'이 되풀이되었다. 광란이 어찌나 격렬한지 나는 팔을 묶은 붕대를 풀지 못했다."], ["The only spark of encouragement in the case, was, that my hand upon the sufferer’s breast had this much soothing influence, that for minutes at a time it tranquillised the figure.", "다만 그것이 아프지 않도록 살펴는 두었다. 이 환자에게서 유일하게 희망의 불씨가 된 것은, 고통받는 그녀의 가슴에 얹은 내 손이 한 번에 몇 분씩 그 몸을 진정시키는, 그만한 달래는 효력이 있다는 점이었다."], ["It had no effect upon the cries; no pendulum could be more regular.", "그것은 비명에는 아무런 효과가 없었으니, 어떤 추도 그보다 규칙적일 수는 없었다."]], [["“For the reason that my hand had this effect (I assume), I had sat by the side of the bed for half an hour, with the two brothers looking on, before the elder said:", "내 손이 이러한 효력이 있는 까닭에(나는 그리 짐작한다), 나는 두 형제가 지켜보는 가운데 반시간 동안 침대 곁에 앉아 있었고, 그제야 형이 입을 열었다."]], [["“‘There is another patient.’", "'환자가 또 하나 있소.'"]], [["“I was startled, and asked, ‘Is it a pressing case?’", "나는 흠칫 놀라 물었다. '급한 경우입니까?'"]], [["“‘You had better see,’ he carelessly answered; and took up a light.", "'직접 보는 게 낫겠소.' 그는 아무렇지 않게 대답하고는, 등불을 집어 들었다."]], [["“The other patient lay in a back room across a second staircase, which was a species of loft over a stable.", "또 하나의 환자는 두번째 계단 건너편의 뒷방에 누워 있었는데, 그곳은 마구간 위의 다락 같은 곳이었다."], ["There was a low plastered ceiling to a part of it; the rest was open, to the ridge of the tiled roof, and there were beams across.", "그 일부에는 회칠한 낮은 천장이 있었고, 나머지는 기와지붕의 용마루까지 트여 있었으며, 가로질러 들보가 놓여 있었다."], ["Hay and straw were stored in that portion of the place, fagots for firing, and a heap of apples in sand.", "그곳 한쪽에는 건초와 짚이 쌓여 있었고, 땔감용 장작단과, 모래 속에 묻어둔 사과 더미가 있었다."], ["I had to pass through that part, to get at the other.", "다른 환자에게 가려면 나는 그 부분을 가로질러야 했다."], ["My memory is circumstantial and unshaken.", "내 기억은 상세하며 흔들림이 없다."], ["I try it with these details, and I see them all, in this my cell in the Bastille, near the close of the tenth year of my captivity, as I saw them all that night.", "나는 이 세부들로 그것을 시험해보거니와, 내가 갇혀 지낸 지 열 해째가 끝나갈 무렵 이 바스띠유의 감방에서, 그날 밤 보았던 것들을 모두 다시 본다."]], [["“On some hay on the ground, with a cushion thrown under his head, lay a handsome peasant boy--a boy of not more than seventeen at the most.", "바닥에 깔린 건초 위에, 머리 밑에 쿠션을 받친 채, 잘생긴 농민 소년 하나가 누워 있었으니 — 많아야 열일곱을 넘지 않은 소년이었다."], ["He lay on his back, with his teeth set, his right hand clenched on his breast, and his glaring eyes looking straight upward.", "그는 등을 대고 누워, 이를 악물고, 오른손을 가슴에 움켜쥔 채, 부릅뜬 눈으로 곧장 위쪽을 올려다보고 있었다."], ["I could not see where his wound was, as I kneeled on one knee over him; but, I could see that he was dying of a wound from a sharp point.", "나는 그 위에 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 앉아서도 상처가 어디인지 알 수 없었으나, 그가 날카로운 것에 찔린 상처로 죽어가고 있다는 것은 알 수 있었다."]], [["“‘I am a doctor, my poor fellow,’ said I.", "'나는 의사란다, 가엾은 녀석.' 내가 말했다."], ["‘Let me examine it.’", "'내가 상처를 좀 보자꾸나.'"]], [["“‘I do not want it examined,’ he answered; ‘let it be.’", "'보이고 싶지 않아요.' 그가 대답했다. '그냥 두세요.'"]], [["“It was under his hand, and I soothed him to let me move his hand away.", "상처는 그의 손 밑에 있었고, 나는 그를 달래어 그 손을 치우게 했다."], ["The wound was a sword-thrust, received from twenty to twenty-four hours before, but no skill could have saved him if it had been looked to without delay.", "상처는 스무 시간에서 스물네 시간 전쯤에 입은 칼에 찔린 자국이었는데, 지체 없이 손을 썼더라도 어떤 솜씨로도 그를 구하지는 못했을 것이다."], ["He was then dying fast.", "그때 그는 빠르게 죽어가고 있었다."], ["As I turned my eyes to the elder brother, I saw him looking down at this handsome boy whose life was ebbing out, as if he were a wounded bird, or hare, or rabbit; not at all as if he were a fellow-creature.", "내가 형에게로 눈길을 돌렸을 때, 나는 그가 생명이 꺼져가는 이 잘생긴 소년을, 마치 상처 입은 새나 산토끼나 집토끼라도 되는 듯이 — 결코 같은 인간이라고는 여기지 않는 듯이 내려다보고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“‘How has this been done, monsieur?’ said I.", "'무슈, 어쩌다 이렇게 되었습니까?' 내가 말했다."]], [["“‘A crazed young common dog!", "'미친 천한 강아지 새끼!"], ["A serf!", "농노 놈이!"], ["Forced my brother to draw upon him, and has fallen by my brother’s sword--like a gentleman.’", "내 동생이 칼을 뽑게 만들더니, 신사답게 — 내 동생의 칼에 쓰러진 거요.'"]], [["“There was no touch of pity, sorrow, or kindred humanity, in this answer.", "이 대답에는 연민이나 슬픔, 혹은 같은 인간으로서의 인정이라곤 한 점도 없었다."], ["The speaker seemed to acknowledge that it was inconvenient to have that different order of creature dying there, and that it would have been better if he had died in the usual obscure routine of his vermin kind.", "말하는 자는 저 다른 부류의 생물이 거기서 죽어가는 것이 성가신 일임을 시인하는 듯했고, 그 해충 같은 족속이 늘 그러듯 이름 없이 죽어갔더라면 더 나았으리라는 투였다."], ["He was quite incapable of any compassionate feeling about the boy, or about his fate.", "그는 그 소년이나 그 운명에 대해 어떤 동정심도 느낄 능력이 전혀 없었다."]], [["“The boy’s eyes had slowly moved to him as he had spoken, and they now slowly moved to me.", "그가 말하는 동안 소년의 눈은 천천히 그에게로 옮겨 갔고, 이제 천천히 나에게로 옮겨 왔다."]], [["“‘Doctor, they are very proud, these Nobles; but we common dogs are proud too, sometimes.", "'의사 선생님, 이 귀족들은 무척 거만해요. 하지만 우리 천한 강아지들도 때로는 자존심이 있어요."], ["They plunder us, outrage us, beat us, kill us; but we have a little pride left, sometimes.", "저들은 우리를 약탈하고, 능욕하고, 때리고, 죽이지만, 우리에게도 때로는 작은 자존심이 남아 있어요."], ["She--have you seen her, Doctor?’", "누이를 — 선생님, 누이를 보셨어요?'"]], [["“The shrieks and the cries were audible there, though subdued by the distance.", "거리 탓에 잦아들기는 했어도 비명과 외침은 거기서도 들렸다."], ["He referred to them, as if she were lying in our presence.", "그는 마치 그녀가 우리 앞에 누워 있기라도 한 듯 그 소리들을 가리켰다."]], [["“I said, ‘I have seen her.’", "나는 말했다. '보았단다.'"]], [["“‘She is my sister, Doctor.", "'제 누이예요, 선생님."], ["They have had their shameful rights, these Nobles, in the modesty and virtue of our sisters, many years, but we have had good girls among us.", "이 귀족들은 여러 해 동안 우리 누이들의 정숙함과 정절을 두고 그 수치스러운 권리를 누려왔지만, 우리 중에도 착한 처녀들이 있었어요."], ["I know it, and have heard my father say so.", "저는 알아요, 아버지가 그렇게 말씀하시는 것도 들었고요."], ["She was a good girl.", "누이는 착한 처녀였어요."], ["She was betrothed to a good young man, too: a tenant of his.", "좋은 청년과 약혼도 했고요. 저 사람의 소작인이었지요."], ["We were all tenants of his--that man’s who stands there.", "우리는 모두 저 사람의 — 저기 서 있는 저 사람의 소작인이었어요."], ["The other is his brother, the worst of a bad race.’", "다른 한 사람은 그 동생인데, 나쁜 족속 중에서도 가장 나쁜 자예요.'"]], [["“It was with the greatest difficulty that the boy gathered bodily force to speak; but, his spirit spoke with a dreadful emphasis.", "소년이 말할 기력을 몸에서 그러모으는 것은 더없이 힘겨운 일이었으나, 그의 정신은 무서운 힘을 실어 말했다."]], [["“‘We were so robbed by that man who stands there, as all we common dogs are by those superior Beings--taxed by him without mercy, obliged to work for him without pay, obliged to grind our corn at his mill, obliged to feed scores of his tame birds on our wretched crops, and forbidden for our lives to keep a single tame bird of our own, pillaged and plundered to that degree that when we chanced to have a bit of meat, we ate it in fear, with the door barred and the shutters closed, that his people should not see it and take it from us--I say, we were so robbed, and hunted, and were made so poor, that our father told us it was a dreadful thing to bring a child into the world, and that what we should most pray for, was, that our women might be barren and our miserable race die out!’", "'우리는 저기 서 있는 저 사람한테 어찌나 수탈당했는지, 우리 천한 강아지들이 다 그 잘난 존재들한테 당하듯이 말이에요 — 저 사람한테 사정없이 세금을 뜯기고, 삯도 없이 그를 위해 일해야 했고, 우리 곡식을 그의 방앗간에서 빻아야 했고, 우리의 보잘것없는 수확으로 그가 키우는 길든 새 수십 마리를 먹여야 했고, 우리 자신은 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 길든 새 한 마리조차 기르는 게 금지되었어요. 어쩌다 고깃점이라도 생기면 그의 사람들이 보고 빼앗아 갈까봐 문을 걸어 잠그고 덧창을 닫은 채 두려움에 떨며 먹어야 할 만큼 약탈당하고 노략질당했어요 — 말하자면, 우리는 어찌나 수탈당하고 쫓기고 가난해졌는지, 아버지는 아이를 세상에 낳는 것이 끔찍한 일이라고, 우리가 무엇보다 빌어야 할 것은 우리 여자들이 아이를 못 배고 우리 비참한 족속이 절멸하는 것이라고 말씀하셨어요!'"]], [["“I had never before seen the sense of being oppressed, bursting forth like a fire.", "나는 억압받는다는 느낌이 불길처럼 터져 나오는 것을 그전에는 본 적이 없었다."], ["I had supposed that it must be latent in the people somewhere; but, I had never seen it break out, until I saw it in the dying boy.", "그것이 사람들 어딘가에 잠재해 있으리라고는 짐작했으나, 그것이 터져 나오는 것은, 죽어가는 그 소년에게서 보기 전까지는 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“‘Nevertheless, Doctor, my sister married.", "'그래도 선생님, 제 누이는 결혼했어요."], ["He was ailing at that time, poor fellow, and she married her lover, that she might tend and comfort him in our cottage--our dog-hut, as that man would call it.", "그 가엾은 사람은 그때 병을 앓고 있었는데, 누이는 자기 연인과 결혼해서 우리 오두막에서 — 저 사람 같으면 개집이라 부를 그곳에서 — 그를 돌보고 위로해주려 했어요."], ["She had not been married many weeks, when that man’s brother saw her and admired her, and asked that man to lend her to him--for what are husbands among us!", "결혼한 지 몇 주 되지도 않았는데, 저 사람의 동생이 누이를 보고 탐을 내더니, 저 사람한테 누이를 자기에게 빌려달라고 했어요 — 우리들 사이에서 남편이 무슨 대수겠어요!"], ["He was willing enough, but my sister was good and virtuous, and hated his brother with a hatred as strong as mine.", "저 사람은 기꺼이 응했지만, 제 누이는 착하고 정숙했으며, 저 사람의 동생을 저만큼이나 강하게 미워했어요."], ["What did the two then, to persuade her husband to use his influence with her, to make her willing?’", "그러자 그 두 사람은 누이의 남편을 부추겨 누이에게 영향력을 행사하게 해서, 누이가 순순히 따르도록 만들려고 무슨 짓을 했을까요?'"]], [["“The boy’s eyes, which had been fixed on mine, slowly turned to the looker-on, and I saw in the two faces that all he said was true.", "내 눈에 박혀 있던 소년의 눈이 천천히 그 구경꾼에게로 돌아갔고, 나는 그 두 얼굴에서 그가 한 말이 모두 진실임을 보았다."], ["The two opposing kinds of pride confronting one another, I can see, even in this Bastille; the gentleman’s, all negligent indifference; the peasant’s, all trodden-down sentiment, and passionate revenge.", "서로 맞선 두 종류의 자존심이 마주하고 있는 것을, 나는 이 바스띠유에서조차 볼 수 있다. 신사의 자존심은 온통 무심한 무관심이었고, 농민의 자존심은 온통 짓밟힌 감정과 격렬한 복수심이었다."]], [["“‘You know, Doctor, that it is among the Rights of these Nobles to harness us common dogs to carts, and drive us.", "'선생님도 아시다시피, 이 귀족들의 권리 중에는 우리 천한 강아지들을 수레에 매어 몰고 다니는 것도 있어요."], ["They so harnessed him and drove him.", "저들은 그렇게 그를 수레에 매어 몰았어요."], ["You know that it is among their Rights to keep us in their grounds all night, quieting the frogs, in order that their noble sleep may not be disturbed.", "또 저들의 고귀한 잠이 방해받지 않도록, 밤새 우리를 자기네 영지에 붙들어두고 개구리를 조용히 시키게 하는 것도 그 권리 중 하나라는 걸 아시잖아요."], ["They kept him out in the unwholesome mists at night, and ordered him back into his harness in the day.", "저들은 밤이면 그를 해로운 안개 속에 붙들어두고, 낮이면 다시 멍에를 메라고 명령했어요."], ["But he was not persuaded. No!", "하지만 그는 설복당하지 않았어요."], ["Taken out of harness one day at noon, to feed--if he could find food--he sobbed twelve times, once for every stroke of the bell, and died on her bosom.’", "아니에요! 어느 날 정오에 끼니를 — 먹을 것을 구할 수 있다면 — 먹으라고 멍에에서 풀려났을 때, 그는 종이 울릴 때마다 한 번씩, 열두 번을 흐느끼고는 누이의 품에서 죽었어요.'"]], [["“Nothing human could have held life in the boy but his determination to tell all his wrong.", "자신이 당한 부당함을 모두 털어놓겠다는 결심 말고는 어떤 인간적인 것도 그 소년에게 생명을 붙들어둘 수 없었을 것이다."], ["He forced back the gathering shadows of death, as he forced his clenched right hand to remain clenched, and to cover his wound.", "그는 움켜쥔 오른손을 그대로 움켜쥔 채 상처를 덮고 있게 하듯이, 몰려드는 죽음의 그림자를 밀어냈다."]], [["“‘Then, with that man’s permission and even with his aid, his brother took her away; in spite of what I know she must have told his brother--and what that is, will not be long unknown to you, Doctor, if it is now--his brother took her away--for his pleasure and diversion, for a little while.", "'그러고 나서, 저 사람의 허락하에, 아니 거들기까지 하는 가운데, 저 사람의 동생이 누이를 데려갔어요 — 누이가 그 동생에게 틀림없이 말했을 것임을 제가 아는데도요 — 그게 무엇인지는, 선생님이 지금 모르신다 해도 머지않아 아시게 될 거예요 — 저 사람의 동생은 누이를 데려갔어요 — 잠시 동안, 자기 쾌락과 소일거리로요."], ["I saw her pass me on the road.", "저는 길에서 누이가 제 곁을 지나가는 것을 보았어요."], ["When I took the tidings home, our father’s heart burst; he never spoke one of the words that filled it.", "제가 그 소식을 집에 전했을 때, 아버지의 심장은 터져버렸어요. 그 심장에 가득 찬 말을 아버지는 한마디도 하지 못하셨어요."], ["I took my young sister (for I have another) to a place beyond the reach of this man, and where, at least, she will never be his vassal.", "저는 어린 누이를(또 하나가 있거든요) 이 사람의 손이 닿지 않는, 그리고 적어도 결코 그자의 종이 되지는 않을 곳으로 데려갔어요."], ["Then, I tracked the brother here, and last night climbed in--a common dog, but sword in hand.--Where is the loft window?", "그러고 나서, 저는 그 동생을 여기까지 뒤쫓아, 어젯밤에 기어 올라왔어요 — 천한 강아지지만, 칼을 손에 들고요. 다락 창이 어디예요?"], ["It was somewhere here?’", "여기 어디쯤이었는데?'"]], [["“The room was darkening to his sight; the world was narrowing around him.", "방이 그의 시야에서 어두워지고 있었고, 세계가 그의 주위로 좁아들고 있었다."], ["I glanced about me, and saw that the hay and straw were trampled over the floor, as if there had been a struggle.", "나는 둘레를 둘러보았고, 건초와 짚이 바닥 여기저기 밟혀 흩어져 있는 것이, 마치 몸싸움이라도 있었던 듯한 것을 보았다."]], [["“‘She heard me, and ran in.", "'누이가 제 소리를 듣고 달려 들어왔어요."], ["I told her not to come near us till he was dead.", "저는 그가 죽기 전에는 우리 가까이 오지 말라고 일렀어요."], ["He came in and first tossed me some pieces of money; then struck at me with a whip.", "그가 들어와서는 먼저 제게 동전 몇 닢을 던지더니, 채찍으로 저를 후려쳤어요."], ["But I, though a common dog, so struck at him as to make him draw.", "하지만 저는, 천한 강아지일망정, 그가 칼을 뽑게 만들 만큼 그를 후려쳤어요."], ["Let him break into as many pieces as he will, the sword that he stained with my common blood; he drew to defend himself--thrust at me with all his skill for his life.’", "제 천한 피로 더럽힌 그 칼이, 산산조각 나든 말든 마음대로 하라죠. 그는 제 몸을 지키려고 칼을 뽑았어요 — 제 목숨을 노리고 온 솜씨를 다해 저를 찔렀어요.'"]], [["“My glance had fallen, but a few moments before, on the fragments of a broken sword, lying among the hay.", "내 눈길은 불과 조금 전에, 건초 사이에 놓인 부러진 칼의 조각들에 가닿은 적이 있었다."], ["That weapon was a gentleman’s.", "그 무기는 신사의 것이었다."], ["In another place, lay an old sword that seemed to have been a soldier’s.", "다른 자리에는, 군인의 것이었던 듯한 낡은 칼 한 자루가 놓여 있었다."]], [["“‘Now, lift me up, Doctor; lift me up.", "'이제 저를 일으켜주세요, 선생님. 저를 일으켜주세요."], ["Where is he?’", "그자는 어디 있어요?'"]], [["“‘He is not here,’ I said, supporting the boy, and thinking that he referred to the brother.", "'그는 여기 없단다.' 나는 그 소년을 부축하며, 그가 동생을 두고 말하는 줄로 여기며 말했다."]], [["“‘He!", "'그자요!"], ["Proud as these nobles are, he is afraid to see me.", "이 귀족들이 아무리 거만해도, 그자는 저를 보기가 두려운 거예요."], ["Where is the man who was here?", "여기 있던 사람은 어디 있어요?"], ["Turn my face to him.’", "제 얼굴을 그쪽으로 돌려주세요.'"]], [["“I did so, raising the boy’s head against my knee.", "나는 그리해주었고, 소년의 머리를 내 무릎에 기대게 했다."], ["But, invested for the moment with extraordinary power, he raised himself completely: obliging me to rise too, or I could not have still supported him.", "그러나 한순간 비상한 힘을 얻은 그는 완전히 몸을 일으켰다. 나도 따라 일어서지 않을 수 없게 만들었으니, 그러지 않았다면 나는 그를 계속 부축할 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“‘Marquis,’ said the boy, turned to him with his eyes opened wide, and his right hand raised, ‘in the days when all these things are to be answered for, I summon you and yours, to the last of your bad race, to answer for them.", "'후작.' 소년은 눈을 크게 뜨고 오른손을 치켜든 채 그를 향해 말했다."], ["I mark this cross of blood upon you, as a sign that I do it.", "'이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날이 오면, 나는 당신과 당신의 일족을, 당신의 나쁜 족속이 마지막 하나까지, 그 책임을 물으려 불러내겠소."], ["In the days when all these things are to be answered for, I summon your brother, the worst of the bad race, to answer for them separately.", "내가 그리한다는 표시로, 나는 당신에게 이 피의 십자가를 새기오. 이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날이 오면, 나는 당신의 동생을, 그 나쁜 족속 중에서도 가장 나쁜 자를, 따로 그 책임을 물으려 불러내겠소."], ["I mark this cross of blood upon him, as a sign that I do it.’", "내가 그리한다는 표시로, 나는 그에게 이 피의 십자가를 새기오.'"]], [["“Twice, he put his hand to the wound in his breast, and with his forefinger drew a cross in the air.", "두 번, 그는 가슴의 상처에 손을 댔다가, 집게손가락으로 허공에 십자가를 그었다."], ["He stood for an instant with the finger yet raised, and as it dropped, he dropped with it, and I laid him down dead.", "그는 손가락을 아직 치켜든 채 한순간 서 있었고, 그것이 떨어지자 그도 함께 쓰러졌으며, 나는 죽은 그를 뉘어주었다."]], [["“When I returned to the bedside of the young woman, I found her raving in precisely the same order of continuity.", "내가 그 젊은 여인의 침대 곁으로 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그녀가 정확히 똑같은 순서로 이어가며 헛소리하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["I knew that this might last for many hours, and that it would probably end in the silence of the grave.", "나는 이것이 여러 시간 이어질 수 있으며, 아마 무덤의 침묵으로 끝나리라는 것을 알았다."]], [["“I repeated the medicines I had given her, and I sat at the side of the bed until the night was far advanced.", "나는 그녀에게 주었던 약을 다시 먹였고, 밤이 깊도록 침대 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["She never abated the piercing quality of her shrieks, never stumbled in the distinctness or the order of her words.", "그녀는 비명의 날카로움을 결코 누그러뜨리지 않았고, 그 말들의 또렷함이나 순서에서 결코 더듬거리지 않았다."], ["They were always ‘My husband, my father, and my brother!", "그것은 언제나 '내 남편, 내 아버지, 그리고 내 오라비!"], ["One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten, eleven, twelve.", "하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯, 일곱, 여덟, 아홉, 열, 열하나, 열둘."], ["Hush!’", "쉿!'이었다."]], [["“This lasted twenty-six hours from the time when I first saw her.", "이것은 내가 그녀를 처음 본 때로부터 스물여섯 시간 동안 이어졌다."], ["I had come and gone twice, and was again sitting by her, when she began to falter.", "나는 두 번 오갔으며, 다시 그녀 곁에 앉아 있을 때, 그녀가 차츰 약해지기 시작했다."], ["I did what little could be done to assist that opportunity, and by-and-bye she sank into a lethargy, and lay like the dead.", "나는 그 고비를 거들기 위해 할 수 있는 얼마 안 되는 일을 했고, 이윽고 그녀는 혼수상태에 빠져 죽은 사람처럼 누워 있었다."]], [["“It was as if the wind and rain had lulled at last, after a long and fearful storm.", "마치 길고 무서운 폭풍이 지난 끝에 바람과 비가 마침내 잦아든 것 같았다."], ["I released her arms, and called the woman to assist me to compose her figure and the dress she had torn.", "나는 그녀의 팔을 풀어주고, 여자를 불러 그녀의 몸가짐과 그녀가 찢어놓은 옷을 추스르는 것을 돕게 했다."], ["It was then that I knew her condition to be that of one in whom the first expectations of being a mother have arisen; and it was then that I lost the little hope I had had of her.", "바로 그때 나는 그녀의 상태가, 어머니가 되리라는 첫 기대가 막 생겨난 여인의 것임을 알았다. 그리고 바로 그때 나는 그녀에게 품었던 얼마 안 되는 희망마저 잃었다."]], [["“‘Is she dead?’ asked the Marquis, whom I will still describe as the elder brother, coming booted into the room from his horse.", "'죽었소?' 후작이 — 나는 그를 여전히 형이라 일컫겠다 — 말을 타고 와서 부츠를 신은 채 방으로 들어서며 물었다."]], [["“‘Not dead,’ said I; ‘but like to die.’", "'죽지는 않았습니다.' 내가 말했다. '하지만 죽을 것 같습니다.'"]], [["“‘What strength there is in these common bodies!’ he said, looking down at her with some curiosity.", "'이 천한 몸뚱이에 무슨 그런 기력이 있담!' 그는 얼마간 호기심 어린 눈으로 그녀를 내려다보며 말했다."]], [["“‘There is prodigious strength,’ I answered him, ‘in sorrow and despair.’", "'슬픔과 절망에는.' 나는 그에게 대답했다. '놀라운 기력이 있지요.'"]], [["“He first laughed at my words, and then frowned at them.", "그는 처음에는 내 말에 웃더니, 이내 그 말에 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["He moved a chair with his foot near to mine, ordered the woman away, and said in a subdued voice,", "그는 발로 의자를 내 곁으로 끌어당기고, 여자에게 물러가라 명한 뒤, 낮춘 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“‘Doctor, finding my brother in this difficulty with these hinds, I recommended that your aid should be invited.", "'의사 양반, 내 동생이 이 촌놈들 때문에 이런 곤란을 겪는 것을 알고서, 나는 당신의 도움을 청하라고 권했소."], ["Your reputation is high, and, as a young man with your fortune to make, you are probably mindful of your interest.", "당신의 명성은 높고, 또 앞날을 일궈가야 할 젊은이로서 당신은 아마 자기 이익에 마음을 쓰겠지."], ["The things that you see here, are things to be seen, and not spoken of.’", "여기서 당신이 본 것들은, 보기만 하고 입에 담아서는 안 될 것들이오.'"]], [["“I listened to the patient’s breathing, and avoided answering.", "나는 환자의 숨소리에 귀를 기울이며, 대답을 피했다."]], [["“‘Do you honour me with your attention, Doctor?’", "'내 말에 주의를 기울여주시겠소, 의사 양반?'"]], [["“‘Monsieur,’ said I, ‘in my profession, the communications of patients are always received in confidence.’ I was guarded in my answer, for I was troubled in my mind with what I had heard and seen.", "'무슈.' 내가 말했다. '제 직업에서, 환자의 이야기는 언제나 비밀로 받아들여집니다.' 나는 내가 듣고 본 것으로 마음이 어지러웠으므로, 대답에 신중을 기했다."]], [["“Her breathing was so difficult to trace, that I carefully tried the pulse and the heart.", "그녀의 숨은 너무도 미약하여 좇기 어려웠으므로, 나는 조심스럽게 맥박과 심장을 짚어보았다."], ["There was life, and no more.", "생명이 있기는 했으나, 그 이상은 아니었다."], ["Looking round as I resumed my seat, I found both the brothers intent upon me.", "자리에 다시 앉으며 둘레를 둘러보았을 때, 나는 두 형제가 나를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I write with so much difficulty, the cold is so severe, I am so fearful of being detected and consigned to an underground cell and total darkness, that I must abridge this narrative.", "나는 너무도 힘겹게 이 글을 쓰고 있으며, 추위는 너무도 혹독하고, 발각되어 지하 감방의 완전한 어둠 속에 처박힐까봐 너무도 두려운 나머지, 이 이야기를 줄여야겠다."], ["There is no confusion or failure in my memory; it can recall, and could detail, every word that was ever spoken between me and those brothers.", "내 기억에는 아무런 혼란도 결함도 없으니, 나와 그 형제들 사이에 오간 한마디 한마디를 떠올릴 수 있고, 또 낱낱이 적어낼 수도 있다."]], [["“She lingered for a week.", "그녀는 일주일을 더 버텼다."], ["Towards the last, I could understand some few syllables that she said to me, by placing my ear close to her lips.", "마지막에 이르러, 나는 그녀의 입술에 귀를 바싹 대고서 그녀가 내게 하는 몇 음절을 알아들을 수 있었다."], ["She asked me where she was, and I told her; who I was, and I told her.", "그녀는 내게 자기가 어디에 있는지 물었고, 나는 일러주었다. 내가 누구인지 물었고, 나는 일러주었다."], ["It was in vain that I asked her for her family name.", "그녀에게 가문의 이름을 물어본 것은 헛일이었다."], ["She faintly shook her head upon the pillow, and kept her secret, as the boy had done.", "그녀는 베개에 머리를 댄 채 희미하게 가로저으며, 소년이 그러했듯이 자기 비밀을 지켰다."]], [["“I had no opportunity of asking her any question, until I had told the brothers she was sinking fast, and could not live another day.", "내가 형제들에게 그녀가 빠르게 가라앉고 있으며 하루도 더 살지 못할 것이라고 말하기 전까지는, 그녀에게 어떤 질문도 할 기회가 없었다."], ["Until then, though no one was ever presented to her consciousness save the woman and myself, one or other of them had always jealously sat behind the curtain at the head of the bed when I was there.", "그때까지는, 그녀의 의식에 떠오른 사람이라곤 그 여자와 나뿐이었음에도, 내가 거기 있을 때면 그 둘 중 하나가 늘 시샘하듯 침대 머리맡의 휘장 뒤에 앉아 있었다."], ["But when it came to that, they seemed careless what communication I might hold with her; as if--the thought passed through my mind--I were dying too.", "그러나 그 지경에 이르자, 그들은 내가 그녀와 무슨 이야기를 나누든 개의치 않는 듯했으니, 마치 — 이런 생각이 내 머리를 스쳤다 — 나 또한 죽어가고 있다는 듯이."]], [["“I always observed that their pride bitterly resented the younger brother’s (as I call him) having crossed swords with a peasant, and that peasant a boy.", "나는 그들의 자존심이, 동생(내가 그렇게 부르는 자)이 농민과, 그것도 소년인 농민과 칼을 맞댔다는 사실을 쓰라리게 분개한다는 것을 늘 느꼈다."], ["The only consideration that appeared to affect the mind of either of them was the consideration that this was highly degrading to the family, and was ridiculous.", "그 둘 중 어느 쪽의 마음에든 영향을 미치는 것으로 보인 유일한 고려는, 이것이 가문에 몹시 수치스러운 일이며 가소로운 일이라는 고려뿐이었다."], ["As often as I caught the younger brother’s eyes, their expression reminded me that he disliked me deeply, for knowing what I knew from the boy.", "동생의 눈과 마주칠 때마다, 그 표정은 내가 소년에게서 알게 된 것을 알고 있다는 이유로 그가 나를 깊이 싫어한다는 것을 일깨워주었다."], ["He was smoother and more polite to me than the elder; but I saw this.", "그는 형보다 내게 더 부드럽고 더 공손했으나, 나는 이것을 보았다."], ["I also saw that I was an incumbrance in the mind of the elder, too.", "나는 또한 형의 마음에도 내가 거추장스러운 존재임을 보았다."]], [["“My patient died, two hours before midnight--at a time, by my watch, answering almost to the minute when I had first seen her.", "내 환자는 자정 두 시간 전에 죽었다 — 내 시계로는, 내가 그녀를 처음 본 순간과 거의 분 단위까지 일치하는 때였다."], ["I was alone with her, when her forlorn young head drooped gently on one side, and all her earthly wrongs and sorrows ended.", "그녀의 처량한 젊은 머리가 한쪽으로 가만히 떨구어지며 그녀의 이승의 모든 부당함과 슬픔이 끝났을 때, 나는 그녀와 단둘이 있었다."]], [["“The brothers were waiting in a room down-stairs, impatient to ride away.", "형제들은 아래층 방에서 어서 말을 타고 떠나고 싶어 안달하며 기다리고 있었다."], ["I had heard them, alone at the bedside, striking their boots with their riding-whips, and loitering up and down.", "나는 침대 곁에 홀로 있으면서, 그들이 승마용 채찍으로 부츠를 치며 이리저리 서성이는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“‘At last she is dead?’ said the elder, when I went in.", "'마침내 죽었소?' 내가 들어가자 형이 말했다."]], [["“‘She is dead,’ said I.", "'죽었습니다.' 내가 말했다."]], [["“‘I congratulate you, my brother,’ were his words as he turned round.", "'축하하네, 아우.' 그가 돌아서며 한 말이었다."]], [["“He had before offered me money, which I had postponed taking.", "그는 앞서 내게 돈을 내밀었으나, 나는 받기를 미뤘었다."], ["He now gave me a rouleau of gold.", "그가 이제 금화 한 두루마리를 주었다."], ["I took it from his hand, but laid it on the table.", "나는 그의 손에서 그것을 받았으나, 탁자 위에 내려놓았다."], ["I had considered the question, and had resolved to accept nothing.", "나는 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해보았고, 아무것도 받지 않기로 마음먹었던 것이다."]], [["“‘Pray excuse me,’ said I.", "'부디 양해해주십시오.' 내가 말했다."], ["‘Under the circumstances, no.’", "'이런 사정에서는, 받지 않겠습니다.'"]], [["“They exchanged looks, but bent their heads to me as I bent mine to them, and we parted without another word on either side.", "그들은 서로 눈길을 주고받았으나, 내가 그들에게 고개를 숙이자 그들도 내게 고개를 숙였고, 우리는 어느 쪽도 더는 한마디 없이 헤어졌다."]], [["“I am weary, weary, weary--worn down by misery.", "나는 지쳤다, 지쳤다, 지쳤다 — 비참함에 짓눌려 기진했다."], ["I cannot read what I have written with this gaunt hand.", "이 수척한 손으로 쓴 것을 나는 읽어낼 수가 없다."]], [["“Early in the morning, the rouleau of gold was left at my door in a little box, with my name on the outside.", "이튿날 이른 아침, 금화 두루마리가 겉에 내 이름이 적힌 작은 상자에 담겨 내 집 문 앞에 놓여 있었다."], ["From the first, I had anxiously considered what I ought to do.", "나는 처음부터 내가 어찌해야 할지 노심초사 궁리했다."], ["I decided, that day, to write privately to the Minister, stating the nature of the two cases to which I had been summoned, and the place to which I had gone: in effect, stating all the circumstances.", "나는 그날, 내가 불려갔던 두 환자의 사정과 내가 갔던 장소를 — 요컨대 모든 정황을 적어 장관에게 사적으로 편지를 쓰기로 결심했다."], ["I knew what Court influence was, and what the immunities of the Nobles were, and I expected that the matter would never be heard of; but, I wished to relieve my own mind.", "나는 궁정의 영향력이 어떤 것인지, 귀족들의 면책특권이 어떤 것인지 알았으므로, 그 일이 결코 거론되지 않으리라 예상했다. 그러나 나는 내 마음의 짐을 덜고 싶었다."], ["I had kept the matter a profound secret, even from my wife; and this, too, I resolved to state in my letter.", "나는 그 일을 아내에게조차 철저히 비밀로 해두었는데, 이것 또한 내 편지에 적기로 마음먹었다."], ["I had no apprehension whatever of my real danger; but I was conscious that there might be danger for others, if others were compromised by possessing the knowledge that I possessed.", "나 자신의 진짜 위험에 대해서는 아무런 염려도 없었다. 그러나 내가 지닌 지식을 다른 이들이 알게 되어 연루된다면, 그들에게 위험이 닥칠 수 있음은 의식하고 있었다."]], [["“I was much engaged that day, and could not complete my letter that night.", "그날 나는 일이 많아, 그날 밤 편지를 끝내지 못했다."], ["I rose long before my usual time next morning to finish it.", "이튿날 아침 나는 그것을 마무리하려고 평소보다 한참 일찍 일어났다."], ["It was the last day of the year.", "그날은 그해의 마지막 날이었다."], ["The letter was lying before me just completed, when I was told that a lady waited, who wished to see me.", "막 다 쓴 편지가 내 앞에 놓여 있을 때, 어떤 부인이 나를 보고자 기다리고 있다는 전갈을 받았다."]], [["“I am growing more and more unequal to the task I have set myself.", "나는 내가 스스로에게 부과한 일을 점점 더 감당하지 못하게 되어간다."], ["It is so cold, so dark, my senses are so benumbed, and the gloom upon me is so dreadful.", "너무도 춥고, 너무도 어두우며, 내 감각은 너무도 무뎌졌고, 나를 짓누르는 우울은 너무도 끔찍하다."]], [["“The lady was young, engaging, and handsome, but not marked for long life.", "그 부인은 젊고, 매력적이며, 아름다웠으나, 오래 살 사람으로는 보이지 않았다."], ["She was in great agitation.", "그녀는 몹시 동요하고 있었다."], ["She presented herself to me as the wife of the Marquis St. Evrémonde.", "그녀는 자신을 쌩떼브레몽드 후작 부인이라고 소개했다."], ["I connected the title by which the boy had addressed the elder brother, with the initial letter embroidered on the scarf, and had no difficulty in arriving at the conclusion that I had seen that nobleman very lately.", "나는 소년이 형을 부를 때 쓴 그 칭호를 스카프에 수놓인 머리글자와 연결지었고, 어렵지 않게 내가 바로 얼마 전에 그 귀족을 보았다는 결론에 이르렀다."]], [["“My memory is still accurate, but I cannot write the words of our conversation.", "내 기억은 여전히 정확하나, 우리가 나눈 대화의 말들을 나는 적을 수가 없다."], ["I suspect that I am watched more closely than I was, and I know not at what times I may be watched.", "내가 전보다 더 면밀히 감시당하고 있는 듯하고, 언제 감시당할지 알 수 없기 때문이다."], ["She had in part suspected, and in part discovered, the main facts of the cruel story, of her husband’s share in it, and my being resorted to.", "그녀는 그 잔혹한 이야기의 주요 사실들을, 그리고 거기에 자기 남편이 가담한 것과 내가 불려간 것을, 일부는 짐작했고 일부는 알아냈다."], ["She did not know that the girl was dead.", "그녀는 그 처녀가 죽은 것은 몰랐다."], ["Her hope had been, she said in great distress, to show her, in secret, a woman’s sympathy.", "그녀의 바람은, 몹시 괴로워하며 말하기를, 은밀히 그 처녀에게 한 여인의 동정을 보이려는 것이었다고 했다."], ["Her hope had been to avert the wrath of Heaven from a House that had long been hateful to the suffering many.", "그녀의 바람은, 고통받는 많은 이들에게 오랫동안 미움받아온 한 가문에서 하늘의 진노를 돌려놓으려는 것이었다."]], [["“She had reasons for believing that there was a young sister living, and her greatest desire was, to help that sister.", "그녀에게는 살아 있는 어린 누이가 있다고 믿을 만한 근거가 있었고, 그녀의 가장 큰 소망은 그 누이를 돕는 것이었다."], ["I could tell her nothing but that there was such a sister; beyond that, I knew nothing.", "나는 그런 누이가 있다는 것 말고는 그녀에게 아무것도 일러줄 수 없었다."], ["Her inducement to come to me, relying on my confidence, had been the hope that I could tell her the name and place of abode.", "그 이상은 나도 아는 것이 없었다. 내 신의를 믿고 나를 찾아온 그녀의 동기는, 내가 그 이름과 거처를 일러줄 수 있으리라는 희망이었다."], ["Whereas, to this wretched hour I am ignorant of both.", "그러나 이 비참한 순간까지도 나는 그 둘 다를 알지 못한다."]], [["“These scraps of paper fail me.", "이 종이 쪽들이 내게서 동난다."], ["One was taken from me, with a warning, yesterday.", "어제는 경고와 함께 한 장을 빼앗겼다."], ["I must finish my record to-day.", "나는 오늘 내 기록을 끝내야 한다."]], [["“She was a good, compassionate lady, and not happy in her marriage.", "그녀는 착하고, 자비로운 부인이었으며, 결혼생활은 행복하지 못했다."], ["How could she be!", "어찌 행복할 수 있었겠는가!"], ["The brother distrusted and disliked her, and his influence was all opposed to her; she stood in dread of him, and in dread of her husband too.", "그 동생은 그녀를 못 미더워하고 싫어했으며, 그의 영향력은 온통 그녀에게 맞섰다. 그녀는 그를 두려워했고, 자기 남편 또한 두려워했다."], ["When I handed her down to the door, there was a child, a pretty boy from two to three years old, in her carriage.", "내가 그녀를 부축해 문까지 내려가니, 그녀의 마차 안에 아이가, 두세 살쯤 된 예쁜 사내아이가 있었다."]], [["“‘For his sake, Doctor,’ she said, pointing to him in tears, ‘I would do all I can to make what poor amends I can.", "'이 아이를 위해서, 선생님.' 그녀는 눈물을 흘리며 아이를 가리키며 말했다."], ["He will never prosper in his inheritance otherwise.", "'저는 제가 할 수 있는 보잘것없는 속죄를 하기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 일을 하겠어요."], ["I have a presentiment that if no other innocent atonement is made for this, it will one day be required of him.", "그러지 않으면 이 아이는 결코 그 상속의 복을 누리지 못할 거예요. 이 일에 대해 다른 어떤 죄 없는 속죄도 이루어지지 않는다면, 언젠가 이 아이에게 그 대가가 요구되리라는 예감이 들어요."], ["What I have left to call my own--it is little beyond the worth of a few jewels--I will make it the first charge of his life to bestow, with the compassion and lamenting of his dead mother, on this injured family, if the sister can be discovered.’", "제 것이라 부를 수 있는 것이라곤 — 보석 몇 점의 값어치를 조금 넘을 뿐이지만 — 만약 그 누이를 찾을 수 있다면, 저는 이 아이가 죽은 어미의 연민과 비탄을 담아 그 상처 입은 가문에 그것을 베푸는 일을, 이 아이 삶의 첫번째 의무로 삼게 하겠어요.'"]], [["“She kissed the boy, and said, caressing him, ‘It is for thine own dear sake. Thou wilt be faithful, little Charles?’ The child answered her bravely, ‘Yes!’ I kissed her hand, and she took him in her arms, and went away caressing him. I never saw her more.", "그녀는 아이에게 입을 맞추고, 그를 어루만지며 말했다. '이건 다 너 자신을 위한 거란다. 너는 신의를 지키겠지, 어린 샤를?' 아이는 의젓하게 대답했다. '네!' 나는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었고, 그녀는 아이를 품에 안고서 그를 어루만지며 떠나갔다. 나는 그녀를 다시는 보지 못했다."]], [["“As she had mentioned her husband’s name in the faith that I knew it, I added no mention of it to my letter.", "그녀가 내가 자기 남편의 이름을 안다고 믿고 그 이름을 입에 올렸으므로, 나는 그 이름에 대한 언급을 내 편지에 덧붙이지 않았다."], ["I sealed my letter, and, not trusting it out of my own hands, delivered it myself that day.", "나는 편지를 봉했고, 그것을 내 손에서 내놓기가 미덥지 않아, 그날 직접 전했다."]], [["“That night, the last night of the year, towards nine o’clock, a man in a black dress rang at my gate, demanded to see me, and softly followed my servant, Ernest Defarge, a youth, up-stairs.", "그날 밤, 그해의 마지막 밤, 아홉시쯤에, 검은 옷을 입은 남자가 내 집 대문에서 종을 울려 나를 만나기를 청하고는, 젊은이인 내 하인 에르네스뜨 드파르주를 살그머니 따라 위층으로 올라왔다."], ["When my servant came into the room where I sat with my wife--O my wife, beloved of my heart!", "내 하인이 내가 아내와 함께 앉아 있던 방으로 들어왔을 때 — 오, 내 마음의 사랑하는 아내여!"], ["My fair young English wife!--we saw the man, who was supposed to be at the gate, standing silent behind him.", "나의 곱고 젊은 영국인 아내여! — 우리는 대문에 있는 줄 알았던 그 남자가 하인 뒤에 말없이 서 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“An urgent case in the Rue St. Honore, he said.", "쌩또노레 거리에 급한 환자가 있다고 그는 말했다."], ["It would not detain me, he had a coach in waiting.", "오래 붙들지 않을 것이며, 마차를 대기시켜두었다고 했다."]], [["“It brought me here, it brought me to my grave.", "그것이 나를 이곳으로 데려왔고, 나를 내 무덤으로 데려왔다."], ["When I was clear of the house, a black muffler was drawn tightly over my mouth from behind, and my arms were pinioned.", "내가 집을 벗어나자, 뒤에서 검은 천이 내 입을 단단히 틀어막았고, 두 팔이 묶였다."], ["The two brothers crossed the road from a dark corner, and identified me with a single gesture.", "두 형제가 어두운 모퉁이에서 길을 건너와, 단 한 번의 몸짓으로 나를 지목했다."], ["The Marquis took from his pocket the letter I had written, showed it me, burnt it in the light of a lantern that was held, and extinguished the ashes with his foot.", "후작은 주머니에서 내가 쓴 편지를 꺼내 내게 보이고는, 누군가 들고 있던 등불의 불빛에 그것을 태운 뒤, 발로 그 재를 비벼 껐다. 한마디도 오가지 않았다."], ["Not a word was spoken. I was brought here, I was brought to my living grave.", "나는 이곳으로 끌려왔고, 나는 산 채로 묻힌 내 무덤으로 끌려왔다."]], [["“If it had pleased God to put it in the hard heart of either of the brothers, in all these frightful years, to grant me any tidings of my dearest wife--so much as to let me know by a word whether alive or dead--I might have thought that He had not quite abandoned them.", "이 모든 끔찍한 세월 동안, 만약 하느님께서 그 형제 중 어느 쪽의 모진 마음에라도 나의 가장 사랑하는 아내의 소식을 — 살았는지 죽었는지 한마디로나마 알게 해줄 만큼이라도 — 내게 전하고자 하는 뜻을 심어주셨더라면, 나는 그분께서 그들을 아주 저버리지는 않으셨다고 여겼을지도 모른다."], ["But, now I believe that the mark of the red cross is fatal to them, and that they have no part in His mercies.", "그러나 이제 나는 그 붉은 십자가의 표지가 그들에게 치명적이며, 그들에게는 그분의 자비에 들 몫이 없다고 믿는다."], ["And them and their descendants, to the last of their race, I, Alexandre Manette, unhappy prisoner, do this last night of the year 1767, in my unbearable agony, denounce to the times when all these things shall be answered for.", "그리고 그들과 그들의 자손을, 그 족속의 마지막 하나에 이르기까지, 나, 알렉상드르 마네뜨, 불행한 죄수는, 1767년의 이 마지막 밤에, 견딜 수 없는 고통 속에서, 이 모든 일에 책임을 물을 그날을 향해 고발한다."], ["I denounce them to Heaven and to earth.”", "나는 그들을 하늘과 땅에 고발한다.」"]], [["A terrible sound arose when the reading of this document was done.", "이 문서의 낭독이 끝나자 무시무시한 소리가 일었다."], ["A sound of craving and eagerness that had nothing articulate in it but blood.", "갈망하고 열망하는, 피 말고는 또렷한 것이라곤 하나도 담기지 않은 소리였다."], ["The narrative called up the most revengeful passions of the time, and there was not a head in the nation but must have dropped before it.", "이 이야기는 그 시대의 가장 복수심에 찬 격정을 불러일으켰으니, 온 나라에서 그 앞에 떨궈지지 않을 머리는 하나도 없었을 것이다."]], [["Little need, in presence of that tribunal and that auditory, to show how the Defarges had not made the paper public, with the other captured Bastille memorials borne in procession, and had kept it, biding their time.", "그 법정과 그 방청객 앞에서, 드파르주 부부가 노획한 다른 바스띠유 기록물들과 함께 행렬에 실려 나온 이 문서를 공개하지 않고, 때를 기다리며 간직해두었던 까닭을 굳이 밝힐 필요는 거의 없었다."], ["Little need to show that this detested family name had long been anathematised by Saint Antoine, and was wrought into the fatal register.", "이 가증스러운 가문의 이름이 오랫동안 쌩땅뚜안의 저주를 받아왔으며, 운명의 명부에 새겨져 있었다는 것을 굳이 밝힐 필요도 거의 없었다."], ["The man never trod ground whose virtues and services would have sustained him in that place that day, against such denunciation.", "그러한 고발에 맞서 그날 그 자리에서 그를 지탱해줄 만한 미덕과 공덕을 갖춘 자란 이 세상에 없었다."]], [["And all the worse for the doomed man, that the denouncer was a well-known citizen, his own attached friend, the father of his wife.", "게다가 그 운명 지어진 사내에게 더욱 고약했던 것은, 고발한 자가 잘 알려진 시민이자 그 자신의 헌신적인 벗이며 그의 아내의 아버지라는 점이었다."], ["One of the frenzied aspirations of the populace was, for imitations of the questionable public virtues of antiquity, and for sacrifices and self-immolations on the people’s altar.", "민중의 광적인 열망 가운데 하나는, 미심쩍은 옛 시대의 공적 미덕을 본뜨는 것이었고, 민중의 제단에 바치는 희생과 자기 봉헌이었다."], ["Therefore when the President said (else had his own head quivered on his shoulders), that the good physician of the Republic would deserve better still of the Republic by rooting out an obnoxious family of Aristocrats, and would doubtless feel a sacred glow and joy in making his daughter a widow and her child an orphan, there was wild excitement, patriotic fervour, not a touch of human sympathy.", "그리하여 재판장이 (그러지 않았다면 그 자신의 머리가 어깨 위에서 떨렸을 것이다) 공화국의 훌륭한 의사는 가증스러운 한 귀족 가문을 뿌리 뽑음으로써 공화국에 더욱더 공헌하게 될 것이며, 자기 딸을 과부로, 그 손주를 고아로 만드는 데서 분명 신성한 기쁨과 환희를 느끼리라고 말하자, 격렬한 흥분과 애국적 열기가 일었으니, 인간적 동정이라곤 한 점도 없었다."]], [["“Much influence around him, has that Doctor?”", "\"그 의사, 자기 주위에 영향력이 대단하지?\""], ["murmured Madame Defarge, smiling to The Vengeance.", "드파르주 부인이 복수의 여신을 향해 미소 지으며 속삭였다."], ["“Save him now, my Doctor, save him!”", "\"이제 그를 구해보시지, 우리 의사 선생, 그를 구해봐!\""]], [["At every juryman’s vote, there was a roar.", "배심원이 한 표 한 표 던질 때마다, 함성이 일었다."], ["Another and another.", "또 한 표, 또 한 표."], ["Roar and roar.", "함성에 함성."]], [["Unanimously voted.", "만장일치로 가결되었다."], ["At heart and by descent an Aristocrat, an enemy of the Republic, a notorious oppressor of the People.", "마음으로나 혈통으로나 귀족이며, 공화국의 적이고, 악명 높은 민중의 압제자라는 것이었다."], ["Back to the Conciergerie, and Death within four-and-twenty hours!", "다시 콩시에르주리로, 그리고 스물네 시간 안에 사형!"]], [["# CHAPTER XI. Dusk", "# CHAPTER XI. Dusk"]], [["The wretched wife of the innocent man thus doomed to die, fell under the sentence, as if she had been mortally stricken.", "죽음을 선고받은 죄 없는 사내의 가련한 아내는, 그 선고가 마치 치명상이라도 입힌 듯 무너져 내렸다."], ["But, she uttered no sound; and so strong was the voice within her, representing that it was she of all the world who must uphold him in his misery and not augment it, that it quickly raised her, even from that shock.", "그러나 그녀는 아무 소리도 내지 않았다. 세상 모든 사람들 가운데 오직 자신만이 고통 속의 남편을 떠받쳐야 하며 그 고통을 더해서는 안 된다고 일러주는 내면의 목소리가 어찌나 강했던지, 그 충격 속에서도 그녀를 곧 일으켜 세웠다."]], [["The Judges having to take part in a public demonstration out of doors, the Tribunal adjourned.", "판사들이 옥외의 공개 시위에 참가해야 했으므로, 재판정은 휴정에 들어갔다."], ["The quick noise and movement of the court’s emptying itself by many passages had not ceased, when Lucie stood stretching out her arms towards her husband, with nothing in her face but love and consolation.", "여러 통로를 통해 법정이 비워지는 빠르고 부산한 소음과 움직임이 채 그치기도 전에, 루시는 얼굴에 사랑과 위로밖에는 어린 것이 없는 채로 남편을 향해 두 팔을 뻗으며 일어섰다."]], [["“If I might touch him!", "\"그이를 만질 수 있다면!"], ["If I might embrace him once!", "한번만 안아볼 수 있다면!"], ["O, good citizens, if you would have so much compassion for us!”", "오, 선량한 시민 여러분, 우리에게 그만한 자비를 베풀어주신다면!\""]], [["There was but a gaoler left, along with two of the four men who had taken him last night, and Barsad.", "남아 있는 사람이라고는 간수 하나와, 어젯밤 그를 잡아간 네 사람 가운데 둘, 그리고 바사드뿐이었다."], ["The people had all poured out to the show in the streets.", "사람들은 모두 거리의 구경거리를 향해 쏟아져 나간 터였다. 바사드가 나머지에게 제안했다."], ["Barsad proposed to the rest, “Let her embrace him then; it is but a moment.”", "\"그러면 안아보게 합시다. 잠깐이면 되니까.\""], ["It was silently acquiesced in, and they passed her over the seats in the hall to a raised place, where he, by leaning over the dock, could fold her in his arms.", "사람들은 말없이 동의했고, 그들은 그녀를 홀의 좌석들 너머로 옮겨 한 단 높은 자리로 데려갔다. 거기서 그는 피고석 너머로 몸을 숙여 그녀를 두 팔로 감쌀 수 있었다."]], [["“Farewell, dear darling of my soul.", "\"잘 가오, 내 영혼의 사랑하는 사람."], ["My parting blessing on my love.", "내 사랑에게 작별의 축복을 보내오."], ["We shall meet again, where the weary are at rest!”", "우리는 다시 만날 거요, 지친 자들이 안식하는 그곳에서!\""]], [["They were her husband’s words, as he held her to his bosom.", "그녀를 가슴에 끌어안으며 그녀의 남편이 한 말이었다."]], [["“I can bear it, dear Charles.", "\"견딜 수 있어요, 사랑하는 찰스."], ["I am supported from above: don’t suffer for me.", "저는 위로부터 떠받쳐지고 있어요. 저 때문에 괴로워하지 마세요."], ["A parting blessing for our child.”", "우리 아이에게도 작별의 축복을.\""]], [["“I send it to her by you.", "\"당신을 통해 그애에게 그것을 보내오."], ["I kiss her by you.", "당신을 통해 그애에게 입맞추오."], ["I say farewell to her by you.”", "당신을 통해 그애에게 작별을 고하오.\""]], [["“My husband.", "\"여보. 안돼요!"], ["No! A moment!”", "잠깐만요!\""], ["He was tearing himself apart from her.", "그가 그녀에게서 몸을 떼어내고 있었다. \"우리가 오래 떨어져 있지는 않을 거예요."], ["“We shall not be separated long.", "머지않아 이것이 제 가슴을 찢어놓으리라는 걸 느껴요."], ["I feel that this will break my heart by-and-bye; but I will do my duty while I can, and when I leave her, God will raise up friends for her, as He did for me.”", "하지만 할 수 있는 동안은 제 도리를 다하겠어요. 그리고 제가 그애를 떠나면, 하느님이 그애를 위해 벗들을 일으켜주실 거예요, 제게 그러셨던 것처럼.\""]], [["Her father had followed her, and would have fallen on his knees to both of them, but that Darnay put out a hand and seized him, crying:", "그녀의 아버지가 그녀를 따라왔고, 두 사람 앞에 무릎이라도 꿇으려 했으나, 다네이가 손을 내밀어 그를 붙들며 외쳤다."]], [["“No, no!", "\"안됩니다, 안돼요!"], ["What have you done, what have you done, that you should kneel to us!", "무슨 일을 하셨기에, 무슨 일을 하셨기에 저희에게 무릎을 꿇으려 하십니까!"], ["We know now, what a struggle you made of old.", "이제 저희는 압니다, 당신이 지난날 어떤 싸움을 치르셨는지를."], ["We know, now what you underwent when you suspected my descent, and when you knew it.", "이제 저희는 압니다, 제 혈통을 의심하셨을 때, 그리고 그것을 아셨을 때 당신이 무엇을 견뎌내셨는지를."], ["We know now, the natural antipathy you strove against, and conquered, for her dear sake.", "이제 저희는 압니다, 당신이 그애를 위해 맞서 싸워 이겨내신 그 본능적인 반감을."], ["We thank you with all our hearts, and all our love and duty.", "진심을 다해, 온 사랑과 도리를 다해 당신께 감사드립니다."], ["Heaven be with you!”", "하늘이 당신과 함께하시기를!\""]], [["Her father’s only answer was to draw his hands through his white hair, and wring them with a shriek of anguish.", "그녀의 아버지의 유일한 대답은 하얀 머리칼 사이로 두 손을 쓸어 넣고는, 고뇌의 비명과 함께 그 손을 비트는 것이었다."]], [["“It could not be otherwise,”", "\"달리 될 수는 없었습니다.\""], ["said the prisoner.", "죄수가 말했다."], ["“All things have worked together as they have fallen out.", "\"모든 일이 닥쳐온 그대로 맞물려 돌아갔지요."], ["It was the always-vain endeavour to discharge my poor mother’s trust that first brought my fatal presence near you.", "가엾은 어머니의 부탁을 이행하려던, 늘 헛되기만 했던 그 노력이 처음으로 제 운명적인 존재를 당신 곁에 데려다놓았습니다."], ["Good could never come of such evil, a happier end was not in nature to so unhappy a beginning.", "그런 악에서 선이 나올 수는 없었지요, 그토록 불행한 시작에 더 행복한 끝이란 자연의 이치상 있을 수 없었습니다."], ["Be comforted, and forgive me.", "마음을 다잡으시고, 저를 용서해주십시오."], ["Heaven bless you!”", "하늘이 당신을 축복하시기를!\""]], [["As he was drawn away, his wife released him, and stood looking after him with her hands touching one another in the attitude of prayer, and with a radiant look upon her face, in which there was even a comforting smile.", "그가 끌려나가자, 그의 아내는 그를 놓아주고, 두 손을 맞댄 기도하는 자세로 그의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었으니, 그 얼굴에는 환한 빛이 어렸고, 거기에는 위로하는 듯한 미소마저 깃들어 있었다."], ["As he went out at the prisoners’ door, she turned, laid her head lovingly on her father’s breast, tried to speak to him, and fell at his feet.", "그가 죄수용 문으로 나가자, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 아버지의 가슴에 머리를 사랑스럽게 기대고는, 그에게 무어라 말하려다가, 그의 발치에 쓰러졌다."]], [["Then, issuing from the obscure corner from which he had never moved, Sydney Carton came and took her up.", "그러자, 한 번도 움직이지 않았던 그 어두운 구석에서 나온 시드니 카턴이 다가와 그녀를 안아 일으켰다."], ["Only her father and Mr. Lorry were with her.", "그녀 곁에는 아버지와 로리 씨뿐이었다."], ["His arm trembled as it raised her, and supported her head.", "그녀를 들어올려 머리를 받칠 때 그의 팔은 떨렸다."], ["Yet, there was an air about him that was not all of pity--that had a flush of pride in it.", "그러나 그에게는 온전히 연민만은 아닌 분위기가 — 자긍심의 홍조가 어린 그런 분위기가 감돌았다."]], [["“Shall I take her to a coach?", "\"그녀를 마차로 옮길까요?"], ["I shall never feel her weight.”", "저는 그녀의 무게를 조금도 느끼지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["He carried her lightly to the door, and laid her tenderly down in a coach.", "그는 그녀를 가볍게 안아 문까지 데려가, 마차 안에 부드럽게 뉘었다."], ["Her father and their old friend got into it, and he took his seat beside the driver.", "그녀의 아버지와 그들의 오랜 벗이 마차에 올랐고, 그는 마부 옆자리에 앉았다."]], [["When they arrived at the gateway where he had paused in the dark not many hours before, to picture to himself on which of the rough stones of the street her feet had trodden, he lifted her again, and carried her up the staircase to their rooms.", "몇 시간 전 어둠 속에서 그가 멈춰 서서, 거리의 거친 돌들 가운데 어느 것을 그녀의 발이 밟았을까 그려보던 그 문간에 다다랐을 때, 그는 다시 그녀를 들어올려 계단을 지나 그들의 방으로 데려갔다."], ["There, he laid her down on a couch, where her child and Miss Pross wept over her.", "거기서 그는 그녀를 긴 의자에 뉘었고, 그녀의 아이와 프로스 양이 그녀를 굽어보며 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["“Don’t recall her to herself,”", "\"그녀를 깨우지 마세요.\""], ["he said, softly, to the latter, “she is better so.", "그가 후자에게 부드럽게 말했다. \"이대로가 나아요."], ["Don’t revive her to consciousness, while she only faints.”", "잠시 기절해 있을 뿐이니, 의식을 되돌리지 마세요.\""]], [["“Oh, Carton, Carton, dear Carton!”", "\"오, 카턴, 카턴, 사랑하는 카턴!\""], ["cried little Lucie, springing up and throwing her arms passionately round him, in a burst of grief.", "어린 루시가 벌떡 일어나 슬픔이 북받쳐 그에게 두 팔을 열정적으로 두르며 외쳤다."], ["“Now that you have come, I think you will do something to help mamma, something to save papa!", "\"이제 아저씨가 오셨으니, 엄마를 도와줄 무언가를, 아빠를 구해줄 무언가를 해주실 거죠! 오, 엄마를 보세요, 사랑하는 카턴 아저씨!"], ["O, look at her, dear Carton! Can you, of all the people who love her, bear to see her so?”", "엄마를 사랑하는 그 모든 사람들 가운데 아저씨가, 엄마가 이런 모습인 걸 차마 보고 있을 수 있나요?\""]], [["He bent over the child, and laid her blooming cheek against his face.", "그는 아이에게 몸을 숙여, 그 발그레한 뺨을 자기 얼굴에 가져다 댔다."], ["He put her gently from him, and looked at her unconscious mother.", "그는 아이를 가만히 떼어놓고, 의식을 잃은 아이의 어머니를 바라보았다."]], [["“Before I go,”", "\"가기 전에,\""], ["he said, and paused--“I may kiss her?”", "그가 말하다 멈추었다. \"그녀에게 입맞춰도 될까요?\""]], [["It was remembered afterwards that when he bent down and touched her face with his lips, he murmured some words.", "훗날 사람들이 기억하기로는, 그가 몸을 숙여 입술로 그녀의 얼굴을 스칠 때 무언가 말을 중얼거렸다고 한다."], ["The child, who was nearest to him, told them afterwards, and told her grandchildren when she was a handsome old lady, that she heard him say, “A life you love.”", "그에게 가장 가까이 있던 아이가 나중에 그것을 전했고, 또 자신이 아름다운 노부인이 되었을 때 손주들에게 전하기를, 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다고 했다. \"네가 사랑하는 한 생명을.\""]], [["When he had gone out into the next room, he turned suddenly on Mr. Lorry and her father, who were following, and said to the latter:", "옆방으로 나온 뒤, 그는 뒤따라오던 로리 씨와 그녀의 아버지를 향해 갑자기 돌아서서, 후자에게 말했다."]], [["“You had great influence but yesterday, Doctor Manette; let it at least be tried.", "\"바로 어제만 해도 당신은 큰 영향력이 있었지요, 마네뜨 박사님. 적어도 그것을 한번 시도해봅시다."], ["These judges, and all the men in power, are very friendly to you, and very recognisant of your services; are they not?”", "그 판사들과, 권력을 쥔 모든 사람들이 당신께 매우 호의적이고, 당신의 공로를 무척 인정하고 있지 않습니까?\""]], [["“Nothing connected with Charles was concealed from me.", "\"찰스와 관련된 일은 무엇 하나 내게 숨겨지지 않았소."], ["I had the strongest assurances that I should save him; and I did.”", "나는 그를 구하리라는 더없이 강한 확신을 받았고, 실제로 구해냈소.\""], ["He returned the answer in great trouble, and very slowly.", "그는 몹시 괴로워하며, 아주 천천히 그 대답을 돌려주었다."]], [["“Try them again.", "\"다시 한번 시도해보세요."], ["The hours between this and to-morrow afternoon are few and short, but try.”", "지금부터 내일 오후까지의 시간은 적고 짧지만, 그래도 해보세요.\""]], [["“I intend to try.", "\"해볼 작정이오."], ["I will not rest a moment.”", "한순간도 쉬지 않겠소.\""]], [["“That’s well.", "\"잘됐습니다."], ["I have known such energy as yours do great things before now--though never,”", "저는 당신 같은 정력이 전에도 큰일을 해내는 것을 보아왔습니다 — 비록,\""], ["he added, with a smile and a sigh together, “such great things as this.", "그는 미소와 한숨을 함께 띠며 덧붙였다, \"이만한 큰일을 해낸 적은 없었지만요. 하지만 해보세요!"], ["But try! Of little worth as life is when we misuse it, it is worth that effort.", "우리가 잘못 쓸 때 생명이란 보잘것없는 것이지만, 그래도 그만한 노력의 값어치는 있지요."], ["It would cost nothing to lay down if it were not.”", "그렇지 않다면 목숨을 내려놓는 데 아무 대가도 들지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["“I will go,”", "\"내가 가겠소.\""], ["said Doctor Manette, “to the Prosecutor and the President straight, and I will go to others whom it is better not to name.", "마네뜨 박사가 말했다. \"검사와 재판장에게 곧장 가고, 이름을 대지 않는 편이 나은 다른 이들에게도 가겠소."], ["I will write too, and--But stay!", "글도 쓰겠소, 그리고 — 아니, 잠깐!"], ["There is a Celebration in the streets, and no one will be accessible until dark.”", "거리에서 축제가 벌어지고 있으니, 어두워지기 전에는 아무도 만날 수가 없겠군.\""]], [["“That’s true.", "\"그렇겠지요."], ["Well!", "하긴!"], ["It is a forlorn hope at the best, and not much the forlorner for being delayed till dark.", "잘해야 가망 없는 희망이고, 어두워질 때까지 미뤄진다고 해서 딱히 더 가망 없어질 것도 없으니까요."], ["I should like to know how you speed; though, mind!", "일이 어떻게 되어가는지 알고 싶습니다. 하지만, 명심하세요!"], ["I expect nothing!", "저는 아무것도 기대하지 않습니다!"], ["When are you likely to have seen these dread powers, Doctor Manette?”", "그 두려운 권력자들을 언제쯤 만나보실 수 있을 것 같습니까, 마네뜨 박사님?\""]], [["“Immediately after dark, I should hope.", "\"어두워진 직후이기를 바라야겠지."], ["Within an hour or two from this.”", "지금부터 한두 시간 안에.\""]], [["“It will be dark soon after four.", "\"네시가 조금 지나면 어두워질 겁니다."], ["Let us stretch the hour or two.", "그 한두 시간을 늘려 잡읍시다."], ["If I go to Mr. Lorry’s at nine, shall I hear what you have done, either from our friend or from yourself?”", "제가 아홉시에 로리 씨 댁으로 가면, 당신이 무엇을 하셨는지 우리 벗에게서든 당신에게서든 들을 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“May you prosper!”", "\"잘되시기를!\""]], [["Mr. Lorry followed Sydney to the outer door, and, touching him on the shoulder as he was going away, caused him to turn.", "로리 씨는 시드니를 따라 바깥문까지 갔고, 그가 떠나려 할 때 어깨를 짚어 그를 돌아서게 했다."]], [["“I have no hope,” said Mr.", "\"나는 아무 희망도 없네.\""], ["Lorry, in a low and sorrowful whisper.", "로리 씨가 낮고 슬픈 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“Nor have I.”", "\"저도 없습니다.\""]], [["“If any one of these men, or all of these men, were disposed to spare him--which is a large supposition; for what is his life, or any man’s to them!--I doubt if they durst spare him after the demonstration in the court.”", "\"이 사람들 중 누구 하나라도, 아니 이 사람들 전부가 그를 살려줄 마음이 있다 한들 — 이건 너무 큰 가정이지, 저들에게 그의 목숨이, 아니 그 누구의 목숨인들 무슨 대수겠나! — 법정에서의 그 시위가 있은 뒤에 과연 감히 그를 살려줄 수 있을지 의심스럽네.\""]], [["“And so do I.", "\"저도 그렇습니다."], ["I heard the fall of the axe in that sound.”", "저는 그 소리 속에서 도끼가 떨어지는 소리를 들었지요.\""]], [["Mr. Lorry leaned his arm upon the door-post, and bowed his face upon it.", "로리 씨는 팔을 문설주에 기대고, 그 위에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“Don’t despond,”", "\"낙담하지 마세요.\""], ["said Carton, very gently; “don’t grieve.", "카턴이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. \"슬퍼하지 마세요."], ["I encouraged Doctor Manette in this idea, because I felt that it might one day be consolatory to her.", "제가 마네뜨 박사님께 이 생각을 부추긴 것은, 언젠가 그것이 그녀에게 위안이 될지도 모른다고 느꼈기 때문입니다."], ["Otherwise, she might think ‘his life was wantonly thrown away or wasted,’ and that might trouble her.”", "그렇지 않으면 그녀는 '그의 목숨이 부질없이 내던져졌거나 허비되었다'고 생각할지도 모르고, 그것이 그녀를 괴롭힐 테니까요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes,” returned Mr.", "\"그래, 그래, 그렇지.\""], ["Lorry, drying his eyes, “you are right.", "로리 씨가 눈물을 닦으며 대답했다. \"자네 말이 옳네."], ["But he will perish; there is no real hope.”", "하지만 그는 죽고 말 거야. 진정한 희망이란 없네.\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["He will perish: there is no real hope,”", "그는 죽고 말겠지요. 진정한 희망이란 없습니다.\""], ["echoed Carton.", "카턴이 메아리처럼 되받았다."]], [["And walked with a settled step, down-stairs.", "그러고는 흔들림 없는 발걸음으로 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII. Darkness", "# CHAPTER XII. Darkness"]], [["Sydney Carton paused in the street, not quite decided where to go.", "시드니 카턴은 어디로 갈지 정하지 못한 채 거리에서 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["“At Tellson’s banking-house at nine,”", "\"아홉시에 텔슨 은행에서.\""], ["he said, with a musing face.", "그는 생각에 잠긴 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“Shall I do well, in the mean time, to show myself?", "\"그동안에 내 모습을 드러내두는 게 좋을까?"], ["I think so.", "그래야겠지."], ["It is best that these people should know there is such a man as I here; it is a sound precaution, and may be a necessary preparation.", "이 사람들이 나 같은 인간이 여기 있다는 걸 알아두는 게 가장 좋아. 빈틈없는 예방책이고, 어쩌면 꼭 필요한 준비일지도 모르지."], ["But care, care, care!", "하지만 조심, 조심, 조심해야 해!"], ["Let me think it out!”", "차근차근 생각해보자!\""]], [["Checking his steps which had begun to tend towards an object, he took a turn or two in the already darkening street, and traced the thought in his mind to its possible consequences.", "어떤 목적지를 향해 이끌리기 시작한 발걸음을 멈추고, 그는 이미 어두워져가는 거리를 한두 바퀴 돌면서 그 생각이 빚어낼 수 있는 결과들을 마음속으로 더듬어보았다."], ["His first impression was confirmed.", "그의 첫 인상은 굳어졌다."], ["“It is best,”", "\"가장 좋아.\""], ["he said, finally resolved, “that these people should know there is such a man as I here.”", "마침내 결심을 굳히고 그는 말했다. \"이 사람들이 나 같은 인간이 여기 있다는 걸 알아두는 게.\""], ["And he turned his face towards Saint Antoine.", "그러고는 쌩땅뚜안 쪽으로 얼굴을 돌렸다."]], [["Defarge had described himself, that day, as the keeper of a wine-shop in the Saint Antoine suburb.", "드파르주는 그날, 자신을 쌩땅뚜안 변두리에서 술집을 하는 사람이라고 소개한 바 있었다."], ["It was not difficult for one who knew the city well, to find his house without asking any question.", "이 도시를 잘 아는 사람이라면 아무에게도 묻지 않고 그의 집을 찾기란 어렵지 않았다."], ["Having ascertained its situation, Carton came out of those closer streets again, and dined at a place of refreshment and fell sound asleep after dinner.", "그 위치를 확인한 뒤, 카턴은 다시 그 비좁은 거리들에서 나와 어느 음식점에서 식사를 하고 식후에 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["For the first time in many years, he had no strong drink.", "여러 해 만에 처음으로, 그는 독한 술을 입에 대지 않았다."], ["Since last night he had taken nothing but a little light thin wine, and last night he had dropped the brandy slowly down on Mr. Lorry’s hearth like a man who had done with it.", "어젯밤 이후로 그는 가볍고 묽은 포도주만 조금 마셨을 뿐이고, 어젯밤에는 브랜디를 다 끝낸 사람처럼 로리 씨의 벽난로에 천천히 떨어뜨려 버렸다."]], [["It was as late as seven o’clock when he awoke refreshed, and went out into the streets again.", "개운하게 깨어나 다시 거리로 나섰을 때는 벌써 일곱시나 되어 있었다."], ["As he passed along towards Saint Antoine, he stopped at a shop-window where there was a mirror, and slightly altered the disordered arrangement of his loose cravat, and his coat-collar, and his wild hair.", "쌩땅뚜안 쪽으로 걸어가다가, 그는 거울이 걸려 있는 어느 가게의 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서서, 헐겁게 맨 넥타이며 외투 깃이며 헝클어진 머리칼의 어수선한 매무새를 살짝 매만졌다."], ["This done, he went on direct to Defarge’s, and went in.", "그러고 나서 곧장 드파르주의 술집으로 가서 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["There happened to be no customer in the shop but Jacques Three, of the restless fingers and the croaking voice.", "마침 가게 안에는 안절부절못하는 손가락에 쉰 목소리를 가진 자끄 쓰리 말고는 손님이 없었다."], ["This man, whom he had seen upon the Jury, stood drinking at the little counter, in conversation with the Defarges, man and wife.", "배심원석에서 보았던 이 사내는 작은 계산대에서 술을 마시며 드파르주 부부와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["The Vengeance assisted in the conversation, like a regular member of the establishment.", "라 방죙스도 마치 이 가게의 정식 일원이라도 되는 양 그 대화에 끼어 있었다."]], [["As Carton walked in, took his seat and asked (in very indifferent French) for a small measure of wine, Madame Defarge cast a careless glance at him, and then a keener, and then a keener, and then advanced to him herself, and asked him what it was he had ordered.", "카턴이 걸어 들어와 자리를 잡고 (아주 서툰 프랑스어로) 포도주 한 잔을 청하자, 마담 드파르주는 무심한 눈길을 한번 던지더니, 이어 더 날카롭게, 또 한층 더 날카롭게 그를 바라보다가, 마침내 직접 그에게 다가와 무엇을 시켰느냐고 물었다."]], [["He repeated what he had already said.", "그는 이미 했던 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“English?”", "\"영국 사람이오?\""], ["asked Madame Defarge, inquisitively raising her dark eyebrows.", "마담 드파르주가 검은 눈썹을 의심스럽게 치켜올리며 물었다."]], [["After looking at her, as if the sound of even a single French word were slow to express itself to him, he answered, in his former strong foreign accent.", "프랑스어 단 한마디조차 입에서 더디게 나오기라도 하는 듯 그녀를 바라본 뒤, 그는 아까처럼 진한 외국인 억양으로 대답했다. \"그렇소, 부인, 그렇소."], ["“Yes, madame, yes. I am English!”", "나는 영국 사람이오!\""]], [["Madame Defarge returned to her counter to get the wine, and, as he took up a Jacobin journal and feigned to pore over it puzzling out its meaning, he heard her say, “I swear to you, like Evrémonde!”", "마담 드파르주는 포도주를 가지러 계산대로 돌아갔고, 그가 자코뱅파 신문을 집어 들고 그 뜻을 알아내려 애쓰며 골똘히 들여다보는 시늉을 하고 있을 때, 그녀가 이렇게 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"내 맹세코, 에브레몽드를 꼭 닮았다니까!\""]], [["Defarge brought him the wine, and gave him Good Evening.", "드파르주가 포도주를 가져다주며 안녕하시냐고 인사했다."]], [["“Good evening.”", "\"안녕하시오.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Good evening, citizen,”", "안녕하시오, 시민.\""], ["filling his glass.", "그는 잔을 채우며 말했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["and good wine.", "좋은 술이군."], ["I drink to the Republic.”", "공화국을 위하여 마시겠소.\""]], [["Defarge went back to the counter, and said, “Certainly, a little like.”", "드파르주는 계산대로 돌아가서 말했다. \"확실히 조금 닮긴 했어.\""], ["Madame sternly retorted, “I tell you a good deal like.”", "마담이 매섭게 받아쳤다. \"꽤 많이 닮았다니까.\""], ["Jacques Three pacifically remarked, “He is so much in your mind, see you, madame.”", "자끄 쓰리가 누그러뜨리려는 듯 한마디 했다. \"마담, 당신 마음속에 그자가 그토록 깊이 박혀 있는 거요.\""], ["The amiable Vengeance added, with a laugh, “Yes, my faith!", "상냥한 라 방죙스가 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 정말이지!"], ["And you are looking forward with so much pleasure to seeing him once more to-morrow!”", "게다가 내일 그자를 한번 더 보게 될 걸 그토록 즐겁게 기다리고 있잖아요!\""]], [["Carton followed the lines and words of his paper, with a slow forefinger, and with a studious and absorbed face.", "카턴은 느릿한 집게손가락으로 신문의 글줄과 단어들을 짚어가며, 열중해서 골몰하는 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["They were all leaning their arms on the counter close together, speaking low.", "그들은 모두 계산대에 바짝 붙어 팔을 괴고 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있었다."], ["After a silence of a few moments, during which they all looked towards him without disturbing his outward attention from the Jacobin editor, they resumed their conversation.", "잠시 침묵이 흐르는 동안, 그들은 모두 그쪽을 바라보았으나 자코뱅파 논설에 쏠린 그의 겉으로 드러난 주의를 흩뜨리지는 않았고, 이윽고 다시 대화를 이어갔다."]], [["“It is true what madame says,”", "\"마담 말이 옳아.\" 자끄 쓰리가 말했다."], ["observed Jacques Three.", "\"왜 멈춘단 말이오?"], ["“Why stop? There is great force in that.", "그 말에는 큰 힘이 있소."], ["Why stop?”", "왜 멈춘단 말이오?\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"글쎄, 글쎄.\""], ["reasoned Defarge, “but one must stop somewhere.", "드파르주가 따져보듯 말했다. \"그래도 어딘가에서는 멈춰야 하는 법이지."], ["After all, the question is still where?”", "결국 문제는 어디서 멈추느냐 하는 거 아니겠소?\""]], [["“At extermination,”", "\"몰살에서지.\""], ["said madame.", "마담이 말했다."]], [["“Magnificent!”", "\"훌륭하오!\""], ["croaked Jacques Three.", "자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["The Vengeance, also, highly approved.", "라 방죙스 역시 크게 찬동했다."]], [["“Extermination is good doctrine, my wife,”", "\"몰살이야 좋은 신조지, 여보.\" 드파르주가 다소 곤혹스러운 듯 말했다."], ["said Defarge, rather troubled; “in general, I say nothing against it.", "\"대체로 나는 거기에 반대할 게 없소. 하지만 이 의사는 고초를 많이 겪은 사람이오."], ["But this Doctor has suffered much; you have seen him to-day; you have observed his face when the paper was read.”", "당신도 오늘 그를 봤잖소. 그 서류가 낭독될 때 그의 얼굴을 똑똑히 봤을 거요.\""]], [["“I have observed his face!”", "\"내가 그자의 얼굴을 봤지!\""], ["repeated madame, contemptuously and angrily.", "마담이 경멸스럽고 노여운 어조로 되받았다."], ["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I have observed his face.", "그자의 얼굴을 봤어."], ["I have observed his face to be not the face of a true friend of the Republic.", "그자의 얼굴이 공화국의 진정한 벗의 얼굴이 아니라는 걸 봤다고."], ["Let him take care of his face!”", "제 얼굴이나 잘 간수하라지!\""]], [["“And you have observed, my wife,”", "\"그리고 당신도 봤잖소, 여보.\" 드파르주가 만류하는 투로 말했다."], ["said Defarge, in a deprecatory manner, “the anguish of his daughter, which must be a dreadful anguish to him!”", "\"그 딸의 비통함을. 그것은 그에게 끔찍한 고통일 게 분명하오!\""]], [["“I have observed his daughter,”", "\"내가 그자의 딸을 봤지.\""], ["repeated madame; “yes, I have observed his daughter, more times than one.", "마담이 되풀이했다. \"그래, 그자의 딸을 봤어, 한두 번이 아니라."], ["I have observed her to-day, and I have observed her other days.", "오늘도 봤고, 다른 날에도 봤어."], ["I have observed her in the court, and I have observed her in the street by the prison.", "법정에서도 봤고, 감옥 옆 거리에서도 봤다고. 내 손가락 하나만 들어 올리면 ——!\""], ["Let me but lift my finger--!” She seemed to raise it (the listener’s eyes were always on his paper), and to let it fall with a rattle on the ledge before her, as if the axe had dropped.", "그녀는 손가락을 들어 올리는 듯하더니 (귀 기울이고 있는 사내의 눈은 줄곧 신문에 가 있었다), 마치 도끼날이 떨어지기라도 하듯 앞에 놓인 계산대 선반 위로 그것을 탁 내리쳤다."]], [["“The citizeness is superb!”", "\"여시민은 굉장하시오!\""], ["croaked the Juryman.", "배심원이 쉰 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“She is an Angel!”", "\"천사예요!\""], ["said The Vengeance, and embraced her.", "라 방죙스가 말하며 그녀를 껴안았다."]], [["“As to thee,”", "\"당신으로 말할 것 같으면,\""], ["pursued madame, implacably, addressing her husband, “if it depended on thee--which, happily, it does not--thou wouldst rescue this man even now.”", "마담이 단호하게 남편을 향해 계속 말했다. \"당신한테 달린 일이라면 —— 다행히도 그렇지 않지만 —— 당신은 지금이라도 이자를 구해주려 들 테지.\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니!\""], ["protested Defarge.", "드파르주가 항변했다."], ["“Not if to lift this glass would do it!", "\"이 잔을 들어 올리는 것만으로 구할 수 있다 해도 그러지 않을 거요!"], ["But I would leave the matter there.", "하지만 나는 이 일을 거기서 그만두겠소."], ["I say, stop there.”", "거기서 멈추자는 말이오.\""]], [["“See you then, Jacques,”", "\"그렇다면 잘 들어, 자끄.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, wrathfully; “and see you, too, my little Vengeance; see you both!", "마담 드파르주가 노여워하며 말했다. \"그리고 당신도 잘 들어, 나의 작은 라 방죙스도."], ["Listen!", "둘 다 잘 들어!"], ["For other crimes as tyrants and oppressors, I have this race a long time on my register, doomed to destruction and extermination.", "들으라고! 폭군이자 압제자로서 저지른 다른 죄들 때문에, 나는 그 일족을 오래전부터 내 명부에 올려두었어, 파멸과 몰살의 운명으로 정해서."], ["Ask my husband, is that so.”", "내 남편한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\""]], [["“It is so,”", "\"사실이오.\""], ["assented Defarge, without being asked.", "드파르주가 묻기도 전에 동의했다."]], [["“In the beginning of the great days, when the Bastille falls, he finds this paper of to-day, and he brings it home, and in the middle of the night when this place is clear and shut, we read it, here on this spot, by the light of this lamp.", "\"위대한 날들이 시작되던 무렵, 바스띠유가 함락될 때, 그이가 오늘 나온 이 서류를 찾아내서 집으로 가져왔지. 그리고 한밤중에 이곳이 텅 비고 문이 닫혔을 때, 우리는 그것을 읽었어, 바로 이 자리에서, 이 등불 빛으로."], ["Ask him, is that so.”", "그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\""]], [["“It is so,”", "\"사실이오.\""], ["assented Defarge.", "드파르주가 동의했다."]], [["“That night, I tell him, when the paper is read through, and the lamp is burnt out, and the day is gleaming in above those shutters and between those iron bars, that I have now a secret to communicate.", "\"그날 밤, 서류를 끝까지 다 읽고 등불이 다 타버리고 동이 터서 저 덧문 위로, 저 쇠창살 사이로 햇빛이 비쳐들 무렵, 나는 그이한테 이제 전해줄 비밀이 하나 있다고 했어."], ["Ask him, is that so.”", "그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\""]], [["“It is so,”", "\"사실이오.\""], ["assented Defarge again.", "드파르주가 다시 동의했다."]], [["“I communicate to him that secret.", "\"나는 그이한테 그 비밀을 전했어."], ["I smite this bosom with these two hands as I smite it now, and I tell him, ‘Defarge, I was brought up among the fishermen of the sea-shore, and that peasant family so injured by the two Evrémonde brothers, as that Bastille paper describes, is my family.", "지금 이렇게 치듯이 이 두 손으로 이 가슴을 치면서, 그이한테 말했지. '드파르주, 나는 바닷가 어부들 틈에서 자랐고, 그 바스띠유 서류에 적힌 대로 에브레몽드 형제에게 그토록 해를 입은 그 농민 가족이 바로 내 가족이오."], ["Defarge, that sister of the mortally wounded boy upon the ground was my sister, that husband was my sister’s husband, that unborn child was their child, that brother was my brother, that father was my father, those dead are my dead, and that summons to answer for those things descends to me!’ Ask him, is that so.”", "드파르주, 땅바닥에 쓰러져 치명상을 입은 그 소년의 누이가 내 언니였고, 그 남편이 내 형부였고, 태어나지 못한 그 아이가 그들의 아이였고, 그 오라비가 내 오빠였고, 그 아버지가 내 아버지였고, 그 죽은 이들이 내 죽은 이들이며, 그 일들의 책임을 묻는 소환장이 이제 나에게로 내려온 거요!' 그이한테 물어봐, 그게 사실인지.\""]], [["“It is so,”", "\"사실이오.\""], ["assented Defarge once more.", "드파르주가 한번 더 동의했다."]], [["“Then tell Wind and Fire where to stop,”", "\"그러니 바람과 불더러 어디서 멈추라고 일러.\""], ["returned madame; “but don’t tell me.”", "마담이 받아쳤다. \"하지만 나한테는 말하지 마.\""]], [["Both her hearers derived a horrible enjoyment from the deadly nature of her wrath--the listener could feel how white she was, without seeing her--and both highly commended it.", "그녀의 노여움이 품은 그 죽음 같은 본성에서, 두 청자는 끔찍한 즐거움을 끌어냈고 —— 신문에 귀 기울이던 사내는 그녀를 보지 않고도 그녀가 얼마나 새파랗게 질려 있는지 느낄 수 있었다 —— 둘 다 그것을 크게 칭송했다."], ["Defarge, a weak minority, interposed a few words for the memory of the compassionate wife of the Marquis; but only elicited from his own wife a repetition of her last reply.", "미약한 소수파인 드파르주가 후작의 그 자비로운 부인을 추모하며 몇 마디 끼워 넣었으나, 자기 아내에게서 방금 했던 그 대답만 되풀이하게 했을 뿐이었다."], ["“Tell the Wind and the Fire where to stop; not me!”", "\"바람과 불더러 어디서 멈추라고 일러. 나한테는 말고!\""]], [["Customers entered, and the group was broken up.", "손님들이 들어오자 무리는 흩어졌다."], ["The English customer paid for what he had had, perplexedly counted his change, and asked, as a stranger, to be directed towards the National Palace.", "그 영국 손님은 자기가 마신 값을 치르고, 어리둥절한 표정으로 거스름돈을 세더니, 이방인답게 국민궁으로 가는 길을 일러달라고 청했다."], ["Madame Defarge took him to the door, and put her arm on his, in pointing out the road.", "마담 드파르주는 그를 문간으로 데려가, 길을 가리키며 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 얹었다."], ["The English customer was not without his reflections then, that it might be a good deed to seize that arm, lift it, and strike under it sharp and deep.", "그 영국 손님은 그때 그 팔을 붙잡아 들어 올린 다음 그 아래를 날카롭고 깊숙이 찔러버리는 것이 어쩌면 좋은 일일지도 모른다는 생각이 들지 않은 것은 아니었다."]], [["But, he went his way, and was soon swallowed up in the shadow of the prison wall.", "그러나 그는 제 갈 길을 갔고, 곧 감옥 담장의 그늘 속으로 삼켜져버렸다."], ["At the appointed hour, he emerged from it to present himself in Mr. Lorry’s room again, where he found the old gentleman walking to and fro in restless anxiety.", "약속한 시각에 그는 그곳에서 나와 다시 로리 씨의 방에 모습을 드러냈는데, 그곳에서 노신사가 불안에 안절부절못하며 방안을 서성이고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He said he had been with Lucie until just now, and had only left her for a few minutes, to come and keep his appointment.", "그는 방금 전까지 루시와 함께 있다가 약속을 지키러 오느라 잠깐 자리를 떴을 뿐이라고 말했다."], ["Her father had not been seen, since he quitted the banking-house towards four o’clock.", "그녀의 아버지는 네시 무렵 은행을 나선 뒤로 모습을 보이지 않았다."], ["She had some faint hopes that his mediation might save Charles, but they were very slight.", "그녀는 아버지의 중재로 찰스를 구할 수 있을지도 모른다는 희미한 희망을 품고 있었으나, 그것은 아주 미약한 것이었다."], ["He had been more than five hours gone: where could he be?", "그가 나간 지 다섯 시간이 넘었다. 대체 어디 있는 걸까?"]], [["Mr. Lorry waited until ten; but, Doctor Manette not returning, and he being unwilling to leave Lucie any longer, it was arranged that he should go back to her, and come to the banking-house again at midnight.", "로리 씨는 열시까지 기다렸다."], ["In the meanwhile, Carton would wait alone by the fire for the Doctor.", "그러나 마네뜨 의사가 돌아오지 않고, 또 그로서는 더 이상 루시를 혼자 두고 싶지 않았으므로, 그가 그녀에게 돌아갔다가 자정에 다시 은행으로 오기로 정해졌다. 그동안 카턴은 의사를 기다리며 난롯가에 혼자 있기로 했다."]], [["He waited and waited, and the clock struck twelve; but Doctor Manette did not come back.", "그는 기다리고 또 기다렸고, 시계가 열두시를 쳤다. 그러나 마네뜨 의사는 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Mr. Lorry returned, and found no tidings of him, and brought none.", "로리 씨가 돌아왔으나 그에 관한 아무 소식도 듣지 못했고, 아무 소식도 가져오지 못했다."], ["Where could he be?", "대체 어디 있는 걸까?"]], [["They were discussing this question, and were almost building up some weak structure of hope on his prolonged absence, when they heard him on the stairs.", "두 사람이 이 문제를 의논하면서 그의 길어진 부재를 두고 미약한 희망의 구조물을 거의 쌓아 올릴 즈음, 그들은 계단을 오르는 그의 발소리를 들었다."], ["The instant he entered the room, it was plain that all was lost.", "그가 방에 들어선 순간, 모든 것이 끝장났음이 분명해졌다."]], [["Whether he had really been to any one, or whether he had been all that time traversing the streets, was never known.", "그가 정말로 누군가를 찾아갔던 것인지, 아니면 그 시간 내내 거리를 헤매고 다녔던 것인지는 끝내 알 수 없었다."], ["As he stood staring at them, they asked him no question, for his face told them everything.", "그가 멍하니 그들을 바라보며 서 있을 때, 그들은 아무것도 묻지 않았다. 그의 얼굴이 모든 것을 말해주었기 때문이다."]], [["“I cannot find it,”", "\"찾을 수가 없어.\""], ["said he, “and I must have it.", "그가 말했다. \"그게 있어야 하는데."], ["Where is it?”", "어디 있지?\""]], [["His head and throat were bare, and, as he spoke with a helpless look straying all around, he took his coat off, and let it drop on the floor.", "머리와 목이 드러나 있었고, 그가 어찌할 바 모르는 눈길을 사방으로 돌리며 말하는 사이, 그는 외투를 벗어 바닥에 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["“Where is my bench?", "\"내 작업대는 어디 있지?"], ["I have been looking everywhere for my bench, and I can’t find it.", "작업대를 사방으로 찾았는데, 못 찾겠어."], ["What have they done with my work?", "내 작업물을 어떻게 해버린 거야?"], ["Time presses: I must finish those shoes.”", "시간이 촉박한데. 그 구두를 마저 만들어야 해.\""]], [["They looked at one another, and their hearts died within them.", "두 사람은 서로를 바라보았고, 가슴속이 무너져 내렸다."]], [["“Come, come!”", "\"자, 어서!\""], ["said he, in a whimpering miserable way; “let me get to work.", "그가 흐느끼는 듯 비참한 투로 말했다. \"일을 하게 해줘."], ["Give me my work.”", "내 작업물을 줘.\""]], [["Receiving no answer, he tore his hair, and beat his feet upon the ground, like a distracted child.", "아무 대답도 못 듣자, 그는 제정신이 아닌 아이처럼 머리칼을 쥐어뜯고 바닥에 발을 굴렀다."]], [["“Don’t torture a poor forlorn wretch,”", "\"가엾고 버림받은 인간을 괴롭히지 말아줘.\""], ["he implored them, with a dreadful cry; “but give me my work!", "그가 끔찍한 비명을 지르며 그들에게 애원했다. \"그저 내 작업물을 달라고!"], ["What is to become of us, if those shoes are not done to-night?”", "그 구두를 오늘 밤 안에 마치지 못하면 우리는 어떻게 되는 거야?\""]], [["Lost, utterly lost!", "망가졌다, 완전히 망가져버렸다!"]], [["It was so clearly beyond hope to reason with him, or try to restore him, that--as if by agreement--they each put a hand upon his shoulder, and soothed him to sit down before the fire, with a promise that he should have his work presently.", "그를 타이르거나 제정신으로 돌려놓으려는 것은 너무도 명백히 가망 없는 일이었으므로 —— 마치 약속이라도 한 듯 —— 두 사람은 각자 그의 어깨에 한 손씩 얹고, 곧 작업물을 가져다주겠노라 약속하며 그를 달래어 난롯가에 앉혔다."], ["He sank into the chair, and brooded over the embers, and shed tears.", "그는 의자에 푹 주저앉아 잉걸불을 바라보며 골똘히 잠겼고, 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["As if all that had happened since the garret time were a momentary fancy, or a dream, Mr. Lorry saw him shrink into the exact figure that Defarge had had in keeping.", "다락방 시절 이후로 일어난 모든 일이 한순간의 환상이거나 꿈이기라도 한 듯, 로리 씨는 그가 드파르주가 데리고 있던 바로 그 모습 그대로 움츠러드는 것을 보았다."]], [["Affected, and impressed with terror as they both were, by this spectacle of ruin, it was not a time to yield to such emotions.", "이 파멸의 광경에 두 사람 모두 마음이 흔들리고 공포에 사로잡혔으나, 지금은 그런 감정에 굴복할 때가 아니었다."], ["His lonely daughter, bereft of her final hope and reliance, appealed to them both too strongly.", "마지막 희망이자 의지처를 잃은 그의 외로운 딸이 두 사람 모두에게 너무도 강렬하게 호소해왔다."], ["Again, as if by agreement, they looked at one another with one meaning in their faces.", "다시금, 마치 약속이라도 한 듯, 그들은 같은 뜻을 얼굴에 담고 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Carton was the first to speak:", "카턴이 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“The last chance is gone: it was not much.", "\"마지막 기회가 사라졌소. 큰 것은 아니었지만."], ["Yes; he had better be taken to her.", "그래요. 저분은 따님에게 데려다드리는 게 좋겠소."], ["But, before you go, will you, for a moment, steadily attend to me?", "하지만 가기 전에, 잠깐 동안만, 정신을 차리고 내 말을 들어주시겠소?"], ["Don’t ask me why I make the stipulations I am going to make, and exact the promise I am going to exact; I have a reason--a good one.”", "내가 하려는 약조를 왜 하는지, 또 내가 받아내려는 약속을 왜 받아내려는지 묻지 마시오. 내게는 이유가 —— 충분한 이유가 있소.\""]], [["“I do not doubt it,”", "\"의심치 않소.\""], ["answered Mr.", "로리 씨가 대답했다."], ["Lorry. “Say on.”", "\"말씀해보시오.\""]], [["The figure in the chair between them, was all the time monotonously rocking itself to and fro, and moaning.", "두 사람 사이 의자에 앉은 그 형상은 줄곧 단조롭게 앞뒤로 몸을 흔들며 신음하고 있었다."], ["They spoke in such a tone as they would have used if they had been watching by a sick-bed in the night.", "그들은 마치 밤에 병상을 지키고 있기라도 한 듯한 어조로 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Carton stooped to pick up the coat, which lay almost entangling his feet.", "카턴은 거의 발에 걸릴 듯 놓여 있는 외투를 집어 들려고 몸을 굽혔다."], ["As he did so, a small case in which the Doctor was accustomed to carry the lists of his day’s duties, fell lightly on the floor.", "그렇게 하는 사이, 의사가 그날 해야 할 일들의 목록을 넣어 다니던 작은 갑이 바닥에 가볍게 떨어졌다."], ["Carton took it up, and there was a folded paper in it.", "카턴이 그것을 집어 드니, 안에 접힌 종이가 한 장 들어 있었다."], ["“We should look at this!”", "\"이걸 봐두는 게 좋겠소!\""], ["he said. Mr.", "그가 말했다."], ["Lorry nodded his consent.", "로리 씨가 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다."], ["He opened it, and exclaimed, “Thank God!”", "그가 그것을 펼치더니 외쳤다. \"하느님, 감사합니다!\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭐요?\""], ["asked Mr. Lorry, eagerly.", "로리 씨가 다급하게 물었다."]], [["“A moment!", "\"잠깐만!"], ["Let me speak of it in its place.", "차례가 되면 말하겠소."], ["First,”", "우선,\""], ["he put his hand in his coat, and took another paper from it, “that is the certificate which enables me to pass out of this city.", "그는 외투 안에 손을 넣어 또 다른 종이를 꺼냈다. \"이것은 내가 이 도시를 빠져나갈 수 있게 해주는 통행증이오."], ["Look at it.", "보시오."], ["You see--Sydney Carton, an Englishman?”", "알겠소? —— 시드니 카턴, 영국인이라고?\""]], [["Mr. Lorry held it open in his hand, gazing in his earnest face.", "로리 씨는 그것을 손에 펼쳐 든 채, 그의 진지한 얼굴을 응시했다."]], [["“Keep it for me until to-morrow.", "\"내일까지 이걸 맡아주시오."], ["I shall see him to-morrow, you remember, and I had better not take it into the prison.”", "기억하시겠지만 나는 내일 그를 만날 텐데, 이걸 감옥에 가지고 들어가지 않는 편이 낫겠소.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 그렇소?\""]], [["“I don’t know; I prefer not to do so.", "\"모르겠소. 그저 그러고 싶지 않을 뿐이오."], ["Now, take this paper that Doctor Manette has carried about him.", "자, 이제 마네뜨 의사가 몸에 지니고 다니던 이 서류를 받으시오."], ["It is a similar certificate, enabling him and his daughter and her child, at any time, to pass the barrier and the frontier!", "이것도 비슷한 통행증인데, 이분과 따님, 그리고 그 아이가 언제라도 관문과 국경을 통과할 수 있게 해주는 거요!"], ["You see?”", "알겠소?\""]], [["“Yes!”", "\"그렇소!\""]], [["“Perhaps he obtained it as his last and utmost precaution against evil, yesterday.", "\"아마 어제 화를 막으려는 마지막의 최후 예방책으로 이걸 얻었던 모양이오. 날짜가 언제로 되어 있소?"], ["When is it dated?", "하지만 상관없소."], ["But no matter; don’t stay to look; put it up carefully with mine and your own.", "들여다보느라 지체하지 마시오. 내 것과 당신 것과 함께 조심스럽게 챙겨두시오."], ["Now, observe!", "자, 잘 들으시오!"], ["I never doubted until within this hour or two, that he had, or could have such a paper.", "한두 시간 전까지만 해도 나는 그가 그런 서류를 가지고 있거나 가질 수 있으리라곤 조금도 의심치 않았소."], ["It is good, until recalled.", "그것은 취소되기 전까지는 유효하오."], ["But it may be soon recalled, and, I have reason to think, will be.”", "하지만 곧 취소될지도 모르고, 내가 보기엔 그렇게 될 거요.\""]], [["“They are not in danger?”", "\"그분들이 위험에 처한 거요?\""]], [["“They are in great danger.", "\"큰 위험에 처해 있소."], ["They are in danger of denunciation by Madame Defarge.", "마담 드파르주에게 고발당할 위험에 말이오."], ["I know it from her own lips.", "나는 그것을 그 여자 입에서 직접 들었소."], ["I have overheard words of that woman’s, to-night, which have presented their danger to me in strong colours.", "오늘 밤 그 여자의 말을 엿들었는데, 그 말이 그분들의 위험을 내게 짙은 빛깔로 보여주었소."], ["I have lost no time, and since then, I have seen the spy.", "나는 한시도 지체하지 않았고, 그 뒤로 그 첩자를 만났소."], ["He confirms me.", "그자가 내 짐작을 확인해주었소."], ["He knows that a wood-sawyer, living by the prison wall, is under the control of the Defarges, and has been rehearsed by Madame Defarge as to his having seen Her”--he never mentioned Lucie’s name--“making signs and signals to prisoners.", "그자가 알기로는 감옥 담장 옆에 사는 어느 톱장이가 드파르주 부부의 손아귀에 있는데, 마담 드파르주가 그자에게 그녀가 —— 그는 루시의 이름을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다 —— 죄수들에게 신호와 암호를 보내는 것을 봤다고 진술하도록 미리 연습시켜두었다는 거요."], ["It is easy to foresee that the pretence will be the common one, a prison plot, and that it will involve her life--and perhaps her child’s--and perhaps her father’s--for both have been seen with her at that place.", "그 구실이 흔해빠진 것, 곧 감옥 음모로 둔갑하리라는 것, 그리고 그것이 그녀의 목숨을 —— 어쩌면 그 아이의 목숨도 —— 어쩌면 그 아버지의 목숨까지도 —— 옭아매리라는 것은 쉽게 내다볼 수 있소. 두 사람 다 그곳에서 그녀와 함께 있는 것이 목격되었으니까."], ["Don’t look so horrified.", "그렇게 질겁한 얼굴은 하지 마시오."], ["You will save them all.”", "당신이 그들 모두를 구할 테니.\""]], [["“Heaven grant I may, Carton!", "\"하느님께서 그럴 수 있게 해주시기를, 카턴!"], ["But how?”", "하지만 어떻게 말이오?\""]], [["“I am going to tell you how.", "\"어떻게 해야 하는지 내가 말해주겠소."], ["It will depend on you, and it could depend on no better man.", "그것은 당신에게 달려 있고, 당신보다 더 나은 사람에게 맡길 수는 없소."], ["This new denunciation will certainly not take place until after to-morrow; probably not until two or three days afterwards; more probably a week afterwards.", "이 새로운 고발은 분명 내일 이후에야 이루어질 거요. 아마도 이삼일은 지나서, 어쩌면 일주일은 지나서일 가능성이 더 크오."], ["You know it is a capital crime, to mourn for, or sympathise with, a victim of the Guillotine.", "단두대의 희생자를 애도하거나 동정하는 것이 사형에 처할 죄라는 건 당신도 알 거요."], ["She and her father would unquestionably be guilty of this crime, and this woman (the inveteracy of whose pursuit cannot be described) would wait to add that strength to her case, and make herself doubly sure.", "그녀와 그 아버지는 틀림없이 이 죄를 짓게 될 것이고, 그 여자는 (그 추적의 집요함은 이루 말로 다 할 수 없소) 자기 사건에 그 무게를 더해 두 배로 확실하게 하려고 기다릴 거요."], ["You follow me?”", "내 말을 따라오고 있소?\""]], [["“So attentively, and with so much confidence in what you say, that for the moment I lose sight,”", "\"어찌나 주의 깊게, 또 당신 말에 어찌나 깊은 신뢰를 가지고 듣는지, 순간만큼은,\""], ["touching the back of the Doctor’s chair, “even of this distress.”", "그가 의사의 의자 등받이를 만지며 말했다. \"이 비통함조차 잊을 정도요.\""]], [["“You have money, and can buy the means of travelling to the seacoast as quickly as the journey can be made.", "\"당신에게는 돈이 있고, 길을 갈 수 있는 한 빨리 해안까지 갈 수단을 마련할 수 있소."], ["Your preparations have been completed for some days, to return to England.", "영국으로 돌아갈 준비는 며칠 전에 이미 끝나 있소."], ["Early to-morrow have your horses ready, so that they may be in starting trim at two o’clock in the afternoon.”", "내일 아침 일찍 말들을 준비시켜서 오후 두시에 출발할 채비가 갖추어지도록 하시오.\""]], [["“It shall be done!”", "\"그렇게 하겠소!\""]], [["His manner was so fervent and inspiring, that Mr. Lorry caught the flame, and was as quick as youth.", "그의 태도가 어찌나 열정적이고 사람을 고무하는지, 로리 씨도 그 불길에 옮아붙어 젊은이처럼 민첩해졌다."]], [["“You are a noble heart.", "\"당신은 고귀한 마음을 지닌 사람이오."], ["Did I say we could depend upon no better man?", "당신보다 더 나은 사람에게 맡길 수 없다고 내가 말하지 않았소?"], ["Tell her, to-night, what you know of her danger as involving her child and her father.", "오늘 밤 그녀에게, 그 위험이 아이와 아버지까지 옭아맨다는 걸, 당신이 아는 대로 말해주시오."], ["Dwell upon that, for she would lay her own fair head beside her husband’s cheerfully.”", "그 점을 거듭 강조하시오. 그렇지 않으면 그녀는 자기 고운 머리를 남편 곁에 기꺼이 내려놓으려 할 테니.\""], ["He faltered for an instant; then went on as before.", "그는 한순간 말을 멈칫하더니, 이내 다시 이어갔다."], ["“For the sake of her child and her father, press upon her the necessity of leaving Paris, with them and you, at that hour.", "\"그녀의 아이와 아버지를 위해서라도, 그 시각에 그들과 당신과 함께 빠리를 떠나야 한다는 걸 그녀에게 강력히 권하시오."], ["Tell her that it was her husband’s last arrangement.", "그것이 남편의 마지막 당부였다고 말하시오."], ["Tell her that more depends upon it than she dare believe, or hope.", "그것에 그녀가 감히 믿거나 바라는 것보다 더 많은 것이 걸려 있다고 말하시오."], ["You think that her father, even in this sad state, will submit himself to her; do you not?”", "그녀의 아버지가, 이 슬픈 상태에서도, 그녀에게 순순히 따르리라고 생각하시오? 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“I am sure of it.”", "\"확신하오.\""]], [["“I thought so.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각했소."], ["Quietly and steadily have all these arrangements made in the courtyard here, even to the taking of your own seat in the carriage.", "여기 안마당에서 이 모든 준비를 조용히 그리고 차분하게 갖추시오, 당신이 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉는 것까지 말이오."], ["The moment I come to you, take me in, and drive away.”", "내가 당신에게 가는 순간, 나를 태우고 곧장 달리시오.\""]], [["“I understand that I wait for you under all circumstances?”", "\"어떤 일이 있어도 당신을 기다려야 한다는 거지요?\""]], [["“You have my certificate in your hand with the rest, you know, and will reserve my place.", "\"아시다시피 내 통행증이 나머지 것들과 함께 당신 손에 있으니, 내 자리를 비워두시오."], ["Wait for nothing but to have my place occupied, and then for England!”", "내 자리가 채워지는 것 말고는 아무것도 기다리지 말고, 그다음엔 영국으로!\""]], [["“Why, then,” said Mr.", "\"그렇다면 말이오,\""], ["Lorry, grasping his eager but so firm and steady hand, “it does not all depend on one old man, but I shall have a young and ardent man at my side.”", "로리 씨가 열정적이면서도 그토록 굳건하고 흔들림 없는 그의 손을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"이 모든 게 한 늙은이에게만 달린 게 아니라, 내 곁에 젊고 열정적인 사람이 함께 있게 되는구려.\""]], [["“By the help of Heaven you shall!", "\"하느님의 도우심으로 그렇게 될 거요!"], ["Promise me solemnly that nothing will influence you to alter the course on which we now stand pledged to one another.”", "우리가 지금 서로에게 맹세한 이 길을 어떤 일이 있어도 바꾸지 않겠노라고 엄숙히 약속해주시오.\" \"무슨 일이 있어도 그러지 않겠소, 카턴.\""]], [["“Nothing, Carton.”", "\"내일 이 말을 기억하시오."], ["“Remember these words to-morrow: change the course, or delay in it--for any reason--and no life can possibly be saved, and many lives must inevitably be sacrificed.”", "길을 바꾸거나, 그 길에서 지체하면 —— 어떤 이유에서든 —— 단 한 사람의 목숨도 구할 수 없을 것이고, 많은 목숨이 어쩔 수 없이 희생되고 말 거요.\""]], [["“I will remember them.", "\"기억하겠소."], ["I hope to do my part faithfully.”", "내 몫을 충실히 해내기를 바라오.\""]], [["“And I hope to do mine.", "\"나도 내 몫을 해내기를 바라오."], ["Now, good bye!”", "자, 그럼, 안녕히!\""]], [["Though he said it with a grave smile of earnestness, and though he even put the old man’s hand to his lips, he did not part from him then.", "그는 진심 어린 엄숙한 미소를 띠고 그 말을 했고, 심지어 노인의 손을 자기 입술에 가져다 대기까지 했으나, 그때 그와 헤어지지는 않았다."], ["He helped him so far to arouse the rocking figure before the dying embers, as to get a cloak and hat put upon it, and to tempt it forth to find where the bench and work were hidden that it still moaningly besought to have.", "그는 꺼져가는 잉걸불 앞에서 몸을 흔들고 있는 그 형상을 일으켜 세우도록, 거기에 외투와 모자를 입히고, 그가 아직도 신음하며 달라고 애원하는 작업대와 작업물이 어디 숨겨져 있는지 찾으러 나가도록 꾀어내는 일을 거들었다."], ["He walked on the other side of it and protected it to the courtyard of the house where the afflicted heart--so happy in the memorable time when he had revealed his own desolate heart to it--outwatched the awful night.", "그는 그 형상의 다른 한쪽에서 나란히 걸으며 그를 보호하여, 저 비통한 마음이 —— 그가 자신의 황량한 마음을 그 마음에 털어놓았던 그 잊지 못할 시절에는 그토록 행복했던 마음이 —— 그 무서운 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새우고 있는 집의 안마당까지 데려갔다."], ["He entered the courtyard and remained there for a few moments alone, looking up at the light in the window of her room.", "그는 안마당으로 들어가 잠시 홀로 그곳에 남아, 그녀 방의 창문에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 올려다보았다."], ["Before he went away, he breathed a blessing towards it, and a Farewell.", "떠나기 전에, 그는 그 불빛을 향해 축복을, 그리고 작별 인사를 나직이 띄워 보냈다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIII. Fifty-two", "# CHAPTER XIII. Fifty-two"]], [["In the black prison of the Conciergerie, the doomed of the day awaited their fate.", "검은 콩씨에르주리 감옥에서, 그날의 사형수들은 자신들의 운명을 기다리고 있었다."], ["They were in number as the weeks of the year.", "그 수는 한 해의 주(週)의 수와 같았다."], ["Fifty-two were to roll that afternoon on the life-tide of the city to the boundless everlasting sea.", "쉰두 명이 그날 오후, 도시의 생명의 물결을 타고 끝없이 영원한 바다로 흘러갈 것이었다."], ["Before their cells were quit of them, new occupants were appointed; before their blood ran into the blood spilled yesterday, the blood that was to mingle with theirs to-morrow was already set apart.", "그들이 감방을 비우기도 전에 새로운 입주자가 정해졌고, 그들의 피가 어제 흘린 피 속으로 흘러들기도 전에 내일 그들의 피와 뒤섞일 피가 이미 따로 마련되어 있었다."]], [["Two score and twelve were told off.", "쉰두 명이 호명되었다."], ["From the farmer-general of seventy, whose riches could not buy his life, to the seamstress of twenty, whose poverty and obscurity could not save her.", "그 많은 재산으로도 목숨을 살 수 없었던 일흔살의 징세 청부인부터, 가난과 미천함으로도 자신을 구할 수 없었던 스무살의 침모에 이르기까지."], ["Physical diseases, engendered in the vices and neglects of men, will seize on victims of all degrees; and the frightful moral disorder, born of unspeakable suffering, intolerable oppression, and heartless indifference, smote equally without distinction.", "인간의 악덕과 태만에서 비롯된 육체의 질병이 온갖 계층의 희생자를 덮치듯, 말할 수 없는 고통과 견딜 수 없는 압제와 무정한 무관심에서 태어난 그 무시무시한 도덕적 혼란도 차별 없이 똑같이 내리쳤다."]], [["Charles Darnay, alone in a cell, had sustained himself with no flattering delusion since he came to it from the Tribunal.", "찰스 다네이는 감방에 홀로 있으면서, 재판소에서 그곳으로 돌아온 이래 어떤 달콤한 망상으로도 스스로를 지탱하지 않았다."], ["In every line of the narrative he had heard, he had heard his condemnation.", "그가 들은 진술의 한줄 한줄에서, 그는 자신의 유죄 선고를 들었다."], ["He had fully comprehended that no personal influence could possibly save him, that he was virtually sentenced by the millions, and that units could avail him nothing.", "그는 어떤 개인적인 영향력으로도 자신을 구할 수 없으며, 사실상 수백만의 사람들에게 선고받은 셈이고, 한두 사람의 힘으로는 아무것도 할 수 없다는 것을 충분히 깨닫고 있었다."]], [["Nevertheless, it was not easy, with the face of his beloved wife fresh before him, to compose his mind to what it must bear.", "그렇지만 사랑하는 아내의 얼굴이 눈앞에 생생한 채로, 견뎌야 할 일을 향해 마음을 가라앉히기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["His hold on life was strong, and it was very, very hard, to loosen; by gradual efforts and degrees unclosed a little here, it clenched the tighter there; and when he brought his strength to bear on that hand and it yielded, this was closed again.", "삶을 붙드는 그의 손아귀는 강했고, 그것을 풀어놓기란 무척이나, 무척이나 힘겨웠다. 점차적인 노력과 단계를 거쳐 여기를 조금 펴놓으면, 저기가 더 단단히 움켜쥐었고, 그 손에 힘을 모아 그것이 누그러지면, 이번에는 이쪽이 다시 닫혔다."], ["There was a hurry, too, in all his thoughts, a turbulent and heated working of his heart, that contended against resignation.", "게다가 그의 모든 생각 속에는 조급함이, 체념에 맞서 싸우는 격렬하고 뜨거운 심장의 요동이 있었다."], ["If, for a moment, he did feel resigned, then his wife and child who had to live after him, seemed to protest and to make it a selfish thing.", "한순간 정말로 체념한 듯하다가도, 그가 죽은 뒤에도 살아가야 할 아내와 아이가 항의하며 그것을 이기적인 일로 만드는 것만 같았다."]], [["But, all this was at first.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 처음의 일이었다."], ["Before long, the consideration that there was no disgrace in the fate he must meet, and that numbers went the same road wrongfully, and trod it firmly every day, sprang up to stimulate him.", "머잖아, 자신이 맞이해야 할 운명에 아무런 치욕도 없으며, 수많은 사람들이 부당하게 똑같은 길을 갔고 날마다 그 길을 의연히 밟아간다는 생각이 솟아올라 그를 북돋웠다."], ["Next followed the thought that much of the future peace of mind enjoyable by the dear ones, depended on his quiet fortitude.", "다음으로는 사랑하는 이들이 앞으로 누릴 수 있는 마음의 평화가 상당 부분 자신의 조용한 의연함에 달려 있다는 생각이 뒤따랐다."], ["So, by degrees he calmed into the better state, when he could raise his thoughts much higher, and draw comfort down.", "그리하여 그는 차츰 더 나은 상태로 가라앉았고, 그제야 생각을 훨씬 더 높은 곳으로 끌어올려 위안을 끌어내릴 수 있었다."]], [["Before it had set in dark on the night of his condemnation, he had travelled thus far on his last way.", "유죄 선고를 받은 그날 밤 어둠이 내리기 전에, 그는 마지막 길을 이만큼 걸어와 있었다."], ["Being allowed to purchase the means of writing, and a light, he sat down to write until such time as the prison lamps should be extinguished.", "글 쓸 도구와 등불을 살 수 있게 허락받자, 그는 감옥의 등불이 꺼질 때까지 글을 쓰려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["He wrote a long letter to Lucie, showing her that he had known nothing of her father’s imprisonment, until he had heard of it from herself, and that he had been as ignorant as she of his father’s and uncle’s responsibility for that misery, until the paper had been read.", "그는 루시에게 긴 편지를 써서, 그녀에게서 직접 듣기 전에는 그녀의 아버지가 투옥되었던 일을 전혀 몰랐으며, 그 문서가 낭독되기 전까지는 그 비참한 일에 자기 아버지와 숙부가 책임이 있었다는 사실을 그녀만큼이나 알지 못했음을 밝혔다."], ["He had already explained to her that his concealment from herself of the name he had relinquished, was the one condition--fully intelligible now--that her father had attached to their betrothal, and was the one promise he had still exacted on the morning of their marriage.", "그가 버린 이름을 그녀에게 숨겼던 것은 — 이제는 충분히 이해할 만한 — 그녀의 아버지가 그들의 약혼에 붙인 단 하나의 조건이었으며, 결혼식날 아침에도 여전히 요구했던 단 하나의 약속이었음을 그는 이미 그녀에게 설명한 바 있었다."], ["He entreated her, for her father’s sake, never to seek to know whether her father had become oblivious of the existence of the paper, or had had it recalled to him (for the moment, or for good), by the story of the Tower, on that old Sunday under the dear old plane-tree in the garden.", "그는 그녀에게, 그녀의 아버지를 위해서라도, 그 옛날 일요일 정원의 그리운 플라타너스나무 아래에서 들은 탑 이야기로 인해 그녀의 아버지가 그 문서의 존재를 잊어버렸는지, 아니면 (일시적으로든 영원히든) 그것을 다시 떠올리게 되었는지를 결코 알려 하지 말라고 간청했다."], ["If he had preserved any definite remembrance of it, there could be no doubt that he had supposed it destroyed with the Bastille, when he had found no mention of it among the relics of prisoners which the populace had discovered there, and which had been described to all the world.", "만일 그가 그것에 대한 어떤 분명한 기억을 간직하고 있었다 해도, 민중이 바스띠유에서 찾아내어 온 세상에 알려진 죄수들의 유물 가운데 그것에 대한 언급이 전혀 없었으니, 그것이 바스띠유와 함께 파괴되었다고 짐작했으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["He besought her--though he added that he knew it was needless--to console her father, by impressing him through every tender means she could think of, with the truth that he had done nothing for which he could justly reproach himself, but had uniformly forgotten himself for their joint sakes.", "그는 그녀에게 — 비록 그럴 필요가 없음을 안다고 덧붙이면서도 — 그녀가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 다정한 방법으로 아버지에게, 자기는 정당하게 스스로를 책망할 만한 일을 한 적이 없으며 오로지 두 사람을 위해 한결같이 자신을 잊었을 뿐이라는 진실을 새겨주어 그를 위로해달라고 빌었다."], ["Next to her preservation of his own last grateful love and blessing, and her overcoming of her sorrow, to devote herself to their dear child, he adjured her, as they would meet in Heaven, to comfort her father.", "그녀가 그 자신의 마지막 감사의 사랑과 축복을 간직하고, 슬픔을 이겨내어 사랑하는 아이에게 헌신하는 것 다음으로, 그는 천국에서 다시 만날 것을 두고, 그녀에게 아버지를 위로해달라고 당부했다."]], [["To her father himself, he wrote in the same strain; but, he told her father that he expressly confided his wife and child to his care.", "그녀의 아버지 자신에게도 그는 같은 어조로 썼다. 다만 그는 장인에게, 자기 아내와 아이를 그의 보살핌에 분명히 맡긴다고 말했다."], ["And he told him this, very strongly, with the hope of rousing him from any despondency or dangerous retrospect towards which he foresaw he might be tending.", "그리고 장인이 빠져들지도 모른다고 예견되는 어떤 낙담이나 위험한 회상에서 그를 일깨우려는 바람에서, 이를 무척이나 힘주어 말했다."]], [["To Mr. Lorry, he commended them all, and explained his worldly affairs.", "로리 씨에게는 그들 모두를 부탁하며, 자신의 세속적인 일들을 설명했다."], ["That done, with many added sentences of grateful friendship and warm attachment, all was done.", "그 일을 마치고, 감사 어린 우정과 따뜻한 애정의 문장을 여러 줄 덧붙이니, 모든 것이 끝났다."], ["He never thought of Carton.", "그는 카턴을 떠올리지 못했다."], ["His mind was so full of the others, that he never once thought of him.", "그의 마음이 다른 이들로 가득 차 있어, 단 한번도 그를 생각하지 못했다."]], [["He had time to finish these letters before the lights were put out.", "그는 불이 꺼지기 전에 이 편지들을 마칠 시간이 있었다."], ["When he lay down on his straw bed, he thought he had done with this world.", "짚으로 된 침상에 몸을 누이며, 그는 이 세상과의 인연을 다했다고 생각했다."]], [["But, it beckoned him back in his sleep, and showed itself in shining forms.", "그러나 세상은 그가 잠든 사이 그를 다시 불러들였고, 빛나는 모습으로 나타났다."], ["Free and happy, back in the old house in Soho (though it had nothing in it like the real house), unaccountably released and light of heart, he was with Lucie again, and she told him it was all a dream, and he had never gone away.", "자유롭고 행복하게, 쏘호의 옛집으로 돌아와 (비록 그 안에는 진짜 집과 닮은 것이 하나도 없었지만), 영문 모르게 풀려나 마음 가벼이, 그는 루시와 다시 함께 있었고, 그녀는 그에게 그 모든 것이 꿈이었으며 그는 결코 떠난 적이 없다고 말했다."], ["A pause of forgetfulness, and then he had even suffered, and had come back to her, dead and at peace, and yet there was no difference in him.", "망각의 한순간이 지나자, 그는 심지어 고통을 겪기도 했고, 죽어 평온해진 채 그녀에게 돌아왔으되, 그에게는 아무런 달라짐이 없었다."], ["Another pause of oblivion, and he awoke in the sombre morning, unconscious where he was or what had happened, until it flashed upon his mind, “this is the day of my death!”", "또 한번의 망각의 순간이 지나자, 그는 어두운 아침에 깨어나, 자신이 어디 있는지 무슨 일이 있었는지도 의식하지 못하다가, 문득 머릿속에 번뜩 떠올랐다. \"오늘이 내가 죽는 날이로구나!\""]], [["Thus, had he come through the hours, to the day when the fifty-two heads were to fall.", "이렇게 그는 시간을 헤치고, 쉰두 개의 머리가 떨어질 그날에 이르렀다."], ["And now, while he was composed, and hoped that he could meet the end with quiet heroism, a new action began in his waking thoughts, which was very difficult to master.", "이제, 그가 마음을 가라앉히고 조용한 영웅적 자세로 마지막을 맞이할 수 있기를 바라고 있을 때, 깨어 있는 그의 생각 속에 새로운 작용이 시작되었으니, 다스리기가 무척 어려운 것이었다."]], [["He had never seen the instrument that was to terminate his life.", "그는 자신의 목숨을 끝낼 그 기구를 한번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["How high it was from the ground, how many steps it had, where he would be stood, how he would be touched, whether the touching hands would be dyed red, which way his face would be turned, whether he would be the first, or might be the last: these and many similar questions, in nowise directed by his will, obtruded themselves over and over again, countless times.", "그것이 땅에서 얼마나 높은지, 계단이 몇 개인지, 자신이 어디에 세워질지, 어떻게 손이 닿을지, 그 닿는 손이 붉게 물들어 있을지, 자신의 얼굴이 어느 쪽으로 향할지, 자신이 첫번째일지 아니면 마지막이 될지. 이런 질문들과 그 비슷한 많은 질문들이, 그의 의지와는 전혀 무관하게, 거듭하여, 헤아릴 수 없이 여러번 끼어들었다."], ["Neither were they connected with fear: he was conscious of no fear.", "그것들이 두려움과 연결된 것도 아니었으니, 그는 아무런 두려움도 의식하지 못했다."], ["Rather, they originated in a strange besetting desire to know what to do when the time came; a desire gigantically disproportionate to the few swift moments to which it referred; a wondering that was more like the wondering of some other spirit within his, than his own.", "오히려 그것들은 때가 오면 무엇을 해야 할지 알고 싶은 기이하게 끈질긴 욕구에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그것이 가리키는 짧고 빠른 몇 순간에 비하면 엄청나게 어울리지 않게 거대한 욕구였고, 그 자신의 의아함이라기보다는 그의 안에 있는 어떤 다른 영혼의 의아함에 더 가까운 것이었다."]], [["The hours went on as he walked to and fro, and the clocks struck the numbers he would never hear again.", "그가 이리저리 거니는 동안 시간은 흘러갔고, 시계는 그가 다시는 듣지 못할 숫자들을 쳤다."], ["Nine gone for ever, ten gone for ever, eleven gone for ever, twelve coming on to pass away.", "아홉시가 영영 가버렸고, 열시가 영영 가버렸고, 열한시가 영영 가버렸고, 열두시가 다가와 지나가려 했다."], ["After a hard contest with that eccentric action of thought which had last perplexed him, he had got the better of it.", "그를 마지막으로 어지럽혔던 그 기이한 생각의 작용과 힘겨운 싸움을 벌인 끝에, 그는 그것을 이겨냈다."], ["He walked up and down, softly repeating their names to himself.", "그는 이리저리 거닐며, 그들의 이름을 나직이 되뇌었다."], ["The worst of the strife was over.", "싸움의 가장 험한 고비는 지나갔다."], ["He could walk up and down, free from distracting fancies, praying for himself and for them.", "그는 마음을 흩뜨리는 공상에서 벗어나, 자신을 위해 그리고 그들을 위해 기도하며 이리저리 거닐 수 있었다."]], [["Twelve gone for ever.", "열두시가 영영 가버렸다."]], [["He had been apprised that the final hour was Three, and he knew he would be summoned some time earlier, inasmuch as the tumbrils jolted heavily and slowly through the streets.", "그는 마지막 시각이 세시라는 것을 들어 알고 있었고, 호송 수레가 거리를 무겁고 느리게 덜컹거리며 지나가는 만큼 그보다 얼마쯤 일찍 호출되리라는 것을 알았다."], ["Therefore, he resolved to keep Two before his mind, as the hour, and so to strengthen himself in the interval that he might be able, after that time, to strengthen others.", "그래서 그는 두시를 그 시각으로 마음속에 새겨두기로 했고, 그 사이에 스스로를 단단히 다져, 그 시각이 지난 뒤에는 다른 이들을 다잡을 수 있게 되기로 결심했다."]], [["Walking regularly to and fro with his arms folded on his breast, a very different man from the prisoner, who had walked to and fro at La Force, he heard One struck away from him, without surprise.", "가슴에 팔짱을 끼고 규칙적으로 이리저리 거닐며, 라포르스에서 이리저리 거닐던 그 죄수와는 사뭇 다른 사람이 되어, 그는 한시를 알리는 소리가 멀리서 울리는 것을 놀라움 없이 들었다."], ["The hour had measured like most other hours.", "그 한시간은 다른 대부분의 시간들과 다름없이 흘러갔던 것이다."], ["Devoutly thankful to Heaven for his recovered self-possession, he thought, “There is but another now,” and turned to walk again.", "되찾은 침착함에 대해 하늘에 경건히 감사하며, 그는 \"이제 한시간이 남았을 뿐이다\"라고 생각하고는 다시 거닐려고 돌아섰다."]], [["Footsteps in the stone passage outside the door.", "문밖 돌 복도에 발소리가 났다."], ["He stopped.", "그는 멈춰 섰다."]], [["The key was put in the lock, and turned.", "열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂혀 돌아갔다. 문이 열리기 전에, 혹은 열리면서, 한 남자가 영어로 나직이 말했다."], ["Before the door was opened, or as it opened, a man said in a low voice, in English: “He has never seen me here; I have kept out of his way.", "\"그는 여기서 한번도 날 본 적이 없소. 내가 그의 눈에 띄지 않게 피해 있었으니."], ["Go you in alone; I wait near.", "당신 혼자 들어가시오. 나는 근처에서 기다리겠소."], ["Lose no time!”", "한순간도 허비하지 마시오!\""]], [["The door was quickly opened and closed, and there stood before him face to face, quiet, intent upon him, with the light of a smile on his features, and a cautionary finger on his lip, Sydney Carton.", "문이 재빨리 열렸다 닫혔고, 그의 앞에 얼굴을 맞대고 서 있는 이가 있었으니, 조용하게, 그에게 골똘히 시선을 모은 채, 얼굴에는 미소의 빛을 띠고, 입술에는 조심하라는 손가락을 댄, 시드니 카턴이었다."]], [["There was something so bright and remarkable in his look, that, for the first moment, the prisoner misdoubted him to be an apparition of his own imagining.", "그의 표정에는 무언가 너무나 환하고 예사롭지 않은 것이 있어서, 첫 순간 죄수는 그가 자신의 상상이 빚어낸 환영이 아닐까 의심했다."], ["But, he spoke, and it was his voice; he took the prisoner’s hand, and it was his real grasp.", "그러나 그가 말을 했고, 그것은 그의 목소리였다. 그가 죄수의 손을 잡았고, 그것은 그의 실제 손아귀였다."]], [["“Of all the people upon earth, you least expected to see me?”", "\"이 세상 모든 사람 가운데, 자네가 만나리라고 가장 예상하지 못한 사람이 나겠지?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I could not believe it to be you.", "\"자네라는 것을 믿을 수가 없었네."], ["I can scarcely believe it now.", "지금도 좀처럼 믿기지가 않아. 자네는 설마—\""], ["You are not”--the apprehension came suddenly into his mind--“a prisoner?”", "갑자기 어떤 불안이 그의 마음에 떠올랐다. \"죄수는 아니지?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닐세."], ["I am accidentally possessed of a power over one of the keepers here, and in virtue of it I stand before you.", "나는 우연히 이곳 간수 하나에게 영향력을 갖게 되었고, 그 덕에 자네 앞에 서 있는 거야."], ["I come from her--your wife, dear Darnay.”", "나는 그녀에게서 — 자네 아내에게서 왔네, 다네이.\""]], [["The prisoner wrung his hand.", "죄수는 그의 손을 꽉 잡았다."]], [["“I bring you a request from her.”", "\"그녀에게서 부탁을 하나 가지고 왔네.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭔가?\""]], [["“A most earnest, pressing, and emphatic entreaty, addressed to you in the most pathetic tones of the voice so dear to you, that you well remember.”", "\"자네가 잘 기억하는, 그토록 소중한 그 목소리의 더없이 애절한 어조로 자네에게 건네는, 더없이 간절하고 절박하고 단호한 청일세.\""]], [["The prisoner turned his face partly aside.", "죄수는 얼굴을 반쯤 옆으로 돌렸다."]], [["“You have no time to ask me why I bring it, or what it means; I have no time to tell you.", "\"내가 왜 그것을 가져왔는지, 그게 무슨 뜻인지 물을 시간이 자네에겐 없네. 나도 말해줄 시간이 없고."], ["You must comply with it--take off those boots you wear, and draw on these of mine.”", "자네는 그것에 따라야 하네 — 자네가 신은 그 장화를 벗고, 이 내 장화를 신게.\""]], [["There was a chair against the wall of the cell, behind the prisoner.", "죄수 뒤로, 감방 벽에 의자가 하나 놓여 있었다."], ["Carton, pressing forward, had already, with the speed of lightning, got him down into it, and stood over him, barefoot.", "카턴은 앞으로 밀고 들어가, 번개 같은 속도로 이미 그를 그 의자에 앉혀놓고는, 맨발로 그를 굽어보고 섰다."]], [["“Draw on these boots of mine.", "\"이 내 장화를 신게."], ["Put your hands to them; put your will to them.", "손을 갖다 대게. 의지를 갖다 대."], ["Quick!”", "어서!\""]], [["“Carton, there is no escaping from this place; it never can be done.", "\"카턴, 이곳에서 빠져나갈 길은 없네. 결코 해낼 수 없는 일이야."], ["You will only die with me.", "자네만 나와 함께 죽게 될 뿐일세."], ["It is madness.”", "이건 미친 짓이야.\""]], [["“It would be madness if I asked you to escape; but do I?", "\"내가 자네에게 도망치라고 청한다면 미친 짓이겠지. 하지만 내가 그러던가?"], ["When I ask you to pass out at that door, tell me it is madness and remain here.", "내가 자네더러 저 문으로 나가라고 청하거든, 그때 미친 짓이라고 말하고 여기 남게."], ["Change that cravat for this of mine, that coat for this of mine.", "그 넥타이를 이 내 것으로 바꾸고, 그 외투를 이 내 것으로 바꾸게."], ["While you do it, let me take this ribbon from your hair, and shake out your hair like this of mine!”", "자네가 그러는 동안, 자네 머리에서 이 리본을 풀고, 자네 머리를 이 내 머리처럼 흐트러뜨리게 해주게!\""]], [["With wonderful quickness, and with a strength both of will and action, that appeared quite supernatural, he forced all these changes upon him.", "놀라운 민첩함으로, 그리고 자못 초자연적으로 보이는 의지와 행동의 힘으로, 그는 이 모든 변화를 그에게 강제했다."], ["The prisoner was like a young child in his hands.", "죄수는 그의 손안에서 어린아이 같았다."]], [["“Carton!", "\"카턴!"], ["Dear Carton!", "카턴! 이건 미친 짓일세."], ["It is madness.", "이뤄질 수 없는 일이야."], ["It cannot be accomplished, it never can be done, it has been attempted, and has always failed.", "결코 해낼 수 없네. 시도된 적이 있었지만 늘 실패했어."], ["I implore you not to add your death to the bitterness of mine.”", "내 죽음의 쓰라림에 자네의 죽음까지 더하지 말아주게. 제발 비네.\""]], [["“Do I ask you, my dear Darnay, to pass the door?", "\"내가 자네에게, 다네이, 저 문으로 나가라고 청하던가?"], ["When I ask that, refuse.", "내가 그것을 청하거든 거절하게."], ["There are pen and ink and paper on this table.", "이 탁자 위에 펜과 잉크와 종이가 있네."], ["Is your hand steady enough to write?”", "자네 손이 글을 쓸 만큼 흔들리지 않나?\""]], [["“It was when you came in.”", "\"자네가 들어왔을 때는 그랬네.\""]], [["“Steady it again, and write what I shall dictate.", "\"다시 다잡고, 내가 부르는 대로 쓰게."], ["Quick, friend, quick!”", "어서, 이 사람아, 어서!\""]], [["Pressing his hand to his bewildered head, Darnay sat down at the table.", "어리둥절한 머리에 손을 짚으며, 다네이는 탁자에 앉았다."], ["Carton, with his right hand in his breast, stood close beside him.", "카턴은 오른손을 가슴에 넣은 채, 그의 바로 곁에 섰다."]], [["“Write exactly as I speak.”", "\"내가 말하는 그대로 쓰게.\""]], [["“To whom do I address it?”", "\"누구 앞으로 쓰는 건가?\""]], [["“To no one.”", "\"아무에게도 아닐세.\""], ["Carton still had his hand in his breast.", "카턴은 여전히 손을 가슴에 넣고 있었다."]], [["“Do I date it?”", "\"날짜를 적나?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니.\""]], [["The prisoner looked up, at each question.", "질문할 때마다 죄수는 고개를 들었다."], ["Carton, standing over him with his hand in his breast, looked down.", "손을 가슴에 넣은 채 그를 굽어보고 선 카턴은 아래를 내려다보았다."]], [["“‘If you remember,’”", "\"'당신이 만일,'\""], ["said Carton, dictating, “‘the words that passed between us, long ago, you will readily comprehend this when you see it.", "카턴이 받아쓰게 했다. \"'오래전에 우리 사이에 오간 말을 기억한다면, 이것을 볼 때 쉽게 이해할 것이오."], ["You do remember them, I know.", "당신이 그 말을 기억한다는 것을 나는 아오."], ["It is not in your nature to forget them.’”", "그것을 잊는 것은 당신의 천성에 없는 일이니.'\""]], [["He was drawing his hand from his breast; the prisoner chancing to look up in his hurried wonder as he wrote, the hand stopped, closing upon something.", "그는 가슴에서 손을 빼내고 있었다. 죄수가 글을 쓰다가 다급한 의아함에 우연히 고개를 들자, 그 손이 무언가를 움켜쥔 채 멈췄다."]], [["“Have you written ‘forget them’?”", "\"'그것을 잊는'이라고 썼나?\""], ["Carton asked.", "카턴이 물었다."]], [["“I have.", "\"썼네."], ["Is that a weapon in your hand?”", "자네 손에 든 게 무기인가?\""]], [["“No; I am not armed.”", "\"아닐세. 나는 무장하지 않았어.\""]], [["“What is it in your hand?”", "\"자네 손에 든 게 뭔가?\""]], [["“You shall know directly.", "\"곧 알게 될 걸세."], ["Write on; there are but a few words more.”", "계속 쓰게. 이제 몇 마디 남지 않았어.\""], ["He dictated again.", "그는 다시 받아쓰게 했다."], ["“‘I am thankful that the time has come, when I can prove them.", "\"'나는 그것을 증명할 수 있는 때가 왔음에 감사하오."], ["That I do so is no subject for regret or grief.’”", "내가 그리하는 것은 후회나 슬픔의 대상이 아니오.'\""], ["As he said these words with his eyes fixed on the writer, his hand slowly and softly moved down close to the writer’s face.", "이 말을 하며 쓰는 이에게 눈길을 고정한 채, 그의 손이 천천히 그리고 부드럽게 쓰는 이의 얼굴 가까이로 내려갔다."]], [["The pen dropped from Darnay’s fingers on the table, and he looked about him vacantly.", "펜이 다네이의 손가락에서 탁자 위로 떨어졌고, 그는 멍하니 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“What vapour is that?”", "\"이게 무슨 김인가?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Vapour?”", "\"김?\""]], [["“Something that crossed me?”", "\"무언가 내 곁을 스쳤는데?\""]], [["“I am conscious of nothing; there can be nothing here.", "\"나는 아무것도 느끼지 못하네. 여기 아무것도 있을 리 없어."], ["Take up the pen and finish.", "펜을 들고 마저 쓰게."], ["Hurry, hurry!”", "어서, 어서!\""]], [["As if his memory were impaired, or his faculties disordered, the prisoner made an effort to rally his attention.", "마치 기억이 손상되었거나 정신이 흐트러진 듯, 죄수는 주의를 가다듬으려 애썼다."], ["As he looked at Carton with clouded eyes and with an altered manner of breathing, Carton--his hand again in his breast--looked steadily at him.", "흐릿한 눈으로, 달라진 숨결로 카턴을 바라보았을 때, 카턴은 — 다시 손을 가슴에 넣고 — 그를 물끄러미 바라보았다."]], [["“Hurry, hurry!”", "\"어서, 어서!\""]], [["The prisoner bent over the paper, once more.", "죄수는 다시 한번 종이 위로 몸을 숙였다."]], [["“‘If it had been otherwise;’”", "\"'만일 사정이 달랐더라면,'\""], ["Carton’s hand was again watchfully and softly stealing down; “‘I never should have used the longer opportunity.", "카턴의 손이 다시 조심스럽게 그리고 부드럽게 슬며시 내려가고 있었다. \"'나는 결코 그 더 긴 기회를 쓰지 않았을 것이오."], ["If it had been otherwise;’”", "만일 사정이 달랐더라면,'\""], ["the hand was at the prisoner’s face; “‘I should but have had so much the more to answer for.", "손이 죄수의 얼굴에 이르렀다. \"'나는 그저 그만큼 더 많은 책임을 져야 했을 것이오."], ["If it had been otherwise--’”", "만일 사정이 달랐더라면—'\""], ["Carton looked at the pen and saw it was trailing off into unintelligible signs.", "카턴은 펜을 바라보았고, 그것이 알아볼 수 없는 표시로 흐려져 가는 것을 보았다."]], [["Carton’s hand moved back to his breast no more.", "카턴의 손은 더이상 가슴으로 돌아가지 않았다."], ["The prisoner sprang up with a reproachful look, but Carton’s hand was close and firm at his nostrils, and Carton’s left arm caught him round the waist.", "죄수가 책망하는 눈빛으로 벌떡 일어섰으나, 카턴의 손은 그의 콧구멍에 바싹 단단히 갖다 댄 채였고, 카턴의 왼팔이 그의 허리를 휘감았다."], ["For a few seconds he faintly struggled with the man who had come to lay down his life for him; but, within a minute or so, he was stretched insensible on the ground.", "잠시 그는 자신을 위해 목숨을 내려놓으러 온 그 사람과 힘없이 몸싸움을 벌였으나, 일분쯤 지나자 의식을 잃고 바닥에 쭉 뻗어버렸다."]], [["Quickly, but with hands as true to the purpose as his heart was, Carton dressed himself in the clothes the prisoner had laid aside, combed back his hair, and tied it with the ribbon the prisoner had worn.", "재빠르게, 그러나 그 마음만큼이나 그 목적에 충실한 손으로, 카턴은 죄수가 벗어둔 옷으로 갈아입고, 머리를 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 뒤 죄수가 매고 있던 리본으로 묶었다. 그러고는 나직이 불렀다."], ["Then, he softly called, “Enter there!", "\"거기 들어오시오! 들어와요!\""], ["Come in!” and the Spy presented himself.", "그러자 그 첩자가 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“You see?”", "\"보이시오?\""], ["said Carton, looking up, as he kneeled on one knee beside the insensible figure, putting the paper in the breast: “is your hazard very great?”", "카턴이 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 의식 잃은 사람 곁에서 그 가슴에 종이를 넣으며 고개를 들고 말했다. \"당신의 위험이 그리 큰가요?\""]], [["“Mr. Carton,”", "\"카턴 씨,\""], ["the Spy answered, with a timid snap of his fingers, “my hazard is not that, in the thick of business here, if you are true to the whole of your bargain.”", "첩자가 겁먹은 듯 손가락을 딱 튕기며 대답했다. \"여기 일이 한창인 마당에, 당신이 거래 전부에 충실하기만 하다면, 내 위험은 그 정도는 아니오.\""]], [["“Don’t fear me.", "\"나를 두려워 마시오."], ["I will be true to the death.”", "나는 죽을 때까지 충실할 테니.\""]], [["“You must be, Mr.", "\"그래야 하오, 카턴 씨."], ["Carton, if the tale of fifty-two is to be right.", "쉰두 명이라는 셈이 맞으려면 말이오."], ["Being made right by you in that dress, I shall have no fear.”", "당신이 그 옷차림으로 셈을 맞춰주니, 나는 아무 두려움이 없을 거요.\""]], [["“Have no fear!", "\"두려워 마시오!"], ["I shall soon be out of the way of harming you, and the rest will soon be far from here, please God!", "나는 곧 당신을 해칠 처지에서 벗어날 테고, 나머지 사람들도 곧 이곳에서 멀리 떠나리다, 하느님 뜻이라면!"], ["Now, get assistance and take me to the coach.”", "자, 사람을 불러 나를 마차로 데려가시오.\""]], [["“You?”", "\"당신을?\""], ["said the Spy nervously.", "첩자가 초조하게 말했다."]], [["“Him, man, with whom I have exchanged.", "\"이 사람 말이오, 내가 신분을 바꾼 이 사람."], ["You go out at the gate by which you brought me in?”", "당신은 나를 데리고 들어온 그 문으로 나가지요?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론이오.\""]], [["“I was weak and faint when you brought me in, and I am fainter now you take me out.", "\"당신이 나를 데리고 들어올 때 나는 약하고 어지러웠고, 당신이 나를 데리고 나가는 지금은 더 어지럽소."], ["The parting interview has overpowered me.", "작별의 면회가 나를 압도해버렸으니."], ["Such a thing has happened here, often, and too often.", "이런 일은 여기서 흔히, 너무도 흔히 있어왔소."], ["Your life is in your own hands.", "당신 목숨은 당신 손에 달려 있소."], ["Quick!", "어서!"], ["Call assistance!”", "사람을 부르시오!\""]], [["“You swear not to betray me?”", "\"나를 배신하지 않겠다고 맹세하시오?\""], ["said the trembling Spy, as he paused for a last moment.", "떨리는 첩자가 마지막 한순간 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["“Man, man!”", "\"이 사람아, 이 사람아!\""], ["returned Carton, stamping his foot; “have I sworn by no solemn vow already, to go through with this, that you waste the precious moments now?", "카턴이 발을 구르며 응수했다. \"내가 이 일을 끝까지 해내겠다고 이미 엄숙한 맹세를 하지 않았소, 한데 당신은 지금 이 귀중한 순간들을 허비하는 거요?"], ["Take him yourself to the courtyard you know of, place him yourself in the carriage, show him yourself to Mr. Lorry, tell him yourself to give him no restorative but air, and to remember my words of last night, and his promise of last night, and drive away!”", "당신이 직접 그를 당신이 아는 그 안마당으로 데려가, 당신이 직접 그를 마차에 태우고, 당신이 직접 그를 로리 씨에게 보여주고, 당신이 직접 그에게 바깥 공기 말고는 어떤 회복제도 쓰지 말라고, 어젯밤 내 말과 어젯밤 그의 약속을 기억하라고 이르고, 마차를 몰아 떠나시오!\""]], [["The Spy withdrew, and Carton seated himself at the table, resting his forehead on his hands.", "첩자가 물러갔고, 카턴은 탁자에 앉아 두 손에 이마를 괴었다."], ["The Spy returned immediately, with two men.", "첩자는 곧 두 남자와 함께 돌아왔다."]], [["“How, then?”", "\"그래, 어찌 된 일이오?\""], ["said one of them, contemplating the fallen figure.", "그 가운데 하나가 쓰러진 사람을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“So afflicted to find that his friend has drawn a prize in the lottery of Sainte Guillotine?”", "\"제 친구가 거룩한 단두대의 추첨에서 당첨됐다는 걸 알고 이리도 비통해한단 말이오?\""]], [["“A good patriot,”", "\"훌륭한 애국자라도,\""], ["said the other, “could hardly have been more afflicted if the Aristocrat had drawn a blank.”", "다른 하나가 말했다. \"저 귀족이 꽝을 뽑았다 한들 이보다 더 비통해하지는 못했을 거요.\""]], [["They raised the unconscious figure, placed it on a litter they had brought to the door, and bent to carry it away.", "그들은 의식 없는 사람을 일으켜, 문가에 가져다 놓은 들것에 누이고는, 몸을 숙여 그를 메고 나가려 했다."]], [["“The time is short, Evrémonde,”", "\"시간이 얼마 없소, 에브레몽드.\""], ["said the Spy, in a warning voice.", "첩자가 경고하는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“I know it well,”", "\"잘 알고 있소.\""], ["answered Carton.", "카턴이 대답했다."], ["“Be careful of my friend, I entreat you, and leave me.”", "\"부탁이니 내 친구를 조심해서 다루고, 나를 혼자 있게 해주시오.\""]], [["“Come, then, my children,”", "\"자, 그럼, 친구들,\""], ["said Barsad.", "바사드가 말했다."], ["“Lift him, and come away!”", "\"그를 들고, 갑시다!\""]], [["The door closed, and Carton was left alone.", "문이 닫혔고, 카턴은 홀로 남았다."], ["Straining his powers of listening to the utmost, he listened for any sound that might denote suspicion or alarm.", "그는 귀 기울이는 힘을 한껏 곤두세워, 의심이나 경보를 알릴 만한 어떤 소리라도 들리는지 귀를 기울였다."], ["There was none.", "아무 소리도 없었다."], ["Keys turned, doors clashed, footsteps passed along distant passages: no cry was raised, or hurry made, that seemed unusual.", "열쇠가 돌아가고, 문이 쾅 닫히고, 발소리가 먼 복도를 따라 지나갔다. 예사롭지 않게 여겨질 만한 외침도, 부산함도 일지 않았다."], ["Breathing more freely in a little while, he sat down at the table, and listened again until the clock struck Two.", "잠시 뒤 좀더 자유로이 숨을 쉬며, 그는 탁자에 앉아, 시계가 두시를 칠 때까지 다시 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Sounds that he was not afraid of, for he divined their meaning, then began to be audible.", "그가 두려워하지 않는 소리들이 — 그 뜻을 짐작했기에 — 그제야 들려오기 시작했다."], ["Several doors were opened in succession, and finally his own.", "문들이 잇따라 열렸고, 마침내 그 자신의 문이 열렸다."], ["A gaoler, with a list in his hand, looked in, merely saying, “Follow me, Evrémonde!” and he followed into a large dark room, at a distance.", "손에 명단을 든 간수가 안을 들여다보며, 그저 \"따라오시오, 에브레몽드!\"라고 말했고, 그는 멀찍이 떨어진 크고 어두운 방으로 따라 들어갔다."], ["It was a dark winter day, and what with the shadows within, and what with the shadows without, he could but dimly discern the others who were brought there to have their arms bound.", "어두운 겨울날이었고, 안의 그늘과 밖의 그늘이 어우러져, 그는 거기 끌려와 팔을 묶이게 된 다른 이들을 어렴풋이 분간할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 어떤 이들은 서 있었고, 어떤 이들은 앉아 있었다."], ["Some were standing; some seated. Some were lamenting, and in restless motion; but, these were few.", "어떤 이들은 한탄하며 가만있지 못하고 움직였으나, 그런 이들은 소수였다."], ["The great majority were silent and still, looking fixedly at the ground.", "대다수는 말없이 조용히, 바닥을 물끄러미 바라보고 있었다."]], [["As he stood by the wall in a dim corner, while some of the fifty-two were brought in after him, one man stopped in passing, to embrace him, as having a knowledge of him.", "그가 어둑한 구석 벽 곁에 서 있는 동안, 쉰두 명 가운데 몇몇이 그의 뒤를 이어 끌려 들어왔는데, 한 남자가 지나가다 멈춰 서서, 그를 아는 듯 그를 끌어안았다."], ["It thrilled him with a great dread of discovery; but the man went on.", "발각될지 모른다는 큰 두려움에 그는 오싹했으나, 그 남자는 그냥 지나갔다."], ["A very few moments after that, a young woman, with a slight girlish form, a sweet spare face in which there was no vestige of colour, and large widely opened patient eyes, rose from the seat where he had observed her sitting, and came to speak to him.", "그러고 나서 아주 잠시 뒤, 가냘픈 소녀 같은 몸매에, 핏기라곤 한 점도 없는 곱고 야윈 얼굴에, 크고 활짝 열린 인내심 어린 눈을 한 젊은 여인이, 그가 앉아 있는 것을 보았던 그 자리에서 일어나, 그에게 말을 건네러 다가왔다."]], [["“Citizen Evrémonde,”", "\"에브레몽드 시민,\""], ["she said, touching him with her cold hand.", "그녀가 차가운 손으로 그를 건드리며 말했다."], ["“I am a poor little seamstress, who was with you in La Force.”", "\"저는 라포르스에서 당신과 함께 있던 가난한 어린 침모예요.\""]], [["He murmured for answer: “True.", "그가 대답으로 중얼거렸다. \"그렇군요."], ["I forget what you were accused of?”", "당신이 무슨 죄목으로 고발당했는지 잊었는데요?\""]], [["“Plots.", "\"음모요."], ["Though the just Heaven knows that I am innocent of any.", "정의로운 하늘은 제가 어떤 음모와도 무관하다는 걸 알지만요."], ["Is it likely?", "그럴 법이나 한가요?"], ["Who would think of plotting with a poor little weak creature like me?”", "저처럼 가난하고 어리고 연약한 것과 누가 음모를 꾸미려 들겠어요?\""]], [["The forlorn smile with which she said it, so touched him, that tears started from his eyes.", "그녀가 그 말을 하며 짓는 쓸쓸한 미소가 그의 마음을 너무도 울려서, 그의 눈에서 눈물이 솟았다."]], [["“I am not afraid to die, Citizen Evrémonde, but I have done nothing.", "\"저는 죽음이 두렵지 않아요, 에브레몽드 시민. 하지만 저는 아무 짓도 하지 않았어요."], ["I am not unwilling to die, if the Republic which is to do so much good to us poor, will profit by my death; but I do not know how that can be, Citizen Evrémonde.", "우리 같은 가난한 사람들에게 그토록 많은 좋은 일을 해줄 공화국이 제 죽음으로 덕을 본다면, 저는 죽기를 마다하지 않아요. 하지만 그게 어떻게 그럴 수 있는지 모르겠어요, 에브레몽드 시민."], ["Such a poor weak little creature!”", "저처럼 가난하고 연약하고 어린 것이 말이에요!\""]], [["As the last thing on earth that his heart was to warm and soften to, it warmed and softened to this pitiable girl.", "그의 마음이 따뜻해지고 부드러워질 이 세상에서의 마지막 대상으로서, 그 마음은 이 가엾은 소녀에게 따뜻해지고 부드러워졌다."]], [["“I heard you were released, Citizen Evrémonde.", "\"당신이 풀려났다고 들었어요, 에브레몽드 시민."], ["I hoped it was true?”", "그게 사실이기를 바랐는데요?\""]], [["“It was.", "\"사실이었지요."], ["But, I was again taken and condemned.”", "하지만 다시 붙잡혀 선고받았소.\""]], [["“If I may ride with you, Citizen Evrémonde, will you let me hold your hand?", "\"제가 당신과 함께 타고 갈 수 있다면, 에브레몽드 시민, 당신 손을 잡게 해주시겠어요?"], ["I am not afraid, but I am little and weak, and it will give me more courage.”", "저는 두렵지 않지만, 저는 작고 연약하니, 그러면 좀더 용기가 날 거예요.\""]], [["As the patient eyes were lifted to his face, he saw a sudden doubt in them, and then astonishment.", "그 인내심 어린 눈이 그의 얼굴로 들려졌을 때, 그는 그 눈에 문득 의혹이, 이어 놀라움이 떠오르는 것을 보았다."], ["He pressed the work-worn, hunger-worn young fingers, and touched his lips.", "그는 일에 닳고 굶주림에 닳은 그 젊은 손가락들을 꼭 쥐고, 입술을 그곳에 갖다 댔다."]], [["“Are you dying for him?”", "\"당신은 그를 위해 죽는 거군요?\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."]], [["“And his wife and child.", "\"그리고 그의 아내와 아이를 위해서요."], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["Yes.”", "그렇소.\""]], [["“O you will let me hold your brave hand, stranger?”", "\"오 제가 당신의 용감한 손을 잡게 해주실 거지요, 낯선 분이여?\""]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Yes, my poor sister; to the last.”", "그렇소, 가엾은 누이여. 마지막 순간까지.\""]], [["The same shadows that are falling on the prison, are falling, in that same hour of the early afternoon, on the Barrier with the crowd about it, when a coach going out of Paris drives up to be examined.", "같은 그늘이, 이른 오후의 바로 그 시각에, 감옥 위로 내리는 그 그늘이, 군중이 에워싼 관문 위로도 내리고 있었으니, 빠리를 빠져나가는 마차 한 대가 검문을 받으러 다가서고 있었다."]], [["“Who goes here?", "\"누가 지나가는가?"], ["Whom have we within?", "안에 누구를 태웠나?"], ["Papers!”", "증명서!\""]], [["The papers are handed out, and read.", "증명서가 건네지고, 읽힌다."]], [["“Alexandre Manette.", "\"알렉상드르 마네뜨."], ["Physician.", "의사. 프랑스인."], ["French. Which is he?”", "어느 쪽이 그자인가?\""]], [["This is he; this helpless, inarticulately murmuring, wandering old man pointed out.", "이 사람이다. 이 무력하게, 알아들을 수 없이 중얼거리며, 정신을 놓은 노인이 지목된다."]], [["“Apparently the Citizen-Doctor is not in his right mind?", "\"보아하니 시민-의사는 제정신이 아닌 모양이군?"], ["The Revolution-fever will have been too much for him?”", "혁명열이 그에게 너무 과했나 보지?\""]], [["Greatly too much for him.", "너무도 과했다."]], [["“Hah!", "\"하!"], ["Many suffer with it.", "많은 이들이 그것으로 고생하지."], ["Lucie.", "루시."], ["His daughter.", "그의 딸. 프랑스인."], ["French. Which is she?”", "어느 쪽이 그 여자인가?\""]], [["This is she.", "이 사람이다."]], [["“Apparently it must be.", "\"보아하니 그런 모양이군."], ["Lucie, the wife of Evrémonde; is it not?”", "루시, 에브레몽드의 아내. 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["It is.", "그렇다."]], [["“Hah!", "\"하!"], ["Evrémonde has an assignation elsewhere.", "에브레몽드는 다른 데 만날 약속이 있지."], ["Lucie, her child.", "루시, 그녀의 아이."], ["English.", "영국인."], ["This is she?”", "이 아이인가?\""]], [["She and no other.", "다름 아닌 그 아이다."]], [["“Kiss me, child of Evrémonde.", "\"내게 입을 맞추렴, 에브레몽드의 아이야."], ["Now, thou hast kissed a good Republican; something new in thy family; remember it!", "자, 너는 훌륭한 공화주의자에게 입을 맞췄다. 네 집안에 새로운 일이지."], ["Sydney Carton.", "잊지 마라! 시드니 카턴."], ["Advocate.", "변호사."], ["English.", "영국인."], ["Which is he?”", "어느 쪽이 그자인가?\""]], [["He lies here, in this corner of the carriage.", "그는 여기, 마차의 이 구석에 누워 있다."], ["He, too, is pointed out.", "그도 지목된다."]], [["“Apparently the English advocate is in a swoon?”", "\"보아하니 영국인 변호사는 기절했군?\""]], [["It is hoped he will recover in the fresher air.", "좀더 신선한 공기를 쐬면 회복하기를 바란다고 한다."], ["It is represented that he is not in strong health, and has separated sadly from a friend who is under the displeasure of the Republic.", "그가 건강이 좋지 못하며, 공화국의 노여움을 산 친구와 슬프게 헤어진 참이라고 설명된다."]], [["“Is that all?", "\"그게 다인가?"], ["It is not a great deal, that!", "그쯤이야 대단한 일도 아니지!"], ["Many are under the displeasure of the Republic, and must look out at the little window.", "많은 이들이 공화국의 노여움을 사고, 그 작은 창으로 내다봐야 하니까."], ["Jarvis Lorry.", "자비스 로리."], ["Banker.", "은행가."], ["English.", "영국인."], ["Which is he?”", "어느 쪽이 그자인가?\""]], [["“I am he.", "\"내가 그 사람이오."], ["Necessarily, being the last.”", "마지막이니, 당연히 나일 수밖에.\""]], [["It is Jarvis Lorry who has replied to all the previous questions.", "앞선 모든 질문에 대답한 것은 자비스 로리다."], ["It is Jarvis Lorry who has alighted and stands with his hand on the coach door, replying to a group of officials.", "마차에서 내려, 한 손을 마차 문에 얹은 채, 한 무리의 관리들에게 대답하는 것도 자비스 로리다. 그들은 느긋이 마차 둘레를 돌고, 느긋이 마부석에 올라, 지붕에 실린 얼마 안 되는 짐을 살핀다."], ["They leisurely walk round the carriage and leisurely mount the box, to look at what little luggage it carries on the roof; the country-people hanging about, press nearer to the coach doors and greedily stare in; a little child, carried by its mother, has its short arm held out for it, that it may touch the wife of an aristocrat who has gone to the Guillotine.", "주위를 어슬렁거리던 시골 사람들이 마차 문 가까이 몰려들어 탐욕스레 안을 들여다본다. 어미 품에 안긴 어린아이가, 단두대로 간 귀족의 아내를 만져볼 수 있도록 짧은 팔을 내밀어진다."]], [["“Behold your papers, Jarvis Lorry, countersigned.”", "\"여기 증명서요, 자비스 로리. 부서까지 마쳤소.\""]], [["“One can depart, citizen?”", "\"이제 떠나도 되겠소, 시민?\""]], [["“One can depart.", "\"떠나도 좋소."], ["Forward, my postilions!", "앞으로, 마부들!"], ["A good journey!”", "부디 좋은 여행 되시길!\""]], [["“I salute you, citizens.--And the first danger passed!”", "\"여러분께 경의를 표하오. — 첫번째 위기는 넘겼구나!\""]], [["These are again the words of Jarvis Lorry, as he clasps his hands, and looks upward.", "이것도 자비스 로리의 말이니, 두 손을 맞잡고 위를 올려다보며 하는 말이다."], ["There is terror in the carriage, there is weeping, there is the heavy breathing of the insensible traveller.", "마차 안에는 공포가 있고, 흐느낌이 있고, 의식 없는 길손의 거친 숨소리가 있다."]], [["“Are we not going too slowly?", "\"우리가 너무 느리게 가고 있는 것 아닌가요?"], ["Can they not be induced to go faster?”", "좀더 빨리 가도록 할 수 없을까요?\""], ["asks Lucie, clinging to the old man.", "루시가 노인에게 매달리며 묻는다."]], [["“It would seem like flight, my darling.", "\"그건 도망치는 것처럼 보일 거다, 얘야."], ["I must not urge them too much; it would rouse suspicion.”", "저들을 너무 다그쳐서는 안 돼. 의심을 살 테니.\""]], [["“Look back, look back, and see if we are pursued!”", "\"뒤를 봐요, 뒤를 봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요!\""]], [["“The road is clear, my dearest.", "\"길이 훤하구나, 얘야."], ["So far, we are not pursued.”", "아직까지는 쫓기지 않아.\""]], [["Houses in twos and threes pass by us, solitary farms, ruinous buildings, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, open country, avenues of leafless trees.", "집들이 두세 채씩 우리 곁을 지나가고, 외딴 농가들, 허물어진 건물들, 염색 공장들, 무두질 공장들 따위가, 트인 들판이, 잎 떨어진 나무들의 가로수길이 지나간다."], ["The hard uneven pavement is under us, the soft deep mud is on either side.", "단단하고 고르지 못한 포석이 우리 아래에 있고, 부드럽고 깊은 진흙이 양옆에 있다."], ["Sometimes, we strike into the skirting mud, to avoid the stones that clatter us and shake us; sometimes, we stick in ruts and sloughs there.", "때로 우리는 우리를 덜컹거리고 뒤흔드는 돌멩이들을 피하려고 가장자리 진흙 속으로 파고들고, 때로는 그곳 바퀴 자국과 수렁에 박힌다."], ["The agony of our impatience is then so great, that in our wild alarm and hurry we are for getting out and running--hiding--doing anything but stopping.", "그럴 때면 조급함의 고통이 너무도 커서, 우리는 미친 듯한 경악과 다급함에 마차에서 내려 달리고 싶어진다 — 숨고 싶어진다 — 멈추는 것 말고는 무슨 짓이든 하고 싶어진다."]], [["Out of the open country, in again among ruinous buildings, solitary farms, dye-works, tanneries, and the like, cottages in twos and threes, avenues of leafless trees.", "트인 들판을 벗어나, 다시금 허물어진 건물들, 외딴 농가들, 염색 공장들, 무두질 공장들 따위 사이로, 두세 채씩의 오두막들, 잎 떨어진 나무들의 가로수길 사이로 든다."], ["Have these men deceived us, and taken us back by another road?", "이자들이 우리를 속여, 다른 길로 우리를 도로 데려간 것 아닌가?"], ["Is not this the same place twice over?", "이곳이 같은 자리를 두번 지나는 것 아닌가?"], ["Thank Heaven, no.", "다행히도, 아니다."], ["A village.", "마을이다."], ["Look back, look back, and see if we are pursued!", "뒤를 봐요, 뒤를 봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요!"], ["Hush!", "쉿!"], ["the posting-house.", "역참이다."]], [["Leisurely, our four horses are taken out; leisurely, the coach stands in the little street, bereft of horses, and with no likelihood upon it of ever moving again; leisurely, the new horses come into visible existence, one by one; leisurely, the new postilions follow, sucking and plaiting the lashes of their whips; leisurely, the old postilions count their money, make wrong additions, and arrive at dissatisfied results. All the time, our overfraught hearts are beating at a rate that would far outstrip the fastest gallop of the fastest horses ever foaled.", "느긋이, 우리 네 마리 말이 풀려난다. 느긋이, 마차는 작은 거리에 말을 빼앗긴 채, 다시는 움직일 가망이라곤 보이지 않게 서 있다. 느긋이, 새 말들이 한 마리씩 눈앞에 나타난다. 느긋이, 새 마부들이 채찍 끈을 빨고 땋으며 뒤따른다. 느긋이, 옛 마부들이 받은 돈을 세고, 셈을 틀리게 하고, 불만스러운 결과에 다다른다. 그러는 내내, 짐을 너무 실은 우리 가슴은, 일찍이 태어난 가장 빠른 말의 가장 빠른 질주보다 훨씬 더 앞지를 박자로 두근거린다."]], [["At length the new postilions are in their saddles, and the old are left behind.", "마침내 새 마부들이 안장에 올라타고, 옛 마부들은 뒤에 남는다."], ["We are through the village, up the hill, and down the hill, and on the low watery grounds.", "우리는 마을을 지나, 언덕을 오르고, 언덕을 내려, 낮고 물기 어린 땅 위에 다다른다."], ["Suddenly, the postilions exchange speech with animated gesticulation, and the horses are pulled up, almost on their haunches.", "갑자기 마부들이 활기찬 손짓과 함께 말을 주고받고, 말들이 거의 엉덩방아를 찧을 듯 멈춰 선다."], ["We are pursued?", "우리가 쫓기는 것인가?"]], [["“Ho!", "\"여보!"], ["Within the carriage there.", "마차 안의 양반들."], ["Speak then!”", "어디 말 좀 해보쇼!\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이오?\""], ["asks Mr. Lorry, looking out at window.", "로리 씨가 창밖을 내다보며 묻는다."]], [["“How many did they say?”", "\"몇 명이라고들 했소?\""]], [["“I do not understand you.”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""]], [["“--At the last post.", "\"— 지난 역참에서 말이오."], ["How many to the Guillotine to-day?”", "오늘 단두대로 몇 명이 가오?\""]], [["“Fifty-two.”", "\"쉰두 명.\""]], [["“I said so!", "\"내 그랬지!"], ["A brave number!", "멋들어진 숫자야!"], ["My fellow-citizen here would have it forty-two; ten more heads are worth having.", "여기 내 동료 시민은 마흔두 명이라고 우겼다오. 열 개의 머리는 더 가질 만하지."], ["The Guillotine goes handsomely.", "단두대가 아주 잘 돌아가는군."], ["I love it.", "나는 그게 좋아."], ["Hi forward.", "이랴, 앞으로."], ["Whoop!”", "워이!\""]], [["The night comes on dark.", "밤이 어둡게 다가온다. 그가 더 움직인다."], ["He moves more; he is beginning to revive, and to speak intelligibly; he thinks they are still together; he asks him, by his name, what he has in his hand.", "정신을 차리기 시작하여, 알아들을 수 있게 말한다. 그는 그들이 아직 함께 있는 줄 안다."], ["O pity us, kind Heaven, and help us!", "그의 이름을 부르며, 그의 손에 무엇을 들고 있느냐고 묻는다."], ["Look out, look out, and see if we are pursued.", "오 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서, 자비로운 하늘이여, 우리를 도우소서! 내다봐요, 내다봐요, 우리가 쫓기고 있는지 봐줘요."]], [["The wind is rushing after us, and the clouds are flying after us, and the moon is plunging after us, and the whole wild night is in pursuit of us; but, so far, we are pursued by nothing else.", "바람이 우리를 뒤쫓아 몰아치고, 구름이 우리를 뒤쫓아 날아가고, 달이 우리를 뒤쫓아 내달리고, 거친 밤이 온통 우리를 뒤쫓는다. 그러나 아직까지는, 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 우리는 쫓기지 않는다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIV. The Knitting Done", "# CHAPTER XIV. The Knitting Done"]], [["In that same juncture of time when the Fifty-Two awaited their fate Madame Defarge held darkly ominous council with The Vengeance and Jacques Three of the Revolutionary Jury.", "쉰두 명이 자기들의 운명을 기다리고 있던 바로 그 시각에, 드파르주 부인은 복수의 여신, 그리고 혁명 배심원의 자끄 쓰리와 더불어 음험하고 불길한 회의를 열고 있었다."], ["Not in the wine-shop did Madame Defarge confer with these ministers, but in the shed of the wood-sawyer, erst a mender of roads.", "드파르주 부인이 이 측근들과 의논을 나눈 곳은 포도주 가게가 아니라, 한때 도로 보수공이었던 톱장이의 헛간이었다."], ["The sawyer himself did not participate in the conference, but abided at a little distance, like an outer satellite who was not to speak until required, or to offer an opinion until invited.", "톱장이 자신은 그 회의에 끼지 않고 조금 떨어져서 머물러 있었는데, 부르기 전에는 입을 열어서도 안 되고 청하기 전에는 의견을 내서도 안 되는 바깥쪽 위성과도 같았다."]], [["“But our Defarge,”", "\"하지만 우리 드파르주는,\""], ["said Jacques Three, “is undoubtedly a good Republican?", "자끄 쓰리가 말했다, \"틀림없이 훌륭한 공화주의자겠지?"], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["“There is no better,”", "\"프랑스에,\""], ["the voluble Vengeance protested in her shrill notes, “in France.”", "수다스러운 복수의 여신이 새된 목소리로 항변했다, \"그보다 나은 사람은 없어요.\""]], [["“Peace, little Vengeance,”", "\"진정해라, 작은 복수의 여신아.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, laying her hand with a slight frown on her lieutenant’s lips, “hear me speak.", "드파르주 부인이 약간 눈살을 찌푸리며 부관의 입술에 손을 갖다 대고 말했다. \"내 말을 들어."], ["My husband, fellow-citizen, is a good Republican and a bold man; he has deserved well of the Republic, and possesses its confidence.", "내 남편은, 동지여, 훌륭한 공화주의자이고 담대한 사람이야. 공화국을 위해 큰 공을 세웠고, 공화국의 신임도 받고 있어."], ["But my husband has his weaknesses, and he is so weak as to relent towards this Doctor.”", "하지만 내 남편에게는 약점이 있는데, 그는 너무 나약해서 이 의사에게 마음이 약해진단 말이야.\""]], [["“It is a great pity,”", "\"그것 참 안된 일이로군.\" 자끄 쓰리가 의심쩍게 고개를 저으며, 잔인한 손가락을 굶주린 입가에 댄 채 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["croaked Jacques Three, dubiously shaking his head, with his cruel fingers at his hungry mouth; “it is not quite like a good citizen; it is a thing to regret.”", "\"훌륭한 시민답지가 않아. 유감스러운 일이지.\""]], [["“See you,”", "\"보세요.\""], ["said madame, “I care nothing for this Doctor, I.", "부인이 말했다. \"나는 이 의사 따위에는 아무 관심도 없어요."], ["He may wear his head or lose it, for any interest I have in him; it is all one to me.", "내가 그자에게 무슨 관심이 있다고, 머리가 붙어 있든 떨어져 나가든 알 바 아니에요."], ["But, the Evrémonde people are to be exterminated, and the wife and child must follow the husband and father.”", "다 마찬가지죠. 하지만 에브레몽드 일족은 멸절되어야 하고, 아내와 아이는 남편이자 아비의 뒤를 따라야 해요.\""]], [["“She has a fine head for it,”", "\"그 여자라면 거기에 어울리는 고운 머리를 가졌지.\""], ["croaked Jacques Three.", "자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I have seen blue eyes and golden hair there, and they looked charming when Samson held them up.”", "\"거기서 파란 눈과 금발 머리를 본 적이 있는데, 쌍송이 그걸 들어올렸을 때 아주 매력적으로 보이더군.\""], ["Ogre that he was, he spoke like an epicure.", "식인귀 같은 주제에, 그는 미식가처럼 말했다."]], [["Madame Defarge cast down her eyes, and reflected a little.", "드파르주 부인은 눈을 내리깔고 잠시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“The child also,”", "\"그 아이도,\""], ["observed Jacques Three, with a meditative enjoyment of his words, “has golden hair and blue eyes.", "자끄 쓰리가 자기 말을 음미하듯 즐기며 한마디 보탰다, \"금발 머리에 파란 눈을 가졌지."], ["And we seldom have a child there.", "게다가 우리는 거기서 아이를 보는 일이 드물잖아."], ["It is a pretty sight!”", "참 보기 좋은 광경일 거야!\""]], [["“In a word,”", "\"한마디로,\""], ["said Madame Defarge, coming out of her short abstraction, “I cannot trust my husband in this matter.", "짧은 상념에서 깨어나며 드파르주 부인이 말했다, \"이 일에서는 내 남편을 믿을 수가 없어요."], ["Not only do I feel, since last night, that I dare not confide to him the details of my projects; but also I feel that if I delay, there is danger of his giving warning, and then they might escape.”", "어젯밤 이후로 내 계획의 세부 사항을 그에게 털어놓을 엄두가 나지 않을 뿐만 아니라, 내가 지체하다가는 그가 경고를 흘릴 위험이 있고, 그러면 그들이 달아날 수도 있다는 느낌이 들어요.\""]], [["“That must never be,”", "\"그건 절대 안 돼.\""], ["croaked Jacques Three; “no one must escape.", "자끄 쓰리가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"아무도 달아나서는 안 돼."], ["We have not half enough as it is.", "지금도 절반밖에 못 채우고 있는걸."], ["We ought to have six score a day.”", "하루에 백이십 명은 처리해야 하는데.\""]], [["“In a word,”", "\"한마디로,\""], ["Madame Defarge went on, “my husband has not my reason for pursuing this family to annihilation, and I have not his reason for regarding this Doctor with any sensibility.", "드파르주 부인이 말을 이었다, \"내 남편에게는 이 가족을 끝까지 추적해 절멸시킬 나만한 이유가 없고, 나에게는 이 의사를 어떤 정으로 대할 그이만한 이유가 없어요."], ["I must act for myself, therefore.", "그러니 나는 나 자신을 위해 움직여야 해요."], ["Come hither, little citizen.”", "이리 와, 작은 시민.\""]], [["The wood-sawyer, who held her in the respect, and himself in the submission, of mortal fear, advanced with his hand to his red cap.", "죽을 듯한 공포심에서 그녀를 떠받들고 스스로 복종하던 톱장이가, 빨간 모자에 손을 갖다 댄 채 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“Touching those signals, little citizen,”", "\"그 신호들 말인데, 작은 시민.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, sternly, “that she made to the prisoners; you are ready to bear witness to them this very day?”", "드파르주 부인이 엄하게 말했다. \"그 여자가 죄수들에게 보낸 그 신호들 말이야, 자네 바로 오늘 그것에 대해 증언할 준비가 되어 있겠지?\""]], [["“Ay, ay, why not!” cried the sawyer.", "\"예, 예, 안 될 게 뭐 있겠습니까!\""], ["“Every day, in all weathers, from two to four, always signalling, sometimes with the little one, sometimes without.", "톱장이가 외쳤다. \"날마다, 날씨가 어떻든, 두시부터 네시까지, 늘 신호를 보냈죠, 어떤 때는 어린것이랑, 어떤 때는 혼자서요."], ["I know what I know.", "저는 제가 아는 걸 압니다."], ["I have seen with my eyes.”", "제 눈으로 똑똑히 봤다고요.\""]], [["He made all manner of gestures while he spoke, as if in incidental imitation of some few of the great diversity of signals that he had never seen.", "그는 말하는 내내 온갖 몸짓을 해 보였는데, 마치 한 번도 본 적 없는 그 갖가지 신호들 가운데 몇 가지를 즉흥적으로 흉내 내기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["“Clearly plots,”", "\"명백한 음모로군.\""], ["said Jacques Three.", "자끄 쓰리가 말했다."], ["“Transparently!”", "\"환히 들여다보여!\""]], [["“There is no doubt of the Jury?”", "\"배심원단은 의심할 여지가 없겠지요?\""], ["inquired Madame Defarge, letting her eyes turn to him with a gloomy smile.", "드파르주 부인이 음울한 미소를 띠며 그에게로 눈을 돌리며 물었다."]], [["“Rely upon the patriotic Jury, dear citizeness.", "\"애국적인 배심원단을 믿으시오, 친애하는 여성 시민."], ["I answer for my fellow-Jurymen.”", "동료 배심원들에 대해서는 내가 책임지겠소.\""]], [["“Now, let me see,”", "\"자, 어디 보자.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, pondering again.", "드파르주 부인이 다시 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["“Yet once more!", "\"한 번만 더!"], ["Can I spare this Doctor to my husband?", "이 의사는 남편을 위해 살려둘 수 있을까?"], ["I have no feeling either way.", "나는 어느 쪽으로도 별 감정이 없어."], ["Can I spare him?”", "그자를 살려둘 수 있을까?\""]], [["“He would count as one head,”", "\"그자도 머리 하나로 셈해질 텐데.\""], ["observed Jacques Three, in a low voice.", "자끄 쓰리가 나직한 목소리로 한마디 했다."], ["“We really have not heads enough; it would be a pity, I think.”", "\"우린 정말 머리가 모자라거든. 살려두면 아까운 노릇이지, 내 생각엔.\""]], [["“He was signalling with her when I saw her,”", "\"내가 그 여자를 봤을 때 그자도 그 여자와 함께 신호를 보내고 있었어요.\" 드파르주 부인이 따졌다."], ["argued Madame Defarge; “I cannot speak of one without the other; and I must not be silent, and trust the case wholly to him, this little citizen here.", "\"한쪽을 빼놓고 다른 쪽만 말할 수는 없어요. 게다가 나는 입을 다물고 이 일을 전부 이 사람에게, 여기 이 작은 시민에게 맡겨둘 수가 없어요."], ["For, I am not a bad witness.”", "나도 나름 괜찮은 증인이니까요.\""]], [["The Vengeance and Jacques Three vied with each other in their fervent protestations that she was the most admirable and marvellous of witnesses.", "복수의 여신과 자끄 쓰리는 그녀야말로 가장 감탄스럽고 놀라운 증인이라고 앞다투어 열렬히 단언했다."], ["The little citizen, not to be outdone, declared her to be a celestial witness.", "작은 시민도 질세라, 그녀를 천상의 증인이라고 선언했다."]], [["“He must take his chance,”", "\"그자도 운에 맡겨야지.\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."], ["“No, I cannot spare him!", "\"아냐, 살려둘 수 없어!"], ["You are engaged at three o’clock; you are going to see the batch of to-day executed.--You?”", "자네는 세시에 일이 있지. 자네는 오늘 처형되는 무리를 보러 갈 거야.—그렇지?\""]], [["The question was addressed to the wood-sawyer, who hurriedly replied in the affirmative: seizing the occasion to add that he was the most ardent of Republicans, and that he would be in effect the most desolate of Republicans, if anything prevented him from enjoying the pleasure of smoking his afternoon pipe in the contemplation of the droll national barber.", "그 질문은 톱장이를 향한 것이었고, 그는 황급히 그렇다고 대답하면서, 이 기회를 놓치지 않고, 자기야말로 가장 열렬한 공화주의자이며, 만일 무슨 일이라도 생겨서 그 익살맞은 국가의 이발사를 바라보며 오후의 담배 한 대 피우는 즐거움을 누리지 못한다면 자기야말로 가장 비참한 공화주의자가 될 거라고 덧붙였다."], ["He was so very demonstrative herein, that he might have been suspected (perhaps was, by the dark eyes that looked contemptuously at him out of Madame Defarge’s head) of having his small individual fears for his own personal safety, every hour in the day.", "그가 이 대목에서 어찌나 요란하게 떠들어대는지, 하루 종일 매시간 제 한 몸의 안위가 걱정스러워 사소하나마 개인적인 두려움을 품고 있는 게 아닌가 의심받을 만했다(아마도 드파르주 부인의 머릿속에서 경멸스럽게 그를 내려다보던 그 검은 눈에는 정말 그렇게 보였으리라)."]], [["“I,”", "\"나도,\""], ["said madame, “am equally engaged at the same place.", "부인이 말했다, \"같은 장소에 일이 있어."], ["After it is over--say at eight to-night--come you to me, in Saint Antoine, and we will give information against these people at my Section.”", "그게 끝난 뒤에—오늘 밤 여덟시쯤이라고 하지—생땅뚜안으로 나를 찾아와. 그러면 우리 구역에 이자들을 고발하는 거야.\""]], [["The wood-sawyer said he would be proud and flattered to attend the citizeness.", "톱장이는 여성 시민을 모시게 되어 영광이고 황송하다고 말했다."], ["The citizeness looking at him, he became embarrassed, evaded her glance as a small dog would have done, retreated among his wood, and hid his confusion over the handle of his saw.", "여성 시민이 그를 바라보자, 그는 당황해서 작은 개가 그러듯 그녀의 시선을 피해, 자기 장작더미 사이로 물러나서는, 톱 손잡이에 매달린 채 어쩔 줄 모르는 기색을 감추었다."]], [["Madame Defarge beckoned the Juryman and The Vengeance a little nearer to the door, and there expounded her further views to them thus:", "드파르주 부인은 배심원과 복수의 여신에게 문가로 조금 더 가까이 오라고 손짓하고는, 거기서 자신의 다음 계획을 그들에게 이렇게 설명했다."]], [["“She will now be at home, awaiting the moment of his death.", "\"그 여자는 지금쯤 집에서 그자가 죽을 순간을 기다리고 있을 거야."], ["She will be mourning and grieving.", "슬퍼하고 비탄에 잠겨 있겠지."], ["She will be in a state of mind to impeach the justice of the Republic.", "공화국의 정의를 비난할 만한 심리 상태에 빠져 있을 테고."], ["She will be full of sympathy with its enemies.", "공화국의 적들에게 한껏 동정심을 품고 있을 거야."], ["I will go to her.”", "내가 그 여자한테 가야지.\""]], [["“What an admirable woman; what an adorable woman!”", "\"이 얼마나 감탄스러운 여인인가, 이 얼마나 사랑스러운 여인인가!\""], ["exclaimed Jacques Three, rapturously.", "자끄 쓰리가 황홀해하며 외쳤다."], ["“Ah, my cherished!”", "\"아, 내 사랑하는 동지!\""], ["cried The Vengeance; and embraced her.", "복수의 여신이 외치며, 그녀를 끌어안았다."]], [["“Take you my knitting,”", "\"내 뜨개질감을 가져가.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, placing it in her lieutenant’s hands, “and have it ready for me in my usual seat.", "드파르주 부인이 자기 뜨개질감을 부관의 손에 쥐여주며 말했다. \"그리고 내 늘 앉던 자리에 챙겨놔."], ["Keep me my usual chair.", "내 늘 앉던 의자를 맡아둬."], ["Go you there, straight, for there will probably be a greater concourse than usual, to-day.”", "곧장 거기로 가, 오늘은 아마 평소보다 사람이 더 많이 몰릴 테니까.\""]], [["“I willingly obey the orders of my Chief,”", "\"제 대장의 명령에 기꺼이 따르지요.\""], ["said The Vengeance with alacrity, and kissing her cheek.", "복수의 여신이 잽싸게 대답하고는, 그녀의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“You will not be late?”", "\"늦지 않을 거죠?\""]], [["“I shall be there before the commencement.”", "\"시작 전에 가 있을 거야.\""]], [["“And before the tumbrils arrive.", "\"호송 수레들이 도착하기 전에도요."], ["Be sure you are there, my soul,”", "꼭 거기 있어야 해요, 내 영혼이여.\""], ["said The Vengeance, calling after her, for she had already turned into the street, “before the tumbrils arrive!”", "복수의 여신이 그녀의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다, 그녀가 이미 거리로 접어들었기 때문이다. \"호송 수레들이 도착하기 전에요!\""]], [["Madame Defarge slightly waved her hand, to imply that she heard, and might be relied upon to arrive in good time, and so went through the mud, and round the corner of the prison wall.", "드파르주 부인은 손을 살짝 흔들어, 들었으며 제때에 도착할 테니 믿어도 좋다는 뜻을 내비치고는, 그렇게 진흙탕을 헤치며 감옥 담장 모퉁이를 돌아갔다."], ["The Vengeance and the Juryman, looking after her as she walked away, were highly appreciative of her fine figure, and her superb moral endowments.", "복수의 여신과 배심원은 그녀가 멀어져 가는 모습을 지켜보며, 그녀의 멋진 풍모와 빼어난 도덕적 자질을 한껏 칭송했다."]], [["There were many women at that time, upon whom the time laid a dreadfully disfiguring hand; but, there was not one among them more to be dreaded than this ruthless woman, now taking her way along the streets.", "그 시절 시대가 무섭도록 흉하게 그 손길을 들이댄 여인들이 많았지만, 그들 가운데 지금 거리를 따라 걷는 이 무자비한 여인보다 더 두려워할 만한 사람은 없었다."], ["Of a strong and fearless character, of shrewd sense and readiness, of great determination, of that kind of beauty which not only seems to impart to its possessor firmness and animosity, but to strike into others an instinctive recognition of those qualities; the troubled time would have heaved her up, under any circumstances.", "강인하고 두려움을 모르는 성격에, 예리한 분별력과 민첩함, 굳센 결단력, 그리고 가진 자에게 굳건함과 적의를 부여하는 듯할 뿐 아니라 남들에게도 본능적으로 그러한 자질을 알아채게 만드는 부류의 아름다움까지 갖춘 그녀를, 그 어지러운 시대는 어떤 상황에서든 떠받들어 올렸을 것이다."], ["But, imbued from her childhood with a brooding sense of wrong, and an inveterate hatred of a class, opportunity had developed her into a tigress.", "그러나 어린 시절부터 짓밟혔다는 음울한 의식과 한 계급에 대한 뿌리 깊은 증오가 배어 있던 그녀를, 기회가 암호랑이로 키워냈다."], ["She was absolutely without pity.", "그녀에게는 연민이라고는 전혀 없었다."], ["If she had ever had the virtue in her, it had quite gone out of her.", "만일 한때 그런 미덕을 지녔다 한들, 이제는 그녀에게서 완전히 빠져나가 버렸다."]], [["It was nothing to her, that an innocent man was to die for the sins of his forefathers; she saw, not him, but them.", "선조들의 죄 때문에 한 무고한 사내가 죽어야 한다는 사실 따위는 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 그가 아니라 그들을 보고 있었다."], ["It was nothing to her, that his wife was to be made a widow and his daughter an orphan; that was insufficient punishment, because they were her natural enemies and her prey, and as such had no right to live.", "그의 아내가 과부가 되고 딸이 고아가 된다는 사실 또한 그녀에게는 아무것도 아니었다. 그것으로는 처벌이 모자랐는데, 그들은 그녀의 타고난 적이요 먹잇감이라, 그런 자들로서는 살아갈 권리가 없었기 때문이다."], ["To appeal to her, was made hopeless by her having no sense of pity, even for herself.", "그녀에게 호소해본들 부질없는 일이었으니, 그녀는 자기 자신에 대해서마저 연민이라는 것을 느끼지 못했기 때문이다."], ["If she had been laid low in the streets, in any of the many encounters in which she had been engaged, she would not have pitied herself; nor, if she had been ordered to the axe to-morrow, would she have gone to it with any softer feeling than a fierce desire to change places with the man who sent her there.", "그녀가 가담했던 그 숱한 충돌 가운데 어느 하나에서 거리에 쓰러져 죽었다 한들, 그녀는 자신을 가엾게 여기지 않았으리라. 또한 내일 도끼 앞으로 끌려가라는 명령을 받았다 한들, 자기를 그리로 보낸 자와 처지를 맞바꾸고 싶다는 격렬한 욕망 말고는 그 어떤 부드러운 감정도 없이 그리로 갔으리라."]], [["Such a heart Madame Defarge carried under her rough robe.", "드파르주 부인은 그 거친 겉옷 아래 그런 심장을 품고 다녔다."], ["Carelessly worn, it was a becoming robe enough, in a certain weird way, and her dark hair looked rich under her coarse red cap.", "아무렇게나 걸쳤어도, 어딘지 기묘하게 잘 어울리는 겉옷이었고, 그녀의 검은 머리는 거친 빨간 모자 아래에서 풍성해 보였다."], ["Lying hidden in her bosom, was a loaded pistol.", "그녀의 가슴팍에는 장전된 권총이 숨겨져 있었다."], ["Lying hidden at her waist, was a sharpened dagger.", "그녀의 허리춤에는 날 선 단검이 숨겨져 있었다."], ["Thus accoutred, and walking with the confident tread of such a character, and with the supple freedom of a woman who had habitually walked in her girlhood, bare-foot and bare-legged, on the brown sea-sand, Madame Defarge took her way along the streets.", "그렇게 무장한 채, 그런 성격에 어울리는 자신만만한 걸음걸이로, 소녀 시절에 갈색 바닷모래밭을 맨발에 맨다리로 늘 거닐던 여자다운 나긋한 자유로움을 지니고, 드파르주 부인은 거리를 따라 걸어갔다."]], [["Now, when the journey of the travelling coach, at that very moment waiting for the completion of its load, had been planned out last night, the difficulty of taking Miss Pross in it had much engaged Mr. Lorry’s attention.", "그런데 바로 그 순간 짐이 다 실리기를 기다리고 있던 그 여행용 마차의 여정을 어젯밤 짜놓을 때, 프로스 양을 거기에 태우는 일의 어려움이 로리 씨의 마음을 꽤나 사로잡았었다."], ["It was not merely desirable to avoid overloading the coach, but it was of the highest importance that the time occupied in examining it and its passengers, should be reduced to the utmost; since their escape might depend on the saving of only a few seconds here and there.", "단순히 마차에 짐을 너무 많이 싣는 일을 피하는 게 바람직하다는 차원이 아니라, 마차와 그 승객들을 검문하는 데 걸리는 시간을 최대한으로 줄이는 일이 무엇보다 중요했다. 그들의 탈출이 여기저기서 단 몇 초를 아끼는 데 달려 있을지도 몰랐기 때문이다."], ["Finally, he had proposed, after anxious consideration, that Miss Pross and Jerry, who were at liberty to leave the city, should leave it at three o’clock in the lightest-wheeled conveyance known to that period.", "마침내 그는 노심초사한 끝에, 도시를 떠나는 게 자유로운 프로스 양과 제리가 그 시대에 알려진 가장 가벼운 바퀴 달린 탈것으로 세시에 도시를 떠나자고 제안했다."], ["Unencumbered with luggage, they would soon overtake the coach, and, passing it and preceding it on the road, would order its horses in advance, and greatly facilitate its progress during the precious hours of the night, when delay was the most to be dreaded.", "짐에 매이지 않은 그들은 곧 마차를 따라잡을 테고, 그것을 앞질러 길에서 먼저 가면서, 미리 말을 주문해두어, 지체가 가장 두려운 그 귀중한 밤 시간 동안 마차의 진행을 크게 수월하게 해줄 것이었다."]], [["Seeing in this arrangement the hope of rendering real service in that pressing emergency, Miss Pross hailed it with joy.", "이 계획에서 그 급박한 위기에 진정한 도움이 될 희망을 발견한 프로스 양은 그것을 기쁘게 반겼다."], ["She and Jerry had beheld the coach start, had known who it was that Solomon brought, had passed some ten minutes in tortures of suspense, and were now concluding their arrangements to follow the coach, even as Madame Defarge, taking her way through the streets, now drew nearer and nearer to the else-deserted lodging in which they held their consultation.", "그녀와 제리는 마차가 출발하는 것을 지켜보았고, 솔로몬이 누구를 데려왔는지도 알았으며, 한 십 분쯤 마음 졸이는 고통 속에서 보낸 뒤, 이제 막 마차를 뒤따를 준비를 마무리하고 있었다. 바로 그때 드파르주 부인은 거리를 헤치고 나아가, 그들이 의논을 나누고 있는, 그밖에는 텅 비어 있는 그 거처로 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“Now what do you think, Mr. Cruncher,”", "\"그런데 어떻게 생각하세요, 크런처 씨.\""], ["said Miss Pross, whose agitation was so great that she could hardly speak, or stand, or move, or live: “what do you think of our not starting from this courtyard?", "너무 흥분한 나머지 말하는 것도, 서 있는 것도, 움직이는 것도, 살아 있는 것조차 힘들 지경인 프로스 양이 말했다. \"이 안마당에서 출발하지 않는 게 어떨까 하는데요?"], ["Another carriage having already gone from here to-day, it might awaken suspicion.”", "오늘 이미 다른 마차가 여기서 떠났으니, 의심을 살지도 모르잖아요.\""]], [["“My opinion, miss,”", "\"제 의견은요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Mr.", "크런처 씨가 대답했다."], ["Cruncher, “is as you’re right.", "\"아가씨 말씀이 옳다는 겁니다."], ["Likewise wot I’ll stand by you, right or wrong.”", "그리고 옳든 그르든 저는 아가씨 편에 서겠습니다요.\""]], [["“I am so distracted with fear and hope for our precious creatures,”", "\"우리의 그 소중한 분들 때문에 두려움과 희망에 어찌나 정신이 산란한지,\""], ["said Miss Pross, wildly crying, “that I am incapable of forming any plan.", "프로스 양이 마구 울먹이며 말했다. \"도무지 무슨 계획도 세울 수가 없네요."], ["Are you capable of forming any plan, my dear good Mr. Cruncher?”", "당신은 무슨 계획이라도 세울 수 있겠어요, 친절하고 착한 크런처 씨?\""]], [["“Respectin’ a future spear o’ life, miss,”", "\"앞으로의 영생에 관한 거라면요, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Mr.", "크런처 씨가 대답했다."], ["Cruncher, “I hope so.", "\"그러길 바랍니다."], ["Respectin’ any present use o’ this here blessed old head o’ mine, I think not.", "한데 여기 이 축복받은 늙은 대가리가 지금 당장 무슨 쓸모가 있느냐 하면요, 그건 아닌 것 같습니다요."], ["Would you do me the favour, miss, to take notice o’ two promises and wows wot it is my wishes fur to record in this here crisis?”", "아가씨, 제가 이 위기에 꼭 남겨두고 싶은 두 가지 약속과 맹세에 좀 주목해주시는 호의를 베풀어주시겠습니까요?\""]], [["“Oh, for gracious sake!”", "\"아이고, 제발!\""], ["cried Miss Pross, still wildly crying, “record them at once, and get them out of the way, like an excellent man.”", "프로스 양이 여전히 마구 울먹이며 외쳤다. \"당장 남겨두고 처치해버려요, 훌륭한 양반답게요.\""]], [["“First,” said Mr.", "\"첫째,\""], ["Cruncher, who was all in a tremble, and who spoke with an ashy and solemn visage, “them poor things well out o’ this, never no more will I do it, never no more!”", "온몸을 부들부들 떨며, 잿빛으로 질린 엄숙한 얼굴로 크런처 씨가 말했다. \"저 가엾은 분들이 여기서 무사히 빠져나가면요, 다시는, 다시는 그 짓을 안 하겠습니다요!\""]], [["“I am quite sure, Mr.", "\"틀림없이요, 크런처 씨.\""], ["Cruncher,”", "프로스 양이 대답했다."], ["returned Miss Pross, “that you never will do it again, whatever it is, and I beg you not to think it necessary to mention more particularly what it is.”", "\"그게 뭐든 간에 당신은 다시는 그 짓을 안 할 거예요. 그러니 그게 뭔지 더 자세히 말씀하실 필요는 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“No, miss,”", "\"예, 아가씨.\""], ["returned Jerry, “it shall not be named to you.", "제리가 대답했다. \"아가씨께 그게 뭔지는 입에 올리지 않겠습니다요."], ["Second: them poor things well out o’ this, and never no more will I interfere with Mrs. Cruncher’s flopping, never no more!”", "둘째: 저 가엾은 분들이 여기서 무사히 빠져나가면요, 다시는, 다시는 크런처 부인이 엎드려 기도하는 데 참견하지 않겠습니다요!\""]], [["“Whatever housekeeping arrangement that may be,”", "\"그게 무슨 살림살이 방식이든 간에요,\""], ["said Miss Pross, striving to dry her eyes and compose herself, “I have no doubt it is best that Mrs.", "프로스 양이 눈물을 닦고 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["Cruncher should have it entirely under her own superintendence.--O my poor darlings!”", "\"크런처 부인이 그걸 온전히 자기 뜻대로 알아서 하시는 게 가장 좋으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없네요.—오, 내 가엾은 분들!\""]], [["“I go so far as to say, miss, moreover,” proceeded Mr.", "\"게다가 한 가지 더 말씀드리자면요, 아가씨.\""], ["Cruncher, with a most alarming tendency to hold forth as from a pulpit--“and let my words be took down and took to Mrs.", "크런처 씨가 마치 설교단에서 일장 연설이라도 늘어놓을 듯한 심히 불안한 기색으로 말을 이었다. \"그리고 제 말을 받아 적으셨다가 아가씨를 통해 크런처 부인한테 전해주십시오."], ["Cruncher through yourself--that wot my opinions respectin’ flopping has undergone a change, and that wot I only hope with all my heart as Mrs. Cruncher may be a flopping at the present time.”", "엎드려 기도하는 데 관한 제 의견이 바뀌었으니, 부디 진심으로 바라건대 크런처 부인이 바로 지금 이 순간에 엎드려 기도하고 있기를 바란다고요.\""]], [["“There, there, there!", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그래요!"], ["I hope she is, my dear man,”", "나도 그분이 그러고 계시기를 바라요, 착한 양반.\""], ["cried the distracted Miss Pross, “and I hope she finds it answering her expectations.”", "정신이 산란한 프로스 양이 외쳤다. \"그리고 그분이 그 기도로 바라던 응답을 얻으시기를 바라요.\""]], [["“Forbid it,” proceeded Mr.", "\"부디 그런 일은 없기를요.\""], ["Cruncher, with additional solemnity, additional slowness, and additional tendency to hold forth and hold out, “as anything wot I have ever said or done should be wisited on my earnest wishes for them poor creeturs now!", "한층 더 엄숙하게, 한층 더 느릿느릿하게, 그리고 한층 더 일장 연설을 늘어놓고 손을 뻗칠 듯한 기색으로 크런처 씨가 말을 이었다. \"제가 일찍이 했던 말이나 행동이 지금 저 가엾은 분들을 위한 제 간절한 소망에 화로 돌아오는 일은 없기를요!"], ["Forbid it as we shouldn’t all flop (if it was anyways conwenient) to get ’em out o’ this here dismal risk!", "우리 모두가 (어떻게든 형편이 닿는다면) 엎드려 빌어서라도 저분들을 이 음산한 위험에서 구해내지 못하는 일은 없기를요!"], ["Forbid it, miss!", "부디, 아가씨!"], ["Wot I say, for-bid it!” This was Mr.", "제 말은, 부-디 그런 일은 없기를요!\""], ["Cruncher’s conclusion after a protracted but vain endeavour to find a better one.", "이것이, 더 나은 결론을 찾으려 한참을 헛되이 애쓴 끝에 크런처 씨가 내린 결론이었다."]], [["And still Madame Defarge, pursuing her way along the streets, came nearer and nearer.", "그리고 드파르주 부인은 여전히 거리를 따라 나아가며, 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“If we ever get back to our native land,”", "\"우리가 고국 땅으로 다시 돌아가게 되면요,\""], ["said Miss Pross, “you may rely upon my telling Mrs.", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["Cruncher as much as I may be able to remember and understand of what you have so impressively said; and at all events you may be sure that I shall bear witness to your being thoroughly in earnest at this dreadful time.", "\"당신이 그토록 감명 깊게 하신 말씀을 제가 기억하고 이해할 수 있는 만큼은 크런처 부인께 전해드릴 테니 믿으셔도 좋아요. 그리고 어쨌든 이 끔찍한 때에 당신이 정말 진심이었다는 걸 제가 증언해드릴 테니 안심하세요."], ["Now, pray let us think!", "자, 이제 좀 생각해봅시다!"], ["My esteemed Mr. Cruncher, let us think!”", "존경하는 크런처 씨, 우리 생각해봐요!\""]], [["Still, Madame Defarge, pursuing her way along the streets, came nearer and nearer.", "여전히 드파르주 부인은 거리를 따라 나아가며, 점점 더 가까이 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“If you were to go before,”", "\"당신이 먼저 가서요,\""], ["said Miss Pross, “and stop the vehicle and horses from coming here, and were to wait somewhere for me; wouldn’t that be best?”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"탈것과 말이 이리로 오지 못하게 막고, 어디선가 저를 기다린다면요, 그게 제일 낫지 않을까요?\""]], [["Mr. Cruncher thought it might be best.", "크런처 씨는 그게 제일 나을 것 같다고 생각했다."]], [["“Where could you wait for me?”", "\"어디서 저를 기다리시겠어요?\""], ["asked Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 물었다."]], [["Mr. Cruncher was so bewildered that he could think of no locality but Temple Bar.", "크런처 씨는 어찌나 어리둥절했는지 템플 바 말고는 떠오르는 장소가 없었다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["Temple Bar was hundreds of miles away, and Madame Defarge was drawing very near indeed.", "템플 바는 수백 마일이나 떨어진 곳이었고, 드파르주 부인은 정말이지 아주 가까이 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“By the cathedral door,”", "\"대성당 문 앞에서요.\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["“Would it be much out of the way, to take me in, near the great cathedral door between the two towers?”", "\"두 탑 사이에 있는 그 큰 대성당 문 가까이에서 저를 태운다면, 길에서 그리 많이 벗어나진 않겠지요?\""]], [["“No, miss,”", "\"아닙니다, 아가씨.\""], ["answered Mr. Cruncher.", "크런처 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Then, like the best of men,”", "\"그럼, 훌륭한 양반답게,\""], ["said Miss Pross, “go to the posting-house straight, and make that change.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"곧장 역마차 정거장으로 가서 그 교환을 해주세요.\""]], [["“I am doubtful,” said Mr.", "\"미덥지가 않아서요,\""], ["Cruncher, hesitating and shaking his head, “about leaving of you, you see.", "크런처 씨가 머뭇거리며 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"아가씨를 두고 가는 게 말입니다요."], ["We don’t know what may happen.”", "무슨 일이 생길지 모르잖습니까.\""]], [["“Heaven knows we don’t,”", "\"무슨 일이 생길지 모르는 건 하느님은 아시지요.\""], ["returned Miss Pross, “but have no fear for me.", "프로스 양이 대답했다. \"하지만 제 걱정은 마세요."], ["Take me in at the cathedral, at Three o’Clock, or as near it as you can, and I am sure it will be better than our going from here.", "세시에, 아니면 거기에 최대한 가까운 시각에 대성당에서 저를 태워주세요. 그게 우리가 여기서 떠나는 것보다 분명 나을 거예요."], ["I feel certain of it.", "확신해요."], ["There!", "자!"], ["Bless you, Mr. Cruncher!", "당신께 축복이 있기를, 크런처 씨!"], ["Think-not of me, but of the lives that may depend on both of us!”", "저는 생각 말고, 우리 두 사람에게 달려 있을지도 모를 그 목숨들을 생각하세요!\""]], [["This exordium, and Miss Pross’s two hands in quite agonised entreaty clasping his, decided Mr. Cruncher.", "이 서두와, 프로스 양의 두 손이 사뭇 애절하게 그의 손을 움켜쥔 것이 크런처 씨의 마음을 정하게 했다."], ["With an encouraging nod or two, he immediately went out to alter the arrangements, and left her by herself to follow as she had proposed.", "그는 격려하듯 한두 번 고개를 끄덕이고는, 곧장 채비를 바꾸러 나가면서, 그녀가 제안한 대로 뒤따라오도록 그녀를 홀로 남겨두었다."]], [["The having originated a precaution which was already in course of execution, was a great relief to Miss Pross.", "이미 실행에 옮겨지고 있는 어떤 대비책을 자기가 발의했다는 사실은 프로스 양에게 큰 위안이 되었다."], ["The necessity of composing her appearance so that it should attract no special notice in the streets, was another relief.", "거리에서 특별히 눈에 띄지 않도록 자기 차림새를 단정하게 해야 할 필요가 있다는 것도 또 다른 위안이었다."], ["She looked at her watch, and it was twenty minutes past two.", "시계를 보니 두시 이십분이었다."], ["She had no time to lose, but must get ready at once.", "지체할 시간이 없었고, 당장 채비를 갖춰야 했다."]], [["Afraid, in her extreme perturbation, of the loneliness of the deserted rooms, and of half-imagined faces peeping from behind every open door in them, Miss Pross got a basin of cold water and began laving her eyes, which were swollen and red.", "극도로 마음이 동요한 나머지, 텅 빈 방들의 적막함과, 그 안의 활짝 열린 문마다 그 뒤에서 반쯤 상상으로 빚어낸 얼굴들이 엿보는 것 같은 느낌에 겁이 난 프로스 양은, 찬물을 한 대야 떠다가 퉁퉁 붓고 빨개진 눈을 적시기 시작했다."], ["Haunted by her feverish apprehensions, she could not bear to have her sight obscured for a minute at a time by the dripping water, but constantly paused and looked round to see that there was no one watching her.", "열에 들뜬 듯한 불안에 시달린 그녀는, 단 한순간이라도 떨어지는 물에 시야가 가려지는 것을 견딜 수 없어, 끊임없이 멈추고 둘러보며 자기를 지켜보는 이가 없는지 확인했다."], ["In one of those pauses she recoiled and cried out, for she saw a figure standing in the room.", "그렇게 멈추었던 어느 순간, 그녀는 흠칫 뒤로 물러서며 비명을 질렀으니, 방 안에 한 사람이 서 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["The basin fell to the ground broken, and the water flowed to the feet of Madame Defarge.", "대야가 바닥에 떨어져 깨지고, 물이 드파르주 부인의 발치로 흘러갔다."], ["By strange stern ways, and through much staining blood, those feet had come to meet that water.", "기이하고 가혹한 길을 거쳐, 숱한 피로 얼룩진 길을 지나, 그 두 발은 그 물과 마주하기에 이른 것이었다."]], [["Madame Defarge looked coldly at her, and said, “The wife of Evrémonde; where is she?”", "드파르주 부인이 그녀를 차갑게 바라보며 말했다. \"에브레몽드의 아내, 그 여자는 어디 있지?\""]], [["It flashed upon Miss Pross’s mind that the doors were all standing open, and would suggest the flight.", "프로스 양의 머릿속에 문이 죄다 열려 있어서 그들이 달아났음을 짐작하게 하리라는 생각이 번뜩 스쳤다."], ["Her first act was to shut them.", "그녀가 가장 먼저 한 일은 문을 닫는 것이었다."], ["There were four in the room, and she shut them all.", "방에는 문이 넷 있었고, 그녀는 그것을 모두 닫았다."], ["She then placed herself before the door of the chamber which Lucie had occupied.", "그런 다음 루시가 쓰던 방의 문 앞에 자기 몸을 가져다 세웠다."]], [["Madame Defarge’s dark eyes followed her through this rapid movement, and rested on her when it was finished.", "드파르주 부인의 검은 눈은 이 재빠른 동작을 내내 좇았고, 그것이 끝나자 그녀에게 머물렀다. 프로스 양에게는 아름다운 구석이라고는 하나도 없었다."], ["Miss Pross had nothing beautiful about her; years had not tamed the wildness, or softened the grimness, of her appearance; but, she too was a determined woman in her different way, and she measured Madame Defarge with her eyes, every inch.", "세월도 그 사나운 인상을 길들이지도, 그 험상궂은 모습을 누그러뜨리지도 못했다. 그러나 그녀 역시 나름의 방식으로 단호한 여자였고, 그녀도 한 치 한 치 눈으로 드파르주 부인을 가늠했다."]], [["“You might, from your appearance, be the wife of Lucifer,”", "\"당신은 그 꼴을 보아하니, 루시퍼의 아내쯤 되겠구려.\""], ["said Miss Pross, in her breathing.", "프로스 양이 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다."], ["“Nevertheless, you shall not get the better of me.", "\"그래도, 당신이 날 이기지는 못해."], ["I am an Englishwoman.”", "나는 영국 여자야.\""]], [["Madame Defarge looked at her scornfully, but still with something of Miss Pross’s own perception that they two were at bay.", "드파르주 부인은 그녀를 경멸스럽게 바라보면서도, 두 사람이 막다른 처지에 몰렸다는 것을 프로스 양과 마찬가지로 어느 정도는 알아챘다."], ["She saw a tight, hard, wiry woman before her, as Mr. Lorry had seen in the same figure a woman with a strong hand, in the years gone by.", "그녀는 자기 앞에 옹골차고 단단하며 깡마른 여자가 있는 것을 보았으니, 지난날 로리 씨가 바로 그 모습에서 억센 손을 가진 여자를 보았던 것과 같았다."], ["She knew full well that Miss Pross was the family’s devoted friend; Miss Pross knew full well that Madame Defarge was the family’s malevolent enemy.", "그녀는 프로스 양이 그 가족의 헌신적인 친구임을 잘 알고 있었고, 프로스 양은 드파르주 부인이 그 가족의 악의에 찬 적임을 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["“On my way yonder,”", "\"저기로 가는 길에 말이야.\""], ["said Madame Defarge, with a slight movement of her hand towards the fatal spot, “where they reserve my chair and my knitting for me, I am come to make my compliments to her in passing.", "드파르주 부인이 손을 그 운명의 장소 쪽으로 살짝 움직이며 말했다. \"거기 사람들이 내 의자와 내 뜨개질감을 챙겨두는 그곳으로 말이지, 지나는 길에 그 여자한테 인사나 하려고 왔어."], ["I wish to see her.”", "그 여자를 좀 봐야겠어.\""]], [["“I know that your intentions are evil,”", "\"당신 속셈이 사악하다는 걸 알아.\""], ["said Miss Pross, “and you may depend upon it, I’ll hold my own against them.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"그러니 똑똑히 알아둬, 나는 그 속셈에 맞서 끝까지 버틸 거야.\""]], [["Each spoke in her own language; neither understood the other’s words; both were very watchful, and intent to deduce from look and manner, what the unintelligible words meant.", "각자 자기 언어로 말했으니, 어느 쪽도 상대의 말을 알아듣지 못했다. 둘 다 몹시 경계하며, 그 알아들을 수 없는 말이 무슨 뜻인지 표정과 태도에서 헤아려내려고 골몰했다."]], [["“It will do her no good to keep herself concealed from me at this moment,”", "\"이 마당에 나한테서 몸을 숨겨봐야 그 여자한테 이로울 게 없을 텐데.\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."], ["“Good patriots will know what that means.", "\"선량한 애국자라면 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알 거야."], ["Let me see her.", "그 여자를 보게 해."], ["Go tell her that I wish to see her.", "가서 내가 그 여자를 보고 싶어한다고 전해."], ["Do you hear?”", "알아듣겠어?\""]], [["“If those eyes of yours were bed-winches,”", "\"당신의 그 두 눈이 침대 죔쇠라 해도,\""], ["returned Miss Pross, “and I was an English four-poster, they shouldn’t loose a splinter of me.", "프로스 양이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 내가 영국제 사주식 침대라 해도, 내 몸에서 나무 부스러기 하나 풀어내지 못할 거야."], ["No, you wicked foreign woman; I am your match.”", "안 돼, 이 사악한 외국 여편네야. 나는 네 적수야.\""]], [["Madame Defarge was not likely to follow these idiomatic remarks in detail; but, she so far understood them as to perceive that she was set at naught.", "드파르주 부인이 이런 관용적인 말들을 일일이 알아들었을 리는 없었다. 그러나, 자기가 깡그리 무시당하고 있다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 알아들었다."]], [["“Woman imbecile and pig-like!”", "\"멍청하고 돼지 같은 여편네!\""], ["said Madame Defarge, frowning.", "드파르주 부인이 눈살을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“I take no answer from you.", "\"너한테서는 아무 대답도 받지 않겠다."], ["I demand to see her.", "나는 그 여자를 보겠다고 요구하는 거야."], ["Either tell her that I demand to see her, or stand out of the way of the door and let me go to her!”", "내가 그 여자를 보겠다고 요구한다고 그 여자한테 전하든지, 아니면 그 문 앞에서 비켜서서 내가 그 여자한테 가게 하든지 해!\""], ["This, with an angry explanatory wave of her right arm.", "이렇게 말하며, 그녀는 성난 듯 오른팔을 휘둘러 설명하려 했다."]], [["“I little thought,”", "\"나는 꿈에도 생각 못했어.\" 프로스 양이 말했다."], ["said Miss Pross, “that I should ever want to understand your nonsensical language; but I would give all I have, except the clothes I wear, to know whether you suspect the truth, or any part of it.”", "\"내가 언젠가 네 그 터무니없는 말을 알아듣고 싶어할 줄은. 하지만 입은 옷만 빼고 내가 가진 전부를 다 줘서라도, 네가 진실을, 아니 그 일부라도 눈치챘는지 알고 싶구나.\""]], [["Neither of them for a single moment released the other’s eyes.", "두 사람 모두 단 한순간도 서로의 눈에서 시선을 떼지 않았다."], ["Madame Defarge had not moved from the spot where she stood when Miss Pross first became aware of her; but, she now advanced one step.", "드파르주 부인은 프로스 양이 처음 그녀를 알아챘을 때 서 있던 자리에서 꼼짝도 하지 않았었다. 그러나, 이제 그녀는 한 걸음 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["“I am a Briton,”", "\"나는 영국 사람이야.\""], ["said Miss Pross, “I am desperate.", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"나는 죽기를 각오했어."], ["I don’t care an English Twopence for myself.", "내 한 몸 따위는 영국 동전 2펜스만큼도 아깝지 않아."], ["I know that the longer I keep you here, the greater hope there is for my Ladybird.", "내가 너를 여기 오래 붙들어둘수록, 우리 아가씨한테는 그만큼 희망이 커진다는 걸 알아."], ["I’ll not leave a handful of that dark hair upon your head, if you lay a finger on me!”", "네가 내게 손가락 하나라도 댄다면, 네 머리에 그 검은 머리카락 한 줌도 남겨두지 않을 테다!\""]], [["Thus Miss Pross, with a shake of her head and a flash of her eyes between every rapid sentence, and every rapid sentence a whole breath.", "이렇게 프로스 양은, 빠르게 한 문장 한 문장 내뱉을 때마다 고개를 한 번씩 내젓고 두 눈을 번뜩이며, 그 빠른 문장 하나하나에 숨을 통째로 실어 말했다."], ["Thus Miss Pross, who had never struck a blow in her life.", "이렇게 프로스 양은, 평생 단 한 번도 누구를 때려본 적 없는 그 프로스 양은 말했다."]], [["But, her courage was of that emotional nature that it brought the irrepressible tears into her eyes.", "그러나, 그녀의 용기는 그런 감정적인 부류여서, 걷잡을 수 없이 눈물이 두 눈에 차오르게 만들었다."], ["This was a courage that Madame Defarge so little comprehended as to mistake for weakness.", "이것은 드파르주 부인이 너무도 이해하지 못한 나머지 나약함으로 오해할 만한 용기였다."], ["“Ha, ha!”", "\"하, 하!\""], ["she laughed, “you poor wretch!", "그녀가 웃었다. \"이 가엾은 것아!"], ["What are you worth!", "네까짓 게 무슨 가치가 있다고!"], ["I address myself to that Doctor.”", "나는 그 의사한테 말을 거는 거야.\""], ["Then she raised her voice and called out, “Citizen Doctor!", "그런 다음 그녀는 목소리를 높여 외쳤다. \"의사 시민!"], ["Wife of Evrémonde!", "에브레몽드의 아내!"], ["Child of Evrémonde!", "에브레몽드의 자식!"], ["Any person but this miserable fool, answer the Citizeness Defarge!”", "이 가련한 바보 말고 누구든, 시민 드파르주에게 대답하라!\""]], [["Perhaps the following silence, perhaps some latent disclosure in the expression of Miss Pross’s face, perhaps a sudden misgiving apart from either suggestion, whispered to Madame Defarge that they were gone.", "뒤이은 침묵 때문이었는지, 프로스 양의 표정에 어린 무언가 숨은 단서 때문이었는지, 아니면 그 어느 쪽 암시와도 무관한 어떤 불현듯한 의구심 때문이었는지, 무언가가 드파르주 부인에게 그들이 가버렸다고 속삭였다."], ["Three of the doors she opened swiftly, and looked in.", "그녀는 문 셋을 잽싸게 열어젖히고 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["“Those rooms are all in disorder, there has been hurried packing, there are odds and ends upon the ground.", "\"방들이 죄다 어질러졌고, 황급히 짐을 꾸린 흔적이 있고, 바닥에 자질구레한 것들이 널려 있군."], ["There is no one in that room behind you!", "네 뒤에 있는 저 방에는 아무도 없을 텐데!"], ["Let me look.”", "어디 좀 보자.\""]], [["“Never!”", "\"절대 안 돼!\""], ["said Miss Pross, who understood the request as perfectly as Madame Defarge understood the answer.", "프로스 양이 말했으니, 그녀는 그 요구를, 드파르주 부인이 그 대답을 알아들은 것만큼이나 정확히 알아들었다."]], [["“If they are not in that room, they are gone, and can be pursued and brought back,”", "\"저 방에 그들이 없다면, 가버린 거고, 그렇다면 뒤쫓아 다시 잡아올 수 있어.\""], ["said Madame Defarge to herself.", "드파르주 부인이 속으로 말했다."]], [["“As long as you don’t know whether they are in that room or not, you are uncertain what to do,”", "\"네가 그들이 저 방에 있는지 없는지 모르는 한, 너는 어떻게 해야 할지 확신을 못할 테지.\" 프로스 양이 속으로 말했다."], ["said Miss Pross to herself; “and you shall not know that, if I can prevent your knowing it; and know that, or not know that, you shall not leave here while I can hold you.”", "\"그리고 내가 네가 알아내는 걸 막을 수 있는 한, 너는 그걸 알지 못할 거야. 그걸 알든 모르든, 내가 너를 붙들 수 있는 한 너는 여기서 나가지 못해.\""]], [["“I have been in the streets from the first, nothing has stopped me, I will tear you to pieces, but I will have you from that door,”", "\"나는 처음부터 거리를 누볐고, 아무것도 나를 막지 못했어. 너를 갈가리 찢어서라도, 저 문에서 너를 떼어내고야 말겠다.\""], ["said Madame Defarge.", "드파르주 부인이 말했다."]], [["“We are alone at the top of a high house in a solitary courtyard, we are not likely to be heard, and I pray for bodily strength to keep you here, while every minute you are here is worth a hundred thousand guineas to my darling,”", "\"우리는 외딴 안마당에 선 어느 높다란 집 꼭대기에 단둘이 있고, 우리 소리는 누구한테도 들릴 리 없으니, 내가 너를 여기 붙들어둘 몸의 힘을 달라고 기도하마. 네가 여기 있는 매 순간이 우리 아가씨한테는 십만 기니의 값어치가 있으니까.\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."]], [["Madame Defarge made at the door.", "드파르주 부인이 문으로 달려들었다."], ["Miss Pross, on the instinct of the moment, seized her round the waist in both her arms, and held her tight.", "프로스 양은 그 순간의 본능으로, 두 팔로 그녀의 허리를 끌어안고 꽉 붙들었다."], ["It was in vain for Madame Defarge to struggle and to strike; Miss Pross, with the vigorous tenacity of love, always so much stronger than hate, clasped her tight, and even lifted her from the floor in the struggle that they had.", "드파르주 부인이 몸부림치고 후려쳐봐도 소용없었다. 프로스 양은, 증오보다 늘 훨씬 강한 사랑의 그 억센 끈기로, 그녀를 단단히 끌어안고, 둘이 벌인 몸싸움에서 심지어 그녀를 바닥에서 들어올리기까지 했다."], ["The two hands of Madame Defarge buffeted and tore her face; but, Miss Pross, with her head down, held her round the waist, and clung to her with more than the hold of a drowning woman.", "드파르주 부인의 두 손이 그녀의 얼굴을 후려치고 할퀴었다. 그러나, 프로스 양은 고개를 숙인 채, 그녀의 허리를 끌어안고, 물에 빠진 여자가 매달리는 것보다 더 악착같이 그녀에게 매달렸다."]], [["Soon, Madame Defarge’s hands ceased to strike, and felt at her encircled waist.", "이윽고, 드파르주 부인의 두 손이 후려치기를 멈추고, 그녀에게 감겨 있는 자기 허리께를 더듬었다."], ["“It is under my arm,”", "\"그건 내 팔 밑에 있어.\""], ["said Miss Pross, in smothered tones, “you shall not draw it.", "프로스 양이 숨죽인 목소리로 말했다. \"너는 그걸 뽑지 못해."], ["I am stronger than you, I bless Heaven for it.", "내가 너보다 더 세거든, 하느님께 그 점을 감사드린다."], ["I hold you till one or other of us faints or dies!”", "우리 둘 중 하나가 기절하거나 죽을 때까지 너를 붙들고 있을 거야!\""]], [["Madame Defarge’s hands were at her bosom.", "드파르주 부인의 두 손이 그녀의 가슴팍에 가 닿았다."], ["Miss Pross looked up, saw what it was, struck at it, struck out a flash and a crash, and stood alone--blinded with smoke.", "프로스 양은 고개를 들어, 그것이 무엇인지 보고는, 그것을 후려쳤고, 한 줄기 섬광과 한 차례 굉음을 후려쳐 일으키고는, 홀로 서 있었다—연기에 눈이 먼 채로."]], [["All this was in a second.", "이 모든 것이 순식간에 일어났다."], ["As the smoke cleared, leaving an awful stillness, it passed out on the air, like the soul of the furious woman whose body lay lifeless on the ground.", "연기가 걷히며 무시무시한 정적을 남기자, 그 연기는, 바닥에 시신이 되어 누워 있는 그 격노한 여인의 영혼처럼, 허공으로 흩어져 나갔다."]], [["In the first fright and horror of her situation, Miss Pross passed the body as far from it as she could, and ran down the stairs to call for fruitless help.", "자기 처지에 대한 처음의 공포와 경악에 휩싸인 프로스 양은, 그 시신에서 되도록 멀찍이 떨어져 그 곁을 지나, 계단을 뛰어 내려가 헛되이 도움을 청하려 했다."], ["Happily, she bethought herself of the consequences of what she did, in time to check herself and go back.", "다행히, 그녀는 자기가 하려는 짓의 결과를 제때 떠올리고는, 스스로를 멈춰 세우고 되돌아갔다."], ["It was dreadful to go in at the door again; but, she did go in, and even went near it, to get the bonnet and other things that she must wear.", "그 문으로 다시 들어가는 것은 끔찍한 일이었다. 그러나, 그녀는 안으로 들어갔고, 심지어 그 곁까지 다가가, 꼭 써야 할 보닛과 그밖의 것들을 챙겼다."], ["These she put on, out on the staircase, first shutting and locking the door and taking away the key.", "그녀는 먼저 문을 닫아 잠그고 열쇠를 빼낸 다음, 그것들을 계단참에서 몸에 걸쳤다."], ["She then sat down on the stairs a few moments to breathe and to cry, and then got up and hurried away.", "그런 다음 잠시 계단에 앉아 숨을 고르고 울고는, 일어나서 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["By good fortune she had a veil on her bonnet, or she could hardly have gone along the streets without being stopped.", "다행히도 그녀의 보닛에는 베일이 달려 있었으니, 그렇지 않았다면 거리를 따라가다가 제지당하지 않고는 도저히 갈 수 없었으리라."], ["By good fortune, too, she was naturally so peculiar in appearance as not to show disfigurement like any other woman.", "또한 다행히도, 그녀는 본디 생김새가 하도 유별나서 다른 여느 여자처럼 흉한 자국을 드러내지 않았다."], ["She needed both advantages, for the marks of gripping fingers were deep in her face, and her hair was torn, and her dress (hastily composed with unsteady hands) was clutched and dragged a hundred ways.", "그녀에게는 그 두 가지 이점이 다 필요했으니, 얼굴에는 움켜쥐었던 손가락 자국이 깊이 패었고, 머리는 헝클어졌으며, (떨리는 손으로 황급히 추슬렀던) 옷은 사방으로 움켜쥐어지고 잡아당겨져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["In crossing the bridge, she dropped the door key in the river.", "다리를 건너면서, 그녀는 그 문 열쇠를 강물에 떨어뜨렸다."], ["Arriving at the cathedral some few minutes before her escort, and waiting there, she thought, what if the key were already taken in a net, what if it were identified, what if the door were opened and the remains discovered, what if she were stopped at the gate, sent to prison, and charged with murder!", "호위병보다 몇 분 먼저 대성당에 다다라 거기서 기다리던 중에, 그녀는 이런 생각을 했다. 그 열쇠가 벌써 그물에 걸려 올라왔으면 어쩌나, 그게 누구 것인지 밝혀졌으면 어쩌나, 그 문이 열려 시신이 발견되었으면 어쩌나, 내가 성문에서 붙들려 감옥에 끌려가 살인죄로 기소되면 어쩌나!"], ["In the midst of these fluttering thoughts, the escort appeared, took her in, and took her away.", "이렇게 마음이 뒤숭숭한 생각들에 잠겨 있는데, 호위병이 나타나, 그녀를 태우고는, 데려갔다."]], [["“Is there any noise in the streets?”", "\"거리가 소란스럽나요?\""], ["she asked him.", "그녀가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“The usual noises,” Mr.", "\"여느 때 소란이지요.\""], ["Cruncher replied; and looked surprised by the question and by her aspect.", "크런처 씨가 대답하고는, 그 질문과 그녀의 행색에 놀란 듯한 표정을 지었다."]], [["“I don’t hear you,”", "\"안 들려요.\""], ["said Miss Pross.", "프로스 양이 말했다."], ["“What do you say?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨어요?\""]], [["It was in vain for Mr. Cruncher to repeat what he said; Miss Pross could not hear him.", "크런처 씨가 자기가 한 말을 되풀이해봐야 소용없었으니, 프로스 양은 그의 말을 들을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["“So I’ll nod my head,” thought Mr.", "\"그럼 고개를 끄덕여야지.\""], ["Cruncher, amazed, “at all events she’ll see that.”", "깜짝 놀란 크런처 씨가 생각했다. \"어쨌든 그건 보겠지.\""], ["And she did.", "과연 그녀는 보았다."]], [["“Is there any noise in the streets now?”", "\"지금 거리가 소란스러운가요?\""], ["asked Miss Pross again, presently.", "프로스 양이 잠시 후 다시 물었다."]], [["Again Mr. Cruncher nodded his head.", "크런처 씨가 또다시 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“I don’t hear it.”", "\"안 들려요.\""]], [["“Gone deaf in an hour?” said Mr.", "\"한 시간 만에 귀가 먹었나?\""], ["Cruncher, ruminating, with his mind much disturbed; “wot’s come to her?”", "마음이 몹시 어지러워진 크런처 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"이 양반한테 무슨 일이 생긴 거지?\""]], [["“I feel,”", "\"느낌이,\""], ["said Miss Pross, “as if there had been a flash and a crash, and that crash was the last thing I should ever hear in this life.”", "프로스 양이 말했다. \"한 줄기 섬광과 한 차례 굉음이 있었던 것 같고, 그 굉음이 제가 이 생에서 듣게 될 마지막 소리인 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“Blest if she ain’t in a queer condition!”", "\"이거야 원, 이 양반 상태가 이상하잖아!\""], ["said Mr. Cruncher, more and more disturbed.", "점점 더 마음이 어지러워진 크런처 씨가 말했다."], ["“Wot can she have been a takin’, to keep her courage up?", "\"용기를 북돋우려고 뭘 마셨길래 이런담? 들어봐요!"], ["Hark! There’s the roll of them dreadful carts!", "저 끔찍한 수레들이 굴러가는 소리가 들리잖아요!"], ["You can hear that, miss?”", "그건 들리지요, 아가씨?\""]], [["“I can hear,”", "\"제게는 들리지 않아요.\""], ["said Miss Pross, seeing that he spoke to her, “nothing.", "그가 자기한테 말하는 것을 알아챈 프로스 양이 말했다."], ["O, my good man, there was first a great crash, and then a great stillness, and that stillness seems to be fixed and unchangeable, never to be broken any more as long as my life lasts.”", "\"아무것도요. 오, 착한 양반, 처음에 큰 굉음이 있었고, 그다음에 큰 정적이 왔는데, 그 정적이 못박힌 듯 변함없이 자리 잡아서, 제 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 다시는 깨지지 않을 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“If she don’t hear the roll of those dreadful carts, now very nigh their journey’s end,” said Mr.", "\"이제 그 여정이 거의 끝나가는 저 끔찍한 수레들이 굴러가는 소리도 이 양반이 못 듣는다면,\""], ["Cruncher, glancing over his shoulder, “it’s my opinion that indeed she never will hear anything else in this world.”", "크런처 씨가 어깨 너머로 흘끗 돌아보며 말했다. \"내 생각엔 정말이지 이 양반은 이 세상에서 다시는 그 무엇도 듣지 못할 거야.\""]], [["And indeed she never did.", "그리고 과연 그녀는 다시는 듣지 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV. The Footsteps Die Out For Ever", "# CHAPTER XV. The Footsteps Die Out For Ever"]], [["Along the Paris streets, the death-carts rumble, hollow and harsh.", "빠리의 거리를 따라 죽음의 수레들이 공허하고 거칠게 덜컹거리며 굴러간다."], ["Six tumbrils carry the day’s wine to La Guillotine.", "여섯 대의 호송 수레가 그날의 포도주를 라 기요띤으로 실어 나른다."], ["All the devouring and insatiate Monsters imagined since imagination could record itself, are fused in the one realisation, Guillotine.", "상상이 스스로를 기록할 수 있게 된 이래로 상상해온 모든 게걸스럽고 만족을 모르는 괴물들이 단 하나의 실체, 곧 기요띤으로 녹아들었다."], ["And yet there is not in France, with its rich variety of soil and climate, a blade, a leaf, a root, a sprig, a peppercorn, which will grow to maturity under conditions more certain than those that have produced this horror.", "그러나 토양과 기후가 풍요롭게 다양한 프랑스에도, 이 공포를 만들어낸 조건들보다 더 확실한 조건 아래서 무르익도록 자라날 풀잎 하나, 나뭇잎 하나, 뿌리 하나, 가지 하나, 후추 한 알도 없다."], ["Crush humanity out of shape once more, under similar hammers, and it will twist itself into the same tortured forms.", "비슷한 망치들 아래 인간성을 다시 한번 형체를 잃도록 으깨어보라, 그러면 그것은 똑같이 고통받는 모습으로 비틀려갈 것이다."], ["Sow the same seed of rapacious license and oppression over again, and it will surely yield the same fruit according to its kind.", "탐욕스러운 방종과 압제의 똑같은 씨앗을 다시 뿌려보라, 그러면 그것은 그 종류대로 반드시 똑같은 열매를 맺을 것이다."]], [["Six tumbrils roll along the streets.", "여섯 대의 호송 수레가 거리를 따라 굴러간다."], ["Change these back again to what they were, thou powerful enchanter, Time, and they shall be seen to be the carriages of absolute monarchs, the equipages of feudal nobles, the toilettes of flaring Jezebels, the churches that are not my father’s house but dens of thieves, the huts of millions of starving peasants!", "힘센 마법사인 시간이여, 이것들을 본래의 모습으로 되돌려놓아보라, 그러면 그것들이 절대군주의 마차였고, 봉건 귀족의 마차였고, 화려한 이세벨들의 화장대였고, 내 아버지의 집이 아니라 도둑들의 소굴이었던 교회였고, 굶주린 수백만 농민의 오두막이었음이 보이리라!"], ["No; the great magician who majestically works out the appointed order of the Creator, never reverses his transformations.", "아니다. 창조주가 정하신 질서를 장엄하게 이루어가는 저 위대한 마법사는 결코 자신의 변형을 되돌리지 않는다."], ["“If thou be changed into this shape by the will of God,”", "지혜로운 아라비아 이야기 속에서, 예언자들은 마법에 걸린 자에게 이렇게 말한다."], ["say the seers to the enchanted, in the wise Arabian stories, “then remain so!", "\"그대가 신의 뜻으로 이 모습으로 변했다면, 그대로 머물러 있으라!"], ["But, if thou wear this form through mere passing conjuration, then resume thy former aspect!”", "그러나 그대가 한낱 스쳐 지나가는 주문으로 이 형상을 걸쳤을 뿐이라면, 그대의 본래 모습으로 돌아가라!\""], ["Changeless and hopeless, the tumbrils roll along.", "변함도 없고 희망도 없이, 호송 수레들은 굴러간다."]], [["As the sombre wheels of the six carts go round, they seem to plough up a long crooked furrow among the populace in the streets.", "여섯 대의 수레의 음울한 바퀴가 돌아가면서, 거리에 모인 군중 사이로 길고 구불구불한 고랑을 가는 것처럼 보인다."], ["Ridges of faces are thrown to this side and to that, and the ploughs go steadily onward.", "얼굴들의 이랑이 이쪽저쪽으로 던져지고, 쟁기는 꾸준히 앞으로 나아간다."], ["So used are the regular inhabitants of the houses to the spectacle, that in many windows there are no people, and in some the occupation of the hands is not so much as suspended, while the eyes survey the faces in the tumbrils.", "그 집들의 붙박이 주민들은 이 광경에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지, 많은 창문에는 사람이 없고, 어떤 창문에서는 눈으로 호송 수레 속의 얼굴들을 훑어보면서도 손놀림조차 멈추지 않는다."], ["Here and there, the inmate has visitors to see the sight; then he points his finger, with something of the complacency of a curator or authorised exponent, to this cart and to this, and seems to tell who sat here yesterday, and who there the day before.", "여기저기에서 그 광경을 구경하러 온 손님을 맞은 주민이 있다. 그러면 그는 박물관 관리인이나 공인된 해설가 같은 흡족함을 띠고 이 수레와 저 수레를 손가락으로 가리키며, 어제는 누가 여기 앉아 있었고 그제는 누가 저기 앉아 있었는지 일러주는 것 같다."]], [["Of the riders in the tumbrils, some observe these things, and all things on their last roadside, with an impassive stare; others, with a lingering interest in the ways of life and men.", "호송 수레에 탄 자들 가운데 어떤 이들은 이런 것들을, 그리고 자신들의 마지막 길가에 있는 모든 것을 무표정한 눈길로 바라본다. 또 어떤 이들은 삶과 사람들의 방식에 미련을 둔 채 바라본다."], ["Some, seated with drooping heads, are sunk in silent despair; again, there are some so heedful of their looks that they cast upon the multitude such glances as they have seen in theatres, and in pictures.", "어떤 이들은 고개를 떨군 채 앉아 말없는 절망에 잠겨 있다. 또다른 이들은 자기 모습에 너무 신경을 쓴 나머지, 극장이나 그림에서 본 듯한 눈길을 군중에게 던진다."], ["Several close their eyes, and think, or try to get their straying thoughts together.", "몇몇은 눈을 감고 생각에 잠기거나, 흩어진 생각을 한데 모으려 애쓴다."], ["Only one, and he a miserable creature, of a crazed aspect, is so shattered and made drunk by horror, that he sings, and tries to dance.", "오직 한 사람, 정신이 나간 듯한 가련한 존재만이 공포로 산산이 부서지고 취해버려, 노래를 부르고 춤을 추려 한다."], ["Not one of the whole number appeals by look or gesture, to the pity of the people.", "그 전부 가운데 어느 누구도 눈길이나 몸짓으로 사람들의 동정에 호소하지 않는다."]], [["There is a guard of sundry horsemen riding abreast of the tumbrils, and faces are often turned up to some of them, and they are asked some question.", "호송 수레들과 나란히 말을 탄 갖가지 기병들로 이루어진 호위대가 따라가고, 얼굴들이 종종 그들 가운데 몇몇을 향해 쳐들어지며, 그들에게 무언가를 묻는다."], ["It would seem to be always the same question, for, it is always followed by a press of people towards the third cart.", "늘 똑같은 질문인 듯한데, 그 질문 뒤에는 언제나 사람들이 세번째 수레 쪽으로 밀려들기 때문이다."], ["The horsemen abreast of that cart, frequently point out one man in it with their swords.", "그 수레와 나란히 가는 기병들은 자주 칼로 그 안의 한 남자를 가리킨다."], ["The leading curiosity is, to know which is he; he stands at the back of the tumbril with his head bent down, to converse with a mere girl who sits on the side of the cart, and holds his hand.", "가장 큰 호기심은 누가 그 사람인지 아는 것이다. 그는 호송 수레 뒤쪽에 서서 고개를 숙인 채, 수레 옆에 앉아 자기 손을 잡고 있는 한 어린 처녀와 이야기를 나눈다."], ["He has no curiosity or care for the scene about him, and always speaks to the girl.", "그는 주위의 광경에 아무런 호기심도 관심도 없이, 줄곧 그 처녀에게만 말한다."], ["Here and there in the long street of St. Honore, cries are raised against him.", "쌩또노레의 긴 거리 여기저기에서 그를 향해 고함이 터져나온다."], ["If they move him at all, it is only to a quiet smile, as he shakes his hair a little more loosely about his face.", "그것이 그를 조금이라도 움직인다면, 그는 그저 조용한 미소를 지으며 얼굴 위로 머리카락을 조금 더 느슨하게 흔들 뿐이다."], ["He cannot easily touch his face, his arms being bound.", "두 팔이 묶여 있어 얼굴을 쉬이 만질 수 없는 것이다."]], [["On the steps of a church, awaiting the coming-up of the tumbrils, stands the Spy and prison-sheep.", "호송 수레가 다가오기를 기다리며, 어느 교회의 계단 위에 그 첩자, 곧 감옥의 양이 서 있다."], ["He looks into the first of them: not there.", "그는 첫번째 수레를 들여다본다. 거기에 없다."], ["He looks into the second: not there.", "두번째 수레를 들여다본다. 거기에 없다."], ["He already asks himself, “Has he sacrificed me?”", "그는 벌써 \"저자가 나를 희생시킨 것일까?\""], ["when his face clears, as he looks into the third.", "하고 자문하다가, 세번째 수레를 들여다보고는 얼굴이 환해진다."]], [["“Which is Evrémonde?”", "\"어느 자가 에브레몽드요?\""], ["says a man behind him.", "그의 뒤에 있던 남자가 말한다."]], [["“That.", "\"저 사람이오."], ["At the back there.”", "저기 뒤쪽에.\""]], [["“With his hand in the girl’s?”", "\"처녀의 손을 잡고 있는?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["The man cries, “Down, Evrémonde!", "남자가 외친다. \"처형하라, 에브레몽드!"], ["To the Guillotine all aristocrats!", "모든 귀족을 기요띤으로!"], ["Down, Evrémonde!”", "처형하라, 에브레몽드!\""]], [["“Hush, hush!”", "\"쉿, 쉿!\""], ["the Spy entreats him, timidly.", "첩자가 겁먹은 듯 그에게 간청한다."]], [["“And why not, citizen?”", "\"왜 안되오, 시민?\""]], [["“He is going to pay the forfeit: it will be paid in five minutes more.", "\"저자는 죗값을 치르러 가는 길이오. 앞으로 오 분이면 다 치를 거요."], ["Let him be at peace.”", "그냥 평온하게 내버려두시오.\""]], [["But the man continuing to exclaim, “Down, Evrémonde!”", "그러나 남자가 계속해서 \"처형하라, 에브레몽드!\""], ["the face of Evrémonde is for a moment turned towards him.", "하고 외치자, 에브레몽드의 얼굴이 잠시 그를 향해 돌려진다."], ["Evrémonde then sees the Spy, and looks attentively at him, and goes his way.", "그러자 에브레몽드는 그 첩자를 보고, 그를 유심히 바라보더니, 다시 제 길을 간다."]], [["The clocks are on the stroke of three, and the furrow ploughed among the populace is turning round, to come on into the place of execution, and end.", "시계는 세시를 칠 참이고, 군중 사이로 갈아놓은 고랑은 방향을 틀어, 처형장 안으로 들어와 끝을 맺으려 한다."], ["The ridges thrown to this side and to that, now crumble in and close behind the last plough as it passes on, for all are following to the Guillotine.", "이쪽저쪽으로 던져졌던 이랑들이, 이제 지나가는 마지막 쟁기 뒤로 허물어져 모여드니, 모두가 기요띤을 향해 따라가기 때문이다."], ["In front of it, seated in chairs, as in a garden of public diversion, are a number of women, busily knitting.", "그 앞에는, 마치 공공 유원지의 정원에서처럼, 의자에 앉은 여인들이 여럿 부지런히 뜨개질을 하고 있다."], ["On one of the fore-most chairs, stands The Vengeance, looking about for her friend.", "맨 앞줄 의자 가운데 하나에는 복수의 여신이 서서 자기 친구를 두리번두리번 찾고 있다."]], [["“Thérèse!”", "\"떼레즈!\""], ["she cries, in her shrill tones.", "그녀가 날카로운 목소리로 외친다."], ["“Who has seen her?", "\"그 사람 본 사람 있어?"], ["Thérèse Defarge!”", "떼레즈 드파르주!\""]], [["“She never missed before,”", "\"저 사람은 전에 한번도 빠진 적이 없는데.\""], ["says a knitting-woman of the sisterhood.", "같은 자매단의 한 뜨개질하는 여인이 말한다."]], [["“No; nor will she miss now,”", "\"맞아. 이번에도 빠질 리 없지.\""], ["cries The Vengeance, petulantly.", "복수의 여신이 짜증스럽게 외친다."], ["“Thérèse.”", "\"떼레즈.\""]], [["“Louder,”", "\"더 크게 불러봐.\""], ["the woman recommends.", "여인이 권한다."]], [["Ay!", "아아!"], ["Louder, Vengeance, much louder, and still she will scarcely hear thee.", "더 크게, 복수의 여신이여, 훨씬 더 크게 불러도, 그래도 그녀는 그대의 소리를 거의 듣지 못하리라."], ["Louder yet, Vengeance, with a little oath or so added, and yet it will hardly bring her.", "더욱더 크게, 복수의 여신이여, 욕설이라도 한두 마디 보태어 불러도, 그래도 그녀를 데려오기는 어려우리라."], ["Send other women up and down to seek her, lingering somewhere; and yet, although the messengers have done dread deeds, it is questionable whether of their own wills they will go far enough to find her!", "다른 여인들을 이리저리 보내어, 어딘가에서 꾸물거리고 있을 그녀를 찾게 해보라. 그러나 그 심부름꾼들이 끔찍한 짓들을 저질러온 자들이라 한들, 제 발로 그녀를 찾을 만큼 멀리까지 갈지는 의문이로다!"]], [["“Bad Fortune!”", "\"운도 없지!\""], ["cries The Vengeance, stamping her foot in the chair, “and here are the tumbrils!", "복수의 여신이 의자 위에서 발을 구르며 외친다. \"벌써 호송 수레가 왔잖아!"], ["And Evrémonde will be despatched in a wink, and she not here!", "에브레몽드는 눈 깜짝할 새에 처치될 텐데, 그 사람은 여기 없다니!"], ["See her knitting in my hand, and her empty chair ready for her.", "봐, 내 손에 저 사람 뜨개질감이 있고, 빈 의자도 저 사람 자리로 준비돼 있는데."], ["I cry with vexation and disappointment!”", "분하고 실망스러워서 소리라도 지르고 싶어!\""]], [["As The Vengeance descends from her elevation to do it, the tumbrils begin to discharge their loads.", "복수의 여신이 그렇게 하려고 높은 자리에서 내려오는 사이, 호송 수레들이 그 짐을 부려놓기 시작한다."], ["The ministers of Sainte Guillotine are robed and ready.", "성녀 기요띤의 사제들은 예복을 갖추고 채비를 마쳤다. 쾅!"], ["Crash!--A head is held up, and the knitting-women who scarcely lifted their eyes to look at it a moment ago when it could think and speak, count One.", "머리 하나가 들어올려지고, 조금 전 그것이 생각하고 말할 수 있었을 때는 거의 눈길조차 들어 쳐다보지 않던 뜨개질하는 여인들이 하나, 하고 센다."]], [["The second tumbril empties and moves on; the third comes up.", "두번째 호송 수레가 비워지고 앞으로 나아간다. 세번째 수레가 다가온다."], ["Crash!--And the knitting-women, never faltering or pausing in their Work, count Two.", "쾅! 그러자 뜨개질하는 여인들은 일손을 한번도 더듬거나 멈추는 일 없이, 둘, 하고 센다."]], [["The supposed Evrémonde descends, and the seamstress is lifted out next after him.", "에브레몽드로 여겨지는 자가 내려오고, 그다음으로 그 재봉사 처녀가 들려 내려진다."], ["He has not relinquished her patient hand in getting out, but still holds it as he promised.", "그는 내리면서도 그녀의 참을성 있는 손을 놓지 않은 채, 약속한 대로 여전히 그 손을 잡고 있다."], ["He gently places her with her back to the crashing engine that constantly whirrs up and falls, and she looks into his face and thanks him.", "그는 끊임없이 윙윙거리며 올라갔다 떨어지는 그 우당탕거리는 기계를 등지도록 그녀를 가만히 세워주고, 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그에게 고마워한다."]], [["“But for you, dear stranger, I should not be so composed, for I am naturally a poor little thing, faint of heart; nor should I have been able to raise my thoughts to Him who was put to death, that we might have hope and comfort here to-day.", "\"낯선 분이지만 당신이 아니었다면, 저는 이렇게 침착하지 못했을 거예요. 저는 본디 가엾고 보잘것없는 데다 마음이 약한 것이라서요."], ["I think you were sent to me by Heaven.”", "또 우리가 오늘 여기서 희망과 위안을 얻을 수 있도록 죽임을 당하신 그분께로 제 생각을 들어올리지도 못했을 거예요. 당신은 하늘이 제게 보내주신 분 같아요.\""]], [["“Or you to me,” says Sydney Carton.", "\"아니면 당신이 내게 보내진 것이거나.\""], ["“Keep your eyes upon me, dear child, and mind no other object.”", "시드니 카턴이 말한다. \"내게서 눈을 떼지 마시오, 사랑스러운 아이여, 다른 어떤 것에도 마음 쓰지 말고.\""]], [["“I mind nothing while I hold your hand.", "\"당신 손을 잡고 있는 한 저는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않아요."], ["I shall mind nothing when I let it go, if they are rapid.”", "이 손을 놓더라도, 그들이 빠르게만 해준다면, 저는 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“They will be rapid.", "\"빠를 거요."], ["Fear not!”", "두려워 마시오!\""]], [["The two stand in the fast-thinning throng of victims, but they speak as if they were alone.", "두 사람은 빠르게 줄어드는 희생자들의 무리 가운데 서 있지만, 마치 단둘만 있는 듯 이야기를 나눈다."], ["Eye to eye, voice to voice, hand to hand, heart to heart, these two children of the Universal Mother, else so wide apart and differing, have come together on the dark highway, to repair home together, and to rest in her bosom.", "눈과 눈을 맞추고, 목소리와 목소리를 맞추고, 손과 손을 맞잡고, 가슴과 가슴을 맞댄 채, 그러지 않았다면 그토록 멀리 떨어진 채 서로 달랐을 만물의 어머니의 이 두 자식이, 함께 집으로 돌아가 그 품에서 쉬려고, 어두운 한길 위에서 함께 만난 것이다."]], [["“Brave and generous friend, will you let me ask you one last question?", "\"용감하고 너그러운 친구여, 마지막으로 한 가지만 여쭤봐도 될까요?"], ["I am very ignorant, and it troubles me--just a little.”", "저는 아주 무지한 데다, 그게 저를 괴롭혀요. 그저 조금요.\""]], [["“Tell me what it is.”", "\"그게 무엇인지 말해보시오.\""]], [["“I have a cousin, an only relative and an orphan, like myself, whom I love very dearly.", "\"제겐 사촌이 하나 있어요. 저처럼 단 하나뿐인 혈육이고 고아인데, 제가 무척 사랑하는 아이예요."], ["She is five years younger than I, and she lives in a farmer’s house in the south country.", "저보다 다섯 살 어리고, 남쪽 지방의 어느 농가에서 살고 있어요. 가난이 우리를 갈라놓았고, 그 애는 제 운명에 대해 아무것도 몰라요."], ["Poverty parted us, and she knows nothing of my fate--for I cannot write--and if I could, how should I tell her!", "저는 글을 쓸 줄 모르니까요. 또 쓸 줄 안다 한들, 어떻게 그 애에게 알리겠어요!"], ["It is better as it is.”", "이대로가 더 나아요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes: better as it is.”", "\"그렇소, 그래요. 이대로가 더 낫소.\""]], [["“What I have been thinking as we came along, and what I am still thinking now, as I look into your kind strong face which gives me so much support, is this:--If the Republic really does good to the poor, and they come to be less hungry, and in all ways to suffer less, she may live a long time: she may even live to be old.”", "\"우리가 여기로 오는 동안 제가 줄곧 생각하던 것, 그리고 제게 이토록 큰 힘을 주는 당신의 다정하고 굳센 얼굴을 올려다보는 지금도 여전히 생각하고 있는 것은 이거예요. 만약 공화국이 정말로 가난한 사람들에게 좋은 일을 해주어서, 그 애가 덜 굶주리게 되고 모든 면에서 덜 고통받게 된다면, 그 애는 오래 살지도 몰라요. 어쩌면 늙도록 살 수도 있겠지요.\""]], [["“What then, my gentle sister?”", "\"그러면 어떻다는 거요, 상냥한 누이여?\""]], [["“Do you think:”", "\"그러면 이런 생각이 들어요.\""], ["the uncomplaining eyes in which there is so much endurance, fill with tears, and the lips part a little more and tremble: “that it will seem long to me, while I wait for her in the better land where I trust both you and I will be mercifully sheltered?”", "그토록 많은 인내가 담긴 불평 없는 눈에 눈물이 차오르고, 입술이 조금 더 벌어지며 떨린다. \"당신과 제가 둘 다 자비롭게 보살핌받으리라 믿는 그 더 나은 땅에서, 제가 그 애를 기다리는 동안, 그 시간이 제겐 길게 느껴질까요?\""]], [["“It cannot be, my child; there is no Time there, and no trouble there.”", "\"그럴 리 없소, 나의 아이여. 그곳에는 시간이라는 것도 없고, 괴로움도 없으니.\""]], [["“You comfort me so much!", "\"당신은 제게 정말 큰 위안을 주시는군요!"], ["I am so ignorant.", "저는 너무도 무지해요."], ["Am I to kiss you now?", "이제 당신께 입맞춰도 될까요?"], ["Is the moment come?”", "그 순간이 온 건가요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["She kisses his lips; he kisses hers; they solemnly bless each other.", "그녀가 그의 입술에 입맞추고, 그가 그녀의 입술에 입맞추니, 두 사람은 엄숙하게 서로를 축복한다."], ["The spare hand does not tremble as he releases it; nothing worse than a sweet, bright constancy is in the patient face.", "그가 그녀의 가냘픈 손을 놓을 때, 그 손은 떨리지 않는다. 그 참을성 있는 얼굴에는 달콤하고 환한 한결같음 말고는 아무것도 깃들어 있지 않다."], ["She goes next before him--is gone; the knitting-women count Twenty-Two.", "그녀가 그보다 먼저 앞으로 나아가고, 사라진다. 뜨개질하는 여인들이 스물둘, 하고 센다."]], [["“I am the Resurrection and the Life, saith the Lord: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live: and whosoever liveth and believeth in me shall never die.”", "\"나는 부활이요 생명이니, 주께서 이르시되, 나를 믿는 자는 죽어도 살겠고, 무릇 살아서 나를 믿는 자는 영원히 죽지 아니하리라.\""]], [["The murmuring of many voices, the upturning of many faces, the pressing on of many footsteps in the outskirts of the crowd, so that it swells forward in a mass, like one great heave of water, all flashes away.", "많은 목소리들의 웅성거림, 많은 얼굴들이 쳐들리는 모습, 많은 발소리들이 군중의 가장자리에서 밀려들어, 마치 한차례 거대한 물결처럼 무리가 앞으로 부풀어오르는 것, 이 모든 것이 한순간에 번쩍 사라진다."], ["Twenty-Three.", "스물셋."]], [["They said of him, about the city that night, that it was the peacefullest man’s face ever beheld there.", "그날 밤 그 도시에서 사람들은 그에 대해, 그곳에서 일찍이 본 가장 평화로운 사람의 얼굴이었다고 말했다."], ["Many added that he looked sublime and prophetic.", "많은 이들은 그가 숭고하고 예언자적으로 보였다고 덧붙였다."]], [["One of the most remarkable sufferers by the same axe--a woman--had asked at the foot of the same scaffold, not long before, to be allowed to write down the thoughts that were inspiring her.", "같은 도끼에 스러진 가장 주목할 만한 수난자 가운데 한 사람, 곧 어느 여인은 얼마 전 바로 그 단두대 발치에서, 자신을 사로잡고 있던 생각들을 적어내려갈 수 있게 해달라고 청한 바 있었다."], ["If he had given any utterance to his, and they were prophetic, they would have been these:", "만약 그가 자신의 생각들을 어떤 말로든 토로했고, 또 그 말들이 예언적인 것이었다면, 그것들은 이러했으리라."]], [["“I see Barsad, and Cly, Defarge, The Vengeance, the Juryman, the Judge, long ranks of the new oppressors who have risen on the destruction of the old, perishing by this retributive instrument, before it shall cease out of its present use.", "\"나는 본다. 바사드와 클라이, 드파르주, 복수의 여신, 그 배심원, 그 판사, 옛 압제자들의 파멸 위에 새로이 일어선 새 압제자들의 긴 행렬이, 이 응징의 도구가 지금의 쓰임을 다하기 전에 바로 그 도구에 의해 스러져가는 것을."], ["I see a beautiful city and a brilliant people rising from this abyss, and, in their struggles to be truly free, in their triumphs and defeats, through long years to come, I see the evil of this time and of the previous time of which this is the natural birth, gradually making expiation for itself and wearing out.", "나는 본다. 이 심연에서 아름다운 도시와 빛나는 사람들이 솟아오르고, 진정 자유로워지려는 그들의 투쟁 속에서, 그들의 승리와 패배 속에서, 앞으로 다가올 긴 세월에 걸쳐, 이 시대의 악과 그 자연스러운 소산인 이전 시대의 악이 차츰 스스로 속죄하며 닳아 없어지는 것을."]], [["“I see the lives for which I lay down my life, peaceful, useful, prosperous and happy, in that England which I shall see no more.", "\"나는 본다. 내가 목숨을 바치는 그 생명들이, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 저 영국에서 평화롭고 쓸모 있고 번영하며 행복하게 사는 것을."], ["I see Her with a child upon her bosom, who bears my name.", "나는 그녀가 내 이름을 지닌 아이를 품에 안고 있는 것을 본다."], ["I see her father, aged and bent, but otherwise restored, and faithful to all men in his healing office, and at peace.", "나는 그녀의 아버지가, 나이 들고 허리가 굽었으되 그밖에는 회복되어, 그 치유의 직분으로 만인에게 충실하며 평온하게 지내는 것을 본다."], ["I see the good old man, so long their friend, in ten years’ time enriching them with all he has, and passing tranquilly to his reward.", "나는 그토록 오래 그들의 벗이었던 그 선량한 노인이, 십 년 뒤 자신이 가진 모든 것으로 그들을 부유하게 해주고, 평온하게 그의 보상을 받으러 떠나는 것을 본다."]], [["“I see that I hold a sanctuary in their hearts, and in the hearts of their descendants, generations hence.", "\"나는 본다. 내가 그들의 가슴속에, 그리고 그들의 후손들 가슴속에, 여러 세대가 지나도록 성소를 차지하고 있는 것을."], ["I see her, an old woman, weeping for me on the anniversary of this day.", "나는 그녀가 늙은 여인이 되어, 이날의 기일마다 나를 위해 우는 것을 본다."], ["I see her and her husband, their course done, lying side by side in their last earthly bed, and I know that each was not more honoured and held sacred in the other’s soul, than I was in the souls of both.", "나는 그녀와 그녀의 남편이, 그들의 여정을 마치고, 이 세상에서의 마지막 잠자리에 나란히 누워 있는 것을 보며, 그 둘 가운데 어느 누구도 서로의 영혼 속에서 받은 만큼의 존경과 신성함보다, 내가 그 두 사람의 영혼 속에서 받은 존경과 신성함이 덜하지 않았음을 안다."]], [["“I see that child who lay upon her bosom and who bore my name, a man winning his way up in that path of life which once was mine.", "\"나는 본다. 그녀의 품에 안겨 내 이름을 지녔던 그 아이가, 한때 나의 것이었던 그 삶의 길에서 자기 길을 헤쳐 올라가는 어른이 된 것을."], ["I see him winning it so well, that my name is made illustrious there by the light of his.", "나는 그가 그 길을 너무도 훌륭히 헤쳐나가, 그의 빛으로 인해 그곳에서 내 이름이 빛나게 되는 것을 본다."], ["I see the blots I threw upon it, faded away.", "나는 내가 그 이름 위에 던졌던 오점들이 바래어 사라지는 것을 본다. 나는 본다."], ["I see him, fore-most of just judges and honoured men, bringing a boy of my name, with a forehead that I know and golden hair, to this place--then fair to look upon, with not a trace of this day’s disfigurement--and I hear him tell the child my story, with a tender and a faltering voice.", "그가, 의로운 판관들과 존경받는 사람들 가운데 으뜸가는 사람이 되어, 내가 아는 이마와 금빛 머리카락을 지닌, 내 이름을 가진 사내아이를 이곳으로—오늘의 흉한 자취라곤 하나도 없이 바라보기에 아름다운 이곳으로—데려오는 것을. 그리고 나는 그가 다정하고 떨리는 목소리로 그 아이에게 내 이야기를 들려주는 것을 듣는다."]], [["“It is a far, far better thing that I do, than I have ever done; it is a far, far better rest that I go to than I have ever known.”", "\"내가 지금 하는 일은, 내가 일찍이 한 그 어떤 일보다도 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 일이다. 내가 지금 가서 누릴 안식은, 내가 일찍이 알던 그 어떤 안식보다도 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 안식이다.\""]]];
